window.KG = {"chapters":{"k:1":{"book":"kural","num":1,"title":"கடவுள் வாழ்த்து","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"An unnamed First One, known only through epithets, is made the ground of learning, freedom from anxiety and the crossing of the sea. The chapter's recurring claim is exclusive: without reaching those feet, these things are out of reach."},"k:2":{"book":"kural","num":2,"title":"வான்சிறப்பு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Rain is the unacknowledged condition of everything: food, farming, the sea's life, worship, giving, austerity and conduct. The chapter makes virtue and the gods depend on a natural cause."},"k:3":{"book":"kural","num":3,"title":"நீத்தார் பெருமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The greatness of those who have renounced: immeasurable, grounded in mastery of the senses, and shown in the force of their word and anger. It ends by saying that the name அந்தணர் belongs to those whose conduct is cool grace toward all beings."},"k:4":{"book":"kural","num":4,"title":"அறன்வலியுறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Virtue's power: nothing brings more gain, and forgetting it is the worst loss. The chapter defines it inwardly, as a clean mind free of four faults, and presses that it must be practised now and continually."},"k:5":{"book":"kural","num":5,"title":"இல்வாழ்க்கை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The household life as the support of everyone else and as the fullest form of virtue. Having just exalted renunciants in chapter 3, the book now asks what they could gain by leaving home, and the commentators work hard to limit that challenge."},"k:6":{"book":"kural","num":6,"title":"வாழ்க்கைத் துணைநலம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The wife as the household's real wealth. The chapter praises her steadfastness and even grants her power over the rain, yet it is told largely from the husband's side, and it measures her by what she makes possible for his life."},"k:7":{"book":"kural","num":7,"title":"புதல்வரைப் பெறுதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Children are the greatest thing one can obtain: first for the fruit they bring beyond this life, then for sensuous joy in them, and finally for their worth in the world. It moves from possessing them to being known by them, and the last verse has the father judged by the son."},"k:8":{"book":"kural","num":8,"title":"அன்புடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Love cannot be hidden, gives itself away to the bone, and is what makes a body alive. Without it, virtue burns a person and a life is a withered tree."},"k:9":{"book":"kural","num":9,"title":"விருந்தோம்பல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Hospitality is the reason for having a home and wealth at all. Hosting does not impoverish, refusing it makes a rich person poor, and it depends finally on the face with which a guest is met."},"k:10":{"book":"kural","num":10,"title":"இனியவைகூறல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Sweet speech is defined as loving, guileless and true, then ranked above generous giving. Its closing argument is almost one of common sense: why would anyone choose the unripe fruit?"},"k:11":{"book":"kural","num":11,"title":"செய்ந்நன்றி அறிதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Gratitude: help is measured by its timing, its motive and the worth of the one who receives it, not by its size. Memory is ordered accordingly, so that good is kept and harm is let go, and ingratitude is called the one sin with no escape."},"k:12":{"book":"kural","num":12,"title":"நடுவு நிலைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Impartiality: holding to what is due, whoever is involved, and giving up any gain that requires leaving it. The chapter's central image is the balance, and it treats loss incurred by being fair as no real loss."},"k:13":{"book":"kural","num":13,"title":"அடக்கமுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Self-restraint is the treasure that decides one's end. It works through body, speech and mind, and the chapter is most vivid on the tongue, whose burn alone never heals."},"k:14":{"book":"kural","num":14,"title":"ஒழுக்கமுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Conduct is to be guarded above life itself. It is what high birth really consists of, it outranks study, and its final test is living in accord with the world."},"k:15":{"book":"kural","num":15,"title":"பிறனில் விழையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Desire for another man's wife is the height of folly and a loss in every register at once. The chapter speaks to men, frames the woman as belonging to another, and calls restraint here the greatest manhood."},"k:16":{"book":"kural","num":16,"title":"பொறையுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Forbearance as strength: it wins the victory, it outlasts revenge's one day of pleasure, and for those who can bear harsh words it outranks fasting. Beyond it lies forgetting the wrong entirely."},"k:17":{"book":"kural","num":17,"title":"அழுக்காறாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Envy is a self-inflicted ruin that needs no enemy. The chapter insists that the envious never rise and the unenvious never fall, and yet it admits the world sometimes shows otherwise, which it says is to be pondered."},"k:18":{"book":"kural","num":18,"title":"வெஃகாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Coveting another's goods destroys one even in the wanting. Not wanting is presented as the real security of wealth, and it ends as a kind of pride and victory."},"k:19":{"book":"kural","num":19,"title":"புறங்கூறாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Backbiting is placed below open wrongdoing because it adds falsity to harm: the smile to the face after the slander behind the back. The remedy is simple and hard: to see one's own faults as one sees a stranger's."},"k:20":{"book":"kural","num":20,"title":"பயனில சொல்லாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Empty speech, especially before many, ruins the speaker's standing and even his claim to being fully human. The chapter ranks uselessness of speech as a graver failing than unkindness or lack of grace, and ends with a bare rule."},"k:21":{"book":"kural","num":21,"title":"தீவினையச்சம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Fear of evil deeds is presented as a mark of excellence and as enlightened self-interest. Harm done to others does not stay with them: it pursues the doer as surely as a shadow, so whoever loves himself will refrain."},"k:22":{"book":"kural","num":22,"title":"ஒப்புரவறிதல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"ஒப்புரவு is the obligation to make one's means serve the community, expecting nothing back as rain expects nothing. It is treated as the mark of being alive, and it holds even in poverty and at the cost of ruin."},"k:23":{"book":"kural","num":23,"title":"ஈகை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"True giving is giving to those who have nothing, and everything else is a kind of loan. The chapter ranks giving above reward, above ascetic endurance and even above life itself, and it argues from the giver's own joy and safety as well as from virtue."},"k:24":{"book":"kural","num":24,"title":"புகழ்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renown, earned above all through giving, is the one gain that outlasts the body. The chapter treats a life without it as a disgrace and a burden on the earth, indeed as no life at all."},"k:25":{"book":"kural","num":25,"title":"அருளுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"அருள், compassion extended to all living beings, is the highest wealth and the basis of other virtue. The chapter promises its possessor freedom from suffering and denies its lack any recovery, then closes by showing how it can be cultivated."},"k:26":{"book":"kural","num":26,"title":"புலான்மறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Eating flesh is incompatible with அருள், and the chapter argues this like a debate, answering those who say that intent is what matters and those who say that the guilt lies only with the killer. It ends by exalting the one who neither kills nor eats."},"k:27":{"book":"kural","num":27,"title":"தவம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"தவம் is defined simply as bearing pain and harming no one, and is then credited with immense powers, over enemies, wishes, and even death. The chapter makes it the one truly self-regarding work and the reason so few prosper."},"k:28":{"book":"kural","num":28,"title":"கூடாவொழுக்கம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The false renunciant, whose outward form hides an unrenounced heart, is exposed through a run of precise images. The chapter insists that conduct, not appearance, is the test, and ends by declaring the outward marks of renunciation unnecessary."},"k:29":{"book":"kural","num":29,"title":"கள்ளாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Theft begins as a thought, and its wish alone is an offence. The chapter sets களவு against அளவு, stealth against measure, as two settled dispositions that can't live together, and promises ruin to the one and heaven to the other."},"k:30":{"book":"kural","num":30,"title":"வாய்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Truthfulness is defined by what speech does rather than by whether it matches facts: speech without harm is truth, and a falsehood yielding faultless good counts as truth. The chapter then raises this virtue above austerity, giving, and every other good."},"k:31":{"book":"kural","num":31,"title":"வெகுளாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Anger is the self-destroying fire: it kills smiles and joy, burns the raft that would save a person, and returns on the one who holds it. Restraint counts only where anger could have had its way, and the one who drops it equals those who have renounced everything."},"k:32":{"book":"kural","num":32,"title":"இன்னாசெய்யாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Doing no hurt holds even when hurt would bring great wealth and even toward those who hurt first. The chapter's ground is self-knowledge, since a person who knows pain in his own life has no reason to inflict it, and it adds that hurt returns to its doer by the same day's end."},"k:33":{"book":"kural","num":33,"title":"கொல்லாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Not killing is the first of all goods, ranked explicitly above truthfulness and above all renunciation. It is absolute, holding even at the cost of one's own life and regardless of any gain, and the chapter ends with the fate of those who kill."},"k:34":{"book":"kural","num":34,"title":"நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Nothing lasts: wealth disperses like a theatre crowd, each day is a blade, and life is a lodger in the body. The error is mistaking this for permanence, and the practical conclusion is urgency: do good now."},"k:35":{"book":"kural","num":35,"title":"துறவு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renunciation is the removal of attachment to what is 'mine' and even to the 'I'. It begins with freedom from each thing's pain and reaches release, while those who keep any hold stay in the net. The chapter ends in the paradox that one must cling to get free of clinging."},"k:36":{"book":"kural","num":36,"title":"மெய்யுணர்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Seeing what is really so is what ends suffering and birth: mistaking the unreal for the real starts it, and stripping off appearances ends it. The chapter never says what மெய்ப்பொருள் is, and this silence is where its commentators divide most."},"k:37":{"book":"kural","num":37,"title":"அவாவறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Craving is the seed of all birth and the sorrow of sorrows, and its removal, not any other act, is the direct cause of an unchanging state. The whole section on renunciation ends here, by locating release at the moment when wanting stops."},"k:38":{"book":"kural","num":38,"title":"ஊழ்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Fate as the force behind wealth, understanding, and pleasure and pain. It reaches even the qualities a person might rely on against it, such as effort and intelligence."},"k:39":{"book":"kural","num":39,"title":"இறைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes a king excellent: not power alone but a set of resources and qualities that must be held without lapse, and above all how he is to the people who approach him. His standing is conferred from below, by those who praise him, shelter under him, and set him apart."},"k:40":{"book":"kural","num":40,"title":"கல்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Learning as the thing that makes a person see, belong anywhere, and possess what can't be lost. It begins and is only complete in conduct: to learn is also to stand by what is learned."},"k:41":{"book":"kural","num":41,"title":"கல்லாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The emptiness of being unlearned: the unlearned person's speech, native wit, looks, wealth and rank all fail to count. The chapter is the harshest in the book on this point, and its contempt is largely about assemblies and public standing."},"k:42":{"book":"kural","num":42,"title":"கேள்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Listening to the wise as a form of learning open even to those who haven't studied. The chapter treats the ear as an organ of nourishment and taste, rivalling the mouth, and it softens the verdict of the previous chapter."},"k:43":{"book":"kural","num":43,"title":"அறிவுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding as protection and as practice: it steers the mind, tests what is heard, moves with the world, foresees what is coming and knows what to fear. After three chapters on acquiring knowledge, this one asks what it is to have it."},"k:44":{"book":"kural","num":44,"title":"குற்றங்கடிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A ruler's real enemy is his own faults: arrogance, anger, desire, avarice, misplaced pride and self-regard. Small ones must be treated as large and dealt with before they arrive, and only one who has cleared his own is fit to judge others."},"k:45":{"book":"kural","num":45,"title":"பெரியாரைத் துணைக்கோடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A ruler's power depends on those greater than himself whom he can win and keep, and above all on those who will correct him. The chapter counts this as the highest strength and calls its lack self-destruction."},"k:46":{"book":"kural","num":46,"title":"சிற்றினஞ்சேராமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding and goodness are not sealed within a person; they take on the nature of the company one keeps, as water takes on its soil. The chapter gradually grants the mind its goodness while insisting that company still strengthens or spoils it."},"k:47":{"book":"kural","num":47,"title":"தெரிந்துசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Acting well begins before the act: weighing cost and return, taking counsel, and matching the means to the person and to one's own position. The chapter's vocabulary is that of a careful ledger, and its fear is less of failure than of being laughed at for failing."},"k:48":{"book":"kural","num":48,"title":"வலியறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Knowing strength: the deed's, one's own, the opponent's and the allies', and treating wealth as a form of strength with a limit. The theme is limits, and most of the chapter warns against zeal, generosity and pride that outrun them."},"k:49":{"book":"kural","num":49,"title":"காலமறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Strength is not a fixed quantity but depends on when it is used. The chapter's real teaching is about waiting: holding back, even bowing and hiding one's anger, then striking without hesitation when the moment comes. Its stance is cold and tactical."},"k:50":{"book":"kural","num":50,"title":"இடனறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Power belongs to the ground it stands on. Through animals and vehicles, the chapter shows the strong undone off their own ground and the weak made strong on theirs, and it warns against attacking even people with nothing but their home."},"k:51":{"book":"kural","num":51,"title":"தெரிந்துதெளிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"How a ruler comes to trust someone: test first and trust only after that, and once trust is settled, don't doubt it. The chapter doesn't expect the flawless. It looks at what predominates, and deeds are its final test."},"k:52":{"book":"kural","num":52,"title":"தெரிந்துவினையாடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Having tested someone, how to use them: match person, means, task and time, hand the work over fully, and don't resent the familiarity it brings, yet look at their conduct every day. Trust and scrutiny are both required, and the chapter doesn't entirely settle how."},"k:53":{"book":"kural","num":53,"title":"சுற்றந்தழால்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Holding kin close as a political and material matter: kin stay when wealth goes, their love multiplies gain, and wealth's use lies in being surrounded by them. They have to be kept through giving, soft speech and absence of anger, and they can be won back once estranged."},"k:54":{"book":"kural","num":54,"title":"பொச்சாவாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Heedlessness, especially the forgetting that comes with joy and success, is more dangerous than rage. To keep in mind what one has resolved is the skill on which everything else depends, and it is an inward fort or an inward leak."},"k:55":{"book":"kural","num":55,"title":"செங்கோன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Justice as the root of a king's power: the people live by his sceptre as the world lives by rain, and it wins him battles, brings rain and protects him in turn. The chapter ends by defending punishment, even death, as part of just rule."},"k:56":{"book":"kural","num":56,"title":"கொடுங்கோன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Oppressive rule as a crime and a self-destruction: the king who extorts is a murderer and a highwayman, and his people's tears wear away his wealth. Its effects reach everything, turning wealth into a burden, and in the older reading failing the rain, the cows and the sacred books."},"k:57":{"book":"kural","num":57,"title":"வெருவந்தசெய்யாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Punishment belongs to rule, but dread does not. Disproportion, inaccessibility, a harsh face and harsh words turn subjects' fear into the slow or sudden ruin of the one who causes it."},"k:58":{"book":"kural","num":58,"title":"கண்ணோட் டம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Kindly regard is what eyes are for and what keeps the world's dealings going; eyes without it are wounds or dead wood. Yet the chapter also sets a limit on it, that it must not undo one's duty, and then praises enduring poison for its sake."},"k:59":{"book":"kural","num":59,"title":"ஒற்றாடல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Intelligence-gathering is treated as the sight of the state and as a discipline of evidence: a spy must watch everyone and hold firm, and the king must not trust any one spy."},"k:60":{"book":"kural","num":60,"title":"ஊக்கமுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Energy of mind is the only possession that truly belongs to a person. Wealth leaves and returns, but this inner drive sets how high one rises, and without it strength and even humanity count for little."},"k:61":{"book":"kural","num":61,"title":"மடியின்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Sloth is not a private weakness but the ruin of a lineage: it puts out the family's light, hands a person to enemies, and can be reversed only by the one who harbours it."},"k:62":{"book":"kural","num":62,"title":"ஆள்வினையுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Sustained labour builds the doer, funds the virtue of giving and supports one's kin. Faced with fate, the chapter shifts the question: failure of fortune carries no blame, labour always earns its wage, and persistence can outlast what opposes it."},"k:63":{"book":"kural","num":63,"title":"இடுக்கணழியாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Trouble need not break a person: it can be laughed at, thought away, outlasted, or expected as the body's lot, until suffering itself seems to suffer. The chapter is a series of stances, not one remedy, and it closes the book's treatment of the king."},"k:64":{"book":"kural","num":64,"title":"அமைச்சு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The minister is defined by what he can do: judge means and time, split and join alliances, unite innate acuity with learning and with the ways of the world. The chapter also assigns him a duty to speak even to a king who won't listen, and warns that a false minister is worse than a multitude of enemies."},"k:65":{"book":"kural","num":65,"title":"சொல்வன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Speech is an excellence in its own right and a source of gain and ruin. Its mastery means fitting the hearer, being unanswerable, being brief when one can and expansive when one must; learning that can't be spoken has no fragrance."},"k:66":{"book":"kural","num":66,"title":"வினைத்தூய்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The worth of an action lies in its cleanness: no gain, necessity or even a mother's hunger justifies what the worthy condemn, because tainted gain departs as it came and leaves ruin behind."},"k:67":{"book":"kural","num":67,"title":"வினைத்திட்பம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Firmness in action is firmness of mind, and nothing else substitutes for it. Speaking is easy and doing is hard, but one who stays firm gets what he has thought of, however small he looks and whatever pain lies on the way."},"k:68":{"book":"kural","num":68,"title":"வினைசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Deliberation ends in decision and decision must not stall; beyond that, the chapter gives a practical method of action and alliance, measured to whether one is stronger, an equal, or weaker than the other side."},"k:69":{"book":"kural","num":69,"title":"தூது","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"An envoy must combine love, knowledge, learning and presence with exact speech, courage and incorruptibility, and must carry his sovereign's interest to a foreign court even at the cost of his life."},"k:70":{"book":"kural","num":70,"title":"மன்னரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Serving a king is like warming oneself at a fire: useful, dangerous, and demanding constant awareness of distance. The chapter gives rules for courtiers who can't presume on favour, age, kinship or old intimacy, and is candid about the danger behind the courtesy."},"k:71":{"book":"kural","num":71,"title":"குறிப்பறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Reading what is unspoken from face and eye is the highest social faculty. The chapter places this skill near the divine, treats the eyes that can't do it as useless, and holds that the face betrays what the heart holds."},"k:72":{"book":"kural","num":72,"title":"அவையறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Speech can't be judged apart from its hearers. One must read the assembly and adjust: shine among the bright, stay silent or whitewashed among the dull, step carefully among the great, and never spill nectar where it can't be received."},"k:73":{"book":"kural","num":73,"title":"அவையஞ்சாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Fearlessness before an assembly is rarer than courage in battle, and it's the test of learning: knowledge that can't be spoken there is a sword in a frightened hand, and its holder counts for less than the unlearned and little more than the dead."},"k:74":{"book":"kural","num":74,"title":"நாடு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes a land deserve the name: people and yield that do not fail, resilience under burden and damage, and freedom from hunger, disease and faction. All of it is void if land and ruler are not in accord."},"k:75":{"book":"kural","num":75,"title":"அரண்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The fort: what makes it hard to take and easy to hold, from terrain and walls to food, arms and fighters. Its last verse says all of this depends on the people inside knowing how to act."},"k:76":{"book":"kural","num":76,"title":"பொருள்செயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The making of wealth: it is the great social force that raises the despised, drives out enemies and brings virtue and pleasure within reach, but only wealth rightly gained deserves pursuit."},"k:77":{"book":"kural","num":77,"title":"படைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"What makes an army: inherited courage, unity before death, honour, and discipline rather than numbers. It fails when the king neglects it or when it lacks leaders."},"k:78":{"book":"kural","num":78,"title":"படைச்செருக்கு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The warrior's pride, spoken largely from within: boast, disdain for easy prey, mercy as courage's edge, and death sought as honour. Its ethic is one of glory and is not softened by the rest of the Kural."},"k:79":{"book":"kural","num":79,"title":"நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendship as a rare achievement and the strongest guard. It grows with use, lives in the heart rather than on the face, corrects as well as comforts, and moves to help as instinctively as a hand to a slipping garment."},"k:80":{"book":"kural","num":80,"title":"நட்பாராய்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Testing friends before committing, because a friendship once formed should not be left. It asks what to test for, finds that ruin is the most reliable test, and accepts that bad friendships must sometimes be paid to end."},"k:81":{"book":"kural","num":81,"title":"பழைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Old friendship as the freedom to take liberties with each other. It teaches welcoming what a friend presumes to do, reading hurt charitably, and staying even through harm the friend causes."},"k:82":{"book":"kural","num":82,"title":"தீ நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A catalogue of false friendship: the self-serving, the unreliable, fools, jesters and the two-faced. Its rhetoric of comparison ranks all of them below loneliness and even below enemies."},"k:83":{"book":"kural","num":83,"title":"கூடாநட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendship with one who is inwardly hostile: smiles, soft words, folded hands and even tears can hide a weapon. In the end the chapter tells the reader to answer such a person with the same outward show."},"k:84":{"book":"kural","num":84,"title":"பேதைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Folly as choosing loss over gain. It is not ignorance as such: its worst form belongs to the learned who do not restrain themselves, and it spoils whatever the fool touches, whether work, wealth, friendship or an assembly."},"k:85":{"book":"kural","num":85,"title":"புல்லறிவாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Petty knowledge that thinks itself wise. It is the poverty that counts most, it does worse harm than any enemy, and it can't be taught, because the person who has it is already sure that he knows."},"k:86":{"book":"kural","num":86,"title":"இகல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Hostility, as a disease of the disposition to quarrel. Giving it up is shown as gain and strength more than as saintliness, and pursuing it as a way to one's own ruin."},"k:87":{"book":"kural","num":87,"title":"பகைமாட்சி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The enemy worth having, seen coldly: pick the weak, and notice who makes himself easy to beat through anger, ignorance, stinginess and isolation. Read another way, its list of vices is a portrait of how not to become someone's easy enemy."},"k:88":{"book":"kural","num":88,"title":"பகைத்திறந்தெரிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"How to handle enmity as a strategic matter. Don't seek it, avoid certain enemies above all, turn some into friends and others into neutrals, hide your weakness, and cut the rest down while they are still young."},"k:89":{"book":"kural","num":89,"title":"உட்பகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The enemy within the circle: kin and companions whose hostility is hidden. It is worse than an open foe because it is unseen, it wears a family down slowly, and even a sesame-sized amount of it holds ruin."},"k:90":{"book":"kural","num":90,"title":"பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not offending the great: to slight those with real power is to call down one's own ruin, and no wealth, fort or ally protects against them. Who the great are is the key question, and the verses themselves mostly leave it open."},"k:91":{"book":"kural","num":91,"title":"பெண்வழிச்சேறல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A man who lets his wife's will or his desire for her govern him loses virtue, wealth, standing and even pleasure. The chapter treats household subordination as a failure of his own agency and of his duties to others, and it speaks throughout from the husband's side."},"k:92":{"book":"kural","num":92,"title":"வரைவின்மகளிர்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Women who sell intimacy are portrayed as a danger to men because their words and embrace are divided from their hearts. The chapter's argument is that the falseness is detectable, and that who touches them reveals the quality of the man. It gives no view from the women's side."},"k:93":{"book":"kural","num":93,"title":"கள்ளுண்ணாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Drink destroys standing, shame and understanding, can't be hidden, and can't be argued away. The only exit the chapter offers is the drinker's own recognition during a sober moment."},"k:94":{"book":"kural","num":94,"title":"சூது","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Gambling is ruin even when it pays, because a win is the bait that holds the player. It takes wealth first, then truthfulness, compassion and the basics of living, and the attachment grows with each loss."},"k:95":{"book":"kural","num":95,"title":"மருந்து","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Medicine as a matter of measure. Illness comes from imbalance, the best remedy is eating with judgment so that no remedy is needed, and when illness does come, healing depends on careful diagnosis and on a whole system of people."},"k:96":{"book":"kural","num":96,"title":"குடிமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"High birth as a set of obligations and marks: integrity, shame, truthfulness, generous speech and giving, held to even in poverty. The chapter speaks as if birth produced these qualities, while its own tests make conduct the evidence of birth, and the commentators pull hard in opposite directions on that point."},"k:97":{"book":"kural","num":97,"title":"மானம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Honour ranks above profit, fame and survival itself: one abandons even indispensable acts that would lower one, and one who has lost standing is as worthless as fallen hair. The chapter ends by praising those who would rather die than live degraded."},"k:98":{"book":"kural","num":98,"title":"பெருமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Greatness is not given by birth, rank or wealth. It consists of high aim, self-guarding, doing the rare thing, freedom from conceit and covering others' weakness, and smallness is the same set of features reversed."},"k:99":{"book":"kural","num":99,"title":"சான்றாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Fullness of character: the worthy hold all good qualities as their own, rest on five pillars, don't tell others' faults, and do good to those who harm them. This fullness doesn't depend on poverty or wealth, and the chapter finally places the earth's stability upon it."},"k:100":{"book":"kural","num":100,"title":"பண்புடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Courtesy of character, பண்பு, is what makes one fully human and keeps the world going. It reaches even to enemies, begins in being approachable, and without it sharp intelligence and great wealth come to nothing."},"k:101":{"book":"kural","num":101,"title":"நன்றியில்செல்வம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Wealth that is neither enjoyed nor given is effectively no wealth. It does nothing for the holder, the needy or the holder's heirs, and it turns a person into something the world doesn't want. The chapter measures wealth entirely by its use."},"k:102":{"book":"kural","num":102,"title":"நாணுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Shame over wrongdoing is the distinguishing mark of a human being and the seat of worth. It ranks above life, conduct and the whole earth, and those without it only imitate being alive."},"k:103":{"book":"kural","num":103,"title":"குடிசெயல்வகை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Raising one's people is the highest greatness. It requires ceaseless effort and knowledge, draws divine help and willing kin, ignores season and pride, and costs the one who does it his own bodily ease. A family with no one to carry it falls."},"k:104":{"book":"kural","num":104,"title":"உழவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Farming is the world's first occupation because everyone, including kings and renouncers, ends up depending on it. Those who live by it alone owe nothing to anyone, and the land itself responds to attention, as a wife or a mocking woman would."},"k:105":{"book":"kural","num":105,"title":"நல்குரவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Poverty as the worst suffering, one that has no comparison. It strips lineage, speech, learning and even a mother's regard, it returns day after day, and the chapter's last verse asks whether such a person should go on as they are."},"k:106":{"book":"kural","num":106,"title":"இரவு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Asking is permitted, even graceful, when the giver is one who can't conceal. The chapter then goes further and says that the world needs askers, since without them giving, fame and even being alive would lose their point. It closes by telling the asker not to be angry at refusal."},"k:107":{"book":"kural","num":107,"title":"இரவச்சம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Dread of begging: never asking, even from the best givers, is worth ten million times what asking could bring. The chapter's fury goes so far as a curse on whoever ordered the world so that begging is needed, and it holds the withholder guiltier still."},"k:108":{"book":"kural","num":108,"title":"கயமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"A bitterly ironic portrait of the base: they look perfectly human, are as free as gods, and are happier than the good because nothing troubles them. Only fear and greed move them, and at the first trouble they sell themselves. The chapter ends the whole Book of Wealth."},"k:109":{"book":"kural","num":109,"title":"தகையணங்குறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man's first sight of the woman, told as an injury: her beauty and returned gaze attack him like a goddess, an army, Death and a rutting elephant. The chapter treats love's beginning as the defeat of a strong man, and ends by claiming that love, unlike liquor, intoxicates through sight alone."},"k:110":{"book":"kural","num":110,"title":"குறிப்பறிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Reading desire that won't declare itself. The lovers communicate through lowered eyes, half-smiles and feigned indifference, until spoken words are declared useless."},"k:111":{"book":"kural","num":111,"title":"புணர்ச்சிமகிழ்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man's joy in union, in which one person holds all five senses, cures the sickness she caused, and rivals heaven and the virtuous householder's meal. Its last verse compares this desire to knowledge, which only reveals how much more there is."},"k:112":{"book":"kural","num":112,"title":"நலம்புனைந்துரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The man praises her beauty through deliberate hyperbole, defeating the standard comparisons: flowers, the moon, swan's down. As the praise goes on, it becomes a claim of exclusivity, and what is seen by many ceases to compare with what he alone sees."},"k:113":{"book":"kural","num":113,"title":"காதற்சிறப்புரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Each lover declares what this love is. He makes it a bond of body and life, and she makes it an indwelling so complete that separation is impossible, which leaves the townspeople's talk of an absent, unloving man simply mistaken."},"k:114":{"book":"kural","num":114,"title":"நாணுத்துறவுரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Declaring that shame has been given up. The man threatens to ride the palmyra horse, a public humiliation that forces the issue, while the woman, who may not do so, watches her love go into the streets on its own."},"k:115":{"book":"kural","num":115,"title":"அலரறிவுறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The lovers turn the town's gossip around. Talk meant to shame and stop them feeds the love, keeps the man alive, and can even press for marriage or elopement. The chapter treats public scandal as an unintended gift."},"k:116":{"book":"kural","num":116,"title":"பிரிவாற்றாமை","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The inability to bear parting. Mostly in the woman's voice, the chapter argues against his leaving before it happens: promises of a quick return are useless to one who won't survive, and a broken 'fear not' makes trust itself hard."},"k:117":{"book":"kural","num":117,"title":"படர்மெலிந்திரங்கல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman wastes away in grief through the night, caught between a love she can't hide and a shame that forbids telling him. Love is measured against the sea and against the night, and the only company that remains is the night itself."},"k:118":{"book":"kural","num":118,"title":"கண்விதுப்பழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman puts her own eyes on trial. They showed her the man, so they caused the sickness; now they weep, can't sleep and betray her secret, and she answers with bitter laughter and a curse. Blaming a body part makes her suffering speakable."},"k:119":{"book":"kural","num":119,"title":"பசப்புறுபருவரல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The pallor of longing (பசலை), the woman's most visible symptom, personified as a creature that rides her body, moves in the instant he moves away, and earns her the town's blame while he escapes it. She ends by offering her body as the price of his good name."},"k:120":{"book":"kural","num":120,"title":"தனிப்படர்மிகுதி","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The surfeit of lonely grief turns into an argument about reciprocity. Mutual love is the seedless fruit and the timely rain; one-sided love is a carrying pole weighted at one end. The chapter asks what love is worth if it isn't returned."},"k:121":{"book":"kural","num":121,"title":"நினைந்தவர்புலம்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Remembering the absent lover as both the only thing keeping one alive and a burning pain. The chapter starts with a man's claim that memory itself gives joy, and turns to a woman who wonders whether she exists in his thoughts at all."},"k:122":{"book":"kural","num":122,"title":"கனவுநிலையுரைத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Dreams as the woman's way of staying alive. She argues that a lover who won't come when she is awake comes when she sleeps, and so waking life is the villain and the gossips are simply people who have never dreamed of a beloved."},"k:123":{"book":"kural","num":123,"title":"பொழுதுகண்டிரங்கல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Evening, once a friend, returns as an executioner. The woman speaks to the hour, quarrels with it and maps her sickness through the day, ending on the man whose nature, she says, has become wealth itself."},"k:124":{"book":"kural","num":124,"title":"உறுப்புநலனழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman's body as a public record of his absence. Eyes, shoulders and forehead lose their beauty, and each speaker reads that record differently: the friend sees an accusation, the woman refuses to let it be one, and the man, far away and turning for home, sees a reason to hurry."},"k:125":{"book":"kural","num":125,"title":"நெஞ்சொடுகிளத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The woman argues with her own heart about what to do: find a remedy, stop suffering, go to him, or forget him. The heart is a separate party that can be scolded, sent away and accused, and the chapter ends without any option having won."},"k:126":{"book":"kural","num":126,"title":"நிறையழிதல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The breaking of self-containment (நிறை). Desire is an axe against a door, a sneeze that can't be held back, a tyrant working her at midnight. The chapter ends with a woman who sets out to sulk and finds her heart has already gone over to him."},"k:127":{"book":"kural","num":127,"title":"அவர்வயின்விதும்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Impatience for reunion on both sides of the distance. She has worn down her fingers counting days and rehearses how she will greet him, and, on Parimelazhagar's reading, he is stuck on campaign and starting to fear he may come back too late."},"k:128":{"book":"kural","num":128,"title":"குறிப்பறிவுறுத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Communicating what can't be said. Thread within crystal, scent within a bud, and the movement of her gaze from bangles to shoulders to feet: unspoken signs carry the fear of a new parting and the resolve to go with him. It is the book's mature counterpart to the first signs of chapter 110."},"k:129":{"book":"kural","num":129,"title":"புணர்ச்சிவிதும்பல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The eagerness for union overrides every plan to sulk. She can't see his faults once he is in view, and sets out to quarrel but her heart betrays her. The chapter's most quoted verse states why: love is more delicate than a flower, and few catch its moment."},"k:130":{"book":"kural","num":130,"title":"நெஞ்சொடுபுலத்தல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Quarrelling with one's own heart for taking the other side. The woman charges it with disloyalty for loving him when she wants to sulk, and the chapter ends in the man's voice, on Parimelazhagar's reading, with both lovers facing the same discovery: one's own heart may not be on one's side."},"k:131":{"book":"kural","num":131,"title":"புலவி","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Sulking and its right measure. The quarrel is salt: just enough makes love tasty and a little more spoils it. Nothing holds it in check except that the other person cares, as the chapter's closing verses, in which the man finds her sulk has gone too far, make clear."},"k:132":{"book":"kural","num":132,"title":"புலவி நுணுக்கம்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The fine points of sulking, a comedy of jealous logic. Every word he says, every sneeze, whether he hides it or not, whether he pleads or looks, is turned into evidence. It is the most purely entertaining chapter in the Kural, and it also shows how love trains two people in close reading."},"k:133":{"book":"kural","num":133,"title":"ஊடலுவகை","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The joy of the quarrel, and the end of the Kural. Sulking is here set above heaven, and above union itself as digestion is above eating; the loser wins, and a man prays for the night to last. The whole work ends not on virtue or God but on a condition about an embrace."},"n:1":{"book":"naladiyar","num":1,"title":"செல்வம் நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Wealth does not stay with anyone: the feasting rich end up begging and kings lose their wives to enemies. Because death is near and certain, wealth should be shared and spent on virtue now, and the hoarder who neither enjoys nor gives has in effect already lost it."},"n:2":{"book":"naladiyar","num":2,"title":"இளமை நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Youth does not last: old age, the loss of friends and the decay of the beloved's body come to everyone, and death can take the young as well as the old. The wise therefore renounce early and do virtue while they have the means."},"n:3":{"book":"naladiyar","num":3,"title":"யாக்கை நிலையாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The body does not last: kings die like everyone, wedding drums turn into funeral drums, and the body is a bubble, a passing cloud or dew on grass that the life within leaves behind. Because of this, the body should be used for its proper fruit, virtue and service, while it lasts."},"n:4":{"book":"naladiyar","num":4,"title":"அறன் வலியுறுத்தல்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"An urging to virtue: human birth is rare and the body brief, so its fruit, virtue, must be gathered now. Those who live on the merit of past births without adding to it end at others' gates; those who have gathered it face death without grief, and even a small gift to the worthy grows vast."},"n:5":{"book":"naladiyar","num":5,"title":"தூய்தன்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"The body is impure: beneath skin, perfume and garlands it is a vessel of flesh, fluids and filth, and in death it is food for vultures and a grinning skull. Desire for a woman's body rests on not seeing this, and the renunciant who has seen it is not moved by the conventional praise of eyes and teeth."},"n:6":{"book":"naladiyar","num":6,"title":"துறவு","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Renunciation, argued from impermanence: the wise see that home, youth, wealth and pleasure do not last and that pleasure is mixed with pain, so they leave household life without delay, keep their vows under hardship, pity those who insult them, and master the senses to reach release."},"n:7":{"book":"naladiyar","num":7,"title":"சினம் இன்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Freedom from anger: the worthy let honour and insult pass alike, guard their tongues, bear harsh words from those beneath them and do not return harm, while their anger, when it comes, cools of itself. The base nurse and broadcast theirs."},"n:8":{"book":"naladiyar","num":8,"title":"பொறையுடைமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Forbearance carried beyond insult into friendship and privation: avoid fools, bear inferiors, value a friend's harsh words, bear with or quietly leave an erring friend, starve rather than beg from the graceless, and refuse blameworthy pleasure, harm and falsehood even at the greatest cost."},"n:9":{"book":"naladiyar","num":9,"title":"பிறர்மனை நயவாமை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Not desiring another man's wife, argued mostly from consequences: constant fear, small pleasure, the king's punishment, family disgrace, hell and a degraded rebirth. The last quatrains turn from the act to desire itself, a fire worse than any outer fire, which the strong suppress in silence."},"n:10":{"book":"naladiyar","num":10,"title":"ஈகை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Giving: give gladly and daily within one's means, even when poor and even a grain's worth, to those who cannot repay, because death and age are near and wealth comes and goes by past merit, not by clutching. The fame of giving outlasts all else, and one who cannot give should at least not beg."},"n:11":{"book":"naladiyar","num":11,"title":"பழவினை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Old deeds (பழவினை) find their doer and settle each person's share of joy and suffering. Wishing, effort, holiness and learning cannot move that share, so the chapter ends by asking why anyone should grieve over it."},"n:12":{"book":"naladiyar","num":12,"title":"மெய்ம்மை","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Things as they truly are (மெய்ம்மை): the hard facts of the world, as opposed to how people would like it to be. Natures do not change, kin come with fortune and leave with it, the rich are heard and the poor are not, and in the end only one's deeds go with one."},"n:13":{"book":"naladiyar","num":13,"title":"தீவினையச்சம்","section":"அறத்துப்பால்","theme":"Dread of evil deeds (தீவினையச்சம்): harm done to creatures returns in kind, and bad company is itself a source of evil, turning even the upright. The chapter ends by urging the heart to give up household attachment, since only good done profits the soul."},"n:14":{"book":"naladiyar","num":14,"title":"கல்வி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Learning (கல்வி) is the only true beauty, a medicine for delusion and the best legacy, and it outranks birth. Because life is short, one must choose what to learn, keep learned company, and prefer the books of wisdom that free one from confusion to merely worldly study."},"n:15":{"book":"naladiyar","num":15,"title":"குடிப்பிறப்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Noble birth (குடிப்பிறப்பு) shows in conduct that holds through poverty: the well-born keep their principles, courtesy and generosity when they have nothing, while the low-born fail to do good even when they can. The chapter treats these qualities as belonging to birth."},"n:16":{"book":"naladiyar","num":16,"title":"மேன்மக்கள்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Portraits of the noble (மேன்மக்கள்): they cannot bear a stain, aim high whatever the outcome, stay within bounds in want, keep their speech clean under abuse and welcome those who come to ask. Wealth draws the base, but meeting the well-born is the real treasure."},"n:17":{"book":"naladiyar","num":17,"title":"பெரியாரைப் பிழையாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not wronging the great: their goodwill, once lost, cannot be restored, their anger reaches into any fortress, and their esteem is the only esteem worth having. Time with them is precious, and their bond grows where the bond of the small shrinks."},"n:18":{"book":"naladiyar","num":18,"title":"நல்லினம் சேர்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Joining good company: it washes out the faults of youth, raises the lowly as river water sanctifies sewage, protects the weak and hides meanness. Life's pain makes the company of the wise the thing to seek, and bad company ruins even the pure."},"n:19":{"book":"naladiyar","num":19,"title":"பெருமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Greatness of character: it renounces when the season of giving and loving has passed, sees home and its supports as passing, and keeps giving even in decline. Its faults show more than others' crimes, it stays unshaken by slander, and it feeds others before itself."},"n:20":{"book":"naladiyar","num":20,"title":"தாளாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Energetic effort (தாளாண்மை): it makes a person, as a twig hardens into an elephant post, supports kin and an ageing father, and is what lineage really consists of. Manliness is persisting when things fail, and food won by one's own effort tastes better than a patron's feast."},"n:21":{"book":"naladiyar","num":21,"title":"சுற்றந்தழால்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Holding one's own close: the sympathy of kin dissolves suffering, the worthy bear everyone who leans on them without sorting people into ours and others, and plain food from those who love is sweeter than a feast from those who don't. The bond is proved when it goes into the fire with you."},"n:22":{"book":"naladiyar","num":22,"title":"நட்பாராய்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Test a friend before taking him: by learning, by family, by loyalty under injury, by constancy, and by whether he actually helps. The reason is given at the end: once a bond is formed, parting hurts even when the friend was a snake."},"n:23":{"book":"naladiyar","num":23,"title":"நட்பிற் பிழை பொறுத்தல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Once a friend has been chosen, bear their faults: everything has its husk, the patience of one keeps two in friendship, and one who exposes or pries into a friend's faults is worse than the friend."},"n:24":{"book":"naladiyar","num":24,"title":"கூடா நட்பு","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Friendships that don't fit: with those who stay only for their own ends, whose bond is shallow, who are of another nature, or who lack discernment. Such friendship fades, pains, and drags down even the discerning, while the company of the wise is like heaven."},"n:25":{"book":"naladiyar","num":25,"title":"அறிவுடைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Understanding shown in conduct: restraint toward fallen foes and in poverty, judging people by mind and conduct rather than birthplace or kind, steadiness in friendship, and knowing that a person raises or lowers himself. It ends with the picture of a life that completes work, pleasure and virtue."},"n:26":{"book":"naladiyar","num":26,"title":"அறிவின்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Want of understanding (அறிவின்மை) is the real poverty. The unlearned and the base cannot take in good counsel, show themselves for what they are among the learned, and set up petty assemblies of their own, while the learned keep silent before those who would not absorb what they say."},"n:27":{"book":"naladiyar","num":27,"title":"நன்றியில் செல்வம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Wealth that does no good (நன்றியில் செல்வம்): the riches of the base and ungiving are of no use to anyone near them, and the poor turn away to givers however far. The chapter also faces the scandal that wealth goes to the senseless while the good are poor, and explains it by former deeds."},"n:28":{"book":"naladiyar","num":28,"title":"ஈயாமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Not giving (ஈயாமை): to cook and eat is to share; the miser who neither gives nor enjoys is mocked by his own hoard, loses it to strangers and heirs, and is worse off than the poor. Giving must be done now, in whatever measure one can, and wealth held without giving is a house of suffering."},"n:29":{"book":"naladiyar","num":29,"title":"இன்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Poverty (இன்மை): the world honours a few coins over noble birth, kin and flatterers leave with wealth, and poverty strips away lineage, valour, learning, goodness and even sharpness of mind. For one who cannot help those who ask, the chapter says it is better to leave home and live as a guest or a beggar elsewhere."},"n:30":{"book":"naladiyar","num":30,"title":"மானம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Honour (மானம்): those who have it burn at the arrogance and contempt of the rich, will not lay their need before the unworthy, keep away from patrons who demean them, and would rather die, or refuse heaven itself, than incur sin, blame or loss of honour. Honour is set in a scale of what people fear and is said to bring good in this world and the next."},"n:31":{"book":"naladiyar","num":31,"title":"இரவச்சம்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The dread of begging (இரவச்சம்): a person of worth would rather go hungry and poor than ask, even of willing givers, because the mind melts at the thought of asking and a refusal kills. The only givers the chapter allows are those who welcome one in, and the last quatrain turns to the help owed to an old friend."},"n:32":{"book":"naladiyar","num":32,"title":"அவையறிதல்","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Knowing the assembly (அவையறிதல்): where learning should be spoken and before whom. The worthy keep silent before the ignorant and the quarrelsome, while those who memorise texts without understanding them expose themselves. A book's meaning is harder than its letter, and only those who can explain it, by the full method of commentary, count as scholars."},"n:33":{"book":"naladiyar","num":33,"title":"புல்லறிவாண்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Petty understanding (புல்லறிவாண்மை): the small-minded scorn the teaching of virtue, see life slipping away and still do no good, nurse quarrels, hoard and put things off, until old age or death leaves them nothing. Folly is measured here by wasted time and wasted wealth."},"n:34":{"book":"naladiyar","num":34,"title":"பேதைமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Folly (பேதைமை): the fool enjoys a world in which death is already at work, puts off virtue, relies on birth and status, clings to the rich and the unworthy, praises himself and quarrels for nothing. Most quatrains are comparisons that make his position look absurd."},"n:35":{"book":"naladiyar","num":35,"title":"கீழ்மை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Baseness (கீழ்மை): the base keep their nature whatever is done for them. Teaching, invitation, honour, wealth and care do not change them, and the chapter sets them beside the worthy, whom prosperity does not change either."},"n:36":{"book":"naladiyar","num":36,"title":"கயமை","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"The base (கயமை): their nature is not changed by age, learning or wealth. They give only under force, remember insults and forget kindnesses, speak ill of others to their faces, and hold their wealth without softening."},"n:37":{"book":"naladiyar","num":37,"title":"பன்னெறி","section":"பொருட்பால்","theme":"Various ways (பன்னெறி): a closing miscellany of the Book of Wealth. It weighs the wife as what makes a house a home or a ruin, ranks the lives a person may lead with austerity first, and observes that sons inherit their fathers' minds, that the world turns upside down, and that the unfeeling are better dead."},"n:38":{"book":"naladiyar","num":38,"title":"பொது மகளிர்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"Courtesans (பொது மகளிர்): their love lasts as long as a man's money and turns hostile when it ends. The chapter blames the men who trust them as much as the women, calling them beasts, dupes and people whose bodies are full of sin."},"n:39":{"book":"naladiyar","num":39,"title":"கற்புடை மகளிர்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The chaste wife (கற்புடை மகளிர்): her worth is measured by self-guarding, hospitality in poverty, modesty that never wears off, and timely sulking. In the last four quatrains she speaks herself, as a wife wronged by a husband who goes to courtesans."},"n:40":{"book":"naladiyar","num":40,"title":"காமநுதலியல்","section":"காமத்துப்பால்","theme":"The nature of love (காமநுதலியல்): love needs both union and the sulking that interrupts it, and its pain is separation, felt most at evening and in the rains. The chapter ends with a girl's flight with her lover, seen by her mother and herself, and a woman's grief at a lover gone to seek wealth."}},"concepts":{"aram":{"tamil":"அறம்","gloss":"virtue; moral order"},"oozh":{"tamil":"ஊழ்","gloss":"fate; what is apportioned"},"vinai":{"tamil":"வினை","gloss":"deed and its consequence"},"muyarchi":{"tamil":"முயற்சி","gloss":"effort"},"kalvi":{"tamil":"கல்வி","gloss":"learning"},"arivu":{"tamil":"அறிவு","gloss":"understanding, innate or acquired"},"selvam":{"tamil":"செல்வம்","gloss":"wealth, material or figurative"},"perumai":{"tamil":"பெருமை","gloss":"greatness of character"},"sirumai":{"tamil":"சிறுமை","gloss":"smallness of character"},"panivu":{"tamil":"பணிவு","gloss":"humility"},"pirappu":{"tamil":"பிறப்பு","gloss":"birth, as social origin"},"piravi":{"tamil":"பிறவி","gloss":"rebirth; the cycle of births"},"kudi":{"tamil":"குடி","gloss":"lineage; high birth"},"manam":{"tamil":"மனம்","gloss":"inner state of mind"},"kurippu":{"tamil":"குறிப்பு","gloss":"unspoken sign or intention"},"kaamam":{"tamil":"காமம்","gloss":"love and desire"},"samanilai":{"tamil":"சமநிலை","gloss":"equanimity"},"sutram":{"tamil":"சுற்றம்","gloss":"human kinship and fellowship"},"iraivan":{"tamil":"இறைவன்","gloss":"God; the Lord, in the Kural usually named only by epithet"},"idumbai":{"tamil":"இடும்பை","gloss":"suffering; affliction"},"pugazh":{"tamil":"புகழ்","gloss":"praise; renown"},"adakkam":{"tamil":"அடக்கம்","gloss":"self-restraint"},"ozhukkam":{"tamil":"ஒழுக்கம்","gloss":"conduct; a disciplined way of living"},"mazhai":{"tamil":"மழை","gloss":"rain, as the condition of life and of virtue"},"pasi":{"tamil":"பசி","gloss":"hunger"},"eegai":{"tamil":"ஈகை","gloss":"giving and sharing with others"},"thavam":{"tamil":"தவம்","gloss":"austerity; penance"},"thuravu":{"tamil":"துறவு","gloss":"renunciation"},"vekuli":{"tamil":"வெகுளி","gloss":"anger"},"arul":{"tamil":"அருள்","gloss":"compassion toward all living beings"},"avaa":{"tamil":"அவா","gloss":"craving; desire that binds"},"azhukkaaru":{"tamil":"அழுக்காறு","gloss":"envy"},"inbam":{"tamil":"இன்பம்","gloss":"joy; pleasure"},"pazhi":{"tamil":"பழி","gloss":"blame; what deserves blame"},"ilvaazhkkai":{"tamil":"இல்வாழ்க்கை","gloss":"household life"},"virundhu":{"tamil":"விருந்து","gloss":"the guest; hospitality"},"anbu":{"tamil":"அன்பு","gloss":"love; affection"},"porai":{"tamil":"பொறை","gloss":"forbearance; endurance"},"vaazhkkaithunai":{"tamil":"வாழ்க்கைத் துணை","gloss":"life's companion; the wife"},"karpu":{"tamil":"கற்பு","gloss":"a wife's steadfastness; chastity"},"makkal":{"tamil":"மக்கள்","gloss":"children, and the good of having them"},"inchol":{"tamil":"இன்சொல்","gloss":"pleasant speech"},"vaaimai":{"tamil":"வாய்மை","gloss":"truthfulness"},"nanri":{"tamil":"நன்றி","gloss":"a good done; gratitude for it"},"natpu":{"tamil":"நட்பு","gloss":"friendship"},"naduvu":{"tamil":"நடுவு","gloss":"impartiality; keeping to what is fair"},"piranil":{"tamil":"பிறனில்","gloss":"another man's wife, named through his home"},"vehkaamai":{"tamil":"வெஃகாமை","gloss":"not coveting what belongs to others"},"purangooral":{"tamil":"புறங்கூறல்","gloss":"backbiting; speaking ill of someone in their absence"},"sol":{"tamil":"சொல்","gloss":"speech; the spoken word and its effects"},"payan":{"tamil":"பயன்","gloss":"use; fruit; what something yields"},"nayan":{"tamil":"நயன்","gloss":"goodness of conduct or manner; variously justice, grace, liking"},"acham":{"tamil":"அச்சம்","gloss":"fear, especially fear of wrongdoing as a moral faculty"},"oppuravu":{"tamil":"ஒப்புரவு","gloss":"the obligation to serve the community with one's means"},"pulaal":{"tamil":"புலால்","gloss":"flesh as food; abstention from it"},"kollaamai":{"tamil":"கொல்லாமை","gloss":"not killing"},"koodaozhukkam":{"tamil":"கூடாவொழுக்கம்","gloss":"conduct at odds with one's professed state; hypocrisy of the renunciant"},"kalavu":{"tamil":"களவு","gloss":"stealth; taking another's goods by deceit"},"alavu":{"tamil":"அளவு","gloss":"right measure; proportion"},"innaa":{"tamil":"இன்னா","gloss":"what hurts; harm done to others"},"nilaiyaamai":{"tamil":"நிலையாமை","gloss":"impermanence"},"pattru":{"tamil":"பற்று","gloss":"attachment; clinging"},"meyyunarvu":{"tamil":"மெய்யுணர்வு","gloss":"perception of what is truly so"},"arasu":{"tamil":"அரசு","gloss":"kingship; the ruler and the office of ruling"},"maanam":{"tamil":"மானம்","gloss":"honour; self-respect one won't compromise"},"sengol":{"tamil":"செங்கோல்","gloss":"just rule; the straight sceptre"},"panpu":{"tamil":"பண்பு","gloss":"good character; nature that holds"},"kallaamai":{"tamil":"கல்லாமை","gloss":"lack of learning, as a condition of a person"},"kelvi":{"tamil":"கேள்வி","gloss":"learning by listening to the wise"},"thunai":{"tamil":"துணை","gloss":"support; those or that on which one can lean"},"kutram":{"tamil":"குற்றம்","gloss":"fault; flaw of character"},"ivaral":{"tamil":"இவறல்","gloss":"avarice; clinging to wealth when it should be used"},"vali":{"tamil":"வலி","gloss":"strength, one's own and others', to be weighed"},"therithal":{"tamil":"தெரிதல்","gloss":"examining and discerning before choosing or acting"},"inam":{"tamil":"இனம்","gloss":"the company one keeps"},"kaalam":{"tamil":"காலம்","gloss":"right timing; the season for acting"},"idam":{"tamil":"இடம்","gloss":"right place; the ground on which one acts"},"thelivu":{"tamil":"தெளிவு","gloss":"settled trust reached after examination"},"naanam":{"tamil":"நாண்","gloss":"modesty; shame that restrains"},"pochchaappu":{"tamil":"பொச்சாப்பு","gloss":"heedlessness; forgetting oneself and one's purpose"},"kolai":{"tamil":"கொலை","gloss":"killing, as crime or as punishment"},"kodungol":{"tamil":"கொடுங்கோல்","gloss":"oppressive rule; the bent sceptre"},"ali":{"tamil":"அளி","gloss":"tender regard for those in one's care"},"veruvu":{"tamil":"வெருவு","gloss":"dread; the fear a ruler instils"},"kol":{"tamil":"கோல்","gloss":"the sceptre; the quality of a ruler's rule, just or cruel"},"oruththal":{"tamil":"ஒறுத்தல்","gloss":"punishing; penalty as an act of rule"},"kadunjol":{"tamil":"கடுஞ்சொல்","gloss":"harsh speech"},"kannottam":{"tamil":"கண்ணோட்டம்","gloss":"the grace of the eye; kindly regard, especially for those one knows"},"aran":{"tamil":"அரண்","gloss":"fortress; protection, literal or figurative"},"otru":{"tamil":"ஒற்று","gloss":"spying; the spy; intelligence for the state"},"ookkam":{"tamil":"ஊக்கம்","gloss":"energy of mind; inner drive"},"velanmai":{"tamil":"வேளாண்மை","gloss":"helping others; liberality"},"madi":{"tamil":"மடி","gloss":"sloth; inertia"},"adimai":{"tamil":"அடிமை","gloss":"bondage; servitude"},"aalvinai":{"tamil":"ஆள்வினை","gloss":"sustained purposeful labour; manly exertion"},"idukkan":{"tamil":"இடுக்கண்","gloss":"trouble; hardship; suffering"},"amaichu":{"tamil":"அமைச்சு","gloss":"ministership; the minister"},"solvanmai":{"tamil":"சொல்வன்மை","gloss":"power of speech; eloquence"},"pagai":{"tamil":"பகை","gloss":"enmity; the enemy"},"avai":{"tamil":"அவை","gloss":"the assembly; those before whom one speaks"},"thooimai":{"tamil":"தூய்மை","gloss":"purity, especially of action and means"},"saandror":{"tamil":"சான்றோர்","gloss":"the worthy; those complete in character"},"thitpam":{"tamil":"திட்பம்","gloss":"firmness of mind and execution"},"soozhchi":{"tamil":"சூழ்ச்சி","gloss":"deliberation; strategic planning"},"thoothu":{"tamil":"தூது","gloss":"embassy; the envoy"},"mannar":{"tamil":"மன்னர்","gloss":"the ruler, seen from those who serve him"},"anjaamai":{"tamil":"அஞ்சாமை","gloss":"fearlessness"},"naadu":{"tamil":"நாடு","gloss":"land; realm as a living whole"},"vendhan":{"tamil":"வேந்தன்","gloss":"king; ruler"},"padai":{"tamil":"படை","gloss":"army; fighting force"},"maram":{"tamil":"மறம்","gloss":"martial valour; fierce courage"},"saakkaadu":{"tamil":"சாக்காடு","gloss":"death, as something faced or chosen"},"pedhaimai":{"tamil":"பேதைமை","gloss":"folly"},"idithal":{"tamil":"இடித்தல்","gloss":"rebuke that corrects"},"kedu":{"tamil":"கேடு","gloss":"ruin; loss of fortune"},"aaraaydhal":{"tamil":"ஆராய்தல்","gloss":"testing; careful inquiry into a person"},"pazhaimai":{"tamil":"பழைமை","gloss":"old friendship; a bond with long-standing claims"},"kizhamai":{"tamil":"கிழமை","gloss":"the liberty of intimacy; rightful claim on a friend"},"vanjam":{"tamil":"வஞ்சம்","gloss":"deceit; hidden hostility"},"pullarivu":{"tamil":"புல்லறிவு","gloss":"petty understanding that thinks itself wise"},"igal":{"tamil":"இகல்","gloss":"hostility; the disposition to quarrel"},"utpagai":{"tamil":"உட்பகை","gloss":"enmity from within one's own circle"},"penvazhi":{"tamil":"பெண்வழிச்சேறல்","gloss":"being led by a woman's will; a husband's subordination"},"aanmai":{"tamil":"ஆண்மை","gloss":"manliness; capacity to act and govern"},"varaivinmagalir":{"tamil":"வரைவின்மகளிர்","gloss":"women who draw no line; women who sell intimacy"},"kal":{"tamil":"கள்","gloss":"liquor; intoxication"},"soodhu":{"tamil":"சூது","gloss":"gambling"},"noy":{"tamil":"நோய்","gloss":"disease; affliction"},"marundhu":{"tamil":"மருந்து","gloss":"medicine; remedy"},"undi":{"tamil":"உண்டி","gloss":"food; eating"},"saandraanmai":{"tamil":"சான்றாண்மை","gloss":"fullness of character; moral completeness"},"uzhavu":{"tamil":"உழவு","gloss":"farming; ploughing as the base of society"},"iravu":{"tamil":"இரவு","gloss":"asking; begging from others"},"inmai":{"tamil":"இன்மை","gloss":"poverty; lack"},"karavu":{"tamil":"கரவு","gloss":"concealing what one has from one who asks; refusal"},"kayamai":{"tamil":"கயமை","gloss":"baseness; lack of any inner moral restraint"},"anangu":{"tamil":"அணங்கு","gloss":"a goddess or spirit that afflicts; beauty as a wounding power"},"kootru":{"tamil":"கூற்று","gloss":"Death personified"},"kan":{"tamil":"கண்","gloss":"the eyes as agents of love: they wound, steal, speak and suffer"},"nalam":{"tamil":"நலம்","gloss":"bodily beauty and wellbeing, as something love gives or takes"},"kaamanoy":{"tamil":"காமநோய்","gloss":"love as sickness; the pain of longing"},"kalaviyal":{"tamil":"களவியல்","gloss":"secret love before marriage"},"punarchi":{"tamil":"புணர்ச்சி","gloss":"the union of lovers, physical and emotional"},"uyir":{"tamil":"உயிர்","gloss":"life; the life-breath at stake in love"},"oodal":{"tamil":"ஊடல்","gloss":"lovers' quarrel; the sulking that love enjoys"},"nenju":{"tamil":"நெஞ்சு","gloss":"the heart as a separate party the lover speaks to"},"pirivu":{"tamil":"பிரிவு","gloss":"separation of lovers"},"ninaivu":{"tamil":"நினைவு","gloss":"remembering the absent lover; constant thought"},"alar":{"tamil":"அலர்","gloss":"public gossip about the lovers"},"madal":{"tamil":"மடல்","gloss":"riding the palmyra-stem horse in public to shame or force a beloved's kin"},"maalai":{"tamil":"மாலை","gloss":"evening as the hour when longing flowers"},"nirai":{"tamil":"நிறை","gloss":"self-containment; holding feeling within"},"melivu":{"tamil":"மெலிவு","gloss":"wasting of the body in longing, shown by slipping bangles and thinning arms"},"pasalai":{"tamil":"பசலை","gloss":"the pallor of longing, spreading over a woman's skin in separation"},"orutalai":{"tamil":"ஒருதலை","gloss":"one-sided love; love that isn't returned"},"kanavu":{"tamil":"கனவு","gloss":"dream, as a place where the absent lover can be met"},"parathai":{"tamil":"பரத்தை","gloss":"the courtesan or other woman; a man's straying, as a commentators' setting"},"ilamai":{"tamil":"இளமை","gloss":"youth, as a stage that passes"},"yaakkai":{"tamil":"யாக்கை","gloss":"the body, as a perishable and impure frame"},"veedu":{"tamil":"வீடு","gloss":"release; liberation from the cycle of births"},"azhagu":{"tamil":"அழகு","gloss":"beauty, outward or true"},"marumai":{"tamil":"மறுமை","gloss":"the next world or life, for which good deeds are provision"},"udanpokku":{"tamil":"உடன்போக்கு","gloss":"a girl's going away with her lover, against her family, through the wilderness"}},"verses":{"k:1":{"book":"kural","num":1,"t":["அகர முதல எழுத்தெல்லாம் ஆதி","பகவன் முதற்றே உலகு"],"p":"As the letter A is the first of all letters, so the eternal God is first in the world.","r":"As all letters have 'a' as their first, the world has the Primal Lord as its first.","n":"The verse gives a simile of being first and says nothing of creating. Parimelazhagar reads it as establishing that the unseen deity exists, arguing from the visible world, which is more than the couplet states.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:2":{"book":"kural","num":2,"t":["கற்றதனால் ஆய பயனென்கொல் வாலறிவன்","நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்"],"p":"What Profit have those derived from learning, who worship not the good feet of Him who is possessed of pure knowledge ?","r":"What has learning achieved if the learned do not bow at the good feet of the one of pure knowledge?","n":"The question assumes that learning has a purpose beyond itself. It is the chapter's first conditional, and it is put to the learned, not to the ignorant.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","iraivan","panivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The one of pure knowledge is God, and scriptural knowledge bears fruit by worship that cuts off rebirth.","ஆகம அறிவிற்குப் பயன் அவன் தாளைத் தொழுது பிறவியறுத்தல்"],["kalaignar","The one meant is any person greater in knowledge, and the failing is a lack of humility before such people.","தன்னைவிட அறிவில் மூத்த பெருந்தகையாளரின் முன்னே வணங்கிநிற்கும் பண்பு"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:3":{"book":"kural","num":3,"t":["மலர்மிசை ஏகினான் மாணடி சேர்ந்தார்","நிலமிசை நீடுவாழ் வார்"],"p":"They who are united to the glorious feet of Him who passes swiftly over the flower of the mind, shall flourish long above all worlds.","r":"Those who reach the noble feet of the one who walked upon the flower will live long upon the earth.","n":"The Tamil says நிலமிசை, on the earth, so reading it as a world above is an interpretation. Parimelazhagar also rejects an identification of 'the one who walked on the flower' with another deity, which shows the epithet was already disputed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The long life is deathless life in the world of release, above all worlds.","எல்லா உலகிற்கும் மேலாய் வீட்டு உலகின்கண் அழிவின்றி வாழ்வார்"],["manakkudavar","The general word 'land' covers this world and the next, with enjoyment named before release.","இவ்வுலகின்கண்ணும் மேலுலகின்கண்ணு மென்று கொள்ளப்படும்"],["kalaignar","The long life is the lasting renown of those who follow such a person.","புகழ்வாழ்வு, உலகில் நெடுங்காலம் நிலைத்து நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:4":{"book":"kural","num":4,"t":["வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலானடி சேர்ந்தார்க்கு","யாண்டும் இடும்பை இல"],"p":"To those who meditate the feet of Him who is void of desire or aversion, evil shall never come.","r":"Those who reach the feet of the one without liking or aversion have no suffering, anywhere or at any time.","n":"The one sought is defined by what he lacks. Parimelazhagar's explanation makes the benefit mimetic: those who reach him also lose liking and aversion, and so lose the suffering these cause.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","idumbai","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The suffering removed is that of rebirth, in its three classical kinds.","எக்காலத்தும் பிறவித் துன்பங்கள் உளவாகா."],["kalaignar","The one meant is a selfless human example, and following such a person keeps ordinary suffering away.","விருப்பு வெறுப்பற்றுத் தன்னலமின்றித் திகழ்கின்றவரைப் பின்பற்றி நடப்பவர்களுக்கு"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:5":{"book":"kural","num":5,"t":["இருள்சேர் இருவினையும் சேரா இறைவன்","பொருள்சேர் புகழ்புரிந்தார் மாட்டு"],"p":"The two-fold deeds that spring from darkness shall not adhere to those who delight in the true praise of God.","r":"The two kinds of deed born of darkness do not cling to those devoted to the Lord's true praise.","n":"The verse says both kinds, so good deeds are also among what must not cling. That is a stronger claim than 'worship removes sin'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","iraivan","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even good deeds cause birth, and the praise is true only of the one who has all divine qualities.","நல்வினையும் பிறத்தற்கு ஏதுவாகலான்"],["kalaignar","Those who understand what 'the Lord' means and seek renown meet good and bad with equal measure.","நன்மை தீமைகளை ஒரே அளவில் எதிர் கொள்வார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:6":{"book":"kural","num":6,"t":["பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான் பொய்தீர் ஒழுக்க","நெறிநின்றார் நீடுவாழ் வார்"],"p":"Those shall long proposer who abide in the faultless way of Him who has destroyed the five desires of the senses.","r":"Those who stand firm on the truthful path of conduct of the one who quenched the five senses' desires will live long.","n":"This is the only verse in the chapter that asks for a way of living instead of reaching or praising. Parimelazhagar says the path was taught by the one who quenched the five, as a poem belongs to its poet, which makes him a teacher and not only an object of worship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","adakkam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:7":{"book":"kural","num":7,"t":["தனக்குவமை இல்லாதான் தாள்சேர்ந்தார்க் கல்லால்","மனக்கவலை மாற்றல் அரிது"],"p":"Anxiety of mind cannot be removed, except from those who are united to the feet of Him who is incomparable.","r":"Except for those who reach the feet of the incomparable one, it is hard to remove the mind's anxiety.","n":"The verse says அரிது, hard. Parimelazhagar explicitly takes this as impossibility, which hardens the claim beyond its plain wording.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:1"},"k:8":{"book":"kural","num":8,"t":["அறவாழி அந்தணன் தாள்சேர்ந்தார்க் கல்லால்","பிறவாழி நீந்தல் அரிது"],"p":"None can swim the sea of vice, but those who are united to the feet of that gracious Being who is a sea of virtue.","r":"Except for those who reach the feet of the gracious one who is a sea of virtue, the other seas are hard to swim.","n":"The sea of virtue set against 'other seas' is the image's whole point. Pope's gloss, 'sea of vice', is not in the Tamil.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The other seas are wealth and pleasure, set apart from virtue in the three aims of life.","ஏனைப் பொருளும் இன்பமும் 'பிற' எனப்பட்டன."],["kalaignar","The அந்தணன் is a worthy human being, whose example carries others across seas of trouble.","அந்தணர் என்பதற்குப் பொருள் சான்றோர் என்பதால்,"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:9":{"book":"kural","num":9,"t":["கோளில் பொறியின் குணமிலவே எண்குணத்தான்","தாளை வணங்காத் தலை"],"p":"The head that worships not the feet of Him who is possessed of eight attributes, is as useless as a sense without the power of sensation.","r":"A head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities is as useless as a sense organ that cannot sense.","n":"The verse never lists the eight. Parimelazhagar gives a list from Saiva scripture and records rival lists, so any particular list is the commentator's. Manakkudavar goes further and says such bodies lack life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iraivan","panivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The eight qualities are those taught in the Saiva agamas, though others list them differently.","இவ்வாறு சைவாகமத்துக் கூறப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:10":{"book":"kural","num":10,"t":["பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல் நீந்துவர் நீந்தார்","இறைவன் அடிசேரா தார்"],"p":"None can swim the great sea of births but those who are united to the feet of God.","r":"Those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of births, and those who do not reach them cannot.","n":"The chapter closes with both outcomes in one couplet. Verses 7 and 8 hedge with 'hard', and this one drops the hedge. Parimelazhagar's 'reach' comes from the verse's own closing negative, since the positive verb is left implied.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The sea is the shoreless chain of cause and effect in rebirth.","காரண காரியத் தொடர்ச்சியாய் கரை இன்றி வருதலின்"],["kalaignar","The sea is life in this world, which is hard to cross without following a leading person.","வாழ்க்கை எனும் பெருங்கடலை நீந்திக் கடக்க"]],"ch":"k:1"},"k:11":{"book":"kural","num":11,"t":["வான்நின்று உலகம் வழங்கி வருதலால்","தான்அமிழ்தம் என்றுணரற் பாற்று"],"p":"By the continuance of rain the world is preserved in existence; it is therefore worthy to be called ambrosia.","r":"Because the world keeps going through the rain's constancy, rain deserves to be understood as amrita.","n":"The word உணரற்பாற்று makes this a matter of recognition, since rain is ordinary and easily taken for granted. Manakkudavar draws this out by saying it should not be thought of as a mere element.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:12":{"book":"kural","num":12,"t":["துப்பார்க்குத் துப்பாய துப்பாக்கித் துப்பார்க்குத்","துப்பாய தூஉம் மழை"],"p":"Rain produces good food, and is itself food.","r":"Rain produces the food that eaters eat, and rain is itself food to those who drink it.","n":"Five uses of துப்பு pack the couplet, and the wordplay enacts the idea: the same substance is the maker, the made and the eater's need.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","pasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:13":{"book":"kural","num":13,"t":["விண்இன்று பொய்ப்பின் விரிநீர் வியனுலகத்து","உள்நின்று உடற்றும் பசி"],"p":"If the cloud, withholding rain, deceive (our hopes) hunger will long distress the sea-girt spacious world.","r":"If the sky fails, hunger stays and torments this wide world, though it is surrounded by spreading water.","n":"The irony is in விரிநீர்: the world is ringed by water and still starves. Parimelazhagar makes this explicit. பொய்ப்பின் also treats the sky as something that can break its word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:14":{"book":"kural","num":14,"t":["ஏரின் உழாஅர் உழவர் புயல்என்னும்","வாரி வளங்குன்றிக் கால்"],"p":"If the abundance of wealth imparting rain diminish, the labour of the plough must cease.","r":"If the bounty of the rains declines, ploughmen will not plough.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the verse as explaining why food runs out. The human labour is real but placed downstream of a yield the farmer doesn't control.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:15":{"book":"kural","num":15,"t":["கெடுப்பதூஉம் கெட்டார்க்குச் சார்வாய்மற் றாங்கே","எடுப்பதூஉம் எல்லாம் மழை"],"p":"Rain by its absence ruins men; and by its existence restores them to fortune.","r":"Rain is what ruins, and then it is rain again that supports and lifts up the ruined.","n":"The verse says only that rain ruins. The commentators here take the ruin as failure to fall, though a reader might expect flood too. The power to destroy and restore belongs to one impersonal agent, with no moral reason given.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:16":{"book":"kural","num":16,"t":["விசும்பின் துளிவீழின் அல்லால்மற் றாங்கே","பசும்புல் தலைகாண்பு அரிது"],"p":"If no drop falls from the clouds, not even the green blade of grass will be seen.","r":"Unless drops fall from the sky, it is hard to see even the head of a green blade of grass.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the implied 'even': if the grass is gone, not even a life with one sense remains. The argument descends to the smallest thing that lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:17":{"book":"kural","num":17,"t":["நெடுங்கடலும் தன்நீர்மை குன்றும் தடிந்தெழிலி","தான்நல்கா தாகி விடின்"],"p":"Even the wealth of the wide sea will be diminished, if the cloud that has drawn (its waters) up gives them not back again (in rain).","r":"Even the vast sea would lose its nature if the cloud held back after taking from it.","n":"The source of rain needs rain, so the dependence runs in a circle. Parimelazhagar makes this the point, and Kalaignar turns the same cycle into a social lesson.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தடிந்து means the cloud scoops up the sea's water, and the sea's 'nature' is its living creatures and gems.","ஈண்டுக் 'குறைத்தல்' என்றது முகத்தலை."],["manakkudavar","He takes the word as the cloud flashing, and reports that others read it as dividing.","மின்னி மழையானது பெய்யாவிடின்"],["kalaignar","The verse also teaches that those who rise from a society must serve it for it to live.","மனித சமுதாயத்திலிருந்து புகழுடன் உயர்ந்தவர்களும் அந்தச் சமுதாயத்திற்கே பயன்பட்டால்தான் அந்தச் சமுதாயம் வாழும்."]],"ch":"k:2"},"k:18":{"book":"kural","num":18,"t":["சிறப்பொடு பூசனை செல்லாது வானம்","வறக்குமேல் வானோர்க்கும் ஈண்டு"],"p":"If the heaven dry up, neither yearly festivals, nor daily worship will be offered in this world, to the celestials.","r":"If the sky dries up, festival and worship will cease here, even for the gods.","n":"The word 'even' reverses the usual order: the celestials depend on what the sky provides through human offering. Kalaignar's comment adds scepticism with 'those said to live in the sky', which the verse does not express.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:19":{"book":"kural","num":19,"t":["தானம் தவம்இரண்டும் தங்கா வியன்உலகம்","வானம் வழங்கா தெனின்"],"p":"If rain fall not, penance and alms-deeds will not dwell within this spacious world.","r":"If the sky does not give, neither giving nor austerity will last in this wide world.","n":"Parimelazhagar maps giving broadly to the householder and austerity to the renunciant, so this verse quietly announces the two paths that chapters 3 and 5 go on to compare. Even austerity, which might seem independent of plenty, is included.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai","thavam","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:2"},"k:20":{"book":"kural","num":20,"t":["நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகெனின் யார்யார்க்கும்","வான்இன்று அமையாது ஒழுக்கு"],"p":"If it be said that the duties of life cannot be discharged by any person without water, so without rain there cannot be the flowing of water.","r":"If no one's world can do without water, then its flow can't exist without the sky.","n":"The whole dispute rests on ஒழுக்கு, which can mean a flow or conduct. On the second reading, the chapter ends by saying morality depends on rain.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["mazhai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒழுக்கு is the unceasing flow of water, and he records the conduct reading as someone else's.","அந்நீர் இடையறாது ஒழுகும் ஒழுக்கும் வானை இன்றி அமையாது."],["kalaignar","ஒழுக்கு is conduct, which may decay if there is no rain.","உலகில் மழையே இல்லையென்றால் ஒழுக்கமே கெடக்கூடும்"]],"ch":"k:2"},"k:21":{"book":"kural","num":21,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்து நீத்தார் பெருமை விழுப்பத்து","வேண்டும் பனுவல் துணிவு"],"p":"The end and aim of all treatise is to extol beyond all other excellence, the greatness of those who, while abiding in the rule of conduct peculiar to their state, have abandoned all desire.","r":"The settled view of the texts is to place the greatness of those who renounce within their discipline above all other excellence.","n":"The verse says only ஒழுக்கத்து, in discipline. Parimelazhagar's ladder from duties of varna to release is his own construction. The commentators also give very different reasons for the agreement among texts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","perumai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a shared conclusion of all religious texts, though they conflict on everything else.","ஒன்றையொன்று ஒவ்வாத சமய நூல்கள் எல்லாவற்றிற்கும் இஃது ஒத்த துணிவு"],["manakkudavar","Even a false text praises renunciants, so that people will honour it.","யாதானுமொரு பொய்யைச் சொல்லும் நூலும்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:22":{"book":"kural","num":22,"t":["துறந்தார் பெருமை துணைக்கூறின் வையத்து","இறந்தாரை எண்ணிக்கொண் டற்று"],"p":"To describe the measure of the greatness of those who have forsaken the two-fold desires, is like counting the dead.","r":"Trying to state the measure of renouncers' greatness is like counting all the dead who have ever lived.","n":"It's a strange image of praise. The dead aren't only numerous but past counting in principle. My reading, offered tentatively, is that comparing renouncers to the dead also suits people who have left the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:3"},"k:23":{"book":"kural","num":23,"t":["இருமை வகைதெரிந்து ஈண்டுஅறம் பூண்டார்","பெருமை பிறங்கிற்று உலகு"],"p":"The greatness of those who have discovered the properties of both states of being, and renounced the world, shines forth on earth (beyond all others).","r":"The greatness of those who discern the two ways and take on virtue here stands highest in the world.","n":"ஈண்டு, here, is important: this choice is made in this life. Parimelazhagar uses an implied 'only' to place them above even emperors.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thuravu","perumai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The two are birth and release, weighed for their suffering and joy, and the virtue taken up is renunciation.","பிறப்பு வீடு என்னும் இரண்டனது துன்ப இன்பக் கூறுபாடுகளை ஆராய்ந்து அறிந்து"],["kalaignar","The two are good and evil, and anyone who chooses the good is great.","நன்மை எது, தீமை எது என்பதை ஆய்ந்தறிந்து"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:24":{"book":"kural","num":24,"t":["உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான் ஓரைந்தும் காப்பான்","வரனென்னும் வைப்பிற்கோர் வித்து"],"p":"He who guides his five senses by the hook of wisdom will be a seed in the world of heaven.","r":"One who guards the five with the goad of firmness is a seed for the finest ground.","n":"The goad implies the elephants, which the verse doesn't mention, and then the image shifts to farming. வரன் leaves the 'field' open: Parimelazhagar makes it release, while Kalaignar makes it renunciation itself. The word உரன் suggests strength, though Parimelazhagar says it refers here to understanding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:3"},"k:25":{"book":"kural","num":25,"t":["ஐந்தவித்தான் ஆற்றல் அகல்விசும்பு ளார்கோமான்","இந்திரனே சாலுங் கரி"],"p":"Indra, the king of the inhabitants of the spacious heaven, is himself, a sufficient proof of the strength of him who has subdued his five senses.","r":"Of the strength of one who has quenched the five, Indra, lord of the wide heavens, is witness enough.","n":"The verse does not say how Indra testifies. The commentators supply two opposed stories, one in which Indra failed and another in which he fears.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Indra, cursed for failing to quench the five, shows by his own failure what the power is worth.","தான் ஐந்து அவியாது சாபம் எய்தி நின்று, அவித்தவனது ஆற்றல் உணர்த்தினான்"],["manakkudavar","Indra trembles for his throne when anyone does great penance, so the ascetic is stronger than the gods.","அவன் தன் பதம் இழக்கின்றானாக நடுங்குமாதலான்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:26":{"book":"kural","num":26,"t":["செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர் சிறியர்","செயற்கரிய செய்கலா தார்"],"p":"The great will do those things which is difficult to be done; but the mean cannot do them.","r":"Those who do what is hard to do are great, and those who can't do it are small.","n":"The verse doesn't say what is hard, so it reads as general. In its context the commentators narrow it to yoga. Manakkudavar turns it into a warning within the chapter: renouncing alone does not make one great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is hard is the eight limbs of yoga; what is easy is following the senses.","செயற்கு அரிய ஆவன, இமயம், நியமம் முதலாய எண்வகை யோக உறுப்புக்கள்."],["manakkudavar","Those who have renounced are still small if they can't do these hard things.","துறந்த மாத்திரத்தானே பெரியரென்று கொள் ளப்படார்"]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:27":{"book":"kural","num":27,"t":["சுவைஒளி ஊறுஓசை நாற்றமென ஐந்தின்","வகைதெரிவான் கட்டே உலகு"],"p":"The world is within the knowledge of him who knows the properties of taste, sight, touch, hearing and smell.","r":"The world lies within the one who discerns the kinds of the five: taste, light, touch, sound and smell.","n":"கட்டே is the crux. Pope's 'owns his sway' and Parimelazhagar's 'within his knowledge' suggest different kinds of mastery. Parimelazhagar's whole twenty-five principles are not in the couplet, which names only the five.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu","adakkam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is knowledge of the twenty-five principles of Samkhya, beyond which there is no world.","சாங்கிய நூலுள் ஓதியவாற்றான் ஆராய்தல்."],["kalaignar","This is the ability to understand and master the senses, which the world honours.","ஐம்புலன்களின் இயல்பை உணர்ந்து அவற்றை அடக்கியாளும் திறன் கொண்டவனையே உலகம் போற்றும்."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:28":{"book":"kural","num":28,"t":["நிறைமொழி மாந்தர் பெருமை நிலத்து","மறைமொழி காட்டி விடும்"],"p":"The hidden words of the men whose words are full of effect, will shew their greatness to the world.","r":"The greatness of those whose word is full is shown on earth by their hidden words.","n":"நிறைமொழி and மறைமொழி echo each other: a complete word and a concealed one. The verse doesn't say whether the proof is what the words do or what they teach.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Renunciants' words take effect, and their commanding mantras prove it visibly.","அவர் ஆணையாகச் சொல்லிய மந்திரங்களே கண்கூடாகக் காட்டும்."],["manakkudavar","'Full' describes people of complete learning, whose mantras circulate as proof.","நிரம்பிய கல்வியையுடைய மாந்தரது"],["kalaignar","The proof is the enduring ethical books of the worthy.","அவர்களது அறவழி நூல்களே எடுத்துக் காட்டும்."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:29":{"book":"kural","num":29,"t":["குணமென்னும் குன்றேறி நின்றார் வெகுளி","கணமேயும் காத்தல் அரிது"],"p":"The anger of those who have ascended the mountain of goodness, though it continue but for a moment, cannot be resisted.","r":"The anger of those who have climbed the hill of good qualities, even for a moment, is hard to withstand.","n":"The crux is whose capacity காத்தல் refers to, and the commentators turn the verse in opposite directions. Pope sides with the threatening reading.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Their anger lasts only a moment, yet its target cannot ward it off.","தான் உள்ள அளவு கணமே ஆயினும், வெகுளப்பட்டாரால் தடுத்தல் அரிது."],["manakkudavar","The harm from their anger can't be avoided, as in Nahusha's becoming a serpent.","நகுஷன் பெரும்பாம்பாயினான்."],["kalaignar","Such people can't hold on to anger, even for a moment.","அந்தக் கோபம் அவர்கள் உள்ளத்தில் ஒரு கணம்கூட நிலைத்து நிற்காது."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:30":{"book":"kural","num":30,"t":["அந்தணர் என்போர் அறவோர்மற் றெவ்வுயிர் க்கும்","செந்தண்மை பூண்டொழுக லான்"],"p":"The virtuous are truly called Anthanar; because in their conduct towards all creatures they are clothed in kindness.","r":"Those called அந்தணர் are the virtuous, because they live clothed in cool kindness toward every living being.","n":"The verse defines a status word by conduct, and its logic is a reason (ஒழுகலான்), so the name follows from the behaviour. Placed right after 29, its coolness is the counterweight to the sage's terrifying anger.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The name's reason restricts it to those with such grace, namely those in renunciation.","அந்தணரென்று சொல்லப்படுவார் துறவறத்தில் நின்றவர்."],["manakkudavar","Having said some renunciants are small, the verse says some non-renunciants are great.","துற வாதாரினும் பெரியாருளரென்று கூறினார்."],["kalaignar","Any worthy person with love for all beings is an அந்தணர்.","சான்றோர் எவராயினும் அவர் அந்தணர் எனப்படுவார்."]],"ch":"k:3"},"k:31":{"book":"kural","num":31,"t":["சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும் அறத்தினூஉங்கு","ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Virtue will confer heaven and wealth; what greater source of happiness can man possess ?","r":"It yields distinction and it yields wealth: what gain is greater for a living being than virtue?","n":"The appeal is to self-interest, as virtue is praised for what it brings. Parimelazhagar takes சிறப்பு as release and செல்வம் as heaven, while Manakkudavar takes the second more plainly as wealth and sees the verse as answering those who say prosperity comes from wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:32":{"book":"kural","num":32,"t":["அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கமும் இல்லை அதனை","மறத்தலின் ஊங்கில்லை கேடு"],"p":"There can be no greater source of good than (the practice of) virtue; there can be no greater source of evil than the forgetfulness of it.","r":"No gain surpasses virtue, and no ruin surpasses forgetting it.","n":"The opposite of virtue here is மறத்தல், forgetting, not doing wrong. Ruin comes through inattention, so the danger is ordinary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:33":{"book":"kural","num":33,"t":["ஒல்லும் வகையான் அறவினை ஓவாதே","செல்லும்வாய் எல்லாஞ் செயல்"],"p":"As much as possible, in every way, incessantly practise virtue.","r":"As far as you are able, without stopping, do the work of virtue wherever it can go.","n":"ஒல்லும் வகையான் sets the measure by capacity, so the demand is relentless in duration but not impossible in scale. Parimelazhagar gives 'wherever' as mind, speech and body, while Kalaignar turns it into a requirement that all one's acts be virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:34":{"book":"kural","num":34,"t":["மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்","ஆகுல நீர பிற"],"p":"Let him who does virtuous deeds be of spotless mind; to that extent is virtue; all else is vain show.","r":"To be spotless in mind is the whole of virtue, and everything else is noisy show.","n":"ஆகுல means clamour, so the charge against outward virtue is that it wants an audience. Parimelazhagar reads the verse with 33, so it concerns the mind of someone already doing good, not the mind in place of deeds. Manakkudavar's comment says the four faults were named earlier, which implies his text placed 35 before this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:35":{"book":"kural","num":35,"t":["அழுக்காறு அவாவெகுளி இன்னாச்சொல் நான்கும்","இழுக்கா இயன்றது அறம்"],"p":"That conduct is virtue which is free from these four things, viz, malice, desire, anger and bitter speech.","r":"Virtue is what proceeds without slipping into these four: envy, craving, anger and harsh speech.","n":"Virtue is defined by exclusion. The verse only lists the faults, and Parimelazhagar supplies a causal chain in which craving leads to anger and anger to harsh speech. Three of the four are inward and one is spoken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","azhukkaaru","avaa","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:36":{"book":"kural","num":36,"t":["அன்றறிவாம் என்னாது அறஞ்செய்க மற்றது","பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை"],"p":"Defer not virtue to another day; receive her now; and at the dying hour she will be your undying friend.","r":"Do virtue without saying 'we'll see to it later': when you perish, it is a companion that doesn't perish.","n":"The pun பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றா turns on one verb: you perish and it doesn't. The verse doesn't say how virtue survives, and that is where the commentators part.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","When the body dies, virtue stays joined to the soul and travels into the next body.","செய்த உடம்பு அழியவும் உயிரோடு ஒன்றி ஏனை உடம்பினுட் சேறலின்"],["kalaignar","Virtue survives as lasting renown after death.","அது ஒருவர் இறந்தபின் கூட அழியாப் புகழாய் நிலைத்துத் துணை நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:37":{"book":"kural","num":37,"t":["அறத்தாறு இதுவென வேண்டா சிவிகை","பொறுத்தானோடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை"],"p":"The fruit of virtue need not be described in books; it may be inferred from seeing the bearer of a palanquin and the rider therein.","r":"There is no need to say 'this is the way of virtue': look between the one who bears the palanquin and the one who rides in it.","n":"The couplet is very compressed, and it never says what difference we are to see. Taken at face value, it treats social rank as visible evidence of moral merit, which is one of the harder claims in the book.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Rider and bearer are direct perceptual evidence of virtue's fruit, so scripture needn't be cited.","சிவிகையைக் காவுவானோடு செலுத்துவானிடைக் காட்சியளவை தன்னானே உணரப்படும்."],["kalaignar","This is an analogy of attitude: the virtuous ride through joy and sorrow lightly, and others carry life as a burden.","வாழ்க்கையில் வரும் இன்ப துன்பங்கள் இரண்டையும் எளியவாகக் கருதி மகிழ்வுடன் பயணத்தை மேற்கொள்வார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:38":{"book":"kural","num":38,"t":["வீழ்நாள் படாஅமை நன்றாற்றின் அஃதொருவன்","வாழ்நாள் வழியடைக்கும் கல்"],"p":"If one allows no day to pass without some good being done, his conduct will be a stone to block up the passage to other births.","r":"If you do good so that no day falls away unused, that is a stone blocking the road of your days.","n":"வீழ்நாள் and வாழ்நாள் are set against each other, so fallen days are contrasted with living days. The stone's job depends entirely on what வாழ்நாள் means here.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The stone blocks the road by which embodied days would return, which means it ends rebirth.","அச்செயல் அவன் யாக்கையோடு கூடும் நாள் வரும் வழியை வாராமல் அடைக்குங் கல்லாம்."],["kalaignar","The stone is a paving stone that smooths the road of this life.","வாழ்க்கைப் பாதையைச் சீராக்கி அமைத்துத் தரும் கல்லாக"]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:39":{"book":"kural","num":39,"t":["அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம் மற் றெல்லாம்","புறத்த புகழும் இல"],"p":"Only that pleasure which flows from domestic virtue is pleasure; all else is not pleasure, and it is without praise.","r":"Only what comes through virtue is joy; everything else lies outside it and has no renown either.","n":"Pope's gloss restricts this to 'domestic virtue', which follows Parimelazhagar and is not in the Tamil. The verse has a strong implication: joy is not separate from virtue and can't be got apart from it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","aram","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The joy is sensual enjoyment that accords with household life; illicit pleasure turns to pain.","இல்லறத்தோடு பொருந்தி வருவதே இன்பம் ஆவது"],["manakkudavar","All enjoyment of any kind comes through virtue.","இஃது எல்லாப் போக நுகர்ச்சியும் இதனானே வருமென்றது."],["kalaignar","The joy arises from the renown a virtuous life brings.","அறவழி வாழ்க்கையில் வருகின்ற புகழால் ஏற்படுவதே இன்பமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:4"},"k:40":{"book":"kural","num":40,"t":["செயற்பால தோரும் அறனே ஒருவற்கு","உயற்பால தோரும் பழி"],"p":"That is virtue which each ought to do, and that is vice which each should shun.","r":"What a person should do is virtue, and what a person should escape is blame.","n":"Virtue's counterpart here is பழி, blame, not sin. Parimelazhagar explains it as what is blamed, and takes it to mean bad deeds. The term gives virtue a social measure as well as a cosmic one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:4"},"k:41":{"book":"kural","num":41,"t":["இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் இயல்புடைய மூவர்க்கும்","நல்லாற்றின் நின்ற துணை"],"p":"He will be called a (true) householder, who is a firm support to the virtuous of the three orders in their good path.","r":"The one called a householder is the steady support, on their good path, of the three who keep their proper way.","n":"The verse doesn't name the three, and what is meant by இயல்புடைய decides who is included.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The three are the other life-stages, and he says Valluvar takes the first two from other people's doctrine.","இவருள் முன்னை இருவரையும் பிறர் மதம் மேற்கொண்டு கூறினார்."],["kalaignar","The three are one's parents, spouse and children.","பெற்றோர், வாழ்க்கைத் துணை, குழந்தைகள் என இயற்கையாக அமைந்திடும் மூவர்க்கும்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:42":{"book":"kural","num":42,"t":["துறந்தார்க்கும் துவ்வாதவர்க்கும் இறந்தார்க்கும்","இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை"],"p":"He will be said to flourish in domestic virtue who aids the forsaken, the poor, and the dead.","r":"The householder is the support of those who have let go, those who have nothing and those who have died.","n":"A single support reaches even the dead. Chapter 3 uses துறந்தார் for renouncers, so Parimelazhagar's different reading here gives the same word a very different sense.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","eegai","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துறந்தார் are people abandoned by those who should support them, and இறந்தார் are those who die alone at the door.","களைகண் ஆனவரால் துறக்கப்பட்டார்க்கும்"],["manakkudavar","துறந்தார் are renouncers who have given up caste and name.","வருணத்தினையும் நாமத்தினையுந் துறந்தார்க்கும்"],["kalaignar","They are ascetics, the hungry and the unprotected, so there is no duty to the dead here.","பற்றற்ற துறவிகட்கும், பசியால் வாடுவோர்க்கும், பாதுகாப்பற்றவர்க்கும்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:43":{"book":"kural","num":43,"t":["தென்புலத்தார் தெய்வம் விருந்தொக்கல் தானென்றாங்கு","ஐம்புலத்தாறு ஓம்பல் தலை"],"p":"The chief (duty of the householder) is to preserve the five-fold rule (of conduct) towards the manes, the Gods, his guests, his relations and himself.","r":"To tend properly the five, namely ancestors, gods, guests, kin and oneself, is the chief duty.","n":"The self is last on a list of duties. Parimelazhagar's reason is that one must exist in order to do any virtue, so self-care is a moral requirement. Kalaignar replaces ancestors and gods with memory of the dead and honour for those who live well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","virundhu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:44":{"book":"kural","num":44,"t":["பழியஞ்சிப் பாத்தூண் உடைத்தாயின் வாழ்க்கை","வழியெஞ்சல் எஞ்ஞான்றும் இல்"],"p":"His descendants shall never fail who, living in the domestic state, fears vice (in the acquisition of property) and shares his food (with others).","r":"If a life fears blame and shares its food, its line never fails at any time.","n":"Fear of blame comes before sharing because generosity can't launder what was wrongly got. Parimelazhagar gives a striking account of how merit and fault split in that case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","pazhi","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","If someone shares wealth that was wrongly taken, the merit goes to the owner and the sin stays with the sharer.","அறம் பொருளுடையார் மேலும், பாவம் தன் மேலுமாய் நின்று வழி எஞ்சும்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:45":{"book":"kural","num":45,"t":["அன்பும் அறனும் உடைத்தாயின் இல்வாழ்க்கை","பண்பும் பயனும் அது"],"p":"If the married life possess love and virtue, these will be both its duty and reward.","r":"If household life has love and virtue, these are its character and its fruit.","n":"The verse makes the reward internal: the fruit of having love and virtue is having them. Parimelazhagar directs love to the wife and virtue to sharing, whereas Manakkudavar directs love to everyone and says no further reward is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","aram","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:46":{"book":"kural","num":46,"t":["அறத்தாற்றின் இல்வாழ்க்கை ஆற்றின் புறத்தாற்றில்","போஒய்ப் பெறுவ தெவன்?"],"p":"What will he who lives virtuously in the domestic state gain by going into the other, (ascetic) state ?","r":"If one conducts household life in the way of virtue, what would one gain by going off along the outside way?","n":"The rhetorical question challenges chapter 3 directly. Everything depends on how far 'outside way' reaches.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thuravu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The outside way is only the forest stage, which leaves full renunciation out of the comparison.","இல்லை விட்டு வனத்துச் செல்லும் நிலை."],["manakkudavar","The outside way is austerity in general, and the householder gets the fruit of austerity too.","புறநெறியாகிய தவத்திற் போய்ப் பெறுவது யாதோ?"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:47":{"book":"kural","num":47,"t":["இயல்பினான் இல்வாழ்க்கை வாழ்பவன் என்பான்","முயல்வாருள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"Among all those who labour (for future happiness) he is greatest who lives well in the household state.","r":"One who lives the household life according to its nature stands first among all who strive.","n":"The verse doesn't say what they strive for, so the comparison group changes with each commentator.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those striving are ascetics trying to give up the senses, and the householder gets the result without such effort.","புலன்களை விட முயல்வார் எல்லாருள்ளும் மிக்கவன்."],["manakkudavar","Those striving are people striving for wealth.","முயறல் - பொருட்கு முயறல்."]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:48":{"book":"kural","num":48,"t":["ஆற்றின் ஒழுக் கி அறனிழுக்கா இல்வாழ் க்கை","நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து"],"p":"The householder who, not swerving from virtue, helps the ascetic in his way, endures more than those who endure penance.","r":"A household life that keeps others on their way and doesn't itself slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance.","n":"The word chosen, நோன்மை, is the ascetic's own virtue claimed for the householder. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the ascetic bears only their own pain, while the householder also bears that of others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","thavam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The householder also endures the troubles that come upon others.","பிறரை உற்ற நோயும் பொறுத்தல் இன்மையின்"]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:49":{"book":"kural","num":49,"t":["அறன் எனப் பட்டதே இல்வாழ்க்கை அஃதும்","பிறன்பழிப்ப தில்லாயின் நன்று"],"p":"The marriage state is truly called virtue. The other state is also good, if others do not reproach it.","r":"What is called virtue is household life, and that too is good if there is nothing in it others can blame.","n":"The whole verse turns on what அஃதும் points to. On one reading it is a rebuke to renunciants, and on another it is a condition on householders. Manakkudavar's explanation also shows a caste assumption of his own.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","aram","pazhi","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'That too' is renunciation, which is good only if it is free of hypocrisy.","ஏனைத் துறவறமோ எனின், அதுவும் பிறனால் பழிக்கப்படுவது இல்லையாயின்"],["manakkudavar","'That too' is household life, and the blame in question would be a wife of low birth.","பழிக்கப்படுவதென்றது இழிகுலத்தாளாகிய மனையாளை."],["kalaignar","A blameless household life is what is honoured as the household dharma.","பழிப்புக்கு இடமில்லாத இல்வாழ்க்கை இல்லறம் எனப் போற்றப்படும்."]],"ch":"k:5"},"k:50":{"book":"kural","num":50,"t":["வையத்துள் வாழ்வாங்கு வாழ்பவன் வான்உநற்யும்","தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He who on earth has lived in the conjugal state as he should live, will be placed among the Gods who dwell in heaven.","r":"One who lives on earth as life should be lived will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky.","n":"வைக்கப்படும், 'will be placed', could mean a destiny or an estimation. Manakkudavar treats it as being honoured by all, while Parimelazhagar also expects actual godhood later on. The Tamil word for how to live, வாழ்வாங்கு, is left undefined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:5"},"k:51":{"book":"kural","num":51,"t":["மனைக்தக்க மாண்புடையள் ஆகித்தற் கொண்டான்","வளத்தக்காள் வாழ்க்கைத் துணை"],"p":"She who has the excellence of home virtues, and can expend within the means of her husband, is a help in the domestic state.","r":"She who has the excellence the home needs, and lives within the means of the man who took her, is the companion of life.","n":"Income literacy is half the definition, and it comes before chastity is mentioned. Parimelazhagar makes her skills concrete: knowing what is needed, cooking and neighbourly help. Manakkudavar instead reads மனை as her birth family, which shifts the standard from her work to where she comes from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","selvam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:52":{"book":"kural","num":52,"t":["மனைமாட்சி இல்லாள்கண் இல்லாயின் வாழ்க்கை","எனைமாட்சித் தாயினும் இல்"],"p":"If the wife be devoid of domestic excellence, whatever (other) greatness be possessed, the conjugal state, is nothing.","r":"If the wife lacks household excellence, the life has nothing, whatever other distinction it may have.","n":"The final word இல், nothing, is blunt. The verse doesn't say the life is worse, it says the life isn't there at all, and Parimelazhagar says this is because it bears no fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:53":{"book":"kural","num":53,"t":["இல்லதென் இல்லவள் மாண்பானால் உள்ளதென்","இல்லவள் மாணாக் கடை?"],"p":"If his wife be eminent (in virtue), what does (that man) not possess ? If she be without excellence, what does (he) possess ?","r":"What is lacking if the wife has worth, and what is present if she does not?","n":"Two questions with no answers given. Manakkudavar supplies the sharp version: though the man has nothing, nothing is lacking, and though he has everything, nothing is there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:54":{"book":"kural","num":54,"t":["பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க யாவுள கற்பென்னும்","திண்மைஉண் டாகப் பெறின்"],"p":"What is more excellent than a wife, if she possess the stability of chastity ?","r":"What is greater than a woman, if she has the firmness called கற்பு?","n":"The word திண்மை presents கற்பு as a strength, not as mere purity. Whether the verse is ranking a possession or a form of life is what divides the commentators.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","A wife is ranked among the things a man can obtain, and none is better.","ஒருவன் எய்தும் பொருள்களுள் இல்லாளின் மேம்பட்ட பொருள்கள் யாவை உள"],["manakkudavar","The comparison is about being born a woman, which nothing exceeds if it has this firmness.","பெண் பிறப்புப்போல் மேம்பட்டன யாவையுள்?"],["kalaignar","The firmness is womanhood's own strength of character.","பெண்மையின் உறுதிப் பண்பைப் பெற்றுவிட்டால்,"]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:55":{"book":"kural","num":55,"t":["தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் கொழுநன் தொழுதெழுவாள்","பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை"],"p":"If she, who does not worship God, but who rising worships her husband, say, \"let it rain,\" it will rain.","r":"She who rises worshipping her husband and not a god: if she says 'rain', it rains.","n":"The verse says flatly that she doesn't worship a god. Parimelazhagar softens this by adding 'other' gods. The line is both the strongest claim of a woman's power in the book and the most troubling, and Kalaignar's entry reads the rain as an image of fearful servitude, not of power.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","mazhai","iraivan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Her husband is her own god, and her steadfastness is so powerful that the deities obey her.","பிற தெய்வம் தொழாது தன் தெய்வம் ஆகிய கொழுநனைத் தொழாநின்று துயிலெழுவாள்"],["manakkudavar","She regards all gods as being her husband.","எல்லாத் தெய்வ முந் தன்கணவனென்றே கருதி"],["kalaignar","Such a wife is like rain falling in fear at a command: she has made herself a slave.","அஞ்சி நடுங்கிப் பெய்கின்ற மழையைப் போலத் தன்னை அடிமையாக எண்ணிக் கொள்பவளாவாள்."]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:56":{"book":"kural","num":56,"t":["தற்காத்துத் தற்கொண்டாற் பேணித் தகைசான்ற","சொற்காத்துச் சோர்விலாள் பெண்"],"p":"She is a wife who unweariedly guards herself, takes care of her husband, and preserves an unsullied fame.","r":"She is a woman who guards herself, cares for the man who took her, keeps their good name and does not flag.","n":"பெண் here is a title that has to be earned, as the verse defines who counts as a woman. Parimelazhagar takes சொல் as a shared reputation for wife and husband, which means she is responsible for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","pugazh","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:57":{"book":"kural","num":57,"t":["சிறைகாக்கும் காப்பெவன் செய்யும் மகளிர்","நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பே தலை"],"p":"What avails the guard of a prison ? The chief guard of a woman is her chastity.","r":"What use is the guarding of women by confinement? The guard that their own steadfastness keeps is the best.","n":"The verse rejects confinement because it does not work, and it doesn't speak of the woman's rights. It leaves in place the assumption that she needs guarding, but it moves that guard inside her.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Walls and door-guards are useless without நிறை, the holding of her heart to chastity.","காவல் இரண்டினும் நிறைக் காவல் இல்வழி ஏனைச் சிறைக்காவலால் பயன் இல்லை"],["kalaignar","The verse is a charge against men: treating such women as slaves is ignorance.","அடிமைகளாக நடத்த எண்ணுவது அறியாமையாகும்."]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:58":{"book":"kural","num":58,"t":["பெற்றாற் பெறின்பெறுவர் பெண்டிர் பெருஞ்சிறப்புப்","புத்தேளிர் வாழும் உலகு"],"p":"If women shew reverence to their husbands, they will obtain great excellence in the world where the gods flourish.","r":"If women obtain the one who obtained them, they will gain great distinction in the world where the gods live.","n":"The word play பெற்றாற் பெறின் leaves open what 'obtaining' a husband means. Who has to be worthy depends on how the gap is filled.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The missing word is worship: women who serve their husbands are honoured by the gods.","தற்கொண்டாற் பேணிய மகளிர் புத்தேளிரால் பேணப்படுவர்"],["kalaignar","If a woman obtains a virtuous husband, household life becomes a new world for her.","நற்பண்பு பெற்றவனைக் கணவனாகப் பெற்றால்,"]],"ch":"k:6"},"k:59":{"book":"kural","num":59,"t":["புகழ்புரிந்த இல்லிலோர்க்கு இல்லை இகழ்வார்முன்","ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை"],"p":"The man whose wife seeks not the praise (of chastity) cannot walk with lion-like stately step, before those who revile them.","r":"Those whose home does not seek renown cannot walk with a great beast's proud gait before those who deride them.","n":"The man's public bearing is said to depend on his wife's conduct. ஏறு needn't mean a lion as Pope and Parimelazhagar have it, since the word covers male animals generally, and Manakkudavar describes the gait without naming an animal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","perumai","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:60":{"book":"kural","num":60,"t":["மங்கலம் என்ப மனைமாட்சி மற்று அதன்","நன்கலம் நன்மக்கட் பேறு"],"p":"The excellence of a wife is the good of her husband; and good children are the jewels of that goodness.","r":"They call a home's excellence its blessing, and good children its fine ornament.","n":"Parimelazhagar says this verse prepares for the next chapter by naming the wife's ornament. Children are ornaments here and do not yet have the value they get in chapter 7. Kalaignar takes மனைமாட்சி as the family's culture and not specifically the wife's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","makkal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:6"},"k:61":{"book":"kural","num":61,"t":["பெறுமவற்றுள் யாமறிவது இல்லை அறிவறிந்த","மக்கட்பேறு அல்ல பிற"],"p":"Among all the benefits that may be acquired, we know no greater benefit than the acquisition of intelligent children.","r":"Of all that can be gained, we know of nothing else like children who come to know what should be known.","n":"The first-person யாம் அறிவது is unusual and it sounds personal. The word அறிவறிந்த makes this a conditional praise: not children as such, but children who come to understand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The epithet of understanding restricts மக்கள் to sons, excluding daughters.","'மக்கள்' என்னும் பெயர் பெண் ஒழித்து நின்றது."],["kalaignar","The verse is about intelligent children in general, with no such exclusion.","அறிவில் சிறந்த நல்ல பிள்ளைகளைவிட"]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:62":{"book":"kural","num":62,"t":["எழுபிறப்பும் தீயவை தீண்டா பழிபிறங்காப்","பண்புடை மக்கட் பெறின்"],"p":"The evils of the seven births shall not touch those who abtain children of a good disposition, free from vice.","r":"Evils will not touch one for seven births, if one has children of good character who bring no blame.","n":"The verse assumes a child's character can protect a parent across births. Kalaignar's reading turns seven births into seven generations, which reverses the direction from the parent's future to the descendants', but no aligned Parimelazhagar or Manakkudavar comment is available to set against it here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","piravi","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:63":{"book":"kural","num":63,"t":["தம்பொருள் என்பதம் மக்கள் அவர்பொருள்","தம்தம் வினையான் வரும்"],"p":"Men will call their sons their wealth, because it flows to them through the deeds which they (sons) perform on their behalf.","r":"People call their children their wealth, because the wealth of those children comes by way of their own deeds.","n":"தம்தம் வினை is the crux: whose deeds, and whose wealth? Pope's couplet and his own gloss take opposite sides of this.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Sons' good deeds done on the parents' behalf bring what they earned to the parents in the next world.","அப்புதல்வர் செய்த பொருள் தம்மை நோக்கி அவர் செய்யும் நல்வினையானே தம்பால் வரும்"],["kalaignar","Each child's wealth comes from that child's own actions.","அம்மக்களின் பொருள்கள் அவரவர் செயல்களின் விளைவாக வரக் கூடியவை."]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:64":{"book":"kural","num":64,"t":["அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிதேதம் மக்கள்","சிறுகை அளாவிய கூழ்"],"p":"The rice in which the little hand of their children has dabbled will be far sweeter (to the parent) than ambrosia.","r":"Far sweeter than amrita is the plain gruel stirred by the small hands of one's own children.","n":"The food is கூழ், a humble gruel, and it is sweet because of the mess. Parimelazhagar illustrates the mess by citing Purananuru 188, which makes this verse a real thread across books.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:65":{"book":"kural","num":65,"t":["மக்கள்மெய் தீண்டல் உடற்கின்பம் மற்று அவர்","சொற்கேட்டல் இன்பம் செவிக்கு"],"p":"The touch of children gives pleasure to the body, and the hearing of their words, pleasure to the ear.","r":"Touching one's children is joy to the body, and hearing their words is joy to the ear.","n":"The chapter dwells on the senses here, after verses that praise restraining them. Parimelazhagar notes that சொல் is deliberately general, so it covers grown learned speech as well as baby talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:66":{"book":"kural","num":66,"t":["குழல் இனிது யாழ்இனிது என்பதம் மக்கள்","மழலைச்சொல் கேளா தவர்"],"p":"\"The pipe is sweet, the lute is sweet,\" say those who have not heard the prattle of their own children.","r":"'The flute is sweet, the lute is sweet', say those who haven't heard their own children's prattle.","n":"The verse never actually says prattle is sweeter. It leaves that to be inferred, and Parimelazhagar notes this implied ending. Such praise comes in the form of mild scorn for the musically refined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["makkal","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:67":{"book":"kural","num":67,"t":["தந்தை மகற்காற்று நன்றி அவையத்து","முந்தி இருப்பச் செயல்"],"p":"The benefit which a father should confer on his son is to give him precedence in the assembly of the learned.","r":"The good a father does for a son is to make him sit at the front in the assembly.","n":"Parimelazhagar rules out nepotism: the son must actually surpass the assembly in learning. He adds that making him rich is no real good, since wealth brings pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:68":{"book":"kural","num":68,"t":["தம்மின்தம் மக்கள் அறிவுடைமை மாநிலத்து","மன்னுயிர்க் கெல்லாம் இனிது"],"p":"That their children should possess knowledge is more pleasing to all men of this great earth than to themselves.","r":"Their children's understanding is sweet to all beings on the great earth, more than to themselves.","n":"தம்மின், than themselves, can attach to two different things. Pope's couplet and gloss again split, one on each side.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is sweeter to all the world, especially the assembly, than it is to the parents.","மன்உயிர்க்கு எல்லாம் தம்மின் இனிது"],["kalaignar","It concerns children who are wiser than their parents, which gladdens everyone.","பெற்றோரைக் காட்டிலும் பிள்ளைகள் அறிவிற் சிறந்து விளங்கினால்,"]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:69":{"book":"kural","num":69,"t":["ஈன்ற பொழுதின் பெரிதுவக்கும் தன்மகனைச்","சான்றோன் எனக்கேட்ட தாய்"],"p":"The mother who hears her son called \"a wise man\" will rejoice more than she did at his birth.","r":"A mother who hears her son called worthy rejoices more than at the hour she bore him.","n":"The verse says she hears. Parimelazhagar's explanation is his own and is telling: he says women cannot know this for themselves. The verse may simply show that public recognition is what is wanted. It sets the birth's joy as the baseline and goes beyond it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She has to hear it because, by a woman's nature, she cannot judge it herself.","பெண்ணியல்பால் தானாக அறியாமையின் 'கேட்ட தாய்'"]],"ch":"k:7"},"k:70":{"book":"kural","num":70,"t":["மகன்தந்தைக்கு ஆற்றும் உதவி இவன்தந்தை","என்நோற்றான் கொல் எனும் சொல்"],"p":"(So to act) that it may be said \"by what great penance did his father beget him,\" is the benefit which a son should render to his father.","r":"The help a son gives his father is the saying, 'What penance did this one's father do?'","n":"The repayment is a sentence spoken by others. The son can't deliver it directly, only live so that others say it. The verse uses the language of austerity (நோற்றான்), so fatherhood is framed as a kind of penance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","pugazh","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:7"},"k:71":{"book":"kural","num":71,"t":["அன்பிற்கும் உண்டோ அடைக்குந்தாழ் ஆர்வலர்","புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்"],"p":"Is there any fastening that can shut in love ? Tears of the affectionate will publish the love that is within.","r":"Is there any bolt that can shut in even love? The small tears of the loving will proclaim it.","n":"A tiny tear (புன்கணீர்) produces an uproar (பூசல்), and the contrast in scale is the point. Parimelazhagar specifies that the tears fall at the beloved's pain, and he treats them as logical inference: love is unseen but can be inferred.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:72":{"book":"kural","num":72,"t":["அன்பிலார் எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர் அன்புடையார்","என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு"],"p":"Those who are destitute of love appropriate all they have to themselves; but those who possess love cons ider even their bones to belong to others.","r":"The loveless belong to themselves in everything, and the loving belong to others even to their bones.","n":"Love is framed as a question of ownership. Parimelazhagar glosses the bones by citing Purananuru 43, on the king who gave his own flesh for a dove, which is a direct thread to the Sangam corpus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:73":{"book":"kural","num":73,"t":["அன்போடு இயைந்த வழக்கென்ப ஆருயிர்க்கு","என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு"],"p":"They say that the union of soul and body in man is the fruit of the union of love and virtue (in a former birth).","r":"They say the bond of the precious life with the bone is the way that came to be joined with love.","n":"The syntax is compressed, and the verse could give embodiment a purpose or a past cause. On Parimelazhagar's reading, the body exists for loving, which is an unusually positive view of embodiment. Pope's gloss follows the past-birth view.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The soul is joined to a body for the purpose of love, since love can't be done without one.","உடம்போடு இயைந்தல்லது அன்பு செய்யலாகாமையின், அது செய்தற் பொருட்டு இத்தொடர்ச்சி உளதாயிற்று"],["manakkudavar","The present embodiment results from love practised in a previous birth.","முற்பிறப்பின்கண் அன்போடு பொருந்தச் சென்ற செலவென்று சொல்லுவர்"],["kalaignar","The verse is an analogy: love and action should fit as soul and body do.","உயிரும் உடலும்போல் அன்பும் செயலும் இணைந்திருப்பதே உயர்ந்த பொருத்தமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:74":{"book":"kural","num":74,"t":["அன்பு ஈனும் ஆர்வம் உடைமை அதுஈனும்","நண்பு என்னும் நாடாச் சிறப்பு"],"p":"Love begets desire: and that (desire) begets the immeasureable excellence of friendship.","r":"Love gives rise to eagerness for others, and that gives rise to the unsought distinction called friendship.","n":"Love is a seed with a sequence. Parimelazhagar widens the final term a great deal: love for one's own extends to everyone, until the person has neither enemies nor neutrals. நாடா, unsought, suggests this distinction arrives without being chased.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:75":{"book":"kural","num":75,"t":["அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கென்ப வையகத்து","இன்புற்றார் எய்தும் சிறப்பு"],"p":"They say that the felicity which those who, after enjoying the pleasure (of the conjugal state) in this world, obtain in heaven is the result of their domestic state imbued with love.","r":"They say that the distinction won by those who have known joy in this world is the outcome of a life settled in love.","n":"The commentators disagree about the timeline. Manakkudavar puts the love in a past birth, Parimelazhagar puts the distinction in heaven after this life, and Kalaignar keeps both in this life. Parimelazhagar's contrast is a pointed one: ascetics reach heaven through pain, while householders reach it through joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:76":{"book":"kural","num":76,"t":["அறத்திற்கே அன்புசார் பென்ப அறியார்","மறத்திற்கும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"The ignorant say that love is an ally to virtue only, but it is also a help to get out of vice.","r":"The ignorant say that love supports only virtue, but it is also the ally against, or of, மறம்.","n":"The word மறம் is the problem. It can mean sin or valour, and the ambiguous dative 'for மறம்' leaves open whether love helps to remove it or to do it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறம் is hostile feeling, and love helps remove it when one treats an enemy as a friend.","மறத்தை நீக்குதற்கும் துணையாம்"],["manakkudavar","Love also stands by those who do மறம்.","அவ்வன்பு மறஞ் செய்வார்க்குத் துணையாம்,"],["kalaignar","மறம் is heroism, and heroic action is also backed by love.","வீரச் செயல்களுக்கும் அன்பு துணையாகத் திகழ்கிறது"]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:77":{"book":"kural","num":77,"t":["என்பி லதனை வெயில்போலக் காயுமே","அன்பி லதனை அறம்"],"p":"So loveless beings virtue's power to nothing brings.","r":"Virtue burns what has no love, as the sun burns what has no bones.","n":"It is a chilling image, and on Parimelazhagar's explanation the sun bears no malice: it shines equally, and the boneless creature perishes by its own nature. Virtue is thus not vindictive, which makes this verse more unsettling.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aram","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is அறம் personified as a deity, impersonal like the sun, and the loveless soul perishes before it.","அன்பில்லாத உயிரை அறக்கடவுள்."],["kalaignar","What burns is one's own conscience, in someone who knows virtue but doesn't follow it.","அவரது மனச்சாட்சியே வாட்டி வதைக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:78":{"book":"kural","num":78,"t":["அன்பகத் தில்லா உயிர்வாழ்க்கை வன்பாற்கண்","வற்றல் மரந்தளிர்த் தற்று"],"p":"The domestic state of that man whose mind is without love is like the flourishing of a withered tree upon the parched desert.","r":"A life without love inside is like a dried tree putting forth shoots on hard ground.","n":"The image is an impossibility, as Parimelazhagar says and as Manakkudavar explains: there is nothing in the tree to cause sprouting. The verse says உயிர்வாழ்க்கை, a living being's life, and Parimelazhagar and Pope narrow it to household life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:79":{"book":"kural","num":79,"t":["புறத்துறுப் பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்பு ம் யாக்கை","அகத்துறுப்பு அன்பி லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Of what avail are all the external members (of the body) to those who are destitute of love, the internal member.","r":"What use are all the outer parts to those without love, the inner part of the body?","n":"Love is classed as an organ, which is a striking way to make it non-optional. Parimelazhagar explicitly refutes the bodily-organs reading that Kalaignar's comment follows.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anbu","manam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Outer 'parts' are household means such as place, wealth and servants, because the body reading has no connection to household life.","புறத்து உறுப்பாவன: இடனும், பொருளும், ஏவல் செய்வாரும் முதலாயின."],["kalaignar","Outer parts are the body's beauty, which is pointless without love.","புறத்து உறுப்புகள் அழகாக இருந்து என்ன பயன்?"]],"ch":"k:8"},"k:80":{"book":"kural","num":80,"t":["அன்பின் வழியது உயிர்நிலை அஃதிலார்க்கு","என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு"],"p":"That body alone which is inspired with love contains a living soul: if void of it, (the body) is bone overlaid with skin.","r":"The seat of life goes by way of love; for those without it, a body is bones wrapped in skin.","n":"The final verse is about what counts as alive, and it isn't an exaggeration about ethics. Manakkudavar connects உயிர்நிலை to release, whereas Parimelazhagar connects it to the household virtue that makes a body live.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:8"},"k:81":{"book":"kural","num":81,"t":["இருந்தோம்பி இல்வாழ்வ தெல்லாம் விருந்தோம்பி","வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"The whole design of living in the domestic state and laying up (property) is (to be able) to exercise the","r":"All of staying at home and living by guarding one's goods is for the purpose of caring for guests and doing them good.","n":"The verse gives household life a purpose outside itself. Parimelazhagar draws the consequence: without this, the toil of home and earning has no fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:82":{"book":"kural","num":82,"t":["விருந்து புறத்ததாத் தானுண்டல் சாவா","மருந்தெனினும் வேண் டற்பாற் றன்று"],"p":"It is not fit that one should wish his guests to be outside (his house) even though he were eating the food of immortality.","r":"Even if it were the medicine of immortality, eating it alone while the guest stays outside is not to be desired.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a second, sharper reading as acceptable: if someone calls even one meal eaten without a guest a medicine of immortality, reject it. Either way, the argument sets a refusal to eat alone over the prospect of not dying.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:83":{"book":"kural","num":83,"t":["வருவிருந்து வைகலும் ஓம்புவான் வாழ்க்கை","பருவந்து பாழ்படுதல் இன்று"],"p":"The domestic life of the man that daily entertains the guests who come to him shall not be laid waste by poverty.","r":"The life of one who daily tends the guest who comes will not suffer and fall into ruin.","n":"It answers the obvious worry that hosting every day would be ruinous. Parimelazhagar goes further than the verse's 'not ruined': he says wealth will keep branching out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:84":{"book":"kural","num":84,"t":["அகனமர்ந்து செய்யாள் உறையும் முகனமர்ந்து","நல்விருந்து ஓம்புவான் இல்"],"p":"Lakshmi with joyous mind shall dwell in the house of that man who, with cheerful countenance, entertains the good as guests.","r":"The fair goddess dwells with a glad heart in the home of one who tends good guests with a glad face.","n":"A chiasm links the two gladnesses: the host's face and the goddess's heart. Parimelazhagar's reason is that she's pleased when her wealth goes the right way. He also takes நல், 'good', to limit the guests to those of high knowledge and conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:85":{"book":"kural","num":85,"t":["வித்தும் இடல்வேண்டும் கொல்லோ விருந்தோம்பி","மிச்சில் மிசைவான் புலம்"],"p":"Is it necessary to sow the field of the man who, having feasted his guests, eats what may remain ?","r":"Must one even sow the field of the person who feeds guests and then eats what's left?","n":"மிச்சில், leftovers, is a low word for the host's own food, as if the guest's plate comes first. The rhetorical question doesn't say what the answer is, and the commentators answer it in opposite ways.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","No sowing is needed, since the field will yield by itself.","'தானே விளையும்' என்பது குறிப்பெச்சம்."],["kalaignar","Such a host would use even the seed set aside for the field to feed guests.","தன் நிலத்திற்குரிய விதையைக்கூட விருந்தோம்பலுக்குப் பயன்படுத்தாமல் இருப்பானா?"]],"ch":"k:9"},"k:86":{"book":"kural","num":86,"t":["செல்விருந்து ஓம்பி வருவிருந்து பார்த்திருப்பான்","நல்வருந்து வானத் தவர்க்கு"],"p":"He who, having entertained the guests that have come, looks out for others who may yet come, will be a welcome guest to the inhabitants of heaven.","r":"One who tends the guest who is leaving and watches for the guest to come will be a good guest to those in the sky.","n":"The host becomes the guest in heaven, and the verse has an elegant reciprocity. Manakkudavar gives 'watching' a concrete rule: not eating without a guest. Kalaignar's 'those in the sky of renown' removes the literal heaven that Parimelazhagar assumes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","iraivan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:87":{"book":"kural","num":87,"t":["இனைத்துணைத் தென்பதொன் றில்லை விருந்தின்","துணைத்துணை வேள்விப் பயன்"],"p":"The advantages of benevolence cannot be measured; the measure (of the virtue) of the guests (entertained) is the only measure.","r":"The fruit of this sacrifice has no set measure: it is as great as the guest's worth.","n":"Calling hospitality வேள்வி claims the language of ritual for a household meal. The measure is the guest and not the cost, and Parimelazhagar supports this with a Naladiyar verse. This also introduces a hierarchy among guests, which sits awkwardly with welcoming whoever comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:88":{"book":"kural","num":88,"t":["பரிந்தோம்பிப் பற்றற்றேம் என்பர் விருந்தோம்பி","வேள்வி தலைப்படா தார்"],"p":"Who cherish not their guests, nor kindly help supply.","r":"Those who don't take up the sacrifice of tending guests guard their goods anxiously and then say 'we have no support'.","n":"It is a late regret: the wealth they guarded with such pain is gone. பற்றற்றேம் can also be read as the renouncer's boast, 'we're without attachment', and that echo seems to me to sharpen the irony, though no commentator here says so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:89":{"book":"kural","num":89,"t":["உடைமையுள் இன்மை விருந்தோம்பல் ஓம்பா","மடமை மடவார்கண் உண்டு"],"p":"That stupidity which excercises no hospitality is poverty in the midst of wealth. It is the property of the stupid.","r":"Poverty in the midst of having is the folly that neglects hospitality, and it is found only among fools.","n":"The verse redefines poverty as a failure of use, not a lack of goods. Parimelazhagar explains: since folly takes away the benefit one gets from wealth, it makes having into not having.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:90":{"book":"kural","num":90,"t":["மோப்பக் குழையும் அனிச்சம் முகந்திரிந்து","நோக்கக் குநழ்யும் விருந்து"],"p":"As the Anicham flower fades in smelling, so fades the guest when the face is turned away.","r":"The anicham flower wilts when smelled, and a guest wilts when the host's face changes on seeing them.","n":"Parimelazhagar's analysis has the guest more delicate than the flower, as the flower needs to be touched but the guest wilts at a distance. He gives a sequence of pleasant face, pleasant word and good deed, which leads directly into the next chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["virundhu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:9"},"k:91":{"book":"kural","num":91,"t":["இன்சொலால் ஈரம் அளைஇப் படிறுஇலவாம்","செம்பொருள் கண்டார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"Sweet words are those which imbued with love and free from deceit flow from the mouth of the virtuous.","r":"Sweet words are those from the mouths of people who see the true thing, moist with love and free of deceit.","n":"The definition rules out flattery. Sweetness needs truthfulness and love, and ஈரம், moisture, makes love a physical quality of the words. Whether it allows rebuke is what the commentators bring out.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","anbu","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செம்பொருள் is virtue, and the speakers are those who understand it.","அறத்தினை உணர்ந்தார் வாயிற்சொற்கள்."],["manakkudavar","The speakers judge disputes between two parties, and they must rebuke sweetly too.","இஃது ஒருவனைக் கடிந்து சொல்லவேண்டு மிடத்தும் இன்சொல்லாலே கடிய வேண்டுமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:10"},"k:92":{"book":"kural","num":92,"t":["அகன்அமர்ந்து ஈதலின் நன்றே முகனமர்ந்து","இன்சொலன் ஆகப் பெறின்"],"p":"Sweet speech, with a cheerful countenance is better than a gift made with a joyous mind.","r":"Better than giving with a glad heart is to be, with a glad face, a person of sweet speech.","n":"This is a surprising ranking in a book so committed to giving. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that sweet speech is in one's own power, where a gift depends on wealth, yet it comes naturally only to a virtuous heart, so it's the rarer thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:93":{"book":"kural","num":93,"t":["முகத்தான் அமர்ந் துஇனிது நோக்கி அகத்தானாம்","இன்சொ லினதே அறம்"],"p":"Sweet speech, flowing from the heart (uttered) with a cheerful countenance and a sweet look, is true virtue.","r":"Virtue lies in sweet words from the heart, after looking kindly with a glad face.","n":"The sequence is face, then eye, then heart, then word, and அகத்தானாம் is what keeps it from being mere manners. Parimelazhagar takes the emphatic ending as 'not in giving'. That's a strong inference: the verse may only be emphasizing speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","aram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:94":{"book":"kural","num":94,"t":["துன்புறூஉம் துவ்வாமை இல்லாகும் யார்மாட்டும்","இன்புறூஉம் இன்சொ லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Sorrow-increasing poverty shall not come upon those who use towards all, pleasure-increasing sweetness of speech.","r":"Grievous want won't come to those whose sweet speech gives everyone joy.","n":"The two forms உறூஉம் balance grief and joy in matching words. The promise seems to be social, not magical: Parimelazhagar reasons that one who has only friends comes to all wealth.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துவ்வாமை is material poverty, in which the senses have nothing to enjoy.","துன்பத்தை மிகுவிக்கும் நல்குரவு இல்லையாம்."],["kalaignar","The want removed is a poverty of friendship.","'நட்பில் வறுமை' எனும் துன்பமில்லை."]],"ch":"k:10"},"k:95":{"book":"kural","num":95,"t":["பணிவுடையன் இன்சொலன் ஆதல் ஒருவற்கு","அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற"],"p":"Humility and sweetness of speech are the ornaments of man; all others are not (ornaments).","r":"Being humble and sweet-spoken is a person's ornament, and nothing else is.","n":"The verse states humility without limit. Parimelazhagar limits it: one is to be humble toward those who deserve deference and sweet-spoken toward everybody. That's a notable narrowing by the commentator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:96":{"book":"kural","num":96,"t":["அல்லவை தேய அறம்பெருகும் நல்லவை","நாடி இனிய சொலின்"],"p":"If a man, while seeking to speak usefully, speaks also sweetly, his sins will diminish and his virtue increase.","r":"If one seeks out good things and speaks them sweetly, what is not virtue wears away and virtue grows.","n":"நாடி, having sought out, makes sweetness conditional on content. Parimelazhagar adds the converse: good counsel spoken harshly isn't virtue. For him the effect is on the speaker's own merit, whereas Kalaignar makes it a method of guiding others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:97":{"book":"kural","num":97,"t":["நயன் ஈன்று நன்றி பயக்கும் பயன்ஈன்று","பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியாச் சொல்"],"p":"That speech which, while imparting benefits ceases not to please, will yield righteousness (for this world) and merit (for the next world).","r":"Speech that yields benefit and never parts from gentleness produces justice and brings forth good.","n":"பயன் and பண்பு are held together, so usefulness alone doesn't suffice and neither does charm. Parimelazhagar defines நயன் strikingly as being in accord with the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:98":{"book":"kural","num":98,"t":["சிறுமையுவு நீங்கிய இன்சொல் மறுமையும்","இம்மையும் இன்பம் தரும்"],"p":"Sweet speech, free from harm to others, will give pleasure both in this world and in the next.","r":"Sweet speech free of pettiness gives joy in the next life and in this one.","n":"The next life is named first, and Parimelazhagar says this is because its joy is greater. Kalaignar reads this as renown during and after life, which removes the afterlife. Pope's and Parimelazhagar's 'harm to others' is more specific than சிறுமை, smallness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inchol","inbam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:99":{"book":"kural","num":99,"t":["இன்சொல் இனிதீன்றல் காண்பான் எவன்கொலோ","வன்சொல் வழங்கு வது?"],"p":"Why does he use harsh words, who sees the pleasure which sweet speech yields ?","r":"Why would one who sees how sweetly sweet speech yields use harsh words?","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this an argument from experience: you know how others' sweet words feel to you, and harsh words are just as painful to them. The verse doesn't command, it only asks what the point is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:100":{"book":"kural","num":100,"t":["இனிய உளவாக இன்னாத கூறல்","கனிஇருப்பக் காய்கவர்ந் தற்று"],"p":"To say disagreeable things when agreeable are at hand is like eating unripe fruit when there is ripe.","r":"Speaking unpleasant things when pleasant ones are there is like grabbing raw fruit when ripe fruit is at hand.","n":"The final charge is stupidity, not wickedness. Parimelazhagar makes the stakes much higher by reading the fruit as Avvai's nelli, which is like amrita, and the raw one as poison. He adds that harsh speech ends up hurting the speaker, and this allusion is a thread to the Sangam story of Avvai and the king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:10"},"k:101":{"book":"kural","num":101,"t":["செய்யாமல் செய்த உதவிக்கு வையகமும்","வானகமும் ஆற்றல் அரிது"],"p":"(The gift of) heaven and earth is not an equivalent for a benefit which is conferred where none had been received.","r":"For help given when no help was given first, earth and heaven together are hardly repayment.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives the reason: all repayment has a cause, and this help has none, so nothing can match it. He also records a variant reading, in which the helped person could not return it, which changes the focus from the giver's freedom to the receiver's helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He reports another reading of the text, about help given to those unable to return it.","மறித்து உதவமாட்டாமை உள்ளவிடத்துச் செய்த உதவி என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர்."]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:102":{"book":"kural","num":102,"t":["காலத்தி னாற்செய்த நன்றி சிறிதெனினும்","ஞாலத்தின் மாணப் பெரிது"],"p":"A favour conferred in the time of need, though it be small (in itself), is (in value) much larger than the world.","r":"Help given in its time, though small, is far greater than the earth.","n":"Value here is relative to the need of the moment. Parimelazhagar's phrase is striking: it's the moment of mortal crisis that is being weighed, not the gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:103":{"book":"kural","num":103,"t":["பயன்தூக்கார் செய்த உதவி நயன்தூக்கின்","நன்மை கடலின் பெரிது"],"p":"If we weigh the excellence of a benefit which is conferred without weighing the return, it is larger than the sea.","r":"If you weigh the good of help given by those who don't weigh the return, it is greater than the sea.","n":"The verb தூக்கு is used twice: the giver doesn't weigh, and the recipient should. The three opening comparisons go from earth and heaven to earth to sea, and the verses seem interested in measure as such.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:104":{"book":"kural","num":104,"t":["தினைத்துணை நன்றி செயினும் பனைத்துணையாக்","கொள்வர் பயன்தெரி வார்"],"p":"Though the benefit conferred be as small as a millet seed, those who know its advantage will consider it as large as a palmyra fruit.","r":"Though the good done is only as much as a millet, those who understand its fruit take it as a palm.","n":"The enlargement happens in the recipient's mind, and it isn't an error, it's a discipline. Parimelazhagar says the fruit in question is what comes to the one who regards it this way, so gratitude also benefits the grateful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:105":{"book":"kural","num":105,"t":["உதவி வரைத்தன்று உதவி உதவி","செயப்பட்டார் சால்பின் வரைத்து"],"p":"The benefit itself is not the measure of the benefit; the worth of those who have received it is its measure.","r":"Help is not bounded by the help given: its measure is the worth of those to whom it was done.","n":"The verse repeats உதவி three times and it isn't clear which help is being measured.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This concerns repayment: what the receiver gives back is measured by the receiver's own worth, not by the original help.","கைம்மாறான உதவி, காரணத்தானும் பொருளானும் காலத்தானும் ஆகிய மூவகையானும் முன் செய்த உதவியளவிற்று அன்று;"],["kalaignar","This concerns the help itself: its value is judged by the quality of whoever receives it.","அந்த உதவியைப் பெறுபவரின் பண்பைப் பொறுத்தே அதன் அளவு மதிப்பிடப்படும்."]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:106":{"book":"kural","num":106,"t":["மறவற்க மாசற்றார் கேண்மை துறவற்க","துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு"],"p":"Forsake not the friendship of those who have been your staff in adversity. Forget not be benevolence of the blameless.","r":"Do not forget the bond of the stainless, and do not abandon the friendship of those who were your strength in sorrow.","n":"Two different people are owed loyalty: those of good character and those who helped when it was needed. Parimelazhagar says the first serves the next life and the second this one. The second debt holds whatever the helper's character is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:107":{"book":"kural","num":107,"t":["எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர் தங்கண்","விழுமந் துடைத்தவர் நட்பு"],"p":"(The wise) will remember throughout their seven-fold births the love of those who have wiped away their affliction.","r":"For all seven births they will remember the friendship of those who wiped away their sorrow.","n":"துடைத்தவர், those who wiped, has a physical sense of wiping tears away. Parimelazhagar notes the past tense conveys speed, and he cites the lost Valaiyapathi for the phrase, which is a valuable thread.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","nanri","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Seven births are meant literally: affection for the helper carries on in each birth.","பிறப்புதோறும் தொடர்ந்து அன்புடையராதல்."],["kalaignar","Seven births is a figure of exaggeration meaning that there is no time limit.","மிகைப்படுத்திச் சொல்லுவதுபோல,"]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:108":{"book":"kural","num":108,"t":["நன்றி மறப்பது நன்றன்று நன்றல்லது","அன்றே மறப்பது நன்று"],"p":"It is not good to forget a benefit; it is good to forget an injury even in the very moment (in which it is inflicted).","r":"It's not good to forget a good done; what's good is to forget, that very day, what isn't good.","n":"நன்று repeats through the verse, so 'good' is both the thing remembered and the act of letting go. Parimelazhagar says this is about one person who has done both good and harm, which is narrower than a general call to forget all wrongs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:109":{"book":"kural","num":109,"t":["கொன்றன்ன இன்னா செயினும் அவர்செய்த","ஒன்றுநன்று உள்ளக் கெடும்"],"p":"Though one inflict an injury great as murder, it will perish before the thought of one benefit (formerly) conferred.","r":"Even if they do something as painful as killing, it vanishes when one thinks of a single good they did.","n":"This is a hard saying, as it places the burden on the injured person. Parimelazhagar explains it through 104: the millet taken as a palm outweighs everything. Kalaignar makes it more therapeutic, a way to keep from being wounded inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:11"},"k:110":{"book":"kural","num":110,"t":["எந்நன்றி கொன்றார்க்கும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்வில்லை","செய்ந்நன்றி கொன்ற மகற்கு"],"p":"He who has killed every virtue may yet escape; there is no escape for him who has killed a benefit.","r":"Those who kill any good may have an escape, but there is no escape for one who kills a good done to him.","n":"Ingratitude is ranked as the sin with no remedy. Parimelazhagar's list of lesser crimes comes from Purananuru 34, whose speaker makes the same comparison, so this verse is a direct thread to that poem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even the great crimes, cutting a cow's udder, destroying a womb and killing a Brahmin, can be expiated, as Purananuru 34 lists.","'ஆன்முலை அறுத்தலும், மகளிர் கருவினைச் சிதைத்தலும், பார்ப்பார்த்தபுதலும்' (புறநா 34)"]],"ch":"k:11"},"k:111":{"book":"kural","num":111,"t":["தகுதி எனவொன்று நன்றே பகுதியால்","பாற்பட்டு ஒழுகப் பெறின்"],"p":"That equity which consists in acting with equal regard to each of (the three) divisions of men [enemies, strangers and friends] is a pre-eminent virtue.","r":"That one thing called rightness is good if one can keep to it, part by part, in what is due.","n":"Parimelazhagar says பெறின் conveys how rare this is. The whole verse depends on what பகுதி divides.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The parts are enemy, stranger and friend, toward each of whom one keeps the same rule.","பகை, நொதுமல், நட்பு எனும் பகுதிதோறும், தன் முறைமையை விடாது ஒழுகப் பெறின்,"],["manakkudavar","The parts are each person's own station, so what is due varies with position.","அவரவர் நிலைமைப்பகுதியோடே அறத்தின் பாற்பட்டு ஒழுகப் பெறுமாயின்,"]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:112":{"book":"kural","num":112,"t":["செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கஞ் சிதைவின்றி","எச்சத்திற் கேமாப்பு உடைத்து"],"p":"The wealth of the man of rectitude will not perish, but will bring happiness also to his posterity.","r":"The wealth of a person of rectitude doesn't decay and is a strength to those who come after.","n":"The claim concerns durability, not amount. Parimelazhagar explains that it lasts because it comes together with virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:113":{"book":"kural","num":113,"t":["நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கத்தை","அன்றே யொழிய விடல்"],"p":"Forsake in the very moment (of acquisition) that gain which, though it should bring advantage, is without equity.","r":"Even if it brings nothing but good, let go that very day of gain that comes from leaving what is fair.","n":"The commentators differ on the force of 'even if'. For Parimelazhagar it's only a concession, because such gain can't really bring good. Kalaignar's wording allows that it may, which makes giving it up a costlier demand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:114":{"book":"kural","num":114,"t":["தக்கார் தகவிலர் என்பது அவரவர்","எச்சத்தாற் காணப்ப படும்"],"p":"The worthy and unworthy may be known by the existence or otherwise of good offsprings.","r":"Whether people are worthy or unworthy will be seen from what each leaves behind.","n":"எச்சம், what remains, is left unspecified, and the choice has large consequences. Parimelazhagar's claim that the unworthy have no good children is a very confident generalisation, and it isn't in the verse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naduvu","pugazh","makkal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What remains is good offspring, whose presence or absence is a sure mark.","தக்கார்க்கு எச்சம் உண்டாதலும் தகவிலார்க்கு இல்லையாதலும் ஒரு தலையாகலின்,"],["kalaignar","What remains is the praise or blame left after a person is gone.","அவருக்குப் பின் எஞ்சி நிற்கப் போகும் புகழ்ச் சொல்லைக் கொண்டோ அல்லது பழிச் சொல்லைக் கொண்டோதான்"]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:115":{"book":"kural","num":115,"t":["கேடும் பெருக்கமும் இல்லல்ல நெஞ்சத்துக்","கோடாமை சான்றோர்க் கணி"],"p":"Loss and gain come not without cause; it is the ornament of the wise to preserve evenness of mind (under both).","r":"Loss and increase are not absent from life; not letting the heart bend is the ornament of the worthy.","n":"The verse doesn't explain where loss and gain come from, and Pope's 'not mere accident' assumes one view. The important move is that bias is presented as useless as well as wrong.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","samanilai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They are already laid down by past deeds, so bending to get or avoid them achieves nothing.","'அவற்றிற்குக் காரணம் பழவினையே; கோடுதல் அன்று'"],["kalaignar","Rise and fall are simply the nature of the world.","ஒருவர்க்கு வாழ்வும், தாழ்வும் உலக இயற்கை;"]],"ch":"k:12"},"k:116":{"book":"kural","num":116,"t":["கெடுவல்யான் என்பது அறிகதன் நெஞ்சம்","நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்"],"p":"Let him whose mind departing from equity commits sin well consider thus within himself, \"I shall perish.\"","r":"If one's heart leaves what is fair and does wrong, let one know: 'I will perish'.","n":"The subject is the heart, so the act is inward. Parimelazhagar draws this out: thinking is the same as doing, and the very thought is an omen to be read. The first-person கெடுவல் யான் makes the warning a sentence one says to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:117":{"book":"kural","num":117,"t":["கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம் நடுவாக","நன்றிக்கண் தங்கியான் தாழ்வு"],"p":"The great will not regard as poverty the low estate of that man who dwells in the virtue of equity.","r":"The world won't count as ruin the low state of one who remains in virtue by standing fair.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses உலகம் as the noble, as commentators often do, and adds that they regard such poverty as wealth. That's a real narrowing, since the actual world may well despise it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:118":{"book":"kural","num":118,"t":["சமன்செய்து சீர்தூக்குங் கோல்போல் அமைந்தொருபால்","கோடாமை சான்றோர்க் கணி"],"p":"To incline to neither side, but to rest impartial as the even-fixed scale is the ornament of the wise.","r":"To be settled and lean to no side, like the balance that first makes itself level and then weighs, is the ornament of the worthy.","n":"A balance isn't indifferent: it first levels itself, then registers exactly what's put on it. Fairness here is a precise response, not neutrality. Parimelazhagar carries the image into assembly practice, with weighing as hearing both sides and not leaning as stating the result equally to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:119":{"book":"kural","num":119,"t":["சொற்கோட்டம் இல்லது செப்பம் ஒருதலையா","உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை பெறின்"],"p":"F reedom from obliquity of speech is rectitude, if there be (corresponding) freedom from bias of mind.","r":"Rectitude is speech without bend, provided one surely has no bend within.","n":"Parimelazhagar's corollary is subtle: a correct statement made from a biased mind isn't impartiality. Manakkudavar adds that it excludes clever words which suit both sides.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naduvu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:120":{"book":"kural","num":120,"t":["வாணிகம் செய்வார்க்கு வாணிகம் பேணிப்","பிறவும் தமபோல் செயின்"],"p":"The true merchandize of merchants is to guard and do by the things of others as they do by their own.","r":"For traders, true trade is caring for others' goods as if they were their own.","n":"Manakkudavar glosses வாணிகம் as profit, so honesty is what actually pays. Parimelazhagar's phrasing, taking no more and giving no less, closely resembles a description of merchants in Pattinappalai, as I recall it. That is my own observation, and it should be checked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:12"},"k:121":{"book":"kural","num":121,"t":["அடக்கம் அமரருள் உய்க்கும் அடங்காமை","ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும்"],"p":"Self-control will place (a man) among the Gods; the want of it will drive (him) into the thickest darkness (of hell).","r":"Restraint leads one among the immortals, and lack of restraint drives one into thick darkness.","n":"Both verbs are of driving, so restraint is itself a force that carries a person. The final உய்த்துவிடும் has a sense of finality.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The destinations are literal: the gods' world and a particular hell called darkness.","'இருள்' என்பது ஓர் நரக விசேடம்."],["kalaignar","The results are this-worldly: lasting renown and a darkened life.","அடக்கம் அழியாத புகழைக் கொடுக்கும். அடங்காமை வாழ்வையே இருளாக்கி விடும்."]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:122":{"book":"kural","num":122,"t":["காக்க பொருளா அடக்கத்தை ஆக்கம்","அதனினூஉங் கில்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Let self-control be guarded as a treasure; there is no greater source of good for man than that.","r":"Guard restraint as your wealth, for life has no greater gain than it.","n":"Restraint is pictured as something to guard, as misers guard money, so the language of acquisition is turned to its opposite. Parimelazhagar notes this concerns humans, who can understand it and benefit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:123":{"book":"kural","num":123,"t":["செறிவறிந்து சீர்மை பயக்கும் அறிவறிந்து","ஆற்றின் அடங்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"Knowing that self-control is knowledge, if a man should control himself, in the prescribed course, such self-control will bring him distinction among the wise.","r":"If one understands and keeps restrained in the right way, the restraint, recognised, will yield distinction.","n":"The syntax is knotted, and the commentators parse the repeated அறிந்து differently: Parimelazhagar says one learns that restraint is itself knowledge, and Manakkudavar says one learns the five objects and the five organs. I'm unsure which is closest.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:124":{"book":"kural","num":124,"t":["நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான் தோற்றம்","மலையினும் மாணப் பெரிது"],"p":"More lofty than a mountain will be the greatness of that man who without swerving from his domestic state, controls himself.","r":"The stature of one who is restrained and doesn't stray from his station is much greater than a mountain's.","n":"தோற்றம், how one looks or appears, makes this a visual comparison. The dispute over நிலை decides whether the verse has a social hierarchy in it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The station is household life, and the restraint is exercised while enjoying sense objects.","பொறிகளால் புலன்களை நுகராநின்றே அடங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","The station is the order of varna and stage of life.","நிலை - வன்னாச்சிரம தன்மம்."],["kalaignar","It's a matter of firmness of mind joined to unostentatious restraint.","உறுதியான உள்ளமும், அத்துடன் ஆர்ப்பாட்டமற்ற அடக்க உணர்வும்"]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:125":{"book":"kural","num":125,"t":["எல்லார்க்கும் நன்றாம் பணிதல் அவருள்ளும்","செல்வர்க்கே செல்வம் தகைத்து"],"p":"Humility is good in all; but especially in the rich it is (the excellence of) higher riches.","r":"Bowing is good for all, and among them, for the rich it is a further wealth.","n":"Parimelazhagar asks why the rich are singled out. Those with learning or birth may be humble without it being notable, but for the rich it stands out. Manakkudavar reads செல்வம் simply as abundance, which weakens the pun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","selvam","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:126":{"book":"kural","num":126,"t":["ஒருமையுள் ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல் ஆற்றின்","எழுமையும் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Should one throughout a single birth, like a tortoise keep in his five senses, the fruit of it will prove a safe-guard to him throughout the seven-fold births.","r":"If in one birth one can draw in the five like a tortoise, it is a protection for seven births.","n":"The tortoise withdraws out of self-protection, and the image suggests retreat from danger, not conquest. The numbers one and seven make for an argument about return on effort. Kalaignar translates the seven births as all time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:127":{"book":"kural","num":127,"t":["யாகாவா ராயினும் நாகாக்க காவாக்கால்","சோகாப்பர் சொல்லிழுக்குப் பட்டு"],"p":"Whatever besides you leave unguarded, guard your tongue; otherwise errors of speech and the consequent misery will ensue.","r":"Whatever else they fail to guard, let them guard the tongue; if they don't, they'll suffer from the fault of their words.","n":"This is a rare concession in a demanding chapter: it sets priorities for someone who cannot do everything. The opening யாகாவாராயினும் accepts failure in advance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:128":{"book":"kural","num":128,"t":["ஒன்றானுந் தீச்சொல் பொருட்பயன் உண்டாயின்","நன்றாகா தாகி விடும்"],"p":"If a man's speech be productive of a single evil, all the good by him will be turned into evil.","r":"If even one bad word has its effect, it turns out that nothing is good.","n":"The verse doesn't say what stops being good, and the commentators offer three scopes, from one's speech to one's whole moral record.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inchol","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One harm caused by evil words turns the good of the speaker's other virtues to evil.","அவனுக்குப் பிற அறங்களான் உண்டான நன்மை தீதாய்விடும்."],["manakkudavar","It concerns a word sweet to the hearer but harmful in effect, so flattery is also bad.","இது சால மொழி கூறினாலுந் தீதாமென்றது."],["kalaignar","One bad word in a speech spoils all of that speech's good words, like poison in milk.","ஒரு குடம் பாலில் துளி நஞ்சுபோல்,"]],"ch":"k:13"},"k:129":{"book":"kural","num":129,"t":["தீயினாற் சுட்டபுண் உள்ளாறும் ஆறாதே","நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு"],"p":"The wound which has been burnt in by fire may heal, but a wound burnt in by the tongue will never heal.","r":"A wound burned by fire heals inside, but a scar burned by the tongue doesn't heal.","n":"புண் and வடு are carefully chosen: Parimelazhagar notes the first heals and is a wound, while the second stays and is a scar. For him the fire's mark may stay on the body but is healed in the mind, which reverses what one expects to be permanent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:130":{"book":"kural","num":130,"t":["கதங்காத்துக் கற்றடங்கல் ஆற்றுவான் செவ்வி","அறம்பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து"],"p":"Virtue, seeking for an opportunity, will come into the path of that man who, possessed of learning and self-control, guards himself against anger.","r":"Virtue itself looks for its moment and enters by the path to the one who has guarded against anger and has learned restraint.","n":"The usual relation is reversed: virtue is a seeker waiting on a person. Parimelazhagar's gloss of செவ்வி pictures virtue as a petitioner awaiting a good moment to present its plea. Manakkudavar adds what must be restrained: the pride that comes from learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vekuli","adakkam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:13"},"k:131":{"book":"kural","num":131,"t":["ஒழுக்கம் விழுப்பந் தரலான் ஒழுக்கம்","உயிரினும் ஓம்பப் படும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct leads to eminence, it should therefore be preserved more carefully than life.","r":"Since conduct gives excellence, conduct is to be guarded more than life.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reasoning is careful: life is the best of all things, but it doesn't confer distinction the way conduct does, so conduct gets greater care. He adds that this holds for high and low alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:132":{"book":"kural","num":132,"t":["பரிந்தோம்பிக் காக்க ஒழுக்கம் தெரிந்தோம்பித்","தேரினும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"Let propriety of conduct be laboriously preserved and guarded; though one know and practise and excel in many virtues, that will be an eminent aid.","r":"Guard conduct with great effort; however much you search and weigh, it alone is the help.","n":"The three forms of ஓம்பி, தெரிந்து and தேர்ந்து make it sound like a long investigation with one result. Parimelazhagar makes it a survey of all virtues, of which only this one helps in both lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:133":{"book":"kural","num":133,"t":["ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை இழுக்கம்","இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct is true greatness of birth, and impropriety will sink into a mean birth.","r":"Having conduct is what noble birth is, and failing in it becomes low birth.","n":"The verse redefines birth by behaviour. Even the conservative commentators allow that one's status can really change, though for Parimelazhagar it is within a varna frame that the verse doesn't state.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Status really changes: a low-born person of good conduct becomes high, and a high-born person who fails falls.","உயர்ந்த வருணத்துப் பிறந்தாராயினும் ஒழுக்கத்தில் தவறத் தாழ்ந்த வருணத்தராவர்"],["kalaignar","Conduct is the evidence of good birth, and failures are regarded as low-born whoever they are.","ஒழுக்கம் தவறுகிறவர்கள் யாவராயினும் அவர்கள் இழிந்த குடியில் பிறந்தவர்களாகவே கருதப்படுவர்."]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:134":{"book":"kural","num":134,"t":["மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும் பார் ப்பான்","பிறப்பொழுக்கங் குன்றக் கெடும்"],"p":"A Brahman though he should forget the Vedas may recover it by reading; but, if he fail in propriety of conduct even his high bi rth will be destroyed.","r":"Even if forgotten, the Veda can be learned again, but a Brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct declines.","n":"This is a pointed ranking, because study is what the Brahmin is supposed to be defined by. It makes birth a thing that can be lost. Parimelazhagar says the rule extends from this high example to the other varnas.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ozhukkam","pirappu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:135":{"book":"kural","num":135,"t":["அழுக்கா றுடையான்கண் ஆக்கம்போன்று இல்லை","ஒழுக்க மிலான்கண் உயர்வு"],"p":"Just as the envious man will be without wealth, so will the man of destitute of propriety of conduct be without greatness.","r":"As there is no prosperity in the envious, so there is no elevation in one who lacks conduct.","n":"The simile takes as given what chapter 17 has yet to argue, that envy destroys wealth. Parimelazhagar uses verse 166 to extend it: as an envious man's kin suffer, so do a person's kin when conduct fails.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","azhukkaaru","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:136":{"book":"kural","num":136,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்தின் ஒல்கார் உரவோர் இழுக்கத்தின்","ஏதம் படுபாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"Those firm in mind will not slacken in their observance of the proprieties of life, knowing, as they do, the misery that flows from the transgression from them.","r":"The strong don't slacken in conduct, knowing what harm arises from a lapse.","n":"The motive given is prudence, not love of virtue. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar differ a little on உரவோர், as strength of mind and understanding respectively, the same question raised by உரன் in verse 24.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:137":{"book":"kural","num":137,"t":["ஒழுக்கத்தின் எய்துவர் மேன்மை இழுக்கத்தின்","எய்துவர் எய்தாப் பழி"],"p":"From propriety of conduct men obtain greatness; from impropriety comes insufferable disgrace.","r":"From conduct people attain high standing, and from lapses they attain blame not properly theirs.","n":"எய்தாப் பழி is the sharp part. Parimelazhagar sees that after one slip, even a false charge from an enemy is believed. Conduct is therefore a shield as well as a good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","pazhi","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","When enemies make false charges, the world believes them because of the earlier lapse.","பகை பற்றி அடாப்பழி கூறியவழி, அதனையும் இழுக்கம்பற்றி உலகம் அடுக்கும் என்று கொள்ளுமாகலின்,"]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:138":{"book":"kural","num":138,"t":["நன்றிக்கு வித்தாகும் நல்லொழுக்கம் தீயொழுக்கம்","என்றும் இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"Propriety of conduct is the seed of virtue; impropriety will ever cause sorrow.","r":"Good conduct is a seed of good, and bad conduct always gives suffering.","n":"The two halves are asymmetrical: one is a seed with a future harvest, the other suffers 'always'. Parimelazhagar shows each half implies what the other leaves out. Manakkudavar makes the seed one of release, which the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","idumbai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:139":{"book":"kural","num":139,"t":["ஒழுக்க முடையவர்க்கு ஒல்லாவே தீய","வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்"],"p":"Those who study propriety of conduct will not speak evil, even forgetfully.","r":"Speaking evil with their mouths, even by a slip, is not possible for those who have conduct.","n":"It's a strong claim: conduct has become so ingrained that the slip itself can't occur. Parimelazhagar adds speech improper to one's varna to the list of evil words, which is his addition. He also says the redundant 'with the mouth' implies that it's a mouth used to good words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:14"},"k:140":{"book":"kural","num":140,"t":["உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல் பலகற்றும்","கல்லார் அறிவிலா தார்"],"p":"Those who know not how to act agreeably to the world, though they have learnt many things, are still ignorant.","r":"Those who haven't learned to live in accord with the world have studied much and still lack understanding.","n":"All three commentators narrow உலகம் to the noble. Yet Parimelazhagar makes an unusual concession in explaining why the verse says 'world': scripture contains things that no longer suit the present, and it omits things that do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Living with the world includes setting aside what the moral texts say that doesn't suit this age, and taking up what suits it.","அறநூல் சொல்லியவற்றுள் இக்காலத்திற்கு ஏலாதன ஒழிந்து, சொல்லாதனவற்றுள் ஏற்பன கொண்டு வருதலான்"]],"ch":"k:14"},"k:141":{"book":"kural","num":141,"t":["பிறன்பொருளாள் பெட்டொழுகும் பேதைமை ஞாலத்து","அறம்பொருள் கண்டார்கண் இல்"],"p":"The folly of desiring her who is the property of another will not be found in those who know (the attributes of) virtue and (the rights of) property.","r":"The folly of desiring a woman who is another's possession doesn't exist in those who have seen virtue and wealth in this world.","n":"பிறன்பொருளாள், the verse's own word, treats the woman as property. Parimelazhagar explains why only two of the three aims are named: the science of pleasure has a term for this very act, so its experts can't be cited against it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","aram","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:142":{"book":"kural","num":142,"t":["அறன்கடை நின்றாருள் எல்லாம் பிறன்கடை","நின்றாரின் பேதையார் இல்"],"p":"Among all those who stand on the outside of virtue, there are no greater fools than those who stand outside their neighbour's door.","r":"Among all who stand outside virtue, none is a greater fool than those who stand outside another man's door.","n":"The repeated கடை sets one threshold on another: outside virtue and outside the door. Parimelazhagar's reason is telling: other sinners at least get their pleasure, whereas this man is too afraid even for that. So the charge is stupidity, and even pleasure is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","aram","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:143":{"book":"kural","num":143,"t":["விளிந்தா஡஢ன் வேறல்லர் மன்ற தெளிந்தாரில்","தீமை புரிந்து ஒழுகு வார்"],"p":"Certainly they are no better than dead men who desire evil towards the wife of those who undoubtingly confide in them.","r":"Those who take to evil in the home of people who trust them are surely no different from the dead.","n":"The wrong here is defined by trust, as தெளிந்தார் shows: Parimelazhagar points out that the act is only possible because the husband trusts him. It's betrayal of a friend as much as sexual wrongdoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","natpu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:144":{"book":"kural","num":144,"t":["எனைத்துணையர் ஆயினும் என்னாம் தினைத்துணையும்","தேரான் பிறனில் புகல்"],"p":"However great one may be, what does it avail if, without at all considering his guilt, he goes unto the wife of another ?","r":"However great they are, what does it come to, when one enters another's home without weighing it even a millet's worth?","n":"A millet's worth of thought would be enough to stop it. Parimelazhagar names Indra as the example of greatness lost. He also reports a reading of தேரான் பிறன் as 'the unsuspecting husband'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:145":{"book":"kural","num":145,"t":["எளிதென இல்லிறப்பான் எய்துமெஞ் ஞான்றும்","விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி"],"p":"He who thinks lightly of going into the wife of another acquires guilt that will abide with him imperishably and for ever.","r":"One who crosses into a home thinking it easy gets blame that never dies.","n":"The contrast is in timescale: 'easy' describes one moment and the blame lasts forever. Parimelazhagar makes it the family's blame, so the cost falls on others too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:146":{"book":"kural","num":146,"t":["பகைபாவம் அச்சம் பழியென நான்கும்","இகவாவாம் இல்லிறப்பான் கண்"],"p":"Hatred, sin, fear, disgrace; these four will never leave him who goes in to his neighbour's wife.","r":"Enmity, sin, fear and blame: these four never leave one who crosses into a home.","n":"The four cover different kinds of consequence: social, moral, psychological and reputational. 'Home' throughout this chapter stands for the wife, an equation that the Tamil title itself makes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","pazhi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:147":{"book":"kural","num":147,"t":["அறனியலான் இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் பிறனியலாள்","பெண்மை நயவா தவன்"],"p":"He who desires not the womanhood of her who should walk according to the will of another will be praised as a virtuous house-holder.","r":"The one called a householder in the way of virtue is he who doesn't desire the womanhood of the one who belongs to another's way.","n":"It's a negative definition of the householder, after chapter 5's positive ones. Parimelazhagar makes it exclusive: he alone is such a householder.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilvaazhkkai","piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:148":{"book":"kural","num":148,"t":["பிறன்மனை நோக்காத பேராண்மை சான்றோர்க்கு","அறனொன்றோ ஆன்ற வொழுக்கு"],"p":"That noble manliness which looks not at the wife of another is the virtue and dignity of the great.","r":"The great manhood that doesn't look at another's wife is, for the worthy, both virtue and complete conduct.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is a fine one: those who can subdue outer enemies can still find this inner enemy hard, so this is the greater manhood. He takes நோக்காத beyond looking, to not holding her in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","adakkam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:149":{"book":"kural","num":149,"t":["நலக்குரியார் யாரெனின் நாமநீர் வைப்பின்","பிறர்க்குரியாள் தோள்தோயா தார்"],"p":"Is it asked, \"who are those who shall obtain good in this world surrounded by the terror-producing sea ?\" Those who touch not the shoulder of her who belongs to another.","r":"Who deserve good on this world ringed by the awesome sea? Those who don't touch the shoulders of a woman belonging to another.","n":"The question-and-answer form and the immense sea are grand for such a plain answer. தோள் is the vocabulary of love poetry. The commentators differ on நாமநீர், as Parimelazhagar has fearful water and Manakkudavar has water that grants wishes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:150":{"book":"kural","num":150,"t":["அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும் பிறன்வரையாள்","பெண்மை நயவாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though a man perform no virtuous deeds and commit (every) vice, it will be well if he desire not the womanhood of her who is within the limit (of the house) of another.","r":"Even if one doesn't practise virtue and does what is wrong, it's good not to desire the womanhood of one within another's bounds.","n":"The verse sets a minimum for someone who has failed at everything else. Parimelazhagar says this quality alone stands out, and Manakkudavar says that it brings good even with no other virtue. It doesn't say the person is thereby virtuous.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["piranil","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:15"},"k:151":{"book":"kural","num":151,"t":["அகழ்வாரைத் தாங்கும் நிலம்போலத் தம்மை","இகழ்வார்ப் பொறுத்தல் தலை"],"p":"To bear with those who revile us, just as the earth bears up those who dig it, is the first of virtues.","r":"Like the earth that holds up those who dig into it, bearing with those who scorn you is the highest thing.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss brings out the image: the earth keeps the digger from falling, so the one injured is still supporting the one who injures. Forbearance here is more than not responding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:152":{"book":"kural","num":152,"t":["பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும் அதனை","மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று"],"p":"Bear with reproach even when you can retaliate; but to forget it will be still better than that.","r":"Always bear with excess against you; forgetting it is better still.","n":"Parimelazhagar explains why forgetting ranks higher: to forbear, one must still hold the wrong in mind. He adds that one should forbear even when one has the power to punish, which is what makes this more than helplessness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:153":{"book":"kural","num":153,"t":["இன்நம்யுள் இன்மை விருந்தொரால் வன்மையுள்","வன்மை மடவார்ப் பொறை"],"p":"To neglect hospitality is poverty of poverty. To bear with the ignorant is might of might.","r":"The poverty of poverties is turning away a guest, and the strength of strengths is forbearance toward fools.","n":"The verse is an analogy, as Parimelazhagar notes: just as turning a guest away isn't wealth, bearing with fools isn't weakness. The first half is there to change what we think the second half means.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["porai","virundhu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","விருந்து means a guest, and turning one away is the worst poverty.","வறுமையுள் வைத்து வறுமையாவது விருந்தினரை ஏற்றுக் கொள்ளாது நீக்குதல்;"],["manakkudavar","விருந்து means novelty: the weakest weakness is failing to reject the strange and unheard-of.","புதுமை யென்றது கேட்டறியாதது."]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:154":{"book":"kural","num":154,"t":["நிறையுடைமை நீங்காமை வேண்டின் பொநற்யுடைமை","போற்றி யொழுகப் படும்"],"p":"If you desire that greatness should never leave, you preserve in your conduct the exercise of patience.","r":"If you want நிறை never to leave you, keep and live by forbearance.","n":"Forbearance is presented as the means, and நிறை is what is at stake. Parimelazhagar takes the latter as worth of character, and Manakkudavar as a guarded discipline: keeping what should be kept and rejecting what should be rejected.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:155":{"book":"kural","num":155,"t":["ஒறுத்தாரை ஒன்றாக வையாரே வைப்பர்","பொறுத்தாரைப் பொன்போற் பொதிந்து"],"p":"(The wise) will not at all esteem the resentful. They will esteem the patient just as the gold which they lay up with care.","r":"They don't count at all those who retaliate, but those who forbear they keep wrapped up like gold.","n":"It's a claim about social estimation, which is why the motive is reputational. Parimelazhagar gives the reason: the person who retaliates has become the wrongdoer's equal. He reads 'wrapping up' as constantly bearing them in mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:156":{"book":"kural","num":156,"t":["ஒறுத்தார்க்கு ஒருநாளை இன்பம் பொறுத்தார்க்குப்","பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்"],"p":"The pleasure of the resentful continues for a day. The praise of the patient will continue until (the final destruction of) the world.","r":"Those who retaliate have one day's joy, and those who forbear have renown until they perish.","n":"The verse honestly concedes that revenge is a pleasure. Parimelazhagar plays this down with 'false joy'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","pugazh","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The word 'world' is supplied, so the renown lasts until the world itself ends.","பொறுத்தார்க்கு உலகம் அழியுமளவும் புகழ் உண்டாம்."],["manakkudavar","The renown lasts as long as the forbearing person lives.","பொறுத்த வர்க்குத் தாம் சாமளவும் புகழுண்டாம்,"]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:157":{"book":"kural","num":157,"t":["திறனல்ல தற்பிறர் செய்யினும் நோநொந்து","அறனல்ல செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though others inflict injuries on you, yet compassionating the evil (that will come upon them) it will be well not to do them anything contrary to virtue.","r":"Though others do you wrong, it's good to feel the pain of it and not do what is contrary to virtue.","n":"நோநொந்து, grieving at the pain, doesn't say whose pain. On the first reading below, the wronged person pities the wrongdoer, which is a remarkable demand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","aram","arul"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One grieves for the suffering that will come to the wrongdoer, who may fall into hell.","அவர்க்கு அதனால் வரும் துன்பத்திற்கு நொந்து,"],["kalaignar","One feels hurt by the base act done to oneself, and still does not take revenge.","பிறர் செய்திடும் இழிவான காரியங்களுக்காகத் துன்பமுற்று வருந்தி;"]],"ch":"k:16"},"k:158":{"book":"kural","num":158,"t":["மிகுதியான் மிக்கவை செய்தாரைத் தாந்தம்","தகுதியான் வென்று விடல்"],"p":"Let a man by patience overcome those who through pride commit excesses.","r":"Those who do wrong out of their excess, defeat them by your own rightness.","n":"The language is of winning. Manakkudavar says it most directly: forbearing isn't defeat, it is victory. மிகுதி and தகுதி, excess and fitness, are set against each other as rival kinds of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:159":{"book":"kural","num":159,"t":["துறந்தாரின் தூய்மை உடையர் இறந்தார்வாய்","இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்கிற் பவர்"],"p":"Those who bear with the uncourteous speech of the insolent are as pure as the ascetics.","r":"Those who bear the harsh words from the mouths of those who go beyond bounds are as pure as those who have renounced.","n":"Parimelazhagar spells out the implication: this is someone still in household life. The purity of renunciation can therefore be reached without renouncing. Here இறந்தார் means those who overstep, not the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thuravu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:160":{"book":"kural","num":160,"t":["உண்ணாது நோற்பார் பெரியர் பிறர்சொல்லும்","இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்பாரின் பின்"],"p":"Those who endure abstinence from food are great, next to those who endure the uncourteous speech of others.","r":"Those who fast are great, but only after those who bear what others say in harshness.","n":"The verse first grants greatness and then takes the top rank away. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is subtle: bearing insult is done while one's sense of 'I' and 'mine' is still in place, the very things that make it hard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thavam","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:16"},"k:161":{"book":"kural","num":161,"t":["ஒழுக்காறாக் கொள்க ஒருவன்தன் நெஞ்சத்து","அழுக்காறு இலாத இயல்பு"],"p":"Let a man esteem that disposition which is free from envy in the same manner as propriety of conduct.","r":"Let one take as one's rule of conduct a nature whose heart has no envy.","n":"ஒழுக்காறு and அழுக்காறு differ by one sound, so the sound of the verse sets conduct and envy against each other. Coming right after chapter 14, it makes an inner state into a rule of conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","ozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:162":{"book":"kural","num":162,"t":["விழுப்பேற்றின் அஃதொப்பது இல்லையார் மாட்டும்","அழுக்காற்றின் அன்மை பெறின்"],"p":"Amongst all attainable excellences there is none equal to that of being free from envy towords others.","r":"Among choice gains nothing equals it, if one can be free of envy toward anyone at all.","n":"யார்மாட்டும், toward anyone at all, is what makes it hard. Parimelazhagar says this includes one's enemies. It's a further 'nothing greater' claim to be read along with those for virtue, restraint and children.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:163":{"book":"kural","num":163,"t":["அறன்ஆக்கம் வேண்டாதான் என்பான் பிறனாக்கம்","பேணாது அழுக்கறுப் பான்"],"p":"Of him who instead of rejoicing in the wealth of others, envies it, it will be said \"he neither desires virtue not wealth.\"","r":"He who doesn't care for another's prosperity and envies it is called one who wants neither virtue nor gain.","n":"The verse argues that envy works against what the envier wants. பேணாது goes beyond 'not envying': Parimelazhagar glosses it as failing to help, so what's required is positive support for another's prosperity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:164":{"book":"kural","num":164,"t":["அழுக்காற்றின் அல்லவை செய்யார் இழுக்காற்றின்","ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து"],"p":"(The wise) knowing the misery that comes from transgression will not through envy commit unrighteous deeds.","r":"They do no wrong out of envy, because they know the harm that arises from a wrong path.","n":"The second line nearly repeats verse 136, and here too the motive is prudence. Parimelazhagar's list of wrongs from envy covers thought, speech and action against those with wealth or learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:165":{"book":"kural","num":165,"t":["அழுக்காறு உடையார்க்கு அதுசாலும் ஒன்னார்","வழுக்காயும் கேடீன் பது"],"p":"To those who cherish envy that is enough. Though free from enemies that (envy) will bring destruction.","r":"For the envious that's enough: even without enemies, it brings about their ruin.","n":"The verse is sardonic, and அதுசாலும், 'that is enough', sounds almost like a verdict. The envious person's enemy is within, and no outside enemy is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:166":{"book":"kural","num":166,"t":["கொடுப்பது அழுக்கறுப்பான் சுற்றம் உடுப்பதூஉம்","உண்பதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"He who is envious at a gift (made to another) will with his relations utterly perish destitute of food and rainment.","r":"The kin of one who envies what is given will perish, with nothing to wear or eat.","n":"The cost falls on those near him. Parimelazhagar says his own ruin needn't be stated. Manakkudavar makes the fault active, that he stops another's giving, whereas Parimelazhagar makes it the feeling itself. Either way, the verse punishes innocents, and it doesn't address this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:167":{"book":"kural","num":167,"t":["அவ்வித்து அழுக்காறு உடையானைச் செய்யவள்","தவ்வையைக் காட்டி விடும்"],"p":"Lakshmi envying (the prosperity) of the envious man will depart and introduce him to her sister.","r":"The fair goddess, grudging the envious man, will show him to her elder sister.","n":"The verse describes the goddess's act with the word for envy's own attitude, அவ்வித்து, so the envier gets what he gives. The elder sister is misfortune. Kalaignar distances himself by saying this is how people describe it. Parimelazhagar also records a different reading of the verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The goddess too can't bear it, and leaves after showing him to her elder sister.","திருமகள் தானும் பொறாது, தன் தவ்வைக்கு காட்டி நீங்கும்."]],"ch":"k:17"},"k:168":{"book":"kural","num":168,"t":["அழுக்காறு எனஒரு பாவி திருச்செற்றுத்","தீயுழி உய்த்து விடும்"],"p":"Envy will destroy (a man's) wealth (in his world) and drive him into the pit of fire (in the world to come.)","r":"The one evil called envy destroys good fortune and drives one to the bad place.","n":"பாவி personifies a quality as a villain. Parimelazhagar acknowledges it's a figure of speech: a quality isn't a person, but this one is so cruel to its maker that it's called one. Kalaignar has 'evil path' where the older commentators have hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:169":{"book":"kural","num":169,"t":["அவ்விய நெஞ்சத்தான் ஆக்கமும் செவ்வியான்","கேடும் நினைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"The wealth of a man of envious mind and the poverty of the righteous will be pondered.","r":"The prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are matters to be pondered.","n":"This verse admits what the next one denies. It doesn't state a resolution, only that it must be thought about. Parimelazhagar cites Silappatikaram 15.91-93 as an example of someone pondering this in the story of Kovalan's suffering, a direct thread between the two works.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhukkaaru","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These results can't come from crookedness or uprightness, so the wise ask what past deed caused them.","'இதற்கு ஏது ஆகிய பழவினை யாது?' என்று ஆராயப்படுதலின்,"],["kalaignar","This is simply a matter for astonishment, with no appeal to past deeds.","வியப்புக்குரிய செய்தியாகும்."]],"ch":"k:17"},"k:170":{"book":"kural","num":170,"t":["அழுக்கற்று அகன்றாரும் இல்லை அஃதுஇல்லார்","பெருக்கத்தில் தீர்ந்தாரும் இல்"],"p":"Never have the envious become great; never have those who are free from envy been without greatness.","r":"None have grown great through envy, and none free of it have lost out on increase.","n":"It's an absolute claim, with 'none' twice. Parimelazhagar takes this as the moral cause of gain and ruin, immediately after his past-deeds reading of 169, without explaining how the two fit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["azhukkaaru","perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:17"},"k:171":{"book":"kural","num":171,"t":["நடுவின்றி நன்பொருள் வெஃகின் குடிபொன்றிக்","குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்"],"p":"If a man departing from equity covet the property (of others), at that very time will his family be destroyed and guilt be incurred.","r":"If one covets another's good things and leaves aside fairness, the family perishes, and faults come at once.","n":"The condition is only வெஃகின், if one covets, and ஆங்கே, right then, makes the penalty immediate. Parimelazhagar gives நடுவு a specific meaning: the recognition that what belongs to others isn't ours to take.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","naduvu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:172":{"book":"kural","num":172,"t":["படுபயன் வெஃகிப் பழிப்படுவ செய்யார்","நடுவன்மை நாணு பவர்"],"p":"Those who blush at the want of equity will not commit disgraceful acts through desire of the profit that may be gained.","r":"Those ashamed of unfairness won't do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain to be had.","n":"நாணு, shame, is the operative word and it means this person is restrained from inside. Parimelazhagar glosses it with fear, which is a less interior motive than the verse's word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","naduvu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:173":{"book":"kural","num":173,"t":["சிற்றின்பம் வெஃகி அறனல்ல செய்யாரே","மற்றின்பம் வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire the higher pleasures (of heaven) will not act unjustly through desire of the trifling joy. (in this life.)","r":"Those who want the other joy won't do what's contrary to virtue out of desire for a small joy.","n":"சிற்றின்பம் here refers to pleasure from taking others' goods, which isn't its common later meaning of sexual pleasure. The verse leaves 'the other joy' unnamed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","inbam","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The other joy is the lasting joy that comes from virtue.","அறத்தான் வரும் நிலையுடைய இன்பத்தைக் காதலிப்பவர்."],["manakkudavar","The other joy is the great joy of release.","பேரின்பமாகிய வீடுபேற்றைக் காமிப்பவர்,"],["kalaignar","It's a lasting benefit that one wants in place of an immediate one.","அறவழியில் நிலையான பயனை விரும்புகிறவர்"]],"ch":"k:18"},"k:174":{"book":"kural","num":174,"t":["இலமென்று வெஃகுதல் செய்யார் புலம்வென்ற","புன்மையில் காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"The wise who have conquered their senses and are free from crime, will not covet (the things of others), with the thought \"we are destitute.\"","r":"Those of faultless sight, who have conquered the senses, won't covet at the thought 'we have nothing'.","n":"Need is the hardest case, and the verse rules out that excuse. Parimelazhagar goes a step further and says that such people don't count as poor at all, which removes the case instead of answering it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","adakkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:175":{"book":"kural","num":175,"t":["அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவென்னாம் யார்மாட்டும்","வெஃகி வெறிய செயின்"],"p":"What is the advantage of extensive and accurate knowledge if a man through covetousness act senselessly towards all ?","r":"What is the use of fine and wide understanding if, coveting, one acts senselessly toward everyone?","n":"The sound of அஃகி, to be fine, is played against வெஃகி, to covet, and a learned mind is shown undone by the same sound. Parimelazhagar repeats a principle from 140: the purpose of understanding is what one refrains from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:176":{"book":"kural","num":176,"t":["அருள்வெஃகி ஆற்றின்கண் நின்றான் பொருள்வெஃகிப்","பொல்லாத சூழக் கெடும்"],"p":"If he, who through desire of the virtue of kindness abides in the domestic state i.e., the path in which it may be obtained, covet (the property of others) and think of evil methods (to obtain it), he will perish.","r":"One standing on the path out of desire for grace will perish if, desiring wealth, he plots wrongs.","n":"வெஃகி is used twice: he desires grace and he desires wealth, in the one verb. The other notable word is சூழ, to plot: Parimelazhagar notes that it says the plotting alone destroys him, let alone the doing.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","arul","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The path is household life, called a path because one can't renounce until understanding matures in it.","இல்லற நெறியில் அறிவு முதிர்ந்துழி அல்லது துறக்கப் படாமையின், அதனைத் துறவறத்திற்கு 'ஆறு' என்றார்."],["manakkudavar","The path is simply the way of virtue, and even one with grace can fall.","அருளை விரும்பி யறனெறியிலே நின்றவனும்,"]],"ch":"k:18"},"k:177":{"book":"kural","num":177,"t":["வேண்டற்க வெஃகியாம் ஆக்கம் விளைவயின்","மாண்டற் கரிதாம் பயன்"],"p":"Desire not the gain of covetousness. In the enjoyment of its fruits there is no glory.","r":"Don't seek gain that comes by coveting: when it ripens, its fruit is hard to make good.","n":"The word விளைவயின் brings in a harvest image: the wrong shows only when the crop comes in, so the problem is one of delayed consequences.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:178":{"book":"kural","num":178,"t":["அஃகாமை செல்வத்திற்கு யாதெனின் வெஃகாமை","வேண்டும் பிறன்கைப் பொருள்"],"p":"If it is weighed, \"what is the indestructibility of wealth,\" it is freedom from covetousness.","r":"What keeps wealth from shrinking? Not coveting what's in another's hand, which that person wants.","n":"The chapter's sound play, அஃகாமை and வெஃகாமை, is itself the answer. It offers a security for wealth that the wealthy might not expect: leaving others' goods alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:179":{"book":"kural","num":179,"t":["அறனறிந்து வெஃகா அறிவுடையார்ச் சேரும்","திறன்அறிந் தாங்கே திரு"],"p":"Lakshmi, knowing the manner (in which she may approach) will immediately come to those wise men who, knowing that it is virtue, covet not the property of others.","r":"Fortune, knowing the way to come, approaches those who understand virtue and do not covet.","n":"Here the goddess has to work out the right way to approach, as if she were the supplicant. Parimelazhagar's gloss explains the 'way' as time, place and the right moment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vehkaamai","selvam","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:180":{"book":"kural","num":180,"t":["இறலீனும் எண்ணாது வெஃகின் விறல்ஈனும்","வேண்டாமை என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"To covet (the wealth of another) regardless of consequences will bring destruction. That greatness (of mind) which covets not will give victory.","r":"Coveting without thought yields destruction, and the pride called not-wanting yields victory.","n":"செருக்கு normally means arrogance, and here it's a virtue. Parimelazhagar explains why it's a victory: it puts beneath it all those who scramble after wealth. Not wanting is a form of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vehkaamai","perumai","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:18"},"k:181":{"book":"kural","num":181,"t":["அறங்கூறான் அல்ல செயினும் ஒருவன்","புறங்கூறான் என்றல் இனிது"],"p":"Though one do not even speak of virtue and live in sin, it will be well if it be said of him \"he does not backbite.\"","r":"Though one doesn't speak of virtue and does wrong, it's pleasing if it is said, 'he doesn't backbite'.","n":"The merit here is what's said about a person, which fits the chapter: a backbiter is judged by talk, and this man is praised by it. Virtue need not even be spoken of, which sets a very low floor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","aram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:182":{"book":"kural","num":182,"t":["அறனழீஇ அல்லவை செய்தலின் தீதே","புறனழீஇப் பொய்த்து நகை"],"p":"To smile deceitfully (in another's presence) after having reviled him to his destruction (behind his back) is a greater evil than the commission of (every other) sin and the destruction of (every) virtue.","r":"Worse than denying virtue and doing wrong is destroying someone behind their back and then smiling falsely.","n":"The ranking is what's striking. Parimelazhagar takes அறனழீஇ as openly saying there's no such thing as virtue, so the moral denier is rated above the smiling gossip. Honest wickedness is treated as less bad than a hidden betrayal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","aram","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:183":{"book":"kural","num":183,"t":["புறங்கூறிப் பொய்த்துயிர் வாழ்தலின் சாதல்","அறங்கூற்றும் ஆக்கத் தரும்"],"p":"Death rather than life will confer upon the deceitful backbiter the profit which (the treatises on) virtue point out.","r":"Dying brings more of the gain that virtue speaks of than living on by backbiting and falsehood.","n":"Death is compared favourably here, and the terms are moral accounting. Parimelazhagar's reason is that death at least stops the backbiting. Manakkudavar pictures a person who refuses to gossip and dies poor, which suggests that gossip may be a livelihood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:184":{"book":"kural","num":184,"t":["கண்ணின்று கண்ணறச் சொல்லினும் சொல்லற்க","முன்னின்று பின்நோக்காச் சொல்"],"p":"Though you speak without kindness before another's face speak not in his absence words which regard not the evil subsequently resulting from it.","r":"Though you speak harshly to a person's face, do not speak words that have no regard for what follows, when they are not there.","n":"The verse is a concession, as open harshness is the lesser evil, and it goes against chapter 10's standards. Manakkudavar takes care to add that this isn't permission for harsh speech.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","inchol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The forbidden word disregards the harm that will come later, in the person's absence.","அவன் எதிரின்றிப்பின் வரும் குற்றத்தை நோக்காத சொல்லைச் சொல்லாதொழிக."],["manakkudavar","The forbidden word is one that will leave you unable to look the person in the eye later.","பிற்காலத்து அவன் முன்னே நின்று எதிர்முகம் நோக்க வொண் ணாத சொல்லைச் சொல்லா தொழிக,"]],"ch":"k:19"},"k:185":{"book":"kural","num":185,"t":["அறஞ்சொல்லும் நெஞ்சத்தான் அன்மை புறஞ்சொல்லும்","புன்மையாற் காணப் படும்"],"p":"The emptiness of that man's mind who (merely) praises virtue will be seen from the meanness of reviling another behind his back.","r":"That someone's heart doesn't really speak virtue will be seen from the meanness of their backbiting.","n":"It's a test: talk about virtue is checked against talk about others. Parimelazhagar makes it specific: a backbiter can praise virtue, but because his mind is bad, what he says shouldn't be accepted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","manam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:186":{"book":"kural","num":186,"t":["பிறன்பழி கூறுவான் தன்பழி யுள்ளும்","திறன்தெரிந்து கூறப் படும்"],"p":"The character of the faults of that man who publishes abroad the faults of others will be sought out and published.","r":"One who talks of another's faults will have his own faults picked out and talked about.","n":"The passive voice doesn't say who will do the talking. Parimelazhagar makes it the injured party, who picks out from the speaker's many faults the ones that would hurt most, and says them to his face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:187":{"book":"kural","num":187,"t":["பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர் நகச்சொல்லி","நட்பாடல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Those who know not to live in friendship with amusing conversation will by back-biting estrange even their relatives.","r":"Those who don't know how to make friends through words that delight will divide even their own people with words that divide.","n":"பகச்சொல்லி and நகச்சொல்லி are one sound apart, like divide and delight. The verse gives a diagnosis, that gossip is a social failure: those who can't please must separate. Parimelazhagar says even kin are affected, and strangers all the more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","natpu","inchol","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:188":{"book":"kural","num":188,"t":["துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும் மரபினார்","என்னைகொல் ஏதிலார் மாட்டு"],"p":"What will those not do to strangers whose nature leads them to publish abroad the faults of their intimate friends ?","r":"Those whose way is to spread abroad even the faults of those closest to them, what would they do to strangers?","n":"மரபினார் suggests a settled habit or even a family tradition, not a lapse. Parimelazhagar reports a variant reading, 'what sort of people are they?'","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:189":{"book":"kural","num":189,"t":["அறன்நோக்கி ஆற்றுங்கொல் வையம் புறன்நோக்கிப்","புன்சொல் உரைப்பான் பொறை"],"p":"The world through charity supports the weight of those who reproach others observing their absence.","r":"Is it out of regard for virtue that the earth bears the weight of one who watches for a person's back and speaks mean words?","n":"The earth, the model of forbearance, finds this one hard. Parimelazhagar imagines the earth telling itself that bearing this is its duty. Manakkudavar adds darkly that otherwise it would shake him off.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["purangooral","porai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:19"},"k:190":{"book":"kural","num":190,"t":["ஏதிலார் குற்றம்போல் தங்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்","தீதுண்டோ மன்னும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"If they observed their own faults as they observe the faults of others, would any evil happen to men ?","r":"If people could see their own faults as they see strangers' faults, what harm would come to any living being?","n":"The whole chapter resolves into a problem of seeing. காண்கிற்பின் means 'if they were able', and Parimelazhagar notes that this impartial comparison is rare.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["purangooral","arivu","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Seeing the fault ends it, so there's no sin, and thus no suffering in the births to come.","கண்டவழி ஒழிதலின் பாவம் இன்றாம், ஆகவே வரும் பிறவிகளினும் துன்பம் இல்லை என்பது நோக்கி,"],["kalaignar","The habit of backbiting goes and one's life becomes peaceful.","புறங்கூறும் பழக்கமும் போகும்; வாழ்க்கையும் நிம்மதியாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:19"},"k:191":{"book":"kural","num":191,"t":["பல்லார் முனியப் பயனில சொல்லுவான்","எல்லாரும் எள்ளப் படும்"],"p":"He who to the disgust of many speaks useless things will be despised by all.","r":"A person who talks emptily to the irritation of many ends up despised by all.","n":"The verse says only 'many'. Parimelazhagar narrows them to the discerning, which turns the move from 'many' to 'all' into a causal chain: once the wise are repelled, everyone else follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sol","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:192":{"book":"kural","num":192,"t":["பயனில பல்லார்முன் சொல்லல் நயனில","நட்டார்கண் செய்தலிற் றீது"],"p":"To speak useless things in the presence of many is a greater evil than to do unkind things towards friends.","r":"Saying useless things before many is worse than doing what is unwelcome to one's friends.","n":"The ranking is startling: a word to an audience outweighs a deed against a friend. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that the talk brings more disgrace, so his measure is the speaker's standing and not the harm done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan","nayan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:193":{"book":"kural","num":193,"t":["நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும் பயனில","பாரித் துரைக்கும் உரை"],"p":"That conversation in which a man utters forth useless things will say of him \"he is without virtue.\"","r":"Expansive talk about useless things itself declares that the speaker is one without நயன்.","n":"No one needs to accuse the talker, because the speech testifies against him. நயன் shifts through this chapter: Parimelazhagar takes it as liking in 192 and as justice here, so the word should not be given a single translation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:194":{"book":"kural","num":194,"t":["நயன்சாரா நன்மையின் நீக்கும் பயன்சாராப்","பண்பில்சொல் பல்லா ரகத்து"],"p":"The words devoid of profit or pleasure which a man speaks will, being inconsistent with virtue, remove him from goodness.","r":"Graceless words that serve no purpose, spoken among many, lack நயன் and take a person away from what is good.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The words fall short of justice toward the listeners, and cost the speaker his good qualities.","அவை அவர்மாட்டு நீதியோடு படாவாய், அவனை நற்குணங்களின் நீக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","நயன் is even-handedness, and it is the speaker who loses that balance and falls from goodness.","அவன் நடு சாராது நன்மையி னீங்கும்"],["kalaignar","நயன் is the gladness of the gathering, and such words destroy it along with good.","மகிழ்ச்சியைக் குலைத்து, நன்மையை மாய்க்கும்."]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:195":{"book":"kural","num":195,"t":["சீர்மை சிறப்பொடு நீங்கும் பயனில","நீர்மை யுடையார் சொலின்"],"p":"If the good speak vain words their eminence and excellence will leave them.","r":"If those of gentle nature speak useless things, their worth departs and the regard it earned goes with it.","n":"The verse aims at the good, not the habitual babbler of 196. Standing already earned is not secure against this one lapse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:196":{"book":"kural","num":196,"t":["பயனில் சொல் பராட்டு வானை மகன்எனல்","மக்கட் பதடி யெனல்"],"p":"Call not him a man who parades forth his empty words. Call him the chaff of men.","r":"Don't call a person who keeps up useless talk a man; call him chaff among people.","n":"For Parimelazhagar the image means a husk without the kernel of understanding, so the talk reveals emptiness and doesn't merely offend.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sol","payan","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பாராட்டுவான் is one who says such things repeatedly: the fault is the habit.","பயன் இல்லாத சொற்களைப் பலகாலுஞ் சொல்லுவானை"],["manakkudavar","He is one who prizes and celebrates useless speech: the fault is the relish.","பயனில்லாத சொல்லைக் கொண்டாடுவானை"],["kalaignar","He is one who hopes to profit from empty talk: the fault is the motive.","பயனற்றவைகளைச் சொல்லிப் பயன்பெற நினைப்பவனை"]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:197":{"book":"kural","num":197,"t":["நயனில சொல்லினுஞ் சொல்லுக சான்றோர்","பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று"],"p":"Let the wise if they will, speak things without excellence; it will be well for them not to speak useless things.","r":"Let the worthy say what lacks நயன் if they must; it is good that they not say what lacks use.","n":"The concession is rhetorical, as Parimelazhagar observes: 'even if they say it' implies they should not. Its force is the comparison, which keeps the sting whichever sense of நயன் is taken.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","nayan","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நயன் is justice, so even speech at odds with justice is ranked less bad than useless speech.","நயன் இலவற்றினும் பயன் இல தீய என்பதாம்."],["kalaignar","நயன் is pleasantness, so the worthy may say what is unsweet, which is a much milder claim.","பண்பாளர்கள், இனிமையல்லாத சொற்களைக்கூடச் சொல்லி விடலாம்;"]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:198":{"book":"kural","num":198,"t":["அரும்பயன் ஆயும் அறிவினார் சொல்லார்","பெரும்பயன் இல்லாத சொல்"],"p":"The wise who seek after rare pleasures will not speak words that have not much weight in them.","r":"Those whose understanding searches out rare ends do not say what lacks great use.","n":"The bar rises here from useless to not greatly useful, and Parimelazhagar draws this out: words of small use are excluded too. His identification of the rare ends with release and higher destinies is his own; Manakkudavar says only 'rare matters'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sol","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rare ends are release and passage to higher worlds.","அறிதற்கு அரிய பயன்களாவன: வீடு பேறும் மேற்கதிச் செலவும் முதலாயின."]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:199":{"book":"kural","num":199,"t":["பொருள்தீர்ந்த பொச்சாந்துஞ் சொல்லார் மருள்தீர்ந்த","மாசறு காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those wise men who are without faults and are freed from ignorance will not even forgetfully speak things that profit not.","r":"Those of stainless sight, freed from delusion, do not say meaningless things even by a lapse.","n":"பொச்சாந்தும் moves the claim from choice to nature: for such people the restraint survives inattention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:20"},"k:200":{"book":"kural","num":200,"t":["சொல்லுக சொல்லிற் பயனுடைய சொல்லற்க","சொல்லிற் பயனிலாச் சொல்"],"p":"Speak what is useful, and speak not useless words.","r":"Say what among words has use; do not say what among words has none.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that சொல்லில் is grammatically surplus and kept for the figure of repeated words. It may be a quiet wit that a chapter against waste in speech ends on a deliberate surplus, though that is my reading and no commentator's.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","payan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சொல்லில் means 'among words': the verse sorts words into two kinds.","சொற்களில் பயன் உடைய சொற்களைச் சொல்லுக"],["manakkudavar","The first சொல்லில் means 'if you speak', which leaves silence as an option.","சொல்லுவனாயின் பயனுடைய சொற்களைச் சொல்லுக"]],"ch":"k:20"},"k:201":{"book":"kural","num":201,"t":["தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்","தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"Those who have experience of evil deeds will not fear, but the excellent will fear the pride of sin.","r":"Those given to evil deeds don't fear the intoxication called evil deed; the excellent do.","n":"Fear is the sign of moral standing here, not a weakness. Parimelazhagar adds an insight about habit: long practice has removed the fear in one group, and never having done such things keeps it alive in the other.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செருக்கு is delusion: the evil deed is called by the name of the confusion it brings on.","தீவினை என்று சொல்லப்படும் மயக்கத்தை"],["manakkudavar","செருக்கு is the elation of wrongdoing.","தீவினை யாகிய களிப்பை"],["kalaignar","செருக்கு is a pleasure the deed may yield, and the wise fear the deed even so.","தீவினையால் மகிழ்ச்சி ஏற்படுவதாயினும்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:202":{"book":"kural","num":202,"t":["தீயவை தீய பயத்தலால் தீயவை","தீயினும் அஞ்சப் படும்"],"p":"Because evil produces evil, therefore should evil be feared more than fire.","r":"Since evil deeds yield evil, they are to be feared more than fire.","n":"The verse's reason is only that evil begets evil, sounded in the play of தீயவை on தீ. Parimelazhagar supplies a further reason, that fire does not burn across time, place and bodies, which brings in rebirth that the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fire can't burn in another time, another place or another body, and an evil deed can.","பிறிதொரு காலத்தும், பிறிதொரு தேயத்தும், பிறிதோர் உடம்பினும் சென்று சுடுதல் தீக்கு இன்மையின்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:203":{"book":"kural","num":203,"t":["அறிவினுள் எல்லாந் தலையென்ப தீய","செறுவார்க்கும் செய்யா விடல்"],"p":"To do no evil to enemies will be called the chief of all virtues.","r":"They say the chief of all understanding is to leave off doing harm even to those who hate you.","n":"The word is அறிவு, not virtue as Pope's gloss has it. Forbearance appears here as the height of intelligence, and Parimelazhagar makes the logic prudential: refraining keeps suffering from coming back to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","acham"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is the chief understanding because one infers that refraining, even when able to strike, keeps suffering away.","செய்யத் தக்குழியுஞ் செய்யாது ஒழியவே தமக்குத் துன்பம் வாராது என உய்த்துணர்தலின்"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:204":{"book":"kural","num":204,"t":["மறந்தும் பிறன்கேடு சூழற்க சூழின்","அறஞ்சூழம் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு"],"p":"Even though forgetfulness meditate not the ruin of another. Virtue will meditate the ruin of him who thus meditates.","r":"Don't plot another's ruin even by a lapse; if you do, அறம் plots the ruin of the plotter.","n":"The same verb, சூழ், serves both parties, so the plotting already sets the reply in motion and harm need never be carried out. Parimelazhagar draws the point: even thinking of it is forbidden.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறம் is a deity of virtue, and its 'plotting' is resolving to leave the man to his ruin.","அறக்கடவுள் எண்ணுதலாவது, அவன் கெடத் தான் நீங்க நினைத்தல்."],["manakkudavar","அறம் actively returns harm for harm, and gets in before the intended victim can respond.","தீமை செய்தார்க்குத் தீமை பயக்கும் அறந்தானே சூழும்"],["kalaignar","சூழும் means surrounds: moral order closes in on him like a siege.","அவனுக்குக் கேடு உண்டாக்க அவனை அறம் முற்றுகையிட்டு விடும்."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:205":{"book":"kural","num":205,"t":["இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க செய்யின்","இலனாகும் மற்றும் பெயர்த்து"],"p":"Commit not evil, saying, \"I am poor\": if you do, you will become poorer still.","r":"Don't do evil saying 'I have nothing'; if you do, you'll have nothing all over again.","n":"The verse answers the strongest excuse for wrongdoing on its own terms: it doesn't defend the poor man's honour but says the plan won't work. Parimelazhagar also reports other glossators who take the second poverty as poverty of good qualities.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The poverty returns across lives, birth after birth.","அத் தீவினையால் பிறவிதோறும் இலன் ஆம் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The deed simply fails to produce wealth, with no appeal to rebirth.","பின்பும் நல்குரவினனாவன்; அது செல் வம் பயவாது"]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:206":{"book":"kural","num":206,"t":["தீப்பால தான்பிறர்கண் செய்யற்க நோய்ப்பால","தன்னை அடல்வேண்டா தான்"],"p":"Let him not do evil to others who desires not that sorrows should pursue him.","r":"One who doesn't want afflicting things to overpower him should not do evil things to others.","n":"The balance of தீப்பால and நோய்ப்பால gives the rule its shape: what one portions out as evil returns as a portion of pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:207":{"book":"kural","num":207,"t":["எனைப்பகை யுற்றாரும் உய்வர் வினைப்பகை","வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்"],"p":"However great be the enmity men have incurred they may still live. The enmity of sin will incessantly pursue and kill.","r":"However great the enmity they've incurred, people survive it; the enmity of deeds doesn't die and follows them to kill.","n":"The deed is made an enemy of a special kind, one that can't be outrun because it travels with the doer. Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar gloss it as entering wherever one enters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:208":{"book":"kural","num":208,"t":["தீயவை செய்தார் கெடுதல் நிழல்தன்னை","வீயாது அஇஉறைந் தற்று"],"p":"Destruction will dwell at the heels of those who commit evil even as their shadow that leaves them not.","r":"The ruin of those who do evil is like a person's shadow, which never perishes and stays at the foot.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a shadow that stretches far but comes back to rest at the foot, so a consequence can seem distant and still be right there. He expressly rejects another reading, in which the shadow stands for a deed latent until its time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image is the shadow's return to the foot, not a deed lying hidden until its season.","ஒருவன் நிழல் நொடிதாகப் போயும், அவன்றன்னை விடாது வந்து அடியின்கண் தங்கி தன்மைத்து."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:209":{"book":"kural","num":209,"t":["தன்னைத்தான் காதல னாயின் எனைத்தொன்றும்","துன்னற்க தீவினைப் பால்"],"p":"If a man love himself, let him not commit any sin however small.","r":"If a person loves himself, let him not go near the portion of evil deeds, however small.","n":"The appeal rests on self-love, using காதலன், a word of real attachment. The verse doesn't ask a person to overcome self-regard: it asks him to think it through, and on Parimelazhagar's reading harm to others is harm to oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:21"},"k:210":{"book":"kural","num":210,"t":["அருங்கேடன் என்பது அறிக மருங்கோடித்","தீவினை செய்யான் எனின்"],"p":"Know ye that he is freed from destruction who commits no evil, going to neither side of the right path.","r":"Know that one who doesn't stray aside and do evil is a person for whom ruin is rare.","n":"அருங்கேடன் on its face says ruin is 'rare' or 'hard'. Parimelazhagar argues that அருமை here means absence, which turns a strong probability into a guarantee. Kalaignar also says no ruin at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருமை means absence, so the man has no ruin at all.","அருமை:இன்மை."]],"ch":"k:21"},"k:211":{"book":"kural","num":211,"t":["கைம்மாறு வேண்டா கடப்பாடு மாரிமாட்டு","என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ உலகு"],"p":"Benevolence seeks not a return. What does the world give back to the clouds ?","r":"Obligation seeks no return: what does the world do for the rain in exchange?","n":"The word chosen is கடப்பாடு, what is owed, and not gift. Parimelazhagar points out that the name itself implies it can't be dropped. Giving with no return is therefore framed as debt, a nicely paradoxical idea.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:212":{"book":"kural","num":212,"t":["தாளாற்றித் தந்த பொருளெல்லாம் தக்கார்க்கு","வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"All the wealth acquired with perseverance by the worthy is for the exercise of benevolence.","r":"All the wealth got through exertion exists for doing வேளாண்மை, with தக்கார் standing in some relation to it.","n":"The grammar of தக்கார்க்கு decides whether this sets a limit on recipients or describes the owner, so I have left it open in the reading.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தக்கார் are the owners: for those who are worthy, all wealth is for benevolence, not for private consumption or hoarding.","தக்கார்க்கு தகுதி உடையார்க்கு ஆயின்;"],["manakkudavar","தக்கார் are the recipients: the wealth is for helping those who deserve it.","ஒருவன் முயன்று ஈட்டிய பொருளெல்லாம் தகுதியுடையார்க்கு உபகரித்தற்காகவாம்"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:213":{"book":"kural","num":213,"t":["புத்தே ளுலகத்தும் ஈண்டும் பெறலரிதே","ஒப்புரவின் நல்ல பிற"],"p":"It is difficult to obtain another good equal to benevolence either in this world or in that of the gods.","r":"Whether in the world of புத்தேள் or here, it is hard to find any other thing as good as ஒப்புரவு.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a striking reason for the gods' world: all there are alike, with no giver and no receiver, so this good can't exist there. On that view the human condition of need is what makes the highest good possible.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புத்தேள் உலகு is the world of the gods.","தேவர் உலகத்தும் இவ்வுலகத்தும்"],["kalaignar","It is a new world yet to come, which removes the gods from the verse.","இன்றைய உலகிலும், இனிவரும் புதிய உலகிலும் காண்பது அரிது."]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:214":{"book":"kural","num":214,"t":["ஒத்த தறவோன் உயிர்வாழ்வான் மற்றையான்","செத்தாருள் வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He truly lives who knows (and discharges) the proper duties (of benevolence). He who knows the m not will be reckoned among the dead.","r":"One who knows what is fitting truly lives; anyone else is counted among the dead.","n":"Knowing here includes doing: Parimelazhagar glosses it as knowing and acting, because the dead neither know nor act.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oppuravu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is fitting is the way of the world as a whole, and lapses in it can't be repaired as lapses in Vedic practice can.","உலகநடை வழு வேதநடை வழுப்போலத் தீர்திறன் உடைத்து அன்று"],["manakkudavar","What is fitting is specifically ஒப்புரவு, and those who lack it are like corpses.","ஒப்புரவறியாதார் பிணத்தோ டொப்ப ரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:215":{"book":"kural","num":215,"t":["ஊருணி நீர்நிறைந் தற்றே உலகவாம்","பேரறி வாளன் திரு"],"p":"The wealth of that man of eminent knowledge who desires to exercise the benevolence approved of by the world, is like the full waters of a city-tank.","r":"The wealth of a greatly wise person connected with the world's desiring is like water filling the village's drinking tank.","n":"The image puts the wealth in a common place, and the owner disappears from the picture: people simply draw from it. This verse calls such a person பேரறிவாளன, wise, not merely generous.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This person desires and practises the world's way.","உலகநடையை விரும்பிச் செய்யும் பெரிய அறிவினை யுடையவனது செல்வம்"],["manakkudavar","This person is the one desired by all the world.","உலகத்தா ரெல்லாராலும் நச்சப்படுகின்ற"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:216":{"book":"kural","num":216,"t":["பயன்மரம் உள்ளூர்ப் பழுத்தற்றால் செல்வம்","நயனுடை யான்கண் படின்"],"p":"The wealth of a man (possessed of the virtue) of benevolence is like the ripening of a fruitful tree in the midst of a town.","r":"Wealth falling to one who has நயன் is like a fruit-giving tree ripening in the heart of the town.","n":"Parimelazhagar says that நயன் here is ஒப்புரவு as the chief of worldly justice, Manakkudavar makes it being loved by others, and Kalaignar a tender heart. The tree's public position matters as much as its fruit, since no one has to go looking for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","nayan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:217":{"book":"kural","num":217,"t":["மருந்தாகித் தப்பா மரத்தற்றால் செல்வம்","பெருந்தகை யான்கண் படின்"],"p":"If wealth be in the possession of a man who has the great excellence (of benevolence), it is like a tree which as a medicine is an infallible cure for disease.","r":"Wealth falling to one of great nobility is like a tree that is medicine and never fails.","n":"This goes further than the fruit tree of 216. Parimelazhagar has every part of the tree serving as cure, with no regard for its own lack, which suggests giving that costs the giver something. He and Manakkudavar agree that தப்பா includes never being hidden from those who seek it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:218":{"book":"kural","num":218,"t":["இடனில் பருவத்தும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்","கடனறி காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"The wise who know what is duty will not scant their benevolence even when they are without wealth.","r":"Even at a time without means, those whose sight knows what is due don't falter in ஒப்புரவு.","n":"The verse cuts ஒப்புரவு free from wealth. If it were a by-product of surplus it would lapse with the surplus.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:219":{"book":"kural","num":219,"t":["நயனுடையான் நல்கூர்ந்தா னாதல் செயும்நீர","செய்யாது அமைகலா வாறு"],"p":"The poverty of a benevolent man, is nothing but his inability to exercise the same.","r":"Poverty, for one with நயன், is just the condition of being unable to rest while what should be done goes undone.","n":"Poverty is redefined from the inside. Parimelazhagar makes the contrast explicit: it isn't going without one's own enjoyments. The deprivation is of the power to give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","nayan","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:22"},"k:220":{"book":"kural","num":220,"t":["ஒப்புரவி னால்வரும் கேடெனின் அஃதொருவன்","விற்றுக்கோள் தக்க துடைத்து"],"p":"If it be said that loss will result from benevolence, such loss is worth being procured even by the sale of one's self.","r":"If ruin is said to come through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth acquiring even by selling oneself.","n":"The verse deals with an objection: 'if it is said'. For Parimelazhagar nothing is normally worth selling oneself for, so this is meant to startle, and the reason he gives is the fame such loss yields.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oppuravu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Selling oneself is meant literally as the limit case: the loss is worth buying even at that price.","அக்கேடு தன்னை விற்றாயினும் கொள்ளும் தகுதியை உடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","It is a commercial image, selling one thing to obtain another, so the 'loss' is really a trade for later benefit.","ஒன்றை விற்று ஒன்றைக் கொள்ளுகின்ற வாணிகமாகக் கொள்ளுந் தகுதி புடைத்து"]],"ch":"k:22"},"k:221":{"book":"kural","num":221,"t":["வறியார்க்கொன்று ஈவதே ஈகைமற் றெல்லாம்","குறியெதிர்ப்பை நீர துடைத்து"],"p":"To give to the destitute is true charity. All other gifts have the nature of (what is done for) a measured return.","r":"Giving something to those who have nothing is what giving is; all the rest has the nature of a measured loan.","n":"குறியெதிர்ப்பு is a technical word: taking a stated measure and returning that same measure, as Parimelazhagar glosses it. All gifts among equals are thus reclassified, however generous they feel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:222":{"book":"kural","num":222,"t":["நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல்தீது மேலுலகம்","இல்லெனினும் ஈதலே நன்று"],"p":"To beg is evil, even though it were said that it is a good path (to heaven). To give is good, even though it were said that those who do so cannot obtain heaven.","r":"Even if it were called a good path, taking is bad; even if there were no higher world, giving alone is good.","n":"The verse cuts the value of giving loose from its reward by a hypothetical. How strong that hypothetical is, is exactly what the commentators disagree about.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Both 'even ifs' are purely hypothetical: no one actually says such things, and heaven isn't in doubt.","'எனினும்' என்பது இரு வழியும் அங்ஙனம் கூறுவார் இன்மை விளக்கி நின்றது."],["kalaignar","Giving won't obtain any higher world, and the giving life is best regardless.","கொடை வழங்குவதால் மேலுலகம் என்று சொல்லப்படுவது கிட்டிவிடப் போவதில்லை;"]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:223":{"book":"kural","num":223,"t":["இலனென்னும் எவ்வம் உரையாமை ஈதல்","குலனுடையான் கண்ணே யுள"],"p":"(Even in a low state) not to adopt the mean expedient of saying \"I have nothing,\" but to give, is the characteristic of the mad of noble birth.","r":"Not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing', and giving, are found only in one of good family.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists several rival interpretations, including giving before the asker has to speak, and giving so that he need not go on to ask anyone else.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are two virtues: not begging oneself, and not refusing those who beg.","யான் வறியன் என்று இரப்பான் சொல்லும் இளிவரவைத் தான் பிறர்கண் சொல்லாமையும்"],["manakkudavar","It is one act: giving without telling the suppliant 'I have nothing'.","இரந்து வந்தார்க்கு இல னென்னாகின்ற துன்பத்தைக் கூறாது ஈதலும்"],["kalaignar","It means giving without showing the distress of one's own poverty.","தமக்குள்ள வறுமைத் துன்பத்தைக் காட்டிக்கொள்ளாமல் பிறருக்கு ஈவது"]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:224":{"book":"kural","num":224,"t":["இன்னாது இரக்கப் படுதல் இரந்தவர்","இன்முகங் காணும் அளவு"],"p":"To see men begging from us in disagreeable, until we see their pleasant countenance.","r":"Being begged from is itself painful, until one sees the glad face of the one who begged.","n":"A candid observation: being asked is uncomfortable even for the generous. Parimelazhagar attributes this to fear of not being able to give all that is sought.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இரக்கப்படுதல் is being the one asked, and resolving to give.","இரக்கப்படுதல்-'இரப்பார்க்கு ஈவல்' என்று இருத்தல்."],["kalaignar","It is feeling pity (இரக்கம்) for the supplicant, a different root.","அவருக்காக இரக்கப்படுவதும் ஒரு துன்பமாகவே தோன்றும்."]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:225":{"book":"kural","num":225,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பசிஆற்றல் அப்பசியை","மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்"],"p":"The power of those who perform penance is the power of enduring hunger. It is inferior to the power of those who remove the hunger (of others).","r":"The strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that ranks behind the strength of those who remove hunger.","n":"The verse says only ஆற்றுவார், those with strength. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both understand ascetics, which makes this a direct ranking of the giver over the renunciant. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the ascetic hungers and can't relieve others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:226":{"book":"kural","num":226,"t":["அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல் அஃதொருவன்","பெற்றான் பொருள்வைப் புழி"],"p":"The removal of the killing hunger of the poor is the place for one to lay up his wealth.","r":"Removing the ruinous hunger of the destitute is where a person who has come into wealth should store it.","n":"அழிபசி is hunger that destroys: Parimelazhagar says all goods, Manakkudavar says a person's qualities. The financial image is made pointed by Parimelazhagar: the wealth comes back to help the giver. He supplies 'for virtue's sake' himself, so the verse is plainer about the bank than he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:227":{"book":"kural","num":227,"t":["பாத்தூண் மரீஇ யவனைப் பசியென்னும்","தீப்பிணி தீண்டல் அரிது"],"p":"The fiery disease of hunger shall never touch him who habitually distributes his food to others.","r":"The evil disease called hunger is unlikely to touch one who is habituated to sharing his food.","n":"Manakkudavar reads this as a reply to the miser's fear that giving will lead to hunger. Understood that way, the verse is a piece of argument rather than a promise. மரீஇ makes the claim about habit, not about any one gift.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It answers the fear that giving will reduce wealth and bring on hunger.","பகுத்துண்ணப் பசி வாராதென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:228":{"book":"kural","num":228,"t":["ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம் அறியார்கொல் தாமுடைமை","வைத்திழக்கும் வன்க ணவர்"],"p":"Do the hard-eyed who lay up and lose their possessions not know the happiness which springs from the pleasure of giving ?","r":"Do those hard-eyed people who hoard what they have and then lose it not know the joy of giving and gladdening?","n":"The appeal is to pleasure, not to duty: the hoarder has simply missed out. 'Keep and lose' in one word, வைத்திழக்கும், makes the loss seem part of keeping. Parimelazhagar's own wording suggests the joy belongs to those with அருள், so this reaches forward to chapter 25.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:229":{"book":"kural","num":229,"t":["இரத்தலின் இன்னாது மன்ற நிரப்பிய","தாமே தமியர் உணல்"],"p":"Solitary and unshared eating for the sake of filling up one's own riches is certainly much more unpleasant than begging.","r":"Eating alone to fill up one's own store is surely more bitter than begging.","n":"Parimelazhagar's comparison: begging has only a moment's humiliation and no lasting poverty, while the solitary eater has both. The verse takes the worst thing most people can think of and names something worse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நிரப்பிய refers to filling a fixed hoard target, so the fault is accumulation.","ஒரோ எண்களைக்குறித்து அத்துணை ஈட்டுதும் என ஈட்டத்தையே மேற்கொண்டு இவறக் கூட்டுதல்."],["manakkudavar","It refers to the food one has obtained, and தமியர் to eating out of everyone's sight.","தேடின வுணவைத தாமே தமியராயிருந் துண்டல்"]],"ch":"k:23"},"k:230":{"book":"kural","num":230,"t":["சாதலின் இன்னாத தில்லை இனிததூஉம்","ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை"],"p":"Nothing is more unpleasant than death: yet even that is pleasant where charity cannot be exercised.","r":"Nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even that is sweet when giving is out of reach.","n":"The chapter's last ranking sets giving above life. Parimelazhagar's reason is that it frees one from a bodily burden no use to anyone. This is hyperbole about value, not advice to die, and Kalaignar softens it to a comparison of pains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:23"},"k:231":{"book":"kural","num":231,"t":["ஈதல் இசைபட வாழ்தல் அதுவல்லது","ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Give to the poor and live with praise. There is no greater profit to man than that.","r":"Give, and live so that renown arises: there is no other profit for a living being.","n":"ஊதியம is a word for profit, so the verse puts its ethics in the language of a balance sheet. Parimelazhagar notes that learning and valour can also win fame, and that giving is named as the highest route to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pugazh","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:232":{"book":"kural","num":232,"t":["உரைப்பார் உரைப்பவை எல்லாம் இரப்பார்க்கொன்று","ஈவார்மேல் நிற்கும் புகழ்"],"p":"Whatsoever is spoken in the world will abide as praise upon that man who gives alms to the poor.","r":"All that speakers have to say amounts to praise resting on those who give something to those who ask.","n":"Parimelazhagar extends speech to poets' song, and he dismisses a reading that lets others have praise too ('say what you will, fame stays with the giver'), because it misses fame's excellence. The verse's sweep ('all that speakers say') is a large claim, even as hyperbole.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:233":{"book":"kural","num":233,"t":["ஒன்றா உலகத்து உயர்ந்த புகழல்லால்","பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன் றில்"],"p":"There is nothing that stands forth in the world imperishable, except fame, exalted in solitary greatness.","r":"Apart from towering, unmatched renown, there is nothing in the world that stands undying.","n":"The verse doesn't say what makes fame unmatched. Parimelazhagar specifies gifts that are hardest to give, of life, limb and property, which raises the bar far above ordinary repute.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:234":{"book":"kural","num":234,"t":["நிலவரை நீள்புகழ் ஆற்றின் புலவரைப்","போற்றாது புத்தேள் உலகு"],"p":"If one has acquired extensive fame within the limits of this earth, the world of the Gods will no longer praise those sages who have attained that world.","r":"If someone achieves far-reaching renown within the earth's bounds, the world of the gods doesn't honour the புலவர்.","n":"The verse is elliptical: it doesn't say whom heaven honours in their place, and everything hinges on the identity of புலவர். I'm not confident any reading here is the verse's own. Parimelazhagar cites Purananuru 27 for the idea that the famous gain both worlds.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புலவர் are sages who have reached that world, and it prefers the famous to them.","புத்தேள் உலகம் அவனையல்லது தன்னை எய்தி நின்ற ஞானிகளைப் பேணாது."],["manakkudavar","புலவர் are the gods themselves, so named because they have wisdom.","புலவரென்றார் தேவரை; அவர் புலனுடையாராதலான்."],["kalaignar","புலவர் are the learned and the world is a future one: it will not honour intellect alone over the selfless and famous.","அறிவாற்றல் உடையவரை மட்டும் போற்றிக் கொண்டிராது."]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:235":{"book":"kural","num":235,"t":["நத்தம்போல் கேடும் உளதாகும் சாக்காடும்","வித்தகர்க் கல்லால் அரிது"],"p":"Prosperity to the body of fame, resulting in poverty to the body of flesh and the stability to the former arising from the death of the latter, are achievable only by the wise.","r":"A ruin that is really growth and a death that is really existence are hard to come by except for the skilled.","n":"வித்தகர் praises skill, not goodness: for Parimelazhagar, the art of turning what doesn't last into what does. The reading depends on his derivation of நத்தம் from நந்து, growth, and on taking போல் as a filler, so its foundation is a philological argument.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a doctrine of two bodies: the elemental body grows poor and dies while the body of fame grows rich and stands.","ஆக்கமாகும் கேடாவது, புகழ் உடம்பு செல்வம் எய்தப் பூத உடம்பு நல்கூர்தல்."],["kalaignar","It speaks of strength of character that grows fame amid hardship and establishes it even in dying, with no second body.","துன்பங்களுக்கிடையேகூட அவற்றைத் தாங்கும் வலிமையால் தமது புகழை வளர்த்துக் கொள்வதும்,"]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:236":{"book":"kural","num":236,"t":["தோன்றின் புகழொடு தோன்றுக அஃதிலார்","தோன்றலின் தோன்றாமை நன்று"],"p":"If you are born (in this world), be born with qualities conductive to fame. From those who are destitute of them it will be better not to be born.","r":"If you appear, appear with renown; for those without it, not appearing is better than appearing.","n":"Taken as birth, a command about how to be born can't be obeyed. Each commentator has to resolve that oddity, and how they do it shapes their positions.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means being born human, and for those lacking the qualities that lead to fame, an animal birth is better, since no one reviles animals.","அக்குணம் இல்லாதார் மக்களாய்ப் பிறத்தலின் விலங்காய்ப் பிறத்தல் நன்று."],["manakkudavar","It means plainly that not being born is better.","அஃதிலார் பிறக்குமதிற் பிறவாமை நன்று"],["kalaignar","தோன்று means entering a field of activity: excel there, or stay out of it.","எந்தத் துறையில் ஈடுபட்டாலும் அதில் புகழுடன் விளங்கவேண்டும்;"]],"ch":"k:24"},"k:237":{"book":"kural","num":237,"t":["புகழ்பட வாழாதார் தந்நோவார் தம்மை","இகழ்வாரை நோவது எவன்?"],"p":"Why do those who cannot live with praise, grieve those who despise them, instead of grieving themselves for their own inability.","r":"Those who cannot live with renown: why do they resent the people who scorn them, instead of resenting themselves?","n":"The scorn is accepted as a fair verdict. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that a good name was within reach, so the failure is one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:238":{"book":"kural","num":238,"t":["வசையென்ப வையத்தார்க் கெல்லாம் இசையென்னும்","எச்சம் பெறாஅ விடின்"],"p":"Not to beget fame will be esteemed a disgrace by the wise in this world.","r":"They say it is a disgrace for everyone in the world not to obtain the legacy called renown.","n":"எச்சம் is what remains after one dies, and it also means offspring, which Pope's 'childless' seizes on. Manakkudavar and Parimelazhagar make a severe point: the lack of fame needs no other fault to justify disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:239":{"book":"kural","num":239,"t":["வசையிலா வண்பயன் குன்றும் இசையிலா","யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்"],"p":"The ground which supports a body without fame will diminish in its rich produce.","r":"Land that bears a body without renown will decline in its blameless rich yield.","n":"யாக்கை, 'body', is pointed: for Parimelazhagar, what lacks renown is merely a body, a load the land must carry. Moral failure has an effect on the soil here, which Kalaignar weakens to how the land is regarded.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:240":{"book":"kural","num":240,"t":["வசையொழிய வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார் இசையொழிய","வாழ்வாரே வாழா தவர்"],"p":"Those live who live without disgrace. Those who live without fame live not.","r":"Those who live free of blame are the ones who live; those who live free of renown are the ones who don't.","n":"The balanced form leaves no neutral ground, and Parimelazhagar states it outright: to lack blame is to have renown and to lack renown is to have blame. A quiet, harmless, obscure life has no place in this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:24"},"k:241":{"book":"kural","num":241,"t":["அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம் பொருட்செல்வம்","பூரியார் கண்ணும் உள"],"p":"The wealth of kindness is wealth of wealth, in as much as the wealth of property is possessed by the basest of men.","r":"The wealth of அருள் is the wealth among wealths, because the wealth of goods is found even among the base.","n":"The argument is about distribution: what the worst can have can't be the best thing. It doesn't say goods are bad, and 247 will concede how much depends on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:242":{"book":"kural","num":242,"t":["நல்லாற்றாள் நாடி அருளாள்க பல்லாற்றால்","தேரினும் அஃதே துணை"],"p":"(Stand) in the good path, consider, and be kind. Even considering according to the conflicting tenets of the different sects, kindness will be your best aid, (in the acquisition of heavenly bliss.)","r":"Examine by sound means and hold to அருள்; however many ways you investigate, that alone is the companion.","n":"Compassion here is what inquiry concludes, not an alternative to it. Parimelazhagar reads பல்லாற்றால் as the competing religious systems, which disagree on means of knowledge and objects but converge here. The verse itself mentions no sects, so this ecumenical reading is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Mutually opposed schools agree on this one point.","அன்னவாயினும், அருள் துணை என்றற்கண் ஒக்கும் என்பதாம்."]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:243":{"book":"kural","num":243,"t":["அருள்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்க் கில்லை இருள்சேர்ந்த","இன்னா உலகம் புகல்"],"p":"They will never enter the world of darkness and wretchedness whose minds are the abode of kindness.","r":"Those whose hearts are joined with அருள் will not enter the painful world joined with darkness.","n":"அருள் and இருள் rhyme across the verse, and the same word, சேர்ந்த, joins each to its noun.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a literal hell, a place in the lower world.","தன்கண்ணே புக்கார்க்குத் துன்பம் செய்வதோர் நரகத்தை"],["kalaignar","The darkness is ignorance, and the suffering is of this life.","அறியாமை எனும் இருள் சூழ்ந்த துன்ப உலகில்"]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:244":{"book":"kural","num":244,"t":["மன்னுயிர் ஓம்பி அருளாள்வார்க் கு இல்லென்ப","தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை"],"p":"(The wise) say that the evils, which his soul would dread, will never come upon the man who exercises kindness and protects the life (of other creatures)","r":"They say that one who sustains living beings and holds to அருள் has no deeds his own life need fear.","n":"Care for other lives removes the fear for one's own. Parimelazhagar takes மன் as making souls eternal, a doctrine not required by the word, and gives a causal link: such a person won't kill, and hence won't go to hell.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arul","vinai","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:245":{"book":"kural","num":245,"t":["அல்லல் அருளாள்வார்க்கு இல்லை வளிவழங்கும்","மல்லன்மா ஞாலங் கரி"],"p":"This great rich earth over which the wind blows, is a witness that sorrow never comes upon the kind-hearted.","r":"Those who hold to அருள் have no distress, and the wide rich earth where the wind moves is the witness.","n":"Parimelazhagar's witness is negative: no one has ever seen such a person suffer, so the whole world can testify. Kalaignar instead makes the wind an analogy for அருள் moving the mind. The claim of no distress is bold, and should be read with the reservation of 169.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:246":{"book":"kural","num":246,"t":["பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார் என்பர் அருள்நீங்கி","அல்லவை செய்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"(The wise) say that those who neglect kindness and practise cruelties, neglected virtue (in their former birth), and forgot (the sorrows which they must suffer.)","r":"Those who abandon அருள் and carry on doing what should not be done, they say, have parted from பொருள் and forgotten.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொருள் is virtue: they failed to practise it before and have forgotten the suffering they are in as a result.","முன்னும் உறுதிப் பொருளைச் செய்யாது தாம் துன்புறுகின்றமையை மறந்தவர்"],["manakkudavar","பொருள் is wealth, and the verse spans lives: lacking அருள் in a former birth, they are now both poor and forgetful.","இப்பிறப்பின்கண் பொருளினின்று நீங்கி மறவியு முடையவ"],["kalaignar","With no former birth in view, the cruel become poor and forget their duty.","பொருளற்றவர்களாகவும், கடமை மறந்தவர்களாகவும் ஆவர்."]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:247":{"book":"kural","num":247,"t":["அருளில்லார்க்கு அவ்வுலகம் இல்லை பொருளில்லார்க்கு","இவ்வுலகம் இல்லாகி யாங்கு"],"p":"As this world is not for those who are without wealth, so that world is not for those who are without kindness.","r":"As this world is not for those without wealth, that world is not for those without அருள்.","n":"The comparison gets its force from its blunt premise about money, which the verse states as obvious fact. A chapter that opened by belittling goods grants them complete authority over this world.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","That world is release.","உயிர்கள்மேல் அருள் இல்லாதார்க்கு வீட்டுலகத்து இன்பம் இல்லை"],["manakkudavar","That world is heaven.","இஃது அருளில்லாதார் சுவர்க்கம் புகாரென்றது."],["kalaignar","It isn't another world at all: wealth is to the household life as compassion is to the renouncer's life.","கருணை உள்ளம் இல்லாதவர்களின் துறவற வாழ்க்கையும் சிறப்பாக அமையாது."]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:248":{"book":"kural","num":248,"t":["பொருளற்றார் பூப்பர் ஒருகால் அருளற்றார்","அற்றார்மற் றாதல் அரிது"],"p":"Those who are without wealth may, at some future time, become prosperous; those who are destitute of kindness are utterly destitute; for them there is no change.","r":"Those without wealth may flower one day; those without அருள் are finished, and it is hard for them to become anything again.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees this as withdrawing the parallel just drawn in 247, since the two lacks aren't alike after all. His addition of 'through fate' to the poor man's state is not in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:249":{"book":"kural","num":249,"t":["தெருளாதான் மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டற்றால் தேரின்","அருளாதான் செய்யும் அறம்"],"p":"If you consider, the virtue of him who is without kindness is like the perception of the true being by him who is without wisdom.","r":"Virtue done by one without அருள், if examined, is like the muddled person seeing the true reality.","n":"Parimelazhagar concludes that அருள் is the root of all other virtues. That goes beyond the comparison the verse draws, but it is a natural development.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","aram","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Such virtue can occur, as a passing glimpse can, but the person's own lack destroys it.","அருளாதான் இடையே அறஞ்செய்தால் அதனைத் தன் அருளாமையால் தானே அழித்துவிடும்"],["manakkudavar","Such people can't practise virtue at all.","இஃது அறஞ்செய்யவும் மாட்டாரென்றது,"]],"ch":"k:25"},"k:250":{"book":"kural","num":250,"t":["வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க தான் தன்னின்","மெலியார்மேல் செல்லு மிடத்து"],"p":"When a man is about to rush upon those who are weaker than himself, let him remember how he has stood (trembling) before those who are stronger than himself.","r":"When you go against those weaker than yourself, think of yourself before those stronger.","n":"The chapter's only instruction on how compassion arises doesn't appeal to a principle: it asks you to remember your own fear. Parimelazhagar calls this the means by which அருள் is born, and extends the weaker to animals too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:25"},"k:251":{"book":"kural","num":251,"t":["தன்னூன் பெருக்கற்குத் தான்பிறிது ஊனுண்பான்","எங்ஙனம் ஆளும் அருள்?"],"p":"How can he be possessed of kindness, who to increase his own flesh, eats the flesh of other creatures.","r":"How can one who eats another's flesh to swell his own hold to அருள்?","n":"ஊன் appears on both sides, so the eater is flesh too and the act is flesh feeding flesh. Parimelazhagar adds that fattening the body is itself useless, so the cruelty buys nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:252":{"book":"kural","num":252,"t":["பொருளாட்சி போற்றாதார்க்கு இல்லை அருளாட்சி","ஆங்கில்லை ஊன்தின் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"As those possess no property who do not take care of it, so those possess no kindness who feed on flesh.","r":"Holding wealth isn't for those who don't guard it, and likewise holding அருள் isn't for those who eat flesh.","n":"ஆட்சி, holding and using, is the operative word: compassion is a possession that lapses through neglect. For Parimelazhagar the verse answers those who say a flesh-eater who wishes no creature harm hasn't compromised அருள்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arul","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse rebuts the claim that good intention makes flesh-eating harmless to அருள்.","ஊன் தின்றாராயினும் உயிர்கட்கு ஒரு தீங்கும் நினையாதார்க்கு அருள் ஆள்தற்கு இழுக்கு இல்லை என்பாரை மறுத்து,"]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:253":{"book":"kural","num":253,"t":["படைகொண்டார் நெஞ்சம்போல் நன்னூக்காது ஒன்றன்","உடல்சுவை உண்டார் மனம்"],"p":"Like the (murderous) mind of him who carries a weapon (in his hand), the mind of him who feasts with pleasure on the body of another (creature), has no regard for goodness.","r":"The mind of one who has relished another creature's body doesn't incline to good, like the heart of one holding a weapon.","n":"The argument concerns attention rather than guilt: what the hand holds, or the tongue relishes, takes over the mind. சுவை matters here, since the verse speaks of savouring and not merely of eating.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arul","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:254":{"book":"kural","num":254,"t":["ருளல்லது யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்","பொருளல்லது அவ்வூன் தினல்"],"p":"If it be asked what is kindness and what its opposite, the answer would be preservation and destruction of life; and therefore it is not right to feed on the flesh (obtained by taking away life).","r":"If asked what is அருள் and what isn't, it comes down to not killing and killing; eating that flesh is not பொருள்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","arul","pulaal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருள் is not killing and its opposite is killing, and பொருள் here means virtue, so eating is sin. He also reports another division of the words.","அக்கோறலான் வந்த ஊனைத் தின்கை பாவம்."],["manakkudavar","Non-அருள் is destroying the vow of not killing, and பொருள் means gain, so eating harms அருள் and gets one nothing.","அருள் கெடுதலேயன்றி, பெறுவதொரு பயனுமில்லை என்றது."]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:255":{"book":"kural","num":255,"t":["உண்ணாமை உள்ளது உயிர்நிலை ஊனுண்ண","அண்ணாத்தல் செய்யாது அளறு"],"p":"Not to eat flesh contributes to the continuance of life; therefore if a man eat flesh, hell will not open its mouth (to let him escape out, after he has once fallen in).","r":"The standing of life lies in not eating flesh; if one eats, hell doesn't open its mouth.","n":"The image is of a mouth that has swallowed and won't open to release. Parimelazhagar offers a population argument for உயிர்நிலை: creatures that are eaten dwindle while others multiply. Kalaignar takes அளறு as a mud pit, not the hell of the older commentators.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:256":{"book":"kural","num":256,"t":["தினற்பொருட்டால் கொல்லாது உலகெனின் யாரும்","விலைப்பொருட்டால் ஊன்றருவா ரில்"],"p":"If the world does not destroy life for the purpose of eating, then no one would sell flesh for the sake of money.","r":"If the world didn't kill for the sake of eating, nobody would bring flesh for the sake of a price.","n":"This is a demand-and-supply argument. The buyer who says he killed nothing is told that his purchase causes the killing. Parimelazhagar formalizes the reply as inference by necessary implication.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","kollaamai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It answers those who ask what fault there is in buying and eating without having killed.","கொன்று தின்னாது விலைக்குக் கொண்டு தின்பார்க்குக் குற்றமென்னை யென்றார்க்கு , அதனாலுங் கொலைப்பாவம் வருமென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:26"},"k:257":{"book":"kural","num":257,"t":["உண்ணாமை வேண்டும் புலாஅல் பிறிதொன்றன்","புண்ணது உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If men should come to know that flesh is nothing but the unclean ulcer of a body, let them abstain from eating it.","r":"If there were people who perceived flesh as another creature's wound, it would not be eaten.","n":"பெறின் suggests such perceivers are rare. The commentators stress impurity, yet 'another's wound' can equally appeal to fellow-feeling, as the wound belongs to someone. That second emphasis is my own reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pulaal","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:258":{"book":"kural","num":258,"t":["செயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த காட்சியார் உண்ணார்","உயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த ஊன்"],"p":"The wise, who have freed themselves from mental delusion, will not eat the flesh which has been severed from an animal.","r":"Those whose vision has parted from fault don't eat flesh that has parted from life.","n":"The two lines mirror each other with தலைப்பிரிந்த: a person parted from defect faces flesh parted from life. Parimelazhagar says they see for themselves that it is a corpse, so no rule is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pulaal","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:259":{"book":"kural","num":259,"t":["அவிசொரிந் தாயிரம் வேட்டலின் ஒன்றன்","உயிர்செகுத் துண்ணாமை நன்று"],"p":"Not to kill and eat (the flesh of) an animal, is better than the pouring forth of ghee etc., in a thousand sacrifices.","r":"Better than pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices is not taking one creature's life and eating.","n":"A thousand rites weigh less than one abstention. The commentators treat this as a comparison of merit, but the verse can also be read as a challenge to ritual, and that sharper reading isn't one they give.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:260":{"book":"kural","num":260,"t":["கொல்லான் புலாலை மறுத்தானைக் கைகூப்பி","எல்லா உயிருந் தொழும்"],"p":"All creatures will join their hands together, and worship him who has never taken away life, nor eaten flesh.","r":"All living beings fold their hands and worship the one who doesn't kill and has refused flesh.","n":"The beings that have been spared do the worshipping, an image rather than a report. Parimelazhagar insists that the two abstentions are only fruitful together, which closes the loophole of the previous verses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","pulaal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:26"},"k:261":{"book":"kural","num":261,"t":["உற்றநோய் நோன்றல் உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை","அற்றே தவத்திற் குரு"],"p":"The nature of religious discipline consists, in the endurance (by the ascetic) of the sufferings which it brings on himself, and in abstaining from giving pain to others.","r":"Bearing the pain that comes and causing no pain to living beings: such alone is the form of தவம்.","n":"The definition mentions no gods, rites or forest, and half of it is ethical. Parimelazhagar takes the pain as self-imposed through such things as reduced food, while Manakkudavar says only pain that has come to one. Kalaignar turns it into a heart that can bear anything, which would make tapas available in any life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:262":{"book":"kural","num":262,"t":["தவமும் தவமுடையார்க்கு ஆகும் அதனை","அஃதிலார் மேற்கொள் வது"],"p":"Austerities can only be borne, and their benefits enjoyed, by those who have practised them (in a former birth); it will be useless for those who have not done so, to attempt to practise them (now).","r":"தவம் too comes to those who have தவம்; for those without it, taking it up is futile.","n":"The form is circular, a discouraging one for a would-be beginner. The chapter's praise of effort in the following verses sits awkwardly with it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One needs தவம் from an earlier birth, which gives the familiarity needed to complete it.","தவந்தானும் உண்டாவது முன்தவம் உடையார்க்கே"],["manakkudavar","It requires earlier good deeds of any kind, not earlier austerity as such.","தவஞ்செய்தலும் முன்பு நல்வினை செய்தார்க்கு வரும்"],["kalaignar","It requires present firmness and self-control, with no former birth involved.","உறுதிப்பாடும், மன அடக்கமும் உடையவருக்கே தவத்தின் பெருமை வாய்க்கும்."]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:263":{"book":"kural","num":263,"t":["துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி மறந்தார்கொல்","மற்றை யவர்கள் தவம்"],"p":"It is to provide food etc, for the ascetics who have abandoned (the desire of earthly possessions) that other persons have forgotten (to practise) austerity ?","r":"Is it in wanting to provide for those who have renounced that the others have forgotten தவம்?","n":"The chiding question implies that தவம் isn't reserved for renunciants. Both older commentators draw the conclusion that தவம் is greater than giving, but the verse only implies it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Householders, drawn by love of giving, have forgotten what is greater.","தானத்தினும் தவம் மிக்கது என்பது பெற்றாம்."]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:264":{"book":"kural","num":264,"t":["ஒன்னார்த் தெறலும் உவந்தாரை ஆக்கலும்","எண்ணின் தவத்தான் வரும்"],"p":"If (the ascetic) desire the destruction of his enemies, or the aggrandizement of his friends, it will be effected by (the power of) his austerities.","r":"Crushing foes and raising those who rejoice with one come through தவம், if one so intends.","n":"எண்ணின், 'if one thinks of it', is doing much here. Parimelazhagar takes it to imply that such people naturally don't think of it. An ascetic with enemies is a problem for him, and he solves it by moving the power to தவம் itself.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The foes are enemies of virtue, and the friends are those who delight in it.","அறத்திற்குப் பகையாய் அழிவு செய்தாரைக் கெடுத்தலும்"],["manakkudavar","These are ordinary enemies and friends, and the power comes from austerity done earlier.","பகைவரைத் தெறுதலும், நட்டோரையாக்குதலு மாகிய வலி ஆராயின், முன்செய்த தவத்தினாலே வரும்"]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:265":{"book":"kural","num":265,"t":["வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங் கெய்தலால் செய்தவம்","ஈண்டு முயலப் படும்"],"p":"Religious dislipline is practised in this world, because it secures the attainment of whatever one may wish to enjoy (in the world to come).","r":"Because whatever is wanted is obtained as it is wanted, தவம் is striven for here.","n":"The motive offered is getting what you want, in a chapter that condemns desire in the very next verse. Parimelazhagar infers from ஈண்டு, 'here', that the results come in the next life and include release, but the verse doesn't say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:266":{"book":"kural","num":266,"t":["தவஞ் செய்வார் தங்கருமஞ் செய்வார்மற் றல்லார்","அவஞ்செய்வார் ஆசையுட் பட்டு"],"p":"Those discharge their duty who perform austerities; all others accomplish their own destruction, through the entanglement of the desire (of riches and sensual pleasure).","r":"Those who do தவம் are doing their own work; the rest, caught in desire, do what is futile.","n":"தங்கருமம் is telling: austerity is presented as true self-interest, and worldly activity as neglect of one's real business. Parimelazhagar adds 'having renounced' and identifies 'the rest' as those pursuing wealth and pleasure, which sweeps in most of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:267":{"book":"kural","num":267,"t":["சுடச்சுடரும் பொன்போல் ஒளிவிடும் துன்பஞ்","சுடச்சுட நோற்கிற் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"Just as gold is purified as heated in the fire, will those shine, who have endured the burning of pain (in frequent austerities).","r":"For those able to endure, pain burning them makes them shine, as gold shines the more it is burned.","n":"The doubled சுடச்சுட appears in both lines and makes the image of repeated firing audible. What the shining stands for is left open: for Parimelazhagar sin leaves and knowledge grows, for Manakkudavar deeds leave, and for Kalaignar it is fame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:268":{"book":"kural","num":268,"t":["தன்னுயிர் தான்அறப் பெற்றானை ஏனைய","மன்னுயி ரெல்லாந் தொழும்"],"p":"All other creatures will worship him who has attained the control of his own soul.","r":"All other living beings worship the one who has completely gained his own self.","n":"The disagreement is nearly a reversal: for one reading the person comes to own the self, and for the other he is rid of it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He has obtained his own self as truly his own, and அற intensifies this: nothing is harder to obtain.","தன் உயிரைத் தான் தனக்கு உரித்தாகப் பெற்றவனை"],["manakkudavar","அற marks cessation: he has got rid of the thought of 'I', and தான் is the ego.","தானறு தலாவது அகங்கார மறு தல்."],["kalaignar","He has neither attachment to 'my life' nor the pride of 'I'.","\"தனது உயிர்\" என்கிற பற்றும், \"தான்\" என்கிற செருக்கும் கொள்ளாதவர்களை"]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:269":{"book":"kural","num":269,"t":["கூற்றம் குதித்தலும் கைகூடும் நோற்றலின்","ஆற்றல் தலைப்பட் டவர்க்குல்"],"p":"Those who have attained the power which religious discipline confers, will be able also to pass the limit of Yama, (the God of death).","r":"Even leaping past death becomes possible for those who have attained the power of austerity.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the 'even' can signal how nearly impossible it is. Manakkudavar illustrates with Markandeya, which is his example and isn't in the verse. Kalaignar takes it as conquering death through steadfastness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:27"},"k:270":{"book":"kural","num":270,"t":["இலர்பல ராகிய காரணம் நோற்பார்","சிலர்பலர் நோலா தவர்"],"p":"Because there are few who practise austerity and many who do not, there are many destitute and few rich in this world.","r":"The reason the have-nots are many: those who practise austerity are few and those who don't are many.","n":"The verse says just 'those who lack', without saying what they lack. As an explanation of poverty it is arresting and severe, and the commentators each soften or redirect it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Wealth and poverty here include having and lacking understanding.","செல்வம் நல்குரவு என்பன ஈண்டு அறிவினது உண்மை இன்மைகளையும் குறித்து நின்றன;"],["manakkudavar","It is about those without goods, plainly.","பொருளில்லாதார் உலகத்துப் பலராதற்குக் காரணம், தவஞ் செய்வார் சிலராதல்;"],["kalaignar","It is about those lacking capability, and the few are simply those with firm resolve.","ஆற்றலற்றவர்கள் பலராக இருப்பதற்குக் காரணம், மன உறுதி கொண்டவர் சிலராக இருப்பதும்"]],"ch":"k:27"},"k:271":{"book":"kural","num":271,"t":["வஞ்ச மனத்தான் படிற்றொழுக்கம் பூதங்கள்","ஐந்தும் அகத்தே நகும்"],"p":"The five elements (of his body) will laugh within him at the feigned conduct of the deceitful minded man.","r":"The five elements laugh inwardly at the concealed conduct of one with a deceitful mind.","n":"A hypocrite can't escape witnesses, because his own body is one of them. Parimelazhagar identifies the hidden conduct as sexual, and he carries this through 273 and 274, but the verse doesn't specify.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The elements themselves are the cosmic witnesses, mingled in his body and laughing without his knowing.","உடம்பாய் அவனோடு கலந்து நிற்கின்ற பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் கண்டு தம்முள்ளே நகும்."],["manakkudavar","'Elements' stands for the senses, their products, which know what he does.","பூதங்களென்றது அவையிற்றின் காரியமாகிய பொறிகளை ."]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:272":{"book":"kural","num":272,"t":["வானுயர் தோற்றம் எவன்செய்யும் தன்னெஞ்சம்","தான்அறி குற்றப் படின்"],"p":"What avails an appearance (of sanctity) high as heaven, if his mind suffers (the indulgence) of conscious sin.","r":"What will an appearance as high as the sky do, if one's own heart sinks into a fault one knows to be a fault?","n":"தான்அறி, 'that one knows', is the crux. Parimelazhagar's point is that a fault done unknowingly can be washed away, but one done knowingly can't, so the outward habit only frightens others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:273":{"book":"kural","num":273,"t":["வலியில் நிலைமையான் வல்லுருவம் பெற்றம்","புலியின்தோல் போர்த்துமேய்ந் தற்று"],"p":"The assumed appearance of power, by a man who has no power (to restrain his senses and perform austerity), is like a cow feeding on grass covered with a tiger's skin.","r":"A person without strength taking on the form of the strong is like a cow grazing wrapped in a tiger's skin.","n":"Parimelazhagar presses the image hard. The guards don't chase the 'tiger' because tigers don't eat grass, and because they are afraid, and the cow leaves its own grass for another's crop. So the renouncer leaves his wife and pursues other men's. The strength lacking is mastery of one's own mind; the rest is his extrapolation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:274":{"book":"kural","num":274,"t":["தவமறைந்து அல்லவை செய்தல் புதல்மறைந்து","வேட்டுவன் புள்சிமிழ்த் தற்று"],"p":"He who hides himself under the mask of an ascetic and commits sins, like a sportsman who conceals himself in the thicket to catch birds.","r":"Hiding in austerity and doing what isn't austerity is like a hunter hiding in a bush and snaring birds.","n":"With 273 this gives two opposed animals: the cow is harmless inside a predator's skin, and the hunter is a predator behind harmless leaves. The hypocrite is both, weak before his desire and dangerous to others. That pairing is my own observation. Manakkudavar gives Arjuna as an example.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:275":{"book":"kural","num":275,"t":["பற்றற்றேம் என்பார் படிற்றொழுக்கம் எற்றெற்றென்று","ஏதம் பலவுந் தரும்"],"p":"The false conduct of those who say they have renounced all desire will one day bring them sorrows that will make them cry out, \"Oh! what have we done, what have we done.\"","r":"The hidden conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' brings many evils, with cries of 'what, what!'","n":"என்பார், 'those who say', quietly marks the claim as words only, and Parimelazhagar says that the attachment was only cut in speech.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The cry belongs to the hypocrites, who later lament what they have done.","பின் 'என் செய்தோம்! என் செய்தோம்! என்று தாமே இரங்கும்வகை,"],["manakkudavar","The cry belongs to everyone, who exclaim at such a person, and எற்று is a regional word.","எல்லாரும் எற்றெற்றென்று சொல்லும்படி பலகுற்றமும் உண்டாக்கும்,"]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:276":{"book":"kural","num":276,"t":["நெஞ்சின் துறவார் துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து","வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்"],"p":"Amongst living men there are none so hard-hearted as those who without to saking (desire) in their heart, falsely take the appearance of those who have forsaken (it).","r":"None are more hard-eyed than those who, unrenounced in heart, live by deceiving as though they had renounced.","n":"Why is deceit the height of cruelty? Parimelazhagar has a remarkable answer: the victims are donors, whose gifts to the unworthy earn them a low birth, so the fraud returns harm for help. He supports this from the Civakacintamani, not from the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They harm those who feed them, since gifts to the unrestrained bring donors a low birth.","தமக்கு ஆவன செய்தார்க்கு ஆகாதன விளைத்தலின் 'வன்கணார் இல்' என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:277":{"book":"kural","num":277,"t":["புறங்குன்றி கண்டனைய ரேனும் அகங்குன்றி","முக்கிற் கரியார் உடைத்து"],"p":"(The world) contains persons whose outside appears (as fair) as the (red) berry of the Abrus, but whose inside is as black as the nose of that berry.","r":"The world holds those who, though looking outwardly like the red kunri seed, are inwardly black as its tip.","n":"The kunri is a closely observed choice, since its red and black belong to a single small seed. உடைத்து, 'it holds', says only that such people exist. Manakkudavar takes this as a warning to the wise not to judge ascetics by form.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:278":{"book":"kural","num":278,"t":["மனத்தது மாசாக மாண்டார் நீராடி","மறைந்தொழுகு மாந்தர் பலர்"],"p":"There are many men of masked conduct, who perform their ablutions, and (make a show) of greatness, while their mind is defiled (with guilt).","r":"Many people, with impurity in their minds, bathe as though distinguished and go about concealed.","n":"பலர், 'many', comes right after the 'some' of 277 and is a social observation. Water cleans what isn't dirty and leaves what is. Parimelazhagar specifies the impurity as desire, anger and delusion.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They literally bathe, to appear distinguished by austerity, and hide behind this.","பிறர்க்குத் தவத்தான் மாட்சிமையுடையராய் நீரின் மூழ்கிக் காட்டி;"],["manakkudavar","நீராடி isn't bathing: they put on the நீர்மை, the nature, of the distinguished.","மாட்சிமைப் பட்டாரது நீர்மையைப் பூண்டு,"],["kalaignar","Water is an analogy: they hide under the name of greatness as a diver hides under water.","நீருக்குள் மூழ்கியோர் தம்மை மறைத்துக் கொள்வது போல,"]],"ch":"k:28"},"k:279":{"book":"kural","num":279,"t":["கணைகொடிது யாழ்கோடு செவ்விதுஆங் கன்ன","வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்"],"p":"As, in its use, the arrow is crooked, and the curved lute is straight, so by their deeds, (and not by their appearance) let (the uprightness or crookedness of) men be estimated.","r":"The arrow is cruel and the curved lute is straight, so know people by the nature of their deeds.","n":"The verse makes shape deceive in both directions: the straight kills and the curved delights. It does more than warn that some who look good are bad, since it also suggests that some who look wrong are good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["koodaozhukkam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:280":{"book":"kural","num":280,"t":["மழித்தலும் நீட்டலும் வேண்டா உலகம்","பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்"],"p":"There is no need of a shaven crown, nor of tangled hair, if a man abstain from those deeds which the wise have condemned.","r":"Neither shaving nor long hair is needed, if one gives up what the world condemns.","n":"The test of renunciation is set by what the ordinary world condemns, and not by a special ascetic code. Parimelazhagar narrows உலகம் to the high-minded, but Manakkudavar leaves it as people generally. The verse removes the outward marks, not renunciation itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["koodaozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:28"},"k:281":{"book":"kural","num":281,"t":["எள்ளாமை வேண்டுவான் என்பான் எனைத்தொன்றும்","கள்ளாமை காக்கதன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Let him, who desires not to be despised, keep his mind from (the desire of) defrauding another of the smallest thing.","r":"One who is called a person wanting not to be scorned should guard his own heart against stealing anything at all.","n":"The verse guards the heart, not the hand, and so the offence is relocated. Parimelazhagar uses எள்ளாமை for a long attack on Lokayata materialism, and this polemic is entirely his.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means not scorning release: the person wants liberation and is respected by ascetics for that.","வீட்டினை இகழாது விரும்புவான் இவன் என்று தவத்தோரான் நன்கு மதிக்கப்படுவான்;"],["manakkudavar","It means not being scorned by other people, a matter of ordinary repute.","பிறரா லிகழப்படாமையை வேண்டுவா னிவனென்று சொல் லப்படுமவன்,"]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:282":{"book":"kural","num":282,"t":["உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும் தீதே பிறன்பொருளைக்","கள்ளத்தால் கள்வேம் எனல்"],"p":"Even the thought (of sin) is sin; think not then of crafiily stealing the property of another.","r":"Even thinking it in the mind is wrong, saying 'we will steal another's goods by stealth'.","n":"Parimelazhagar limits this high standard to renunciants, whose minds he says are special, but the verse names no such group. The distinction matters, as a universal thought-crime is a stronger claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:283":{"book":"kural","num":283,"t":["களவினால் ஆகிய ஆக்கம் அளவிறந்து","ஆவது போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The property, which is acquired by fraud, will entirely perish, even while it seems to increase.","r":"Gain got by stealth will perish beyond all measure, even as it seems to grow.","n":"ஆவது போல, 'as if growing', places the ruin inside the appearance of success. Parimelazhagar gives அளவிறந்து a sharp sense: as it goes, stolen wealth leaves sin and blame behind and takes earlier virtue along with it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","selvam","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:284":{"book":"kural","num":284,"t":["களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல் விளைவின்கண்","வீயா விழுமம் தரும்"],"p":"The eager desire of defrauding others will, when it brings forth its fruit, produce undying sorrow.","r":"Love that has ripened in stealth yields, at harvest, a sorrow that doesn't die.","n":"The verb and noun suggest a crop: கன்றிய, then விளைவு. Using காதல் for the urge to steal treats theft as an attachment, and Parimelazhagar makes the point that the craving breeds repetition, which is why the sorrow doesn't end.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:285":{"book":"kural","num":285,"t":["அருள்கருதி அன்புடைய ராதல் பொருள்கருதிப்","பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார்கண் இல்"],"p":"The study of kindness and the exercise of benevolence is not with those who watch for another's forgetfulness, though desire of his property.","r":"Having a loving nature with அருள் in view isn't found in those who, with goods in view, watch for others' lapses.","n":"The rhyme of அருள்கருதி and பொருள்கருதி sets two objects of attention side by side as if a mind could hold only one. பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார் is a precise picture of the thief's patient watching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:286":{"book":"kural","num":286,"t":["அளவின்கண் நின்றொழுகல் ஆற்றார் களவின்கண்","கன்றிய காத லவர்"],"p":"They cannot walk steadfastly, according to rule, who eagerly desire to defraud others.","r":"Those whose love has ripened in stealth can't keep to the path of அளவு.","n":"The rhyme அளவு and களவு runs through the next several verses, so the chapter has built its argument into one letter's difference.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அளவு is reasoned inquiry measuring the soul, karma and release, which he identifies with a Jain term.","உயிர் முதலியவற்றை அளத்தலாகிய நெற்றியின்கண் நின்று அதற்கு ஏற்ப ஒழுகமாட்டார்;"],["manakkudavar","அளவு is simply rectitude.","களவின்கண்ணே மிக்க ஆசையையுடையவர் நேரின் கணின்று ஒழுகுதலைச் செய்யமாட்டார்,"],["kalaignar","அளவு is living within limits, and failing to do so leads to theft, which reverses the causal direction.","ஓர் எல்லைக்குட்பட்டு வாழ்வைச் செம்மையாக அமைத்துக் கொள்ளாதவர்கள்,"]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:287":{"book":"kural","num":287,"t":["களவென்னும் காரறி வாண்மை அளவென்னும்","ஆற்றல் புரிந்தார்கண்ட இல்"],"p":"That black-knowledge which is called fraud, is not in those who desire that greatness which is called rectitude.","r":"The dark knowledge called stealth isn't found in those who have set their love on the strength called அளவு.","n":"Stealth is granted to be a kind of intelligence, just a dark one, so cleverness alone is not a virtue in the Kural. Parimelazhagar says stealth and renunciation are opposed as dark and light.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","alavu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:288":{"book":"kural","num":288,"t":["அளவறாந்தார் நெஞ்சத் தறம்போல நிற்கும்","களவறிந்தார் நெஞ்சில் கரவு"],"p":"Deceit dwells in the mind of those who are conversant with fraud, even as virtue in the mind s of those who are conversant with rectitude.","r":"Deceit stands in the heart of those who know stealth, just as virtue stands in the heart of those who know அளவு.","n":"The comparison gives vice the same stability as virtue. Manakkudavar draws the discouraging conclusion that once learned, stealth can't be given up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","alavu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:29"},"k:289":{"book":"kural","num":289,"t":["அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர் களவல்ல","மற்றைய தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Those, who are acquainted with nothing but fraud, will perish in the very commission of transgression.","r":"Those who know nothing but stealth perish right there, having done what is beyond measure.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalavu","alavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The ruin is immediate and inward: they are lost the moment they think such thoughts, and 'other things' are the renunciant's permitted food and lay gifts.","அவ்வளவல்லாத தீய நினைவுகளை நினைத்த பொழுதே கெடுவர்;"],["manakkudavar","The ruin is civil: kings execute thieves.","இது கள் வாரை அரசர் கொல்வரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:290":{"book":"kural","num":290,"t":["கள்வார்க்குத் தள்ளும் உயிர்நிலை கள்வார்க்குத்","தள்ளாது புத்தே ளுளகு"],"p":"Even their body will fail the fraudulent; but even the world of the gods will not fail those who are free from fraud.","r":"For those who steal, உயிர்நிலை falls away; for those who don't, the world of the gods doesn't fall away.","n":"The shared verb தள்ளு balances the near against the far, in Parimelazhagar's phrase: even what is closest fails the one, and even what is remote doesn't fail the other.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kalavu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உயிர்நிலை is the body, lost when the king punishes.","களவினைப் பயில்வார்க்குத் தம்மின் வேறல்லாத உடம்பும் தவறும்;"],["manakkudavar","உயிர்நிலை is release, so thieves miss liberation just as non-thieves gain heaven.","பிறர் பொருளைக் கள்வார்க்கு உயிர்நிலையாகிய வீடு பெறுதல் தப்பும்;"],["kalaignar","It is life itself, and the gods' world becomes a world of fame.","களவை நினைத்தும் பார்க்காதவர்க்கோ, புகழுலக வாழ்க்கை தவறவே தவறாது."]],"ch":"k:29"},"k:291":{"book":"kural","num":291,"t":["வாய்மை எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும்","தீமை இலாத சொலல்"],"p":"Truth is the speaking of such words as are free from the least degree of evil (to others).","r":"If you ask what truth is, it is speech containing no evil at all.","n":"A surprising definition, because nothing here refers to facts. Parimelazhagar spells out the consequence: stating what happened isn't the definition, and even an accurate report is falsehood if it harms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","sol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Reporting what actually occurred is excluded as the definition of truth.","இதனான் நிகழ்ந்தது கூறல் என்பது நீக்கப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:292":{"book":"kural","num":292,"t":["பொய்மையும் வாய்மை யிடத்த புரைதீர்ந்த","நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்"],"p":"Even falsehood has the nature of truth, if it confer a benefit that is free from fault.","r":"Even falsehood is in the place of truth, if it yields good that is free of fault.","n":"புரைதீர்ந்த does the limiting work: this excuses no convenient lie. Parimelazhagar's example is a falsehood that saves a creature from ruin or death, and he builds 291 and 292 into a four-way scheme crossing fact with effect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaaimai","sol","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Truth and falsehood are classified by effect: useful non-fact is truth, and harmful or useless fact is falsehood.","'தீங்கு பயவாத நிகழ்ந்தது கூறலும், நன்மை பயக்கும் நிகழாதது கூறலும் மெய்ம்மை'"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:293":{"book":"kural","num":293,"t":["தன்நெஞ் சறிவது பொய்யற்க பொய்த்தபின்","தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்"],"p":"Let not a man knowingly tell a lie; for after he has told the lie, his mind will burn him (with the memory of his guilt).","r":"Don't lie about what your own heart knows; once you lie, your own heart will burn you.","n":"The harm returns by the same organ of knowing. Parimelazhagar has the heart as a witness that brings on the sin's result, and Manakkudavar has it as remorse on seeing what the lie did to others. Kalaignar's 'conscience' is a modern word for the same thing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:294":{"book":"kural","num":294,"t":["உள்ளத்தாற் பொய்யா தொழுகின் உலகத்தார்","உள்ளத்து ளெல்லாம் உளன்"],"p":"He who, in his conduct, preserves a mind free from deceit, will dwell in the minds of all men.","r":"If one conducts oneself without being false in one's heart, one lives in the hearts of all the world.","n":"உள்ளம் appears on both sides of the verse: inner truth gives a place in others' inner lives. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' to the elevated, and says it is speech that has to match the heart. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar make it not even thinking a lie.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:295":{"book":"kural","num":295,"t":["மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின் தவத்தொடு","தானஞ்செய் வாரின் தலை"],"p":"He, who speaks truth with all his heart, is superior to those who make gifts and practise austerities.","r":"If one speaks truth together with one's heart, one is above those who practise austerity along with giving.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is about levels of the person: acts of body are outward, but acts of mind and speech are inward. Note that truth is ranked here above the two virtues that chapters 23 and 27 have just exalted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","manam","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:296":{"book":"kural","num":296,"t":["பொய்யாமை அன்ன புகழில்லை எய்யாமை","எல்லா அறமுந் தரும்"],"p":"There is no praise like the praise of never uttering a falsehood: without causing any suffering, it will lead to every virtue.","r":"There is no renown like not lying, and it brings all virtue without எய்யாமை.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","pugazh","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Without bodily strain: it spares the toil that wealth-gathering and fasting demand.","மறுமைக்கு மெய் வருந்தாமல் அவனுக்கு எல்லா அறங்களையும் தானே கொடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","Without his knowing: virtue accrues to him unawares.","பொய்யாமையானது அவனறியாமல் தானே எல்லா அறங்களையுங் கொடுக்குமாதலான்,"],["kalaignar","It brings benefits that never leave.","என்றும் நீங்காத அறவழி நலன்களை அளிப்பது அந்த வாழ்வேயாகும்."]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:297":{"book":"kural","num":297,"t":["பொய்யாமை பொய்யாமை ஆற்றின் அறம்பிற","செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"If a man has the power to abstain from falsehood, it will be well with him, even though he practise no other virtue.","r":"If one keeps up not lying, not lying, then not doing other virtues, not doing them, is good.","n":"The commentators' opposed readings depend on where the doubled words are divided. Parimelazhagar knows and rejects a reading like Manakkudavar's.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Truthfulness alone suffices, and it is better to skip other virtues than to attempt many and fail.","அவன் பிற அறங்களைச் செய்யாமையே செய்யாமையே நன்று."],["manakkudavar","The opposite stress: other virtues are good only if truthfulness is unfailing, and otherwise they turn bad.","பொய்யாமையைப் பொய்யாமல் செலுத்துவனாயின், பிற அறங் களைச் செய்தல் நன்றாம்; அல்லது தீதாம்,"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:298":{"book":"kural","num":298,"t":["புறள்தூய்மை நீரான் அமையும் அகந்தூய்மை","வாய்மையால் காணப் படும்"],"p":"Purity of body is produced by water and purity of mind by truthfulness.","r":"Outer purity comes about through water; inner purity is seen through truth.","n":"காணப்படும் is more ambiguous than it looks. Parimelazhagar explains that what is seen exists, so truth produces purity, whereas Manakkudavar says purity is known by truthful speech, which makes truth its evidence. Parimelazhagar also infers that renunciants need both purities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vaaimai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:299":{"book":"kural","num":299,"t":["எல்லா விளக்கும் விளக்கல்ல சான்றோர்க்குப்","பொய்யா விளக்கே விளக்கு"],"p":"All lamps of nature are not lamps; the lamp of truth is the lamp of the wise.","r":"Not all lamps are lamps; for the worthy, the unfailing lamp alone is the lamp.","n":"பொய்யா, 'unfailing' and also 'unlying', makes truth a kind of light that shows things as they are.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rejected lamps are worldly lights such as sun, moon and fire, which dispel outer darkness only.","புறத்து இருள் கடியும் உலகத்தார் விளக்குகள் எல்லாம் விளக்கு ஆகா;"],["manakkudavar","விளக்கு is the radiance of a good person, and what is rejected is radiance earned by other virtues.","சான்றோர்க்கு எல்லாவறத்தினாலும் உண்டான ஒளியும் ஒளி யல்ல ;"]],"ch":"k:30"},"k:300":{"book":"kural","num":300,"t":["யாமெய்யாக் கண்டவற்றுள் இல்லை எனைத்தொன்றும்","வாய்மையின் நல்ல பிற"],"p":"Amidst all that we have seen (described) as real (excellence), there is nothing so good as truthfulness.","r":"Among the things we have seen as true, there is nothing at all better than truthfulness.","n":"The first person, யாம், is a rare moment when the poet speaks as himself. Parimelazhagar turns 'what we have seen' into the scriptures revealed by the Lord, but Manakkudavar keeps it simple as what we have found to be true, and nothing in the verse mentions texts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:30"},"k:301":{"book":"kural","num":301,"t":["செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான் சினங்காப்பான் அல்லிடத்துக்","காக்கின்என் காவாக்கால் என்?"],"p":"He restrains his anger who restrains it when it can injure; when it cannot injure, what does it matter whether he restrain it, or not ?","r":"One who guards his anger where it can take effect truly guards it; where it can't, what does guarding or not guarding matter?","n":"Virtue is located only where power is. Patience toward those who could crush you may be just caution, and the verse declines to count it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:302":{"book":"kural","num":302,"t":["செல்லா இடத்துச் சினந்தீது செல்லிடத்தும்","இல்அதனின் தீய பிற"],"p":"Anger is bad, even when it cannot injure; when it can injure; there is no greater evil.","r":"Anger is bad where it can't take effect; where it can, nothing else is worse.","n":"This closes the gap left by 301, which could be misread as permission to rage where it doesn't matter. Parimelazhagar sets out why: at the strong it rebounds, and at the weak it brings both blame and sin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:303":{"book":"kural","num":303,"t":["மறத்தல் வெகுளியை யார்மாட்டும் தீய","பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்"],"p":"Forget anger towards every one, as fountains of evil spring from it.","r":"Forget anger at anyone at all; evils are born from it.","n":"The verb is மறத்தல், to forget, not to suppress: the instruction asks for anger to go out of mind and not to be held down. Pope's 'trespass' moves the forgetting from the anger to the offence, which the Tamil doesn't do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:304":{"book":"kural","num":304,"t":["நகையும் உவகையும் கொல்லும் சினத்தின்","பகையும் உளவோ பிற"],"p":"Is there a greater enemy than anger, which kills both laughter and joy ?","r":"Is there any other enemy than the anger that kills both the smile and the joy?","n":"Parimelazhagar assigns the smile to the face and the joy to the heart, so anger destroys both outer and inner. He adds that renunciants have no outer enemies, which is why this inner one stands alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:305":{"book":"kural","num":305,"t":["தன்னைத்தான் காக்கின் சினங்காக்க காவாக்கால்","தன்னையே கொல்லுஞ் சினம்"],"p":"If a man would guard himself, let him guard against anger; if he do not guard it, anger will kill him.","r":"If you would guard yourself, guard against anger; if you don't, it is yourself that anger kills.","n":"தன்னையே, 'yourself and no other', turns anger's target around. Parimelazhagar reads the verse in light of curses: the ascetic who spends his power cursing loses it, so 'kills' means extreme suffering. Manakkudavar takes it as a threat to life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Kills' means intense suffering: the ascetic loses his austerity by cursing and is back in the misery of births.","கொலைச்சொல் ஈண்டுத் துன்பமிகுதி உணர்த்தி நின்றது."],["manakkudavar","Anger endangers life itself.","இஃது உயிர்க்கேடு வருமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:306":{"book":"kural","num":306,"t":["சினமென்னும் சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி இனமென்னும்","ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்"],"p":"The fire of anger will burn up even the pleasant raft of friendship.","r":"Anger, the fire that kills what it comes near, burns even the safe raft of one's own people.","n":"The name சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி is a coinage for fire, 'killer of those it joins'. Parimelazhagar finds a contrast with ordinary fire, which burns only where it touches. It also matters that a raft is being burned by someone who needs it to survive.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","sutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இனம் means fellow renunciants who teach, the raft that crosses the sea of births to release.","முற்றத் துறந்து தவஞானங்களால் பெரியயராய்க் கேட்டார்க்கு உறுதி பயக்கும் மொழிகளை இனியவாகச் சொல்லுவாரை."],["manakkudavar","இனம் means one's friends, who bring one ashore from a sea of sorrow.","தன்னைக் கரையேற விடுகின்ற நட்டோராகிய புணையைச் சுடும்."],["kalaignar","இனம் means one's kin.","அவரைப் பாதுகாக்கும் தோணி போன்ற சுற்றத்தையும் அழித்துவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:307":{"book":"kural","num":307,"t":["சினத்தைப் பொருளென்று கொண்டவன் கேடு","நிலத்தறைந்தான் கைபிழையா தற்று"],"p":"Destruction will come upon him who ragards anger as a good thing, as surely as the hand of him who strikes the ground will not fail.","r":"The ruin of one who takes anger to be a thing of worth is like the hand of one who strikes the ground, which can't miss.","n":"பொருள் also varies: Parimelazhagar makes it a quality that displays one's power, citing Vaisesika usage, and Manakkudavar seems to take it as wealth when he speaks of loss of goods. The image is a fine one because the ground is untouched by the blow.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image is about certainty: the ruin can no more miss than a hand can miss the ground.","நிலத்தின்கண் அறைந்த அவன் கை அந்நிலத்தையுறுத்தல் தப்பாதவாறு போலத் தப்பாது."],["kalaignar","The image is about rebound: it is the striker's own hand that hurts.","நிலத்தைக் கையால் அறைந்தவனுக்கு அவன் கைதான் வலிக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:308":{"book":"kural","num":308,"t":["இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன இன்னா செயினும்","புணரின் வெகுளாமை நன்று"],"p":"Though one commit things against you as painful (to bear) as if a bundle of fire had been thrust upon you, it will be well, to refrain, if possible, from anger.","r":"Even if harm is done to you like the touch of massed flames, not being angry, if you can manage it, is good.","n":"புணரின், 'if it can be managed', is a rare concession in such a chapter. Parimelazhagar notes that such treatment would anger even sages. The commandment admits its own difficulty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vekuli","samanilai","innaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:309":{"book":"kural","num":309,"t":["உள்ளிய தெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும் உள்ளத்தால்","உள்ளான் வெகுளி எனின்"],"p":"If a man never indulges anger in his heart, he will at once obtain whatever he has thought of.","r":"All one has thought of comes together at once, if one doesn't think of anger in the heart.","n":"The root உள் runs through the whole verse: thought, heart, not thinking. It sets one kind of thinking against another. The promise is enormous, and Parimelazhagar assigns it to the ascetic, though the verse itself doesn't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:31"},"k:310":{"book":"kural","num":310,"t":["இறந்தார் இறந்தார் அனையர் சினத்தைத்","துறந்தார் துறந்தார் துணை"],"p":"Those, who give way to excessive anger, are no better than dead men; but those, who are freed from it, are equal to those who are freed (from death).","r":"Those who go to excess in anger are like the dead; those who renounce anger are equal to those who have renounced.","n":"Wordplay carries the argument: இறந்தார் is both 'those who exceed' and 'those who have died'. துறந்தார் doubles in the same way, so a single renunciation is set equal to total renunciation.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They equal those who have attained release, since knowledge is certain for them though they still have a mortal body.","'வீடு பெற்றாரோடு ஒப்பர்'"],["manakkudavar","They equal those who have given up all possessions.","அதனை யொழிந் தார் எல்லாப் பொருளையுந் துறந்தாரோடு ஒப்பர்,"]],"ch":"k:31"},"k:311":{"book":"kural","num":311,"t":["சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும் பிறர்க்கு இன்னா","செய்யாமை மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the determination of the spotless not to cause sorrow to others, although they could (by so causing) obtain the wealth which confers greatness.","r":"Even if it would bring wealth that gives eminence, not hurting others is the settled stand of the stainless.","n":"கோள் is a stance held, not a rule obeyed. The verse puts the temptation first and at its strongest, then refuses it in a word.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The prize is the yogic powers such as becoming minute, so the temptation is an ascetic one.","யோகமாகிய சிறப்புத் தரும் அணிமா முதலிய செல்வங்களைப்"],["manakkudavar","The prize is ordinary wealth, refused even when it would bring no blame.","இது பழி வாராத செல்வம் பெறினும் தவிர வேண்டு மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:312":{"book":"kural","num":312,"t":["கறுத்துஇன்னா செய்தவக் கண்ணும் மறுத்தின்னா","செய்யாமை மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the determination of the spotless not to do evil, even in return, to those who have cherished enmity and done them evil.","r":"Even when someone in hatred has done them hurt, not hurting in return is the settled stand of the stainless.","n":"Manakkudavar supplies a detail that alters the verse's pairing with 313: here the other person's anger was aroused by one's own fault, whereas 313 treats enmity with no cause. On his view, the two verses together cover both provoked and unprovoked enemies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["innaa","samanilai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","The attacker has been angered by a fault of one's own.","தாஞ்செய்த குற்றத்தினாலே வெகுண்டு,"]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:313":{"book":"kural","num":313,"t":["செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும் இன்னாத செய்தபின்","உய்யா விழுமந் தரும்"],"p":"In an ascetic inflict suffering even on those who hate him, when he has not done them any evil, it will afterwards give him irretrievable sorrow.","r":"Doing hurt, even to those who hated you though you did nothing, brings sorrow there is no escaping.","n":"The verse chooses the case where retaliation looks most justified, an unprovoked enemy, and says it still rebounds. Parimelazhagar explains the sorrow by an ascetic's loss of austerity and takes the actor to be a renunciant, as Pope's gloss follows him in doing, but the verse doesn't name one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:314":{"book":"kural","num":314,"t":["இன்னாசெய் தாரை ஒறுத்தல் அவர்நாண","நன்னயஞ் செய்து விடல்"],"p":"The (proper) punishment to those who have done evil (to you), is to put them to shame by showing them kindness, in return and to forget both the evil and the good done on both sides.","r":"The way to punish those who have hurt you is to shame them by doing them good, and then let it go.","n":"ஒறுத்தல், to punish, is kept: retribution isn't abolished but redefined, and shame remains its instrument, so this isn't quite unconditional love. விடல் adds that one should leave the matter there, without savouring the effect.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","nayan","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:315":{"book":"kural","num":315,"t":["அறிவினான் ஆகுவ துண்டோ பிறிதின்நோய்","தந்நோய்போல் போற்றாக் கடை"],"p":"What benefit has he derived from his knowledge, who does not endeavour to keep off pain from another as much as from himself ?","r":"What comes of understanding, if one doesn't guard against another's pain as one's own?","n":"பிறிதின், 'of another', is neuter and so can reach beyond humans. போற்று is active guarding, so understanding that doesn't produce care is judged to have no result at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","innaa","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:316":{"book":"kural","num":316,"t":["இன்னா எனத்தான் உணர்ந்தவை துன்னாமை","வேண்டும் பிறன்கண் செயல்"],"p":"Let not a man consent to do those things to another which, he knows, will cause sorrow.","r":"What one has perceived to be painful, one must not come near doing to another.","n":"A negative golden rule, but with உணர்ந்தவை, what has been felt or perceived, as the basis rather than a principle. Pope's couplet says 'his own soul has felt', and Kalaignar too says known through experience, while Manakkudavar only says known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:317":{"book":"kural","num":317,"t":["எனைத்தானும் எஞ்ஞான்றும் யார்க்கும் மனத்தானாம்","மாணாசெய் யாமை தலை"],"p":"It is the chief of all virtues not knowingly to do any person evil, even in the lowest degree, and at any time.","r":"Not doing ill of any degree, at any time, to anyone, with the mind, is the highest thing.","n":"Three universals pile up before the verse arrives at மனத்தானாம், which pulls in two directions. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar extend the ban to harm even in thought, while Pope's 'wilful' and 'knowingly' limit it to harm that is intended.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:318":{"book":"kural","num":318,"t":["தன்னுயிர்ககு ஏன்னாமை தானறிவான் என்கொலோ","மன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னா செயல்"],"p":"Why does a man inflict upon other creatures those sufferings, which he has found by experience are sufferings to himself ?","r":"Why does one who knows what is hurtful to his own life do hurtful things to other living beings?","n":"This is posed as a real question and isn't answered. Manakkudavar's touch is that a person differs from unconscious objects in knowing his pain, which is exactly why this conduct is puzzling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["innaa","arul","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:319":{"book":"kural","num":319,"t":["பிறர்க்கின்னா முற்பகல் செய்யின் தமக்கு இன்னா","பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்"],"p":"If a man inflict sorrow upon others in the morning, it will come upon him unsought in the very evening.","r":"If you do hurt to others in the forenoon, hurts to you will come in the afternoon, of themselves.","n":"தாமே, 'of themselves', removes any agent: as Manakkudavar says, nobody else need do it. The speed is rhetorical, since the verse is not a report on timing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:32"},"k:320":{"book":"kural","num":320,"t":["நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம் நோய்செய்யார்","நோயின்மை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Sorrow will come upon those who cause pain to others; therfore those, who desire to be free from sorrow, give no pain to others.","r":"All pains rest on those who caused pain; those who want to be without pain cause none.","n":"நோய் occurs four times. Taken strictly, 'all pains' has a hard consequence: anyone who suffers must have caused suffering. Manakkudavar accepts it by a time scheme in which present pain derives from past harm, and in that way this chapter leads into the claim of 330.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","Present suffering comes from past harm, and harming no one now protects the future.","இக் காலத்து நுகர்கின்ற துன்பமெல்லாம் முற்காலத்துப் பிறர்க் குத் துன்பம் செய்தார் மாட்டே யுளவாம் ;"]],"ch":"k:32"},"k:321":{"book":"kural","num":321,"t":["அறவினை யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்","பிறவினை எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"Never to destroy life is the sum of all virtuous conduct. The destruction of life leads to every evil.","r":"If you ask what virtuous action is, it is not killing; killing brings all the other kind of deed.","n":"பிறவினை, 'other deeds', leaves the wrongness to be inferred. Parimelazhagar takes it as a claim about results: the suffering killing yields exceeds that of all other sins combined.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:322":{"book":"kural","num":322,"t":["பகுத்துண்டு பல்லுயிர் ஓம்புதல் நூலோர்","தொகுத்தவற்றுள் எல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"The chief of all (the virtues) which authors have summed up, is the partaking of food that has been shared with others, and the preservation of the mainfold life of other creatures.","r":"Sharing one's food and sustaining many lives is the chief of all that the authors have gathered.","n":"Inside a chapter on not killing, the crown goes to a positive act of feeding, so non-harm here isn't only refraining. Parimelazhagar assigns the summary to renunciants, Manakkudavar says it is good for all faiths, and Kalaignar reads an egalitarian creed into it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:323":{"book":"kural","num":323,"t":["ஒன்றாக நல்லது கொல்லாமை மற்றதன்","பின்சாரப் பொய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Not to destroy life is an incomparably (great) good next to it in goodness ranks freedom from falsehood.","r":"Good beyond compare is not killing; following behind it, not lying is good.","n":"A rare moment of the Kural ranking two of its own highest claims. Parimelazhagar recognizes this: since 297 and 300 raised doubt about which is greater, this verse settles it, and it can do so because truth depends on results and therefore shifts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse exists to settle a doubt that the chapter on truth raised.","இரண்டு அறந்துள்ளும் யாது சிறந்தது என்று ஐயம் நிகழுமன்றே!"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:324":{"book":"kural","num":324,"t":["நல்லாறு எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும்","கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி"],"p":"Good path is that which considers how it may avoid killing any creature.","r":"If you ask what is called the good path, it is the way that keeps in view not killing anything at all.","n":"The question form follows 291 and 321, as if the Kural is defining terms. யாதொன்றும் is wide, and Parimelazhagar says it takes in even one-sensed life. சூழும் makes it a matter of constant attention rather than a single vow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:325":{"book":"kural","num":325,"t":["நிலைஅஞ்சி நீத்தாருள் எல்லாம் கொலைஅஞ்சிக்","கொல்லாமை சூழ்வான் தலை"],"p":"Of all those who, fearing the permanence of earthly births, have abandoned desire, he is the chief who, fearing (the guilt of) murder, considers how he may avoid the destruction of life.","r":"Among all who renounced out of fear of their condition, the one who fears killing and keeps not killing in view is chief.","n":"Two fears are set side by side with the repeated அஞ்சி, one about one's own state and the other about another's life. The second outranks the first.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","thuravu","acham"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He is the highest within the class of renunciants, who differ by sect.","கொலைப் பாவத்தை அஞ்சிக் கொல்லாமை ஆகிய அறத்தை மறவாதவன் உயர்ந்தவன்."],["manakkudavar","He may stay a householder and still be above all who renounce.","கொல்லாமையைச் சிந்திப்பான் தலைமை யுடையவன்; இல் வாழ்க்கையில் நிற்பினும்,"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:326":{"book":"kural","num":326,"t":["கொல்லாமை மேற்கொண் டொழுகுவான் வாழ்நாள்மேல்","செல்லாது உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று"],"p":"Yama, the destroyer of life, will not attack the life of him, who acts under the determination of never destroying life.","r":"Death, the devourer of life, does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing.","n":"The symmetry is exact: the one who won't kill is not killed, and the killer of all refrains here.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It promises a full allotted span without interruption, which gives time for knowledge and release.","இப் பேரறம்செய்தான் தானும் கொல்லப்படான்; படானாகவே, அடியிற்கட்டிய வாழ்நாள் இடையூறின்றி எய்தும்"],["manakkudavar","Death doesn't come because there will be no further birth.","பிறவாமை யுண்டாமாதலால் கூற்றுச் செல்லாது என்றார்."],["kalaignar","It is a tribute: death itself hesitates, in awe.","சாவுகூட அவர் உயிரைப் பறிக்கத் தயங்கி நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:327":{"book":"kural","num":327,"t":["தன்னுயிர் நீப்பினும் செய்யற்க தான்பிறிது","இன்னுயிர் நீக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Let no one do that which would destroy the life of another, although he should by so doing, lose his own life.","r":"Even if your own life should go, do not do the deed that takes away another's sweet life.","n":"இன்னுயிர் gives the other's life the word for sweetness, while one's own is just உயிர். This is the Kural's hardest statement of the rule, and it allows no exception of necessity.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The case is a creature that would kill you: even then, because killing increases sin. He rejects a reading about ritual expiation.","'தன்னை அது கொல்லினும் தான் அதனைக் கொல்லற்க'"],["manakkudavar","The case is killing for medicine in mortal illness, which some people hold is no fault.","உயிர்க்குக் கேடுவருங் காலத்து நோய்க்கு மருந்தாகக் கொல்லுதல் குற்ற மன்று என் பார்க்கு, இது கூறப்பட்டது."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:328":{"book":"kural","num":328,"t":["நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும் சான்றோர்க் குக்","கொன்றாகும் ஆக்கங் கடை"],"p":"The advantage which might flow from destroying life in sacrifice, is dishonourable to the wise (who renounced the world), even although it should be said to be productive of great good.","r":"However great the gain that would bring good, gain that comes by killing is the lowest for the worthy.","n":"The verse mentions no sacrifice. That whole issue comes from the older commentators and through them Pope's gloss.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is Vedic sacrifice, valid for householders seeking heaven and base only for those seeking release, so injunction and prohibition don't clash.","துறக்கம் எய்துவார்க்கு ஆம் ஆயினும், வீடு எய்துவார்க்கு ஆகாது"],["manakkudavar","Killing in sacrifice is also not acceptable, as the great seek release and let ritual go.","வேள்வி யின் வரும் கொலையும் ஆகாதென்றது."],["kalaignar","It is any killing that would produce great benefit, and sacrifice isn't mentioned.","பெரிதாக நன்மை தரக்கூடிய அளவுக்கு ஒரு கொலை பயன்படக் கூடுமெனினும்,"]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:329":{"book":"kural","num":329,"t":["கொலைவினைய ராகிய மாக்கள் புலைவினையர்","புன்மை தெரிவா ரகத்து"],"p":"Men who destroy life are base men, in the estimation of those who know the nature of meanness.","r":"People whose work is killing are of base occupation in the eyes of those who discern what baseness is.","n":"Baseness is assigned by work, not birth, as both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar stress, though the word புலை itself carries caste weight. Parimelazhagar's claim here that sacrifice isn't killing at all fits awkwardly with his note on 328.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kollaamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The phrase 'whose work is killing' shows that killing in sacrifice isn't killing.","'கொலை வினையர்' என்றதனான், வேள்விக் கண் கொலையன்மை அறிக."]],"ch":"k:33"},"k:330":{"book":"kural","num":330,"t":["உயிர் உடம்பின் நீக்கியார் என்ப செயிர் உடம்பின்","செல்லாத்தீ வாழ்க்கை யவர்"],"p":"(The wise) will say that men of diseased bodies, who live in degradation and in poverty, are those who separated the life from the body of animals (in a former birth).","r":"Those in wretched bodies, leading an evil and failing life, they say, are people who separated life from body.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', is the verse's own hedge. It doesn't mention a former birth, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar supply. Its logic runs backward from a sufferer to his guilt, and that makes it one of the more troubling claims in this section.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kollaamai","vinai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:33"},"k:331":{"book":"kural","num":331,"t":["நில்லாத வற்றை நிலையின என்றுணரும்","புல்லறி வாண்மை கடை"],"p":"That ignorance which considers those things to be stable which are not so, is dishonourable (to the wise).","r":"The paltry understanding that takes what doesn't last for what lasts is the lowest.","n":"The fault is cognitive before it is moral: a mistake of knowledge, sarcastically dignified with ஆண்மை, mastery. Parimelazhagar limits 'lowest' to those seeking release, for whom this error feeds attachment, and the verse doesn't state that limit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:332":{"book":"kural","num":332,"t":["கூத்தாட்டு அவைக் குழாத் தற்றே பெருஞ்செல்வம்","போக்கும் அதுவிளிந் தற்று"],"p":"The acquisition of wealth is like the gathering together of an assembly for a theatre; its expenditure is like the breaking up of that assembly.","r":"The coming of great wealth is like a crowd gathering at a dance hall, and its going is like that crowd breaking up.","n":"A crowd has its own reason for being there, and Parimelazhagar presses this: wealth comes because of good deeds, of its own accord, and leaves when that cause is spent. So the owner is no more than the hall. His addition that this includes heavenly wealth is his own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:333":{"book":"kural","num":333,"t":["அற்கா இயல்பிற்றுச் செல்வம் அதுபெற்றால்","அற்குப ஆங்கே செயல்"],"p":"Wealth is perishable; let those who obtain it immediately practise those (virtues) which are imperishable.","r":"Wealth has the nature of not staying; if you get it, do straight away what stays.","n":"அற்கா and அற்குப play on one root, so what doesn't stay can be converted into what does. For Parimelazhagar 'what stays' means worship and giving without expecting fruit, leading to release, though the verse doesn't name them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:334":{"book":"kural","num":334,"t":["நாளென ஒன்றுபோற் காட்டி உயிர் ஈரும்","வாளது உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"Time, which shows itself (to the ignorant) as if it were something (real) is in the estimation of the wise (only) a saw which cuts down life.","r":"It shows itself as a thing called a day, but it is a blade that saws through life, if one can find those who perceive it.","n":"Parimelazhagar: the fool rejoices that time is passing, and doesn't realize it is his own time. He grammatically dismisses a rival division of the words at length. உணர்வார்ப் பெறின் admits how few will ever see it this way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:335":{"book":"kural","num":335,"t":["நாச்செற்று விக்குள்மேல் வாராமுன் நல்வினை","மேற்சென்று செய்யப் படும்"],"p":"Let virtuous deeds be done quickly, before the biccup comes making the tongue silent.","r":"Before the hiccup rises and stops the tongue, good deeds must be rushed to.","n":"The image is the plain physical detail of the death rattle, with no metaphor. Parimelazhagar notes that it is the tongue being stopped, meaning that one can't even instruct others to act, and that no one knows when it will come.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:336":{"book":"kural","num":336,"t":["நெருநல் உளனொருவன் இன்றில்லை என்னும்","பெருமை உடைத்துஇவ் வுலகு"],"p":"This world possesses the greatness that one who yesterday was is not today.","r":"This world has the greatness that one who was here yesterday isn't here today.","n":"பெருமை is bitter here. Parimelazhagar takes it as excess, an overflow of impermanence, while Manakkudavar simply repeats 'greatness'. Kalaignar makes it the world's arrogance, boasting of having killed. All three hear the irony, in different keys.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:34"},"k:337":{"book":"kural","num":337,"t":["ஒருபொழுதும் வாழ்வது அறியார் கருதுப","கோடியும் அல்ல பல"],"p":"Innumerable are the thoughts which occupy the mind of (the unwise), who know not that they shall live another moment.","r":"Those who can't know they will live one moment more have thoughts in the many crores.","n":"The disproportion, a moment against crores, is the joke. Parimelazhagar gives a delightful catalogue of what such people plan: acquiring, guarding, overcoming, and raising friends and ruining rivals.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The crores are thoughts and schemes.","கோடியளவும் அன்றி அதனினும் பலவாய நினைவுகளை நினையா நிற்பர் அறிவிலாதார்."],["manakkudavar","The crores are what they assume their lifespan to be.","தமது வாழ்நாளைக் கோடியுமல்ல, பலவாகக்கருதுவர் உலகத்தார்,"]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:338":{"book":"kural","num":338,"t":["குடம்பை தனித்து ஒழியப் புள்பறந் தற்றே","உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு"],"p":"The love of the soul to the body is like (the love of) a bird to its egg which it flies away from and leaves empty.","r":"The friendship between body and life is like a bird flying off and leaving its குடம்பை alone.","n":"நட்பு, friendship, is heavily ironic: for Parimelazhagar there is none, only a union forced by deeds.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குடம்பை is the egg, since a bird grows within it and never returns, and he explicitly rejects 'nest'.","முன் தனியாத முட்டை தனித்துக் கிடப்ப அதனுள் இருந்த புள்ளுப் பருவம் வந்துழிப் பறந்து போன தன்மைத்து,"],["manakkudavar","குடம்பை is the nest, and the point is that life leaves whenever it will.","கூடு தனியே கிடக்கப் புள்ளுப் பறந்து போனாற்போலும்;"],["kalaignar","It is the egg and the chick.","உடலுக்கும் உயிருக்கும் உள்ள உறவு முட்டைக்கும் பறவைக் குஞ்சுக்கும் உண்டான உறவு போன்றதுதான்."]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:339":{"book":"kural","num":339,"t":["உறங்கு வதுபோலுஞ் சாக்காடு உறங்கி","விழிப்பது போலும் பிறப்பு"],"p":"Death is like sleep; birth is like awaking from it.","r":"Death is like falling asleep, and birth is like waking after sleep.","n":"The verse is very short and calm, with nothing about what happens in between. Its tone can be taken as reassuring or as a further turn of the screw. Parimelazhagar says birth is included to show that impermanence is itself what is permanent.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Death and birth alternate naturally and quickly, as sleep and waking do.","சாக்காடும் பிறப்பும் இயல்பாய்க் கடிதின் மாறிமாறி வரும்"],["manakkudavar","It is a statement of rebirth: the life that went is born again.","போன உயிர் மீண்டும் பிறக்கு மென்பதூஉம்,"],["kalaignar","There is no cycle: birth is the waking and death is a sleep with no waking.","திரும்ப விழிக்க முடியாத மீளா உறக்கம் கொள்வதே இறப்பு."]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:340":{"book":"kural","num":340,"t":["புக்கில் அமைந்தின்று கொல்லோ உடம்பினுள்","துச்சில் இருந்த உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"It seems as if the soul, which takes a temporary shelter in a body, had not attained a home.","r":"Has the life that sits as a lodger in the body not found a home of its own?","n":"துச்சில், a lodger's corner in another's house, is homely and sad, and the verse is a soft question rather than a declaration.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","No body has ever been a permanent home to it, given how life comes and goes among bodies.","எஞ்ஞான்றும் இருப்பதோர் இல் இதுகாறும் அமைந்ததில்லை போலும்!"],["manakkudavar","The home is specifically the state of release.","புக்கில் என்பது முத்தி ஸ்தானம்."],["kalaignar","Once it leaves the body, life has no other shelter, which comes close to the reverse.","அதனைப் பிரிந்தால் வேறு புகலிடம் கிடையாது."]],"ch":"k:34"},"k:341":{"book":"kural","num":341,"t":["யாதனின் யாதனின் நீங்கியான் நோதல்","அதனின் அதனின் இலன்"],"p":"Whatever thing, a man has renounced, by that thing; he cannot suffer pain.","r":"From whatever, whatever one has left, from that, from that, one has no pain.","n":"The doubled words make this piecemeal, and Parimelazhagar accepts that: giving up everything at once is best, but each thing left removes its own pain. So renunciation isn't all or nothing in this verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:342":{"book":"kural","num":342,"t":["வேண்டின் உண் டாகத் துறக்க துறந்தபின்","ஈண்டுஇயற் பால பல"],"p":"After a man has renounced (all things), there will still be many things in this world (which he may enjoy); if he should desire them, let him, while it is time abandon. (the world).","r":"If you want it, renounce while there is time; after renouncing, there are many things that come about here.","n":"The verse is elliptical. Parimelazhagar concedes that he has supplied both 'joys' and 'time', so much of what appears to be said is commentary. Its surprise, on his reading, is that renunciation brings joys in this life and not only the end of pain. It also starts with வேண்டின், 'if you want'.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Renounce in time, while young, so as to have these joys for longer.","அவ் இன்பங்களை வேண்டின், அவற்றைக் காலம் பெறத் துறக்க."],["manakkudavar","Renounce if you want gain for your own soul.","தன்னுயிர்க்கு ஆக்கம் உண்டாக வேண்டின்,"],["kalaignar","Renounce while everything is still there to give up.","எல்லாம் இருக்கும் போதே அவற்றைத் துறந்து விடுவானே யானால்,"]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:343":{"book":"kural","num":343,"t":["அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை விடல்வேண்டும்","வேண்டிய வெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"Let the five senses be destroyed; and at the same time, let everything be abandoned that (the ascetic) has (formerly) desired.","r":"The domain of the five must be crushed, and all that one has wanted must be let go at once.","n":"புலம் is the field of the senses, their enjoyment, and not the organs that Pope's gloss has destroyed. Parimelazhagar gives the reason for 'at once': sense enjoyment fed with objects only burns higher, like fire with fuel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:344":{"book":"kural","num":344,"t":["இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கொன்று இன்மை உடைமை","மயலாகும் மற்றும் பெயர்த்து"],"p":"To be altogether destitute is the proper condition of those who perform austerities; if they possess anything, it will change (their resolution) and bring them back to their confused state.","r":"Having nothing at all is natural to austerity; having anything brings back confusion all over again.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reading is psychological: keep one thing and everything given up returns through it. Manakkudavar links the confusion to further birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","thavam","pattru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:345":{"book":"kural","num":345,"t":["மற்றும் தொடர்ப்பாடு எவன்கொல் பிறப்பறுக்கல்","உற்றார்க்கு உடம்பும் மிகை"],"p":"What means the addition of other things those who are attempting to cut off (future) births, when even their body is too much (for them).","r":"For those set on cutting off births, even the body is excess; why then any further ties?","n":"The body is the one tool needed for the work and still counts as excess. Parimelazhagar divides it into gross and subtle bodies, a doctrine the verse doesn't state, and makes this the renunciation of the inner 'I'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:346":{"book":"kural","num":346,"t":["யான் எனது என்னும் செருக்கு அறுப்பான் வானோர்க்கு","உயர்ந்த உலகம் புகும்"],"p":"He who destroys the pride which says \"I\", \"mine\" will enter a world which is difficult even to the Gods to attain.","r":"One who cuts off the pride of saying 'I' and 'mine' enters a world higher than the gods'.","n":"செருக்கு is glossed as delusion by the older commentators, as Parimelazhagar also does at 201: for them, the problem is a false identification and not arrogant manners. The body isn't 'I' and goods aren't 'mine'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is the world of release, beyond even the gods' reach.","வானோர்க்கும் எய்தற்கு அரிய வீட்டுலகத்தை எய்தும்."],["kalaignar","This is earthly fame greater than heaven-high fame.","வான்புகழையும் மிஞ்சுகின்ற உலகப் புகழுக்கு உரியவனாவான்."]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:347":{"book":"kural","num":347,"t":["பற்றி விடாஅ இடும்பைகள் பற்றினைப்","பற்றி விடாஅ தவர் க்கு"],"p":"Sorrows will never let go their hold of those who give not up their hold of desire.","r":"Griefs that hold and won't let go hold those who hold attachment and won't let go.","n":"The two lines use one verb for the person and the griefs, so that the grip one keeps and the grip one suffers are shown to be the same act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:348":{"book":"kural","num":348,"t":["தலைப்பட்டார் தீரத் துறந்தார் மயங்கி","வலைப்பட்டார் மற்றை யவர்"],"p":"Those who have entirely renounced (all things and all desire) have obtained (absorption into God); all others wander in confusion, entangled in the net of (many) births.","r":"Those who have thoroughly renounced have got to the head; the rest, in confusion, are in the net.","n":"தலைப்பட்டார் doesn't say what has been reached, and the past tense suggests it is already done. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar say release, Kalaignar says a high state, and Pope's gloss has 'absorption into God', which adds a theology the Tamil doesn't have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pattru","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:349":{"book":"kural","num":349,"t":["பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும் மற்று","நிலையாமை காணப் படும்"],"p":"At the moment in which desire has been abandoned, (other) births will be cut off; when that has not been done, instability will be seen.","r":"The moment attachment ends it cuts off births; otherwise impermanence is what is seen.","n":"கண்ணே, 'at the very moment', makes release immediate, as an effect stops with its cause. Parimelazhagar takes மற்று as 'if not', and Pope's couplet and gloss diverge on it, the couplet making impermanence what is seen after the release, the gloss what is seen without it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:35"},"k:350":{"book":"kural","num":350,"t":["பற்றுக பற்றற்றான் பற்றினை அப்பற்றைப்","பற்றுக பற்று விடற்கு"],"p":"Desire the desire of Him who is without desire; in order to renounce desire, desire that desire.","r":"Cling to the clinging of the one who has no clinging; cling to that clinging, in order to let clinging go.","n":"பற்று comes six times in seven words, and the chapter on letting go ends by commanding a holding on. A useful attachment is the ladder that lets one drop the others.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The unattached one is the Lord, and what is held is the path to release that he taught.","எல்லாப் பொருளையும் பற்றி நின்றே பற்றற்ற இறைவன் ஓதிய வீட்டு நெறியை,"],["manakkudavar","What is held is a practice: meditative absorption.","பற்றற்றான் பற்றாவது தியான சமாதி."],["kalaignar","The unattached one is any person so unattached, and it is him one should hold to.","எதிலும் பற்றில்லாதவராக யார் இருக்கிறாரோ அவரிடம் மட்டும் பற்றுக் கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:35"},"k:351":{"book":"kural","num":351,"t":["பொருளல்ல வற்றைப் பொருளென்று உணரும்","மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு"],"p":"Inglorious births are produced by the confusion (of mind) which considers those things to be real which are not real.","r":"A joyless birth comes from the confusion that perceives what isn't real as real.","n":"The verse doesn't say which things are unreal. Parimelazhagar fills this in as denying rebirth, the fruit of deeds, and God, adding the stump mistaken for a man and shell for silver. Kalaignar has no birth in view, only a life that goes poorly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The false view is to deny rebirth, the fruit of deeds, and God.","அவ் விபரீத உணர்வாவது, மறுபிறப்பும், இருவினைப் பயனும், கடவுளும் இல்லை எனவும்,"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:352":{"book":"kural","num":352,"t":["இருள்நீங்கி இன்பம் பயக்கும் மருள்நீங்கி","மாசறு காட்சி யவர்க்கு"],"p":"A clear, undimmed vision of things will deliver its possessors from the darkness of future births, and confer the felicity (of heaven).","r":"For those of stainless sight, who have left confusion behind, it removes darkness and brings joy.","n":"What is the darkness? Parimelazhagar says hell, Manakkudavar and Kalaignar say ignorance, and Pope's gloss says the darkness of future births. The verse leaves it as a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:36"},"k:353":{"book":"kural","num":353,"t":["ஐயத்தின் நீங்கித் தெளிந்தார்க்கு வையத்தின்","வானம் நணிய துடைத்து"],"p":"Heaven is nearer than earth to those men of purified minds who are freed from from doubt.","r":"For those who have left doubt and become clear, the sky is nearer than the earth they stand on.","n":"Doubt here is the obstacle in its own right. Parimelazhagar makes an unusually candid point: every school's texts refute the others, so which is true can only be settled through one's own experience. The image of the far as nearer than the near is very bold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:36"},"k:354":{"book":"kural","num":354,"t":["ஐயுணர்வு எய்தியக் கண்ணும் பயமின்றே","மெய்யுணர்வு இல்லா தவர்க்கு"],"p":"Even those who have all the knowledge which can be attained by the five senses, will derive no benefit from it, if they are without a knowledge of the true nature of things.","r":"Even having gained the five-fold awareness, there is no benefit for those who lack true perception.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The five-fold awareness is the mind itself, gathered into concentration, and even this falls short.","ஐந்தாகிய உணர்வு: மனம்."],["manakkudavar","It is all that can be known by the five senses.","மெய் முதலாகிய பொறிகளைந்தினானும் அறியப்படுவனவெல்லாம் அறிந்தவிடத்தும்,"],["kalaignar","It is having subdued the five senses.","தமது ஐம்புலன்களையும் அடக்கி வெற்றி கண்டிருந்தாலும் கூட"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:355":{"book":"kural","num":355,"t":["எப்பொருள் எத்தன்மைத் தாயினும் அப்பொருள்","மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு"],"p":"(True) knowledge is the perception concerning every thing of whatever kind, that that thing is the true thing.","r":"Whatever a thing is and of whatever nature, seeing the true thing in that thing is understanding.","n":"One of the Kural's best-known definitions, and it is doctrinally open. Parimelazhagar removes a Chera king's rank, lineage, looks and names as conventions, down to the elements and beyond, while Kalaignar asks only that we not be fooled by appearances. It is both a metaphysical and a practical line.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","One discards the world's imputed conceptions and sees what remains.","பொருள் தோறும் உலகத்தார் கற்பித்துக் கொண்டு வழங்குகின்ற கற்பனைகளைக் கழித்து, நின்ற உண்மையைக் காண்பது"],["kalaignar","It is about not being taken in by outward looks.","வெளித்தோற்றத்தைப் பார்த்து ஏமாந்து விடாமல்,"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:356":{"book":"kural","num":356,"t":["கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார் தலைப்படுவர்","மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி"],"p":"They, who in this birth have learned to know the True Being, enter the road which returns not into this world.","r":"Those who have learned here and seen the true thing will reach the path of not coming back here.","n":"Here learning is an actual route to the truth, in contrast to 373. For Parimelazhagar this is hearing from an experienced teacher, the first of his three means.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Not coming back' means no return to birth.","மீண்டு இப்பிறப்பின்கண் வாராத நெறியை எய்துவர்."],["kalaignar","It means no return to the household life from renunciation.","மீண்டும் இல்லற வாழ்க்கையை விரும்ப மாட்டார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:357":{"book":"kural","num":357,"t":["ஓர்த்துள்ளம் உள்ளது உணரான் ஒருதலையாப்","பேர்த்துள்ள வேண்டா பிறப்பு"],"p":"Let it not be thought that there is another birth for him whose mind having thoroughly considered (all it has been taught) has known the True Being.","r":"If the mind ponders and knows with certainty what is, one needn't think again of birth.","n":"உள்ளது, 'what is', is the plainest of the chapter's names for the goal. This reading assumes the commentators' உணரின் over the verse text's உணரான், as noted in source_issues.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","There is no need to expect a further birth for such a person.","அவனுக்கு மாறிப் பிறப்புளதாக நினைக்க வேண்டா."],["kalaignar","Those who truly investigate won't believe in rebirth at all, which reverses the claim into a denial of the doctrine.","உண்மையை ஆராய்ந்து உறுதியாக உணர்பவர்கள் மீண்டும் பிறப்பு உண்டு எனக் கருத மாட்டார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:358":{"book":"kural","num":358,"t":["பிறப்பென்னும் பேதைமை நீங்கச் சிறப்பென்னும்","செம்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு"],"p":"True knowledge consists in the removal of ignorance; which is (the cause of) births, and the perception of the True Being who is (the bestower of) heaven.","r":"Understanding is seeing the perfect thing called the excellent, so that the folly called birth departs.","n":"The verse calls birth itself a folly, an astonishing metaphor that all three commentators have to unpack, each differently. Pope's 'man's dignity' for சிறப்பு goes in a different direction from all of them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","piravi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means the ignorance that is the first cause of birth.","பிறப்பிற்கு முதற்காரணமாய அவிச்சை கெட;"],["manakkudavar","The folly is thinking that oneself is born and dies, when one's real standing has neither.","தான் பிறந்தானாக வும் செத்தானாகவும் கருதுகின்ற அறியாமையை விட்டுத்,"],["kalaignar","The ignorance is the very belief in a next birth.","அடுத்த பிறப்பு எனக் கூறப்படும் அறியாமையைப் போக்கித்"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:359":{"book":"kural","num":359,"t":["சார்புணர்ந்து சார்பு கெடஒழுகின் மற்றழித்துச்","சார்தரா சார்தரு நோய்"],"p":"He who so lives as to know Him who is the support of all things and abandon all desire, will be freed from the evils which would otherwise cleave to him and destroy (his efforts after absorption).","r":"If one knows the support and lives so that supports perish, the pains due to cling won't destroy that life and cling.","n":"One word, சார்பு, does double service. Parimelazhagar adds that the fruit even of good deeds counts as 'pain' here, because it causes birth, which is a hard saying.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","vinai","pattru"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The support to be known is the supreme reality on which all rests, and the supports to end are the attachments.","எல்லாப் பொருட்கும் சார்பாய அச்செம்பொருளை உணர்ந்து,"],["manakkudavar","The support is what attaches to oneself, the dependence on deeds.","சார்பு - வினைச் சார்பு;"],["kalaignar","It simply means finding out the causes of one's troubles and letting go of them.","அத்துன்பங்களுக்குக் காரணமானவற்றை உணர்ந்து"]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:360":{"book":"kural","num":360,"t":["காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் இநவ்முன்றன்","நாமம் கெடக்கெடும் நோய்"],"p":"If the very names of these three things, desire, anger, and confusion of mind, be destroyed, then will also perish evils (which flow from them).","r":"When even the names of the three, desire, anger and confusion, perish, pain perishes.","n":"நாமம், 'the name', is what must go, so they aren't merely suppressed. This triad resembles the three poisons in Buddhist teaching, a comparison that is mine and no commentator's. Parimelazhagar derives it by reducing a Sanskrit list of five faults.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["meyyunarvu","kaamam","vekuli"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The Sanskrit list of five faults reduces to these three, since ego falls under ignorance and craving under desire.","இவர் அவற்றுள் அகங்காரம் அவிச்சைக்கண்ணும் அவாவுதல் ஆசைக்கண்ணும் அடங்குதலான், 'மூன்று' என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:36"},"k:361":{"book":"kural","num":361,"t":["அவாஎன்ப எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் எஞ் ஞான்றும்","தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து"],"p":"(The wise) say that the seed, which produces unceasing births, at all times, to all creatures, is desire.","r":"They say craving is the seed that, for all beings at all times, produces unceasing birth.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', is read by Parimelazhagar as marking agreement among all schools. He supplies a mechanism: at death, craving carries the life to its next state. Kalaignar keeps the seed but removes birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:362":{"book":"kural","num":362,"t":["வேண்டுங்கால் வேண்டும் பிறவாமை மற்றது","வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்"],"p":"If anything be desired, freedom from births should be desired; that (freedom from births) will be attained by desiring to be without desire.","r":"If you must want, want not to be born; that comes when you want not to want.","n":"A paradox as neat as 350. The verb வேண்டு occurs in every clause, and the verse doesn't pretend desire can simply be stopped. It redirects desire until it undoes itself.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Non-birth comes of itself to one who wants to want nothing.","அப்பிறவாமைதான் ஒரு பொருளையும் அவாவாமையை வேண்ட அவனுக்குத் தானே உண்டாம்."],["kalaignar","It is a warning: the misery that makes one wish one had never been born comes if desires aren't eliminated.","விரும்புவதானால் பிறக்காமலே இருந்திருக்க வேண்டும் என்று ஒருவன் எண்ணுகிற அளவுக்கு ஏற்படுகிற துன்ப நிலை, ஆசைகளை ஒழிக்காவிடில் வரும்."]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:363":{"book":"kural","num":363,"t":["வேண்டாமை அன்ன விழுச்செல்வம் ஈண்டில்லை","ஆண்டும் அஃதொப்பது இல்"],"p":"There is in this world no excellence equal to freedom from desire; and even in that world, there is nothing like it.","r":"There is no fine wealth here like not wanting, and nothing there equals it either.","n":"It is pointed to name wanting nothing as wealth. Parimelazhagar explains why it is the top: human and divine wealth always have something above them, so only not wanting has no superior.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:364":{"book":"kural","num":364,"t":["தூஉய்மை என்பது அவாவின்மை மற்றது","வாஅய்மை வேண்ட வரும்"],"p":"Purity (of mind) consists in freedom from desire; and that (freedom from desire) is the fruit of the love of truth.","r":"Purity is the absence of craving, and that comes by wanting வாய்மை.","n":"The word தூய்மை also differs: for Parimelazhagar it is release itself, as the soul's stains are gone, and for Manakkudavar it is the removal of defilement.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வாய்மை here stands for the Supreme, who has truth, and wanting it means continuous contemplation.","மெய்ம்மையுடைய பரத்தை ஆகுபெயரால் 'மெய்ம்மை' என்றும் கூறினார்."],["manakkudavar","வாய்மை means speaking truth, and those who speak truth come to have no desire.","அவ்வாசை யின்மை மெய் சொல்லுதலை விரும்ப வரும்,"]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:365":{"book":"kural","num":365,"t":["அற்றவர் என்பார் அவாஅற்றார் மற்றையார்","அற்றாக அற்றது இலர்"],"p":"They are said to be free (from future birth) who are freed from desire; all others (who, whatever else they may be free from, are not freed from desire) are not thus free.","r":"Those called 'freed' are those freed of craving; the rest are not so freed.","n":"அற்றார் is left without an object, and that is the wit. Being cut free of other things is a partial matter, which Parimelazhagar makes explicit: others have only avoided some pains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:366":{"book":"kural","num":366,"t":["அஞ்சுவ தோரும் அறனே ஒருவனை","வஞ்சிப்ப தோரும் அவா"],"p":"It is the chief duty of (an ascetic) to watch against desire with (jealous) fear; for it has power to deceive (and destroy) him.","r":"It is virtue alone to fear; what deceives a person is craving.","n":"Craving is a confidence trick: Manakkudavar has it stand in front like a benefactor and then lead one down. Parimelazhagar has it slip in through a moment's inattention, even for someone close to release. So vigilance has no end point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","acham","aram"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","It deceives by acting first as a friend.","வஞ்சனை செய்தல் - நன்றி செய்வாரைப் போல முன்னே நின்று , பின்னே தீக்கதியுள் உய்த்தல்."]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:367":{"book":"kural","num":367,"t":["அவாவினை ஆற்ற அறுப்பின் தவாவினை","தான்வேண்டு மாற்றான் வரும்"],"p":"If a man thoroughly cut off all desire, the deeds, which confer immortality, will come to him, in the path in which he seeks them.","r":"If one cuts off craving thoroughly, the deeds that don't fail come in the way one wishes.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a bold antinomian comment: once craving is fully cut, no other virtue need be done, and everything one does is virtue. The verse says only that the right deeds arrive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:368":{"book":"kural","num":368,"t":["அவாஇல்லார்க் கில்லாகுந் துன்பம் அஃதுண்டேல்","தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்"],"p":"There is no sorrow to those who are without desire; but where that is, (sorrow) will incessantly come, more and more.","r":"Those without craving have no sorrow; if it exists, sorrow keeps coming, unfailing, on and on.","n":"The claim seems too simple, since those without craving still get sick. Parimelazhagar sees this and limits it: the suffering that came with the body is already earned, and the verse means suffering created now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avaa","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:369":{"book":"kural","num":369,"t":["இன்பம் இடையறா தீண்டும் அவாவென்னும்","துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்"],"p":"Even while in this body, joy will never depart (from the mind, in which) desire, that sorrow of sorrows, has been destroyed.","r":"Joy will be unbroken ஈண்டும், if the sorrow of sorrows called craving perishes.","n":"Craving is not just the cause of sorrow but is itself the worst sorrow. Parimelazhagar explains that it hurts both at its source and in its fruit.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஈண்டும் means 'even here', so joy comes while still in the body.","அவன் வீடு பெற்ற வழியே அன்றி உடம்போடு நின்ற வழியும் இன்பம் இடையறாது."],["manakkudavar","ஈண்டும் means 'it swells', so joy increases without break, and Parimelazhagar notes this view.","இன்ப மானது இடையறாமல் வந்து மிகும்,"]],"ch":"k:37"},"k:370":{"book":"kural","num":370,"t":["ஆரா இயற்கை அவாநீப்பின் அந்நிலையே","பேரா இயற்கை தரும்"],"p":"The removal of desire, whose nature it is never to be satisfied, will immediately confer a nature that can never be changed.","r":"If one gives up craving, whose nature is never to be filled, right then it gives a nature that never shifts.","n":"The rhyme of ஆரா and பேரா sets an insatiable nature against an immovable one, and அந்நிலையே makes it immediate. Parimelazhagar calls this person liberated while living and quotes the Tiruvaymoli. The verse says only that the nature doesn't move.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avaa","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:37"},"k:371":{"book":"kural","num":371,"t":["ஆகூழால் தோன்றும் அசைவின்மை கைப்பொருள்","போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி"],"p":"Perseverance comes from a prosperous fate, and idleness from an adverse fate.","r":"A fate of gain produces tireless effort and a fate of loss produces sloth.","n":"Effort is presented as fate's product, not its rival, so the verse removes the usual escape route from fate.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","muyarchi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate itself generates the effort and sloth that help make or lose wealth.","முயற்சி மடிகளையும் தானே தோற்றுவிக்கும்"],["kalaignar","A natural condition, not fate, gives rise to enthusiasm or laziness.","ஆக்கத்திற்கான இயற்கை நிலை"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:372":{"book":"kural","num":372,"t":["பேதைப் படுக்கும் இழவூழ் அறிவகற்றும்","ஆகலூழ் உற்றக் கடை"],"p":"An adverse fate produces folly, and a prosperous fate produces enlarged knowledge.","r":"A fate of loss makes a person foolish, and a fate of gain, when it arrives, enlarges their understanding.","n":"Intelligence is not a fixed possession here; circumstances of fortune change it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate alters even the understanding one is born with.","இயற்கையானாகிய அறிவையும் வேறுபடுக்கும்"],["kalaignar","Natural conditions of ruin and growth produce ignorance and understanding.","அழிவுதரும் இயற்கை நிலை, அறியாமையை உண்டாக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:373":{"book":"kural","num":373,"t":["நுண்ணிய நூல்பல கற்பினும் மற்றுந்தன்","உண்மை யறிவே மிகும்"],"p":"Although (a man) may study the most polished treatises, the knowledge which fate has decreed to him will still prevail.","r":"However many subtle books a person studies, their own understanding still prevails.","n":"The verse doesn't say whose understanding wins or why. The commentators fill that gap differently, and தன் உண்மை அறிவு carries the dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse concerns one fated to folly, whose fate-given foolishness overrides what study taught.","தன் ஊழான் ஆகிய பேதைமை உணர்வே மேற்படும்"],["manakkudavar","Learning may be present, but understanding that comes from fate is stronger.","கல்வியுண்டாயினும் ஊழானாய்வறிவு வலியுடைத் தென்றார்"],["kalaignar","Natural intelligence rises above study, with no appeal to fate.","அவரது இயற்கை அறிவே மேலோங்கி நிற்கும்."]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:374":{"book":"kural","num":374,"t":["இருவேறு உலகத்து இயற்கை திருவேறு","தெள்ளிய ராதலும் வேறு"],"p":"There are (through fate) two different natures in the world, hence the difference (observable in men) in (their acquisition of) wealth, and in their attainment of knowledge.","r":"The world has two distinct natures: being wealthy is one thing, and being wise is another.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws a pointed conclusion: since the wise can be poor and others rich, wealth does not need wisdom as a cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:38"},"k:375":{"book":"kural","num":375,"t":["நல்லவை எல்லாஅந் தீயவாம் தீயவும்","நல்லவாம் செல்வம் செயற்கு"],"p":"In the acquisition of property, every thing favourable becomes unfavourable, and (on the other hand) everything unfavourable becomes favourable, (through the power of fate).","r":"In making wealth, all that is favourable can turn out badly and all that is unfavourable can turn out well.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The 'good' and 'bad' are favourable conditions such as time, place, means and action, which fate overturns.","'நல்லவை' 'தீயவை' என்பன காலமும், இடனும், கருவியும், தொழிலும் முதலாயவற்றை."],["kalaignar","Good and bad actions can end in the opposite result, which is simply the natural course.","நல்ல செயல்களை ஆற்ற முற்படும்போது அவை தீமையில் போய் முடிந்துவிடுவதும்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:376":{"book":"kural","num":376,"t":["பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல உய்த்துச்","சொரியினும் போகா தம"],"p":"Whatever is not conferred by fate cannot be preserved although it be guarded with most painful care; and that, which fate has made his, cannot be lost, although one should even take it and throw it away.","r":"What is not yours will not stay however carefully you guard it, and what is yours will not leave though you throw it away.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Possession and loss follow fate, not care or neglect.","பொருள்களின் நிலையும் போக்கும் ஊழினான் ஆவதல்லது, காப்பு இகழ்ச்சிகளான் ஆகா"],["kalaignar","The verse is about what one has a right to, not what fate allots.","தனக்கு உரிமையல்லாதவற்றை"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:377":{"book":"kural","num":377,"t":["வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகையல்லால் கோடி","தொகுத்தார்க்கு துய்த்தல் அரிது"],"p":"Even those who gather together millions will only enjoy them, as it has been determined by the disposer (of all things).","r":"Even those who have amassed millions can enjoy them only as the one who apportions has apportioned.","n":"வகுத்தான் gives fate a personal agent. This is one of the chapter's clearest points of disagreement about whether a cosmic agent is present.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The apportioner is a deity who gives each soul the fruit of its own deeds.","ஓர் உயிர் செய்த வினையின் பயன் பிறிதோர் உயிரின்கண் செல்லாமல் அவ்வுயிர்க்கே வகுத்தலின்"],["kalaignar","What is apportioned is an ordered way of life, and wealth is hard to enjoy without following it.","வகுத்து முறைப்படுத்திய வாழ்க்கை நெறியை ஒட்டி நடக்கா விட்டால்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:378":{"book":"kural","num":378,"t":["துறப்பார்மன் துப்புர வில்லார் உறற்பால","ஊட்டா கழியு மெனின்"],"p":"The destitute will renounce desire (and become ascetics), if (fate) do not make them suffer the hindrances to which they are liable, and they pass away.","r":"The destitute would renounce the world, if only what they are bound to suffer would pass without afflicting them.","n":"The irony is dry: the poor already lack what renunciation gives up, yet fate withholds even that peace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They could let go of what they lack anyway, but fate's power keeps them suffering through their attitude.","கருத்து வேறுபாட்டால் துன்பமுறுகின்றது ஊழின் வலியான்"],["kalaignar","If afflicting feelings left them, they would renounce; the obstacle is psychological.","தம்மை வருத்தக்கூடிய உணர்வுகள் வந்து வருத்தாமல் நீங்கிவிடுமானால்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:379":{"book":"kural","num":379,"t":["நன்றாங்கால் நல்லவாக் காண்பவர் அன்றாங்கால்","அல்லற் படுவ தெவன்?"],"p":"How is it that those, who are pleased with good fortune, trouble themselves when evil comes, (since both are equally the decree of fa te) ?","r":"Those who accept things as good when good comes: why do they grieve when it doesn't?","n":"After nine verses on fate's power, this one asks for a response. Fate here is the premise of equanimity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Both are one's own earlier deeds and can't be escaped, so accepting one and resisting the other is unwise.","தாமே முன் செய்து கொண்டமையானும், ஊட்டாது கழியாமையானும்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:380":{"book":"kural","num":380,"t":["ஊழிற் பெருவலி யாவுள மற்றொன்று","சூழினுந் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"What is stronger than fate ? If we think of an expedient (to avert it), it will itself be with us before (the thought).","r":"What is stronger than fate? Even when one devises another way, it gets there first.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate can come through the very plan meant to avert it.","தான் அவ்வுபாயமேயானும் பிறிதொன்றானும் வழியாக வந்து"],["kalaignar","The natural state reasserts itself over artificial arrangements.","இயற்கை நிலையே முதன்மையாக வந்து நிற்பதால்"]],"ch":"k:38"},"k:381":{"book":"kural","num":381,"t":["படைகுடி கூழ்அமைச்சு நட்பரண் ஆறும்","உடையான் அரசருள் ஏறு"],"p":"He who possesses these six things, an army, a people, wealth, ministers, friends and a fortress, is a lion among kings.","r":"One who has all six, an army, a people, resources, ministers, allies and a fort, stands among kings as the male leads its herd.","n":"ஏறு names the male of a species, not specifically a lion. Pope's and Kalaignar's lion is an interpretive choice. Parimelazhagar presses ஆறும்: lacking even one limb, royal policy doesn't function.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:382":{"book":"kural","num":382,"t":["அஞ்சாமை ஈகை அறிவூக்கம் இந்நான்கும்","எஞ்சாமை வேந்தர்க் கியல்பு"],"p":"Never to fail in these four things, fearlessness, liberality, wisdom, and energy, is the kingly character.","r":"Fearlessness, giving, understanding and drive, these four never failing, are the nature of a king.","n":"The weight is on எஞ்சாமை. Parimelazhagar explains that when one quality surfaces in a person others recede, and a king can't afford that, so the verse asks for constancy as well as possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu","eegai","arivu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:383":{"book":"kural","num":383,"t":["தூங்காமை கல்வி துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றும்","நீங்கா நிலனான் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"These three things, viz., vigilance, learning, and bravery, should never be wanting in the ruler of a country.","r":"Promptness, learning and resolve: these three do not leave those who rule the land.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arasu","kalvi","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துணிவு here stands for manly swiftness, acting quickly without regard to anything else.","ஆண்மையாவது, ஒன்றனையும் பாராது கடிதில் செய்வது"],["manakkudavar","துணிவு is a settled decision reached after examining the matter.","ஒரு பொருளை ஆராய்ந்து துணிதலுடைமையும்"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:384":{"book":"kural","num":384,"t":["அறனிழுக்கா தல்லவை நீக்கி மறனிழுக்கா","மானம் உடைய தரசு"],"p":"He is a king who, with manly modesty, swerves not from virtue, and refrains from vice.","r":"A king does not stray from virtue, removes what is not virtue, and keeps an honour that does not stray from valour.","n":"The verse pairs அறன் with மறன், so virtue and martial valour stand side by side as parts of one honour. Parimelazhagar illustrates this honour with rules of war, such as not striking the young, the old or one who flees.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","aram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is removed is wrongdoing in his realm, such as killing and theft. It is a public duty.","அறனல்லவை தன் நாட்டின் கண்ணும் நிகழாமல் கடிந்து"],["manakkudavar","What is removed is desire and anger, the king's own inner faults.","அறமல்லாத காம வெகுளியைக் கடிந்து"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:385":{"book":"kural","num":385,"t":["இயற்றலும் ஈட்டலுங் காத்தலும் காத்த","வகுத்தலும் வல்ல தரசு"],"p":"He is a king who is able to acquire (wealth), to lay it up, to guard, and to distribute it.","r":"A king is one able to create sources of wealth, gather it, guard it, and apportion what is guarded.","n":"Four verbs form a cycle that ends in distribution, not in holding. The verse doesn't say to whom. Parimelazhagar spreads the giving over virtue, wealth and pleasure, while Manakkudavar's example is equipping the army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:386":{"book":"kural","num":386,"t":["காட்சிக் கெளியன் கடுஞ்சொல்லன் அல்லனேல்","மீக்கூறும் மன்னன் நிலம்"],"p":"The whole world will exalt the country of the king who is easy of access, and who is free from harsh language.","r":"If a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech, his land is spoken of above others.","n":"The grammar praises the land, not the king. Parimelazhagar notes that praising the land makes praising the king unnecessary, though he has to supply the world as the speaker.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:387":{"book":"kural","num":387,"t":["இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க வல்லார்க்குத் தன்சொலால்","தான்கண் டனைத்திவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world will praise and submit itself to the mind of the king who is able to give with affability, and to protect all who come to him.","r":"For those who can give and protect with pleasant words, this world falls in with their word and their intent.","n":"The second line is compressed, and my reading of தன் சொலால் தான் கண்டனைத்து is tentative. Pleasant speech is named as what makes the giving work, not as an extra.","x":1,"u":1,"f":1,"c":["arasu","inchol","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சொல் means fame: the world, joined to his renown, becomes as much as he conceives.","இவ்வுலகம் தன் புகழோடு மேவித் தான் கருதிய அளவிற்றாம்"],["manakkudavar","சொல் means his command: by it the world lies in his control.","தன்னேவலாலே இவ்வுலகம் தான் கண்டாற்போலும் தன் வசத்தே கிடக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:388":{"book":"kural","num":388,"t":["முறைசெய்து காப்பாற்றும் மன்னவன் மக்கட்கு","இறையென்று வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"That king, will be esteemed a God among men, who performs his own duties, and protects (his subjects).","r":"The ruler who does justice and protects is set apart as இறை for the people.","n":"வைக்கப்படும் is passive and conditional: the status is awarded for doing justice, not held by right of office.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Though a man by birth, by his deeds he is placed apart as a god for humans.","பிறப்பான் மகனேயாயினும், செய்வான் மக்கட்குக் கடவுள் என்று வேறு வைக்கப்படும்"],["manakkudavar","He is reckoned the lord of people, a political headship with no deification.","மனிதர்க்கு நாயக னென்று எண்ணப்படுவான்"],["kalaignar","He is honoured as the people's leader.","மக்களுக்குத் தலைவன் எனப் போற்றப் படுவான்."]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:389":{"book":"kural","num":389,"t":["செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும் பண்புடை வேந்தன்","கவிகைக்கீழ்த் தங்கும் உலகு"],"p":"The whole world will dwell under the umbrella of the king, who can bear words that embitter the ear.","r":"The world rests under the parasol of a king with the character to bear words that are bitter to the ear.","n":"செவிகைப்ப moves a taste onto the ear, as Parimelazhagar points out. Tolerance of bitterness is presented as what brings the world under the parasol, which reverses the usual picture of power as not having to listen.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The words are the rebukes of counsellors, borne for the good they will produce.","இடிக்கும் துணையாயினார் சொற்களைத் தன் செவி பொறாதாகவும்."],["manakkudavar","The words are reports of what others have done wrong, and the king forgives those offences. He also records a view that they are a priest's words.","பிறர் செய்த குற்றங்களைக் கேட்டு வைத்தும், அதனைப் பொறுக்கவல்ல குணமுடைய"]],"ch":"k:39"},"k:390":{"book":"kural","num":390,"t":["கொடையளி செங்கோல் குடியோம்பல் நான்கும்","உடையானாம் வேந்தர்க் கொளி"],"p":"He is the light of kings who has there four things, beneficence, benevolence, rectitude, and care for his people.","r":"One who has these four, giving, kindness, a straight sceptre and care of the people, is a light among kings.","n":"Manakkudavar reads all four as relief for struggling farmers, including seed and plough and deferred or waived dues. That makes the verse far more concrete than its abstract nouns suggest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arasu","sengol","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:39"},"k:391":{"book":"kural","num":391,"t":["கற்க கசடறக் கற்பவை கற்றபின்","நிற்க அதற்குத் தக"],"p":"Let a man learn thoroughly whatever he may learn, and let his conduct be worthy of his learning.","r":"Learn what is to be learned without flaw, and having learned, stand in keeping with it.","n":"நிற்க gives conduct a posture: the chapter's first verse makes learning unfinished until one stands by it. Parimelazhagar narrows கற்பவை to works on the aims of life, on the grounds that life is too short for the rest, but the verse leaves the scope open.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:392":{"book":"kural","num":392,"t":["எண்ணென்ப ஏனை எழுத்தென்ப இவ்விரண் டும்","கண்ணென்ப வாழும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"Letters and numbers are the two eyes of man.","r":"What are called numbers and what are called letters, these two, are called the eyes of living beings.","n":"The verse says வாழும் உயிர்க்கு and doesn't restrict it. Parimelazhagar limits it to beings of special understanding and treats the two as tools for seeing the aims of life; Kalaignar goes further and makes them the test of being alive at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:393":{"book":"kural","num":393,"t":["கண்ணுடையர் என்பவர் கற்றோர் முகத்திரண்டு","புண்ணுடையர் கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The learned are said to have eyes, but the unlearned have (merely) two sores in their face.","r":"Those called people with eyes are the learned; the unlearned have two sores on their face.","n":"The insult works because the sore is in the same place and looks similar. Parimelazhagar adds that a merely physical eye gives pain through disease. Kalaignar draws an inference absent from the verse, that a blind person who is learned counts as having eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:394":{"book":"kural","num":394,"t":["உவப்பத் தலைக்கூடி உள்ளப் பிரிதல்","அனைத்தே புலவர் தொழில்"],"p":"It is the part of the learned to give joy to those whom they meet, and on leaving, to make them think (Oh! when shall we meet them again.)","r":"To meet with delight and part leaving others thinking of you: such is the way of the learned.","n":"The measure of learning here is purely social, with no mention of knowledge. Manakkudavar makes it a comparison with two people who meet in joy and part in longing, an image close to love. Parimelazhagar's விளக்கம் opens the அத்தன்மைத்து to include right conduct and counsel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:395":{"book":"kural","num":395,"t":["உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல் ஏக்கற்றுங் கற்றார்","கடையரே கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The unlearned are inferior to the learned, before whom they stand begging, as the destitute before the wealthy.","r":"Those who learned while yearning like the poor before the rich are the foremost; those who did not learn are the lowest.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","panivu","kallaamai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The learner stands humbled before the teacher and is thereby foremost. Those ashamed of that posture stay low for good, having lost true learning to false pride.","செல்வர்முன் நல்கூர்ந்தார் நிற்குமாறு போலத் தாமும் ஆசிரியர்முன் ஏக்கற்று நின்றும் கற்றார் தலையாயினார்"],["manakkudavar","It is the unlearned who stand yearning like the poor, and they rank beneath the learned.","அதனைக் காதலித்து நிற்றலுமன்றிக் கற்றாரிடத்தாவர் கல்லாதார்"]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:396":{"book":"kural","num":396,"t":["தொட்டனைத் தூறும் மணற்கேணி மாந்தர்க்குக்","கற்றனைத் தூறும் அறிவு"],"p":"Water will flow from a well in the sand in proportion to the depth to which it is dug, and knowledge will flow from a man in proportion to his learning.","r":"A well in sand gives water as far as it is dug; for people, understanding wells up as far as they have learned.","n":"ஊறும் matters: understanding seeps up from below rather than being poured in, so study opens something rather than simply filling a store. Parimelazhagar openly reconciles this with 373, saying it holds where fate doesn't oppose it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse holds where fate does not stand against it, and so it doesn't conflict with 373.","இஃது ஊழ் மாறு கொள்ளாவழியாகலின்"]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:397":{"book":"kural","num":397,"t":["யாதானும் நாடாமால் ஊராமால் என்னொருவன்","சாந்துணையுங் கல்லாத வாறு"],"p":"How is it that any one can remain without learning, even to his death, when (to the learned man) every country is his own (country), and every town his own (town) ?","r":"Any land becomes one's own, any town one's own; why then does a person stay unlearned until death?","n":"The verse states the belonging without naming who has it; the commentators supply the learned. Parimelazhagar's reason is reception: others' lands give the learned gifts and honour. A reader today may hear something more cosmopolitan than that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:398":{"book":"kural","num":398,"t":["ஒருமைக்கண் தான் கற்ற கல்வி ஒருவற்கு","எழுமையும் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"The learning, which a man has acquired in one birth, will yield him pleasure during seven births.","r":"The learning one acquires in a single span serves as protection through seven.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒருமை and எழுமை are births: learning stays with the soul as deeds do, and follows it into each birth.","வினைகள்போல உயிரின்கண் கிடந்து அது புக்குழிப் புகும்"],["kalaignar","They are generations: what is learned in one generation protects seven generations of descendants.","ஒரு தலைமுறையில் பெறும் கல்வி அறிவனாது, ஏழேழு தலைமுறைக்கும் பாதுகாப்பாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:399":{"book":"kural","num":399,"t":["தாமின் புறுவது உலகின் புறக் கண்டு","காமுறுவர் கற்றறிந் தார்"],"p":"The learned will long (for more learning), when they see that while it gives pleasure to themselves, the world also derives pleasure from it.","r":"Seeing that what delights them delights the world, the learned come to love it all the more.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What they desire further is learning itself, because it delights the world too. Unlike wealth, its pleasure gains from being shared.","கற்றறிந்தார் பின்னும் அதனையே விரும்புவர்"],["manakkudavar","The learned are glad when others enjoy what they enjoy. The verse is about freedom from envy, a virtue that learning brings.","இஃது அழுக்காறு செய்யாது இன்புறுதல் அறமாதலின், அது கல்வியானே வருமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:40"},"k:400":{"book":"kural","num":400,"t":["கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம் கல்வி யொருவற்கு","மாடல்ல மற்றை யவை"],"p":"Learning is the true imperishable riches; all other things are not riches.","r":"Learning is the imperishable, excellent wealth for a person; other things are not wealth.","n":"மாடு is a plain word for property and cattle, and the verse flatly denies that other possessions deserve it. Parimelazhagar defines the imperishability as more than safety from thieves and kings: learning doesn't decrease when given away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:40"},"k:401":{"book":"kural","num":401,"t":["அரங்கின்றி வட்டாடி யற்றே நிரம்பிய","நூலின்றிக் கோட்டி கொளல்"],"p":"To speak in an assembly (of the learned) without fullness of knowledge, is like playing at chess (on a board) without squares.","r":"Holding forth in an assembly without complete learning is like playing a board game with no marked board.","n":"The image is exact: a game without a board isn't a bad game, because no move can mean anything. The fault is the absence of a shared frame, not lack of cleverness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:402":{"book":"kural","num":402,"t":["கல்லாதான் சொற்கா முறுதல் முலையிரண்டும்","இல்லாதாள் பெண்காமுற் றற்று"],"p":"The desire of the unlearned to speak (in an assembly), is like a woman without breasts desiring (the enjoyment of ) woman-hood.","r":"For the unlearned to set their heart on speech is like desiring womanhood, or a woman, in one who lacks both breasts.","n":"A crude comparison that rests on a narrow view of women's bodies. It is one of the verses where the book's period shows, and it shouldn't be softened. The commentators disagree on who is desiring.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The unlearned man himself longs to speak, and this is like such a woman longing for womanhood: it gets nowhere, or brings laughter.","கல்வியில்லாதவன் ஒருவன் அவையின்கண் ஒன்று சொல்லுதலை அவாவுதல்"],["kalaignar","The fault lies with the listener who wants to hear the unlearned, and this is like being infatuated with such a woman.","கல்லாதவனின் சொல்கேட்க விரும்புவது,"]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:403":{"book":"kural","num":403,"t":["கல்லா தவரும் நனிநல்லர் கற்றார்முன்","சொல்லா திருக்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"The unlearned also are very excellent men, if they know how to keep silence before the learned.","r":"Even the unlearned are very good, if only they can stay silent before the learned.","n":"Dry praise. Parimelazhagar takes பெறின் seriously: it marks the condition as hard to meet, since those who don't know themselves can't manage it. The verse's sting is that knowing when to be silent would itself take the knowledge they lack.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:404":{"book":"kural","num":404,"t":["கல்லாதான் ஒட்பம் கழியநன் றாயினும்","கொள்ளார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"Although the natural knowledge of an unlearned man may be very good, the wise will not accept for true knowledge.","r":"However excellent an unlearned person's brightness may be, the wise do not accept it.","n":"The verse grants the brightness and refuses it recognition, so this is a claim about what the wise will credit and not about what the person has.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kallaamai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It isn't real understanding: any good result is a chance hit, like letters traced by accident.","ஏரலெழுத்துப் போல்வதோர் விழுக்காடு"],["kalaignar","The natural intelligence may be real. The wise simply won't accept him as distinguished in learning.","அவனைக் கல்வியில் சிறந்தோன் என்று அறிவுடையோர் ஒப்புக் கொள்ள மாட்டார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:405":{"book":"kural","num":405,"t":["கல்லா ஒருவன் தகைமை தலைப்பெய்து","சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்"],"p":"The self-conceit of an unlearned man will fade away, as soon as he speaks in an assembly (of thelearned).","r":"An unlearned person's standing wilts once he meets and speaks.","n":"The verse doesn't say with whom; the commentators supply a learned person. தகைமை is taken three ways: Parimelazhagar as his self-estimate, Manakkudavar as real greatness, and Kalaignar as a false pose. The first and third make the loss self-delusion ending, while the second makes it a real loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:406":{"book":"kural","num":406,"t":["உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர் அல்லால் பயவாக்","களரனையர் கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The unlearned are like worthless barren land: all that can be said of them is, that they exist.","r":"The unlearned can only be said to exist; beyond that they are like barren saline land that yields nothing.","n":"உளர் என்னும் மாத்திரையர் reduces a person to the bare fact of being said to exist. Kalaignar's walking corpses is stronger than the verse's image, which is about yield.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:407":{"book":"kural","num":407,"t":["நுண்மாண் நுழைபுலம் இல்லான் எழில்நலம்","மண்மாண் புனைபாவை யற்று"],"p":"The beauty and goodness of one who is destitute of knowledge by the study of great and exquisite works, is like (the beauty and goodness) of a painted earthen doll.","r":"The fine looks of one without subtle, penetrating understanding are like those of a well-made clay doll.","n":"Parimelazhagar concedes that a fine body is itself a rare fruit of birth, which makes the point less a scorn of beauty than a statement that it needs learning to be worth anything.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kallaamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:408":{"book":"kural","num":408,"t":["நல்லார்கண் பட்ட வறுமையின் இன்னாதே","கல்லார்கண் பட் ட திரு"],"p":"Wealth, gained by the unlearned, will give more sorrow than the poverty which may come upon the learned.","r":"Wealth lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good.","n":"The verse says நல்லார், the good, and opposes it to கல்லார், the unlearned. The pairing makes the learned and the good one group by sound and by implication. Parimelazhagar glosses நல்லார் as கற்றார் outright.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Wealth ruins the unlearned owner, while poverty doesn't ruin the good. That is why it is more painful.","திருகல்லாரைக் கெடுக்க, வறுமை நல்லாரைக் கெடாது நிற்றலான்"],["manakkudavar","The harm falls on others: given wealth, the unlearned make other people suffer.","இது செல்வமுண்டாயின், பிறரைத் துன்பமுறுவிப்பரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:409":{"book":"kural","num":409,"t":["மேற்பிறந்தா ராயினும் கல்லாதார் கீழ்ப்பிறந்தும்","கற்றார் அனைத்திலர் பாடு"],"p":"The unlearned, though born in a high caste, are not equal in dignity to the learned; though they may have been born in a low caste.","r":"Though born high, the unlearned do not have the worth of those who are learned though born low.","n":"The verse overturns the ranking but keeps the words மேல் and கீழ், so birth rank remains as something to be overturned. The argument is not that the distinction is false, only that learning outweighs it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Caste rank ends with the body while learning goes with the soul, so learning is superior. Caste rank itself is assumed.","உடலோடு ஒழியும் சாதி உயர்ச்சியினும், உயிரோடு செல்லும் கல்வி உயர்ச்சி சிறப்புடைத்து"],["kalaignar","The honour of learning removes the distinction of high and low altogether.","உயர்ந்தவர் தாழ்ந்தவர் என்ற வேறுபாட்டைப் போக்கிவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:41"},"k:410":{"book":"kural","num":410,"t":["விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர் இலங்குநூல்","கற்றாரோடு ஏனை யவர்"],"p":"As beasts by the side of men, so are other men by the side of those who are learned in celebrated works.","r":"As beasts stand to people, so do others stand to those who have studied the shining books.","n":"The chapter ends by removing the unlearned from the human as such. Parimelazhagar's logic is that the surplus of awareness which marks humans over animals is found in the learned, so the two aren't of one birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:41"},"k:411":{"book":"kural","num":411,"t":["செல்வத்துட் செல்வஞ் செவிச்செல்வம் அச்செல்வம்","செல்வத்து ளெல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"Wealth (gained) by the ear is wealth of wealth; that wealth is the chief of all wealth.","r":"The wealth among wealths is the wealth of the ear; that wealth stands first among all wealth.","n":"The sound does the work, with செல்வம் in every half-line. After 400 has already denied other wealth the name, this verse places the ear at the head of that revalued wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:412":{"book":"kural","num":412,"t":["செவுக்குண வில்லாத போழ்து சிறிது","வயிற்றுக்கும் ஈயப் படும்"],"p":"When there is no food for the ear, give a little also to the stomach.","r":"When there is no food for the ear, a little may be given to the stomach too.","n":"The verse is witty, not ascetic. Parimelazhagar catches the two loaded words: சிறிது, and ஈயப்படும், which treats the stomach as a beggar to be given alms. It also says the food is given so that one can go on listening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:413":{"book":"kural","num":413,"t":["செவியுணவிற் கேள்வி யுடையார் அவியுணவின்","ஆன்றாரோ டொப்பர் நிலத்து"],"p":"Those who in this world enjoy instruction which is the food of the ear, are equal to the Gods, who enjoy the food of the sacrifices.","r":"Those who have listening as food for the ear, though on earth, are equal to those fulfilled by the food of oblation.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஆன்றார் here are the gods who receive sacrificial offerings. The listeners are their earthly equals.","நிலத்தின்கண்ணர் ஆயினும் அவியுணவினையுடைய தேவரொடு ஒப்பர்."],["kalaignar","ஆன்றார் are human sages who eat little and are full of understanding, with no sacrifice or gods.","குறைந்த உணவருந்தி நிறைந்த அறிவுடன் விளங்கும் ஆன்றோர்க்கு"]],"ch":"k:42"},"k:414":{"book":"kural","num":414,"t":["கற்றில னாயினுங் கேட்க அஃதொருவற்கு","ஒற்கத்தின் ஊற்றாந் துணை"],"p":"Although a man be without learning, let him listen (to the teaching of the learned); that will be to him a staff in adversity.","r":"Even if you haven't studied, listen; it will be a staff to lean on in one's faltering.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that the உம்மை in ஆயினும் keeps study as the first duty. Listening is not offered as an equal path, only as what remains. He also makes the faltering inward: a mind shaken by poverty or ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","kalvi","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:415":{"book":"kural","num":415,"t":["இழுக்கல் உடையுழி ஊற்றுக்கோல் அற்றே","ஒழுக்க முடையார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"The words of the good are like a staff in a slippery place.","r":"The words from the mouths of those of good conduct are like a staff on slippery ground.","n":"The authority comes from the speaker's conduct, not learning. Parimelazhagar makes the exclusion explicit: the learned without conduct are not to be heard at all. So the chapter's listening is listening to a kind of person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","ozhukkam","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:416":{"book":"kural","num":416,"t":["எனைத்தானும் நல்லவை கேட்க அனைத்தானும்","ஆன்ற பெருமை தரும்"],"p":"Let a man listen, never so little, to good (instruction), even that will bring him great dignity.","r":"Listen to good things, however little; that much too will give full greatness.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even a little brings full greatness. Like drops of rain, the listening accumulates, so don't despise the small.","அக்கேள்வி மழைத் துளைபோல வந்து ஈண்டு எல்லா அறிவுகளையும் உள ஆக்கலின்"],["kalaignar","Greatness comes in proportion: as much as one listens, that much greatness.","நல்லவற்றை எந்த அளவுக்குக் கேட்கிறோமோ அந்த அளவுக்குப் பெருமை கிடைக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:42"},"k:417":{"book":"kural","num":417,"t":["பிழைத் துணர்ந்தும் பேதைமை சொல்லா ரிழைத்துணர்ந்","தீண்டிய கேள்வி யவர்"],"p":"Not even when they have imperfectly understood (a matter), will those men speak foolishly, who have profoundly studied and diligently listened (to instruction).","r":"Those whose listening is gathered upon their own close investigation do not speak folly, even when they have misunderstood.","n":"A subtle claim: being wrong and speaking foolishly are different things. Such a person can err and still not talk nonsense. இழைத்துணர்ந்து comes before கேள்வி, and Parimelazhagar reads this as one's own investigation first, confirmed by consulting those who know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:418":{"book":"kural","num":418,"t":["கேட்பினுங் கேளாத் தகையவே கேள்வியால்","தோட்கப் படாத செவி"],"p":"The ear which has not been bored by instruction, although it hears, is deaf.","r":"Ears not pierced by listening are deaf even though they hear.","n":"தோட்கப்படாத plays on pierced ears, ornament and all. Parimelazhagar's closing remark is apt: the old hole isn't a hole. Listening must make a new opening.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:419":{"book":"kural","num":419,"t":["நுணங்கிய கேள்விய ரல்லார் வணங்கிய","வாயின ராதல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a rare thing to find modesty, a reverend mouth- with those who have not received choice instruction.","r":"It is hard for those without fine listening to have a mouth that bows.","n":"The bowing is done by the mouth, so this is humility as a way of speaking. The causal claim is striking: humility comes from having listened, because those who haven't heard much admire themselves, as Parimelazhagar explains.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:420":{"book":"kural","num":420,"t":["செவியிற் சுவையுணரா வாயுணர்வின் மாக்கள்","அவியினும் வாழினும் என்?"],"p":"What does it matter whether those men live or die, who can judge of tastes by the mouth, and not by the ear ?","r":"What does it matter whether they die or live, these beings who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear?","n":"The verse doesn't say to whom it doesn't matter; both older commentators supply the world, which makes it a social judgment of uselessness, not a cosmic one. மாக்கள் rather than மக்கள் also seems to me a deliberate demotion. It may also echo அவி of 413, oblation there and perishing here, though that is my suggestion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:42"},"k:421":{"book":"kural","num":421,"t":["அறிவற்றங் காக்குங் கருவி செறுவார்க்கும்","உள்ளழிக்க லாகா அரண்"],"p":"Wisdom is a weapon to ward off destruction; it is an inner fortress which enemies cannot destroy.","r":"Understanding is the tool that guards against அற்றம், and an inner fortress enemies can't destroy.","n":"The fort is one of the six limbs of a kingdom (381). This verse puts a fort inside the person, with உள் able to mean either inward or impenetrable from within, as Parimelazhagar notes.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அற்றம் is final ruin, which understanding foresees and prevents.","அரசர்க்கு அறிவு என்பது இறுதி வாராமல் காக்கும் கருவியாம்"],["manakkudavar","அற்றம் is one's own weakness, and understanding covers one's faults, while the fort handles harm from others.","இது தனக்குள்ள குற்றத்தை மறைக்கு மென்றும், பிறரால் வரும் தீமை யைக் காக்குமென்றும்"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:422":{"book":"kural","num":422,"t":["சென்ற இடத்தால் செலவிடா தீதொரீஇ","நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்ப தறிவு"],"p":"Not to permit the mind to go where it lists, to keep it from evil, and to employ it in good, this is wisdom.","r":"Understanding does not let one go wherever things have gone, but turns away from evil and steers toward good.","n":"The verse doesn't name what wanders. Both commentators supply the mind, but they disagree on what is held back and how far.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The mind itself is driven through the senses, as a groom drives a horse. Understanding is a separate quality of the soul.","குதிரையை நிலமறிந்து செலுத்தும் வாதுவன்போல வேறாக்கி மனத்தைப் புலமறிந்து செலுத்துவது அறிவு"],["manakkudavar","The mind may go, but the body must not follow it, and the verse chiefly concerns sexual pleasure.","உள்ளஞ் சென்ற விடத்தே உடம்பையுஞ் செல்லவிடாது"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:423":{"book":"kural","num":423,"t":["எப்பொருள் யார்யார்வாய்க் கேட்பினும் அப்பொருள்","மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்ப தறிவு"],"p":"To discern the truth in every thing, by whomsoever spoken, is wisdom.","r":"Whatever is heard, from whoever's mouth, understanding is seeing the true substance of that very thing.","n":"The repeated யார்யார் removes the speaker from the test. Parimelazhagar gives the sharp consequence: high matters may come from low mouths, harmful ones from friends. He takes மெய்ப்பொருள் as the thing's true use, not its truth in the abstract, which Manakkudavar and Kalaignar prefer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:424":{"book":"kural","num":424,"t":["எண்பொருள வாகச் செலச்சொல்லித் தான்பிறர்வாய்","நுண்பொருள் காண்ப தறிவு"],"p":"To speak so as that the meaning may easily enter the mind of the hearer, and to discern the subtlest thought which may lie hidden in the words of others, this is wisdom.","r":"Understanding is saying things so they go in easily, while seeing the subtle meaning in what comes from others.","n":"The two directions are asymmetric: speech should make the hard simple, and listening should find the fine in the plain. Parimelazhagar goes further, saying to take the use from what others say even if it is flawed in expression.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:425":{"book":"kural","num":425,"t":["உலகம் தழீஇய தொட்பம் மலர்தலும்","கூம்பலும் இல்ல தறிவு"],"p":"To secure the friendship of the great is true wisdom; it is (also) wisdom to keep (that friendship unchanged, and) not opening and closing (like the lotus flower).","r":"Embracing the world is brightness; not opening and closing is understanding.","n":"Parimelazhagar narrows உலகம் to the worthy and contrasts a pond flower that closes with a tree flower that stays open.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What must not open and close is friendship with the worthy. He records another view that it is about wealth and poverty.","அந்நட்பின்கண் முன் மலர்தலும் பின் கூம்புதலும் இன்றி ஒரு நிலையினாவது அறிவாம்."],["kalaignar","What must not open and close is one's response to joy and sorrow, which is equanimity.","இன்பம் துன்பம் ஆகிய இரண்டையும் ஒரே நிலையாகக் கருதுவதே"]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:426":{"book":"kural","num":426,"t":["எவ்வ துறைவது உலகம் உலகத்தோடு","அவ்வ துறைவ தறிவு"],"p":"T o live as the world lives, is wisdom.","r":"As the world lives, so to live with the world is understanding.","n":"This verse needs reading with 423, because the same chapter tells the reader to test everything and to go along.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It restrains a king: he must not think that because he commands the world, no one commands him.","உலகத்தையெல்லாம் யான் நியமித்தலான் என்னை நியமிப்பாரில்லை,"],["manakkudavar","உலகம் means the elevated of earlier times, so this is following the worthy, not the crowd.","முற்பட்ட உயர்ந்தாரோடு பொருந்த ஒழுகுதல் அறிவு என்றார்."]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:427":{"book":"kural","num":427,"t":["அறிவுடையார் ஆவ தறிவார் அறிவிலார்","அஃதறி கல்லா தவர்"],"p":"The wise are those who know beforehand what will happen; those who do not know this are the unwise.","r":"Those with understanding know what will come to be; those without it cannot.","n":"ஆவது could be what will happen or what is good for oneself; Parimelazhagar records both. His gloss makes foreknowledge an act of reasoning ahead, எண்ணி அறிதல், and not prophecy. Not knowing is defined as finding out once it arrives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:428":{"book":"kural","num":428,"t":["அஞ்சுவ தஞ்சாமை பேதைமை அஞ்சுவது","அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்"],"p":"Not to fear what ought to be feared, is folly; it is the work of the wise to fear what should be feared.","r":"Not fearing what is to be feared is folly; fearing what is to be feared is the work of the knowing.","n":"Fear is treated as a skill, தொழில். Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both notice the conflict with the kingly fearlessness of 382 and resolve it. Parimelazhagar defines fear here as considering and refraining.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:429":{"book":"kural","num":429,"t":["எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்க் கில்லை","அதிர வருவதோர் நோய்"],"p":"No terrifying calamity will happen to the wise, who (foresee) and guard against coming evils.","r":"Those whose understanding guards against what is coming have no affliction that comes to shake them.","n":"அதிர வருவது is careful: the claim may be that no calamity shakes them, not that no calamity comes. Parimelazhagar's guarding means removing the cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","oozh"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","Even suffering from past deeds does not come harshly if it is guarded against early.","இது முன்னை வினையால் வருந் துன்பமும் முற்பட்டுக் காக்கின், கடிதாக வாராதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:43"},"k:430":{"book":"kural","num":430,"t":["அறிவுடையார் எல்லா முடையார் அறிவிலார்","என்னுடைய ரேனும் இலர்"],"p":"Those who possess wisdom, possess every thing; those who have not wisdom, whatever they may possess, have nothing.","r":"Those with understanding have everything; those without it have nothing, whatever they have.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a practical reason, not a pious one: all wealth is made and kept by understanding, so the fool has no means of keeping what he has or rebuilding it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:43"},"k:431":{"book":"kural","num":431,"t":["செருக்குஞ் சினமும் சிறுமையும் இல்லார்","பெருக்கம் பெருமித நீர்த்து"],"p":"Truly great is the excellence of those (kings) who are free from pride, anger, and lust.","r":"The prosperity of those without arrogance, anger and smallness has a dignified quality.","n":"The verse puts the virtue in the quality of the prosperity: the same wealth is dignified or not, depending on who holds it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","selvam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","செருக்கு is intoxication from wealth, and சிறுமை is excessive desire, which he calls small because it is what small people do.","மதமும் வெகுளியும் காமமும் ஆகிய குற்றங்கள்"],["manakkudavar","செருக்கு is coveting another man's wife, and சிறுமை is any behaviour of petty people.","பிறர்மனை நயத்தலும், வெகுளியும், சிறியார் செய்வன செய் தொழுகுதலும்"]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:432":{"book":"kural","num":432,"t":["இவறலும் மாண்பிறந்த மானமும் மாணா","உவகையும் ஏதம் இறைக்கு"],"p":"Avarice, undignified pride, and low pleasures are faults in a king.","r":"Stinginess, honour that has passed beyond dignity, and joy that lacks dignity are faults in a ruler.","n":"The surprising items are positive feelings gone wrong. Parimelazhagar makes the undignified joy excessive leniency, கழிகண்ணோட்டம், and the wrong honour includes refusing to bow to elders and carrying a thing through just because one intended it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","ivaral","maanam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:433":{"book":"kural","num":433,"t":["தினைத்துணையாங் குற்றம் வரினும் பனைத்துணையாக்","கொள்வர் பழிநாணு வார்"],"p":"Those who fear guilt, if they commit a fault small as a millet seed, will consider it to be as large as a palmyra tree.","r":"Though a fault comes as small as a millet seed, those who are ashamed of blame take it to be as large as a palmyra.","n":"The motive is shame at blame, பழிநாணுவார், so this is social conscience. Parimelazhagar makes the scale practical: treating it as large is how they ensure it doesn't come again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:434":{"book":"kural","num":434,"t":["குற்றமே காக்க பொருளாகக் குற்றமே","அற்றந் த்ரூஉம் பகை"],"p":"Guard against faults as a matter (of great consequence; for) faults are a deadly enemy.","r":"Guard against fault itself as your aim; fault alone is the enemy that brings ruin.","n":"The double ஏ, குற்றமே twice, refuses any other candidate. Parimelazhagar draws a strong conclusion from a Sanskrit view: external enemies can only bring ruin through such faults. பொருளாக is 'as your aim' for him; Pope's 'wealth' is a different and weaker reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:435":{"book":"kural","num":435,"t":["வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான் வாழ்க்கை எரிமுன்னர்","வைத்தூறு போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The prosperity of him who does not timely guard against faults, will perish like straw before fire.","r":"The life of one who doesn't guard before it comes will perish like straw set before a fire.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is coming is fault, supplied from the chapter's subject; even a small fault burns away great wealth.","குற்றம் வரக் கடவதாகின்ற முற்காலத்திலே அதனைக் காவாத அரசன் வாழ்க்கை"],["manakkudavar","What is coming is suffering, and failing to prepare against it is itself the fault.","இது முந்துற்றுக் காவல் செய்வன செய்யாமையும் குற்ற மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:436":{"book":"kural","num":436,"t":["தன்குற்றம் நீக்கிப் பிறர்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்","என்குற்ற மாகும் இறைக்கு?"],"p":"What fault will remain in the king who has put away his own evils, and looks after the evils of others.","r":"If he removes his own fault and can then see others' faults, what fault can attach to a ruler?","n":"Parimelazhagar turns this into a condition of legitimacy: punishing others' faults is itself a fault until one has cleared one's own, after which it becomes justice. The verse's order, தன்... பிறர், supports him, though the claim about the right to judge is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kutram","arasu","sengol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:437":{"book":"kural","num":437,"t":["செயற்பால செய்யா திவறியான் செல்வம்","உயற்பால தன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"The wealth of the avaricious man, who does not expend it for the purposes for which he ought to expend it will waste away and not continue.","r":"The wealth of one who clings to it, not doing what should be done with it, does not last but perishes.","n":"Stinginess doesn't keep wealth, on this view; it loses it. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that wealth left unused for virtue and growth has nothing to sustain it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:438":{"book":"kural","num":438,"t":["பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை எற்றுள்ளும்","எண்ணப் படுவதொன் றன்று"],"p":"Griping avarice is not to be reckoned as one among other faults; (it stands alone - greater than all).","r":"The miserliness called the clinging heart is not a thing to be counted among any.","n":"பற்றுள்ளம் uses a word close to the attachment renunciation speaks of, here applied to wealth in the Porul section.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","kutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It can't be counted among faults because it exceeds them all: it can dominate even when every good quality is present.","குற்றத் தன்மைகள் எல்லாவற்றுள்ளும் வைத்து எண்ணப்படுவது ஒன்று அன்று; மிக்கது."],["manakkudavar","It isn't counted because it is contemptible: useless to self and others, it is reckoned as nothing.","இஃது உலோபம் தனக்கும் பிறர்க்கும் பயன்படாமையால் ஒரு பொருளாக மதிக்கப்படாதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:44"},"k:439":{"book":"kural","num":439,"t":["வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை நயவற்க","நன்றி பயவா வினை"],"p":"Let no (one) praise himself, at any time; let him not desire to do useless things.","r":"Never admire yourself, and never set your heart on a deed that brings no good.","n":"The two clauses could be separate, or the second a consequence of the first, as Manakkudavar and Kalaignar take it. Parimelazhagar connects them too: self-admiration blinds one to place, time and strength, the subjects of chapters 48 to 50.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kutram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:440":{"book":"kural","num":440,"t":["காதல காதல் அறியாமை உய்க்கிற்பின்","ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்"],"p":"If (a king) enjoys, privately the things which he desires, the designs of his enemies will be useless.","r":"If you can enjoy what you love without others knowing of the love, your enemies' schemes come to nothing.","n":"A striking end. After a chapter on removing faults, the last verse manages one by concealing it. Parimelazhagar concedes that desire, anger and joy aren't to be wholly removed. The verse is a matter of security: a known desire is a door for the enemy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kutram","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:44"},"k:441":{"book":"kural","num":441,"t":["அறனறிந்து மூத்த அறிவுடையார் கேண்மை","திறனறிந்து தேர்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) ponder well its value, and secure the friendship of men of virtue and of mature knowledge.","r":"Knowing their worth and the way to go about it, choose the friendship of those of understanding who have matured in knowledge of virtue.","n":"மூத்த is not simply age: Parimelazhagar counts maturity in understanding, character and years. The verse doesn't say what office such people hold, though Manakkudavar takes them to be priests. For Parimelazhagar திறன் is the way of winning them, and for Manakkudavar it is their capacities.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","aram","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:442":{"book":"kural","num":442,"t":["உற்றநோய் நீக்கி உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும்","பெற்றியார்ப் பேணிக் கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) procure and kindly care for men who can overcome difficulties when they occur, and guard against them before they happen.","r":"Cherish and take as support those who have the quality of removing the affliction that has come and guarding before against what has not.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are priests and ministers: priests handle afflictions from the gods by rites, and ministers those from people by the four policies.","புரோகிதரையும் அமைச்சரையும் கூறியவாறாயிற்று."],["manakkudavar","These are ministers. He takes the verse's general word to refer to them, leaving priests to 441.","இது மந்திரிகளைக் கூட் மாறு கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:45"},"k:443":{"book":"kural","num":443,"t":["அரியவற்று ளெல்லாம் அரிதே பெரியாரைப்","பேணித் தமராக் கொளல்"],"p":"To cherish great men and make them his own, is the most difficult of all difficult things.","r":"To cherish the great and make them one's own is the rarest of all rare things.","n":"அரிது can mean hard to do, as Manakkudavar has it, or rare to attain, as Parimelazhagar does. The second adds his observation that a king who can have every rare thing still prizes this one, because the others come through it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:444":{"book":"kural","num":444,"t":["தம்மிற் பெரியார் தமரா ஒழுகுதல்","வன்மையு ளெல்லாந் தலை"],"p":"So to act as to make those men, his own, who are greater than himself is of all powers the highest.","r":"To conduct oneself so that those greater than oneself become one's own is the chief of all strengths.","n":"A paradox of power: the supreme strength is standing under someone. Parimelazhagar's தாம் அவர் வழிநின்று makes the king the follower, and he ranks this above wealth, army and fortress on the ground that it reaches afflictions they can't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","vali","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:445":{"book":"kural","num":445,"t":["சூழ்வார்கண் ணாக ஒழுகலான் மன்னவன்","சூழ்வாரைக் சூழ்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"As a king must use his ministers as eyes (in managing his kingdom), let him well examine their character and qualifications before he engages them.","r":"Since counsellors serve as his eyes, a ruler must take his counsellors after counsel.","n":"The verse plays on சூழ், which means to deliberate, to plot and to surround, and the commentators split along those senses.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu","therithal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The king should examine his counsellors before taking them on, because they are his eyes.","அரசன் அத்தன்மையராய அமைச்சரை ஆராய்ந்து தனக்குத் துணையாகக் கொள்க."],["manakkudavar","The king should be able to outplot and kill those who plot to kill him.","அரசன் தன்னைச் சூழ்ச்சியாற் கொல்ல நினைப்பாரைத் தானுஞ் சூழ்ச்சியாற் கொல்லவல்லவனாதல்"],["kalaignar","The ruler benefits by keeping the wise around him.","அறிஞர் பெருமக்களைச் சூழ வைத்துக் கொண்டிருப்பதே"]],"ch":"k:45"},"k:446":{"book":"kural","num":446,"t":["தக்கா ரினத்தனாய்த் தானொழுக வல்லானைச்","செற்றார் செயக்கிடந்த தில்"],"p":"There will be nothing left for enemies to do, against him who has the power of acting (so as to secure) the fellowship of worthy men.","r":"There is nothing enemies can do against one able to carry himself as one of the company of the worthy.","n":"தான் ஒழுக வல்லான்: having good company isn't enough; he too must act accordingly. It is the ability that protects, not only the associates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:447":{"book":"kural","num":447,"t":["இடிக்குந் துணையாரை யாள்வரை யாரே","கெடுக்குந் தகைமை யவர்"],"p":"Who are great enough to destroy him who has servants that have power to rebuke him ?","r":"Who has the standing to ruin those who keep as their support people who will rebuke them?","n":"ஆள்வாரை: such people must actually be employed and valued. Parimelazhagar adds that the rebuker needs to be free of the same faults and to love the king, so rebuke alone doesn't qualify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:448":{"book":"kural","num":448,"t":["இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்","கெடுப்பா ரிலானுங் கெடும்"],"p":"The king, who is without the guard of men who can rebuke him, will perish, even though there be no one to destroy him.","r":"A ruler without the guard of those who rebuke will perish even with no one to ruin him.","n":"Parimelazhagar's image is an elephant with no mahout, going off the path and ruining itself. கெடுப்பார் இலானும் makes enemies unnecessary to the story, and 447 and 448 form a pair, protection and exposure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:449":{"book":"kural","num":449,"t":["முதலிலார்க ஊதிய மில்லை மதலையாஞ்","சார்பிலார்க் கில்லை நிலை"],"p":"There can be no gain to those who have no capital; and in like manner there can be no permanence to those who are without the support of adherents.","r":"Those without capital have no profit; those without the support of a pillar have no standing.","n":"A merchant's image for a king's court. Allies are the principal rather than the return: everything else is earned on them. The verse doesn't limit itself to kings; the older commentators supply that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:450":{"book":"kural","num":450,"t":["பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்தே","நல்லார் தொடர்கை விடல்"],"p":"It is tenfold more injurious to abandon the friendship of the good, than to incur the hatred of the many.","r":"To let go one's tie with the good is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many.","n":"Pope and Kalaignar read கைவிடல் as abandoning a tie, whereas Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it as failing to take the great as support. His reason is practical: there are ways out of many enemies, but none out of having no good company.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thunai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:45"},"k:451":{"book":"kural","num":451,"t":["சிற்றினம் அஞ்சும் பெருமை சிறுமைதான்","சுற்றமாச் சூழ்ந்து விடும்"],"p":"(True) greatness fears the society of the base; it is only the low - minded who will regard them as friends.","r":"Greatness fears the small company; smallness at once takes it as kin.","n":"The verse gives the fear to greatness, not to a person lacking resolve. Parimelazhagar explains it through self-knowledge: the wise know their judgment can bend, while the small see only likeness and suspect nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:452":{"book":"kural","num":452,"t":["நிலத்தியல்பால் நீர்திரிந் தற்றாகும் மாந்தர்க்கு","இனத்தியல்ப தாகும் அறிவு"],"p":"As water changes (its nature), from the nature of the soil (in which it flows), so will the character of men resemble that of their associates.","r":"As water changes by the nature of the land, so a person's understanding becomes the nature of their company.","n":"Note the subject: it is அறிவு that changes, not behaviour or reputation. Parimelazhagar sharpens the image, with water pure in the sky and only turning on contact, and so with understanding held alone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:453":{"book":"kural","num":453,"t":["மனத்தானாம் மாந்தர்க் குணர்ச்சி இனத்தானாம்","இன்னான் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்"],"p":"The power of knowing is from the mind; (but) his character is from that of his associates.","r":"A person's perception comes from the mind; the word 'such a one' comes from their company.","n":"A clean division of labour: faculty is inner and reputation is social. Parimelazhagar assigns the mind only basic sense-awareness and gives all cultivated judgment to company, a step further than the verse says. Manakkudavar makes the word specifically blame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:454":{"book":"kural","num":454,"t":["மனத்து ளதுபோலக் காட்டி ஒருவற்கு","இனத்துள தாகும் அறிவு"],"p":"Wisdom appears to rest in the mind, but it really exists to a man in his companions.","r":"Understanding, while showing itself as though in the mind, lies for a person in their company.","n":"The verse is one of the Kural's most radical on the self: where understanding appears to be is not where it is. Parimelazhagar's 'the mind is where it shows, the company is its root' makes it explicit. The verse doesn't explain why.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:455":{"book":"kural","num":455,"t":["மனந்தூய்மை செய்வினை தூய்மை இரண்டும்","இனந்தூய்மை தூவா வரும்"],"p":"Chaste company is the staff on which come, these two things, viz, purity of mind and purity of conduct.","r":"Purity of mind and purity of deeds, these two, come leaning on purity of company.","n":"தூவா is a rare word, which Parimelazhagar glosses as a support and defends with a citation. The claim is causal: company is the thing the other two rest on, not a third kind of purity alongside them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:456":{"book":"kural","num":456,"t":["மனந்தூயார்க் கெச்சம்நன் றாகும் இனந்தூயார்க்கு","இல்லைநன் றாகா வினை"],"p":"To the pure-minded there will be a good posterity. By those whose associates are pure, no deeds will be done that are not good.","r":"Those pure of mind have a good எச்சம்; for those pure of company there is no deed that doesn't turn out well.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["inam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","எச்சம் is children: effects don't differ from their causes.","மனம் தூயராயினார்க்கு மக்கட்பேறு நன்று ஆகும்;"],["manakkudavar","எச்சம் is the wealth that remains after one.","மனநல்லார்க்குப் பின்பு நிற்கும் காண முதலான பொருள்கள் நல்லவாம்;"],["kalaignar","எச்சம் is fame.","மனத்தின் தூய்மையால் புகழும்,"]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:457":{"book":"kural","num":457,"t":["மனநலம் மன்னுயிர்க் காக்கம் இனநலம்","எல்லாப் புகழும் தரும்"],"p":"Goodness of mind will give wealth, and good society will bring with it all praise, to men.","r":"Goodness of mind gains a person prosperity; goodness of company gives all praise as well.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason for the second clause is neat: those able to give praise are the good themselves, and they are the company.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pugazh","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:458":{"book":"kural","num":458,"t":["மனநலம் நன்குடைய ராயினும் சான்றோர்க்கு","இனநலம் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Although they may have great (natural) goodness of mind, yet good society will tend to strengthen it.","r":"Though the worthy have goodness of mind in full, goodness of company is a protection to them.","n":"The verse makes its concession to the mind here, then refuses to let it stand alone. Even சான்றோர் are not exempt.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Good company keeps nurturing a goodness of mind that came from earlier good deeds.","அந் நல்வினை உள்வழியும் மனநலத்தை வளர்த்து வருதலின்"],["manakkudavar","The protection is social: good company keeps the worthy from being looked down on.","இனநலம் இகழ்ச்சி வாராமற் காக்குமென்றது."]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:459":{"book":"kural","num":459,"t":["மனநலத்தின் ஆகும் மறுமைமற் றஃதும்","இனநலத்தின் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"Future bliss is (the result) of goodness of mind; and even this acquires strength from the society of the good.","r":"From goodness of mind comes the next world, and even that gains strength from goodness of company.","n":"The verse names மறுமை in plain terms in the book on polity. Parimelazhagar notes that goodness of mind can slip from time to time, and good company holds it steady.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse concerns joy in the next life, which the mind's goodness yields and good company secures.","ஒருவற்கு மனநன்மையானே மறுமை இன்பம் உண்டாம்;"],["kalaignar","He doesn't mention the next life. The verse is about the support that company gives to a firm mind.","அவர் சார்ந்த இனத்தின் உறுதியும் அவருக்கு வலிமையான துணையாக"]],"ch":"k:46"},"k:460":{"book":"kural","num":460,"t":["நல்லினத்தி னூங்குந் துணையில்லை தீயினத்தின்","அல்லற் படுப்பதூஉம் இல்"],"p":"There is no greater help than the company of the good; there is no greater source of sorrow than the company of the wicked.","r":"There is no greater support than good company and nothing that brings more affliction than bad company.","n":"Parimelazhagar says the chapter's title, avoiding small company, has been argued mainly from the reverse: by praising good company. Only this last verse states both sides together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:46"},"k:461":{"book":"kural","num":461,"t":["அழிவதூஉம் ஆவதூஉம் ஆகி வழிபயக்கும்","ஊதியமும் சூழ்ந்து செயல்"],"p":"Let a man reflect on what will be lost, what will be acquired and (from these) what will be his ultimate gain, and (then, let him) act.","r":"Weigh what will be lost, what will arise, and the gain that comes of it later, and then act.","n":"The verse separates the immediate return from later gain. Parimelazhagar draws out the consequence: a deed that only breaks even now may still be worth doing if the later gain is there. So this is not a plain cost-benefit rule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:462":{"book":"kural","num":462,"t":["தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு","அரும்பொருள் யாதொன்றும் இல்"],"p":"There is nothing too difficult to (be attained by) those who, before they act, reflect well themselves, and thoroughly consider (the matter) with chosen friends.","r":"Nothing is out of reach for those who act after examining it with chosen company and considering it themselves.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the two verbs in order: first with the company, then alone. So consulting doesn't replace judgment. தெரிந்த also makes the counsellors themselves the first thing chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","inam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:463":{"book":"kural","num":463,"t":["ஆக்கம் கருதி முதலிழக்கும் செய்வினை","ஊக்கார் அறிவுடை யார்"],"p":"Wise men will not, in the hopes of profit, undertake works that will consume their principal.","r":"Those with understanding don't undertake a deed that loses the capital for the sake of gain.","n":"Parimelazhagar's example is political: marching to take another's land and losing one's own. He also reads செய்வினை as covering even work already begun, so sunk effort is no excuse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:464":{"book":"kural","num":464,"t":["தெளிவி லதனைத் தொடங்கார் இளிவென்னும்","ஏதப்பாடு அஞ்சு பவர்"],"p":"Those who fear reproach will not commence anything which has not been (thoroughly considered) and made clear to them.","r":"Those who fear the affliction called disgrace don't begin what isn't clear to them.","n":"The stated motive is shame, not loss. Parimelazhagar explains தொடங்கார்: once begun, a thing can't be dropped midway, so scorn would be added to the failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:465":{"book":"kural","num":465,"t":["வகையறச் சூழா தெழுதல் பகைவரைப்","பாத்திப் படுப்பதோ ராறு"],"p":"One way to promote the prosperity of an enemy, is (for a king) to set out (to war) without having thoroughly weighed his ability (to cope with its chances).","r":"Rising up without thinking through every aspect is one way of bedding your enemy in good soil.","n":"பாத்தி is a garden bed. The sting is that the hasty attacker does the enemy's cultivating for them: a failed attack strengthens its target.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:466":{"book":"kural","num":466,"t":["செய்தக்க அல்ல செயக் கெடும் செய்தக்க","செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்"],"p":"He will perish who does not what is not fit to do; and he also will perish who does not do what it is fit to do.","r":"One perishes by doing what is unfit to do, and perishes too by not doing what is fit.","n":"Omission gets the same verdict as commission. தக்க is not obviously moral: Parimelazhagar's list of the unfit includes the too-laborious, the useless and the doubtful, which makes it prudential.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:467":{"book":"kural","num":467,"t":["எண்ணித் துணிக கருமம் துணிந்தபின்","எண்ணுவம் என்பது இழுக்கு"],"p":"Consider, and then undertake a matter; after having undertaken it, to say \"We will consider,\" is folly.","r":"Think and then resolve on a task; to say 'we'll think after resolving' is a lapse.","n":"What is to be thought about isn't stated. For Parimelazhagar it is the means, the four strategies; for Manakkudavar it is the result. The first assumes the deed is already chosen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:468":{"book":"kural","num":468,"t":["ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம் பலர்நின்று","போற்றினும் பொத்துப் படும்"],"p":"The work, which is not done by suitable methods, will fail though many stand to uphold it.","r":"Effort not made in the right way will collapse, even if many stand by to protect it.","n":"The verse corrects the chapter 45 picture of allies as near-sufficient: support can't save effort that is on the wrong path. Parimelazhagar spells out which means suit which kind of person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","muyarchi","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:47"},"k:469":{"book":"kural","num":469,"t":["நன்றாற்ற லுள்ளுந் தவுறுண்டு அவரவர்","பண்பறிந் தாற்றாக் கடை"],"p":"There are failures even in acting well, when it is done without knowing the various dispositions of men.","r":"Even in doing good there is error, when it is done without knowing each person's character.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நன்று is the good stratagem, gifts and sweet words, used on rival kings, and the error is that the task fails.","வேற்று வேந்தர் மாட்டு நன்றான உபாயம் செய்தற்கண்ணும் குற்றம் உண்டாம்;"],["kalaignar","நன்று is kindness to anyone, and misjudged kindness comes back as harm.","ஒருவருடைய இயல்பைப் புரிந்து கொண்டுதான் நன்மையைக் கூடச் செய்ய வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:47"},"k:470":{"book":"kural","num":470,"t":["எள்ளாத எண்ணிச் செயல்வேண்டும் தம்மோடு","கொள்ளாத கொள்ளாது உலகு"],"p":"Let a man reflect, and do things which bring no reproach; the world will not approve, with him, of things which do not become of his position to adopt.","r":"Think and do what won't be scorned; the world doesn't accept what doesn't accord with oneself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","pugazh","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is tactical fit: the strong shouldn't use the means of the weak, such as gifts, and the weak shouldn't use force.","தாம் வலியராய் வைத்து மெலியார்க்கு உரிய கொடுத்தல் முதலிய மூன்றனைச் செய்தலும்,"],["manakkudavar","The issue is propriety: even if a thing can be accomplished, do only what others won't despise.","முடியுமாயினும், பிறராலிகழப்படாதவற்றை எண்ணிச் செய் தல் வேண்டும்;"]],"ch":"k:47"},"k:471":{"book":"kural","num":471,"t":["வினைவலியும் தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும்","துணைவலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்"],"p":"Let (one) weigh well the strength of the deed (he purposes to do), his own strength, the strength of his enemy, and the strength of the allies (of both), and then let him act.","r":"Weigh the strength of the deed, one's own strength, the opponent's strength and the strength of allies, and then act.","n":"The first item is surprising: the deed itself has strength. Parimelazhagar adds a strict rule the verse doesn't state, that one should act only if one's own side is greater, and avoid even an equal contest, since victory there is doubtful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:472":{"book":"kural","num":472,"t":["ஒல்வ தறிவது அறிந்ததன் கண்தங்கிச்","செல்வார்க்குச் செல்லாதது இல்"],"p":"There is nothing which may not be accomplished by those who, before they attack (an enemy), make themselves acquainted with their own ability, and with whatever else is (needful) to be known, and apply themselves wholly to their object.","r":"For those who know what they can do and what must be known, and proceed resting in that, nothing fails.","n":"The chapter's only verse of confidence, and it hangs on அதன்கண் தங்கி, where the commentators pull in nearly opposite directions.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Resting means total commitment: fixing mind, speech and body on the task while marching.","எப்பொழுதும் மன, மொழி, மெய்களை அதன் கண் வைத்துப் பகைமேல் செல்லும் அரசர்க்கு;"],["manakkudavar","Resting means restraint: staying within the measure of what one knows one can do.","அதன் பின்பு அவ்வளவிலே நின்று, ஒழுகுவராயின்,"]],"ch":"k:48"},"k:473":{"book":"kural","num":473,"t":["உடைத்தம் வலியறியார் ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி","இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்"],"p":"There are many who, ignoran t of their (want of) power (to meet it), have haughtily set out to war, and broken down in the midst of it.","r":"Many, not knowing the strength they have, rose on enthusiasm and were broken midway.","n":"A rare verse appealing to history, பலர். ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி uses the very quality demanded of kings in 382 as the cause of the fall. Not knowing runs both ways too, as உடைத்தம் வலி means the strength they do have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:474":{"book":"kural","num":474,"t":["அமைந்தாங் கொழுகான் அளவறியான் தன்னை","வியந்தான் விரைந்து கெடும்"],"p":"He will quickly perish who, ignorant of his own resources flatters himself of his greatness, and does not live in peace with his neighbours.","r":"One who doesn't live in accord, doesn't know his measure and admires himself perishes quickly.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes ஆங்கு அமைந்து about neighbouring kings: one must be either a friend or an enemy who knows his strength. Others read it as a general composure, or as respect for people.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:475":{"book":"kural","num":475,"t":["பீலிபெய் சாகாடும் அச்சிறும் அப்பண்டஞ்","சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்"],"p":"The axle tree of a bandy, loaded only with peacocks' feathers will break, if it be greatly overloaded.","r":"Even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle, if that load is piled too high.","n":"The two உம் make both the cart strong and the load light. The verse speaks only of the cart, and the allegory is supplied by the commentators.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The feathers are weak enemies: even the strong fail when many combine against them.","'எளியர்' என்று பலரோடு பகைகொள்வான். தான் வலியனே ஆயினும் அவர் தொக்க வழி வலியழியும்;"],["manakkudavar","The cart is a fortress: those who trust in strong forts learn that defences fail if the enemy grows too much.","இஃது அரண் மிகுதல் நன் றென்றிருப்பார்க்குப் பகைமிகின் அரண் நில்லா தென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:48"},"k:476":{"book":"kural","num":476,"t":["நுனிக்கொம்பர் ஏறினார் அஃதிறந் தூக்கின்","உயிர்க்கிறுதி ஆகி விடும்"],"p":"There will be an end to the life of him who, having climbed out to the end of a branch, ventures to go further.","r":"Those who have climbed to the tip of a branch and strive further will end their life.","n":"The climber has done well to get there, and that is the danger: this is a warning to the successful. The verse is purely image. Parimelazhagar applies it to a king who has gone as far as he can on a campaign, and Manakkudavar has him turn back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vali","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:477":{"book":"kural","num":477,"t":["ஆற்றின் அறவறிந்து ஈக அதுபொருள்","போற்றி வழங்கு நெறி"],"p":"Let a man know the measure of his ability (to give), and let him give accordingly; such giving is the way to preserve his property.","r":"Give in the proper way, knowing the measure; that is the way of guarding wealth and bestowing it.","n":"The verse fuses the two usually opposed, போற்றி and வழங்கு, into one way. Parimelazhagar's fixed ratio, two parts for spending, one for reserves and one for giving, comes from Tirikadugam, not the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:478":{"book":"kural","num":478,"t":["ஆகாறு அளவிட்டி தாயினுங் கேடில்லை","போகாறு அகலாக் கடை"],"p":"Even though the income (of a king) be small, it will not cause his (ruin), if his outgoings be not larger than his income.","r":"Though the channel of income be narrow, there is no ruin, if the channel of outgo isn't wider.","n":"ஆறு is a path or channel, which gives wealth a hydraulic image. Income's size doesn't determine ruin; only the ratio does. Parimelazhagar adds that even exact balance is safe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:479":{"book":"kural","num":479,"t":["அளவற஧ந்து வாழாதான் வாழ்க்கை உளபோல","இல்லாகித் தோன்றாக் கெடும்"],"p":"The prosperity of him who lives without knowing the measure (of his property), will perish, even while it seems to continue.","r":"The life of one who doesn't live knowing the measure seems to exist, then is not, and perishes without a trace.","n":"The verse has three stages: seeming, not being and not even seeming. Parimelazhagar points out that ruin isn't visible at the start, which is why the first stage is a seeming and the warning needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:480":{"book":"kural","num":480,"t":["உளவரை தூக்காத ஒப்புர வாண்மை","வளவரை வல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The measure of his wealth will quickly perish, whose liberality weighs not the measure of his property.","r":"Helpfulness that doesn't weigh the limit of what one has quickly ends the limit of one's wealth.","n":"Even the most praised social virtue gets a limit. Manakkudavar sees the objection coming: if spending must stay below income, how can one be helpful? This is presented as the answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","selvam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:48"},"k:481":{"book":"kural","num":481,"t":["பகல்வெல்லும் கூகையைக் காக்கை இகல்வெல்லும்","வேந்தர்க்கு வேண்டும் பொழுது"],"p":"A crow will overcome an owl in the day time; so the king who would conquer his enemy must have (a suitable) time.","r":"By day the crow beats the owl; kings who would win a contest need the right time.","n":"The image makes strength relational, as the same two birds reverse by night, a symmetry Manakkudavar makes explicit. Parimelazhagar's definition of time is unexpectedly concrete: weather that lets an army travel without disease and with food and water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:482":{"book":"kural","num":482,"t":["பருவத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல் திருவினைத்","தீராமை ஆர்க்குங் கயிறு"],"p":"Acting at the right season, is a cord that will immoveably bind success (to a king).","r":"Acting in accord with the season is the rope that binds fortune so that it doesn't leave.","n":"A rope only makes sense for something that tends to run. Parimelazhagar draws this from தீராமை: fortune's nature is to leave. Kalaignar takes திருவினை differently, as the success of a good deed, but the older commentators read wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:483":{"book":"kural","num":483,"t":["அருவினை யென்ப உளவோ கருவியான்","காலம் அறிந்து செயின்"],"p":"Is there anything difficult for him to do, who acts, with (the right) instruments at the right time ?","r":"Are there things said to be hard, if one acts with the right means and knows the time?","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that கருவியான் is there to say that even with the means, time is still needed: the tools don't make timing unnecessary.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:484":{"book":"kural","num":484,"t":["ஞாலம் கருதினுங் கைகூடுங் காலம்","கருதி இடத்தாற் செயின்"],"p":"Though (a man) should meditate (the conquest of) the world, he may accomplish it if he acts in the right time, and at the right place.","r":"Even the world will come to hand if one thinks of the time and acts by the place.","n":"This verse joins time and place and forms a bridge into the next chapter. The promise is grand, and the chapter's next verse immediately makes the same ambition the reason for doing nothing yet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:485":{"book":"kural","num":485,"t":["காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது","ஞாலம் கருது பவர்"],"p":"They who thoughtfully consider and wait for the (right) time (for action), may successfully meditate (the conquest of) the world.","r":"Those who have their mind on the world wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time.","n":"Greater ambition means more waiting, not less. Parimelazhagar adds that this applies even if one's strength is already superior, and identifies இருப்பு as a recognised policy, one of six, the opposite of marching out. கலங்காது could go with either the waiting or the aim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:486":{"book":"kural","num":486,"t":["ஊக்க முடையான் ஒடுக்கம் பொருதகர்","தாக்கற்குப் பேருந் தகைத்து"],"p":"The self-restraint of the energetic (while waiting for a suitable opportunity), is like the drawing back of a fighting-ram in order to butt.","r":"The holding back of the person of energy is like a fighting ram drawing back in order to charge.","n":"It reframes restraint as part of attack, so ஒடுக்கம் doesn't signal a lack of ஊக்கம். Kalaignar adds that this is silence in the face of cruelty and that it isn't from fear, which the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:487":{"book":"kural","num":487,"t":["பொள்ளென ஆங்கே புறம்வேரார் காலம்பார்த்து","உள்வேர்ப்பர் ஒள்ளி யவர்"],"p":"The wise will not immediately and hastily shew out their anger; they will watch their time, and restrain it within.","r":"The bright don't flare up outwardly at once; watching the time, they burn within.","n":"The anger isn't removed, only stored. Parimelazhagar gives the unusual reason that it must continue within: if it died away, one could not later stop the offender. Anger here is a resource.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaalam","vekuli","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:488":{"book":"kural","num":488,"t":["செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க இறுவரை","காணின் கிழக்காம் தலை"],"p":"If one meets his enemy, let him show him all respect, until the time for his destruction is come; when that is come, his head will be easily b rought low.","r":"Seeing those who would harm you, bear them; when you see their final limit, the head goes down.","n":"The verse doesn't say what is to be carried or whose head falls. Manakkudavar's தலையினாற் சுமக்க is the literal image behind Parimelazhagar's reading: carry them on the head, then tip them off.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Actually bow to the enemy until his time comes. Your submission makes him careless, and that is why his head falls.","பணியவே, அக்காலம் வந்திறும் வழி அவர் தகைவின்றி இறுவர்."],["kalaignar","Bear one's own feelings of enmity patiently, and the enemy falls by himself.","தங்களின் பகையுணர்வைப் பொறுமையுடன் தாங்கிக் கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:49"},"k:489":{"book":"kural","num":489,"t":["எய்தற் கரியது இயைந்தக்கால் அந்நிலையே","செய்தற் கரிய செயல்"],"p":"If a rare opportunity occurs, while it lasts, let a man do that which is rarely to be accomplished (but for such an opportunity).","r":"When what is hard to get has come, do at that very point what is hard to do.","n":"The double அரிய pairs them: rare time, rare deed. Parimelazhagar makes the best observation on அந்நிலையே: the moment won't stay. All the patience of the preceding verses exists for this one instant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:490":{"book":"kural","num":490,"t":["கொக்கொக்க கூம்பும் பருவத்து மற்றதன்","குத்தொக்க சீர்த்த இடத்து"],"p":"At the time when one should use self-control, let him restrain himself like a heron; and, let him like it, strike, when there is a favourable opportunity.","r":"When it is the season to fold up, be like the heron; when the moment is right, be like its strike.","n":"One image holds both modes, and the verse compares the person first to the bird and then only to the bird's stab. Parimelazhagar notes that the heron looks as though lifeless while it waits, which adds a touch of menace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:49"},"k:491":{"book":"kural","num":491,"t":["தொடங்கற்க எவ்வினையும் எள்ளற்க முற்றும்","இடங்கண்ட பின்அல் லது"],"p":"Let not (a king) despise (an enemy), nor undertake any thing (against him), until he has obtained (a suitable) place for besieging him.","r":"Begin no deed and scorn nothing until you have seen the ground for it in full.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["idam","therithal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","முற்றும் is a verb, to besiege: don't act against an enemy until you have ground for a siege.","பகைவரை முற்றுதற்கு ஆவதோர் இடம் பெற்றபின் அல்லது;"],["manakkudavar","முற்றும் is to complete: don't begin any deed until you see where it can be finished, and don't treat it as easy.","முடியுமிடங் கண்டாலல்லது, யாதொரு வினையுந் தொடங்கா தொழிக; எளிதென்றிகழாதொழிக,"],["kalaignar","முற்றும் is 'fully': choose a completely right place.","முற்றிலும் சரியான இடத்தைத் தேர்ந்தெடுத்து அச்செயலில் இறங்க வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:492":{"book":"kural","num":492,"t":["முரண்சேர்ந்த மொய்ம்பி னவர்க்கும் அரண்சேர்ந்தாம்","ஆக்கம் பலவுந் தரும்"],"p":"Even to those who are men of power and expedients, an attack in connection with a fortification will yield many advantages.","r":"Even for those of contentious strength, the gain that comes by joining a fortress gives many benefits.","n":"Even the strong: உம் concedes the objection. Manakkudavar takes the fort as one's own place, and Parimelazhagar, who thinks of a king on campaign, as a siege position. Either way, it is a protection for those who think they don't need it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:493":{"book":"kural","num":493,"t":["ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப இடனறிந்து","போற்றார்கண் போற்றிச் செயின்"],"p":"Even the powerless will become powerful and conquer, if they select a proper field (of action), and guard themselves, while they make war on their enemies.","r":"Even those without strength become strong and win, if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves.","n":"The ஆற்றி in ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி plays on the same root: place does not merely help the weak but turns them into the strong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:494":{"book":"kural","num":494,"t":["எண்ணியார் எண்ணம் இழப்பர் இடனறிந்து","துன்னியார் துன்னிச் செயின்"],"p":"If they who draw near (to fight) choose a suitable place to approach (their enemy), the latter, will have to relinquish the thought which they once entertained, of conquering them.","r":"Those who had planned will lose their plan, if those who approach know the place and hold to it.","n":"The loss is of a thought: the enemy's victory has been defeated before fighting starts.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துன்னியார் are the kings who have marched there and who stay close to their fort.","தாம் வினை செய்தற்கு ஏற்ற இடத்தினை அறிந்து சென்ற அரசர்;"],["manakkudavar","துன்னியார் are allies acting with one mind, so the verse is about concord among friends as well as place.","வினைசெய்யும் இடமறிந்து நட்டோரானவர் மனம் பொருந்திச் செய்வாராயின்."]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:495":{"book":"kural","num":495,"t":["நெடும்புனலுள் வெல்லும் முதலை அடும்புனலின்","நீங்கின் அதனைப் பிற"],"p":"In deep water, a crocodile will conquer (all other animals); but if it leave the water, other animals will conquer it.","r":"A crocodile wins in deep water; if it leaves the water, others kill it.","n":"The very same animal, and the reversal is total: பிற, anything at all. Parimelazhagar's maxim is that everyone is strong on their own ground, and the practical point is to fight where the enemy can't stand.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:496":{"book":"kural","num":496,"t":["கடலோடா கால்வல் நெடுந்தேர் கடலோடும்","நாவாயும் ஓடா நிலத்து"],"p":"Wide chariots, with mighty wheels, will not run on the ocean; neither will ships that the traverse ocean, move on the earth.","r":"Long chariots with strong wheels don't run on the sea; ships that run on the sea don't run on land.","n":"The verse states an obvious fact without comment, and the plainness is the point. Parimelazhagar notes the qualities listed, strong wheels and great size, as just those that don't transfer. He and Manakkudavar read it as being about fitting equipment to the terrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:497":{"book":"kural","num":497,"t":["அஞ்சாமை அல்லால் துணைவேண்டா எஞ்சாமை","எண்ணி இடத்தால் செயின்"],"p":"You will need no other aid than fearlessness, if you thoroughly reflect (on what you are to do), and select (a suitable) place for your operations.","r":"No support is needed but fearlessness, if you think everything through and act by the place.","n":"This verse suggests an answer to how 382's fearlessness can sit with 428's fear of what should be feared: fearlessness is what remains once the thinking is complete, எஞ்சாமை எண்ணி. Parimelazhagar glosses அஞ்சாமை as firmness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","therithal","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:498":{"book":"kural","num":498,"t":["சிறுபடையான் செல்லிடம் சேரின் உறுபடையான்","ஊக்கம் அழிந்து விடும்"],"p":"The power of one who has a large army will perish, if he goes into ground where only a small army can act.","r":"If one with a large army enters where one with a small army can move, his zeal is destroyed.","n":"The large army's size becomes the problem. Parimelazhagar explains that it can't act together and has no practice on that ground. What is destroyed is ஊக்கம், the fighting spirit, not only the force.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:499":{"book":"kural","num":499,"t":["சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும் மாந்தர்","உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a hazardous thing to attack men in their own country, although they may neither have power nor a good fortress.","r":"Even if they have no fortress or standing, it is hard to take on people on the land where they live.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the reason in psychology: attacked at home, such people won't resolve to flee; they will resolve to die, and even a large army breaks on that. The verse says மாந்தர், people, not soldiers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["idam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:50"},"k:500":{"book":"kural","num":500,"t":["காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும் கண்ணஞ்சா","வேலாள் முகத்த களிறு"],"p":"A fox can kill a fearless, warrior-faced elephant, if it go into mud in which its legs sink down.","r":"The jackal kills, in mud where feet sink, the unflinching elephant with a warrior's face.","n":"The chapter closes with its most extreme reversal, the jackal over the elephant. கண் அஞ்சா also splits the commentators, between a beast unafraid at a glance and one that won't obey its handlers.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idam","vali"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The elephant is one that strikes down spearmen. He rejects the variant வேலாழ், because a wounded elephant makes the jackal's success unremarkable.","'வேலாழ் முகத்த' என்று பாடம் ஓதுவாரும் உளர்; வேற்படை குளித்த முகத்தவாயின் அதுவும் நரி அடுதற்கு ஏதுவாய் முடிதலின், அது பாடம் அன்மை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","The elephant's face has spears sunk in it: he follows the variant reading.","கண்ணஞ்சாத வேலழுத்தப்பட்ட முகத்தினையுடைய களிற்றைக்"]],"ch":"k:50"},"k:501":{"book":"kural","num":501,"t":["அறம்பொருள் இன்பம் உயிரச்சம் நான்கின்","திறந்தெரிந்து தேறப் படும்"],"p":"Let (a minister) be chosen, after he has been tried by means of these four things, viz,-his virtue, (love of) money, (love of) sexual pleasure, and tear of (losing) life.","r":"A person is to be trusted after their nature has been examined through four things: virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life.","n":"My observation: the usual fourth term after virtue, wealth and pleasure is liberation, and here it is fear for life, the most worldly substitute possible. Parimelazhagar defends the wording against a variant, உயிரெச்சம், saying others changed it for not knowing the Sanskrit source.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","thelivu","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are four entrapment tests from Sanskrit polity, in which agents tempt the minister with staged plots, wealth, a woman and threats. Passing shows he won't change in future.","உபதை நான்கின் திறத்தான் மனவியல்பு ஆராய்ந்தால் பின்பு தெளியப்படும்."],["kalaignar","These are qualities to look for: firm in virtue, honest with money, not lost in pleasure and unafraid for his life.","தன்னுயிருக்கு அஞ்சாதவனாகவும் இருப்பவனையே ஆய்ந்தறிந்து ஒரு பணிக்கு அமர்த்த வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:502":{"book":"kural","num":502,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்து குற்றத்தின் நீங்கி வடுப்பரியும்","நாணுடையான் சுட்டே தெளிவு"],"p":"(The king's) choice should (fall) on him, who is of good family, who is free from faults, and who has the modesty which fears the wounds (of sin).","r":"Trust lies in one born of good family, free of faults and with a shame that recoils from any blemish.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","kudi","kutram","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","High birth is one of the qualifications in its own right, alongside the others.","உயர்ந்த குடியில் பிறந்து; குற்றத்தின் நீங்கி"],["kalaignar","He reverses the logic: one who is faultless and ashamed of wrong is the one to be considered high-born.","இருப்பவனையே உயர்குடியில் பிறந்தவன் எனத் தெளிவு கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:503":{"book":"kural","num":503,"t":["அரியகற்று ஆசற்றார் கண்ணும் தெரியுங்கால்","இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு"],"p":"When even men, who have studied the most difficult works, and who are free from faults, are (carefully) examined, it is a rare thing to find them without ignorance.","r":"Even in those who have studied the hard books and are flawless, it is rare, when examined, to find no blankness.","n":"வெளிறு is whiteness or emptiness, a striking word for ignorance: a pale spot in a person otherwise solid. Coming right after the demand for faultlessness, this verse admits no one fully passes, which prepares 504. Parimelazhagar takes it as occasional, due to an unsteady mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:504":{"book":"kural","num":504,"t":["குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி அவற்றுள்","மிகைநாடி மிக்க கொளல்"],"p":"Let (a king) consider (a man's) good qualities, as well as his faults, and then judge (of his character) by that which prevails.","r":"Examine the qualities, examine the faults too, examine which of these predominates, and take that.","n":"நாடி three times. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is what makes the verse humane: no one in the world is only good or only bad. Dominance can be either in weight or in number, so this isn't simple counting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["therithal","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:505":{"book":"kural","num":505,"t":["பெருமைக்கும் ஏனைச் சிறுமைக்கும் தத்தம்","கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்"],"p":"A man's deeds are the touchstone of his greatness and littleness.","r":"For greatness and for smallness alike, one's own deeds are the touchstone.","n":"The ஏ in கருமமே excludes other tests, which sits awkwardly with 502's demand for birth. Parimelazhagar says explicitly that birth, quality and understanding produce the greatness and that deeds are only the final test of it. Manakkudavar turns it into practice: give a small task first, then promote.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:506":{"book":"kural","num":506,"t":["அற்றாரைத் தேறுதல் ஓம்புக மற்றவர்","பற்றிலர் நாணார் பழி"],"p":"Let (a king) avoid choosing men who have no relations; such men have no attachment, and thereforehave no fear of crime.","r":"Beware of trusting those who are cut off: they have no attachments and aren't ashamed of blame.","n":"The logic is that attachment makes a person accountable: without ties there is nothing that shame can work on.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","sutram","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அற்றார் are those with no kin. Without them a person lacks the worldly custom that restrains him from blame.","சுற்றம் இல்லாரைத் தெளிதலை ஒழிக;"],["manakkudavar","அற்றார் are those without moral conduct.","ஒழுக்கமற்றாரைத் தேறுதலைத் தவிர்க;"],["kalaignar","They are those without principles, not those without kin.","நெறியற்றவர்களை ஒரு பணிக்குத் தேர்வு செய்வது கூடாது."]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:507":{"book":"kural","num":507,"t":["காதன்மை கந்தா அறிவறியார்த் தேறுதல்","பேதைமை எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"To choose ignorant men, through partiality, is the height of folly.","r":"To trust the ignorant with fondness as your support brings every kind of folly.","n":"Manakkudavar shows this is an argument, naming Parasara as holding that a king may trust those who love him. The verse refutes that. Parimelazhagar notes the folly is the king's, not only the appointee's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:508":{"book":"kural","num":508,"t":["தேரான் பிறனைத் தெளிந்தான் வழிமுறை","தீரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"Sorrow that will not leave even his posterity will come upon him chooses a stranger whose character he has not known.","r":"One who trusts an outsider without examining him brings trouble that doesn't end, even down his line.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thelivu","therithal","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வழிமுறை means descendants: the suffering passes to them, as the kin fall under the enemy.","அத்தெளிவு தன் வழிமுறையினும் நீங்காத துன்பத்தைக் கொடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","வழிமுறை means hereditary succession: the error is trusting an outsider as if he were of the ministerial line, and those from such lines may be trusted.","பிறனை ஆராயாதே வழிமுறையென்று தெளிந்தவனுக்கு அத் தெளிவு,"]],"ch":"k:51"},"k:509":{"book":"kural","num":509,"t":["தே றற்க யாரையும் தேராது தேர்ந்தபின்","தேறுக தேறும் பொருள்"],"p":"Let (a king) choose no one without previous consideration; after he has made his choice, let him unhesitatingly select for each such duties as are appropriate.","r":"Trust no one without examining them; having examined, trust what is to be trusted.","n":"The commentators limit the second half differently. For Parimelazhagar, trust comes for tasks suited to each person's capacity, while Manakkudavar has trust in one matter extend to all matters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:510":{"book":"kural","num":510,"t":["தேரான் தெளிவும் தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்","தீரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"To make choice of one who has not been examined, and to entertain doubts respecting one who has been chosen, will produce irremediable sorrow.","r":"Trusting without examining, and doubting one who has been trusted: both bring endless trouble.","n":"The second error is the unusual one. Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: the person senses it and thinks 'this won't last', so he slackens and becomes easy for enemies to detach. Doubt creates the very betrayal it fears.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:51"},"k:511":{"book":"kural","num":511,"t":["நன்மையும் தீமையும் நாடி நலம்புரிந்த","தன்மையான் ஆளப் படும்"],"p":"He should be employed (by a king), whose nature leads him to choose the good, after having weighed both the evil and the good in any undertaking.","r":"One whose nature, having looked at good and bad, has settled on the good is to be employed.","n":"Note the sequence: this person also looks at the bad. The verse doesn't ask for innocence. Parimelazhagar finds in the past tense புரிந்த a probationary stage: a trial task given first, then a more important one.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:512":{"book":"kural","num":512,"t":["வாரி பெருக்கி வளம்படுத்து உற்றவை","ஆராய்வான் செய்க வினை"],"p":"Let him do (the king's) work who can enlarge the sources (of revenue), increase wealth and considerately prevent the accidents (which would destroy it).","r":"Let him do the work who enlarges the sources, increases the yield and looks into what befalls them.","n":"A purely administrative definition of a good servant, with no moral term. உற்றவை includes, for Parimelazhagar, what comes from the king himself, whose interference counts among the hazards.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:513":{"book":"kural","num":513,"t":["அன்பறிவு தேற்றம் அவாவின்மை இந்நான்கும்","நன்குடையான் கட்டே தெளிவு"],"p":"Let the choice (of a king) fall upon him who largely possesses these four things, love, knowledge, a clear mind and freedom from covetousness.","r":"Trust lies in one who firmly has these four: love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire.","n":"Love comes first, even after the warnings of 507. Parimelazhagar directs it to the king and places the absence of desire at the moment when money comes into his hands, which is a very specific temptation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thelivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:514":{"book":"kural","num":514,"t":["எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும் வினைவகையான்","வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்"],"p":"Even when (a king) has tried them in every possible way, there are many men who change, from the nature of the works (in which they may be employed).","r":"Even when trusted after every kind of test, many people turn different through the nature of the work.","n":"The cause of change is the job itself, வினைவகையான். Office alters the person. Parimelazhagar's example is Kattiyankaran, the treacherous minister of Jivaka Chintamani, and he says it is rare for people to resist the pleasures of royal power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thelivu","vinai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:515":{"book":"kural","num":515,"t":["அறிந்தாற்றிச் செய்கிற்பாற்கு அல்லால் வினைதான்","சிறந்தானென்று ஏவற்பாற் றன்று"],"p":"(A king's) work can only be accomplished by a man of wisdom and patient endurance; it is not of a nature to be given to one from mere personal attachment.","r":"Work itself isn't such as to be given to anyone except one who knows and endures and can do it, just because he is favoured.","n":"The grammar makes வினை the subject. Work has its own nature and doesn't admit of favourites. Parimelazhagar takes சிறந்தான் as someone dear to the king, and Kalaignar as someone merely thought excellent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:516":{"book":"kural","num":516,"t":["செய்வானை நாடி வினைநாடிக் காலத்தோடு","எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்"],"p":"Let (a king) act, after having considered the agent (whom he is to employ), the deed (he desires to do), and the time which is suitable to it.","r":"Examine the doer, examine the deed, and act understanding how they fit with the time.","n":"The doer comes before the deed, which is not the expected order. Parimelazhagar's last step is a judgment on the conjunction: at this time, one of this quality doing a deed of this nature will succeed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:517":{"book":"kural","num":517,"t":["இதனை இதனால் இவன்முடிக்கும் என்றாய்ந்து","அதனை அவன்கண் விடல்"],"p":"After having considered, \"this man can accomplish this, by these means\", let (the king) leave with him the discharge of that duty.","r":"Having reasoned 'this one will finish this by this means', leave that to him.","n":"The verse is all pointing, with இதனை, இதனால், இவன், then அதனை, அவன். The distal pronouns of the second line themselves enact the handing over: the thing moves away from the king. விடல் is a real letting go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["therithal","vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:518":{"book":"kural","num":518,"t":["வினைக் குரிமை நாடிய பின்றை அவனை","அதற்குரிய னாகச் செயல்"],"p":"Having considered what work a man is fit for, let (the king) employ him in that work.","r":"After seeing a person is fit for the work, make him fully entitled to it.","n":"Recognition isn't enough: authority has to be conferred. Parimelazhagar makes it elevation so that he can carry the work out himself, and warns that without it the work fails. Kalaignar reads this as simply examining then appointing, which leaves it close to 517.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:519":{"book":"kural","num":519,"t":["வினைக்கண் வினையுடையான் கேண்மைவே றாக","நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு"],"p":"Prosperity will leave (the king) who doubts the friendship of the man who steadily labours in the discharge of his duties.","r":"Fortune leaves one who takes differently the closeness of a person devoted to the work.","n":"A rare observation about what success at work produces: familiarity. Parimelazhagar's setting is sharp: rivals tell the king this man acts as if he were family, and the king takes it as disrespect. If he punishes it, no one will commit themselves again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","thelivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:52"},"k:520":{"book":"kural","num":520,"t":["நாடோ றும் நாடுக மன்னன் வினைசெய்வான்","கோடாமை கோடா துலகு"],"p":"Let a king daily examine the conduct of his servants; if they do not act crookedly, the world will not act crookedly.","r":"Let the ruler look into it daily: if the one doing the work doesn't bend, the world won't bend.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the difficulty with trust and adds that the inspection should be done privately, without harming the person's entitlement.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The ruler inspects the officer's conduct. The officer is the one point through which the whole world can be checked.","வினை செய்வான் கோடாது ஒழிய உலகம் கோடாது;"],["kalaignar","The state checks on workers' condition. If those who labour don't wither, the world's prosperity doesn't wither.","உழைப்போர் உள்ளம் வாடாமல் இருக்கும் வரையில் உலகின் செழிப்பும் வாடாமல் இருக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:52"},"k:521":{"book":"kural","num":521,"t":["பற்றற்ற கண்ணும் பழைமைபா ராட்டுதல்","சுற்றத்தார் கண்ணே உள"],"p":"Even when (a man's) property is all gone, relatives will act towards him with their accustomed (kindness).","r":"Even when all is gone, to keep honouring old ties is found only among kin.","n":"பழைமை points back to shared history. Parimelazhagar takes it as help the fallen man gave when he had means, which turns kin loyalty into a kind of remembered debt. Kalaignar turns the description into a definition: those with this trait are kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:522":{"book":"kural","num":522,"t":["விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம் இயையின் அருப்பறா","ஆக்கம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"If (a man's) relatives remain attached to him with unchanging love, it will be a source of ever-increasing wealth.","r":"If kin whose love doesn't lapse come together, they yield many gains that keep on branching.","n":"Pope's flower and fadeless blossom come from reading அருப்பு as a bud. The commentators read it as branching, கிளைத்தல், which gives a neat play: கிளை also means kin. So kin make wealth put out kin of its own, though that last step is my reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:523":{"book":"kural","num":523,"t":["அளவளா வில்லாதான் வாழ்க்கை குளவளாக்","கோடின்றி நீர்நிறைந் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of one who does not mingle freely with his relatives, will be like the filling of water in a spacious tank that has no banks.","r":"The life of one who doesn't mingle freely is like a broad tank filling with water, with no bank.","n":"The tank does fill: the failure isn't in earning. Without kin, the wealth has nothing to hold it and spreads away uselessly. அளவளாவு is intimacy, and Parimelazhagar explains it through mutual sharing of mind, so mere proximity isn't enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:524":{"book":"kural","num":524,"t":["சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப் படஒழுகல் செல்வந்தான்","பெற்றத்தால் பெற்ற பயன்"],"p":"To live surrounded by relatives, is the advantage to be derived from the acquisition of wealth.","r":"The fruit of having gained wealth is to live surrounded by one's kin.","n":"It is striking how far this goes: a whole purpose for wealth in four words. Parimelazhagar's reason keeps it in Porul: it is what enables ruling free of enemies. Read without him, it is more tender than that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sutram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:525":{"book":"kural","num":525,"t":["கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின் அடுக்கிய","சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப் படும்"],"p":"He will be surrounded by numerous relatives who manifests generosity and affability.","r":"One who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in layer upon layer.","n":"Parimelazhagar identifies these with the Sanskrit policies of dāna and sāma. So the tender acts of the household are also two of the four instruments of statecraft, which he used in 467 against enemies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sutram","eegai","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:526":{"book":"kural","num":526,"t":["பெருங்கொடையான் பேணான் வெகுளி அவனின்","மருங்குடையார் மாநிலத்து இல்"],"p":"No one, in all the world, will have so many relatives (about him), as he who makes large gift, and does not give way to anger.","r":"No one on earth has more of a following than one who gives greatly and doesn't cherish anger.","n":"பேணான் doesn't mean he has no anger, only that he doesn't tend it. Parimelazhagar even takes it as not exceeding what a king needs. In 487 anger is nursed within against enemies. The verses may simply be about different audiences.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","eegai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:527":{"book":"kural","num":527,"t":["காக்கை கரவா கரைந்துண்ணும் ஆக்கமும்","அன்னநீ ரார்க்கே உள"],"p":"The crows do not conceal (their prey), but will call out for others (to share with them) while they eat it; wealth will be with those who show a similar disposition (towards their relatives).","r":"Crows don't hide, they call and eat; gain too belongs only to those of that nature.","n":"A humble bird is the model for royal conduct. The verse's உம் says gain too comes this way, and Parimelazhagar concludes that sharing yields wealth as well as virtue and pleasure. Hiding, கரவா, is what is excluded, so a person's private hoard is the fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:528":{"book":"kural","num":528,"t":["பொதுநோக்கான் வேந்தன் வரிசையா நோக்கின்","அதுநோக்கி வாழ்வார் பலர்"],"p":"Many relatives will live near a king, when they observe that he does not look on all alike, but that he looks on each man according to his merit.","r":"If a king looks at people not in common but by rank, many will live looking to that.","n":"A real problem: equal treatment drives away those who see themselves as higher. Parimelazhagar openly gives that as the reason.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Each kinsman is to be regarded according to his own standing; treating all alike would make the eminent leave.","எல்லாரையும் ஒரு தன்மையராக நோக்காது அரசன் தத்தம் தகுதிக்கு ஏற்ப நோக்குமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about singling out one person: it is the advice to appoint a crown prince.","இஃது ஒருவனை இளவரசாக்க வேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","All people are equal, and regard should follow ability, not rank.","அனைத்து மக்களும் சமம் எனினும், அவரவர்க்குரிய ஆற்றலுக்கேற்ப அவர்களைப் பயன்படுத்திக் கொண்டால்,"]],"ch":"k:53"},"k:529":{"book":"kural","num":529,"t":["தமராகிக் தற்றுறந்தார் சுற்றம் அமராமைக்","காரணம் இன்றி வரும்"],"p":"Those who have been friends and have afterwards forsaken him, will return and join themselves (to him), when the cause of disagreement is not to be found in him.","r":"Those who were kin and left will return to kinship when the cause of discord is gone.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The cause was in the king. If he removes it, they come back by themselves, as natural affection returns without being won again.","அவ்வமராமைக் காரணம் தன் மாட்டு இல்லையாகத் தானே உளதாம்."],["kalaignar","The cause was their pretext. They come back on realising it doesn't hold up.","அந்தக் காரணம் பொருந்தாது என்று உணரும்போது மீண்டும் உறவு கொள்ள வருவார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:53"},"k:530":{"book":"kural","num":530,"t":["உழைப்பிரிந்து காரணத்தின் வந்தானை வேந்தன்","இழைத் திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்"],"p":"When one may have left him, and for some cause has returned to him, let the king fulfil the object (for which he has come back) and thoughtfully receive him again.","r":"One who left and came back for a reason, the king should accept with care after attending to it.","n":"The contrast with 529 is subtle, as Parimelazhagar notes: there a reason for leaving is removed, and here there was none for leaving, only one for returning. He advises to grant what the person came for, but stay cautious: otherwise he may go to the enemy, but he has also shown no love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","arasu","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:53"},"k:531":{"book":"kural","num":531,"t":["இறந்த வெகுளியின் தீதே சிறந்த","உவகை மகிழ்ச்சியிற் சோர்வு"],"p":"More evil than excessive anger, is forgetfulness which springs from the intoxication of great joy.","r":"The slackness that comes of the elation of great joy is worse than excessive anger.","n":"A surprising ranking, because joy isn't usually the danger. சோர்வு is a letting go, and it comes when things go well. Parimelazhagar's reason is exact: anger sometimes at least kills an enemy, while this kills only oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:532":{"book":"kural","num":532,"t":["பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை அறிவினை","நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன் றாங்கு"],"p":"Forgetfulness will destroy fame, even as constant poverty destroys knowledge.","r":"Heedlessness kills fame as unremitting poverty kills understanding.","n":"The comparison is the striking claim. நிச்ச நிரப்பு is not poverty but chronic poverty, and Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar make it daily begging to fill the stomach. Understanding is treated as something that material conditions can destroy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","pugazh","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:533":{"book":"kural","num":533,"t":["பொச்சாப்பார்க் கில்லை புகழ்மை அதுஉலகத்து","எப்பால்நூ லோர்க்கும் துணிவு"],"p":"Thoughtlessness will never acquire fame; and this tenet is upheld by all treatises in the world.","r":"The heedless have no fame: that is the settled view of writers of every kind in the world.","n":"The Kural rarely appeals to an all-schools consensus. Parimelazhagar explains why it can: every aim of life fails when one is heedless, so the claim isn't limited to polity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pochchaappu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:534":{"book":"kural","num":534,"t":["அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை ஆங்கில்லை","பொச்சாப் புடையார்க்கு நன்கு"],"p":"Just as the coward has no defence (by whatever fortifications ha may be surrounded), so the thoughtless has no good (whatever advantages he may possess).","r":"The fearful have no fortress; likewise the heedless have no good.","n":"A fort is useless to one whose fear is inside it: the verse places the failure in the mind, not in the walls. So the heedless may have resources, and they do them no good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:535":{"book":"kural","num":535,"t":["முன்னுறக் காவாது இழுக்கியான் தன்பிழை","பின்னூறு இரங்கி விடும்"],"p":"The thoughtless man, who provides not against the calamities that may happen, will afterwards repent for his fault.","r":"One who slipped by not guarding beforehand will later, when struck, grieve over his own error.","n":"Regret is presented as the penalty. தன்பிழை matters: there is no one else to blame. Parimelazhagar adds that the things that could have been guarded against are enemies' moves, made by watching for such lapses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:536":{"book":"kural","num":536,"t":["இழுக்காமை யார்மாட்டும் என்றும் வழுக்காமை","வாயின் அதுவொப்பது இல்"],"p":"There is nothing comparable with the possession of unfailing thoughtfulness at all times; and towards all persons.","r":"If not slipping, with anyone, at any time, is had without fail, nothing equals it.","n":"Three universals. Parimelazhagar assigns each: toward all people, in good times and bad, in every matter. The condition வாயின் concedes how seldom this is met. Manakkudavar takes it as not making errors in giving justice, a narrower sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:537":{"book":"kural","num":537,"t":["அரியஎன்று ஆகாத இல்லைபொச் சாவாக்","கருவியால் போற்றிச் செயின்"],"p":"There is nothing too difficult to be accomplished, if a man set about it carefully, with unflinching endeavour.","r":"Nothing fails to be done on the ground of being hard, if done with the tool of unforgetting care.","n":"Attention is called a கருவி, a tool. Parimelazhagar notes that it is an inner instrument, the mind, set beside the external means of 483.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pochchaappu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:538":{"book":"kural","num":538,"t":["புகழ்ந்தவை போற்றிச் செயல்வேண்டும் செய்யாது","இகழ்ந்தார்க்கு எழுமையும் இல்"],"p":"Let (a man) observe and do these things which have been praised (by the wise); if he neglects and fails to perform them, for him there will be no (happiness) throughout the seven births.","r":"What has been praised must be carefully done; those who slight it and don't do it have nothing through seven births.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","piravi","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is praised is what polity texts prescribe to kings, and lapsing means hell, as with neglect of caste duty.","நீதி நூலுடையார் இவை அரசர்க்கு உரியன என்று உயர்த்துக் கூறிய செயல்களைக் கடைப்பிடித்துச் செய்க;"],["manakkudavar","What is praised is virtue, as praised by the exalted, and the verse is about not neglecting it.","இஃது அறத்தின்கண் இகழாமற் செய்வது கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","They are one's duties, and neglect means no rise in life. He doesn't mention births.","அப்படிச் செய்யாமல் புறக்கணிப்பவர்களுக்கு வாழ்க்கையில் உயர்வே இல்லை."]],"ch":"k:54"},"k:539":{"book":"kural","num":539,"t":["இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக தாந்தம்","மகிழ்ச்சியின் மைந்துறும் போழ்து"],"p":"Let (a king) think of those who have been ruined by neglect, when his mind is elated with joy.","r":"When swollen with one's own joy, think of those who were ruined by negligence.","n":"A practical remedy: history is used deliberately against one's own mood, at the moment it is hardest to think of it. Parimelazhagar notes that negligence and ruin arise together.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The memory is a corrective to mood: thinking 'we too would fall that way' stops one exulting.","கெட்டாரை உள்ளவே, 'நாமும் அவ்வாறே கெடுதும்' என்று அதன்கண் மைந்துறார் என்பது கருத்து"],["manakkudavar","It leads to a specific precaution: a king should have what he eats, wears and smears checked, because past kings died that way.","இஃது உண்பவை, உடுப்பவை, பூசுபவை சோதித்துக் கொள்க என்றது."]],"ch":"k:54"},"k:540":{"book":"kural","num":540,"t":["உள்ளியது எய்தல் எளிதுமன் மற்றுந்தான்","உள்ளியது உள்ளப் பெறின்"],"p":"It is easy for (one) to obtain whatever he may think of, if he can again think of it.","r":"To gain what one has thought of is easy, if one can keep thinking of what one has thought of.","n":"The verse is built on repetition of உள்ளியது, which enacts its own lesson. Parimelazhagar catches the particle மன்: it hints what remains unsaid, that this is just what people can't do. So 'easy' is an ironic word. It is easy only in the way the conditional describes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pochchaappu","manam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:54"},"k:541":{"book":"kural","num":541,"t":["ஓர்ந்துகண் ணோடாது இறைபுரிந்து யார்மாட்டும்","தேர்ந்துசெய் வஃதே முறை"],"p":"To examine into (the crimes which may be committed), to show no favour (to any one), to desire to act with impartiality towards all, and to inflict (such punishments) as may be wisely resolved on, constitute rectitude.","r":"To investigate, show no favour to anyone, hold to what is fitting for a ruler and act after consideration: that is justice.","n":"The definition is mainly procedural, four steps with no mention of outcome. கண்ணோடாது is a strong word for a book with a whole chapter praising கண்ணோட்டம். Parimelazhagar extends யார்மாட்டும் to those dearer than life, and Manakkudavar to one's own kin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","therithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:542":{"book":"kural","num":542,"t":["வானோக்கி வாழும் உலகெல்லாம் மன்னவன்","கோல் நோக்கி வாழுங் குடி"],"p":"When there is rain, the living creation thrives; and so when the king rules justly, his subjects thrive.","r":"All the world lives looking to the sky; the people live looking to the ruler's sceptre.","n":"நோக்கி has the sense of dependence as well as looking up. Parimelazhagar narrows the point: the sky gives food and the sceptre gives security, and without security the food is no use to the people. So the sceptre is the more basic of the two for them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:543":{"book":"kural","num":543,"t":["அந்தணர் நூற்கும் அறத்திற்கும் ஆதியாய்","நின்றது மன்னவன் கோல்"],"p":"The sceptre of the king is the firm support of the Vedas of the Brahmin, and of all virtues therein described.","r":"The ruler's sceptre stands as the origin of the books of the அந்தணர் and of virtue.","n":"ஆதி is a bold word. It makes politics the source of learning and morals, the opposite of what might be expected. Parimelazhagar notices this, because the Vedas are eternal for him, and explains ஆதி as meaning they don't function without the sceptre.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","aram","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அந்தணர் நூல் is the Veda: beginningless itself, but it needs the sceptre in order to operate.","வேதமும் அறனும் - அநாதியாயினும், செங்கோல் இல்வழி நடவா ஆகலின்,"],["kalaignar","They are books of the virtuous, with no reference to the Veda or to Brahmins.","ஓர் அரசின் செங்கோன்மைதான் அறவோர் நூல்களுக்கும் அறவழிச் செயல்களுக்கும் அடிப்படையாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:544":{"book":"kural","num":544,"t":["குடிதழீஇக் கோலோச்சும் மாநில மன்னன்","அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும் உலகு"],"p":"The world will constantly embrace the feet of the great king who rules over his subjects with love.","r":"The world stands embracing the feet of the great ruler who embraces his people and wields the sceptre.","n":"The same word தழீஇ appears twice, and the reciprocity is the point: the king embraces his people and the world embraces his feet. The embrace from above must come first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:545":{"book":"kural","num":545,"t":["இயல்புளிக் கோலோச்சும் மன்னவன் நாட்ட","பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு"],"p":"Rain and plentiful crops will ever dwell together in the country of the king who sways his sceptre with justice.","r":"Rain and harvest gather together in the land of the ruler who wields the sceptre according to its nature.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a literal effect: sky and earth work together and yield abundance where rule is just.","வானும் நிலனும் சேரத் தொழிற்பட்டு வளம் சுரக்கும் என்பதாம்."],["kalaignar","It is a comparison: just government is like seasonal rain and a fine harvest.","பெய்யும் மழையினால் வளமான விளைச்சல் கிடைப்பதற்கு ஒப்பானதாகும்."]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:546":{"book":"kural","num":546,"t":["வேலன்று வென்றி தருவது மன்னவன்","கோலதூஉங் கோடா தெனின்"],"p":"It is not the javelin that gives victory, but the king's sceptre, if it do no injustice.","r":"It is not the spear that gives victory but the ruler's sceptre, if it doesn't bend.","n":"வேல் and கோல் rhyme and look alike as long shafts. The comparison also lets the sceptre be read as itself a weapon. The verse makes the claim without saying how justice wins battles. Parimelazhagar only says a spear strikes true when the sceptre is straight, and quotes a Sangam poem in support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","vali","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:547":{"book":"kural","num":547,"t":["இறைகாக்கும் வையகம் எல்லாம் அவனை","முறைகாக்கும் முட்டாச் செயின்"],"p":"The king defends the whole world; and justice, when administered without defect, defends the king.","r":"The ruler guards all the world; justice guards him, if he doesn't falter in it.","n":"முட்டாச் செயின் means even when it costs. Parimelazhagar's examples are the most extreme ones in Tamil memory: the king who gave judgment on his own son and the king who cut off his own hand, both from the Silappatikaram. That shows what 'not faltering' is taken to require.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:548":{"book":"kural","num":548,"t":["எண்பதத்தான் ஓரா முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்","தண்பதத்தான் தானே கெடும்"],"p":"The king who gives not facile audience (to those who approach him), and who does not examine and pass judgment (on their complaints), will perish in disgrace.","r":"A ruler who isn't easily approached and who doesn't examine and do justice falls low and perishes by himself.","n":"தானே கெடும் is close to 448: the ruin needs no enemy. Parimelazhagar's 'low state' is sin and blame, and he supports it by quoting that virtue is death to those who have done wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:549":{"book":"kural","num":549,"t":["குடிபுறங் காத்தோம்பிக் குற்றம் கடிதல்","வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்"],"p":"In guarding his subjects (against injury from others), and in preserving them himself; to punish crime is not a fault in a king, but a duty.","r":"Guarding the people, caring for them and removing wrongdoing are no stain on a king: they are his work.","n":"வடு அன்று answers a charge that must have been in the air. Parimelazhagar makes it explicit: punishing one's subjects could seem against virtue, and he replies that purifying them of wrong is the king's caste duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","arasu","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:55"},"k:550":{"book":"kural","num":550,"t":["கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல் பைங்கூழ்","களைகட் டதனொடு நேர்"],"p":"For a king to punish criminals with death, is like pulling up the weeds in the green corn.","r":"A king's dealing with the cruel by death is equal to weeding out the young crop.","n":"The weeding image makes killing seem an act of care for the crop. It says nothing about who decides which are the weeds, and the next chapter opens on a king who may be one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kolai","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The king is to punish by death those who burn, poison, rob, and those who desire another's wife. Not doing so harms the world.","அரசன் கொடியவர்களைக் கொலையான் ஒறுத்துத் தக்கோரைக் காத்தல்;"],["manakkudavar","The cruel are a narrower list: thieves, highway robbers and plunderers.","கொடியாராவார் கள்வர், ஆறலைப்பார், சூறைகொள்வார்."],["kalaignar","கொலை is the crime and not the penalty: the state simply punishes those who commit murder and similar cruelty.","கொலை முதலிய கொடுமைகள் புரிவோரை, ஓர் அரசு தண்டனைக்குள்ளாக்குவது"]],"ch":"k:55"},"k:551":{"book":"kural","num":551,"t":["கொலைமேற்கொண் டாரிற் கொடிதே அலைமேற்கொண்டு","அல்லவை செய்தொழுகும் வேந்து"],"p":"The king who gives himself up to oppression and acts unjustly (towards his subjects) is more cruel than the man who leads the life of a murderer.","r":"A king who takes up harassing people and goes on doing wrong is crueller than those who have taken up murder.","n":"Both are given the same verb மேற்கொண்டு, taken up as a trade. Parimelazhagar's reason concerns duration: the killer's harm is for a moment, the oppressor's all the time. He adds the reason is wanting wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kolai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:552":{"book":"kural","num":552,"t":["வேலொடு நின்றான் இடுவென் றதுபோலும்","கோலொடு நின்றான் இரவு"],"p":"The request (for money) of him who holds the sceptre is like the word of a highway robber who stands with a weapon in hand and says \"give up your wealth\".","r":"The asking of one who stands with a sceptre is like one standing with a spear saying 'hand it over'.","n":"The cutting word is இரவு: the king is begging, or calls it that. A request backed by the power to punish isn't a request. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar agree that this is about demands beyond the fixed dues. Manakkudavar adds it is like robbery even when worded as a request.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:553":{"book":"kural","num":553,"t":["நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்","நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்"],"p":"The country of the king who does not daily examine into the wrongs done and distribute justice, will daily fall to ruin.","r":"A ruler who doesn't examine daily and do justice loses his land day by day.","n":"The repeated நாடொறும், and the play of நாடி and நாடு, make the decline as gradual as the neglect. Parimelazhagar notes two ways to read it: that the king loses the land's fruits or that the land itself decays.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","sengol","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:554":{"book":"kural","num":554,"t":["கூழுங் குடியும் ஒருங்கிழக்கும் கோல்கோடிச்","சூழாது செய்யும் அரசு"],"p":"The king, who, without reflecting (on its evil consequences), perverts justice, will lose at once both his wealth and his subjects.","r":"A ruler who bends the sceptre and acts without reflection loses resources and people together.","n":"சூழாது is the failure here, not malice. Pope's 'who counsel doth refuse' is more specific than the Tamil. Parimelazhagar makes the people the source of future wealth, so a bad king loses past and future income at a stroke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:555":{"book":"kural","num":555,"t":["அல்லற்பட்டு ஆற்றாது அழுதகண் ணீரன்றே","செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை"],"p":"Will not the tears, shed by a people who cannot endure the oppression which they suffer (from their king), become a saw to waste away his wealth ?","r":"Are not the tears shed by those who can't bear their affliction the weapon that wears away wealth?","n":"தேய்க்கும் is slow wearing: tears work like an abrasive. Parimelazhagar sees a saw and a tree in it, and says the real agent is the sin of causing affliction, shifted onto the tears. So he supplies a moral mechanism the verse leaves unstated. Kalaignar has the tears destroy the regime, though the verse says wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:556":{"book":"kural","num":556,"t":["மன்னர்க்கு மன்னுதல் செங்கோன்மை அஃதின்றேல்","மன்னாவாம் மன்னர்க் கொளி"],"p":"Righteous government gives permanence to (the fame of) kings; without that their fame will have no endurance.","r":"For rulers, enduring is by just rule; without it, the lustre of rulers doesn't endure.","n":"The verse plays on மன்னர், மன்னுதல், மன்னா, ruler and enduring in one root, as if a ruler is by definition one who lasts. Parimelazhagar takes ஒளி as fame and adds the implication that the ruler himself doesn't endure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sengol","kodungol","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:557":{"book":"kural","num":557,"t":["துளியின்மை ஞாலத்திற்கு எற்றற்றே வேந்தன்","அளியின்மை வாழும் உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"As is the world without rain, so live a people whose king is without kindness.","r":"As the lack of rain is to the earth, so the king's lack of tender regard is to those who live.","n":"The negative of 542, as Parimelazhagar points out. But note the shift: 542 needs the sceptre, and 557 fears the lack of அளி, kindness. The failure of a king here is one of feeling as well as of law.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","ali","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:558":{"book":"kural","num":558,"t":["இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை முறைசெய்யா","மன்னவன் கோற்கீழ்ப் படின்"],"p":"Property gives more sorrow than poverty, to those who live under the sceptre of a king without justice.","r":"Under the sceptre of a ruler who doesn't do justice, having is more painful than having nothing.","n":"Pope's couplet, adding a sting to poverty, misses this, though his gloss has it. Parimelazhagar spells out the reason with grim realism: only those who have something get shackled and tortured for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:56"},"k:559":{"book":"kural","num":559,"t":["முறைகோடி மன்னவன் செய்யின் உறைகோடி","ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்"],"p":"If the king acts contrary to justice, rain will become unseasonable, and the heavens will withhold their showers.","r":"If the ruler bends justice in what he does, the sky, bending the rain, won't pour.","n":"கோடி is used of both king and cloud, so the same bending passes between them, and the grammar itself does the work of linking them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is literal: rain in fact fails to come in season, and the next verse gives the cause.","அவன் நாட்டுப் பருவமழை இன்றாம் வகை மேகம் பொழிதலைச் செய்யாது."],["kalaignar","It is a practical chain: bad government lets reservoirs fall into ruin, so rain can't be stored.","நீரைத் தேக்கிப் பயனளிக்கும் இடங்கள் பாழ்பட்டுப் போகுமாதலால்,"]],"ch":"k:56"},"k:560":{"book":"kural","num":560,"t":["ஆபயன் குன்றும் அறுதொழிலோர் நூல்மறப்பர்","காவலன் காவான் எனின்"],"p":"If the guardian (of the country) neglects to guard it, the produce of the cows will fail, and the men of six duties viz., the Brahmins will forget the vedas.","r":"If the guardian doesn't guard, the yield of cows declines and those of the six occupations forget their books.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kodungol","kalvi","arasu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those of the six duties are Brahmins. Without milk and mantras there is no sacrifice, and that is why rain fails, which completes 559.","பசுக்கள் பால் குன்றியவழி அவியின்மையானும், அது கொடுத்தற்குரியார் மந்திரம் கற்பம் என்பன ஓதாமையானும், வேள்வி நடவாதாம்;"],["kalaignar","They are not a caste: the verse means that productive work and key occupations dwindle.","ஆக்கப்பணிகள் எதுவும் நடக்காது; முக்கியமான தொழில்களும் தேய்ந்து விடும்."]],"ch":"k:56"},"k:561":{"book":"kural","num":561,"t":["தக்காங்கு நாடித் தலைச்செல்லா வண்ணத்தால்","ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது வேந்து"],"p":"He is a king who having equitably examined (any injustice which has been brought to his notice), suitably punishes it, so that it may not be again committed.","r":"A king is one who weighs a case fairly and then punishes in proportion, so that the wrong does not go further.","n":"The chapter opens by defining the king through punishment, not against it. As I read it, dread is set apart from penalty by inquiry and proportion; the two matching words தக்காங்கு and ஒத்தாங்கு carry the whole standard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oruththal","kol","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:562":{"book":"kural","num":562,"t":["கடிதோச்சி மெல்ல எறிக நெடிதாக்கம்","நீங்காமை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Let the king, who desires that his prosperity may long remain, commence his preliminary enquires wi th strictness, and then punish with mildness.","r":"Those who want their prosperity to last long should raise the rod sharply and bring it down lightly.","n":"The rod image gives two audiences to one act: the raising is seen by would-be wrongdoers and the light landing by everyone else. If Manakkudavar is right, this verse softens the exact proportion that 561 has just asked for.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oruththal","veruvu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Begin as if going beyond measure, then act within measure: the penalty remains proportionate and only looks mild beside its start.","அளவிறப்பச் செய்வார்போல் தொடங்கி; மெல்ல எறிக-செய்யுங்கால் அளவிறவாமல் செய்க"],["manakkudavar","The penalty should actually be less than the offence deserves.","இது குற்றத்திற்குத் தக்க தண்டத்தைக் குறையச் செய்யவேண்டு மென்றது."],["kalaignar","The severity lies in admonishing to prevent wrongs, and the gentleness in the punishing itself.","கண்டிக்கும்போது கடுமை காட்டித், தண்டிக்கும் போது மென்மை காட்டுகிறவர்களின்"]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:563":{"book":"kural","num":563,"t":["வெருவந்த செய்தொழுகும் வெங்கோல னாயின்","ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The cruel-sceptred king, who acts so as to put his subjects in fear, will certainly and quickly come to ruin.","r":"A cruel-sceptred ruler who goes on doing what terrifies people will certainly and quickly perish.","n":"The verse supplies no mechanism: it asserts certainty (ஒருவந்தம்) and speed (ஒல்லை) and leaves the how to the verses that follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","kol"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:564":{"book":"kural","num":564,"t":["இறைகடியன் என்றுரைக்கும் இன்னாச்சொல் வேந்தன்","உறைகடுகி ஒல்லைக் கெடும்"],"p":"The king who is spoken of as cruel will quickly perish; his life becoming shortened.","r":"A king about whom the painful words 'our lord is harsh' are spoken will quickly perish, his tenure cut short.","n":"The harmful speech here is arguably the subjects', not the king's. A phrase people say of him can do what no enemy has done.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","kadunjol","kol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The painful word is what wounded subjects say of the king, and உறை means his days: his life shrinks and his wealth goes.","ஆயுளும் குறைந்து செல்வமும் கடிதின் இழக்கும்"],["manakkudavar","உறை is where he dwells, which turns against him; even if land and residence bear it, divine force ruins him.","இது நாடும் தான் உறையும் இடமும் பொறுப்பினும் தெய்வத்தினாற் கெடுவ னென்றது."],["kalaignar","The harsh speech is the ruler's own, and what is lost is the regime's standing.","கடுஞ்சொல் உரைக்கும் கொடுங்கோல் என்று குடி மக்களால் கருதப்படும் அரசு"]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:565":{"book":"kural","num":565,"t":["அருஞ்செவ்வி இன்னா முகத்தான் பெருஞ்செல்வம்","பேஎய்கண் டன்னது உடைத்து"],"p":"The great wealth of him who is difficult of access and possesses a sternness of countenance, is like that which has been obtained by a devil.","r":"The great wealth of one who is hard to get an audience with and grim of face has something of the ghost about it.","n":"Inaccessibility and a grim face count as violence here, though no one has been touched.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The wealth is as if a ghost had seized it: since no one approaches, it benefits neither him nor others.","அவனது செல்வம் தனக்கும் பிறர்க்கும் பயன்படாது"],["manakkudavar","The wealth is frightening, like seeing a ghost: no one will take from it, so no army gathers.","இது செல்வத்தை வாங்குவார் இன்மையின் படை சேராதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:566":{"book":"kural","num":566,"t":["கடுஞ்சொல்லன் கண்ணிலன் ஆயின் நெடுஞ்செல்வம்","நீடின்றி ஆங்கே கெடும்"],"p":"The abundant wealth of the king whose words are harsh and whose looks are void of kindness, will instantly perish instead of abiding long, with him.","r":"If a ruler is harsh of speech and without kindly regard, even vast wealth is lost on the spot instead of lasting.","n":"நெடுஞ்செல்வம் and ஆங்கே are set against each other: length of holdings gives no length of time. This verse's instant loss sits oddly beside the slow filing of 567, so the chapter seems to offer no single speed of ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","kannottam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:567":{"book":"kural","num":567,"t":["கடுமொழியும் கையிகந்த தண்டமும் வேந்தன்","அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்"],"p":"Severe words and excessive punishments will be a file to waste away a king's power for destroying","r":"Harsh words and punishment past all bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength.","n":"A file works by small repeated strokes, and here the king holds it against his own iron. Parimelazhagar assigns the harm: harsh words lose the army, and excessive punishment loses the country.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","oruththal","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:568":{"book":"kural","num":568,"t":["இனத்தாற்றி எண்ணாத வேந்தன் சினத்தாற்றிச்","சீறிற் சிறுகும் திரு"],"p":"The prosperity of that king will waste away, who without reflecting (on his affairs himself), commits them to his ministers, and (when a failure occurs) gives way to anger, and rages against them.","r":"The fortune of a king shrinks if he does not think matters through with his circle and then lashes out in anger.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","veruvu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He leaves deliberation to his ministers without sharing it, then rages at them when it fails, and they withdraw from him in fear.","அமைச்சர்மேல் வைத்து அவரோடு தானும் எண்ணிச் செய்யாத அரசன்"],["manakkudavar","The context is justice: he does not examine others' offences together with his council, and acts from anger.","பிறர் செய்த குற்றத்தைத் தனக்கு இனமானாரோடே அமைந்து ஆராயாத அரசன்"],["kalaignar","The fault is a failure to consult, followed by anger and crooked conduct, and the regime falls by itself.","கூட்டாளிகளிடம் கலந்து பேசாமல் சினத்திற்கு ஆளாகிக்"]],"ch":"k:57"},"k:569":{"book":"kural","num":569,"t":["செருவந்த போழ்திற் சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்","வெருவந்து வெய்து கெடும்"],"p":"The king who has not provided himself with a place of defence, will in times of war be seized with fear and quickly perish.","r":"A king who has not made his defences will, when war comes, be seized by fear and quickly perish.","n":"வெரு returns here pointed the other way: in a chapter on causing dread, the king himself ends up dreading. Manakkudavar draws this out by saying that a king must also not do what brings fear on himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["veruvu","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:570":{"book":"kural","num":570,"t":["கல்லார்ப் பிணிக்கும் கடுங்கோல் அதுவல்லது","இல்லை நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"The earth bears up no greater burden than ignorant men whom a cruel sceptre attaches to itself (as the ministers of its evil deeds).","r":"The harsh sceptre binds the unlearned to itself, and the earth carries no burden heavier than this.","n":"Cruelty is presented as having a staffing logic: it gathers those who lack the learning to resist it. Parimelazhagar puts the weight on the alliance and Manakkudavar on the king who forms it, which comes to much the same.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kol","kalvi","veruvu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:57"},"k:571":{"book":"kural","num":571,"t":["கண்ணோட்டம் என்னும் கழிபெருங் காரிகை","உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world exists through that greatest ornament (of princes), a gracious demeanour.","r":"Because that surpassingly great beauty called kindly regard exists, this world exists.","n":"A word of ornament (காரிகை) is given the weight of a cosmic condition. The chapter's elevated claim starts by calling kindness a beauty, not a duty.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The beauty is the king's, and without it his subjects live like deer beside a tiger.","கண்ணோட்டம் என்று சொல்லப்படுகின்ற சிறப்பு உடைய அழகு அரசர்மாட்டு உண்டு ஆகலான்"],["kalaignar","The verse is about anyone who has this quality, and they bring the world its greatness.","பெரும் அழகைக் கொண்டவர்கள் இருப்பதால்தான் பெருமை அடைகிறது."]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:572":{"book":"kural","num":572,"t":["கண்ணோட்டத் துள்ளது உலகியல் அஃதிலார்","உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"The prosperity of the world springs from the kindliness, the existence of those who have no (kindliness) is a burden to the earth.","r":"The world's ordinary workings rest on kindly regard, and the mere existence of those without it is a weight on the earth.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists what உலகியல் consists of: mutual helpfulness, protection, and forgiving lapses. On that reading the grace of the eye is social infrastructure, not private sentiment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:573":{"book":"kural","num":573,"t":["பண்என்னாம் பாடற்கு இயைபின்றேல் கண்என்னாம்","கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்"],"p":"Of what avail is a song if it be inconsistent with harmony ? what is the use of eyes which possess no kindliness.","r":"Of what use is melody that does not fit the song? Of what use is an eye with no kindly regard?","n":"The analogy treats kindness as attunement: the eye is related to regard as melody is related to singing, so it needs the other to fulfil itself. Parimelazhagar's long list of musical terms is a resource for the music tradition more than for the verse's argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:574":{"book":"kural","num":574,"t":["உளபோல் முகத்தெவன் செய்யும் அளவினால்","கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்"],"p":"Beyond appearing to be in the face, what good do they do, those eyes in which is no well-regulated kindness ?","r":"What do eyes that lack measured kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be there in the face?","n":"அளவினால் is easy to skip: even while condemning the lack, the verse measures the virtue, and Parimelazhagar takes this as excluding excess of it. This prepares for 578.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:575":{"book":"kural","num":575,"t":["கண்ணிற்கு அணிகலம் கண்ணோட்டம் அஃதின்றேல்","புண்ணென்று உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Kind looks are the ornaments of the eyes; without these they will be considered (by the wise) to be merely two sores.","r":"Kindly regard is the eye's only jewel, and without it the eye is known for a wound.","n":"The wound is what the observer perceives (உணரப் படும்), so the verse judges the eye by how it meets others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:576":{"book":"kural","num":576,"t":["மண்ணோ டியைந்த மரத்தனையர் கண்ணோ","டியைந்துகண் ணோடா தவர்"],"p":"They resemble the trees of the earth, who although they have eyes, never look kindly (on others).","r":"Those who have eyes yet do not turn them kindly on others are like trees fixed in the soil.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means a real tree, which stands joined to earth: they walk about but are as unresponsive as trees. He rejects the plaster-puppet reading.","அதனைச் சுதைமண்ணோடு கூடிய மரப்பாவை என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர். அஃது உரையன்மை"],["manakkudavar","It means a wooden doll made with plaster: people whose eyes have met another's and still show no regard are like the doll.","சுதைமண்ணோடு கூடச் செய்த மாப்பாவையோடு ஒப்பார்"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:577":{"book":"kural","num":577,"t":["கண்ணோட் டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர் கண்ணுடையார்","கண்ணோட்டம் இன்மையும் இல்"],"p":"Men without kind looks are men without eyes; those who (really) have eyes are also not devoid of kind looks.","r":"Those without kindly regard have no eyes, and those who truly have eyes do not lack it.","n":"The verse redefines possession of eyes by function. The second half turns the first around, closing off the idea of a sighted person who merely chooses not to look kindly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:58"},"k:578":{"book":"kural","num":578,"t":["கருமம் சிதையாமல் கண்ணோட வல்லார்க்கு","உரிமை உடைத்திவ் வுலகு"],"p":"The world is theirs (kings) who are able to show kindness, without injury to their affairs, (administration of justice).","r":"The world belongs to those able to extend kindly regard without damaging the task at hand.","n":"வல்லார் marks this as a skill and a difficult one. After seven verses of unqualified praise, the condition here gives the whole chapter its hard edge.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The task is the king's justice, which fails if he spares associates who have harmed others.","முறை செய்தல் ஆகிய தன் தொழில் அழியாமல்"],["manakkudavar","The task is the king's own interests: kindness must not cost him material loss.","அரசர்க்குப் பொருட்கேடு வாராமல் கண்ணோட வேண்டுமென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:579":{"book":"kural","num":579,"t":["ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும்கண் ணோடிப்","பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை"],"p":"Patiently to bear with, and show kindness to those who grieve us, is the most excellent of all dispositions.","r":"The highest disposition is to extend kindly regard and bear with even those whose way is to hurt you.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kannottam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பண்பினார் implies familiarity: those meant are people close to the king, and this is the top quality in kings.","'பண்பினார்' என்றதனான், அவர் பயிற்சி பெற்றாம்."],["kalaignar","It extends to anyone at all who is inclined to destroy you.","அழிக்க நினைத்திடும் இயல்புடையவரிடத்திலும் பொறுமை காட்டுவது"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:580":{"book":"kural","num":580,"t":["பெயக்கண்டும் நஞ்சுண் டமைவர் நயத்தக்க","நாகரிகம் வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire (to cultivate that degree of) urbanity which all shall love, even after swallowing the poison served to them by their friends, will be friendly with them.","r":"Those who want a refinement worth loving will see poison poured, drink it, and stay at ease.","n":"The verse names no pourer: the 'friends' in Pope and the associates in Parimelazhagar are supplied. Parimelazhagar's support comes from Natrinai 355, which offers a thread beyond the Kural, and it is where the identification of நாகரிகம with kindly regard rests.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","porai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நாகரிகம is kindly regard, and the poison comes from associates whom one's eyes cannot refuse.","நாகரிகம் என்பது கண்ணோட்டமாதல்"],["manakkudavar","நாகரிகம is the broader possession of good qualities across virtue, wealth and pleasure.","நாகரிகம் - அறம் பொருள் இன்பத்தின்கண் நற்குணங்கள் பலவும் உடைமை"]],"ch":"k:58"},"k:581":{"book":"kural","num":581,"t":["ஒற்றும் உரைசான்ற நூலும் இவையிரண்டும்","தெற்றென்க மன்னவன் கண்"],"p":"Let a king consider as his eyes these two things, a spy and a book (of laws) universally esteemed.","r":"Let a ruler hold spies and the well-regarded treatise, these two, firmly as his eyes.","n":"Immediately after a chapter that defines the eye by kindness, the eye is redefined by information. Parimelazhagar's pairing makes the spy the eye of flesh, which sees what is happening, and the treatise the eye of wisdom, which says what to do about it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:582":{"book":"kural","num":582,"t":["எல்லார்க்கும் எல்லாம் நிகழ்பவை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","வல்லறிதல் வேந்தன் தொழில்"],"p":"It is the duty of a king to know quickly (by a spy) what all happens, daily, amongst all men.","r":"To know quickly, at all times, everything happening to everyone is the king's work.","n":"Knowing is called the work itself. Parimelazhagar explains that this is a figure, since both favour and punishment depend on timely knowledge.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Everyone' is the three classes named in 584, and what is known is their good and bad words and acts.","'எல்லார்க்கும்' என்றது மூன்று திறத்தாரையும்."],["manakkudavar","'Everyone' is the surrounding kings, whether enemy, ally, middle or neutral, and both their circle and his own.","பகைவராகியும் நட்டாராகியும் மத்திமராகியும் உதாசீனராகியும் இருக்கின்ற அரசர்க்கும்"]],"ch":"k:59"},"k:583":{"book":"kural","num":583,"t":["ஒற்றினான் ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரியா மன்னவன்","கொற்றங் கொளக்கிடந்தது இல்"],"p":"There is no way for a king to obtain conquests, who knows not the advantage of discoveries made by a spy.","r":"A ruler who does not gather through spies and discern what it all means has no victory lying ready to take.","n":"The verse has two conditions: spying and discerning (பொருள் தெரியா). Gathering reports without weighing them fails as well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:584":{"book":"kural","num":584,"t":["வினைசெய்வார் தம்சுற்றம் வேண்டாதார் என்றாங்கு","அனைவரையும் ஆராய்வது ஒற்று"],"p":"He is a spy who watches all men, to wit, those who are in the king's employment, his relatives, and his enemies.","r":"A spy is one who examines everyone: those doing the king's work, his kin, and those who wish him ill.","n":"The list begins with the king's own servants and relatives; enemies come last. Surveillance turns inward first, and Kalaignar draws out of this a professional ethic: the spy must not sort people by favour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:585":{"book":"kural","num":585,"t":["கடாஅ உருவொடு கண்ணஞ்சாது யாண்டும்","உகாஅமை வல்லதே ஒற்று"],"p":"A spy is one who is able to assume an appearance which may create no suspicion (in the minds of others), who fears no man's face, and who never reveals (his purpose).","r":"A spy is one who can take an appearance that raises no suspicion, not flinch before eyes that probe him, and never let anything spill.","n":"உகாஅமை is the image of not spilling. Parimelazhagar's gloss shows his own social world: the unsuspected forms are those of Brahmins, merchants and the like.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:586":{"book":"kural","num":586,"t":["துறந்தார் படிவத்த ராகி இறந்தாராய்ந்து","என்செயினும் சோர்விலது ஒற்று"],"p":"He is a spy who, assuming the appearance of an ascetic, goes into (whatever place he wishes), examines into (all, that is needful), and never discovers himself, whatever may be done to him.","r":"A spy goes in the guise of renunciants, pushes through to examine what must be examined, and gives nothing away whatever is done to him.","n":"என்செயினும் quietly admits torture. Parimelazhagar sees this: having mentioned the four methods already, he says force is raised again because it is the hardest to bear.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","thuravu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He lives as complete renunciants and vow-keepers such as pilgrims do, and this commentator records separately that others read படிவம் as costume.","இதனுள் 'படிவம்' என்றதனை வேடமாக்கி, 'துறந்தார் வேடத்தாராகி' என்று உரைப்பாரும் உளர்."],["manakkudavar","He wears the costume of an ascetic and crosses the country's border to gather news.","தவஞ் செய்வார் வேடத்தராகி, நாட்டெல்லையைக் கடந்து போய்"],["kalaignar","No renunciant is involved: he simply seems unconnected with the affair.","நிகழ்வில் தொடர்பற்றவரைப் போலக் காட்டிக் கொண்டு"]],"ch":"k:59"},"k:587":{"book":"kural","num":587,"t":["மறைந்தவை கேட்கவற் றாகி அறிந்தவை","ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே ஒற்று"],"p":"A spy is one who is able to discover what is hidden and who retains no doubt concerning what he has known.","r":"A spy is one able to hear what has been concealed and to be free of doubt about what he has learned.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: a king can't act on a report delivered with doubt. The next verse then refuses to accept the spy's certainty, so in this chapter confidence is required of the source but not trusted by the user.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:588":{"book":"kural","num":588,"t":["ஒற்றொற்றித் தந்த பொருளையும் மற்றுமோர்","ஒற்றினால் ஒற்றிக் கொளல்"],"p":"Let not a king receive the information which a spy has discovered and made known to him, until he has examined it by another spy.","r":"Even what a spy has found and brought back should be confirmed through another spy.","n":"The commentators explain why from a suspicion of corruption: Parimelazhagar thinks of collusion with those being watched, and Manakkudavar of a spy taking money from rival kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:589":{"book":"kural","num":589,"t":["ஒற்றெற் றுணராமை ஆள்க உடன்மூவர்","சொற்றொக்க தேறப் படும்"],"p":"Let a king employ spies so that one may have no knowledge of the other; and when the information of three agrees together, let him receive it.","r":"Run spies so that one does not know of another, and accept what three say in agreement.","n":"This is a statement about independent evidence: agreement counts only among witnesses who could not have conferred. Parimelazhagar adds that agreement need only be in substance (பயனால்), and that if the accounts differ, the inquiry continues.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["otru","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:590":{"book":"kural","num":590,"t":["சிறப்பறிய ஒற்றின்கண் செய்யற்க செய்யின்","புறப்படுத்தான் ஆகும் மறை"],"p":"Let not a king publicly confer on a spy any marks of his favour; if he does, he will divulge his own secret.","r":"Do not honour a spy where others can see it; if the king does, he has himself let out the secret.","n":"The exposure comes from the patron, not the spy, and from gratitude, not carelessness: the honour itself is the leak. The verse does not forbid the reward, only its being seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["otru"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:59"},"k:591":{"book":"kural","num":591,"t":["உடையர் எனப்படுவது ஊக்கம் அஃ தில்லார்","உடையது உடையரோ மற்று"],"p":"Energy makes out the man of property; as for those who are destitute of it, do they (really) possess what they possess ?","r":"What makes someone 'one who has' is energy of mind; without it, do people really have even what they have?","n":"Ownership is put in question, not merely wealth's worth. Parimelazhagar supplies a practical reason: without the power to guard it, they will lose it too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:592":{"book":"kural","num":592,"t":["உள்ளம் உடைமை உடைமை பொருளுடைமை","நில்லாது நீங்கி விடும்"],"p":"The possession of (energy of) mind is true property; the possession of wealth passes away and abides not.","r":"Possession of the inner drive is real possession; possession of goods does not stay and slips away.","n":"The verse's word is உள்ளம், not ஊக்கம். Parimelazhagar makes the identification by metonymy, and Pope's '(energy of)' imports it, so the verse itself may be claiming somewhat more broadly that the mind is the true holding.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","selvam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:593":{"book":"kural","num":593,"t":["க்கம் இழந்தேமென்று அல்லாவார் ஊக்கம்","ஒருவந்தம் கைத்துடை யார்"],"p":"They who are possessed of enduring energy will not trouble themselves, saying, \"we have lost our property.\"","r":"Those who hold steady energy as their cash in hand do not fret, 'We have lost our gains'.","n":"கைத்து is money in the hand, and the image is the verse's point: energy is liquid in a way that lost property is not. Their calm is a calculation of assets, not detachment, and this differs from the equanimity grounded in fate at 379.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:594":{"book":"kural","num":594,"t":["க்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும் அசைவிலா","ஊக்க முடையா னுழை"],"p":"Wealth will find its own way to the man of unfailing energy.","r":"Prosperity itself asks the way and goes to the one whose energy does not waver.","n":"Wealth is personified as a traveller who asks directions, so the seeker has become the sought. Parimelazhagar glosses அசைவின்மை as not flagging in hardship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:595":{"book":"kural","num":595,"t":["வெள்ளத் தனைய மலர்நீட்டம் மாந்தர்தம்","உள்ளத் தனையது உயர்வு"],"p":"The stalks of water-flowers are proportionate to the depth of water; so is men's greatness proportionate to their minds.","r":"The length of a water-flower's stem matches the depth of the water, and a person's height matches their inner drive.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the point of the image: the stem keeps lengthening as long as the water rises, so there is no fixed ceiling. The analogy has a gap, though: water is outside the plant, whereas the mind is within. 'Lotus' is Pope's and Kalaignar's addition; the verse says only மலர்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","manam","perumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The height gained is worldly: increase in wealth and military force.","உயர்தல் - பொருள் படைகளான் மிகுதல்."],["manakkudavar","What is measured by the mind is energy itself, so the verse says how energy arises.","மாந்தரது உள்ளத்தின் அளவினது ஊக்கம்"]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:596":{"book":"kural","num":596,"t":["உள்ளுவ தெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல் மற்றது","தள்ளினுந் தள்ளாமை நீர்த்து"],"p":"In all that a king thinks of, let him think of his greatness; and if it should be thrust from him (by fate), it will have the nature of not being thrust from him.","r":"Whatever you think about, think of rising; even if it is thrust aside, it has the quality of not having been thrust aside.","n":"The verse names nothing that thrusts the aim aside. 'Fate' comes from Pope and from Parimelazhagar's பால்வகை, so this is less clearly a verse about fate than it appears in English.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Even if fate denies kings the height, the aim stands: good people will not blame it, since their exertion did not fail.","அவ்வுயர்ச்சி பால்வகையாற் கூடிற்றில்லையாயினும், அக்கருத்துத் தள்ளாமை நீர்மையுடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","A failed high aim is equal to having achieved it, and is beyond blame.","முயன்று பெற்றதனோடு ஒக்கும்"],["kalaignar","This is an instruction: even if it does not succeed, do not give up the thought.","அதற்காக அந்த நினைப்பை விடக்கூடாது."]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:597":{"book":"kural","num":597,"t":["சிதைவிடத்து ஒல்கார் உரவோர் புதையம்பிற்","பட்டுப்பா டூன்றுங் களிறு"],"p":"The strong minded will not faint, even when all is lost; the elephant stands firm, even when wounded by a shower of arrows.","r":"The strong in mind do not give way when things fall apart, as a bull elephant holds its ground under a sheaf of arrows.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads a crossover: 'not giving way' belongs to the elephant and 'upholding one's greatness' to the strong, so that each half lends the other its verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ookkam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:598":{"book":"kural","num":598,"t":["உள்ளம் இலாதவர் எய்தார் உலகத்து","வள்ளியம் என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"Those who have no (greatness of) mind, will not acquire the joy of saying in the world, \"we have excercised liaberality\".","r":"Those without inner drive never reach, in this world, the proud joy of saying 'we have been generous'.","n":"செருக்கு is a fault in 431, yet here it is a joy worth wanting. Generosity is presented as something one may feel proud about, and this is not a usual picture of selfless giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","velanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உள்ளம் is energy: without it the chain of effort, wealth, giving and pride never starts.","ஊக்கம் இல்லையாகவே முயற்சி, பொருள், கொடை செருக்கு இவை முறையே இலவாம்"],["kalaignar","உள்ளம் is the eagerness to give: without it one can't take pride in being a giver.","அள்ளி வழங்கும் ஆர்வம் இல்லாத ஒருவர்"]],"ch":"k:60"},"k:599":{"book":"kural","num":599,"t":["பரியது கூர்ங்கோட்டது ஆயினும் யானை","஦வ்ருஉம் புலிதாக் குறின்"],"p":"Although the elephant has a large body, and a sharp tusk, yet it fears the attack of the tiger.","r":"Though its body is huge and its tusks are sharp, an elephant fears when a tiger attacks.","n":"The verse says nothing about energy or kings. Parimelazhagar names it an allegory (பிறிது மொழிதல்), and the chapter's setting is all that justifies the moral.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:600":{"book":"kural","num":600,"t":["உரமொருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கைஅஃ தில்லார்","மரம்மக்க ளாதலே வேறு"],"p":"Energy is mental wealth; those men who are destitute of it are only trees in the form of men.","r":"A person's real firmness is the abundance of the inner drive; those without it are trees, and being human is the only difference.","n":"Parimelazhagar sharpens the insult: trees bear fruit and serve in temples, houses, chariots and ships, so the only thing left separating such people from trees is that they are not as useful. He also reads உரம் as understanding, as in 24, which joins will and intelligence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ookkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:60"},"k:601":{"book":"kural","num":601,"t":["குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம் மடியென்னும்","மாசூர மாய்ந்து கெடும்"],"p":"By the darkness, of idleness, the indestructible lamp of family (rank) will be extinguished.","r":"The unfailing lamp called lineage goes dark and dies when the grime called sloth creeps over it.","n":"A lamp said to be unfailing fails. Parimelazhagar explains மாசு through tamas (தாமத குணம்); that is his Samkhya vocabulary, which the verse does not use.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:602":{"book":"kural","num":602,"t":["மடியை மடியா ஒழுகல் குடியைக்","குடியாக வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Let those, who desire that their family may be illustrious, put away all idleness from their conduct.","r":"Those who want their family to be a family should conduct themselves with sloth treated as sloth.","n":"The repetition is the verse's device: மடியை மடியா and குடியைக் குடியாக. To be a family is an achievement here, not a birth fact.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மடியா means 'as sloth': regard it for what it is, as one says to treat fire as fire. He records the other reading.","நெருப்பிற் கொடியது பிறிதின்மை பற்றி, நெருப்பை நெருப்பாகவே கருதுக"],["manakkudavar","மடியா means killing: destroy the habit of sloth.","மடி செய்தலை மடித்து ஒழுகுக"]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:603":{"book":"kural","num":603,"t":["மடிமடிக் கொண்டொழுகும் பேதை பிறந்த","குடிமடியும் தன்னினும் முந்து"],"p":"The (lustre of the) family of the ignorant man, who acts under the influence of destructive laziness will perish, even before he is dead.","r":"The family of a fool who carries deadly sloth inside him dies before he does.","n":"The cruelty is in the order: he survives to see it. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the family depends on him for support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:604":{"book":"kural","num":604,"t":["குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும் மடிமடிந்து","மாண்ட உஞற்றி லவர்க்கு"],"p":"Family (greatness) will be destroyed, and faults will increase, in those men who give way to laziness, and put forth no dignified exertions.","r":"For those sunk in sloth without any excellent effort, the family declines and faults multiply.","n":"The contrast is not sloth against mere activity but against மாண்ட உஞற்று, refined exertion. The verse thus sets a higher bar than being busy; 607 repeats the phrase.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:605":{"book":"kural","num":605,"t":["நெடுநீர் மறவி மடிதுயில் நான்கும்","கெடுநீரார் காமக் கலன்"],"p":"Procrastination, forgetfulness, idleness, and sleep, these four things, form the vessel which is desired by those destined to destruction.","r":"Delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep, these four, are the pleasure-craft of those bound for ruin.","n":"The image lies in காமக் கலன்: a boat you board because you want to, and which then drops you mid-sea. Parimelazhagar records but does not accept a reading of கலன் as a desired ornament. Manakkudavar reads the four as one sequence: delaying a task, forgetting it, slacking, and sleeping instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:606":{"book":"kural","num":606,"t":["படியுடையார் பற்றமைந்தக் கண்ணும் மடியுடையார்","மாண்பயன் எய்தல் அரிது"],"p":"It is a rare thing for the idle, even when possessed of the riches of kings who ruled over the whole earth, to derive any great benefit from it.","r":"Even when what world-rulers possess comes to them, the slothful rarely reach any great fruit.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The wealth of world-rulers comes to them unearned, but without effort to guard it, it perishes. He records another reading, that such kings become their allies.","நிலம் முழுவதும் ஆண்டாரது செல்வம் தானே வந்து எய்திய இடத்தும்"],["manakkudavar","It is the kings of the earth themselves who, however well provided, lose out if they are slothful.","பூமியையுடைய வேந்தர் பல்பொருளினாலும் அமைந்த விடத் தும் மடியுடையாராயின்"],["kalaignar","படி means worthiness: even those who hold the affection of worthy people get nothing.","தகுதியுடையவரின் அன்புக்குப் பாத்திரமானவராக இருப்பினும்"]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:607":{"book":"kural","num":607,"t":["இடிபுரிந்து எள்ளுஞ் சொல் கேட்பர் மடிபுரிந்து","மாண்ட உஞற்றி லவர்"],"p":"Those who through idleness, and do not engage themselves in dignified exertion, will subject themselves to rebukes and reproaches.","r":"Those who cling to sloth and make no excellent effort end up hearing words of rebuke and scorn.","n":"கேட்பர் may hold the sting: they can only listen. Parimelazhagar builds a small story from இடி: friends scold first, see no result, and only then turn to contempt, and if friends do this, others need not be mentioned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:608":{"book":"kural","num":608,"t":["மடிமை குடிமைக்கண் தங்கின்தன் ஒன்னார்க்கு","அடிமை புகுத்தி விடும்"],"p":"If idleness take up its abode in a king of high birth, it will make him a slave of his enemies.","r":"If sloth settles in one of a family-building nature, it delivers him into slavery to his enemies.","n":"மடிமை, குடிமை and அடிமை rhyme in sequence, and the sound performs the fall from sloth through standing to bondage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madi","kudi","adimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:609":{"book":"kural","num":609,"t":["குடியாண்மை யுள்வந்த குற்றம் ஒருவன்","மடியாண்மை மாற்றக் கெடும்"],"p":"When a man puts away idleness, the reproach which has come upon himself and his family will disappear.","r":"When a person ends the rule of sloth over him, the faults that have come into his family and his manhood are gone.","n":"Sloth is a ruler here (மடியாண்மை), and one's own effort deposes it. This is the chapter's one verse of repair. Parimelazhagar makes the promise wide, since even faults from other causes vanish; Manakkudavar limits the faults to neglecting what the family needs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","kudi","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:61"},"k:610":{"book":"kural","num":610,"t":["மடியிலா மன்னவன் எய்தும் அடியளந்தான்","தாஅய தெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"The king who never gives way to idleness will obtain entire possession of (the whole earth) passed over by him who measured (the worlds) with His foot.","r":"A ruler free from sloth obtains, all at once, the whole expanse crossed by the one who measured with his foot.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madi","muyarchi","kol"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The one who measured is the deity who spanned all worlds with his stride, and the verse leaves him unnamed.","தன் அடியளவானே எல்லா உலகையும் அளந்த இறைவன்"],["kalaignar","It is a tireless human king who brings every place he goes within his footstep's range.","சோர்வில்லாத ஒரு மன்னன், அவன் சென்ற இடமனைத்தையும்"]],"ch":"k:61"},"k:611":{"book":"kural","num":611,"t":["அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை வேண்டும்","பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்"],"p":"Yield not to the feebleness which says, \"this is too difficult to be done\"; labour will give the greatness (of mind) which is necessary (to do it).","r":"One must not slacken by saying 'this is hard', because effort brings the greatness needed.","n":"Difficulty is measured against a self that labour itself enlarges, so the calculation 'this is too much for me' is wrong in its premise. Parimelazhagar makes this explicit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","perumai","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:612":{"book":"kural","num":612,"t":["வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல் வினைக்குறை","தீர்ந்தாரின் தீர்ந்தன்று உலகு"],"p":"Take care not to give up exertion in the midst of a work; the world will abandon those who abandon their unfinished work.","r":"Guard against letting a task die in the middle of the doing, for the world's bond with a person turns on how they have dealt with the work's unmet need.","n":"A short verse gives opposite readings of தீர்ந்தார்: those who left the work or those who finished it. Parimelazhagar glosses குறை from Purananuru 188, which is a thread beyond the Kural.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தீர்ந்தார் are those who abandoned the needed work, and the world abandons them.","வினையாகிய குறையைச் செய்யாது விட்டாரை உலகம் விட்டது"],["manakkudavar","தீர்ந்தார் are those who completed it, and the world does not leave them.","வினைக் குறையை முடித்தாரினின்றும் உலகம் விடப்பட்டதன்று"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:613":{"book":"kural","num":613,"t":["தாளாண்மை என்னும் தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்றே","வேளாண்மை என்னுஞ் செருக்கு"],"p":"The lustre of munificence will dwell only with the dignity of laboriousness or efforts.","r":"The proud distinction called helping others stands on the quality called labour.","n":"Manakkudavar draws the implication plainly: even doing virtue comes from this. A moral excellence has an economic foundation, and தாளாண்மை and வேளாண்மை rhyme to tie them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","velanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:614":{"book":"kural","num":614,"t":["தாளாண்மை இல்லாதான் வேளாண்மை பேடிகை","வாளாண்மை போலக் கெடும்"],"p":"The liberality of him, who does not labour, will fail, like the manliness of a hermaphrodite, who has a sword in its hand.","r":"The generosity of one who won't labour comes to nothing, like a sword in the hand of a பேடி.","n":"The image depends on a period contempt for a third-gender person, which commentators extend by saying the figure wishes to fight but is too afraid. Manakkudavar alone drops the word and says 'one who fears at the sight of arms'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","velanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:615":{"book":"kural","num":615,"t":["இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான் தன்கேளிர்","துன்பம் துடைத்தூன்றும் தூண்"],"p":"He who desires not pleasure, but desires labour, will be a pillar to sustain his relations, wiping away their sorrows.","r":"One who desires the work and not pleasure is a pillar who wipes away his kin's troubles and bears them up.","n":"Parimelazhagar finds a principle of causes here: wanting the cause rather than the effect gets all effects, and wanting the effect without the cause gets nothing. The pillar stands under others' weight, so personal renunciation of pleasure is here for one's household, not for liberation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","sutram","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:62"},"k:616":{"book":"kural","num":616,"t":["முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும் முயற்றின்மை","இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்"],"p":"Labour will produce wealth; idleness will bring poverty.","r":"Effort builds prosperity, and the absence of effort ushers in the absence of everything.","n":"முயற்றின்மை இன்மை: absence brings absence. The verse's symmetry leaves no room for a third cause, and that is what 371 and 377 dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","திருவினை means wealth, and specifically a king's six limbs of state.","அரசர்மாட்டு உளதாய முயற்சி அவரது செல்வத்தினை வளர்க்கும்"],["kalaignar","It yields excellent deeds and achievements, not wealth as such.","முயற்சிதான் சிறப்பான செயல்பாடுகளுக்குக் காரணமாக அமையும்."]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:617":{"book":"kural","num":617,"t":["மடியுளாள் மாமுகடி என்ப மடியிலான்","தாளுளான் தாமரையி னாள்"],"p":"They say that the black Mudevi (the goddess of adversity) dwells with laziness, and the Latchmi (the goddess of prosperity) dwells with the labour of the industrious.","r":"They say the dark goddess of misfortune lives in sloth, and she of the lotus lives in the labour of the unslothful.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', keeps a little distance from the claim: the verse reports it. தாள் can mean both foot and exertion, and so Lakshmi may be at the feet of the one who works.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madi","aalvinai","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The goddesses are taken as they are, dwelling respectively in sloth and in effort, with sin's blackness assigned to the first.","பாவத்தின் கருமை அதன் பயனாக முகடிமேல் ஏற்றப்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","The names are mere words used to point at the energetic person and the idler.","சொற்கள் முறையே முயற்சியில் ஊக்கமுடையவரையும்,"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:618":{"book":"kural","num":618,"t":["பொறியின்மை யார்க்கும் பழியன்று அறிவறிந்து","ஆள்வினை இன்மை பழி"],"p":"Adverse fate is no disgrace to any one; to be without exertion and without knowing what should be known, is disgrace.","r":"Lacking good fortune is no one's disgrace; failing to work, after learning what must be known, is the disgrace.","n":"The verse does not say effort defeats fate. It moves the question from causes to blame, and that is a different and more defensible claim than 620's. அறிவறிந்து adds a condition: blind labour isn't what is being asked for.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","arivu","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொறி is fate: people blame present faults, not fruits of past deeds.","உலகம் பழவினை பற்றிப் பழியாது, ஈண்டைக் குற்றமுடைமை பற்றியே பழிப்பது"],["manakkudavar","பொறி is religious merit: lacking it is no fault, and this answers those who say effort is useless without merit.","யார்க்கும் புண்ணியமின்மை குற்றமாகாது"],["kalaignar","The idea of blame from fate is itself wrong.","விதிப்பயனால் பழி ஏற்படும் என்பது தவறு"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:619":{"book":"kural","num":619,"t":["தெய்வத்தான் ஆகா தெனினும் முயற்சிதன்","மெய்வருத்தக் கூலி தரும்"],"p":"Although it be said that, through fate, it cannot be attained, yet labour, with bodily exertion, will yield its reward.","r":"Though divine order may deny the end sought, effort still pays the wage of the body's strain.","n":"கூலி is a modest word: a labourer's wage, not the prize. The verse concedes the goal may be lost, and secures only a floor.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தெய்வம் is fate: where it withholds, one gets a wage, and where it favours, much more. So don't wait on it.","முயன்ற வினை பால்வகையால் கருதிய பயனைத் தாராதாயினும்"],["manakkudavar","தெய்வம் stands for merit: without it there's no gain, yet effort pays in proportion to strain.","புண்ணியம் இன்மையால் ஆக்கம் இல்லையாயினும்"],["kalaignar","It refers to God and prayer: what crying out to God won't achieve, labour will.","கடவுளே என்று கூவி அழைப்பதால் நடக்காத காரியம்"]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:620":{"book":"kural","num":620,"t":["ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர் உலைவின்றித்","தாழாது உஞற்று பவர்"],"p":"They who labour on, without fear and without fainting will see even fate (put) behind their back.","r":"Those who toil on without flagging or delay will see even fate's back.","n":"உப்பக்கம் காண்பர் is a battle image: to see an enemy's back as it retreats. Pope's rendering, with fate put 'behind their back', turns the image around.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","aalvinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fate can obstruct once or twice but not always, so repeated effort outlasts it.","ஊழ் ஒருகாலாக இருகாலாக அல்லது விலக்கலாகாமையின், பலகால் முயல்வார் பயன் எய்துவர்"],["manakkudavar","They actually conquer fate, turning back the bad karma that blocks the fruit.","இஃது ஊழ்தன்னையும் வெல்வ ரென்றது."]],"ch":"k:62"},"k:621":{"book":"kural","num":621,"t":["இடுக்கண் வருங்கால் நகுக அதனை","அடுத்தூர்வது அஃதொப்ப தில்"],"p":"If troubles come, laugh; there is nothing like that, to press upon and drive away sorrow.","r":"When trouble comes, laugh; nothing else presses back on it as well.","n":"The laugh is treated as a tactic, not a sign of being unaffected. Parimelazhagar says it borrows the joy of a finished task in the middle of the task, and that the lift it gives supplies the strength to push through.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:622":{"book":"kural","num":622,"t":["வெள்ளத் தனைய இடும்பை அறிவுடையான்","உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்"],"p":"A flood of troubles will be overcome by the (courageous) thought which the minds of the wise will entertain, even in sorrow.","r":"Even a flood of suffering is gone when a person of understanding merely thinks it through in the mind.","n":"The verse doesn't say which thought. That gap is the whole dispute, and Pope's '(courageous)' fills it with one more answer.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","arivu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Suffering is nothing but a stance in the mind, and it vanishes when that stance is reversed.","இடும்பையாவது உள்ளத்து ஒரு கோட்பாடேயன்றிப் பிறிதில்லை"],["manakkudavar","The thought is that this is the fruit of one's deeds.","நெஞ்சினாலே வினைப்பய னென்று நினைக்கக் கெடும்"],["kalaignar","The thought is a practical one: how to defeat the trouble.","அதனை வெல்லும் வழி யாது என்பதை அறிவுடையவர்கள் நினைத்த மாத்திரத்திலேயே"]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:623":{"book":"kural","num":623,"t":["இடும்பைக்கு இடும்பை படுப்பர் இடும்பைக்கு","இடும்பை படாஅ தவர்"],"p":"They give sorrow to sorrow, who in sorrow do not suffer sorrow.","r":"Those who do not suffer at suffering make suffering suffer.","n":"The verse enacts its turn in four occurrences of one word. This reversal of subject and object recurs in 624 and 625, so the chapter's signature is grammatical. Parimelazhagar gives the mechanism: persisting finishes the task, and finishing ends all the troubles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:624":{"book":"kural","num":624,"t":["மடுத்தவா யெல்லாம் பகடன்னான் உற்ற","இடுக்கண் இடர்ப்பாடு உடைத்து"],"p":"Troubles will vanish (i.e., will be troubled) before the man who (struggles against difficulties) as a buffalo (drawing a cart) through deep mire.","r":"Trouble that meets one who pulls through every obstruction like a draught ox itself has trouble.","n":"Nothing about this image is heroic: Parimelazhagar cites Civakacintamani's picture of the ox on its side, nose to the ground. It sits beside the elephant of 597 and shows a humbler kind of strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:625":{"book":"kural","num":625,"t":["அடுக்கி வரினும் அழிவிலான் உற்ற","இடுக்கண் இடுக்கட் படும்"],"p":"The troubles of that man will be troubled (and disappear) who, however thickly they may come upon him , does not abandon (his purpose).","r":"Though they come stacked one on another, the troubles of one who is not undone are themselves in trouble.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:626":{"book":"kural","num":626,"t":["அற்றேமென்று அல்லற் படுபவோ பெற்றேமென்று","ஓம்புதல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"Will those men ever cry out in sorrow, \"we are destitute\" who, (in their prosperity), give not way to (undue desire) to keep their wealth.","r":"Would those who never knew how to clutch at wealth, saying 'we have got it', grieve, saying 'we have lost it'?","n":"Grief at losing is settled earlier, at the moment of having. The verse praises a lack of skill (தேற்றாதவர்), which is a wry way to describe a virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","selvam","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:627":{"book":"kural","num":627,"t":["இலக்கம் உடம்பிடும்பைக் கென்று கலக்கத்தைக்","கையாறாக் கொள்ளாதாம் மேல்"],"p":"The great will not regard trouble as trouble, knowing that the body is the butt of trouble.","r":"Knowing that the body is a target for suffering, they do not take trouble as a thing to be shaken by.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam","samanilai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மேல் means 'upon them', and the verse concerns bodies in all four states of birth, targets for a sword called suffering.","நாற்கதியினும் உள்ள உடம்புகள் இடும்பை என்னும் வாளுக்கு இலக்கு என்று தெளிந்து"],["manakkudavar","மேல் means 'the superior', and the context is the hardship taken on in austerity.","இது மேல் நன்மையாற் றவஞ் செய்யுங்கால் வருந் துன்பத்திற்கு அழியாதா ரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:628":{"book":"kural","num":628,"t":["இன்பம் விழையான் இடும்பை இயல்பென்பான்","துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்"],"p":"That man never experiences sorrow, who does not seek for pleasure, and who considers distress to be natural (to man).","r":"One who does not chase pleasure and calls suffering natural does not come to pain.","n":"Pain is prevented by an expectation, not a defence. Parimelazhagar sees two conditions: wanting pleasure brings pain, and so does trying to ward off suffering as if it were not natural.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:629":{"book":"kural","num":629,"t":["இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான் துன்பத்துள்","துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்"],"p":"He does not suffer sorrow, in sorrow who does not look for pleasure in pleasure.","r":"One who does not crave the pleasure within pleasure does not feel the pain within pain.","n":"The experience is not denied: the phrasing இன்பத்துள், துன்பத்துள் places a person within each. Parimelazhagar says they undergo them, yet do not desire or grieve, so that the deeds bear no fruit. That karmic consequence is his addition.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["samanilai","idukkan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Pleasure and pain here are those arising through work and effort, and both are held in the same way.","இரண்டையும் ஒரு தன்மையாகக் கோடலின், பயன்களும் இலவாயின."],["manakkudavar","It concerns desire: one who doesn't lose himself in sensual pleasure isn't undone by the pain it brings.","இது காமத்தால் வரும் துன்பத்திற்கு அழியாதாரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:63"},"k:630":{"book":"kural","num":630,"t":["இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின் ஆகுந்தன்","ஒன்னார் விழையுஞ் சிறப்பு"],"p":"The elevation, which even his enemies will esteem, will be gained by him, who regards pain as pleasure.","r":"If a person takes what hurts as pleasure, there comes to him a distinction even his enemies admire.","n":"The reward is a rival's respect, which is the one kind that can't be flattery. Parimelazhagar's reason is philosophical: pain is no part of the self but a passing mental stance (கணிகம், momentary), which the verse does not claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["idukkan","manam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:63"},"k:631":{"book":"kural","num":631,"t":["கருவியும் காலமும் செய்கையும் செய்யும்","அருவினையும் மாண்டது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who can make an excellent choice of means, time, manner of execution, and the difficult undertaking (itself).","r":"A minister is one excellent in means, timing, method and the difficult task itself.","n":"Manakkudavar makes this concrete in a military way: the hard task is seizing another's realm, the means elephants and horses, and the time a season with water and shade. The verse itself says nothing so specific.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:632":{"book":"kural","num":632,"t":["வன்கண் குடிகாத்தல் கற்றறிதல் ஆள்வினையோடு","ஐந்துடன் மாண்டது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who in addition to the aforesaid five things excels in the possession of firmness, protection of subjects, clearness by learning, and perseverance.","r":"A minister is one excellent in five things together, including resolve, protecting the people, learned knowing and labour.","n":"வன்கண் is hard-heartedness when it condemns impostors in 276, but here it is a qualification for office. The word's moral value depends on where it is pointed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","kalvi","aalvinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This verse lists four, and 'the five' are those of 631. He criticises those who split items here to reach five.","மேற்சொல்லிய அங்கங்கள் ஐந்துடனே திருந்த உடையானே அமைச்சனாவான்"],["manakkudavar","The five are named in this verse, including the guarding of the senses, and are needed before one becomes a minister.","அஞ்சாமையும், குடிகாத்தலும், இந்திரியங்களைக் காத்தலும், நூல் முகத்தானறிதலும், முயற்சியும்"]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:633":{"book":"kural","num":633,"t":["பிரித்தலும் பேணிக் கொளலும் பிரிந்தார்ப்","பொருத்தலும் வல்ல தமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who can effect discord (among foes), maintain the good-will of his friends and restore to friendship those who have seceded (from him).","r":"A minister is one able to divide others, keep his own side close, and bring back those who have broken away.","n":"The verse states three skills of division and joining, with no moral comment. Kalaignar's '(for the nation's good)' and his 'those who reformed' are moral framing added by him. Parimelazhagar links two of them to Sanskrit terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:634":{"book":"kural","num":634,"t":["தெரிதலும் தேர்ந்து செயலும் ஒருதலையாச்","சொல்லலும் வல்லது அமைச்சு"],"p":"The minister is one who is able to comprehend (the whole nature of an undertaking), execute it in the best manner possible, and offer assuring advice (in time of necessity).","r":"A minister is one able to discern, to act after weighing, and to speak in a way that settles the matter.","n":"ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல் doesn't name its audience. For Parimelazhagar the settling speech is aimed at the parties being divided or joined, and for Manakkudavar it settles doubtful matters, so eloquence here is persuasion on one view and judgement on the other.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:635":{"book":"kural","num":635,"t":["அறனறிந்து ஆன்றமைந்த சொல்லான்எஞ் ஞான்றுந்","திறனறிந்தான் தேர்ச்சித் துணை"],"p":"He is the best helper (of the king) who understanding the duties, of the latter, is by his special learning, able to tender the fullest advice, and at all times conversant with the best method (of","r":"One who knows what is right, whose speech is rich and settled, and who always knows the ways of doing things is the companion for deliberation.","n":"Virtue comes first in the list, in a chapter otherwise about competence. But Parimelazhagar narrows it to 'virtues done by the king', which makes it knowledge of duties more than the minister's own goodness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","aram","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:636":{"book":"kural","num":636,"t":["மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்க்கு அதிநுட்பம்","யாவுள முன்நிற் பவை"],"p":"What (contrivances) are there so acute as to resist those who possess natural acuteness in addition to learning ?.","r":"What over-subtle scheme can stand before those who have keen native intelligence together with treatises?","n":"Parimelazhagar names மதிநுட்பம் first since it must be given by the divine, leaving effort to the learning alone. His contrast of natural and artificial knowledge is a useful frame for the next verse. He also defends the text அதிநுட்பம் against அதினுட்பம், 'finer than that', the sense Manakkudavar's gloss gives; the verse's point survives either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:637":{"book":"kural","num":637,"t":["செயற்கை அறிந்தக் கடைத்தும் உலகத்து","இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்"],"p":"Though you are acquainted with the (theoretical) methods (of performing an act), understand the ways of the world and act accordingly.","r":"Even after knowing the method of doing a thing, act with knowledge of how the world goes.","n":"செயற்கை and இயற்கை are opposed: the made and the given. In this verse a treatise's knowledge is the artifice and the world is the nature, which is a surprising way to rank them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Treatise methods must fit the prevailing ways of the world, or the world will blame them.","நூல் நெறியே ஆயினும் உலக நெறியோடு பொருந்தாதன செய்யற்க"],["manakkudavar","'The world' means the court: the character of kings and the standing of their retinue.","உலகநடை அறிதலாவது அரசர் சீலமும் பரிவாரத்திலுள்ளார் நிலைமையும் அறிதல்."]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:638":{"book":"kural","num":638,"t":["அறிகொன்று அறியான் எனினும் உறுதி","உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்"],"p":"Although the king be utterly ignorant, it is the duty of the minister to give (him) sound advice.","r":"Though the king destroys wise counsel and himself knows nothing, the one at his side is bound to tell him what is good for him.","n":"The word for the minister here is உழையிருந்தான், 'the one close by'. Duty arises from nearness, and Parimelazhagar adds that the world will lay the king's fall at the silent minister's door.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறிகொன்று means destroying the understanding of those who advise, even with contempt.","அறிந்து சொல்லியாரது அறிவையும் அழித்து அரசன் தானும் அறியானே ஆயினும்"],["manakkudavar","He splits the phrase differently: 'let the king know' what the minister said, and even if he knows nothing, the minister must speak.","அரசன் அமைச்சன் கூறிய பொருளை யறிக"]],"ch":"k:64"},"k:639":{"book":"kural","num":639,"t":["பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரியின் பக்கததுள் தெவ்வோர்","எழுபது கோடி உறும்"],"p":"Far better are seventy crores of enemies (for a king) than a minister at his side who intends (his) ruin.","r":"Better seventy crore enemies than a minister at one's side who plots harm.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is that open enemies can be guarded against, but the one inside cannot. 'Seventy crore' is his example of a huge number, not a figure with meaning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["amaichu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:640":{"book":"kural","num":640,"t":["முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர்","திறப்பாடு இலாஅ தவர்"],"p":"Those ministers who are destitute of (executive) ability will fail to carry out their projects, although they may have contrived aright.","r":"Though they plan things properly, those without practical capacity do only what comes to no conclusion.","n":"A good plan is not enough. Parimelazhagar splits capacity into knowing remedies for obstacles and being firm in oneself, so this anticipates the chapter on firmness in action.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["amaichu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:64"},"k:641":{"book":"kural","num":641,"t":["நாநலம் என்னும் நலனுடைமை அந்நலம்","யாநலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று"],"p":"The possession of that goodness which is called the goodness of speech is (even to others) better than any other goodness.","r":"Having the goodness called goodness of tongue is a good that isn't contained in any other good.","n":"The verse is about the excellence of speech as such; it does not mention ministers. Parimelazhagar draws them in from the chapter's position and also argues that if others need it, ministers need it more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:642":{"book":"kural","num":642,"t":["ஆக்கமுங் கேடும் அதனால் வருதலால்","காத்தோம்பல் சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு"],"p":"Since (both) wealth and evil result from (their) speech, ministers should most carefully guard themselves against faultiness therein.","r":"Since gain and ruin both come from it, guard against any lapse in speech.","n":"சோர்வு is a slip, not malice. Parimelazhagar widens the stakes: a minister's lapse falls on all living beings at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:643":{"book":"kural","num":643,"t":["கேட்டார்ப் பிணிக்கும் தகையவாய்க் கேளாரும்","வேட்ப மொழிவதாம் சொல்"],"p":"The (minister's) speech is that which seeks (to express) elements as bind his friends (to himself) and is so delivered as to make even his enemies desire (his friendship).","r":"Speech is what is said so that it binds those who hear it and makes even those who have not heard want it.","n":"The second half is the striking one: speech is judged partly by its effect on those not present. Parimelazhagar lists good speech's qualities here as freedom from error, brevity, clarity, sweetness and valuable result.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Hearers are allies who accepted him and non-hearers are hostile parties won over. He criticises all others for reading தகையவாய் as 'having worth'.","நட்பாய் ஏற்றுக் கொண்டாரைப் பின் வேறுபடாமல் பிணிக்கும் குணங்களை அவாவி"],["manakkudavar","Those are the ones who asked and the ones who did not ask.","வினாவினாரைப் பிணித்துக் கொள்ளுந் தகையவாய், வினாவாதா ரும் விரும்புமாறு"]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:644":{"book":"kural","num":644,"t":["திறனறிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லை அறனும்","பொருளும் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்"],"p":"Understand the qualities (of your hearers) and (then) make your speech; for superior to it, there is neither virtue nor wealth.","r":"Speak with knowledge of what the moment and hearer require, for no virtue or gain goes beyond that.","n":"A surprising claim: fit speech ranks at the top of both virtue and gain. Parimelazhagar explains that the cause stands for the effect.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","aram","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The 'capacities' are differences of rank by birth, learning, conduct, wealth, form and age, and to speak according to them is the virtue.","குடிப்பிறப்பு, கல்வி, ஒழுக்கம், செல்வம், உருவம், பருவம் என்பவற்றான் வரும் தகுதி வேறுபாடுகள்."],["manakkudavar","It's the way of speaking: knowing this avoids slander, empty talk and lying, so it becomes virtue.","புறங்கூறாமையும் பயனில சொல்லாமையும் பொய்கூறா மையும் உளவாம்"],["kalaignar","It means knowing clearly the reason for saying something.","காரணத்தைத் தெளிவாக அறிந்து ஒன்றைச் சொல்ல வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:645":{"book":"kural","num":645,"t":["சொல்லுக சொல்லைப் பிறிதோர்சொல் அச்சொல்லை","வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து"],"p":"Deliver your speech, after assuring yourself that no counter speech can defeat your own.","r":"Speak your word once you know there is no other word that can defeat it.","n":"சொல் occurs five times, so the verse is a sort of closed argument. Parimelazhagar says winning means excelling in the qualities of speech, which is a broader standard than mere refutation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:646":{"book":"kural","num":646,"t":["வேட்பத்தாஞ் சொல்லிப் பிறர்சொல் பயன்கோடல்","மாட்சியின் மாசற்றார் கோள்"],"p":"It is the opinion of those who are free from defects in diplomacy that the minister should speak so as to make his hearers desire (to hear more) and grasp the meaning of what he hears himself.","r":"It is the way of those without blemish in excellence to speak so that others want more and to take the fruit from what others say.","n":"Mastery of speech includes a discipline of hearing. Parimelazhagar adds generosity: a master doesn't scorn others' faulty words, and takes what is useful in them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:647":{"book":"kural","num":647,"t":["சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான் அவனை","இகல்வெல்லல் யார்க்கும் அரிது"],"p":"It is impossible for any one to conquer him by intrique who possesses power of speech, and is neither faulty nor timid.","r":"It's hard for anyone to beat in a contest a person who can speak, doesn't slip, and isn't afraid.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss of 'speaking so that it is accepted' is uncomfortable: making people decide 'yes' even where the thing isn't actually in their interest. Eloquence, in that reading, isn't truthful by nature, and the chapter doesn't address this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:648":{"book":"kural","num":648,"t":["விரைந்து தொழில்கேட்கும் ஞாலம் நிரந்தினிது","சொல்லுதல் வல்லார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If there be those who can speak on various subjects in their proper order and in a pleasing manner, the world would readily accept them.","r":"The world quickly responds if it gets those who can speak in order and sweetly.","n":"பெறின், 'if it gets', implies rarity. Parimelazhagar cites a Sanskrit saying that a true speaker is one in a hundred thousand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தொழில் is what is being said, and the world accepts these matters readily.","சொல்லப்படும் காரியங்களை நிரல்படக் கோத்து"],["manakkudavar","தொழில் is work to do: people rush up and ask what they must do.","உலகத்தார் மேவி விரைந்து சென்று செய்யுந் தொழில் யாது என்று கேட்பர்"]],"ch":"k:65"},"k:649":{"book":"kural","num":649,"t":["பலசொல்லக் காமுறுவர் மன்றமா சற்ற","சிலசொல்லல் தேற்றா தவர்"],"p":"They will desire to utter many words, who do not know how to speak a few faultless ones.","r":"Those who don't know how to say a few faultless words surely long to say many.","n":"Loquacity is explained as a substitute for an ability, not an excess of it. Parimelazhagar takes மன்ற as a firm answer to those who actually admire nonstop speech as eloquence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:650":{"book":"kural","num":650,"t":["இண்ருழ்த்தும் நாறா மலரனையர் கற்றது","உணர விரித்துரையா தார்"],"p":"Those who are unable to set forth their acquirements (before others) are like flowers blossoming in a cluster and yet without fragrance.","r":"Those who can't unfold what they've learned so that others understand are like flowers blooming in clusters without scent.","n":"Coming right after 649's praise of few words, this verse praises expansion. Manakkudavar notices this: brevity alone isn't enough, and one must also expand where needed. In the image, learning is already in bloom; what is missing is the part that travels to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["solvanmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:65"},"k:651":{"book":"kural","num":651,"t":["துணைநலம் ஆக்கம் த்ருஉம் வினைநலம்","வேண்டிய எல்லாந் தரும்"],"p":"The efficacy of support will yield (only) wealth; (but) the efficacy of action will yield all that is desired.","r":"Good companions bring prosperity, while good action brings everything one could want.","n":"The verse does not say what 'everything' includes. Parimelazhagar expands it to virtue, wealth and pleasure here and desired stations hereafter, and he frames the contrast as between the visible good of allies and the inferred good of deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thooimai","vinai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:652":{"book":"kural","num":652,"t":["என்றும் ஒருவுதல் வேண்டும் புகழொடு","நன்றி பயவா வினை"],"p":"Ministers should at all times avoid acts which, in addition to fame, yield no benefit (for the future).","r":"One must at all times avoid action that yields neither renown nor good.","n":"என்றும் is pointed. Parimelazhagar says it holds in rising, shrinking and stable circumstances; Manakkudavar says it holds even when one has no means of doing anything else. The rule doesn't relax under pressure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:653":{"book":"kural","num":653,"t":["ஒஓதல் வேண்டும் ஒளிமாழ்கும் செய்வினை","ஆஅதும் என்னு மவர்"],"p":"Those who say, \"we will become (better)\" should avoid the performance of acts that would destroy (their fame).","r":"Those who say 'we shall rise' must refrain from the deed that dims their light.","n":"Ambition itself is the ground of the restraint. Parimelazhagar's summary is that gain that dims one's light isn't gain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thooimai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:654":{"book":"kural","num":654,"t":["இடுக்கண் படினும் இளிவந்த செய்யார்","நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those who have infallible judgement though threatened with peril will not do acts which have brought disgrace (on former ministers).","r":"Even when caught in trouble, those of unshaken clarity won't do what is degrading.","n":"Pope's 'ever-during vision' suggests mysticism; the Tamil நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி is steadiness of judgement. Parimelazhagar's reasoning is a calculation: trouble passes but disgrace doesn't, and what must come will come anyway.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","idukkan","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:655":{"book":"kural","num":655,"t":["எற்றென்று இரங்குவ செய்யற்க செய்வானேல்","மற்றன்ன செய்யாமை நன்று"],"p":"Let a minister never do acts of which he would have to grieve saying, \"what is this I have done\"; (but) should he do (them), it were good that he grieved not.","r":"Do nothing that makes you cry out 'What have I done!'; but if you do, it's good not to do the like.","n":"The second line turns on what அன்ன points to. Pope gives both answers: his couplet says sin no more, and his gloss says do not grieve.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Once done in confusion, don't go on grieving: regret displays a lack of remedy and firmness and spreads one's own shame. He says all others miss the connection.","பின் இருந்து அவ்விரங்கல்களைச் செய்யாதொழிதல் நன்று."],["manakkudavar","If one acts at all, one should not do anything like such deeds.","அவைபோல்வனவுஞ் செய்யாமையே நல்லது."],["kalaignar","Having done it, the good thing is not to continue it.","ஒருகால் அப்படிச் செய்து விட்டாலும் அச்செயலை மீண்டும் தொடராதிருப்பதே நன்று."]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:656":{"book":"kural","num":656,"t":["ஈன்றாள் பசிகாண்பான் ஆயினுஞ் செய்யற் க","சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Though a minister may see his mother starve; let him do not act which the wise would (treat with contempt).","r":"Even if you must watch the mother who bore you go hungry, don't do what the worthy condemn.","n":"The verse picks the hardest case it can imagine and refuses the exception. Its reach is the dispute.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","saandror","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This overrides a general dharma rule permitting wrong to feed aged parents, wife and infant, but only for ministers, who have standing and royal duties.","தீயன பலவுஞ் செய்தாயினும் புறந்தருக' என்னும் அறநூற்பொது விதி"],["kalaignar","It applies to anyone, with no exception: not even to relieve a mother in the throes of hunger.","பசியால் துடிக்கும் தனது தாயின் வேதனையைத் தணிப்பதற்காகக்கூட"]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:657":{"book":"kural","num":657,"t":["பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கத்தின் சான்றோர்","கழிநல் குரவே தலை"],"p":"Far more excellent is the extreme poverty of the wise than wealth obtained by heaping up of sinful deeds.","r":"The utter poverty of the worthy is above wealth gained under a load of blame.","n":"Manakkudavar sees this as a reply to an objection: living by these rules will leave you poor. The verse concedes it. Poverty is not denied or softened by கழி, 'extreme'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","pazhi","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:658":{"book":"kural","num":658,"t":["கடிந்த கடிந்தொரார் செய்தார்க்கு அவைதாம்","முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும்"],"p":"The actions of those, who have not desisted from doing deeds forbidden (by the great), will, even if they succeed, cause them sorrow.","r":"Even when they succeed, the forbidden things people do without shunning them bring anguish.","n":"முடிந்தாலும் answers the practical objection that the end might come off. Parimelazhagar goes further and doubts it: such acts won't usually succeed, and even if somehow they do, pain follows.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thooimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:659":{"book":"kural","num":659,"t":["அழக் கொண்ட எல்லாம் அழப்போம் இழப்பினும்","பிற்பயக்கும் நற்பா லவை"],"p":"All that has been obtained with tears (to the victim) will depart with tears (to himself); but what has been by fair means; though with loss at first, will afterwards yield fruit.","r":"All that is taken with tears leaves with tears; what comes from good ways, even if lost, pays later.","n":"The verse names no one in அழ. The commentators divide the tears, so the victim weeps on taking and the taker on losing, and this moral symmetry is part of the sound of the line. பின் covers the next birth for Parimelazhagar, which the verse does not specify.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:66"},"k:660":{"book":"kural","num":660,"t":["சலத்தால் பொருள்செய்தே மார்த்தல் பசுமண்","கலத்துள்நீர் பெய்திரீஇ யற்று"],"p":"(For a minister) to protect (his king) with wealth obtained by foul means is like preserving a vessel of wet clay by filling it with water.","r":"Making wealth by deceit and feeling secure in it is like pouring water into an unfired clay pot.","n":"Both pot and water are lost: the wrongly got wealth also destroys the one it was meant to protect.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","thooimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏமார்த்தல் means making the king safe with such wealth. He attacks those who read it as rejoicing, since that breaks the simile's form.","பிறரெல்லாம், 'ஏமாத்தல்' என்று பாடமோதி, அதற்கு மகிழ்தல் என்றும்"],["manakkudavar","It means delight: one enjoys what one gained by deception, and both person and wealth perish.","வஞ்சத்தாலே பொருள் தேடி மகிழ்ந்திருத்தல்"]],"ch":"k:66"},"k:661":{"book":"kural","num":661,"t":["வினைத்திட்பம் என்பது ஒருவன் மனத்திட்பம்","மற்றைய எல்லாம் பிற"],"p":"Firmness in action is (simply) one's firmness of mind; all other (abilities) are not of this nature.","r":"What is called firmness in action is one's firmness of mind; all else is something other.","n":"Parimelazhagar points to the word choice: மற்றைய admits these others are related, and பிற denies that they count here. Manakkudavar takes 'the others' to be means and methods.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","manam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:662":{"book":"kural","num":662,"t":["ஊறொரால் உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை இவ்விரண்டின்","ஆறென்பர் ஆய்ந்தவர் கோள்"],"p":"Not to perform a ruinous act, and not to be discouraged by the ruinous termination of an act, are the two maxims which, the wise say, from the principles of those who have investigated the subject.","r":"Avoiding harm beforehand and not giving way once it has come are, those who have studied it say, the path of these two.","n":"Parimelazhagar traces the maxim to the ministers of gods and demons, Jupiter and Venus, and so explains why clean action slips in here. The verse itself mentions only 'those who have studied'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","idukkan","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Avoid deeds likely to fail, and don't flag if fate spoils a deed. He rejects the variant ஓரார் as ungrammatical.","பழுதுபடும் வினைகளைச் செய்யாமையும், செய்யும் வினை தெய்வத்தான் பழுதுபட்ட வழி அதற்குத் தளராமையும்"],["manakkudavar","Reading ஓரார்: don't dwell on the suffering that action involves, and if it comes, don't weaken.","வினை செய்யுங்கால் உறும் துன்பத்தை ஓரார் ஆதலும்"],["kalaignar","Work to remove obstacles before they come, and don't lose heart if they still do.","இடையூறு வருவதற்கு முன்பே அதனை நீக்கிட முனைவது"]],"ch":"k:67"},"k:663":{"book":"kural","num":663,"t":["கடைக்கொட்கச் செய்தக்க தாண்மை இடைக்கொட்கின்","எற்றா விழுமந் தரும்"],"p":"So to perform an act as to publish it (only) at its termination is (true) manliness; for to announce it beforehand, will cause irremediable sorrow.","r":"True manliness is doing it so that it comes round only at the end; if it comes round midway, it brings unending trouble.","n":"The verb கொட்க carries the verse, and firmness comes out as secrecy on one reading and as persistence on the other. The two make different claims about what ஆண்மை is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கொட்க means becoming known: conceal the deed until its end, for enemies who learn of it midway will block it.","செய்யப்படும் வினையை முடிவின்க புலப்படும் வகை முன்னெல்லாம் மறைத்துச் செய்வதே திட்பமாவது"],["manakkudavar","கொட்க means turning back: go through to the end and return, for returning midway brings harm.","ஒரு வினையைத் தொடங்கினால், முடிவிலே சென்று மீளல் செய் வது ஆண்மையாவது"]],"ch":"k:67"},"k:664":{"book":"kural","num":664,"t":["சொல்லுதல் யார்க்கும் எளிய அரியவாம்","சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்"],"p":"To say (how an act is to be performed) is (indeed) easy for any one; but far difficult it is to do according to what has been said.","r":"Speaking is easy for anyone; doing as one has spoken is hard.","n":"Pope's 'shape one's life' turns it into a general moral. Both early commentators restrict it to the plan: saying 'this is how we will do this'. After a full chapter on eloquence, this comes as a quiet correction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","solvanmai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:665":{"book":"kural","num":665,"t":["வீறெய்தி மாண்டார் வினைத்திட்பம் வேந்தன்கண்","ஊறெய்தி உள்ளப் படும்"],"p":"The firmness in action of those who have become great by the excellence (of their counsel) will, by attaining its fulfilment in the person of the king, be esteemed (by all).","r":"The firmness in action of those great in excellence, once it takes effect on the king, comes to be held in everyone's regard.","n":"On Parimelazhagar's reading the fruits lodge in the king, wealth and fame being his; the minister's firmness earns only regard. Kalaignar seems to have the firmness attract the ruler instead, so what ஊறெய்தி describes is unclear.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","amaichu","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:666":{"book":"kural","num":666,"t":["எண்ணிய எண்ணியாங்கு எய்து எண்ணியார்","திண்ணியர் ஆகப் பெறின்"],"p":"If those who have planned (an undertaking) possess firmness (in executing it) they will obtain what they have desired even as they have desired it.","r":"What people have thought of, they obtain as they thought of it, if those who thought it turn out firm.","n":"The first line sounds unconditional, and it is often quoted that way. The condition is in the second line: thinking must be backed by firmness. Parimelazhagar adds that such people don't think otherwise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thitpam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:667":{"book":"kural","num":667,"t":["உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை வேண்டும் உருள்பெருந்தேர்க்கு","அச்சாணி அன்னார் உடைத்து"],"p":"Let none be despised for (their) size; (for) the world has those who resemble the linch-pin of the big rolling car.","r":"Do not scorn anyone for how they look: the great rolling chariot has its linchpin.","n":"The image answers the appearance: the pin bears great loads because it is placed exactly at the point of strain. Parimelazhagar applies this to small-bodied ministers while Manakkudavar applies it to everyone (யாவரையும்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:668":{"book":"kural","num":668,"t":["கலங்காது கண்ட வினைக்கண் துளங்காது","தூக்கங் கடிந்து செயல்"],"p":"An act that has been firmly resolved on must be as firmly carried out without delay.","r":"With the deed one has looked at clearly and decided, act unwaveringly and without delay.","n":"Sequence matters: கலங்காது before துளங்காது. Clarity comes first, then speed, and Parimelazhagar notes that a confused mind makes what should be dropped seem worth doing. தூக்கம் here is delay, not sleep as in Pope's 'unslumbering'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","madi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:669":{"book":"kural","num":669,"t":["துன்பம் உறவரினும் செய்க துணிவாற்றி","இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை"],"p":"Though it should cause increasing sorrow (at the outset), do with firmness the act that yield bliss (in the end).","r":"Even though pain closes in, do resolutely the deed that yields joy.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses technical language to weigh momentary pain of effort (கணிகம்) against the lasting pleasure of the result (பரிணாம இன்பம்). The verse only says 'joy-yielding'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","idukkan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:670":{"book":"kural","num":670,"t":["எனைத்திட்பம் எய் தியக் கண்ணும் வினைத்திட்பம்","வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது உலகு"],"p":"The great will not esteem those who esteem not firmness of action, whatever other abilities the latter may possess.","r":"Whatever other firmness they have attained, the world doesn't want those who don't want firmness of action.","n":"வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது mirrors the rejection. Parimelazhagar reads உலகு as 'the high', and Pope's gloss has 'the great', but the verse's own word is the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thitpam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:67"},"k:671":{"book":"kural","num":671,"t":["சூழ்ச்சி முடிவு துணிவெய்தல் அத்துணிவு","தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது"],"p":"Consultation ends in forming a resolution (to act); (but) delay in the execution of that resolve is an evil,”","r":"Deliberation ends in arriving at resolve, and it's a fault if that resolve lingers in delay.","n":"The verse gives thought a limit from both sides: it must run until resolve, as Parimelazhagar notes, and it has no right to run after. Manakkudavar supplies the risk: enemies learn of the delay and take precautions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai","thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:672":{"book":"kural","num":672,"t":["தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால தூங்கற்க","தூங்காது செய்யும் வினை"],"p":"Sleep over such (actions) as may be slept over; (but) never over such as may not be slept over.","r":"Sleep over what should be done slowly; do not sleep over what should be done without sleeping.","n":"தூங்கு means both to sleep and to delay. The verse repeats it so that the word itself has to be sorted, and it does not tell you how to tell the two kinds of task apart; Parimelazhagar says they are known through strength and timing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:673":{"book":"kural","num":673,"t":["ஙல்லும்வா யெல்லாம் வினைநன்றே ஒல்லாக்கால்","செல்லும்வாய் நோக்கிச் செயல்"],"p":"Whenever it is possible to overcome your enemy the act {of fighting) is certainly good; if not, endeavour to employ some more ‘successful method,","r":"Wherever it's possible, the deed is good; where it isn't, act by looking for a way that will go.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vinai","pagai","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வினை here means war: where stronger, fight, since force is what is feared; otherwise use one of the other three means (conciliation, division, gifts).","இயலுமிடத்தெல்லாம் போராற் செய்தல் நன்று"],["manakkudavar","No reference to war: act when possible, and when not, keep it in mind and wait for an opening.","இயலாத காலத்து அதனை நினைந்திருந்து, இயலும் இடம் பார்த்துச் செய்க"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:674":{"book":"kural","num":674,"t":["வினைபகை என்றிரண்டின் எச்சம் நினையுங்கால்","தீயெச்சம் போலத் தெறும்"],"p":"When duly considered, the incomplete execution of an undertaking and hostility will grow and destroy one like the {unextinguished} remnant of a fire,","r":"If you think about it, what is left over from a task or an enmity scorches like what is left over from a fire.","n":"நினையுங்கால் suggests that this only becomes clear on reflection: a remainder seems small at the moment. Parimelazhagar assigns this to the stronger party, for whom leaving things unfinished seems safe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:675":{"book":"kural","num":675,"t":["பொருள்கருவி காலம் வினையிடனொடு ஐந்தும்","இருள்தீர எண்ணிச் செயல்"],"p":"Do an act alter a due consideration of the {following} five, viz., Money, means, time, execution and place.","r":"Act after thinking through until the darkness clears these five: resources, means, time, the deed and the place.","n":"The list recalls that of 631, but both early commentators double each item: one's own and the enemy's. The verse doesn't say this, and Kalaignar's checklist has only one side. If they're right, this is a list about comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:676":{"book":"kural","num":676,"t":["முடிவும் இடையூறும் முற்றியாங்கு எய்தும்","படுபயனும் பார்த்துச் செயல்"],"p":"An act is to be performed after considering the exertion required, the obstacles to be encountered, and the great to be gained {on its completion).","r":"Act after looking at how it would be finished, what will hinder it, and the great yield when complete.","n":"Parimelazhagar states it as a cost-benefit test: act if the yield outweighs the effort and obstacles. Manakkudavar reads முடிவு as the manner of ending, and Parimelazhagar as effort by metonymy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","vinai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:677":{"book":"kural","num":677,"t":["செய்வினை செய்வான் செயன்முறை அவ்வினை","உள்ளறிவான் உள்ளம் கொளல்"],"p":"The method of performance for one who has begun an act is to ascertain the mind of him who knows the secret thereof,","r":"The proper way for someone doing a task is to learn the mind of the one who knows it from inside.","n":"After the preceding checklists, this verse says they aren't enough, and that someone who has done it should be consulted. Parimelazhagar's உள் அறிவான் is the person who has done this before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soozhchi","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:678":{"book":"kural","num":678,"t":["வினையான் வினையாக்கிக் கோடல் நனைகவுள்","யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று"],"p":"To make one undertaking the means of accomplishing another (similar to it) is like making one rutting elephant the means of capturing arother.","r":"Using one task to accomplish another is like binding a rutting elephant with another elephant.","n":"The image takes for granted the practice of capturing elephants with an elephant, and பிணித்தல் is itself a skill of the deed. The elephants of 597 and 599 are about character; this one is about technique.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Plan each task so that it becomes a means to finishing the next similar one.","தொடங்கிய வினையானே பிறிதும் ஓர் வினையை முடித்தற்கு உபாயம் ஆமாறு எண்ணிச் செய்க."],["manakkudavar","It means setting an enemy's enemies against him, so that enmity is used against enmity.","தமக்கு ஒரு பகைவர் தோன்றினால் அவர்க்குப் பகையாயினாரை அவ ரோடு பகைக்குமாறு"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:679":{"book":"kural","num":679,"t":["நட்டார்க்கு நல்ல செயலின் விரைந்ததே","ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல்"],"p":"One should rather hasten to secure the alliance of the foes (of one's foes} rather than perform good offices to one’s friends,","r":"Bringing the unallied over to one's side is more urgent than doing good to one's friends.","n":"Friends can wait because they are friends. This chilly ranking of urgency is about strategy, not love, and Parimelazhagar's reason is timing: it must be done before enemies see one's weakness.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","soozhchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒட்டார் are those not allied with one's enemy, i.e. the enemy's enemies, to be made friends. He allows another sense: keeping one's own foes from joining others.","தன் பகைவரோடு ஒட்டாரைத் தனக்கு நட்பாக்கிக்கோடல்."],["manakkudavar","ஒட்டார் are simply one's enemies, to be reconciled, and this is put ahead of doing good for one's own kin.","தஞ் சுற்றத்திற்கு நல்லவை செய்தலினும், பகைவரைப் பொருந்தி நட்பாகக் கொள்ளுதலை"]],"ch":"k:68"},"k:680":{"book":"kural","num":680,"t":["உறைசிறியார் உள்நடுங்கல் அஞ்சிக் குறைபெறின்","கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து"],"p":"Ministers of smali states, afraid of their people being frightened will yield to and acknowledge thelr superior foes, it the latter offer them a chance of reconciliation","r":"Those whose domain is small, afraid of the trembling within, will bow to the great and accept what they can get if it is offered.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees the verse's tone: since bowing isn't what the honourable would choose, கொள்வர் reports what is done, without praising it. His reason is that if one's own side panics, all is lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","panivu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:68"},"k:681":{"book":"kural","num":681,"t":["அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல் வேந்தவாம்","பண்புடைமை தூதுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"The qualification of an ambassador are affection (for his relations) a fitting birth, and the possession of attributes pleasing to royalty.","r":"Love, birth in an honourable family, and qualities kings desire make up the nature of one who carries a message.","n":"Whatever அன்பு means here, the envoy's list begins from the affections, not the skills. Parimelazhagar's 'qualities kings desire' also serve abroad: foreign kings too come under his sway.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The love is for one's own kin, which keeps them from harm, and the birth is in a family fit for ministry.","தன் சுற்றத்தார்மாட்டு அன்புடையனாதலும்"],["manakkudavar","The love is for the king, and 'qualities kings desire' means not desiring what the king desires.","வேந்தனால் விரும்பப்படும் குணமுடைமையாவது அவன் விரும்புவனவற் றைத் தான் விரும்பாமை."]],"ch":"k:69"},"k:682":{"book":"kural","num":682,"t":["அன்பறிவு ஆராய்ந்த சொல்வன்மை தூதுரைப்பார்க்கு","இன்றி யமையாத மூன்று"],"p":"Love (to his sovereign), knowledge (of his affairs), and a discriminating power of speech (before other sovereigns) are the three sine qua non qualifications of an ambassador.","r":"Love, knowledge and carefully considered eloquence are the three things an envoy can't do without.","n":"ஆராய்ந்த qualifies eloquence, and Parimelazhagar takes it as choosing words that will win assent. Parimelazhagar reads this verse's love as being for the king, so he has given the two adjacent verses two different objects of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:683":{"book":"kural","num":683,"t":["நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன் ஆகுதல் வேலாருள்","வென்றி வினையுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"To be powerful in politics among those who are learned (in ethics) is the character of him who speaks to lance-bearing kings on matters of triumph (to his own sovereign).","r":"It is the mark of one who speaks of victory among spear-bearers to be strong in the treatises among those who know them.","n":"The two audiences are set side by side: those with treatises and those with spears. Parimelazhagar sharpens the first: the ministers there merely understand the texts, while the envoy must be able to set them out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:684":{"book":"kural","num":684,"t":["அறிவுரு வாராய்ந்த கல்விஇம் மூன்றன்","செறிவுடையான் செல்க வினைக்கு"],"p":"He may go on a mission (to foreign rulers) who has combined in him all these three. viz., (natural) sense, an attractive bearing and well-tried learning.","r":"Let one who has these three together go on this work: understanding, presence and well-tested learning.","n":"உரு, bodily presence, counts as a requirement. Parimelazhagar says all three earn respect, on which the work depends, and that such a combination in one person is rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:685":{"book":"kural","num":685,"t":["தொகச் சொல்லித் தூவாத நீக்கி நகச்சொல்லி","நன்றி பயப்பதாந் தூது"],"p":"He is an ambassador who (in the presence of foreign rulers) speaks briefly, avoids harshness, talks so as to make them smile, and thus brings good (to his own sovereign).","r":"The envoy is one who speaks in brief, removes what would offend, speaks so as to bring a smile, and thereby gets good done.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives each tactic a psychology: when told a compressed reason, hearers who would refuse many matters come round; when told in a pleasing way, they accept hard things without noticing they are hard. Courtesy is presented as technique.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:686":{"book":"kural","num":686,"t":["கற்றுக்கண் அஞ்சான் செலச்சொல்லிக் காலத்தால்","தக்கது அறிவதாம் தூது"],"p":"He is an ambassador who having studied (politics) talks impressively, is not afraid of angry looks, and knows (to employ) the art suited to the time.","r":"The envoy is one who is learned, does not fear hostile eyes, speaks so that it goes home, and knows what fits the time.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that காலத்தால் தக்கது allows for ends reached by means other than what the treatises taught. Manakkudavar makes the learning very specific: how to turn aside the enemy king's anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:687":{"book":"kural","num":687,"t":["கடனறிந்து காலங் கருதி இடனறிந்து","எண்ணி உரைப்பான் தலை"],"p":"He is chief (among ambassadors) who understands the proper decorum (before foreign princes), seeks the (proper) occasion, knows the (most suitable) place, and delivers his message after (due) consideration.","r":"The highest is the one who knows the protocol, considers the moment, knows the place, and speaks after thinking it through.","n":"Parimelazhagar's எண்ணி is rehearsal in chains: what I say, what they answer, what I say next. His இடம் is a place where one's allies are present, so a place is political, not physical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","soozhchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:688":{"book":"kural","num":688,"t":["தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றின்","வாய்மை வழியுரைப்பான் பண்பு"],"p":"The qualifications of him who faithfully delivers his (sovereign's) message are purity, the support (of foreign ministers), and boldness, with truthfulness in addition to the (aforesaid) three.","r":"Purity, support, resolve and truthfulness in these three mark the one who delivers the message faithfully.","n":"Parimelazhagar's purity is immunity to bribes of money and women, so that the message isn't altered. Truth here is a qualification for being believed. These are professional virtues as he explains them, not an inner holiness.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","thooimai","vaaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துணைமை is having the foreign court's ministers as support, which can calm a king's anger.","தமக்கு அவரமைச்சர் துணையாந்தன்மையும்"],["manakkudavar","துணைமை is having one's own retinue, and purity is of body and mind.","தூய்மையுடைமையும், சுற்றமுடைமையும்"]],"ch":"k:69"},"k:689":{"book":"kural","num":689,"t":["விடுமாற்றம் வேந்தர்க்கு உரைப்பான் வடுமாற்றம்","வாய்சேரா வன்க ணவன்"],"p":"He alone is fit to communicate (his sovereign's) reply, who possesses the firmness not to utter even inadvertently what may reflect discredit (on the latter).","r":"One who conveys the king's reply to kings is a hard man whose mouth will not let slip a word that shames.","n":"வன்கண் is again a virtue, as in 632, and Parimelazhagar shows why: the temptation comes from fear for oneself. The slip would be a phrase softening his king's standing in order to save his skin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["thoothu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:690":{"book":"kural","num":690,"t":["இறுதி பயப்பினும் எஞ்சாது இறைவற் கு","உறுதி பயப்பதாம் தூது"],"p":"He is the ambassador who fearlessly seeks his sovereign's good though it should cost him his life (to deliver his message).","r":"The envoy is one who, even if it brings his end, doesn't hold back and secures what is good for his lord.","n":"இறுதி is the limit, and the chapter stops there. The ideal is unconditional, and the verse gives no reason why the envoy should owe so much; it's taken for granted.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thoothu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:69"},"k:691":{"book":"kural","num":691,"t":["அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க","இகல்வேந்தர்ச் சேர்ந்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Ministers who serve under fickle-minded monarchs should, like those who warm themselves at the fire, be neither (too) far, nor (too) near.","r":"Those who attach themselves to quarrelsome kings should be like people warming at a fire: not going off, not going close.","n":"The image avoids praising or blaming the king: fire doesn't have intentions. Parimelazhagar spells out both halves: at a distance one gets nothing, and up close one is burned. இகல் doesn't say which kings, and may imply all of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:692":{"book":"kural","num":692,"t":["மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை மன்னரால்","மன்னிய ஆக்கந் தரும்"],"p":"","r":"Not desiring what kings desire brings, through those kings, lasting prosperity.","n":"A kind of worldly detachment that works as strategy. Parimelazhagar gives the mechanism: kings fear those who would rival them in pleasures, finery and rank, and they reward one who doesn't. So the restraint is prudence and may not be virtue at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:693":{"book":"kural","num":693,"t":["போற்றின் அரியவை போற்றல் கடுத்தபின்","தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது"],"p":"Ministers who would save themselves should avoid (the commission of) serious errors for if the king's suspicion is once roused, no one can remove it.","r":"If you would guard yourself, guard against the gravest things; once suspicion has arisen, nobody can clear it.","n":"Parimelazhagar's list of grave faults is betrayal, intimacy with royal women and seizing treasure. His comparison is memorable: like a debt that comes to mind at each sight of the debtor, a cleared suspicion recurs each time the king sees you.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:694":{"book":"kural","num":694,"t":["செவிச்சொல்லும் சேர்ந்த நகையும் அவித்தொழுகல்","ஆன்ற பெரியா ரகத்து"],"p":"While in the presence of the sovereign, ministers should neither whisper to nor smile at others.","r":"In the presence of the great and established, suppress whispers and shared smiles.","n":"These small signs are dangerous because a ruler reads them: Parimelazhagar says he will assume they concern his own faults. The next chapter's interest in reading signs here falls on those who are read. The verse says 'great ones', and only commentators limit this to kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:695":{"book":"kural","num":695,"t":["எப்பொருளும் ஓரார் தொடரார்மற் றப்பொருளை","விட்டக்கால் கேட்க மறை"],"p":"(When the king is engaged) in secret counsel (with others), ministers should neither over-hear anything whatever nor pry into it with inquisitive questions, but (wait to) listen when it is divulged (by the king himself).","r":"Don't listen in to anything or press for anything; hear a secret when it has been let go.","n":"Manakkudavar says that even the desire to hear is a fault. The whole risk is that curiosity about power looks like a threat to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","amaichu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:696":{"book":"kural","num":696,"t":["குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி வெறுப்பில","வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்"],"p":"Knowing the (king's disposition and seeking the right time, (the minister) should in a pleasing manner suggest things such as are desirable and not disagreeable.","r":"Knowing the mood and considering the time, say what is needed and not disliked, so that it is wanted.","n":"Each word is doing work: வெறுப்பில, 'not disliked', is a test of the hearer, while வேண்டுப, 'needed', is a test of the matter. Parimelazhagar explains that a thing a king doesn't accept won't get done, so the point is practical, and that during lust or anger advice is useless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kurippu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:697":{"book":"kural","num":697,"t":["வேட்பன சொல்லி வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கேட்பினும் சொல்லா விடல்"],"p":"Ministers should (always) give agreeable advice but on no occasion recommend useless actions, though requested (to do so).","r":"Say what is wanted, and never say what is useless even if you are asked.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Say what is of great benefit and desired, even if not asked, and don't say what is profitless, even if the king asks.","எஞ்ஞான்றும் பயனிலவாயவற்றைத் தானே கேட்டாலும் சொல்லாது விடுக."],["manakkudavar","Say what the king wants; and if an enemy king questions an envoy about the size of the army, evade: 'Don't you know?'","பகையரசன் வினாவினால், நீ அறியாததொன்றோ வென்று அளவு கூறாமை."]],"ch":"k:70"},"k:698":{"book":"kural","num":698,"t":["இளையர் இனமுறையர் என்றிகழார் நின்ற","ஒளியோடு ஒழுகப் படும்"],"p":"Ministers should behave in accordance with the (Divine) light in the person of kings and not despise them saying, \"He is our junior (in age) and connected with our family!\".","r":"Without belittling him as 'he's younger' or 'he's kin of such a degree', one should conduct oneself in keeping with the light he stands in.","n":"நின்ற ஒளி: the light is one he stands in, and this can be read as belonging to the office, whoever holds it. Pope's '(Divine)' comes from Parimelazhagar.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The light is the king's divinity: he guards the world while asleep, and they must act as men before gods.","அவர் கடவுளரும் தாம் மக்களுமாய் ஒழுகுதல்."],["manakkudavar","The light is simply the headship he has obtained.","அவர் பெற்று நின்ற தலைமையோடே பொருந்த ஒழுக வேண்டும்"],["kalaignar","It is the eminence of those in positions of government.","அவர்கள் அடைந்துள்ள பெருமைக் கேற்பப் பண்புடன் நடந்து கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:70"},"k:699":{"book":"kural","num":699,"t":["கொளப்பட்டேம் என்றெண்ணிக் கொள்ளாத செய்யார்","துளக்கற்ற காட்சி யவர்"],"p":"Those whose judgement is firm will not do what is disagreeable (to the sovereign) saying (within themselves) \"We are esteemed by the king\".","r":"Those of unshaken clarity won't, thinking 'we are in favour', do what is unacceptable.","n":"Parimelazhagar's diagnosis is one of changed self-image: those who fall think of themselves as new people after being favoured. Clarity, then, is seeing that one is the same person as before.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:700":{"book":"kural","num":700,"t":["பழையம் எனக்கருதிப் பண்பல்ல செய்யும்","கெழுதகைமை கேடு தரும்"],"p":"The (foolish) claim with which a minister does unbecoming acts because of his (long) familiarity (with the king) will ensure his ruin.","r":"The intimacy that, thinking 'we go back a long way', does what is improper brings ruin.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is the chapter's coldest note: when the king can no longer bear it, he takes the life without any kindly regard for the old bond. The chapter on kindly regard asks for exactly what the courtier here can't count on.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mannar","kannottam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:70"},"k:701":{"book":"kural","num":701,"t":["கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","மாறாநீர் வையக் கணி"],"p":"The minister who by looking (at the king) understands his mind without being told (of it), will be a perpetual ornament to the world which is surrounded by a never-drying sea.","r":"One who sees and knows what is meant before it is said is an ornament to the earth, whose waters never fail.","n":"The verse doesn't say whose mind is being read. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as the king's, Kalaignar as anyone's. After the previous chapter, a minister's skill seems likely, though it is not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:702":{"book":"kural","num":702,"t":["ஐயப் படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானைத்","தெய்வத்தோ டொப்பக் கொளல்"],"p":"He is to be esteemed a god who is able to ascertain without a doubt what is within (one's mind).","r":"Regard as equal to a god one who knows, without doubt, what is within.","n":"ஐயப்படாஅது is the claim's high bar: accurate conjecture isn't enough. Parimelazhagar locates the divinity narrowly in this one capacity, while the person stays human in body. Kalaignar's phrase 'if it is said that this power is god's' hedges the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:703":{"book":"kural","num":703,"t":["குறிப்பிற் குறிப்புணர் வாரை உறுப்பினுள்","யாது கொடுத்தும் கொளல்"],"p":"The king should ever give whatever (is asked) of his belongings and secure him who, by the indications (of his own mind) is able to read those of another.","r":"Give whatever part of what you have, and gain those who read a sign by a sign.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reading contains a small theory of other minds: since inner workings are alike in all people, knowing one's own gives access to another's. The verse's குறிப்பிற் குறிப்பு is short enough to allow this but does not state it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","amaichu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They read another's sign through their own inner sign; 'parts' are outer possessions such as wealth, land, elephants.","உள் நிகழும் நெறி யாவர்க்கும் ஒத்தலின், பிறர் குறிப்பறிதற்குத் தம் குறிப்புக் கருவியாயிற்று."],["manakkudavar","They read the mind's intent through the sign in the face, and 'parts' can be limbs of the body or of the realm.","முகக் குறிப்பினாலே உள்ளக்கருத்தை அறியுமவர்களை"],["kalaignar","They read the face, and the offer is any post in government.","எந்தப் பொறுப்பைக் கொடுத்தாவது துணையாக்கிக் கொள்ள வேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:71"},"k:704":{"book":"kural","num":704,"t":["குறித்தது கூறாமைக் கொள்வாரோ டேனை","உறுப்போ ரனையரால் வேறு"],"p":"Those who understand one's thoughts without being informed (thereof) and those who do not, may (indeed) resemble one another bodily; still are they different (mentally).","r":"Those who take what is meant without its being said and all the others are alike in limbs, yet they are different.","n":"Parimelazhagar completes the thought: lacking the highest knowing, the others are to be classed as beasts. The verse just says வேறு, 'different', and the understatement may be stronger than the gloss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:705":{"book":"kural","num":705,"t":["குறிப்பிற் குறிப்புணரா வாயின் உறுப்பினுள்","என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்?"],"p":"Of what use are the eyes amongst one's members, if they cannot by their own indications dive those of another ?.","r":"If they can't read a sign by a sign, of what use, among one's limbs, are eyes?","n":"Eyes are asked to do what is not seeing in the ordinary sense. Parimelazhagar says awareness itself is being attributed to the eye here because of how much it helps. Manakkudavar says such people are like the blind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:706":{"book":"kural","num":706,"t":["அடுத்தது காட்டும் பளிங்குபோல் நெஞ்சம்","கடுத்தது காட்டும் முகம்"],"p":"As the mirror reflects what is near so does the face show what is uppermost in the mind.","r":"As crystal shows what is near it, the face shows what is strongest in the heart.","n":"பளிங்கு is crystal, and it takes on a neighbouring colour. Pope and Kalaignar's 'mirror' changes the figure: a mirror shows what stands opposite, and crystal changes colour within itself. கடுத்தது is what is most intense, so the face shows peaks and not everything.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:707":{"book":"kural","num":707,"t":["முகத்தின் முதுக்குறைந்தது உண்டோ உவப்பினும்","காயினும் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"Is there anything so full of knowledge as the face ? (No.) it precedes the mind, whether (the latter is) pleased or vexed.","r":"Is there anything as knowing as the face? Whether one is pleased or angry, it gets there first.","n":"Parimelazhagar treats this as a rejoinder to those who say a face, made of elements, can't know anything, and reads the verse's question as a mock refutation. The point isn't that it knows, but that it arrives before the words do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:708":{"book":"kural","num":708,"t":["முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும் அகம்நோக்கி","உற்ற துணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If the king gets those who by looking into his mind can understand (and remove) what has occurred (to him) it is enough that he stand looking at their face.","r":"If you have those who look within and understand what has happened, it's enough to stand and look them in the face.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It concerns a petitioner: he need only stand before those who will read his need and resolve it, since speaking shames both.","அவ்வெல்லையைக் கடந்து சொல்லுமாயின் இருவர்க்குஞ் சிறுமையாமாகலின்"],["manakkudavar","It's the reverse relation: the king, too, must read his ministers.","இஃது அமாத்தியர் குறிப்பை அரசனும் அறியவேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","It concerns the reader: if he has the skill, he only needs to stand in front of someone.","ஒருவரின் முகத்துக்கு எதிரில் நின்றாலே போதுமானது."]],"ch":"k:71"},"k:709":{"book":"kural","num":709,"t":["பகைமையும் கேண்மையும் கண்ணுரைக்கும் கண்ணின்","வகைமை உணர்வார்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"If a king gets ministers who can read the movements of the eye, the eyes (of foreign kings) will (themselves) reveal (to him) their hatred or friendship.","r":"The eye itself will tell of enmity and friendship, if you have those who understand the eye's kinds.","n":"The eye is said to speak (உரைக்கும்), and speech itself isn't needed. Parimelazhagar applies this to foreign kings as read by ministers, so this is diplomatic intelligence at its most subtle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:710":{"book":"kural","num":710,"t":["நுண்ணியம் என்பார் அளக்குங்கோல் காணுங்கால்","கண்ணல்லது இல்லை பிற"],"p":"The measuring-rod of those (ministers) who say \"we are acute\" will on inquiry be found to be their (own) eyes and nothing else.","r":"When you look into it, the measuring rod of those who call themselves subtle is nothing but the eye.","n":"என்பார் may carry a slight smile: 'those who say we are fine-minded'. Parimelazhagar explains why the eye: gesture, form, action and speech can all be hidden once one knows they're being read, but a gaze is mixed with the mind. He also records a variant reading, அலைக்குங்கோல்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:71"},"k:711":{"book":"kural","num":711,"t":["அவையறிநது ஆராய்ந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லின்","தொகையறிந்த தூய்மை யவர்"],"p":"Let the pure who know the arrangement of words speak with deliberation after ascertaining (the nature of) the court (then assembled).","r":"Those whose purity lies in knowing the body of words should speak after knowing the assembly and examining the matter.","n":"Pope's 'pure in heart' gives the wrong impression. For Parimelazhagar, தூய்மை here is selection: discarding what doesn't suit and keeping what does. It's a purity of diction, not of character, though the verse doesn't rule out more.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","solvanmai","thooimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:712":{"book":"kural","num":712,"t":["இடைதெரிந்து நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லின்","நடைதெரிந்த நன்மை யவர்"],"p":"Let the good who know the uses of words speak with a clear knowledge after ascertaining the time (suited to the court).","r":"Those with the goodness of knowing how words work should discern the opening, understand clearly, and then speak.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இடை is the moment: when the assembly is willing to hear.","செவ்வி; கேட்டற்கண் விருப்புடைமை."],["manakkudavar","இடை is the fault: think about what wrong a word could bring.","சொன்னால் அதற்கு வருங்குற்றத்தை ஆராய்ந்து"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:713":{"book":"kural","num":713,"t":["அவையறியார் சொல்லல்மேற் கொள்பவர் சொல்லின்","வகையறியார் வல்லதூஉம் இல்"],"p":"Those who undertake to speak without knowing the (nature of the) court are ignorant of the quality of words as well as devoid of the power (of learning).","r":"Those who undertake to speak without knowing the assembly don't know the kinds of speech, and have no ability at all.","n":"A strong claim about what speech is. Parimelazhagar's reason is that kinds of speech follow from kinds of hearers' understanding, so if you don't know the hearer, you don't know the words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:714":{"book":"kural","num":714,"t":["ஒளியார்முன் ஒள்ளிய ராதல் வெளியார்முன்","வான்சுதை வண்ணம் கொளல்"],"p":"Ministers should be lights in the assembly of the enlightned, but assume the pure whiteness of mortar (ignorance) in that of fools.","r":"Before the bright be bright; before the empty, take on the colour of pure white plaster.","n":"The verse plays on வெளி, the blank and white. Parimelazhagar connects it to வெளிறு, wood without heart, and says one should appear even whiter than they are so they will respect you. It's unusually candid advice to feign emptiness; the commentators don't soften it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:715":{"book":"kural","num":715,"t":["நன்றென்ற வற்றுள்ளும் நன்றே முதுவருள்","முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு"],"p":"The modesty by which one does not rush forward and speak in (an assembly of) superiors is the best among all (one's) good qualities.","r":"The best of all things called good is the restraint of not speaking first among elders.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the exact limit: only speaking first is forbidden, so speaking with them or after them remains allowed. Manakkudavar extends முதுவர் beyond age to austerity, family and learning, and this matters because the verse gives only the one word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:716":{"book":"kural","num":716,"t":["ஆற்றின் நிலைதளர்ந் தற்றே வியன்புலம்","ஏற்றுணர்வார் முன்னர் இழுக்கு"],"p":"(For a minister) to blunder in the presence of those who have acquired a vast store of learning and know (the value thereof) is like a good man stumbling (and falling away) from the path (of virtue).","r":"A slip before those who have taken in and understood wide learning is like faltering from one's footing on the path.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The path is the path to liberation: it is like an ascetic falling away, with loss of fruit and scorn as well.","வீடு எய்தற்பொருட்டு நன்னெறிக்கண் நின்றானொருவன்"],["manakkudavar","It is simply someone who is on a path of life and loses his standing, and is scorned.","ஒரு நெறியின்கண் நின்றார் அந்நிலைமை குலைந்தாற்போல் இகழப்படும்"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:717":{"book":"kural","num":717,"t":["கற்றறிந்தார் கல்வி விளங்கும் கசடறச்","சொல்தெரிதல் வல்லார் அகத்து"],"p":"The learning of those who have read and understood (much) will shine in the assembly of those who faultlessly examine (the nature of) words.","r":"The learning of those who have studied and understood shines where people can examine words faultlessly.","n":"Learning needs a qualified audience to appear at all. Manakkudavar puts this as a limit: only the learned can tell how good learning is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Those with the skill to examine words are the assembly: speak in such a gathering.","வழுப்படாமல் சொற்களை ஆராய்தல் வல்லார் அவைக்கண் சொல்லின்"],["kalaignar","They are the speakers themselves: learning shows in those who choose flawless words.","மாசற்ற சொற்களைத் தேர்ந்தெடுத்து உரை நிகழ்த்துவோரிடமே"]],"ch":"k:72"},"k:718":{"book":"kural","num":718,"t":["உணர்வ துடையார்முன் சொல்லல் வளர்வதன்","பாத்தியுள் நீர்சொரிந் தற்று"],"p":"Lecturing to those who have the ability to understand (for themselves) is like watering a bed of plants that are growing (of themselves).","r":"Speaking before those with understanding is like pouring water on a bed where the crop is already growing.","n":"The image could suggest redundancy, since the crop grows anyway. Parimelazhagar takes the opposite point: what would grow by itself grows all the more, and what grows is the speaker's own learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:719":{"book":"kural","num":719,"t":["புல்லவையுள் பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லற்க நல்லவையுள்","நன்குசலச் சொல்லு வார்"],"p":"Those who are able to speak good things impressively in an assembly of the good should not even forgetfully speak them in that of the low","r":"Those able to speak well and effectively in a good assembly should not, even by forgetting, speak in a mean one.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is double: the good assembly scorns you for not knowing your audience, and the mean one for not grasping the matter. பொச்சாந்தும், 'even by forgetting', treats it as a slip one must be on guard against.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:720":{"book":"kural","num":720,"t":["அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்தற்றால் தங்கணத்தார்","அல்லார்முன் கோட்டி கொளல்"],"p":"To utter (a good word) in the assembly of those who are of inferior rank is like dropping nectar on the ground.","r":"Holding forth before those who aren't of one's own group is like nectar spilled in a dirty yard.","n":"The verse's division is தங்கணத்தார் அல்லார், those not of one's group, which is a matter of fit and not explicitly of worth. Pope's 'alien herd' and 'inferior rank' sharpen it. Parimelazhagar sees the waste as twofold: those who would know it as deathless food never get it, and the yard gains nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:72"},"k:721":{"book":"kural","num":721,"t":["வகையறிந்து வல்லவை வாய்சோரார் சொல்லின்","தொகையறிந்த தூய்மை யவர்"],"p":"The pure who know the classification of words having first ascertained the nature (of the court) will not (through fear) falter in their speech before the powerful body.","r":"Those whose purity lies in knowing the body of words, having known the kinds, don't let their speech slip before the capable.","n":"The second line is identical to 711's. There the same people adjust to their hearers, and here they keep from faltering. Parimelazhagar brings in fear from the chapter's title; the verse only says வாய்சோரார்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","anjaamai","solvanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வகை is the kinds of assembly, and வல்லவை is a capable assembly. He records a reading as 'what they have mastered'.","கற்றுவல்ல அவை, அல்லா அவை என்னும் அவை வகையினை அறிந்து"],["manakkudavar","வகை is the kinds of harm that come from a slip.","தப்பினால் வருங் குற்றவகையை யறிந்து"]],"ch":"k:73"},"k:722":{"book":"kural","num":722,"t":["கற்றாருள் கற்றார் எனப்படுவர் கற்றார்முன்","கற்ற செலச்சொல்லு வார்"],"p":"Those who can agreeably set forth their acquirements before the learned will be regarded as the most learned among the learned.","r":"Those who make what they've learned go home before the learned are called learned among the learned.","n":"The verse is about reputation (எனப்படுவர்). Parimelazhagar's reasoning is that the world only knows the learned through such occasions, so public recognition depends on this one skill, not on how much is known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","kalvi","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:723":{"book":"kural","num":723,"t":["பகையகத்துச் சாவார் எளியர் அரியர்","அவையகத்து அஞ்சா தவர்"],"p":"Many indeed may (fearlessly) die in the presence of (their) foes; (but) few are those who are fearless in the assembly (of the learned).","r":"Those who die amid enemies are easy to find; those who don't fear within an assembly are hard to find.","n":"பகையகத்து and அவையகத்து are parallel: the assembly is a battlefield with a higher casualty of nerve. This is one of the Kural's boldest reversals of martial values. Parimelazhagar reads எளியர் simply as 'many' and அரியர் as 'few', so the claim concerns numbers and isn't a ranking of worth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","avai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:724":{"book":"kural","num":724,"t":["கற்றார்முன் கற்ற செலச்சொல்லித் தாம்கற்ற","மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்"],"p":"(Ministers) should agreeably set forth their acquirements before the learned and acquire more (knowledge) from their superiors (in learning).","r":"Make what you have learned go home before the learned, and take the more from those who know more.","n":"Fearlessness has an epistemic pay-off. Parimelazhagar's reason: no one can learn everything, so speaking in a mixed assembly prompts others to speak. Manakkudavar goes so far as to define fearlessness of the assembly as this exchange.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["avai","kalvi","anjaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:725":{"book":"kural","num":725,"t":["ஆற்றின் அளவறிந்து கற்க அவையஞ்சா","மாற்றங் கொடுத்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"In order to reply fearlessly before a foreign court, (ministers) should learn logic according to the rules (of grammar).","r":"Learn by the proper path and with its measure, so as to give replies in the assembly without fear.","n":"Fear is treated as a gap in training, not of temperament. The verse's ஆற்றின் அளவு is brief; Parimelazhagar expands it into grammar followed by logic, listing Nyaya terms for disputation. Pope follows him. That learned apparatus isn't in the verse itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","anjaamai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:726":{"book":"kural","num":726,"t":["வாளொடென் வன்கண்ணர் அல்லார்க்கு நூலொடென்","நுண்ணவை அஞ்சு பவர்க்கு"],"p":"What have they to do with a sword who are not valiant, or they with learning who are afraid of an intelligent assembly ?","r":"What is a sword to those who are not hard-eyed? What are treatises to those who fear a subtle assembly?","n":"வன்கண் is once again positive, as in 632 and 689. Parimelazhagar's conclusion is that such people have no claim on the treatises, and this goes further than saying they fail to use them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:727":{"book":"kural","num":727,"t":["பகையகத்துப் பேடிகை ஒள்வாள் அவையகத்து","அஞ்சு மவன்கற்ற நூல்"],"p":"The learning of him who is diffident before an assembly is like the shining sword of an hermaphrodite in the presence of his foes.","r":"The treatise learned by one who fears the assembly is a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி in the midst of enemies.","n":"The image relies on a period contempt, and Manakkudavar again drops the word, saying only 'one who fears'. Parimelazhagar works the comparison out carefully: field and sword are both good, so the failure lies wholly in the holder, as with the assembly and the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:728":{"book":"kural","num":728,"t":["பல்லவை கற்றும் பயமிலரே நல்லவையுள்","நன்கு செலச்சொல்லா தார்"],"p":"Those who cannot agreeably speak good things before a good assembly are indeed unprofitable persons inspite of all their various acquirements.","r":"Though they learn many things, they are of no use if they can't speak well and tellingly in a good assembly.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads பயமிலர் as of no use to the world, and records another view, that they have no fruit from their learning. Who is deprived is therefore not settled, the world or the scholar. The verse's own 'good assembly' is the same condition as 719.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","avai","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:729":{"book":"kural","num":729,"t":["கல்லா தவரின் கடையென்ப கற்றறிந்தும்","நல்லா ரவையஞ்சு வார்"],"p":"They who, though they have learned and understood, are yet afraid of the assembly of the good, are said to be inferior (even) to the illiterate.","r":"They say that those who have studied and understood, and yet fear an assembly of the good, are lower than the unlearned.","n":"என்ப, 'they say', reports this as public opinion. Parimelazhagar makes it pointed: such people benefit neither themselves nor others and have obtained only the pain of study.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","anjaamai","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:730":{"book":"kural","num":730,"t":["உளரெனினும் இல்லாரொடு ஒப்பர் களன்அஞ்சிக்","கற்ற செலச்சொல்லா தார்"],"p":"Those who through fear of the assembly are unable to set forth their learning in an interesting manner, though alive, are yet like the dead.","r":"Those who, fearing the arena, can't make what they've learned go home are like the absent, even though they exist.","n":"The chapter ends with a social death: to Parimelazhagar they are like the dead since the world doesn't count them. களன் can be a battlefield as well, so the word may close the chapter on the same comparison as 723, though Parimelazhagar glosses it as those seated there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["anjaamai","kalvi","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:73"},"k:731":{"book":"kural","num":731,"t":["தள்ளா விளையுளும் தக்காரும் தாழ்விலாச்","செல்வரும் சேர்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that in which (those who carry on) a complete cultivation, virtuous persons, and merchants with inexhaustible wealth, dwell together.","r":"A land is where unfailing yield, worthy people and wealthy people whose wealth does not sink all come together.","n":"The definition opens with who and what gathers in a place, not with territory or borders. A land here is a convergence.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","விளையுள் stands for the cultivators, தக்கார் for renunciants and brahmins, and செல்வர் for merchants, so the verse lists three kinds of people.","'விளையுள்' என்பது உழவர்மேல் நின்றது."],["manakkudavar","விளையுள் is land itself, fields that do not fail even when rain does.","தள்ளா விளையுள் - மழையில்லாத காலத்தினும் சாவிபோகாத நிலம்."],["kalaignar","The wealthy who count are those who do not spend their wealth on evil.","செல்வத்தைத் தீயவழியில் செலவிடாதவர்களும்"]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:732":{"book":"kural","num":732,"t":["பெரும்பொருளால் பெட்டக்க தாகி அருங்கேட்டால்","ஆற்ற விளைவது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which is desire for its immense wealth, and which grows greatly in prosperity, being free from destructive causes.","r":"A land is desirable for its great wealth and yields abundantly because ruin rarely touches it.","n":"The verse names no king. Parimelazhagar lists six calamities and credits their absence to the king's virtue and his might, which is his addition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:733":{"book":"kural","num":733,"t":["பொறையொருங்கு மேல்வருங்கால் தாங்கி இறைவற்கு","இறையொருங்கு நேர்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which can bear any burden that may be pressed on it (from adjoining kingdoms) and (yet) pay the full tribute to its sovereign.","r":"A land bears burdens that fall on it all at once and still gives its ruler his full due.","n":"நேர்வது suggests consent as well as payment. Parimelazhagar's gloss உடம்பட்டு makes the land a willing payer, not a squeezed one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The burdens are refugees and herds driven from other lands by war, misrule or famine, whom this land absorbs.","பாரங்கள் - மக்கள் தொகுதியும் ஆன் எருமை முதலிய விலங்குத் தொகுதியும்."],["manakkudavar","The burdens are the land's own, the extra exactions laid on subjects beyond the fixed tax.","குடிமையாவது கடமையொழிய வருவது."]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:734":{"book":"kural","num":734,"t":["உறுபசியும் ஓவாப் பிணியும் செறுபகையும்","சேரா தியல்வது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which continues to be free from excessive starvation, irremediable epidemics, and destructive foes.","r":"A land is one that goes on without severe hunger, unceasing disease or destructive enemies coming to it.","n":"சேரா answers சேர்வது in 731. The chapter defines a land by what gathers in it and then by what does not. Each ill also carries an intensifier, so the verse excludes the crushing forms and not all hardship.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:735":{"book":"kural","num":735,"t":["பல்குழுவும் பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகையும் வேந்தலைக்கும்","கொல்குறும்பும் இல்லது நாடு"],"p":"A kingdom is that which is without various (irregular) associations, destructive internal enemies, and murderous savages who (sometimes) harass the sovereign.","r":"A land is free of many factions, of ruinous enemies within, and of murderous marauders who harass the king.","n":"Parimelazhagar says the factions are united by caste or by god, a striking gloss on பல்குழு, and his 'enemies within' include robbers, slanderers and even wild animals. Chapter 89 uses உட்பகை much more intimately.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:736":{"book":"kural","num":736,"t":["கேடறியாக் கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா","நாடென்ப நாட்டின் தலை"],"p":"The learned say that the best kingdom is that which knows no evil (from its foes), and, if injured (at all), suffers no diminution in its fruitfulness.","r":"The foremost of lands is one that does not know ruin, and whose richness does not fall even if ruin comes.","n":"The verse does not rest on safety alone. It adds a second test for after the first has failed, so the highest praise goes to resilience. The commentators disagree about the ruin: Parimelazhagar means enemies, Manakkudavar pests and storms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:737":{"book":"kural","num":737,"t":["இருபுனலும் வாய்ந்த மலையும் வருபுனலும்","வல்லரணும் நாட்டிற்கு உறுப்பு"],"p":"The constituents of a kingdom are the two waters (from above and below), well situated hills and an undestructible fort.","r":"The limbs of a land are the two waters, a well-placed hill, the water that comes from it, and a strong fort.","n":"Three of the four limbs involve water or its source, and the fort comes last. Parimelazhagar's hill stores rain and releases it in summer, a piece of water reasoning.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The two waters are ground water and surface water, and வருபுனல் is what flows down from the hill.","'கீழ் நீர், மேல் நீர்' எனப்பட்ட தன்கண் நீரும்"],["kalaignar","The two waters are river and sea, and வருபுனல் is rain.","ஆறு, கடல் எனும் இருபுனலும்"]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:738":{"book":"kural","num":738,"t":["பிணியின்மை செல்வம் விளைவின்பம் ஏமம்","அணியென்ப நாட்டிவ் வைந்து"],"p":"Freedom from epidemics, wealth, produce, happiness and protection (to subjects); these five, the learned, say, are the ornaments of a kingdom.","r":"Freedom from disease, wealth, yield, happiness and protection: these five, they say, are a land's ornaments.","n":"Health stands ahead of wealth. Calling these ornaments rather than foundations is odd, and Parimelazhagar's explanation is that such beauty makes people of other lands desire it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:739":{"book":"kural","num":739,"t":["நாடென்ப நாடா வளத்தன நாடல்ல","நாட வளந்தரு நாடு"],"p":"The learned say that those are kingdom whose wealth is not laboured for, and those not, whose wealth is only obtained through labour.","r":"They call those lands whose riches need no seeking; lands that give riches only when sought are no lands.","n":"The verse puns on நாடு as land and as seek. The verdict is on the land, not on the person who toils, but it still sits awkwardly with a text that honours labour. Parimelazhagar treats seeking as hardship, and the verse does not say no work at all is done.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","selvam","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:74"},"k:740":{"book":"kural","num":740,"t":["ஆங்கமை வெய்தியக் கண்ணும் பயமின்றே","வேந்தமை வில்லாத நாடு"],"p":"Although in possession of all the above mentioned excellences, these are indeed of no use to a country, in the absence of harmony between the sovereign and the sujects.","r":"Even with all these attained, a land is without use if it has no accord with its king.","n":"After nine verses of conditions, the tenth makes them insufficient.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naadu","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வேந்து அமைவு is mutual: the people's love for the king and his grace toward them.","குடிகள் அவன்மாட்டு அன்புடையராதலும், அவன்தான் இவர்மாட்டு அருளுடையனாதலும் அடங்கின."],["manakkudavar","The requirement falls on the king, who must himself be of good character.","இது நாட்டுக்கு அரசனும் பண்புடையனாகல் வேண்டுமென்றது."],["kalaignar","The point is the absence of good government.","நல்ல அரசு அமையாத நாட்டில்"]],"ch":"k:74"},"k:741":{"book":"kural","num":741,"t":["ஆற்று பவர்க்கும் அரண்பொருள் அஞ்சித்தற்","போற்று பவர்க்கும் பொருள்"],"p":"A fort is an object of importance to those who march (against their foes) as well as to those who through fear (of pursuers) would seek it for shelter.","r":"A fort matters to those strong enough to march out, and it matters as much to those who, afraid, look to their own safety.","n":"The verse sets no shame on அஞ்சி. Fear is a normal condition of statecraft here. Parimelazhagar names the strong first because even they perish without a fort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:742":{"book":"kural","num":742,"t":["மணிநீரும் மண்ணும் மலையும் அணிநிழற்","காடும் உடைய தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has everlasting water, plains, mountains and cool shady forests.","r":"A fort has jewel-clear water, open ground, a hill, and a forest of dense shade.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The fort is ringed by all four defences, natural and built, and the ground is a bare waste left so enemies find no foothold.","நீரரண், நிலவரண், மலையரண், காட்டரண் என இயற்கையும் செயற்கையுமாய இந்நான்கு அரணும் சூழப்படுவது அரண் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","Any one of these is enough, whether water, bare land, hill or forest.","மலையாயினும், அழகிய நிழலினையுடைய காடாயினும் உடையது அரணாம்"],["kalaignar","The water is a deep, wide moat.","ஆழமும் அகலமும் கொண்ட அகழ்"]],"ch":"k:75"},"k:743":{"book":"kural","num":743,"t":["உயர்வகலம் திண்மை அருமைஇந் நான்கின்","அமைவரண் என்றுரைக்கும் நூல்"],"p":"The learned say that a fortress is an enclosure having these four (qualities) viz., height, breadth, strength and inaccessibility.","r":"The treatises say a fort is a wall with height, breadth, strength and inaccessibility in full.","n":"The verse appeals openly to a technical literature (நூல்), which Valluvar is summarizing. Parimelazhagar fills in அருமை with a long catalogue of war machines from the Silappatikaram, which the verse doesn't list.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:744":{"book":"kural","num":744,"t":["சிறுகாப்பிற் பேரிடத்த தாகி உறுபகை","ஊக்கம் அழிப்ப தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has an extensive space within, but only small places to be guarded, and such as can destroy the courage of besieging foes.","r":"A fort has little to guard and much room within, and it breaks the spirit of the enemy who comes against it.","n":"The final test is psychological. For Parimelazhagar the enemy who comes meaning to take it at once loses heart merely on seeing it, so a good fort wins some of its battle before it begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:745":{"book":"kural","num":745,"t":["கொளற்கரிதாய்க் கொண்டகூழ்த் தாகி அகத்தார்","நிலைக்கெளிதாம் நீரது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which cannot be captured, which abounds in suitable provisions, and affords a position of easy defence to its inmates.","r":"A fort is hard to seize, holds stored food, and is easy for those inside to hold their ground in.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நீரது means of such a nature: it is built so the defenders' position is easy, with their missiles reaching out and the enemy's not reaching in.","அகத்தாரது போர்நிலைக்கு எளிதாய நீர்மையையுடையதே அரணாவது."],["manakkudavar","நீரது means having water: there is water easy for those inside and scarce for those outside.","அகத்துறைவார்க்கு நிற்றற்கு எளிதாகும் நீரை யுடைத்தாயிருப்பது அரணாவது"]],"ch":"k:75"},"k:746":{"book":"kural","num":746,"t":["எல்லாப் பொருளும் உடைத்தாய் இடத்துதவும்","நல்லாள் உடையது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which has all (needful) things, and excellent heroes that can help it against destruction (by foes).","r":"A fort has every needed thing and good men who help at the critical place.","n":"நல்லாள் is not merely a strong man. Parimelazhagar reads the 'good' as love of the king, honour, valour and vigilance, so the defenders' character is already part of the fort's definition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:747":{"book":"kural","num":747,"t":["முற்றியும் முற்றா தெறிந்தும் அறைப்படுத்தும்","பற்றற் கரியது அரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which cannot be captured by blockading, assaulting, or undermining it.","r":"A fort is hard to take by encircling it, by storming it without encircling, or by working on those inside.","n":"Pope's 'mine beneath the ground' turns அறைப்படுத்தல் into tunnelling, while Parimelazhagar makes it subversion, turning trusted insiders so they open the gates. On his reading the third danger is treachery. He says stores answer the first threat and good men the other two, so walls answer none of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:748":{"book":"kural","num":748,"t":["முற்றாற்றி முற்றி யவரையும் பற்றாற்றிப்","பற்றியார் வெல்வது அரண்"],"p":"That is a fort whose inmates are able to overcome without losing their ground, even abler men who have besieged it.","r":"A fort lets those who hold it stand fast and defeat even those strong enough to besiege it.","n":"Parimelazhagar stresses that a small force does not merely endure a large one but wins, which raises the claim from survival to victory. He also says others explain this verse differently, and his text doesn't give their view.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:749":{"book":"kural","num":749,"t":["முனைமுகத்து மாற்றலர் சாய வினைமுகத்து","வீறெய்தி மாண்ட தரண்"],"p":"A fort is that which derives excellence from the stratagems made (by its inmates) to defeat their enemies in the battlefield.","r":"A fort gains its distinction from the actions by which its people bring down enemies as soon as battle begins.","n":"The chapter's language shifts here from what the fort has to what is done in it, preparing for 750.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","padai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:750":{"book":"kural","num":750,"t":["எனைமாட்சித் தாகியக் கண்ணும் வினைமாட்சி","இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்"],"p":"Although a fort may possess all (the above-said) excellence, it is, as it were without these, if its inmates possess not the excellence of action.","r":"Whatever excellences it has, a fort is nothing in the hands of those without excellence in action.","n":"Pope's 'craven souls' narrows this to cowardice. வினைமாட்சி is broader, and Parimelazhagar includes sitting idle, acting without measure and doing what is unsuitable. A brave incompetent would fail this verse as well.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aran","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:75"},"k:751":{"book":"kural","num":751,"t":["பொருளல் லவரைப் பொருளாகச் செய்யும்","பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்"],"p":"Besides wealth there is nothing that can change people of no importance into those of (some) importance.","r":"Apart from wealth, nothing makes persons of no account into persons of account.","n":"பொருள் appears four times, playing on 'wealth' and 'something that counts'. Whether this is approval or a dry observation is left open. Parimelazhagar names the unlearned and low-born and concludes that wealth alone should be amassed, while Kalaignar calls those raised unworthy of regard, which has an edge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:752":{"book":"kural","num":752,"t":["இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர் செல்வரை","எல்லாரும் செய்வர் சிறப்பு"],"p":"All despise the poor; (but) all praise the rich.","r":"Everyone scorns those who have nothing and everyone honours the rich.","n":"The verse states a social fact without saying it is just. Parimelazhagar sharpens it, adding that the poor are scorned though they have every good and the rich honoured though they have every evil, words which aren't in the verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:753":{"book":"kural","num":753,"t":["பொருளென்னும் பொய்யா விளக்கம் இருளறுக்கும்","எண்ணிய தேயத்துச் சென்று"],"p":"The imperishable light of wealth goes into regions desired (by its owner) and destroys the darkness (of enmity therein).","r":"The unfailing lamp called wealth goes to whatever country one has in mind and cuts away the darkness.","n":"Unlike a lamp, this one travels to where it is intended.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The darkness is enmity, and the lamp reaches enemy lands on its owner's behalf.","தன்னைச் செய்தவர்க்கு அவர் நினைத்த தேயத்துச் சென்று பகை என்னும் இருளைக் கெடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","The darkness is enmity, and a wealthy king's command runs wherever he intends.","அரசன் பொரு ளுடையனானால், தான் கருதிய தேசமெல்லாம் தன்னாணை நடத்துவானென்றது."],["kalaignar","The darkness is ordinary suffering, which money in hand drives away anywhere one goes.","இருள் என்னும் துன்பத்தைத் துரத்தி விட முடிகிறது."]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:754":{"book":"kural","num":754,"t":["அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும் திறனறிந்து","தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்"],"p":"The wealth acquired with a knowledge of the proper means and without foul practices will yield virtue and happiness.","r":"Wealth that comes with knowledge of the right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure.","n":"The commentators differ on the audience. Parimelazhagar makes 'without wrong' a matter of a king not being tyrannical, while Manakkudavar means no harm to anyone else, and the verse itself names no king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:755":{"book":"kural","num":755,"t":["அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாராப் பொருளாக்கம்","புல்லார் புரள விடல்"],"p":"(Kings) should rather avoid than seek the accumulation of wealth which does not flow in with mercy and love.","r":"Accumulation of wealth that does not come with grace and love should not be embraced, but let go.","n":"புல்லார் புரள விடல் is more than refusing. It is the gesture of letting something roll away. Coming right after 754 and before 756, it is the chapter's clearest limit.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arul","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Coming with grace and love means, for a king, taxing no more than the rightful one-sixth.","அவற்றொடு கூடி வருதலாவது, ஆறிலொன்றாய் வருதல்."],["manakkudavar","It applies to any earner: not profiting from others' distress and not losing affection for one's intimates while earning.","பொருள் தேடுங்கால் பிறர் வருத்தத்திற்கு உபகரியாதும், பயின்றார் மாட்டுக் காதலில்லாமலும்"]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:756":{"book":"kural","num":756,"t":["உறுபொருளும் உல்கு பொருளும்தன் ஒன்னார்த்","தெறுபொருளும் வேந்தன் பொருள்"],"p":"Unclaimed wealth, wealth acquired by taxes, and wealth (got) by conquest of foes are (all) the wealth of the king.","r":"Wealth that falls in on its own, wealth from customs, and wealth from crushing his enemies are the king's wealth.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes this as the income beyond the one-sixth of 755. Without that bridge, an income by force would sit oddly next to wealth that comes with grace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","vendhan","pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உறுபொருள் is ownerless wealth: long-buried treasure and estates without heirs.","வைத்தார் இறந்துபோக நெடுங்காலம் நிலத்தின்கண் கிடந்து பின் கண்டெடுத்தூஉம், தாயத்தார் பெறாததூஉமாம்."],["manakkudavar","உறுபொருள் is revenue connected with protection.","உறு பொருள் - காவற் பொருள்."]],"ch":"k:76"},"k:757":{"book":"kural","num":757,"t":["அருளென்னும் அன்பீன் குழவி பொருளென்னும்","செல்வச் செவிலியால் உண்டு"],"p":"The child mercy which is borne by love grows under the care of the rich nurse of wealth.","r":"The child called grace, born of love, is raised by the wealthy foster-mother called wealth.","n":"This gives a genealogy of compassion. Parimelazhagar distinguishes love, which runs along ties, from grace, which goes to any sufferer. The mother gives birth but cannot rear alone, so wealth is necessary without being the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","anbu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:758":{"book":"kural","num":758,"t":["குன்றேறி யானைப் போர் கண்டற்றால் தன்கைத்தொன்று","உண்டாகச் செய்வான் வினை"],"p":"An undertaking of one who has wealth in one's hands is like viewing an elephant-fight from a hill-top.","r":"Undertaking a task with money in hand is like watching an elephant fight from a hilltop.","n":"The picture's point is the safety of distance. Parimelazhagar says the funded person commissions the able and finishes the work without fear or strain, which is a view of capital as insulation from risk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:759":{"book":"kural","num":759,"t":["செய்க பொருளைச் செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்","எஃகதனிற் கூரிய தில்"],"p":"Accumulate wealth; it will destroy the arrogance of (your) foes; there is no weapon sharper than it.","r":"Make wealth; there is no steel sharper than it for cutting down the pride of enemies.","n":"The target is செருக்கு and not the enemy's body. Parimelazhagar seizes on this: other blades can't cut an intangible. The verse is one of the chapter's few bald imperatives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:760":{"book":"kural","num":760,"t":["ஒண்பொருள் காழ்ப்ப இயற்றியார்க்கு எண்பொருள்","ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"To those who have honestly acquired an abundance of riches, the other two, (virtue and pleasure) are things easy (of acquisition).","r":"For those who have made bright wealth in full measure, the other two are easily had, together.","n":"ஒண் does a lot of work: the claim is for bright wealth and not for any wealth. Parimelazhagar reads ஒண் as wealth gained by the right path, while Manakkudavar simply glosses it as bright. Everything else in the claim hinges on that adjective.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:76"},"k:761":{"book":"kural","num":761,"t":["உறுப்பமைந்து ஊறஞ்சா வெல்படை வேந்தன்","வெறுக்கையுள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"The army which is complete in (its) parts and conquers without fear of wounds is the chief wealth of the king.","r":"A conquering army complete in its parts and unafraid of wounds is the chief of all a king's wealth.","n":"வெறுக்கை calls the army wealth, directly after the chapter on wealth. Parimelazhagar's reason is that it guards the other limbs of the state and the king himself. The commentators count the parts differently: he has four, Manakkudavar five.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","selvam","vendhan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:762":{"book":"kural","num":762,"t":["உலைவிடத்து ஊறஞ்சா வன்கண் தொலைவிடத்துத்","தொல்படைக் கல்லால் அரிது"],"p":"Ancient army can alone have the valour which makes it stand by its king at the time of defeat, fearless of wounds and unmindful of its reduced strength.","r":"The hardness that fears no wound when the king is faltering and the force itself is reduced is rare except in an ancient army.","n":"The verse locates courage in continuity. Only தொல் is the verse's own; Parimelazhagar makes it a technical term, ranking the ancestral force first among six kinds, above hired, allied and enemy-derived troops.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:763":{"book":"kural","num":763,"t":["ஒலித்தக்கால் என்னாம் உவரி எலிப்பகை","நாகம் உயிர்ப்பக் கெடும்"],"p":"What if (a host of) hostile rats roar like the sea ? They will perish at the mere breath of the cobra.","r":"What if a host of hostile rats roar like the sea? They perish when the cobra merely breathes.","n":"The verse doesn't mention armies at all. Parimelazhagar reads it as an allegory recommending one hero over many who are not. The cobra doesn't even strike: its breath is enough.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:764":{"book":"kural","num":764,"t":["அழிவின்றி அறைபோகா தாகி வழிவந்த","வன்க ணதுவே படை"],"p":"That indeed is an army which has stood firm of old without suffering destruction or deserting (to the enemy).","r":"An army is one that does not collapse, cannot be turned by subversion, and has courage that has come down to it.","n":"அறைபோகாது repeats the danger of 747: the same threat of being turned from inside. Parimelazhagar's supporting verse is a woman's boast of the men of her house who fell in battle, so the inheritance includes grief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:765":{"book":"kural","num":765,"t":["கூற்றுடன்று மேல்வரினும் கூடி எதிர்நிற்கும்","ஆற்ற லதுவே படை"],"p":"That indeed is an army which is capable of offering a united resistance, even if Yama advances against it with fury.","r":"An army is one that, even when Death comes raging at it, has the power to stand together and face it.","n":"The operative word is கூடி. The courage asked for is collective. Parimelazhagar says the many are of one heart because they love the king, and that ஆற்றல் here is strength of mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:766":{"book":"kural","num":766,"t":["மறமானம் மாண்ட வழிச்செலவு தேற்றம்","எனநான்கே ஏமம் படைக்கு"],"p":"Valour, honour, following in the excellent-footsteps (of its predecessors) and trust-worthiness; these four alone constitute the safeguard of an army.","r":"Valour, honour, walking the proven path and தேற்றம் are the four things that protect an army.","n":"Parimelazhagar's 'path' includes not striking those who have turned their backs, so honour limits valour. He concludes that such men need no other fort.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","maram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தேற்றம் is being trusted by the king.","அரசனால் தேறப்படுதலும்"],["manakkudavar","தேற்றம் is the army's own clarity, and the good path is steadiness in fighting.","நல்லவழிச்சேறலாவது மறஞ் செய்யுங் காலத்துக் கலக்கமின்மை."],["kalaignar","It is winning the trust of the leader, close to Parimelazhagar but placed on the leader.","தலைவனின் நம்பிக்கையைப் பெறுதல்"]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:767":{"book":"kural","num":767,"t":["தார்தாங்கிச் செல்வது தானை தலைவந்த","போர்தாங்கும் தன்மை அறிந்து"],"p":"That is an army which knowing the art of warding off an impending struggle, can bear against the dust-van (of a hostile force).","r":"An army is one that knows how to meet the battle coming at it and advances bearing the தார்.","n":"This is the chapter's one verse about skill. Parimelazhagar sends readers to Sanskrit treatises on battle formation and declines to describe them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தார் is the enemy's vanguard, held off before pressing forward.","அவர் தூசியைத் தன்மேல் வாராமல் தடுத்துத்தான் அதன்மேற்செல்வதே படையாவது."],["kalaignar","தார் is the garland of victory that the capable army carries.","வெற்றி மாலை தாங்கிச் செல்லக்கூடிய சிறந்த படையாகும்."]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:768":{"book":"kural","num":768,"t":["அடல்தகையும் ஆற்றலும் இல்லெனினும் தானை","படைத்தகையால் பாடு பெறும்"],"p":"Though destitute of courage to fight and strength (to endure), an army may yet gain renown by the splendour of its appearance.","r":"Even without striking power or endurance, an army gains stature through its martial bearing.","n":"A surprising concession that appearance has real force. Parimelazhagar's பாடு is the fear an army causes at first sight, so display works on the enemy's mind as 744's fort does.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Display gives some standing, though the wording itself shows striking power and endurance remain indispensable.","'இல்லெனினும்' எனவே, அவற்றது இன்றியமையாமை பெறப்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","The army's parade appearance should be more distinguished than either valour or endurance.","இரண்டையும் விட ஒரு படையின் அணிவகுப்புத் தோற்றம் சிறப்புடையதாக"]],"ch":"k:77"},"k:769":{"book":"kural","num":769,"t":["சிறுமையும் செல்லாத் துனியும் வறுமையும்","இல்லாயின் வெல்லும் படை"],"p":"An army can triumph (over its foes) if it is free from diminution; irremediable aversion and poverty.","r":"An army wins if it is free of shrinking, of a resentment that will not leave, and of poverty.","n":"The verse states an army's conditions, but Parimelazhagar assigns the blame upward. Desertion and poverty come from a king who doesn't pay, and resentment from outrages such as seizing their women. A verse on the army becomes a verse on the king's conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:770":{"book":"kural","num":770,"t":["நிலைமக்கள் சால உடைத்தெனினும் தானை","தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்"],"p":"Though an army may contain a large number of permanent soldiers, it cannot last if it has no generals.","r":"Even with plenty of steadfast men, there is no army where there are no leaders.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanation is striking. If leaders go, the men say there is no one to witness and leave too. On this view courage needs an audience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["padai","vendhan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:77"},"k:771":{"book":"kural","num":771,"t":["என்னைமுன் நில்லன்மின் தெவ்விர் பலரென்னை","முன்நின்று கல்நின் றவர்"],"p":"O my foes, stand not before my leader; (for) many are those who did so but afterwards stood (in the shape of) statues.","r":"Enemies, do not stand before my lord; many who stood before him now stand in stone.","n":"The verse puns on நில்: to stand in battle and to stand as a hero-stone. The stone is both a threat and an honour, a fact that makes the boast double-edged. It is a voice in the Kural that isn't Valluvar's own, and Parimelazhagar notes the warrior places his valour on his king.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","pugazh","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:772":{"book":"kural","num":772,"t":["கான முயலெய்த அம்பினில் யானை","பிழைத்தவேல் ஏந்தல் இனிது"],"p":"It is more pleasant to hold the dart that has missed an elephant than that which has hit hare in the forest.","r":"Carrying a spear that missed an elephant is sweeter than carrying an arrow that hit a forest hare.","n":"The verse measures an act by its target, not its result. Parimelazhagar supplies a dramatic setting, a warrior ashamed that the opposing force fled, but the verse itself gives only the comparison.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:773":{"book":"kural","num":773,"t":["பேராண்மை என்ப தறுகண்ஒன் றுற்றக்கால்","ஊராண்மை மற்றதன் எஃகு"],"p":"The learned say that fierceness (incontest with a foe) is indeed great valour; but to become a benefactor in case of accident (to a foe) is the extreme (limit) of that valour.","r":"They call fierceness great manhood; but when something befalls the foe, generous help is its cutting edge.","n":"The image is exact: the kindness isn't an exception to valour but its sharpest point. Parimelazhagar's example is Rama telling the defeated Ravana to go and come back tomorrow, an epic illustration that the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","arul","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:774":{"book":"kural","num":774,"t":["கைவேல் களிற்றொடு போக்கி வருபவன்","மெய்வேல் பறியா நகும்"],"p":"The hero who after casting the lance in his hand on an elephant, comes (in search of another) will pluck the one (that sticks) in his body and laugh (exultingly).","r":"Having sent his spear away with an elephant, he comes on seeking another, and laughs as he pulls one from his own body.","n":"The laugh is the verse's whole shock. For Parimelazhagar he is happy because he has found what he was seeking, and rage had kept him from noticing the wound. A wound registers as supply.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:775":{"book":"kural","num":775,"t":["விழித்தகண் வேல்கொண டெறிய அழித்திமைப்பின்","ஒட்டன்றோ வன்க ணவர்க்கு"],"p":"Is it not a defeat to the valiant to wink and destroy their ferocious look when a lance in cast at them (by their foe) ?","r":"When the eye that glares at the enemy gives way and blinks at a flung spear, is that not flight, to the fierce?","n":"Valour is carried to the reflex. Parimelazhagar's line is that even withdrawing the angry gaze counts as retreat.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maram","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The spear is thrown at the man, and giving way means ending the glare by blinking.","அவர் வேலைக்கொண்டு எறிய அஃது ஆற்றாது அந்நோக்கை அழித்து இமைக்குமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","The spear is thrust into the eye itself, and blinking again at that is equal to defeat.","மாற்றாரோடு எதிர்த்துச் சிவந்து நோக்கின கண்ணிலே ஒரு வேலினாலே எறிய"]],"ch":"k:78"},"k:776":{"book":"kural","num":776,"t":["விழுப்புண் படாதநாள் எல்லாம் வழுக்கினுள்","வைக்கும்தன் நாளை எடுத்து"],"p":"The hero will reckon among wasted days all those on which he had not received severe wounds.","r":"Going over his days, he sets among the wasted all those on which he took no noble wound.","n":"விழுப்புண் is a wound of a certain kind. Parimelazhagar restricts it to face and chest, so that it marks one who did not turn. A ledger of days is a sober image for a very unsober ethic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:777":{"book":"kural","num":777,"t":["சுழலும் இசைவேண்டி வேண்டா உயிரார்","கழல்யாப்புக் காரிகை நீர்த்து"],"p":"The fastening of ankle-ring by those who disire a world-wide renown and not (the safety of) their lives is like adorning (themselves).","r":"For those who want fame that circles the world and do not want life, the tying on of the war-anklet is an adornment.","n":"The verse names only fame. Parimelazhagar adds heaven (துறக்கம்), saying fame remains encircling the earth while the hero goes on, so the afterlife is his reading. காரிகை turns a mark of danger into jewellery.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pugazh","maram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:778":{"book":"kural","num":778,"t":["உறின்உயிர் அஞ்சா மறவர் இறைவன்","செறினும் சீர்குன்றல் இலர்"],"p":"The heroes who are not afraid of losing their life in a contest will not cool their ardour, even if the king prohibits (their fighting).","r":"Warriors who do not fear for their lives when battle comes will not lessen in their distinction even if their lord is angry.","n":"This verse makes a real space between warrior and king, which the army chapter's language of loyalty doesn't.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maram","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They press on to battle even if the king angrily forbids it, because their ardour doesn't diminish.","தம் இறைவன் அது வேண்டா என்று முனியினும் அவ் வீரமிகுதி குன்றார்."],["kalaignar","The true warriors are those who go on doing their duty though their leader is angry with them.","தலைவன் சினந்தாலும் சிறப்புக் குறையாமல் கடமை ஆற்றுபவர்கள்தான்"]],"ch":"k:78"},"k:779":{"book":"kural","num":779,"t":["இழைத்தது இகவாமைச் சாவாரை யாரே","பிழைத்தது ஒறுக்கிற் பவர்"],"p":"Who would reproach with failure those who seal their oath with their death ?","r":"Who could reproach those who die so that what they vowed is not broken, for having failed?","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a bold move: dying in the attempt counts as fulfilling the vow. The verse's rhetorical question closes off blame for one who failed, without calling the failure a success.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saakkaadu","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:780":{"book":"kural","num":780,"t":["புரந்தார்கண் நீர்மல்கச் சாகிற்பின் சாக்காடு","இரந்துகோள் தக்கது உடைத்து"],"p":"If (heroes) can so die as to fill with tears the eyes of their rulers, such a death deserves to be obtained even by begging.","r":"If one can die so that the eyes of those who sustained one fill with tears, that death is worth begging for.","n":"The measure of the death is another's tears, and the one who weeps is the patron. Parimelazhagar sets it against dying sick at home among weeping kin, which only spends old deeds, whereas this earns heaven. The verse itself mentions neither of those.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saakkaadu","vendhan","maram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:78"},"k:781":{"book":"kural","num":781,"t":["செயற்கரிய யாவுள நட்பின் அதுபோல்","வினைக்கரிய யாவுள காப்பு"],"p":"What things are there so difficult to acquire as friendship ? What guards are there so difficult to break through by the efforts (of one's foes) ?","r":"What is as hard to make as friendship, and what guard is as hard for hostile deeds to get past?","n":"The two questions are linked: the difficulty of getting it is the reason for its value as protection. Parimelazhagar opens by classifying friendship as natural or made and says this chapter treats the made kind, kin being treated elsewhere.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:782":{"book":"kural","num":782,"t":["நிறைநீர நீரவர் கேண்மை பிறைமதிப்","பின்னீர பேதையார் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of the wise waxes like the new moon; (but) that of fools wanes like the full moon.","r":"Friendship with the good grows like the crescent moon filling, and that of fools shrinks like the moon after it is full.","n":"The moon's phases make growth depend on the starting point: the good begin small and fools begin large. Parimelazhagar's reason is knowledge. Each pair at first doesn't know the other and later does, and it is that knowing which makes one friendship grow and the other shrink.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:783":{"book":"kural","num":783,"t":["நவில்தொறும் நூல்நயம் போலும் பயில்தொறும்","பண்புடை யாளர் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Like learning, the friendship of the noble, the more it is cultivated, the more delightful does it become.","r":"The more it is practised, the bond with people of character is like the more a book is studied, its nayam.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Nayam is delight: both friendship and study give increasing pleasure.","நூற் பொருள் கற்குந்தோறும் கற்றார்க்கு இன்பஞ் செய்தலை ஒக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","The gain is understanding: a friend of good character teaches as a book does.","இது குணவானோடு நட்புக் கொள்ளின், அறிவுண்டா மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:79"},"k:784":{"book":"kural","num":784,"t":["நகுதற் பொருட்டன்று நட்டல் மிகுதிக்கண்","மேற்செனறு இடித்தற் பொருட்டு"],"p":"Friendship is to be practised not for the purpose of laughing but for that of being beforehand in giving one another sharp rebukes in case of transgression.","r":"Friendship is not for laughing together, but for going ahead to rebuke when one oversteps.","n":"மேற்சென்று has timing in it. The rebuke goes out before the wrong is done, says Parimelazhagar, and it is harsh because sweet words will not turn the person back. This is not a claim that friends should never laugh, only that laughter isn't the purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","idithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:785":{"book":"kural","num":785,"t":["புணர்ச்சி பழகுதல் வேண்டா உணர்ச்சிதான்","நட்பாங் கிழமை தரும்"],"p":"Living together and holding frequent intercourse are not necessary (for friendship); (mutual) understanding can alone create a claim for it.","r":"Neither meeting nor long acquaintance is needed; a shared feeling alone gives the claim of friendship.","n":"Parimelazhagar's example is Kopperuncholan and Pisirandaiyar, who never met and whose bond was so close that one died with the other. The verse itself offers no example and says nothing of dying together.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:786":{"book":"kural","num":786,"t":["முகநக நட்பது நட்பன்று நெஞ்சத்து","அகநக நட்பது நட்பு"],"p":"The love that dwells (merely in the smiles of the face is not friendship; (but) that which dwells deep in the smiles of the heart is true friendship.","r":"Friendship that makes the face smile is not friendship; friendship that makes the heart within smile is friendship.","n":"The verse sounds as if the face doesn't matter. Parimelazhagar says that an implied 'also' is present, so both should bloom together. That softens a sharper opposition in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:787":{"book":"kural","num":787,"t":["அழிவி னவைநீக்கி ஆறுய்த்து அழிவின்கண்","அல்லல் உழப்பதாம் நட்பு"],"p":"(True) friendship turns aside from evil (ways) makes (him) walk in the (good) way, and, in case of loss if shares his sorrow (with him).","r":"Friendship turns one away from what destroys, sets one on the path, and in ruin shares the suffering.","n":"The order is a plan of action: prevent, then guide, then, if all that fails, suffer alongside. Parimelazhagar adds that ruin in the third stage comes from a divine cause and so can't be averted. The verse names no such cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:788":{"book":"kural","num":788,"t":["உடுக்கை இழந்தவன் கைபோல ஆங்கே","இடுக்கண் களைவதாம் நட்பு"],"p":"(True) friendship hastens to the rescue of the afflicted (as readily) as the hand of one whose garment is loosened (before an assembly).","r":"Like the hand of a man whose garment slips, friendship clears away distress there and then.","n":"The simile is about speed and also about no deliberation. Parimelazhagar notes that the hand gets there before the mind does. The garment also implies that the friend's trouble is a shame to be covered, and that the friend is as close as one's own limb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:789":{"book":"kural","num":789,"t":["நட்பிற்கு வீற்றிருக்கை யாதெனின் கொட்பின்றி","ஒல்லும்வாய் ஊன்றும் நிலை"],"p":"Friendship may be said to be on its throne when it possesses the power of supporting one at all times and under all circumstances, (in the practice or virtue and wealth).","r":"Where does friendship sit enthroned? In standing firm without wavering, supporting however one can.","n":"ஒல்லும்வாய் sets a realistic limit: as far as one is able. The throne is unwavering support, not unlimited power. Parimelazhagar specifies support in virtue and wealth, which Pope follows in parentheses, while the verse itself gives no object.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:790":{"book":"kural","num":790,"t":["இனையர் இவரெமக்கு இன்னம்யாம் என்று","புனையினும் புல்லென்னும் நட்பு"],"p":"Though friends may praise one another saying, \"He is so intimate with us, and we so much (with him)\"; (still) such friendship will appear mean.","r":"'They are this to us and we are that to them': even when so adorned, friendship looks shabby.","n":"A subtle point. For Parimelazhagar friendship has no 'I' and 'they', so that listing what each is to the other brings in a difference that did not exist. The praise itself is what damages it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:79"},"k:791":{"book":"kural","num":791,"t":["நாடாது நட் டலிற் கேடில்லை நட்டபின்","வீடில்லை நட்பாள் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"As those who are of a friendly nature will not forsake (a friend) after once loving (him), there is no evil so great as contracting a friendship without due inquiry.","r":"Nothing brings ruin like befriending without inquiry, since for those devoted to friendship there is no leaving once it is formed.","n":"The logic turns on faithfulness: the reason for caution is precisely the commitment to stay. Parimelazhagar adds that with no distinction between friends, the friend's sins and blame become one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:792":{"book":"kural","num":792,"t":["ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான் கேண்மை கடைமுறை","தான்சாம் துயரம் தரும்"],"p":"The friendship contracted by him who has not made repeated inquiry will in the end grieve (him) to death.","r":"The friendship of one who does not inquire again and again ends by bringing grief that kills him.","n":"The doubled ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து asks for repeated testing and not a single check. For Parimelazhagar no enemy is needed, for one brings it on oneself, and the bad friend's enemies become one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:793":{"book":"kural","num":793,"t":["குணமும் குடிமையும் குற்றமும் குன்றா","இனனும் அறிந்தியாக்க நட்பு"],"p":"Make friendship (with one) after ascertaining (his) character, birth, defects and the whole of one's relations.","r":"Know his qualities, his family, his faults and his inan, and only then form a friendship.","n":"Faults are on the list to be known, not to be absent. Parimelazhagar's point is that no one is faultless, so the question is whether the faults can be borne.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","kudi","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குன்றா இனன் is a friend's undiminished kin, since those bound to kin bring that bond along with them.","சுற்றப் பிணிப்புடையார் நட்டாரோடும் பிணிப்புண்டு வருதலின்"],["kalaignar","The phrase means qualities that don't decline, not relatives.","குறையாத இயல்புகள் எவை?"]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:794":{"book":"kural","num":794,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்து தன்கண் பழிநாணு வானைக்","கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of one who belongs to a (good) family and is afraid of (being charged with) guilt, is worth even purchasing.","r":"One of good family who feels shame at blame on himself: that friendship should be had even by giving.","n":"பழிநாணுவான் is a person who is governed by shame. The commercial phrase is startling for something the previous chapter praises so highly. Kalaignar softens it to 'by any means', while Parimelazhagar says plainly: buy it for a price.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kudi","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:795":{"book":"kural","num":795,"t":["அழச்சொல்லி அல்லது இடித்து வழக்கறிய","வல்லார்நடபு ஆய்ந்து கொளல்"],"p":"You should examine and secure the friendship of those who can speak so as to make you weep over a crime (before its commission) or rebuke you severely (after you have done it) and are able to teach you (the ways of) the world.","r":"Inquire and take the friendship of those able to speak until one weeps, to rebuke, and to teach what is proper.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idithal","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The verse describes friends, who dissuade before the act, press hard after it, and make one do what is proper.","தாம் உலக வழக்கல்லது செய்யக் கருதின்சோகம் பிறக்கும்வகை சொல்லி விலக்கியும்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about which ministers a ruler should befriend.","இது மந்திரிகளுள் நட்பாக்கற் பாலாரைக் கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:796":{"book":"kural","num":796,"t":["கேட்டினும் உண்டோ ர் உறுதி கிளைஞரை","நீட்டி அளப்பதோர் கோல்"],"p":"Even in ruin there is some good; (for) it is a rod by which one may measure fully (the affection of one's) relations.","r":"Even in ruin there is one benefit: it is a rod that measures one's people to full length.","n":"The benefit is knowledge rather than comfort. Parimelazhagar says the true extent of each friend's bond stays hidden in wealthy times, and that ruin measures it for choosing better afterward.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:797":{"book":"kural","num":797,"t":["ஊதியம் என்பது ஒருவற்குப் பேதையார்","கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"It is indead a gain for one to renounce the friendship of fools.","r":"Gain for a person means giving up and leaving the friendship of fools.","n":"The verse calls a loss a gain. Parimelazhagar says one must also move away, because like sandal and vengai burning together, nearness alone brings harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:798":{"book":"kural","num":798,"t":["உள்ளற்க உள்ளம் சிறுகுவ கொள்ளற்க","அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார் நட்பு"],"p":"Do not think of things that discourage your mind, nor contract friendship with those who would forsake you in adversity.","r":"Do not think on what shrinks the spirit, and do not take the friendship of those who cut away in distress.","n":"The verse pairs a rule about thought and a rule about company, and Parimelazhagar treats the first as an illustration of the second. Fair-weather friends are thus a thing that shrinks the mind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:799":{"book":"kural","num":799,"t":["கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார் கேண்மை அடுங்காலை","உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்"],"p":"The very thought of the friendship of those who have deserted one at the approach of adversity will burn one's mind at the time of death.","r":"Friendship with those who forsake one in ruin burns the heart, even to think of, at the moment of death.","n":"Betrayal is measured against death and is what burns. Parimelazhagar goes further: the mere thought of it is worse than Death, and even for an unconnected person who thinks of it. He also records another reading in which the thought is of the harm one brought on oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kedu","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:80"},"k:800":{"book":"kural","num":800,"t":["மருவுக மாசற்றார் கேண்மைஒன் றீத்தும்","ஒருவுக ஒப்பிலார் நட்பு"],"p":"Continue to enjoy the friendship of the pure; (but) renounce even with a gift, the friendship of those who do not agree (with the world).","r":"Keep close to the friendship of the stainless; give something if you must, but leave the friendship of those who are oppilar.","n":"The cost of a bad friendship is paid at the end as well. It balances 794: one may pay to get a friend and pay to be rid of one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஒப்பிலார் are those out of accord with the world.","உலகோடு ஒத்தலில்லார் நட்பினை அறியாது கொண்டாராயின்"],["manakkudavar","ஒப்பிலார் are those not one's equals.","தனக்கு நிகரில்லாதார் நட்பினின்று நீங்குக"],["kalaignar","They are simply those who carry a stain, the contrary of the first half.","மாசு உள்ளவர்களின் நட்பை, விலைகொடுத்தாவது விலக்கிடவேண்டும்."]],"ch":"k:80"},"k:801":{"book":"kural","num":801,"t":["பழைமை எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதும்","கிழமையைக் கீழ்ந்திடா நட்பு"],"p":"Imtimate friendship is that which cannot in the least be injured by (things done through the) right (of longstanding intimacy).","r":"What is called old friendship? It is friendship that in no way cuts down the liberties that intimacy gives.","n":"The definition is not about time, and Parimelazhagar says so outright. His list of permitted liberties is concrete: acting without asking, even acting to one's harm, taking what one needs, showing neither deference nor fear.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:802":{"book":"kural","num":802,"t":["நட்பிற் குறுப்புக் கெழுதகைமை மற்றதற்கு","உப்பாதல் சான்றோர் கடன்"],"p":"The constituents of friendship are (things done through) the right of intimacy; to be pleased with such a right is the duty of the wise.","r":"Intimate liberty is a limb of friendship, and being as salt to it is the duty of the worthy.","n":"உப்பு: the worthy person doesn't merely tolerate liberties but gives them savour. Manakkudavar gives a homely comparison: a bitter gourd stays bitter however much seasoning goes in, so without consent no sweetness comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:803":{"book":"kural","num":803,"t":["பழகிய நட்பெவன் செய்யுங் கெழுதகைமை","செய்தாங்கு அமையாக் கடை"],"p":"Of what avail is long-standing friendship, if friends do not admit as their own actions done through the right of intimacy ?","r":"What use is long-practised friendship if one does not accept a friend's liberty as if one had done it oneself?","n":"செய்தாங்கு is the core: treat the act as your own. Parimelazhagar adds that this holds even for what one wouldn't agree to, which is where the test really lies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:804":{"book":"kural","num":804,"t":["விழைதகையான் வேண்டி இருப்பர் கெழுதகையாற்","கேளாது நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"If friends, through the right of friendship, do (anything) without being asked, the wise will be pleased with them on account of its desirability.","r":"When friends act unasked out of intimacy, those who are wise welcome it for how desirable it is.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The act is one's own business done for one by a friend unasked, and nothing is better than finding it done.","ஒருவர்க்குத் தம் கருமம் தாம் அறியாமல் முடிந்திருத்தலின் ஊங்கு நன்மை யின்மையின்"],["manakkudavar","The act is something one doesn't approve, and one should still welcome it and not only permit it.","கேளாது இசைவில்லாதவற்றைச் செய்வாராயின், அதற்கு முனியாது"]],"ch":"k:81"},"k:805":{"book":"kural","num":805,"t":["பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க","நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"If friends should perform what is painful, understand that it is owing not only to ignorance, but also to the strong claims of intimacy.","r":"If friends do something that hurts, understand it as either folly or great intimacy.","n":"The verse is a rule of interpretation: two readings are allowed, and lack of love isn't one of them. Parimelazhagar brings in fate, saying the folly comes from a fate of loss. The verse does not. Pope's 'not only... but also' blurs the either-or that the commentators find.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","pedhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:806":{"book":"kural","num":806,"t":["எல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் துறவார் தொலைவிடத்தும்","தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Those who stand within the limits (of true friendship) will not even in adversity give up the intimacy of long-standing friends.","r":"Those who hold to the bounds will not give up the bond of those who stand in old friendship, even when ruin comes.","n":"For Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar the ruin comes from that friend, which makes this harder than loyalty in hard times. The verse's தொலைவிடத்தும் doesn't itself say who caused the ruin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:807":{"book":"kural","num":807,"t":["அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார் அன்பின்","வழிவந்த கேண்மை யவர்"],"p":"Those who have (long) stood in the path of affection will not give it up even if their friends cause (them) their ruin.","r":"Even when destructive things are done to them, those whose bond has come by the way of love do not cut off love.","n":"The verse does not say the friendship goes unaltered in every way, only that அன்பு does not end. It locates the constancy in love and not in the deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","pazhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:808":{"book":"kural","num":808,"t":["கேளிழுக்கம் கேளாக் கெழுதகைமை வல்லார்க்கு","நாளிழுக்கம் நட்டார் செயின்"],"p":"To those who understand that by which they should not listen to (tales about) the faults of their friends, that is a (profitable) day on which the latter may commit a fault.","r":"For those who have the strength of intimacy not to listen to reports of a friend's lapse, the day the friend lapses has its own meaning.","n":"The verse has a sting: the lapse is what reveals the strength. Parimelazhagar says the bond only shows when it is tested, so other days don't count.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kizhamai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The day a friend lapses is a day of real worth, for it alone shows such intimacy.","செய்து போந்துழியல்லது அவ்வுரிமை வெளிப்படாமையின், செய்யாதன நாளல்லவாயின."],["manakkudavar","Such a day is a good day.","நட்டோர் தப்புச் செய்யின், அந்த நாள் நல்ல நாளாம்"],["kalaignar","The reverse: if they do wrong to one so trusting, all the days of that friendship were wasted.","அவர்களுடன் நட்புக் கொண்டிருந்த நாளெல்லாம் வீணான நாளாகும்."]],"ch":"k:81"},"k:809":{"book":"kural","num":809,"t":["கெடாஅ வழிவந்த கேண்மையார் கேண்மை","விடாஅர் விழையும் உலகு"],"p":"They will be loved by the world, who have not forsaken the friendship of those with whom they have kept up an unbroken long-standing intimacy.","r":"The world loves those who do not give up the friendship of friends whose bond has come down unbroken.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a motive: everyone reasons that such a person would be like this to them too, and comes to seek friendship. Loyalty to one is an advertisement to all. Manakkudavar's வழிவந்த points rather to friendship inherited through family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:810":{"book":"kural","num":810,"t":["விழையார் விழையப் படுப பழையார்கண்","பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியா தார்"],"p":"Even enemies will love those who have never changed in their affection to their long-standingfriends.","r":"Those who do not depart from their nature toward old friends are wanted even by those who do not want them.","n":"The commentators take விழையார் as enemies. The verse's own word is softer, 'those who don't desire', and the tight wordplay விழையார் விழையப் works without naming any foe.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","panpu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:81"},"k:811":{"book":"kural","num":811,"t":["பருகுவார் போலினும் பண்பிலார் கேண்மை","பெருகலிற் குன்றல் இனிது"],"p":"The decrease of friendship with those who look as if they would eat you up (through excess of love) while they are really destitute of goodness is far better than its increase.","r":"Though they seem to drink you up, the friendship of those without character is better shrinking than growing.","n":"பருகுவார் is the language of passion. Parimelazhagar cites a matching line of poetry and Manakkudavar adds 'with the eyes'. The verse warns that intensity is not evidence. The advice is modest too, since it only says that shrinking is better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:812":{"book":"kural","num":812,"t":["உறின்நட்டு அறின்ஙருஉம் ஒப்பிலார் கேண்மை","பெறினும் இழப்பினும் என்?"],"p":"Of what avail is it to get or lose the friendship of those who love when there is gain and leave when there is none ?","r":"What does it matter whether one gains or loses the friendship of misfits who are friends when there is profit and leave when there is none?","n":"The rhetorical shrug is the point. Parimelazhagar concludes that one should simply be neutral toward them (நொதுமல்). They are neither to be sought nor fought, which sets them apart from the verses that say to run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:813":{"book":"kural","num":813,"t":["உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பும் பெறுவது","கொள்வாரும் கள்வரும் நேர்"],"p":"Friendship who calculate the profits (of their friendship), prostitutes who are bent on obtaining their gains, and thieves are (all) of the same character.","r":"Friends who weigh what they stand to gain, those who take what they get, and thieves are all alike.","n":"The middle term in the verse says only 'those who take what they get'. It is the commentators who identify them as prostitutes. In the verse's shape, the calculation of friends is the real target and the other two are comparisons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:814":{"book":"kural","num":814,"t":["அமரகத்து ஆற்றறுக்கும் கல்லாமா அன்னார்","தமரின் தனிமை தலை"],"p":"Solitude is more to be desired than the society of those who resemble the untrained horses which throw down (their riders) in the fields of battle.","r":"Better solitude than having as one's own those like an untrained horse that throws its rider in battle.","n":"The horse has been carrying its rider well until the moment that counts. Parimelazhagar also notes the verse doesn't praise solitude, which is only less bad. Valluvar isn't recommending it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:815":{"book":"kural","num":815,"t":["செய்தேமஞ் சாராச் சிறியவர் புன்கேண்மை","எய்தலின் எய்தாமை நன்று"],"p":"It is far better to avoid that to contract the evil friendship of the base who cannot protect (their friends) even when appointed to do so.","r":"It is better not to get than to get the poor friendship of the small, who are no protection even after one has made them friends.","n":"ஏமம் brings in the guard of 781 again: a friend who doesn't protect has failed the definition.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The small are base people in general, whose failure is that they leave in ruin.","அரணாகாமை - தொலைவின்கண் விட்டு நீங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","The small are particular kinds of people, gamblers, hunters and women among them, a gendered list which the verse doesn't contain.","சிறியார் - சூதர், வேட்டைக் காரர், பெண்டிர் போல்வார்."]],"ch":"k:82"},"k:816":{"book":"kural","num":816,"t":["பேதை பெருங்கெழீஇ நட்பின் அறிவுடையார்","ஏதின்மை கோடி உறும்"],"p":"The hatred of the wise is ten-million times more profitable than the excessive intimacy of the fool.","r":"Being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool.","n":"ஏதின்மை is literally not-belonging. Pope and the commentators read it as enmity. Parimelazhagar's argument is that the enmity of the wise does no harm while the fool's friendship does every harm.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","natpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:817":{"book":"kural","num":817,"t":["நகைவகைய ராகிய நட்பின் பகைவரால்","பத்தடுத்த கோடி உறும்"],"p":"What comes from enemies is a hundred million times more profitable than what comes from the friendship of those who cause only laughter.","r":"Ten crore times better than friendship of the kind that is all laughter is what comes from enemies.","n":"The multiplier goes up ten times from 816. Parimelazhagar says even the pains that enemies cause exceed the pleasures of these friends, identifies them as hangers-on who amuse for profit, and dismisses other commentators' readings as ungrammatical.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:818":{"book":"kural","num":818,"t":["ஒல்லும் கருமம் உடற்று பவர்கேண்மை","சொல்லாடார் சோர விடல்"],"p":"Gradually abandon without revealing (beforehand) the friendship of those who pretend inability to carry out what they (really) could do.","r":"With those who make hard work of what they could easily do, let the friendship slip without a word.","n":"A practical subtlety. Parimelazhagar says speaking would only lead them to patch things up and go on, so the silence is tactical. It is the opposite of a formal break.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:819":{"book":"kural","num":819,"t":["கனவினும் இன்னாது மன்னோ வினைவேறு","சொல்வேறு பட்டார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"The friendship of those whose actions do not agree with their words will distress (one) even in (one's) dreams.","r":"Even in a dream, the bond with those whose deeds are one thing and words another is bitter.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss is exact: enemies in deed and friends in word. The dream adds that the harm follows into sleep, a place where in other verses love is what lives.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:820":{"book":"kural","num":820,"t":["எனைத்தும் குறுகுதல் ஓம்பல் மனைக்கெழீஇ","மன்றில் பழிப்பார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Avoid even the least approach to a contraction of friendship with those who would love you in private but ridicule you in public.","r":"Take care not to come near at all to the bond of those who are friendly at home and defame you in the assembly.","n":"The contrast is between private and public, and it's the public that matters: a friend is tested by what is said in the மன்று. எனைத்தும் leaves no room for degree, unlike the 'better to shrink' of 811.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:82"},"k:821":{"book":"kural","num":821,"t":["சீரிடம் காணின் எறிதற்குப் பட் டடை","நேரா நிரந்தவர் நட்பு"],"p":"The friendship of those who behave like friends without inward affection is a weapon that may be thrown when a favourable opportunity presents itself.","r":"The friendship of those who come close without inner consent is an anvil for striking, once they find the right place.","n":"A பட்டடை seems to hold a thing up and in fact is what makes the blow cut, so Pope's gloss 'weapon' loses the image. The false friend need not be the blade. The support he gives is what enables it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:822":{"book":"kural","num":822,"t":["இனம்போன்று இனமல்லார் கேண்மை மகளிர்","மனம்போல வேறு படும்"],"p":"The friendship of those who seem to be friends while they are not, will change like the love of women.","r":"The friendship of those who seem one's own but aren't will change the way women's minds change.","n":"The verse itself makes a general comparison about women, and the commentators' responses show how uneasy it is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Women's minds are changeable once there is an opportunity, and he supports it from another text.","இடம் பெற்றால் பெண்பாலார் மனம் போல வேறுபடும்."],["kalaignar","The comparison concerns only prostitutes, who pretend to the virtue that women have, so women in general are not the target.","நடிக்கும் விலை மகளிரின் மனம்போல உள்ளொன்றும் புறமொன்றுமாக"]],"ch":"k:83"},"k:823":{"book":"kural","num":823,"t":["பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்து மனநல்லர்","ஆகுதல் மாணார்க் கரிது"],"p":"Though (one's) enemies may have mastered many good books, it will be impossible for them to become truly loving at heart.","r":"Though they have studied many good things, it is hard for the unworthy to become good at heart.","n":"The verse separates learning from moral change. This limit matters to a text that elsewhere praises study so highly.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","kalvi","vanjam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மாணார் are enemies, and learning won't make them friends, so don't trust them because they are learned.","அதனான் மனம் திருந்தி நட்பாதல் பகைவர்க்கு இல்லை"],["manakkudavar","மாணார் are simply those lacking excellence, and the point is about what learning can reveal.","மனநல்லாராகு தல் மாட்சிமையில்லார்க்கு அரிது"]],"ch":"k:83"},"k:824":{"book":"kural","num":824,"t":["முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா","வஞ்சரை அஞ்சப் படும்"],"p":"One should fear the deceitful who smile sweetly with their face but never love with their heart.","r":"Fear the deceivers who smile sweetly with the face while within they are bitter.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives the reason for fear: the face is the instrument for reading intentions, and here the malice doesn't show on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:825":{"book":"kural","num":825,"t":["மனத்தின் அமையா தவரை எனைத்தொன்றும்","சொல்லினால் தேறற்பாற்று அன்று"],"p":"In nothing whatever is it proper to rely on the words of those who do not love with their heart.","r":"Those not in accord with you in mind are not to be trusted on the strength of their words in anything.","n":"The verse states no authority, but Parimelazhagar supplies one: 'the treatises on policy' say so. He reads the line as counsel on whom to trust in practical affairs (கருமங்கள்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["manam","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:826":{"book":"kural","num":826,"t":["நட்டார்போல் நல்லவை சொல்லினும் ஒட்டார்சொல்","ஒல்லை உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Though (one's) foes may utter good things as though they were friends, once will at once understand (their evil, import).","r":"Though they say good things as friends do, the words of the unreconciled are quickly seen through.","n":"This verse is more optimistic than its neighbours about detection. Parimelazhagar reads the concession to mean they rarely say good things at all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:827":{"book":"kural","num":827,"t":["சொல்வணக்கம் ஒன்னார்கண் கொள்ளற்க வில்வணக்கம்","தீங்கு குறித்தமை யான்"],"p":"Since the bending of the bow bespeaks evil, one should not accept (as good) the humiliating speeches of one's foes.","r":"Do not take humility in an enemy's speech as humility, for the bending of a bow means harm.","n":"Parimelazhagar marks it as 'word-bowing' and not real bowing, since the man himself doesn't bow. The bend is what stores the force, so the humility may be the very preparation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vanjam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:828":{"book":"kural","num":828,"t":["தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும் ஒன்னார்","அழுதகண் ணீரும் அனைத்து"],"p":"A weapon may be hid in the very hands with which (one's) foes adore (him) (and) the tears they shed are of the same nature.","r":"A weapon hides even inside hands folded in worship, and an enemy's shed tears are the same.","n":"The chapter moves from words to the body, and it picks the gestures hardest to doubt, reverence and grief. Parimelazhagar's line is that those able to read intent see the weapon even in the moment of reassurance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["vanjam","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:829":{"book":"kural","num":829,"t":["மிகச்செய்து தம்மெள்ளு வாரை நகச்செய்து","நட்பினுள் சாப்புல்லற் பாற்று"],"p":"It is the duty of kings to affect great love but make it die (inwardly); as regard those foes who shew them great friendship but despise them (in their heart).","r":"Those who overdo friendship and despise you inwardly should be made to smile, and embraced in friendship so that it dies.","n":"The verse itself gives no audience, but Parimelazhagar supplies 'royal policy' and says plainly that having one thing inside and another outside is wrong in general but proper toward enemies. He treats it as a licensed exception. சாப்புல்லல் is a hard phrase: this reading follows the commentators, and Pope's couplet ('slap') goes elsewhere.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vanjam","pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:830":{"book":"kural","num":830,"t":["பகைநட்பாம் காலம் வருங்கால் முகநட்டு","அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்"],"p":"When one's foes begin to affect friendship, one should love them with one's looks, and, cherishing no love in the heart, give up (even the former).","r":"When the time comes that an enemy turns friend, befriend with the face, let go in the heart, and then let it go altogether.","n":"Parimelazhagar narrows the permission to a moment when open rejection isn't possible, and stresses a final exit. So the mask is a temporary means, not a settled way of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:83"},"k:831":{"book":"kural","num":831,"t":["பேதைமை என்பதொன்று யாதெனின் ஏதங்கொண்டு","ஊதியம் போக விடல்"],"p":"Folly is one (of the chief defects); it is that which (makes one) incur loss and forego gain.","r":"What is the one thing called folly? It is taking hold of harm and letting gain go.","n":"This is a definition by behaviour rather than by lack of knowledge. Parimelazhagar lists poverty, blame and sin as the harm and wealth, fame and virtue as the gain, so he reads the choice as ruining both worlds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:832":{"book":"kural","num":832,"t":["பேதைமையுள் எல்லாம் பேதைமை காதன்மை","கையல்ல தன்கட் செயல்"],"p":"The greatest folly is that which leads one to take delight in doing what is forbidden.","r":"Of all follies the worst is to set one's love on what is not for one's hand.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means conduct forbidden by the treatises, loved and done anyway.","இருமைக்கும் ஆகாதென்று நூலோர் கடிந்த செயல்களை விரும்பிச் செய்தல் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","It means something out of reach, loved even though effort won't get it.","தனக்குக் கைவாராத பொருளின்கண் காதன்மை செய்தல்"],["kalaignar","It means tasks beyond one's ability, and meddling in them.","தன்னால் இயலாத செயல்களை விரும்பி, அவற்றில் தலையிடுவது"]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:833":{"book":"kural","num":833,"t":["நாணாமை நாடாமை நாரின்மை யாதொன்றும்","பேணாமை பேதை தொழில்"],"p":"Shamelessness indifference (to what must be sought after), harshness, and aversion for everything (that ought to be desired) are the qualities of the fool.","r":"No shame, no searching, no tenderness, no care for anything: this is what a fool does.","n":"The fool is described by four absences, in a chain of words ending in -மை. தொழில் suggests something habitual: Parimelazhagar says these are his constant nature. நார் is a fibre, a bond, and also fellow-feeling.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","naanam","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:834":{"book":"kural","num":834,"t":["ஓதி உணர்ந்தும் பிறர் க்குரைத்தும் தானடங்காப்","பேதையின் பேதையார் இல்"],"p":"There are no greater fools than he who, though he has read and understood (a great deal) and even taught it to others, does not walk according to his own teaching.","r":"There is no greater fool than one who studies, understands and teaches others but does not restrain himself.","n":"All three stages of learning are granted, and restraint alone is missing. Parimelazhagar makes it medical: this folly isn't cured by its own remedy, and no other remedy exists.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalvi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:835":{"book":"kural","num":835,"t":["ஒருமைச் செயலாற்றும் பேதை எழுமையும்","தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு"],"p":"A fool can procure in a single birth a hell into which he may enter and suffer through all the seven births.","r":"A fool can, in one life's doing, make the mire he will enter and sink into for seven.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The mire is hell, and all future births are lived in hell's suffering because of one life's cruel deeds.","வரும் பிறவிகள் எல்லாம் தான் புக்கு அழுந்தும் நிரயத்தினை"],["kalaignar","ஒருமை is acting on one's own whim, and எழுமை simply means always. The mire is suffering with no births or hell involved.","எக் காலத்திலும் துன்பமெனும் சகதியில் அழுந்திக் கிடக்க நேரிடும்."]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:836":{"book":"kural","num":836,"t":["பொய்படும் ஒன்றோ புனைபூணும் கையறியாப்","பேதை வினைமேற் கொளின்"],"p":"If the fool, who knows not how to act undertakes a work, he will (certainly) fail. (But) is it all ? He will even adorn himself with fetters.","r":"If a fool who doesn't know how takes up a task, it comes to nothing, and more than that, he wears fetters.","n":"பூணும் is what one does with jewellery, so the fetters are worn like ornaments. Manakkudavar takes பொய்படும் to mean he himself gets called false, whereas Parimelazhagar means the work collapses inside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:837":{"book":"kural","num":837,"t":["ஏதிலார் ஆரத் தமர்பசிப்பர் பேதை","பெருஞ்செல்வம் உற்றக் கடை"],"p":"If a fool happens to get an immense fortune, his neighbours will enjoy it while his relations starve.","r":"When a fool gets great wealth, strangers feast and his own people go hungry.","n":"The verse says உற்றக்கடை, 'when it has come to him'. Parimelazhagar takes this as wealth got by fate, on the argument that a fool couldn't have made it himself. The next verse's பெறின் gets the same treatment. Both are his additions.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pedhaimai","selvam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:838":{"book":"kural","num":838,"t":["மையல் ஒருவன் களித்தற்றால் பேதைதன்","கையொன்று உடைமை பெறின்"],"p":"A fool happening to possess something is like the intoxication of one who is (already) giddy.","r":"When a fool gets something into his hand, it is like a man already deranged getting drunk.","n":"The image is of something added to an existing state. Wealth doesn't produce the folly but compounds it, and ஒன்று, 'some one thing', suggests it doesn't take much.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:839":{"book":"kural","num":839,"t":["பெரிதினிது பேதையார் கேண்மை பிரிவின்கண்","பீழை தருவதொன் றில்"],"p":"The friendship between fools is exceedingly delightful (to each other): for at parting there will be nothing to cause them pain.","r":"The friendship of fools is very sweet, for there is nothing painful about parting from them.","n":"பிரிவு is a word that carries great weight in the Book of Love, so the 'sweetness' measured by painless parting reads like the opposite of what love is.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","natpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is mock praise, blame dressed as praise: the friendship wanes daily, so parting hurts no one.","புகழ்வார் போன்று பழித்தவாறு."],["manakkudavar","It concerns women's relations with foolish men, and how a fool enjoys desire.","இது பேதை காமந் துய்க்குமாறு கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","He gives the literal sense, with no irony marked: it is sweet because parting brings no sorrow.","பிரியும்போது எந்தத் துன்பமும் ஏற்படுவதில்லை."]],"ch":"k:84"},"k:840":{"book":"kural","num":840,"t":["கழாஅக்கால் பள்ளியுள் வைத்தற்றால் சான்றோர்","குழாஅத்துப் பேதை புகல்"],"p":"The appearance of a fool in an assembly of the learned is like placing (one's) unwashed feet on a bed.","r":"A fool's entering an assembly of the worthy is like placing an unwashed foot on a bed.","n":"The harm passes to the place: the bed and the assembly are both defiled, not the foot or the fool. Parimelazhagar notes that 'unwashed' is a polite cover for what the foot has trodden, which Kalaignar spells out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:84"},"k:841":{"book":"kural","num":841,"t":["அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை பிறிதின்மை","இன்மையா வையா துலகு"],"p":"The want of wisdom is the greatest of all wants; but that of wealth the world will not regard as such.","r":"Lack of understanding is the poverty of poverties; other lack the world does not count as lack.","n":"வையாது உலகு attributes the valuation to the world. Parimelazhagar's reason is that the wise keep this world's and the next world's fruit even in poverty, whereas the petty-minded don't even with wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:842":{"book":"kural","num":842,"t":["அறிவிலான் நெஞ்சுவந்து ஈதல் பிறிதியாதும்","இல்லை பெறுவான் தவம்"],"p":"(The cause of) a fool cheerfully giving (something) is nothing else but the receiver's merit (in a former birth).","r":"When a person without understanding gives with a glad heart, the cause is nothing other than the receiver's merit.","n":"A strange and rather cold claim: a good act by such a person earns him nothing. Parimelazhagar raises and rebuts the objection that fools also do good. Kalaignar has simply good luck (பெற்றபேறு), which removes தவம் from the picture.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:843":{"book":"kural","num":843,"t":["அறிவிலார் தாந்தம்மைப் பீழிக்கும் பீழை","செறுவார்க்கும் செய்தல் அரிது"],"p":"The suffering that fools inflict upon themselves is hardly possible even to foes.","r":"The suffering those without understanding do to themselves is hard even for their enemies to do.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a clever reason: an enemy has to watch for a moment and knows only so much, whereas self-injury never pauses and reaches poverty, blame and sin all at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:844":{"book":"kural","num":844,"t":["வெண்மை எனப்படுவ தியாதெனின் ஒண்மை","உடையம்யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"What is called want of wisdom is the vanity which says, \"We are wise\".","r":"What is called வெண்மை? It is the pride that says 'we have brightness.'","n":"வெண்மை, whiteness, is rawness or unripeness. ஒண்மை is brightness. The two words sound alike and describe opposite things, and the fool can't tell whether he is lit or blank. Parimelazhagar calls it a delusion because such people persist knowing the world mocks them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:845":{"book":"kural","num":845,"t":["கல்லாத மேற்கொண் டொழுகல் கசடற","வல்லதூஉம் ஐயம் தரும்"],"p":"Fools pretending to know what has not been read (by them) will rouse suspicion even as to what they have thoroughly mastered.","r":"Taking on what one hasn't learned brings doubt even on what one has mastered without flaw.","n":"The verse is about how credibility works. The pretence costs real knowledge too. This is not a moral claim but a practical one about reputation, and it grants that the pretender may have actual skill.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:846":{"book":"kural","num":846,"t":["அற்றம் மறைத்தலோ புல்லறிவு தம்வயின்","குற்றம் மறையா வழி"],"p":"Even to cover one's nakedness would be folly, if (one's) faults were not covered (by forsaking them).","r":"Is covering one's nakedness not itself petty, when one's faults aren't covered?","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","To cover a fault means to get rid of it. Covering the lesser thing while leaving the greater and thinking oneself decent is the folly.","குற்றம் மறைத்தலாவது, அவற்றை இலவாக்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","To cover a fault means keeping it from others' notice, as clothing hides the body from view.","தம்பாலுள்ள குற்றத்தைப் பிறரறியாமல் தாம் மறையாத காலத் து"]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:847":{"book":"kural","num":847,"t":["அருமறை சோரும் அறிவிலான் செய்யும்","பெருமிறை தானே தனக்கு"],"p":"The fool who neglects precious counsel does, of his own accord, a great injury to himself.","r":"One without understanding who lets slip the precious மறை does great harm to himself by himself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kedu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறை is rare teaching which the fool doesn't take in. He rejects the other reading explicitly, since leaking a secret would be carelessness and not this fault.","அது பேணாமை என்னும் பேதைமையாவதன்றிப் புல்லறிவாண்மையன்மை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","மறை is a closely held secret, and letting it slip means telling others.","சோரவிடுதல் - பிறர்க்குச் சொல்லுதல்."]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:848":{"book":"kural","num":848,"t":["ஏவவும் செய்கலான் தான்தேறான் அவ்வுயிர்","போஒம் அளவுமோர் நோய்"],"p":"The fool will not perform (his duties) even when advised nor ascertain them himself; such a soul is a burden (to the earth) till it departs (from the body).","r":"Not doing it when told, not seeing it himself, that life is a disease until it departs.","n":"The verse doesn't say for whom. Parimelazhagar says for the earth that bears him, Manakkudavar for the people of the world, and Kalaignar for himself. These are three very different diseases. The verse's two failures also cover both routes to knowledge, one's own and another's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:849":{"book":"kural","num":849,"t":["காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான் காணாதான்","கண்டானாம் தான்கண்ட வாறு"],"p":"One who would teach a fool will (simply) betray his folly; and the fool would (still) think himself \"wise in his own conceit\".","r":"One who shows things to a person who doesn't see becomes himself unseeing, and the unseeing one goes on having seen what he saw.","n":"The compact wordplay on காண் is the content: the teacher fails and the pupil remains satisfied. Parimelazhagar says the teacher is blamed by the pupil and ends up the ignorant one, and concludes that good sense can't be instilled here by any means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:85"},"k:850":{"book":"kural","num":850,"t":["உலகத்தார் உண்டென்பது இல்லென்பான் வையத்து","அலகையா வைக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He who denies the existence of what the world believes in will be regarded as a demon on earth.","r":"One who says 'it isn't' to what the world says is will be set down on this earth as a demon.","n":"The verse names neither what's denied nor who 'the world' is, and everything turns on those blanks.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What is denied is God, rebirth, and the fruit of deeds, as affirmed by the eminent.","கடவுளும், மறுபிறப்பும், இருவினைப் பயனும் முதலாக அவர் உள என்பன பலவேனும்"],["manakkudavar","What is denied is what the many wise affirm, with no specific doctrine named.","உலகத்தில் அறிவுடையார் பலர் உண்டென்பதாகிய ஒரு பொருளை இல்லையென்று"],["kalaignar","What is denied is what's been shown clearly by evidence, and the denial is deliberate.","ஆதாரங்களைக் காட்டி இதுதான் உண்மை என்று தெளிவாகக் கூறப்படுகிற ஒன்றை"]],"ch":"k:85"},"k:851":{"book":"kural","num":851,"t":["இகலென்ப எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் பகலென்னும்","பண்பின்மை பாரிக்கும் நோய்"],"p":"The disease which fosters the evil of disunion among all creatures is termed hatred by the wise.","r":"They call hostility the disease that spreads, among all living beings, the ill nature of division.","n":"Hostility here is not an event but a disease that spreads a disposition. Parimelazhagar sees in எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் a judgement: quarrelling humans are being put with the animals.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:852":{"book":"kural","num":852,"t":["பகல்கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும் இகல்கருதி","இன்னாசெய் யாமை தலை"],"p":"Though disagreeable things may be done from (a feeling of) disunion, it is far better that nothing painful be done from (that of) hatred.","r":"Even if someone does what you can't bear, intending division, it is best not to do harm in return, intending hostility.","n":"The reason Parimelazhagar gives is strategic rather than saintly. Retaliate and enmity grows and you sink, abstain and it dies down and you rise. This is non-retaliation argued in the Book of Wealth's terms.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:853":{"book":"kural","num":853,"t":["இகலென்னும் எவ்வநோய் நீக்கின் தவலில்லாத்","தாவில் விளக்கம் தரும்"],"p":"To rid one-self of the distressing dtsease of hatred will bestow (on one) a never-decreasing imperishable fame.","r":"If the painful disease called hostility is removed, it gives a light that doesn't diminish and has no flaw.","n":"Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar differ on விளக்கம்: fame for one, radiance for the other. His route to fame is a long chain. Everyone becomes a friend, then every wealth comes, then giving, then renown. The verse takes no such steps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:854":{"book":"kural","num":854,"t":["இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் பயக்கும் இகலென்னும்","துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்"],"p":"If hatred which is the greatest misery is destroyed, it will yield the greatest delight.","r":"If the suffering of sufferings called hostility is destroyed, it brings the joy of joys.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","inbam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The joy is worldly: with everyone a friend, one obtains every fruit.","யாவரும் நட்பாகலின் எல்லாப் பயனும் எய்தியுறுவது."],["manakkudavar","The joy is liberation, the highest of all joys.","எல்லா இன்பத்தின் மிக்க வீடுபேற்றின்பம் எய்தும் என்றவாறு."]],"ch":"k:86"},"k:855":{"book":"kural","num":855,"t":["இகலெதிர் சாய்ந்தொழுக வல்லாரை யாரே","மிக்லூக்கும் தன்மை யவர்"],"p":"Who indeed would think of conquering those who naturally shrink back from hatred ?","r":"Who would set out to defeat those who are able to lean away from hostility?","n":"வல்லாரை marks it as an ability, not a weakness. Manakkudavar faces the objection directly: wouldn't giving way be a defeat? The verse answers that no one can defeat them. Parimelazhagar adds it is particularly hard for kings.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["igal","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:856":{"book":"kural","num":856,"t":["இகலின் மிகலினிது என்பவன் வாழ்க்கை","தவலும் கெடலும் நணித்து"],"p":"Failure and ruin are not far from him who says it is sweet to excel in hatred.","r":"The life of one who says it is sweet to excel in hostility is close to failure and to ruin.","n":"The problem is the relish: என்பவன் indicts the person who enjoys conflict and not one drawn into it. Kalaignar gives 'easy' (எளிது) for the verse's 'sweet' (இனிது), which changes the charge from pleasure to overconfidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:857":{"book":"kural","num":857,"t":["மிகல்மேவல் மெய்ப்பொருள் காணார் இகல்மேவல்","இன்னா அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"Those whose judgement brings misery through its connection with hatred cannot understand the triumphant nature of truth.","r":"Those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the true thing that holds victory.","n":"Parimelazhagar's point: hostility itself clouds the mind, so that the very quarrel blinds one to how to win. A combative person is therefore bad at conflict.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மெய்ப்பொருள் here is the content of the treatises on policy, which brings success to those who follow them.","வெற்றி பொருந்துதலையுடைய நீதி நூற்பொருளை அறியமாட்டார்."],["manakkudavar","It is truth as such, which those in conflict can't perceive.","இது மாறுபடுவார்க்கு மெய்ப்பொருள் தோன்றாதென்றது."]],"ch":"k:86"},"k:858":{"book":"kural","num":858,"t":["இகலிற்கு எதிர்சாய்தல் ஆக்கம் அதனை","மிக்லூக்கின் ஊக்குமாம் கேடு"],"p":"Shrinking back from hatred will yield wealth; indulging in its increase will hasten ruin.","r":"Leaning away from hostility is gain; if one drives on to excel at it, ruin drives on to come.","n":"Ruin mirrors what one does: ஊக்கின் ஊக்கும். Parimelazhagar locates the turning point in one's own mind, at the moment when hostility arises and isn't taken up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:859":{"book":"kural","num":859,"t":["இகல்காணான் ஆக்கம் வருங்கால் அதனை","மிகல்காணும் கேடு தரற்கு"],"p":"At the approach of wealth one will not think of hatred (but) to secure one's ruin, one will look to its increase.","r":"When gain is coming one doesn't look at hostility, and to bring on ruin one looks to excel at it.","n":"This complicates 858. Hostility may not be the cause of ruin but the sign of it. Parimelazhagar says these are forerunners of gain and loss, and Manakkudavar links them to one's merit, which reduces how much the choice in 858 is really free.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:860":{"book":"kural","num":860,"t":["இகலானாம் இன்னாத எல்லாம் நகலானாம்","நன்னயம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"All calamities are caused by hatred; but by the delight (of friendship) is caused the great wealth of good virtues.","r":"From hostility come all ills, and from friendly gladness comes the pride of good and fitting ways.","n":"செருக்கு is usually a fault in the Kural (844) and here it is praise. Parimelazhagar reads it as great wealth, and நன்னயம் as good policy. So the virtue is presented as a prosperous kind of wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:86"},"k:861":{"book":"kural","num":861,"t":["வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக ஓம்பா","மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை"],"p":"Avoid offering resistance to the strong; (but) never fail to cherish enmity towards the weak.","r":"Avoid opposing the strong, and without holding back, favour enmity against the weak.","n":"Whatever this is, it is not a moral teaching, which is why the commentators' disagreement matters so much.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a plain rule of statecraft: avoid the strong, and keep enmity with the weak, since beating them doesn't drain one's strength.","ஏனை மெலியார்க்குப் பகையாதலை ஒழியாது விரும்புக."],["manakkudavar","The weak in view are those who don't respect one, which narrows the rule.","தம்மைப் போற்றாத எளியார்மாட்டுப் பகைகோடலை மேவுக"],["kalaignar","The opposite: true excellence in enmity is leaving the weak alone and taking on the strong.","மெலியோரை விடுத்து, வலியோரை எதிர்த்துப் போரிட விரும்புவதே"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:862":{"book":"kural","num":862,"t":["அன்பிலன் ஆன்ற துணையிலன் தான்துவ்வான்","என்பரியும் ஏதிலான் துப்பு"],"p":"How can he who is unloving, destitute of powerful aids, and himself without strength overcome the might of his foe ?","r":"With no love, no worthy allies and no strength of his own, how can he wear down an enemy's might?","n":"அன்பு appears in a list of military assets. For Parimelazhagar it is love of one's kin, so without it there's no kin, and for an attacker such a person only adds strength.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:863":{"book":"kural","num":863,"t":["அஞ்சும் அறியான் அமைவிலன் ஈகலான்","தஞ்சம் எளியன் பகைக்கு"],"p":"In the estimation of foes miserably weak is he, who is timid, ignorant, unsociable and niggardly.","r":"He fears, doesn't know, doesn't fit in with others, doesn't give: he is utterly easy for an enemy.","n":"The list includes not giving, which is an ethical failing elsewhere and here is a military one. Kalaignar's 'compassion' softens the word. Parimelazhagar says அஞ்சும் means fearing what needn't be feared, the mirror of 428.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:864":{"book":"kural","num":864,"t":["நீங்கான் வெகுளி நிறையிலன் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","யாங்கணும் யார்க்கும் எளிது"],"p":"He who neither refrains from anger nor keeps his secrets will at all times and in all places be easily conquered by all.","r":"Not free from rage and without நிறை: at any time, in any place, he is easy for anyone.","n":"The commentators differ on நிறை. Parimelazhagar means keeping secrets and Kalaignar means self-control. Parimelazhagar's logic is neat: the enemy no longer needs to watch for the right time, place or strength because the angry man's leaked plans give it all away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:865":{"book":"kural","num":865,"t":["வழிநோக்கான் வாய்ப்பன செய்யான் பழிநோக்கான்","பண்பிலன் பற்றார்க்கு இனிது"],"p":"(A) pleasing (object) to his foes is he who reads not moral works, does nothing that is enjoined by them cares not for reproach and is not possessed of good qualities.","r":"He doesn't look to the வழி, doesn't do what is suitable, doesn't look to blame, has no character: he is a pleasure to his enemies.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","panpu","pazhi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வழி is the treatise on policy, handed down from the ancients, which he doesn't study.","ஒருவன் நீதிநூலை ஓதான்"],["manakkudavar","வழி is the consequence of an action, which he doesn't consider when he starts it.","ஒருவினை செய்யத் தொடங்குங்கால் பின் வருவன பாரான்"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:866":{"book":"kural","num":866,"t":["காணாச் சினத்தான் கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்","பேணாமை பேணப் படும்"],"p":"Highly to be desired is the hatred of him whose anger is blind, and whose lust increases beyond measure.","r":"One of blinding anger and ever-growing lust: his not caring for you is something to care for.","n":"பேணாமை பேணப்படும் is the wordplay and the whole point. Such a person's hostility is an asset, since he will destroy himself. Parimelazhagar says all become his enemies through the anger, and in the lust he loses sight of his affairs. Kalaignar turns காமம் into greed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:867":{"book":"kural","num":867,"t":["கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற அடுத்திருந்து","மாணாத செய்வான் பகை"],"p":"It is indeed necessary to obtain even by purchase the hatred of him who having begun (a work) does what is not conductive (to its accomplishment).","r":"One who stays close and does what is unbecoming: it is truly necessary to get his enmity, even by giving something.","n":"Even the enmity has a price worth paying. The verse's hardest word is அடுத்திருந்து, and the commentators each supply a different object.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He is a ruler who has begun a task and does what doesn't fit it, so losing a little to fight him pays many times over.","வினையைத் தொடங்கியிருந்து அதற்கு ஏலாதன செய்வான் பகைமையை"],["manakkudavar","He has taken up enmity and does what doesn't serve it, so he will not actually hurt you.","இஃது இவன் பகைசெய்யமாட்டானாதலால், அப்பகை கோடலாமென்றது."],["kalaignar","He is someone staying at your side and doing wrong, and you must pay to make him an open enemy.","தன்னோடு இருந்துகொண்டே தனக்குப் பொருந்தாத காரியங்களைச் செய்து கொண்டிருப்பவனைப்"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:868":{"book":"kural","num":868,"t":["குணனிலனாய்க் குற்றம் பலவாயின் மாற்றார்க்கு","இனனிலனாம் ஏமாப் புடைத்து"],"p":"He will become friendless who is without (any good) qualities. and whose faults are many; (such a character) is a help to (his) foes.","r":"With no qualities and many faults he has no one around him, and this is a protection to his enemies.","n":"ஏமாப்பு, the word for protection, has been turned around. A man's faults become his enemies' shield. Parimelazhagar lists what deserts him: kin, friends, wealth, army.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","panpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:869":{"book":"kural","num":869,"t":["செறுவார்க்குச் சேணிகவா இன்பம் அறிவிலா","அஞ்சும் பகைவர்ப் பெறின்"],"p":"There will be no end of lofty delights to the victorious, if their foes are (both) ignorant and timid.","r":"Joy doesn't go far from those fighting, if they get enemies who are unknowing and afraid.","n":"பெறின் sounds like luck. Parimelazhagar says that's because foes with neither knowledge nor resolve are rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:87"},"k:870":{"book":"kural","num":870,"t":["கல்லான் வெகுளும் சிறுபொருள் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","ஒல்லானை ஒல்லா தொளி"],"p":"The light (of fame) will never be gained by him who gains not the trifling reputation of having fought an unlearned (foe).","r":"Fame will never come to one who cannot manage the small thing of being angry at the unlearned.","n":"The syntax is hard and the commentaries don't converge. The reading given follows Parimelazhagar, who says others have not kept to the chapter's subject.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","pugazh","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Fighting the untaught is cheap in effort, and one who can't manage even that won't win fame.","நீதி அறியாதானை வேறல் எளிதாயிருக்கவும், அது மாட்டாதானை வெற்றியான் வரும் புகழ் கூடாது என்பதாம்"],["manakkudavar","The words describe one enemy who is unlearned, angry and poor, and it is he whom light never reaches.","கல்லாதானுமாய், வெகுளியுடையனுமாய், சிறு பொருளனுமாகிய பகைவனை எல்லா நாளும் ஒளி பொருந்தாது"]],"ch":"k:87"},"k:871":{"book":"kural","num":871,"t":["பகைஎன்னும் பண்பி லதனை ஒருவன்","நகையேயும் வேண்டற்பாற்று அன்று"],"p":"The evil of hatred is not of a nature to be desired by one even in sport.","r":"The thing called enmity, which has no good in it, shouldn't be wanted by anyone, even in jest.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a psychological reason for நகையேயும்: desired in play, the resentment grows and it becomes real. The verse sits at odds with the chapter that comes just before it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:872":{"book":"kural","num":872,"t":["வில்லேர் உழவர் பகைகொளினும் கொள்ளற்க","சொல்லேர் உழவர் பகை"],"p":"Though you may incur the hatred of warriors whose ploughs are bows, incur not that of ministers whose ploughs are words.","r":"Though you take on the enmity of ploughmen whose plough is the bow, do not take on that of ploughmen whose plough is the word.","n":"A famous line, often read now as about writers and poets, but the older commentators don't read it that way. Parimelazhagar means those with counsel and policy, whose harm reaches one's descendants, and Manakkudavar means ministers. Kalaignar's 'learned' is the broadest. Both groups are 'ploughmen', borrowing the honour chapter 104 gives to that work.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:873":{"book":"kural","num":873,"t":["ஏமுற் றவரினும் ஏழை தமியனாய்ப்","பல்லார் பகைகொள் பவன்"],"p":"He who being alone, incurs the hatred of many is more infatuated than even mad men.","r":"One who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many is more wretched than those who are ஏமுற்றவர்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏமுற்றவர் are the mad, who at least aren't harmed by their delusion, while this one is.","மயக்கத்தால் ஒப்பாராயினும் ஏமுற்றவர் அதனால் தீங்கு எய்தாமையின்"],["kalaignar","ஏமுற்றவர் are the arrogant, and this person is better called a fool than arrogant.","ஆணவம் பிடித்தவன் என்பதைவிட அறிவிலி என்பதே பொருத்தமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:874":{"book":"kural","num":874,"t":["பகைநட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும் பண்புடை யாளன்","தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்று உலகு"],"p":"The world abides in the greatness of that good-natured man who behaves so as to turn hatred into friendship.","r":"The world rests in the greatness of a person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship.","n":"நட்பாக் கொண்டு is ambiguous: making them friends, or treating them as friends? The whole verse turns on it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","A king who, when it is needed, wins enemies over, and whose power therefore holds the world.","வேண்டியவழிப் பகையை வேறுபடுத்துத் தனக்கு நட்பாகச் செய்து கொண்டொழுகும் இயல்பினையுடைய அரசனது"],["manakkudavar","One able to behave toward enemies as if they were friends.","பகைவரை நட்புபோலக் கொண்டொழுக வல்ல பண்புடையவன்"],["kalaignar","It is a moral nobility in anyone who thinks of enemies as friends, and the world praises it.","பகைவர்களையும் நண்பர்களாகக் கருதிப் பழகுகின்ற பெருந்தன்மையான பண்பை"]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:875":{"book":"kural","num":875,"t":["தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால் தான்ஒருவன்","இன்துணையாக் கொள்கவற்றின் ஒன்று"],"p":"He who is alone and helpless while his foes are two should secure one of them as an agreeable help (to himself).","r":"If you have no ally and two enemies, being alone, take one of them as a pleasing ally.","n":"The word is இன்துணை, a sweet ally, which is warm for what it describes. Parimelazhagar adds 'for that time', and picks the one suited to defeating the other. The temporariness is his addition, though it's probably correct as politics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:876":{"book":"kural","num":876,"t":["தேறினும் தேறா விடினும் அழிவின்கண்","தேறான் பகாஅன் விடல்"],"p":"Though (one's foe is) aware or not of one's misfortune one should act so as neither to join nor separate (from him).","r":"Whether or not you have trusted him, when ruin comes neither join with him nor break from him; let him be.","n":"A rule for neutrality. Parimelazhagar explains the two halves separately: don't join, because he could harm from inside; don't break, because he could help your ruin along. In adversity an enemy is best left unprovoked.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:877":{"book":"kural","num":877,"t":["நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு மேவற்க","மென்மை பகைவர் அகத்து"],"p":"Relate not your suffering even to friends who are ignorant of it, nor refer to your weakness in the presence of your foes.","r":"Don't complain to those who don't know of your pain, and don't show softness in front of enemies.","n":"The pairing is the surprise. Parimelazhagar takes those who don't know to be friends who haven't noticed on their own, which is itself a judgement on them. Pain should be read and not announced, which ties this to the chapters on signs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:878":{"book":"kural","num":878,"t":["வகையறிந்து தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப மாயும்","பகைவர்கண் பட்ட செருக்கு"],"p":"The joy of one's foes will be destroyed if one guards oneself by knowing the way (of acting) and securing assistance.","r":"Knowing the way, building oneself up and guarding oneself will end the swagger on the enemy's side.","n":"All three verbs are about oneself and none about the enemy, yet the enemy is what changes. Parimelazhagar's enemy is celebrating an expected win. Manakkudavar calls this 'the way to kill enemies', which shows how much he thinks self-strengthening does.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:879":{"book":"kural","num":879,"t":["இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க களையுநர்","கைகொல்லும் காழ்த்த இடத்து"],"p":"A thorny tree should be felled while young, (for) when it is grown it will destroy the hand of the feller.","r":"Cut a thorn tree while it is young; grown hard, it will destroy the hand that cuts it.","n":"The verse itself doesn't mention enemies, and Parimelazhagar labels it allegory. Its logic of acting before a threat matures has no room in it for what a sapling might have become, or for whether it is really a thorn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:88"},"k:880":{"book":"kural","num":880,"t":["உயிர்ப்ப உளரல்லர் மன்ற செயிர்ப்பவர்","செம்மல் சிதைக்கலா தார்"],"p":"Those who do not destroy the pride of those who hate (them) will certainly not exist even to breathe.","r":"Those who don't break the pride of those hostile to them are, surely, not alive even to breathe.","n":"Parimelazhagar adds that this is about one who could do it and neglects it out of contempt.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Their enemies will grow and destroy them, so they won't live even long enough to draw breath. He allows that it may be the enemy's breath that kills.","அவர் உயிர்த்த துணையானே தாம் இறப்பர் எனினும் அமையும்."],["manakkudavar","It is the enemy's breath that kills, and the lesson is not to take on the strong as enemies at all.","இது பகை கொள்ளுங்கால் வலியாரோடு பகைகோடலாகா தென்றது."],["kalaignar","They can't be said to live merely because they are breathing.","சுவாசிக்கிற காரணத்தினாலேயே, உயிரோடிருப்பதாக நிச்சயமாகச் சொல்ல முடியாது."]],"ch":"k:88"},"k:881":{"book":"kural","num":881,"t":["நிழல்நீரும் இன்னாத இன்னா தமர்நீரும்","இன்னாவாம் இன்னா செயின்"],"p":"Shade and water are not pleasant, (if) they cause disease; so are the qualities of (one's) relations not agreeable, (if) they cause pain.","r":"Even shade and water are bad if they do harm, and the நீர் of one's own people too is bad, if it does harm.","n":"நீர் does double work, as water and as nature: தமர்நீர் is the nature of kin, sitting right next to actual water. Parimelazhagar's kin are specifically the relatives who could become inner enemies, and his illnesses from bad water are quite concrete. Manakkudavar reads நிழல்நீர் as one thing, the water in the shade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:882":{"book":"kural","num":882,"t":["வாள்போல பகைவரை அஞ்சற்க அஞ்சுக","கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Fear not foes (who say they would cut) like a sword; (but) fear the friendship of foes (who seemingly act) like relations.","r":"Don't fear enemies who are like swords, but fear the bond with enemies who are like kin.","n":"The verse says to fear the தொடர்பு and not the person. Parimelazhagar explains that the danger already lies in the existing bond, the one that has been made, and that open foes can be guarded against because they are known.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:883":{"book":"kural","num":883,"t":["உட்பகை அஞ்சித்தற் காக்க உலைவிடத்து","மட்பகையின் மாணத் தெறும்"],"p":"Fear internal enmity and guard yourself; (if not) it will destroy (you) in an evil hour, as surely as the tool which cuts the potter's clay.","r":"Fear the enemy within and guard yourself; when you falter, it will cut surely, like the potter's cutter.","n":"A மட்பகை is what the potter uses to take a pot off the wheel. Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's point is that the cut is clean and unnoticed. உலைவிடத்து adds patience, as this enemy doesn't need to attack until you are already down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:884":{"book":"kural","num":884,"t":["மனமாணா உட்பகை தோன்றின் இனமாணா","ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"The secret enmity of a person whose mind in unreformed will lead to many evils causing disaffection among (one's) relations.","r":"If an inner enmity of unreformed mind appears, it brings many ills of a circle that has gone bad.","n":"The damage is secondary: it spoils the other relationships. Parimelazhagar's analysis is that the enemy works from within to turn others, the king's trust then fails too, and the whole thing compounds. Kalaignar places the mind that won't reform in the person himself, not in another.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:885":{"book":"kural","num":885,"t":["உறல்முறையான் உட்பகை தோன்றின் இறல்முறையான்","ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்"],"p":"If there appears internal hatred in a (king's) family; it will lead to many a fatal crime.","r":"If an inner enmity appears that has the form of kinship, it brings many ills that have the form of death.","n":"The verse's two lines balance on முறையான், so the closer the tie, the closer to death.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","sutram","saakkaadu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is about a king: an outwardly related enemy who may kill him, aid outside foes, and erode his ministers.","புறப்பகைத் துணையாய் நின்றே அது தோன்றாமல் கோறல் முதலிய வஞ்சனை செய்தலும்"],["kalaignar","It is about enmity among close relatives in general, who all then suffer.","நெருங்கிய உறவினருக்கிடையே தோன்றும் உட்பகையானது"]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:886":{"book":"kural","num":886,"t":["ஒன்றாமை ஒன்றியார் கட்படின் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பொன்றாமை ஒன்றல் அரிது"],"p":"If hatred arises among (one's) own people, it will be hardly possible (for one) to escape death.","r":"If not-being-one arises among those who are one, it is ever hard to reach not-dying.","n":"The wordplay runs through three lines: ஒன்றாமை, ஒன்றியார், ஒன்றல். Oneness is what is broken and also what is needed. Parimelazhagar's எஞ்ஞான்றும் means even when one is at the peak of power.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:887":{"book":"kural","num":887,"t":["செப்பின் புணர்ச்சிபோல் கூடினும் கூடாதே","உட்பகை உற்ற குடி"],"p":"Never indeed will a family subject to internal hatred unite (really) though it may present an apparent union like that of a casket and its lid.","r":"Though joined like a casket and its lid, a family that has inner enmity is not joined.","n":"The image shows a perfect fit with a seam all the same. The lid and the box remain two objects.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The members look united but are not, and will split when an outside enemy comes. He notes other readings with the enmity itself as what doesn't join.","உட்பகையான் மனம் வேறுபட்டமையின், புறப்பகை பெற்றுழி வீற்றுவீற்றாவர் என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The enmity and the family are what fit together, and the lid moves aside for the one who comes to take what's inside, as the insider assists the thief.","செப்பகத் துப் பொருள் வாங்குவார்க்கு மூடி துணையாய் அகன்று நிற்குமதுபோல அகன்று நிற்பரென்றவாறு."]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:888":{"book":"kural","num":888,"t":["அரம்பொருத பொன்போலத் தேயும் உரம்பொருது","உட்பகை உற்ற குடி"],"p":"A family subject to internal hatred will wear out and lose its strength like iron that has been filed away.","r":"As பொன் that is rubbed by a file wears away, a family with inner enmity wears down its strength by rubbing against itself.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பொன் is iron, the hard metal worn down by a file, and the insider seems to help while slowly separating people.","அரத்தாற் பொரப்பட்ட இரும்பு போல அதனால் பொரப்பட்டு வலி தேயும்."],["manakkudavar","பொன் is gold, which is polished as it is filed. So inner enmity seems to beautify while it wastes strength.","இஃது உட்பகை அழகு செய்வது போலப் பலத்தைக் கெடுக்கு மென்றது."]],"ch":"k:89"},"k:889":{"book":"kural","num":889,"t":["எட்பக வன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்","உட்பகை உள்ளதாங் கேடு"],"p":"Although internal hatred be as small as the fragment of the sesamum (seed), still does destruction dwell in it.","r":"Though only as small as a split sesame seed, inner enmity has ruin within it.","n":"உள்ளதாம் lets the word play with உட்பகை: the ruin is inside what is inside. Parimelazhagar notes the smallness is relative to a king's greatness, and that the large ruin stays contained until its hour comes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:890":{"book":"kural","num":890,"t":["உடம்பாடு இலாதவர் வாழ்க்கை குடங்கருள்","பாம்போடு உடனுறைந் தற்று"],"p":"Living with those who do not agree (with one) is like dwelling with a cobra (in the same) hut.","r":"Living with those not in accord with you is like living with a snake inside a hut.","n":"Pope's 'domestic life' narrows what the verse leaves open. It says only 'those not in accord', which could be anyone one lives with. Parimelazhagar's point is about smallness and familiarity: in a confined space with long proximity, the bite is a certainty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["utpagai","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:89"},"k:891":{"book":"kural","num":891,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் இகழாமை போற்றுவார்","போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை"],"p":"Not to disregard the power of those who can carry out (their wishes) is more important than all the watchfulness of those who guard (themselves against evil).","r":"Not slighting the power of those able to act is the highest of all safeguards for those who guard themselves.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes a large claim from this: fort, army, wealth and friends can all be undone by such people, so this safeguard outranks them. It closes a stretch of chapters that were all about those other safeguards.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:892":{"book":"kural","num":892,"t":["பெரியாரைப் பேணாது ஒழுகிற் பெரியாரால்","பேரா இடும்பை தரும்"],"p":"To behave without respect for the great (rulers) will make them do (us) irremediable evils.","r":"Going on without regard for the great will, through the great, bring ills that do not leave.","n":"The grammar makes the conduct itself the agent, and the great are only the instrument. Parimelazhagar says this was meant to show people do it to themselves. He has the king as the one at fault, whereas Manakkudavar has a person failing to honour sages.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:893":{"book":"kural","num":893,"t":["கெடல்வேண்டின் கேளாது செய்க அடல்வேண்டின்","ஆற்று பவர்கண் இழுக்கு"],"p":"If a person desires ruin, let him not listen to the righteous dictates of law, but commit crimes against those who are able to slay (other sovereigns).","r":"If you want to be ruined, don't listen, and commit a fault against those able to destroy.","n":"A piece of grim irony, phrased as an instruction. The commentators differ on what isn't listened to: Parimelazhagar means the treatises on policy, and Manakkudavar means the great themselves. His Purananuru citation makes such kings swifter than Death, who at least waits for the time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:894":{"book":"kural","num":894,"t":["கூற்றத்தைக் கையால் விளித்தற்றால் ஆற்றுவார்க்கு","ஆற்றாதார் இன்னா செயல்"],"p":"The weak doing evil to the strong is like beckoning Yama to come (and destroy them).","r":"For those who can't do harm to those who can is like beckoning Death with the hand.","n":"The word is விளி, to call, so the gesture does it. Parimelazhagar adds that Death would have come anyway, so the action only brings it sooner, and that the hand gesture itself carries a scornful tone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:895":{"book":"kural","num":895,"t":["யாண்டுச்சென் றியாண்டு முளராகார் வெந்துப்பின்","வேந்து செறப்பட் டவர்"],"p":"Those who have incurred the wrath of a cruel and mighty potentate will not prosper wherever they may go.","r":"Wherever they go, they won't exist anywhere, those who've drawn the anger of a king of fierce might.","n":"Parimelazhagar is shrewdly political: other kings won't shelter the fugitive and may kill him to win favour or his goods. The verse's point is not the king's reach but what everyone around him will do because of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vendhan","perumai","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:896":{"book":"kural","num":896,"t":["எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்யார்","பெரியார்ப் பிழைத்தொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Though burnt by a fire (from a forest), one may perhaps live; (but) never will he live who has shown disrespect to the great (devotees).","r":"Even if burned by fire, there can be survival; those who go on offending the great will not survive.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The great are ascetics, whose wrath strikes at the life itself in an instant where fire only reaches the body first.","தவத்தாற் பெரியாரைப் பிழைத்து ஒழுகுவார், எவ்வாற்றானும் உயிருய்யார்."],["manakkudavar","The great are sages, with the same conclusion.","இது முனிவரைப் பிழைத்தலினால் வருங்குற்றம் கூறிற்று."],["kalaignar","The great are simply those mighty in capacity, with no asceticism involved.","ஆற்றல் மிகுந்த பெரியோரிடம் தவறிழைப்போர் தப்பிப் பிழைப்பது முடியாது."]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:897":{"book":"kural","num":897,"t":["வகைமாண்ட வாழ்க்கையும் வான்பொருளும் என்னாம்","தகைமாண்ட தக்கார் செறின்"],"p":"If a king incurs the wrath of the righteous great, what will become of his government with its splendid auxiliaries and (all) its untold wealth ?","r":"What are a fully appointed life and lofty wealth, if worthy ones of great stature are angered?","n":"Parimelazhagar says தகை means greatness that can curse and bless. Note his remark that செறின் implies they usually don't get angry, which holds for the verses after this. The verse's question doesn't state a mechanism, while he describes royal wealth burning up in a moment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:898":{"book":"kural","num":898,"t":["குன்றன்னார் குன்ற மதிப்பின் குடியொடு","நின்றன்னார் மாய்வர் நிலத்து"],"p":"If (the) hill-like (devotees) resolve on destruction, those who seemed to be everlasting will be destroyed root and branch from the earth.","r":"If hill-like people are held low or themselves turn to bringing low, those who seemed to stand forever will vanish from earth with their line.","n":"நின்றன்னார், those who 'seem to stand', and குன்றன்னார், those who 'are like hills': the verse compares two kinds of standing. குன்ற is also a verbal pun on the hill.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குன்ற மதித்தல் is what the hill-like ascetics do, resolving on someone's ruin, and the rich die with their kin at once.","குன்றத்தை ஒக்கும் அருந்தவர் கெட நினைப்பாராயின்"],["manakkudavar","It is done to them: someone thinks little of those who are great as hills.","குன்ற மதித்தல் - அவமதித்தல்."]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:899":{"book":"kural","num":899,"t":["ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின் இடைமுரிந்து","வேந்தனும் வேந்து கெடும்"],"p":"If those of exalted vows burst in a rage, even (Indra) the king will suffer a sudden loss and be entirely ruined.","r":"If those of exalted resolve flare up, even the வேந்தன் breaks midway and loses his kingship.","n":"ஏந்திய கொள்கை and வேந்தன் carry all the weight in this verse, and the disagreement could hardly be wider, from gods and vows to politics.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["perumai","vendhan"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The வேந்தன் is Indra, himself toppled by ascetics of great vows, with Nahusha's fall from Indra's seat through Agastya's anger in mind.","அவராற்றலான் இந்திரனும் இடைய தன் பதம் இழந்து கெடும்."],["manakkudavar","It is also Indra, who loses his realm, and this is a loss of wealth.","உயர்ந்த கோட்பாட்டையுடையார் வெகுள்வராயின், இந்திரனும் இடையிலே இற்றுத் தன்னரசு இழக்கும்"],["kalaignar","Those with firm principles rise up and a repressive regime collapses, with no god or vow in it.","அடக்குமுறை ஆட்சி நிலை குலைந்து அழிந்துவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:90"},"k:900":{"book":"kural","num":900,"t":["இறந்தமைந்த சார்புடையர் ஆயினும் உய்யார்","சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் செறின்"],"p":"Though in possession of numerous auxiliaries, they will perish who are-exposed to the wrath of the noble whose penance is boundless.","r":"Even those with overwhelming support will not survive if those of supreme distinction are angered.","n":"சார்பு gets a precise list from Parimelazhagar: fort, army, wealth, friends, which are the chapters just passed through. His example is the burning of the three cities. The verse's own சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் doesn't specify asceticism or any cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:90"},"k:901":{"book":"kural","num":901,"t":["மனைவிழைவார் மாண்பயன் எய்தார் வினைவிழையார்","வேண்டாப் பொருளும் அது"],"p":"Those who lust after their wives will not attain the excellence of virtue; and it is just this that is not desired by those who are bent on acquiring wealth.","r":"Men who fix their desire on the wife do not reach the fine fruit of life, and this same desire is what men intent on action refuse to want.","n":"The verse says only மனை, 'home', and மாண்பயன், 'fine fruit', without naming either. Parimelazhagar supplies virtue as the fruit and wealth-making as the action, so the claim that the household's own goal is lost is partly his construction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["penvazhi","kaamam","aram","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:902":{"book":"kural","num":902,"t":["பேணாது பெண்விழைவான் ஆக்கம் பெரியதோர்","நாணாக நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"The wealth of him who, regardless (of his manliness), devotes himself to his wife's feminine nature will cause great shame (to ali men) and to himself;","r":"The prosperity of one who desires his woman without caring for something else brings him shame, and a great shame in the world besides.","n":"பேணாது has no object in the verse, so what is being neglected is exactly what the commentators must decide.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","naanam","selvam","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What he abandons is his manliness, and his wealth shames all men because she controls its use.","தன் ஆண்மையை விட்டு மனையாளது பெண்மையை விழைவான்"],["manakkudavar","What he fails to tend is virtue and wealth, with no appeal to manliness.","அறத்தினையும் பொருளினையும் பேணாது மனையாளைக் காதலிப் பானது செல்வம்"],["kalaignar","What he neglects is the principle he adopted, as he trails after a woman.","ஏற்றுக்கொண்ட கொள்கையினைப் பேணிக் காத்திடாமல்"]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:903":{"book":"kural","num":903,"t":["இல்லாள்கண் தாழ்ந்த இயல்பின்மை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","நல்லாருள் நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"The frailty that stoops to a wife will always make (her husband) feel ashamed among the good.","r":"The unnatural condition of stooping before one's wife brings a man shame among good people at all times.","n":"The shame is social and comparative. It arises among நல்லார், not in the house.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","naanam","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The fault is a husband's fear of any wife: fear is natural to her, so in him it is unnatural and leaves her uncontrolled.","அவள் தான் அஞ்சி ஒழுகுதல் இயல்பாகலின், அவளை அஞ்சுதல் இயல்பின்மையாயிற்று."],["kalaignar","The fault is yielding to a wife of bad character instead of trying to correct her.","நற்குணமில்லாத மனைவியைத் திருத்த முனையாமல் பணிந்து போகிற கணவன்"]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:904":{"book":"kural","num":904,"t":["மனையாளை அஞ்சும் மறுமையி லாளன்","வினையாண்மை வீறெய்த லின்று"],"p":"The undertaking of one, who fears his wife and is therefore destitute of (bliss), will never be applauded.","r":"A man who fears his wife and has no share in the other life gets no distinction from his capacity for action.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","vinai","aanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறுமை is the next world, whose fruit he forfeits because he cannot perform household virtue.","தன் மனையாளை அஞ்சி ஒழுகுகின்ற மறுமைப்பயன் இல்லாதானுக்கு"],["kalaignar","The man fears running a family and misses the fruit of the new life that marriage opens; no next world is involved.","மணம் புரிந்து புதுவாழ்வின் பயனை அடையாமல் குடும்பம் நடத்த அஞ்சுகின்றவனின்"]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:905":{"book":"kural","num":905,"t":["இல்லாளை அஞ்சுவான் அஞ்சுமற் றெஞ்ஞான்றும்","நல்லார்க்கு நல்ல செயல்"],"p":"He that fears his wife will always be afraid of doing good deeds (even) to the good.","r":"One who fears his wife will always be afraid to do good to good people.","n":"The harm named is the loss of generosity to outsiders, so fear in the house is treated as a public failure of giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He fears giving to guests, ascetics and elders, even out of wealth he earned himself.","தான் தேடிய பொருளே யாயினும் அதனால் நல்லார்க்கு நல்லன செய்தலை"],["kalaignar","He reverses the sense: a man anxious to do good without error heeds the wife who watches for error.","தவறு நேராமல் கண்காணிக்கும் மனைவிக்கு அஞ்சி நடப்பான்."]],"ch":"k:91"},"k:906":{"book":"kural","num":906,"t":["இமையாரின் வாழினும் பாடிலரே இல்லாள்","அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சு பவர்"],"p":"They that fear the bamboo-like shoulders of their wives will be destitute of manliness though they may flourish like the Gods.","r":"Even if they live like the gods, those who fear the bamboo-like shoulders of their wives have no worth.","n":"The bamboo epithet is usually praise, but here it makes the fear ridiculous: Parimelazhagar takes it to show how slight the object of terror is, and contrasts it with warriors' shoulders. Kalaignar again limits the verse, to a wife lacking wisdom and character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","aanmai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:907":{"book":"kural","num":907,"t":["பெண்ணேவல் செய்தொழுகும் ஆண்மையின் நாணுடைப்","பெண்ணே பெருமை உடைத்து"],"p":"Even shame faced womanhood is more to be esteemed than the shameless manhood that performs the behests of a wife.","r":"A woman who has shame holds more greatness than the manhood of one who goes about doing a woman's bidding.","n":"The verse uses gender as a rank and then overturns it: நாண் outranks manhood. Its force still depends on the assumption that being below a woman is a disgrace, so it cuts both ways.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","aanmai","perumai","penvazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:908":{"book":"kural","num":908,"t":["நட்டார் குறைமுடியார் நன்றாற்றார் நன்னுதலாள்","பெட் டாங்கு ஒழுகு பவர்"],"p":"Those who yield to the wishes of their wives will neither relieve the wants of (their) friends nor perform virtuous deeds.","r":"Those who act as the woman of the fine brow wishes neither meet their friends' needs nor do good.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is practical: she can neither issue the needed orders on her own nor hand over the wealth, so the household's decision-making stalls.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","sutram","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:909":{"book":"kural","num":909,"t":["அறவினையும் ஆன்ற பொருளும் பிறவினையும்","பெண்ஏவல் செய்வார்கண் இல்"],"p":"From those who obey the commands of their wives are to be expected neither deeds of virtue, nor those of wealth nor (even) those of pleasure.","r":"Deeds of virtue, ample wealth and the other pursuits are absent in those who do a woman's bidding.","n":"பிறவினை is left open. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as pleasure, which gives the verse its sting: the man who surrenders for the sake of pleasure ends up losing pleasure too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["penvazhi","aram","selvam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:910":{"book":"kural","num":910,"t":["எண்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சத் திடனுடையார்க்கு எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பெண்சேர்ந்தாம் பேதைமை இல்"],"p":"The foolishness that results from devotion to a wife will never be found in those who possess a reflecting mind and a prosperity (flowing) therefrom.","r":"Those whose minds hold deliberation and who have strength of heart will never have the foolishness that comes of attachment to a woman.","n":"The positive turns the chapter into a claim about mind, not women: the fault is called பேதைமை and is the man's own. Commentators split on இடன், which Parimelazhagar takes as wealth in kings and Manakkudavar as breadth of mind, while Kalaignar hears firmness of heart.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["penvazhi","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:91"},"k:911":{"book":"kural","num":911,"t":["அன்பின் விழையார் பொருள்விழையும் ஆய்தொடியார்","இன்சொல் இழுக்குத் தரும்"],"p":"The sweet words of elegant braceleted (prostitutes) who desire (a man) not from affection but from avarice, will cause sorrow.","r":"The sweet words of bangled women who desire a man for his wealth and not from love will bring him harm.","n":"The harm is located in the sweet speech, not the sex. The chapter opens as a chapter on deceptive language.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:912":{"book":"kural","num":912,"t":["பயன்தூக்கிப் பண்புரைக்கும் பண்பின் மகளிர்","நயன்தூக்கி நள்ளா விடல்"],"p":"One must ascertain the character of the ill-natured women who after ascertaining the wealth (of a man) speak (as if they were) good natured-ones, and avoid intercourse (with them).","r":"Weigh the conduct of faithless women who weigh the profit and then speak of their own virtue, and keep away from them.","n":"தூக்கி is repeated: she weighs the gain, so he must weigh her in return. Parimelazhagar calls this behaviour their சாதி தருமம், which is a commentator's social classification and not in the verse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நயன் is their conduct, examined so that one can leave them.","ஒழுகலாற்றினை ஆராய்ந்தறிந்து அவரைப் பொருந்தாது விடுக."],["manakkudavar","நயன் is the pleasure they offer, which on examination is not worth approaching.","இன்பத்தை யாராய்ந்து பார்த்து, அவரைச் சாராதொழிக"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:913":{"book":"kural","num":913,"t":["பொருட்பெண்டிர் பொய்ம்மை முயக்கம் இருட்டறையில்","ஏதில் பிணந்தழீஇ அற்று"],"p":"The false embraces of wealth-loving women are like (hired men) embracing a strange corpse in a dark room.","r":"The false embrace of women of wealth is like embracing a stranger's corpse in a dark room.","n":"A quick reader takes the client to be holding a corpse, as Pope's couplet does. Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar make the woman the embracer: she is the hired bearer, and the unwanted client is the corpse she handles for pay. On that reading the disgust is hers, which complicates the chapter's usual stance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The image describes her inner state: outwardly embracing, inwardly recoiling for the sake of money, like a paid corpse-bearer.","அகத்தால் அருவராநின்றும் பொருள் நோக்கிப் புறத்தால் தழுவுவர்"],["kalaignar","She embraces someone for money and acts out false love.","பணத்துக்காக மட்டுமே ஒருவரைத் தழுவிப் பொய்யன்பு காட்டி நடிப்பது"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:914":{"book":"kural","num":914,"t":["பொருட்பொருளார் புன்னலந் தோயார் அருட்பொருள்","ஆயும் அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"The wise who seek the wealth of grace will not desire the base favours of those who regard wealth (and not pleasure) as (their) riches.","r":"Those whose understanding weighs the wealth of grace will not plunge into the base charms of women whose wealth is only wealth.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arul","selvam","varaivinmagalir","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அருட்பொருள் is wealth joined to grace: the wise still pursue wealth, but examine how to use it graciously.","அருளொடு கூடிய பொருளை ஆராய்ந்து செய்யும் அறிவினையுடையார்"],["manakkudavar","அருட்பொருள் treats grace itself as the wealth one seeks.","அருளைப் பொருளாக ஆராயும் அறிவுடையார்"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:915":{"book":"kural","num":915,"t":["பொதுநலத்தார் புன்னலம் தோயார் மதிநலத்தின்","மாண்ட அறிவி னவர்"],"p":"Those whose knowledge is made excellent by their (natural) sense will not covet the trffling delights of those whose favours are common (to all).","r":"Those whose knowledge has been refined by the goodness of their intellect will not plunge into the base charms of those whose goodness belongs to everyone.","n":"பொதுநலம், 'common goodness', is a pointed phrase, since நலம் elsewhere means personal excellence. Parimelazhagar explains மதிநலம் as clarity earned in former births; the verse names no births.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:916":{"book":"kural","num":916,"t":["தந்நலம் பாரிப்பார் தோயார் தகைசெருக்கிப்","புன்னலம் பாரிப்பார் தோள்"],"p":"Those who would spread (the fame of) their own goodness will not desire the shoulders of those,who rejoice in their accomplishments and bestow their despicable favours (on all who pay).","r":"Those who would spread their own goodness will not touch the shoulders of women who, proud of their charm, spread a base goodness.","n":"The same verb பாரிப்பார் is used for both parties: each is spreading a நலம், and the verse sets reputation against availability as two kinds of publicity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:917":{"book":"kural","num":917,"t":["நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர் தோய்வார் பிறநெஞ்சிற்","பேணிப் புணர்பவர் தோள்"],"p":"Those who are destitute of a perfectly (reformed) mind will covet the shoulders of those who embrace (them) while their hearts covet other things.","r":"Those with no firmness of heart touch the shoulders of women who join with them while tending other things in their hearts.","n":"A divided heart on one side is paired with an unguarded heart on the other. The verse's பிற is unspecified; the commentators fill it with wealth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["varaivinmagalir","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:918":{"book":"kural","num":918,"t":["ஆயும் அறிவினர் அல்லார்க்கு அணங்கென்ப","மாய மகளிர் முயக்கு"],"p":"The wise say that to such as are destitute of discerning sense the embraces of faithless women are (as ruinous as those of) the celestail female.","r":"For those lacking discerning understanding, the embrace of deceiving women is said to be an அணங்கு.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","arivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அணங்கு is a divine woman who takes life through desire, so the embrace is sweet and then fatal.","காமநெறியான் உயிர் கொள்ளும் தெய்வமகள்."],["manakkudavar","அணங்கு simply means affliction, with no divine being.","வருத்தமாமென்று சொல்லுவர்"]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:919":{"book":"kural","num":919,"t":["வரைவிலா மாணிழையார் மென்தோள் புரையிலாப்","பூரியர்கள் ஆழும் அளறு"],"p":"The delicate shoulders of prostitutes with excellent jewels are a hell into which are plunged the ignorant base.","r":"The soft shoulders of women of fine jewels who draw no line are the mire in which base men with no worth sink.","n":"வரைவு, 'limit', gives the chapter its title. Parimelazhagar says it means no line between high and low clients, and insists பூரியர் here means low in understanding, not in caste. அளறு means hell and mire at once, and softness of shoulder fits sinking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:92"},"k:920":{"book":"kural","num":920,"t":["இருமனப் பெண்டிரும் கள்ளும் கவறும்","திருநீக்கப் பட்டார் தொடர்பு"],"p":"Treacherous women, liquor, and gambling are the associates of such as have forsaken by Fortune.","r":"Double-minded women, liquor and dice are the company of those whom Fortune has cast off.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","kal","soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These companions are signs by which one can tell that the goddess has already abandoned a man.","திரு நீக்கிப் பட்டமை இக்குறிகளான் அறியப்படும்"],["kalaignar","Consorting with them is what causes life's distinction to depart.","தொடர்பு கொண்டு உழல்வோரை விட்டு வாழ்வில் அமைய வேண்டிய சிறப்பு அகன்றுவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:92"},"k:921":{"book":"kural","num":921,"t":["உட்கப் படாஅர் ஒளியிழப்பர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கட்காதல் கொண்டொழுகு வார்"],"p":"Those who always thirst after drink will neither inspire fear (in others) nor retain the light (of their fame).","r":"Those who go on loving liquor are never held in awe, and they lose their lustre.","n":"Parimelazhagar reads this as a king's loss of enemies' fear, while Manakkudavar has ordinary esteem from others, so the same verse can be statecraft or general ethics.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:922":{"book":"kural","num":922,"t":["உண்ணற்க கள்ளை உணில்உண்க சான்றோரான்","எண்ணப் படவேண்டா தார்"],"p":"Let no liquor be drunk; if it is desired, let it be drunk by those who care not for esteem of the great.","r":"Do not drink; if anyone does, let it be those who don't wish to be counted by the worthy.","n":"The permission is a trap. It sounds like a concession but actually states the price.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kal","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:923":{"book":"kural","num":923,"t":["ஈன்றாள் முகத்தேயும் இன்னாதால் என்மற்றுச்","சான்றோர் முகத்துக் களி"],"p":"Intoxication is painful even in the presence of (one's) mother; what will it not then be in that of the wise ?","r":"Drunken joy is painful even in the sight of the woman who bore you; what then in the sight of the worthy?","n":"The argument runs from the most forgiving audience to the least, so the mother works as a limit case. களி, the word for drunken joy, is otherwise a word of delight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:924":{"book":"kural","num":924,"t":["நாண்என்னும் நல்லாள் புறங்கொடுக்கும் கள்ளென்னும்","பேணாப் பெருங்குற்றத் தார்க்கு"],"p":"The fair maid of modesty will turn her back on those who are guilty of the great and abominable crime of drunkenness.","r":"Shame, the good woman, turns her back on those with the great and repugnant fault called liquor.","n":"புறங்கொடுக்கும் may carry a battlefield sense of turning one's back in flight; that is my suggestion, not a commentator's. Parimelazhagar explains the feminine personification as following a Sanskrit convention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","kal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:925":{"book":"kural","num":925,"t":["கையறி யாமை உடைத்தே பொருள்கொடுத்து","மெய்யறி யாமை கொளல்"],"p":"To give money and purchase unconsciousness is the result of one's ignorance of (one's own actions).","r":"To give up wealth and take self-oblivion in return must come from கையறியாமை.","n":"The irony is commercial: one pays good money to buy ignorance.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu","selvam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கையறியாமை is helpless not-knowing what to do, itself the fruit of former deeds.","அவன் பழவினைப் பயனாய செய்வதறியாமையைத் தனக்குக் காரணமாக உடைத்து."],["manakkudavar","கையறியாமை is simply not seeing what benefit results.","பயன் அறியாமை யுடைத்து"],["kalaignar","It is indescribable stupidity, with no deed or fruit involved.","விவரிக்கவே முடியாத மூடத்தனமாகும்."]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:926":{"book":"kural","num":926,"t":["துஞ்சினார் செத்தாரின் வேறல்லர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","நஞ்சுண்பார் கள்ளுண் பவர்"],"p":"They that sleep resemble the deed; (likewise) they that drink are no other than poison-eaters.","r":"Those asleep are no different from the dead, and those who drink are always eating poison.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is an analogy. Sleepers differ from the dead yet lack awareness just as they do; drinkers differ from poison-eaters yet lack awareness permanently. He rejects a rival crosswise pairing.","கள்ளுண்பார் நஞ்சுண்பாரின் வேறாதல் உடையரேனும், எக்காலத்தும் அறிவின்மையான் வேறு எனப்பட்டார்"],["kalaignar","Drinkers and poison-eaters are the same, and therefore the drinker's sleep is the same as death.","மது அருந்துவோர்க்கும் நஞ்சு அருந்துவோர்க்கும் வேறுபாடு கிடையாது"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:927":{"book":"kural","num":927,"t":["உள்ளொற்றி உள்ளூர் நகப்படுவர் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","கள்ளொற்றிக் கண்சாய் பவர்"],"p":"Those who always intoxicate themselves by a private (indulgence in) drink; will have their secrets detected and laughed at by their fellow-townsmen.","r":"Those who always seek out liquor and sink with drooping eyes are seen through and laughed at by their own town.","n":"ஒற்றி occurs twice: he resorts to drink in hiding, and the town spies out what is within. Parimelazhagar says it is secret drinking and Manakkudavar seeking out where liquor is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:928":{"book":"kural","num":928,"t":["களித்தறியேன் என்பது கைவிடுக நெஞ்சத்து","ஒளித்ததூஉம் ஆங்கே மிகும்"],"p":"Let (the drunkard) give up saying \"I have never drunk\"; (for) the moment (he drinks) he will simply betray his former attempt to conceal.","r":"Give up saying you have never been drunk, because what is hidden in the heart grows on that very spot.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The speaker is a secret drinker claiming sobriety when not drinking; as soon as he drinks, the hidden fault comes out larger.","மறைந்துண்டு வைத்து யான் கள்ளுண்டறியேன் என்று உண்ணாத பொழுது தம் ஒழுக்கங் கூறுதலையொழிக"],["manakkudavar","The speaker claims drink does not intoxicate him; drink itself makes the tongue slip and reveal the hidden things of the mind.","கள்ளுண்டால் களித்தறியே னென்பதனைக் கைவிடுக"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:929":{"book":"kural","num":929,"t":["களித்தானைக் காரணம் காட்டுதல் கீழ்நீர்க்","குளித்தானைத் தீத்துரீஇ அற்று"],"p":"Reasoning with a drunkard is like going under water with a torch in search of a drowned man.","r":"To offer reasons to the drunk is like reaching with fire for one who has plunged beneath the water.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","துரீஇ is searching with a lamp: as light doesn't enter water, so reason doesn't enter his mind.","நீருள் மூழ்கினான் ஒருவனைப் பிறனொருவன் விளக்கினல் நாடுதலை யொக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","துரீஇ is burning him with fire: a submerged man can't be touched by it.","நீரின் கீழே முழுகினானைத் தீயினாற் சுட்டது போலும்"]],"ch":"k:93"},"k:930":{"book":"kural","num":930,"t":["கள்ளுண்ணாப் போழ்திற் களித்தானைக் காணுங்கால்","உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு"],"p":"When (a drunkard) who is sober sees one who is not, it looks as if he remembered not the evil effects of his (own) drink.","r":"When sober, and seeing another man drunk, doesn't he reflect on the lapse of his own drinking?","n":"After 929's closed door the chapter leaves one open: self-recognition. Parimelazhagar names the logic as inference: what he sees in the other he could infer about himself. The question form suggests the chance is seldom taken.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kal","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:93"},"k:931":{"book":"kural","num":931,"t":["வேண்டற்க வென்றிடினும் சூதினை வென்றதூஉம்","தூண்டிற்பொன் மீன்விழுங்கி அற்று"],"p":"Though able to win, let not one desire gambling; (for) even what is won is like a fish swallowing the iron in fish-hook.","r":"Don't want gambling even if you can win, for what you win is like the metal of the hook swallowed by a fish.","n":"The verse attacks gambling at its strongest case, a win. Parimelazhagar adds that the winnings are a shackle keeping the gambler from leaving. பொன் here means the hook's metal, not gold, though the sound of 'gold' sharpens the image.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:932":{"book":"kural","num":932,"t":["ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் சூதர்க்கும் உண்டாங்கொல்","நன்றெய்தி வாழ்வதோர் ஆறு"],"p":"Is there indeed a means of livelihood that can bestow happiness on gamblers who gain one and lose a hundred ?","r":"Is there any way of living well for gamblers, who get one and lose a hundred?","n":"ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் is sequence as well as ratio. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar hear the hope that follows the first gain, so the loss is driven by the win.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:933":{"book":"kural","num":933,"t":["உருளாயம் ஓவாது கூறின் பொருளாயம்","போஒய்ப் புறமே படும்"],"p":"If the king is incessantly addicted to the rolling dice in the hope of gain, his wealth and the resources thereof will take their departure and fall into other's hands.","r":"If one keeps calling the falling of the dice, the gains of wealth will leave and pass elsewhere.","n":"The chiming of உருளாயம் and பொருளாயம் sets one income against another: the income of the dice swallows the income of wealth. Parimelazhagar makes the subject a king and the destination his enemies, which the verse doesn't state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:934":{"book":"kural","num":934,"t":["சிறுமை பலசெய்து சீரழிக்கும் சூதின்","வறுமை தருவதொன்று இல்"],"p":"There is nothing else that brings (us) poverty like gambling which causes many a misery and destroys (one's) reputation.","r":"There is nothing that gives poverty like gambling, which does many small harms and destroys distinction.","n":"சிறுமை elsewhere is smallness of character. The commentators read it here as miseries, but both senses are available, since gambling diminishes a person as well as afflicting him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","sirumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:935":{"book":"kural","num":935,"t":["கவறும் கழகமும் கையும் தருக்கி","இவறியார் இல்லாகி யார்"],"p":"Penniless are those who by reason of their attachment would never forsake gambling, the gambling-place and the handling (of dice).","r":"Those who, in pride, clung to the dice, the gaming hall and the skilled hand have come to nothing.","n":"The past tense makes this an appeal to history. Parimelazhagar names the example: the Pandavas losing their kingdom and going to the forest. The verse names no one, so this epic reference is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:936":{"book":"kural","num":936,"t":["அகடாரார் அல்லல் உழப்பர்சூ தென்னும்","முகடியான் மூடப்பட் டார்"],"p":"Those who are swallowed by the goddess called \"gambling\" will never have their hunger satisfied, but suffer the pangs of hell in the next world.","r":"Those covered by the goddess of misfortune named gambling will not fill themselves and will suffer distress.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அகடு is the belly: they go hungry here, and suffer hell in the next world.","இம்மைக்கண் வயிறாரப் பெறார்; மறுமைக்கண் நிரயத் துன்பம் உழப்பர்."],["manakkudavar","அகடு is what lies within, the senses and the mind, which are never satisfied, and the covering hides their good qualities.","தமக்கு உள்ளாகிய இந்திரியங்களும் மனமும் இன்புற்று நிறை யப்பெறார்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:937":{"book":"kural","num":937,"t":["பழகிய செல்வமும் பண்பும் கெடுக்கும்","கழகத்துக் காலை புகின்"],"p":"To waste time at the place of gambling will destroy inherited wealth and goodness of character.","r":"If காலை enters the gaming hall, it will ruin long-held wealth and character.","n":"Whichever reading is taken, time is the stake, not money: ruin comes from what the hours would have been spent on.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","selvam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","காலை means the time set apart for virtue, wealth and pleasure, especially a king's.","அறம் பொருள் இன்பங்கட்கு அடைத்த காலம் அரசனுக்குச் சூதாடு களத்தின்கண் கழியுமாயின்"],["manakkudavar","காலை means the morning hour.","சூது கழகத்தின்கண்ணே காலைப்பொழுது புகுவானாயின்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:938":{"book":"kural","num":938,"t":["பொருள் கெடுத்துப் பொய்மேற் கொளீஇ அருள்கெடுத்து","அல்லல் உழப்பிக்கும் சூது"],"p":"Gambling destroys property, teaches falsehood, puts an end to benevolence, and brings in misery (here and hereafter).","r":"Gambling destroys wealth, makes one take up lying, destroys compassion and drives one through misery.","n":"The list is a causal ladder, and the moral rungs come after the financial one. Parimelazhagar assigns the means: loss robs, the winner's position breeds lies, and the rancour of the game kills compassion. In his reading, then, wins and losses alike do damage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","arul","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:939":{"book":"kural","num":939,"t":["உடைசெல்வம் ஊண்ஒளி கல்விஎன்று ஐந்தும்","அடையாவாம் ஆயங் கொளின்"],"p":"The habit of gambling prevents the attainment of these five: clothing, wealth, food, fame and learning.","r":"Clothing, wealth, food, lustre and learning, these five don't come near one who takes to gaming.","n":"அடையாவாம் says that they won't arrive, while Pope's 'depart' adds that they were held already. The list runs from bare needs to learning, and puts survival and culture under one head.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["soodhu","selvam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:94"},"k:940":{"book":"kural","num":940,"t":["இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல் துன்பம்","உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்"],"p":"As the gambler loves (his vice) the more he loses by it, so does the soul love (the body) the more it suffers through it.","r":"As a gambler loves the game more with every loss, so the living being's love grows with every suffering it goes through.","n":"An unexpected turn to the human condition: the chapter's closing verse uses gambling to diagnose attachment to life itself. Parimelazhagar insists the real target is the gambler, with the soul there to drive the point home, but the verse's grammar makes the soul the thing explained.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["soodhu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The soul's love is for the body, through which it suffers; as the one quitting gambling is admired, so is the soul that spurns the body.","சூதினை வெறுத்து ஒழிவானை யொக்கும் உடம்பினை வெறுத்தொழியும் உயிர்"],["kalaignar","The love is for life itself, which grows as the body suffers; no renunciation is implied.","உடலுக்குத் துன்பம் தொடர்ந்து வரவர உயிர்மீது கொள்ளுகிற ஆசையும்"]],"ch":"k:94"},"k:941":{"book":"kural","num":941,"t":["மிகினும் குறையினும் நோய்செய்யும் நூலோர்","வளிமுதலா எண்ணிய மூன்று"],"p":"If (food and work are either) excessive or deficient, the three things enumerated by (medical) writers, flatulence, biliousness, and phlegm, will cause (one) disease.","r":"The three counted by the learned, starting with wind, cause disease whether they exceed or fall short.","n":"Deficiency is as dangerous as excess. The verse defers to the நூலோர் and doesn't claim the theory as its own.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","alavu","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","What exceeds or falls short is food and activity, relative to one's constitution, and that disturbs the three.","உணவும் செயல்களும் ஒருவன் பகுதிக்கு ஒத்த அளவின் அன்றி"],["manakkudavar","What exceeds or falls short is food, sleep and sexual union.","உணவும் உறக்கமும் இணைவிழைச்சும் தன்னுடம்பின் அளவிற்கு மிகினும் குறையினும்"],["kalaignar","What exceeds or falls short is the three humours themselves.","மூன்றில் ஒன்று அளவுக்கு அதிகமானாலும் குறைந்தாலும் நோய் உண்டாகும்."]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:942":{"book":"kural","num":942,"t":["மருந்தென வேண்டாவாம் யாக்கைக்கு அருந்தியது","அற்றது போற்றி உணின்"],"p":"No medicine is necessary for him who eats after assuring (himself) that what he has (already) eaten has been digested.","r":"No medicine is called for by the body if one eats only after attending to the digestion of what was eaten.","n":"போற்றி, 'with care', turns digestion into something to watch. Parimelazhagar lists bodily signs of it, such as lightness and clean belching. His remark on உணின் concedes that this is hard to do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","marundhu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:943":{"book":"kural","num":943,"t":["அற்றால் அறவறிந்து உண்க அஃதுடம்பு","பெற்றான் நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு"],"p":"If (one's food has been) digested let one eat with moderation; (for) that is the way to prolong the life of an embodied soul.","r":"When digestion is complete, eat knowing the measure: that is how one who has gained a body can carry it far.","n":"உடம்பு பெற்றான், 'one who has received a body', treats the body as a gift. Parimelazhagar pushes further: a human body is the only vehicle for this world, the next and liberation, so length of life serves those ends. The verse itself states no such purpose.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:944":{"book":"kural","num":944,"t":["அற்றது அறிந்து கடைப்பிடித்து மாறல்ல","துய்க்க துவரப் பசித்து"],"p":"(First) assure yourself that your food has been digested and never fail to eat, when very hungry, whatever is not disagreeable (to you).","r":"Knowing the food is digested, hold to what is not incompatible and eat it after real hunger.","n":"Parimelazhagar sets out three kinds of incompatibility: with one's constitution, with the season and hour, and between foods. His example is honey and ghee in equal amounts turning to poison, which Manakkudavar shares.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:945":{"book":"kural","num":945,"t":["மாறுபாடு இல்லாத உண்டி மறுத்துண்ணின்","ஊறுபாடு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு"],"p":"There will be no disaster to one's life if on e eats with moderation, food that is not disagreeable.","r":"If one eats with மறுத்து food that has no incompatibility, life takes no injury.","n":"I'm unsure of Manakkudavar's sense. His gloss, with its example of ginger after jackfruit, doesn't obviously fit the verse, and I record it without claiming to understand it fully.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["undi","alavu","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறுத்து means refusing the amount the heart wants and stopping at what brings no disease.","தன் உள்ளம் வேண்டிய அளவினான் அன்றிப் பிணிவாரா அளவினால்"],["manakkudavar","He takes it as eating by setting aside the food, with incompatibility illustrated by a compensating food.","சுவையும் வீரியமும் மாறுபாடில்லாத உணவை நீக்கி யுண்ப னாயின்"]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:946":{"book":"kural","num":946,"t":["இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்கண் இன்பம்போல் நிற்கும்","கழிபேர் இரையான்கண் நோய்"],"p":"As pleasure dwells with him who eats moderately, so disease (dwells) with the glutton who eats voraciously.","r":"As joy stays with the one who eats knowing how to stop short, disease stays with the one who eats enormously.","n":"இரையான், 'feeder', is a word for animals' feed. Parimelazhagar notes that this likens the glutton to a beast. The contrast is inbam and noy, not health and disease: moderation yields pleasure, so restraint isn't presented as a sacrifice.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","noy","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:947":{"book":"kural","num":947,"t":["தீயள வன்றித் தெரியான் பெரிதுண்ணின்","நோயள வின்றிப் படும்"],"p":"He will be afflicted with numberless diseases, who eats immoderately, ignorant (of the rules of health).","r":"If one eats hugely, not discerning the measure of the fire within, disease will arise beyond all measure.","n":"அளவு occurs on both sides: disregard the measure of the fire, and disease loses its measure. Manakkudavar and Kalaignar read தீ simply as hunger, while Parimelazhagar reads it as the belly's fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["undi","noy","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:948":{"book":"kural","num":948,"t":["நோய்நாடி நோய்முதல் நாடி அதுதணிக்கும்","வாய்நாடி வாய்ப்பச் செயல்"],"p":"Let the physician enquire into the (nature of the) disease, its cause and its method of cure and treat it faithfully according to (medical rule).","r":"Investigate the disease, then its root, then the means that subdue it, and act so as to get it right.","n":"நாடி recurs three times and sets the order: identify, trace, select, then act. Kalaignar adds that this applies to the diseases of society, which is his extension. Parimelazhagar lists surgery and cautery among the means.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","marundhu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:949":{"book":"kural","num":949,"t":["உற்றான் அளவும் பிணியளவும் காலமும்","கற்றான் கருதிச் செயல்"],"p":"The learned (physician) should ascertain the condition of his patient; the nature of his disease, and the season (of the year) and (then) proceed (with his treatment).","r":"The learned one should act having considered the measure of the patient, the measure of the illness and the time.","n":"Learning alone isn't enough: the verse makes the patient's measure come first, so that treatment is individual. Parimelazhagar explicitly wants both the book's way and the physician's own discernment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["noy","alavu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:95"},"k:950":{"book":"kural","num":950,"t":["உற்றவன் தீர்ப்பான் மருந்துழைச் செல்வானென்று","அப்பால் நாற் கூற்றே மருந்து"],"p":"Medical science consists of four parts, viz., patient, physician, medicine and compounder; and each of these (again) contains four sub-divisions.","r":"The patient, the healer, the medicine and the one who attends: medicine has these four parts.","n":"The verse calls the whole arrangement மருந்து, so the patient is himself part of the medicine, not its recipient.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marundhu","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உழைச்செல்வான் is the compounder, and each of the four has four sub-qualities of its own.","நான்கு பகுதியையுடைய நான்கு திறத்தது"],["kalaignar","உழைச்செல்வான் is one who stands by to help, and treatment simply has four parts.","அருகிருந்து துணைபுரிபவர் என மருத்துவமுறை நான்கு வகையாக அமைந்துள்ளது."]],"ch":"k:95"},"k:951":{"book":"kural","num":951,"t":["இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை இயல்பாகச்","செப்பமும் நாணும் ஒருங்கு"],"p":"Consistency (of thought, word and deed) and fear (of sin) are conjointly natural only to the high-born.","r":"Only in those born to a good house do integrity and shame exist together, naturally.","n":"இயல்பாக and ஒருங்கு both limit the claim: it is about these qualities coming naturally and together, which leaves open whether others can acquire them.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","naanam","pirappu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Birth decides: others can be taught these qualities but won't keep them for long.","பிறர்க்காயின் கற்பித்த வழியும் நெடிது நில்லா"],["kalaignar","Conduct defines birth: only those with these qualities can be regarded as high-born.","மற்றவர்களை உயர்ந்த குடியில் பிறந்தவர்களாகக் கருத முடியாது."]],"ch":"k:96"},"k:952":{"book":"kural","num":952,"t":["ஒழுக்கமும் வாய்மையும் நாணும் இம் மூன்றும்","இழுக்கார் குடிப்பிறந் தார்"],"p":"The high-born will never deviate from these three; good manners, truthfulness and modesty.","r":"Those of good birth don't slip in these three: conduct, truthfulness and shame.","n":"Parimelazhagar maps the three onto body, speech and mind, and adds that this comes without learning. Manakkudavar takes நாண் physically, as covering one's nakedness, while Kalaignar has honour instead and again makes these the test of who counts as high-born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","vaaimai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:953":{"book":"kural","num":953,"t":["நகைஈகை இன்சொல் இகழாமை நான்கும்","வகையென்ப வாய்மைக் குடிக்கு"],"p":"A cheerful countenance, liberality, pleasant words, and an unreviling disposition, these four are said to be the proper qualities of the truly high-born.","r":"A smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning: these four, they say, belong to a truly good lineage.","n":"None of the four is about ancestry, and all are ways of treating someone else. Parimelazhagar specifies the setting: when the destitute come, for everyone scorns those who have nothing. That turns etiquette into a test.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","eegai","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:954":{"book":"kural","num":954,"t":["அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","குன்றுவ செய்தல் இலர்"],"p":"Though blessed with immense wealth, the noble will never do anything unbecoming.","r":"Even if they gain crores upon crores, the well-born do nothing that diminishes them.","n":"பெறினும் can mean holding wealth or being offered it. Parimelazhagar and Pope lean toward the first, and Kalaignar toward a bribe, which makes this a claim about being unbuyable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","selvam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:955":{"book":"kural","num":955,"t":["வழங்குவ துள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும் பழங்குடி","பண்பில் தலைப்பிரிதல் இன்று"],"p":"Though their means fall off, those born in ancient families, will not lose their character (for liberality).","r":"Even when what they can give has shrunk, an old family doesn't part from its nature.","n":"The verse measures their poverty by what they can give, not by what they have. Parimelazhagar explains பழங்குடி with the Chera, Chola and Pandya, excellent since creation, which ties this to royal houses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","eegai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:956":{"book":"kural","num":956,"t":["சலம்பற்றிச் சால்பில செய்யார்மா சற்ற","குலம்பற்றி வாழ்தும் என் பார்"],"p":"Those who seek to preserve the irreproachable honour of their families will not viciously do what is detrimental thereto.","r":"Those who say 'we will live holding to our spotless lineage' will not do unworthy things through deceit.","n":"என்பார் makes lineage a resolve: it applies to those who declare they will live by it. That moves lineage from something one has to something one takes on. Parimelazhagar adds 'even in poverty'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:957":{"book":"kural","num":957,"t":["குடிப்பிறந்தார் கண்விளங்கும் குற்றம் விசும்பின்","மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து"],"p":"The defects of the noble will be observed as clearly as the dark spots in the moon.","r":"A fault in the well-born stands out high, like the spot on the moon in the sky.","n":"A cost of height: the fault is not larger, but more visible. Each part maps: lineage to sky, virtues to moon, fault to spot. Manakkudavar draws the practical moral.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:958":{"book":"kural","num":958,"t":["நலத்தின்கண் நாரின்மை தோன்றின் அவனைக்","குலத்தின்கண் ஐயப் படும்"],"p":"If one of a good family betrays want of affection, his descent from it will be called in question.","r":"If want of affection shows in one of a good house, people will have doubts about his lineage.","n":"The chapter's claim protects itself here: when a well-born man is cruel, the doubt falls on his birth and not on the rule that birth gives virtue. Reading conduct as evidence of birth also makes the doubt a slur on his parentage, which is not stated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:959":{"book":"kural","num":959,"t":["நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால்காட்டும் காட்டும்","குலத்தில் பிறந்தார்வாய்ச் சொல்"],"p":"As the sprout indicates the nature of the soil, (so) the speech of the noble indicates (that of one's birth).","r":"A sprout shows what lies in the ground; the words from the mouth show the lineage of the one born in it.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the disguise: it looks as if the verse is teaching how to identify lineage, but it is really a command to speak sweetly. Note கால், a sprout: the evidence appears at the earliest moment. Kalaignar's version has the mature crop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:96"},"k:960":{"book":"kural","num":960,"t":["நலம்வேண்டின் நாணுடைமை வேண்டும் குலம் வேண்டின்","வேண்டுக யார்க்கும் பணிவு"],"p":"He who desires a good name must desire modesty; and he who desires (the continuance of) a family greatness must be submissive to all.","r":"If you want goodness, want shame; if you want lineage, want humility toward all.","n":"வேண்டின் treats lineage as something one can want and go after. This is the verse most at odds with 951, since a thing given by birth need not be wanted.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","panivu","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","யார்க்கும் means everyone who is due deference, such as priests, the worthy, ascetics and parents, shown by rising and going to meet them.","பணியப்படுவார் யாவர் மாட்டும் பணிதலை வேண்டுக"],["manakkudavar","யார்க்கும் means all people without limit.","யாவர்மாட்டும் தாழ்ந் தொழுகுதலை விரும்புக"]],"ch":"k:96"},"k:961":{"book":"kural","num":961,"t":["இன்றி அமையாச் சிறப்பின ஆயினும்","குன்ற வருப விடல்"],"p":"Actions that would degrade (one's) family should not be done; though they may be so important that not doing them would end in death.","r":"Even if they are so excellent as to be indispensable, give up things that would diminish you.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this a deliberate polemic: he says it rejects a Sanskrit rule that one may do degrading things to survive. The verse names no rival.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The acts are ones whose omission would mean death, and they must still be refused.","'இறப்பவரும் வழி இளிவந்தன செய்தாயினும் உய்க' என்னும் வடநூல் முறையை மறுத்து"],["manakkudavar","What is given up is wealth and pleasure that would reduce one's nature, however great the amount.","இது பொருளும் இன்பமும் மிகினும், தன்மை குறைவன் செய்யற்க"],["kalaignar","They are obligatory actions, to be avoided if they lessen one's dignity; death is not raised.","கட்டாயமாகச் செய்து தீர வேண்டிய செயல்கள் என்றாலும்கூட"]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:962":{"book":"kural","num":962,"t":["சீரினும் சீரல்ல செய்யாரே சீரொடு","பேராண்மை வேண்டு பவர்"],"p":"Those who desire (to maintain their) honour, will surely do nothing dishonourable, even for the sake of fame.","r":"Those who want renown and great manliness together won't do unworthy things even for renown.","n":"சீர் is used against itself: renown bought with unworthy acts is not renown. பேராண்மை is explained by Parimelazhagar as a firmness that doesn't falter anywhere, and he takes it here as honour, not prowess.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","pugazh","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:963":{"book":"kural","num":963,"t":["பெருக்கத்து வேண்டும் பணிதல் சிறிய","சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு"],"p":"In great prosperity humility is becoming; dignity, in great adversity.","r":"In abundance you must bow, and in slight and shrunken circumstances you must stand tall.","n":"The posture runs against the circumstances: humility when the world would permit pride, and height when the world would press one down. Humility and honour, often seen as rivals, are the same discipline at different times.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","panivu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:964":{"book":"kural","num":964,"t":["தலையின் இழிந்த மயிரனையர் மாந்தர்","நிலையின் இழிந்தக் கடை"],"p":"They who have fallen from their (high) position are like the hair which has fallen from the head.","r":"People who have fallen from their position are like hair that has fallen from the head.","n":"The verse says மாந்தர், people generally, and Parimelazhagar narrows it to the well-born. Nothing about the hair changes when it falls, only its place, so worth is presented as depending on where it stands.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:965":{"book":"kural","num":965,"t":["குன்றின் அனையாரும் குன்றுவர் குன்றுவ","குன்றி அனைய செயின்"],"p":"Even those who are exalted like a hill will be thought low, if they commit deeds that are debasing.","r":"Even those like a hill will sink, if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed.","n":"குன்று works four ways: hill, sinking, lowering, and the tiny red seed குன்றி. The sound itself demonstrates how little separates the great from the small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:966":{"book":"kural","num":966,"t":["புகழ்இன்றால் புத்தேள்நாட்டு உய்யாதால் என்மற்று","இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று நிலை"],"p":"Of what good is it (for the high-born) to go and stand in vain before those who revile him ? it only brings him loss of honour and exclusion from heaven.","r":"It brings no praise and doesn't carry one to the gods' land: why then go and stand behind those who scorn you?","n":"The argument is from utility, not pride: it weighs both possible rewards and finds neither.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maanam","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Heaven is a real reward that this conduct fails to gain.","ஏனைப் புத்தேளுலகத்துச் செலுத்தாது"],["kalaignar","Heaven is non-existent, and the line asks rhetorically whether it could be obtained this way.","இல்லாத சொர்க்கமா கிடைக்கும்?"]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:967":{"book":"kural","num":967,"t":["ஒட் டார்பின் சென்றொருவன் வாழ்தலின் அந்நிலையே","கெட்டான் எனப்படுதல் நன்று"],"p":"It is better for a man to be said of him that he died in his usual state than that he eked out his life by following those who disgraced him.","r":"Rather than live by following those who don't accept you, it is better to be called ruined in that very condition.","n":"கெட்டான் means ruined and can mean dead. Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar take death, while Manakkudavar stays with the word. Parimelazhagar says this replies to someone who would still go for wealth and survival. The verse's சொல் is about what is said of a person, so reputation is the stake.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:968":{"book":"kural","num":968,"t":["மருந்தோமற்று ஊன்ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை பெருந்தகைமை","பீடழிய வந்த இடத்து"],"p":"For the high-born to keep their body in life when their honour is gone will certainly not prove a remedy against death.","r":"When greatness comes to be stripped of its dignity, is a life nursing the flesh a medicine?","n":"The commentators complete the question: medicine against dying. Since death will come anyway, living on buys nothing. ஊன் reduces the body to meat, which Parimelazhagar notes is what a person is after honour goes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maanam","marundhu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:969":{"book":"kural","num":969,"t":["மயிர்நீப்பின் வாழாக் கவரிமா அன்னார்","உயிர்நீப்பர் மானம் வரின்"],"p":"Those who give up (their) life when (their) honour is at stake are like the yark which kills itself at the loss of (even one of) its hairs.","r":"Those who are like the yak that won't live once its hair is gone give up their lives when honour's moment comes.","n":"The image is presented as nature, which Parimelazhagar says shows that this is inborn in them. Kalaignar distances himself with என்பார்கள், 'people say'. The verse doesn't say whether the death is by one's own hand; Kalaignar's next gloss, on 970, does.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மானம் வரின் means the point at which honour is won by giving up life.","உயிர் நீக்கத்தான் மானம் எய்தும் எல்லை வரின்"],["manakkudavar","மானம் வரின் means when honour is about to be destroyed.","மான முடையார், மானம் அழியவரின் உயிர்விடுவர்"]],"ch":"k:97"},"k:970":{"book":"kural","num":970,"t":["இளிவரின் வாழாத மானம் உடையார்","ஒளிதொழுது ஏத்தும் உலகு"],"p":"The world will (always) praise and adore the fame of the honourable who would rather die than suffer indignity.","r":"The world worships and praises the radiance of the honourable who won't live when disgrace comes.","n":"The reward is entirely posthumous and in others' mouths, and ஒளி is the fame that outlasts the person. Parimelazhagar adds heaven by citing Purananuru, which reverses 966's point only in appearance, since there heaven was what degrading service failed to earn.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:97"},"k:971":{"book":"kural","num":971,"t":["ஒளிஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை இளிஒருவற்கு","அஃதிறந்து வாழ்தும் எனல்"],"p":"One's light is the abundance of one's courage; one's darkness is the desire to live destitute of such (a state of mind.)","r":"One's radiance is the wealth of aspiration, and one's disgrace is saying 'we will live without it'.","n":"ஒளி and இளி close the previous chapter's last verse (970) and open this one, so the two chapters are stitched together by these words. வெறுக்கை means wealth, so aspiration is presented as a holding. Disgrace lies in the stated resolve to live without it, not in any failure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["perumai","muyarchi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:972":{"book":"kural","num":972,"t":["பிறப்பொக்கும் எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் சிறப்பொவ்வா","செய்தொழில் வேற்றுமை யான்"],"p":"All human beings agree as regards their birth but differ as regards their characteristics, because of the different qualities of their actions.","r":"Birth is alike for all living beings, and distinction is unequal because of the difference in the work they do.","n":"The verse says எல்லா உயிர்க்கும், wider than humanity. செய்தொழில் could be occupation or conduct, and the whole verse's social force depends on that word.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pirappu","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Birth is alike only in being embodied through karma; the work that tests greatness itself differs by varna and individual.","தொழிற்பாகுபாடுகள் வருணந்தோறும் யாக்கைதோறும் வேறுபடுதலின்"],["manakkudavar","Greatness simply can't be recognized by lineage.","பெருமையாவது குலத்தினால் அறியப்படா தென்பதூஉம்"],["kalaignar","All are equal by birth, and difference appears only in the skill shown at one's work.","பிறப்பினால் அனைவரும் சமம்."]],"ch":"k:98"},"k:973":{"book":"kural","num":973,"t":["மேலிருந்தும் மேலல்லார் மேலல்லர் கீழிருந்தும்","கீழல்லார் கீழல் லவர்"],"p":"Though (raised) above, the base cannot become great; though (brought) low, the great cannot become base.","r":"Those who aren't high are not high even when placed high, and those who aren't low are not low even when placed low.","n":"A single word, மேல் or கீழ், names both position and worth, and the verse pries them apart. Pope's couplet takes the position as birth, but Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as the seat that wealth gives, a high couch against bare ground.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:974":{"book":"kural","num":974,"t":["ஒருமை மகளிரே போலப் பெருமையும்","தன்னைத்தான் கொண்டொழுகின் உண்டு"],"p":"Even greatness, like a woman's chastity, belongs only to him who guards himself.","r":"Greatness too, like single-minded women, exists only if one holds on to oneself.","n":"The model of masculine greatness is a woman. Conduct expected of wives becomes the standard for great men, a striking move given chapter 91. Parimelazhagar explains self-holding as restraint of mind, speech and body.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:975":{"book":"kural","num":975,"t":["பெருமை யுடையவர் ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றின்","அருமை உடைய செயல்"],"p":"(Though reduced) the great will be able to perform, in the proper way, deeds difficult (for others to do).","r":"Those with greatness will accomplish in the right way what is hard to do.","n":"ஆற்றின் adds a limit: the rare deed must be done in the right way. Parimelazhagar adds 'even when poor', which the verse doesn't say. Kalaignar reverses the direction, so that those who do such things are the ones who become great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:976":{"book":"kural","num":976,"t":["சிறியார் உணர்ச்சியுள் இல்லை பெரியாரைப்","பேணிக் கொள் வேம் என்னும் நோக்கு"],"p":"It is never in the nature of the base to seek the society of the great and partake of their nature.","r":"The aim of cherishing and taking on the great does not exist in the minds of the small.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sirumai","perumai","kalvi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They don't seek to serve the great and acquire their nature, since those proud of birth, wealth or learning think they already have it.","பெரியாரை வழிபட்டு அவர் இயல்பினையாம் கோடும்"],["manakkudavar","They don't seek to take the great as support, so having great men as allies is itself greatness.","இது பெரியாரைப் பெறுதலும் பெருமையென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:98"},"k:977":{"book":"kural","num":977,"t":["இறப்பே புரிந்த தொழிற்றாம் சிறப்புந்தான்","சீரல் லவர்கண் படின்"],"p":"Even nobility of birth, wealth and learning, if in (the possession of) the base, will (only) produce everincreasing pride.","r":"Even distinction, should it fall to the unworthy, turns entirely into acts of excess.","n":"Distinction is morally neutral and amplifies whoever has it. Manakkudavar takes சிறப்பு as wealth, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the surplus of birth, wealth and learning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:978":{"book":"kural","num":978,"t":["பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை சிறுமை","அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து"],"p":"The great will always humble himself; but the mean will exalt himself in self-admiration.","r":"Greatness always bows, and smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself.","n":"பணியும் and அணியும் rhyme, so what looks like opposite motions sound alike. Parimelazhagar reports another reading in which this is a wonder at the way of the world: the high go low and the low go high.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:979":{"book":"kural","num":979,"t":["பெருமை பெருமிதம் இன்மை சிறுமை","பெருமிதம் ஊர்ந்து விடல்"],"p":"Freedom from conceit is (the nature of true) greatness; (while) obstinacy therein is (that of) meanness.","r":"Greatness is the absence of pride, and smallness is riding on pride and being done with it.","n":"பெருமிதம், 'pride', shares its root with பெருமை, so the verse defines greatness by the absence of what sounds like its own excess. Manakkudavar marks this as where the chapter turns from negative points to defining greatness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:980":{"book":"kural","num":980,"t":["அற்றம் மறைக்கும் பெருமை சிறுமைதான்","குற்றமே கூறி விடும்"],"p":"The great hide the faults of others; the base only divulge them.","r":"Greatness hides another's weakness, and smallness only tells their faults.","n":"அற்றம் is literally a vulnerable exposure, so this hides a nakedness as much as a fault. Silence, not achievement, finishes the portrait of greatness that opened with high aim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:98"},"k:981":{"book":"kural","num":981,"t":["கடன்என்ப நல்லவை எல்லாம் கடன்அறிந்து","சான்றாண்மை மேற்கொள் பவர்க்கு"],"p":"It is said that those who are conscious of their duty and behave with a perfect goodness will regard as natural all that is good.","r":"For those who know what is fitting and take on fullness of character, all that is good is said to be கடன்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கடன் means what is natural: all good qualities are inherent, otherwise the word சான்றாண்மை wouldn't apply.","நல்லனவாய குணங்கள் எல்லாம் இயல்பாயிருக்கும்"],["kalaignar","கடன் means duty, and all efforts made to fulfil it count as good duties.","நல்ல கடமைகள் என்றே கொள்ளப்படும்."]],"ch":"k:99"},"k:982":{"book":"kural","num":982,"t":["குணநலம் சான்றோர் நலனே பிறநலம்","எந்நலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று"],"p":"The only delight of the perfect is that of their goodness; all other (sensual) delights are not to be included among any (true) delights.","r":"The goodness of the worthy is the goodness of character; any other goodness isn't counted among goodnesses at all.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes பிறநலம் as bodily good looks, and says it doesn't even appear in the lists of goods such as birth and learning. Kalaignar also has அழகு. The verse's own பிற is open, and a stronger reading dismisses everything other than character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:983":{"book":"kural","num":983,"t":["அன்புநாண் ஒப்புரவு கண்ணோட்டம் வாய்மையொடு","ஐந்துசால் ஊன்றிய தூண்"],"p":"Affection, fear (of sin), benevolence, favour and truthfulness; these are the five pillars on which perfect goodness rests.","r":"Love, shame, fellow-feeling, kindly regard and truthfulness: these five are the pillars planted to bear worth.","n":"Four of the five are about how one regards other people. Parimelazhagar stretches two of them: love extends beyond kin, and truth holds everywhere. Their absence makes worth fall, not merely diminish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","naanam","vaaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:984":{"book":"kural","num":984,"t":["கொல்லா நலத்தது நோன்மை பிறர்தீமை","சொல்லா நலத்தது சால்பு"],"p":"Penance consists in the goodness that kills not , and perfection in the goodness that tells not others' faults.","r":"Austerity has the goodness of not killing, and worth has the goodness of not telling others' evil.","n":"The verse sets speech at the level of the ascetic's non-violence. Talking of another's fault is thus the moral equivalent of what killing is for a tapas. Kalaignar makes the silence cover harm done by others, which leaves open how it fits with exposing wrongdoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:985":{"book":"kural","num":985,"t":["ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல் அதுசான்றோர்","மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை"],"p":"Stooping (to inferiors) is the strength of those who can accomplish (an undertaking); and that is the weapon with which the great avert their foes.","r":"The strength of those who accomplish is to bow; that is the weapon by which the worthy turn foes around.","n":"படை and மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் use the language of war, and the pun is that a foe (மாற்றார்) gets changed (மாற்றும்), not destroyed. Parimelazhagar makes the bowing concrete and strategic: stooping to gather allies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","saandraanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:986":{"book":"kural","num":986,"t":["சால்பிற்குக் கட்டளை யாதெனின் தோல்வி","துலையல்லார் கண்ணும் கொளல்"],"p":"The touch-stone of perfection is to receive a defeat even at the hands of one's inferiors.","r":"If asked what the touchstone of worth is, it is accepting defeat even from those who aren't one's equal.","n":"Parimelazhagar specifies that one accepts it while able to win. Without that, the verse describes mere loss. The defeat then lifts one above the other person, an inversion of the usual sense of victory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:987":{"book":"kural","num":987,"t":["இன்னாசெய் தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்","என்ன பயத்ததோ சால்பு"],"p":"Of what avail is perfect goodness if it cannot do pleasing things even to those who have pained (it) ?","r":"If you don't do what is sweet even to those who have done you harm, what has worth brought?","n":"A demanding test is framed as a question about use: worth is judged by what it produces in the hardest case.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:988":{"book":"kural","num":988,"t":["இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று சால்பென்னும்","திண்மை உண் டாகப் பெறின்"],"p":"Poverty is no disgrace to one who abounds in good qualities.","r":"Poverty is no disgrace for one who has the firmness called worth.","n":"திண்மை makes worth a hardness able to bear pressure. The verse doesn't call poverty good, only that it doesn't carry the disgrace it otherwise would.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","selvam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:989":{"book":"kural","num":989,"t":["ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம்பெயரார் சான்றாண்மைக்கு","ஆழி எனப்படு வார்"],"p":"Those who are said to be the shore of the sea of perfection will never change, though ages may change.","r":"Those called the ஆழி of worth won't shift even when the age shifts.","n":"ஊழி and ஆழி chime. The cosmic dissolution is a scale reference for constancy.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஆழி means shore: worth is the sea, and they are what holds it in when other seas overflow.","சால்புடைமையாகிய கடற்குக் கரை என்று சொல்லப்படுவார்"],["kalaignar","ஆழி means sea: they stand as the sea that doesn't change when all seas are overturned.","தம்நிலை மாறாத கடலாகத் திகழ்வார்கள்."]],"ch":"k:99"},"k:990":{"book":"kural","num":990,"t":["சான்றவர் சான்றாண்மை குன்றின் இருநிலந்தான்","தாங்காது மன்னோ பொறை"],"p":"If there is a defect in the character of the perfect, (even) the great world cannot bear (its) burden.","r":"If the worthy decline in worth, the great earth itself will not bear its load.","n":"Parimelazhagar says both events are impossible, so this is a thought experiment showing their dependence. He also reports a reading in which the worthy are the earth's helpers in bearing. Kalaignar reads பொறை as the world's forbearance: it won't patiently tolerate their decline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:99"},"k:991":{"book":"kural","num":991,"t":["எண்பதத்தால் எய்தல் எளிதென்ப யார்மாட்டும்","பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு"],"p":"If one is easy of access to all, it will be easy for one to obtain the virtue called goodness.","r":"They say the practice called courtesy is easy to reach through being accessible to everyone.","n":"வழக்கு makes courtesy a public custom, not an inner trait. Being available turns out to be the whole method, and யார்மாட்டும் gives it no exception. Parimelazhagar calls it something that wins over the whole world, which adds a strategic note.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:992":{"book":"kural","num":992,"t":["அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல் இவ்விரண்டும்","பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு"],"p":"Affectionateness and birth in a good family, these two constitute what is called a proper behaviour to all.","r":"Love and being born in a seasoned family: these two are the way called courtesy.","n":"This puts birth among courtesy's sources. Parimelazhagar softens it, saying குடிப்பிறத்தல் here means the conduct of those so born, and neither factor suffices alone. Kalaignar goes further, asking only that one have the marks of such birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:993":{"book":"kural","num":993,"t":["உறுப்பொத்தல் மக்களொப்பு அன்றால் வெறுத்தக்க","பண்பொத்தல் ஒப்பதாம் ஒப்பு"],"p":"Resemblance of bodies is no resemblance of souls; true resemblance is the resemblance of qualities that attract.","r":"Likeness of limbs isn't likeness among people; true likeness is likeness of settled character.","n":"வெறுத்தக்க usually means hateful, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as செறியத்தக்க, 'to be drawn close', which is what makes the line work. Pope's gloss, 'qualities that attract', follows him. I can't confirm this sense from the verse alone.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The issue is likeness to good people: bodily likeness doesn't amount to that, because the body doesn't last and character does.","செறியத்தகாத உடம்பால் ஒத்தல் ஒருவனுக்கு நன்மக்களோடு ஒப்பாகாமையின்"],["kalaignar","The issue is membership of humankind: those without good character shouldn't be counted as people.","நற்பண்புகளால் ஒத்திருப்பவர்களே மக்கள் எனப்படுவர்."]],"ch":"k:100"},"k:994":{"book":"kural","num":994,"t":["யனொடு நன்றி புரிந்த பயனுடையார்","பண்புபா ராட்டும் உலகு"],"p":"The world applauds the character of those whose usefulness results from their equity and charity.","r":"The world honours the character of those whose useful lives come from doing good with fairness.","n":"The world honours the பண்பு, not the deeds or their outcome, which makes deeds evidence of character. Parimelazhagar says that it is useful to oneself and to others alike.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:995":{"book":"kural","num":995,"t":["நகையுள்ளும் இன்னா திகழ்ச்சி பகையுள்ளும்","பண்புள பாடறிவார் மாட்டு"],"p":"Reproach is painful to one even in sport; those (therefore) who know the nature of others exhibit (pleasing) qualities even when they are hated.","r":"Scorn hurts even in jest, so those who know how others feel have courtesy even in enmity.","n":"Parimelazhagar insists that the two clauses are one argument, and criticizes others who read them apart: knowing that scorn hurts is the reason for courtesy. பாடு here is what another person undergoes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:996":{"book":"kural","num":996,"t":["பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம் அதுஇன்றேல்","மண்புக்கு மாய்வது மன்"],"p":"The (way of the) world subsists by contact with the good; if not, it would bury itself in the earth and perish.","r":"The world exists by resting upon those of courtesy; otherwise it would enter the earth and perish.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes உலகம் here the way of the world, not the planet. Kalaignar makes it a should: worldly practices ought to depend on such people. A claim about reality becomes a recommendation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:997":{"book":"kural","num":997,"t":["அரம்போலும் கூர்மைய ரேனும் மரம்போல்வர்","மக்கட்பண்பு இல்லா தவர்"],"p":"He who is destitute of (true) human qualities (only) resembles a tree, though he may possess the sharpness of a file.","r":"Even though sharp as a file, those without human courtesy are like trees.","n":"The chime of அரம் and மரம் has the point in it. Parimelazhagar says the file wears down what it touches without wearing itself, and the tree has only one sense. Intelligence used without courtesy isn't merely inadequate, it is destructive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:998":{"book":"kural","num":998,"t":["நண்பாற்றார் ஆகி நயமில செய்வார்க்கும்","பண்பாற்றார் ஆதல் கடை"],"p":"It is wrong (for the wise) not to exhibit (good) qualities even towards those who bearing no friendship (for them) do only what is hateful.","r":"Failing in courtesy, even toward those who act without friendship and without kindness, is lowness.","n":"Parimelazhagar's reason is reflexive: respond in kind and you become like them. The insult harms the character of the one who returns it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:999":{"book":"kural","num":999,"t":["நகல்வல்லர் அல்லார்க்கு மாயிரு ஞாலம்","பகலும்பாற் பட்டன்று இருள்"],"p":"To those who cannot rejoice, the wide world is buried darkness even in (broad) day light.","r":"For those who aren't capable of laughing, the great wide world lies in darkness even in the daytime.","n":"Laughter is treated as a skill (வல்லர்), and the lack of it as blindness. Parimelazhagar makes it the joy of mingling, and says such people don't understand the world because they can't mix with it. He reports a variant reading too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:1000":{"book":"kural","num":1000,"t":["பண்பிலான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம் நன்பால்","கலந்தீமை யால்திரிந் தற்று"],"p":"The great wealth obtained by one who has no goodness will perish like pure milk spoilt by the impurity of the vessel.","r":"Great wealth obtained by one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel.","n":"Wealth is good milk here, and the fault is the holder's alone. பெற்ற, 'obtained', suggests to Parimelazhagar that he couldn't have created it himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:100"},"k:1001":{"book":"kural","num":1001,"t":["வைத்தான்வாய் சான்ற பெரும்பொருள் அஃதுண்ணான்","செத்தான் செயக்கிடந்தது இல்"],"p":"He who does not enjoy the immense riches he has heaped up in his house, is (to be reckoned as) dead, (for) there is nothing achieved (by him).","r":"One who has stored up great wealth to the brim and doesn't eat it, is dead: there is nothing left for him to do.","n":"உண்ணான், 'doesn't eat', treats wealth as food. Parimelazhagar widens it to the five senses.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[["manakkudavar","The man who does not use it is himself dead, and has nothing further to do with it.","அதனை நுகரானாயின், செத்தான்; அவன் பின்பு செய்யக்கிடந்தது யாது மில்லை."],["parimelazhagar","An alternative he allows: once dead, one has no right over it, so enjoy it as soon as it comes.","வையாது பெற்றபொழுதே நுகர்க"],["kalaignar","It is about a literal death: what good is the wealth to one who dies without enjoying it?","அதனை அனுபவிக்காமல் செத்துப்போகிறவனுக்கு."]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1002":{"book":"kural","num":1002,"t":["பொருளானாம் எல்லாமென்று ஈயாது இவறும்","மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு"],"p":"He who knows that wealth yields every pleasure and yet is so blind as to lead miserly life will be born a demon.","r":"Through the delusion of clinging without giving, thinking all things come by wealth, comes a மாணாப் பிறப்பு.","n":"The delusion isn't the belief that wealth achieves everything, a point the Kural makes elsewhere: it lies in clinging while holding that belief.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","eegai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It means rebirth as a ghost that hungers amid food, matching those who watched others' hunger amid plenty.","நிறைதலில்லாத பேய்ப்பிறப்பு உண்டாம்."],["kalaignar","It describes a person: he becomes a low being with no distinction, with no rebirth involved.","எந்தச் சிறப்புமில்லாத இழி பிறவியாவான்."]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1003":{"book":"kural","num":1003,"t":["ஈட்டம் இவறி இசைவேண்டா ஆடவர்","தோற்றம் நிலக்குப் பொறை"],"p":"A burden to the earth are men bent on the acquisition of riches and not (true) fame.","r":"The appearance on earth of men who cling to accumulation and don't want renown is a burden to the land.","n":"Parimelazhagar identifies the renown with virtue achieved by giving, so the choice isn't between wealth and vanity. Manakkudavar draws the harshest conclusion: it would be better if they hadn't been born.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1004":{"book":"kural","num":1004,"t":["எச்சமென்று என்எண்ணுங் கொல்லோ ஒருவரால்","நச்சப் படாஅ தவன்"],"p":"What will the miser who is not liked (by any one) regard as his own (in the world to come) ?","r":"What does one wanted by no one imagine will remain behind him?","n":"The issue is what survives a person, and the question implies nothing does, since fame only rests on givers. Pope's gloss moves this to 'the world to come' while his couplet does not. The Tamil says எச்சம், what is left behind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1005":{"book":"kural","num":1005,"t":["கொடுப்பதூஉம் துய்ப்பதூஉம் இல்லார்க்கு அடுக்கிய","கோடியுண் டாயினும் இல்"],"p":"Those who neither give (to others) nor enjoy (their property) are (truly) destitute, though possessing immense riches.","r":"Those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing, even with crores upon crores.","n":"An ownership paradox: possession is defined by use. Parimelazhagar says giving is named first because virtue outranks pleasure. Kalaignar fuses the two: those not happy in giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1006":{"book":"kural","num":1006,"t":["ஏதம் பெருஞ்செல்வம் தான்துவ்வான் தக்கார்க்கொன்று","ஈதல் இயல்பிலா தான்"],"p":"He who enjoys not (his riches) nor relieves the wants of the worthy is a disease to his wealth.","r":"One who doesn't use it himself and doesn't naturally give any of it to the deserving is a misfortune with great wealth.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man is a disease of the wealth, which could have done both things.","இரண்டும் செய்தற்கு இடனுடைத்தாய செல்வத்திற்கு ஒரு நோய்."],["manakkudavar","The great wealth obtained by such a person is itself faulty.","பெற்ற பெருஞ் செல்வம் குற்றமுடைத்து"]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1007":{"book":"kural","num":1007,"t":["அற்றார்க்கொன்று ஆற்றாதான் செல்வம் மிகநலம்","பெற்றாள் தமியள்மூத் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of him who never bestows anything on the destitute is like a woman of beauty growing old without a husband.","r":"The wealth of one who gives nothing to the destitute is like a woman of exceeding beauty growing old alone.","n":"The simile mourns what is wasted, and treats a woman's worth as realized through a husband. Parimelazhagar's gloss deepens it: she is unmarried for want of anyone to give her away, a giver being missing in both halves, and she herself loses something too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1008":{"book":"kural","num":1008,"t":["நச்சப் படாதவன் செல்வம் நடுவூருள்","நச்சு மரம்பழுத் தற்று"],"p":"The wealth of him who is disliked (by all) is like the fruit-bearing of the etty tree in the midst of a town.","r":"The wealth of one who isn't wanted is a poison tree bearing fruit in the middle of town.","n":"நச்சப்படாதவன், 'unwanted', and நச்சு, 'poison', chime, and it is what makes the verse work. Being close by is what makes it worse: Parimelazhagar says he is near the needy and still gives nothing. Manakkudavar extends it, saying any who eat out of desire will die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1009":{"book":"kural","num":1009,"t":["அன்பொரீஇத் தற்செற்று அறநோக்காது ஈட்டிய","ஒண்பொருள் கொள்வார் பிறர்"],"p":"Strangers will inherit the riches that have been acquired without regard for friendship, comfort and charity.","r":"Others will take the bright wealth amassed by giving up love, by self-denial and by ignoring virtue.","n":"The list gives three prices paid, and the last word takes away the prize. Parimelazhagar says பிறர் excludes even his own line. Kalaignar makes ignoring virtue into unethical earning, which is a different charge from not giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1010":{"book":"kural","num":1010,"t":["சீருடைச் செல்வர் சிறுதுனி மாரி","வறங்கூர்ந் தனையது உடைத்து"],"p":"The short-lived poverty of those who are noble and rich is like the clouds becoming poor (for a while).","r":"The சிறுதுனி of the renowned rich has the nature of rain-clouds going dry.","n":"As a closing verse it gives the positive pole of the chapter's whole contrast: the wealth that does give.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","சிறுதுனி is brief poverty: those who gave everything soon recover, like a cloud. He rejects the other reading as not suited to this chapter.","நிற்கும் காலம் சிறிதாய வறுமை"],["manakkudavar","சிறுதுனி is slight displeasure that stops their giving, and the world suffers as with failed rain.","சீருடைய செல்வர் சிறுதுனியால் ஈயாதொழிதல், மாரி பெய் யாமை மிக்காற் போலும்"]],"ch":"k:101"},"k:1011":{"book":"kural","num":1011,"t":["கருமத்தால் நாணுதல் நாணுந் திருநுதல்","நல்லவர் நாணுப் பிற"],"p":"True modesty is the fear of (evil) deeds; all other modesty is (simply) the bashfulness of virtuous maids.","r":"Shame is being ashamed through one's actions; anything other is the shame of women of the bright brow.","n":"The verse separates moral shame from bodily modesty and assigns the second to women. Whichever women are meant, the distinction is gendered.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","vinai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The women are honoured family women, and he rejects explicitly the reading that they are courtesans.","அழகிய நுதலினையுடைய குலமகளிர் நாண்கள்."],["manakkudavar","They are courtesans, good only in their brow, so that any other shame is merely like theirs.","அழகிய நுதலினாலே நல்லாராகிய கணிகையர் நாணத்தோ டொக்கும்."]],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1012":{"book":"kural","num":1012,"t":["ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல","நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு"],"p":"Food, clothing and the like are common to all men but modesty is peculiar to the good.","r":"Food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and the distinction of humans is having shame.","n":"The verse seems to separate humans from other life, but the commentators draw the line within humanity. Parimelazhagar contrasts all people with good people, and Manakkudavar contrasts base people with leaders. Parimelazhagar lists the rest of what is shared as sleep, fear and desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1013":{"book":"kural","num":1013,"t":["ஊனைக் குறித்த உயிரெல்லாம் நாண்என்னும்","நன்மை குறித்தது சால்பு"],"p":"As the body is the abode of the spirit, so the excellence of modesty is the abode of perfection.","r":"As all lives are fixed on flesh, worth is fixed on the good called shame.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","saandraanmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஊன் is the body: worth can't have effect without shame, as a soul can't without a body. He reports ஊண் as a variant.","உயிர் உடம்பொடு கூடியல்லது பயனெய்தாத வாறுபோலச் சால்பு நாணோடு கூடியல்லது பயன் எய்தாது"],["manakkudavar","The word is food: living things want food above all, and worth wants shame above all.","பலவகை உயிரும் மேற்கூறிய எல்லாவற்றினும் உண்டியைக் கருதிற்று"]],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1014":{"book":"kural","num":1014,"t":["அணிஅன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு அஃதின்றேல்","பிணிஅன்றோ பீடு நடை"],"p":"Is not the modesty ornament of the noble ? Without it, their haughtiness would be a pain (to others).","r":"Isn't shame an ornament to the worthy? Without it, isn't a proud walk a disease?","n":"அணி and பிணி rhyme. Parimelazhagar makes this a disease for those who have to watch it. A walk, in other words, is public property and judged by the watcher.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","saandraanmai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1015":{"book":"kural","num":1015,"t":["பிறர்பழியும் தம்பழியும் நாணுவார் நாணுக்கு","உறைபதி என்னும் உலகு"],"p":"The world regards as the abode of modesty him who fear his own and other's guilt.","r":"The world calls those who feel shame for others' disgrace as for their own the dwelling of shame.","n":"This enlarges shame beyond guilt: one feels it for what one did not do. Moral concern here can't be reduced to self-regard.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1016":{"book":"kural","num":1016,"t":["நாண்வேலி கொள்ளாது மன்னோ வியன்ஞாலம்","பேணலர் மேலா யவர்"],"p":"The great make modesty their barrier (of defence) and not the wide world.","r":"The high don't care for the wide world unless they have shame for a fence.","n":"The verse can mean they would take shame rather than the world, or that they won't want the world without it; Parimelazhagar holds the first and reports the second. Pope's couplet follows the second. Kalaignar has shame as the best of protections, with no choice involved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1017":{"book":"kural","num":1017,"t":["நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் உயிர்ப்பொருட்டால்","நாண்துறவார் நாணாள் பவர்"],"p":"The modest would rather lose their life for the sake of modesty than lose modesty for the sake of life.","r":"Those ruled by shame will give up life for shame, and won't give up shame for life.","n":"The syntax folds on itself like the choice it describes. நாணாள்பவர் can be 'those who wield shame', as a commander does, and not those who are ruled by it. Kalaignar glosses the object as மானம், showing how closely the two virtues run.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1018":{"book":"kural","num":1018,"t":["பிறர்நாணத் தக்கது தான்நாணா னாயின்","அறம்நாணத் தக்கது உடைத்து"],"p":"Virtue is likely to forsake him who shamelessly does what others are ashamed of.","r":"If one is not ashamed of what makes others ashamed, it's a fault that virtue itself would be ashamed of.","n":"நாண recurs and virtue becomes a person who blushes. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read this as virtue leaving such a man, since virtue doesn't approach the shameless, so shame is a precondition of virtue and not one of its parts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1019":{"book":"kural","num":1019,"t":["குலஞ்சுடும் கொள்கை பிழைப்பின் நலஞ்சுடும்","நாணின்மை நின்றக் கடை"],"p":"Want of manners injures one's family; but want of modesty injures one's character.","r":"A lapse in principle burns one's lineage, and lasting shamelessness burns every good thing.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws the ranking out: destroying shame is worse than destroying conduct, which is significant given the rank chapter 14 gives conduct. Fire (சுடும்) is used for both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","ozhukkam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1020":{"book":"kural","num":1020,"t":["நாண்அகத் தில்லார் இயக்கம் மரப்பாவை","நாணால் உயிர்மருட்டி அற்று"],"p":"The actions of those who are without modesty at heart are like those of puppet moved by a string.","r":"The movements of those with no shame within are like a wooden puppet feigning life by its string.","n":"The pun is on நாண், which means both shame and string. The puppet has the one thing the man lacks, as Parimelazhagar notes, in a neat inversion. It leaves a man without shame to be moved from outside.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:102"},"k:1021":{"book":"kural","num":1021,"t":["கருமம் செயஒருவன் கைதூவேன் என்னும்","பெருமையின் பீடுடையது இல்"],"p":"There is no higher greatness than that of one saying. I will not cease in my effort (to raise my family).","r":"No greatness has more dignity than that of one who says 'I will not stay my hand from the work'.","n":"The verse says only கருமம், and family comes from the chapter's title. Parimelazhagar rejects two rival readings, one's own work and others' work, the second because serving others isn't leadership. Greatness here sits in the declaration (என்னும்), before any result.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1022":{"book":"kural","num":1022,"t":["ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும் எனஇரண்டின்","நீள்வினையால் நீளும் குடி"],"p":"One's family is raised by untiring perseverance in both effort and wise contrivances.","r":"By long-continued work in the two things called effort and mature knowledge, the family is extended.","n":"நீள்வினையால் நீளும் is a chime, in which the long labour lengthens the line.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","குடி is one's own family, and it rises.","ஒருவன் குடி உயரும்."],["kalaignar","குடி is the people attached to him, whose pride rises.","அவனைச் சேர்ந்துள்ள குடிமக்களின் பெருமை உயரும்."]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1023":{"book":"kural","num":1023,"t":["குடிசெய்வல் என்னும் ஒருவற்குத் தெய்வம்","மடிதற்றுத் தான்முந் துறும்"],"p":"The Deity will clothe itself and appear before him who resolves on raising his family.","r":"For one who says 'I will raise my people', தெய்வம் tucks up its garment and goes ahead on its own.","n":"The image of tightened dress is homely and physical: the divine as a worker who gets ready to help.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","oozh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The deity is said to lead, and leading is an act ascribed to destiny (நியதி).","முன் நடப்பார் செயல் நியதிமேல் ஏற்பட்டது."],["kalaignar","What is called divine action is the power of nature.","தெய்வச் செயல் எனக்கூறப்படும் இயற்கையின் ஆற்றல்கூட"]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1024":{"book":"kural","num":1024,"t":["சூழாமல் தானே முடிவெய்தும் தம்குடியைத்","தாழாது உஞற்று பவர்க்கு"],"p":"Those who are prompt in their efforts (to better their family) need no deliberation, such efforts will of themselves succee d.","r":"For those who work without delay for their people, the end arrives by itself, without deliberation.","n":"The agent is unnamed. Parimelazhagar supplies the deity of 1023, while Manakkudavar supplies the man's good deeds (நல்வினை), and Kalaignar leaves success to come by itself. Each fills the gap to suit his view.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","muyarchi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1025":{"book":"kural","num":1025,"t":["குற்றம் இலனாய்க் குடிசெய்து வாழ்வானைச்","சுற்றமாச் சுற்றும் உலகு"],"p":"People will eagerly seek the friendship of the prosperous soul who has raised his family without foul means.","r":"The world gathers around one who lives faultlessly raising his people, as kin.","n":"The chime of சுற்றம் and சுற்றும் turns the noun into what kin do. Parimelazhagar's motive for this is unsentimental: people come for their own benefit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1026":{"book":"kural","num":1026,"t":["நல்லாண்மை என்பது ஒருவற்குத் தான்பிறந்த","இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்"],"p":"A man's true manliness consists in making himself the head and benefactor of his family.","r":"What's called good manliness is making oneself capable of ruling the house one was born to.","n":"நல்லாண்மை and இல்லாண்மை chime. Parimelazhagar says the adjective நல் is there to exclude the warrior's manliness. Kalaignar reverses the sense: one who rules well brings pride to his birth-family, so it isn't about ruling the family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1027":{"book":"kural","num":1027,"t":["அமரகத்து வன்கண்ணர் போலத் தமரகத்தும்","ஆற்றுவார் மேற்றே பொறை"],"p":"Like heroes in the battle-field, the burden (of protection etc.) is borne by those who are the most efficient in a family.","r":"As with the stout-hearted in battle, the burden among kin falls on the capable.","n":"The commentators stress that many enter the field and many are born in a family: it isn't that others are forbidden, but that it falls to whoever can carry it. A question of ability, not of seniority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1028":{"book":"kural","num":1028,"t":["குடிசெய்வார்க் கில்லை பருவம் மடிசெய்து","மானங் கருதக் கெடும்"],"p":"As a family suffers by (one's) indolence and false dignity there is to be so season (good or bad) to those who strive to raise their family.","r":"Those who raise their people have no season; if they idle and think about மானம், it will be ruined.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","maanam","muyarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மானம் is the pride of asking 'why must I alone suffer while the rest are comfortable?'","'இக்குடியிலுள்ளார் யாவரும் இன்பமுற இக்காலத்துத் துன்பமுறுவேன் யானோ?'"],["kalaignar","It is being shaken by degrading attacks made on oneself.","தன்மீது நடத்தப்படும் இழிவான தாக்குதலைக் கண்டு கலங்கினாலோ"]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1029":{"book":"kural","num":1029,"t":["இடும்பைக்கே கொள்கலம் கொல்லோ குடும்பத்தைக்","குற்ற மறைப்பான் உடம்பு"],"p":"Is it only to suffering that his body is exposed who undertakes to preserve his family from evil ?","r":"Is the body of one who covers his household against fault only a vessel for suffering?","n":"Parimelazhagar treats this as a pointed remark and not self-pity: the man accepts it, since the family's rise is his reward.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","noy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","மறைத்தல் is shielding, as with a shield against spears: he protects them from the three kinds of suffering.","மூவகைத் துன்பமும் உறற்பாலதாய தன் குடியை அவை உறாமற்காக்க முயல்வானது உடம்பு"],["manakkudavar","மறைத்தல் is concealing the failings found among kin.","சுற்றத்தார் மாட்டு உளதாகிய குறையை மறைக்கக் கருதுவான் உடம்பு"]],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1030":{"book":"kural","num":1030,"t":["இடுக்கண்கால் கொன்றிட வீழும் அடுத்தூன்றும்","நல்லாள் இலாத குடி"],"p":"If there are none to prop up and maintain a family (in distress), it will fall at the stroke of the axe of misfortune.","r":"When trouble cuts it at the foot, a family with no good person to prop it will fall.","n":"A tree image is implied: Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar supply the axe. They take நல்லாள் as a good man (ஆண்மகன்), while Kalaignar has anyone with the ability. The same word names a personified good woman in 924 and 1040; here it divides as நல் ஆள், 'good person', which doesn't itself specify a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:103"},"k:1031":{"book":"kural","num":1031,"t":["சுழன்றும்ஏர்ப் பின்னது உலகம் அதனால்","உழந்தும் உழவே தலை"],"p":"Agriculture, though laborious, is the most excellent (form of labour); for people, though they go about (in search of various employments), have at last to resort to the farmer.","r":"Though it whirls about, the world follows the plough; therefore, though toilsome, farming is foremost.","n":"The verse concedes the toil and doesn't romanticize it (உழந்தும்). Its case is a structural one: all other paths end up at the same place. சுழன்றும் may also suggest the world turning like a wheel behind the plough, and Kalaignar's version keeps this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1032":{"book":"kural","num":1032,"t":["உழுவார் உலகத்தார்க்கு ஆணிஅஃ தாற்றாது","எழுவாரை எல்லாம் பொறுத்து"],"p":"Agriculturists are (as it were) the linch-pin of the world for they support all other workers who cannot till the soil.","r":"Those who plough are the linchpin of the world's people, for they carry all those who can't do it and rise to other things.","n":"Parimelazhagar says உழுவார் extends to those who have others plough for them, citing a parallel, which brings landowners into the honour. The verse doesn't say so. He reports a variant, தொழுவாரே, in which those unable to plough cringe to their patrons, which anticipates 1033.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uzhavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1033":{"book":"kural","num":1033,"t":["உழுதுண்டு வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார்மற் றெல்லாம்","தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல் பவர்"],"p":"They alone live who live by agriculture; all others lead a cringing, dependent life.","r":"Only those who live by eating from their own ploughing truly live; all others bow to eat, and walk behind.","n":"Parimelazhagar's வாழ்வார் are those who belong to themselves (தமக்குரியராய்): the verse is about independence as much as food.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The rest bow to other patrons and live on what they are given.","பிறரைத் தொழுது, அவர் சில கொடுப்பத் தம் உயிரோம்பி"],["kalaignar","The rest bow to the farmers themselves.","மற்றவர்கள் அவர்களைத் தொழுதுண்டு வாழ வேண்டியிருக்கிறது."]],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1034":{"book":"kural","num":1034,"t":["பலகுடை நீழலும் தங்குடைக்கீழ்க் காண்பர்","அலகுடை நீழ லவர்"],"p":"Patriotic farmers desire to bring all other states under the control of their own king.","r":"Those with the shade of grain-ears will see many umbrellas' shade under their own umbrella.","n":"The royal umbrella is set against the grain's shade. Manakkudavar notices a pun: an 'umbrella-less shade'. The verse claims that farming, not armies, underwrites conquest.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The umbrella is that of their king, whose conquests their grain makes possible.","பலவேந்தர் - குடை நிழலதாய மண் முழுதினையும் தம் வேந்தர்குடைக்கீழே காண்பர்."],["kalaignar","Farmers themselves have the strength to bring all governments under them.","தமது குடைநிழலின் கீழ் கொண்டு வரும் வலிமை பெற்றவர்கள் உழவர்கள்."]],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1035":{"book":"kural","num":1035,"t":["இரவார் இரப்பார்க்கொன்று ஈவர் கரவாது","கைசெய்தூண் மாலை யவர்"],"p":"Those whose nature is to live by manual labour will never beg but give something to those who beg.","r":"Those whose way is to eat what their hands produce don't ask, and give to those who ask without hiding.","n":"The hand is the verse's key: it labours and it gives, and it never stretches out. Parimelazhagar restricts கைசெய் to ploughing, while Kalaignar makes it any self-earned work. The verse anticipates the chapters on begging, whose word கரவாது it uses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1036":{"book":"kural","num":1036,"t":["உழவினார் கைம்மடங்கின் இல்லை விழைவதூஉம்","விட்டேம்என் பார்க்கும் நிலை"],"p":"If the farmer's hands are slackened, even the ascetic state will fail.","r":"If farmers fold their hands, even those who say they have given up desire won't be able to stay in that state.","n":"என்பார், 'those who say', carries a slight edge. Parimelazhagar extends the verse to all of this world's aims, the next world's and liberation. He and another reading differ on whether விழைவது is food or desire in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uzhavu","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1037":{"book":"kural","num":1037,"t":["தொடிப்புழுதி கஃசா உணக்கின் பிடித்தெருவும்","வேண்டாது சாலப் படும்"],"p":"If the land is dried so as to reduce one ounce of earth to a quarter, it will grow plentifully even without a handful of manure.","r":"If you dry the soil so that a measure of earth falls to a quarter, it will grow abundantly without even a handful of manure.","n":"A rare place where the Kural gives technical advice, with exact weights. Kalaignar reads the reduction as coming from repeated ploughing, while Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar have sun-drying. The moral of attention before input is left unstated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1038":{"book":"kural","num":1038,"t":["ஏரினும் நன்றால் எருவிடுதல் கட்டபின்","நீரினும் நன்றதன் காப்பு"],"p":"Manuring is better than ploughing; after weeding, watching is better than watering (it).","r":"Manuring is better than ploughing, and after weeding, guarding is better than watering.","n":"A ladder of care in which each stage beats the previous one, and watchfulness comes out above water, the thing plants most obviously need. Parimelazhagar sets out the sequence of five.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","alavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1039":{"book":"kural","num":1039,"t":["செல்லான் கிழவன் இருப்பின் நிலம்புலந்து","இல்லாளின் ஊடி விடும்"],"p":"If the owner does not (personally) attend to his cultivation, his land will behave like an angry wife and yield him no pleasure.","r":"If the owner stays away and doesn't go, the land will resent and sulk like a wife.","n":"ஊடி is a term from love poetry, used here in farm advice. கிழவன், 'owner', also means husband, a double meaning that makes the comparison work. Parimelazhagar says that the owner must go himself and not send others, which is in some tension with his widening of உழுவார் in 1032.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1040":{"book":"kural","num":1040,"t":["இலமென்று அசைஇ இருப்பாரைக் காணின்","நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்"],"p":"The maiden, Earth, will laug h at the sight of those who plead poverty and lead an idle life.","r":"Seeing those who say 'we have nothing' and sit idle, the good woman called Land laughs.","n":"The Earth doesn't scold but laughs. Parimelazhagar notes this is the bridge to the next chapter on poverty, and he has a variant இரப்பாரை, 'those who beg', which Manakkudavar uses, pointing to the later chapters too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uzhavu","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:104"},"k:1041":{"book":"kural","num":1041,"t":["இன்மையின் இன்னாதது யாதெனின் இன்மையின்","இன்மையே இன்னா தது"],"p":"There is nothing that afflicts (one) like poverty.","r":"If asked what is more painful than poverty, what's more painful than poverty is poverty.","n":"The sentence tries to find a comparison and fails. As Parimelazhagar says, if it has no equal, nothing can exceed it. Tautology is the argument.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1042":{"book":"kural","num":1042,"t":["இன்மை எனவொரு பாவி மறுமையும்","இம்மையும் இன்றி வரும்"],"p":"When cruel poverty comes on, it deprives one of both the present and future (bliss).","r":"The sinner called poverty comes, with this world and the next both lost.","n":"பாவி personifies it as an evil actor, although Manakkudavar reads a poor person who has the sin. The next world is lost, in his reason, because one can't afford charity, so poverty denies access to virtue itself. That cuts against the assumption that merit is equally open to all.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1043":{"book":"kural","num":1043,"t":["தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும் தொகையாக","நல்குரவு என்னும் நசை"],"p":"Hankering poverty destroys at once the greatness of (one's) ancient descent and (the dignity of one's) speech.","r":"The desire called poverty destroys at once one's ancient descent and தோல்.","n":"The verse identifies poverty with desire, நசை. Parimelazhagar explains that where there's no craving there's no poverty, which is a remarkable reframing of an economic state as a mental one.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தோல் means graceful speech, as Tolkappiyam uses it, and he rejects reading it as the body.","பழைய குடிவரவினையும் அதற்கு ஏற்ற சொல்லினையும் ஒருங்கே கெடுக்கும்."],["manakkudavar","தோல் means beauty of form.","குடிப்பிறப்பினையும் வடிவழகினையும் ஒருங்கு கெடுக்கும்"],["kalaignar","தோல் means fame, and the desire is a greed that poverty causes.","பரம்பரைப் பெருமையையும், புகழையும் ஒரு சேரக் கெடுத்துவிடும்."]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1044":{"book":"kural","num":1044,"t":["இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும் இன்மை இளிவந்த","சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு தரும்"],"p":"Even in those of high birth, poverty will produce the fault of uttering mean words.","r":"Even in the well-born, poverty brings the lapse from which degrading words are born.","n":"Parimelazhagar says what the words are: 'you must give to us'. He describes the lapse precisely: at the height of suffering, they forget their birth for a moment. The uncontrolled word is இளிவந்த, degrading, and that is the verdict of chapter 97 on such a person.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1045":{"book":"kural","num":1045,"t":["நல்குரவு என்னும் இடும்பையுள் பல்குரைத்","துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்"],"p":"The misery of poverty brings in its train many (more) miseries.","r":"Within the suffering called poverty, many sufferings arrive and settle.","n":"Parimelazhagar lists a day's humiliations in order: going to the rich man's door, seeing him, being refused, accepting what he gives, and working out how to use it. One of the most vivid passages in his commentary, it is his own and not the verse's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1046":{"book":"kural","num":1046,"t":["நற்பொருள் நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லினும் நல்கூர்ந்தார்","சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்"],"p":"The words of the poor are profitless, though they may be sound in thought and clear in expression.","r":"Even if they speak of good matters, fully understood, the words and meaning of the poor go slack.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a social mechanism and not a cognitive one: nobody listens, in case they come to feel obliged to help. So the meaning fails not in the speaker but in what the listeners are afraid of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","arivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1047":{"book":"kural","num":1047,"t":["அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு ஈன்றதா யானும்","பிறன்போல நோக்கப் படும்"],"p":"He that is reduced to absolute poverty will be regarded as a stranger even by his own mother.","r":"One in poverty unconnected with virtue is looked at as a stranger, even by the mother who bore him.","n":"The mother is again the limit case, as in 923. Parimelazhagar's reason is unsentimental: she fears that he would be taking and not giving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அறஞ்சாரா describes the poverty: it doesn't come from virtue nor yield virtue.","காரண காரியங்களுள் ஒன்றானும் இயையாமை."],["kalaignar","It describes the man, who has left the path of virtue because of poverty.","அறநெறியிலிருந்து விலகி நிற்பவனை"]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1048":{"book":"kural","num":1048,"t":["இன்றும் வருவது கொல்லோ நெருநலும்","கொன்றது போலும் நிரப்பு"],"p":"Is the poverty that almost killed me yesterday, to meet me today too ?","r":"Will it come today too, the poverty that as good as killed me yesterday?","n":"The verse switches to first-person speech. Parimelazhagar imagines the speaker: someone who with great difficulty filled his belly yesterday. In போலும் he survived what felt like death, which means he will face it again.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1049":{"book":"kural","num":1049,"t":["நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும் நிரப்பினுள்","யாதொன்றும் கண்பாடு அரிது"],"p":"One may sleep in the midst of fire; but by no means in the midst of poverty.","r":"One can even sleep in the middle of fire, but in the middle of poverty, there is no way of closing the eyes.","n":"Parimelazhagar explains the sleep in fire as a possibility through mantra and medicine, which rationalizes what looks like hyperbole. He hears the same speaker as in 1048.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1050":{"book":"kural","num":1050,"t":["துப்புர வில்லார் துவரத் துறவாமை","உப்பிற்கும் காடிக்கும் கூற்று"],"p":"The destitute poor, who do not renounce their bodies, only consume their neighbour's salt and water.","r":"If those without means don't fully renounce, they are death to salt and to gruel.","n":"The mockery falls on tiny things, salt and sour gruel: the poor person's mere continuing destroys these and nothing more.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","To renounce fully is to give up the body itself, for honour's sake; those left by kin have done everything else.","நின்ற தம் உடம்பினையும் துறத்தல்."],["manakkudavar","It is to give up attachment to wealth, which brings pleasure to the poor.","இது துறப்பாராயின், இன்ப முறலா மென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:105"},"k:1051":{"book":"kural","num":1051,"t":["இரக்க இரத்தக்கார்க் காணின் கரப்பின்","அவர்பழி தம்பழி அன்று"],"p":"If you meet with those that may be begged of, you may beg; (but) if they withhold (their gift) it is their blame and not yours.","r":"Ask if you find those worth asking; if they withhold, the blame is theirs and not yours.","n":"The chapter starts by moving the moral burden from the asker to the withholder. Parimelazhagar says such givers understand before anything is said, and the blame sticks to them as a stain does on pure white cloth, because they're rare.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1052":{"book":"kural","num":1052,"t":["இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல் இரந்தவை","துன்பம் உறாஅ வரின்"],"p":"Even begging may be pleasant, if what is begged for is obtained without grief (to him that begs).","r":"Even asking is pleasure to a person, if what is asked for comes without suffering.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","eegai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The suffering is the asker's: the courting, reading the giver's moods and being refused, which is spared if the giver anticipates.","அவையுறாமல் வருதலாவது, அவர் முன்னுணர்ந்து ஈயக்கோடல்."],["kalaignar","The suffering is that of both, giver and receiver.","வழங்குபவர், வாங்குபவர் ஆகிய இருவர் மனத்திற்கும் துன்பம் எதுவுமின்றி"]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1053":{"book":"kural","num":1053,"t":["கரப்பிலா நெஞ்சின் கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று","இரப்புமோ ரேஎர் உடைத்து"],"p":"There is even a beauty in standing before and begging of those who are liberal in their gifts and understand their duty (to beggars).","r":"Even standing and asking before those of unconcealing heart who know கடன் has a certain beauty.","n":"முன்நின்று is significant: for Parimelazhagar merely standing is enough, and it's the absence of spoken words that keeps the grace.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["iravu","karavu","maanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","கடன் is honour, and it is the asker's: he reads 963 to explain why such a giver can sense a poor person's dignity.","கரத்தல் இல்லாத நெஞ்சினையுடைய மானம் அறிவார்"],["manakkudavar","கடன் is social obligation, the duty of helping.","கரப்பிலாத நெஞ்சினை யுடைய ஒப்புரவறிவார்"],["kalaignar","கடன் is the giver's sense of duty.","உள்ளதை ஒளிக்காத உள்ளமும், கடமையுணர்வும் கொண்டவரிடத்தில்"]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1054":{"book":"kural","num":1054,"t":["இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும் கரத்தல்","கனவிலும் தேற்றாதார் மாட்டு"],"p":"To beg of such as never think of withholding (their charity) even in their dreams, is in fact the same as giving (it oneself);","r":"Even asking is like giving, from those who don't conceive of concealment even in a dream.","n":"An extraordinary equivalence that erases the difference in rank. Parimelazhagar tempers it: asking brings no fame, but nor does it damage any you already have, so that is all the likeness. The strong statement is the verse's and the caveat is his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1055":{"book":"kural","num":1055,"t":["கரப்பிலார் வையகத்து உண்மையால் கண்ணின்று","இரப்பவர் மேற்கொள் வது"],"p":"As there are in the world those that give without refusing, there are (also) those that prefer to beg by simply standing before them.","r":"It's because there are people on earth who don't conceal that askers take up standing before them.","n":"Parimelazhagar adds the alternative: without such people they would give up their lives, since they can't give up honour. So the existence of generous people is what keeps them alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1056":{"book":"kural","num":1056,"t":["கரப்பிடும்பை யில்லாரைக் காணின் நிரப்பிடும்பை","எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்"],"p":"All the evil of begging will be removed at the sight of those who are far from the evil of refusing.","r":"If one sees those without the affliction of concealment, every affliction of poverty is destroyed at once.","n":"Concealing is called இடும்பை, the same word as poverty, so the verse treats the wealthy miser as a sufferer too. The cure comes merely at the sight (காணின்). Manakkudavar concludes one shouldn't ask from those who are merely rich.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","inmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1057":{"book":"kural","num":1057,"t":["இகழ்ந்தெள்ளாது ஈவாரைக் காணின் மகிழ்ந்துள்ளம்","உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து"],"p":"Beggars rejoice exceedingly when they behold those who bestow (their alms) with kindness and courtesy.","r":"If one sees those who give without scorn and contempt, the heart is delighted and rejoices deep inside.","n":"The joy lies in being given to without being lowered. உள்ளுள் உவப்பது goes deeper than the material relief, and it's the manner of the gift that's being measured.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1058":{"book":"kural","num":1058,"t":["இரப்பாரை இல்லாயின் ஈர்ங்கண்மா ஞாலம்","மரப்பாவை சென்றுவந் தற்று"],"p":"If there were no beggars, (the actions done in) the cool wide world would only resemble the movement of a puppet.","r":"If there were no askers, this wide cool world would be wooden puppets coming and going.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","eegai","pugazh"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Without someone to give to, others would earn no fame or merit, and they might as well be lifeless.","அவர்க்கு ஈதலைச் செய்து புகழும் புண்ணியமும் எய்தாமையின், உயிருடையரல்லார்"],["manakkudavar","Asking and giving are the order of the world, so asking shouldn't be treated as low.","இரத்தலும் ஈதலும் உலகியல்பாதலான், இரத்தல் இழிவென்று கொள்ளப்படா"],["kalaignar","He changes the subject: those who won't let askers near are the puppets.","தம்மை நெருங்கக் கூடாது என்கிற மனிதர்களுக்கும்"]],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1059":{"book":"kural","num":1059,"t":["ஈவார்கண் என்னுண்டாம் தோற்றம் இரந்துகோள்","மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை"],"p":"What (praise) would there be to givers (of alms) if there were no beggars to ask for and reveive (them).","r":"What renown could givers have, if there were none who want to ask and take?","n":"The logic has an uncomfortable side: if givers' renown requires askers, a world without poverty would be poorer in virtue. The verse doesn't address this, and it treats askers as a need, not as a problem.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1060":{"book":"kural","num":1060,"t":["இரப்பான் வெகுளாமை வேண்டும் நிரப்பிடும்பை","தானேயும் சாலும் கரி"],"p":"He who begs ought not to be angry (at a refusal); for even the misery of (his own) poverty should be a sufficient reason (for so doing).","r":"The one who asks shouldn't be angry; the affliction of his own poverty is proof enough.","n":"His poverty proves that wealth is hard to come by, so the refuser may have a real reason. This turns the sufferer's own case into a reason for patience. It is a counterweight to 1051, which puts blame on those who withhold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:106"},"k:1061":{"book":"kural","num":1061,"t":["கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும்","இரவாமை கோடி உறும்"],"p":"Not to beg (at all) even from those excellent persons who cheerfully give without refusing, will do immense good.","r":"Not asking, even from those precious as eyes who give gladly without concealing, is worth ten million times more.","n":"The verse picks the best case for asking, then refuses it. Parimelazhagar narrows this: it's about not asking even friends who already know of one's poverty, and he concludes one should try to live by hard effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1062":{"book":"kural","num":1062,"t":["இரந்தும் உயிர்வாழ்தல் வேண்டின் பரந்து","கெடுக உலகியற்றி யான்"],"p":"If the Creator of the world has decreed even begging as a means of livelihood, may he too go abegging and perish.","r":"If even by begging one must live, let the one who made the world wander and perish.","n":"The Kural's sharpest line against any cosmic order. It doesn't assert that a creator exists, since the curse is conditional. Kalaignar makes that explicit: the one 'said to be' the creator.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","oozh","inmai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The creator decrees each being's livelihood along with its karma at conception; if that includes begging, the evil returns on him and he should suffer the same.","சில உயிர்க்கு இரத்தலையும் ஒரு செய் தொழிலாகக் கற்பித்தானாயின், அத்தீவினையால் தானும் அத்துன்பமுறல் வேண்டும்"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about those who choose to beg to survive; the conclusion is that death is better than begging.","இஃது இரக்கு மதனின், இறத்தல் அமையு மென்றது."],["kalaignar","He distances himself from the idea: it is whoever is 'said' to have created the world.","உலகத்தைப் படைத்தவனாகச் சொல்லப்படுபவனும் கெட்டொழிந்து திரியட்டும்."]],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1063":{"book":"kural","num":1063,"t":["இன்மை இடும்பை இரந்துதீர் வாமென்னும்","வன்மையின் வன்பாட்ட தில்"],"p":"There is no greater folly than the boldness with which one seeks to remedy the evils of poverty by begging (rather than by working).","r":"Nothing is harder than the hardness that says 'we will end the misery of poverty by begging'.","n":"வன்மை, hardness, is the opposite of what one would expect of an asker's position. Begging isn't weakness here but a kind of obstinate insensitivity. Manakkudavar adds that it doesn't even work, since it won't end poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","muyarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1064":{"book":"kural","num":1064,"t":["இடமெல்லாம் கொள்ளாத் தகைத்தே இடமில்லாக்","காலும் இரவொல்லாச் சால்பு"],"p":"Even the whole world cannot sufficiently praise the dignity that would not beg even in the midst of destitution.","r":"The worth that won't consent to asking even when no means remain is too great for all of space to contain.","n":"இடம் is used twice: as all of space, and as means or room. A man with no room left holds something that no room can hold.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandraanmai","iravu","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1065":{"book":"kural","num":1065,"t":["தெண்ணீர் அடுபுற்கை ஆயினும் தாள்தந்தது","உண்ணலின் ஊங்கினிய தில்"],"p":"Even thin gruel is ambrosia to him who has obtained it by labour.","r":"Though it is gruel thin as clear water, nothing is sweeter than eating what one's own effort brought.","n":"தாள், literally the foot, stands for effort. Sweetness has become a moral quality here and doesn't describe the taste. Parimelazhagar says it's equal to ambrosia, because it's one's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","undi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1066":{"book":"kural","num":1066,"t":["ஆவிற்கு நீரென்று இரப்பினும் நாவிற்கு","இரவின் இளிவந்த தில்"],"p":"There is nothing more disgraceful to one's tongue than to use it in begging water even for a cow.","r":"Even if one asks for water for a cow, nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking.","n":"A limit case with the worthiest cause and the smallest request, and it's still degrading. Parimelazhagar: virtue itself should be done by effort, never by begging. He also notes that நாவிற்கு confines the disgrace to the spoken word, and that this still exceeds every other disgrace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","aram","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1067":{"book":"kural","num":1067,"t":["இரப்பன் இரப்பாரை எல்லாம் இரப்பின்","கரப்பார் இரவன்மின் என்று"],"p":"I beseech all beggars and say, \"If you need to beg, never beg of those who give unwillingly.\"","r":"I beg of all who beg: if you must beg, don't beg of those who conceal.","n":"The poet himself takes on the posture he has just condemned, begging beggars. The repetition of இரப்பன், இரப்பாரை and இரப்பின் is almost a chant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1068":{"book":"kural","num":1068,"t":["இரவென்னும் ஏமாப்பில் தோணி கரவென்னும்","பார்தாக்கப் பக்கு விடும்"],"p":"The unsafe raft of begging will split when it strikes on the rock of refusal.","r":"The unsafe boat called asking will split apart when it strikes the rock called concealment.","n":"The sea of poverty is supplied by both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar. The verse's point isn't the rock alone: the boat itself is flawed, and this is what ஏமாப்பில் says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1069":{"book":"kural","num":1069,"t":["இரவுள்ள உள்ளம் உருகும் கரவுள்ள","உள்ளதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்"],"p":"To think of (the evil of) begging is enough to melt one's heart; but to think of refusal is enough to break it.","r":"Thinking of begging, the heart melts; thinking of concealment, even what remains is gone.","n":"The heart has two stages: it melts, and then there's nothing left to melt. Concealment is ranked as the worse.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","karavu","manam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The melting heart is that of an onlooker who imagines the cruelty; he notes that all others say it's the asker's.","இதற்குப் பிறரெல்லாம் 'இரக்கின்றவர் உள்ளம் உருகும்' என்று உரைத்தார்."],["manakkudavar","It concerns the asker, who has nothing to gain.","இஃது இரப்பார்க்கு ஆக்கமில்லை என்றது."]],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1070":{"book":"kural","num":1070,"t":["கரப்பவர்க்கு யாங்கொளிக்கும் கொல்லோ இரப்பவர்","சொல்லாடப் போஒம் உயிர்"],"p":"Saying \"No\" to a beggar takes away his life. (but as that very word will kill the refuser) where then would the latter's life hide itself ?","r":"As the asker's life goes out at the word of refusal, where does the life of the one who conceals hide?","n":"The rhetorical question is a charge: the word that kills the hearer ought to kill the speaker first. Parimelazhagar glosses 'life goes' as being stunned with helplessness, a less literal sense than the verse's own. He reports another reading as a dread-filled man's speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karavu","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:107"},"k:1071":{"book":"kural","num":1071,"t":["மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர் அவரன்ன","ஒப்பாரி யாங்கண்ட தில்"],"p":"The base resemble men perfectly (as regards form); and we have not seen such (exact) resemblance (among any other species).","r":"The base look just like human beings; we have never seen so close a resemblance.","n":"Bitter praise opens the portrait. The likeness is complete, and that is the whole problem, since there's no outward way of telling.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a likeness of body between two different kinds, distinguished only by whether they have good qualities.","மக்கட் சாதிக்கும் கயச்சாதிக்கும் வடிவு ஒத்தலின்"],["kalaignar","It is hypocrisy within one person, who is base while acting good.","குணத்தில் ஒருவர் கயவராக இருப்பார். ஆனால், நல்லவரைப் போலக் காட்டிக் கொள்வார்."]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1072":{"book":"kural","num":1072,"t":["நன்றறி வாரிற் கயவர் திருவுடையர்","நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்"],"p":"The low enjoy more felicity than those who know what is good; for the former are not troubled with anxiety (as to the good).","r":"The base are more fortunate than those who know what's good, for they have no anguish in their heart.","n":"A real observation under the sarcasm: moral knowledge has a psychological cost. Parimelazhagar spells it out: the good worry that they don't do enough, about obstacles to their good deeds, and about what might result. திரு here is mock praise, as he says.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","arivu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1073":{"book":"kural","num":1073,"t":["தேவர் அனையர் கயவர் அவருந்தாம்","மேவன செய்தொழுக லான்"],"p":"The base resemble the Gods; for the base ac t as they like.","r":"The base are like the gods, for they too act as they please.","n":"Having looked like humans in 1071, they now look like gods. Parimelazhagar says the difference between them goes unstated, since the gods act by understanding and the base by lowness. Kalaignar's gloss says 'the gods of the Puranas', which gives the barb a second target.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1074":{"book":"kural","num":1074,"t":["அகப்பட்டி ஆவாரைக் காணின் அவரின்","மிகப்பட்டுச் செம்மாக்கும் கீழ்"],"p":"The base feels proud when he sees persons whose acts meaner than his own.","r":"Seeing அகப்பட்டி, the base outdo them and swagger with pride.","n":"அகப்பட்டி is hard to fix, and I've left it untranslated. The readings below differ so widely that I can't be confident of either.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","These are people who behave as they please like strays, on a smaller scale than the base man, and he boasts by exceeding them.","தன்னிற் சுருங்கிய பட்டியாய் ஒழுகுவாரைக் கண்டானாயின்"],["manakkudavar","These are women at home with grace, and the base pride themselves on being more graceful still.","மனையகத்திருந்து பொற்புடையாராகிய பெண்டிரைக் காணின்"]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1075":{"book":"kural","num":1075,"t":["அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம் எச்சம்","அவாவுண்டேல் உண்டாம் சிறிது"],"p":"(The principle of) behaviour in the mean is chiefly fear; if not, hope of gain, to some extent.","r":"Fear is the conduct of the base, and beyond that there's some, if there's desire.","n":"A theory of external motivation: there's no inner source of good conduct in them, so it can only come from fear of punishment and hope of gain. Parimelazhagar names the king as what they fear, and Kalaignar reverses the order and makes it pretence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1076":{"book":"kural","num":1076,"t":["அறைபறை அன்னர் கயவர்தாம் கேட்ட","மறைபிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்க லான்"],"p":"The base are like a drum that is beaten, for they unburden to others the secrets they have heard.","r":"The base are like a beaten drum, for they carry any secret they hear and proclaim it to others.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes that the drum only sounds when beaten, a working likeness. உய்த்து, 'carrying', suggests to him the secret is a heavy burden to such a man, so that he has to go and put it down.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1077":{"book":"kural","num":1077,"t":["ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார் கயவர் கொடிறுடைக்கும்","கூன்கையர் அல்லா தவர்க்கு"],"p":"The mean will not (even) shake off (what sticks to) their hands (soon after a meal) to any but those who would break their jaws with their clenched fists.","r":"The base won't even shake their wet hands for any but those with bent fists who would break their jaws.","n":"The smallest gift is shaking drops of water off a washed hand. The gift is measured by what it takes to get it. Parimelazhagar sums up: they give nothing to the weak and everything to those who hurt them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1078":{"book":"kural","num":1078,"t":["சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர் கரும்புபோல்","கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்"],"p":"The great bestow (their alms) as soon as they are informed; (but) the mean, like the sugar-cane, only when they are tortured to death.","r":"The worthy are of use at a word, and the base, like sugar-cane, only when crushed.","n":"A sugar-cane is sweet, so the image concedes that the base do have something to give. But it comes out only under pressure. கொல்ல, 'to kill', is unusually strong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandraanmai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1079":{"book":"kural","num":1079,"t":["உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் காணின் பிறர்மேல்","வடுக்காண வற்றாகும் கீழ்"],"p":"The base will bring an evil (accusation) against others, as soon as he sees them (enjoying) good food and clothing.","r":"If they see others clothed and fed, the base are able to find a fault in them.","n":"Ordinary eating and dressing are enough to bring it on; no great success is required.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sirumai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","They invent faults that don't exist, with skill enough to make them believable.","அவர்மாட்டு வடுவில்லையாகவும் உண்டாக்கவல்லனாம்."],["manakkudavar","They search out faults that do exist in such people.","அவர் மாட்டு உள்ள குற்றங்களை ஆராயவல்லாராவர் கயவர்"]],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1080":{"book":"kural","num":1080,"t":["எற்றிற் குரியர் கயவரொன்று உற்றக்கால்","விற்றற்கு உரியர் விரைந்து"],"p":"The base will hasten to sell themselves as soon as a calamity has befallen them. For what else are they fitted ?","r":"What are the base fit for? When something befalls them, they are fit to sell themselves, quickly.","n":"The Book of Wealth closes with a person as a thing sold. Parimelazhagar's reading of விரைந்து is grim: they hurry before buyers learn what they are. An answer to the question is the insult: this is the only use they have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","maanam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:108"},"k:1081":{"book":"kural","num":1081,"t":["அணங்குகொல் ஆய்மயில் கொல்லோ கனங்குழை","மாதர்கொல் மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Is this jewelled female a celestial, a choice peahen, or a human being ? My mind is perplexed.","r":"The man, on first seeing her, cannot tell whether this woman in heavy earrings is a goddess, a rare peahen or a human being, and his heart reels.","n":"The three guesses aren't mere compliments. Parimelazhagar ties each to evidence: her indescribable form and his distress suggest a goddess, her grace and the grove suggest a peahen, and the way she looks back suggests a woman. Kalaignar's rendering drops the goddess and gives 'beauty that torments me' instead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kaamam","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1082":{"book":"kural","num":1082,"t":["நோக்கினாள் நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல் தாக்கணங்கு","தானைக்கொண் டன்ன துடைத்து"],"p":"This female beauty returning my looks is like a celestial maiden coming with an army to contend against me.","r":"The man says that when she answers his look with her own, it is as if a goddess who strikes on her own had brought an army with her.","n":"The returned look is the blow. Parimelazhagar says he has now settled that she is human, so the goddess of 1081 survives only as a figure, and his pain has grown from her beauty to her meaningful glance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1083":{"book":"kural","num":1083,"t":["பண்டறியேன் கூற்றென் பதனை இனியறிந்தேன்","பெண்டகையால் பேரமர்க் கட்டு"],"p":"I never knew before what is called Yama; I see it now; it is the eyes that carry on a great fight with (the help of) female qualities.","r":"The man says he never knew what Death was, and now he does: it has a woman's grace and large eyes that make war.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses the first line as a contrast between hearsay and experience: Death was something known from what the learned say, and only now seen. He adds that the man calls her Death because the suffering outweighs the pleasure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1084":{"book":"kural","num":1084,"t":["கண்டார் உயிருண்ணும் தோற்றத்தால் பெண்டகைப்","பேதைக்கு அமர்த்தன கண்"],"p":"These eyes that seem to kill those who look at them are as it were in hostilities with this feminine simplicity.","r":"The man says the eyes of this artless, womanly girl have a look that eats the life of those who see them, and he sets those eyes against, or alongside, her gentleness.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kootru","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","அமர்த்தன means the eyes are at odds with her gentle nature; they are cruel where she is not.","அமர்த்தல்: மாறுபடுதல்."],["manakkudavar","The word means the eyes fit her: they suit the girl in the work they do.","அமர்தல் - மேவல்."],["kalaignar","The eyes are a puzzling contradiction in someone so womanly.","ஏனிந்த மாறுபாடு?"]],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1085":{"book":"kural","num":1085,"t":["கூற்றமோ கண்ணோ பிணையோ மடவரல்","நோக்கமிம் மூன்றும் உடைத்து"],"p":"Is it Yama, (a pair of) eyes or a hind ?- Are not all these three in the looks of this maid ?","r":"The man asks whether it is Death, an eye or a doe, since the young woman's look has the nature of all three.","n":"The list holds opposites: the look kills, it seeks him out, and it startles like a frightened animal. Parimelazhagar sums this up as pleasure and pain given together, which sets up the double look of 1091.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1086":{"book":"kural","num":1086,"t":["கொடும்புருவம் கோடா மறைப்பின் நடுங்கஞர்","செய்யல மன்இவள் கண்"],"p":"Her eyes will cause (me) no trembling sorrow, if they are properly hidden by her cruel arched eye-brows.","r":"The man says that if her curved brows would only stop bending and screen her eyes, those eyes could not cause him this trembling pain.","n":"The conceit turns on the word கோடா, 'unbending'. Parimelazhagar treats the brows as the eyes' close friends, who could restrain them only if they were straight themselves, because anyone who rebukes a friend must be upright. Manakkudavar reads a small scene into it: she has lowered her head and her brows now shade her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1087":{"book":"kural","num":1087,"t":["கடாஅக் களிற்றின்மேற் கட்படாம் மாதர்","படாஅ முலைமேல் துகில்"],"p":"The cloth that covers the firm bosom of this maiden is (like) that which covers the eyes of a rutting elephant.","r":"The man says the cloth over her firm breasts is like the face-cloth over the eyes of a rutting elephant.","n":"A covering usually suggests safety, but this one announces danger. Parimelazhagar makes modesty the reason for the cloth, while the man, seeing strength through it, thinks of what it holds back. Manakkudavar has the man address her directly as மாதரே, while Parimelazhagar has him speak about her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anangu","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1088":{"book":"kural","num":1088,"t":["ஒண்ணுதற் கோஒ உடைந்ததே ஞாட்பினுள்","நண்ணாரும் உட்குமென் பீடு"],"p":"On her bright brow alone is destroyed even th at power of mine that used to terrify the most fearless foes in the battlefield.","r":"The man laments that his might, feared in battle even by enemies who never met him there, has broken before her bright forehead alone.","n":"The ஓ marks wonder at the mismatch: so great a strength and so small a thing as a forehead. Parimelazhagar is careful to note that this isn't boasting, because the man is grieving for what he has lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1089":{"book":"kural","num":1089,"t":["பிணையேர் மடநோக்கும் நாணும் உடையாட்கு","அணியெவனோ ஏதில தந்து"],"p":"Of what use are other jewels to her who is adorned with modesty, and the meek looks of a hind ?","r":"The man asks what use foreign ornaments are to one who already has a doe's shy look and modesty.","n":"It sounds like a pure compliment, but both older commentators hear a complaint. For Parimelazhagar, whoever adorned her lacks sense, because the jewels burden her and harm him; for Manakkudavar, he praises her while seeming to accuse her of cruelty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kan","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1090":{"book":"kural","num":1090,"t":["உண்டார்கண் அல்லது அடுநறாக் காமம்போல்","கண்டார் மகிழ்செய்தல் இன்று"],"p":"Unlike boiled honey which yields delight only when it is drunk, love gives pleasure even when looked at.","r":"Brewed liquor delights only those who drink it, not those who merely see it as love does; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies an unspoken close: love is better than liquor, yet he hasn't obtained it because he hasn't yet read her intent. He also takes காமம் here to mean the person in whom love is enjoyed.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks as he sets out to read her intent.","தலைமகள் குறிப்பறிதல் உற்றான் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman thinks this, pained after seeing the man, which reverses the chapter's point of view.","இது தலைமகள் தலைமகனைக்கண்டுழி வருத்தமுற்றுக் கருதியது."]],"ch":"k:109"},"k:1091":{"book":"kural","num":1091,"t":["இருநோக்கு இவளுண்கண் உள்ளது ஒருநோக்கு","நோய்நோக்கொன் றந்நோய் மருந்து"],"p":"There are two looks in the dyed eyes of this (fair one); one causes pain, and the other is the cure thereof.","r":"The man sees two looks in her kohl-lined eyes: one gives him the sickness and the other is its cure.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The painful look discloses desire in her mind and the healing look shows her love; he rejects calling the first a neutral look, since one-sided pain wouldn't be mutual love.","நோய் செய்யும் நோக்கு அவள் மனத்தனாய காமக்குறிப்பினை வெளிப்படுத்துகின்ற நோக்கு."],["manakkudavar","The two looks are successive: the first look that hurt him, then a later one mixed with shyness and concern for his pain.","நோய்நோக்கென்பது முதல் நோக்கின நோக்கம்"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1092":{"book":"kural","num":1092,"t":["கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம் காமத்தில்","செம்பாகம் அன்று பெரிது"],"p":"A single stolen glance of her eyes is more than half the pleasure (of sexual embrace).","r":"The man says the brief look her eyes steal is not half of love's fulfilment but more than half.","n":"The arithmetic is odd until you see why it works. Parimelazhagar says the glance guarantees that union will follow, so its value lies in what it promises. Stealing also suits the setting: this is secret love, and the eyes are the first thieves.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kalaviyal","kaamam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1093":{"book":"kural","num":1093,"t":["நோக்கினாள் நோக்கி இறைஞ்சினாள் அஃதவள்","யாப்பினுள் அட்டிய நீர்"],"p":"She has looked (at men) and stooped (her head); and that (sign) waters as it were (the corn of) our love.","r":"The man says she looked, and having looked lowered her head, and that gesture was water poured into the bond between them.","n":"The verse says only யாப்பு, a binding, and water poured into it. The growing crop of love that makes the image cohere comes from the commentators, and Parimelazhagar notes that the metaphor is only partly stated. Lowering her head is what waters the bond, so modesty here works for desire and not against it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","naanam","kurippu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1094":{"book":"kural","num":1094,"t":["யான்நோக்கும் காலை நிலன்நோக்கும் நோக்காக்கால்","தான்நோக்கி மெல்ல நகும்"],"p":"When I look, she looks down; when I do not, she looks and smiles gently.","r":"The man observes that when he looks she looks at the ground, and when he doesn't she looks at him and smiles softly.","n":"The whole verse is timing. Their eyes never meet, and that very avoidance tells him she is watching. Parimelazhagar takes மெல்ல நகும் as rejoicing inwardly without showing it, and mentions that others read it as a visible smile.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","naanam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1095":{"book":"kural","num":1095,"t":["குறிக்கொண்டு நோக்காமை அல்லால் ஒருகண்","சிறக்கணித்தாள் போல நகும்"],"p":"She not only avoids a direct look at me, but looks as it were with a half-closed eye and smiles.","r":"The man says that she doesn't look at him directly, but smiles as though narrowing one eye.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She narrows one eye as she glances; the 'as though' is there because even this isn't openly done.","சிறக்கணித்தல்: சுருங்குதல்."],["manakkudavar","He reads the line as 'at one moment', and the verb as agreeing: she smiles as one who has consented.","ஒருகால் உடம்பட்டாள் போல"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1096":{"book":"kural","num":1096,"t":["உறாஅ தவர்போல் சொலினும் செறாஅர்சொல்","ஒல்லை உணரப் படும்"],"p":"Though they may speak harshly as if they were strangers, the words of the friendly are soon understood.","r":"The man reflects that the words of those without real anger are quickly seen through, even when they speak like strangers.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The harsh speaker is the friend, who is sending him away while really seeking to help; he says this to himself.","தோழி சேண்படுத்தவழி அவள் குறிப்பு அறிந்த தலைமகன் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The case is one where the body shows agreement and speech refuses, which is taken as consent.","உறுப்பினாலிசைவுகாட்டி, உரையினால் மறுப்பினும் உடன்படுதலா மென்றது."],["kalaignar","The harsh speaker is the woman herself, hiding love under a stranger's manner.","அவள் அகத்தில் கோபமின்றி அன்பு கொண்டிருப்பது"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1097":{"book":"kural","num":1097,"t":["செறாஅச் சிறுசொல்லும் செற்றார்போல் நோக்கும்","உறாஅர்போன்று உற்றார் குறிப்பு"],"p":"Little words that are harsh and looks that are hateful are (but) the expressions of lovers who wish to act like strangers.","r":"The man reasons that small harsh words without anger and looks like an enemy's are the signs of those who are close while seeming distant.","n":"1096 and 1097 give him a rule for treating refusal as disguise, and the verses rest it entirely on செறாஅ, the absence of real anger. In my reading that condition is doing all the work, and it is exactly what the man can't verify from outside. Parimelazhagar's conclusion is that he needn't fear such signs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1098":{"book":"kural","num":1098,"t":["அசையியற்கு உண்டாண்டோ ர் ஏஎர்யான் நோக்கப்","பசையினள் பைய நகும்"],"p":"When I look, the pitying maid looks in return and smiles gently; and that is a comforting sign for me.","r":"The man says that when he looks, she softens and smiles gently, and there is something special about the swaying woman at that moment.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","ஏர் here is the good sign that shows in her smile, a promise that won't fail him after his pleading look.","தோன்றுகின்றதோர் நன்மைக் குறிப்பு உண்டு."],["manakkudavar","ஏர் is beauty, the quality that surpasses her form at that moment.","அழகு - தன்வடிவினுள் மிக்க குணம்"]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1099":{"book":"kural","num":1099,"t":["ஏதிலார் போலப் பொதுநோக்கு நோக்குதல்","காதலார் கண்ணே உள"],"p":"Both the lovers are capable of looking at each other in an ordinary way, as if they were perfect strangers.","r":"Looking at each other with a stranger's common glance is found only in lovers; Parimelazhagar gives this to the friend, who is working out the affair, while the other commentators name no speaker.","n":"A public pretence of indifference becomes the evidence. The couple's disguise gives them away to the one person placed to notice, so this is close observation from outside the romance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","kalaviyal","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1100":{"book":"kural","num":1100,"t":["கண்ணொடு கண்இணை நோக்கொக்கின் வாய்ச்சொற்கள்","என்ன பயனும் இல"],"p":"The words of the mouths are of no use whatever, when there is perfect agreement between the eyes (of lovers).","r":"Once eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words have no use at all; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the man for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes the claim very concrete: the useless words are the couple's cover stories, hers about guarding the millet field and his about hunting. Out of context it reads as a maxim about wordless love.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kurippu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend, having heard both, speaks in general terms about what she has noticed.","இருவர் சொல்லும் கேட்டு உலகியல்மேல் வைத்துக் கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The man, about to approach her, speaks to his own heart.","இது சார்தலுறுகின்ற தலைமகன் தன்னெஞ்சிற்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:110"},"k:1101":{"book":"kural","num":1101,"t":["கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும் ஐம்புலனும்","ஒண்தொடி கண்ணே உள"],"p":"The (simultaneous) enjoyment of the five senses of sight, hearing, taste, smell and touch can only be found with bright braceleted (women).","r":"The man says that all five senses, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching, find their objects in this one bright-braceleted woman.","n":"The claim is about concentration. Parimelazhagar's gloss is that pleasures usually taken from different things at different times are here had at once, from one person. He also folds in the erotic techniques named in Sanskrit treatises, which the verse doesn't mention.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1102":{"book":"kural","num":1102,"t":["பிணிக்கு மருந்து பிறமன் அணியிழை","தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து"],"p":"The remedy for a disease is always something different (from it); but for the disease caused by this jewelled maid, she is herself the cure.","r":"The man says the cure for a disease is usually something other than its cause, but this jewelled woman is herself the cure for the sickness she brings.","n":"Parimelazhagar sharpens பிற: ordinary medicine is opposite in nature to the disease's cause. Love therefore breaks a rule of medicine, not only of common sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1103":{"book":"kural","num":1103,"t":["தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள் துயிலின் இனிதுகொல்","தாமரைக் கண்ணான் உலகு"],"p":"Can the lotus-eyed Vishnu's heaven be indeed as sweet to those who delight to sleep in the delicate arms of their beloved ?","r":"The man asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one can be sweeter than sleeping on the soft arms of the one you love.","n":"The question is left open, and the commentators answer it for him. Parimelazhagar has the man's friend object that one fit for supreme bliss shouldn't sink to this small pleasure, and takes the man's point to be that heaven requires hard austerities which this sleep does not.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The world is Vishnu's, reached by ascetics who renounce the senses; he rejects the Indra reading on the ground that lotus-eyed isn't Indra's name.","தாமரைக்கண்ணான் என்பது அவனுக்குப் பெயரன்மையின், அஃது உரையன்மையறிக."],["manakkudavar","The world is Indra's heaven, and the point is that heaven's pleasure is simply this.","இந்திரனது சுவர்க்கம்"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1104":{"book":"kural","num":1104,"t":["நீங்கின் தெறூஉம் குறுகுங்கால் தண்ணென்னும்","தீயாண்டுப் பெற்றாள் இவள்?"],"p":"From whence has she got this fire that burns when I withdraw and cools when I approach ?","r":"The man asks where she found this fire that burns when he moves away and cools when he comes near.","n":"Parimelazhagar observes a small sleight: the fire is the man's own desire, which he attributes to her because she is what draws it out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1105":{"book":"kural","num":1105,"t":["வேட் ட பொழுதின் அவையவை போலுமே","தோட் டார் கதுப்பினாள் தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders of her whose locks are adorned with flowers delight me as if they were the very sweets I have desired (to get).","r":"The man says her shoulders, with flowers in her hair, feel like each thing one craves at the moment of craving it.","n":"The point is freshness across repetition. For Parimelazhagar, they have already met several times and she still binds him as if new. Manakkudavar's text differs: where the verse has hair adorned with flowers, he has hair fallen loose on her shoulders during love, which changes the picture from adornment to its undoing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1106":{"book":"kural","num":1106,"t":["உறுதோறு உயிர்தளிர்ப்பத் தீண்டலால் பேதைக்கு","அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders of this fair one are made of ambrosia, for they revive me with pleasure every time I embrace them.","r":"The man says that since her touch makes his life put out fresh shoots each time they meet, this girl's shoulders must be made of ambrosia.","n":"Ambrosia is inferred from an effect, in the manner of a logical argument. உயிர்தளிர்ப்ப also makes his life a plant, withering between meetings, which Manakkudavar pushes further: without her that life would die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1107":{"book":"kural","num":1107,"t":["தம்மில் இருந்து தமதுபாத்து உண்டற்றால்","அம்மா அரிவை முயக்கு"],"p":"The embraces of a gold-complexioned beautiful female are as pleasant as to dwell in one's own house and live by one's own (earnings) after distributing (a portion of it in charity).","r":"The man says the embrace of this lovely dark-gold woman is like living in your own home and eating your own food after sharing it.","n":"A pleasure is measured by a virtue. The comparison brings the whole ethic of the householder into the bedroom, as a kind of satisfaction that has nothing to hide or to owe.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["punarchi","aram"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Her friend has urged marriage, and he declines: this secret union already gives him the reward of the householder's life.","'இல்லறஞ்செய்தார் எய்தும் துறக்கத்து இன்பம் எனக்கு இப்புணர்ச்சியே தரும்' என வரைவு உடன்படான் கூறியவாறாயிற்று."],["kalaignar","The woman embraced is already his wife, so the verse describes married pleasure.","தனது அழகிய காதல் மனைவியைத் தழுவுகின்ற இன்பத்துக்கு"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1108":{"book":"kural","num":1108,"t":["வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே வளியிடை","போழப் படாஅ முயக்கு"],"p":"To ardent lovers sweet is the embrace that cannot be penetrated even by a breath of breeze.","r":"The man says an embrace so close that not even a breeze can part it is sweet to two who desire each other.","n":"Parimelazhagar finds an evasion in the word இருவர், 'two'. The friend has urged the uninterrupted union of marriage, and he answers that this holds for two people, whereas they are not two. Manakkudavar sees only the praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He agrees in form but declines the marriage she urges, because here there aren't two.","'ஈண்டு இருவர் இல்லை இன்மையான், இஃது ஒவ்வாது' என்பது கருத்து."]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1109":{"book":"kural","num":1109,"t":["ஊடல் உணர்தல் புணர்தல் இவைகாமம்","கூடியார் பெற்ற பயன்"],"p":"Love quarrel, reconciliation and intercourse - these are the advantages reaped by those who marry for lust.","r":"The man names quarrelling, making up and uniting as the rewards of those who have come together in love.","n":"The verse puts the quarrel inside love's rewards, and first in the list. That is the seed of the book's final chapter.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a dismissal: quarrels belong to married men who stray to courtesans, and a couple as one as they are wants no such 'reward'.","அப்பயன் இருதலைப் புள்ளிள் ஓருயிராய் உழுவலன்புடைய எமக்கு வேண்டா"],["manakkudavar","This is delight: the man has just ended her sulk and united with her, and celebrates the sequence.","ஊடற் குறிப்புத் தோன்ற நின்ற தலைமகளை ஊடல் தீர்த்துப் புணர்ந்த தலைமகன்"]],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1110":{"book":"kural","num":1110,"t":["அறிதோறு அறியாமை கண்டற்றால் காமம்","செறிதோறும் சேயிழை மாட்டு"],"p":"As (one's) ignorance is discovered the more one learns, so does repeated intercourse with a well-adorned female (only create a desire for more).","r":"The man says that closeness to this red-jewelled woman works like knowledge: each advance shows how much was lacking before.","n":"Parimelazhagar closes the chapter by noting that the woman feels this joy too, but it shows in her only through signs and never in speech. Nine verses of male voice therefore rest on a convention, and he says so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","arivu","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:111"},"k:1111":{"book":"kural","num":1111,"t":["நன்னீரை வாழி அனிச்சமே நின்னினும்","மென்னீரள் யாம்வீழ் பவள்"],"p":"May you flourish, O Anicham! you have a delicate nature. But my beloved is more delicate than you.","r":"The man addresses the anicham flower, granting it delicacy and telling it that the woman he loves is more delicate still.","n":"வாழி sounds like a blessing, but for Parimelazhagar it is a polite way to end the flower's pride: stop thinking you are the softest. He also says the man has just touched her, so the praise comes from touch and not sight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1112":{"book":"kural","num":1112,"t":["மலர்காணின் மையாத்தி நெஞ்சே இவள்கண்","பலர்காணும் பூவொக்கும் என்று"],"p":"O my soul, fancying that flowers which are seen by many can resemble her eyes, you become confused at the sight of them.","r":"The man scolds his heart for reeling whenever it sees flowers, supposing that her eyes resemble what anyone can look at.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kan","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The flowers don't match her eyes, and the heart is rebuked for its false comparison now that he alone has seen them.","அம்மலர்கள் ஒவ்வாமை கண்டு, ஒப்புமை கருதிய நெஞ்சை இகழ்ந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","Her eyes match flowers in colour but not in quality.","கண் பூவினது நிறமொக்குமாயினும்"],["kalaignar","The verse is direct praise: her eyes are indeed a flower that many see and admire.","பலரும் கண்டு வியக்கும் மலராகவே திகழ்கிறது."]],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1113":{"book":"kural","num":1113,"t":["முறிமேனி முத்தம் முறுவல் வெறிநாற்றம்","வேலுண்கண் வேய்த்தோ ளவட்கு"],"p":"The complexion of this bamboo-shouldered one is that of a shoot; her teeth, are pearls; her breath, fragrance; and her dyed eyes, lances.","r":"The man describes her: bamboo shoulders, a body like young shoots, pearl teeth, fragrance, and kohl-lined eyes that are spears.","n":"Parimelazhagar offers a witty setting as an alternative: her friend claims not to know whom he means, so this list is a description for identifying her. Everything in it is soft except the eyes, the one weapon among the delights, as in chapter 109.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1114":{"book":"kural","num":1114,"t":["காணின் குவளை கவிழ்ந்து நிலன்நோக்கும்","மாணிழை கண்ணொவ்வேம் என்று"],"p":"If the blue lotus could see, it would stoop and look at the ground saying, \"I can never resemble the eyes of this excellent jewelled one.\"","r":"The man says that if blue water-lilies could see, they would bow their heads and look at the ground, saying they can't match the eyes of this finely jewelled woman.","n":"The lilies would take the posture of a modest woman, which is the very gesture she makes in 1094. Parimelazhagar draws out the unstated corollary: lacking sight and shame, real lilies stand proudly staring at the sky.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","naanam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1115":{"book":"kural","num":1115,"t":["அனிச்சப்பூக் கால்களையாள் பெய்தாள் நுகப்பிற்கு","நல்ல படாஅ பறை"],"p":"No merry drums will be beaten for the (tender) waist of her who has adorned herself with the anicham without having removed its stem.","r":"The man says that she has worn anicham flowers without removing their stems, so no good drums will sound for her waist.","n":"This is a mock funeral. As Parimelazhagar spells it out, the stems' weight will snap her waist, and then only the drum for the dead will sound. Praise by imagined catastrophe is one of the book's boldest jokes. He glosses கால் as the flower's bud or stalk end, and Manakkudavar as its stems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1116":{"book":"kural","num":1116,"t":["மதியும் மடந்தை முகனும் அறியா","பதியின் கலங்கிய மீன்"],"p":"The stars have become confused in their places not being able to distinguish between the moon and the maid's countenance.","r":"The man says the stars have strayed from their places, unable to tell the moon from the young woman's face.","n":"This gives a fanciful explanation for why stars move at all: they are lost. Manakkudavar says so directly, that the poet presents their motion as confusion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1117":{"book":"kural","num":1117,"t":["அறுவாய் நிறைந்த அவிர்மதிக்குப் போல","மறுவுண்டோ மாதர் முகத்து"],"p":"Could there be spots in the face of this maid like those in the bright full moon ?","r":"The man asks whether a woman's face has spots, as the bright moon does when it fills out its lost parts.","n":"This undoes the previous verse. Waning and spots should have told the stars the difference, so Parimelazhagar hears mockery of the stars: they had every means of knowing, and still failed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1118":{"book":"kural","num":1118,"t":["மாதர் முகம்போல் ஒளிவிட வல்லையேல்","காதலை வாழி மதி"],"p":"If you can indeed shine like the face of women, flourish, O moon, for then would you be worth loving ?","r":"The man tells the moon that if it could shine as her face does, it too could be loved.","n":"வாழி varies in force: Parimelazhagar calls it agreement when spoken to the flower in 1111 and contempt here, because the moon's spots make the condition impossible. Manakkudavar treats it as an empty particle. The tone of the verse depends on that small word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1119":{"book":"kural","num":1119,"t":["மலரன்ன கண்ணாள் முகமொத்தி யாயின்","பலர்காணத் தோன்றல் மதி"],"p":"O moon, if you wish to resemble the face of her whose eyes are like (these) flowers, do not appear so as to be seen by all.","r":"The man tells the moon that if it wants to resemble the face of the flower-eyed woman, it must not appear where many can see it.","n":"The criterion has moved from brightness to privacy. For Parimelazhagar, what disqualifies the moon is that it shows itself to everyone, while he alone has fully enjoyed her face. Praise here shades into possession.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1120":{"book":"kural","num":1120,"t":["அனிச்சமும் அன்னத்தின் தூவியும் மாதர்","அடிக்கு நெருஞ்சிப் பழம்"],"p":"The anicham and the feathers of the swan are to the feet of females, like the fruit of the (thorny) Nerunji.","r":"The man says anicham flowers and swan's down are to her feet what the nerunji fruit is.","n":"The verse says நெருஞ்சிப் பழம், the fruit, and Kalaignar and Pope turn it into a thorn. Parimelazhagar explains the choice of fruit, since it is hard at the tip.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","This is a covert refusal: her friend has proposed elopement, and feet like these couldn't cross the wilderness.","உடன் போக்கு உரைத்த தோழிக்கு அதனது அருமை கூறி மறுத்தது."],["manakkudavar","It is plain praise: her feet are softer than these.","இஃது அவையிற்றினும் மெல்லியது அடியென்று கூறிற்று."]],"ch":"k:112"},"k:1121":{"book":"kural","num":1121,"t":["பாலொடு தேன்கலந் தற்றே பணிமொழி","வாலெயிறு ஊறிய நீர்"],"p":"The water which oozes from the white teeth of this soft speeched damsel is like a mixture of milk and honey.","r":"The man says the moisture at the white teeth of this soft-spoken woman is like milk mixed with honey.","n":"Parimelazhagar notices why the comparison is a mixture and not one sweet thing: milk and honey each have a known taste, while their blend has a sweetness you can't name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1122":{"book":"kural","num":1122,"t":["உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை என்னமற் றன்ன","மடந்தையொடு எம்மிடை நட்பு"],"p":"The love between me and this damsel is like the union of body and soul.","r":"The man says the bond between him and this girl is what the bond between body and life is.","n":"நட்பு, the word for friendship, is what the verse uses. Parimelazhagar's reading is that these are words for her: she is privately wondering who he is, whether he loves her and whether they will meet again, and he answers what he reads in her face. He files the verse under fear of parting, and Manakkudavar agrees: I can't leave you.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","uyir","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1123":{"book":"kural","num":1123,"t":["கருமணியிற் பாவாய்நீ போதாயாம் வீழும்","திருநுதற்கு இல்லை இடம்"],"p":"O you image in the pupil (of my eye)! depart; there is no room for (my) fair-browed beloved.","r":"The man tells the image in the pupil of his eye to leave, because there is no room there for the fair-browed woman he loves.","n":"The conceit depends on the Tamil word for the pupil's reflection, பாவை, a little figure living inside the eye. Parimelazhagar sets this at the moment she is leaving him: since he can't bear not seeing her, she must move in.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1124":{"book":"kural","num":1124,"t":["வாழ்தல் உயிர்க்கன்னள் ஆயிழை சாதல்","அதற்கன்னள் நீங்கும் இடத்து"],"p":"My fair-jewelled one resembles the living soul (when she is in union with me), the dying soul when she leaves me.","r":"The man says the finely jewelled woman is to his life what living is when they are together, and what dying is when she goes.","n":"She isn't compared to life and death as things, but to what happens to a life, its staying in the body and its leaving. Parimelazhagar notes that the ideas of 'for me' and 'in union' have to be supplied, since the verse says only 'when she leaves'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1125":{"book":"kural","num":1125,"t":["உள்ளுவன் மன்யான் மறப்பின் மறப்பறியேன்","ஒள்ளமர்க் கண்ணாள் குணம்"],"p":"If I had forgotten her who has bright battling eyes, I would have remembered (thee); but I never forget her. (Thus says he to her maid).","r":"The man, answering her friend's question whether he remembers them, says he would recall the qualities of the bright-eyed woman if he ever forgot them, but he doesn't know how to forget.","n":"The answer is a logical joke that turns the friend's question over: remembering presupposes forgetting, so no, he never thinks of her. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both mark this as the end of the man's speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1126":{"book":"kural","num":1126,"t":["கண்ணுள்ளின் போகார் இமைப்பின் பருகுவரா","நுண்ணியர்எம் காத லவர்"],"p":"My lover would not depart from mine eyes; even if I wink, he would not suffer (from pain); he is so ethereal.","r":"The woman says her lover won't leave her eyes and isn't hurt even if she blinks, so subtle is he.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives this a strategic setting: she fears her friend will blame the absent man, and speaks to herself so that the friend will overhear. Her defence of him is that he hasn't gone anywhere. The invisible subtle body sounds like metaphysics, but he explains it as ceaseless thinking grown so ripe that the man is always before her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1127":{"book":"kural","num":1127,"t":["கண்ணுள்ளார் காத லவராகக் கண்ணும்","எழுதேம் கரப்பாக்கு அறிந்து"],"p":"As my lover abides in my eyes, I will not even paint them, for he would (then) have to conceal himself.","r":"The woman says that since her lover lives in her eyes, she doesn't even line them with kohl, knowing he would be hidden.","n":"A real sign of separation, an unadorned face, is given a loving explanation. Manakkudavar adds a second one: she is so busy watching him within that she has no time to dress.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","ninaivu","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1128":{"book":"kural","num":1128,"t":["நெஞ்சத்தார் காத லவராக வெய்துண்டல்","அஞ்சுதும் வேபாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"As my lover is in my heart, I am afraid of eating (anything) hot, for I know it would pain him.","r":"The woman says that since her lover lives in her heart, she is afraid to eat hot food, knowing he would be burned.","n":"Manakkudavar supplies the occasion: her friend has asked why she isn't eating. Loss of appetite is one of the visible symptoms, and this is a graceful evasion of the question.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1129":{"book":"kural","num":1129,"t":["இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல் அனைத்திற்கே","ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்"],"p":"I will not wink, knowing that if I did, my lover would hide himself; and for this reason, this town says, he is unloving.","r":"The woman says she won't blink, knowing he would be hidden, and for this much the town calls him loveless.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a quarrel behind 'this town': the friend has criticised the man to help her endure, and she, hurt, refuses to name the friend, calling her the town. Sleeplessness, the very evidence against him, she presents as her own act of care.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1130":{"book":"kural","num":1130,"t":["உவந்துறைவர் உள்ளத்துள் என்றும் இகந்துறைவர்","ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்"],"p":"My lover dwells in my heart with perpetual delight; but the town says he is unloving and (therefore) dwells afar.","r":"The woman says he lives joyfully in her heart always, while this town says he is loveless and lives apart.","n":"The last words are identical to 1129's, and the refrain suggests how worn out she is by this argument. Whether her certainty is comfort or denial, the verse doesn't say.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:113"},"k:1131":{"book":"kural","num":1131,"t":["காமம் உழந்து வருந்தினார்க்கு ஏமம்","மடலல்லது இல்லை வலி"],"p":"To those who after enjoyment of sexual pleasure suffer (for want of more), there is no help so efficient as the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says that for those who suffer in love there is no safeguard and no strength but the palmyra horse.","n":"ஏமம, 'safeguard', is a surprising word for a public humiliation. The safety he means is pressure: Parimelazhagar explains that the man has given up on the friend's help and intends to get his way as men of old did.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உழந்து assumes that he has already enjoyed love and has now lost access to it.","அரியராய மகளிரோடு காமத்தை அனுபவித்துப் பின் அது பெறாது துன்புற்ற ஆடவர்க்கு"],["manakkudavar","The word means only striving and suffering because of love, and says nothing of past union.","காமம் காரணமாக முயன்று வருந்தினார்க்கு"]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1132":{"book":"kural","num":1132,"t":["நோனா உடம்பும் உயிரும் மடலேறும்","நாணினை நீக்கி நிறுத்து"],"p":"Having got rid of shame, the suffering body and soul save themselves on the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says his body and life, unable to bear it any longer, will mount the palmyra horse, once shame has been put aside.","n":"He says body and life will do this, and not 'I', as though his person had dissolved into what still suffers. Parimelazhagar adds that with the threat, the man is really looking for the friend to see his desperation and grant his plea quickly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","naanam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1133":{"book":"kural","num":1133,"t":["நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன் இன்றுடையேன்","காமுற்றார் ஏறும் மடல்"],"p":"Modesty and manliness were once my own; now, my own is the palmyra horse that is ridden by the lustful.","r":"The man says he once had shame and sound manliness, and today he has the palmyra horse that lovers ride.","n":"The form is an inventory of property, then and now. Parimelazhagar defines the two lost goods: shame keeps one from base acts, and manliness is not giving way to anything. Notably, it is the man who claims shame here; நாண் in the Kural isn't only a woman's virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","aanmai","madal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1134":{"book":"kural","num":1134,"t":["காமக் கடும்புனல் உய்க்கும் நாணொடு","நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை"],"p":"The raft of modesty and manliness, is, alas, carried-off by the strong current of lust.","r":"The man says that the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and manliness.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a neat debating context: she objects that shame and manliness are rafts against the flood, and so can't be lost. He concedes that they are rafts, and answers that this flood takes the raft.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1135":{"book":"kural","num":1135,"t":["தொடலைக் குறுந்தொடி தந்தாள் மடலொடு","மாலை உழக்கும் துயர்"],"p":"Has brought me 'horse of palm,' and pangs of eventide!","r":"The man says that the girl with bangles like a garland has given him the palmyra horse and the anguish of evening.","n":"Parimelazhagar's explanatory note gives the point: love exists at all times but flowers at evening, and the very name for her implies a young girl, who only echoes her friend. So the man tells the friend that what the girl gave, the friend can take away.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1136":{"book":"kural","num":1136,"t":["மடலூர்தல் யாமத்தும் உள்ளுவேன் மன்ற","படல்ஒல்லா பேதைக்கென் கண்"],"p":"Mine eyes will not close in sleep on your mistress's account; even at midnight will I think of mounting the palmyra horse.","r":"The man says that because of that simple girl his eyes can't close, so even at midnight he will surely think of mounting the palmyra horse.","n":"Parimelazhagar stresses that பேதை here refers to her foolishness and not her age. Manakkudavar has the friend teasing that he'll forget by morning, and his answer turns his insomnia into a guarantee.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["madal","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1137":{"book":"kural","num":1137,"t":["கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும் மடலேறாப்","பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க தில்"],"p":"There is nothing so noble as the womanly nature that would not ride the palmyra horse, though plunged a sea of lust.","r":"Nothing has greater worth than womanhood, which suffers a sea of desire and doesn't mount the palmyra horse; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"The praise also rests on a restriction: she may not take the only remedy he has been boasting about. Who says it changes its force, since from him it is an excuse, and from the friend it is a demand.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["madal","kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man answers the friend's taunt that men ought to endure better: it is the birth of women that has this restraint, and he lacks it.","'பிறப்பு விசேடத்தால் அவ்வடக்கம் எனக்கு இல்லையாகா நின்றது; நீ அஃது அறிகின்றிலை,' என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man that his beloved suffers just as much, and that women's lot is simply not to ride, to calm his distress.","தலைமகன் ஆற் றாமை நீங்குதற்பொருட்டுத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1138":{"book":"kural","num":1138,"t":["நிறையரியர் மன்அளியர் என்னாது காமம்","மறையிறந்து மன்று படும்"],"p":"Even the Lust (of women) transgresses its secrecy and appears in public, forgetting that they are too chaste and liberal (to be overcome by it).","r":"Love doesn't stop to think that they are firm in restraint or deserving of pity, but breaks through secrecy and comes out in public; the speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a hint under the generalisation: she seems to talk about all women, but means her own love has grown, and that the matter must now be disclosed to the family.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nirai","alar","kaamam","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman speaks, her guarded love having grown; desire isn't afraid of women's firmness, நிறை அரியர்.","'இவர் நிறையால் நாம் மீதூர்தற்கு அரியர்' என்று அஞ்சுதல் செய்யாது"],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks to refuse a daytime meeting because of scandal, and the woman lacks restraint.","இஃது அம்பலம் அலரும் ஆகாவென்று தோழி பகற்குறி மறுத்தது."]],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1139":{"book":"kural","num":1139,"t":["அறிகிலார் எல்லாரும் என்றேஎன் காமம்","மறுகின் மறுகும் மருண்டு"],"p":"My lust, feeling that it is not known by all, reels confused in the streets (of this town).","r":"The woman says that her love, thinking that not everyone knows, wanders bewildered round the streets.","n":"Desire is made a separate creature, resentful of her former self-control. She can't ride the horse, so, in effect, her love goes out into the street without her. Parimelazhagar assigns the two verbs to two stages of gossip, அம்பல் and அலர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1140":{"book":"kural","num":1140,"t":["யாம்கண்ணின் காண நகுப அறிவில்லார்","யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா ஆறு"],"p":"Even strangers laugh (at us) so as to be seen by us, for they have not suffered.","r":"The woman says fools laugh within her sight because they have not suffered as she has.","n":"Parimelazhagar names the unnamed fools: it is her friend, who has told her family and now teases her. He also reports, and rejects, a reading in which the man says this to the friend, because a man's speech wouldn't fit here at this point in the chapter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:114"},"k:1141":{"book":"kural","num":1141,"t":["அலரெழ ஆருயிர் நிற்கும் அதனைப்","பலரறியார் பாக்கியத் தால்"],"p":"My precious life is saved by the raise of rumour, and this, to my good luck no others are aware of.","r":"A precious life holds on because the gossip has risen, and by good fortune the many don't know this; for Parimelazhagar the man speaks of his own life, while Manakkudavar speaks of her life and leaves the speaker unnamed.","n":"The joke rests on the second line: if the gossips knew they were helping, they would stop. Commentators differ on the source of luck. Parimelazhagar says தெய்வத்தால் and Manakkudavar says புண்ணியத்தாலே; one is divine and the other is merit, for a verse whose word is the Sanskrit-derived பாக்கியம்.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","uyir"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, unable to reach her, is sustained by talk that makes her seem attainable.","அங்ஙனம் அரியாளை எளியளாக்கி எடுக்கின்றமையின், அஃது அவ்வாருயிர்க்குப் பற்றுக்கோடாக நின்றது"],["manakkudavar","It is her life that the gossip sustains.","அவளது ஆருயிர் நிற்கும்"],["kalaignar","It is the couple's life, which survives on hope that talk will help their love succeed.","எம் உயிர் போகாமல் இருக்கிறது"]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1142":{"book":"kural","num":1142,"t":["மலரன்ன கண்ணாள் அருமை அறியாது","அலரெமக்கு ஈந்ததிவ் வூர்"],"p":"Not knowing the value of her whose eyes are like flowers this town has got up a rumour about me.","r":"The man says this town, not knowing how rare the flower-eyed woman is, has made him a present of gossip.","n":"ஈந்தது is the verb for a generous gift. Parimelazhagar takes it that way, as an ironic thanks. The rarity differs by commentator: for Parimelazhagar, she is hard to reach, and for Manakkudavar, it is her noble birth that the gossips ignore.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1143":{"book":"kural","num":1143,"t":["உறாஅதோ ஊரறிந்த கெளவை அதனைப்","பெறாஅது பெற்றன்ன நீர்த்து"],"p":"Will I not get a rumour that is known to the (whole) town ? For what I have not got is as if I had got it (already).","r":"The man asks whether the gossip the whole town knows isn't his to have, since it feels like having what he hasn't yet obtained.","n":"The gossip isn't true yet, and that is its use: being talked about as a couple gives a foretaste of being one. Manakkudavar gives a more practical reason, in which the man tells the friend that the talk will bring the families round.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1144":{"book":"kural","num":1144,"t":["கவ்வையால் கவ்விது காமம் அதுவின்றேல்","தவ்வென்னும் தன்மை இழந்து"],"p":"Rumour increases the violence of my passion; without it it would grow weak and waste away.","r":"The man says gossip makes his love flourish, and that without it the love would lose its nature and droop.","n":"The sound carries the argument: கவ்வை, கவ்விது, தவ்வென்னும். Parimelazhagar explains கவ்விது as a coinage from the noun, and தவ் as a word that imitates sagging. The claim is that love depends in part on being seen.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1145":{"book":"kural","num":1145,"t":["களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால் காமம்","வெளிப்படுந் தோறும் இனிது"],"p":"As drinking liquor is delightful (to one) whenever one is in mirth, so is lust delightful to me whenever it is the subject of rumour.","r":"Love grows sweeter each time it is exposed, as drinking becomes more desired each time one gets drunk; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man says that his own desire is so strong that gossip itself gives him pleasure.","வேட்கை மிகுதியால் அலரும் இன்பஞ் செய்யாநின்றது என்பதாம்."],["manakkudavar","The friend replies to the man, who has asked whether this gossip is hurting them.","நுமக்குத் துன்பமாயிற்றே இவ்வலரென்று வினாவிய தலைமகற்குத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1146":{"book":"kural","num":1146,"t":["கண்டது மன்னும் ஒருநாள் அலர்மன்னும்","திங்களைப் பாம்புகொண் டற்று"],"p":"It was but a single day that I looked on (my lover); but the rumour thereof has spread like the seizure of the moon by the serpent.","r":"The woman says she saw him on only one day, and the gossip has spread like the serpent seizing the moon.","n":"An eclipse is an event no one can miss, and a tiny private act is set against it. Parimelazhagar has her speaking to her friend with the man in earshot, so the complaint is really a demand for marriage. Kalaignar pauses to mark the serpent as an imaginary notion, which the commentators before him don't do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1147":{"book":"kural","num":1147,"t":["ஊரவர் கெளவை எருவாக அன்னைசொல்","நீராக நீளும்இந் நோய்"],"p":"This malady (of lust) is manured by the talk of women and watered by the (harsh) words of my mother.","r":"The woman says this sickness grows long, with the town's gossip as its manure and her mother's words as its water.","n":"Whatever is meant to shrink the love feeds it, as Parimelazhagar spells out. The one verse in the chapter to name her mother shows where the gossip actually hurts: at home. Parimelazhagar takes ஊரவர் to mean the women of the town.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1148":{"book":"kural","num":1148,"t":["நெய்யால் எரிநுதுப்பேம் என்றற்றால் கெளவையால்","காமம் நுதுப்பேம் எனல்"],"p":"To say that one could extinguish passion by rumour is like extinguishing fire with ghee.","r":"The woman says that to think gossip can put out love is like thinking ghee can put out fire.","n":"The grammar frames this as an error in thought, 'to say we will extinguish'. Parimelazhagar places it in the same scene as 1147, so it is also her answer to the friend's advice that she should take the talk seriously and bear up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1149":{"book":"kural","num":1149,"t":["அலர்நாண ஒல்வதோ அஞ்சலோம்பு என்றார்","பலர்நாண நீத்தக் கடை"],"p":"When the departure of him who said \"fear not\" has put me to shame before others, why need I be ashamed of scandal.","r":"The woman asks how she can be ashamed of gossip when the man who told her not to fear has left her, in a way that shames everyone who sees it.","n":"This verse is the turn in the chapter, with gossip now no longer a game. The promise 'fear not' is quoted against him. Shame has moved from what she feels about the town to what those watching feel on her behalf, so she has none left to spend on rumour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["alar","naanam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1150":{"book":"kural","num":1150,"t":["தாம்வேண்டின் நல்குவர் காதலர் யாம்வேண்டும்","கெளவை எடுக்கும்இவ் வூர்"],"p":"The rumour I desire is raised by the town (itself); and my lover would if desired consent (to my following him).","r":"The speaker, whom Parimelazhagar identifies as the friend talking to the woman while the man overhears, says the town is raising the very gossip they want, and the lover will grant it if asked or if he wishes.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["alar","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தாம் வேண்டின் means 'if we ask', and the friend is steering the man towards elopement, which the gossip now justifies.","இனிக் காதலர்தாமும்யாம் வேண்டியக்கால் அதனை இனிதின் நேர்வர்"],["kalaignar","The words mean 'if the lover also wishes', and his consent depends on his own desire.","காதலரும் விரும்பினால் அதை ஒப்புக் கொள்வார்."]],"ch":"k:115"},"k:1151":{"book":"kural","num":1151,"t":["செல்லாமை உண்டேல் எனக்குரை மற்றுநின்","வல்வரவு வாழ்வார்க் குரை"],"p":"If it is not departure, tell me; but if it is your speedy return, tell it to those who would be alive then.","r":"If you aren't leaving, tell me; if you mean to return quickly, tell that to those who will still be alive by then; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"One of the book's sharpest lines, a refusal to accept comfort. If the friend is the speaker, then Parimelazhagar's explanation for 'me' matters: she says எனக்கு for the woman because the two aren't distinct. He notes that some give the verse to the woman.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend answers the man, who has promised a quick return, and aims to stop his leaving.","'பிரிந்து கடிதின் வருவல்' என்ற தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman answers the friend, who has told her he will come back soon.","இது கடித்து வருவாரென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் ஆற்றாமையாற் கூறி யது."]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1152":{"book":"kural","num":1152,"t":["இன்கண் உடைத்தவர் பார்வல் பிரிவஞ்சும்","புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு"],"p":"His very look was once pleasing; but (now) even intercourse is painful through fear of separation.","r":"The woman says that பார்வல் alone once gave her delight, and now union itself holds the pain of fearing separation.","n":"The cruelty is that the most that love can give is spoiled by what it anticipates. Parimelazhagar cites Kalittokai for the telling sign: he now fusses over her more than before, which is how she knows he is leaving.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பார்வல் is his look back in courtship, sweet because it promised union.","அவர் நோக்கு மாத்திரமும் புணர்ச்சி குறித்தமையான் நமக்கு இன்ப முடைத்தாயிருக்கும்"],["manakkudavar","It is her own watching for his arrival, a present joy set against the anxiety of being with him.","நங்காதலரை வரவு பார்த்திருக்கு மது"]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1153":{"book":"kural","num":1153,"t":["அரிதரோ தேற்றம் அறிவுடையார் கண்ணும்","பிரிவோ ரிடத்துண்மை யான்"],"p":"As even the lover who understands (everything) may at times depart, confidence is hardly possible.","r":"The woman says trust is hard, since parting sometimes happens even with those who understand.","n":"அறிவுடையார் isn't a compliment to his learning. Parimelazhagar's gloss is that he knows his own word and knows she can't bear it, which makes his going worse. The verse shifts from this man's fault to the general difficulty of trusting anyone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1154":{"book":"kural","num":1154,"t":["அளித்தஞ்சல் என்றவர் நீப்பின் தெளித்தசொல்","தேறியார்க்கு உண்டோ தவறு"],"p":"If he who bestowed his love and said \"fear not\" should depart, will it be the fault of those who believed in (his) assuring words ?","r":"The woman asks, if the one who was gracious and told her not to fear should leave, whether any fault lies with those who believed his assuring words.","n":"She refers to herself in the third person as தேறியார், 'those who trusted', which both commentators note. This turns the private complaint into a general moral question about a promise and who answers for it. Parimelazhagar names the fault as the mismatch between his word and deed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1155":{"book":"kural","num":1155,"t":["ஓம்பின் அமைந்தார் பிரிவோம்பல் மற்றவர்","நீங்கின் அரிதால் புணர்வு"],"p":"If you would save (my life), delay the departure of my destined (husband); for if he departs, intercourse will become impossible.","r":"The woman tells her friend that if she wants to protect her, she must prevent the departure of the one who is fit for her, because once he goes, union will be hard.","n":"அமைந்தார் is a quiet word: the one who is fitting. Parimelazhagar adds divine ordering, saying he is the one fit by தெய்வம் to be her lord, and Pope follows with 'destined'. The verse itself doesn't mention destiny. He also explains why union would be hard: her life would have gone with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1156":{"book":"kural","num":1156,"t":["பிரிவுரைக்கும் வன்கண்ணர் ஆயின் அரிதவர்","நல்குவர் என்னும் நசை"],"p":"If he is so cruel as to mention his departure (to me), the hope that he would bestow (his love) must be given up.","r":"The woman says that if he can be so hard as to speak of leaving, the hope that he would come back and be gracious is pointless.","n":"The offence is the announcement, even more than the journey. For Parimelazhagar, one who could plan this while still at her side isn't likely to soften at a distance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1157":{"book":"kural","num":1157,"t":["துறைவன் துறந்தமை தூற்றாகொல் முன்கை","இறைஇறவா நின்ற வளை"],"p":"Do not the rings that begin to slide down my fingers forebode the separation of my lord ?","r":"The woman asks if the bangles slipping past her wrist don't already announce that the lord of the shore has left her.","n":"தூற்றா is the verb for spreading gossip, so her own body has joined the town. Parimelazhagar sees a rebuke to the friend, who brings the news: 'you too only do what these bangles do', when she should have stopped him. The past tense 'has left' is anticipatory, since the news alone has made her thin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1158":{"book":"kural","num":1158,"t":["இன்னாது இனன்இல்ஊர் வாழ்தல் அதனினும்","இன்னாது இனியார்ப் பிரிவு"],"p":"Painful is it to live in a friendless town; but far more painful is it to part from one's lover.","r":"The woman says living in a town without one's own people is painful, and being parted from the one who is dear is more painful still.","n":"This sounds like a proverb, but Parimelazhagar says it is aimed. Her friend agreed to his leaving instead of preventing it, and so she speaks as if she has no companion here at all, while seeming only to speak about the way of the world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1159":{"book":"kural","num":1159,"t":["தொடிற்சுடின் அல்லது காமநோய் போல","விடிற்சுடல் ஆற்றுமோ தீ"],"p":"Fire burns when touched; but, like the sickness of love, can it also burn when removed ?","r":"Fire burns only when touched, and can it burn when one moves away, as the sickness of love does? The speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman answers the friend, who has said love burns only where it is, like fire, and that she should therefore bear it.","'காமம் தீயே போன்று தான் நின்ற இடத்தைச் சுடுமாகலான் நீ ஆற்றல் வேண்டும்' என்ற தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks, on seeing the woman's distress after the man has left.","தலைமகன் பிரிந்துழித் தலைமகளாற்றாமை கண்டு தோழிகூறியது."]],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1160":{"book":"kural","num":1160,"t":["அரிதாற்றி அல்லல்நோய் நீக்கிப் பிரிவாற்றிப்","பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்"],"p":"As if there were many indeed that can consent to the impossible, kill their pain, endure separation and yet continue to live afterwards.","r":"The woman says that many agree to the impossible, remove their pain, endure the parting, and stay on alive afterwards.","n":"It is all in the tone. Taken flatly, as Pope's couplet takes it, this says many cope; read as Parimelazhagar does, 'many' means no one, and the unstated close is that she too will die. Kalaignar makes the turn explicit by adding, 'but I?'. The three verbs slow down the task of enduring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:116"},"k:1161":{"book":"kural","num":1161,"t":["மறைப்பேன்மன் யானிஃதோ நோயை இறைப்பவர்க்கு","ஊற்றுநீர் போல மிகும்"],"p":"I would hide this pain from others; but it (only) swells like a spring to those who drain it.","r":"The woman says she would hide this pain, but, as water does in a spring when one draws from it, it only rises.","n":"The commentators differ quietly over what the drawing out is. Kalaignar makes concealing itself the action, so hiding is what increases it. For Parimelazhagar, the concealment simply fails, as water wells up for one who draws from a spring.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1162":{"book":"kural","num":1162,"t":["கரத்தலும் ஆற்றேன்இந் நோயைநோய் செய்தார்க்கு","உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்"],"p":"I cannot conceal this pain, nor can I relate it without shame to him who has caused it.","r":"The woman says she cannot hide this sickness, and telling the one who caused it also brings her shame.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a remarkable reason for the shame: if she sends a messenger, it will imply she is still alive. The grief that is meant to prove love would be disproved by being reported. Her friend has offered her two options, and she refuses both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1163":{"book":"kural","num":1163,"t":["காமமும் நாணும் உயிர்காவாத் தூங்கும்என்","நோனா உடம்பின் அகத்து"],"p":"(Both) lust and shame, with my soul for their shoulder pole balance themselves on a body that cannot bear them.","r":"The woman says love and shame hang from the ends of a carrying pole, which is her life, within her body that can't bear it.","n":"The verb தூங்கும் conveys the balance: as Parimelazhagar reads it, neither side outweighs the other, so she can't commit to either, and the pole will snap. Kalaignar shifts the image so that the body is the pole; the verse says the life is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","naanam","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1164":{"book":"kural","num":1164,"t":["காமக் கடல்மன்னும் உண்டே அதுநீந்தும்","ஏமப் புணைமன்னும் இல்"],"p":"There is indeed a flood of lust; but there is no raft of safety to cross it with.","r":"The woman says the sea of love certainly exists, and the safe raft for crossing it certainly doesn't.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes the missing raft as a reproach to the friend, who should have sent word and been the raft. Manakkudavar agrees that she means she has no helper. It is more personal than a cosmic complaint.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1165":{"book":"kural","num":1165,"t":["துப்பின் எவனாவர் மன்கொல் துயர்வரவு","நட்பினுள் ஆற்று பவர்"],"p":"He who can produce sorrow from friendship, what can he not bring forth out of enmity ?","r":"The woman asks what those who can bring such grief in friendship would do in enmity.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is a rebuke to the friend, for not sending a messenger when she could have prevented the grief.","தூது விடாமை நோக்கித் தோழியோடு புலந்து சொல்லியது"],["kalaignar","It is about the lover, who gives her the grief of parting even while they are close.","நட்பாக இருக்கும்போதே பிரிவுத்துயரை நமக்குத் தரக்கூடியவர்"]],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1166":{"book":"kural","num":1166,"t":["இன்பம் கடல்மற்றுக் காமம் அஃதடுங்கால்","துன்பம் அதனிற் பெரிது"],"p":"The pleasure of lust is (as great as) the sea; but the pain of lu st is far greater.","r":"The woman says the pleasure of love is a sea, and when love afflicts her, the pain is greater than the sea.","n":"This rejects a consoling arithmetic. The friend has said that those who've had the joy should expect the pain, and the woman's point, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, is that an equal measure could be borne, but this isn't equal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1167":{"book":"kural","num":1167,"t":["காமக் கடும்புனல் நீந்திக் கரைகாணேன்","யாமத்தும் யானே உளேன்"],"p":"I have swam across the terrible flood of lust, but have not seen its shore; even at midnight I am alone; still I live.","r":"The woman says she swims the fierce flood of love and sees no shore, and even at midnight there is only herself.","n":"யானே உளேன் can mean 'I alone am here', and for Parimelazhagar also 'I am still alive', which he attributes to some evil deed's result. That idea of past deeds is his and isn't in the verse. Pope's prose follows him by adding 'still I live'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1168":{"book":"kural","num":1168,"t":["மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் துயிற்றி அளித்திரா","என்னல்லது இல்லை துணை"],"p":"The night which graciously lulls to sleep all living creatures, has me alone for her companion.","r":"The woman says the night, which puts all living beings to sleep, is to be pitied, as it has no companion but her.","n":"Everything turns on அளித்து. It is usually a word of pity or grace, and Parimelazhagar marks it as contempt: the night has foolishly chosen as its companion someone about to die. She isn't lamenting her solitude so much as mocking her only companion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1169":{"book":"kural","num":1169,"t":["> கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய இந்நாள்","நெடிய கழியும் இரா"],"p":"The long nights of these days are far more cruel than the heartless one who is torturing me.","r":"The woman says these long nights passing now are crueller than the cruelty of that cruel man.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses the implicit contrast: nights that were short when they were happy have now grown long. The word கொடியார், 'the cruel one', replaces his name.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1170":{"book":"kural","num":1170,"t":["உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின் வெள்ளநீர்","நீந்தல மன்னோஎன் கண்"],"p":"Could mine eyes travel like my thoughts to the abode (of my absent lord), they would not swim in this flood of tears.","r":"The woman says that if her eyes could go where he is as her heart does, they wouldn't be swimming in a flood of tears.","n":"For Parimelazhagar, the mind's journey is only thought, while eyes need a body to walk there, so what the heart can do isn't really a substitute for seeing. He says this verse opens the next chapter, and the eyes' complaint begins here.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:117"},"k:1171":{"book":"kural","num":1171,"t":["கண்தாம் கலுழ்வ தெவன்கொலோ தண்டாநோய்","தாம்காட்ட யாம்கண் டது"],"p":"As this incurable malady has been caused by my eyes which showed (him) to me, why should they now weep for (him).","r":"The woman asks why her eyes weep, when it was they who showed him to her, and so she came to know this unending sickness.","n":"The verse says 'we saw the sickness' with no mention of him, and the object of 'showed' has to be supplied, as Parimelazhagar says. In her account, seeing him and seeing the sickness are the same act.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1172":{"book":"kural","num":1172,"t":["தெரிந்துணரா நோக்கிய உண்கண் பரிந்துணராப்","பைதல் உழப்பது எவன்?"],"p":"The dyed eyes that (then) looked without foresight, why should they now endure sorrow, without feeling sharply (their own fault).","r":"The woman asks why these kohl-lined eyes, which looked as they did, now suffer in misery without coming to terms with it.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","arivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","தெரிந்துணரா means they looked without considering what would follow, so they should now bear what they caused.","மேல் விளைவதனை ஆராய்ந்தறியாது அன்று காதலரை நோக்கி நின்ற உண்கண்கள்"],["manakkudavar","It means the opposite: they looked after judging him good, and now they no longer recognise that goodness.","முன்பு அவர் நல்லரென்று தெரிந்து உணர்ந்து நோக்கிய உண்கண் கள்"]],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1173":{"book":"kural","num":1173,"t":["கதுமெனத் தாநோக்கித் தாமே கலுழும்","இதுநகத் தக்க துடைத்து"],"p":"They themselves looked eagerly (on him) and now they weep. Is not this to be laughed at ?","r":"The woman says it's laughable that her eyes looked at him so quickly on their own and now weep on their own.","n":"The repeated தாம் is the whole charge: they acted of their own accord both times. Manakkudavar pictures real laughter, a distracted woman laughing through her distress and being asked why, which makes this more disturbing than wit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1174":{"book":"kural","num":1174,"t":["பெயலாற்றா நீருலந்த உண்கண் உயலாற்றா","உய்வில்நோய் என்கண் நிறுத்து"],"p":"These painted eyes have caused me a lasting mortal disease; and now they can weep no more, the tears having dried up.","r":"The woman says her kohl-lined eyes, having fixed in her an inescapable, fatal sickness, have dried up and can no longer even weep.","n":"Dry eyes come after much weeping. What seems a relief is presented as the eyes' punishment: in Parimelazhagar's words, harm they did her has come back to them by itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1175":{"book":"kural","num":1175,"t":["படலாற்றா பைதல் உழக்கும் கடலாற்றாக்","காமநோய் செய்தஎன் கண்"],"p":"Mine eyes have caused me a lust that is greater than the sea and (they themselves) endure the torture of sleeplessness.","r":"The woman says her eyes, which gave her a sickness too large for the sea, now suffer torments and can't sleep.","n":"The commentators use the language of moral consequence with some wit: Parimelazhagar says அத் தீவினையால், 'by that evil deed', and Manakkudavar has harm returning to those who harm others. The verse just shows the sequence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1176":{"book":"kural","num":1176,"t":["ஓஒ இனிதே எமக்கிந்நோய் செய்தகண்","தாஅம் இதற்பட் டது"],"p":"The eyes that have given me this disease have themselves been seized with this (suffering). Oh! I am much delighted.","r":"The woman exclaims how sweet it is that the eyes that gave her this sickness have fallen into it themselves.","n":"Vengeance against one's own organ can only hurt the avenger, and that absurdity is the point. Parimelazhagar's gloss, 'as if my suffering had ended', keeps the distance between the delight and any real relief.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1177":{"book":"kural","num":1177,"t":["உழந்துழந் துள்நீர் அறுக விழைந்திழைந்து","வேண்டி அவர்க்கண்ட கண்"],"p":"The eyes that became tender and gazed intently on him, may they suffer so much as to dry up the fountain of their tears.","r":"The woman curses them: may the eyes that longed and melted and begged to see him suffer on until their inner water is gone.","n":"The verse echoes itself: உழந்துழந்து against விழைந்திழைந்து, suffering matching longing in the same rhythm. Pay-back is built into the sound.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamanoy","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1178":{"book":"kural","num":1178,"t":["பேணாது பெட்டார் உளர்மன்னோ மற்றவர்க்","காணாது அமைவில கண்"],"p":"He is indeed here who loved me with his lips but not with his heart but mine eyes suffer from not seeing him.","r":"The woman says there is one who desired without cherishing, and her eyes can't be at peace without seeing him.","n":"The syntax allows several readings, and the second one Parimelazhagar gives rests on a different text, கொண்கனை. I'm unsure which is best. The steady part is the last clause: whatever he is, the eyes can't do without him.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend says he hasn't really gone away. She replies: yes, someone who loved in words and not at heart is here, but what use is that if the eyes don't see him?","நெஞ்சால் விழையாது வைத்துச் சொல் மாத்திரத்தால் விழைந்தவர் இவ்விடத்தே உளர்"],["manakkudavar","It's a question, with no claim that he's near: does anyone desire what shouldn't be desired? He has gone without wishing to see them, and the eyes still can't settle.","விரும்பத்தகாததனை விரும்புவாரும் உளரோ?"]],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1179":{"book":"kural","num":1179,"t":["வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின்துஞ்சா ஆயிடை","ஆரஞர் உற்றன கண்"],"p":"When he is away they do not sleep; when he is present they do not sleep; in either case, mine eyes endure unbearable agony.","r":"The woman says that when he doesn't come her eyes don't sleep, and when he does they still don't sleep, so either way they suffer terribly.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies the two reasons: they stay up watching for him, and when he is there they stay up for fear he'll leave. Kalaignar's gloss reads this more generally, as the lot of all lovers' eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1180":{"book":"kural","num":1180,"t":["மறைபெறல் ஊரார்க்கு அரிதன்றால் எம்போல்","அறைபறை கண்ணார் அகத்து"],"p":"It is not difficult for the people of this place to understand the secret of those whose eyes, like mine, are as it were beaten drums.","r":"The woman says it isn't hard for the town to learn the secret in the hearts of those like her, whose eyes are beaten drums.","n":"The eyes' final offence is treason. The friend has asked her to hide his cruelty so the town won't blame him, and she answers that the eyes make that impossible. Parimelazhagar notes the variant reading அறைபறைக் கண்ணார், which removes the poetic strain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","alar","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:118"},"k:1181":{"book":"kural","num":1181,"t":["நயந்தவர்க்கு நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன் பசந்தவென்","பண்பியார்க்கு உரைக்கோ பிற"],"p":"I who (then) consented to the absence of my loving lord, to whom can I (now) relate the fact of my having turned sallow.","r":"The woman, to herself, asks to whom she can describe her pallor, since she herself agreed to the absence of the man who loves her.","n":"Consent has closed off complaint. Parimelazhagar reads every word of this as her logic: having agreed, she feels it would be a further wrong even to tell anyone. 1181 is the first verse of the chapter, so the book's most visible symptom starts with her taking responsibility for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1182":{"book":"kural","num":1182,"t":["அவர்தந்தார் என்னும் தகையால் இவர்தந்தென்","மேனிமேல் ஊரும் பசப்பு"],"p":"","r":"The woman says the pallor climbs on and rides over her body, proud that he is the one who gave it.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses the chariot-and-rider sense of இவர்தல் and adds that this is a claim of right, like a retainer's, which she can't resist because it comes from him. By his reading she is also comforting her friend: it's riding me by right, so don't worry. For Manakkudavar, she is saying that it isn't her doing, so take it up with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1183":{"book":"kural","num":1183,"t":["சாயலும் நாணும் அவர்கொண்டார் கைம்மாறா","நோயும் பசலையும் தந்து"],"p":"He has taken (away) my beauty and modesty, and given me instead disease and sallowness.","r":"The woman says he took away her grace and modesty, and gave her sickness and pallor in exchange.","n":"The language is commercial: கைம்மாறா, 'in exchange'. Parimelazhagar notices the crossed pairing, in which sickness replaces modesty, since the more she tries to suppress it, the more it grows, and pallor replaces grace. He takes சாயல் as bodily beauty, and Manakkudavar as femininity (பெண்மை).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","naanam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1184":{"book":"kural","num":1184,"t":["உள்ளுவன் மன்யான் உரைப்பது அவர்திறமால்","கள்ளம் பிறவோ பசப்பு"],"p":"I think (of him); and what I speak about is but his excellence; still is there sallowness; and this is","r":"The woman says she thinks of him and speaks only of his merits, and still the pallor comes, a kind of cheating.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes this a small argument: body follows mind and speech, and both are faithfully his, so the body shouldn't have paled. The verse never accuses her of faithlessness; the pallor is the deceiver.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1185":{"book":"kural","num":1185,"t":["உவக்காண்எம் காதலர் செல்வார் இவக்காண்என்","மேனி பசப்பூர் வது"],"p":"Just as my lover departed then, did not sallowness spread here on my person ?","r":"The woman says: look there, her lover is going, and look here, the pallor is spreading over her body.","n":"The verse is built only from the pointing words உவக்காண் and இவக்காண், which turn distance into simultaneity. Parimelazhagar compares his leaving and the pallor's coming to the changing of day and night. He treats it as a memory of a past parting, and Manakkudavar as happening right now.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1186":{"book":"kural","num":1186,"t":["விளக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும் இருளேபோல் கொண்கன்","முயக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும் பசப்பு"],"p":"Just as darkness waits for the failing light; so does sallowness wait for the laxity of my husband's intercourse.","r":"The woman says pallor watches for her lover's embrace to weaken, as darkness watches for a lamp to falter.","n":"Darkness isn't an enemy that attacks; it is simply what is there when the lamp fails. Read so, pallor is her natural state, and his embrace is what holds it off. Parimelazhagar's conclusion: if it waits for this tiny opening, what won't it do in a real separation?","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1187":{"book":"kural","num":1187,"t":["புல்லிக் கிடந்தேன் புடைபெயர்ந்தேன் அவ்வளவில்","அள்ளிக்கொள் வற்றே பசப்பு"],"p":"I who was in close embrace just turned aside and the moment I did so, sallowness came on me like something to be seized on.","r":"The woman says she lay in his embrace and turned slightly, and at that the pallor was on her so thick it could be scooped up.","n":"This is the chapter's most vivid hyperbole, and it is almost comic in its domesticity. Manakkudavar's setting makes it sad: he has left without telling her, and the friend sees the change in her next morning and is told this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1188":{"book":"kural","num":1188,"t":["பசந்தாள் இவள்என்பது அல்லால் இவளைத்","துறந்தார் அவர்என்பார் இல்"],"p":"Besides those who say \"she has turned sallow\" there are none who say \"he has forsaken her\".","r":"The woman says people will say she has grown pale, and no one says he has left her.","n":"A flat remark about how blame is apportioned. The cause is invisible and the symptom is on her skin, so she bears the talk. Parimelazhagar reads the plural 'people' as aimed at the friend, whom she refuses to name in her anger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1189":{"book":"kural","num":1189,"t":["பசக்கமன் பட்டாங்கென் மேனி நயப்பித்தார்","நன்னிலையர் ஆவர் எனின்"],"p":"If he is clear of guilt who has conciliated me (to his departure) let my body suffer its due and turn sallow.","r":"The woman says let her body go pale as it will, if the man who persuaded her is in a good state.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","நன்னிலையர் means blameless in the friend's eyes: since he must be held faultless, what does her body matter? This is bitter.","முன் இப்பிரிவின் கொடுமையறியாத என்னை இதற்கு உடம்படுத்திப் பிரிந்தவர் தவறிலராகவே வேண்டுவது"],["manakkudavar","The condition means he should suffer as she does, which is close to a curse.","நம்மைக் காதலித்த வரும் நம்மைப் போலத் துன்பமுறுவராயின்"],["kalaignar","It means if he is well, and so it is a loving sacrifice.","காதலர் நலமாக இருப்பார் என்றால்"]],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1190":{"book":"kural","num":1190,"t":["பசப்பெனப் பேர்பெறுதல் நன்றே நயப்பித்தார்","நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்"],"p":"It would be good to be said of me that I have turned sallow, if friends do not reproach with unkindness him who pleased me (then).","r":"The woman says that being called pale is good, if they won't spread talk of how unkind the man who won her has been.","n":"Parimelazhagar pushes பசப்பெனப் பேர் to its limit: not 'she has pallor' but 'she is pallor', a loss of identity she says she will welcome. He also says she speaks in scorn at the friend, who has criticised him to help her. Her good name is what she is ready to give for his.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","alar","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:119"},"k:1191":{"book":"kural","num":1191,"t":["தாம்வீழ்வார் தம்வீழப் பெற்றவர் பெற்றாரே","காமத்துக் காழில் கனி"],"p":"The women who are beloved by those whom they love, have they have not got the stone-less fruit of sexual delight ?","r":"The woman says those who are loved by the ones they love have obtained the stoneless fruit of love.","n":"A fruit without a stone is enjoyment with nothing to spit out. The verse is general and ungendered, while Parimelazhagar makes it 'women loved by husbands', adds that this needs good deeds from before, and supplies the unstated close that she hasn't got this fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1192":{"book":"kural","num":1192,"t":["வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்றால் வீழ்வார்க்கு","வீழ்வார் அளிக்கும் அளி"],"p":"The bestowal of love by the beloved on those who love them is like the rain raining (at the proper season) on those who live by it.","r":"The woman says the grace shown by the beloved to the one who loves them is like the rain to those who live by it.","n":"The grace here is measured and timely, as rain is, and Parimelazhagar takes that to include a man who returns from duty when he should. He brings out the shadow: if the rain fails, those dependent on it die.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1193":{"book":"kural","num":1193,"t":["வீழுநர் வீழப் படுவார்க்கு அமையுமே","வாழுநம் என்னும் செருக்கு"],"p":"The pride that says \"we shall live\" suits only those who are loved by their beloved (husbands).","r":"The woman says the pride of saying 'we shall live' suits only those who are loved by those they love.","n":"செருக்கு, 'pride', is usually a vice in the Kural, and here it is a privilege she envies. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar agree on the corollary: what suits her is death.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1194":{"book":"kural","num":1194,"t":["வீழப் படுவார் கெழீஇயிலர் தாம்வீழ்வார்","வீழப் படாஅர் எனின்"],"p":"Even those who are esteemed (by other women) are devoid of excellence, if they are not loved by their beloved.","r":"The woman says that those who are esteemed are still without something, if they aren't loved by the one they love.","n":"Manakkudavar ends with a proverb, கொண்டான் கயிற் கண்டான் காயும்: if the husband is angry at her, all who see her will be too. That gives a social reason why public esteem can't survive this loss.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The esteemed one is honoured by chaste women, and கெழீஇயிலர் means she lacks good deeds, that is, she has bad karma.","கெழீஇயின்மை; நல்வினையின்மை"],["manakkudavar","The esteemed one is honoured by the world, and the word means she becomes one without being desired.","விருப்பமில்லா ராவர்"],["kalaignar","The verse is about the partner who withdraws love, who is then thought to lack the sense of friendship.","அந்தக் காதலர் நட்புணர்வு இல்லாதவராகவே கருதப்படுவார்."]],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1195":{"book":"kural","num":1195,"t":["நாம்காதல் கொண்டார் நமக்கெவன் செய்பவோ","தாம்காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை"],"p":"He who is beloved by me, what will he do to me, if I am not beloved by him ?","r":"The woman asks what those she loves can do for her if they themselves don't love her.","n":"It answers a compliment. Parimelazhagar has the friend say that she bore this well because of her love; the reply is that love has brought her only pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1196":{"book":"kural","num":1196,"t":["ஒருதலையான் இன்னாது காமம்காப் போல","இருதலை யானும் இனிது"],"p":"Lust, like the weight of the KAVADI, pains if it lies in one end only but pleases if it is in both.","r":"The woman says love is painful when it is on one side only, and sweet on both sides, like the load of a carrying pole.","n":"It is a physical fact that a load at both ends is easier than half that load at one. The verse therefore doesn't claim only that shared love is pleasant; shared love is also lighter to carry.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["orutalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1197":{"book":"kural","num":1197,"t":["பருவரலும் பைதலும் காணான்கொல் காமன்","ஒருவர்கண் நின்றொழுகு வான்"],"p":"Would not cupid who abides and contends in one party (only) witness the pain and sorrow (in that party)?","r":"The woman asks whether Kaman, who works on one side alone, doesn't see her misery and grief.","n":"A rare appearance of a named love god in the Kural, and the verse makes him a partial combatant. The older commentators take the theology seriously: for Parimelazhagar, a god should know all and be impartial, and for Manakkudavar, he wouldn't hurt her if he saw, being a god. Kalaignar's version has him as simply indifferent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1198":{"book":"kural","num":1198,"t":["வீழ்வாரின் இன்சொல் பெறாஅது உலகத்து","வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்"],"p":"There is no one in the world so hard-hearted as those who can live without receiving (even) a kind word from their beloved.","r":"The woman says that no one in the world is harder than those who go on living without receiving a kind word from their beloved.","n":"வன்கண் is ambiguous between hardness and courage, and Pope chooses 'braver'. The commentators read it as an accusation she makes against herself. Parimelazhagar notes that any word at all from him would count as sweet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1199":{"book":"kural","num":1199,"t":["நசைஇயார் நல்கார் எனினும் அவர்மாட்டு","இசையும் இனிய செவிக்கு"],"p":"Though my beloved bestows no love on one, still are his words sweet to my ears.","r":"The woman says that though the man she longs for gives no grace, even இசை from him is sweet to her ears.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","orutalai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","இசை means any word at all about him, even that he isn't coming, and she hasn't got even that.","'அவர் வாரார் என்னுஞ் சொல்லாயினும் அமையும்,' என்பதுபட நின்றது."],["kalaignar","The word means his fame, and words that praise him are honey to her.","அவரைப் பற்றிய புகழ் உரை என் செவிக்குச் செந்தேனாகும்."]],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1200":{"book":"kural","num":1200,"t":["உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய் கடலைச்","செறாஅஅய் வாழிய நெஞ்சு"],"p":"Live, O my soul, would you who relate your great sorrow to strangers, try rather to fill up your own sea (of sorrow).","r":"The woman tells her heart that it wants to tell its pain to one who is not close, and that it may as well try to fill up the sea.","n":"வாழிய, 'may you live', is sarcasm here for Parimelazhagar. The chapter ends with her declining what she spent the chapter wanting, and the sea from 1137 and 1164 comes back as a job to be done with a basket. It is an image of utter futility.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:120"},"k:1201":{"book":"kural","num":1201,"t":["உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ் செய்தலால்","கள்ளினும் காமம் இனிது"],"p":"Sexuality is sweeter than liquor, because when remembered, it creates a most rapturous delight.","r":"The man says love is sweeter than liquor, because even thinking of it brings a great joy that doesn't fade.","n":"Parimelazhagar's headnote has him speaking to the friend who has carried his message: his claim is also a report of loneliness and faithfulness. The older comparison with liquor gains a new term here, since liquor needs drinking while love needs only memory.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1202":{"book":"kural","num":1202,"t":["எனைத்தொனறு ஏனிதேகாண் காமம்தாம் வீழ்வார்","நினைப்ப வருவதொன்று ஏல்"],"p":"Even to think of one's beloved gives one no pain. Sexuality, in any degree, is always delightful.","r":"Love is sweet in every way, since thinking of the beloved brings no pain; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man says how he has coped: love is sweet in union and in parting alike.","தான் ஆற்றிய வகை கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The woman rejects the friend's warning that her inability to endure will bring pain.","இது நீ இவ்வாறு ஆற்றாயாகின்றது துன்பம் பயக்குமென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் மறுத்துக் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1203":{"book":"kural","num":1203,"t":["நினைப்பவர் போன்று நினையார்கொல் தும்மல்","சினைப்பது போன்று கெடும்"],"p":"I feel as if I am going to sneeze but do not, and (therefore) my beloved is about to think (of me) but does not.","r":"The woman wonders if he seems about to think of her and doesn't, since a sneeze seems to come and then dies away.","n":"She reads her own body for evidence about his mind, using a folk belief that a sneeze means a distant loved one is thinking of you. Manakkudavar notes that she is repeating what ordinary women say, a rare moment of commentary on popular custom. An aborted sneeze makes for deflating, near-comic evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1204":{"book":"kural","num":1204,"t":["யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து எந்நெஞ்சத்து","ஓஒ உளரே அவர்"],"p":"He continues to abide in my soul, do I likewise abide in his ?","r":"The woman asks whether she too has a place in his heart, for, ah, he is always in hers.","n":"The verse uses the same indwelling that she argued for with such confidence in chapter 113, but now with doubt. Parimelazhagar extends the doubt into a tender either-or: is he kept away by unfinished work, or does he not remember her?","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1205":{"book":"kural","num":1205,"t":["தம்நெஞ்சத்து எம்மைக் கடிகொண்டார் நாணார்கொல்","எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்"],"p":"He who has imprisoned me in his soul, is he ashamed to enter incessantly into mine.","r":"The woman asks whether he, who has barred her from his heart, isn't ashamed to keep coming into hers.","n":"Her thoughts of him are treated as his actions, so she can rebuke him for trespassing. கடிகொண்டார் means he has guarded his heart against her; Pope's prose, 'imprisoned me in his soul', gets this backwards, though his couplet has it right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","nenju","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1206":{"book":"kural","num":1206,"t":["மற்றியான் என்னுளேன் மன்னோ அவரொடி யான்","உற்றநாள் உள்ள உளேன்"],"p":"I live by remembering my (former) intercourse with him; if it were not so, how could I live ?","r":"The woman asks what else she could live by, since she lives only by remembering the days she shared with him.","n":"Parimelazhagar sets it as a reply to the friend's advice to forget. He lists the things she lacks that would otherwise sustain her: his messenger, sending her own. Memory is what is left.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1207":{"book":"kural","num":1207,"t":["மறப்பின் எவனாவன் மற்கொல் மறப்பறியேன்","உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்"],"p":"I have never forgotten (the pleasure); even to think of it burns my soul; could I live, if I should ever forget it ?","r":"The woman asks what would become of her if she forgot, since she doesn't know forgetting, and even remembering burns.","n":"A precise bind: remembering burns her, and forgetting would kill her, as Parimelazhagar supplies. Compare 1206, where memory is what keeps her alive.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1208":{"book":"kural","num":1208,"t":["எனைத்து நினைப்பினும் காயார் அனைத்தன்றோ","காதலர் செய்யும் சிறப்பு"],"p":"He will not be angry however much I may think of him; is it not so much the delight my beloved affords me ?","r":"The woman says that however much she thinks of him, he doesn't get angry; that much is the honour her lover does her.","n":"All she can claim is that he doesn't object to being thought of. Parimelazhagar hears the sarcasm directed at her friend's promise that he will come and bring her joy. Manakkudavar defines grace as not getting angry even at a fault, so the 'honour' is very small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1209":{"book":"kural","num":1209,"t":["விளியுமென் இன்னுயிர் வேறல்லம் என்பார்","அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து"],"p":"My precious life is wasting away by thinking too much on the cruelty of him who said we were not different.","r":"The woman says her dear life is ending, as she thinks too much about the lack of grace in one who said they weren't separate.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a key distinction: in her own words, it's not the parting that is killing her, but his lack of love.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1210":{"book":"kural","num":1210,"t":["விடாஅது சென்றாரைக் கண்ணினால் காணப்","படாஅதி வாழி மதி"],"p":"May you live, O Moon! Do not set, that I mine see him who has departed without quitting my soul.","r":"The woman speaks to the moon, blessing it and asking about seeing with her eyes the man who went away without leaving her.","n":"விடாஅது சென்றாரை, 'who left without leaving', packs the whole chapter's paradox into two words.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She begs the moon not to set, so that their eyes can meet on it while both look at it.","மதி இருவரானும் நோக்கப்படுதலின் இருவர் கண்ணும் அதன்கண்ணே சேர்தல்."],["manakkudavar","She reproaches the moon for not setting: if it set she could sleep and see him in dreams.","பட்டதாயின், என் கண் உறங்கும்; உறங்கினால், அவரைக் காணலாமென் பது கருத்து."],["kalaignar","She asks it not to set, to give her light to seek him out with her eyes.","நான் என் கண்களால் தேடிக் கண்டுபிடித்திடத் துணையாக நீ மறையாமல் இருப்பாயாக."]],"ch":"k:121"},"k:1211":{"book":"kural","num":1211,"t":["காதலர் தூதொடு வந்த கனவினுக்கு","யாதுசெய் வேன்கொல் விருந்து"],"p":"Where with shall I feast the dream which has brought me my dear one's messenger ?","r":"What feast can be given to the dream that came with a message from the lover? The speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"The Kural's great social duty of hospitality is turned on a dream guest. Parimelazhagar's little note gets the joke exactly: it is a guest for which one can find nothing to serve.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman herself saw the dream and says this.","தலைமகன் தூது வரக் கண்டாள் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend says she dreamed that messengers came, in order to comfort the woman.","இது தலைமகளாற்றுதற் பொருட்டுக் காதலர் வாராநின்றாரென்று தூதர் வாக் கனாக் கண்டேனென்று தோழி சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1212":{"book":"kural","num":1212,"t":["கயலுண்கண் யானிரப்பத் துஞ்சிற் கலந்தார்க்கு","உயலுண்மை சாற்றுவேன் மன்"],"p":"If my fish-like painted eyes should, at my begging, close in sleep, I could fully relate my sufferings to my lord.","r":"The woman says that if her carp-like kohl-lined eyes would sleep when she begs them to, she would tell the one who joined with her that she is surviving.","n":"She must beg her eyes for sleep, after cursing them in chapter 118. Parimelazhagar notes her reason for preferring this to a messenger: there are things kept back from messengers, or shortened, which she could tell him in full.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","kan","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1213":{"book":"kural","num":1213,"t":["நனவினால் நல்கா தவரைக் கனவினால்","காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்"],"p":"My life lasts because in my dream I behold him who does not favour me in my waking hours.","r":"The woman says her life continues because she sees in her dreams the man who gives no grace in waking life.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes it as a reassurance to the friend who worries: 'don't worry, I'm bearing up'. It may comfort the friend, but what it actually says is hardly comforting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1214":{"book":"kural","num":1214,"t":["கனவினான் உண்டாகும் காமம் நனவினான்","நல்காரை நாடித் தரற்கு"],"p":"There is pleasure in my dream, because in it I seek and obtain him who does not visit me in my wakefulness.","r":"The woman says there is pleasure in dreams, since they seek out and bring her the man who gives no grace in waking life.","n":"The dream becomes an agent, a go-between travelling to where he is. That is what she wanted from her friend. Manakkudavar has the friend ask what use a dream is without real enjoyment, and the verse is her answer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1215":{"book":"kural","num":1215,"t":["நனவினால் கண்டதூஉம் ஆங்கே கனவுந்தான்","கண்ட பொழுதே இனிது"],"p":"I saw him in my waking hours, and then it was pleasant; I see him just now in my dream, and it is (equally) pleasant.","r":"The woman says that what she saw in waking life was sweet only then, and likewise the dream is sweet at the time it is seen.","n":"A strong philosophical move: both are equally over when over. Waking joy has no special standing in memory. Parimelazhagar's gloss is plain: 'the two are equal for me', and what she had before was no more than this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1216":{"book":"kural","num":1216,"t":["நனவென ஒன்றில்லை ஆயின் கனவினால்","காதலர் நீங்கலர் மன்"],"p":"Were there no such thing as wakefulness, my beloved (who visited me) in my dream would not depart from me.","r":"The woman says that if there were no such thing as waking, her lover in the dream wouldn't leave.","n":"Parimelazhagar supplies a charged word, பாவி, 'sinner', for waking, and reads ஒன்று as pointing up its cruelty. Waking is what barges in and parts them, so the person responsible for leaving is no longer him but waking itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1217":{"book":"kural","num":1217,"t":["நனவினால் நல்காக் கொடியார் கனவனால்","என்எம்மைப் பீழிப் பது"],"p":"The cruel one who would not favour me in my wakefulness, what right has he to torture me in my dreams?","r":"The woman asks why the cruel one who gives no grace in waking life torments her in dreams.","n":"A flat reversal of the last six verses, with the comfort now a torment. Parimelazhagar explains: on waking she doesn't find him, and because he vanishes from her shoulder, that too brings pain. He also tests a proverb against her case, that what isn't in waking isn't in dreams.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kanavu","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1218":{"book":"kural","num":1218,"t":["துஞ்சுங்கால் தோள்மேலர் ஆகி விழிக்குங்கால்","நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து"],"p":"When I am asleep he rests on my shoulders, (but) when I awake he hastens into my soul.","r":"The woman says that when she sleeps he is on her shoulder, and when she wakes he quickly moves into her heart.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's reading she is defending him against her friend: he never leaves her at all. விரைந்து, 'quickly', gives the speed of a fugitive, and Manakkudavar hears the friend's tease that prompted it: where does he go when sleep ends?","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1219":{"book":"kural","num":1219,"t":["நனவினால் நல்காரை நோவர் கனவினால்","காதலர்க் காணா தவர்"],"p":"They who have no dear ones to behold in their dreams blame him who visits me not in my waking hours.","r":"The woman says those who haven't seen their lovers in dreams are the ones who blame the man for not being gracious in waking life.","n":"This is an attack by pretending to speak about outsiders, as both commentators note. For Parimelazhagar she is hinting that the friend has no lover, which is a harsh thing to imply, and marks how angry the blaming makes her.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1220":{"book":"kural","num":1220,"t":["நனவினால் நம்நீத்தார் என்பர் கனவினால்","காணார்கொல் இவ்வூ ரவர்"],"p":"The women of this place say he has forsaken me in my wakefulness. I think they have not seen him visit me in my dreams.","r":"The woman says that the people of this town say he has left her in waking life, and asks if they don't see him in dreams.","n":"A surreal question, since no one else could see her dream. Parimelazhagar makes sense of it by way of the unity between her and her friend: if they are truly one, what the woman saw, the friend has seen too, and in not seeing she has become an outsider, 'this town'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kanavu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:122"},"k:1221":{"book":"kural","num":1221,"t":["மாலையோ அல்லை மணந்தார் உயிருண்ணும்","வேலைநீ வாழி பொழுது"],"p":"Live, O you evening are you (the former) evening? No, you are the season that slays (married) women.","r":"The woman tells the hour, with a bitter blessing, that it isn't evening at all but வேலை, which eats the life of those once wed.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","வேலை is a time word, the hour of ending, and he mentions but sets aside others who take it as 'you've become a spear'.","மகளிர் உயிரையுண்ணும் இறுதிக்காலமாய் இருந்தாய்."],["manakkudavar","The word is வேல் plus ஐ: you have become a spear that eats life. He adds evening is hot, so it's not really evening.","உயிரை உண்பதாகிய வொரு வேலாயிருந்தாய்"],["kalaignar","Also a spear that drinks life, with the blessing made explicit.","உயிரைக் குடிக்கும் வேலாக இருப்பதற்காக உனக்கோர் வாழ்த்து!"]],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1222":{"book":"kural","num":1222,"t":["புன்கண்ணை வாழி மருள்மாலை எம்கேள்போல்","வன்கண்ண தோநின் துணை"],"p":"A long life to you, O dark evening! You are sightless. Is your help-mate (also) as hard-hearted as mine.","r":"The woman says to the bewildered evening, may you live, you look sad-eyed; is your partner as hard-eyed as mine?","n":"The tender moment of the chapter: she stops fighting the hour and sees it as a fellow sufferer. Parimelazhagar explains that the pale evening's fading of light is what suggests its grief, and says she has put her own inner state onto it. The word மருள் means both the mixing of day and night and being confused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1223":{"book":"kural","num":1223,"t":["பனிஅரும்பிப் பைதல்கொள் மாலை துனிஅரும்பித்","துன்பம் வளர வரும்"],"p":"The evening that (once) came in with trembling and dimness (now) brings me an aversion for life and increasing sorrow.","r":"The woman says that evening, which comes with chill and pale, now comes with revulsion budding and sorrow growing.","n":"Parimelazhagar glosses துனி strongly, as disgust with living. Evening used to tremble and pale before her when she was happy; now she trembles before it. Manakkudavar keeps the reversal smaller, with today's evening following yesterday's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1224":{"book":"kural","num":1224,"t":["காதலர் இல்வழி மாலை கொலைக்களத்து","ஏதிலர் போல வரும்"],"p":"In the absence of my lover, evening comes in like slayers on the field of slaughter.","r":"The woman says that when her lover is absent, evening comes like enemies coming to a killing ground.","n":"ஏதிலர் is the word she has been using of the town and of the man's accusers; Parimelazhagar glosses it here as 'those with no grace at all', executioners. That evening was once her friend is part of the pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1225":{"book":"kural","num":1225,"t":["காலைக்குச் செய்தநன்று என்கொல் எவன்கொல்யான்","மாலைக்குச் செய்த பகை?"],"p":"What good have I done to morning (and) what evil to evening?","r":"The woman asks what good she has done to morning and what enmity she has shown to evening.","n":"It is framed as a legal puzzle about reward and punishment. Both commentators note a hidden reversal: morning used to be the hated hour, when they feared his leaving, and now it's the shore at the end of the flood of night. Evening made the opposite change.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1226":{"book":"kural","num":1226,"t":["மாலைநோய் செய்தல் மணந்தார் அகலாத","காலை அறிந்த திலேன்"],"p":"Previous to my husband's departure, I know not the painful nature of evening.","r":"The woman says she didn't know that evening brings sickness, while he who wed her had not yet left.","n":"It answers the most painful question: why did you consent? Parimelazhagar spells out that she would have refused had she known this. So 1181's self-accusation gets its answer: she agreed in ignorance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1227":{"book":"kural","num":1227,"t":["காலை அரும்பிப் பகலெல்லாம் போதாகி","மாலை மலரும்இந் நோய்"],"p":"This malady buds forth in the morning, expands all day long and blossoms in the evening.","r":"The woman says this sickness buds in the morning, ripens through the day and opens in the evening.","n":"A flower is made a sickness, in a lovely, cruel inversion. Parimelazhagar's reasons are as vivid as the verse: at morning she is still comforted by a dream, by day memory thins, and at evening she watches creatures and people returning to their mates.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamanoy","maalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1228":{"book":"kural","num":1228,"t":["அழல்போலும் மாலைக்குத் தூதாகி ஆயன்","குழல்போலும் கொல்லும் படை"],"p":"The shepherd's flute now sounds as a fiery forerunner of night, and is become a weapon that slays (me).","r":"The woman says the herdsman's flute is a messenger for the burning evening and a weapon that kills.","n":"The one sound in the chapter, pastoral and sweet in itself. Parimelazhagar reads it as a change from 'pleasant before' to deadly now. Evening has its own envoy and arms, like a king making war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1229":{"book":"kural","num":1229,"t":["பதிமருண்டு பைதல் உழக்கும் மதிமருண்டு","மாலை படர்தரும் போழ்து"],"p":"When night comes on confusing (everyone's) mind, the (whole) town will lose its sense and be plunged in sorrow.","r":"The woman says that when evening spreads, bewildering the mind, the whole town is bewildered and suffers.","n":"Parimelazhagar's gloss is missing in this source, but his note says the unstated thought is 'I shall die', and he treats evening as Death, implying that the town grieves for her. He adds that others read it as her own mind reeling, and with so little of his comment left, I can't say which he preferred in full.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["maalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1230":{"book":"kural","num":1230,"t":["பொருள்மாலை யாளரை உள்ளி மருள்மாலை","மாயும்என் மாயா உயிர்"],"p":"My (hitherto) unextinguished life is now lost in this bewildering night at the thought of him who","r":"The woman says that thinking of the one whose nature is wealth, in this confused evening, her life that wouldn't die is dying.","n":"The wordplay does much of the work: பொருள்மாலை, 'wealth-natured', against மருள்மாலை, 'confused evening', and மாயும் against மாயா. For Parimelazhagar, he has broken his word to keep to wealth, so he has lost his nature to it. Manakkudavar says she takes him to have neither love nor virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["selvam","maalai","uyir"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:123"},"k:1231":{"book":"kural","num":1231,"t":["சிறுமை நமக்கொழியச் சேட்சென்றார் உள்ளி","நறுமலர் நாணின கண்"],"p":"While we endure the unbearable sorrow, your eyes weep for him who is gone afar, and shun (the","r":"The friend tells her that as she thinks of the man gone far away, leaving them this misery, her eyes are ashamed before fragrant flowers.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He left the pain with them when he went far away, and the friend is the speaker.","இவ்வாற்றாமை நம்கண்ணே நிற்பத் தாம் சேணிடைச் சென்ற காதலரை"],["manakkudavar","He went so that their hardship would end, which gives his journey a very different motive.","நமக்குத் துன்பம் ஒழிய வேண்டி, நெடு நெறிக்கண் சென்றாரை"]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1232":{"book":"kural","num":1232,"t":["நயந்தவர் நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்","பசந்து பனிவாரும் கண்"],"p":"The discoloured eyes that shed tears p rofusely seem to betray the unkindness of our beloved.","r":"The friend says that her eyes, pale and running with tears, seem to be telling others of the unkindness of the one they desired.","n":"போலும், 'seem', is doing precise work. Parimelazhagar calls it inference: others deduce what the eyes don't actually say. For the friend, the point is that weeping exposes him, so stop.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","pasalai","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1233":{"book":"kural","num":1233,"t":["தணந்தமை சால அறிவிப்ப போலும்","மணந்தநாள் வீங்கிய தோள்"],"p":"The shoulders that swelled on the day of our union (now) seem to announce our separation clearly (to the public).","r":"Her shoulders, which swelled on the day of union, seem to announce loudly that he has left; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"The record needs both moments. Parimelazhagar says those who saw the swelling and now the wasting will quickly work out what happened. It is a before-and-after that is hard to explain away.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","alar"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend addresses her: your shoulders are doing what they shouldn't.","இன்ப மிகுதியால் பூரித்த நின் தோள்கள்"],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks to her friend, who has seen her shoulders waste.","பிரிவிடை யாற்றாளாகிய தலைமகள் தனது தோள் வாட்ட முற்றது கண்ட தோழிக்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1234":{"book":"kural","num":1234,"t":["பணைநீங்கிப் பைந்தொடி சோரும் துணைநீங்கித்","தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள்"],"p":"In the absence of your consort, your shoulders having lost their former beauty and fulness, your bracelets of pure gold have become loose.","r":"Her shoulders, their old beauty faded with her partner gone, have lost their fullness, and her bright bangles slip; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"தொல்கவின், 'old beauty', matters: for Parimelazhagar she has lost both the beauty he gave and the beauty she had before him. Commentators also differ over the bangle, gold for Parimelazhagar, emerald for Manakkudavar.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend says these shoulders are betraying his lack of love and his false word.","இவை இங்ஙனம் செயற்பாலவல்ல."],["manakkudavar","The woman tells her friend that her shoulders are now thinning as well as losing their beauty.","தோள் அழகழிதலே யன்றி மெலி வதுஞ் செய்யாநின்றதென்று தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1235":{"book":"kural","num":1235,"t":["கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும் தொடியொடு","தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள்"],"p":"The (loosened) bracelets, and the shoulders from which the old beauty has faded, relate the cruelty of the pitiless one.","r":"The friend says the shoulders, with bangles lost and old beauty faded, are declaring the cruelty of the cruel one.","n":"The friend now says openly what 1232-1234 only implied, with both commentators attributing it to her. Manakkudavar gives the purpose: she blames him to help the woman bear up. That prepares the next verse.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","alar","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1236":{"book":"kural","num":1236,"t":["தொடியொடு தோள்நெகிழ நோவல் அவரைக்","கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து"],"p":"I am greatly pained to hear you call him a cruel man, just because your shoulders are reduced and your bracelets loosened.","r":"The woman tells her friend that with bangles and shoulders slackening, what hurts is hearing him called cruel.","n":"The friend's strategy has backfired. Parimelazhagar spells out the claim: it's not his absence I can't bear, but what you say. Kalaignar widens the accusers from the friend to anyone who sees her. It's the same defence as in 1190.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["melivu","pirivu","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1237":{"book":"kural","num":1237,"t":["பாடு பெறுதியோ நெஞ்சே கொடியார்க்கென்","வாடுதோட் பூசல் உரைத்து"],"p":"Can you O my soul! gain glory by relating to the (so-called) cruel one the clamour of my fading shoulders?","r":"The woman asks her heart if it could win any glory by telling the 'cruel' one of the uproar over her wasting shoulders.","n":"A sardonic challenge. For Parimelazhagar, she says கொடியார் only in quotation, as in 'the one they call cruel'. He reads பூசல் as the friend's blame and her own distress, while Manakkudavar reads it as the town's gossip.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","melivu","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1238":{"book":"kural","num":1238,"t":["முயங்கிய கைகளை ஊக்கப் பசந்தது","பைந்தொடிப் பேதை நுதல்"],"p":"When I once loosened the arms that were in embrace, the forehead of the gold-braceleted women turned sallow.","r":"The man remembers that when he once loosened the arms that held her, the forehead of the girl with bright bangles turned pale.","n":"The voice changes suddenly: we have been with her, and now he is far away and remembering. The conclusion, as Parimelazhagar gives it: if that much did this, what will this absence do? I must go quickly.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He loosened his arms out of tenderness, thinking the tight embrace would hurt her.","'இவட்கு நோம்' என்று கருதி ஒரு ஞான்று யான் நெகிழ்ந்தேனாக"],["manakkudavar","He loosened them because he was intending to leave, and she sensed this.","யான் பிரிவதாக நினைத்து, அவள் முயங்கியகைகளை நீக்கினே னாக"]],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1239":{"book":"kural","num":1239,"t":["முயக்கிடைத் தண்வளி போழப் பசப்புற்ற","பேதை பெருமழைக் கண்"],"p":"When but a breath of breeze penetrated our embrace, her large cool eyes became sallow.","r":"The man remembers that when a cool breeze slipped between them in their embrace, the girl's large cool eyes grew pale.","n":"Parimelazhagar draws the arithmetic: if a breeze could do this, what about mountains, forests and whole countries now between them? Manakkudavar says three verses of the man's speech begin here, which is a different division from Parimelazhagar's.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","punarchi","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1240":{"book":"kural","num":1240,"t":["கண்ணின் பசப்போ பருவரல் எய்தின்றே","ஒண்ணுதல் செய்தது கண்டு"],"p":"Was it at the sight of what the bright forehead had done that the sallowness of her eyes became sad?","r":"The man asks whether the pallor in her eyes itself grew distressed on seeing what her bright forehead had done.","n":"An odd and very ingenious image, and the commentary has to work hard. For Parimelazhagar, the eye's pallor is ashamed because the forehead paled faster, so her parts are competing to lose their beauty, and he should hurry. I find this plausible but can't be sure the verse means that much.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pasalai","kan"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:124"},"k:1241":{"book":"kural","num":1241,"t":["நினைத்தொன்று சொல்லாயோ நெஞ்சே எனைத்தொன்றும்","எவ்வநோய் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து"],"p":"O my soul, will you not think and tell me some medicine be it what it may, that can cure this incurable malady?","r":"The woman asks her heart to think and tell her of some medicine, of whatever kind, for this incurable sickness.","n":"Parimelazhagar takes எனைத்தொன்றும், 'of any kind', very far: even a remedy that means giving up shame, which is dearer than life. The heart is asked because she herself no longer has the power to know.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1242":{"book":"kural","num":1242,"t":["காதல் அவரிலர் ஆகநீ நோவது","பேதைமை வாழியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"May you live, O my soul! While he is without love, for you to suffer is (simple) folly.","r":"The woman tells her heart, with a mocking blessing, that it's folly for it to suffer when he has no love.","n":"Parimelazhagar sees a surprising purpose in this: it isn't telling the heart to give up, but to stop waiting and go, since going would be wise. On this reading, it's the first move towards a woman going after a man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1243":{"book":"kural","num":1243,"t":["இருந்துள்ளி என்பரிதல் நெஞ்சே பரிந்துள்ளல்","பைதல்நோய் செய்தார்கண் இல்"],"p":"O my soul! why remain (here) and suffer thinking (of him)? There are no lewd thoughts (of you) in him who has caused you this disease of sorrow.","r":"The woman asks her heart why it sits and suffers, thinking of him, when the one who caused this sickness has no thought of pity.","n":"இருந்து, 'sitting here', is the barb, because the alternatives are stated in Parimelazhagar's gloss: neither going to him nor dying. Waiting has become the worst option.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kaamanoy","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1244":{"book":"kural","num":1244,"t":["கண்ணும் கொளச்சேறி நெஞ்சே இவையென்னைத்","தின்னும் அவர்க்காணல் உற்று"],"p":"O my soul! take my eyes also with you, (if not), these would eat me up (in their desire) to see him.","r":"The woman tells her heart to take the eyes with it if it goes, since they are eating her alive with longing to see him.","n":"A practical request and one of the book's most physical: the body has parts she wants to send away. By Parimelazhagar's reading, this is a way to mean that she herself will go, since she can't part with her eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","kan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1245":{"book":"kural","num":1245,"t":["செற்றார் எனக்கை விடல்உண்டோ நெஞ்சேயாம்","உற்றால் உறாஅ தவர்"],"p":"O my soul! can he who loves not though he is beloved, be forsaken saying he hates me (now)?","r":"The woman asks her heart if one can really abandon him as an enemy, simply because he doesn't respond when she draws close.","n":"The commentators shift the emphasis subtly. For Parimelazhagar, she lacks the strength to abandon him; for Manakkudavar, the issue is whether it would be proper. One treats loyalty as weakness, and the other treats it as what's right.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1246":{"book":"kural","num":1246,"t":["கலந்துணர்த்தும் காதலர்க் கண்டாற் புலந்துணராய்","பொய்க்காய்வு காய்திஎன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! when you see the dear one who remove dislike by intercourse, you are displeased and continue to be so. Nay, your displeasure is (simply) false.","r":"The woman tells her heart that when it sees the man who ends quarrels by union, it can't stay angry, and so its present anger is false.","n":"She exposes her own anger as fake, calling it பொய்க்காய்வு. The heart is angry only while he's absent, and is no use when it would count.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1247":{"book":"kural","num":1247,"t":["காமம் விடுஒன்றோ நாண்விடு நன்னெஞ்சே","யானோ பொறேன்இவ் விரண்டு"],"p":"O my good soul, give up either lust or honour, as for me I can endure neither.","r":"The woman tells her good heart to give up either desire or shame, since she can't bear both.","n":"நன்னெஞ்சே, 'good heart', isn't flattery alone. For Parimelazhagar, the heart is good because holding on to both keeps her womanhood intact, but she can't afford that goodness, as her life is at stake. யானோ implies a split: 'you may be able to, I can't.'","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","naanam","nenju"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1248":{"book":"kural","num":1248,"t":["பரிந்தவர் நல்காரென்று ஏங்கிப் பிரிந்தவர்","பின்செல்வாய் பேதைஎன் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"You are a fool, O my soul! to go after my departed one, while you mourn that he is not kind enough to favour you.","r":"The woman calls her heart a fool for following the man who has left, while lamenting that he isn't gracious.","n":"Parimelazhagar's surviving note gives the logic: he saw her distress and left anyway, so how would telling him at a distance change anything? This reverses 1242-1244, where she was urging the heart to go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","orutalai","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1249":{"book":"kural","num":1249,"t":["உள்ளத்தார் காத லவரால் உள்ளிநீ","யாருழைச் சேறியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! to whom would you repair, while the dear one is within yourself?","r":"The woman asks her heart whom it goes looking for, since her lover is within it.","n":"Parimelazhagar says it's laughable to go looking outside for one who is inside. To me, this resembles the later devotional complaint about seeking God outside, though this resemblance isn't something the verse or its commentators claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1250":{"book":"kural","num":1250,"t":["துன்னாத் துறந்தாரை நெஞ்சத்து உடையேமா","இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்"],"p":"If I retain in my heart him who has left me without befriending me, I shall lose even the (inward) beauty that remains.","r":"The woman says that if she keeps in her heart the man who left without coming close, she will lose still more of her கவின்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ninaivu","nalam","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She has lost her outward beauty, and will now lose her inner beauty too, meaning her dignity and restraint.","முன் இழந்த புறக்கவினேயன்றி நின்ற அகக்கவினும் இழப்போம்."],["kalaignar","It is bodily beauty that will fade further as she wastes away.","மேலும் மேனியெழில் இழந்து மெலிந்து அழிய வேண்டியுள்ளது."]],"ch":"k:125"},"k:1251":{"book":"kural","num":1251,"t":["காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் நிறையென்னும்","நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு"],"p":"The axe of lust can break the door of chastity which is bolted with the bolt of modesty.","r":"The woman says that the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint, bolted with the bar of shame.","n":"The architecture is exact, as Parimelazhagar explains: restraint is the door that keeps what is inside from being taken, shame is the bolt, since restraint holds as long as shame does. Manakkudavar has அறிவு, 'understanding', as the door, though the verse says நிறை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","naanam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1252":{"book":"kural","num":1252,"t":["காமம் எனவொன்றோ கண்ணின்றென் நெஞ்சத்தை","யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்"],"p":"Even at midnight is my mind worried by lust, and this one thing, alas! is without mercy.","r":"The woman says that this thing called desire has no pity, for it puts her heart to work even at midnight.","n":"The image is forced labour: everyone else's work stops at midnight, and hers doesn't. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar identify the task as sending the heart to him, which, Parimelazhagar notes, isn't a proper task for a woman, and is why she says 'this thing called desire'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","nenju","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1253":{"book":"kural","num":1253,"t":["மறைப்பேன்மன் காமத்தை யானோ குறிப்பின்றித்","தும்மல்போல் தோன்றி விடும்"],"p":"I would conceal my lust, but alas, it yields not to my will but breaks out like a sneeze.","r":"The woman says she would hide her desire, but like a sneeze it comes out without her intending it.","n":"The comparison is deliberately undignified. குறிப்பின்றி means without intent, using a word that in chapter 110 named her coded signs, which were deliberate. Now the sign sends itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","nirai","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1254":{"book":"kural","num":1254,"t":["நிறையுடையேன் என்பேன்மன் யானோஎன் காமம்","மறையிறந்து மன்று படும்"],"p":"I say I would be firm, but alas, my malady breaks out from its concealment and appears in public.","r":"The woman says she used to say she had restraint, yet her desire passes beyond concealment and comes out in public.","n":"The tense matters, on Parimelazhagar's gloss: 'until today I thought of myself as restrained'. She has lost not only the restraint but also a belief about who she is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nirai","kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1255":{"book":"kural","num":1255,"t":["செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை காமநோய்","உற்றார் அறிவதொன்று அன்று"],"p":"The dignity that would not go after an absent lover is not known to those who are sticken by love.","r":"The woman says the greatness of not going after one who has turned hostile is something those who are sick with love can't know.","n":"Honour isn't rejected, but it's placed out of reach. Parimelazhagar gives the other half: those who don't have the sickness can know it, and he calls them the people of honour (மானம் உடையார்). He also softens going after to continual thought, and softens செற்றார், 'enemy', as one who has made time painful.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","perumai","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1256":{"book":"kural","num":1256,"t":["செற்றவர் பின்சேறல் வேண்டி அளித்தரோ","எற்றென்னை உற்ற துயர்"],"p":"The sorrow I have endured by desiring to go after my absent lover, in what way is it excellent?","r":"The woman exclaims, in irony, how fine is the grief that has come to her, making her want to go after one who has turned hostile.","n":"Both older commentators hear sarcasm in அளித்து, 'sweet', as they did in the night verse 1168, meaning the opposite. Her comment on her own condition has become a dry detachment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1257":{"book":"kural","num":1257,"t":["நாணென ஒன்றோ அறியலம் காமத்தால்","பேணியார் பெட்ப செயின்"],"p":"I know nothing like shame when my beloved does from love (just) what is desired (by me).","r":"The woman says she knows nothing of shame when the man she loves does in desire what she longs for.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The context is that he has returned from courtesans; the shame she failed to feel is shame at touching a chest they have touched.","நாண் - பரத்தையர் தோய்ந்த மார்பைத் தோய்தற்கு நாணுதல்."],["manakkudavar","It's a general statement on women and desire, and doesn't mention any courtesans.","நாணென்பதொன்று அறியார் மகளிர், காமம் காரணமாக"]],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1258":{"book":"kural","num":1258,"t":["பன்மாயக் கள்வன் பணிமொழி அன்றோநம்","பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை"],"p":"Are not the enticing words of my trick-abounding roguish lover the weapon that breaks away my feminine firmness?","r":"The woman says that the soft speech of that thief of many deceptions is the weapon that breaks her womanhood.","n":"The chapter's picture of siege ends here, with Parimelazhagar glossing பெண்மை as restraint, a fortress taken by words. She knows them to be deceptive, and that doesn't protect her. பணிமொழி was how he praised her, in 1121.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nirai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1259":{"book":"kural","num":1259,"t":["புலப்பல் எனச்சென்றேன் புல்லினேன் நெஞ் சம்","கலத்தல் உறுவது கண்டு"],"p":"I said I would feign dislike and so went (away); (but) I embraced him the moment I say my mind began to unite with him!","r":"The woman says she went away meaning to sulk, and embraced him when she saw her heart beginning to join with him.","n":"She didn't yield to him but to her heart, and embracing is what's left when her instrument defects. Parimelazhagar says it exactly: the heart that is the means of sulking has itself turned into the means of joining.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1260":{"book":"kural","num":1260,"t":["நிணந்தீயில் இட்டன்ன நெஞ்சினார்க்கு உண்டோ","புணர்ந்தூடி நிற்பேம் எனல்"],"p":"Is it possible for those whose hearts melt like fat in the fire to say they can feign a strong dislike and remain so?","r":"The woman asks whether those whose hearts are like fat thrown in a fire can ever say 'we will join and then hold out in a sulk'.","n":"An extraordinary image of unlovely and total melting: not wax or butter, but animal fat.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","புணர்ந்து here means simply coming close, or meeting; sulking fails at first encounter.","புணர்தல் - ஈண்டு மிக நணுகுதல்; எதிர்ப்படுதலுமாம்."],["kalaignar","The word means after union; one can't then keep up the quarrel.","கூடிக் களித்தபின் ஊடல் கொண்டு அதில் உறுதியாக இருக்க முடியுமா?."]],"ch":"k:126"},"k:1261":{"book":"kural","num":1261,"t":["வாளற்றுப் புற்கென்ற கண்ணும் அவர்சென்ற","நாளொற்றித் தேய்ந்த விரல்"],"p":"My finger has worn away by marking (on the wall) the days he has been absent while my eyes have lost their lustre and begin to fail.","r":"The woman says her eyes have lost their light and her fingers have worn down from marking the days since he left.","n":"Fingers wear down from touching marks on a wall, in Parimelazhagar's and Kalaignar's picture, and from folding to count, in Manakkudavar's. The verse itself just says they marked the days. Either way, this is the book's most concrete image of waiting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kan","melivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1262":{"book":"kural","num":1262,"t":["இலங்கிழாய் இன்று மறப்பின்என் தோள்மேல்","கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து"],"p":"O you bright-jewelled maid, if I forget (him) today, my shoulders will lose their beauty even in the other life and make my bracelets loose.","r":"The woman tells her bright-jewelled friend that if she forgets him today, her beauty will leave and the bangles will slip from her shoulders thereafter.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["ninaivu","melivu","piravi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","'Today' is the day of her death and மேல் is the next birth: remembering him at the moment of death is how she can reach him in the next life.","இவ்வெல்லைக்கண் நினைந்தால் மறுமைக்கண் அவரை எய்தி அன்புறலாம்;"],["manakkudavar","The verse is about this life: if she forgets, her body will fail, and the address means 'you who have no pain'.","தம்மழகினை நீக்கி வளை முதலான அணிகலங்களையும் கழலவிடும்"]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1263":{"book":"kural","num":1263,"t":["உரன்நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்","வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்"],"p":"I still live by longing for the arrival of him who has gone out of love for victory and with valour as his guide.","r":"The woman says she still lives by longing for the return of the man who left, longing for victory, with his own resolve as his companion.","n":"The pointed word is துணை, 'companion'. Parimelazhagar spells it out: he has scorned her as a partner and taken resolve instead. There are two longings set side by side, நசைஇ in each line, for victory and for him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","uyir","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1264":{"book":"kural","num":1264,"t":["கூடிய காமம் பிரிந்தார் வரவுள்ளிக்","கோடுகொ டேறுமென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My heart is rid of its sorrow and swells with rapture to think of my absent lover returning with his love.","r":"The woman says that thinking of the return of the man who left with the love they shared, her heart கோடுகொடேறும்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The heart puts out branches, swelling and rising as a tree does, with sorrow gone.","என் நெஞ்சு வருத்தமொழிந்து மேன்மேற் பணைத்தெழாநின்றது."],["manakkudavar","The heart climbs a tree to look for him, because from the top one can see someone coming from afar.","என்னெஞ்சம் மரத்தின் மேலேறிப் பாராநின்றது"]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1265":{"book":"kural","num":1265,"t":["காண்கமன் கொண்கனைக் கண்ணாரக் கண்டபின்","நீங்கும்என் மென்தோள் பசப்பு"],"p":"May I look on my lover till I am satisfied and thereafter will vanish the sallowness of my slender shoulders.","r":"The woman says may she see her husband till her eyes are full, and after that the pallor of her soft shoulders will leave.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes what this is answering: the friend brings news that he's coming, and asks why she still pales. Her answer is that hearing won't do it, since the eyes must see. The news of the cure isn't the cure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kan","pasalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1266":{"book":"kural","num":1266,"t":["வருகமன் கொண்கன் ஒருநாள் பருகுவன்","பைதல்நோய் எல்லாம் கெட"],"p":"May my husband return some day; and then will I enjoy (him) so as to destroy all this agonizing sorrow.","r":"The woman says may her husband come one day, and she'll drink him in so that all this agonising sickness is destroyed.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes பருகுவன் drinking through all five senses, recalling 1101, and says that in that single day she'll destroy not only all past pain but all that might still come. That one day is asked to carry a lot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["punarchi","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1267":{"book":"kural","num":1267,"t":["புலப்பேன்கொல் புல்லுவேன் கொல்லோ கலப்பேன்கொல்","கண்அன்ன கேளிர் விரன்"],"p":"On the return of him who is as dear as my eyes, am I displeased or am I to embrace (him); or am I to do both?","r":"The woman asks, should he who is dear as her eyes return, whether she will sulk, embrace him or do both.","n":"The chapter's real interest here is the planning, with him not yet home. Parimelazhagar sets aside an alternative, that the third means to behave as if nothing happened: she wants to do all three, and that's her impatience.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1268":{"book":"kural","num":1268,"t":["வினைகலந்து வென்றீக வேந்தன் மனைகலந்து","மாலை அயர்கம் விருந்து"],"p":"Let the king fight and gain (victories); (but) let me be united to my wife and feast the evening.","r":"Let the king fight and win, and let there be a home-joined feast for the evening; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and Kalaignar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"Parimelazhagar rejects the woman's reading at length, on the grounds that she wouldn't name the king rather than the husband. But Manakkudavar gives a reason for that: the king's unfinished war is what holds him back. Evening, the woman's executioner, is for the man a feast to come.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","maalai","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, stuck on campaign, says to himself that he wants to join his wife; மனை here stands for the wife.","வேந்தற்கு உற்றுழிப் பிரிந்த தலைமகன் வினை முடிவு நீட்டித் துழித் தலைமகளை நினைந்து தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks: let the king win, and I will prepare a feast at home, with மனை meaning the kitchen.","நம் காதலர்க்கு விருந்து செய்வே மாக"],["kalaignar","The man speaks: if the leader wins, he will feast with his wife.","என் மனைவியுடன் எனக்கு மாலைப்பொழுதில் இன்ப விருந்துதான்."]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1269":{"book":"kural","num":1269,"t":["ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல் செல்லும்சேண் சென்றார்","வருநாள்வைத்து ஏங்கு பவர்க்கு"],"p":"To those who suffer waiting for the day of return of their distant lovers one day is as long as seven days.","r":"One day passes like seven for those who pine with an eye on the day when those far away return; Parimelazhagar gives it to the man, projecting her pain, and the other commentators name no speaker.","n":"Parimelazhagar uses the general phrasing as proof that it can't be the woman: she would say 'me', so the man is imagining how it is for her. If he's right, it's a rare moment in the book when a man grasps the other side of his absence. He takes 'seven' as meaning many.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1270":{"book":"kural","num":1270,"t":["பெறின்என்னாம் பெற்றக்கால் என்னாம் உறினென்னாம்","உள்ளம் உடைந்துக்கக் கால்"],"p":"After (my wife) has died of a broken heart, what good will there be if she is to receive me, has received me, or has even embraced me?","r":"What use is it if she gets, or has got, or even touches, once the heart has broken and spilled? The speaker is the man, of her, for Parimelazhagar, and the woman, of herself, for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["uyir","pirivu","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man fears that she may die before he gets back, and it can't be her voice, since then it would be a lament and not impatience.","இது தலைமகள் கூற்றாயவழி இரங்கலாவதல்லது விதுப்பாகாமை அறிக."],["manakkudavar","The woman answers the friend who says that he will come: what use, if my own heart has already broken?","இது வருவா ரென்ற தோழிக்குத் தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:127"},"k:1271":{"book":"kural","num":1271,"t":["கரப்பினுங் கையிகந் தொல்லாநின் உண்கண்","உரைக்கல் உறுவதொன் றுண்டு"],"p":"Though you would conceal (your feelings), your painted eyes would not, for, transgressing (their bounds), they tell (me) something.","r":"The man tells her that though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes have gone beyond her control and have something they want to say.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's setting the man has returned and is lavishing affection on her, and she takes fright, which makes this a kind of tragic misunderstanding. Kalaignar has the eyes declare love that resists separation. The verse only says there is something.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kan","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1272":{"book":"kural","num":1272,"t":["கண்ணிறைந்த காரிகைக் காம்பேர்தோட் பேதைக்குப்","பெண்நிறைந்த நீர்மை பெரிது"],"p":"Unusually great is the female simplicity of your maid whose beauty fills my eyes and whose shoulders resemble the bamboo.","r":"The man says this artless girl who fills his eyes, with her bamboo shoulders, has a great measure of womanly nature.","n":"The compliment may be a rebuke. Parimelazhagar glosses நீர்மை as மடமை, 'naivety', and has him complain to her friend that she fears a separation he has no thought of. Kalaignar takes it as simple praise.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nalam","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1273":{"book":"kural","num":1273,"t":["மணியில் திகழ்தரு நூல்போல் மடந்தை","அணியில் திகழ்வதொன்று உண்டு"],"p":"There is something that is implied in the beauty of this woman, like the thread that is visible in a garland of gems.","r":"The man says that, like the thread that shows in strung gems, there is something that shows through this young woman's beauty.","n":"The gem image needs transparent stones, and Parimelazhagar says crystal: inside but visible. What is inside isn't compatible with the outer beauty, he says, but appears with it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It's an intent that he can't himself identify, and he asks her friend to find out what it is.","மடந்தையது அணியகத்துக் கிடந்து புறத்துப் புலனாகின்றதொரு குறிப்பு உண்டு."],["manakkudavar","It's a hidden sorrow, which shows though she conceals it.","இவள் மறைக்கவும் தோற்றுகின்றதொரு துன்பம் உண்டு"]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1274":{"book":"kural","num":1274,"t":["முகைமொக்குள் உள்ளது நாற்றம்போல் பேதை","நகைமொக்குள் உள்ளதொன் றுண்டு"],"p":"There is something in the unmatured smile of this maid like the fragrance that is contained in an unblossomed bud.","r":"The man says there is something within the bud of this girl's smile, like the scent within a closed bud.","n":"It's a companion to 1273, except that here nothing shows: scent inside a bud can't be perceived, so his certainty about it is a matter of faith. Manakkudavar sets it in courtship, with the man telling his own heart that her smile promises joy, and Kalaignar says the smile holds the memory of her lover.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1275":{"book":"kural","num":1275,"t":["செறிதொடி செய்திறந்த கள்ளம் உறுதுயர்","தீர்க்கும் மருந்தொன்று உடைத்து"],"p":"The well-meant departure of her whose bangles are tight-fitting contains a remedy that can cure my great sorrow.","r":"The stealth with which the tight-bangled woman slipped away holds a medicine for great sorrow; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kaamanoy","kalaviyal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man tells the friend that she has hidden from him out of a false fear, and the cure is for the friend to explain that he isn't leaving.","என்கண் இல்லாத தொன்றனை உட்கொண்டு அது காரணமாக என்னை மறைத்துப் போன குறிப்பு"],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man, in courtship, that the woman's slipping away coyly means that his plea will succeed.","எதிர்ப்பட்ட தலைமகற்கு நின்குறை முடியும்; நீ இவ்விடைச் செல்லென்று தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1276":{"book":"kural","num":1276,"t":["பெரிதாற்றிப் பெட்பக் கலத்தல் அரிதாற்றி","அன்பின்மை சூழ்வ துடைத்து"],"p":"The embrace that fills me with comfort and gladness is capable of enduring (my former) sorrow and meditating on his want of love.","r":"The woman tells her friend that his great comfort and eager lovemaking contains in it her having to endure hardship again and to think about his lack of love.","n":"A sad piece of reading. His tenderness is the very thing that gives him away, since she reasons from excess of kindness to a coming parting. Both older commentators give it to her, speaking to the friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","punarchi","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1277":{"book":"kural","num":1277,"t":["தண்ணந் துறைவன் தணந்தமை நம்மினும்","முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை"],"p":"My bracelets have understood before me the (mental) separation of him who rules the cool seashore.","r":"The woman says her bangles knew before she did that the lord of the cool shore had parted from her.","n":"Parimelazhagar makes the separation inward: he is still physically with her but has left in his mind. Her body has registered this before her understanding did. So bangles, the book's most public symptom, are here her private source of knowledge.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["melivu","pirivu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1278":{"book":"kural","num":1278,"t":["நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார்எம் காதலர் யாமும்","எழுநாளேம் மேனி பசந்து"],"p":"It was but yesterday my lover departed (from me); and it is seven days since my complexion turned sallow.","r":"The woman says that her lover left only yesterday, and her body has been pale for seven days.","n":"The sum doesn't add up, on purpose. The pain started before the event, because she read it coming.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["pasalai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","He hasn't physically left: 'left yesterday' means he made up his mind then, shown by yesterday's tenderness.","'நெருநற்றுச் செய்த தலையளியாற் பிரிவு துணியப்பட்டது' என்பாள், 'நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார்' என்றும்"],["manakkudavar","He has actually left, and she was grieving before he went.","இஃது அவர் பிரிவதன் முன்னும் பிரிவரென் றேங்கி, இன்புற்றில் மென்று தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1279":{"book":"kural","num":1279,"t":["தொடிநோக்கி மென்தோளும் நோக்கி அடிநோக்கி","அஃதாண் டவள்செய் தது"],"p":"She looked at her bracelets, her tender shoulders, and her feet; this was what she did there (significantly).","r":"Parimelazhagar's speaker, the friend, tells the man that she looked at her bangles, looked at her soft shoulders and looked at her feet: this is what she did there.","n":"The whole verse is a silent gesture, and the commentators read it as a sentence: 'these will slip if you leave me; these will thin; so I'll go with you on these'. The verse itself doesn't state the conclusion. This is perhaps the most purely dramatic thing in the Kural, and the friend's 'that's what she did' leaves him to work it out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","melivu","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1280":{"book":"kural","num":1280,"t":["பெண்ணினால் பெண்மை உடைத்தென்ப கண்ணினால்","காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு"],"p":"To express their love-sickness by their eyes and resort to begging bespeaks more than ordinary female excellence.","r":"Those who know say that telling love's sickness with the eyes and begging adds more womanhood to womanhood; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kurippu","kan","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man answers: he praises her decision to go with him, as a way of telling her friend he won't be leaving at all.","தன் பிரிவின்மைக் குறிப்பினை அறிவுறுப்பான், அவள் பெண்மையினைப் பிறர்மேலிட்டு வியந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The friend speaks to the man, having seen the woman's response to news of his leaving.","இது பிரிவுணர்த்திய தலைமகள் குறிப்புக்கண்டு தலைமகற்குத் தோழி சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:128"},"k:1281":{"book":"kural","num":1281,"t":["உள்ளக் களித்தலும் காண மகிழ்தலும்","கள்ளுக்கில் காமத்திற் குண்டு"],"p":"To please by thought and cheer by sight is peculiar, not to liquor but lust.","r":"The woman says that being intoxicated at a thought and delighted at a look belong not to liquor but to love.","n":"Parimelazhagar's setting is pointed: the friend teases her about why she isn't angry at a man planning to leave. So the famous line is an excuse. He also distinguishes the words: களித்தல், with awareness intact, and மகிழ்தல், with awareness lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1282":{"book":"kural","num":1282,"t":["தினைத்துணையும் ஊடாமை வேண்டும் பனைத் துணையும்","காமம் நிறைய வரின்"],"p":"If women have a lust that exceeds even the measure of the palmyra fruit, they will not desire (to feign) dislike even as much as the millet.","r":"The woman says that when love fills to the size of a palmyra tree, there should be no sulking, not even the size of a millet seed.","n":"The huge and tiny are the proverbial measures. Parimelazhagar says she seems to be giving others good advice, and is in fact describing her own eagerness. Manakkudavar has her say this to her own heart, which is thinking of sulking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1283":{"book":"kural","num":1283,"t":["பேணாது பெட்பவே செய்யினும் கொண்கனைக்","காணா தமையல கண்"],"p":"Though my eyes disregard me and do what is pleasing to my husband, still will they not be satisfied unless they see him.","r":"The woman says that though he disregards her and does only what pleases him, her eyes won't rest without seeing him.","n":"It's a clear admission, with no self-deception. He is at fault, she knows it, and the eyes don't care. Pope's prose wrongly makes the eyes the ones who disregard her; the commentators and his couplet agree it is the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1284":{"book":"kural","num":1284,"t":["ஊடற்கண் சென்றேன்மன் தோழி அதுமறந்து","கூடற்கண் சென்றது என் னெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my friend! I was prepared to feign displeasure but my mind forgetting it was ready to embrace him.","r":"The woman tells her friend that she went there to sulk, but her heart forgot that and went there to unite.","n":"The same verb, சென்றேன் and சென்றது, is used for both: she and her heart set out together and went to different places. Parimelazhagar's word for this is அறைபோக, treason in a military sense.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nenju","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1285":{"book":"kural","num":1285,"t":["எழுதுங்கால் கோல்காணாக் கண்ணேபோல் கொண்கன்","பழிகாணேன் கண்ட இடத்து"],"p":"Like the eyes which see not the pencil that paints it, I cannot see my husband's fault (just) when I meet him.","r":"The woman says that, as the eye can't see the stick that lines it at the moment of lining, she doesn't see her husband's fault when she sees him.","n":"A wonderfully precise image: the object is at its closest and most intimate exactly when it can't be seen. Parimelazhagar notes that the eye knows the stick well enough at other times, and so she too knows his faults.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1286":{"book":"kural","num":1286,"t":["காணுங்கால் காணேன் தவறாய காணாக்கால்","காணேன் தவறல் லவை"],"p":"When I see my husband, I do not see any faults; but when I do not see him, I do not see anything but faults.","r":"The woman says that when she sees him she doesn't see his faults, and when she doesn't see him she sees nothing but his faults.","n":"A perfectly symmetrical verse about what presence does to judgement. As with the previous verse, this isn't presented as an illusion that she can see through and correct; it's just how it is. Sight is what removes her power to judge him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","kaamam","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1287":{"book":"kural","num":1287,"t":["உய்த்தல் அறிந்து புனல்பாய் பவரேபோல்","பொய்த்தல் அறிந்தென் புலந்து"],"p":"Like those who leap into a stream which they know will carry them off, why should a wife feign dislike which she knows cannot hold out long?","r":"The woman asks what use it is to sulk, knowing it will fail, like those who jump into a stream knowing it will carry them off.","n":"Parimelazhagar registers a strain in the syntax, and offers an alternate shape: 'I learned by trying once that sulking fails; I won't do it again'. Pope's couplet is rather different in having a rescue. The swimmer who knows she'll be swept away jumps in anyway, and the verse doesn't deny that this might be the fun of it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1288":{"book":"kural","num":1288,"t":["இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும் களித்தார்க்குக்","கள்ளற்றே கள்வநின் மார்பு"],"p":"O you rogue! your breast is to me what liquor is to those who rejoice in it, though it only gives them an unpleasant disgrace.","r":"Thief, your chest is to us as liquor is to those who drink it, though it does them shameful harm; the speaker is the friend for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","naanam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The friend tells the man that she has seen the woman's eagerness, and uses 'us' for them both.","தலைமகள் புணர்ச்சி? விதுப்பு அறிந்த தோழி, தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks to the man herself, having let go of the wish to sulk.","இது புலவிக்குறிப்பு நீங்கின தலை மகள் தலைமகற்குச் சொல்லியது"]],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1289":{"book":"kural","num":1289,"t":["மலரினும் மெல்லிது காமம் சிலர்அதன்","செவ்வி தலைப்படு வார்"],"p":"Sexual delight is more delicate than a flower, and few are those who understand its real nature.","r":"Love is softer than a flower, and few attain its moment; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman, to her heart, for Manakkudavar.","n":"The famous line has a practical use. Parimelazhagar lists what makes this moment rare: two people need to share intent, desire, enjoyment and pleasure at one time, in a fit place, moment and setting. He has the man saying this to end her sulk, so the maxim is also a plea.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["kaamam","punarchi"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks during a sulk that won't yield to his pleading, in order to end it.","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman, seeing that he desires union, tells her own heart that this isn't the moment to sulk.","இப்பொழுது ஊடுவையாயின், இக்காமஞ் செவ்விதப்பு மென்று புணர்ச்சி வேட்கையால் தலைமகள் நெஞ்சோடு கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1290":{"book":"kural","num":1290,"t":["கண்ணின் துனித்தே கலங்கினாள் புல்லுதல்","என்னினும் தான்விதுப் புற்று"],"p":"She once feigned dislike in her eyes, but the warmth of her embrace exceeded my own.","r":"The man recalls that she sulked with her eyes alone and then, more eager than he for the embrace, was undone.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives this an edge: spoken during her present sulk, it means 'the woman who gave up so quickly was not this one'. Manakkudavar has him simply observing her now. A rare moment of him looking back on her eagerness, and one that might flatter him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kan","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:129"},"k:1291":{"book":"kural","num":1291,"t":["அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல் கண்டும் எவன்நெஞ்சே","நீஎமக்கு ஆகா தது"],"p":"O my soul! although you have seen how his soul stands by him, how is it you do not stand by me?","r":"The woman asks her heart why it isn't hers, when it has seen how his heart belongs to him.","n":"An elegantly sharp opening. Two hearts are compared as servants, and his has a better idea of loyalty. Parimelazhagar adds the sting: even those who can't figure out what to do can copy someone else, and the heart won't do even that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1292":{"book":"kural","num":1292,"t":["உறாஅ தவர்க்கண்ட கண்ணும் அவரைச்","செறாஅரெனச் சேறியென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"O my soul! although you have known him who does not love me, still do you go to him, saying \"he will not be displeased.\"","r":"The woman says that her heart, even after seeing that he isn't close to her, goes to him as if he weren't hostile.","n":"The heart's reason is the small hope, that he won't be angry. Parimelazhagar says it's relying on old familiarity, which is a thin basis, and calls it the greatest folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","orutalai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1293":{"book":"kural","num":1293,"t":["கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டார்இல் என்பதோ நெஞ்சேநீ","பெட்டாங்கு அவர்பின் செலல்"],"p":"O my soul! do you follow him at pleasure under the belief that the ruined have no friends?","r":"The woman asks her heart if it follows him as it pleases, on the principle that the ruined have no friends.","n":"A piece of proverbial wisdom about false friends, turned against the heart. By using it she also admits that she is the one ruined. Parimelazhagar glosses கெட்டார் as her loss of honour, and the verse casts her heart as a fair-weather friend.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1294":{"book":"kural","num":1294,"t":["இனிஅன்ன நின்னொடு சூழ்வார்யார் நெஞ்சே","துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்காண் மற்று"],"p":"O my soul! you would not first seem sulky and then enjoy (him); who then would in future consult you about such things?","r":"The woman tells her heart that it won't first sulk and then enjoy, so no one will plan such things with it any more.","n":"It's almost comic: she drops the heart from her team as an unreliable accomplice. Sulking here is a strategy with a sequence to it, sulk first and enjoy later, and the heart can't wait.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1295":{"book":"kural","num":1295,"t":["பெறாஅமை அஞ்சும் பெறின்பிரிவு அஞ்சும்","அறாஅ இடும்பைத்தென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My soul fears when it is without him; it also fears when it is with him; it is sub ject to incessant sorrow.","r":"The woman says her heart fears not having him and, on having him, fears parting, so it is in endless grief.","n":"In Parimelazhagar's setting she says this within the hearing of a friend who has come as an envoy, so it also means she will let him in. He observes that there is no third state, which makes this grief complete.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1296":{"book":"kural","num":1296,"t":["தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால் என்னைத்","தினிய இருந்ததென் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My mind has been (here) in order to eat me up (as it were) whenever I think of him in my solitude.","r":"The woman says that when she sits alone and thinks of him, her heart is there only to eat her.","n":"Parimelazhagar notes the irony: it stays with her only to work on her, not to help her quarrel. Manakkudavar gives this a small domestic turn: she tells the friend, 'you've come now, and so I've escaped it'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1297":{"book":"kural","num":1297,"t":["நாணும் மறந்தேன் அவர்மறக் கல்லாஎன்","மாணா மடநெஞ்சிற் பட்டு"],"p":"I have even forgotten my modesty, having been caught in my foolish mind which is not dignified enough to forget him.","r":"The woman says that, stuck with this worthless, foolish heart that can't forget him, she has even forgotten shame.","n":"There are two forgettings in tension. Parimelazhagar's definition of shame is one of the finest things in his commentary: however long two have been together, to shrink as if seeing the other for the first time. His definition of the heart's foolishness: remembering when he's away, forgetting when he's there.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["naanam","nenju","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1298":{"book":"kural","num":1298,"t":["எள்ளின் இளிவாம்என்று எண்ணி அவர்திறம்","உள்ளும் உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு"],"p":"My soul which clings to life thinks only of his (own) gain in the belief that it would be disgraceful for it to despise him.","r":"The woman says her heart, thinking it would be disgraceful to despise him, keeps thinking of what pertains to him, out of உயிர்க்காதல்.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","uyir","ninaivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","உயிர்க்காதல் means the heart loves its own life: it won't die, and so it plans reconciliation.","உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு - உயிர்மேல் காதலையுடைய என் நெஞ்சு"],["kalaignar","It's a heart whose love is as dear as life, and it thinks on his greatness.","அவருடைய பெருமையைப் பற்றியே என்னுயிர்க் காதல் நெஞ்சம்"]],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1299":{"book":"kural","num":1299,"t":["துன்பத்திற்கு யாரே துணையாவார் தாமுடைய","நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி"],"p":"Who would help me out of one's distress, when one's own soul refuses help to one?","r":"Who will be a companion in grief, when one's own heart is no companion? The speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"If it's the man, then he has come to the state she has been in for ten verses. His heart is doing to him what hers did: urging union when he should be standing firm.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man speaks, in a sulk that won't yield, whose heart still wants her.","உணர்ப்புவயின் வாரா ஊடற்கண் தலைமகன் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The woman replies to the friend who asks who would help.","இது தலைமகள் துணையாவார் யாரென்ற தோழிக்குக் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1300":{"book":"kural","num":1300,"t":["தஞ்சம் தமரல்லர் ஏதிலார் தாமுடைய","நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி"],"p":"It is hardly possible for strangers to behave like relations, when one's own soul acts like a stranger.","r":"The man, for Parimelazhagar, says that it's no wonder if strangers aren't one's own, when one's own heart isn't.","n":"Parimelazhagar gives a witty aim: 'stranger' here is the sulking woman, who no longer seems his beloved, so if his own heart pains him for her sake, it's a small thing if she, a stranger, sulks. The other commentators don't name a speaker, and read it as a maxim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nenju","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:130"},"k:1301":{"book":"kural","num":1301,"t":["புல்லா திராஅப் புலத்தை அவர் உறும்","அல்லல்நோய் காண்கம் சிறிது"],"p":"Let us witness awhile his keen suffering; just feign dislike and embrace him not.","r":"The friend tells the woman not to embrace him but to sulk, so that they may watch his sharp pain for a little while.","n":"It's mischief with a purpose. Parimelazhagar says the friend has actually come as his envoy, and sides with the woman, teasing, as a way of bringing her round. சிறிது, 'a little', is the word that matters: the quarrel mustn't go on long.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1302":{"book":"kural","num":1302,"t":["உப்பமைந் தற்றால் புலவி அதுசிறிது","மிக்கற்றால் நீள விடல்"],"p":"A little dislike is like salt in proportion; to prolong it a little is like salt a little too much.","r":"The friend says that sulking is like the right amount of salt, and letting it go on a little too long is like a little too much salt.","n":"The image puts sulking in the kitchen, as an everyday and necessary ingredient without any drama. The second line is exact: just a little too much is enough to spoil it. Kalaignar makes the measure a time span, and Parimelazhagar makes it knowing when desire is about to fade.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1303":{"book":"kural","num":1303,"t":["அலந்தாரை அல்லல்நோய் செய்தற்றால் தம்மைப்","புலந்தாரைப் புல்லா விடல்"],"p":"For men not to embrace those who have feigned dislike is like torturing those already in agony.","r":"The woman says that not embracing those who have sulked with you is like adding sharp pain to those already in distress.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamanoy","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She means courtesans: go back to them, she tells the man returning from them, since they'll suffer without you. It's sarcasm.","நும்மைப் பெறாது புலந்தூடியிருக்கின்ற பரத்தையரைப் போய்ப் புலவி நீக்கிப் புல்லீராயின், அவராற்றார்"],["kalaignar","It's a general truth about lovers: leaving a sulking partner unreconciled is cruel.","ஊடல் கொண்டவரின் ஊடல் நீக்கித் தழுவாமல் விடுதல்"]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1304":{"book":"kural","num":1304,"t":["ஊடி யவரை உணராமை வாடிய","வள்ளி முதலரிந் தற்று"],"p":"Not to reconcile those who have feigned dislike is like cutting a faded creeper at its root.","r":"The woman says that not reconciling those who have sulked is like cutting a withered creeper at its root.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai","pirivu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","She again means the other women, and she adds that she has no right to sulk, since she has their sons to look at; so go to them.","நும்மோடு ஊடிய பரத்தையரை ஊடலுணர்த்திக் கூடாதொழிதல்"],["kalaignar","It's about any partner who stays aloof from a sulking lover.","ஊடல்புரிந்து பிணங்கியிருப்பவரிடம் அன்பு செலுத்திடாமல் விலகியே இருப்பின்"]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1305":{"book":"kural","num":1305,"t":["நலத்தகை நல்லவர்க்கு ஏஎர் புலத்தகை","பூஅன்ன கண்ணார் அகத்து"],"p":"An increased shyness in those whose eyes are like flowers is beautiful even to good and virtuous husbands.","r":"The grace of those good in their virtues is the depth of sulking in flower-eyed women; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","nalam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man, reconciled, reflects to himself that even faultless men find this sulking lovely.","தலைமகளைப் புலவி நீக்கிக் கூடிய தலைமகன் தன்னுள்ளே சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the woman that sulking suits courtesans, and that she shouldn't sulk at those not worth it.","இது பரத்தையரோடு புலந்து கூறிய தலைமகட்குத் தோழி கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1306":{"book":"kural","num":1306,"t":["துனியும் புலவியும் இல்லாயின் காமம்","கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று"],"p":"Sexual pleasure, without prolonged and short-lived dislike, is like too ripe, and unripe fruit.","r":"Without serious and light quarrels, love is like over-ripe fruit and unripe fruit; the speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar and the woman for Manakkudavar.","n":"There are two kinds of quarrels for two kinds of failure, and the verse uses them in order: no deep quarrel, and love is fruit going off; no light quarrel, and love is unripe. Manakkudavar supplies the tastes, sour and astringent. Pope's couplet reads 'ripened fruit' as a good thing, and the commentators don't.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man praises both kinds of quarrel after making up.","இவ்விரண்டும் வேண்டும் என்று வியந்து கூறியவாறு."],["manakkudavar","The woman speaks, saying that there is some good in the sulking that is then healed.","இஃது உணர்தற்கு நல்லது உளதென்று தலைமகள் கூறியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1307":{"book":"kural","num":1307,"t":["ஊடலின் உண்டாங்கோர் துன்பம் புணர்வது","நீடுவ தன்று கொல் என்று"],"p":"The doubt as to whether intercourse would take place soon or not, creates a sorrow (even) in feigned dislike.","r":"The man says there is one pain even within sulking: the worry about whether union will be delayed or not.","n":"This is where the game has its risk. Parimelazhagar takes it as the worry that union will be put off, and Kalaignar as a worry that the time for union will shrink. In either case, the player can't be sure the quarrel will end in time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1308":{"book":"kural","num":1308,"t":["நோதல் எவன்மற்று நொந்தாரென்று அஃதறியும்","காதலர் இல்லா வழி"],"p":"What avails sorrow when I am without a wife who can understand the cause of my sorrow?","r":"What use is it to suffer, when there is no lover who knows that this person is suffering? The speaker is the man for Parimelazhagar, and the friend, speaking to him, for Manakkudavar.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","orutalai","kaamanoy"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The man is himself sulking, because she won't yield to his pleading.","இவள் நம் காதலியல்லள்; அன்மையின், இந்நோவு அறியாள்"],["manakkudavar","The friend tells the man this when she has come inside, knowing about his sulking.","அதனை யறிந்து அகம்புக்க தோழி அவனுக்குச் சொல்லியது."]],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1309":{"book":"kural","num":1309,"t":["நீரும் நிழலது இனிதே புலவியும்","வீழுநர் கண்ணே இனிது"],"p":"Like water in the shade, dislike is delicious only in those who love.","r":"The man says water is sweet in the shade, and sulking too is sweet only with those who love.","n":"It's a reciprocal theory of sulking. A quarrel is a pleasure only inside a shared love, and without it there is only heat. Parimelazhagar applies this pointedly: she doesn't feel this for him, and so her sulk is unpleasant.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1310":{"book":"kural","num":1310,"t":["ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவாரோடு என்நெஞ்சம்","கூடுவேம் என்பது அவா"],"p":"It is nothing but strong desire that makes her mind unite with me who can leave her to her own dislike.","r":"The man, in Parimelazhagar's reading, says that his heart's wish to join with someone who leaves a sulk to wither is simply craving.","n":"அவா, 'craving', is a strong word to use on one's own heart, with a long negative history in the Kural's ethics. He diagnoses himself exactly as the ascetic chapter might, and it doesn't stop him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","nenju","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:131"},"k:1311":{"book":"kural","num":1311,"t":["பெண்ணியலார் எல்லாரும் கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்","நண்ணேன் பரத்தநின் மார்பு"],"p":"You are given to prostitution; all those who are born as womankind enjoy you with their eyes in an ordinary way. I will not embrace you.","r":"The woman calls him a philanderer and refuses his chest, because all women feast on him in common with their eyes.","n":"The offence is only that he was seen, and being seen is a form of being consumed. பரத்த is applied to him in the vocative, and is the verse's own word for his straying.","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kan","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","பெண்ணியலார் means courtesans, who, lacking other virtues, have only the nature of women. They look at him as his procession passes.","பரத்தையர்க்குள்ளது பெண் இயற்கை மாத்திரமே என்னுங் கருத்தால், 'பெண் இயலார்' என்றாள்."],["manakkudavar","It simply means all women, who think of his chest as common property.","நின் மார்பைப் பெண்மை யுடையா ரெல்லாரும் தமக்குப் பொதுவாக நினைத்துக்"]],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1312":{"book":"kural","num":1312,"t":["ஊடி இருந்தேமாத் தும்மினார் யாம்தம்மை","நீடுவாழ் கென்பாக் கறிந்து"],"p":"When I continued to be sulky he sneezed and thought I would (then) wish him a long life.","r":"The woman tells her friend that while she was sulking he sneezed, knowing she would then say 'long life' and so break her silence.","n":"A real gem of observed domestic strategy. Parimelazhagar says what makes this a 'fine point': she takes a natural sneeze for a deliberate sign. We can't tell if he meant it, and that is the fun.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1313":{"book":"kural","num":1313,"t":["கோட்டுப் பூச் சூடினும் காயும் ஒருத்தியைக்","காட்டிய சூடினீர் என்று"],"p":"Even if I were adorned with a garland of branch-flowers, she would say I did so to show it to another woman.","r":"The man tells her friend that even if he wears a wreath of bent flowers, she is angry and says he wore it to show some other woman.","n":"The word கோட்டுப்பூ is a puzzle. Parimelazhagar offers flowers woven in a curve or flowers from another landscape, Manakkudavar offers side-worn flowers, and Kalaignar flowers from a tree-branch. The offence doesn't depend on the answer, which is the point.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1314":{"book":"kural","num":1314,"t":["யாரினும் காதலம் என்றேனா ஊடினாள்","யாரினும் யாரினும் என்று"],"p":"When I said I loved her more than any other woman, she said \"more than others, yes, more than others,\" and remained sulky.","r":"The man says he told her he loved more than anyone, and she sulked: 'than whom, than whom?'","n":"A grammatical quarrel. He meant 'we love more than any other couple', and she took it to mean 'more than the other women I love'. The commentators spell this out, and the verse gives only her repeated words, one of the most natural bits of speech in the book.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1315":{"book":"kural","num":1315,"t":["இம்மைப் பிறப்பில் பிரியலம் என்றேனாக்","கண்நிறை நீர்கொண் டனள்"],"p":"When I said I would never part from her in this life her eyes were filled with tears.","r":"The man says he told her they wouldn't part in this birth, and her eyes filled with tears.","n":"A promise made in the language of rebirth backfires on its own qualifier: 'in this birth' implies 'not in the next'. Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar explain it so. Kalaignar has her ask 'is there a next birth, then?', which is a modern touch that isn't in the verse. Rebirth is taken for granted, as material for a joke.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","pirivu","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1316":{"book":"kural","num":1316,"t":["உள்ளினேன் என்றேன்மற் றென்மறந்தீர் என்றென்னைப்","புல்லாள் புலத்தக் கனள்"],"p":"When I said I had remembered her, she said I had forgotten her and relaxing her embrace, began to feign dislike.","r":"The man says he told her he had thought of her, and she said 'why did you forget?', and wouldn't embrace him, and went into a sulk.","n":"A strict logical inference, and Parimelazhagar uses a term from Indian logic for it, அருத்தாபத்தி: remembering presupposes forgetting. She is using the man's own argument from 1125.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1317":{"book":"kural","num":1317,"t":["வழுத்தினாள் தும்மினேன் ஆக அழித்தழுதாள்","யாருள்ளித் தும்மினீர் என்று"],"p":"When I sneezed she blessed me, but at once changed (her mind) and wept, asking, \"At the thought of whom did you sneeze?\"","r":"The man says that when he sneezed she blessed him, and then undid it and wept: 'who were you thinking of, that you sneezed?'","n":"The blessing comes automatically and the suspicion comes a second later, and the verse catches that interval. Parimelazhagar notes that the belief behind this is a women's usage (மகளிர் வழக்கு) and that she's taking it as literal fact.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1318":{"book":"kural","num":1318,"t":["தும்முச் செறுப்ப அழுதாள் நுமர்உள்ளல்","எம்மை மறைத்திரோ என்று"],"p":"When I suppressed my sneezing, she wept saying, \"I suppose you (did so) to hide from me your own people's remembrance of you\".","r":"The man says that when he suppressed a sneeze, she wept: 'are you hiding from me that your people are thinking of you?'","n":"It's the next stage of the farce. He has learned from 1317, and that's now the crime. Parimelazhagar sums it up: it's a fault to sneeze and a fault not to. So what is there to do?","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1319":{"book":"kural","num":1319,"t":["தன்னை உணர்த்தினும் காயும் பிறர்க்கும்நீர்","இந்நீரர் ஆகுதிர் என்று"],"p":"Even when I try to remove her dislike, she is displeased and says, \"This is the way you behave towards (other women).\"","r":"The man says that even when he tries to appease her, she is angry and says that this is how he behaves with other women too.","n":"The cure has become evidence. Parimelazhagar notes the extra detail: he has even admitted to faults he didn't commit in order to placate her, and that confession is what now counts against him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","parathai"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1320":{"book":"kural","num":1320,"t":["நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும் காயும் அனைத்துநீர்","யாருள்ளி நோக்கினீர் என்று"],"p":"Even when I look on her contemplating (her beauty), she is displeased and says, \"With whose thought have you (thus) looked on my person?\"","r":"The man says that even when he just sits and gazes at her in thought, she is angry: 'whom are you thinking of as you look me over?'","n":"Silence and adoration are his last resort, and they fail too. For Parimelazhagar, her logic is very neat: as no one woman could match all her parts, he must be comparing each part with a different woman, so there must be several. So she wants them named.","x":0,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kan","ninaivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:132"},"k:1321":{"book":"kural","num":1321,"t":["இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு ஆயினும் ஊடுதல்","வல்லது அவர்அளிக்கு மாறு"],"p":"Although my husband is free from defects, the way in which he embraces me is such as to make me feign dislike.","r":"The woman says that even though he has no fault, the way he shows his grace has the power to bring on a sulk.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":1,"c":["oodal","kaamam","parathai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","It is jealousy: his love is so delightful that others surely enjoy it too, and she can't stand the thought.","யான் எய்தற் பாலதாய இத்தலையளி ஒழிந்தாரும் எய்துவர் எனக்கருதி, அது பொறாமையான் ஊடல்நிகழா நின்றது"],["kalaignar","He reverses the direction: the quarrel, between faultless lovers, builds up their love.","அவர்களின் அன்பை மிகுதியாக வளர்க்கக் கூடியது."]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1322":{"book":"kural","num":1322,"t":["ஊடலின் தோன்றும் சிறுதுனி நல்லளி","வாடினும் பாடு பெறும்"],"p":"His love will increase though it may (at first seem to) fade through the short-lived distress caused by (my) dislike.","r":"The woman says that even if his fine grace droops a little through the small misery sulking brings, it rises to greatness.","n":"She is justifying the risk to a friend who has pointed out she could have his kindness without quarrelling. வாடினும், 'though it droop', uses a word for a plant that comes back stronger. Parimelazhagar keeps the concession small: a quarrel with no real fault passes quickly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1323":{"book":"kural","num":1323,"t":["புலத்தலின் புத்தேள்நாடு உண்டோ நிலத்தொடு","நீரியைந் தன்னார் அகத்து"],"p":"Is there a celestial land that can please like the feigned dislike of those whose union resembles that of earth and water?","r":"The woman asks if there is any heaven to compare with sulking with one to whom one is joined as water to earth.","n":"Readers of Tamil poetry will recall Kuruntokai 40 here, with its rain and red earth. I offer that as an echo. In the sequence Manakkudavar's text gives, these are the last words of the Kural.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","anbu"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","Water takes on the character of the ground it's in, so men take on the nature of the women they touch, which is the reason for her sulking. It's a surprisingly suspicious reading of a famous image.","நீர் தான் நின்ற நிலத்தியல்பிற்றாமாறு போலக் காதலரும் தாம் கூடிய மகளிரியல்பினராகலான்"],["manakkudavar","Earth and water together combine heat and coolness, and in the same way lovers experience joy and pain as one.","நிலத்தொடு நீரியை தலால் அவை வெப்பமுந்தட்பமுங் கூடியிருக்குமாறு போல்"],["kalaignar","புத்தேள்நாடு isn't a divine world but a new one, which removes heaven from the verse.","ஊடல் கொள்வதைவிடப் புதிய உலகம் வேறொன்று இருக்க முடியுமா?"]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1324":{"book":"kural","num":1324,"t":["புல்லி விடாஅப் புலவியுள் தோன்றுமென்","உள்ளம் உடைக்கும் படை"],"p":"In prolonged dislike after an embrace there is a weapon that can break my heart.","r":"Within the sulk that follows an embrace and won't let go arises the weapon that breaks 'my' heart; the speaker is the woman for Parimelazhagar and the man for Manakkudavar.","n":"Both identify the weapon: his bows and soft words for Parimelazhagar, and soft words (பணிமொழி) for Manakkudavar. Being broken is exactly what she is after, so the battle imagery here is pure pleasure. Compare the same weapon in 1258.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","nirai"],"pos":[["parimelazhagar","The woman answers the friend's question about what will end this sulk.","'அப்புலவி இனி யாதான் நீங்கும்?' என்றாட்குச் சொல்லியது."],["manakkudavar","He calls the group at this point the man's speech.","இவையெட்டும் தலைமகன் கூற்று ."]],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1325":{"book":"kural","num":1325,"t":["தவறிலர் ஆயினும் தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள்","அகறலின் ஆங்கொன் றுடைத்து"],"p":"Though free from defects, men feel pleased when they cannot embrace the delicate shoulders of those whom they love.","r":"The man, delighted after making up, says that though men may be faultless, being kept from the soft arms of those they love has a certain something.","n":"ஆங்கொன்று, 'a certain something', is an honest refusal to name it, and Parimelazhagar takes it that way: the pleasure is beyond words. Even the wrongly accused gets something from this. It matches 1321, in which she sulks at a man with no fault.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1326":{"book":"kural","num":1326,"t":["உணலினும் உண்டது அறல்இனிது காமம்","புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது"],"p":"To digest what has been eaten is more delightful than to eat more; likewise love is more delightful in dislike than intercourse.","r":"The man says digesting what you have eaten is sweeter than eating, and for love sulking is sweeter than union.","n":"Earthy and unusual, as digestion rarely comes into love poetry. The thought behind it is appetite: as Parimelazhagar says, hunger makes food better, and distance makes union better.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1327":{"book":"kural","num":1327,"t":["ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார் அதுமன்னும்","கூடலிற் காணப் படும்"],"p":"Those are conquerors whose dislike has been defeated and that is proved by the love (which follows).","r":"The man says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses wins, as can be seen when they come together.","n":"A paradox with a precise explanation in Parimelazhagar: the one who gives way first gets the great joy, and only later learns this. He adds the first-person point: I gave way, and so I got this joy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1328":{"book":"kural","num":1328,"t":["ஊடிப் பெறுகுவம் கொல்லோ நுதல்வெயர்ப்பக்","கூடலில் தோன்றிய உப்பு"],"p":"Will I enjoy once more through her dislike, the pleasure of that love that makes her forehead perspire?","r":"The man asks whether by sulking they might again win that savour which appeared in union, with her forehead wet with sweat.","n":"உப்பு, 'salt', stands for sweetness here, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as இனிமை, which he explains as all five senses at once, recalling 1101. It's a frankly physical detail near the end of the Kural, and Manakkudavar notes that sulking belongs to both of them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1329":{"book":"kural","num":1329,"t":["ஊடுக மன்னோ ஒளியிழை யாமிரப்ப","நீடுக மன்னோ இரா"],"p":"May the bright-jewelled one feign dislike, and may the night be prolonged for me to implore her!","r":"The man wishes that the bright-jewelled woman would keep on sulking, and that the night would last, so that he may go on pleading.","n":"The pleading has become what he wants, and not merely a way to get to what he wants. யாமிரப்ப, 'that we may plead': the man who began the book with his might broken now prays for the chance to beg.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"k:1330":{"book":"kural","num":1330,"t":["ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம் அதற்கின்பம்","கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்"],"p":"Dislike adds delight to love; and a hearty embrace (thereafter) will add delight to dislike.","r":"The man says sulking is love's pleasure, and the pleasure of sulking is being able to come together and embrace.","n":"In the received order, the last verse of the entire work ends on பெறின், 'if one gets to'. So 1,330 verses on virtue, wealth and love close with a chained definition and an open condition. Parimelazhagar explains the condition: knowing how far to go is the hard part, so that the union will still be possible.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"k:133"},"n:1":{"book":"naladiyar","num":1,"t":["அறுசுவை யுண்டி அமர்ந்தில்லாள் ஊட்ட","மறுசிகை நீக்கியுண் டாரும் - வறிஞராய்ச்","சென்றிரப்பர் ஓரிடத்துக் கூழ்எனின், செல்வம்ஒன்று","உண்டாக வைக்கற்பாற் றன்று."],"p":"Those who ate erewhile, course after course, food of six flavours, supplied by their complaisant spouse, now roam as paupers and beg a mess of pottage here and there; if so, let wealth be counted as a thing of nought!","r":"Those who once ate six-flavoured food served by a willing wife, waving away a second helping, may go begging for gruel somewhere; so wealth is not a thing to be counted as lasting.","n":"The detail of refusing the second mouthful sets the excess of the past against the single bowl of gruel begged later; the argument rests on a picture, not on a general claim.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:2":{"book":"naladiyar","num":2,"t":["துகள்தீர் பெருஞ்செல்வம் தோன்றியக்கால் தொட்டுப்","பகடு நடந்தகூழ் பல்லாரோ டுண்க","அகடுற யார்மாட்டும் நில்லாது செல்வம்","சகடக்கால் போல வரும்."],"p":"When you own ample wealth acquired by blameless means, with many sharing eat the grain that steers have trodden out! In centre poised prosperity stands with no man, but revolves like the waggon's wheel.","r":"When blameless wealth comes, from that very day share its food with many, because wealth never stays level with anyone but turns like a cart wheel.","n":"துகள்தீர் (blameless) qualifies the wealth to be shared: the verse assumes it was rightly gained. Mudaliar reads the food trodden out by beasts as a hint that others have a share in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:3":{"book":"naladiyar","num":3,"t":["யானை எருத்தம் பொலியக் குடைநிழற்கீழ்ச்","சேனைத் தலைவராய்ச் சென்றோரும் - ஏனை","வினைஉலப்ப வேறாகி வீழ்வர்தாம் கொண்ட","மனையாளை மாற்றார் கொள."],"p":"Those who rode resplendent forth on the neck of an elephant, beneath the state umbrella's shade, as the leaders of the host, when 'other deeds' destroy, shallchange and fall, while foes lead away their wives as captives.","r":"Even kings who rode in state on elephants under the royal umbrella, at the head of armies, fall and are changed when their deeds turn against them, and enemies take the wives they married.","n":"Both commentators take ஏனை வினை as evil deeds that destroy; Pope keeps it as 'other deeds'. The verse itself names only 'the other deeds', so the cause of the fall is left in the vocabulary of karma rather than of war.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","vinai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:4":{"book":"naladiyar","num":4,"t":["நின்றன நின்றன நில்லா எனவுணர்ந்து","ஒன்றின ஒன்றின வல்லே செயின்செய்க","சென்றன சென்றன வாழ்நாள் செறுத்துடன்","வந்தது வந்தது கூற்று."],"p":"The things of which you said, ‘they stand, they stand,' stand not; mark this, and perform what befits, yea! what befits, with all your power! Your days are gone, are gone! and death close pressing on is come, is come!","r":"Realise that the things you think will stand do not stand, and do what good you can at once, for your days are passing and Death is coming in anger.","n":"Mudaliar notes that the first two doublings express abundance and the last two continuity: the form of the verse enacts days going and Death arriving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","kootru","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:5":{"book":"naladiyar","num":5,"t":["என்னானும் ஒன்றுதம் கையுறப் பெற்றக்கால்","பின்னாவ தென்று பிடித்திரா - முன்னே","கொடுத்தார் உயப்போவர் கோடில்தீக் கூற்றம்","தொடுத்தாறு செல்லும் சுரம்."],"p":"When you have gained and hold in hand any single thing, retain it not with the thought, ‘This will serve some other day!’ Those who have given betimes shall escape the desert road along which death, an unyielding foe, drags his captives away.","r":"Whatever comes into your hand, do not hold it back thinking it can be given later; those who gave first will escape the waste land through which impartial Death drags his captives.","n":"கோடில் makes Death impartial: it is the one force that does not favour the rich, which is why only what was given away counts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:6":{"book":"naladiyar","num":6,"t":["இழைத்தநாள் எல்லை இகவா பிழைத்தெரிணஇக்","கூற்றம் குதித்துய்ந்தார் ஈங்கில்லை - ஆற்றப்","பெரும்பொருள் வைத்தீர், வழங்குமின், நாளைத்","தழீஇம் தழீஇம் தண்ணம் படும்."],"p":"Man's days pass not their assigned bound. None here on earth have ever escaped death's power, made off and got free. O hoarder of ample wealth, dispense it! On the morn the funeral drum will sound.","r":"The appointed span of days cannot be exceeded and no one here has leapt clear of Death and escaped; you who hold great wealth, give it away, for tomorrow the funeral drum may sound.","n":"Mudaliar glosses the 'appointed day' as the lifespan set by past deeds, and cites Kural 269 for the verb குதித்தல், the very verb the verse uses to deny that anyone escapes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","eegai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:7":{"book":"naladiyar","num":7,"t":["தோற்றம்சால் ஞாயிறு நாழியா வைகலும்","கூற்றம் அளந்துநும் நாளுண்ணும் - ஆற்ற","அறஞ்செய் தருளுடையீர் ஆகுமின் யாரும்","பிறந்தும் பிறவாதா ரில்."],"p":"Death every day takes that fount of light the sun as a measure, metes out your days, and so devours.Do deeds of virtue full of kindly grace. Though all are born, none are exempt from death.","r":"Using the sun as a measure, Death daily measures out and eats your days, so do much virtue and be compassionate; the closing line is read in more than one way.","n":"The image makes each sunrise a scoop of grain taken from one's store of days. Pope reads the last line as 'none who are born escape death', which is a third reading beside the two commentators'.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","arul","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Whoever fails to do this, though born human, is counted among the unborn, that is, with beasts.","அஃதாவது விலங்கொடு வைத்து எண்ணப்படுவர்."],["shanmuganar","Be compassionate like the great who have reached the state of no further birth.","பிறவா நிலை யெய்திய பெரியோர் போல அருள் உடையவராகுக!"]],"ch":"n:1"},"n:8":{"book":"naladiyar","num":8,"t":["செல்வர்யாம் என்றுதாம் செல்வுழி எண்ணாத","புல்லறி வாளர் பெருஞ்செல்வம் - எல்லில்","கருங்கொண்மூ வாய்திறந்த மின்னுப்போல் தோன்றி","மருங்கறக் கெட்டு விடும்."],"p":"The ample wealth of men of mean understanding, who say, ' We're rich,' yet ponder not their path and end, appears, and perishes, and leaves no trace; like the flash, when the black thunder-cloud by night opens its mouth.","r":"The great wealth of the petty-minded, who say 'we are rich' and never think of where they must go, appears like lightning from a dark cloud at night and vanishes without a trace.","n":"Both commentators take 'where they must go' as the next world, so the fault is not having wealth but forgetting the journey it cannot make.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","nilaiyaamai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:9":{"book":"naladiyar","num":9,"t":["உண்ணான் ஒளிநிறான் ஓங்கு புகழ்செய்யான்","துன்னருங் கேளிர் துயர்களையான் - கொன்னே","வழங்கான் பொருள்காத் திருப்பானேல், அஆ","இழந்தான்என் றெண்ணப் படும்."],"p":"He eats not, sheds no light of splendour around, performs no deeds that merit lofty praise, soothes no sorrow that choice friends feel, spends nought, but hoards his wealth in vain : “Aha! he's lost it all,' shall men pronounce.","r":"One who neither eats, nor wins honour, nor does deeds of renown, nor relieves the distress of close kin, nor gives, but only guards his wealth in vain, will be thought of with pity as one who has lost it.","n":"The miser is pronounced to have lost his wealth while he still holds it: possession without use is already loss.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","pugazh","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:10":{"book":"naladiyar","num":10,"t":["உடாஅதும் உண்ணாதும் தம்உடம்பு செற்றும்","கெடாஅத நல்லறமும் செய்யார் - கொடாஅது","வைத்தீட்டி னார்இழப்பர், வான்தோய் மலைநாட","உய்த்தீட்டும் தேனீக் கா஢."],"p":"Those who stint in clothes and food, and mortify their bodies, yet do not deeds of deathless virtue, and bestow nothing, hoarding shall suffer loss:— Lord of the cloud-capped hills !--this the hoarding honey-bee attests. ---------","r":"Those who stint on clothing and food, wear down their bodies, do no lasting good and give nothing, will lose what they hoard, as the honey bee's store shows.","n":"Mudaliar spells out the comparison: as someone else takes the honey the bees gathered, thieves and others take the hoard. The verse names only the bee as witness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:1"},"n:11":{"book":"naladiyar","num":11,"t":["நரைவரும் என்றெண்ணி நல்லறி வாளர்","குழவி யிடத்தே துறந்தார் - புரைதீரா","மன்னா இளமை மகிழ்ந்தாரே கோல்ஊன்றி","இன்னாங் கெழுந்திருப் பார்."],"p":"\"Grey eld will come,’—the wise remembering this renounce the world even in tender age; but they who joy in youth, unstable, never free from fault, shall erewhile painfully rise up leaning on a staff.","r":"Knowing that grey hair will come, the wise renounced in childhood itself, while those who delighted in faulty, fleeting youth will later rise painfully leaning on a staff.","n":"குழவியிடத்தே ('in infancy') pushes renunciation as early as language allows; the verse treats the foresight of age, not age itself, as the mark of wisdom.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","thuravu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:12":{"book":"naladiyar","num":12,"t":["நட்புநார் அற்றன நல்லாரும் அஃகினார்","அற்புத் தளையும் அவிழ்ந்தன - உட்காணாய்","வாழ்தலின் ஊதியம் என்னுண்டாம்? வந்ததே","ஆழ்கலத் தன் ன கலி."],"p":"Served are the ties of friendship; minished are the pleasant ones; love's bonds are loosened too; then look within and say, what profit is there in this joyous life of thine ? The cry comes up as from a sinking ship!","r":"The cords of friendship are cut, those once close have drawn back, the bonds of love have come loose: look within and ask what profit there is in living on, for misery has come as on a sinking ship.","n":"The verse is spoken to an ageing person, perhaps the speaker himself (உட்காணாய், 'look within'). Pope's 'joyous life' is his addition; the Tamil asks only about the gain of living.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","natpu","anbu","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","நல்லார் are women, whose affection has waned.","நல்லாரும் - மாதர்களும்."],["shanmuganar","நல்லார் are the learned and good, who have lessened their closeness.","நல்லறிஞரும் நெருக்கம் குறைத்தனர்;"]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:13":{"book":"naladiyar","num":13,"t":["சொல்தளர்ந்து கோல்ஊன்றிச் சோர்ந்த நடையினராய்ப்","பல்கழன்று பண்டம் பழிகாறும் - இல்செறிந்து","காம நெறிபடரும் கண்ணினார்க்கு இல்லையே","ஏம நெறிபடரு மாறு."],"p":"Speech falters, they lean on a staff, and walk tottering, their teeth fall out; yet, till the vessel (the body) is scorned by all, they linger in the house, still indulging fond desires; to these no way of safety opens out.","r":"Those whose speech fails, who lean on a staff, walk unsteadily and lose their teeth, yet stay in the house with their eyes on desire until the body is scorned, have no way to the path of safety.","n":"Both commentators take the 'path of safety' as the way to release and இல்செறிந்து as clinging to household life, so the verse faults not old age but desire that outlives the body's capacity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","ilvaazhkkai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:14":{"book":"naladiyar","num":14,"t":["தாழாத் தளராத் தலைநடுங்காத் தண்டுன்றா","வீழா இறக்கும் இவள்மாட்டும் - காழ்இலா","மம்மர்கொள் மாந்தர்க் கணங்காகும் தன்கைக்கோல்","அம்மனைக்கோல் ஆகிய ஞான்று."],"p":"To men that cherish weak desire for her that's doomed to droop and fail, supporting her palsied limbs with a staff, and then to fall and pass away, what anguish comes, when she grasps in her hands the staff her mother held!","r":"When a once-desired woman, bent, frail, head shaking and leaning on a staff, comes to hold the staff her mother held, she becomes a torment to men of unsteady mind who still desire her.","n":"அணங்கு is read by Mudaliar as an enchanting goddess and by Shanmuganar and Pope as pain; the line is hard, and the readings below differ on whose mother is meant.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","அம்மனைக்கோல் is the staff of her own mother.","அம்மனைக் கோல் - அவள் தாயின் ஊன்றுகோல்."],["shanmuganar","Her staff looks to the men like the staff their own mother leaned on, and that pains them.","இவள் ஊன்றும் கைத்தடி, தம் தாய் ஊன்றிய தடிபோல் தெரியும்போது"]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:15":{"book":"naladiyar","num":15,"t":["எனக்குத்தாய் ஆகியாள் என்னைஈங் கிட்டுத்","தனக்குத்தாய் நாடியே சென்றாள் - தனக்குத்தாய்","ஆகி யவளும் அதுவானால் தாய்த்தாய்க்கொண்டு","ஏகும் அளித்திவ் வுலகு."],"p":"My mother bare me, left me here, and went to seek her mother, who in the selfsame manner has gone in search; and thus in ceaseless round goes on the mother-quest. Such is the grace this world affords !","r":"My mother left me here and went in search of her mother, and that mother did the same; this world goes on, mother seeking mother, a pitiable thing.","n":"Shanmuganar reads the 'seeking' as taking a new birth, so the chain is the cycle of births; Pope and Mudaliar keep it as each generation following the last into death. அளித்து is read as 'pitiable' by Mudaliar's gloss எளிமையுடைத்து; Pope renders it ironically as 'grace'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:16":{"book":"naladiyar","num":16,"t":["வெறியயர் வெங்களத்து வேல்மகன் பாணி","முறியார் நறங்கண்ணி முன்னர்த் தயங்க","மறிகுள குண்டன்ன மன்னா மகிழ்ச்சி","அறிவுடை யாளர்கண் இல்."],"p":"The lamb in the ruddy slaughter-house will crop the fragrant shoots that dangle from the garland in the slayer's hand; such transient gladness of the thoughtless, youthful hour is never found amid the wise.","r":"The fleeting joy of youth, like a sacrificial lamb nibbling the tender leaves of the garland held before it in the slaughter ground, is not found among the wise.","n":"The garland that feeds the lamb is the sign of its slaughter: youthful pleasure is enjoyed in the presence of what ends it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","inbam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:17":{"book":"naladiyar","num":17,"t":["பனிபடு சோலைப் பயன்மர மெல்லாம்","கனியுதிர்ந்து வீழ்ந்தற் றிளமை - நனிபெரிதும்","வேல்கண்ணள் என்றிவளை வெஃகன்மின் மற்றிவளும்","கோல்கண்ண ளாகும் குனிந்து."],"p":"The sweet fruit from every tree that bears in the dewy grove must fall to earth. Thus youth decays. Desire not her whose eyes gleam bright as darts. Full soon she too will walk bent down, with a staff to aid her dim sight.","r":"Youth falls as fruit drops from every tree in a cool grove; do not desire this woman for her spear-like eyes, for she too will stoop and walk with a staff for eyes.","n":"The wordplay turns வேல்கண்ணள் (spear-eyed) into கோல்கண்ணள் (staff-eyed): the same compound form names her beauty and her decline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","kaamam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:18":{"book":"naladiyar","num":18,"t":["பருவம் எனைத்துள பல்லின்பால் ஏனை","இருசிகையும் உண்டீரோ என்று - வரிசையால்","உண்ணாட்டம் கொள்ளப் படுதலால் யாக்கைக்கோள்","எண்ணார் அறிவுடை யார்."],"p":"’How old are you ?' 'How last your teeth ?' and, ‘Do you eat two courses yet?' men ask with kindly courtesy. By such close questions urged, the wise will learn to judge the body as a thing of nought.","r":"Because others ask in turn, 'How old are you? How are your teeth? Do you still eat twice?', and so examine one's body, the wise do not count on the body's vigour.","n":"The questions are polite and routine; the verse's point is that such solicitude already treats one as declining. Mudaliar records two readings of இருசிகை, a second mouthful or two meals a day.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","ilamai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:19":{"book":"naladiyar","num":19,"t":["மற்றறிவாம் நல்வினை யாம்இளையம் என்னாது","கைத்துண்டாம் போழ்தே கரவா - தறம்செய்ம்மின்","முற்றி யிருந்த கனியொழியத் தீவளியால்","நற்காய் உதிர்தலும் உண்டு."],"p":"Say not, 'in after time we'll learn virtue, we're young;' but while wealth is yours conceal it not; do virtuous deeds. When evil tempests rage, not the ripe fruit alone, but the unripe fruit's fair promise also falls.","r":"Do not say 'we are young, we will learn good deeds later'; while wealth is in hand, do virtue without hiding it, for a harsh wind can drop good unripe fruit while the ripe stays on the tree.","n":"Mudaliar notes the alternative reading of கனி ஒழிய as 'the ripe fruit remaining on the tree', which sharpens the point: death may take the young and leave the old.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ilamai","aram","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:2"},"n:20":{"book":"naladiyar","num":20,"t":["ஆட்பார்த் துழலும் அருளில்கூற் றுண்மையால்","தோட்கோப்புக் காலத்தால் கொண்டுய்ம்மின் - பீட்பிதுக்கிப்","பிள்ளையைத் தாய்அலறக் கோடலான் மற்றதன்","கள்ளம் கடைப்பிடித்தல் நன்று."],"p":"Relentless death is roaming round, and eyes his man! 'Tis true. Take up your wallet, scape betimes. He bears away the new-born babe, while the mother sorely laments. It is good to bear in mind his guile. --------","r":"Since merciless Death roams looking for his people, take provisions for the journey in good time; it is well to keep his stealth in mind, for he takes even the unborn child while its mother wails.","n":"Mudaliar reads தோட்கோப்பு, a bundle carried on the shoulder, as the good deeds one needs for the next world; Pope renders it 'wallet'. The verse leaves the provisions as a figure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","ilamai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The shoulder-bundle is the stock of good deeds needed for the next world.","தோள் கோட்பு-தோளிற் கோக்கப்படுவது அஃதாவது கட்டுச்சோற்று மூட்டை; இங்கு மறுமைக்கும் வேண்டும். நல்வினைக்கு ஆயிற் றென்க."]],"ch":"n:2"},"n:21":{"book":"naladiyar","num":21,"t":["மலைமிசைத் தோன்றும் மதியம்போல் யானைத்","தலைமிசைக் கொண்ட குடையர் - நிலமிசைத்","துஞ்சினார் என்றெடுத்துத் தூற்றப்பட் டாரல்லால்","எஞ்சினார் இவ்வுலகத் தில்."],"p":"Even kings that rode on elephants beneath the state umbrella's shade, like the moon appearing over some bill, have had their names proclaimed on earth as dead ;— not any in this world have escaped. Time is fleeting,—use it As the measure of your days the shining orb each day unfailing rises; so before your joyous days have passed away, perform ye 'fitting deeds of grace'; for none abide on earth.","r":"Even kings who rode on elephants under umbrellas like the moon rising over a hill have been carried off and spoken of as dead; no one in this world has been left over.","n":"Mudaliar takes தூற்றப்பட்டார் as 'were disparaged by many', while Pope has 'had their names proclaimed' and Shanmuganar 'spoken of lightly'; whether the dead kings are mocked or merely announced is left open by the verb.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","arasu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:22":{"book":"naladiyar","num":22,"t":["வாழ்நாட் கலகா வயங்கொளி மண்டிலம்","வீழ்நாள் படாஅ தெழுதலால் - வாழ்நாள்","உலவாமுன் ஒப்புர வாற்றுமின் யாரும்","நிலவார் நிலமிசை மேல்."],"p":"","r":"Because the bright sun, as the measure of one's days, rises without missing a day, do ஒப்புரவு before your days run out; no one stays on the earth beyond them.","n":"ஒப்புரவு is the Kural's word for serving the community with one's means; Mudaliar cites Parimelazhagar's definition. He also says some read the last line in strained ways, without giving them. Pope's translation of this quatrain is attached to 21 in the source.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","oppuravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:23":{"book":"naladiyar","num":23,"t":["மன்றம் கறங்க மணப்பறை யாயின","அன்றவர்க் காங்கே பிணப்பறையாய்ப் - பின்றை","ஒலித்தலும் உண்டாமென்று உய்ந்துபோம் ஆறே","வலிக்குமாம் மாண்டார் மனம்."],"p":"The marriage drums that sounded out in the festive hall, there and that very day have served for him as funeral drums! Men of lofty minds will note that thus it haps, and will strive to gain the way to escape.","r":"The drums that sounded through the hall as wedding drums may sound that same day, in that same place, as funeral drums for the bridegroom; seeing this, the minds of the worthy hold fast to the way of escape.","n":"The same instruments serve both rites; the verse makes the reversal one of meaning, not of sound. Shanmuganar names the way of escape as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","yaakkai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:24":{"book":"naladiyar","num":24,"t":["சென்றே எறிப ஒருகால் சிறுவரை","நின்றே எறிப பறையினை - நன்றேகாண்","முக்காலைக் கொட்டினுள் மூடித்தீக் கொண்டுஎழுவர்","செத்தாரைச் சாவார் சுமந்து."],"p":"They march and then strike once! A little while they wait, then strike the drum a second time. Behold, how fine! The third stroke sounds. They veil it, take the fire, and go forth :—the dying bear the deed!","r":"The drummers beat once on arrival, wait a little and beat again, and at the third beat the corpse is covered and the fire taken up; the dying carry the dead.","n":"நன்றேகாண் ('fine, see!') is sarcasm for Mudaliar and a question, 'is such a life good?', for Shanmuganar; either way the last line carries the point, that the bearers are already on the same road.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:25":{"book":"naladiyar","num":25,"t":["கணம்கொண்டு சுற்றத்தார் கல்லென் றலறப்","பிணம்கொண்டு காட்டுய்ப்பார்க் கண்டும் - மணம் கொண்டீண்டு","உண்டுண்டுண் டென்னும் உணர்வினால் சாற்றுமே","டொண்டொண்டொ டென்னும் பறை."],"p":"To him, who, although he sees them bear the corpse to the burning ground, while friends in troops loudly lament, boldly asserts that wedded life is bliss on earth, the funeral drum speaks out, and mocks his vain utterance.","r":"To the one who sees kin wailing in crowds as a corpse is carried to the burning ground, yet marries and believes there is joy here, the funeral drum answers with its டொண் டொண் டொண்.","n":"The poem sets the man's 'உண்டு உண்டு உண்டு' (there is, there is) against the drum's 'டொண் டொண் டொண்'; Mudaliar calls the drum's reply a poetic fancy. Pope's 'mocks his vain utterance' paraphrases that echo.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","inbam","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:26":{"book":"naladiyar","num":26,"t":["நார்த்தொடுத் தீர்க்கிலென் நன்றாய்ந் தடக்கிலென்","பார்த்துழிப் பெய்யிலென் பல்லோர் பழிக்கிலென்","தோற்பையுள் நின்று தொழிலறச் செய்தூடும்","கூத்தன் புறப்பட்டக் கால்."],"p":"When the 'soul', that, taking its stand in this skin clad frame, has fully wrought its works, and partaken of life's experiences, has gone forth, what matters it whether you attach ropes to the body and drag it away, or carefully bury it, or throw it aside in any place you light upon, or if many revile the departed ?","r":"Once the dancer who stayed in this skin bag and did its work has left, what does it matter whether the body is dragged off with a rope, buried with care, thrown anywhere or reviled by many?","n":"The life is a performer and the body a costume; once it has gone, the treatment of the remains has no meaning. Mudaliar lists several readings of ஊட்டும், among them the life enjoying, or being made to enjoy, the fruit of its deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","uyir","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:27":{"book":"naladiyar","num":27,"t":["படுமழை மொக்குளின் பல்காலும் தோன்றிக்","கெடுமிதோர் யாக்கையென் றெண்ணித் - தடுமாற்றம்","தீர்ப்பேம்யாம் என்றுணரும் திண்ணறி வாளரை","நேர்ப்பார்யார் நீணிலத்தின் மேல்."],"p":"'Like a bubble, that in pelting rain appears full oft, and disappears, is this our frame.' So sages have judged, steadfast in wisdom, and have decided to end this dubious strife. On this wide earth who equal these?","r":"Who on this wide earth equals those firm in understanding who see this body as a bubble in pelting rain that forms and bursts again and again, and resolve to end their wandering?","n":"'Again and again' (பல்காலும்) suggests repeated births as well as the bubble's quickness, and Mudaliar takes தடுமாற்றம் as the confusion of births. Shanmuganar reads it as worldly confusion.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:28":{"book":"naladiyar","num":28,"t":["யாக்கையை யாப்புடைத்தாப் பெற்றவர் தாம்பெற்ற","யாக்கையா லாய பயன்கொள்க - யாக்கை","மலையாடு மஞ்சுபோல் தோன்றிமற் றாங்கே","நிலையாது நீத்து விடும்."],"p":"Those who've gained and held fast by this well-knit frame (a human body) should take the gain the body they have gained is intended to yield. Like a cloud that wanders over the hills, the body here appears, and abiding not, departs leaving no trace behind.","r":"Those who have a sturdy body should take the fruit that body can yield, for it appears like mist on a hill and departs there without staying.","n":"The verse does not despise the body here; it treats it as a means whose use must be taken quickly. Shanmuganar names the fruit as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","payan","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:29":{"book":"naladiyar","num":29,"t":["புல்நுனிமேல் நீர்போல் நிலையாமை என்றெண்ணி","இன்னினியே செய்க அறவினை - இன்னினியே","நின்றான் இருந்தான் கிடந்தான்தன் கேள்அலறச்","சென்றான் எனப்படுத லால்."],"p":"Considering that all things are transient as the dew-drop on the tip of a blade of grass, now, now at once, do virtuous deeds! \"Even now he stood, he sat, he fell—while his kindred cried aloud he died :' such is man's history!","r":"Knowing that the body lasts like water on the tip of a blade of grass, do virtuous deeds right now, for people say 'he stood, he sat, he lay down, and with his kin wailing he was gone'.","n":"The four verbs compress a death into one breath of speech, the way it will be reported; Mudaliar supplies 'the body' as the subject of நிலையாமை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:30":{"book":"naladiyar","num":30,"t":["கேளாதே வந்து கிளைகளாய் இல்தோன்றி","வாளாதே போவரால் மாந்தர்கள் - வாளாதே","சேக்கை மரன்ஒழியச் சேண்நீங்கு புள்போல","யாக்கை தமர்க்கொழிய நீத்து."],"p":"Unasked men come, appear in the home as kinsmen, and then silently go. As the bird silently deserts the tree where its nest yet remains, and goes far off, so these leave but their body to their friends. -----------","r":"Unasked they come and appear in the home as kin, and wordlessly they go, leaving the body to their own people as a bird flies far off leaving its nesting tree.","n":"The double வாளாதே (silently) frames a life with two unannounced events, arrival and departure; family is a place of passage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","sutram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:3"},"n:31":{"book":"naladiyar","num":31,"t":["அகத்தாரே வாழ்வார்என் றண்ணாந்து நோக்கிப்","புகத்தாம் பெறாஅர் பறங்கடை பற்றி","மிகத்தாம் வருந்தி இருப்பரே மேலைத்","தவத்தால் தவம்செய்யா தார்."],"p":"‘Yet those within are blest,' so saying, they look up, but obtain no entrance: their place is at the outer gate. There will they suffer much, who thro' lack of former penitence do no penance now. [In a former state penance won for them a human shape. As men they have now failed.]","r":"Those who, on the strength of earlier austerity, do no austerity now will look up at a house and say 'those inside are the ones who live', unable to enter, clinging to the outer door and grieving.","n":"The quatrain implies merit is a stock that is used up: present comfort is paid for by past austerity, and without renewal the next state is the beggar's at the gate. Pope's main text reads the cause as a lack of former penance, but his bracketed note agrees with Shanmuganar that past penance won their present state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai","iravu"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","Those who enjoyed the fruit of past virtue and do none in this birth will stand outside in their next birth.","முற்பிறப்பில் செய்த அறத்தால் (தவத்தால்) இன்பம் துய்த்து இப்பிறப்பில் அறம் செய்யாதவர்கள்,"]],"ch":"n:4"},"n:32":{"book":"naladiyar","num":32,"t":["ஆவாம்நாம் ஆக்கம் நசைஇ அறமறந்து","போவாம்நாம் என்னாப் புலைநெஞ்சே - ஓவாது","நின்றுஞற்றி வாழ்தி எனினும்நின் வாழ்நாள்கள்","சென்றன செய்வ துரை."],"p":"Say not, O silly soul, we will live desiring wealth and die forgetting virtue! We'll say that ceaselessly toiling thou shalt live long; but tell me, what wilt thou do when all thy happy days are over?","r":"Base heart, which says 'let us prosper, desiring wealth and forgetting virtue', even if you live on toiling without rest, your days are gone: tell me what you will do.","n":"The address is to one's own heart (புலைநெஞ்சே), and the closing question is left unanswered; the speaker is arguing with himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:33":{"book":"naladiyar","num":33,"t":["வினைப்பயன் வந்தக்கால் வெய்ய உயிரா","மனத்தின் அழியுமாம் பேதை - நினைத்ததனைத்","தொல்லைய தென்றுணர் வாரே தடுமாற்றத்து","எல்லை இகந்தொருவு வார்."],"p":"When the ‘fruit of deeds' is come, the fool sighs heavily, and all his soul dies out; but those who reflect and say, ,'Tis old desert,' will pass beyond the bound of life's perplexity, and escape (by devoting themselves to virtue).","r":"When the fruit of past deeds arrives, the fool sighs hotly and his mind breaks; those who think of what comes as old desert pass beyond the bounds of confusion and are freed.","n":"Accepting suffering as the ripening of one's own past deeds is presented as the way out of the round itself, not only as consolation. Pope's 'by devoting themselves to virtue' is his addition.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","samanilai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:34":{"book":"naladiyar","num":34,"t":["அரும்பெறல் யாக்கையைப் பெற்ற பயத்தால்","பெரும்பயனும் ஆற்றவே கொள்க - கரும்பூர்ந்த","சாறுபோல் சாலவும் பின்உதவி மற்றதன்","கோதுபோல் போகும் உடம்பு."],"p":"As the gain from the mortal frame now reached —and which is so hard to reach —with all thy might lay hold of virtue's lasting good. As the juice expressed from the sugar-cane 'twill afterwards be thine aid, when the body goes like refuse flung away.","r":"With the gain of having obtained this rare body, take its great fruit fully; like juice pressed from cane, it serves you afterwards, and the body goes like the crushed refuse.","n":"The body is valued only as the cane is: for what can be pressed out of it. The juice, virtue, outlasts the body and is what helps 'afterwards'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:35":{"book":"naladiyar","num":35,"t":["கரும்பாட்டிக் கட்டி சிறுகாலைக் கொண்டார்","துரும்பெழுந்து வேம்கால் துயராண் டுழவார்","வருந்தி உடம்பின் பயன்கொண்டார் கூற்றம்","வருங்கால் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"Those who have pressed the sugar-cane, and early taken the juice, when the refuse heaped up burns, will suffer no grief: those who have toiled and gained the fruit won from embodied existence will feel no pangs when death shall come.","r":"As those who crushed their cane and took the jaggery early do not grieve when the refuse catches fire and burns, those who laboured to take the body's fruit feel no distress when Death comes.","n":"சிறுகாலை (early) carries the argument: the jaggery was made in time. The burning of refuse quietly suggests cremation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kootru","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:36":{"book":"naladiyar","num":36,"t":["இன்றுகொல் அன்றுகொல் என்றுகொல் என்னாது","பின்றையே நின்றது கூற்றமென் றெண்ணி","ஒருவுமின் தீயவை ஒல்லும் வகையால்","மருவுமின் மாண்டார் அறம்."],"p":"‘This day ?’ That day? What day? O question not the time! Bethink you death stands behind you ever waiting! Put from you every evil thing; and with all your powers embrace the virtue which sages teach.","r":"Without asking whether it is today, that day or some other day, think that Death stands right behind you; leave evil as far as you can and hold to the virtue the worthy practise.","n":"ஒல்லும் வகையால் (as far as you can) sets a measure within reach, unlike the absolute demands of renunciation elsewhere in these chapters.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kootru","aram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:37":{"book":"naladiyar","num":37,"t":["மக்களா லாய பொரும்பயனும் ஆயுங்கால்","எத்துணையும் ஆற்றப் பலவானால் - தொக்க","உடம்பிற்கே ஒப்புராவு செய்தொழுகா தும்பர்க்","கிடந்துண்ணப் பண்ணப் படும்."],"p":"When you examine closely the mighty gains to be acquired by birth in a human shape, if they seem manifold, perform not deeds which suit the body's frame alone, but deeds whose fruit is joy in the world to come!","r":"If the gains that come through human birth are many when examined, one should not live doing service only to the gathered-up body, but should act so as to enjoy them in the world above.","n":"The verse applies ஒப்புரவு, normally service to the community, to the body itself, ironically: serving the body is a misplaced kind of duty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","aram","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:38":{"book":"naladiyar","num":38,"t":["உறக்கும் துணையதோர் ஆலம்வித் தீண்டி","இறப்ப நிழற்பயந் தாஅங்கு - அறப்பயனும்","தான்சிறி தாயினும் தக்கார்கைப் பட்டக்கால்","வான்சிறிதாப் போர்த்து விடும்."],"p":"The banyan seed, though it be minute as one might see in dreams, grows to a mighty tree of amplest shade; so gifts from a virtuous hand, received by a worthy hand, though small, will hide the diminish'd heavens.","r":"As a banyan seed of the smallest size grows and casts a vast shade, so the fruit of virtue, though small, when it reaches the hands of the worthy, spreads so that the sky seems small.","n":"The growth depends on the recipient: தக்கார் கைப்பட்டக்கால் (if it falls into worthy hands). Pope reads உறக்கும் துணைய as 'minute as one might see in dreams'; Shanmuganar renders the size differently, but both make the seed tiny.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:39":{"book":"naladiyar","num":39,"t":["வைகலும் வைகல் வரக்கண்டும் அஃதுணரார்","வைகலும் வைகலை வைகுமென் றின்புறுவர்","வைகலும் வைகல்தம் வாழ்நாள்மேல் வைகுதல்","வைகலை வைத்துணரா தார்."],"p":"Daily they see the passing day added to the sum of the days gone by, as a day that is spent from out the store of their days, yet daily, as they see day dawn, they say joyously, 'This day will abide with us till the close of day?’","r":"Seeing day follow day, they do not grasp it, and rejoice each day that the day will pass well; they do not count each passing day as a day taken from their own lives.","n":"The quatrain plays வைகல் (day; passing) through eight forms, so the sound of repetition becomes the experience it describes. Mudaliar reports another reading of வைகுதல் as 'staying', and supplies the unstated conclusion: the wise would act on virtue at once.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:40":{"book":"naladiyar","num":40,"t":["மான அருங்கலம் நீக்கி இரவென்னும்","ஈன இளிவினால் வாழ்வேன்மன் - ஈனத்தால்","ஊட்டியக் கண்ணும் உறுதிசேர்ந் திவ்வுடம்பு","நீட்டித்து நிற்கும் எனின்."],"p":"Parting with honour's jewel I might still consent to live a suppliant's life of shame, if when maintained by such disgrace, this body could abide in strength and last for length of days. --------------","r":"I would put off the rare jewel of honour and live by the shame of begging, if a body fed by such disgrace would gain strength and last.","n":"The conditional is counterfactual: the body will not last, so honour is never worth selling for it. Mudaliar notes the particle மன் signals exactly this unfulfilled 'I would'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","iravu","yaakkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:4"},"n:41":{"book":"naladiyar","num":41,"t":["மாக்கேழ் மடநல்லாய் என்றரற்றும் சான்றவர்","நோக்கார்கொல் நொய்யதோர் புக்கிலை-யாக்கைக்கோர்","ஈச்சிற கன்னதோர் தோல்அறினும் வேண்டுமே","காக்கை கடிவதோர் கோல்."],"p":"‘O gentle maiden, fair and good !' These paragons that thus rave, know they not 'the heavenly home invisible?’ Let a bit of skin be broken slight as an insect's wing, and you need the stick that drives a way the crows !","r":"The 'worthies' who cry 'O fair, lovely girl': do they not see the body's paltry dwelling? If skin the size of a fly's wing is torn from it, a stick is needed to drive away the crows.","n":"Both commentators take புக்கில் as the body, a temporary lodging of the life; Pope renders it 'the heavenly home invisible'. Mudaliar notes that சான்றவர் (worthies) is used ironically for the petty-minded. The crows show how little stands between the admired body and carrion.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","pullarivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The புக்கில் is the body itself, a lodging where the life stays for a while.","துச்சிலை-(உயிரானது ஒதுக்குக் குடியிருக்கும்) வீடாகிய உடம்பின் தன்மையை."],["shanmuganar","The admirers do not know that the body is a paltry shelter.","உடம்பு அற்பமான ஓர் ஒதுக்கிடம் என்பதை அறியார் போலும் !"]],"ch":"n:5"},"n:42":{"book":"naladiyar","num":42,"t":["தோல்போர்வை மேலும் தொளைபலவாய்ப் பொய்ம்மறைக்கும்","மீப்போர்வை மாட்சித் துடம்பானால் - மீப்போர்வை","பொய்ம்மறையாக் காமம் புகலாது மற்றதனைப்","பைம்மறியாப் பார்க்கப் படும்."],"p":"If the body which, with a covering of skin above, possesses many apertures, owes its beauty only to that outward cloak that veils the false (the inner foulness), then is it fitting to say no word of lustful desire which hides the false from itself by the covering veil, but to regard that body as an undeveloped embryo.","r":"If the body, full of openings beneath its skin, is handsome only through an outer covering that hides its falsity, then without speaking desire one should look at it as a bag turned inside out.","n":"The commentators agree on the bag turned inside out; Pope instead renders பைம்மறி as 'an undeveloped embryo'. The verse asks for a change of viewpoint, not a change in the body.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Look at the body as one looks at a bag turned so that the inside faces out.","பை மறியா-பையைப் போல,(உட்பக்கம் வெளிப் பக்கமாம்படி) மாற்றியதாக."],["shanmuganar","Turn the body inside out like a bag and its truth becomes plain.","பையைத் திருப்பிப் பார்ப்பதுபோல் அவ்வுடலைத் திருப்பிப் பார்த்தால் உண்மை விளங்கும்."]],"ch":"n:5"},"n:43":{"book":"naladiyar","num":43,"t":["தக்கோலம் தின்று தலைநிறையப் பூச் சூடி","பொய்க்கோலம் செய்ய ஒழியுமே - எக்காலும்","உண்டி வினையுள் உறைக்கும் எனப்பெரியோர்","கண்டுகை விட்ட மயல்."],"p":"Will impurity ever cease from the worthless body which the great have abandoned, knowing it to be reeking with odours from processes connected with nutrition, though aromatics be chewed, the head covered with garlands, and the body adornedwith false splendour?","r":"Will chewing fragrant spices, filling the head with flowers and putting on a false finery remove the foulness the great saw and abandoned, which is always strengthened by the work of eating?","n":"Mudaliar takes மயல் as the impurities of a woman's body; Shanmuganar applies it to the body in general. The verse says only that the great saw it and gave it up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","undi","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:44":{"book":"naladiyar","num":44,"t":["தெண்ணீர்க் குவளை பொருகயல் வேலென்று","கண்ணில்புன் மாக்கள் கவற்ற விடுவேனோ","உண்ணீர் களைந்தக்கால் நுங்குசூன் றிட்டன்ன","கண்ணீர்மை கண்டொழுகு வேன்."],"p":"Shall I abandon (my ascetic purpose) because blind, low men worry me, saying (that woman's eye is like) the water-lily in the clear stream, or the warring carp, or a javelin? I will pursue my virtuous way as having seen (that) the eye's real nature (is), like (that of) the palm-tree fruit (which is) scooped out, after the water has been drained off!","r":"Shall I give way when blind and petty men trouble me by calling eyes water-lilies in clear water, darting carp or spears? I will go on my way having seen what an eye is: like a palm fruit scooped out once its water is drained.","n":"The speaker is a renunciant guarding his resolve; the verse is personal resistance rather than general teaching. கண்ணில் (eyeless) turns the praisers' subject back on them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:45":{"book":"naladiyar","num":45,"t":["முல்லை முகைமுறுவல் முத்தென் றிவைபிதற்றும்","கல்லாப்புன் மாக்கள் கவற்ற விடுவெனோ","எல்லாரும் காணப் புறங்காட் டுதிர்ந்துக்க","பல்லென்பு கண்டொழுகு வேன3"],"p":"Though worthless men untaught should fret my soul and rave of teeth like jasmine buds and pearls, shall I forego my fixed resolve, who have seen in the burning ground those bones—the fallen teeth —strewn round for all to see ?","r":"Shall I give way when untaught petty men chatter that smiles are jasmine buds and pearls? I will go on my way having seen the teeth and bones fallen and scattered in the burning ground for all to see.","n":"A companion to 44 with the same frame; the evidence here is not anatomy but the cremation ground, so impurity and impermanence meet.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:46":{"book":"naladiyar","num":46,"t":["குடரும் கொழுவும் குருதியும் என்பும்","தொடரும் நரம்பொடு தோலும் - இடையிடையே","வைத்த தடியும் வழும்புமாம் மற்றிவற்றுள்","எத்திறத்தாள் ஈர்ங்கோதை யாள்."],"p":"(The body) is entrails, and marrow, and blood, and bone, and connecting tendons, and skin, and here and there flesh interposed, and fat. In the midst of these, what sort of a being is she who wears the fresh garlands?","r":"Entrails, fat, blood, bone, linking sinews, skin, and flesh and grease set here and there: among these, which part is the woman with the fresh garland?","n":"The question is a search for the person among the parts, and implies she is none of them; the verse uses the list, not argument, to dissolve the beloved.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:47":{"book":"naladiyar","num":47,"t":["ஊறி உவர்த்தக்க ஒன்பது வாய்ப்புலனும்","கோதிக் குழம்பலைக்கும் கும்பத்தைப் - பேதை","பெருந்தோளி பெய்வளாய் என்னுமீப் போர்த்த","கருந்தோலால் கண்விளக்கப் பட்டு."],"p":"The fool will address the earthen pot (the body), from which defilement oozes, which from nine disgusting outlets scatters pollution, and in which slimy liquids move to and fro—and say, 'O thou of the rounded arms,' ‘O thou with armlets decked, ‘—because it is made bright to his eyes by a covering of black skin.","r":"The fool calls out 'broad-shouldered one, bangle-wearer' to a pot that seeps filth from nine openings and churns with slime, because its covering of dark skin dazzles his eyes.","n":"The terms of address the fool uses are the stock epithets of love poetry, so the verse aims at a literary convention as well as at desire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:48":{"book":"naladiyar","num":48,"t":["பண்டம் அறியார் படுசாந்தும் கோதையும்","கண்டுபா ராட்டுவார் கண்டிலர்கொல் - மண்டிப்","பெடைச்சேவல் வன்கழுகு பேர்த்திட்டுக் குத்தும்","முடைச்சாகா டச்சிற் றுழி."],"p":"They know not what the body is; with sandal paste and flowers they make it fine. Have they not seen, I pray, the vultures and their mates in flocks with busy beaks devour the body foul when the chariot-axle is snapt?","r":"Those who praise sandal paste and garlands without knowing what the body is: have they not seen vultures, male and female, crowd in and tear at the stinking cart once its axle breaks?","n":"Mudaliar reads the axle as the life and the cart as the body. The cart image recurs from 2, where wealth turned like a wheel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:49":{"book":"naladiyar","num":49,"t":["கழிந்தார் இடுதலை கண்டார்நெஞ் சுட்கக்","குழிந்தாழ்ந்த கண்ணவாய்த் தோன்றி - ஒழிந்தாரைப்","போற்றி நெறிநின்மின் இற்றிதன் பண்பென்று","சாற்றுங்கொல் சாலச் சிரித்து."],"p":"The skulls of the dead, at the sight of which the gazer fears, with deep cavernous eyes appear, and grinning say to those who still survive, 'Guard well! In virtue's path stand fast. This is the body's grace and worth.''","r":"The skulls of the dead lying about, with deep sunken eye-holes that frighten those who see them, seem to laugh loudly and tell the living 'guard yourselves and keep to the path: this is what the body comes to'.","n":"The speech is a poetic fancy, as Mudaliar notes (தற்குறிப்பேற்றம்). He names the path as renunciation; Shanmuganar names it as virtue.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","nilaiyaamai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:50":{"book":"naladiyar","num":50,"t":["உயிர்போயார் வெண்டலை உட்கச் சிரித்துச்","செயிர்தீர்க்கும் செம்மாப் பவரைச் - செயிர்தீர்ந்தார்","கண்டிற் றிதன்வண்ண மென்பதனால் தம்மையோர்","பண்டத்துள் வைப்ப திலர்."],"p":"The skulls of the dead, grinning so as to excite disgust, cure the vain lovers of life of their folly. Those who are cured of this folly, seeing (the skulls in the burning ground), say “such is this body,' and so value themselves as nothing. --------------","r":"The white skulls of the dead, grinning so as to frighten, cure the infatuated of their fault; those so cured, seeing that this is the body's nature, do not count themselves as anything of worth.","n":"Mudaliar takes the infatuated as men besotted with women and 'themselves' as their bodies; Shanmuganar takes it as the proud, cured of arrogance. The verse's own words allow both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["yaakkai","kaamam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:5"},"n:51":{"book":"naladiyar","num":51,"t":["விளக்குப் புகஇருள் மாய்ந்தாங்கு ஒருவன்","தவத்தின்முன் நில்லாதாம் பாவம் - விளக்குநெய்","தேய்விடத்துச் சென்றிருள் பாய்ந்தாங்கு நல்வினை","தீர்விடத்து நிற்குமாம் தீது."],"p":"As when a lamp enters darkness dies, so sin stands not before man's penitence. As, when in a lamp the oil wastes, darkness rushes in, so evil takes its stand where deeds of virtue cease.","r":"As darkness dies when a lamp comes in, sin cannot stand before a person's austerity; and as darkness rushes back when the lamp's oil runs out, evil settles in once good deeds are exhausted.","n":"The second half limits the first: austerity protects only while the merit that feeds it lasts, so the lamp makes merit something burned up, not something owned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Austerity here stands for the whole way of renunciation.","தவம் என்றது ஈண்டுத் துறவறத்தின் மேனின்றது."]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:52":{"book":"naladiyar","num":52,"t":["நிலையாமை நோய்மூப்புச் சாக்காடென் றெண்ணித்","தலையாயார் தங்கருமம் செய்வார் - தொலைவில்லாச்","சத்தமும் சோதிடமும் என்றாங் கிவைபிதற்றும்","பித்தரின் பேதையார் இல்."],"p":"The chief of men reflect on change, disease, old age, and death, and do their needful work. Those who raving teach the endless round of sevenfold science, and the lore of stars, are maddest of the foolish throng.","r":"The best of people reflect on impermanence, disease, old age and death and do the work that is truly theirs; none are more foolish than the madmen who prattle endlessly of சத்தம் and astrology.","n":"The target is not learning as such but study that never ends, pursued in place of what death makes urgent. 'Their own work' (தங்கருமம்) echoes Kural 266, which Mudaliar quotes.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","thavam","kalvi","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","'Their own work' is austerity, and the endless lore is grammar and mathematics or astrology, tool-sciences studied for a lifetime instead of the way of renunciation.","தங் கருமம்-தமக்கு உறுதியைச் செய்யுந் தொழிலாகிய தவத்தையே"],["shanmuganar","The wise do the good deeds fitting for them, and the endless lore is Vedic sastra and astrology.","மேலோர் தமக்கேற்ற நல்வினைகளைச் செய்வர். எனவே, வேத சாத்திரம், சோதிடம் என்றெல்லாம் பிதற்றுகிற"]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:53":{"book":"naladiyar","num":53,"t":["இல்லம் இளமை எழில்வனப்பு மீக்கூற்றம்","செல்வம் வலிஎன் றிவையெல்லாம் - மெல்ல","நிலையாமை கண்டு நெடியார் துறப்பர்","தலையாயார் தாம்உய்யக் கொண்டு."],"p":"The chief of men in quite thought discern how house, and youth, and beauty's grace, and high estate, and wealth, and strength, all pass away; and thus, to save themselves, prolonging not the time, renounce all these.","r":"Seeing that home, youth, beauty, high repute, wealth and strength all fail to last, the best of people, considering slowly, renounce them without delay so as to save themselves.","n":"மெல்ல and நெடியார் pull two ways: the examination is unhurried, the renouncing is not. Mudaliar keeps both, glossing one as deliberate inquiry and the other as acting without delay.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","nilaiyaamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:54":{"book":"naladiyar","num":54,"t":["துன்பம் பலநாள் உழந்தும் ஒருநாளை","இன்பமே காமுறுவர் ஏழையார் - இன்பம்","இடைதெரிந் தின்னாமை நோக்கி மனையாறு","அடைவொழிந்தார் ஆன்றமைந் தார்."],"p":"Though wretched men suffer afflictions many a day, yet one day's delight they eagerly desire. The men of calm and full wisdom, in pleasure's core see pain, and quit the pleasant household paths.","r":"Fools, after many days of suffering, still long for a single day's pleasure; the full and settled, discerning pleasure for what it is and seeing its pain, keep off the household way.","n":"The verse names household life (மனையாறு) as what the wise leave, which makes it a direct answer to the Kural's defence of the householder.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","inbam","ilvaazhkkai","idumbai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The wise weigh, impartially, how small household pleasure is.","இன்பத்தின் சிறுமையை நடுவுநின்று ஆராய்ந்து அறிந்து"],["shanmuganar","The wise see that pleasure comes only now and then, a little at a time, and pain is the greater part.","இன்பம் இடையிடையே சிறிதளவு வருவதறிந்தும்"]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:55":{"book":"naladiyar","num":55,"t":["கொன்னே கழிந்தன் றிளமையும் இன்னே","பிணியொடு மூப்பும் வருமால் - துணிவொன்றி","என்னொடு சூழா தெழுநெஞ்சே போதியோ","நன்னெறி சேர நமக்கு."],"p":"In vain is my youth spent. Even now disease and old age will come. O soul ! be bold; wrangle no more with me, but rise! Wilt thou not go where both thou and I may gain virtue's path?","r":"The speaker tells his own heart that youth has passed to no purpose and disease and old age are coming now, and asks it to stop going its own way and set out with him on the good path.","n":"The heart is treated as a separate, wayward party to be persuaded, not commanded. Mudaliar takes the good path to be release through renunciation; Shanmuganar says only 'a good way'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","manam","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:56":{"book":"naladiyar","num":56,"t":["மாண்ட குணத்தொடு மக்கட்பே றில்லெனினும்","பூண்டான் கழித்தற் கருமையால் - பூண்ட","மிடியென்னும் காரணத்தின் மேன்முறைக் கண்ணே","கடியென்றார் கற்றறிந் தார்."],"p":"Though your wife possess no excellence and bear no child, it is hard to get rid of the marriage bond. For this cause since he who weds puts sorrow on, in olden days the learned made marriage a synonym of dread.","r":"Even if a wife lacks good qualities and bears no children, the husband cannot put her away; because marriage brings this hardship, the learned gave it the name கடி.","n":"The argument turns on a pun, since கடி means both marriage and 'shun'. The verse finds no fault in marriage to a good wife; it argues from the worst case that the bond, once made, can't be undone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","idumbai","thuravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The word கடி has both senses, marriage and removal, and the poet invents a reason for the name, a figure Mudaliar calls நிருக்தி.","'கடி' என்னுஞ் சொற்கு 'மணம்' 'நீக்கு' ஆகிய இரண்டு பொருள்க ளுண்டு."]],"ch":"n:6"},"n:57":{"book":"naladiyar","num":57,"t":["ஊக்கித்தாம் கொண்ட விரதங்கள் உள்ளுடையத்","தாக்கருந் துன்பங்கள் தாந்தலை வந்தக்கால்","நீக்கி நிறூஉம் உரவோரே நல்லொழுக்கம்","காக்கும் திருவத் தவர்."],"p":"When troubles arise, hard to resist, to cause them to break the vows which their lofty spirits have pledged, the men of power set griefs aside and firmly fix their souls in right. These are the blessed, guarding decorum's rule.","r":"When unbearable troubles come that would break the vows they took on with zeal, those who push the troubles aside and hold the vows firm are the blessed ones who guard good conduct.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","porai","thitpam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:58":{"book":"naladiyar","num":58,"t":["தம்மை யிகழ்ந்தமை தாம்பொறுப்ப தன்றிமற்று","எம்மை இகழ்ந்த வினைப்பயத்தால் - உம்மை","எரிவாய் நிரயத்து வீழ்வர்கொல் என்று","பரிவதூஉம் சான்றோர் கடன்."],"p":"To bear with those that speak contemptuous words; yea, more, to say, 'Ah, will these sink in the other world to hell, the place of fire, as fruit of their contemptuous words;' and to grieve, is duty of the perfect man.","r":"Bearing contempt is not enough: the worthy also grieve for those who scorned them, fearing that the deed will drop them into the fiery hell.","n":"The pity looks to the offender's future, not to one's own injury, so forbearance becomes a form of compassion. Parimelazhagar cites this quatrain when glossing Kural 157.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","arul","vinai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:59":{"book":"naladiyar","num":59,"t":["மெய்வாய்கண் மூக்குச் செவியெனப் பேர்பெற்ற","ஐவாய வேட்கை அவாவினைக் - கைவாய்","கலங்காமல் காத்துய்க்கும் ஆற்றல் உடையான்","விலங்காது வீடு பெறும்."],"p":"He who undisturbed, in the ordered way of right, has power to guard and guide the desires and lusts that find entrance by the five sense-gates, called 'body, mouth, eye, nose, and ear' - unfailing shall gain 'release.'","r":"One who has the strength to guard and steer, without wavering, the craving that enters through the five gates named body, mouth, eyes, nose and ears will surely reach release.","n":"The senses are called five mouths (ஐவாய) through which craving feeds; the verse asks for them to be steered, not destroyed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","avaa","veedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:60":{"book":"naladiyar","num":60,"t":["துன்பமே மீதூரக் கண்டும் துறவுள்ளார்","இன்பமே காமுறுவர் ஏழையார் - இன்பம்","இசைதொறும் மற்றதன் இன்னாமை நோக்கிப்","பசைதல் பரியாதாம் மேல்."],"p":"Though wretched men behold afflictions urge and press, renunciation is not in their thoughts; delight they eagerly desire. The great in every joy behold its pain, and seek it not. -----","r":"Fools, though they see suffering mount, have no thought of renouncing and long only for pleasure; the higher ones, whenever pleasure comes, look to the pain in it and do not cling to it.","n":"It reworks 54 almost line for line, but where 54 has the wise leave household life, this has them refuse attachment as each pleasure arrives: a discipline inside experience rather than a change of state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inbam","pattru","thuravu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:6"},"n:61":{"book":"naladiyar","num":61,"t":["மதித்திறப் பாரும் இறக்க மதியா","மதித்திறப் பாரும் இறக்க - மதித்தேறி","ஈயும் தலைமேல் இருத்தலால் அஃதறிவார்","காயும் சுதமின்மை நன்று."],"p":"Who pass esteeming us, let them pass on! And those who contemn and trample on us as they pass, let them too pass on! If even a fly (especially unclean) should climb, trampling on their head, it is well that the wise who know its worth, should feel no wrath.","r":"Let those who honour us pass, and let those who trample us pass too; since even a fly can climb onto one's head, those who understand this do well to be free of burning anger.","n":"The fly deflates the insult: being stepped on is something even the smallest creature does, so it carries no weight. The printed second and third lines repeat மதித்- where both commentators read மிதித்- (trample), and the last line's wording looks corrupt.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","porai","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:62":{"book":"naladiyar","num":62,"t":["தண்டாச் சிறப்பின்தம் இன்னுயிரைத் தாங்காது","கண்டுழி யெல்லாம் துறப்பவோ - மண்டி","அடிபெயரா தாற்ற இளிவந்த போழ்தின்","முடிகிற்கும் உள்ளத் தவர்."],"p":"Although disgraces throng thickly, and may not be repulsed, will those whose minds are set upon finishing the work begun, renounce sweet life's unfailing worth in their impatience, whenever they see (evils)?","r":"Those whose minds can carry a task through will not throw away their precious life whenever they meet disgrace, even when it presses so hard they cannot move a step.","n":"Placed in a chapter on not being angry, the quatrain can be read as treating death at humiliation as anger turned on oneself. That is an interpretation from its placement; the verse itself argues only from the unfinished task.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","thitpam","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:63":{"book":"naladiyar","num":63,"t":["காவா தொருவன்தன் வாய்திறந்து சொல்லும்சொல்","ஓவாதே தன்னைச் சுடுதலால் - ஓவாதே","ஆய்ந்தமைந்த கேள்வி அறிவுடையார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","காய்ந்தமைந்த சொல்லார் கறுத்து."],"p":"If a man open his mouth and speak unguarded words, his words will ceaselessly burn his soul. The wise who ceaselessly hear, and ponder well and calmly, even in their wrath, will never give utterance to words of fire.","r":"Unguarded words from an open mouth burn the speaker without end, so the learned, who listen and reflect without end, never speak scorching words even when angry.","n":"The repeated ஓவாதே sets the endless burning of careless speech against endless listening as its cure. The burn falls on the speaker, not the hearer.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","kelvi","vekuli","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:64":{"book":"naladiyar","num":64,"t":["நேர்த்து நிகரல்லார் நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால்","வேர்த்து வெகுளார் விழுமியோர் - ஓர்த்ததனை","உள்ளத்தான் உள்ளி உரைத்துராய் ஊர்கேட்பத்","துள்ளித்தூண் முட்டுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"When men who are beneath them confront them, and speak unseemly words, the excellent wax not hot with anger. The base man will brood over it, chafe and rave for all the town to hear, and leap, and dash his head against a post.","r":"The excellent do not grow hot with anger when people beneath them confront them with unfit words; the base brood on it, rant for the town to hear, leap about and butt a pillar.","n":"The comic last line makes anger self-injuring rather than wicked: the head that hits the pillar is the angry man's own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","perumai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:65":{"book":"naladiyar","num":65,"t":["இளையான் அடக்கம் அடக்கம் கிளைபொருள்","இல்லான் கொடையே கொடைப்பயன் - எல்லாம்","ஒறுக்கும் மதுகை உரனுடை யாளன்","பொறுக்கும் பொறையே பொறை."],"p":"The young man's self-restraint is self-restraint. The gift of him who owns no stores of wealth is gift indeed. When wan has means and might to punish every fault, if he forbear, call him the patient man.","r":"Restraint in youth is true restraint, a gift from one without wealth is the true fruit of giving, and the patience of a strong person able to punish everything is true patience.","n":"Each virtue counts only where its opposite would be easy: youth tempts, poverty excuses, power permits.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","adakkam","eegai","vali"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:66":{"book":"naladiyar","num":66,"t":["கல்லெறிந் தன்ன கயவர்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல்","எல்லாரும் காணப் பொறுத்துய்ப்பர் - ஓல்லை","இடுநீற்றால் பைஅவிந்த நாகம்போல் தத்தம்","குடிமையான் வாதிக்கப் பட்டு."],"p":"As stones the base shower down their bitter words: the noble bear, in sight of all, and let them pass, by sense of noble worth constrained : like serpent's crest at once by touch of sacred ash subdued.","r":"The well-born bear the stone-like bitter words of the base in front of everyone and let them pass, held back by their own lineage as a cobra's hood is quelled at once by sacred ash.","n":"The image grants that the noble have venom: their forbearance is power held in, not its absence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","kudi","kadunjol","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:67":{"book":"naladiyar","num":67,"t":["மாற்றாராய் நின்றுதம் மாறேற்பார்க்கு ஏலாமை","ஆற்றாமை என்னார் அறிவுடையார் - ஆற்றாமை","நேர்த்தின்னா மற்றவர் செய்தக்கால் தாம்அவரைப்","பேர்த்தின்னா செய்யாமை நன்று."],"p":"When men stand forth as our enemies, and would begin the conflict, to decline the strife is not, in the language of the wise, lack of power. Even when men have confronted and done us intolerable evils, it is good not to do them evil in return.","r":"The wise do not call it weakness to decline a fight with those who come at you as enemies; even when they confront you and do unbearable harm, it is good not to harm them in return.","n":"The first half answers the charge of weakness before the second recommends restraint. With neither commentary aligned, this reading rests on the Tamil and Pope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","innaa","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:68":{"book":"naladiyar","num":68,"t":["நெடுங்காலம் ஓடினும் நீசர் வெகுளி","கெடுங்காலம் இன்றிப் பரக்கும் - அடுங்காலை","நீர்கொண்ட வெப்பம்போல் தானே தணியுமே","சீர்கொண்ட சான்றோர் சினம்."],"p":"","r":"The anger of the low keeps spreading however long it runs and never dies, while the anger of the worthy cools of itself, like the heat water takes on when boiled.","n":"Water's heat is borrowed from the fire, so the simile can suggest that the worthy's anger comes from outside and leaves them as they were. That is my reading of the image, not the commentators'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vekuli","saandror","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:69":{"book":"naladiyar","num":69,"t":["உபகாரம் செய்ததனை ஓராதே தங்கண்","அபகாரம் ஆற்றச் செயினும் - உபகாரம்","தாம்செய்வ தல்லால் தவற்றினால் தீங்கூக்கல்","வான்தோய் குடிப்பிறந்தார்க் கில்."],"p":"Though men think not of good received, and do much ill to men of family whose fame has touched the sky, these still do good; nor are they wont provoked by faults, to render evil to the thankless ones.","r":"Even when people forget the help they received and do great harm, those born in families whose fame touches the sky only keep doing good, and do not seek to repay the fault with harm.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","nanri","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:70":{"book":"naladiyar","num":70,"t":["கூர்த்துநாய் கெளவிக் கொளக்கண்டும் தம்வாயால்","பேர்த்துநாய் கெளவினார் ஈங்கில்லை - ஈர்த்தன்றிக்","கீழ்மக்கள் கீழாய சொல்லியக்கால் சொல்பவோ","மேன்மக்கள் தம்வாயால் மீட்டு."],"p":"Though a dog, in range, should lay hold of them, there are none in the world who, in return, would lay hold of it with their mouth; and thus, when low men speak, not what is fitting, but low words, will high-minded men, in reply, utter such words with their mouth. ----------","r":"No one bitten by a raging dog bites it back; so when the low speak low words, will the high answer in kind with their own mouths?","n":"The analogy works by ranking the insulter with the dog: answering in kind would mean stooping to its level.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kadunjol","porai","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:7"},"n:71":{"book":"naladiyar","num":71,"t":["கோதை யருவிக் குளர்வரை நன்னாட","பேதையொடு யாதும் உரையற்க - பேதை","உரைக்கிற் சிதைந்துரைக்கும் ஒல்லும் வகையான்","வழக்கிக் கழிதலே நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land, where down the cool mountains the streams fall as garlands!— With a fool hold no converse. If you speak with him, in replying he will pervert your words. To slip away from him as best you can is well.","r":"Lord of the good cool hill country where streams fall like garlands: say nothing at all to a fool, who will twist what you say; slipping away as best you can is best.","n":"Forbearance here takes the form of withdrawal rather than endurance: the wise don't stay to bear the fool's talk.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","sol","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:72":{"book":"naladiyar","num":72,"t":["நேரல்லார் நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால் மற்றது","தாரித் திருத்தல் தகுதிமற் - றோரும்","புகழ்மையாக் கொள்ளாது பொங்குநீர் ஞாலம்","சமழ்மையாக் கொண்டு விடும்."],"p":"When persons not our equals say unfitting things, to bear and to be still is worthy conduct. The world girt by foaming waters regards not a contrary course as praise-worthy, but as discreditable.","r":"When those not one's equals say unfit things, it is fitting to bear them in silence; the world does not count the opposite as praise but as disgrace.","n":"Mudaliar takes the 'world' that judges to be the worthy, not people at large.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","pazhi","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:73":{"book":"naladiyar","num":73,"t":["காதலார் சொல்லும் கடுஞ்சொல் உவந்துரைக்கும்","ஏதிலார் இன்சொலின் தீதாமோ - போதெலாம்","மாதர்வண்டு ஆர்க்கும் மலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","ஆவ தறிவார்ப் பெறின்."],"p":"Lord of the swelling sea's cool shore, where bright insects hum around every flower!—Are severe words from loving lips harder to bear, if men can only rightly estimate their result, than pleasant words that strangers courteously speak?","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the teeming sea, where bees hum over every flower: for one who finds people who know what is good for him, are harsh words from those who love worse than pleasant words that strangers speak with a smile?","n":"The condition carries the weight: harsh words are worth more only if one finds friends who know what is good (ஆவது அறிவார்). Mudaliar reads பெறின் as stressing how rare such friends are.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kadunjol","inchol","idithal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:74":{"book":"naladiyar","num":74,"t":["அறிவ தறிந்தடங்கி அஞ்சுவ தஞ்சி","உறுவ துலகுவப்பச் செய்து - பெறுவதனால்","இன்புற்று வாழும் இயல்பினார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","துன்புற்று வாழ்தல் அரிது."],"p":"Those who know what should be known and rule themselves thereby; who fear what should be feared ; who use all their faculties to bless the world; and whose nature rejoices in all good gained: are for ever free from woes.","r":"Those who know what must be known, keep restraint, fear what must be feared, do what is due so that the world rejoices, and live content with what they get, are hardly ever in suffering.","n":"Mudaliar glosses அடங்கி as 'being forbearing', which ties this broad portrait of contentment to the chapter's theme.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","adakkam","acham","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:75":{"book":"naladiyar","num":75,"t":["வேற்றுமை யின்றிக் கலந்திருவர் நட்டக்கால்","தேற்றா ஓழுக்கம் ஒருவன்கண் உண்டாயின்","ஆற்றும் துணையும் பொறுக்க பொறானாயின்","தூற்றாதே தூர விடல்."],"p":"When two with strict accord unite in friendship's bond, if one betray the other's confidence by unkind act, this latter should endure as best he may. And if he can't endure, he should not divulge it, but withdraw himself.","r":"When two have joined in friendship without any distinction and one of them shows conduct the other cannot make sense of, the other should bear it as far as he can, and if he can't, should withdraw without spreading it abroad.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","purangooral"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The friend's lapse is an act done unknowingly, or one that can't be told to others.","தேற்றா ஒழுக்கம் - அறியாச் செய்கை; (பிறர்க்கு) அறிவிக்கக் கூடாச்செய்கை எனினுமாம்."],["shanmuganar","The friend is found in bad conduct.","ஒருவனிடம் தெளியாத தீயொழுக்கம் காணப்படின்"]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:76":{"book":"naladiyar","num":76,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் இனிய ஒழிகென்று","தன்னையே தான்நோவின் அல்லது - துன்னிக்","கலந்தாரைக் கைவிடுதல் கானக நாட","விலங்கிற்கும் விள்ளல் அரிது."],"p":"but never forsake him. Lord of the woodlands! Separation is hard even to beasts; therefore if friends do things that are unpleasant, think them pleasant. bid yourself cease (from wrath), and blame yourself alone; but forsake not those that have been joined to you in the intimacy of friendship.","r":"Lord of the forest land: even if friends do hurtful things, wish them to pass as if pleasant and blame only yourself, but do not abandon those joined to you closely, for parting is hard even for beasts.","n":"Blaming oneself is left open: Mudaliar records two readings, one moral and one karmic.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","anbu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","One blames oneself either for the bad deeds of a former birth that caused the hurt, or for befriending without first examining the friend.","(அச் செய்கைக்குக் காரணமாகிய தீ வினையை முற்பிறப்பிற் செய்த) தன்னையே எனவும், (முன் நன்கு ஆராயாது அவரொடு நட்புகொண்ட) தன்னையே எனவும் பொருள் கூறுப."]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:77":{"book":"naladiyar","num":77,"t":["பெரியார் பெருநட்புக் கோடல்தாம் செய்த","அரிய பொறுப்பஎன் றன்றோ - அரியரோ","ஒல்லென் அருவி உயர்வரை நன்னாட","நல்லசெய் வார்க்குத் தமர்."],"p":"Is not the reason why the close friendship of the great is sought, that they will bear even with faults hard to endure? Lord of the good land of high mountains, with resounding waterfalls! — to good people are intimate friends rare?","r":"Is it not because the great bear with faults that are hard to bear that people seek their deep friendship? Lord of the good high hills with roaring falls, are close friends rare for those who do good?","n":"The closing question can be taken two ways: Mudaliar reads it as 'friends are not rare for the good', Shanmuganar as 'the great are not hard to win for those who do good without fault'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","porai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:78":{"book":"naladiyar","num":78,"t":["வற்றிமற் றாற்றப் பசிப்பினும் பண்பிலார்க்கு","அற்றம் அறிய உரையற்க - அற்றம்","மறைக்கும் துணையார்க் குரைப்பவே தம்மைத்","துறக்கும் துணிவிலா தார்."],"p":"Though sore wasted with hunger, let not men tell out their destitution to ungracious churls. Those indeed who lack resolution to deny themselves may tell their wants to those who are able and willing to save them from destitution.","r":"Even if wasted and starving, do not tell your need to people without courtesy; those who lack the resolve to give up their life tell it only to those able to cover it.","n":"Both commentators take தம்மைத் துறக்கும் துணிவு as resolve to give up one's life, so the quatrain ranks starving in silence above asking even the right person; Pope reads it as resolve to deny oneself. மறைக்கும் can mean relieving the need or keeping it secret, and Mudaliar allows both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","maanam","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:79":{"book":"naladiyar","num":79,"t":["இன்பம் பயந்தாங் கிழிவு தலைவரினும்","இன்பத்தின் பக்கம் இருந்தைக்க - இன்பம்","ஒழியாமை கண்டாலும் ஓங்கருவி நாட","பழியாகா ஆறே தலை."],"p":"Although shameful things may present themselves. as things that yield pleasure, flee from that pleasure's side! Though thou couldst see pleasures that cease not soon, — Lord of the land of fountains high! — the guiltless way is best.","r":"Lord of the land of high waterfalls: even if disgrace comes along the way pleasure opened, and even if the pleasure looks as if it would never end, the way that brings no blame comes first.","n":"The second concession is the stronger: not only brief pleasures but lasting ones are to be refused if they carry blame. The first two lines are knotted, and Mudaliar resolves இருந்தைக்க as addressed to the listener.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","inbam","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:8"},"n:80":{"book":"naladiyar","num":80,"t":["தான்கெடினும் தக்கார்கே டெண்ணற்க தன்உடம்பின்","ஊன்கெடினும் உண்ணார்கைத் துண்ணற்க - வான்கவிந்த","வையக மெல்லாம் பெறினும் உரையற்க","பொய்யோடு இடைமிடைந்த சொல்."],"p":"Though ruin seize you plan not ruin to the just! Though body's flesh should waste, eat not from hands unfit! Though the whole earth o'er-arched by heaven accrue as gain, never speak word with falsehood mixed !","r":"Though ruined, do not plan the ruin of the worthy; though your flesh wastes, do not eat from the hands of those you must not eat from; though you would gain the whole sky-roofed earth, do not speak words mixed with falsehood.","n":"Each refusal is set against the extreme loss that would excuse it, which is how the quatrain belongs to a chapter on forbearance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vaaimai","undi","porai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those whose food must not be eaten are the guru, the deity and the poor; he records others who say lower castes or those who serve without love.","உண்ணத்தாகாதார் குரு, தெய்வம், வறிய ராதியர்;"]],"ch":"n:8"},"n:81":{"book":"naladiyar","num":81,"t":["அச்சம் பெரிதால் அதற்கின்பம் சிற்றளவால்","நிச்சம் நினையுங்கால் கோக்கொலையால் - நிச்சலும்","கும்பிக்கே கூர்த்த வினையால் பிறன்தாரம்","நம்பற்க நாணுடை யார்."],"p":"The fear it brings is great ; its pleasure is brief ; each day if it is divulged death threatens by the king's decree; and ever it is a deed that tends to the pains of hell; O shamefast men, desire not your neighbour's wife.","r":"The fear is great and the pleasure small; thought through, it risks death at the king's hands and is a deed that daily sharpens toward hell, so those with a sense of shame should not set their hopes on another man's wife.","n":"The case is prudential from start to finish, weighing fear against pleasure, but the appeal at the end is to shame (நாணுடையார).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","acham","naanam","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:82":{"book":"naladiyar","num":82,"t":["அறம்புகழ் கேண்மை பெருமைஇந் நான்கும்","பிறன்தாரம் நச்சுவார்ச் சேரா - பிறன்தாரம்","நச்சுவார்ச் சேரும் பகைபழி பாவம்என்று","அச்சத்தோடு இந்நாற் பொருள்."],"p":"Virtue, praise, friendship, greatness, all these four draw not anigh the men who covet their neighbour's wife. Hatred, disgrace and guilt, with fear, these four possessions abide with men who covet their neighbour's wife.","r":"Virtue, renown, friendship and greatness do not come to those who desire another man's wife; enmity, blame, sin and fear do.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","pazhi","aram","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:83":{"book":"naladiyar","num":83,"t":["புக்க விடத்தச்சம் போதரும் போதச்சம்","துய்க்கு மிடத்தச்சம் தோன்றாமல் காப்பச்சம்","எக்காலும் அச்சம் தருமால் எவன்கொலோ","உட்கான் பிறன்இல் புகல்."],"p":"In entering in there's fear; in home returning fear; during enjoyment is fear; in guarding the secret is fear; it evermore brings fear: why shuns he not with dread the entrance of neighbour's house?","r":"Fear on entering, fear on returning, fear while enjoying, fear in keeping it hidden: it brings fear at every moment, so why does a man enter another's house without dread?","n":"The repetition of அச்சம் enacts the condition it describes. The closing question turns on the same word: the one who lives in fear lacks the one fear that would have kept him out.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:84":{"book":"naladiyar","num":84,"t":["காணின் குடிப்பழியாம் கையுறின் கால்குறையும்","ஆணின்மை செய்யுங்கால் அச்சமாம் - நீள்நிரயத்","துன்பம் பயக்குமால் துச்சாரி, நீகண்ட","இன்பம் எனக்கெனைத்தால் கூறு."],"p":"If any one see, disgrace lights on the house; if any hand should seize, leg's maimed; in the doing of the shameful deed is dread; it yields as fruit vast hell's affliction : tell me, O profligate, what measure of delight is thine ?","r":"If seen, the family is disgraced; if caught, a leg is cut off; the unmanly act itself is fear; and it yields long suffering in hell: tell me, profligate, what pleasure did you find?","n":"Mudaliar reads ஆணின்மை as the absence of the qualities that make a man, so the adulterer's daring is renamed unmanliness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","pazhi","acham","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:85":{"book":"naladiyar","num":85,"t":["செம்மையொன் றின்றிச் சிறியா ரினத்தராய்க்","கொம்மை வரிமுலையாள் தோள்மாணஇ - உம்மை","வலியால் பிறர்மனைமேல் சென்றாரே, இம்மை","அலியாகி ஆடிஉண் பார்."],"p":"Those who, in a former state, without any regard to right, becoming associates of the mean, enjoyed the embraces of beautiful women, and by violence approached their neighbour's wife, in this state will become eunuchs, and dancing shall earn their bread.","r":"Those who in a former birth, with no sense of right and in the company of the mean, went by force to other men's wives become eunuchs in this birth and live by dancing.","n":"The punishment mirrors the offence: those who used male force are reborn without it. This is the chapter's one claim about a past birth; the Kural's chapter on another's wife makes none.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:86":{"book":"naladiyar","num":86,"t":["பல்லா ரறியப் பறையறைந்து நாள்கேட்டுக்","கல்யாணம் செய்து கடிபுக்க - மெல்லியல்","காதல் மனையாளும் இல்லாளா என்ஒருவன்","ஏதில் மனையாளை நோக்கு."],"p":"While his loving wife dwells in his home, the tender one whom he espoused, — seeking (out an auspicious) day, and sounding the drum, for many folks to know,—and 'whom he guards as his own, what means a man's glance at another's wife?","r":"When a man has at home a tender, loving wife, married on a day fixed by asking, with drums beaten for many to know, why should he look at a stranger's wife?","n":"The public wedding is the point: a bond announced to many and guarded (கடி) is set against one that has to be hidden.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:87":{"book":"naladiyar","num":87,"t":["அம்பல் அயல்எடுப்ப அஞ்சித் தமர்பாணஇ","வம்பலன் பெண்மாணஇ மைந்துற்று - நம்பும்","நிலைமைஇல் நெஞ்சத்தான் துப்புரவு பாம்பின்","தலைநக்கி யன்ன துடைத்து."],"p":"The enjoyment of the man of unstable heart, who under the influence of infatuation approaches his neighbour's wife and sets his affections upon her, while neighbours spread abroad his guilt, and kinsmen dread and mourn, is like (that of the person who takes pleasure in) licking a serpent's head.","r":"The pleasure of the unsteady-hearted man who, maddened, unites with another's wife while neighbours gossip and his own people tremble and grieve, is like licking a cobra's head.","n":"The image holds pleasure and lethal danger in a single act, so no separate weighing of costs is needed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piranil","kaamam","alar"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:88":{"book":"naladiyar","num":88,"t":["பரவா, வெளிப்படா, பல்லோர்கண் தங்கா","உரவோர்கண் காமநோய் ஓஓ.","கொடிதே. விரவாருள் நாணுப் படல்அஞ்சி யாதும்","உரையாதுஉள் ஆறி விடும்."],"p":"The disease of lust in men mighty (in wisdom) gains not ascendancy, is not revealed, does not remain fixed on many objects. O! it is a cruel conflict; but fearing to incur shame in the midst of their foes, they say nothing about it, and it is extinguished within them.","r":"In the strong, the sickness of desire does not spread, show itself or settle on many; fearing shame among those outside, they say nothing and it subsides within. Oh, how cruel it is!","n":"The strong are not free of desire, only able to contain it, and the cry 'how cruel' can fall on that containment. Mudaliar reads it so: desire that can't come out and must be suffered inwardly is the cruellest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamanoy","nirai","naanam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Because it doesn't come out but stays within and subsides only after great suffering, the desire of the wise is called very cruel.","வெளிப்பட்டொழியாது உள்ளே நின்று மிக வருந்தி அடங்குதலின், அறிவுடையார் காமம் மிகவுங் கொடிது எனப்படுத லுணர்க."]],"ch":"n:9"},"n:89":{"book":"naladiyar","num":89,"t":["அம்பும் அழலும் அவிர்கதிர் ஞாயிறும்","வெம்பிச் சுடினும் புறம்சுடும் - வெம்பிக்","கவற்றி மனத்தைச் சுடுதலால், காமம்","அவற்றினும் அஞ்சப் படும்."],"p":"Arrow and fire and sun with glistening rays may rage and burn; but these burn the outer man alone. Lust rages and distracts and burns the mind, and is more to be feared than they.","r":"Arrow, fire and the sun's bright rays, however they blaze, burn only the outside; desire, blazing and troubling the mind, burns the mind itself, so it is to be feared more than they.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","manam","acham"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:90":{"book":"naladiyar","num":90,"t":["ஊருள் எழுந்த உருகெழு செந்தீக்கு","நீருள் குளித்தும் உயலாகும் - நீருள்","குளிப்பினும் காமம் சுடுமேகுன் றேறி","ஒளிப்பினும் காமம் சுடும்."],"p":"From the ruddy fire that fiercely rises in the village you may scape by bathing in water. — Although you bathe in water, lust will burn; and though you climb the hill and hide you there, still lust will burn! -----------","r":"From a fierce red fire rising in the town one can escape by plunging into water; but desire burns even if one plunges into water, and burns even if one climbs a hill to hide.","n":"Water and the hilltop are the two refuges from fire, and both fail because this fire travels with the one who flees.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kaamam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:9"},"n:91":{"book":"naladiyar","num":91,"t":["இல்லா விடத்தும் இயைந்த அளவினால்","உள்ள விடம்போல் பெரிதுவந்து - மெல்லக்","கொடையொடு பட்ட குணனுடைய மாந்தர்க்கு","அடையாவாம் ஆண்டைக் கதவு"],"p":"Even in their adverse hour, up to the measure of their means, as in the prosperous times, with large rejoicing heart to give is their nature still. To such good men heaven's gate is never closed.","r":"Those whose nature is to give gladly, as much as they can, even when they have little, as if they had plenty, will not find the doors of that world shut against them.","n":"Gladness is part of the qualification: the gift is measured against the giver's means and his spirit, not its size.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","manam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The doors are those of the other world, which Pope calls heaven.","ஆண்டைக்கதவு-அவ்வுலகத்துக் கதவுகள்."],["shanmuganar","The doors are those of release (வீடுபேறு), not of a heaven.","வீடுபேற்றுக் கதவுகள் அடைக்கப்படாவாம்."]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:92":{"book":"naladiyar","num":92,"t":["முன்னரே சாம்நாள் முனிதக்க மூப்புள","பின்னரும் பீடழிக்கும் நோயுள - கொன்னே","பரவன்மின் பற்றன்மின் பாத்துண்மின் யாதும்","கரவன்மின் கைத்துண்டாம் போழ்து."],"p":"Before (you) are death's day and age detestable ; behind is disease that humbles pride. Discursive thoughts indulge not. Cling not to earth. Eat, sharing food; hide not your powers while wealth is in your hand.","r":"Death's day and hateful old age stand right before you, and disease that destroys strength comes after; don't run about in vain, don't clutch, share before you eat, and hide nothing while wealth is in your hand.","n":"The case for giving here is impermanence, not merit or fame: wealth is to be used while the hand still holds it. Mudaliar takes பரவன்மின் as 'don't roam after more wealth'; Pope has 'indulge not discursive thoughts'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","nilaiyaamai","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:93":{"book":"naladiyar","num":93,"t":["நடுக்குற்றுத் தற்சேர்ந்தார் துன்பம் துடையார்","கொடுத்துத்தான் துய்ப்பினும் ஈண்டுங்கால் ஈண்டும்","மிடுக்குற்றுப் பற்றினும் நில்லாது செல்வம்","விடுக்கும் வினையுலந்தக் கால்."],"p":"Though dread of want they do not relieve the woes of men who as suppliants draw near! Yet although men enjoy and give, wealth grows in growing time: cling to it, and yet it flies when former deeds that brought wealth have lost their power.","r":"Whether one ignores the misery of those who come trembling or gives and enjoys, wealth gathers when its time to gather comes; and however tightly it is held, it leaves when the good deeds that sent it run out.","n":"The argument removes the miser's motive: giving can't lose wealth that merit holds, and clutching can't keep wealth that merit has stopped holding. Mudaliar notes the variant இடுக்குற்று (clutching tight) for மிடுக்குற்று (with force).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","vinai","oozh","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:94":{"book":"naladiyar","num":94,"t":["இம்மி யரிசித் துணையானும் வைகலும்","நும்மில் இயைவ கொடுத்துண்மின் - உம்மைக்","கொடாஅ தவரென்பர் குண்டுநீர் வையத்(து)","அடாஅ அடுப்பி னவர்."],"p":"Daily having given somewhat, though but the fraction of a grain of rice, according to your ability, eat ye (your own food). Those who gave not in that world, men say on this earth girt by deep waters are those on whose hearth nothing is cooked.","r":"Give daily, before eating, from what you can, even a grain of rice's worth; the quatrain backs this with a saying about those who do not give and those whose hearth is never lit.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உம்மை means 'in a former birth': those who did not give then are the poor with unlit hearths now.","உம்மை- முற்பிறப்பில். என்பர்-என்றிகழப்படுவர்;"],["shanmuganar","உம்மை means 'you': the poor with unlit hearths will scorn you as misers.","கொடாத கருமிகள் என்று உம்மை இழித்து உரைப்பர்."]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:95":{"book":"naladiyar","num":95,"t":["மறுமையும் இம்மையும் நோக்கி ஒருவற்கு","உறுமா றியைவ கொடுத்தல் - வறுமையால்","ஈதல் இசையா தெனினும் இரவாமை","ஈதல் இரட்டி யுறும்."],"p":"Regarding the other world and this world, give to any suppliant, in fitting way, according to your ability. And if on account of poverty giving is not possible, yet refraining from begging is twice as meritorious as giving.","r":"With an eye to the next world and this one, give what you can in a fitting way; and if poverty makes giving impossible, not begging is worth twice as much as giving.","n":"Mudaliar names the two rewards: heavenly joy in the next world and renown in this one. The last line keeps the poor inside the economy of giving: they give by not taking.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:96":{"book":"naladiyar","num":96,"t":["நடுவூருள் வேதிகை சுற்றுக்கோட் புக்க","படுபனை யன்னர் பலர்நச்ச வாழ்வார்","குடிகொழுத்தக் கண்ணுங்கொடுத்துண்ணா மாக்கள்","இடுகாட்டுள் ஏற்றைப் பனை."],"p":"Those who live desired of many are as a fertile palm entered in the altar's enclosure in midmost of the town. Those who, even when their house grows great, give not before they eat, are like the sterile palm in the burning ground.","r":"Those who live so that many desire them are like a fruit-bearing palm enclosed by a platform in the middle of town; those who do not give before eating, even when their household thrives, are like a barren male palm in the burning ground.","n":"Both are palms; fruit and place separate them. The giver stands where everyone gathers, the miser among the dead.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:97":{"book":"naladiyar","num":97,"t":["பெயற்பால் மழைபெய்யாக் கண்ணும் உலகம்","செயற்பால செய்யா விடினும் - கயற்புலால்","புன்னை கடியும் பொருகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப.","என்னை உலகுய்யு மாறு."],"p":"Even when the rain rains not in due season, if all the world should fail in virtuous actions that ought to be done, —Lord of the warring sea's cool shore, where acrid fume of fish in Punnai's perfume dies! - how scapes the world?","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the pounding sea, where the punnai's scent drives off the smell of fish: if the rain does not fall when it should, and the world does not do what it should, how will the world survive?","n":"Both commentators take the 'world' that must act to be its good or wise people, so their giving is set beside rain as a second support of life.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["mazhai","eegai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:98":{"book":"naladiyar","num":98,"t":["ஏற்றகைம் மாற்றாமை என்னானும் தாம்வரையாது","ஆற்றாதார்க் கீவதாம் ஆண்கடன் - ஆற்றின்","மலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப மாறீவார்க் கீதல்","பொலிகடன் என்னும் பெயர்த்து."],"p":"Denying to no out-stretched hand, to give to needy men as he hath power, is duty of a man.—Lord of the swelling sea's cool shore !—A gift to those that can return the gift is usury !","r":"Never refusing an outstretched hand and holding nothing back, to give to those who can't repay is a man's duty; lord of the cool shore, giving to those who will give back earns only the name of a profitable loan.","n":"Mudaliar lists several ways to take தாம் வரையாது: excluding no gift, excluding no person, setting no rules, or keeping nothing back for oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:99":{"book":"naladiyar","num":99,"t":["இறப்பச் சிறிதென்னா தில்லென்னா தென்றும்","அறப்பயன் யார்மாட்டும் செய்க - முறைப்புதவின்","ஐயம் புகூஉம் தவசி கடிஞைபோல்","பைய நிறைத்து விடும்."],"p":"Say not ‘ 'tis passing little,' nor ‘ ' tis nought I give;' on all confer thy boon of virtuous charity, Like the dish the mendicant presents from door to door, by frequent doles 'twill be filled full.","r":"Never say 'it is too little' or 'I have nothing'; always do the good of giving, to anyone, for like the ascetic's bowl filled at door after door, it fills slowly.","n":"The bowl belongs to the one who receives, but it stands for what fills in the giver: Mudaliar says the fruit of virtue fills his life, Shanmuganar that good deeds accumulate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","aram","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:10"},"n:100":{"book":"naladiyar","num":100,"t":["கடிப்பிடு கண்முரசம் காதத்தோர் கேட்பர்","இடித்து முழங்கியதோர் யோசனையோர் கேட்பர்","அடுக்கிய மூவுலகும் கேட்குமே சான்றோர்","கொடுத்தா ரெனப்படும் சொல்."],"p":"The sound of beaten drum a katham off they'll hear; the thunder's voice through a whole yojanai will reach men's ears; the three successive worlds will hear the word that says, ‘Men excellent their gifts have given.' ------------","r":"A drum struck with a stick is heard a காதம் away and rolling thunder a யோசனை, but the word 'they gave', linked with the worthy, is heard through all three stacked worlds.","n":"Mudaliar records a third reading, 'they gave to the worthy'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pugazh","saandror"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The word is 'he gave', spoken by the worthy about a giver.","சான் றோரால் கொடுத்தார் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்லையோவெனில்;"],["shanmuganar","The word is praise that the worthy themselves gave.","பெரியார் கொடுத்தார் எனப்படும் புகழ்ச் சொல்லையோ,"]],"ch":"n:10"},"n:101":{"book":"naladiyar","num":101,"t":["பல்லாவுள் உய்த்து விடினும் குழக்கன்று","வல்லதாம் தாய்நாடிக் கோடலைத் - தொல்லைப்","பழவினையும் அன்ன தகைத்தேதற் செய்த","கிழவனை நாடிக் கொளற்கு."],"p":"Although you send forth the tender calf amid many cows, it has unerring skill to seek out its own mother. Deeds of old days have even so the power to search him out to whom their fruit pertains.","r":"A young calf let loose among many cows can still find its own mother, and an old deed has the same skill in seeking out the one who did it.","n":"The image makes the deed active: it searches, as the calf does, rather than waiting to be met. Neither the verse nor Mudaliar says the deed comes from a former birth; Shanmuganar adds that.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:102":{"book":"naladiyar","num":102,"t":["உருவும் இளமையும் ஒண்பொருளும் உட்கும்","ஒருவழி நில்லாமை கண்டும் - ஒருவழி","ஒன்றேயும் இல்லாதான் வாழ்க்கை உடம்(பு)இட்டு","நின்றுவீழ்ந் தக்க(து) உடைத்து."],"p":"Beauty and youth, and glittering wealth and reverence abide not in one stay. To him who, though he sees this, does no single virtuous act in this one stage of being, life's joy stands with the body and falls with it.","r":"Beauty, youth, fine wealth and the awe one commands do not stay; one who sees this and still does not one good thing has a life that merely takes a body, stands, and falls.","n":"The failure condemned is not vice but the absence of even one good act; the body is wasted rather than misused. Mudaliar offers two senses of ஒருவழி ஒன்றேயும்: any single good deed, or one deed of the kind that leads to the highest state.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:103":{"book":"naladiyar","num":103,"t":["வளம்பட வேண்டாதார் யார்யாரும் இல்லை","அளந்தன போகம் அவர்அவர் ஆற்றால்","விளங்காய் திரட்டினார் இல்லை, களங்கனியைக்","காரெனச் செய்தாரும் இல்."],"p":"Who would not see Prosperity? All seek her gifts; but as men's ways are, so each man's enjoyments are meted out. Who made the Vilam's apple round? Or who gave its dusky hue to the Kalam fruit ?","r":"Everyone wants prosperity, but each person's enjoyments are measured out according to their own course, just as no one rounded the vilam fruit or blackened the kalam berry.","n":"The fruits are chosen because their shape and colour belong to them by nature. The verse says only அவரவர் ஆற்றால்; both commentators take that 'way' to be one's former deeds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:104":{"book":"naladiyar","num":104,"t":["உறற்பால நீக்கல் உறுவர்க்கும் ஆகா,","பெறற்பா லனையவும் அன்னவாம் மாரி","வறப்பின் தருவாரும் இல்லை, அதனைச்","சிறப்பின் தணிப்பாரும் இல்."],"p":"Not even saints can drive away predestined ills; and all the fated gain must needs accrue. In time of drought who can bestow the rain ? Or who can check its rich abundance when it falls?","r":"Not even the உறுவர் can remove what is bound to befall, and what is bound to be gained is the same; no one can bring rain in a drought or check it when it pours.","n":"Mudaliar records Shanmuganar's kind of reading of the second line as another view, under which the verse is a reversed parallel: rain withheld matches gains withheld, rain unstoppable matches pains unstoppable.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","mazhai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The உறுவர் are sages, and destined pleasures can no more be prevented than destined pains.","அன்ன ஆம்-அத் தன்மையவேயாம்; 'நீக்கல் ஆகா' என்றபடி."],["shanmuganar","No strong person can stop what fate brings, and no one can obtain by their own effort what they wish to gain.","பெற விரும்புகிறவற்றைத் தாமாகப் பெறுவதென்பதும் அதுபோன்றே இயலாததாம்"]],"ch":"n:11"},"n:105":{"book":"naladiyar","num":105,"t":["தினைத்துணைய ராகித்தந் தேசுள் அடக்கிப்","பனைத்துணையார் வைகலும் பாடழிந்து வாழ்வர்","நினைப்பக் கிடந்த தெவனுண்டாம் மேலை","வினைப்பயன் அல்லால் பிற."],"p":"Those who lose like stately palms, when their greatness is gone, become small asthe millet seed, hiding their glory within, —and so they pass their days. This is the fruit of deeds of former days: when you think of it, what other cause can there be?","r":"Men once as great as palm trees shrink to the size of a millet seed, hide their brightness within and live on diminished day after day; think about it, and what cause is there but the fruit of former deeds?","n":"The verse turns a common sight, the fallen great, into an argument by elimination: no other cause can be thought of. Mudaliar specifies that the deeds are evil ones.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","perumai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:106":{"book":"naladiyar","num":106,"t":["பல்லான்ற கேள்விப் பயனுணர்வார் வீயவும்","கல்லாதார் வாழ்வ தறிதிரேல் - கல்லாதார்","சேதனம் என்னுமச் சேறகத் தின்மையால்","கோதென்று கொள்ளாதாம் கூற்று."],"p":"Those that know the fruit of varied and profound learning die off, while the unlearned joyously live on. Would you know the cause ? — The unlearned posses within no 'sap of sapience'; — so death deems then refuse stalks, and takes them not!","r":"If you ask why those who know the fruit of deep learning die while the unlearned live on, it is because the unlearned have none of the sap called sense, so Death passes them over as husks.","n":"The verse is a joke with an edge: a long life for the unlearned is recast as Death's contempt. It doesn't say the learned die because of their learning; it supplies a fanciful cause, which Mudaliar names as a figure of imputed cause (ஏதுத் தற்குறிப்பேற்றவணி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:107":{"book":"naladiyar","num":107,"t":["இடும்பைகூர் நெஞ்சத்தார் எல்லாரும் காண","நெடுங்கடை நின்றுழல்வ தெல்லாம் - அடம்பப்பூ","அன்னம் கிழிக்கும் அலைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","முன்னை வினையாய் விடும்."],"p":"Lord of the sea's cool shore, where amid the wave swans sport, tearing to shreds the Adambu flowers ! When those whose hearts are sore with urgent need standbegging, and wander through the long street, in sight of all, this is the fruit of former deeds.","r":"When people with aching hearts stand at the gates of great houses, begging in sight of everyone, all of that is their former deeds, lord of the cool shore where swans tear the adambu flowers.","n":"The address to the lord of the shore frames the quatrain as words spoken to a chieftain. Both commentators make the deeds evil ones from a former birth; the verse says only முன்னை வினை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","vinai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:108":{"book":"naladiyar","num":108,"t":["அறியாரும் அல்லர் அறிவ தறிந்தும்","பழியோடு பட்டவை செய்தல் - வளியோடி","நெய்தல் நறவுயிர்க்கும் நீள்கடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","செய்த வினையான் வரும்."],"p":"They are not ignorant; but, though what man should know they know, yet they do deeds that bring guilt to their souls. — Lord of the wide sea's pleasant shore, where breezes breathe the lily's fragrance round ! — This comes from former deeds.","r":"When people who are far from ignorant, who know what ought to be known, still do blameworthy things, that comes from the deeds they have done, lord of the long cool shore where the breeze carries the honey-scent of the neythal.","n":"The quatrain explains a moral puzzle, knowing the good and doing wrong, by pushing the cause back into earlier deeds. It explains the act; it does not excuse it, and pazhi still attaches.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","arivu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:109":{"book":"naladiyar","num":109,"t":["ஈண்டுநீர் வையத்துள் எல்லாரும் எத்துணையும்","வேண்டார்மன் தீய விழைபயன் நல்லவை","வேண்டினும் வேண்டா விடினும் உற்றபால","தீண்டா விடுதல் அரிது."],"p":"On the earth begirt by gathering waters no men desire in anywise evil things, but choice fruit of good things. Yet whether they desire, or abhor, it is hard to shun the touch of what fate assigns.","r":"No one on this sea-girt earth wants evil in the least, and all want good results; but wanted or not, what is bound to come is hard to keep from touching them.","n":"Desire is shown to be idle in both directions: wanting good does not bring it, and not wanting evil does not prevent it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","vinai","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:110":{"book":"naladiyar","num":110,"t":["சிறுகா பெருகா முறைபிறழ்ந்து வாரா","உறுகாலத் தூற்றாகா ஆமிடத்தே யாகும்","சிறுகாலைப் பட்ட பொறியும் அதனால்","இறுகாலத் தென்னை பரிவூ."],"p":"The early fates diminish not, nor do they increase, they come not in order changedhelp in troublous times is none; what haps will happen, there and then; and so, when all things fail, why grieve? -----------","r":"What was set in the early mark does not shrink or grow, does not come out of order, gives no support in hard times, and arrives where it is due; so why grieve when the time of ruin comes?","n":"சிறுகாலைப் பட்ட பொறி is literally 'the mark set in the small time'; both commentators read it as what was fixed in the womb. The chapter ends not with fear but with an argument for calm, since a fixed sum cannot be changed by grieving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oozh","samanilai","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:11"},"n:111":{"book":"naladiyar","num":111,"t":["இசையா ஒருபொருள் இல்லென்றல் யார்க்கும்","வசையன்று வையத் தியற்கை - நசையழுங்க","நின்றோடிப் பொய்த்தல் நிரைதொடீஇ. செய்ந்நன்றி","கொன்றாரின் குற்ற முடைத்து."],"p":"O maid with many armlets graced! To answer 'no' disgraces no man, when the boon asked exceeds his means. It is the world's course. But to delay and cheat the soul faint with desire is a sin like his who 'slays' a benefit conferred.","r":"Saying 'I have none' about something one cannot give is no disgrace and is the way of the world; but keeping someone waiting till their hope dies and then failing them is a worse fault than killing a kindness received.","n":"The verse doesn't fault refusal; it faults the false promise that turns refusal into torment. Both commentators read கொன்றாரின் as a comparison, worse than ingratitude, where Pope says only 'like'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","eegai","nanri"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:112":{"book":"naladiyar","num":112,"t":["தக்காரும் தக்கவ ரல்லாரும் தந்நீர்மை","எக்காலும் குன்றல் இலராவர் - அக்காரம்","யாவரே தின்னினும் கையாதாம் கைக்குமாம்","தேவரே தின்னினும் வேம்பு."],"p":"Men of worth, and men unworthy too, retain their natures ever unchanged. Whoever they be that eat it, sugar can never grow bitter; and margosa is bitter even when eaten by gods.","r":"The worthy and the unworthy never fall away from their own natures: jaggery is not bitter whoever eats it, and neem is bitter even if gods eat it.","n":"The claim runs both ways: circumstances cannot spoil the good, and they cannot redeem the bad. The gods are named to say that even the highest eater does not change the taste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:113":{"book":"naladiyar","num":113,"t":["காலாடு போழ்தில் கழிகிளைஞர் வானத்து","மேலாடு மீனின் பலராவர் - ஏலா","இடரொருவர் உற்றக்கால் ஈர்ங்குன்ற நாட","தொடர்புடையேம் என்பார் சிலர்."],"p":"When a man moves prosperonsly on, devoted kinsfolk are countless as shining stars that move in the upper heaven. But when grievous affliction haps,—Lord of the dripping hills !—few claim close alliance with him.","r":"When a man prospers, kin who cling to him are more numerous than the stars that move in the sky; when unbearable trouble strikes, few say 'we are related to him', lord of the cool hills.","n":"Mudaliar notes that கிளைஞர் means, by its root, those who cling like branches, which sharpens the irony of their leaving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","selvam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:114":{"book":"naladiyar","num":114,"t":["விடுவிலா வையத்து மன்னிய மூன்றில்","நடுவண தெய்த இருதலையும் எய்தும்","நடுவண தெய்தாதான் எய்தும் உலைப்பெய்து","அடுவது போலும் துயர்."],"p":"Of the three things that endure upon this faultless earth, he who gains the midmost gains the two extremes also. He who gains not the midmost gains the cruel smart that the turtle feels when put into the pot to boil.","r":"Of the three that endure in this world, whoever gains the middle one gains both ends as well; whoever does not gains a suffering like being boiled in a furnace.","n":"The verse doesn't name the three; both commentators identify them as virtue, wealth and pleasure, with wealth in the middle. For a work that elsewhere calls wealth unreliable, this is a strong statement of its worth. Pope's turtle in the pot is his own; Mudaliar has a smith's furnace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","aram","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:115":{"book":"naladiyar","num":115,"t":["நல்லாவின் கன்றாயின் நாகும் விலைபெறூஉம்","கல்லாரே யாயினும் செல்வர்வாய்ச் சொற்செல்லும்","புல்லீரப் போழ்தின் உழவேபோல் மீதாடிச்","செல்லாவாம் நல்கூர்ந்தார் சொல்."],"p":"A young heifer fetches a good price when it is the calf of a good cow; so the words of rich men though unlearned, pass current. Poor men's words, like the plough when moisture is scanty, merely graze the surface and are of no avail.","r":"Even a young calf fetches a price if its mother is a good cow, and the words of the rich are accepted even if they are unlearned; the words of the poor glance off like a plough in barely moist soil.","n":"The calf image says worth is borrowed from the source, not earned; the plough image says the poor man's words do not sink in. The verse reports this as how things are, not as how they should be.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:116":{"book":"naladiyar","num":116,"t":["இடம்பட மெய்ஞ்ஞானம் கற்பினும் என்றும்","அடங்காதார் என்றும் அடங்கார் - தடங்கண்ணாய்","உப்போடு நெய்பால் தயிர்காயம் பெய்திடினும்","கைப்பறா பேய்ச்சுரையின் காய்."],"p":"O wide-eyed one! though you cook the wild gourd pouring in salt, ghee, milk, curds, and spices, it never loses its bitterness. So those who never discipline themselves, though they may learn extensive works of true wisdom, never become disciplined.","r":"Those without restraint stay unrestrained even if they study true wisdom widely, as a wild bitter gourd stays bitter though cooked with salt, ghee, milk, curd and asafoetida.","n":"Mudaliar takes அடங்காதார் as those who are by nature not given to restraint, so the point is that learning cannot change a nature, not that learning is useless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:117":{"book":"naladiyar","num":117,"t":["தம்மை இகழ்வாரைத் தாமவரின் முன்னிகழ்க","என்னை அவரொடு பட்டது - புன்னை","விற்றபூங் கமழ்கானல் வீங்குநீர்ச் சேர்ப்ப","உற்றபால யார்க்கும் உறும்."],"p":"When men scorn you, before their faces scorn them too ! what has a man to do with them? — Lord of shore where beauteous Punnai-flowers perfume the glades that surround the swelling tide, — what fated comes to all!","r":"Scorn those who scorn you, before they do; what have you to do with them? What is bound to come comes to everyone, lord of the shore fragrant with punnai flowers.","n":"The link between the advice and the last line is not stated. One reading is that since what is fated will come anyway, there is nothing to lose by answering scorn. Mudaliar takes அவரின் முன் either as at the very moment they scorn or to their faces rather than behind their backs, and calls the verse a way to silence groundless slanderers.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["igal","oozh","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:118":{"book":"naladiyar","num":118,"t":["ஆவே றுருவின வாயினும் ஆபயந்த","பால்வே றுருவின அல்லவாம் - பால்போல்","ஒருதன்மைத் தாகும் அறநெறி ஆபோல்","உருவு பலகொளல் ஈங்கு."],"p":"Though cows in form are diverse, the milk they yield is not diverse. The way of virtue, like that milk, is one in nature, though the schools that teach it here are like those cows, of many forms.","r":"Cows differ in colour, but the milk they give does not; virtue, like milk, is of one nature, while its ways here take many forms, like the cows.","n":"Mudaliar takes the many ways as the many means of practising one virtue. Pope makes them the schools or sects that teach it, which turns the verse into a statement about religious diversity; the Tamil says only நெறி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:119":{"book":"naladiyar","num":119,"t":["யாஅர் உலகத்தோர் சொல்லில்லார்? தேருங்கால்","யாஅர் உபாயத்தின் வாழாதார்? - யாஅர்","இடையாக இன்னாத தெய்தாதார்? யாஅர்","கடைபோகச் செல்வம்உய்த் தார்?"],"p":"Look well! of whom hath not the world found word to say ? And who have not by prudence prospered in life's way? Ah! who in life's mid course no bitter grief have known ? Ah! who to end of life have kept their wealth their own?","r":"Who in the world has escaped having a word said against them? Who has not lived by stratagem? Who has not met hardship midway? Who has kept their wealth to the end?","n":"The four questions expect the answer 'no one', so the verse states the common lot without saying it. Mudaliar gives two senses of உபாயம்: the four political stratagems, or the ordinary means of livelihood.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pazhi","idumbai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:120":{"book":"naladiyar","num":120,"t":["தாஞ்செய் வினையல்லால் தம்மொடு செல்வதுமற்று","யாங்கணும் தேரின் பிறிதில்லை - ஆங்குத்தாம்","போற்றிப் புனைந்த உடம்பும் பயமின்றே","கூற்றும்கொண் டோ டும் பொழுது."],"p":"Save a man's deed: nought goes with him, search where you will. The body which men cherish so, and adorn, is itself profitless indeed when death shall seize and hurry off with it. ------","r":"Search where you will, nothing goes with a person but the deeds they have done; even the body they guarded and adorned is of no use when Death seizes it and runs.","n":"வினை here is not only good deeds: what goes along can be a burden as well as a support.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","kootru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:12"},"n:121":{"book":"naladiyar","num":121,"t":["துக்கத்துள் தூங்கித் துறவின்கட் சேர்கலா","மக்கள் பிணத்த சுடுகாடு - தொக்க","விலங்கிற்கும் புள்ளிற்கும் காடே புலன்கெட்ட","புல்லறி வாளர் வயிறு."],"p":"The burning ground is filled with the corpses of men that will not give themselves up to a self renouncing life, but oscillate amid sorrows; and the maws of perverted foolish men are a mere burning-ground for beasts and birds.","r":"The burning grounds are full of the corpses of people who linger in sorrow and do not turn to renunciation, and the bellies of senseless, petty-minded people are burning grounds for the beasts and birds they eat.","n":"The two halves mirror each other: people end in the burning ground, and the eater's belly becomes a burning ground for animals. The image says that the meat-eater carries a cremation ground inside.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","pulaal","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The sorrow in which people linger is household life itself; Mudaliar also says the verse rejects meat-eating.","துக்கத்துள் - துன்பமாகிய இல்வாழ்க்கையில்."],["shanmuganar","The sorrow is the world's suffering in general, in which people are bewildered.","உலகத் துன்பத்துள் மயங்கித் துறவுநெறியைக் கொள்ளாத மக்களின்"]],"ch":"n:13"},"n:122":{"book":"naladiyar","num":122,"t":["இரும்பார்க்குங் காலராய் ஏதிலார்க் காளாய்க்","கரும்பார் கழனியுள் சேர்வர் - சுரும்பார்க்கும்","காட்டுளாய் வாழுஞ் சிவலும் குறும்பூமும்","கூட்டுளாய்க் கொண்டுவைப் பார்."],"p":"Their legs in iron bound, as slaves to alien lords they will till the black and barren soil, who snared and kept in cages partridges and quails, that dwell in wilds where beetles hum amid the flowers.","r":"Those who catch partridges and quails that live in the humming forest and keep them in cages will go, with irons clanking on their legs, as slaves to strangers into the fields.","n":"The punishment mirrors the crime: those who confined free birds are themselves confined and made to serve. Mudaliar reads கரும்பு ஆர் கழனி as sugarcane fields, and as an alternative as barren and fertile lands; Pope follows the barren reading. Shanmuganar places the punishment in the next birth; the verse doesn't say when.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["innaa","vinai","adimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:123":{"book":"naladiyar","num":123,"t":["அக்கேபோல் அங்கை யொழிய விரலழுகித்","துக்கத் தொழுநொய் எழுபவே - அக்கால்","அலவனைக் காதலித்துக் கான்முரித்துத் தின்ற","பழவினை வந்தடைந்தக் கால்."],"p":"Like fire their palms shall glow, their fingers rotaway, who loved in other times on crabs to feed, and broke their joints, what time the guilt of ‘olden deeds' comes home, and leprosy's fierce pangs assail.","r":"When the old deed of loving to eat crabs and breaking their legs comes home, people get the painful skin disease, white like conch beads, their fingers rotting away and only the palms left.","n":"Again the punishment copies the act: the crab's legs were broken, and the eater's fingers rot. Mudaliar and Shanmuganar read அக்கே போல் as like conch beads; Pope has palms that glow like fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","pulaal","noy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:124":{"book":"naladiyar","num":124,"t":["நெருப்பழல் சேர்ந்தக்கால் நெய்போல் வதூஉம்","எரிப்பச்சுட் டெவ்வநோய் ஆக்கும் - பரப்பக்","கொடுவினைய ராகுவர் கோடாரும் கோடிக்","கடுவினைய ராகியார்ச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"Even things (soft and soothing) like ghee, when joined with the fierce heat of fire, will blaze and burn, and cause bitter anguish : so even upright men are perverted and give themselves up to deeds of utter evil, when they attach themselves to those whose deeds are evil.","r":"Even ghee, when it meets fire, burns and causes painful wounds; so even upright people turn and do widely cruel deeds when they join those of harsh deeds.","n":"Mudaliar notes the force of the image: ghee is what soothes burns, so the very thing that heals becomes what wounds. The verse makes company stronger than character.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","vinai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:125":{"book":"naladiyar","num":125,"t":["பெரியவர் கேண்மை பிறைபோல நாளும்","வரிசை வரிசையா நந்தும் - வரிசையால்","வானூர் மதியம்போல் வைகலும் தேயுமே","தானே சிறியார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Great men's intimate regard will daily grow in order due like the crescent moon. Mean men's alliance like the full moon that rides the sky daily by degrees dwindles away of itself.","r":"The friendship of the great grows day by day in due order like the crescent moon, while the bond with the small wanes by itself day by day like the full moon moving across the sky.","n":"The word தானே says the waning needs no cause: such a bond decays of itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:126":{"book":"naladiyar","num":126,"t":["சான்றோ ரெனமதித்துச் சார்ந்தாய்மன் சார்ந்தாய்க்குச்","சான்றாண்மை சார்ந்தார்கண் இல்லாயின் - சார்ந்தாய்கேள்","சாந்தகத் துண்டென்று செப்புத் திறந்தொருவன்","பாம்பகத்துக் கண்ட துடைத்து."],"p":"Thou didst attach thyself (to unworthy persons), saying, they are men of absolute integrity! If to thee who hast thus attached thyself, integrity in those thine intimates does not appear; hear, O thou who hast so attached thyself, it is as if one opened a casket, thinking it contained an odorous unguent, and saw a snake within.","r":"You attached yourself to someone, thinking him one of the worthy; if the worth is not in him, listen, it is like opening a casket expecting sandal paste and finding a snake inside.","n":"The repeated சார்ந்தாய் ('you who attached yourself') is a reproach: the fault lies partly with the one who did not examine before attaching.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:127":{"book":"naladiyar","num":127,"t":["யாஅர் ஒருவர் ஒருவர்தம் உள்ளத்தைத்","தேருந் துணைமை யுடையவர் - சாரல்","கனமணி நின்றிமைக்கும் நாடகேள் மக்கள்","மனம்வேறு செய்கையும் வேறு."],"p":"What single man has power to search and clearly know the inmost self of other men? Lord of the land where weighty gems glisten on mountain slope, O hear ! — Men's minds are otherwise, and otherwise their deeds.","r":"Who is there with the power to examine another's mind and know it? Listen, lord of the land where heavy gems glitter on the slopes: people's minds are one thing and their deeds another.","n":"The gap between mind and deed is offered as the reason minds cannot be known: behaviour is no evidence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","kurippu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:128":{"book":"naladiyar","num":128,"t":["உள்ளத்தான் நள்ளா துறுதித் தொழிலராய்க்","கள்ளத்தான் நட்டார் கழிகேண்மை - தெள்ளிப்","புனற்செதும்பு நின்றலைக்கும் பூங்குன்ற நாட","மனத்துக்கண் மாசாய் விடும்."],"p":"Lord of the flowery hilly land where streams wash out and carry hither and thither (precious things) from the marshy land ! — The effusive friendship of those who do not attach themselves (to us) in heart, but perform certain friendly acts merely to strengthen their own position, and who form friendships guilefully, will issue in disappointment to the minds (of those who are intimate with them).","r":"The lavish friendship of those who do not join you at heart but act with an eye to their own gain, befriending you with guile, turns into a stain on the mind, lord of the flowering hills.","n":"Whose mind is stained is left open. Mudaliar says the friendship itself becomes a fault, Shanmuganar that it grows tainted and perishes, and Pope that it brings disappointment to those deceived.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:129":{"book":"naladiyar","num":129,"t":["ஓக்கிய ஒள்வாள்தன் ஒன்னார்கைப் பட்டக்கால்","ஊக்கம் அழிப்பதூஉம் மெய்யாகும் - ஆக்கம்","இருமையுஞ் சென்று சுடுதலால் நல்ல","கருமமே கல்லார்கண் தீர்வு."],"p":"If the glittering swords a man brandishes (be allowed to) fall into the hands of his foes, it will assuredly come about that he will thus destroy also his own power of action. So wealth (bestowed on the foolish) will go and burn up (merit accruing in) both worlds, and therefore the really good thing is to keep clear of the foolish.","r":"If a raised, gleaming sword falls into an enemy's hand it truly destroys one's strength; since ஆக்கம் among the unlearned burns one in both worlds, the good course is to get away from them.","n":"Pope offers a third sense, wealth bestowed on the foolish. On every reading the sword image says that what should protect you turns against you once it is in the wrong hands.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kallaamai","natpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","ஆக்கம் means joining the unlearned, and that association is what burns.","(கல்லார்கண்) ஆக்கம் - (பேதையரிடத்து) ஆகுதல், அஃதாவது சேர்தல்;"],["shanmuganar","ஆக்கம் is one's own growth, which friendship with the unlearned burns up in both worlds.","இம்மையிலும் மறுமையிலும் தம் வளர்ச்சியைச் சுட்டழிக்கு மாதலால்"]],"ch":"n:13"},"n:130":{"book":"naladiyar","num":130,"t":["மனைப்பாசம் கைவிடாய் மக்கட்கென் றேங்கி","எனைத்தூழி வாழ்தியோ, நெஞ்சே - எனைத்தும்","சிறுவரையே யாயினும் செய்தநன் றல்லால்","உறுபயனோ இல்லை உயிர்க்கு."],"p":"O mind! thou leavest not the bonds of home. How many cycles, pray, with thou live yearning still for children? Save the good he has done, however small its measure may be, there is no true result to any living soul.","r":"Heart, you won't let go of attachment to home, and you pine for the sake of children; for how many ages do you think you'll live? Apart from the good one has done, however small, there is no real gain for a living being.","n":"Both commentators read மனைப்பாசம் as attachment to one's wife. The verse doesn't call family evil; it says family is not the gain the soul carries.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pattru","makkal","aram","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:13"},"n:131":{"book":"naladiyar","num":131,"t":["குஞ்சி யழகும் கொடுந்தானைக் கோட்டழகும்","மஞ்சள் அழகும் அழகல்ல - நெஞ்சத்து","நல்லம்யாம் என்னும் நடுவு நிலைமையால்","கல்வி அழகே அழகு."],"p":"Beauty of locks, beauty of circling garments' folds, beauty of saffron tint: these are not beauty true. Integrity of soul that brings the conscience peace is learning's gift: that only is beauty true!","r":"The beauty of a man's hair, of a garment's curving border, of a saffron wash, is not beauty; the beauty of learning, with the fairness of heart that lets one say 'we are good', is beauty.","n":"Mudaliar notes that some read the three adornments as a man's (hair), both sexes' (garment) and a woman's (saffron), and so take the verse to commend learning for women as well as men; he says others give the verse other meanings too.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["azhagu","kalvi","naduvu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","True beauty is learning held together with conduct that lets one judge fairly that one has not strayed from virtue.","ஒழுக்கத்தோடு கல்வியுடைமையும் பெற்றிருத்தலே அழகு என்பது கருத்து."],["shanmuganar","Learning is true beauty because it produces the impartial quality of being good at heart.","நடுநிலைப் பண்பை உண்டாக்குவதால் கல்வி அழகே உண்மை அழகு"]],"ch":"n:14"},"n:132":{"book":"naladiyar","num":132,"t":["இம்மை பயக்குமால் ஈயக் குறைவின்றால்","தம்மை விளக்குமால் தாமுளராக் கேடின்றால்","எம்மை யுலகத்தும் யாம்காணேம் கல்விபோல்","மம்மர் அறுக்கும் மருந்து."],"p":"Since in this world it yields fruit; since given it grows not less ; since it makes men illustrious; since it perishes not as long as (its possessors) themselves exist; in any world we see not any medicine that, like learning, removes the delusions of sense.","r":"Learning yields fruit in this life, does not lessen when given, makes its owner shine, and does not perish while they live; in no world do we see a medicine like it for cutting away delusion.","n":"The disagreement is whether learning lasts as long as its owner or outlasts them through fame. Mudaliar notes that learning is called a medicine without delusion being called a disease, an incomplete metaphor.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","marundhu","pedhaimai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Learning does not perish for as long as its owner lives.","தாம் உளர் ஆ - தாம் உயிருள்ளவராக வாழுமளவும். கேடு இன்று - அழிதலில்லை."],["shanmuganar","Learning makes its owner live on forever through fame and does not perish.","தம்மைப்புகழால் என்றும் உள்ளவராக்கி அழியாது நிற்கும்"]],"ch":"n:14"},"n:133":{"book":"naladiyar","num":133,"t":["களர்நிலத் துப்பிறந்த உப்பினைச் சான்றோர்","விளைநிலத்து நெல்லின் விழுமிதாக் கொள்வர்","கடைநிலத்தோ ராயினும், கற்றறிந் தோரைத்","தலைநிலத்து வைக்கப் படும்."],"p":"The excellent regard the salt produced in brackish ground as choicer than the Nel from fertile soil. It is fitting to place in the first rank the learned wise, though (sprung) from the lowest origin.","r":"The worthy prize salt born in brackish ground above rice grown in fertile fields; so the learned, though of the lowest origin, are to be set in the highest place.","n":"The image works because the place of origin is called low only by habit: salt from barren soil is worth more than grain from good soil. Mudaliar takes கடைநிலத்தோர் as those born in a low caste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:134":{"book":"naladiyar","num":134,"t":["வைப்புழிக் கோட்படா வாய்த்தீயின் கேடில்லை","மிக்க சிறப்பின் அரசர் செறின்வவ்வார்","எச்சம் எனஒருவன் மக்கட்குச் செய்வன","விச்சைமற் றல்ல பிற."],"p":"It cannot be taken from its place of deposit; it does not perish anywhere by fire; if kings of surpassing grandeur are angry they cannot take it away; (and therefore) what any man should provide for his children as a legacy is learning. Other things are not (real wealth).","r":"It cannot be taken from where it is kept, it is not lost when given to a fit person, and kings angered by its eminence cannot seize it; so what a man should leave his children as their inheritance is learning and nothing else.","n":"Each clause is a way ordinary wealth is lost: theft, giving, a king's anger. Both commentators read வாய்த்து ஈயின் as giving to a fit recipient; Pope reads வாய்த் தீயின் and has learning not perish by fire.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","makkal","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:135":{"book":"naladiyar","num":135,"t":["கல்வி கரையில கற்பவர் நாள்சில","மெல்ல நினைக்கின் பிணிபல - தெள்ளிதன்","ஆராய்ந் தமைவுடைய கற்பவே நீரொழியப்","பாலுண் குருகின் தெரிந்து."],"p":"Learning hath no bounds, the learners' days are few. If you think calmly diseases many wait around! With clear discrimination learn what is meet for you, like the swan that leaving the water drinks the milk.","r":"Learning has no shore, learners have few days, and, thought over calmly, many illnesses; so the wise learn with clear discernment what is fitting, like the bird that drinks the milk and leaves the water.","n":"The argument for selection is arithmetic: boundless material, a short life, and illnesses that shorten it further. Mudaliar names the bird as the swan and notes the belief that it can separate milk from water.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","therithal","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:136":{"book":"naladiyar","num":136,"t":["தோணி இயக்குவான் தொல்லை வருணத்துக்","காணிற் கடைப்பட்டான் என்றிகழார் - காணாய்","அவன்துணையா ஆறுபோ யற்றேநூல் கற்ற","மகன்துணையா நல்ல கொளல்."],"p":"As none contemn the ferryman, by old caste rule to lowest rank assigned, but cross the stream by help he lends; so take thou teachings good and wise by help of him who is the learned man.","r":"No one despises the ferryman for being of the lowest of the old castes, but crosses the river with his help; so take what is good from a man who has learned books, whatever his birth.","n":"The argument is from ordinary practice: people already accept help from the low-born when they need it, so they should accept teaching on the same terms. The verse speaks of using the learned man's help, not of honouring him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","pirappu","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:137":{"book":"naladiyar","num":137,"t":["தவலருந் தொல்கேள்வித் தன்மை யுடையார்","இகலிலர் எஃகுடையார் தம்முள் குழீஇ","நகலின் இனிதாயின் காண்பாம் அகல்வானத்(து)","உம்ப ருறைவார் பதி."],"p":"We shall see whether any greater bliss is found in the city inhabited by the dwellers in the ample heaven than is felt when men of natures formed by old imperishable lore, from rivalries exempt keen as tempered steel, meet together and laugh.","r":"If the city of those who live in the wide heaven is sweeter than when people of sound and ancient learning, free of rivalry and sharp of mind, gather and laugh together, then we'll go and see it.","n":"The conditional is a polite refusal: Mudaliar reads it as saying that such joy is found on earth and not in heaven, so there is no wish to go.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","inbam","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:138":{"book":"naladiyar","num":138,"t":["கனைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப கற்றறிந்தார் கேண்மை","நுனியின் கரும்புதின் றற்றே - நுனிநீக்கித்","துரி஢ன்தின் றன்ன தகைத்தரோ பண்பிலா","ஈரமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"diminishes in sweetness. Lord of the cool shore of the resounding sea intimacy with learned people is like eating sugar cane from the (tender, juicy) tip; association with graceless. sapless men is like leaving the (tender) tip and eating it from the (hard, dry) root.","r":"Friendship with the learned is like eating sugarcane from the tip, sweeter as one goes; association with graceless, unfeeling people is like eating it from the root end, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea.","n":"The cane is the same in both cases; only the direction differs. So the verse is about how a bond develops over time, not how it begins.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalvi","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:139":{"book":"naladiyar","num":139,"t":["கல்லாரே யாயினும் கற்றாரைச் சேர்ந்தொழுகின்","நல்லறிவு நாளுந் தலைப்படுவர் - தொல்சிறப்பின்","ஒண்ணிறப் பாதிரிப்பூச் சேர்தலால் புத்தோடு","தண்ணீர்க்குத் தான்பயந் தாங்கு."],"p":"","r":"Even the unlearned, if they keep company with the learned, gain good sense day by day, as a new pot gives its water the scent of the bright, long-prized pathiri flowers placed in it.","n":"Mudaliar explains the image: the new pot is the unlearned person, the flowers the learned, and the scent good sense. The water gains the fragrance without becoming flowers, so the change is real but borrowed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kalvi","kallaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:140":{"book":"naladiyar","num":140,"t":["அலகுசால் கற்பின் அறிவுநூல் கல்லா(து)","உலகநூ லோதுவ தெல்லாம் - கலகல","கூஉந் துணையல்லால் கொண்டு தடுமாற்றம்","போஒம் துணையறிவா ரில்."],"p":"If men leaving works of wisdom, that contain well-weighed instruction, unstudied, devote themselves to the recitation of mere worldly literature, they will acquire a store of empty high-sounding words, but not that wisdom by means of which mental confusion (that treats unreal things as real) is removed. ------------","r":"Of all the many kinds of learning, studying worldly books while leaving the books of wisdom unread amounts only to clamour; there is no one who knows how to make confusion leave by that means.","n":"Mudaliar names the worldly books as logic and grammar, and takes தடுமாற்றம் as the stumbling through births; Pope takes it as the mind's confusion that treats unreal things as real. The verse does not say worldly learning is false, only that it cannot do this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["meyyunarvu","kalvi","piravi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:14"},"n:141":{"book":"naladiyar","num":141,"t":["உடுக்கை உலறி உடம்பழிந்தக் கண்ணும்","குடிப்பிறப் பாளர்தம் கொள்கையில் குன்றார்","இடுக்கண் தலைவந்தக் கண்ணும் அரிமா","கொடிப்புல் கறிக்குமோ மற்று."],"p":"Though their clothes may be old and their body worn with want, men of noble birth diminish nothing of their due observances. Will the lion nibble the creeping grass although sorest need should assail him?","r":"Even when their clothes are in rags and their bodies wasted, the well-born do not fall from their principles; would a lion, however hard pressed, chew creeping grass?","n":"The lion is chosen because grass is not merely beneath it but against its nature, so the verse makes principle a matter of what one is. Mudaliar records a variant reading, வரிமா, which would make the animal a tiger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:142":{"book":"naladiyar","num":142,"t":["சான்றாண்மை சாயல் ஒழுக்கம் இநவ்முன்றும்","வான்றோய் குடிப்பிறந்தார்க் கல்லது - வான்றோயும்","மைதவழ் வேற்ப. படாஅ பெருஞ்செல்வம்","எய்தியக் கண்ணும் பிறர்க்கு."],"p":"Lord of the hills traversed by clouds that touch the heavens! true excellence, and dignity, and good conduct, — these three things belong to men of race that touches heaven, and not to others, even though they may have acquired great wealth.","r":"Fullness of character, gentleness and good conduct belong only to those born of a family that touches the sky; they do not come to others even when they gain great wealth, lord of the hills where clouds creep.","n":"The point is aimed at wealth: money cannot buy these qualities. Mudaliar glosses சாயல் as mildness, restraint in mind, word and body, and notes that some take it as restraint from blame and sin.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","saandraanmai","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:143":{"book":"naladiyar","num":143,"t":["இருக்கை எழலும் எதிர்செலவும் ஏனை","விடுப்ப ஒழிதலோ டின்ன - குடிப்பிறந்தார்","குன்றா வொழுக்கமாக் கொண்டார் கயவரோடு","ஒன்றா வுணரற்பாற் றன்று."],"p":"","r":"Rising from one's seat, going out to meet, seeing a guest off, and the like are what the well-born have taken as unfailing conduct; they are not to be thought of as one with the base.","n":"Only Shanmuganar's commentary is aligned here, and the readings of விடுப்ப ஒழிதல் (as seeing off) and of whom the courtesies are for (elders who visit) are his.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","ozhukkam","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:144":{"book":"naladiyar","num":144,"t":["நல்லவை செய்யின் இயல்பாகும் தீயவை","பல்லவர் தூற்றம் பழியாகும் - எல்லாம்","உணரும் குடிப்பிறப்பின் ஊதிய மென்னோ,","புணரும் ஒருவர்க் கெனின்?"],"p":"If men (of noble birth) do good things it is natural to them. As to evil things (to commit these is impossible to them); for this would be guilt which many would bruit abroad. What greater good then can accrue to men than high-birth, if it be their lot, to which the perception of all (these things) belongs!","r":"If the well-born do good, it is called their nature; if they do evil, it becomes blame spread by many; what then does a person gain from the all-knowing high birth that falls to them?","n":"Pope reads the same question the other way, as praise: what greater good can come to anyone than high birth? So the two readings reach opposite conclusions. Mudaliar's commentary isn't aligned for this quatrain, so this can't be recorded as a contest between commentators.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pazhi","payan"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","The closing question is ironic: high birth brings no gain of its own, since good is credited to birth and evil is loudly blamed.","எல்லாம் உணர்ந்த உயர்குடியில் பிறந்ததால் அவருக்குச் சொந்த நன்மை என்னவோ?"]],"ch":"n:15"},"n:145":{"book":"naladiyar","num":145,"t":["கல்லாமை அச்சம் கயவர் தொழிலச்சம்","சொல்லாமை யுள்ளுமோர் சோர்வச்சம் - எல்லாம்","இரப்பார்க்கொன் றீயாமை அச்சம் மரத்தாரிம்","மாணாக் குடிப்பிறந் தார்."],"p":"Dread of unlearned ignorance; dread of the work that base men do; dread of forgetful slip in words which one must not utter; dread of not giving to those that ask: those who are born of a race not so distinguished by conscientious fears are as trees.","r":"Fear of being unlearned, fear of base work, fear of a slip in speech, and fear of not giving to those who ask: those born of an undistinguished family who lack all these are like wood.","n":"These fears are presented as marks of good birth, a moral sensitivity. The third fear is read two ways: Pope takes it as dread of a careless word, and Shanmuganar as the dismay of being unable to speak in an assembly of the good.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["acham","kudi","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:146":{"book":"naladiyar","num":146,"t":["இனநன்மை இன்சொல்ஒன் றீதல்மற் றேனை","மனநன்மை என்றிவை யெல்லாம் - கனமணி","முத்தோ டிமைக்கு முழங்குவரித் தண்சேர்ப்ப","இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணே யுள."],"p":"Lord of the roaring salt-sea's cool shore, where gleam rare gems with pearls!Association with the good, pleasant speech, a liberal hand, and purity of mind, —all these are only found among the nobly born.","r":"Good company, sweet speech, giving, and goodness of mind are all found only among the well-born, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea where great gems gleam with pearls.","n":"The claim is exclusive: these qualities are found only among the well-born, not merely more often.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inchol","eegai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:147":{"book":"naladiyar","num":147,"t":["செய்கை யழிந்து சிதல்மண்டிற் றாயினும்","பெய்யா ஒருசிறை பேரில் உடைத்தாகும்","எவ்வ முழந்தக் கடைத்துங் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"Though its frame-work has perished, and thronging white ants infest it, in a spacious mansion some room will still afford shelter from the rain. Thus, although want annoys them, the nobly born even yet will do what should be done.","r":"A great house whose woodwork has decayed and which white ants have overrun still keeps one corner where the rain does not fall; so even when hardship presses, the well-born do what is theirs to do.","n":"The image does not claim the house is intact, only that some shelter remains; the well-born, too, can do less but not nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","inmai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:148":{"book":"naladiyar","num":148,"t":["ஒருபுடை பாம்பு கொளினும் ஒருபுடை","அங்கண்மா ஞாலம் விளக்குறூஉம் - திங்கள்போல்","செல்லாமை செவ்வனேர் நிற்பினும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு","ஒல்கார் குடிப்பிறந் தார்."],"p":"Like the moon which affords light to the fair and spacious earth with one side, while the dragon holds the other, the nobly born do not become remiss in works of seemly benevolence, though poverty (inability) stand fronting them.","r":"Like the moon that lights the wide fair earth with one side while the serpent holds the other, the well-born do not slacken in serving others even when poverty stands squarely before them.","n":"The serpent is the eclipse, as Shanmuganar notes. The last line shares its words, ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார், with Kural 218.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","kudi","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:149":{"book":"naladiyar","num":149,"t":["செல்லா விடத்தும் குடிப்பிறந்தார் செய்வன","செல்லிடத்தும் செய்யார் சிறியவர் - புல்வாய்","பருமம் பொறுப்பினும் பாய்பரி மாபோல்","பொருமுரண் ஆற்றுதல் இன்று."],"p":"Men of mean descent, even when all goes well with them, will not perform the good deeds that the well-born will do, even when things do not go well with them. Though the antelope should bear a pillion, it rushes not to war like the prancing charger.","r":"What the well-born do even when they cannot, the small will not do even when they can; an antelope may bear a saddle, but it cannot do battle like a leaping horse.","n":"The saddle is outward equipment, so the verse says that giving the small the means does not give them the nature.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:150":{"book":"naladiyar","num":150,"t":["எற்றொன்றும் இல்லா இடத்தும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","அற்றத்தற் சேர்ந்தார்க் கசைவிடத் தூற்றாவர்","அற்றக் கடைத்தும் அகல்யா றகழ்ந்தக்கால்","தெற்றெனத் தெண்ணீர் படும்."],"p":"The nobly born, even when destitute, are props in time of feebleness to the needy ones that draw nigh to them. So, when the wide river is dry, if you dig in its bed, forth with clear waters gush out. -------------","r":"Even when they have nothing at all, the well-born are a prop to those who come to them in need, as a wide river, when dried up, yields clear water at once if you dig its bed.","n":"The river image implies that the means of giving is still there below the surface; the well-born have to be dug for, not asked from.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","eegai","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:15"},"n:151":{"book":"naladiyar","num":151,"t":["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அகனிலாப் பாரிக்கும்","திங்களும் சான்றோரும் ஒப்பர்மன் - திங்கள்","மறுவாற்றும், சான்றோரஃ தாற்றார் தெருமந்து","தேய்வர் ஒருமா சுறின்."],"p":"The moon that diffuses light through heaven's fair realms, and truly worthy men are alike: yet that endures a spot, while the truly worthy endure it. not; — perplexed and sad they pine away if but one stain appear.","r":"The noble resemble the moon that spreads its light across the sky, except that the moon carries its spot while the noble, if one stain falls on them, pine away and waste.","n":"The comparison turns into a contrast: the moon is the lesser because it puts up with its blemish. Sensitivity to stain is made the mark of superiority.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","maanam","kutram"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Because the moon is specified as the moon in the wide sky, the 'noble' of the chapter title are to be taken as the high-born.","'மேன்மக்கள்' உயர் குடிப் பிறப்புடையார் என்பது குறிப்பிற் கொள்க"]],"ch":"n:16"},"n:152":{"book":"naladiyar","num":152,"t":["இசையும் எனினும் இசையா தெனினும்","வசைதீர எண்ணுவர் சான்றோர் - விசையின்","நா஢மா உளங்கிழித்த அம்பினின் தீதோ,","அரிமாப் பிழைப்பெய்த கோல்?"],"p":"Whether success attend, or do not attend the work, the excellent will even ponder blameless ends.—Is the shaft that missed the lion worse than the arrow sent forth, that with its impulse pierced the jackal's heart?","r":"Whether the aim succeeds or not, the worthy plan only what carries no blame: is the arrow that missed a lion worse than the one that tore open a jackal's breast?","n":"Two ideas sit together: the aim must be blameless, and it must be high. A noble failure outranks a mean success.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","muyarchi","pazhi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:153":{"book":"naladiyar","num":153,"t":["நரம்பெழுந்து நல்கூர்ந்தா ராயினும் சான்றோர்","குரம்பெழுந்து குற்றங்கொ ண்டேறார் - உரங்கவறா","உள்ளமெனும் நாரினால் கட்டி உளவரையால்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"The excellent though emaciated and poor, do not transgress the limits of virtue and commit evil. With wisdom for the pillar, with perseverance as the band, they bind (the mind); and as long as they live they do what it behoves them to do.","r":"Though poverty leaves them all sinew, the worthy do not cross the bounds into wrongdoing; binding the mind with understanding and resolve, they do what ought to be done as far as their means allow.","n":"The last phrase sets a measure: duty is scaled to what one has, not abandoned. 185 ends with the same words, செய்வர் செயற்பாலவை.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","inmai","ozhukkam","ookkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:154":{"book":"naladiyar","num":154,"t":["செல்வுழிக் கண்ணொருநாள் காணினும் சான்றவர்","தொல்வழிக் கேண்மையிற் றோன்றப் புரிந்தியாப்பர்","நல்வரை நாட. சிலநாள் அடிப்படின்","கல்வரையும் உண்டாம் நெறி."],"p":"Lord of the land of goodly hills ! —If trodden for a few days a path is formed over even the craggy hill; so excellent persons, though they have seen (worthy) men only for one day, as they were travelling, will bind them to their soul, exhibiting all the marks of an ancient friendship.","r":"If the worthy see someone just once on the road, they bind that person to them as if in a friendship of long standing, just as a few days' treading makes a path even over rock.","n":"The image cuts two ways: a path on rock needs repeated treading, yet the worthy form the bond at one meeting. Pope adds that those so bound are themselves worthy; the Tamil does not say so.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:155":{"book":"naladiyar","num":155,"t":["புல்லா வெழுத்திற் பொருளில் வறுங்கோட்டி","கல்லா ஒருவன் உரைப்பவும் கண்ணோடி","நல்லார் வருந்தியும் கேட்பரே, மற்றவன்","பல்லாருள் நாணல் பரிந்து."],"p":"Even when one speaks who has an ungrammatical knowledge of the letter, but not of the meaning who is of a low (empty) school, and is unlearned the good with kindly compassion will listen, though it is pain to them, being grieved that he should be put to shame in the presence of many.","r":"When an unlearned man who knows neither grammar nor meaning speaks in an empty gathering, the good listen out of kindly regard, though it pains them, to spare him shame before many.","n":"Mudaliar glosses கண்ணோடி as தாட்சிண்யம், courteous indulgence. The good endure bad speech not for its sake but for the speaker's face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kannottam","kallaamai","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:156":{"book":"naladiyar","num":156,"t":["கடித்துக் கரும்பினைக் கண்தகர நூறி","இடித்துநீர் கொள்ளினும் இன்சுவைத்தே யாகும்","வடுப்பட வைதிறந்தக் கண்ணும் குடிப்பிறந்தார்","கூறார்தம் வாயிற் சிதைந்து."],"p":"Although you bite the sugar-cane, crush it till its joints are broken, grind it, and express its juice, it still will be sweet. The highly born, even when men have passed by abusing them so as to wound, never lose their self-respect so as to utter from their mouth (words of abuse).","r":"Sugar-cane bitten, crushed at the joints and pounded for its juice is still sweet; so the well-born, even when abused so as to wound, do not lose themselves and speak in kind.","n":"Shanmuganar reads the close as positive, that they keep speaking sweetly; the Tamil says only that they do not speak as people undone.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","porai","inchol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:157":{"book":"naladiyar","num":157,"t":["கள்ளார், கள் ளுண்ணார், கடிவ கடிந்தெரிணஇ,","எள்ளிப் பிறரை இகழ்ந்துரையார், - தள்ளியும்","வாயில்பொய் கூறார், வடுவறு காட்சியார்","சாயிற் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"They defraud not, drink not palm-wine, shun what is forbidden, never despise and speak contemptuously of their neighbours, nor even forgetfully do they utter anything untrue with their mouth : these men of faultless vision grieve not though they fall into distress.","r":"Those of faultless vision do not steal, do not drink, shun what is forbidden, do not scorn others, and never lie even by a slip; and if they decline in fortune, they do not grieve.","n":"The list of abstentions ends in an inner state: equanimity in decline is presented as the fruit of clean conduct.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ozhukkam","kalavu","kal","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:158":{"book":"naladiyar","num":158,"t":["பிறர்மறை யின்கண் செவிடாய்த் திறனறிந்து","ஏதிலா ரிற்கண், குருடனாய்த் தீய","புறங்கூற்றின் மூகையாய் நிற்பானேல், யாதும்","அறங்கூற வேண்டா அவற்கு."],"p":"Deaf to others' secrets, blind to his neighbour's wife, dumb to evil backbiting—if, knowing what is befitting, a man thus abides, it is not necessary to teach him any virtuous precepts.","r":"One who is deaf to others' secrets, blind to a neighbour's wife and dumb in evil backbiting, knowing what is fitting, needs no teaching of virtue at all.","n":"Mudaliar reads 'if he stands so' as stressing how rare this is. Virtue here is a matter of restraint of the senses, not of instruction.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","piranil","purangooral","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:159":{"book":"naladiyar","num":159,"t":["பன்னாளும் சென்றக்கால் பண்பிலார் தம்முழை","என்னானும் வேண்டுப என்றிகழ்ப - என்னானும்","வேண்டினும் நன்றுமற் றென்று விமுமியோர்","காண்டொறும் செய்வர் சிறப்பு."],"p":"They who are destitute of kindly courtesy, even after the lapse of many days, will contemn (those that visit them), saying, ‘they will ask something of us'. The excellent will treat them with distinction whenever they see them, saying, ‘if they ask something of us, it is well'.","r":"Those without good character scorn a frequent visitor, thinking he wants something from them; the excellent, thinking it good even if he asks, honour him every time they see him.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","eegai","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:160":{"book":"naladiyar","num":160,"t":["உடையார் இவரென் றொருதலையாப் பற்றிக்","கடையாயார் பின்சென்று வாழ்வா - உடைய","பிலந்தலைப் பட்டது போலாதே, நல்ல","குலந்தலைப் பட்ட விடத்து."],"p":"The lowest sort of men say, there are men of property,’— cling to them steadily, follow them, and so flourish. Is it not as when a mine of treasure has been found, when men of good lineage have become our friends. --------------","r":"The base settle on the rich as their one hope and live by trailing after them; yet meeting people of good family is like coming upon a treasure cave.","n":"The contrast is between attaching oneself to money and to lineage. The verse prizes good birth as company; it does not say what the treasure consists of.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","sirumai","selvam","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:16"},"n:161":{"book":"naladiyar","num":161,"t":["பொறுப்பரென் றெண்ணிப் புரைதீர்ந்தார் மாட்டும்","வெறுப்பன செய்யாமை வேண்டும் - வெறுத்தபின்","ஆர்க்கும் அருவி யணிமலை நன்னாட","பேர்க்குதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land whose hills resounding water-falls adorn ! — You may not even to faultless men do things that enkindle wrath; for when their wrath is once kindled, it is hard for any one to change their mood.","r":"Do not do what offends even the faultless, counting on them to forgive; once they turn away, no one can undo it.","n":"The warning targets presumption: the very forbearance of the great is what tempts the offence. Pope reads the last line as the great one's mood being hard to change; both commentators read it as the resulting harm being impossible to remove.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","porai","kedu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:162":{"book":"naladiyar","num":162,"t":["பொன்னே கொடுத்தும் புணர்தற் கா஢யாரைக்","கொன்னே தலைக்கூடப் பெற்றிருந்தும் - அன்னோ","பயனில் பொழுதாக் கழிப்பரே, நல்ல","நயமில் அறிவி னவர்."],"p":"Although those whose (pretended) wisdom is without moral excellence have gained access to men to whom approach is difficult even by gifts of gold alas! they pass away (the precious moments) as mere waste time.","r":"People of loveless cleverness who have freely gained the company of the great, whom others cannot reach even by paying gold, alas, let the time pass without profit.","n":"Mudaliar takes நயம் as delight or relish, so the fault is an inability to enjoy and draw on what the great offer, not ill will.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pedhaimai","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:163":{"book":"naladiyar","num":163,"t":["அவமதிப்பும் ஆன்ற மதிப்பும் இரண்டும்","மிகைமக்க ளான்மதிக்கற் பால - நயமுணராக்","கையறியா மாக்கள் இழிப்பும் எடுத்தேத்தும்","வையார் வடித்தநூ லார்."],"p":"Both disesteem and thorough esteem are in the class of things that depend on the estimation of the great. Those who have a discriminating knowledge of true science, lay no stress on either the abuse or the fulsome praise of man who comprehend not moral principles, and know not the rules of propriety.","r":"Disesteem and high esteem alike are for the superior to judge; those versed in refined learning take no account of the scorn or the extravagant praise of the base who know neither grace nor propriety.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","pugazh","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:164":{"book":"naladiyar","num":164,"t":["விரிநிற நாகம் விடருள தேனும்","உருமின் கடுஞ்சினம் சேணின்றும் உட்கும்","அருமை யுடைய அரண்சேர்ந்தும் உய்யார்","பெருமை யுடையார் செறின்."],"p":"The serpent rich of hue, though it dwell in the mountain-cleft, from far is frighted by the thunderbolt's fierce wrath; so men escape not though hid in strongholds hard to reach, when those great in virtue are wroth.","r":"A brightly hooded snake living in a crevice still dreads the thunder's fury from afar; so those who have angered the great do not escape even inside a fortress hard to reach.","n":"Pope reads them as those great in virtue, closer to Shanmuganar. On Mudaliar's reading the fortress is a defence against royal power; on the others, against a moral force no wall can stop.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","aran","vekuli"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The 'great' whose anger cannot be escaped are kings.","பெருமையுடையார் செறின்- பெருமை யுடைய‌ அர‌ச‌ர் கோப்பிப்பாராயின்"],["shanmuganar","The great are elders in general, angered by someone who has wronged them.","பிழை செய்தவரைப் பெரியவர் சினந்தால்"]],"ch":"n:17"},"n:165":{"book":"naladiyar","num":165,"t":["எம்மை யறிந்திலிர் எம்போல்வார் இல்லென்று","தம்மைத்தாம் கொள்வது கோளன்று - தம்மை","அரியரா நோக்கி அறனறியும் சான்றோர்","பெரியராக் கொள்வது கோள்."],"p":"‘You know not all our worth, for equals have we none:’ when self thus estimates itself, this is not ‘esteem’ ! When perfect men, proficients in virtue, regard any as dear, and esteem them as great, this is ‘esteem’ !","r":"Saying 'you do not know us; there is none like us' is not esteem; esteem is when the worthy who know virtue see one as rare and accept one as great.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","saandror","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:166":{"book":"naladiyar","num":166,"t":["நளிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப. நாணிழல் போல","விளியும் சிறியவர் கேண்மை - விளிவின்றி","அல்கு நிழற்போல் அகன்றகன் றோடுமே","தொல்புக ழாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Lord of the great sea’scool margin !—Friendships formed with mean men dwindling die like shadows of the morn; friendship of men of old renown as shadows of the after-day will lengthen out and grow.","r":"The friendship of the small dwindles like the morning shadow, while the bond of those of long renown keeps lengthening like the shadow of the declining day.","n":"Mudaliar offers two timings for the shadows (dawn against noon, or forenoon against afternoon); the point, shrinking against growing, is the same either way.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:167":{"book":"naladiyar","num":167,"t":["மன்னர் திருவும் மகளிர் எழினலமும்","துன்னியார் துய்ப்பர் தகல்வேண்டா - துன்னிக்","குழைகொண்டு தாழ்ந்த குளிர்மர மெல்லாம்","உழைதங்கண் சென்றார்க் கொருங்கு."],"p":"Those who press their suit, fitness a part, enjoy the wealth of kings, and the charms of maidens fair; so all the cool (shady) trees that droop earthward with thick foliage, give room to those that thronging seek their shelter.","r":"Those who keep close enjoy the wealth of kings and the beauty of women, without needing to be worthy; just as cool trees heavy with leaf give shelter alike to all who come near.","n":"The verse states a fact about proximity rather than merit. Under this chapter's heading it may counsel staying near the great, but neither commentator draws that out.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["mannar","selvam","nalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:168":{"book":"naladiyar","num":168,"t":["தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலார் கண்ணும்","பிரியப் பெரும்படர் நோய்செய்யும் - பெரிய","உலவா இருங்கழிச் சேர்ப்பயார் மாட்டும்","கலவாமை கோடி யுறும்."],"p":"Severance from even those who have no under standing [lit. no understanding that understands (so as) to understand] causes great and spreading sorrow. — Lord of the shore of the great, unfailing, swelling bay !—To be intimate with none is ten million times the best.","r":"Parting even from those who lack discernment brings great, lingering sorrow; so, lord of the long brimming backwaters, not mixing with anyone at all is ten million times better.","n":"This is the renunciant's conclusion drawn from the pain of attachment, and it cuts against the next chapter's praise of good company. Mudaliar spells out the comparison: not joining is better than joining and then parting.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","pattru","thuravu","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:169":{"book":"naladiyar","num":169,"t":["கல்லாது போகிய நாளும் பெரியவர்கண்","செல்லாது வைகிய வைகலும் - ஒல்வ","கொடாஅ தொழிந்த பகலும் உரைப்பின்","படாஅவாம் பண்புடையார் கண்."],"p":"Days gone by without learning, those passed without any intercourse with the great, those spent without giving what is fitting;— in the case of the excellent, if you tell them over, none such occur.","r":"Days passed without learning, without going to the great, without giving what one can: if you count them, none such are found among people of good character.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panpu","kalvi","eegai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:170":{"book":"naladiyar","num":170,"t":["பெரியார் பெருமை சிறுதகைமை ஒன்றிற்","குரியா ருரிமை யடக்கம் - தெரியுங்கால்","செல்வ முடையாருஞ் செல்வரே தற்சேர்ந்தார்","அல்லல் களைப வெனின்."],"p":"The greatness of the great is [the quality of littleness (in their own sight), i. e.] humility; the (real) acquisition of those who have acquired (any). one (science) is modest self-restraint. If you rightly understand things, those possessors of wealth only are really wealthy who relieve the wants of those that approach them (as suppliants). ----------------","r":"The greatness of the great is humility; the fitting quality of one who owns any one excellence is restraint; and, rightly seen, the wealthy are wealthy only if they relieve the distress of those who come to them.","n":"Mudaliar takes ஒன்றிற்கு as any one of learning, wealth or manliness, and records another reading, 'to the Supreme'. Pope narrows it to one science.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["panivu","adakkam","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:17"},"n:171":{"book":"naladiyar","num":171,"t":["அறியாப் பருவத் தடங்காரோ டொன்றி","நெறியல்ல செய்தொழுகி யவ்வும் - நெறியறிந்த","நற்சார்வு சாரக் கெடுமே வெயில்முறுகப்","புற்பனிப் பற்றுவிட் டாங்கு."],"p":"In youth unwise, though men consort with haughty ones and walk in lawless ways; yet, when they join with those that know the righteous path, their faults shall melt away as dew from off the grass when sunbeams scorch.","r":"Faults committed in thoughtless youth, keeping company with the unruly and going astray, vanish once one joins good people who know the right way, as dew lets go of the grass when the sun grows hot.","n":"Mudaliar names these faults the faults of association (சகவாச தோஷங்கள்). Company is both the cause of the wrong and its cure.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kutram","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:172":{"book":"naladiyar","num":172,"t":["அறிமின் அறநெறி அஞ்சுமின் கூற்றம்","பொறுமின் பிறர்கடுஞ்சொல் போற்றுமின் வஞ்சம்","வெறுமின் வினைதீயார் கேண்மை எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பெறுமின் பெரியார்வாய்ச் சொல்."],"p":"Know virtue's path ! Dread death! Beat others' words severe! Beware ye practise no deceit! Friendship detest with men of evil deeds! Daily get gain of words that fall from greatmen's lips!","r":"Know the path of virtue; dread Death; bear the harsh words of others; guard against deceit; shun friendship with evildoers; and always take in the words of the great.","n":"Mudaliar reads போற்றுமின் வஞ்சம் as preventing deceit from arising in oneself, and allows வாய்ச்சொல் to mean the blessing of the great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","kootru","porai","inam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:173":{"book":"naladiyar","num":173,"t":["அடைந்தார்ப் பிரிவும் அரும்பிணியும் கேடும்","உடங்குடம்பு கொண்டார்க் குறலால் - தொடங்கிப்","பிறப்பின்னா தென்றுணரும் பேரறிவி னாரை","உறப்புணர்க அம்மாஎன் நெஞ்சு."],"p":"Severance of close-joined friends and sore disease and death, all these combined hap to those that have assumed a human body: so the truly wise have felt that birth, from the very first, is bitterness. Ah! soul, cleave close to these!","r":"Since parting from those one is close to, grievous illness and ruin come together to all who take a body, O my heart, cling to those of great understanding who see that birth is bitter from its start.","n":"The speaker addresses his own heart. The reason for seeking the wise is not their virtue but their insight into birth as suffering.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","inam","nilaiyaamai","pirivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","கேடு is harm or misfortune in general.","கேடும் - தீங்கும்"],["shanmuganar","கேடு is death, completing the list of separation, illness and death.","கொடிய பிணியும் சாவும்"]],"ch":"n:18"},"n:174":{"book":"naladiyar","num":174,"t":["இறப்ப நினையுங்கால் இன்னா தெனினும்","பிறப்பினை யாரும் முனியார் - பிறப்பினுள்","பண்பாற்றும் நெஞ்சத் தவர்களோ டெஞ்ஞான்றும்","நண்பாற்றி நட்கப் பெறின்."],"p":"Though when you ponder it, it is surpassing: bitterness, none hate (this mortal) birth, if in (this mortal) birth they ever perform friendly acts to men whose hearts are set on noble excellence, and gain their friendship.","r":"Though birth, when thought through, is extremely bitter, no one would resent it if within it they could keep lasting friendship with people whose hearts practise goodness.","n":"Mudaliar notes that 'if they can get it' (பெறின்) marks how rare such friendship is. The verse redeems birth without denying its pain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:175":{"book":"naladiyar","num":175,"t":["ஊரங் கணநீர் உரவுநீர் சேர்ந்தக்கால்","பேரும் பிறிதாகித் தீர்த்தமாம் - ஓருங்","குலமாட்சி இல்லாரும் குன்றுபோல் நிற்பர்","நலமாட்சி நல்லாரைச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"When water from the town-sewers has joined the great river its very name is changed, and it becomes a 'sacred stream’. Ponder well! Even those who have no greatness of clan, if they ally themselves with good men of goodly fame, shall stand firm as a hill.","r":"Town drain water that joins a great river loses its name and becomes holy water; consider: even those without lineage stand like a hill by joining the good.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kudi","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:176":{"book":"naladiyar","num":176,"t":["ஒண்கதிர் வாள்மதியும் சேர்தலால் ஓங்கிய","அங்கண் விசும்பின் முயலும் தொழப்படுஉம்","குன்றிய சீர்மைய ராயினும் சீர்பெறுவர்,","குன்றன்னார் கேண்மை கொளின்."],"p":"Because it is in the bright-beamed moon the very hare in heaven's lofty fair expanse is worshipped. Though scant their worthiness, men obtain worth who share friendship with those firm as a hill.","r":"The hare in the bright moon is worshipped along with it in the sky; so those of slight worth gain worth by friendship with men as steady as a hill.","n":"Mudaliar explains the hare as the moon's dark mark: no one removes the spot before worshipping the moon, so the flaw shares the honour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","natpu","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:177":{"book":"naladiyar","num":177,"t":["பாலோ டளாயநீர் பாலாகு மல்லது","நீராய் நிறம்தெரிந்து தோன்றாதாம் - தேரின்","சிறியார் சிறுமையும் தோன்றாதாம், நல்ல","பெரியார் பெருமையைச் சார்ந்து."],"p":"Commingled with milk water becomes milk; at least, it shows no more as water by its hue. Look close, the mean men's meanness shows not if they join themselves to greatness of the good and great.","r":"Water mixed with milk becomes milk and no longer shows its own colour; so, on inspection, the smallness of small people does not show when they join the greatness of the good.","n":"The verse says the meanness is hidden, not removed. That is a weaker claim than 171, where faults vanish.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","sirumai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:178":{"book":"naladiyar","num":178,"t":["கொல்லை யிரும்புனத்துக் குற்றி யடைந்தபுல்","ஒல்காவே யாகும் உழவ ருழுபடைக்கு","மெல்லியரே யாயினும் நற்சார்வு சார்ந்தார்மேல்","செல்லாவாம் செற்றார் சினம்."],"p":"Grass close around the stump in the field adjoining the house will not be destroyed by the ploughshare of the farmer ; [thus] though men are weak, if they get under safe protection, the wrath of their foes will not reach them.","r":"Grass growing against a stump in a large field is not moved by the farmer's plough; so the anger of enemies cannot reach the weak who have joined good company.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","aran","pagai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:179":{"book":"naladiyar","num":179,"t":["நிலநலத்தால் நந்திய நெல்லேபோல் தத்தம்","குலநலத்தால் ஆகுவர் சான்றோர் - கலநலத்தைத்","தீவளி சென்று சிதைத்தாங்குச் சான்றாண்மை","தீயினம் சேரக் கெடும்."],"p":"As Nel that flourishes through the goodness of the soil, good men become illustrious by the excellence of their respective clanships. Goodness perishes when it comes near evil associates, as the tornado advances and destroys the excellence of the ship.","r":"As paddy thrives by the goodness of its soil, the worthy flourish by the goodness of their own group; and worth is ruined by joining evil company, as a gale wrecks a sound ship.","n":"Pope's 'clanships' leans toward birth. Which reading one takes decides whether this verse agrees or collides with 195.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","saandraanmai","kudi"],"pos":[["mudaliar","குலம் is the company one joins, keeping the verse within the chapter's theme.","த‌ம்த‌ம் குல‌ம் ந‌ல‌த்தால் - தாந்தாஞ் சேர்கிற‌ கூட்ட‌த்தின் நன்மையான்"],["shanmuganar","குலம் is the family one is born into; the worthy are formed by their birth.","தாம் பிறந்த குடியின் பெருமையால் உயர்ந்தோர் உருவாகின்றனர்"]],"ch":"n:18"},"n:180":{"book":"naladiyar","num":180,"t":["மனத்தான் மறுவில ரேனுந்தாம் சேர்ந்த","இனத்தால் இகழப் படுவர் - புனத்து","வெறிகமழ் சந்தனமும் வேங்கையும் வேமே","எறிபுனம் தீப்பட்டக் கால்."],"p":"Though themselves pure in mind, even good men incur contempt because of their associations. So in the woodland glade when a conflagration takes place (not the worthless brushWood only, but) the fragrant Sandal and Vengai too are consumed. --------------","r":"Though spotless in mind, people are scorned for the company they keep: when a cleared field is set on fire, the fragrant sandal and the vengai burn with the rest.","n":"The verse concerns reputation and fate together: the pure are blamed, and the fire also destroys them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:18"},"n:181":{"book":"naladiyar","num":181,"t":["ஈத லிசையா திளமைசேண் நீங்குதலால்","காத லவரும் கருத்தல்லர் - காதலித்து","ஆதுநா மென்னு மவாவினைக் கைவிட்டுப்","போவதே போலும் பொருள்."],"p":"To give is no longer ours; and youth is gone far off ; our once beloved ones think of us no more! To depart, having abandoned 'desire' that bids us love and hope for future joys, appears the thing that's meet.","r":"We can no longer give, youth has gone far off, and those who once loved us no longer think of us; it seems the right course is to give up the craving that says 'we will love and prosper' and to leave.","n":"The reasons for renouncing here are practical and even rueful, not doctrinal. Mudaliar specifies the departure as going into renunciation, and the loved ones as women.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","avaa","inmai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:182":{"book":"naladiyar","num":182,"t":["இற்சார்வின் ஏமாந்தேம் ஈங்கமைந்தேம் என்றெண்ணிப்","பொச்சாந் தொழுகுவர் பேதையார் - அச்சார்வு","நின்றன போன்று நிலையா எனவுணர்ந்தார்","என்றும் பரிவ திலர்."],"p":"Fools thinking 'we have found joy in the refuge of home,' and 'we are here in perfect repose, forgetful (of the instability of all things), go on their way. Those who have felt that 'refuge' is unstable, while it seems to stand fast, never fix their fond desire (on it).","r":"Fools think 'we found contentment by taking to home life; here we are complete', and go on heedless; those who see that such supports only seem to stand and do not last never grieve.","n":"Mudaliar allows பரிவது to mean either grieving or desiring; Pope takes the second. The verse targets complacency in home life; whether it condemns home life itself depends on the reader.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","ilvaazhkkai","pochchaappu","pattru"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The supports that fail are the attachments of household life.","அ சார்பு- (இல்வாழ்க்கையாகிய) அப் பற்றுக்கள்"]],"ch":"n:19"},"n:183":{"book":"naladiyar","num":183,"t":["மறுமைக்கு வித்து மயலின்றிச் செய்து","சிறுமைப் படாதேநீர் வாழ்மின் - அறிஞராய்","நின்றுழி நின்றே நிறம்வேறாம் காரணம்","இன்றிப் பலவு முள."],"p":"See that ye sow seed that in other world may germinate; free your lives from earth's bewilder-ment and meanness ; stand in your lot as wise men; the changing hue of things shall without cause fade, and many things be new.","r":"Without confusion sow the seed for the next life, and live wisely without being brought low; for standing just where it stands the body changes its colour without any cause, and many ills arise.","n":"Mudaliar supplies the subject: it is the body whose youth and qualities change unbidden. Pope's version speaks more vaguely of 'the changing hue of things'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","piravi","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:184":{"book":"naladiyar","num":184,"t":["உறைப்பருங் காலத்தும் ஊற்றுநீர்க் கேணி","இறைத்துணினும் ஊராற்றும் என்பர் - கொடைக்கடனும்","சாஅயக் கண்ணும் பெரியார்போல் மற்றையார்","ஆஅயக் கண்ணும் அரிது."],"p":"The well of springing water serves the town that draws and drinks, even in time when rains are scant; so great men in adverse hour dispense due gifts that others give not even in their best estate.","r":"They say a spring-fed well, even in a season of scant rain, serves the town that draws from it; the great keep their duty of giving even when they have declined, as others do not even when they have prospered.","n":"Only the Tamil and Pope are available for this quatrain; neither commentary is aligned to it.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["eegai","perumai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:185":{"book":"naladiyar","num":185,"t":["உறுபுனல் தந்துல கூட்டி அறுமிடத்தும்","கல்லூற் றுழியூறும் ஆறேபோல் - செல்வர்","பலர்க்காற்றிக் கெட்டுலந்தக் கண்ணும் சிலர்காற்றிச்","செய்வர் செயற்பா லவை."],"p":"The river pours forth a mighty stream and feeds the world; and when it is dried up, if men dig in its bed, streams gush out! So good men when rich, give to many; and, when ruined, give still at least to some, and do what should be done.","r":"Like a river that poured out abundant water to feed the world and, when dry, still yields water from springs dug in its bed, the generous who gave to many still give to a few when ruined, and do what ought to be done.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","inmai","oppuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:186":{"book":"naladiyar","num":186,"t":["பெருவரை நாட பெரியோர்கட் டீமை","கருநரைமேற் சூடேபோல் தோன்றும் - கருநரையைக்","கொன்றன்ன இன்னா செயினும் சிறியார்மேல்","ஒன்றானும் தோன்றாக் கெடும்."],"p":"Lord of the lofty hills! Any evil in men of moral greatness shows like a brand on a mighty bull; but although mean men do painful deeds, like the slaughter of that same mighty bull, none of these attracts attention.","r":"Lord of the high hills, a fault in the great shows like a brand on a great white bull, while the small may do things as cruel as killing that bull and not one of them is noticed.","n":"This is an observation about visibility, not a defence of the small. The high standard applies because the great are watched.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:187":{"book":"naladiyar","num":187,"t":["இசைந்த சிறுமை இயல்பிலா தார்கண்,","பசைந்த துணையும் பரிவாம் - அசைந்த","நகையேயும் வேண்டாத நல்லறிவி னார்கண்","பகையேயும் பாடு பெறும்."],"p":"In proportion to the degree of one's intimacy with men essentially mean and without good qualities sorrow accrues; but even the hostility of those who do not desire forbidden things even in jest will confer dignity.","r":"Intimacy with the inherently mean brings grief for as long as it lasts, but even enmity with the wise, who would not want harmful things even in jest, brings dignity.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pagai","sirumai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:188":{"book":"naladiyar","num":188,"t":["மெல்லிய நல்லாருள் மென்மை அதுவிறந்(து)","ஒன்னாருள் கூற்றுட்கும் உட்குடைமை - எல்லாம்","சலவருட் சாலச் சலவே நலவருள்","நன்மை வரம்பாய் விடல்."],"p":"With gentle fair ones use gentleness surpassing theirs; with foes display a wrath that death's self might dread; with persistent men show a persistency to match ; amongst men of good do good; let the law of life be thus laid down.","r":"Be gentler than the gentle good; toward foes, be a terror that Death itself would fear; toward the false, utterly false; toward the good, goodness itself: let these be one's bounds.","n":"A policy of matching each person in kind. Pope reads சலவர் as the persistent, to be met with persistence; Mudaliar reads them as liars, to be met with lies. Mudaliar also notes that others construe the verse differently.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pagai","vanjam","panpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Toward liars, one should be wholly a speaker of lies.","சால சலமே-மிகவும் பொய்யே பேசுதற்கும்"]],"ch":"n:19"},"n:189":{"book":"naladiyar","num":189,"t":["கடுக்கி யொருவன் கடுங்குறளைப் பேசி","மயக்கி விடினும் மனப்பிரிப் பொன்றின்றித்","துளக்க மிலாதவர் தூய மனத்தார்","விளக்கினுள் ஒண்சுடரே போன்று."],"p":"These are the pure in heart who, though any vex, and with use of slanderous words would fain perplex them, with calm unruffled mind abide unwavering, like the bright flame within the lamp.","r":"Though someone hurries in and tries to confuse them with cruel slander, the pure in heart stay unshaken, with no division in their mind, like the bright flame inside a lamp.","n":"The flame is steady because the lamp shields it; the image suggests an inner composure that slander cannot reach.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["manam","purangooral","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:190":{"book":"naladiyar","num":190,"t":["முற்றுற்றும் துற்றினை நாளும் அறஞ்செய்து","பிற்றுற்றுத் துற்றுவர் சான்றவர் - அத்துற்று","முக்குற்றம் நீக்கி முடியும் அளவெல்லாம்","துக்கத்துள் நீக்கி விடும்."],"p":"The excellent will daily give to the needy in charity their first-served food; they themselves will eat what is served after: such good conduct removes the three faults, and from sorrow sets men free, through all the days, till comes the end. ---------------","r":"The worthy daily give in charity the food served first and eat what is served after; that eating removes the three faults and keeps them from sorrow as long as life lasts.","n":"Shanmuganar names the three faults as desire, anger and delusion. Pope leaves them unnamed, and no Mudaliar gloss is aligned.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","undi","saandror","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:19"},"n:191":{"book":"naladiyar","num":191,"t":["கோளாற்றக் கொள்ளாக் குளத்தின்கீழ்ப் பைங்கூழ்போல்","கேளீவ துண்டு கிளைகளோ துஞ்சுப","வாளாடு கூத்தியர் கண்போல் தடுமாறும்","தாளாளர்க் குண்டோ தவறு."],"p":"As for kindred that feed upon what relatives give them they will die off like green grain below a tank, which does not hold a sufficient supply (of water). Is failure possible to men of energy that are quick in movement as the eyes of those who perform the sword-dance ?","r":"Like the green crop below a tank that does not hold back its water, kin live by what their relative gives and rest easy; can failure come to the energetic, who move as quickly as the eyes of women in the sword dance?","n":"Pope reads the first half in reverse: kin who live on handouts die off like crops under a tank that holds too little water. The Tamil supports Shanmuganar's 'rest' (துஞ்சுப) at least as well, so the verse probably praises the energetic as the one who feeds the family.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["aalvinai","sutram","muyarchi"],"pos":[["shanmuganar","The other kin live without hardship on what the energetic one gives.","கொடுப்பதை உண்டு மற்ற சுற்றத்தார்கள் துன்பின்றி வாழ்வர்"]],"ch":"n:20"},"n:192":{"book":"naladiyar","num":192,"t":["ஆடுகோ டாகி அதரிடை நின்றதூஉம்","காழ்கொண்ட கண்ணே களிறணைக்கும் கந்தாகும்","வாழ்தலும் அன்ன தகைத்தே ஒருவன்றான்","தாழ்வின்றித் தன்னைச் செயின்."],"p":"What once stood by the wayside, a twig that bent to every touch, when its core is developed within, may become a stake to which they tie an elephant. Life too is thus, if man himself, unfailing make himself !","r":"A twig that swayed by the path, once its core has hardened, becomes a post to tether an elephant; life is like that too, if a person makes himself without slackening.","n":"Shanmuganar reads தாழ்வின்றி as not letting oneself sink; Pope as 'unfailing'. Either way the self is something made over time.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aalvinai","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:193":{"book":"naladiyar","num":193,"t":["உறுபுலி ஊனிரை யின்றி ஒருநாள்","சிறுதேரை பற்றியும் தின்னும் - அறிவினால்","காற்றொழில் என்று கருதற்க கையினால்","மேற்றொழிலும் ஆங்கே மிகும்."],"p":"The huge tiger, when lacking flesh for food one day, may even seize a little frog and eat it. Think not 'By (all my) knowledge I only gain menial tasks;\" to the skillful hand nobler employments shall even there abound.","r":"A mighty tiger that finds no meat one day will catch and eat even a small frog; so do not think your knowledge fits you only for lowly work: through skill, higher work will come in that very place.","n":"Pope titles it 'Stoop to conquer'. Shanmuganar reads the advice as taking the humble job that is available, since better skilled work may follow.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","aalvinai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:194":{"book":"naladiyar","num":194,"t":["இசையா தெனினும் இயற்றியோ ராற்றால்","அசையாது நிற்பதாம் ஆண்மை - இசையுங்கால்","கண்டால் திரையலைக்கும் கானலந் தண்சேர்ப்ப","பெண்டிரும் வாழாரோ மற்று."],"p":"Manliness is working on, in no wise faltering, remaining stedfast though the matter succeed not. When all is successful,— Lord of the cool lovely shore, in whose groves the waves agitate the scented thorn, — will not even women live and flourish?","r":"Manliness is standing firm in the way one has undertaken even when the thing does not succeed; lord of the cool seashore groves, when things succeed by themselves, don't even women manage to live?","n":"The taunt assumes that women cannot persist through failure. The point is that persistence, not success, is the measure of effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aanmai","muyarchi","thitpam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:195":{"book":"naladiyar","num":195,"t":["நல்ல குலமென்றும் தீய குலமென்றும்","சொல்லள வல்லால் பொருளில்லை - தொல்சிறப்பின்","ஒண்பொரு ளொன்றோ தவம்கல்வி யாள்வினை","என்றிவற்றான் ஆகும் குலம்."],"p":"When men speak of 'good caste' and 'bad caste' it is a mere form of speech, and has no real meaning. Not even by possessions, made splendid by ancient glories, but by self-denial, learning, and energy is Caste determined.","r":"'Good caste' and 'bad caste' are only words with nothing behind them; not long-famous wealth but austerity, learning and effort are what make a lineage.","n":"A strong claim from a Jain text that questions birth rank. Shanmuganar takes ஒண்பொருள் ஒன்றோ as asking whether fine wealth alone suffices; Pope reads it as 'not even by possessions'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","pirappu","thavam","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:196":{"book":"naladiyar","num":196,"t":["ஆற்றும் துணையும் அறிவினை உள்ளடக்கி","ஊக்கம் உரையார் உணர்வுடையார் - ஊக்கம்","உறுப்பினால் ஆராயும் ஒண்மை யுடையார்","குறிப்பின்கீழ்ப் பட்ட துலகு."],"p":"The world is subject to the nod of the brilliant (diplomatists) who search out (and know) men's designs from outward indications (lit. from) their members, i.e. from eye, gesture, tone, expression etc.).","r":"The perceptive keep their plans to themselves until the work is done and do not voice their drive; the world lies under the sign of those bright ones who read others' drive from their features.","n":"Mudaliar takes the first ஊக்கம் as one's own effort and the second as the stirring in others' minds, so the verse pairs concealment with insight. He also records a reading in which the world serves at their nod.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kurippu","ookkam","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:197":{"book":"naladiyar","num":197,"t":["சிதலை தினப்பட்ட ஆல மரத்தை","மதலையாய் மற்றதன் வீழுன்றி யாங்குக்","குதலைமை தந்தைகண் தோன்றிற்றான் பெற்ற","புதல்வன் மறைப்பக் கெடும்."],"p":"If the banyan's trunk be eaten by the gnawing ant, its 'branch root' bears it up, like a buttress; even so, when decay appears in the sire, the son he has begotten shall hide it, and weakness is no more.","r":"When termites have eaten the banyan, its aerial roots stand as pillars and hold it up; so when weakness shows in a father, the son he begot covers it and it disappears.","n":"Mudaliar notes that மதலை, the prop, also means a son, so the image and the claim share a word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["makkal","kudi","aalvinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:198":{"book":"naladiyar","num":198,"t":["ஈனமாய் இல்லிருந் தின்றி விளியினும்","மானும் தலைவருவ செய்பவோ? - யானை","வரிமுகம் புண்படுக்கும் வள்ளுகிர் நோன்றாள்","அரிமா மதுகை யவர்."],"p":"The lion's pointed claw and mighty foot will wound the spotted face of an elephant; those who have power like his , — though bereft of all, they die in want within their home, — will they do deeds that bring disgrace?","r":"Those with the strength of a lion, whose sharp claws and mighty paws wound an elephant's spotted face, would sooner die in want and low estate at home than do anything that brings disgrace.","n":"Mudaliar reads மானம் here as dishonour (அவமானம்) and maps the lion's claws onto keen intelligence and resourceful effort.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","inmai","aanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:199":{"book":"naladiyar","num":199,"t":["தீங்கரும் பீன்று திரள்கால் உளையலா஢","தேங்கமழ் நாற்றம் இழந்தாஅங்கு - ஓங்கும்","உயர்குடி யுள்பிறப்பின் என்னாம் பெயர்பொறிக்கும்","பேராண்மை இல்லாக் கடை."],"p":"Like the flowers on a rounded stalk, with hair like filaments that sweet cane bare, when the sweet fragrance they breathed is lost, what gain accrues from birth in a lofty noble house when energy that makes the name noteworthy, is wanting?","r":"As the cane flower on its thick stalk, with its mane of filaments, loses its honeyed scent, what is birth in a lofty family worth without the great manliness that engraves one's name?","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","aanmai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:20"},"n:200":{"book":"naladiyar","num":200,"t":["பெருமுத் தரையர் பெரிதுவந் தீயும்","கருனைச்சோ றார்வர் கயவர் - கருனையைப்","பேரும் அறியார் நனிவிரும்பு தாளாண்மை","நீரும் அமிழ்தாய் விடும்."],"p":"The base feed full of rice and savoury food, that men, great lords of the triple lands, with generous gladness give; but water won with willing strenuous toil by those who know, not savoury food by name even, will turn to nectar. -------------","r":"The base fill themselves on rice and fried dishes that the great Mutharaiyar give with delight; but for those who do not even know the names of such dishes, water won by their own eager effort becomes nectar.","n":"Pope makes the Mutharaiyar 'lords of the triple lands', one of the readings Mudaliar reports. The verse brands even the guests of a generous patron as base for living on another's food.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["muyarchi","undi","kayamai","iravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The Mutharaiyar were best taken as a generous patron of the poet's own time.","முத்தரையரென்பார் ஆசிரியர் காலத்திருந்த கொடை வள்ள லொருவ ரெனக் கோடலே சிறப்புடைத் தென்க"]],"ch":"n:20"},"n:201":{"book":"naladiyar","num":201,"t":["வயாவும் வருத்தமும் ஈன்றக்கால் நோவும்","கவாஅன் மகற்கண்டு தாய்மறந் தாஅங்கு","அசாஅத்தான் உற்ற வருத்தம் உசாஅத்தன்","கேளிரைக் காணக் கெடும்."],"p":"As a mother when she sees her son upon her lap forgets the languors, the pains, and the throes of birth; so trouble arising from weakness will die when a man sees his sympathizing kinsmen around him.","r":"As a mother forgets the cravings of pregnancy, the strain of carrying and the pain of birth when she sees her son on her lap, the trouble a person has suffered through weakness vanishes when he sees kin who ask after him.","n":"The kin do nothing but come and ask how he is (உசாவுதல்). The comfort the verse describes lies in being asked after, not in material help.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","idumbai","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:202":{"book":"naladiyar","num":202,"t":["அழன்மண்டு போழ்தின் அடைந்தவர்கட் கெல்லாம்","நிழல்மரம்போல் நேரொப்பத் தாங்கிப் - பழுமரம்போல்","பல்லார் பயன்துய்ப்பத் தான்வருந்தி வாழ்வதே","நல்லாண் மகற்குக் கடன்."],"p":"To yield ready protection alike to all, as a tree affords shade to those that seek its shelter when the heat grows fierce; and to live toiling so that many may enjoy the gain, resembling thus a fruit producing tree, is the duty of the manly man.","r":"The duty of a good man is to shelter all who come to him alike, as a shade tree does in fierce heat, and to toil himself so that many enjoy what he yields, as a fruiting tree does.","n":"தான் வருந்தி makes the giver's own hardship part of the duty rather than a cost to avoid. Mudaliar takes பழுமரம் as the banyan and notes that others read it as a ripe fruiting tree, as Shanmuganar and Pope do.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oppuravu","naduvu","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:203":{"book":"naladiyar","num":203,"t":["அடுக்கல் மலைநாட. தற்சேர்ந் தவரை","எடுக்கல மென்னார் பெரியோர் - அடுத்தடுத்து","வன்காய் பலபல காய்ப்பினும் இல்லையோ","தன்காய் பொறுக்கலாக் கொம்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land where mountains piled on mountains rise! the great demur not to support their kith and kin ;—there is no bough but will support the fruit it bears, though clustered thick great fruits and many cling thereon.","r":"Lord of the land of piled-up hills, the great never say 'we will not support' those who have come to depend on them; is there any branch that cannot bear its own fruit, however many and heavy they hang?","n":"The image makes dependants the supporter's own fruit, so bearing them is part of his nature rather than an act of charity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","perumai","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:204":{"book":"naladiyar","num":204,"t":["உலகறியத் தீரக் கலப்பினும் நில்லா","சிலபகலாம் சிற்றினத்தார் கேண்மை - நிலைதிரியா","நிற்கும் பெரியோர் நெறியடைய நின்றனைத்தால்","ஒற்கமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Though mingled in a complete intimacy so that all the world knows of it, the friendship of the little will last but little time. Connection with the firm unyielding men endures till the great one's path, who never swerve, is reached.","r":"The friendship of small-natured people does not last beyond a few days, even when they mingle so fully that the world knows it; the bond with those who never falter holds as steadily as the ascetics who keep to their path to the goal.","n":"Both commentators read the comparison as ascetics who never leave the discipline of renunciation, and Mudaliar names the goal as release (வீடு). Pope's translation makes the line say how long the bond lasts, which is less clear in the Tamil.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sirumai","perumai","thavam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:205":{"book":"naladiyar","num":205,"t":["இன்னர் இனையர் எமர்பிறர் என்னும்சொல்","என்னும் இலராம் இயல்பினால் - துன்னித்","தொலைமக்கள் துன்பம்தீர்ப் பாரேயார் மாட்டும்","தலைமக்க ளாகற்பா லார்."],"p":"‘Such are they and so many;’ ‘these are ours; those strange :’ those worthy to be classed as chief of men say nothing like this ; so to speak is not their nature ; for they relieve the distress of ALL that troubled come to them!","r":"Those fit to be counted chief among men have no trace of the talk that sorts people as 'of this kind, so many, ours, others', and they draw near and relieve the distress of anyone who is perishing.","n":"In a chapter on embracing kin, this verse removes the boundary of kinship: for the best people, anyone in distress counts as their own. Mudaliar glosses எமர் and பிறர் as those we want and those we don't.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naduvu","arul","perumai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:206":{"book":"naladiyar","num":206,"t":["பொற்கலத்துப் பெய்த புலியுகிர் வான்புழுக்கல்","அக்காரம் பாலோ டமரார்கைத் துண்டலின்","உப்பிலிப் புற்கை உயிர்போல் கிளைஞர்மாட்டு","எக்காலத் தானு மினிது."],"p":"More sweet than rice, though white as tiger's claw with milk and sugar served on plate of gold, by loveless hands, is any tasteless mess, in any dish, when shared with kindred dear as life.","r":"Saltless millet gruel in any vessel, taken among kin as dear as life, is always sweeter than rice white as a tiger's claw, served with sugar and milk in a golden dish from the hands of those who are not one's own.","n":"The contrast is total on every count, the vessel, the grain, the salt and the giver, and only the giver decides the taste.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","anbu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:207":{"book":"naladiyar","num":207,"t":["நாள்வாய்ப் பெறினும்தந் நள்ளாதா ரில்லத்து","வேளாண்மை வெங்கருனை வேம்பாகும் - கேளாய்,","அபராணப் போழ்தின் அடகிடுவ ரேனும்","தமராயார் மாட்டே இனிது."],"p":"Most bitter (Margosa) is the bounteous meal of dainty food at early dawn in house of those who love us not. Hear thou! though not till evening given, the mess of herbs when eaten with our own is sweet.","r":"A hot fried feast given as hospitality at the proper hour in the house of those not close to one is bitter as neem; greens served at an untimely afternoon hour are sweet when they come from one's own people.","n":"Even the hour is reversed: the stranger's meal is punctual and the kin's is late, and the kin's is still the sweeter.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sutram","undi","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:208":{"book":"naladiyar","num":208,"t":["முட்டிகை போல முனியாது வைகலும்","கொட்டியுண் பாரும் குறடுபோல் கைவிடுவர்","சுட்டுக்கோல் போல எரியும் புகுவரே","நட்டார் எனப்படு வார்."],"p":"","r":"Those who pound at you every day like a hammer, eating off you, let go like tongs when the fire comes; those rightly called intimates enter the fire with you like the smith's poker.","n":"The three smithy tools sort people by how they behave in the heat. Mudaliar offers two readings of முனியாது, 'without tiring' or 'showing affection', and of கொட்டி, 'striking' or 'pestering'. Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","kedu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those who enter the fire are loving kin.","ந‌ட்டார் - அன்புடை உற‌வின‌ர்."],["shanmuganar","Those who enter the fire are true friends.","உண்மை நண்பர் எனப்படுவார், பொருளோடு நெருப்பிலே நிற்கும் உலையாணிக்கோல் போல"]],"ch":"n:21"},"n:209":{"book":"naladiyar","num":209,"t":["நறுமலர்த் தண்கோதாய் நட்டார்க்கு நட்டார்","மறுமையும் செய்வதொன் றுண்டோ - இறுமளவும்","இன்புறுவ இன்புற் றெழீஇ அவரோடு","துன்புறுவ துன்புறாக் கால்."],"p":"O maid adorned with fresh garlands of fragrant flowers! is there one thing that even in other world, friends may perform for friends, if till they die, their joys they share, but shun to share their griefs ?","r":"Maiden with the cool garland of fragrant flowers, if friends share their friends' joys until death but not their sorrows, is there anything they could do for them even in the next world?","n":"The claim is negative: sharing only joys makes no bond at all. Mudaliar again glosses நட்டார் as kin, where Shanmuganar has friends. The verse is addressed to a woman, which Mudaliar notes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:210":{"book":"naladiyar","num":210,"t":["விருப்பிலார் இல்லத்து வேறிருந் துண்ணும்","வெருக்குக்கண் வெங்கருனை வேம்பாம் - விருப்புடைத்","தன்போல்வா ரில்லுள் தயங்குநீர்த் தண்புற்கை","என்போ டியைந்த அமிழ்து."],"p":"The savoury fried curry, (in colour) like a cat's eye, which one eats seated a part in the house of those who are without affection, will be bitter as margosa; but cold gruel (weak and insipid), like clear water, in the house of affectionate equals, is ambrosia that cleaves to the bones. --------------","r":"A hot fried dish the colour of a cat's eye, eaten sitting apart in the house of those without affection, is bitter as neem; watery cold gruel in the house of loving equals is ambrosia that joins with one's bones.","n":"வேறிருந்து, sitting apart, names the slight: the food is served but the guest is kept separate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","undi","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:21"},"n:211":{"book":"naladiyar","num":211,"t":["கருத்துணர்ந்து கற்றறிந்தார் கேண்மையெஞ் ஞான்றுங்","குருத்தின் கரும்புதின் ற்றறே - குருத்திற்கு","எதிர்செலத்தின் றன்ன தகைத்தரோ, என்றும்","மதுர மிலாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Intimacy with those who understand the real intention (of one's words), and who have acquired wisdom by learning, will at all times be like eating sweet cane from the tender shoots; but attachment to those who have no sweetness of disposition is like eating it in a direction opposite to the tender shoot (it grows harder and less sweet).","r":"Friendship with those who have learned with understanding is always like eating sugarcane from the tip down, sweeter as one goes; the bond with those who lack sweetness is like eating it from the root toward the tip.","n":"The quatrain nearly repeats 138 in the chapter on learning, with a different refrain. Mudaliar explains that eating from the tip makes the cane grow sweeter as one goes, and from the root less sweet.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kalvi","arivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The sweetness the second group lacks is the sweetness of learning.","கல்வியறி வாகிய இனிய தன்மை யில்லாதவர்களது நட்பு"],["shanmuganar","The sweetness they lack is a sweet disposition, as Pope also has it.","என்றும் இனிய பண்பு இல்லாதவாது நட்போ"]],"ch":"n:22"},"n:212":{"book":"naladiyar","num":212,"t":["இற்பிறப் பெண்ணி இடைதிரியா ரென்பதோர்","நற்புடை கொண்டமை யல்லது - பொற்கேழ்","புனலொழுகப் புள்ளிரியும் பூங்குன்ற நாட","மனமறியப் பட்டதொன் றன்று."],"p":"Lord of the land of flowery hills, where wild-fowl golden in hue fly, scared by the rush of the waterfall. Regarding the mobility of their birth—these will not swerve:—To say thus is a good ground of confidence—(a good position to take up); but to say ‘their minds are known,' is not any (i. e. real ground).","r":"Lord of the flowering hills, where birds scatter as golden water pours down, one may befriend people on the good ground that, mindful of their family, they will not change midway; apart from that, a person's mind is not something that can be known.","n":"The verse doesn't praise high birth for its own sake. It falls back on lineage because the mind can't be read. Mudaliar draws the rule: befriend those of high family.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kudi","aaraaydhal","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:213":{"book":"naladiyar","num":213,"t":["யானை யனைவர் நண்பெரிணஇ நாயனையார்","கேண்மை கெழீஇக் கொளல்வேண்டும், - யானை","அறிந்தறிந்தும் பாகனையே கொல்லும், எறிந்தவேல்","மெய்யதா வால்குழைக்கும் நாய்."],"p":"Forsaking friendship with those who resemble the elephant, embrace and hold fast intimacy with those who are like the dog; for the elephant will slay even its keeper though it has long known him ; but the dog will wag its tail when it has in its body the javelin (hurled at it by its angry master).","r":"Give up the friendship of those who are like elephants and hold close those who are like dogs: the elephant may kill even its keeper after knowing him for years, while the dog wags its tail with its master's spear in its body.","n":"The test is loyalty under injury, not strength or rank: the elephant is the nobler animal and still loses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:214":{"book":"naladiyar","num":214,"t":["பலநாளும் பக்கத்தா ராயினும் நெஞ்சில்","சிலநாளும் ஒட்டாரோ டொட்டார் - பலநாளும்","நீத்தா ரெனக்கை விடலுண்டோ , தந்நெஞ்சத்","தியாத்தாரோ டியாத்த தொடர்பு."],"p":"Though men dwell side by side for many days when their souls cleave not (are not congenial) for even a few days they retain not their friendship. But is it possible to let go attachment's well-knit ties, though those to whom one's soul is knit dwell many days afar?","r":"Those who are not joined in heart for even a few days do not become close, though they stay beside one for many days; and would anyone let go of the bond with those tied to their heart because they have been away for many days?","n":"Nearness and distance are both set aside: the heart's tie alone makes and keeps a friend. Shanmuganar supplies 'the great' as the subject.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","pazhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:215":{"book":"naladiyar","num":215,"t":["கோட்டுப்பூப் போல மலர்ந்துபிற் கூம்பாது","வேட்டதே வேட்டதாம் நட்பாட்சி - தோட்ட","கயப்பூப்போல் முன்மலர்ந்து பிற்கூம்பு வாரை","நயப்பாரும் நட்பாரும் இல்."],"p":"The use and wont of friendship is that, what once it has loved it loves always,— like the flower on the tree-branch which having once unfolded afterwards closes not; but who will esteem, or make friends of those who are like flowers on the surface of the excavated tank, which unfold and afterwards close themselves?","r":"The true conduct of friendship is to go on loving what it first loved, like a tree's flower that opens and doesn't close again; no one esteems or befriends those who open first and close later, like the flowers of a dug pond.","n":"Pond flowers such as the lotus close each evening, so the fault condemned is changeability, not coldness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:216":{"book":"naladiyar","num":216,"t":["கடையாயார் நட்பிற் கமுகனையர் ஏனை","இடையாயார் தெங்கி னனையர் - தலையாயார்","எண்ணரும் பெண்ணைபோன் றிட்டஞான் றிட்டதே,","தொன்மை யுடையார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"The lowest sort of men in friendship are like the Arecanut tree. The middle sort are like the cocoa-nut tree. Attachments to the chief of men — who are old friends —is like the Palmyra tree of rare worth : what was given that first day was given once for all.","r":"In friendship the lowest are like the areca palm and the middle like the coconut; the bond of the highest, who keep old ties, is like the palmyra of untold worth: what was given on the first day stays given.","n":"Mudaliar explains the trees: the areca yields only with constant tending, the coconut with occasional tending, and the palmyra gives richly after a single watering. On that reading the grades measure how much a friend has to be given before the friendship yields.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pazhaimai","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:217":{"book":"naladiyar","num":217,"t":["கழுநீருள் காரட கேனும் ஒருவன்","விழுமிதாக் கொள்ளின் அமிழ்தாம் - விழுமிய","குய்த்துவையார் வெண்சோறே யாயினும் மேவாதார்","கைத்துண்டல் காஞ்சிரங் காய்."],"p":"If one receive you courteously, though what he gives is but rank herbs dressed in water in which rice had been washed, it is ambrosia. To eat from the hands of those who love us not, though it be white rice with rich spicy condiments, is nux vomica.","r":"Even dark greens cooked in rice-water are ambrosia if someone gives them with high regard; white rice with fine seasonings and relishes, taken from the hand of those not in accord with one, is the bitter strychnos fruit.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["anbu","natpu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:218":{"book":"naladiyar","num":218,"t":["நாய்க்கால் சிறுவிரல்போல் நன்கணிய ராயினும்","ஈக்கால் துணையும் உதவாதார் நட்பென்னாம்?","சேய்த்தானும் சென்று கொளல்வேண்டும், செய்விளைக்கும்","வாய்க்கால் அனையார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Of what value is the friendship of those who being very near like the little claw on a dog's leg, afford not help as much as a fly's foot? Though you go far to seek it, lay hold of the friendship of those who are like the water channel that causes the crops in the field to flourish.","r":"What use is the friendship of those as close as the little toe on a dog's foot who give not even a fly's foot of help? Go even far to gain the bond of those who are like the channel that makes the field yield.","n":"Nearness is set against usefulness, and the channel image implies that real help often comes from a distance.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","velanmai","thunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:219":{"book":"naladiyar","num":219,"t":["தெளிவிலார் நட்பின் பகைநன்று சாதல்","விளியா அருநோயின் நன்றால் - அளிய","இகழ்தலின் கோறல் இனிதேமற் றில்ல","புகழ்தலின் வைதலே நன்று."],"p":"Better hate than friendship of the ignorant. Better death than disease which comes on yielding to no remedy. Sweeter is killing thin contempt that breaks the spirit. Better abuse than undeserved praise.","r":"Enmity is better than the friendship of those without clear understanding, death better than an incurable disease, killing kinder than contempt that wounds, and abuse better than praise of qualities one doesn't have.","n":"Only the first of the four comparisons concerns friendship; the other three give it company. Mudaliar's gloss of கோறல் is printed as 'சொல்லுதல்' (speaking), probably an error for கொல்லுதல் (killing), which Shanmuganar and Pope have.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","pagai","pugazh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:220":{"book":"naladiyar","num":220,"t":["மாணஇப் பலரோடு பன்னாள் முயங்கிப்","பெரிணஇப் பொருட்டக்கார்க் கோடலே வேண்டும்","பாணஇ உயிர்செகுக்கும் பாம்பொடும் இன்னா","மாணஇஇப் பின்னைப் பிரிவு."],"p":"-----------","r":"Mix with many over many days, find who matches you, and take only those worth valuing; for it is painful to part after becoming close, even with a snake whose quick bite kills.","n":"The argument rests on the cost of parting: because breaking a bond hurts even when the friend was deadly, the testing must come first.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","aaraaydhal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:22"},"n:221":{"book":"naladiyar","num":221,"t":["நல்லா ரெனத்தாம் நனிவிரும்பிக் கொண்டாரை","அல்லா ரெனினும் அடக்கிக் கொளல்வேண்டும்.","நெல்லுக் குமியுண்டு, நீர்க்கு நுரைஉண்டு","புல்லிதழ் பூவிற்கும் உண்டு."],"p":"When those to whom with strong desire we clung as good, prove otherwise, keep the sad secret bid, - cling to them still! The growing grain has husks; the water has its foam: flowers too have scentless outer sheath of leaves.","r":"Those one eagerly took as good should be kept close, with their faults held within, even if they prove otherwise; rice has its husk, water its foam, and flowers their rough outer petals.","n":"அடக்கிக் கொளல் can mean holding the friend close or keeping the fault to oneself. Shanmuganar takes the second, and Pope's version has both.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:222":{"book":"naladiyar","num":222,"t":["செறுத்தோ றுடைப்பினும் செம்புனலோ டுடார்,","மறுத்தும் சிறைசெய்வர் நீர்நசைஇ வாழ்நர்","வெறுப்ப வெறுப்பச் செயினும் பொறுப்பரே","தாம்வேண்டிக் கொண்டார் தொடர்பு."],"p":"If though they dam it up, the fresh flood should burst its bonds, men do not feel aggrieved; but straightway imprison it again, for by the precious stream they live: so though their friends again and again do very disagreeable things, men bear with those whose friendship is dear.","r":"Those who live by water don't rage at the fresh flood when it breaks the dam each time they build it, but dam it again; in the same way people bear with those whose bond they sought, even when they do one hateful thing after another.","n":"The friend is needed the way the farmer needs the stream, so here forbearance rests on dependence as much as on goodness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:223":{"book":"naladiyar","num":223,"t":["இறப்பவே தீய செயினுந்தந் நட்டார்","பொறுத்தல் தகுவதொன் றன்றோ - நிறக்கோங்கு","உருவவண் டார்க்கு முயர்வரை நாட","ஒருவர் பொறையிருவர் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the lofty hilly land, where the bees hum through all the flowery Gongu-glade ! — Though friends should work us surpassing ills, the only thing that is meet is forbearance: patience of one is friendship of the twain.","r":"Lord of the high hills where bees hum over the bright kongu flowers, isn't it right to bear with one's friends even when they do great wrong? The patience of one is the friendship of two.","n":"ஒருவர் பொறை இருவர் நட்பு is the maxim: a friendship survives if either party absorbs the injury, so it doesn't need both to be faultless.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:224":{"book":"naladiyar","num":224,"t":["மடிதிரை தந்திட்ட வான்கதிர் முத்தம்","கடுவசை நாவாய் கரையலைக்குஞ் சேர்ப்ப","விடுதற் கா஢யா ரியல்பிலரேல் நெஞ்சம்","சுடுதற்கு மூட்டிய தீ."],"p":"Lord of the shore where pearls of purest lustre are thrown up by circling waves, and where swift darting boats are borne through the surf !—when friends who we may not leave have alien hearts, it is as a scorching fire enkindled in the soul.","r":"Lord of the shore where folding waves cast up bright pearls and swift boats rock against the beach, if those one cannot let go lack good nature, they are a fire lit to burn one's heart.","n":"The verse doesn't say to leave them. It names the pain of a bond one cannot leave. Pope's title, about being angry with those we love, is not in the Tamil. Neither commentary is aligned to this quatrain, though Shanmuganar's comment on it appears under 223.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["natpu","idumbai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:225":{"book":"naladiyar","num":225,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் விடற்பால ரல்லாரைப்","பொன்னாகப் போற்றிக் கொளல்வேண்டும் - பொன்னொடு","நல்லிற் சிதைத்ததீ நாடோ றும் நாடித்தம்","இல்லத்தில் ஆக்குத லால்."],"p":"Though those, from whom you may not part, do grievous things, O maid who art as Lakshmi! you must still cherish your chosen friends—fire destroys men's wealth and happy homes, yet is it sought there and kindled every day.","r":"Those one ought not to part from must be cherished like gold even when they do harm, since the fire that once destroyed a good house along with its gold is still sought out and kindled at home every day.","n":"Fire both destroys and is needed daily, so the friend is cherished because life needs them, not because the harm is forgotten. Pope takes பொன்னாக as an address to a maiden 'as Lakshmi'; Shanmuganar reads it as 'like gold'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:226":{"book":"naladiyar","num":226,"t":["இன்னா செயினும் விடுதற் கா஢யாரைத்","துன்னாத் துறத்தல் தகுவதோ - துன்னருஞ்சீர்","விண்குத்து நீள்வரை வெற்ப. களைபவோ","கண்குத்திற் றென்றுதங் கை."],"p":"When those from whom it is hard to part do evil things should men at once renounce them?—Lord of the lengthening hills that pierce the sky, whence rarest giftsdescend !—Do men cut off their hand because it pricked their eye?","r":"Lord of the sky-piercing hills of rare excellence, is it right to cast off those hard to part from, though they hurt you? Does anyone cut off their own hand because it poked their eye?","n":"The image makes the friend part of one's own body, so cutting them off harms oneself again and undoes nothing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:227":{"book":"naladiyar","num":227,"t":["இலங்குநீர்த் தண்சேர்ப்ப. இன்னா செயினும்","கலந்து பழிகாணார் சான்றோர் - கலந்தபின்","தீமை எடுத்துரைக்கும் திண்ணறி வில்லாதார்","தாமும் அவரிற் கடை."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the shining sea! The perfect ones when they have contracted an intimacy with any, see no faults in them, even if they do things that cause pain. Those who, being without stable wisdom, take up and tell out men's evil deeds after contracting an intimacy with them, are themselves worse than they.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of shining waters, once the worthy have joined with someone they see no fault in them even when hurt; those without firm understanding who bring out another's wrongs after joining with him are themselves lower than he is.","n":"The verse ranks exposing a friend's wrong as worse than the wrong itself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","saandror","purangooral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:228":{"book":"naladiyar","num":228,"t":["ஏதிலார் செய்த திறப்பவே தீதெனினும்","நோதக்க தென்னுண்டாம் நோக்குங்கால் - காதல்","கழுமியார் செய்த கறங்கருவி நாட","விழுமிதாம் நெஞ்சத்துள் நின்று."],"p":"Lord of the land of resounding waterfalls ! Though what those alien to us have done may be surpassingly evil, what is there to be pained at, when you regard it? Things done by affectionate friends, will be excellent when so regarded by the mind.","r":"Lord of the land of roaring falls, what is there to grieve over, on reflection, when strangers do great wrong? And what those bound by love have done becomes good when held within the heart.","n":"The same harm is treated by who did it: from a stranger it is to be shrugged off, from one who loves it is to be turned into something good within.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu","porai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:229":{"book":"naladiyar","num":229,"t":["தமரென்று தாங்கொள்ளப் பட்டவர் தம்மைத்","தமரன்மை தாமறிந்தா ராயின், - அவரைத்","தமா஢னும் நன்கு மதித்துத் தமரன்மை","தம்முள் அடக்கிக் கொளல்."],"p":"If we perceive those we have accepted as our own to be not really ours, we must pay even more respect to them than to our own, and keep concealed in our own mind the fact that they are not really ours.","r":"If you find that those you took as your own are not, honour them even more than your own and keep the knowledge that they are not yours shut within you.","n":"Pope's title says to love them the more; the verse says மதித்து, to value or honour.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","sutram","kutram"],"pos":[["mudaliar","One is to honour them more than one's real friends, with no suggestion of pretence.","த‌ம‌ரினும் ந‌ன்கு ம‌தித்து - ந‌ண்ப‌ரினும் மிக‌க் கௌர‌வித்து"],["shanmuganar","The honour is an outward show while the truth is kept inside.","தம் உறவினரினும் நன்கு மதிப்பது போல் வெளிக்குக் காட்டி"]],"ch":"n:23"},"n:230":{"book":"naladiyar","num":230,"t":["குற்றமும் ஏனைக் குணமும் ஒருவனை","நட்டபின் நாடித் திரிவேனேல் - நட்டான்","மறைகாவா விட்டவன் செல்வுழிச் செல்க","அறைகடல்சூழ் வையம் நக."],"p":"--------------","r":"If, after taking someone as my friend, I go about searching out his faults and his other qualities, may I go where the man who did not guard his friend's secret goes, while the world encircled by the roaring sea laughs.","n":"A curse spoken in the first person. Mudaliar names the destination as hell. Examining a friend belongs before the friendship (chapter 22); after it, examining is treated as betrayal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","kutram","aaraaydhal","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:23"},"n:231":{"book":"naladiyar","num":231,"t":["செறிப்பில் பழங்கூரை சேறணை யாக","இறைத்துநீர் ஏற்றும் கிடப்பர், - கறைக்குன்றம்","பொங்கருவி தாழும் புனல்வரை நன்னாட","தங்கரும் முற்றுந் துணை."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant well-watered mountain land, where boiling waterfalls pour down from the dark hills! (Poor men) linger beneath the old roof that affords no shelter, baling out the water, and making mud embankments, and enduring the down-pour; (even so unreal friends stick by you) till their purpose has been attained.","r":"Lord of the well-watered hills where waterfalls pour from dark peaks, like people who stay under an old leaking roof, making mud dams and bailing out and catching the water, some stay by you only until their own business is done.","n":"The simile is of discomfort put up with for one's own sake: they stay as one stays under a bad roof, because they need it for now. Shanmuganar adds that they pretend to share one's troubles.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","vanjam","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:232":{"book":"naladiyar","num":232,"t":["சீரியார் கேண்மை சிறந்த சிறப்பிற்றாய்","மாரிபோல் மாண்ட பயத்ததாம் - மாரி","வறந்தக்கால் போலுமே வாலருவி நாட","சிறந்தக்கால் சீரிலார் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land of pure (white, forming) waterfalls! The friendship of the virtuous is of exceeding excellence, and yields glorious results — like (seasonable) rain; but the friendship of the vicious, even in the time of its exuberance, is as when the rain fails in the time of drought.","r":"Lord of the land of white waterfalls, the friendship of the excellent is of surpassing worth and fruitful like rain; that of the worthless, even at its height, is like rain that has failed.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","perumai","sirumai","mazhai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:233":{"book":"naladiyar","num":233,"t":["நுண்ணுணர்வி னாரொடு கூடி நுகர்வுடைமை","விண்ணுலகே யொக்கும் விழைவிற்றால் - நுண்ணு஡ல்","உணர்வில ராகிய ஊதியம் இல்லார்ப்","புணர்தல் நிரயத்துள் ஒன்று."],"p":"Enjoyment of the society of men of refinement resembles the heavenly world in the pleasure it affords. Closest contact with those destitute of fine perception of the value of learned pursuits —men who gain no wisdom from you, nor you from them—is one of the hells.","r":"Enjoying the company of people of fine understanding is as desirable as the heavenly world; being joined to profitless people without understanding of subtle learning is one of the hells.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","kalvi","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:234":{"book":"naladiyar","num":234,"t":["பெருகுவது போலத் தோன்றிவைத் தீப்போல்","ஒருபொழுதுஞ் செல்லாதே நந்தும் - அருகெல்லாம்","சந்தன நீள்சோலைச் சாரன் மலைநாட","பந்தமி லாளர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land where wide groves of Sandal cover the hilly slopes! Friendship with those who feel not its real obligation, like fire in the straw (suddenly) appears, seeming as though it would increase, but never advancing dies out.","r":"Lord of the hills whose slopes are lined with long sandal groves, the bond with those who have no real tie seems to grow, then, like fire in straw, dies without lasting even a moment.","n":"Mudaliar glosses பந்தம் as the bond of love; Shanmuganar as true attachment.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:235":{"book":"naladiyar","num":235,"t":["செய்யாத செய்துநாம் என்றலும் செய்வதனைச்","செய்யாது தாழ்த்துக்கொண் டோ ட்டலும் - மெய்யாக","இன்புறூஉம் பெற்றி இகழ்ந்தார்க்கும் அந்நிலையே","துன்புறூஉம் பெற்றி தரும்."],"p":"The promising to do what cannot be done, and the putting off and leaving undone through delay things that could be done, will forthwith bring experience of sorrow even to those who have condemned truthfully the pleasant experiences of life (ascetics and saints).","r":"Saying 'we will do it' of what cannot be done, and putting off what can be done, bring suffering at once even to those who have truly scorned the enjoyment of pleasure.","n":"'Even to ascetics' means these faults hurt whatever one's indifference to pleasure. The verse doesn't mention friends; only its place in this chapter suggests these are marks of an unfit friend.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["vaaimai","madi","idumbai","thuravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:236":{"book":"naladiyar","num":236,"t":["ஒருநீர்ப் பிறந்தொருங்கு நீண்டக் கடைத்தும்","விரிநீர்க் குவளையை ஆம்பலொக் கல்லா","பெருநீரார் கேண்மை கொளினுநீர் அல்லார்","கருமங்கள் வேறு படும்."],"p":"The Ambal (water-lily) does not equalthe expanding Kuvalai though born and growing together with it in the same pool : though they attain to intimacy with those of generous instincts, the deeds of men in whom these instincts are lacking will be diverse.","r":"Though born in the same pool and growing up together, the water-lily never equals the spreading kuvalai; so though they gain the friendship of the large-natured, the deeds of those who lack that nature remain different.","n":"Pope's title speaks of a lotus; the Tamil compares the ஆம்பல் with the குவளை, the blue water-lily.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","panpu","perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:237":{"book":"naladiyar","num":237,"t":["முற்றற் சிறுமந்தி முற்பட்ட தந்தையை","நெற்றுக்கண் டன்ன விரலான் ஞெமிர்த்திட்டுக்","குற்றிப் பறிக்கும் மலைநாட இன்னாதே","ஒற்றுமை கொள்ளாதார் நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the hilly land where the immature little monkey, with its finger like a bean-pod, will flip its father when it meets him, and poke him and snatch fruit from him! Afflictive indeed is friendship with the uncongenial.","r":"Lord of the hills where the unripe little monkey, with fingers like dry bean-pods, pokes and prises at the father who meets it to snatch his food, the friendship of those not of one mind with you is painful.","n":"The image is of closeness without regard: the young monkey takes from the one nearest to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","innaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:238":{"book":"naladiyar","num":238,"t":["முட்டுற்ற போழ்தின் முடுகியென் னாருயிரை","நட்டா னொருவன்கை நீட்டேனேல் - நட்டான்","கடிமனை கட்டழித்தான் செல்வுழிச் செல்க","நெடுமொழி வையம் நக."],"p":"If I hasten not to put forth my hand and offer my precious life to my friend when in distress, may I depart whither he goes who has violated the sanctity of his friend's wedded wife, while the far-famed world laughs!","r":"If, when my friend is in distress, I do not hurry to put my precious life in his hands, may I go where the man who violated his friend's guarded wife goes, while the world of lasting words laughs.","n":"The chapter on unfit friendship includes this vow of what a fit friend owes. Mudaliar names the destination as hell and gives three readings of நெடுமொழி வையம்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","saakkaadu","pazhi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:239":{"book":"naladiyar","num":239,"t":["ஆன்படு நெய்பெய் கலனுள் அதுகளைந்து","வேம்படு நெய்பெய் தனைத்தரோ - தேம்படு","நல்வரை நாட நயமுணர்வார் நண்பெரிணஇப்","புல்லறிவி னாரொடு நட்பு."],"p":"Lord of the land of goodly hills where honey flows! To forsake the friendship of those who know the right, and cultivate that of shallow pretenders to knowledge, is like emptying out cow's ghee from a vessel and pouring into it margosa oil.","r":"Lord of the good hills where honey flows, leaving the friendship of those who know what is good to befriend the petty-minded is like emptying cow's ghee from a vessel and filling it with neem oil.","n":"","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pullarivu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:24"},"n:240":{"book":"naladiyar","num":240,"t":["உருவிற் கமைந்தான்கண் ஊராண்மை யின்மை","பருகற் கமைந்தபால் நீரளா யற்றே","தெரிவுடையார் தீயினத்தா ராகுதல் நாகம்","விரிபெடையோ டாடிவிட் டற்று."],"p":"The absence of generosity in those whose exterior is pleasing, is like the mingling of water with the milk provided for food: when men of understanding take to bad company it is like the disporting of a Cobra with a female viper. -------------","r":"One fitted for good form who lacks generosity toward his town is like milk fit to drink mixed with water; the discerning taking up with bad company is like a cobra mating with a female viper.","n":"Mudaliar records the belief that a cobra mating with a viper dies, so the image predicts ruin.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["velanmai","inam","arivu","kedu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உருவிற்கு அமைந்தான் is first one with some qualities fit for greatness; Mudaliar gives good looks only as another view.","பெருமைக்குரிய சில நற்குணங்களுடையா-னிடத்தே"],["shanmuganar","It is one handsome in form, as Pope also has it.","உருவத்தில் அழகாய் அமைந்திருப்பவனிடம்"]],"ch":"n:24"},"n:241":{"book":"naladiyar","num":241,"t":["பகைவர் பணிவிடம் நோக்கித் தகவுடையார்","தாமேயும் நாணித் தலைச்செல்லார் காணாய்","இளம்பிறை யாயக்கால் திங்களைச் சேராது","அணங்கருந் துப்பின் அரா."],"p":"Worthy men, when they behold where foes are foiled, themselves too feel sore abashed, and do not hasten on to crush them. Behold, the strong invulnerable dragon draws not near the moon (to swallow it) when it is in its tender crescent days!","r":"When the worthy see their enemies humbled, they themselves feel shame and do not advance on them; see, the snake whose strength nothing can trouble does not seize the moon while it is a young crescent.","n":"The shame belongs to the victor, not the defeated. Shanmuganar names the snake as Rahu, the eclipse serpent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pagai","naanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:242":{"book":"naladiyar","num":242,"t":["நளிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப நல்கூர்ந்த மக்கட்கு","அணிகல மாவ தடக்கம் - பணிவில்சீர்","மாத்திரை யின்றி நடக்குமேல் வாமுர்","கோத்திரம் கூறப் படும்."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the spreading sea ! To men in poverty a modest self-restraint is the chiefest ornament. If a man live in unbending pride, and in a manner unbefitting his position, his fellow-townsmen will revile his race.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the vast sea, for the poor, restraint is the ornament; if one lives without humility and beyond measure, the town one lives in will speak ill of one's family.","n":"The cost of a poor man's lack of restraint falls on his lineage, not only on himself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["adakkam","inmai","panivu","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:243":{"book":"naladiyar","num":243,"t":["எந்நிலத்து வித்திடினும் காஞ்சிரங்காய் தெங்காகா","தென்னாட் டவருஞ் சுவர்க்கம் புகுதலால்","தன்னாற்றா னாகும் மறுமை வடதிசையும்","கொன்னாளர் சாலப் பலர்."],"p":"Whatever soil you sow it in, the Strychnos nut grows not into a cocoa-palm. Some of the Southern land have entered Paradise! It is man's way of life that decides his future state. Full many from the Northern land are denizens of hell.","r":"In whatever soil it is sown, a strychnos seed will not become a coconut; since even those of the south enter heaven, one's next world comes from oneself, and in the north too there are very many idle wasters.","n":"Pope calls the northerners 'denizens of hell'; கொன்னாளர் means those who spend their days to no purpose, which is milder.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["piravi","ozhukkam","pirappu","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The south is the Tamil land, and the verse rebuts a northern claim that those born there cannot reach a good state.","தென்னாடு மிலேச்ச தேசமென்றும், அங்குப் பிறந்தார்க்கு நற்கதியில்லை யென் றுங் கூறும் வடநாட்டார் கொள்கையை மேற்கொண்டு ஓராற்றான் மறுத்தவா றிது"],["shanmuganar","The south is Yama's quarter, the direction of death, rather than a region of people.","தென்னாட்டில் (யமதிசையில்) பிறந்த வரும் விண்ணுலகம் அடைவதால்"]],"ch":"n:25"},"n:244":{"book":"naladiyar","num":244,"t":["வேம்பின் இலையுள் கனியினும் வாழைதன்","தீஞ்சுவை யாதும் திரியாதாம் ஆங்கே","இனந்தீ தெனினும் இயல்புடையார் கேண்மை","மனந்தீதாம் பக்கம் அரிது."],"p":"Though ripened amid margosa leaves the fruit of the plantain loses no atom of its sweet flavour. Even so the friendship of men of noble mood, although their race be evil, can hardly work ill to the mind.","r":"Though ripened among neem leaves, the plantain keeps its sweet taste unchanged; so, though their company is bad, the friendship of those of good nature rarely turns to a bad mind.","n":"Pope reads இனம் as 'race'; both commentators read it as the company one keeps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","panpu","natpu","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:245":{"book":"naladiyar","num":245,"t":["கடல்சார்ந்தும் இன்னீர் பிறக்கும், மலைசார்ந்தும்","உப்பீண் டுவரி பிறத்தலால் தத்தம்","இனத்தனையர் அல்லர் எறிகடற்றண் சேர்ப்ப","மனத்தனையர் மக்களென் பார்."],"p":"Though close by the sea, sweet waters oft-times spring up there; on the hill-side the waters often gush out all brine! Thus men are not as their race. — Lord of the dashing sea's cool shore! Men are as their minds.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the dashing sea, since sweet water rises even beside the sea and brackish water even on the hills, people are not as their kind but as their minds.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","manam","kudi"],"pos":[["mudaliar","People are simply not like those of their own group; the mind decides.","தங்கள் தங்கள் இனத்தாரை யொத்தவ ரல்லர்"],["shanmuganar","People are judged not only by their group but by their minds as well, so group still counts.","தம்தம் இனத்திற்கு ஏற்பமட்டும் இருப்பவரல்லர்; தம்தம் மனத்திற்கு ஏற்பவும் மதிக்கப்படுவர்"]],"ch":"n:25"},"n:246":{"book":"naladiyar","num":246,"t":["பராஅரைப் புன்னை படுகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","ஒராஅலும் ஒட்டலுஞ் செய்பவோ? - நல்ல","ம்ருஉச்செய் தியார்மாட்டும் தங்கு மனத்தார்","விராஅஅய்ச் செய்யாமை நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the cool sea-shore, where flourishes the thick-stemmed laurel ! Men whose minds are good (constant), and who adhere to whomsoever they have formed an intimacy with, will not sometimes avoid men, and at other times be intimate. It is good not to have fits of alternating warmth and indifference.","r":"Lord of the cool shore where thick-trunked punnai trees grow, do people of steady mind, who stay with whomever they have formed a good bond, leave and then rejoin? It is good not to mix with people indiscriminately.","n":"Pope takes விராய் as alternating warmth and coldness; both commentators take it as mixing with people without examination.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","manam","aaraaydhal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:247":{"book":"naladiyar","num":247,"t":["உணர உணரும் உணர்வுடை யாரைப்","புணரப் புணருமாம் இன்பம் - புணா஢ன்","தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலா தாரைப்","பிரியப் பிரியுமாம் நோய்."],"p":"Join the men who throughly feel true wisdom's inner sense, and forthwith joy joins you. Join yourself to men devoid of the accurate preception of knowledge, and then parting from them is parting from pain.","r":"Join those whose understanding grasps things as they should be grasped, and joy joins you; if you join those without discernment, the pain parts only when you part from them.","n":"The verse is built on repeated words (உணர உணரும், புணரப் புணரும், தெரியத் தெரியும், பிரியப் பிரியும்), a figure Mudaliar names.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","arivu","inbam","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:248":{"book":"naladiyar","num":248,"t":["நன்னிலைக்கண் தன்னை நிறுப்பானும், தன்னை","நிலைகலக்கிக் கீழிடு வானும் - நிலையினும்","மேன்மே லுயர்த்து நிறுப்பானும், தன்னைத்","தலையாகச் செய்வானும் தான்."],"p":"He that establishes a man in good, and he that disturbs that good position and casts him down, and he that more and more exalts a man and establishes him, and he that makes a man head (among men) is (the MAN) HIMSELF.","r":"The one who sets a person in a good state, the one who unsettles and casts him down, the one who raises him ever higher, and the one who makes him the head of others, is that person himself.","n":"Mudaliar names the cause as one's understanding or lack of it; the verse itself names none. It also names no fate, which sits uneasily with chapter 11 on old deeds, unless those deeds are counted as one's own doing too.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:249":{"book":"naladiyar","num":249,"t":["கரும வரிசையால் கல்லாதார் பின்னும்","பெருமை யுடையாரும் சேறல் - அருமரபின்","ஓதம் அரற்றும் ஒலிகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","பேதைமை யன்ற தறிவு."],"p":"Lord of the cool shore of the sounding sea, where from old time the billows roar!— In the course of their affairs when even great men follow after the unlearned, this is not folly but wisdom.","r":"Lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea, when even the great follow behind the unlearned for the sake of a task, that is not folly but understanding.","n":"The verse allows status to be set aside for a purpose (கரும வரிசையால்); it doesn't say the great should follow the unlearned in general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","perumai","kalvi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:250":{"book":"naladiyar","num":250,"t":["கருமமு முட்படாப் போகமும் துவ்வாத்","தருமமும் தக்கார்க்கே செய்யா - ஒருநிலையே","முட்டின்றி மூன்று முடியமேல் அஃதென்ப","பட்டினம் பெற்ற கலம்."],"p":"If a man has wrought all fitting works, enjoyed all seemly pleasures, done deeds of charity to worthy men: if he shall have accomplished all these three unchecked, in this one state, of him men will say: ‘that is a ship that has gained the haren' -------------","r":"If one does one's work, enjoys one's pleasures, and does virtuous giving to the worthy, and all three are completed without hindrance in a single life, people call it a ship that has reached the port.","n":"A full worldly life is praised here, in a work that mostly urges renunciation, through the image of a trading voyage brought home. Pope reads ஒருநிலையே as 'in this one state'; Shanmuganar as 'all together'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","inbam","eegai","payan"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:25"},"n:251":{"book":"naladiyar","num":251,"t":["நுண்ணுணர் வின்மை வறுமை, அஃதுடைமை","பண்ணப் பணைத்த பெருஞ்செல்வம் - எண்ணுங்கால்","பெண்ணவாய் ஆணிழந்த பேடி யணியாளோ,","கண்ணவாத் தக்க கலம்."],"p":"The want of refined knowledge is poverty, its possession is very great and abundant wealth. When one considers, will not a sexless creature, more woman than man, adorn herself with the jewels that her eye desires ?","r":"Lack of fine understanding is poverty and having it is great wealth; when one thinks about it, a eunuch who longs to be a woman will still put on the jewels that catch every eye.","n":"The simile is left unexplained in the verse. Shanmuganar draws the conclusion that the wealth of the senseless is like the eunuch's jewels: an adornment on something that lacks the essential thing. Pope's heading puts it as 'mere externals are nothing'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:252":{"book":"naladiyar","num":252,"t":["பல்லான்ற கேள்விப் பயனுணர்வார் பாடழிந்து","அல்ல லுழப்ப தறிதிரேல் - தொல்சிறப்பின்","நாவின் கிழத்தி யுறைதலால் சேராளே","பூவின் கிழத்தி புலந்து."],"p":"Men of vast and varied lore are seen in low estate, and suffer want. Would you know the reason? The anciently renowned ‘Lady of the tongue' abides with them). ‘The Lady of the flowers' is jealous, and draws not near !","r":"If you would know why men of wide learning lose standing and suffer want: the ancient Lady of the Tongue lives with them, so the Lady of the Flower keeps away in a sulk.","n":"A playful explanation (Mudaliar names the figure தற்குறிப்பேற்றம், an imputed cause), not a theology of fortune. Mudaliar offers two reasons for the quarrel: Lakshmi and Sarasvati are mother-in-law and daughter-in-law, or two women do not share one man in peace.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","selvam","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:253":{"book":"naladiyar","num":253,"t":["கல்லென்று தந்தை கழற அதனையோர்","சொல்லென்று கொள்ளா திகழ்ந்தவன் - மெல்ல","எழுத்தோலை பல்லார்முன் நீட்ட விளியா","வழுக்கோலைக் கொண்டு விடும்."],"p":"He who, when his father urgently bade him learn, did not take it as a serious matter, but contemned it; when, before many men, some one gently presents a written palm-leaf, will in anger fetch a stick to beat him as guilty of an insult.","r":"One who scorned his father's urging to learn, not taking it as worth heeding, will grab a stick in anger when someone quietly holds out a written leaf to him before many people.","n":"The anger is the unlearned man's shame turned outward: he cannot read the leaf in public. Mudaliar records other readings of the last line, one in which he laughs in contempt, one in which he makes a noise to slip away.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","kalvi","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:254":{"book":"naladiyar","num":254,"t":["கல்லாது நீண்ட ஒருவன் உலகத்து","நல்லறி வாள ரிடைப்புக்கு - மெல்ல","இருப்பினும் நாயிருந் தற்றே, இராஅது","உரைப்பினும் நாய்குரைத் தற்று."],"p":"When a man who has grown up without learning enters the society of the wise, if he sit still, it is as if a dog sat there; and if he rise to speak, it is as though a dog barked.","r":"When a man who grew up without learning enters among the wise, if he sits quietly it is as if a dog sat there, and if he speaks it is as if a dog barked.","n":"Silence does not save him: Mudaliar allows மெல்ல இருப்பினும் to mean 'even if he sits with restraint', which makes the verse deny the unlearned any good way to be present.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kallaamai","avai","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:255":{"book":"naladiyar","num":255,"t":["புல்லாப்புன் கோட்டிப் புலவ ரிடைப்புக்குக்","கல்லாத சொல்லும் கடையெல்லாம் - கற்ற","கடாஅயினும் சான்றவர் சொல்லார் பொருண்மேல்","படாஅ விடுபாக் கறிந்து."],"p":"All the baser sort consorting with scholars of a heterodox and low school, will utter illiterate rubbish; but men replete with learning, though urgently asked, utter not the results of their learning, knowing that (the asker) would fail to apply their minds to the import of what was said.","r":"The low all go among the poets of petty, senseless assemblies and say things they never learned; but the worthy, even when asked, do not speak of what they have learned, knowing it would not be taken in.","n":"Pope calls the assembly 'heterodox'; neither commentator says so. Mudaliar has it as an assembly not joined to understanding, and Shanmuganar's poets are false poets.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","saandror","sol","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:256":{"book":"naladiyar","num":256,"t":["கற்றறிந்த நாவினார் சொல்லார்தம் சோர்வஞ்சி","மற்றைய ராவார் பகர்வர் பனையின்மேல்","வற்றிய ஓலை கலகலக்கும் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","பச்சோலைக் கில்லை யொலி."],"p":"Men of learned tongues are silent, fearing some slip; others (ignorant men) will speak out; on the Palmyra tree the dried-up leaves make a loud rustling noise ; but evermore the green leaf gives forth no sound !","r":"The learned and eloquent hold back, afraid of a lapse, while others talk freely: on the palmyra the dry leaves rattle, but the green leaf never makes a sound.","n":"Mudaliar notes the images answer the statements in reverse order (green leaf to the learned, dry leaves to the rest). Silence here signals life and fullness, noise dryness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","kalvi","adakkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:257":{"book":"naladiyar","num":257,"t":["பன்றிக்கூழ்ப் பத்தரில் தேமா வடித்தற்றால்","நன்றறியா மாந்தர்க் கறத்தா றுரைக்குங்கால்","குன்றின்மேற் கொட்டுந் தறிபோல் தலைதகர்ந்து","சென்றிசையா வாகுஞ் செவிக்கு."],"p":"","r":"Telling the way of virtue to people who do not know the good is like pressing sweet mango juice into a pig's trough; like a stake driven on a rock, it splits at the head and does not go into their ears.","n":"Two similes for two failures: the teaching is wasted (the trough) and it cannot penetrate (the stake on rock). Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain, and Shanmuganar's commentary appears under 256.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","pedhaimai","sol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:258":{"book":"naladiyar","num":258,"t":["பாலால் கழீஇப் பலநாள் உணக்கினும்","வாலிதாம் பக்கம் இருந்தைக் கிருந்தன்று","கோலால் கடாஅய்க் குறினும் புகலொல்லா","நோலா உடம்பிற் கறிவு."],"p":"Though you wash it with milk for many days and dry it, charcoal on no hypothesis becomes white ! So into the undisciplined body wisdom enters not, though you teach it, driving it in with a stick.","r":"Charcoal washed in milk and dried for many days never turns white, and understanding will not enter a body that has not earned it, though you drive it in with a stick.","n":"நோலா உடம்பு is the hinge. Both commentators take it as a body without merit from good deeds (Mudaliar: from a former birth, so without innate understanding); Pope reads 'undisciplined body', which moves the cause from past births to present discipline.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","vinai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:259":{"book":"naladiyar","num":259,"t":["பொழிந்தினிது நாறினும் பூமிசைதல் செல்லாது","இழிந்தவை காமுறூஉம் ஈப்போல், - இழிந்தவை","தாங்கலந்த நெஞ்சினார்க் கென்னாகும் தக்கார்வாய்த்","தேன்கலந்த தேற்றச்சொல் தேர்வு."],"p":"To those whose minds are full of foul things, — like the fly which goes not to feed on the flower that pours forth sweetness and breathes perfume, but fixes its eager desire on ordure,—What clear comprehension can there be of the lucid words full of honied sweetness that issue from the mouths of the worthy?","r":"Like the fly that will not settle on a flower pouring honey and fragrance but craves filth, those whose minds are set on low things get nothing from the clear, honeyed words of the worthy.","n":"The fault is in desire, not capacity: the fly passes the flower by preference. Mudaliar notes that 'low things' is a polite way of naming filth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sol","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:260":{"book":"naladiyar","num":260,"t":["கற்றா ருரைக்கும் கசடறு நுண்கேள்வி","பற்றாது தன்னெஞ் சுதைத்தலால் - மற்றுமோர்","தன்போ லொருவன் முகநோக்கித் தானுமோர்","புன்கோட்டி கொள்ளுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"The base man does not apprehend the faultless words of accurate instruction which the learned utter. These pain his mind. He therefore looks in the face of some other one like himself (for encouragement), and convenes a wretched assembly of his own: i.e. He finds one like-minded, and the two set up a sect. -------------","r":"Because his heart kicks away the faultless, subtle teaching of the learned, the base man looks into the face of another like himself and sets up a petty assembly of his own.","n":"The rejection breeds a rival: two of a kind confirm each other. Pope reads it as founding a sect; Mudaliar's கோட்டி is the talk given there, Shanmuganar's a platform speech.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kelvi","avai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:26"},"n:261":{"book":"naladiyar","num":261,"t":["அருகல தாகிப் பலபழுத்தக் கண்ணும்","பொரிதாள் விளவினை வாவல் குறுகா","பெரிதணிய ராயினும் பீடிலார் செல்வம்","கருதும் கடப்பாட்ட தன்று."],"p":"The bat approaches not the Heronia, with its dry stem, though it be night at hand and bear abundant fruit; so although mean people are very close at hand, their wealth is not a thing that can be counted upon.","r":"Bats do not go to the wood-apple tree with its cracked trunk, though it stands near and bears much fruit; so the wealth of men without worth, however close they live, is not something anyone thinks of counting on.","n":"Nearness is the point: what is close but unapproachable is as good as far. Mudaliar specifies who does not count on it: the poor.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:262":{"book":"naladiyar","num":262,"t":["அள்ளிக்கொள் வன்ன குறுமுகிழ வாயினும்","கள்ளிமேல் கைந்நீட்டார் சூடும்பூ அன்மையால்","செல்வம் பெரிதுடைய ராயினும் கீழ்களை","நள்ளார் அறிவுடை யார்."],"p":"Men reach not out their band to the Kalli (Cactus), though it bears delicate round buds by the handful, because these are not flowers they can weave into a garland to crown themselves withal; so wise people from no friendships with the base however great their wealth may be.","r":"No one reaches for the cactus, though its small buds look lovely enough to gather by the handful, because they are not flowers to wear; so the wise do not seek out the base, however rich.","n":"The cactus buds are attractive but unusable; wealth is the attraction, character the use. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 261.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inam","kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:263":{"book":"naladiyar","num":263,"t":["மல்கு திரைய கடற்கோட் டிருப்பினும்,","வல்லூற் றுவரில் கிணற்றின்கண் சென்றுண்பர்","செல்வம் பெரிதுடைய ராயினும் சேண்சென்றும்","நல்குவார் கட்டே நசை."],"p":"Though men live on the curved shore of the sea with its multitudinous waves, they go and drink at the well, with its perennial fountain of fresh water from the rock; so even if those (who are neighbours) are very wealthy, the desire (of the poor) is towards the liberal, though these may be far to seek.","r":"Though they live on the shore of the wave-filled sea, people go and drink at a well with a strong, sweet spring; so though the rich are near, desire turns to the givers, even far away.","n":"The sea is huge and useless to drink; the small well gives. Size of wealth does not draw anyone, only its being given. Mudaliar's commentary is missing for this quatrain, and Shanmuganar's appears under 261.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","selvam","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:264":{"book":"naladiyar","num":264,"t":["புணர்கடல்சூழ் வையத்துப் புண்ணியமோ வேறே","உணர்வ துடையா ரிருப்ப - உணர்விலா","வட்டும் வழுதுணையும் போல்வாரும் வாழ்வரே","பட்டும் துகிலும் உடுத்து."],"p":"In the world surrounded by the (all-)embracing sea, merit is quite an indifferent matter! Understanding ones are (poor); and even those of no understanding—who are like mere palm tree tufts and brinjals—live prosperously, clad in silks and rich garments.","r":"In this sea-girt world merit is a thing apart: while men of understanding are there, even those with no understanding, like the dice-piece and the brinjal, live clad in silk and fine cloth.","n":"புண்ணியம் (merit) is set apart from understanding: prosperity follows merit, not sense. Mudaliar works out the two images: the dice-piece that returns to the board after being cut down (the shameless who come back to the assembly) and the brinjal that fruits hidden (those who hide their wealth). Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["selvam","arivu","vinai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:265":{"book":"naladiyar","num":265,"t":["நல்லார் நயவர் இருப்ப நயமிலாக்","கல்லார்க்கொன் றாகிய காரணம் - தொல்லை","வினைப்பய னல்லது வேனெடுங் கண்ணாய்","நினைப்ப வருவதொன் றில்."],"p":"While pleasant folk and just abide (in poverty), you ponder why men unjust and ignorant have any joy. It is fruit of 'ancient deeds', thou whose long eyes are darts :—to thoughtful mind no other cause occurs.","r":"Long-eyed one, when the good and just remain poor, the only cause you will find on reflection for the unjust and unlearned having wealth is the fruit of old deeds.","n":"The verse answers the scandal of undeserved wealth with former deeds, and so denies that present wealth says anything about present worth. Mudaliar specifies good deeds of a former birth. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vinai","selvam","oozh"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:266":{"book":"naladiyar","num":266,"t":["நாறாக் தகடேபோல் நன்மலர்மேற் பொற்பாவாய்","நீறாய் நிலத்து விளியரோ - வேறாய","புன்மக்கள் பக்கம் புகுவாய்நீ பொன்போலும்","நன்மக்கள் பக்கம் துறந்து."],"p":"O golden dame, that sittest like a scentless leaf on a beauteous flower! Die, and fall in ashes to the ground: thou enterest homes of worthless men of perverse mind, forsaking good men pure as gold.","r":"Golden lady seated on the fine flower like its scentless outer petal, may you die and turn to ash on the earth: you leave the good, who are like gold, and go to the side of worthless men.","n":"A curse on Lakshmi herself. Mudaliar explains the simile: as the outer petal lacks the flower's scent, the goddess who lives on the lotus has none of its softness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:267":{"book":"naladiyar","num":267,"t":["நயவார்கண் நல்குரவு நாணின்று கொல்லோ","பயவார்கண் செல்வம் பரம்பப் பயின்கொல்","வியவாய்காண் வேற்கண்ணாய் இவ்விரண்டும் ஆங்கே","நயவாது நிற்கு நிலை."],"p":"Has poverty, that bides with men of righteous souls, no shame? Does wealth to ungenerous men stick like glue?O thou of dart-like eye, with wonder see that thus, no just discernment made, these two abide !","r":"Does poverty feel no shame in staying with the good? Is the wealth of the useless a glue that holds fast? See with wonder, dart-eyed one, how these two stay just where they ought not.","n":"Poverty and wealth are made into agents with manners: one shameless, the other sticky. The verse states the wonder without explaining it; 265 gives the explanation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:268":{"book":"naladiyar","num":268,"t":["வலவைக ளல்லாதார் காலாறு சென்று","கலவைகள் உண்டு கழிப்பர் - வலவைகள்","காலாறும் செல்லார் கருனையால் துய்ப்பவே","மேலாறு பாய விருந்து."],"p":"Men who are not void of shame will travel forth on foot, and feed on scraps, —so pass their days ; the shameless ones make no journeys on foot, but feed on dainties at home, perspiring over the feast.","r":"Those who are not shameless walk long roads and live on mixed scraps, while the shameless never stir a foot and feast at home on fried dishes as rivers of ghee and milk flow over them.","n":"Shame is what keeps the decent poor; its absence is what lets the others thrive. Mudaliar takes the scraps as begged food and the feasters as eating alone behind closed doors without giving; Shanmuganar names the feasters' fault as dishonesty and deceit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","inmai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:269":{"book":"naladiyar","num":269,"t":["பொன்னிறச் செந்நெல் பொதியொடு பீள்வாட","மின்னொளிர் வானங் கடலுள்ளுங் கான்றுகுக்கும்","வெண்மை யுடையார் விழுச்செல்வம் எய்தியக்கால்","வண்மையும் அன்ன தகைத்து."],"p":"While the red paddy's golden germ is parched within the ear and dies, the cloud gleaming with lightnings pours forth its treasures on the sea. When silly men gain ample wealth, even so are their liberal gifts bestowed !","r":"While the golden red paddy withers in the ear, the lightning-lit cloud pours its rain into the sea; the giving of fools who come into great wealth is of the same kind.","n":"The fault is not that fools never give but that they give where it is not needed. Mudaliar's 'also' (உம்மை) implies they otherwise do nothing good with wealth either.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","pedhaimai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:270":{"book":"naladiyar","num":270,"t":["ஓதியும் ஓதார் உணர்விலார் ஓதாதும்","ஓதி யனையார் உணர்வுடையார் - தூய்தாக","நல்கூர்ந்தும் செல்வர் இரவாதார், செல்வரும்","நல்கூர்ந்தார் ஈயா ரெனின்."],"p":"Men void of understanding, though they learn, learn not! Men of understanding, though unlearned, are as men learned! They are rich, though utter paupers, who never beg; the rich are paupers if they bestow nothing ! -------------","r":"Those without understanding remain unlearned though they study, and those with understanding are as the learned though they have not studied; those who never beg are rich though poor, and the rich are poor if they do not give.","n":"Four reversals that make the inner quality, not the outward possession, decide the name. Mudaliar takes தூய்தாக as a pure mind, a condition on the poor who do not beg.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","kalvi","iravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:27"},"n:271":{"book":"naladiyar","num":271,"t":["நட்டார்க்கும் நள்ளா தவர்க்கும் உளவரையால்","அட்டது பாத்துண்டல் அட்டுண்டல் - ஆட்டது","அடைத்திருந் துண்டொழுகும் ஆவதின் மாக்கட்கு","அடைக்குமாம் ஆண்டைக் கதவு."],"p":"To eat your own meal, after sharing what you have cooked, to the extent of your ability, both with those who are friends and those who are not friends, is 'cooking and eating' (= is real house keeping). To the good-for-nothing human beings whose habit of life it is to shut themselves up and eat alone what they have cooked, the door of yonder world will be shut.","r":"To share what is cooked, as far as one has, with friends and with those who are not friends, and then eat, is what cooking and eating means; for the worthless who shut the door and eat inside, the door of the other world is shut.","n":"The punishment mirrors the fault: a shut door for a shut door. Mudaliar takes 'friends' as kin and 'not friends' as guests; Pope has 'friends and foes', which widens the duty further than either commentator.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","virundhu","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:272":{"book":"naladiyar","num":272,"t":["எத்துணை யானும் இயைந்த அளவினால்","சிற்றறஞ் செய்தார் தலைப்படுவர் - மற்றைப்","பெருஞ்செல்வம் எய்தியக்கால் பின்னறிதும் என்பார்","அழிந்தார் பழிகடலத் துள்."],"p":"Whatever the measure be, those who do even lesser acts of charity to the measure of their power shall attain to excellence. But those who, when they have obtained great wealth. say 'we will be wise (and give) by and by,' are lost in a sea of guilt.","r":"Those who do small acts of virtue, however little, within their means, rise; those who say they will think about it after they gain great wealth are lost in a sea of blame.","n":"The verse reverses the usual excuse: it is the small giver who is great and the one waiting for great wealth who drowns. Mudaliar notes that 'are lost' is past tense for certainty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","kaalam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:273":{"book":"naladiyar","num":273,"t":["துய்த்துக் கழியான் துறவோர்க்கொன் றீகலான்","வைத்துக் கழியும் மடவோனை - வைத்த","பொருளும் அவனை நகுமே உலகத்து","அருளும் அவனை நகும்."],"p":"The senseless man who spends not time in enjoyment (of his wealth), and who gives nothing to pions devotees, but hoards and dies, —him his hoarded wealth derides; him all that is gracious in the world derides.","r":"The fool who neither enjoys his wealth nor gives anything to renunciants, but hoards it and dies, is laughed at by the very wealth he stored, and by the compassion in the world.","n":"The wealth itself is made to mock him: it outlives him and was never used. The gift named is to renunciants (துறவோர்), a pointer to the Jain setting. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:274":{"book":"naladiyar","num":274,"t":["கொடுத்தலும் துய்த்தலும் தேற்றா இடுக்குடை","உள்ளத்தான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம் - இல்லத்து","உருவுடைக் கன்னியரைப் போலப் பருவத்தால்","ஏதிலான் துய்க்கப் படும்."],"p":"The great wealth obtained by the man of straitened soul, who knows not how either to give or to enjoy, shall be enjoyed by a stranger, in due season—like lovely virgins remaining unmarried in the dwelling.","r":"The great wealth of the tight-hearted man who knows neither how to give nor how to enjoy will in due season be enjoyed by a stranger, like a lovely daughter of the house.","n":"The daughter is raised in the house and goes to an outsider when she comes of age; the image makes the loss natural and certain, not a mishap.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:275":{"book":"naladiyar","num":275,"t":["எறிநீர்ப் பெருங்கடல் எய்தி யிருந்தும்","அறுநீர்ச் சிறுகிணற் றூறல்பார்த் துண்பர்","மறுமை யறியாதா ராக்கத்தின் சான்றோர்","கழிநல் குரவே தலை."],"p":"Though they have got the mighty sea with its dashing waves (to drink from), men wait for the stream slowly issuing from the little well, often dry, and drink there; so the exceeding poverty of the virtuous is preferable to the wealth of those who know not of the world to come.","r":"Though they live by the great sea with its dashing waves, people wait at a small, nearly dry well for its trickle and drink; so the deep poverty of the worthy is better than the wealth of those who know nothing of the next world.","n":"The sea is plenty that cannot be used; the small well is little that can. Shanmuganar takes 'knowing the next world' as knowing and doing the good deeds that belong to it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:276":{"book":"naladiyar","num":276,"t":["எனதென தென்றிருக்கும் ஏழை பொருளை","எனதென தென்றிருப்பன் யானும் - தனதாயின்","தானும் அதனை வழங்கான் பயன்துவ்வான்","யானும் அதனை அது."],"p":"As to the property which the wretched churl claims saying 'It is mine, it is mine ,' I too chime in with \"It is mine, it is mine;' for if it is his, he himself spends it not, nor enjoys the benefit of it; and I, too, neither spend nor enjoy it!","r":"The wealth the fool keeps calling 'mine, mine', I too call 'mine, mine': if it is his, he neither gives it nor enjoys it, and neither do I.","n":"A joke with a sting: ownership that produces no use is no different from not owning. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 275.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:277":{"book":"naladiyar","num":277,"t":["வழங்காத செல்வரின் நல்கூர்ந்தார் உய்ந்தார்","இழந்தா ரெனப்படுதல் உய்ந்தார் - உழந்ததனைக்","காப்புய்ந்தார் கல்லுதலும் உய்ந்தார்தங் கைந்நோவ","யாப்புயந்தார் உய்ந்த பல."],"p":"The poor have escaped much from which rich men that dispense not suffer. They have escaped the reputation of having lost (their substance). They have escaped the toil of saving it. They have escaped (the labour of) digging (to hide it). They have escaped the ache of hands securing it from powerful plunderers. Many are the (sorrows) they have escaped.","r":"The poor have escaped much that troubles the rich who do not give: being called ruined, the toil of guarding, the digging to bury it, the aching hands that clutch it; many are their escapes.","n":"The list is ironic consolation, not praise of poverty: the comparison is only with the miser. Pope reads the last escape as tying it up against powerful plunderers; Mudaliar as gripping it tight.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","ivaral","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:278":{"book":"naladiyar","num":278,"t":["தனதாகத் தான்கொடான் தாயத் தவரும்","தமதாய போழ்தே கொடாஅர் - தனதாக","முன்னே கொடுப்பின் அவர்கடியார் தான்கடியான்","பின்னை அவர்கொடுக்கும் போழ்து."],"p":"While it was his he gave not; and his heirs, how it is theirs, give not. Before, while it was his, if he had given, they would not have reproved (him); and afterwards, if they had given, he would not have reproved them! Men's own avarice is the sole reason for the lack of charity.","r":"While it was his he did not give, and his heirs do not give once it is theirs; had he given first, they would not have stopped him, and when they give later, he cannot stop them.","n":"The argument removes every excuse: nobody prevented either party from giving. Pope's added sentence states the moral, that only one's own avarice stops giving.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["ivaral","eegai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:279":{"book":"naladiyar","num":279,"t":["இரவலர் கன்றாக ஈவார்ஆ வாக","விரகிற் சுரப்பதாம் வண்மை - விரகின்றி","வல்லவர் ஊன்ற வடிஆபோல் வாய்வைத்துக்","கொல்லச் சுரப்பதாம் கீழ்."],"p":"","r":"Liberality is to yield gladly, as a cow does to its calf, the askers being the calf and the givers the cow; the base yield, like a cow milked by force, only when strong men press and torment them.","n":"The same act, giving, is good or base by the way it comes out. Mudaliar records several readings of வாய் வைத்து: by many devices, by reviling, or with instruments. Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","kayamai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:280":{"book":"naladiyar","num":280,"t":["ஈட்டலும் துன்பமற் றீட்டிய வொண்பொருளைக்","காத்தலும் ஆங்கே கடுந்துன்பம் - காத்தல்","குறைபடில் துன்பம் கெடில்துன்பம் துன்பக்கு","உறைபதி மற்றைப் பொருள்."],"p":"Gathering it together is trouble, and even so the guarding of resplendent wealth is severe trouble. If the guarded heap diminish, it is trouble. If it perish, it is trouble. Wealth is trouble's very dwelling-place! ---------------","r":"Gaining wealth is suffering, guarding it is bitter suffering, its dwindling is suffering and its loss is suffering: wealth is the very dwelling place of suffering.","n":"The verse speaks of wealth itself, not only the miser's, though the chapter setting is not giving. Every stage, from earning to loss, is suffering, so no stage is safe. Shanmuganar's commentary is missing.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","idumbai","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:28"},"n:281":{"book":"naladiyar","num":281,"t":["அத்திட்ட கூறை அரைச்சுற்ற வாழினும்","பத்தெட் டுடைமை பலருள்ளும் பாடெய்தும்","ஒத்த குடிப்பிறந்தக் கண்ணுமொன் றில்லாதார்","செத்த பிணத்தின் கடை."],"p":"Though he wraps a cloth dyed red around his loins, a dozen coins or so, will gain (the wretch) respect among many men! The man devoid of wealth, though born of noble race, is viler (in the world's estimation) than a lifeless corpse !","r":"Even one who lives with a dyed cloth wrapped round his waist gains standing among many if he has ten or eight coins; those who have nothing, though born into a fitting family, are lower than a corpse.","n":"The ochre or patched cloth (Mudaliar gives both) marks someone of no standing; a handful of coins outweighs it, while noble birth without money counts for less than a dead body. The verse reports how the world judges, without endorsing it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:282":{"book":"naladiyar","num":282,"t":["நீரினும் நுண்ணிது நெய்யென்பார், நெய்யினும்","யாரும் அறிவர் புகைநுட்பம் - தேரின்","நிரப்பிடும்பை யாளன் புகுமே, புகையும்","புகற்கா஢ய பூழை நுழைந்து."],"p":"Where water cannot enter! the more insinuating ghi glides in; and smoke as a subtler power to penetrate than even ghi. If you look into it, the man debased by poverty will enter haunts where smoke scarce finds a way.","r":"Ghee is said to be finer than water and everyone knows smoke is finer than ghee; but if you consider it, the man in the affliction of want gets in through openings even smoke can hardly pass.","n":"A comic hyperbole of need pressing in anywhere for help. Mudaliar's first reading divides the phrase as இரப்பு இடும்பை, the affliction of begging, and gives நிரப்பு, poverty, as another; Shanmuganar and Pope take poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:283":{"book":"naladiyar","num":283,"t":["கல்லோங் குயர்வரைமேல் காந்தள் மலராக்கால்","செல்லாவாம் செம்பொறி வண்டினம் - கொல்லைக்","கலாஅற் கிளிகடியுங் கானக நாட","இலாஅஅர்க் கில்லை தமர்."],"p":"When on the high hill's crags the Kanthal blooms no more, the crimson-spotted beetle tribe seek not its boughs;—Lord of the hills wherefrom they scare parrots with stones! — the needy have no kin.","r":"Lord of the forest land where they scare parrots from the millet with stones: when the kanthal does not flower on the high rocky hill, the red-spotted bees do not go there; the poor have no kin.","n":"Kin are bees and wealth the flower: they come for what is given, not for the person. The address to a hill chief borrows the frame of the akam poems.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:284":{"book":"naladiyar","num":284,"t":["உண்டாய போழ்தின் உடைந்துழிக் காகம்போல்","தொண்டரா யிரவர் தொகுபவே - வண்டாய்த்","திரிதரும் காலத்துத் தீதிலிரோ என்பார்","ஒருவரும் இல்வுலகத் தில்."],"p":"When wealth is there, obsequious myriads will assemble, like crows around the fallen corpse. When wealth, as the beetle wheels its flight, is gone, no one in all the world will ask, ‘Is it well with you?'","r":"While there is wealth, a thousand gather as servants, like crows at a carcass; when one wanders like a bee, no one in the world asks 'are you well?'","n":"The crows at the carcass make the flatterers feeders on the rich man, not friends. Both commentators make the poor man the one wandering like a bee for food; Pope makes the wealth the thing that flies off like a beetle.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:285":{"book":"naladiyar","num":285,"t":["பிறந்த குலமாயும் பேராண்மை மாயும்","சிறந்ததங் கல்வியும் மாயும் - கறங்கருவி","கன்மேல் க்ழுஉங் கணமலை நன்னாட","இன்மை தழுவப்பட் டார்க்கு."],"p":"Lord of the pleasant land of clustering hills whose crags are washed by sounding waterfalls !—Their race is nought, their manly prowess is nought, their rare learning is nought, when men are held in poverty's embrace.","r":"Lord of the good land of clustered hills where loud waterfalls wash the rocks: for those whom poverty has seized, the family they were born into, great manliness and their excellent learning all perish.","n":"The three goods named are ones normally thought independent of wealth: birth, character and learning. Mudaliar lists the qualities of ஆண்மை as fullness, forbearance, discernment and firmness, or else as valour.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","kudi","aanmai","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:286":{"book":"naladiyar","num":286,"t":["உள்கூர் பசியால் உழைநசைஇச் சென்றார்கட்கு","உள்ளூ ரிருந்துமோன் றாற்றாதான் - உள்ளூர்","இருந்துயிர் கொன்னே கழியாது தான்போய்","விருந்தினன் ஆதலே நன்று."],"p":"Although he dwells within the village, and sees the poor draw nigh with hungry soul desiring aid, he yields them none : why then in the village does he vainly pass his days? It were better he went to be a guest himself !","r":"One who lives in his own village but can give nothing to those who come to him driven by hunger had better go away and be a guest somewhere than waste his life staying there.","n":"Who is meant decides the tone. On Mudaliar's reading it is advice to a poor man, matching 288; on Shanmuganar's and Pope's it is scorn for a miser, and Shanmuganar lets 'guest' also mean a guest of the other world, that is, death.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","inmai","virundhu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The man is poor and unable to help, and is advised to go and live as a guest elsewhere.","ஓர் உதவியுஞ் செய்ய மாட்டாத வறியவன்"],["shanmuganar","The man is a miser who gives nothing, and would do better to leave for another place or the other world.","உள்ளூரில் வாழ்ந்தும் ஒன்றும் கொடுக்காத கருமி"]],"ch":"n:29"},"n:287":{"book":"naladiyar","num":287,"t":["நீர்மையே யன்றி நிரம்ப எழுந்ததங்","கூர்மையும் எல்லாம் ஒருங்கிழப்பர் - கூர்மையின்","முல்லை அலைக்கும் எயிற்றாய். நிரப்பென்னும்","அல்லல் அடையப்பட் டார்."],"p":"O thou whose teeth vie in sharpness with jasmine buds! — When sharp distress of poverty assails, men lose all their attributes of goodness at once, with the mind's acuteness gained from amplest stores of wisdom.","r":"Girl whose teeth outdo the sharp jasmine buds: those overtaken by the affliction called want lose at once not only their good nature but their fully risen sharpness of mind and everything else.","n":"The verse plays on கூர்மை: the sharpness lost by the poor is held up against the sharpness of the girl's teeth. Mudaliar notes that நிரப்பு, literally fullness, is used here for its opposite. Shanmuganar's commentary appears under 286.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","panpu","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:288":{"book":"naladiyar","num":288,"t":["இட்டாற்றுப் பட்டொன் றிரந்தவர்க் காற்றாது","முட்டாற்றுப் பட்டும் முயன்றுள்ளூர் வாழ்தலின்","நெட்டாற்றுச் சென்று நிரைமனையில் கைந்நீட்டும்","கெட்டாற்று வாழ்க்கையே நன்று."],"p":"","r":"Rather than live on in one's own village, straitened and blocked and struggling, unable to give to one who asks, it is better to go a long way and live the ruined life of holding out one's hand at row after row of houses.","n":"Begging is ranked above the shame of refusing an asker at home. Mudaliar says it repeats 286 and cites Kural 478 for இட்டு (narrow). Pope's translation is missing for this quatrain.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","iravu","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:289":{"book":"naladiyar","num":289,"t":["கடகம் செறிந்ததங் கைகளால் வாங்கி","அடகு பறித்துக்கொண் டட்டுக் - குடைகலனா","உப்பிலி வெந்தைதின் றுள்ளற்று வாழ்பவே,","துப்புரவு சென்றுலந்தக் கால்."],"p":"The hands once loaded with golden bracelets now cull the forest-herb and cook the meal; and then eat the mess unseasoned, from the palm-leaf for a dish! Thus sad at heart they live, when fortune is gone and ruin come.","r":"When their means are spent and gone, with the hands that once wore bracelets they pull down branches, pick greens, cook them, and eat the boiled leaves without salt from a folded palm-leaf, living on with broken spirit.","n":"The bracelets on the hands that now strip branches carry the whole fall in one image. Mudaliar cites Kalittokai for குடை as a palm-leaf vessel.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","nilaiyaamai","undi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:290":{"book":"naladiyar","num":290,"t":["ஆர்த்த பொறிய அணிகிளர் வண்டினம்","பூத்தொழி கொம்பின்மேல் செல்லாவாம் - நீர்த்தருவி","தாழா உயர்சிறப்பின் தண்குன்ற நன்னாட","வாழாதார்க் கில்லை தமர்."],"p":"The humming spotted beetle tribes all bright in hue gather not on the branch that has ceased to blow. Lord of the good cool hilly land, of high renown, whence bounteous streams flow down unceasingly—the unprosperous have no kin! --------------","r":"Lord of the cool, good hill land whose waterfalls never fail: the bright, humming, spotted bees do not go to the branch that has flowered and finished; those who have lost their prosperity have no kin.","n":"Mudaliar notes it shares the thought of 283. The difference is small: here the branch has flowered and finished, so the stress falls on prosperity once had and lost.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:29"},"n:291":{"book":"naladiyar","num":291,"t":["திருமதுகை யாகத் திறனிலார் செய்யும்","பெருமிதம் கண்டக் கடைத்தும் - எரிமண்டிக்","கானந் தலைப்பட்ட தீப்போல் கனலுமே,","மான முடையார் மனம்."],"p":"The mind of those possessed of honourable feeling will kindle into flame, like the fire that has caught the forest when the conflagration rages, whenever they behold the haughty acts of those who are destitute of virtuous habits, and to whom their wealth is their only strength.","r":"Whenever they see the arrogance of the worthless, whose only strength is their wealth, the minds of men of honour blaze like fire raging through a forest.","n":"The fire is inward: the verse says the mind burns, not that they act on it. Mudaliar quotes a definition of மானம் as not falling from one's station and not living on when brought low by fate.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","selvam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:292":{"book":"naladiyar","num":292,"t":["என்பாய் உகினும் இயல்பிலார் பின்சென்று","தம்பா டுரைப்பரோ தம்முடையார் - தம்பாடு","உரையாமை முன்னுணரும் ஒண்மை யுடையார்க்கு","உரையாரோ தாமுற்ற நோய்."],"p":"Will (honourable men who are) 'masters of themselves' follow graceless men to tell of their sufferings, tbough fallen away to mere skeletons ? Do they not (rather) tell the pain they have felt to those enlightened souls that understand their sufferings before they speak ?","r":"Will those who own themselves follow the graceless to tell of their plight, even if they waste to bone? Will they not tell their pain to the clear-sighted, who grasp it before it is spoken?","n":"Honour here does not forbid asking; it chooses whom to ask. The right patron reads the need before it is said, so the honourable never have to beg in words.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","iravu","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:293":{"book":"naladiyar","num":293,"t":["யாமாயின் எம்மில்லம் காட்டுதும் தாமாயின்","காணவே கற்பழியும் என்பார்போல் - நாணிப்","புறங்கடை வைத்தீவர் சோறும் அதனால்","மறந்திடுக செல்வர் தொடர்பு."],"p":"As for ourselves we would introduce them (these friends) to our household : but as regards them, they are ashamed (of us), as though they said, as soon as they (the ladies) saw them (the friends,) it would be the destruction of their womanly reserve; and so they seat us at the backdoor and give us rice! Therefore let us dismiss all thought of rich men's friendship.","r":"We would bring our guests into our homes, but they, as if their wives' chastity would be lost if we so much as saw them, shame-faced, set rice before us at the back door; so forget the friendship of the rich.","n":"Both commentators take the poor speaker as one who would let his wife serve guests, while the rich hide theirs and feed the poor at the back. Mudaliar notes another reading in which இல்லம் is simply the house and கற்பு is firmness.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","natpu","selvam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:294":{"book":"naladiyar","num":294,"t":["இம்மையும் நன்றாம் இயல்நெறியும் கைவிடாது","உம்மையும் நல்ல பயத்தலால் - செம்மையின்","நானம் கமழும் கதுப்பினாய். நன்றேகாண்","மான முடையார் மதிப்பு."],"p":"O thou whose locks diffuse the odour of pleasant musk! Behold, especially good is (it to cultivate) that which is esteemed by men of honourable mind; for in this world it is (obviously) good : and, since it leads to perseverance in the way of virtue, it will yield good things in yonder world also.","r":"Girl with hair fragrant with musk, see how good it is, the esteem of men of honour: it does good in this life and, never leaving the right way, yields good things in the next.","n":"Honour is justified by its fruit in both worlds, which makes it a part of virtue rather than pride. Mudaliar glosses மதிப்பு as the principle men of honour hold, and notes another reading: being well regarded by others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","ozhukkam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:295":{"book":"naladiyar","num":295,"t":["பாவமும் ஏனைப் பழியும் படவருவ","சாயினும் சான்றவர் செய்கலார் - சாதல்","ஒருநாள் ஒருபொழுதைத் துன்பம் அவைபோல்","அருநவை ஆற்றுதல் இன்று."],"p":"The men 'fulfilled of excellence,' though death were the alternative, do not deeds that entail sin and guilt. Death is an affliction for one day, and for a little while. There is nothing that works irreparable ill like those (deeds).","r":"The worthy will not do what brings sin and blame, even if they must die: death is the pain of one day, one moment, and nothing does such lasting harm as those.","n":"The argument is a calculation of duration: death's pain is brief, sin and blame's are not. Mudaliar separates them: sin follows one into later lives, blame stays in this world.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["saandror","pazhi","saakkaadu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:296":{"book":"naladiyar","num":296,"t":["மல்லன்மா ஞாலத்து வாழ்பவ ருள்ளெல்லாம்","செல்வ ரெனினும் கொடாதவர் நல்கூர்ந்தார்","நல்கூர்ந்தக் கண்ணும் பெருமுத் தரையரே,","செல்வரைச் சென்றிரவா தார்."],"p":"Poor are the men that give not, even though deemed wealthiest of all that flourish on the teeming apple earth! They who even when they are poor seek not as suppliants wealthy men are 'Lords of the three mighty lands.'","r":"Among all who live in this rich, wide world, those who do not give are poor even if they are rich; and those who, even in poverty, do not go and beg from the rich are the great Muttaraiyar.","n":"பெருமுத்தரையர் is a name of liberality: Mudaliar takes them as generous patrons of the author's time, also named in an earlier quatrain, and Shanmuganar as a royal line; Pope translates the name as 'lords of the three mighty lands'.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["eegai","iravu","inmai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:297":{"book":"naladiyar","num":297,"t":["கடையெலாம் காய்பசி அஞ்சுமற் றேனை","இடையெலாம் இன்னாமை அஞ்சும் - புடைபரந்த","விற்புருவ வேனெடுங் கண்ணாய் தலையெல்லாம்","சொற்பழி அஞ்சி விடும்."],"p":"Thou who hast long dart-like eyes with eye-brows extending far, like a bow! the lowest class of men dread burning hunger; the other (or middle class) dread what is unpleasant; all the chief of men fear words that impute crime.","r":"Girl of long dart-like eyes under wide bow-brows: the lowest all fear burning hunger, the middle fear hardship, and the highest fear only the blame in people's words.","n":"A scale of persons by what they fear. Shanmuganar adds what follows from each fear: the lower will do anything, the highest refrain from wrong.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhi","acham","pasi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:298":{"book":"naladiyar","num":298,"t":["நல்லர் பெரிதளியர் நல்கூர்ந்தார் என்றெள்ளிச்","செல்வர் சிறுநோக்கு நோக்குங்கால் - கொல்லன்","உலையூதும் தீயேபோல் உள்கனலும் கொல்லோ,","தலையாய சான்றோர் மனம்."],"p":"When the very worthy and thoroughly learned see the rich men's glance of disparagement, as they say contemptuously, 'These are good people —persons in greatly reduced circumstances—poor folks,' does not their mind kindle into flame within them, like the fire by the breath of the bellows on the blacksmith's forge?","r":"When the rich look down on them and say with contempt, 'good people, very kind, now poor', the minds of the foremost worthy may burn within like fire blown in a smith's forge.","n":"The insult is disguised as praise. Both commentators take அளியர் as kind or compassionate, and Shanmuganar makes the kindness the cause of the poverty; Pope takes it as pitiable, 'in greatly reduced circumstances'. Mudaliar glosses எற்றி where the verse has எள்ளி.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","saandror","inmai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:299":{"book":"naladiyar","num":299,"t":["நச்சியார்க் கீயாமை நாணன்று நாணாளும்","அச்சத்தால் நாணுதல் நாணன்றாம் - எச்சத்தின்","மெல்லிய ராகித்தம் மேலாயார் செய்தது","சொல்லா திருப்பது நாண்."],"p":"It is no shame (disgrace) not to (be able to give to those who desire it of us. The shrinking on account of fear (felt) day by day is not shame (modesty). But to become reduced in other ways, and not to [dare to] tell what injuries those who love us not have inflicted on us is shame (disgrace, or self-respect).","r":"Not giving to those who come wanting is no shame, and shrinking each day from fear is no shame; the shame, when one is reduced by want, is to leave unspoken what one's superiors did to one.","n":"What the superiors did is left unnamed, so the verse can mean either keeping silent about their slights or failing to acknowledge their help, and the sense of நாண் shifts with it. Mudaliar gives both readings; Pope takes it as not daring to tell their injuries.","x":1,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["naanam","maanam","nanri"],"pos":[["mudaliar","His main reading: what the richer did is the slight they showed, which one does not speak of; he also reports the reading that one fails to proclaim their help.","தம்மினும் மேலான செல்வர்கள் தமக்குச் செய்த எளிமையை"],["shanmuganar","The greatest shame is failing to speak gratefully of help one's superiors gave in one's poverty.","தம்மினும் மேலானவர் தமக்குச் செய்த உதவியை நன்றியுடன் வெளியில் சொல்லாதிருப்பதே"]],"ch":"n:30"},"n:300":{"book":"naladiyar","num":300,"t":["கடமா தொலைச்சிய கானுறை வேங்கை","இடம்வீழ்ந்த துண்ணா திறக்கும் - இடமுடைய","வானகம் கையுறினும் வேண்டார் விழுமியோர்","மானம் மழுங்க வரின்."],"p":"The jungle-haunting tiger that slays the wild cow, refuses to eat and passes by what has fallen (of itself) in its path (i. e. carrion); so the excellent, though the wide realms of heaven were within their reach, would not desire them, if to be obtained (only) by the loss of honour. --------------","r":"The forest tiger that has killed a great beast will starve to death rather than eat it if it falls on its left; so the excellent would not want even wide heaven placed in their hands if it came at the dulling of their honour.","n":"Both commentators take கடமா as the elephant (Mudaliar also gives wild cow) and இடம் வீழ்ந்தது as falling on the left, citing a Purananuru poem on the tiger's custom; Pope reads a wild cow and carrion fallen in its path. Heaven itself, not just wealth, is refused.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","perumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:30"},"n:301":{"book":"naladiyar","num":301,"t":["நம்மாலே யாவரிந் நல்கூர்ந்தார் எஞ்ஞான்றும்","தம்மாலாம் ஆக்கம் இலரென்று - தம்மை","மருண்ட மனத்தார்பின் செல்பவோ தாமும்","தெருண்ட அறிவி னவர்."],"p":"Will men themselves possessed of clear discerning knowledge follow after those who have yielded themselves up to mental bewilderment, and who (erroneously) say (of suppliants), ‘These poor folks depend entirely on us, evermore are they without resources in themselves ?'","r":"Would people of clear understanding follow after the self-deluded rich, who tell themselves that the poor live only through them and have no means of their own?","n":"The fault named is the patron's self-importance, not his stinginess. Mudaliar glosses தம்மை மருண்ட as being dazed by one's own estimate of oneself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:302":{"book":"naladiyar","num":302,"t":["இழித்தக்க செய்தொருவன் ஆர உணலின்","பழித்தக்க செய்யான் பசித்தல் தவறோ?","விழித்திமைக்கு மாத்திரை யன்றோ ஒருவன்","அழித்துப் பிறக்கும் பிறப்பு."],"p":"Is not a man's dying and birth again measured by the twinkling of an eye? Is it then a fault if a man rather choose to suffer hunger, doing no blameworthy actions, than to feed full, doing things that entail disgrace ?","r":"Since dying and being born again takes no longer than opening the eyes and blinking, is it wrong for a man to go hungry without doing anything blameworthy rather than eat his fill by doing what degrades him?","n":"The argument rests on the shortness of a life: a full stomach bought with disgrace feeds a body that will be gone in a blink. Mudaliar gives three ways to take the eye image, one of them sleeping and waking, and cites Kural 339 for it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pasi","pazhi","piravi","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:303":{"book":"naladiyar","num":303,"t":["இல்லாமை கந்தா இரவு துணிந்தொருவர்","செல்லாரும் அல்லர் சிறுநெறி - புல்லா","அகம்புகுமின் உண்ணுமின் என்பவர்மாட் டல்லான்","முகம்புகுதல் ஆற்றுமோ மேல்?"],"p":"There will never be wanting those who, with their destitution as their support (making it their excuse), will venture upon mendicancy, and tread the way of humiliation; but will the noble-minded man enter (as a suppliant) the presence of any save of those who will embrace him and say, 'Enter my dwelling, eat of my food ?'","r":"Some, leaning on their poverty, will dare to beg and take the mean road, but the worthy go before no one except those who embrace them and say 'come in, eat'.","n":"Mudaliar reads முகம் புகுதல் as putting up with a patron's frown, and Shanmuganar as showing one's own face to a patron. Either way, the only giver the worthy approach is one who receives them as a guest.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","virundhu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:304":{"book":"naladiyar","num":304,"t":["திருத்தன்னை நீப்பினும் தெய்வம் செறினும்","உருத்த மனத்தோ டுயர்வுள்ளி னல்லால்","அருத்தம் செறிக்கும் அறிவிலார் பின்சென்று","எருத்திறைஞ்சி நில்லாதாம் மேல்."],"p":"Though fortune forsake him, and fate frown, the man of lofty soul, dwelling with steadfast mind on things above, disdains to stand with bending neck in the train of the foolish who hoard their wealth.","r":"Though fortune leaves him and the gods turn against him, the man of worth keeps his mind on his own high standing and never stands with bowed neck behind fools who hoard their wealth.","n":"திரு and தெய்வம் are the two supports a person might count on, and the verse takes both away before asking for steadiness. The stance is independence from fortune, not defiance of it. Mudaliar takes உருத்த மனம் as a mind roused with energy.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maanam","ookkam","oozh","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:305":{"book":"naladiyar","num":305,"t":["கரவாத திண்ணன்பின கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும்","இரவாது வாழ்வதாம் வாழ்க்கை - இரவினை","உள்ளுங்கால் உள்ளம் உருகுமால் என்கொலோ","கொள்ளுங்கால் கொள்வார் குறிப்பு."],"p":"To live asking naught even from those dear as an eye, whose love is sure, and who never refuse, is happy life. Since the mind dissolves in shame at the very thought of beggary,— when men receive alms, what are the receivers' thoughts, I pray ?","r":"Real living is to live without asking even of those dear as one's eyes, whose love is firm and who hold nothing back; the heart melts at the mere thought of begging, so what must the asker feel at the moment of taking?","n":"The last line moves from the giver to what the receiver feels while taking. Mudaliar reads கொள்வார் குறிப்பு as the taker's state of mind; Shanmuganar names it as shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","manam","karavu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:306":{"book":"naladiyar","num":306,"t":["இன்னா இயைக இனிய ஒழிகென்று","தன்னையே தானிரப்பத் தீர்வதற் - கென்னைகொல்","காதல் கவற்றும் மனத்தினாற் கண்பாழ்பட்டு","ஏதி லவரை இரவு."],"p":"When a man himself, begs of himself, saying, ‘Let troubles come, let joys depart!' the sting of poverty is extracted; why then, for this purpose, should any one, his mind agitated with desire, and his eye wasted with weeping, beg of strangers ?","r":"When poverty can be ended by asking of oneself, 'let pain come, let pleasures go', why should anyone, with a mind racked by desire and eyes sunk, go begging of strangers?","n":"Mudaliar divides தானிரப்ப two ways: தான் நிரப்ப, filling oneself with contentment, or தான் இரப்ப, begging of oneself. Pope takes the second and Shanmuganar the first. On either reading the cure for poverty is inward: consenting to hardship removes the want that drives begging.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","avaa","samanilai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:307":{"book":"naladiyar","num":307,"t":["என்றும் புதியார் பிறப்பினும் இவ்வுலகத்","தென்று மவனே பிறக்கலான் - குன்றின்","பரப்பெலாம் பொன்னொழுகும் பாயருவி நாட","இரப்பாரை எள்ளா மகன்."],"p":"Lord of the land where gold is borne down by rushing waterfalls from every hilly slope! — Though in this world new (kinds of) men are continually born, that very man is never born that does not scorn mendicants.","r":"Though new people are born into this world every day, the man who does not scorn beggars has never been born, lord of the hill country where waterfalls carry gold down every slope.","n":"Mudaliar gives two reasons for the overstatement: such a man reaches the good state and is not born again, or he simply cannot be found. The first plays on பிறக்கலான் ('is not born').","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","sirumai","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:308":{"book":"naladiyar","num":308,"t":["புறுத்துத்தன் இன்மை நலிய அகத்துத்தன்","நன்ஞானம் நீக்கி நிறீஇ ஒருவனை","ஈயாய் எனக்கென் றிரப்பானேல் அந்நிலையே","மாயானோ மாற்றி விடின்."],"p":"Want wastes his outward frame; he lays aside wisdom, his inward being's good; and so resolving, begs of some stranger, saying, 'Give to me.’— If then the boon be refused, must he not that very instant die?","r":"With want wasting his body outside and his good sense put away inside, a man goes to another and says 'give to me'; if he is refused, will he not die on the spot?","n":"Two losses come before the refusal: the body already worn down by want, and the understanding set aside so that asking is possible at all. Shanmuganar adds that the death would come from shame.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["iravu","inmai","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:309":{"book":"naladiyar","num":309,"t":["ஒருவ ரொருவரைச் சார்ந்தொழுகல் ஆற்றி","வழிபடுதல் வல்லுத லல்லால் - பரிசழிந்து","செய்யீரோ என்னானும் என்னுஞ்சொற் கின்னாதே","பையத்தான் செல்லும் நெறி?"],"p":"Men form close intimacies with others and live as their dependants, and this is permissible ; but is it more painful quietly to go one's way (to the hermitage) than, lost to all sense of personal dignity, to say, 'Will ye not do aught for me?’","r":"It is proper to attach oneself to another and serve him with respect, but is the slow road of poverty more painful than losing one's dignity and saying 'won't you do something for me?'","n":"Both commentators allow dependence on a patron, with deference, and forbid only the words of asking, so the line is drawn in speech. Mudaliar derives பைய from பை (suffering); Shanmuganar takes it as 'slowly'. Pope's 'to the hermitage' is his own addition.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["iravu","maanam","sol","inmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:310":{"book":"naladiyar","num":310,"t":["பழமைகந் தாகப் பசைந்த வழியே","கிழமைதான் யாதானும் செய்க - கிழமை","பொறார் அவரென்னின் பொத்தித்தம் நெஞ்சத்","தறாஅச் சுடுவதோர் தீ."],"p":"Relying on old friendship, in an affectionate manner, render such assistance as your intimacy Warrants. If he will not endure (the assistance), will it not be a fire heaped up and burning in his bosom? ----------------","r":"Where old acquaintance has grown into affection, one should do whatever help that intimacy calls for; if the claim of intimacy is not borne, it becomes a fire banked in the heart that burns without end.","n":"Whose heart burns is not settled. Shanmuganar says it is the old friend who came to ask and was turned away. Pope reverses the roles: help is offered, and the needy friend who cannot bear to accept it burns. Mudaliar says only that the one asked fails to give the fitting help.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kizhamai","natpu","iravu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:31"},"n:311":{"book":"naladiyar","num":311,"t":["மெய்ஞ்ஞானக் கோட்டி உறழ்வழி விட்டாங்கோர்","அஞ்ஞானந் தந்திட் டதுவாங் கறத்துழாய்க்","கைஞ்ஞானங் கொண்டொழுகுங் காரறி வாளர்முன்","சொன்ஞானஞ் சோர விடல்."],"p":"","r":"Before people of dark understanding, who leave the way of an assembly of true knowledge, put forward some piece of ignorance, spread it everywhere and live by petty learning, let the wise leave their wisdom unspoken.","n":"The advice is silence, not argument. Mudaliar records another reading of கைஞ்ஞானம் as 'skill of the hand', the habit of striking whoever objects, which would anticipate 312 and 315.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","pullarivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:312":{"book":"naladiyar","num":312,"t":["நாப்பாடம் சொல்லி நயமுணர்வார் போல்செறிக்கும்","தீப்புலவர் சேரார் செறிவுடையார் - தீப்புலவன்","கோட்டியுள் குன்றக் குடிப்பழிக்கும் அல்லர்க்கால்","தோட்புடைக் கொள்ளா எழும்."],"p":"Well-disciplined men come not near the heretic (=man of evil learning, the teacher of an evil system), who stores up (in his memory certain formulas, comp. 304), as though he understood their worth, and repeats them by rote (lit. as a tongue-lesson). The heretic, if worsted in the assembly, will abuse the family (of his opponents); or he will spring up and challenge them to fight.","r":"The disciplined keep away from the bad scholar who recites lessons by rote as if he knew their beauty; worsted in the assembly he will abuse their family, or else slap his shoulders and rise to fight.","n":"Pope calls the தீப்புலவன் a heretic, the teacher of an evil system. Mudaliar's gloss is simply one without learning, and Shanmuganar's a false poet. The danger here is social: the loser turns to insult and violence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","pullarivu","kalvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:313":{"book":"naladiyar","num":313,"t":["சொற்றாற்றுக் கொண்டு சுனைத்தெழுதல் காமுறுவர்,","கற்றாற்றல் வன்மையும் தாம்தேறார் - கற்ற","செலவுரைக்கும ஆறறியார் தோற்ப தறியார்","பலவுரைக்கும் மாந்தர் பலர்."],"p":"Many are the men that utter many things,—who long to raise up (in debate), from mere love of talking and an itching tongue; who do not themselves apprehend the power and might of learning; who know not the way to utter with penetrating force whatthey have learnt; and who know not what defeat means.","r":"Many people talk at length: they itch to rise and debate on the strength of a word, cannot judge the power of learning, do not know how to make what they learned reach the hearer, and do not know when they are beaten.","n":"Mudaliar gives several ways to divide the opening words, one of them 'with words for goads', which Shanmuganar follows. The last failure, not knowing defeat, is what keeps the talk going.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["sol","solvanmai","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:314":{"book":"naladiyar","num":314,"t":["கற்றதூஉ மின்றிக் கணக்காயர் பாடத்தால்","பெற்றதாம் பேதையோர் சூத்திரம் - மற்றதனை","நல்லா ரிடைப்புக்கு நாணாது சொல்லித்தன்","புல்லறிவு காட்டி விடும்."],"p":"Without any learning of his own, the fool has obtained one formula from overhearing the lessons of a tutor (who was teaching others ), yet unabashed he enters the circle of the good, speaks it out, and makes exhibition of his mean understanding.","r":"With no learning of his own, a fool picks up a single sutra by overhearing a teacher's lesson, then walks unabashed into a gathering of the good, recites it, and shows how petty his understanding is.","n":"Mudaliar specifies that the fool has only the bare text (மூல பாடம்) of the sutra, overheard from lessons given to other students.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","pullarivu","avai","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:315":{"book":"naladiyar","num":315,"t":["வென்றிப் பொருட்டால் விலங்கொத்து மெய்கொள்ளார்","கன்றிக் கறுத்தெழுந்து காய்வாரோ - டொன்றி","உரைவித் தகமெழுவார் காண்பவே, கையுள்","சுரைவித்துப் போலுந்தம் பல்."],"p":"They who arise to utter words of wisdom, having associated themselves with the assembly of those who rage and burn and fume with anger, like beasts, for the mere sake of victory, not understanding the truth, shall see their own teeth like pumpkin seeds in their hands!","r":"Those who join people who rage and burn like beasts for the sake of victory and will not accept the truth, and who rise among them to show off their eloquence, will see their own teeth in their hands like gourd seeds.","n":"Mudaliar names the figure: teeth in the hand say, without saying it, that they will be knocked out. The warning falls on the speaker who chooses that company, not on the brawlers.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["avai","igal","solvanmai","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:316":{"book":"naladiyar","num":316,"t":["பாடமே ஓதிப் பயன்தெரிதல் தேற்றாத","முடர் முனிதக்க சொல்லுங்கால் - கேடருஞ்சீர்ச்","சான்றோர் சமழ்த்தனர் நிற்பவே, மற்றவரை","ஈன்றாட் கிறப்பப் பரிந்து."],"p":"When foolish men chant their lesson, not knowing the fruit that lesson yields, but uttering words that gender wrath, the learned ones, whose fame dies not, will stand by ashamed, sorely pitying the mother that bare them.","r":"When fools who only recite the text and cannot make out its meaning say things that provoke anger, the worthy, whose renown does not decay, stand ashamed and grieve deeply for the mother who bore them.","n":"The shame belongs to the listeners, not the fool. Mudaliar quotes the kind of lament the worthy might make, that she bore him for others to laugh at, and names the figure as imputed cause.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kalvi","saandror","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:317":{"book":"naladiyar","num":317,"t":["பெறுவது கொள்பவர் தோள்போல் நெறிப்பட்டுக்","கற்பவர்க் கெல்லாம் எளியநூல் - மற்றம்","முறிபுரை மேனியர் உள்ளம்போன் றியார்க்கும்","அறிதற் கா஢ய பொருள்."],"p":"Like the charms of those who sell their love for what they gain, the sacred texts are easy to those who rightly learn then; but like the minds of these whose forms are soft as tender shoots, the meaning is hard to all.","r":"Books are easy for anyone who learns them in due order, like the shoulders of women who take whatever they are paid, but their meaning is hard for anyone to grasp, like the minds of those same women with skin like tender mango shoots.","n":"One simile does both halves of the work: access to the text is cheap and open, and understanding is closed. Pope's 'sacred texts' narrows நூல், which the commentators leave general.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:318":{"book":"naladiyar","num":318,"t":["புத்தகமே சாலத் தொகுத்தும் பொருடெரியார்","உய்த்தக மெல்லா நிறைப்பினும் - மற்றவற்றைப்","போற்றும் புலவரும் வேறே பொருடெரிந்து","தேற்றும் புலவரும் வேறு."],"p":"Although men gather together books in abundance, and, not understanding their contents, fill the whole house with them; the sages who merely take care of books are of one sort, and the sages who understand their contents, and make them clear to others, are of another sort.","r":"Some collect books in great numbers and fill the whole house with them without understanding what they say; the scholars who keep books are one kind, and those who understand them and make others understand are another.","n":"Shanmuganar takes போற்றும் as keeping the books with worship (பூசை போட்டு) and calls the keepers false scholars. The verse itself says only that they are a different kind.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:32"},"n:319":{"book":"naladiyar","num":319,"t":["பொழிப்பகல நுட்பநூ லெச்சமிந் நான்கின்","கொழித்தகலங் காட்டாதார் சொற்கள் - பழிப்பில்","நிரையாமா சேர்க்கும் நெடுங்குன்ற நாட","உரையாமோ நூலிற்கு நன்கு?"],"p":"Lord of the land of long chains of hills where the wild cattle assemble in herds! Is that a good commentary to a faultless composition, which consists of the words of those who do not sift it thoroughly, in the four divisions of summary, amplification, minute exposition, and supplementary information, and thus exhibit the full import?","r":"Lord of the long hills where herds of wild cattle gather, can the words of those who do not sift a faultless work through the four modes of summary, breadth, subtlety and remainder, and so show its full extent, be a good commentary on it?","n":"A quatrain about commentary, on whose terms its commentators disagree. Mudaliar adds that others define the four modes differently again.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","solvanmai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The four are a method of disputation: breadth raises objections, subtlety answers them, and the remainder states the conclusion they establish.","எச்சவுரையாவது இவ்வாறு தடைவிடைகளான் முடிந்த ஒரு பொருளை யுரைத்தல்."],["shanmuganar","The remainder is research that brings out what lies hidden and unsaid in the text.","நூலில் எஞ்சி மறைந்து கிடப்பதை எடுத்துக்கூறும் ஆராய்ச்சியுரை"]],"ch":"n:32"},"n:320":{"book":"naladiyar","num":320,"t":["இற்பிறப் பில்லா ரெனைத்துநூல் கற்பினும்","சொற்பிறரைக் காக்குங் கருவியரோ? - இற்பிறந்த","நல்லறி வாளர் நவின்றநூல் தேற்றாதார்","புல்லறிவு தாமறிவ தில்."],"p":"Will those who are not of high family, however they may study learned works, be fit instruments to guard others from faults of speech? And men of good family who are well learned will not (seem to) be aware of the deficiencies of those who do not comprehend the works they talk of. -------------","r":"However many books they learn, can those not born of a good house be the means of guarding others' words? And the well-born wise take no notice of the petty understanding of those who do not grasp the books they talk of.","n":"Pope offers a third reading of the first half: guarding others from faults of speech. The second half fits Mudaliar's reading best, since the well-born show the restraint the first half denies to the low-born by overlooking a fool's errors.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kalvi","pullarivu","adakkam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","Those of low birth lack the restraint needed to protect others' words from scorn.","பிறருடைய சொற்களை (இகழாமற்) காத்தற்குரிய (அடக்கம் முதலிய) சாதனமுடையவரோ?"],["shanmuganar","Those of low birth cannot protect and care for others by what they say.","தம் சொல்லால், பிறரைப் போற்றிக் காக்கத்தக்க கருவி போன்றிருப்பரோ?"]],"ch":"n:32"},"n:321":{"book":"naladiyar","num":321,"t":["அருளின் அறமுரைக்கும் அன்புடையார் வாய்ச்சொல்","பொருளாகக் கொள்வர் புலவர் - பொருளல்லா","ஏழை அதனை இகழ்ந்துரைக்கும் பாற்கூழை","முழை சுவையுணரா தாங்கு."],"p":"Men of sense receive as a reality the words from the mouth of the loving ones who graciously teach virtue. The (ignorant)wretch, himself unreal, speaks (of virtue) with contempt. Just so the ladle distinguishes not the flavour of the rice boiled in milk.","r":"The learned take as real worth the words of loving people who teach virtue out of compassion; the fool, himself of no worth, mocks them, as a ladle cannot taste the milk-rice it stirs.","n":"The ladle is in the milk-rice all the time and tastes nothing: being near good teaching is not the same as taking it in. Mudaliar takes பொருளல்லா ஏழை as a fool of no account, and Shanmuganar as one who does not grasp the real meaning.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","aram","kelvi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:322":{"book":"naladiyar","num":322,"t":["அவ்வியம் இல்லார் அறத்தா றுரைக்குங்கால்","செவ்விய ரல்லார் செவிகொடுத்தும் கேட்கலார்","கவ்வித்தோல் தின்னும் குணுங்கர்நாய் பாற்சோற்றின்","செவ்விய கொளல்தேற்றா தாங்கு."],"p":"When men of ungrudging soul declare the way of virtue, those who are not rightly disposed, although they give ear, hear not. Even so the currier's dog seizes on and devours leather, not appreciating the flavour of milk and rice.","r":"When those free of crookedness explain the way of virtue, those not rightly disposed do not hear it even when they lend an ear, just as the tanner's dog that chews leather cannot take the taste of milk-rice.","n":"Mudaliar reads அவ்வியம் as crookedness of mind, or as envy standing for every bad trait. The image draws on a caste occupation: Mudaliar glosses குணுங்கர் as புலையர்.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","kelvi","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:323":{"book":"naladiyar","num":323,"t":["இமைக்கும் அளவிற்றம் இன்னுயிர்போம் மார்க்கம்","எனைத்தானும் தாங்கண் டிருந்தும் - தினைத்துணையும்","நன்றி புரிகல்லா நாணின் மடமாக்கள்","பொன்றிலென் பொன்றாக்கா லென்?"],"p":"What matters it whether they live or die—the shameless stupid people who do no good, not even as much as a grain of millet, though they see by every species of example the way in which their life so sweet to them, passes away in the twinkling of an eye?","r":"What does it matter whether they die or live, those dull and shameless people who see in every way that their dear life may leave in the blink of an eye, and still do not a millet grain's worth of good?","n":"Mudaliar lets நன்றி mean any good deed, or specifically help given to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","aram","pullarivu","naanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:324":{"book":"naladiyar","num":324,"t":["உளநாள் சிலவால் உயிர்க்கேமம் இன்றால்,","பலர்மன்னுந் தூற்றும் பழியால், - பலருள்ளும்","கண்டாரோ டெல்லாம் நகாஅ தெவனொருவன்","தண்டித் தனிப்பகை கோள்."],"p":"Since being's days are few, and life no safeguardowns, and guilt by many blamed is rife, mid many men, why, laughing not with all they see, should any sulk apart, and nurse a sullen hate ?","r":"The days left are few, life has no safeguard, and the blame that many spread is everywhere; why then should anyone, among so many, refuse to smile with those he meets and stand apart nursing a private enmity?","n":"The case against sullen enmity rests on shortage of time rather than on virtue: with life brief and unsafe, hostility is a waste. Mudaliar lets ஏமம் mean pleasure as well as protection.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["igal","nilaiyaamai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:325":{"book":"naladiyar","num":325,"t":["எய்தி யிருந்த அவைமுன்னர்ச் சென்றெள்ளி","வைதான் ஒருவன் ஒருவனை - வைய","வயப்பட்டான் வாளா இருப்பானேல், வைதான்","வியத்தக்கான் வாழும் எனின்."],"p":"A man has gone before the assembly that had gathered together, and contemning another has reviled him. Now if the reviled one remain silent, the reviler is to be wondered at if he survive; (for abuse is his very life, and the patience of the reviled one has closed his mouth.)","r":"If a man goes before a gathered assembly and reviles another, and the one reviled stays silent, it is a wonder if the reviler survives.","n":"The verse doesn't say how the reviler is undone. Pope supplies a reason, that abuse is the reviler's life and silence stops his mouth. The commentators say only that he will not prosper.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["porai","kadunjol","avai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:326":{"book":"naladiyar","num":326,"t":["மூப்புமேல் வாராமை முன்னே அறவினையை","ஊக்கி அதன்கண் முயலாதான் - நூக்கிப்","புறத்திரு போகென்னும் இன்னாச்சொல் இல்லுள்","தொழுத்தையால் கூறப் படும்."],"p":"He who before old age comes, has not undertaken and zealously carried out works of virtue, will be pushed about in the house addressed in harsh language, and bidden to get on one side or to go out, by even the maid-servants.","r":"One who does not eagerly take up virtuous deeds and work at them before old age comes on will be pushed aside in his own house and told 'sit outside, go away' even by the maidservant.","n":"The penalty is social, not karmic: humiliation in old age, inside one's own home.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","muyarchi","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:327":{"book":"naladiyar","num":327,"t":["தாமேயும் இன்புறார், தக்கார்க்கு நன்றாற்றார்","ஏமஞ்சார் நன்னெறியும் சேர்கலார் - தாமயங்கி","ஆக்கத்துள் தூங்கி அவத்தமே வாழ்நாளைப்","போக்குவார் புல்லறிவி னார்."],"p":"Men of scanty wisdom are those who do not themselves enjoy any sweets of life, bestow no benefactions on worthy persons, draw not nigh the good path that safeguards (the soul), but infatuated and absorbed in the acquisition of wealth, pass away their days of life in vain.","r":"People of petty understanding neither enjoy their wealth nor do good to the deserving nor join the good path that protects; dazed and sunk in their prosperity, they spend their days for nothing.","n":"Mudaliar names the cause of the daze: not knowing that body and wealth do not last.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","selvam","eegai","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:328":{"book":"naladiyar","num":328,"t":["சிறுகாலை யேதமக்குச் செல்வுழி வல்சி","இறுகிறுகத் தோட்கோப்புக் கொள்ளார் - இறுகிறுகிப்","பின்னறிவாம் என்றிருக்கும் பேதையார் கைகாட்டும்","பொன்னும் புளிவிளிங்கா யாம்."],"p":"In the very earliest time (in early youth) they take not food for the journey which they must make (into another world), tying tightly the wallet on their shoulders ; but tying tightly (their treasure bags), they say, 'In after days we will learn wisdom;' the gold these idiots will indict with their hands (as legacies, when they are speechless, and in the grasp of death) will be as sour vilam fruit.","r":"Fools who do not tie on tightly, early in life, the provisions for the road they must take, but tighten their grip on their wealth and say 'we will see to it later', will find that the gold they point to with their hands turns into a sour vilam fruit.","n":"The commentators fill in a deathbed scene: the dying man, unable to speak, gestures for gold to give away, and his family takes the gesture to mean a sour vilam fruit. Mudaliar quotes a Civaka Cintamani verse telling this story. The quatrain gives only the gesture and the sour fruit.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","marumai","ivaral","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:329":{"book":"naladiyar","num":329,"t":["வெறுமை யிடத்தும் விழிப்பிணிப் போழ்தும்","மறுமை மனத்தாரே யாகி - மறுமையை","ஐந்தை யனைத்தானும் ஆற்றிய காலத்துச்","சிந்தியார் சிற்றறிவி னார்."],"p":"When poor, or when disease's deadly grasp they feel, to other world alone their minds are given ; but when wealth grows, no thought, small as a grain of mustard seed, give they to other world —these souls unwise!","r":"In poverty and in grave illness the small-minded think of the next world, but once they are able to act they give it not a mustard seed's worth of thought.","n":"The irony is that concern for the next life arrives only when one is least able to act on it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["marumai","aram","pullarivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:330":{"book":"naladiyar","num":330,"t":["என்னேமற் றிவ்வுடம்பு பெற்றும் அறம்நினையார்","கொன்னே கழிப்பர்தம் வாழ்நாளை - அன்னோ","அளவிறந்த காதற்றம் ஆருயி ரன்னார்க்","கொளஇழைக்கும் கூற்றமும் கண்டு."],"p":"Though they have gained a human frame, of virtue think they not! In vain they spent their days. Alas! and yet they see death eagerly hasting to snatch away those infinitely dear, like to their very souls! Why is this so? -------------","r":"Alas, though they have gained this human body they do not think of virtue and waste their days, even while they watch Death preparing to take those they love beyond measure, as dear as their own lives.","n":"Shanmuganar calls the human body hard to obtain; the verse's இவ்வுடம்பு implies as much without saying it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","nilaiyaamai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:33"},"n:331":{"book":"naladiyar","num":331,"t":["கொலைஞர் உலையேற்றித் தீமடுப்ப ஆமை","நிலையறியா தந்நீர்ப் படிந்தாடி யற்றே","கொலைவல் பெருங்கூற்றம் கோள்பார்ப்ப ஈண்டை","வலையகத்துச் செம்மாப்பார் மாண்பு."],"p":"While the turtle's murderers have put it into the pot, and kindled fire beneath, it sports unconscious of its fate: such is their worth who joy entangled in life's not, while death, the mighty murderer, waits to seize them.","r":"As a turtle put in a pot by its killers, with the fire lit beneath, plays in the water without knowing its state, so are those who revel in this world's net while Death, the great killer, waits for the moment to seize them.","n":"The water the turtle enjoys is what will kill it, just as the world's pleasures are the net. Mudaliar notes that மாண்பு ('worth') is used ironically.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","pedhaimai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:332":{"book":"naladiyar","num":332,"t":["பெருங்கட லாடிய சென்றார் ஒருங்குடன்","ஓசை அவிந்தபின் ஆடுது மென்றற்றால்","இற்செய் குறைவினை நீக்கி அறவினை","மற்றறிவாம் என்றிருப்பார் மாண்பு."],"p":"They went to bathe in the great sea, but cried 'we will wait till all its roar is hushed, then bathe!' Such is their worth who say, 'we will get rid of all our household toils and cares and then we will practise virtue and be wise.'","r":"Those who say they will see to virtue once the household's unfinished tasks are done are like people who went to bathe in the great sea and said they would bathe when its roar had died down.","n":"Household duties, like the noise of the sea, never stop, so the simile turns postponement into permanent refusal.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","ilvaazhkkai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:333":{"book":"naladiyar","num":333,"t":["குலந்தவம் கல்வி குடிநம்முப் பைந்தும்","விலங்காமல் எய்தியக் கண்ணும் - நலஞ்சான்ற","மையறு தொல்சீர் உலகம் அறியாமை","நெய்யிலாப் பாற்சோற்றின் நேர்."],"p":"Though a man be of good caste, have performed deeds of ascetic virtue, acquired learning, is of good family and attained a ripe old age, so that in these five points he is faultless; yet his ignorance of what is thoroughly good, without stain, sanctioned by ancient precedent, and of good renown in the world's ways, will render all his advantages like thin, watery milk with boiled rice.","r":"Though one has all five, high caste, austerity, learning, standing and age, without lack, not knowing the good, stainless and anciently honoured உலகம் makes it all like milk-rice without ghee.","n":"The commentators also divide on குடி: household prosperity for Mudaliar, good family for Shanmuganar. Pope's 'thin, watery milk' departs from the Tamil, which has rice without ghee.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","kudi","kalvi","ozhukkam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","உலகம் means the worlds to be reached after this life, heaven and release, so what the fool lacks is knowledge of what leads there.","உலகம்- (மறுமையில் அடையத்தக்க சுவர்க்க மோக்ஷம் என்னும்) உலகங்களின் தகுதியை."],["shanmuganar","உலகம் means the ways of the world, and what the fool lacks is knowledge of how to live by them.","பழம் பெருஞ் சிறப் புடைய உலக நடைமுறையை அறியாது ஒழுகுதல்"]],"ch":"n:34"},"n:334":{"book":"naladiyar","num":334,"t":["கன்னனி நல்ல கடையாய மாக்களின்","சொன்னனி தாமுணரா வாயினும் - இன்னினியே","நிற்றல் இருத்தல் கிடத்தல் இயங்குதலேன்","றுற்றவர்க்குத் தாமுதவ லான்."],"p":"Stones are much better than low men ; since, though those (too) are utterly without apprehension of your words, yet they (stones) are of assistance to those that employ them, inasmuch as they at once stand (where they are fixed), remain (where they are put), lie (where they are laid), and remove (when they are moved).","r":"Stones are far better than base people: though they cannot understand words either, they serve whoever comes to them, at once, for standing, sitting, lying and walking on.","n":"The comparison is not about intelligence, which both lack, but about use: the stone at least gives itself to others.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","payan","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:335":{"book":"naladiyar","num":335,"t":["பெறுவதொன் றின்றியும் பெற்றானே போலக்","கறுவுகொண் டேலாதார் மாட்டும் - கறுவினால்","கோத்தின்னா கூறி உரையாக்கால் பேதைக்கு","நாத்தின்னும் நல்ல சுனைத்து."],"p":"If the fool, though nothing is to be gained (by such conduct), acting as though he had gained something, waxing wroth against those who disdain (or are unable) to contend with him, cannot say and utter forth in his wrath a string of evil words a grievous itching will gnaw his tongue.","r":"Though nothing is gained by it, the fool, as if he had gained something, grows angry even with those who don't deserve it, and if he cannot string harsh words together and say them, a fierce itch gnaws at his tongue.","n":"The targets are read three ways: Mudaliar's ஏலாதார் are those for whom such anger is unfitting, Shanmuganar's (printed under 334) are superiors who are no match for the fool, and Pope's are those who will not or cannot contend with him.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pedhaimai","vekuli","kadunjol"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:336":{"book":"naladiyar","num":336,"t":["தங்கள் மரபில்லார் பின்சென்று தாமவரை","எங்கண் வணக்குதும் என்பவர் - புன்கேண்மை","நற்றளிர்ப் புன்னை மலருங் கடற்சேர்ப்ப","கற்கிள்ளிக் கையிழந் தற்று."],"p":"Lord of the sea-shore where the laurel with its bright foliage flowers! The poor (pretence of) intimacy enjoyed by those who follow after (wealthy but) utterly uncongenial men, and say 'we will bend them to our will,' is like loosing one's hand while digging into a rock.","r":"Those who follow people without good conduct, saying 'we will make them bow to us', have a poor kind of friendship, lord of the shore where the laurel with fine shoots flowers: it is like losing one's hand pinching a rock.","n":"Mudaliar calls the bond a one-sided friendship and cites a Pazhamozhi line saying no one pinches a rock and keeps their hand. Pope supplies 'wealthy' for the people followed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:337":{"book":"naladiyar","num":337,"t":["ஆகா தெனினும் அகத்துநெய் யுண்டாகின்","போகா தெறும்பு புறஞ்சுற்றும் - யாதும்","கொடாஅ ரெனினும் உடையாரைப் பற்றி","விடாஅர் உலகத் தவர்."],"p":"If there be ghi in the pot, though they cannot get at it, the ants will swarm without ceasing around the outside ; and so the people of the world will cling to and not leave the possessors of wealth, though these give them nothing.","r":"If there is ghee inside a pot, ants keep circling its outside without leaving, though they cannot get at it; so people cling to the wealthy and won't leave them, though they give nothing.","n":"The quatrain describes a habit of the world without blaming it in so many words; being placed in this chapter is what marks the habit as folly.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["selvam","pedhaimai","ivaral"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:338":{"book":"naladiyar","num":338,"t":["நல்லவை நாடொறும் எய்தார் அறஞ்செய்யார்","இல்லாதார்க் கியாதொன்றும் ஈகலார் - எல்லாம்","இனியார்தோள் சேரார் இசைபட வாழார்","முனியார்கொல் தாம்வாழும் நாள்."],"p":"No good each day they gain; nor deed of virtue do; nothing to needy men impart; nor know they joy of loved ones' sweet embrace ; devoid of fame they live: do such not loathe the days they live?","r":"They gain no good thing day by day, do no virtue, give nothing to the needy, never hold their beloved in their arms, and do not live with renown: will they not come to loathe the days they live?","n":"The list mixes virtue, giving, married love and fame, so the fool fails at every aim of life, not only at renunciation. Mudaliar allows நல்லவை to be read as நல் அவை, a good assembly, and Shanmuganar takes that reading.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["aram","eegai","pugazh","inbam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:339":{"book":"naladiyar","num":339,"t":["விழைந்தொருவர் தம்மை வியப்ப ஒருவர்","விழைந்திலேம் என்றிருக்கும் கேண்மை - தழங்குகுரல்","பாய்திரைசூழ் வையம் பயப்பினும் இன்னாதே","ஆய்நலம் இல்லாதார் மாட்டு."],"p":"Friendship with those who are destitute of exquisite goodness, and who, though you cultivate them with affectionate deference, (morosely) say we entertain affection for none,'—though it should yield as its fruit the earth, which the sea with its noisy chime of leaping billows girds around,—would be only affliction.","r":"When one person admires and seeks another who answers 'we don't want you', that friendship with those lacking discerning goodness is painful, even if it should yield the whole earth girdled by the roaring sea.","n":"Mudaliar calls the bond a one-sided friendship, or a false one. The fool is the one who rejects the regard offered, but the warning is for the one who keeps offering it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","pedhaimai","sirumai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:340":{"book":"naladiyar","num":340,"t":["கற்றனவும் கண்ணகன்ற சாயலும் இற்பிறப்பும்","பக்கத்தார் பாராட்டப் பாடெய்தும் தானுரைப்பின்","மைத்துனர் பல்கி மருந்தின் தணியாத","பித்தனென் றெள்ளப் படும்."],"p":"The learning a man has acquired, his world - renowned excellence, and his nobility of birth, will gain for him respect when the bystanders celebrate them; but if he himself proclaim them, the young kinsmen crowding around will deride him as one afflicted with a mania not to be mitigated by any medicine. -----------","r":"Learning, wide gentleness and good birth win honour when others praise them; if a man praises them himself, his brothers-in-law multiply and mock him as a madman no medicine can cure.","n":"Mudaliar explains மைத்துனர் (brothers-in-law) as mockers: the joking relationship stands for ridicule.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["perumai","sirumai","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:34"},"n:341":{"book":"naladiyar","num":341,"t":["கப்பி கடவதாக் காலைத்தன் வாய்ப்பெயினும்","குப்பை கிளைப்போவாக் கோழிபோல் - மிக்க","கனம்பொதிந்த நூல்விரித்துக் காட்டினும் கீழ்தன்","மனம்புரிந்த வாறே மிகும்."],"p":"The fowl, though each morning duly you scatter broken grain into its very mouth, will ceaselessly scratch in the refuse-heap; so, though you expound and show the base man works of learning, full of weighty wisdom, he will but the more resolutely go on in the way in which his mind finds delight.","r":"As a hen keeps scratching the rubbish heap though grain is poured into its mouth each morning, so the base man, however you lay open books full of weighty meaning for him, goes further along the way his mind likes.","n":"மிகும் suggests excess, not just persistence: Mudaliar glosses it as going beyond bounds.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kalvi","manam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:342":{"book":"naladiyar","num":342,"t":["காழாய கொண்டு கசடற்றார் தஞ்சாரல்","தாழாது போவாம் எனஉரைப்பின் - கீழ்தான்","உறங்குவாம் என்றெழுந்து போமாம், அஃதன்றி","மறங்குமாம் மற்றொன் றுரைத்து."],"p":"If you say to the base man, ‘Let us without delay go to seek refuge with faultless sages possessors of mature wisdom,' he will probably get up and make off, exclaiming, 'Let us go and slumber,!’ or he will perhaps demur, and change the subject.","r":"Say to the base man 'let us go without delay to the faultless who hold firm learning', and he gets up and leaves saying 'let's sleep', or else refuses with some other excuse.","n":"The comedy is in the excuses: sleep first, and failing that, anything else.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","madi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:343":{"book":"naladiyar","num":343,"t":["பெருநடை தாம்பெறினும் பெற்றி பிழையா","தொருநடைய ராகுவர் சான்றோர் - பெருநடை","பெற்றக் கடைத்தும் பிறங்கருவி நன்னாட","வற்றாம் ஒருநடை கீழ்."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of swelling torrents! If thoroughly worthy men gain some added dignity, their nature knows no deviation, but they go on in the same even path of virtue ; and although the base obtain promotion, his conduct too changes not, (promotion betters him not.)","r":"Even if they gain great prosperity, the worthy keep one even course without falling from their nature; when the base gain it, the verse says only that they are 'able in one course', lord of the good land of shining waterfalls.","n":"Pope agrees with Shanmuganar that the base man's conduct does not change. Mudaliar takes பெருநடை as wealth and records that others read it as great distinction.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","selvam","panpu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","With prosperity the base man becomes adept at a conduct different from his earlier one.","வற்றாம் ஒரு நடை- (முன்னைய நடைக்கு வேறாகிய) ஒரு நடக்கையின் வல்லவனாவன்;"],["shanmuganar","With prosperity the base man keeps to his own low course.","தனது தாழ்ந்த ஒரு போக்கையே பின்பற்ற வல்லவனாம்."]],"ch":"n:35"},"n:344":{"book":"naladiyar","num":344,"t":["தினையனைத்தே யாயினும் செய்தநன் றுண்டால்","பனையனைத்தா உள்ளுவர் சான்றோர் - பளையனைத்","தென்றும் செயினும் இலங்கருவி நன்னாட","நன்றில நன்றறியார் மாட்டு."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of glistening torrents ! The excellent will deem any favour done them, though small as a grain of millet, to be large as a palm tree. You may constantly confer favours huge as a palm tree, but they are not benefits, when conferred on those who are not grateful.","r":"If they have enjoyed a good done to them, though it is only the size of a millet grain, the worthy think of it as a palm tree; but goods the size of a palm, done again and again, are no good at all to those who do not know a good, lord of the land of bright waterfalls.","n":"Mudaliar cites Kural 104 and Parimelazhagar's remark that millet and palm are conventional measures of small and great.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","saandror","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:345":{"book":"naladiyar","num":345,"t":["பொற்கலத் தூட்டிப் புறந்தரினும் நாய்பிறர்","எச்சிற் கிமையாது பார்த்திருக்கும் - அச்சீர்","பெருமை யுடைத்தாக் கொளினுங்கீழ் செய்யும்","கருமங்கள் வேறு படும்."],"p":"The dog, though you tend it with care, feeding it from a golden vessel, will watch, eye fixed for others' leavings ; even so, although you receive the base as if they were possessed of greatness, the deeds they do will be otherwise (= their real character will be seen by their conduct).","r":"A dog, though reared on food from a golden bowl, watches unblinking for others' leftovers; so even if you treat the base as great, the deeds they do will be different.","n":"The golden bowl is the honour shown: it changes the setting, not the appetite.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","panpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:346":{"book":"naladiyar","num":346,"t":["சக்கரச் செல்வம் பெறினும் விழுமியோர்","எக்காலும் சொல்லார் மிகுதிச்சொல் - எக்காலும்","முந்திரிமேற் காணி மிகுவதேல் கீழ்தன்னை","இந்திரனா எண்ணி விடும்."],"p":"Though they obtain imperial wealth, the excellent never utter a vaunting word ; but if at any time the base see some small fraction added to his scanty store, he will deem himself great as Indra.","r":"Even with an emperor's wealth the excellent never speak a boastful word, but if a base man gains a காணி on top of a முந்திரி, he thinks himself Indra.","n":"The two words are tiny fractions. Shanmuganar gives முந்திரி as 1/320 and காணி as 1/80; Mudaliar gives காணி as 1/64 and முந்திரி as a quarter of that, and allows the words to be read in the other order.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","perumai","selvam","panivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:347":{"book":"naladiyar","num":347,"t":["மைதீர் பசும்பொன்மேல் மாண்ட மணியழுத்திச்","செய்த தெனினுஞ் செருப்புத்தன் காற்கேயாம்","எய்திய செல்வத்த ராயினும் கீழ்களைச்","செய்தொழிலால் காணப் படும்."],"p":"Though made of faultless yellow gold, enwrought with choicest pearls, the slipper serves but for one's foot; though the base be deemed prosperous through the wealth they have gained, their baseness will be seen by their actions.","r":"Though a sandal is made of flawless gold set with fine gems, it is still for the foot; so the base, though they have come into wealth, are known by the deeds they do.","n":"The sandal's material is the wealth and its place is the nature: costly making does not move it from the foot.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:348":{"book":"naladiyar","num":348,"t":["கடுக்கெனச் சொல்வற்றாம், கண்ணோட்டம் இன்றாம்","இடுக்கண் பிறர்மாட் டுவக்கும், - அடுத்தடுத்து","வேகம் உடைத்தாம், விறன்மலை நன்னாட","ஏகுமாம் எள்ளுமாம் கீழ்."],"p":"","r":"The base are quick to speak harshly, have no kindly regard, rejoice in others' troubles, fly into rage again and again, go where they please and scorn everyone, lord of the good land of mighty hills.","n":"A plain list with no image, which Mudaliar classes as straightforward description (தன்மை நவிற்சி).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","kadunjol","kannottam","vekuli"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:349":{"book":"naladiyar","num":349,"t":["பழைய ரிவரென்று பன்னாட்பின் நிற்பின்","உழையினிய ராகுவர் சான்றோர் - விழையாதே","கள்ளுயிர்க்கும் நெய்தற் கனைகடல் தண்சேர்ப்ப","எள்ளுவர் கீழா யவர்."],"p":"Lord of the resounding sea's cool shore, where the Neythal breathes honied fragrance round! The worthy, if men for many days stand waiting in their train will say 'these are old acquaintance,' and will make the place pleasant to them; the base in such circumstances feel no affection, but simply despise them.","r":"If people stand by them for many days, the worthy grow kind to them, thinking 'these are old friends'; the base feel no warmth and simply scorn them, lord of the cool shore of the roaring sea where the neydal breathes honey.","n":"Mudaliar takes standing by someone as being subordinate and obedient to them, so the test is how each kind treats long-serving dependants.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pazhaimai","kayamai","saandror","natpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:350":{"book":"naladiyar","num":350,"t":["கொய்புல் கொடுத்துக் குறைத்தென்றும் தீற்றினும்","வையம்பூண் கல்லா சிறுகுண்டை - ஐயகேள்,","எய்திய செல்வத்த ராயினும் கீழ்களைச்","செய்தொழிலாற் காணப் படும்."],"p":"Hear, O Sir! The little bullock-calves will not learn to bear the yoke and draw the chariot, though you give them cut grass, clip them, and constantly rub them down; so, though they have gained wealth, the base will be seen (to be so) by the deeds they do. -----------------","r":"Hear, sir: small bullocks will never learn to wear the yoke and draw the cart however long you feed them cut grass, and the base, though they have come into wealth, are known by the deeds they do.","n":"Care cannot supply a capacity that is not there; the bullocks are well fed and still small.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:35"},"n:351":{"book":"naladiyar","num":351,"t":["ஆர்த்த அறிவினர் ஆண்டிளைய ராயினும்","காத்தோம்பித் தம்மை அடக்குப - மூத்தொறூஉம்","தீத்தொழிலே கன்றித் திரிதந் தெருவைபோல்","போத்தறார் புல்லறிவி னார்."],"p":"The thoroughly wise, though young in years, will guard themselves in stern self-repression. The low unwise as age comes on become mature in evil works, and are like reeds wind-shaken, hollow still!","r":"The fully wise restrain and guard themselves even when young in years, while the petty-minded grow more practised in wrongdoing the older they get and, like the எருவை, never lose their hollowness.","n":"Age is set aside as a cause: wisdom doesn't wait for years, and years don't cure folly. Mudaliar gives two senses for both எருவை and போத்து: a reed that never fills its hollow, or a kite that never sheds its faults. Shanmuganar takes the kite and the faults, and Pope the hollow reed.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pullarivu","adakkam","kayamai","arivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:352":{"book":"naladiyar","num":352,"t":["செழும்பெரும் பொய்கையுள் வாழினும் என்றும்","வழும்பறுக்க கில்லாவாம் தேரை - வழும்பில்சீர்","நூல்கற்றக் கண்ணும் நுணுக்கமொன் றில்லாதார்","தேர்கிற்கும் பெற்றி அரிது."],"p":"Though frogs flourish long in a rich large lake, they never rid themselves of their slime; so it is hard for those void of fine perception, even when they have learned pure and excellent text-books, to acquire the gift of clear understanding.","r":"Frogs that live in a rich, large pond never rid themselves of their slime; in the same way, those without fine discernment rarely gain the power to examine what they read, even after studying flawless, excellent books.","n":"What is missing is not learning but நுணுக்கம், a subtlety that study doesn't supply. The pond's abundance matches the excellence of the books: neither reaches what is already in the creature. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 351.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","arivu","kayamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:353":{"book":"naladiyar","num":353,"t":["கணமலை நன்னாட. கண்ணின் றொருவர்","குணனேயுங் கூற்ற கா஢தால், - குணனழுங்கக்","குற்றம் உழைநின்று கூறுஞ் சிறியவர்கட்கு","எற்றா லியன்றதோ நா."],"p":"Lord of the goodly land of mountain chains ! It is hard to stand before a man, and publish even his praise. How then can mean men's tongues depreciate a man's good qualities and proclaim his faults while standing before him?.","r":"Lord of the good land of clustered hills: it is hard even to speak of a man's virtues when he isn't present, so what are the tongues of petty people made of, that they stand at his side and proclaim his faults until his virtues are spoiled?","n":"Both commentators read கண் இன்று as 'not before his eyes', and Mudaliar rejects splitting it as கண் நின்று. The contrast is then between a decent reluctance to praise someone even behind his back and the brazenness of faulting him beside him. Pope takes the other split and has the praise spoken to the man's face.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","sol","kutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:354":{"book":"naladiyar","num":354,"t":["கோடேந் தகலல்குல் பெண்டிர்தம் பெண்ணீர்மை","சேடியர் போலச் செயல்தேற்றார் - கூடிப்","புதுப்பெருக்கம் போலத்தம் பெண்ணீர்மை காட்டி","மதித்திறப்பர் மற்றை யவர்."],"p":"Chaste women trick not out their charms of womanhood, as is as the wont of the wanton. Like fountains ever fresh those others make display of every charm, and vaunt their beauty as they pass.","r":"Household women do not know how to dress up their womanliness the way their maids do it for them; the other kind, like a fresh flood, put their charms on show themselves, think highly of themselves, and then pass on.","n":"In a chapter on the base the verse works as a figure, and Mudaliar names it பிறிது மொழிதல்: the wise don't praise themselves, and fools vaunt themselves to extract wealth. The verse itself speaks only of the two kinds of women.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","varaivinmagalir","panivu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","இறப்பர் means the courtesans leave a man once they have taken all his wealth; the verse is an allegory of fools who praise themselves to get money.","அறிவிலார் தம்மைப்பிறர் நன்கு மதிக்கும்படி தம்மைத்தாமே புகழ்ந்து பேசிப் பொருள் பறிப்பரென்பதூ"],["shanmuganar","இறப்பர் means they overstep all bounds, rating themselves highly; he reads the verse as a contrast between the women themselves.","தம்மைப் பெரிதாய் மதித்து வரம்பு கடந்து செல்வர்."]],"ch":"n:36"},"n:355":{"book":"naladiyar","num":355,"t":["தளிர்மேலே நிற்பினும் தட்டாமற் செல்லா","உளிநீரார் மாதோ கயவர் - அளிநீரார்க்","கென்னானும் செய்யார் எனைத்தானும் செய்பவே","இன்னாங்கு செய்வார்ப் பெறின்."],"p":"The base are like the chisel, that, though it is over the bud (to be sculptured), moves not without some one to strike it. On those who are courteous these confer no boon whatever. They will do everything for those who use violent means.","r":"The base are like a chisel that won't cut in, even set on a tender shoot, unless someone strikes it: they do nothing at all for the kind, and anything at all for those who treat them harshly.","n":"A tender shoot is the softest thing a chisel could meet, so the image says the base yield to nothing gentle. Mudaliar lets அளிநீரார் mean either the compassionate or those whose state calls for pity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","eegai","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:356":{"book":"naladiyar","num":356,"t":["மலைநலம் உள்ளும் குறவன் பயந்த","விளைநிலம் உள்ளும் உழவன் சிறந்தொருவர்","செய்தநன் றுள்ளுவர் சான்றோர் கயந்தன்னை","வைததை உள்ளி விடும்."],"p":"The hill-man thinks upon the beauty of his hills; the farmer thinks upon his fields that have yielded him rich crops; the good think on the boons bestowed by worthy men; the base man's thoughts are fixed on abuse he has received.","r":"The hill-dweller thinks of the wealth of his hills and the farmer of the field that yielded; the worthy think of a good someone did them, while the base dwell on the time someone abused them.","n":"The first two figures set up the rule that each mind returns to what it values; the last two say what the worthy and the base value. Mudaliar notes the verse calls the base man கயம், a neuter noun, to show how low he is.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:357":{"book":"naladiyar","num":357,"t":["ஒருநன்றி செய்தவர்க் கொன்றி யெழுந்த","பிழைநூறும் சான்றோர் பொறுப்பர் - கயவர்க்கு","எழுநூறு நன்றிசெய் தொன்றுதீ தாயின்","எழுநூறும் தீதாய் விடும்."],"p":"The good attach themselves to those that have done them one act of kindness, and forgive a hundred wrongs that arise. The base receive seven hundred benefits, but one wrong will turn them all to evil.","r":"The worthy bear a hundred faults, coming one after another, from someone who once did them a kindness; for the base, if one thing goes wrong after seven hundred kindnesses, all seven hundred turn into harm.","n":"Mudaliar takes the hundred and the seven hundred as standing for 'many' and 'very many'. The asymmetry is the point: for one kind of person a single kindness outweighs a hundred wrongs, and for the other a single wrong cancels seven hundred kindnesses.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["nanri","porai","kayamai","saandror"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:358":{"book":"naladiyar","num":358,"t":["ஏட்டைப் பருவத்தும் இற்பிறந்தார் செய்வன்","மோட்டிடத்தும் செய்யார் முழுமக்கள் - கோட்டை","வயிரஞ் செறிப்பினும் வாட்கண்ணாய் பன்றி","செயிர்வேழ மாகுத லின்று."],"p":"Worthless persons even in high estate do not the deeds that men of noble birth perform even in their poverty. Okeen-eyed maid! Men may enring and arm the boar's tusks, but it will not even so become a warlike elephant.","r":"What those of good family do even when weakened by want, the utterly foolish don't do even at the height of their fortune; bright-eyed one, fit a boar's tusks with diamond rings and it still won't become an angry war elephant.","n":"The verse doesn't name the deeds. Pope supplies liberality, and Mudaliar says only 'good deeds'. The base are called முழுமக்கள், which Mudaliar glosses as fools, so the lack here is of sense as well as of birth.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kudi","kayamai","selvam","eegai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:359":{"book":"naladiyar","num":359,"t":["இன்றாதும் இந்நிலையே ஆதும் இனிச்சிறிது","நின்றாதும் என்று நினைத்திருந் - தொன்றி","உரையின் மகிழ்ந்துதம் உள்ளம்வே றாகி","மரையிலையின் மாய்ந்தார் பலர்."],"p":"‘To-day, at once, a little hence, we shall gain our end!’ So many speak and think, and joyously tell it out among their friends ; but soon their eager minds are changed; and they have perished like a lotus leaf.","r":"Many people thought 'today we'll have it, right now, or in a little while', rejoiced as they said so to others, and then, their hopes overturned, perished like lotus leaves.","n":"The verse doesn't say what they expected, and names no base person. Mudaliar supplies both: they waited to become rich without doing good, and withered like the lotus leaf that flourishes and dies where it stands. Shanmuganar's commentary for this quatrain is missing.","x":0,"u":1,"f":0,"c":["nilaiyaamai","avaa","aram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:360":{"book":"naladiyar","num":360,"t":["நீருள் பிறந்து நிறம்பசிய தாயினும்","ஈரங் கிடையகத் தில்லாகும் - ஓரும்","நிறைப்பெருஞ் செல்வத்து நின்றக் கடைத்தும்","அறைப்பெருங்கல் அன்னா ருடைத்து."],"p":"Though born in the water, and its hue appear green, the Netti's pith knows no moisture; so in the world are men of amplest wealth whose hearts are hard as stones upon the rocky mountain fell. -------------","r":"Though it is born in water and is green, the netti reed has no moisture in its pith; in the same way the world holds men who stay hard as great rock slabs even while living in full, great wealth.","n":"The image is exact: the plant lives in water and holds none, as these men live among plenty and give none. Mudaliar's gloss on the rock is that it never softens (இளகுதல்).","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","selvam","arul"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:36"},"n:361":{"book":"naladiyar","num":361,"t":["மழைதிளைக்கு மாடமாய் மாண்பமைந்த காப்பாய்","இழைவிளக்கு நின்றிமைப்பின் என்னாம்? - விழைதக்க","மாண்ட மனையாளை இல்லாதான் இல்லகம்","காண்டற் கா஢யதோர் காடு."],"p":"The mansion meets the clouds. A stately band of warders keep watch around. Gems glisten therein like lamps. What then? Where the owner has not a wife of dainty excellence: the house within is a waste, hard to explore.","r":"What use is a mansion where the clouds play, under fine guard, with jewels shining like lamps, if its owner has no admirable wife? That house is a wilderness too grim to look at.","n":"Pope's 'hard to explore' reads காண்டற்கு அரிய as a trackless waste; Mudaliar reads it as a forest too harsh even to look at. Shanmuganar takes இழைவிளக்கு as a wick lamp that never goes out, where Mudaliar has jewels shining like lamps.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:362":{"book":"naladiyar","num":362,"t":["வழக்கெனைத்து மில்லாத வாள்வாய்க் கிடந்தும்","இழுக்கினைத் தாம்பெறுவ ராயின் - இழுக்கெனைத்தும்","செய்குறாப் பாணி சிறிதே அச் சின்மொழியார்","கையுறாப் பாணி பெரிது."],"p":"Though compassed round with a faultless guard of swords, ifonce they gain freedom, in little time they are stained with every fault; and long is the time those soft-voiced once spurn every law of right","r":"Even when women are kept under a faultless guard of swords, the time in which soft-spoken women commit no fault is short and the time in which they don't keep to good conduct is long; the verse's condition on this is disputed.","n":"This is one of the collection's harshest lines on women, and its scope depends on the condition in the second line. Mudaliar cites Kural 57 with it, though 57 praises women's own guard rather than doubting it.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","manam"],"pos":[["mudaliar","He reads the condition as 'even if they get no chance to stray': strict guard can stop the deed, but most women's minds still go astray.","மகளிர் கடுங்காவலின் வைகிக் குற்றஞ் செய்தற்குச் சிறிது மிடம் பெறாராயினும் அவர் மனந் தீவழிச் செல்லுதலே பெரும்பான்மை"],["shanmuganar","He keeps the condition as written: the claim applies if women take the faulty way.","தாம் இழுக்கான வழியில் செல்வா ராயின்"]],"ch":"n:37"},"n:363":{"book":"naladiyar","num":363,"t":["எறியென் றெதிர்நிற்பாள் கூற்றம் சிறுகாலை","அட்டில் புகாதாள் அரும்பிணி - அட்டதனை","உண்டி யுதவாதாள் இல்வாழ்பேய் இம்மூவர்","கொண்டானைக் கொல்லும் படை."],"p":"Death is the wife that stands and dares her spouse to strike! Disease is she who enters not the kitchen betimes! Demon domestic is she who cooks and gives no alms! These three are swords to slay their lords !","r":"The wife who stands up to her husband saying 'strike me' is Death; the one who doesn't go into the kitchen at dawn is an incurable disease; the one who doesn't serve the food she has cooked is a demon in the house; these three are weapons that kill the man who took them.","n":"Pope's 'gives no alms' makes the third fault a failure of charity. Both commentators make it a failure to serve the husband, and Mudaliar adds that she eats the food herself.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","ilvaazhkkai","kootru"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:364":{"book":"naladiyar","num":364,"t":["கடியெனக் கேட்டுங் கடியான், வெடிபட","ஆர்ப்பது கேட்டும் அதுதெளியான் - பேர்த்துமோர்","இற்கொண் டினித஧ருஉம் ஏமுறுதல் என்பவே","கற்கொண் டெறியுந் தவறு."],"p":"He hears (that marriage is another name for) dread, and yet he dreads it not! He hears the fearful funeral drum, but it gives him no sense ! Again to take a wife and to dwell bewildered amidst domestic enjoyments is a fault that merits stoning; sosay (the wise).","r":"He hears marriage called a dread and doesn't give it up; he hears the funeral drum beat loud enough to split heads and still doesn't see; to take a wife again and live lost in pleasure, they say, is the fault of stoning oneself.","n":"Shanmuganar places the drum at the first wife's funeral, which makes this a verse about remarriage. Mudaliar points back to quatrain 56, where கடி names marriage as something to be dreaded. On either reading the verse goes further than a warning about remarrying: it treats household life itself as what the wise tell one to give up.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thuravu","ilvaazhkkai","pattru","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:365":{"book":"naladiyar","num":365,"t":["தலையே தவமுயன்று வாழ்தல் ஒருவர்க்","கிடையே இனியார்கண் தங்கல் - கடையே","புணராதென் றெண்ணிப் பொருள்நசையால் தம்மை","உணரார்பின் சென்று நிலை."],"p":"The best thing is a life spent in penitential practices. The middle course is to live with dear ones around. The worst of all is, with the thought that we have not enough, through desire of wealth subserviently to follow those who understand us not.","r":"The best way to live is to strive in austerity; the middle way is to dwell with those one loves; the lowest is to follow, out of desire for wealth, people who don't recognise one's worth, even thinking it won't come.","n":"இனியார் is general, 'the dear ones', and both commentators narrow it to wives. Mudaliar reads புணராது என்று எண்ணி as 'even while thinking it won't be gained', which makes the lowest life futile as well as servile; Pope's 'not enough' reads it as discontent.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["thavam","ilvaazhkkai","avaa","maanam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:366":{"book":"naladiyar","num":366,"t":["கல்லாக் கழிப்பர் தலையாயார் நல்லவை","துவ்வாக் கழிப்பர் இடைகள் கடைகள்","இனிதுண்ணோம் ஆரப் பெறேமியாம் என்னும்","முனிவினாற் கண்பா டிலர்."],"p":"","r":"The best pass their time in learning, the middle in enjoying good things, and the lowest lie sleepless with resentment, saying 'we haven't eaten well, we haven't got enough'.","n":"கல்லா here means 'having learned', not 'not learning'. Mudaliar specifies books of spiritual knowledge and goods gained by right means; the verse says only learning and good things. The lowest are marked by craving rather than by poverty.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kalvi","inbam","avaa"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:367":{"book":"naladiyar","num":367,"t":["செந்நெல்லா லாய செழுமுளை மற்றுமச்","செந்நெல்லே யாகி விளைதலால் - அந்நெல்","வயனிறையக் காய்க்கும் வளவய லூர","மகனறிவு தந்தை யறிவு."],"p":"","r":"Lord of the rich-fielded town where the paddy fills the fields: since the sprout from red paddy grows again into that same red paddy, a son's understanding is his father's.","n":"The verse states inheritance as a law, the way a seed breeds true. It doesn't say whether the son learns from his father or is born like him. Mudaliar's parallel, 'the son tells the father's worth', reads the son as the sign of his lineage.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["arivu","makkal","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:368":{"book":"naladiyar","num":368,"t":["உடைப்பெருஞ் செல்வரும் சான்றோரும் கெட்டுப்","புடைப்பெண்டிர் மக்களும் கீழும் பெருகிக்","கடைக்கால் தலைக்கண்ண தாகிக் குடைக்கால்போல்","கீழ்மேலாய் நிற்கும் உலகு."],"p":"The wealthy men of great possessions and the perfect have perished, while wantons' sons and base men multiply. The lowest takes chief place, and, like the umbrella's handle, this world is upside down!","r":"The wealthy of long standing and the worthy decline, while concubines' sons and base men flourish; what belongs at the feet comes to the head, and like an umbrella's handle the world stands upside down.","n":"Shanmuganar explains the umbrella: its handle points down when the umbrella is open and up when it is folded, so the same world can flip between orders. The complaint mixes moral and social rank, putting the worthy beside old wealth and the base beside concubines' sons.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kayamai","saandror","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:369":{"book":"naladiyar","num":369,"t":["இனியார்தம் நெஞ்சத்து நோயுரைப்ப அந்நோய்","தணியாத உள்ளம் உடையார் - மணிவரன்றி","வீழும் அருவி விறன்மலை நன்னாட","வாழ்வின் வரைபாய்தல் நன்று."],"p":"Lord of the good land of mighty hills whence streams descend sweeping along pearls! Better men should jump down a precipice than live with a mind not disposed to assuage the pain of the dear ones who tell them of their heart's pain.","r":"Lord of the good land of mighty hills, whose falls sweep down gems: those whose hearts don't ease the pain their dear ones tell them of would do better to leap from a cliff than to live.","n":"The verse doesn't require them to remove the pain, only to be moved by it; a heart unmoved disqualifies a life. Mudaliar takes இனியார் as friends.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["natpu","arul","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:370":{"book":"naladiyar","num":370,"t":["புதுப்புனலும் பூங்குழையார் நட்பும் இரண்டும்","விதுப்புற நாடின்வே றல்ல - புதுப்புனலும்","மாரி அறவே அறுமே, அவரன்பும்","வாரி அறவே அறும்."],"p":"‘The mountain freshed, and the love of those adorned With pendant jewels rare examined calmly prove To differ nought: that fails when rains that feed it fail; And love of these fails too, when income fails !’ ------------","r":"Fresh floodwater and the friendship of women with fine earrings, examined calmly, are no different: the flood ends when the rain ends, and their love ends when the income ends.","n":"Mudaliar records a second reading of விதுப்பு அற, 'without desire', which would make the examiner a man no longer infatuated.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:37"},"n:371":{"book":"naladiyar","num":371,"t":["விளக்கொளியும் வேசையர் நட்பும் இரண்டும்","துளக்கற நாடின்வே றல்ல - விளக்கொளியும்","நெய்யற்ற கண்ணே அறுமே, அவரன்பும்","கையற்ற கண்ணே அறும்."],"p":"The lamp's light and harlot's love examined well are seen to differ not a whit; the lamp's light goes out when the feeding oil is consumed ! and the harlot's love is spent when the lavish hand has spent its wealth.","r":"The lamp's light and the friendship of harlots, examined steadily, are no different: the light ends when the oil runs out, and their love ends when the hand is empty.","n":"Mudaliar takes கை, the hand, as the money held in it.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","nilaiyaamai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:372":{"book":"naladiyar","num":372,"t":["அங்கோட் டகலல்குல் ஆயிழையாள் நம்மோடு","செங்கோடு பாய்துமே என்றாள்மன் - செங்கோட்டின்","மேற்காணம் இன்மையான் மேவா தொழிந்தாளே","காற்கால்நோய் காட்டிக் கலுழ்ந்து."],"p":"She (of enticing beauty), adorned with choice jewels, said forsooth, 'I will leap with you down the steep precipice;' but on the very brow of the precipice, because I had no money, she, weeping and pointing to her aching feet, withdrew and left me alone!","r":"The woman with fine jewels said she would leap with us from the high peak; but at the peak, since we had no gold, she showed a pain in her feet, wept, and wouldn't come on.","n":"The speaker is the dupe himself, and the promise he recalls is a lovers' death pact. Mudaliar takes the foot pain as a lie and the tears as feigned; the verse says only that she showed the pain and wept.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:373":{"book":"naladiyar","num":373,"t":["அங்கண் விசும்பின் அமரர் தொழப்படுஞ்","செங்கண்மா லாயினும் ஆகமன்- தங்கைக்","கொடுப்பதொன் றில்லாரைக் கொய்தளி ரன்னார்","விடுப்பர்தங் கையால் தொழுது."],"p":"Though he be Mal, the fiery-eyed, whom in the heaven's fair homes immortals worship, if in hand he bring no gift the women tender as the buds men cull, will straight dismiss him, bowing low with folded hands.","r":"Even if he were the red-eyed Mal whom the gods of the wide sky worship, women tender as plucked shoots would join their hands in worship and send away a man with nothing in his hand to give.","n":"The joke lies in the gesture: hands joined as if in worship are the hands that dismiss him. Mudaliar takes மால் as Tirumal, with Indra as an alternative; Shanmuganar has Indra.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:374":{"book":"naladiyar","num":374,"t":["ஆணமில் நெஞ்சத் தணிநீலக் கண்ணார்க்குக்","காணமி லாதார் கடுவனையர் - காணவே","செக்கூர்ந்து கொண்டாரும் செய்த பொருளுடையார்","அக்காரம் அன்னார் அவர்க்கு."],"p":"To the damsels of loveless hearts, whose eyes are as beauteous as blue water-lilies, those who have no wealth are as poison! Even those who have turned the (oil) press, in sight of all men, if they have wealth, are as sugar to them.","r":"To women with loveless hearts and lovely blue-lily eyes, men without money are poison; but even a man seen by all driving an oil press is sugar to them if he has earned wealth.","n":"Mudaliar glosses the oil press as a low trade. The verse says money erases even that rank in their eyes.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","selvam","kudi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:375":{"book":"naladiyar","num":375,"t":["பாம்பிற் கொருதலை காட்டி ஒருதலை","தேம்படு தெண்கயத்து மீன்காட்டும் - ஆங்கு","மலங்கன்ன செய்கை மகளிர்தோள் சேர்வர்","விலங்கன்ன வெள்ளறிவி னார்."],"p":"The silly ones, who are as beasts, seek the embraces of women who are like the eel, which shows one head to the snake, and another head to the fish, in the sweet clear lake : (are of a double and deceitful nature).","r":"Like the eel that shows one end to the snake and the other to the fish in the clear, sweet pond, women of double conduct are embraced by the dull-witted, who are like beasts.","n":"Mudaliar explains the eel's trick as matching each man's nature and showing him love. The verse turns part of the blame on the men, who are the beasts. Shanmuganar's commentary on this quatrain appears under 374.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:376":{"book":"naladiyar","num":376,"t":["பொத்தநூற் கல்லும் புணர்பிரியா அன்றிலும்போல்","நித்தலும் நம்மைப் பிரியலம் என்றுரைத்த","பொற்றொடியும் போர்த்தகர்க்கோ டாயினாள் நன்னெஞ்சே","நிற்றியோ போதியோ நீ."],"p":"‘We will never part, like the precious stone strung on its thread, or the Andril,' said the damsel with golden bracelets: she has now become the horn of a fighting ram (she angrily repels me). Dear heart! dost linger still, or wilt thou go?","r":"The woman of golden bangles who said 'we'll never part from you', like a gem strung on its thread and the anril birds that never separate, has turned away like a fighting ram's horn; my good heart, will you stay, or will you go?","n":"The speaker turns from her to his own heart, which is still with her. Both commentators take the last line as 'will you stay with her or come with me?', so the heart is a separate party the man has to win back.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","nenju","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:377":{"book":"naladiyar","num":377,"t":["ஆமாபோல் நக்கி அவர்கைப் பொருள்கொண்டு","சேமாபோல் குப்புறூஉஞ் சில்லைக்கண் அன்பினை","ஏமாந் தெமதென் றிருந்தார் பெறுபவே","தாமாம் பலரால் நகை."],"p":"Those who fondly reckon upon the devoted love of the worthless wanton, that, like the wild ox, licks the hand and despoils men of their wealth, and then, like the buffalo, bounds away, shall suffer the deserved ridicule of many.","r":"Those who are fooled into thinking theirs the love of a worthless woman who licks like a wild cow, takes the wealth from their hands, and then lies down like a balking ox, will be laughed at by many.","n":"Mudaliar cites a belief that the wild cow's lick pleases at first and kills later. குப்புறூஉம் is either lying face down, as Mudaliar takes it first and Shanmuganar takes it, or bounding away, as in Pope.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:378":{"book":"naladiyar","num":378,"t":["ஏமாந்த போழ்தின் இனியார்போன் றின்னாராய்த்","தாமார்ந்த போதே தகர்கோடாம் - மானோக்கின்","தந்நெறிப் பெண்டிர் தடமுலை சேராரே,","செந்நெறிச் சேர்துமென் பார்."],"p":"Those whose avowed purpose it is to walk in the way of rectitude seek not the embrace of the fascinating, fawn-eyed damsels, who walk in a way of their own' who are pleasant when gratified with gifts, and when they are filled( =when no more gifts are to be expected) are like the horn of the fighting ram.","r":"Those who mean to reach the right path won't embrace the large breasts of doe-eyed women who go their own way, women who seem kind while a man is infatuated and turn harsh as a ram's horn 'once they are full'; who is full is disputed.","n":"Mudaliar glosses செந்நெறி as the path to liberation, which makes avoiding these women a renunciant's discipline; Shanmuganar has only 'the good way'.","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","ozhukkam","thuravu"],"pos":[["mudaliar","தாம் ஆர்ந்தபோது refers to the men: when, now poor, they come of their own desire, the women turn harsh.","தாம் ஆர்ந்தபோது - (வறுமையடைந்து) தாமே விரும்பிச் சென்ற போது."],["shanmuganar","It refers to the women: once they are filled with a man's wealth, they turn harsh.","தாம் செல்வம் நிரம்பியபோது இனிமையற்றவராய்"]],"ch":"n:38"},"n:379":{"book":"naladiyar","num":379,"t":["ஊறுசெய் நெஞ்சந்தம் உள்ளடக்கி ஒண்ணுதலார்","தேறா மொழிந்த மொழிகேட்டுத் - தேறி","எமரென்று கொள்வாரும் கொள்பவே யார்க்கும்","தமரல்லர் தம்உடம்பி னார்."],"p":"Let those accept (wantons' false love), who take them as their own believing the words uttered to inspire belief, by the bright-browed ones, who keep concealed within the cruelty that lurks in their heart. Those whose bodies are their sole wealth belong to none, (i. e. they have no souls to give!).","r":"Some do take bright-browed women as their own, trusting words spoken to be believed by women who hide their harmful intent; but such women belong to no one except their own bodies.","n":"Pope adds 'they have no souls to give'. Mudaliar's gloss is narrower: their attachment reaches only as far as their own bodies.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:38"},"n:380":{"book":"naladiyar","num":380,"t":["உள்ளம் ஒருவன் உழையதா ஒண்ணுதலார்","கள்ளத்தாற் செய்யுங் கருத்தெல்லாந் - தெள்ளி","அறிந்த விடத்தும் அறியாராம் பாவம்","செறிந்த உடம்பி னவர்."],"p":"Though the dupes clearly discern and know the guileful intentions entertained by the bright browed ones, even where their minds are (apparently) set upon some persons, they whose bodies are full of sin do not recognise it! ---------","r":"The scheming of bright-browed women whose minds are with another man is clearly known, and still those whose bodies are full of sin don't grasp it; who it is that knows is disputed.","n":"","x":1,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["varaivinmagalir","vanjam","pedhaimai","vinai"],"pos":[["mudaliar","The dupes themselves examine and know the guile, yet don't grasp that the women should be shunned.","(அவர் பரிகரிக்கத் தக்காரென்று) உணரார்கள்."],["shanmuganar","The world knows the guile clearly; the sinful fools alone don't know it.","உலகினரால் தெளிவாக அறியப்பட்டிருந்தும் பாவம் நிறைந்த உடம்பை யுடைய மூடர் அறியமாட்டார்."]],"ch":"n:38"},"n:381":{"book":"naladiyar","num":381,"t":["அரும்பெறற் கற்பின் அயிராணி யன்ன","பெரும்பெயர்ப் பெண்டி ரெனினும் - விரும்பிப்","பெறுநசையால் பின்னிற்பா ரின்மையே பேணும்","நறுநுதலாள் நன்மைத் துணை."],"p":"Though women live famed as Ayirani for rarest gift of chastity, absence of men that stand enamoured of their charms is a help in way of good to those of fragrant brow who guard themselves.","r":"Even among women as famous as Indrani for a chastity hard to attain, the fragrant-browed woman who keeps the quality that leaves no man following her in desire is the good companion.","n":"Pope reads it differently: the absence of admirers is a help to women who guard themselves, as though even the chaste benefit from not being tempted. Both commentators make the absence her own doing. Indrani is the standard of the comparison, so the claim is strong: even such women are measured by this.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["karpu","vaazhkkaithunai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:382":{"book":"naladiyar","num":382,"t":["குடநீரட் டுண்ணும் இடுக்கட் பொழுதும்","கடனீ ரறவுண்ணும் கேளிர் வரினும்","கடனீர்மை கையாறாக் கொள்ளு மடமொழி","மாதர் மனைமாட்சி யாள்."],"p":"her duties with kindness of speech. When in the straitened time they cook and eat with but one pot of water on the hearth,— if relatives arrive enough to drain the sea, the soft-voiced dame, the glory of her home, well fulfils each seemly duty.","r":"Even in hard times, when the household cooks with a single pot of water, and even if kin arrive enough to drink the sea dry, the soft-spoken woman who keeps her duties as her settled way is the glory of the home.","n":"The two hyperboles push in opposite directions, one pot against a sea, and her duty holds between them. Shanmuganar names the duty as hospitality.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["virundhu","vaazhkkaithunai","inmai","sutram"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:383":{"book":"naladiyar","num":383,"t":["நாலாறும் ஆறாய் நனிசிறிதாய் எப்புறனும்","மேலாறு மேலுறை சோரினும் - மேலாய","வல்லாளாய் வாழும்ஊர் தற்புகழு மாண்கற்பின்","இல்லாள் அமர்ந்ததே இல்."],"p":"On every side the narrow dwelling lies open, on every part the rain drips down; yet, if the dame has noble gifts, praised by townsfolk for her modest worth, such a housewife's blest abode is indeed a home! The wife : 'placens uxor.' She is sweet to the eye, and adorned in the way that a lover loves; she enforces awe: her virtue shames the village folk; she is submissive; but in fitting place is stern, yet sweetly relents :—such a soft-voiced dame is a wife.","r":"Though the house is open on all four sides, tiny, and dripping with rain from the roof everywhere, where the excellent wife lives, capable in the highest virtues and praised by the town she lives in, that alone is a home.","n":"Mudaliar draws the consequence the verse leaves unsaid, that other houses are forests, which completes the pair with 361.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","karpu","inmai","ilvaazhkkai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:384":{"book":"naladiyar","num":384,"t":["கட்கினியாள், காதலன் காதல் வகைபுனைவாள்,","உட்குடையாள், ஊர்நாண் இயல்பினாள், - உட்கி","இடனறிந் தூடி இனிதின் உணரும்","மடமொழி மாதராள் பெண்."],"p":"","r":"She is a wife who is lovely to the eye, adorns herself as her husband loves, holds him in awe, has the modesty that is shy before the town, and, in awe of him, knows when to sulk and relents sweetly: the soft-spoken woman.","n":"Mudaliar gives two senses of ஊர்நாண் இயல்பினாள்: one who is shy before the townsfolk, or one whose conduct puts the townsfolk to shame. The translation attached to 383 in Pope's text takes the second. Sulking is counted among a good wife's virtues, provided it is well timed and quickly ended.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["vaazhkkaithunai","naanam","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:385":{"book":"naladiyar","num":385,"t":["எஞ்ஞான்றும் எங்கணவர் எந்தோள்மேற் சேர்ந்தெழினும்","அஞ்ஞான்று கண்டேம்போல் நாணுதுமால் - எஞ்ஞான்றும்","என்னை கெழீஇயினர் கொல்லோ பெருள்நசையால்","பன்மார்பு சேர்ந்தொழுகு வார்."],"p":"‘Whenever our spouse takes us in his embrace, we feel a timid shame as if we saw him then for the first time and they (wantons) daily, through desire of gain, submit to the embraces of many! How can this be?'","r":"However often our husband rises from our shoulders, we feel as shy as if we were seeing him for the first time; how, then, do those who embrace many men's chests for gain do it every day?","n":"The wife speaks with wonder rather than scorn: she can't imagine the life she sets against her own.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","karpu","varaivinmagalir"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:386":{"book":"naladiyar","num":386,"t":["உள்ளத் துணர்வுடையான் ஓதிய நூலற்றால்","வள்ளன்மை பூண்டான்கண் ஒண்பொருள் - தெள்ளிய","ஆண்மகன் கையில் அயில்வாள் அனைத்தரோ","நாணுடையாள் பெற்ற நலம்."],"p":"As the scroll that he reads, whose inmost heart well understands it, is goodly wealth with him who is graced by a generous spirit. As a keen weapon in the hand of a clear-souled hero, is the loveliness that a modest woman owns.","r":"Like the learning of one with understanding in his heart, like bright wealth in the hands of a generous man, like a sharp sword in the hand of a clear-minded hero, is the beauty a modest woman has.","n":"The verse gives three similes and no explanation. Mudaliar supplies one: learning and a giver's wealth serve everyone, but a hero's sword destroys whoever stands against it, so a modest woman's beauty serves her husband alone and ruins any other who desires it. Shanmuganar pairs the first two similes as both useful and calls the sword only notable.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["naanam","nalam","karpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:387":{"book":"naladiyar","num":387,"t":["கருங்கொள்ளும் செங்கொள்ளும் தூணிப் பதக்கென்று","ஒருங்கொப்பக் கொண்டானாம் ஊரன் - ஒருங்கொவ்வா","நன்னுதலார்த் தோய்ந்த வரைமார்பன் நீராடாது","என்னையும் தோய வரும்."],"p":"'The lord of the town has bought, it seems, black gram and red gram, a tuni and a pathakku, as if they were all the same! He, whose chest is broad as a mountain, having associated with the beautiful browed ones , — inferior to me! — unpurified, seeks my society too!'","r":"The lord of the town has bought, it seems, black gram and red gram, a tuni and a pathakku, all as one; the mountain-chested man who embraced bright-browed women unlike me comes, without bathing, to embrace me too.","n":"The grain proverb is the wife's charge of indiscrimination: he treats the courtesans and his wife as one measure. Mudaliar says the verse shows the wife's own purity.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","karpu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:388":{"book":"naladiyar","num":388,"t":["கொடியவை கூறாதி பாண. நீ கூறின்","அடிபைய இட்டொதுங்கிச் சென்று - துடியின்","இடக்கண் அனையம்யாம் ஊரற் கதனால்","வலக்கண் அனையார்க் குரை."],"p":"‘O minstrel, utter not cruel words, or if thou utter them, softly draw back thy step, and go to utter them to those who are like the lute's right side; for we, to the lord of the town, are as its left.'","r":"Minstrel, don't speak cruel words; if you must, step softly aside and go: to the lord of the town we are like the drum's left face, so tell it to those who are like its right face.","n":"The right face of the துடி is struck and the left isn't, as Mudaliar explains: the wife calls herself the side that is no longer played. The minstrel is the husband's go-between.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal","pirivu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:389":{"book":"naladiyar","num":389,"t":["சாய்ப்பறிக்க நீர்திகழும் தண்வய லூரன்மீது","ஈப்பறக்க நொந்தேனும் யானேமன் - தீப்பறக்கத்","தாக்கி முலைபொருத தண்சாந் தணியகலம்","நோக்கி யிருந்தேனும் யான்."],"p":"'I am she, forsooth, that felt a pang when a fly alighted on the lord of the town, surrounded by cool rice-fields over which the waters gleam, where they pluck the rich grass! And I am she who have lived to look upon his breast adorned with cool sandal-wood paste, which has been warmly embraced by others.","r":"I am the one who grieved if a fly so much as flew over the lord of the cool town where water gleams once the reeds are pulled; and I am the one who has sat looking at his chest, its cool sandal paste smeared by breasts that struck it till sparks flew.","n":"The repeated 'I am the one' sets two times side by side without comment, and the grief lies in the gap between them.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","pirivu","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:390":{"book":"naladiyar","num":390,"t":["அரும்பவிழ் தாரினான் எம்அருளும் என்று","பெரும்பொய் உரையாதி, பாண - கரும்பின்","கடைக்கண் அனையம்நாம் ஊரற் கதனால்","இடைக்கண் அனையார்க் குரை."],"p":"'Minstrel , utter not a gross falsehood, saying that he who wears a garland of opening buds will favour us!’ ‘To the lord of the town we are as the (tasteless) tip of the sugar-cane ; therefore, tell (such a tale) to them who are like its middle (sweet, juicy) joints.'","r":"Minstrel, don't tell the great lie that the man with the garland of opening buds will be kind to us; to the lord of the town we are like the end joints of the sugar-cane, so tell it to those who are like the middle joints.","n":"Mudaliar gives the situation: the husband, back from a courtesan and refused, sends the minstrel to soothe her, and she mocks the messenger.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["parathai","oodal"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:39"},"n:391":{"book":"naladiyar","num":391,"t":["முயங்காக்காற் பாயும் பசலைமற் றூடி","உயங்காக்கால் உப்பின்றாம் காமம் - வயங்கோதம்","நில்லாத் திரையலைக்கும் நீள்கழித் தண்சேர்ப்ப","புல்லாப் புலப்பதோர் ஆறு."],"p":"'Lord of the cool shore of the deep bay, where the gleaming ocean's restless billows beat! 'If there be no fond embrace, a sickly hue will spread itself (over her face); and, if there be no lovers' quarrels, love will lack its zest. ‘To embrace and disagree is the one way (of love).'","r":"Lord of the cool shore with long backwaters where the bright sea's restless waves beat: without embrace, pallor spreads; without sulking and pining, love has no salt; embracing and then sulking is one way.","n":"Mudaliar takes உப்பு as sweetness and compares the Sanskrit theorists, who call separation the sweeter mode. The verse sets pallor and saltlessness as twin dangers: love needs both union and its interruption.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["oodal","punarchi","pasalai","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:392":{"book":"naladiyar","num":392,"t":["தம்மமர் காதலர் தார்சூழ் அணியகலம்","விம்ம முயங்கும் துணையில்லார்க் - கிம்மெனப்","பெய்ய எழிலி முழங்கும் திசையெல்லாம்","நெய்தல் அறைந்தன்ன நீர்த்து."],"p":"‘To those who were wont to strain in close embrace their own beloved whose broad breast was girt with garlands, when the rains patter down, it is as though one beat the funeral drum through all the regions where the muttering of the thunder is heard from out the clouds; since they are deprived of their loved one's society.'","r":"For women without the companion whose garlanded chest they would embrace till it swelled, the clouds thundering in every direction as the rain comes down sound like the beat of a funeral drum.","n":"The verse doesn't say why the rain hurts. Mudaliar does: the man promised to come back by the rainy season, so the rains arriving without him are the sign of a broken word.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","kaamanoy","punarchi"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:393":{"book":"naladiyar","num":393,"t":["கம்மஞ்செய் மாக்கள் கருவி ஒடுக்கிய","மம்மர்கொள் மாலை மலராய்ந்து பூத்தொடுப்பாள்","கைம்மாலை இட்டுக் கலுழ்ந்தாள் துணையில்லார்க்கு","இம்மாலை என்செய்வ தென்று."],"p":"At wildering eventide, when workmen all put by their tools, she culled choice flowers, and wove a gay garland ; — then let it fall from her hands, and wailed, 'What can this wreath avail to me who weep alone?’","r":"In the bewildering evening, when craftsmen have put away their tools, she was choosing flowers and weaving a garland; she threw down the garland in her hand and wept: 'What use is this garland to those without a companion?'","n":"The verse ends on இம்மாலை, which can mean 'this garland' or 'this evening'. The commentators take it as the garland, but the double sense may be intended. Mudaliar has the friend report the scene to the man.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:394":{"book":"naladiyar","num":394,"t":["செல்சுடர் நோக்கிச் சிதரா஢க்கண் கொண்டநீர்","மெல்விரல் ஊழ்தெறியா விம்மித்தன் - மெல்விரலின்","நாள்வைத்து நங்குற்றம் எண்ணுங்கொல், அந்தோதன்","தோள்வைத் தணைமேல் கிடந்து."],"p":"","r":"Watching the setting sun, flicking away again and again with soft fingers the tears in her red-streaked eyes, sobbing, does she, alas, count the days on her soft fingers and reckon up my faults, lying on the bed with her arm for a pillow?","n":"Mudaliar has the man speak this to his charioteer on the way home, imagining her grief, and cites Kural 1261. 'Our faults' turns her counting of days into a reckoning of his wrongs.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","ninaivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:395":{"book":"naladiyar","num":395,"t":["கண்கயல் என்னும் கருத்தினால் காதலி","பின்சென்றது அம்ம சிறுசிரல் - பின்சென்றும்","ஊக்கி யெழுந்தும் எறிகல்லா ஒண்புருவம்","கோட்டிய வில்வாக் கறிந்து."],"p":"‘The little kingfisher seeing the eyes of my beloved (as she was disporting in the tank) and taking them for carp, followed her; but though it followed, and poised itself aloft, it darted not down, recognising her gleaming brow bent above them as a bow!","r":"Taking my beloved's eyes for carp, the little kingfisher followed her; but though it followed and rose eager to strike, it didn't dive, recognising her bright brow as a drawn bow.","n":"Mudaliar calls it மயக்கவணி, the figure of mistaken identity, and has the man say it in wonder to his friend. Pope adds that the man is showing his own timid reverence.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["kan","nalam","kaamam"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:396":{"book":"naladiyar","num":396,"t":["அரக்காம்பல் நாறும்வாய் அம்மருங்கிற் கன்னோ","பரற்கானம் ஆற்றின கொல்லோ - அரக்கார்ந்த","பஞ்சிகொண் டுட்டினும் பையெனப் பையெனவென்று","அஞ்சிப்பின் வாங்கும் அடி."],"p":"'When I applied the (softest) cotton soaked in the red dye to the foot of her whose waist is lovely, and whose mouth breathes the fragrance of the red water-lily, did she not cry \"gently, gently,\" and shrinking draw it back? And oh! has it endured the stony, desert path? '","r":"Feet that drew back in fear, crying 'gently, gently', even when red dye was applied with soft cotton: have they, alas, borne the stony desert path, the feet of my girl with lily-scented lips and lovely waist?","n":"Mudaliar identifies the speaker as the girl's mother, who has learned that her daughter has gone with her lover through the wilderness. The verse sets the softest touch the mother knows against the hardest ground she can imagine.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","idumbai"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:397":{"book":"naladiyar","num":397,"t":["ஓலைக் கணக்கர் ஒலியடங்கு புன்செக்கர்","மாலைப் பொழுதில் மணந்தார் பிரிவுள்ளி","மாலை பரிந்திட் டழுதாள் வனமுலைமேல்","கோலஞ்செய் சாந்தந் திமிர்ந்து."],"p":"When those who con the palm leaf scrolls had ceased, in eve's din twilight hour, she thought of her absent spouse! and weeping plucked the flowers from out her wreath, and brushed the odorous sandal from her lovely breast!","r":"In the dim red evening, when the palm-leaf accountants fall silent, she thought of the parting of the man who married her, tore off her garland, and wept, rubbing away the sandal paste that adorned her lovely breasts.","n":"Mudaliar records two settings: the friend describing her grief in his absence, or the friend telling the man she won't consent to his going. The second makes the parting still to come. He also reports a view that the silenced voices are schoolchildren reciting, which Shanmuganar adopts.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["maalai","pirivu","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:398":{"book":"naladiyar","num":398,"t":["கடக்கருங் கானத்துக் காளைபின் நாளை","நடக்கவும் வல்லையோ என்றி - சுடர்த்தொடீஇ","பெற்றா னொருவன் பெருங்குதிரை அந்நிலையே","கற்றான் அஃதூரும் ஆறு."],"p":"'Thou hast said, O maiden with burnished brace - lets ! have you strength to walk on the morrow after your beloved? When one (a warrior) has obtained a splendid horse, that very instant he has learned how to ride it! (his enthusiasm teaches him).","r":"You ask, friend of the bright bangles, whether I can walk tomorrow behind my young bull of a man through the forest that is hard to cross; a man who gets a great horse learns to ride it that very moment.","n":"The answer is a proverb: the ability comes with the thing. Mudaliar spells out the hint, that following one's husband is not hard. Pope's 'his enthusiasm teaches him' is his own explanation.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","ookkam","anbu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:399":{"book":"naladiyar","num":399,"t":["முலைக்கண்ணும் முத்தும் முழுமெய்யும் புல்லும்","இலக்கணம் யாதும் அறியேன் - கலைக்கணம்","வேங்கை ஦வ்ருஉம் நெறிசெலிய போலும்என்","தீம்பாவை செய்த குறி."],"p":"‘My breast, my necklace of pearls, and my whole body she embraced ! I knew not what it portended. It was the sign, it seems, made by my lovely one that she was about to set out on the path where the herds of antelopes flee in fright from the tiger.'","r":"I didn't understand the sign at all when she embraced me so that my nipples, my pearls and my whole body were pressed; it seems the sign my sweet doll made meant she was about to take the path where herds of deer flee from the tiger.","n":"Mudaliar has the mother speak after her daughter's flight. The embrace was a farewell that only the leaving gave meaning to.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["udanpokku","makkal","kurippu"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"},"n:400":{"book":"naladiyar","num":400,"t":["கண்மூன் றுடையானும் காக்கையும் பையரவும்","என்னீன்ற யாயும் பிழைத்ததென் - பொன்னீன்ற","கோங்கரும் பன்ன முலையாய் பொருள்வயின்","பாங்கனார் சென்ற நெறி."],"p":"The triple-eyed (Civan), the crow, the hooded snake; the mother that bare me: what have these done amiss ? Maiden whose bosom bears the Gongu buds, all gold! The way my lover went for wealth is my pain.’","r":"The three-eyed one, the crow, the hooded snake, the mother who bore me: what wrong did they do? Friend with breasts like golden kongu buds, it is the road my lover took for wealth.","n":"The verse names four suspects and acquits them without saying why. Both commentators supply the myths: Siva burned Kama and let him live again, the crow raised the koel's chick, the snake swallowed the moon and released it, and the mother let her live, so each lies behind one torment of separation. On that reading she refuses the easy targets and blames the one real cause, the journey for wealth. The verse closes the Naladiyar.","x":0,"u":0,"f":0,"c":["pirivu","selvam","kaamanoy"],"pos":[],"ch":"n:40"}},"links":[{"a":"k:1","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"The opening verse sets the world under a First One without reservation. In 1062, Valluvar curses whoever ordered the world so that people must beg to live. Read together, they show the book does not treat the world's source as beyond moral judgement.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"Here learning has no fruit without bowing at the feet. In 400, learning itself is the one imperishable wealth, with no such condition.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:6","b":"k:25","type":"same_image","note":"The same word, ஐந்தவித்தான், describes the Lord here and a human renunciant in 25. Sharing the epithet suggests that the divine model and the human achievement are not wholly separate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Here only those at the Lord's feet cross the sea of births. In 38, on the commentators' reading, a daily good deed blocks the road of births, and God is not mentioned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:14","b":"k:1031","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1031, the whole world follows the plough. This verse adds what the praise of agriculture leaves out: the plough itself follows the rain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"Here giving and austerity cannot last without rain. In 34, virtue is simply a spotless mind and everything else is noise. If virtue is only inward, the drought in 19 shouldn't threaten it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:13","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"Hunger is here the world's worst consequence of drought, a natural evil. In 225, relieving hunger outranks the ascetic's power to endure it, which turns this natural fact into a moral priority.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:49","type":"tension","note":"Here the texts' settled view exalts renouncers above all. In 49, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the thing called virtue is household life, and renunciation is good only if it is blameless. The book states both verdicts without reconciling them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:26","b":"k:975","type":"agrees","note":"Both define greatness by rare, difficult acts. Chapter 3 says this of renouncers, while 975 in the chapter on greatness says it of the great in general, so the test is not limited to ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:29","b":"k:301","type":"tension","note":"Here the sage's unstoppable anger shows his stature. In 301, real restraint means holding anger back exactly where it can hurt. By the standard of 301, the anger praised in 29 is what should be held back.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:30","b":"k:134","type":"agrees","note":"Both make conduct the test of priestly status: 30 defines அந்தணர் by compassion, and 134 says a பார்ப்பான் is lost if conduct fails, even though forgotten scripture can be relearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"Here outward virtue is noise if the mind is stained. In 280, a shaven head and matted hair are unnecessary if one avoids what the world condemns. Note that 280 keeps the world's blame as the test, so the inward standard of 34 isn't all that matters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press virtue into the present against the delay of 'later'. 36 promises a companion, and 335 frightens with the hiccup that stops the tongue. Each uses a different motive for the same urgency.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"On the commentators' reading, 37 treats the difference between rider and bearer as virtue made visible. In 972, birth is the same for all beings, and distinction comes from what one does, which still leaves open whether the palanquin bearer's lot is deserved.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:23","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"In 23 those who weigh both paths and take up virtue here stand highest in the world. In 46 the householder has nothing to gain by leaving. Parimelazhagar resolves this by limiting 46 to the forest stage, but the verse doesn't state that limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:322","type":"develops","note":"Sharing food is a household duty here, and in 322 it becomes the chief of all that the authors have listed. What began as one household rule becomes the book's summary of virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:50","b":"k:234","type":"develops","note":"Here a well-lived earthly life earns a place among the gods. In 234 earthly renown goes further, so that the gods' world ceases to praise its own sages. Both measure human achievement against heaven and rate it highly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:55","type":"tension","note":"Verse 9 calls a head that does not bow to the Lord's feet useless. Here a woman who does not worship a god is the one whom the rain obeys. Parimelazhagar's added 'other gods' softens the contrast, but the Tamil does not contain it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:18","b":"k:55","type":"develops","note":"In 18, even the gods' worship depends on rain. Here rain answers to a woman who bypasses the gods. Together the two verses invert the expected order of gods, rain and humans.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:51","b":"k:478","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The wife lives within her husband's income, and 478 applies the same rule to the king's treasury: a small inflow is no ruin if outflow doesn't widen. The Kural's household economics and statecraft share one rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:57","b":"k:1251","type":"tension","note":"Here நிறை is the guard that outdoes all confinement. In 1251, speaking as a woman in love, desire's axe breaks the door of நிறை barred with shame. The book's ethics section treats inner guard as sufficient, and its love section knows it isn't.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:11","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Rain is the world's amrita in 11, and in 64 a child's messy gruel beats amrita. The same word is the top of two different scales: what sustains all life, and what is sweet to one parent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"Parimelazhagar's two comments pull apart. On 63 a son's good deeds reach his parents. On 377 he says the fruit of one soul's deed never goes to another (ch038). The verses themselves don't settle how merit is shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"The father's debt and the son's debt form an exchange: he gives the learning that seats the son at the front, and the son gives back a sentence of praise. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 70 makes the link explicit by calling the father 'the one who made him learned'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:80","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both say that a living body can be dead in effect, and Manakkudavar on 9 also says such bodies lack life. The criterion is worship in chapter 1 and love in chapter 8, so the two chapters compete for what makes a person alive.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:71","b":"k:1251","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a bolted door. Love has no bolt here, and in 1251 desire breaks the door of நிறை that shame has barred. The image passes from the ethics of love to its erotic form, with the same result: it won't stay shut in.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:76","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"This strengthens Kalaignar's reading of 76. In 773, the highest courage is fierce, and its sharp edge is kindness to those in need, so the book elsewhere does join courage to kindness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:64","b":"k:82","type":"same_image","note":"Amrita is beaten twice in these household chapters: a child's stirred gruel is sweeter than it, and even it isn't worth having while a guest waits. In both, food's worth is decided by who else is at the meal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:81","b":"k:212","type":"agrees","note":"Both end in the same phrase, வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு: 81 says the home exists for this, and 212 says wealth earned by effort does. Two different goods are given one purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:1005","type":"develops","note":"Here refusal of guests is poverty amid plenty. In 1005, someone with crores is destitute if he neither gives nor enjoys. The later verse widens the charge by also treating failure to enjoy one's wealth as a failure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:1111","type":"same_image","note":"The anicham measures the guest here and the beloved in 1111, and both are more delicate than it. The book's most tender image for a lover was first used for a stranger at the door.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:92","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the pair அகனமர்ந்து and முகனமர்ந்து. In 84 the goddess's glad heart answers the host's glad face. In 92 the glad face outranks even the giver's glad heart, so the same pair yields a different ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:91","b":"k:292","type":"tension","note":"Sweet words here must be free of deceit. In 292, even a falsehood counts as truth if it yields blameless good. What 91 excludes by definition, 292 may allow when the outcome justifies it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:92","b":"k:225","type":"tension","note":"Here a pleasant word beats a gladly given gift. In 225, relieving hunger is a power above the ascetic's. A hungry person's view of 92 might differ, which suggests the ranking in 92 assumes someone who isn't in need.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"Parimelazhagar limits humility in 95 to those who deserve deference. In 125, bowing is good for all and most of all for the rich, which speaks to who should bow rather than to whom, and doesn't contain his limit.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:200","type":"agrees","note":"97 requires speech to be useful and gentle, and 200 says to speak only what is useful. The later chapter keeps the usefulness test and drops the sweetness, which shows the two tests are independent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:316","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 99 argues from how sweet words feel to oneself. 316 makes the same move in general: don't do to others what you know to be painful. The same reciprocity works for speech and for deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:107","type":"same_image","note":"Both use seven births. In 62 a child's character shields the parent, and in 107 memory of a helper persists. Parimelazhagar on 107 refers back to his explanation at 62, so he treats the phrase as one idea.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:105","type":"agrees","note":"On Kalaignar's reading of 105, both make the recipient the measure of the gift: the guest's worth in 87 and the helped person's character in 105. Parimelazhagar's different reading of 105 separates them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:103","b":"k:221","type":"agrees","note":"Help without weighing the return is vaster than the sea, and in 221 giving outside the destitute is a kind of expected repayment. The two define real generosity by lack of calculation, from the receiver's and the giver's sides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:104","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"The same millet and palm. Here others' small kindnesses are enlarged, and in 433 one's own small faults are. The same mental act, turned in opposite directions, gives gratitude and conscience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"112 promises that a fair person's wealth will last, and 117 allows that such a person may fall into low estate. The chapter keeps both. The second stops the first from being a simple reward promise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:113","b":"k:657","type":"agrees","note":"Gain got by leaving what is fair should be given up, and in 657 even extreme poverty is better for the worthy than wealth got through blame. The later verse states the cost openly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, both rest on past deeds, and each draws a response. 379 asks why one grieves at the bad after accepting the good, and 115 asks that one's heart not bend. The first is about feeling and the second about justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:116","b":"k:282","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar takes 116 to mean that thinking equals doing, and 282 says outright that even thinking of stealing another's goods is wrong. This suggests that the Kural's standard really does reach into thought.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:24","b":"k:126","type":"same_image","note":"The five senses are imagined as animals in both cases. In 24 they're elephants controlled by a goad, and in 126 they're a tortoise's limbs drawn in. One is control by force and the other is retreat to safety.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:25","b":"k:124","type":"tension","note":"The renouncer quenches the five in 25. In 124, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the householder is restrained while enjoying the senses and is also called greater than a mountain. They are two different pictures of what restraint is.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:122","type":"tension","note":"Both say that no gain surpasses this, in the same formula (ஊங்கு), but 32 says it of virtue and 122 of restraint. The superlatives compete, as also in 61 on children, which suggests that the formula praises and doesn't actually rank.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:130","type":"same_image","note":"In both அறம் acts like a person, and Parimelazhagar calls it a deity in both. In 77 it burns the loveless like the sun, and here it waits for the restrained like a petitioner. It's the same power with opposite manners.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:129","type":"develops","note":"Harsh speech in 100 is merely a stupid choice of raw fruit. In 129 it leaves a scar that never heals. The first chapter on speech treats the speaker's loss, and this one treats the listener's lasting injury.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:109","b":"k:128","type":"tension","note":"In 109 one good act erases even murderous harm. In 128 one bad word turns all good to nothing. The ledgers are asymmetric, though the verses keep different books: the first is the recipient's memory, and the second, in Parimelazhagar's reading, is the speaker's merit.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:140","type":"tension","note":"The two ask the same question, what learning is worth, and answer differently. For 2, learning lacks fruit without worship, and for 140 it isn't understanding without fitting conduct in the world. One looks up and the other looks out.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:131","b":"k:327","type":"develops","note":"131 places conduct above life in general. 327 gives a specific case: don't take another's life, even at the cost of your own. It shows what 'guarding it above life' can literally mean.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:973","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate social rank from worth. 133 says this of conduct and birth, and 973 goes further with the high who aren't high and the low who aren't low. Read together, they show a repeated Kural challenge to status by birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:140","b":"k:426","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie understanding to how the world lives. Verse 426 calls it அறிவு to live as the world lives, which is a striking statement beside Parimelazhagar's narrowing of 'world' to the noble in 140.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:25","b":"k:144","type":"same_image","note":"Parimelazhagar uses Indra to illustrate both verses: 25 for the failure that shows the ascetic's power, and 144 for greatness lost through another's wife. This is one story serving two chapters in the commentary, though neither verse tells it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:143","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both call a living person effectively dead. In 80 it's the lack of love, and in 143, as both early commentators say, it's the loss of all three aims. A person can stop being alive in more than one way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:127","b":"k:150","type":"parallel_domain","note":"These are the two 'at least this' verses: however much else is left unguarded, guard the tongue; however wrong the rest, don't desire another's wife. They show what the book treats as the last line of moral defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:148","b":"k:773","type":"qualifies","note":"Both define பேராண்மை, the same word. In 773 it is ferocity in battle whose edge is kindness, and here it is not looking at another's wife. The book redefines the warrior's virtue as self-mastery.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:48","b":"k:160","type":"agrees","note":"Both set a kind of endurance above the ascetic's. 48 says the householder endures more because he also bears others' pain, and 160 says that bearing harsh words comes first. Each reassigns the word நோன்பு or நோன்மை to a life in society.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:57","b":"k:154","type":"parallel_domain","note":"நிறை appears in 57 as a woman's inner guard, and here it's what anyone stands to lose without forbearance. The same inner discipline is asked of women as chastity and of people in general as character.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:153","type":"same_image","note":"Both call refusal of a guest a kind of poverty. 89 says so directly, and 153 uses it as an analogy for something else, which shows that this was a settled saying that could be drawn on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:108","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"Both say that forgetting is what's best. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 108 it's narrowly about one who has also done good. Verse 152 removes that limit, and gives forgetting a rank above forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"157 asks for not retaliating, and 314 goes further: punish the wrongdoer by doing him good so that he's ashamed. What starts as restraint becomes a strategy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:225","type":"agrees","note":"Two verses, for different reasons, place the ascetic's hunger second. In 160 it's after bearing insults, and in 225 it's after relieving others' hunger. The book keeps finding what's greater than fasting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:167","type":"same_image","note":"The same goddess. She settles gladly in the home of the glad-faced host in 84, and here she hands the envious to her sister. Fortune is given a personality that responds to moral character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:166","type":"develops","note":"This is Parimelazhagar's own cross-reference. He cites 166 to read 135's simile: as the envious man's kin suffer, so do a man's kin when he lacks conduct. The commentary reads one verse by way of the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same logic is used in two domains: the passion is its own executioner. Envy needs no enemies, and in 305 anger left unguarded kills its owner. The book treats inner states as weapons pointed inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:170","type":"tension","note":"169 says that the crooked prosper and the upright fall, and that this should be pondered. Then 170 says that it never happens. The two stand side by side, which suggests that 170 is a moral claim and that 169 knows what experience shows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:375","type":"agrees","note":"Both admit that outcomes don't track merit. 375 says so within the chapter on fate, and 169 only asks for thought, though Parimelazhagar explains it through past deeds. The tension in chapter 38 is here in an earlier and quieter form.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:39","b":"k:173","type":"develops","note":"In 39, joy comes only from virtue. Here that claim is put to work: the 'small joy' of taking others' goods is set against a greater one, and on Parimelazhagar's reading, the greater is the same joy of virtue as 39.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:46","b":"k:176","type":"tension","note":"In 46 there's nothing to gain by leaving household life. Parimelazhagar on 176 calls household life a path toward renunciation, so leaving is the natural end. This commentator's way of ordering the stages conflicts with the plain force of 46.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:178","type":"agrees","note":"Both promise that wealth lasts on a moral condition: fairness in 112 and not coveting in 178. The second is the first's inward side, since 171 treats coveting as itself a lapse from fairness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:179","type":"same_image","note":"Virtue in 130 and fortune in 179 both seek out the right person, and they look for the right moment to do so. Parimelazhagar uses செவ்வி in both notes. Moral power is pictured as a courtier waiting for an opening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:180","b":"k:363","type":"agrees","note":"Not wanting yields victory here, and in 363 it's the choicest wealth in this world and the next. Chapter 18 is about not wanting others' goods, and 363 extends this to not wanting at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:185","type":"agrees","note":"34 says that a clean mind is all of virtue and the rest is noise. 185 supplies the test: if you can't tell whether the mind behind fine moral talk is clean, listen to how the person speaks of others.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:184","type":"qualifies","note":"100 calls harsh speech a foolish choice when sweet words are available. 184 ranks it above backbiting. The two together suggest a scale of speech: sweet, then harsh and open, then hidden.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:150","b":"k:181","type":"same_image","note":"These are built on the same frame: though one does no virtue and does wrong, at least this one thing. The verses name different things, chastity toward others' wives and not backbiting, so the 'last virtue' isn't a single item.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:151","b":"k:189","type":"same_image","note":"The earth is the model of forbearance in 151, holding up those who dig it. Here it has to talk itself into bearing the backbiter. This suggests that the backbiter does what digging doesn't: he tests even the earth's patience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:182","b":"k:824","type":"agrees","note":"Both pick out the false smile. In 182 it's the worst part of backbiting, and in 824 the sweet-smiling face over a bitter heart is to be feared. The moral judgment in chapter 19 becomes a practical warning in the book on friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:190","b":"k:436","type":"parallel_domain","note":"These share the word காண்கிற்பின் and the same question about faults. In 190 any person who sees his own faults is safe from harm. In 436 a king who has removed his own and then looks at others' has no fault left. The same self-scrutiny serves an ordinary person and a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:100","type":"tension","note":"100 calls harsh speech a raw fruit taken over a ripe one, yet on Kalaignar's reading 197 tolerates unsweet speech before useless speech. Sweetness is not the highest test of a word.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:200","b":"k:291","type":"tension","note":"291 defines truthfulness as speech without harm; 200 demands use. Harmless idle talk passes the one test and fails the other, so the two chapters set different standards for speech.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:200","b":"k:645","type":"develops","note":"645 echoes the same imperative and raises the standard: useful is not enough if some other word could defeat it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:196","b":"k:1071","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny full humanity by appearance alone: chaff has the look of grain, and the base look like people. For the Kural, being human is something a person can fail at.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:204","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both use 'even by forgetting', 199 for the speech of the clear-sighted and 204 for plotting harm. The first describes an achieved nature, and the second commands what such a nature would be.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:1075","type":"tension","note":"201 makes fear the mark of the excellent; 1075 makes fear the conduct of the base. They can be reconciled only by asking what is feared, which suggests that fear of the deed and fear of the penalty are morally different things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"203 only asks one to refrain from harming an enemy. 314 goes further and makes kindness to the wrongdoer the punishment itself. Prudent restraint becomes active shaming.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:657","type":"qualifies","note":"205 argues that wrongdoing won't cure poverty. 657 removes that premise: even when wealth does come through blame, the poverty of the worthy is better. Moral refusal doesn't depend on the prediction in 205.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:206","b":"k:316","type":"qualifies","note":"They give the same rule different grounds. 206 rests on what will come back to you, 316 on knowing from your own experience what hurts. One argues from consequence and the other from fellow-feeling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:380","type":"same_image","note":"Two pursuers that can't be evaded: the deed follows from behind (பின் சென்று) and fate arrives first (முந்துறும்). Together they close off escape in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:209","b":"k:305","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on தன்னைத்தான், making care for the self the argument for restraint: from evil deeds here, from anger in 305.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:169","type":"tension","note":"210 says the upright man's ruin is absent or rare. 169 admits that this ruin does occur and is something to ponder. The Kural keeps the moral promise and the troubling fact side by side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:211","b":"k:226","type":"tension","note":"211 insists that obligation looks for no return. 226 commends feeding the destitute as the place to store one's wealth, and Parimelazhagar says it comes back to help the giver. One motive disclaims reward and the other promises it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:216","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"An exact inversion: the same tree in mid-town, same ripening. In 216 it bears useful fruit; in 1008 it is a poison tree. The chapters on benevolence and on useless wealth share one picture, and only the owner's character changes it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:214","b":"k:80","type":"agrees","note":"Both move the definition of being alive from body to relationship: 80 to love, 214 to knowing what is due. The body without these is bone and skin, or a corpse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:219","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"219 says the good man's poverty is being unable to give. 230 pushes this further: being unable to give makes even death sweet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"220 only says that ruin from generosity is worth buying. 235 takes up the same word and explains how there can be a ruin that is a gain, and says that only the skilled achieve it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:221","b":"k:212","type":"tension","note":"On Manakkudavar's reading of 212, wealth is for helping those who deserve it. 221 says that true giving goes to those who lack. The worthy recipient and the needy recipient are different tests, and these chapters leave both standing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1051","type":"tension","note":"222 calls taking bad even if it were a path to heaven. 1051 permits begging from the right people and puts any blame on the one who refuses. The chapters on giving and begging judge the same act from different standpoints.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:229","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"229 names something worse than begging; 1066 says that nothing disgraces the tongue more than begging, even for a cow's water. Their standards differ: the misery of a life against the shame of an act.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:225","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"225 places enduring hunger below removing it. 261 defines austerity as endurance plus not harming living things, so the austerity that 225 ranks low is not the fuller ideal of chapter 27.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:1001","type":"develops","note":"228 asks whether hoarders know the joy they forfeit. 1001 gives the outcome: he dies without eating what he amassed, and nothing remains to be done with it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:233","b":"k:331","type":"tension","note":"233 names renown as the one thing in the world that doesn't die. 331 calls taking what doesn't last for what lasts the lowest kind of knowledge. Chapter 34 never excepts fame, so fame's permanence is exactly what the later chapter puts in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:222","type":"tension","note":"222 calls giving good even with no reward in any world. 231 calls giving, with renown, the sole profit of a life. The same act is valued for itself in one and as a gain in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:238","b":"k:114","type":"same_image","note":"Both rest on எச்சம், what is left behind. 114 reads a person's worth from what remains after them, and 238 makes renown the remainder that everyone should leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"The earth is morally responsive in both: it yields less under a body without fame, and laughs at those who sit idle pleading poverty. Land is more than a backdrop to human conduct in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:214","type":"agrees","note":"The same reclassification of the living as dead, with different tests: renown in 240, knowing what is due in 214. The chapters on benevolence and fame are linked as cause and result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:241","b":"k:411","type":"tension","note":"The same formula, செல்வத்துட் செல்வம், crowns different things: அருள் here and hearing in 411. Both can't be 'the' wealth of wealths unless the phrase is a rhetorical form awarded in context, a caution against reading any single chapter as the Kural's ultimate ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:247","b":"k:751","type":"develops","note":"247 mentions as an aside that this world is not for the penniless. 751 makes that its full subject: nothing but wealth makes nobodies into somebodies. The section on virtue quietly grants the premise of the section on wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:248","b":"k:250","type":"tension","note":"248 says those without அருள் are finished and can't become anything. 250, which Parimelazhagar calls the means of arousing அருள், assumes that the person going against the weak can still acquire it. The verdict is absolute, and the very next step offers a way out.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:249","b":"k:34","type":"agrees","note":"34 says stainlessness of mind is the whole of virtue and all else is show. 249 makes the same point about compassion: an outwardly right act without it does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:250","b":"k:318","type":"agrees","note":"Both derive compassion from one's own experience: 250 remembers fear before the strong, and 318 asks how one who knows pain in his own life could inflict it. The Kural's ethics of harm consistently starts from self-knowledge and not from rules.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:253","b":"k:774","type":"tension","note":"253 uses the weapon-holder as the model of a mind closed to good. 774 celebrates a warrior who plucks a spear from his own body and laughs. The Kural honours in the section on polity the same figure that it distrusts in the section on virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:256","b":"k:329","type":"develops","note":"329 condemns killers as base. 256 extends the guilt to the customer who keeps them in business, so the customer can't look down on the butcher.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:259","b":"k:88","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both move the worth of sacrifice elsewhere: 88 calls hospitality the வேள்வி that misers miss, and 259 ranks not killing above a thousand sacrifices. The Kural repeatedly tests ritual by how others are treated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:260","b":"k:268","type":"same_image","note":"Nearly the same ending: all living beings worship. In 260 the object is one who doesn't kill and doesn't eat flesh; in 268 it is one who has mastered, or on another reading lost, his own self. One route looks outward and the other inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:225","type":"tension","note":"225 puts the strength of enduring hunger behind that of removing it, which favours the giver. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar draw from 263 that தவம் exceeds giving. Whatever the verses themselves imply, the commentary tradition faces a real conflict here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:203","type":"tension","note":"203 calls refraining from harm even to enemies the height of understanding, and 261 defines austerity by harmlessness. Yet 264 advertises the power to crush foes. Parimelazhagar's 'enemies of virtue' and his comment that ascetics don't think of it are attempts to close this gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:266","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what a virtuous householder could gain by leaving for the other path. 266 says that those not doing தவம் are doing futile things. The chapters on householding and austerity each claim the highest place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:370","type":"tension","note":"265 recommends austerity because it gets whatever one wants. 370 says the unchanging state comes from removing desire. The means is sold by appeal to the very thing the end abolishes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:268","b":"k:346","type":"agrees","note":"On Manakkudavar's reading of 268, its subject has lost the ego, which is exactly the cutting of 'I' and 'mine' in 346. Parimelazhagar's 'possessing oneself' would make the link weaker.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:269","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"Two different conquests of death: by the power of austerity in 269, and by the vow of not killing in 326, where death's advance doesn't reach. The Kural offers an ethical route alongside the ascetic one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"270 explains poverty as too little austerity, which is something people could change. 377 says enjoyment is only as the apportioner apportioned. The same fact gets a moral cause and a fatal one.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:272","b":"k:293","type":"agrees","note":"Both make one's own heart the witness: 272 says that an outward show can't help when the heart knows the fault, and 293 says the heart will burn the one who lies against what it knows. Self-knowledge is the Kural's court of last resort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:278","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"278's மனத்தது மாசாக is the exact opposite of 34's மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன். 34 states the principle that all else is show, and 278 gives the social picture of the show in practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:827","type":"same_image","note":"827 warns that a bowing enemy's words are like a bow's bend, which signals harm. 279 says the lute's curve is straight and the arrow's straightness is cruel. So the curve is a warning in one verse and no evidence at all in the other, which supports 279's point that shape can't be read directly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:275","b":"k:88","type":"same_image","note":"The same utterance appears in both: in 88, the miser who fed no guests later says பற்றற்றேம் as a lament for lost support, and in 275 the hypocrite says it as a boast of non-attachment. Both are words that the speaker's life makes hollow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:282","b":"k:204","type":"agrees","note":"Both move the offence to the planning mind: 204 says that merely plotting ruin sets virtue plotting in return, and 282 says that merely thinking of theft is wrong. Parimelazhagar reads the standard in 282 as one for renunciants, while 204 has no such limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:479","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe prosperity that seems to exist and then vanishes: stolen gain 'as if growing' in 283, and in 479 the life of one who doesn't live by measure, which seems to exist and is gone. Both also turn on அளவு, so here the moral and the economic meanings of 'measure' meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"659 says that what is taken with tears goes with tears. Together they make the same bet about ill-gotten gain, but 283 stresses its illusion of growth and 659 stresses its justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:300","b":"k:323","type":"tension","note":"300 finds nothing better than truthfulness, while 323 puts not killing first and truthfulness right behind it. The Kural declares its rankings chapter by chapter, and here it comes close to correcting itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:299","b":"k:753","type":"tension","note":"Almost the same phrase is applied to opposite things: பொய்யா விளக்கு is truthfulness here, and 753 calls wealth the பொய்யா விளக்கம் that cuts through darkness. The section on wealth deliberately takes over the language of the section on virtue.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:292","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"297 prizes unbroken not-lying, while 292 admits falsehood under a condition. They are consistent only given 291's redefinition of truth by effect, which shows how much that definition is carrying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:295","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"225 favours giving over austerity, the commentators on 263 favour austerity over giving, and 295 now sets truth above both. The chapters rank a shifting set of virtues rather than a fixed ladder.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:298","b":"k:278","type":"agrees","note":"278 shows the bathers with impure minds, and 298 states the principle that this water only reaches the body. Read together, the chapter on hypocrisy provides the case for this general claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:301","b":"k:250","type":"develops","note":"Both are about the moment of having power over someone weaker. 250 offers a memory that stops you: your own fear before the strong. 301 says that only at this moment does restraint count as virtue at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:305","b":"k:264","type":"tension","note":"264 credits austerity with the power to crush foes. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 305, the ascetic who uses this power in a curse loses his austerity and returns to the misery of births. The power is real, but using it destroys its source.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:304","b":"k:786","type":"same_image","note":"Both distinguish the laughter of the face from that of the heart. 786 says that friendship that smiles only on the face isn't friendship, and 304 says anger kills both, so a smile alone isn't what is at stake.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:157","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for restraint under extreme provocation. 157 goes further and grounds it in pity for the wrongdoer's coming fall, whereas 308 offers no reason, only the admission that it is hard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:265","type":"agrees","note":"The same promise, that what one wants arrives as one wants it, is made for austerity in 265 and for non-anger in 309. On this comparison, dropping anger can do the work of austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:550","type":"tension","note":"Both use ஒறுத்தல், punish. 314 says to punish wrongdoers by shaming them with kindness; 550 says that the king punishing the worst of them with death is simply weeding a crop. The word keeps its form while the ethics of person and state divide.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:315","b":"k:203","type":"agrees","note":"Both test அறிவு by conduct toward others: 203 calls not harming enemies its peak, and 315 says it comes to nothing without guarding others from pain. Understanding here is measured morally rather than by what one knows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:319","b":"k:207","type":"develops","note":"207 says that the enmity of deeds can't be escaped. 319 adds speed: by the same afternoon. One closes off every direction and the other closes off time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"330 reports that the diseased and destitute are paying for their killings. 1062 rages that if people must beg to live, the world's maker should perish. One explains misery as deserved, and the other refuses to accept it at all.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:320","type":"develops","note":"320 says that all pain rests on those who caused pain, looking forward. 330 turns this around and reads a past killing from present suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:550","type":"tension","note":"327 forbids taking life even to save one's own. 550 likens the king's execution of murderers to weeding a crop. Here the Kural's most absolute rule meets its statecraft, and it does not reconcile them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:259","type":"qualifies","note":"259 explicitly mentions sacrifice but only of oblations, and sets not killing above it. 328 doesn't mention sacrifice, but the commentators find animal sacrifice there. That shows how a commentary tradition can place a controversy in the text.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:329","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972 says that birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the work done. 329 applies this to killing: base by occupation. The commentators' emphasis on 'by work' follows the same principle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:753","type":"tension","note":"333 says wealth is by nature unstaying; 753 calls it the unfailing lamp that drives out darkness wherever one intends. The chapter on impermanence and the chapter on acquiring wealth describe the same thing in opposite terms.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:226","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell the owner of unstable wealth where to put it. 226 names the place: the hunger of the destitute. 333 explains why it must be done immediately.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:334","b":"k:257","type":"same_image","note":"Both end உணர்வார்ப் பெறின், 'if those who perceive it could be found'. Flesh as a wound, and a day as a blade: in each, the ordinary hides violence, and the verse doubts that many will see it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:335","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"36 says not to put off virtue until later, since it is a companion that doesn't die when you die. 335 supplies the concrete deadline: before the hiccup stops the tongue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:340","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"340 asks gently whether the lodger has no home of its own. 345 draws the conclusion for one who wants to leave for good: even the lodging is too much, let alone other ties.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:344","type":"tension","note":"341 says each thing given up removes that thing's pain, a piecemeal gain. 344 says keeping even one thing brings it all back. Parimelazhagar accepts both, one as a gain and the other as a risk, but the practical advice differs.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:1101","type":"tension","note":"343 demands that the domain of the five senses be crushed. 1101 finds all five senses' delights gathered in the beloved. The third book takes what renunciation would destroy and fulfils it in one person.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"இன்மை, having nothing, is natural and right for austerity in 344. In 1041, poverty, the same word, is what nothing else can match for misery. The word changes value completely with who has chosen it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:50","type":"qualifies","note":"50 places the good householder among the gods, and 346 and 348 put full renouncers beyond them and everyone else in the net. Parimelazhagar's ladder (fame and heaven for householders, release for renouncers) is how the commentary fits these together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:350","b":"k:7","type":"develops","note":"7 says that only those who join the feet of the incomparable one can be rid of grief. 350 repeats the structure with attachment. Parimelazhagar himself notes that the generic word is kept to match the invocation chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:355","b":"k:423","type":"same_image","note":"Their second lines are identical. 423 applies it to what we are told: whoever says it, find the truth in it. 355 applies it to what things are: whatever their appearance, find the truth in them. The same habit of mind is both critical thinking and metaphysics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"343 demands crushing the domain of the five senses. 354, on Kalaignar's reading, says that this gets you nothing without true perception. The disciplined renouncer of chapter 35 isn't the goal in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:242","type":"qualifies","note":"For Parimelazhagar, the schools conflict on everything in 242 and yet converge on அருள். On 353, he says that their conflict leaves doubt only experience can settle. Together they give his view of religious pluralism: agreement in ethics, and no resolution by argument in metaphysics.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:356","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"356 makes learning (கற்று) the route to seeing the true thing. 373 says however much one studies, one's own understanding prevails. Chapter 38 thus undercuts the very means this one recommends.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as conduct free of envy, craving, anger and harsh words. 360 lists desire, anger and confusion, whose ending ends pain. Anger and desire appear in both lists, and the shift to confusion in 360 marks the shift from conduct to knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:352","type":"same_image","note":"Both describe those of மாசறு காட்சி who are free of மருள். 352 says what it does for them; 199 says what it looks like from outside: they don't utter a meaningless word even by accident.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:362","b":"k:350","type":"same_image","note":"Both solve the same problem by the same paradox: you can't leave desire by force, so desire is used against itself. 350 holds to the one without holding, and 362 wants to be without wanting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:363","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"விழுச்செல்வம் names not wanting here and learning in 400. Taken with 241 and 411, the Kural has awarded the title of true wealth to at least four things, and that suggests the phrase is a rhetorical form and not a doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:364","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says inner purity comes through truth. 364 gives the missing link: purity is the absence of craving, and wanting truth brings it. On Parimelazhagar's view, the meaning of truth has shifted from speech to the Supreme.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:361","b":"k:351","type":"qualifies","note":"351 traces birth to confusion and 361 traces it to craving, and both claim to be the root. Parimelazhagar makes them a sequence, with ignorance first and craving following, but the verses each say it singly.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:201","type":"agrees","note":"Virtue as a kind of fear: in 201 the excellent fear evil deeds, and in 366 what is to be feared is craving, which is behind those deeds. It is the same moral fear moved one step closer to its source.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:320","type":"tension","note":"320 says all pain rests on those who caused pain, and 368 says sorrow comes from craving and doesn't come to those without it. Two single-source explanations of suffering, one ethical and one psychological, sit a few chapters apart.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"Just ten verses apart, this one ends the section on renunciation by promising a nature that can't be shifted, once craving goes. 380 asks what is stronger than fate, which gets there first. The Kural moves from the highest claim for human release straight to the strongest claim against human power.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:365","b":"k:226","type":"same_image","note":"அற்றார் are the destitute in 226, to be fed, and the freed in 365, to be revered. The same word for lacking marks both the most pitiable person and the most complete one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"Here effort is fate's product. In 620, effort puts fate behind you. The tension is sharper than one between effort and fate as rivals, because 371 removes effort as an independent force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:373","b":"k:396","type":"tension","note":"Here one's own understanding prevails over study. In 396, knowledge flows in proportion to study.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:400","type":"qualifies","note":"374 separates wealth and wisdom as different fates, and 400 goes further by calling learning the only true wealth. Read together, they redefine wealth.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:379","b":"k:38","type":"develops","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading both rest on deeds and births: 379 accepts the fruit of past deeds, and 38 stops future births.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:382","b":"k:428","type":"qualifies","note":"The king must never lack அஞ்சாமை, yet 428 calls not fearing what should be feared folly. Read together, kingly fearlessness can't mean fearing nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:672","type":"qualifies","note":"தூங்காமை is a permanent royal quality here. 672 uses the same word to divide tasks: some should be slept on and some should not. The trait becomes a judgment about each case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"467 fixes the order, deliberate and then resolve, which supports Manakkudavar's reading of துணிவு here and sits uneasily with Parimelazhagar's action without regard to anything.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:386","b":"k:565","type":"agrees","note":"The negative of the same pair. Being hard to see and harsh of look turns great wealth into what a demon has found: 386 gives the praise, 565 the horror.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same verb, வைக்கப்படும், raises a just ruler to இறை and a rightly living householder among the gods. Both are placed there by how they live, one in public and one at home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"389 asks the king to bear bitter words; 448 says what follows if no one speaks them: he falls with no enemy needed. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 389 uses the same idea of இடிக்கும் துணை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:140","type":"develops","note":"391 demands standing by what is learned; 140 gives the failure's verdict: those who have learned much but can't act in accord with the world are unlearned and ignorant. Conduct can cancel the very category of learned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:393","b":"k:575","type":"same_image","note":"Both turn eyes into sores, but by different tests: lack of learning here and lack of கண்ணோட்டம், kindly regard, in 575. One verse grounds sight in knowledge and the other in compassion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:398","b":"k:409","type":"develops","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, the two make one argument across chapters: learning travels with the soul through births, while caste rank ends with the body. That is why 409 can rank learning over birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:396","b":"k:416","type":"tension","note":"396 makes understanding strictly proportional to study. In Parimelazhagar's reading of 416, even a small hearing gives full greatness. Reading gains by degree and listening at once.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"404 denies that native brightness counts without learning; 373 says a person's own understanding prevails over any amount of study. On one reading, the Kural both dismisses innate understanding and calls it unbeatable.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:1000","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat wealth as worse for being held by the wrong person, but 1000 faults character and 408 faults learning. 1000's spoilt milk also shows the damage to the good thing, not only to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:409","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"409 uses high and low birth as a working scale, even to overturn it. 972 denies the scale at the root: birth is the same for all beings, and only deeds differ. The two arguments against birth rank are different in kind.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:414","type":"qualifies","note":"After a chapter that places the unlearned among beasts, 414 opens a door: though you haven't studied, listen. The next chapter quietly withdraws the finality of this one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:411","b":"k:241","type":"tension","note":"The same formula, செல்வத்துள் செல்வம், is given to the wealth of the ear here and to the wealth of grace in 241. 400 adds learning. The Kural's chapters each crown their own wealth, which raises the question of how they rank.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:414","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the leaning staff, but 414 has listening as the staff and 415 the speaker's conduct. Read together, the staff only holds if the one speaking has conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:978","type":"develops","note":"978 says greatness bows while smallness vaunts. 419 gives that a source: the bowing mouth comes from listening, so 978's humility is partly a product of hearing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:423","b":"k:355","type":"develops","note":"Nearly the same second line. 355 tests a thing regardless of how it appears; 423 tests it regardless of who says it. Together they strip away both of the surfaces that mislead: appearance and authority.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:423","b":"k:415","type":"tension","note":"423 sets aside the speaker when judging what is said. 415 rests the value of words on the speaker's conduct, and Parimelazhagar says those lacking it shouldn't be heard. Who says it either doesn't matter or matters most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:426","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"In the same chapter, 423 demands testing every claim for yourself and 426 says live as the world lives. The tension is softened if உலகம் means the worthy, as Manakkudavar has it, but the verse doesn't say so.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"380 says fate arrives first even when one devises another way; 429 says foresight spares one from being shaken. Manakkudavar faces this directly, allowing that early guarding softens even what comes from past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:374","type":"tension","note":"374 separates being wealthy from being wise. In Parimelazhagar's gloss on 430, all wealth is made and kept by understanding, while his gloss on 374 draws the conclusion that wealth doesn't need wisdom as its cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:421","b":"k:434","type":"develops","note":"Both turn on அற்றம். Understanding is the inner fort against it, and one's own fault is the enemy that brings it. So the attacker and the defence are both within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"Millet and palmyra both times: 104 inflates a small favour received, and 433 a small fault committed. The same discipline of scale runs in both directions, gratitude for others and severity with oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:436","b":"k:190","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Same shape and the same காண்கிற்பின். 190 asks anyone to see their own faults as they see others', and 436 makes the order a condition for a ruler who judges.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:384","type":"qualifies","note":"384 requires மானம் of a king; 432 forbids மானம் that has passed beyond dignity. The same word marks a virtue and a fault, and Parimelazhagar explicitly separates them in both places.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:440","b":"k:431","type":"tension","note":"431 praises those without desire, and 440 tells one how to enjoy desire safely. Parimelazhagar resolves this by making the fault excess in 431, but the verses themselves pull apart. Removing and hiding are different programmes.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:471","type":"qualifies","note":"444 ranks support as the chief of strengths. 471 lists it as one of four to weigh before acting, alongside the task and the opponent. The superlative here gets absorbed into a calculation there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:448","b":"k:434","type":"develops","note":"434 calls one's own fault the only enemy that brings ruin. 448 shows the mechanism: without those who rebuke, no outside enemy is needed. Self-correction turns out to be something a person needs other people for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:454","b":"k:373","type":"tension","note":"373 says a person's own understanding prevails over all study; 454 says understanding only seems to be one's own and lies in one's company. These are two different accounts of where knowledge comes from, within or among others, and they don't fit together easily.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:455","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"34 makes a spotless mind the whole of virtue. 455 makes that purity lean on purity of company. If so, the root of virtue in 34 has a root of its own outside the person.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:456","b":"k:114","type":"agrees","note":"114 says worthiness is seen by one's எச்சம், and 456 says purity of mind produces a good one. The same disputed word closes the loop between what is inward and what is left behind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:451","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says the knowing fear what should be feared. 451 names one such object, a surprising one: not a danger, but a social circle. It only makes sense given 452's claim that company changes understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:463","b":"k:449","type":"same_image","note":"Both use முதல், capital, from the merchant's world. 449 applies it to allies and 463 to risk: a king's ledger has two different kinds of principal that he can't afford to lose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids what the worthy condemn even if one's mother is starving, the most extreme form of Manakkudavar's reading here, that being able to succeed doesn't excuse scorned means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:464","type":"develops","note":"464 says to avoid beginning what isn't clear. 469 adds that even with the deed and intent clear, the person is another thing to know. Clarity about the act is not clarity about the recipient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:480","b":"k:220","type":"tension","note":"220 says if helpfulness brings ruin, it is worth buying even by selling oneself. 480 says helpfulness that ignores one's means quickly destroys wealth. Aram and Porul give opposite counsel on the same act, and 480 uses the same word, ஒப்புரவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:595","type":"tension","note":"595 says a person's height rises with their spirit, as a lotus stem with the water. 473 says many have been broken by exactly that spirit, ஊக்கம், when it wasn't measured. One counts spirit as the measure and the other demands a measure for spirit.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:479","b":"k:283","type":"same_image","note":"Prosperity that perishes while appearing to exist or grow. 283 blames fraud and 479 blames not knowing the measure, so honest wealth and stolen wealth can fail in the same deceptive way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:477","b":"k:385","type":"develops","note":"385 ends the royal wealth cycle in distribution. 477 sets the limit on that distribution. Parimelazhagar explicitly connects them, sending the reader back to 385 for the way of giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:376","type":"tension","note":"376 says what isn't yours won't stay however you guard it. 482 offers timing as the rope that keeps fortune from leaving. One denies that wealth can be held by care, the other names the means of holding it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:303","type":"tension","note":"303 says to forget anger toward anyone, because evil is born of it. 487 keeps anger alive in secret until it can be used. The Aram book treats anger as something to give up, while the Porul book treats it as something to store.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:828","type":"develops","note":"488 advises bowing to an enemy until he can be struck. 828 warns that a weapon is hidden in an enemy's folded hands. The Kural gives the technique and the defence against it, from either side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:471","type":"qualifies","note":"471 says act after weighing strengths. Parimelazhagar's 485 says that even with greater strength, wait for the time. So a favourable reckoning from 471 still isn't enough.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:495","b":"k:481","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The crow beats the owl by day and the crocodile wins in water. Time and place are two axes of the same argument: strength isn't an attribute of a creature but of a creature in a circumstance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:471","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, 471 says to act only if your side is stronger. 493 says the weak can win by choosing the ground. If so, the calculation of 471 must already have place in it, or 471's rule is too strict.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:444","type":"tension","note":"444 calls greater people as support the chief of all strengths; 497 says no support is needed but fearlessness, if the thinking and place are right. Five chapters later, the book seems to qualify its own superlative.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:500","b":"k:599","type":"same_image","note":"A great elephant is undone in both: by a tiger's spirit in 599 and by a jackal in the mud here. One verse credits the attacker's ஊக்கம் and the other only the ground, so size loses for two quite different reasons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"409 ranks the learned low-born over the unlearned high-born. 502 makes good birth the first condition for trust. The two can be squared, but the book's position on birth clearly depends on what is being asked.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:349","type":"tension","note":"349 has rebirth cut only when பற்று is gone. On Parimelazhagar's reading, 506 won't trust anyone without பற்று. The same absence is liberation in Aram and a security risk in Porul.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:433","type":"develops","note":"433 treats one's own millet-sized fault as a palm. 504 takes others on balance, by what predominates. Read together, the Kural sets a different standard for judging oneself and judging others.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:505","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972 says standing differs because of deeds, and 505 makes deeds the touchstone. Together they offer a test of worth that doesn't refer to birth, and this is at odds with 502, three verses earlier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:791","type":"parallel_domain","note":"791 says of friendship what 510 says of ministers: examine first, because afterwards there is no way out. Both make trust something with a fixed before and after.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:510","type":"tension","note":"510 says doubting one you have trusted brings endless trouble. 520 says inspect him every day. Parimelazhagar sees this and calls for private inspection, but the problem of watching without doubting remains.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:501","type":"qualifies","note":"501 builds an elaborate test and, in Parimelazhagar's words, passing shows the person won't change. 514 says that many change anyway, through the work itself. The most careful selection can't test for what office will do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:512","b":"k:385","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The king creates, gathers, guards and apportions in 385; the officer enlarges, increases and watches in 512. Parimelazhagar connects them. The servant's job is a copy of the master's, without the giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:513","b":"k:507","type":"qualifies","note":"507 condemns trusting on fondness alone. 513 puts love first among four qualities, so the fault in 507 lies in the 'alone', not in the love.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:523","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both show wealth as a full tank. In 215 it is a public pond serving all, and in 523 it has no banks. The first image is about access, the second about containment: wealth has to be held to be shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:524","b":"k:212","type":"tension","note":"212 says all wealth earned exists to serve the worthy. 524 says its fruit is being surrounded by kin. Both state the purpose of wealth, with different beneficiaries, and neither mentions the other.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:796","type":"qualifies","note":"521 declares that kin stay in adversity. 796 calls adversity a measuring rod for kin, which assumes some won't stay. A generalisation about kin in 53 becomes a test of them in 80.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:531","b":"k:305","type":"tension","note":"305 says anger kills the one who has it. In Parimelazhagar's argument for 531, anger at least sometimes reaches the enemy, while elated forgetting only kills oneself. The Aram book's worst self-destroyer gets demoted.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:408","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reasoning on 408, poverty doesn't ruin the good. 532 makes chronic poverty the very image of what destroys understanding. Either understanding survives poverty, or it doesn't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:421","type":"develops","note":"421 builds an inner fort of understanding. 534 shows an outer fort falling to inner fear. Together they say that a fortress is finally a state of mind, which is unusual in a Porul section that counts forts as a limb of the state.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:537","b":"k:483","type":"develops","note":"'Nothing is hard' appears with different conditions: 462 counsel, 472 knowing strength, 483 means and time, and here attention. The refrain's repetition suggests that no single one suffices, and each chapter has claimed the last word.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:540","b":"k:666","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Same structure: what one has in mind is attained, if. 666's condition is firmness and 540's is continued remembering. They might be one quality seen from the will and from attention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:550","b":"k:327","type":"tension","note":"327 says don't take another's life even if it costs your own. 550 says the king's killing of the cruel is like weeding. The book's sharpest split between Aram and Porul: an absolute rule meets the role of king. Parimelazhagar resolves it through caste duty, which the verses don't mention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:578","type":"tension","note":"541 makes justice not showing கண்ணோட்டம் to anyone. 578 gives the world to those able to show it without harming the work. So the king both must and must not, and 578's condition, the work unharmed, is the hinge.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:528","type":"tension","note":"528 tells a king not to look at all alike but by rank; 541 says to show no favour to anyone. Household management and the court of justice have different requirements, though they are only two chapters apart.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:11","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The chapter on rain calls it the world's nectar because it keeps the world going. 542 gives the sceptre that same place for human society, which is why the failure of one can later cause the other in 559.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:543","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"30 defines அந்தணர் by compassion toward all life, and not by birth. If that is carried here, 543's அந்தணர் நூல் need not be the Veda, and this gives Kalaignar's reading a basis inside the Kural.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:546","b":"k:761","type":"tension","note":"761 calls a fearless army the chief of a king's assets. 546 says it isn't the spear but the sceptre that gives victory. Porul itself has two answers to what wins wars, one in its justice chapter and one in its army chapter.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:552","b":"k:546","type":"same_image","note":"546 says it isn't the spear but the sceptre that wins. 552 collapses the difference: a sceptre that asks is a spear. The same pair, வேல் and கோல், marks the line between justice and its corruption.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:551","b":"k:550","type":"develops","note":"550 has a king kill the cruel as weeds. The very next verse calls an oppressive king crueller than killers. My reading: read together, the two raise the question 550 leaves open, of who weeds the weeder, and the chapter's answer is tears, rain and time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"659 says what was taken with tears departs with tears. 555 makes the same moral arithmetic political. 659 is a personal warning about money, and here the crying of a whole people is the weapon against a throne.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:558","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty except poverty itself. 558 names something more painful: wealth under a tyrant. The later chapter's superlative doesn't hold in this one's political conditions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:545","type":"agrees","note":"A mirror: just rule brings rain and harvest in 545, and bent rule stops the rain in 559. Kalaignar rationalises both, a comparison in one and reservoirs in the other, because the older reading asks a modern reader to believe in moral weather.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:560","b":"k:543","type":"develops","note":"543 makes the sceptre the origin of books and virtue, and 560 shows the effect of its absence: the books are forgotten. Parimelazhagar's chain then connects these to rain through sacrifice, which ties 11, 543 and 559 into one circle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:567","type":"qualifies","note":"549 clears the king: punishing crime is no blemish but his work. 567 marks where that clearance ends, since punishment past the limit becomes a file on his own strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:565","b":"k:386","type":"develops","note":"386 promises praise to the king who is easy to see and not harsh of speech. 565 and 566 cost out the opposite, turning two virtues into two ways of losing wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:382","type":"qualifies","note":"382 lists fearlessness as a king's nature. 569 suggests that it also has a material condition: without prepared defences, even a king comes to fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:570","b":"k:151","type":"same_image","note":"The earth in 151 patiently bears even those who dig it, the model of forbearance. In 570 that same earth finds one burden too heavy, which gives the hyperbole its force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:578","b":"k:541","type":"tension","note":"541 defines justice partly as not extending கண்ணோட்டம் (ஓர்ந்துகண் ணோடாது). This chapter says the world depends on it. 578 is the hinge, and Parimelazhagar cites 541 there: the same grace is the condition of the world and a corruption of justice, depending on where it falls.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:578","b":"k:580","type":"tension","note":"578 forbids kindness that damages the work, and 580 praises kindness that swallows poison. Parimelazhagar resolves this by confining forbearance to wrongs against oneself, but the verses themselves don't state that limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:570","type":"same_image","note":"Adjacent chapters use the same phrase, நிலக்குப் பொறை, for two different faults: the alliance of harshness with ignorance, and the mere lack of kindly regard. The second is far milder and yet receives the same verdict.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:575","b":"k:393","type":"same_image","note":"Both call failed eyes wounds. In 393 the eye is failed by lack of learning, and in 575 by lack of kindly regard. Taken together, an eye must both know and regard, and neither alone saves it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:576","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"If Manakkudavar's reading of 576 is right, both verses use the wooden doll for a person hollow inside: 1020 lacks shame and 576 lacks regard. On Parimelazhagar's reading the image is a rooted tree, and this parallel fades.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:580","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"807 says those of long affection don't give it up even when ruined by friends. 580 gives the same stance its most extreme image. If Parimelazhagar is right that the poisoner is an associate, the poison verse belongs to the same ethic as the chapter on old friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:581","b":"k:392","type":"same_image","note":"Both name two things as a person's eyes. 392 gives every living person numbers and letters. 581 gives a ruler spies and the treatise. Each makes sight depend on something outside the body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:581","b":"k:577","type":"tension","note":"In adjacent chapters a ruler's eye is first the grace that spares and then the instrument that watches everyone, kin included (584). The Kural does not reconcile the two eyes, and 578 is the nearest it comes to a rule between them.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:586","b":"k:274","type":"tension","note":"274 condemns hiding behind ascetic practice as a hunter hides in a bush to trap birds. 586 prescribes exactly that kind of cover for the state's spy, on Manakkudavar's reading at least. The Virtue book's hypocrisy becomes the Wealth book's technique.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:589","b":"k:423","type":"parallel_domain","note":"423 tells the individual to find the truth whoever is speaking. 589 turns the same refusal to trust a source into a state procedure, with independence and counting as its method.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:594","b":"k:371","type":"tension","note":"Both turn on the same word. In 594 wealth seeks out அசைவிலா energy. In 371 அசைவின்மை is itself produced by a fate of gain. The causal arrow reverses, so the question becomes whether steadfastness is one's own at all.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:597","b":"k:599","type":"same_image","note":"The elephant carries opposite morals three verses apart: under arrows it models unbreakable resolve, and before a tiger it is bulk without resolve. Parimelazhagar accepts this by locating the difference in the tiger's energy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:598","b":"k:613","type":"develops","note":"Both speak of the pride of giving (செருக்கு) and both root it in exertion. 613 names the vocabulary of labour and liberality that 598 only implies, so generosity is shown to have an economic foundation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:592","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"The Kural keeps naming the one true wealth: learning in 400, what is heard in 411, grace in 241, and energy here. Each dismisses ordinary wealth, but they cannot all be the sole true possession. Each is best read as a chapter's rhetorical peak.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:600","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Tree-people appear for two different lacks: kindly regard in 576 and energy in 600, and again for human quality in 997. The tree image stands for whatever a chapter takes to be essential to being human.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:371","type":"tension","note":"371 says sloth (மடி) arises from a fate of loss. This chapter treats the same மடி as a thing one can depose at will. The tension is direct, and neither chapter acknowledges the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1023","type":"develops","note":"The same goal, raising one's family, and the same word used differently. 602 asks one to drive out மடி, and in 1023 the deity tightens its மடி (waist-cloth) and comes ahead of the one who resolves on this. That verse's தான்முந்துறும் also echoes fate's in 380.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:610","b":"k:1103","type":"tension","note":"Both invoke Vishnu's realm as the highest measure. 610 offers it to the sleepless ruler, and 605 has just listed sleep as a boat to ruin. 1103 asks whether that realm is even as sweet as sleep on a beloved's arms. The Porul and Kamam books rank sleep and sovereignty oppositely.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:607","b":"k:784","type":"develops","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke when one strays, and uses the same word இடி. If Parimelazhagar is right that the scolding in 607 comes from friends, the verse shows what happens when that duty is carried out and fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:620","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"380 asks what could be stronger than fate, which arrives ahead of every stratagem. 620 answers that those who keep toiling see its back. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by saying fate blocks only a few attempts, which the two verses themselves don't say.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:619","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"377 limits what even hoarders of millions can enjoy to what has been apportioned. 619 guarantees effort a wage whatever divine order decides. One sets a ceiling on enjoyment and the other a floor on reward, and they may coexist only just.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:614","b":"k:727","type":"same_image","note":"The same sword in the same hand. In 614 it stands for generosity without labour and in 727 for learning without courage in an assembly. Both say an asset is void without the disposition to use it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:595","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase வெள்ளத் தனைய, 'as much as the flood'. In 595 the flood lifts a stalk, in a figure for the rise of energy. In 622 the flood is suffering itself, dissolved by a thought. In both the mind is what settles the scale.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:626","b":"k:593","type":"develops","note":"Both refuse the cry over lost wealth. 593 says energy is the real asset, so the loss is small. 626 says the one who never clutched has no such cry. One rests on what you still have, and the other on how you first held it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:628","b":"k:615","type":"same_image","note":"Both begin இன்பம் விழையான், 'not desiring pleasure'. In 615 this produces a pillar for kin through labour, and in 628 it produces a person pain can't reach. The same renunciation of pleasure serves worldly work and inner calm alike, without any talk of leaving the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for one response to good and bad, but they give different grounds: 379 reasons from fate that it's inconsistent to accept one and grieve the other, and 629 reasons from the training of desire, without mentioning fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:636","b":"k:373","type":"qualifies","note":"373 sets one's own understanding above study, and 636 asks for both at once: native acuity with treatises. Taken together, the opposition in 373 is not the Kural's last word on learning and nature.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:637","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"140 calls the learned ignorant if they can't move in step with the world; 637 turns this into an instruction for statecraft. The Virtue book's moral rule becomes a professional one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"889 says an inner enemy holds ruin even when small as a sesame fragment; 639 gives the same inner enemy a face at the king's side and a scale, seventy crore outer foes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:32","type":"tension","note":"32 says nothing brings more gain than virtue, and 644 uses the same ஊங்கு formula to put apt speech above both virtue and gain. This is either high rhetoric or a real rearrangement. Parimelazhagar makes apt speech a cause of virtue to avoid the clash.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:642","b":"k:127","type":"agrees","note":"127, in the Virtue book, says guard the tongue or suffer from its errors, and the fear is personal shame. 642 makes the same demand for a public reason, since gain and ruin follow. The same discipline serves different goals.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:646","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"424 defines wisdom by the same two-part structure: making your speech enter easily and finding the fine meaning in others' words. 646 assigns it to excellence in office, so wisdom and diplomacy are shown to share a form.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:69","type":"develops","note":"69 gives a mother's greatest joy as hearing her son called சான்றோன், greater than at the birth. 656 uses the same two words, ஈன்றாள் and சான்றோர், and places that standing above her hunger. Read together, the son's choice could be seen as what she would have wanted, though neither verse says so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"1041 says nothing hurts like poverty. 657 chooses extreme poverty over tainted wealth. The chapter on poverty doesn't withdraw this, so the choice is made in full knowledge of what the poverty will be.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:659","b":"k:319","type":"agrees","note":"Both state a return of harm: 319 in morning and evening, 659 in the taking and the leaving. 659 restricts it to wealth and adds the positive half, that clean gains come back.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:381","type":"qualifies","note":"381 makes six assets (army, people, wealth, ministers, friends, fortress) the mark of a lion among kings. Parimelazhagar's 'others' in 661 are exactly army, fort and friendship, which he says don't count as firmness. The list of assets is empty without the mind that uses them.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:666","b":"k:540","type":"agrees","note":"Two versions of the same promise: obtaining what one thinks of is easy if one keeps thinking of it (540), and if one is firm (666). The first asks for attention to be sustained, and the second for action to be.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:628","type":"tension","note":"628 prevents pain by not desiring pleasure. 669 accepts pain for the sake of the pleasure that results. One asks you to stop wanting joy and the other to work for it. They may differ in scope, since 628 speaks of how one meets pain and 669 of which tasks to take up, but neither verse says so.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:671","b":"k:467","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate thinking from having decided. 467 condemns thinking after deciding as a disgrace, and 671 condemns delay after deciding as a fault, the one seeing it as an intellectual lapse and the other a practical one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:383","type":"qualifies","note":"383 asks a ruler for தூங்காமை, sleeplessness, without condition. 672 divides this: some things should indeed be slept on. Parimelazhagar makes this connection himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:673","b":"k:546","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 673 ranks force first where one is stronger. 546 says it isn't the spear that gives victory but the king's straight sceptre. The Kural's strategy and its ethics may be speaking here to different questions. Manakkudavar's reading of 673 removes this tension.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:674","b":"k:879","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn about what enmity becomes if left to grow: 879 says fell the thorn tree young, and 674 says the left-over ember flares. 879 addresses timing, and 674 completeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:679","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"874 says the world rests in one who can turn enmity into friendship, as a mark of character. 679 turns this into a question of scheduling: it comes before duties to friends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"969 praises those who give up life at the loss of honour, like the yak with its hair. 680 describes the weak who bow and take the terms to keep their people steady. Parimelazhagar sees this: the honourable would not choose it, so the verse only reports it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:586","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Two agents of the king, both ready for death, with opposite methods: the spy says nothing whatever is done to him (586), and the envoy says everything whatever may come (690). They share an unflinching eye: compare 585's கண்ணஞ்சாது with 686's கண் அஞ்சான்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:667","type":"tension","note":"667 says never to scorn anyone for their form. 684 makes form (உரு) one of three requirements of the envoy. Parimelazhagar's explanation is that foreign courts respect the look of a man. The Kural is apparently distinguishing what people should do from what they in fact do.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:803","type":"tension","note":"Both use கெழுதகைமை, the privilege of old intimacy. 803 asks what good long friendship is unless such liberties are accepted as if your own. 700 says to take such liberties with a king brings ruin. Friendship and power apply opposite rules to the same bond.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:697","b":"k:638","type":"tension","note":"638 obliges the man at the king's side to tell him what is good, even if the king destroys wise counsel. 696 and 697 advise saying what is not disliked, in the way that he wants. The same courtier is told to be fearless and to be careful. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by reading 'wanted' as also greatly beneficial.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:695","b":"k:582","type":"tension","note":"582 makes it a king's work to know everything happening to everyone, swiftly and always. 695 tells those near him not even to listen in on his secrets. Knowledge flows one way, and the Kural presents this imbalance as prudence, not as a problem.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:698","b":"k:388","type":"qualifies","note":"388 says a king who rules justly and protects is held to be a god among people: the title is earned. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 698, the divinity belongs to any king, young or kin, and the courtier mustn't ask. Other readings of ஒளி remove the conflict.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:563","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Chapter 57 tells the king not to be dreaded, and this chapter tells the courtier how to live with one who is. Both are realistic, and the Kural addresses each party without assuming the other has listened.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:699","b":"k:654","type":"agrees","note":"Almost the same phrase, துளக்கற்ற காட்சி and நடுக்கற்ற காட்சி. In 654 unshaken clarity won't do what is degrading, even in trouble. In 699 it won't presume on favour. Steadiness shows in both failure and success.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:709","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Chapters 71 and 110 share the title குறிப்பறிதல். 709 has the eye say what kings won't, and 1100 says that when lovers' eyes agree, words are useless. A single theory of the speaking eye serves both state intelligence and love, and neither treats speech as the deeper channel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:706","b":"k:585","type":"tension","note":"706 and 710 say the face and eyes can't but show what is within. 585 asks the spy to stand under suspicious eyes and let nothing spill. If the face always reveals, the spy's art should be impossible, and if the spy's art is possible, this chapter overclaims.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:380","type":"same_image","note":"Both end with தான்முந்துறும், 'it gets there first'. In 380, fate gets ahead of any plan. In 707, the face gets ahead of any words. 1023 uses it again of a deity. The repeated phrase pictures a force that outruns human intention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:577","type":"same_image","note":"The Kural keeps asking what eyes are for. 577 answers kindly regard, 393 learning, and 705 reading signs. Each makes the physical eye worthless by that chapter's standard, so 'real sight' is defined differently each time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:702","b":"k:698","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, the courtier in 698 must treat the king as a god. 702 then gives that same comparison to the man who can read minds, so the servant who can read the god is set beside him.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:718","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"Two poured liquids in three verses: water on a growing bed and nectar in a yard. Nectar is much more precious than water and yet the outcome is reversed. What decides the value of speech is the ground that takes it, not the substance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:720","b":"k:401","type":"develops","note":"Both use the phrase கோட்டி கொளல், holding forth in company. 401 faults the speaker for lacking learning, and 720 faults the hearers for lacking fitness. Together they give both ways a speech can fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:715","b":"k:723","type":"tension","note":"The adjacent chapter says fearless speech in assembly is rarer than courage in battle. 715 says that the best of all good things is holding back among elders. The chapters are a pair and Parimelazhagar reconciles them with precision: only speaking first is restrained.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:780","type":"tension","note":"780 calls a death that brings tears to one's ruler's eyes worth begging for, in the chapter on martial pride. 723 calls those who die among enemies easy to find. The Kural holds both, and each chapter's supreme value is deflated by the other.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:729","b":"k:410","type":"tension","note":"410 makes the unlearned like beasts beside the learned. 729 places a kind of learned person below the unlearned. Learning that seemed to separate humans from animals is here worth less than nothing if the learned can't speak.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:728","b":"k:650","type":"develops","note":"650 uses the image of a scentless flower for those who can't expound their learning. 728 and 730 give the assembly as the place of the failure, and fear as its cause, adding a diagnosis to the image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:619","type":"tension","note":"739 says a land that makes you labour for riches is no land. 619 says bodily labour pays its wage even where the divine does not help. The ideal of the place and the ethic of the person pull in opposite directions.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"545 says rain and crops gather in the just king's land. 732 doesn't mention a king, but Parimelazhagar reads the absence of calamity through the king's virtue, which turns the land's fertility into evidence about its ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same closing move in two chapters: a land with every excellence is useless without accord with its king, and a fort with every excellence is useless without people who can act. Material conditions are never allowed to be sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:741","b":"k:775","type":"tension","note":"741 allows that a ruler may fear and protect himself behind walls. 775 counts even a blink before a spear as flight. The chapter on the state and the chapter on the warrior hold different standards for fear, and both are the Kural's.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:747","b":"k:421","type":"same_image","note":"747's fort is hard to seize from outside and vulnerable to those within. 421 calls understanding an inner fort (உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்) that enemies cannot destroy from within, the very weakness Parimelazhagar finds in 747.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Fort without capable actors and army without leaders end their chapters with the same negative verdict, இல்லது and இல், making human capacity the condition of every material strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:766","type":"qualifies","note":"750 says a fort fails without capable people. In 766 four virtues are the army's ஏமம, and Parimelazhagar concludes that such men need no other fort. Read together, the people are the real wall.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:751","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"Both make exclusive claims with the same structure. 400 says learning is true wealth and all others are not. 751 says nothing but wealth is anything. The Kural lets each chapter speak in absolutes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:753","b":"k:299","type":"tension","note":"Both use பொய்யா விளக்கு. In 299 the unfailing lamp of the wise is truthfulness and all other lamps are no lamps. Here the same phrase names money. Whether this is a deliberate reversal can't be proved, but the two verses compete for one title.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:660","type":"agrees","note":"660 likens wealth made by deceit to water stored in an unfired pot, and Parimelazhagar brings that very image into his comment on 755 to explain why such wealth destroys its maker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:241","type":"qualifies","note":"241 ranks grace far above material wealth, which even the base have. 757 makes material wealth the nurse that raises grace. The ranking stands but the independence doesn't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:678","type":"same_image","note":"Two elephant images for getting a task done without personally bearing the risk. In 678 one elephant is used to catch another, and in 758 the owner watches elephants fight. Both separate the one who directs from the danger.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:39","type":"tension","note":"39 says only what flows from virtue is pleasure, so virtue is the source. 760 makes rightly made wealth the ground from which virtue and pleasure both come easily. The order of the three aims differs by book.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:411","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same formula for two wealths: an army is தலை among a king's riches and listening is தலை among a person's. The phrase shows that 'chief of wealth' is relative to whose life is being described.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:763","b":"k:768","type":"tension","note":"763 mocks the sea-roar of an army without heroes. 768 allows that an army without fight can still gain stature through its show. Noise is nothing in one and display is something in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:765","b":"k:269","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Two ways of confronting Death personified. In 269 ascetic power may leap past கூற்றம், and in 765 an army stands against it together. The renouncer escapes and the soldier faces, and only one of them needs others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:773","b":"k:148","type":"tension","note":"Both use பேராண்மை. Here the word is first attached to fierceness in battle. In 148 the great manhood is not looking at another's wife. The Kural contests what manhood is, and 773's own turn to helping the fallen foe is partway to 148.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:773","type":"tension","note":"Two claims about the sharpest edge (எஃகு). In 759 wealth is the sharpest steel for cutting an enemy's pride. In 773 the edge of valour is helping that enemy when he falls. Cutting and sparing are both called sharpness.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:772","b":"k:596","type":"parallel_domain","note":"596 says aim high, since even a failure is as good as not failing. 772 gives the warrior's version: the spear that missed an elephant is better than the arrow that hit a hare. Aim outranks outcome in both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:776","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Both judge a life day by day and ask for no wasted days. In 38 the good deed makes a day count, and it blocks future births. In 776 the wound makes a day count. Two complete moral accountings use one form.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:780","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"1066 says nothing shames the tongue more than begging, even water for a cow. 780 says this death is worth begging for, with the same root (இர-). Only something beyond the Kural's worst disgrace could justify the word.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:231","type":"parallel_domain","note":"231 says the only profit for a life is to give and live with fame (இசை). 777 wants the same இசை, and pays for it with life itself. Giving and dying are two routes to one reward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"448 says a king with no one to rebuke him falls even without enemies. 784 makes the same office of rebuke the very purpose of friendship. What a counsellor does for a ruler, a friend does for anyone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:785","type":"tension","note":"783 says friendship grows with practice and 785 says practice isn't needed. Parimelazhagar reconciles them by ranking three causes of friendship and placing shared feeling first, but the verses themselves sit in plain tension.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:785","b":"k:801","type":"tension","note":"785 makes acquaintance unnecessary, while a whole chapter on old friendship opens at 801. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 801 eases this by saying that பழைமை is not elapsed time but a certain kind of friendship.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:1110","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both liken a relationship to learning with a 'the more, the more' (-தொறும்). In 783 friendship deepens like study's delight, and in 1110 love with the beloved is like finding one's ignorance with each new thing learned. Learning gives the Kural its model of an inexhaustible bond.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:796","type":"qualifies","note":"791 demands inquiry before a friendship begins. 796 says ruin is what truly measures friends, and Manakkudavar adds it is hard to know them otherwise. So the test that matters most can't be run in advance, and this undercuts the chapter's own program.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:797","type":"tension","note":"791 says those devoted to friendship cannot leave a friend once made. 797 calls leaving a fool's friendship pure gain. Parimelazhagar's 800 offers a way out: leaving is for what one took on without knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:793","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"793 and 794 screen friends by family. 972 says birth is equal for all living beings and only deeds make the difference. The chapter on choosing friends keeps a criterion that the chapter on greatness dissolves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:802","b":"k:1302","type":"same_image","note":"Salt in both, and both about intimacy that hurts a little. In 802 the worthy are salt to friendship's liberties, and in 1302 lovers' sulking is salt that spoils when overdone. 1302 supplies the measure 802 leaves out.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:805","b":"k:797","type":"tension","note":"797 says dropping a fool's friendship is gain. 805 says when an old friend hurts you, consider it folly and go on. The same பேதைமை is a reason for leaving in one chapter and an excuse for staying in the next, and the only difference is how old the bond is.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:807","b":"k:800","type":"tension","note":"800 says pay if you must to escape a bad friend. 807 says love does not end even when an old friend ruins you. Between them lies a question the Kural doesn't settle: at what point does a harmful friend stop being an old friend and become one you never should have made?","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:818","type":"tension","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke, openly and in advance. 818 says that with a certain kind of friend, say nothing and let it fade. Whether one rebukes turns out to depend on whether the friend is still worth the rebuke.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:817","type":"develops","note":"784 says friendship isn't for laughter. 817 raises that to a ten-crore disadvantage against enemies. A verse about purpose becomes a verdict on a whole class of companion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:813","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"911 says the women who desire wealth rather than the man bring ruin through sweet words. 813 applies the same logic to friends who calculate, and the comparison runs the other way: chapter 92's accusation is made first of men's friendships.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:824","b":"k:706","type":"tension","note":"706 says the face shows what is in the heart as crystal shows what is beside it. 824 fears those whose face shows the reverse. Chapter 71 rests on the reliability of signs and chapter 83 on their failure, and Parimelazhagar's note on 824 calls the face the instrument of reading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:824","b":"k:826","type":"tension","note":"824 fears hostility that does not show. 826 says an enemy's fine words are soon seen through. The chapter holds two views on whether concealment can succeed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:279","type":"same_image","note":"279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute is kind, so judge by deed. 827 says the bent bow means harm. The two verses reach for the same geometry to make the same point, that shape doesn't reveal intent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:828","b":"k:71","type":"tension","note":"71 says love can't be locked in, and that tears publish it. 828 says an enemy's tears may hide a weapon. The evidence that the Book of Virtue trusts absolutely is, in the Book of Wealth, a cover.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:34","type":"tension","note":"34 says all virtue comes to a stainless mind and the rest is noise. 829 teaches outward friendship with inward death. Parimelazhagar accepts the conflict openly and resolves it as a rule for enemies only, which is a substantive claim about how the books of the Kural relate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:786","b":"k:830","type":"develops","note":"786 says friendship of the face alone is not friendship. 830 tells the reader to use just that kind on a suspect enemy (முகம் நட்டு). What is a failure in one chapter becomes a technique in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:835","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A precise inversion using the same ஒருமை and எழுமை. In 398 learning gained in one birth protects for seven. In 835 folly done in one birth drowns for seven. One life's leverage on all the lives to come works in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:823","type":"develops","note":"823 says study doesn't reform the hostile. 834 turns the same point on the student and teacher himself: he is the worst fool. Learning fails as moral change in both, first in the other and then in oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:839","b":"k:782","type":"develops","note":"782 says a fool's friendship wanes like the moon. Parimelazhagar uses that waning to explain 839's joke: because it has been shrinking every day, by the time of parting nothing is left to hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:841","b":"k:752","type":"tension","note":"Two reports of what the world does. 752 says everyone despises those with nothing. 841 says the world doesn't count lack of wealth as lack. Both can't be plain observation, so at least one of them is the Kural speaking for how the world ought to judge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:841","b":"k:1041","type":"tension","note":"Same doubled form (இன்மையுள் இன்மை, இன்மையின் இன்மை). 1041 says the only thing as painful as poverty is poverty. 841 gives the title to lack of understanding. The chapter on poverty and this one rank the worst lack differently.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:850","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"850 makes denying the world's consensus demonic. 423 says wisdom is finding the truth in anything, whoever says it. Parimelazhagar's doctrinal list sharpens this, and Kalaignar's 'evidence' moves 850 closer to 423.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:788","type":"same_image","note":"Both are about the garment and the shame of exposure. In 788 a friend's help comes as fast as the hand to a slipping cloth. In 846 the cloth over the body is exposed as a fraud if faults stay bare. One makes the reflex an ideal and the other mocks it as a substitute.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:852","b":"k:314","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both tell the injured not to return harm, but their reasons differ. In 314 the wrongdoer is shamed by kindness. In 852, on Parimelazhagar's reading, one's own position is improved. The same act is justified in moral terms in one book and in terms of advantage in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:852","type":"tension","note":"829 tells one to answer hidden contempt with hidden death. 852 says not to return harm even to one who plans division. They are three chapters apart in the same stretch on friends and foes, and the Kural offers both as sound counsel.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:771","type":"tension","note":"Two kinds of invincibility, each put as 'don't attempt it'. 771's warrior warns that those who stood before his lord now stand as stones. 855's person is unbeatable because he leans away. One's strength is in striking and the other's in declining.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"784 says friendship isn't for laughter (நகுதல்). 860 makes நகல் the very source of good ways, which Parimelazhagar glosses as friendship. The same root gets very different valuations in the chapter on what friendship is for and the chapter on hostility.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:861","b":"k:250","type":"tension","note":"The same two words, வலியார் and மெலியார், with opposite advice. 250 says that when going against the weak, remember how you stand before the strong, which is a brake. 861 tells a ruler to go against the weak with appetite. The Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth are at their furthest apart here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:861","b":"k:871","type":"tension","note":"Two adjacent chapter openings. 861 tells one to keep enmity with the weak. 871 says enmity shouldn't be desired even in jest. Parimelazhagar's way through is that 871 is about unworthy enmity (மாணாத பகை), but the verses don't draw this distinction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:867","b":"k:794","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same phrase, கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும். 794 says to pay for the friendship of a person of honour. 867 says to pay for the enmity of a fool. The Kural prices enemies and friends in the same way, and both are investments.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:830","type":"tension","note":"874 says the world rests on the one who turns enemies into friends. 830 says that when an enemy turns friend, give only your face and drop even that later. If this is the same event seen from both sides, then each party in 874's triumph has reason to be following 830.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:879","b":"k:852","type":"tension","note":"852 and its chapter say it is best not to return harm and to lean away from hostility. 879 says to kill the threat while it is young. One counsels letting enmity die by itself and the other warns that it won't, and both claim the outcome is self-preservation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:880","b":"k:763","type":"same_image","note":"In 763 the rats die when the cobra merely breathes (உயிர்ப்ப). In 880 those who don't break a foe's pride don't survive his breath (on Manakkudavar's reading). The word for breathing is the unit of how little it takes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:877","b":"k:1200","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both warn against telling pain to those not attuned to it. In 877 it is a matter of strategy and self-respect, and in 1200 the lover scolds her own heart for telling sorrow to one who doesn't care. The advice carries across books, and it is much sadder in the second one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:881","type":"qualifies","note":"In 735 the land should be free from உட்பகை, which Parimelazhagar glosses as robbers, slanderers and even wild animals. In 881 the same commentator takes the word as kin. The term shrinks from a realm's internal troubles to a family's, and it hurts more in the smaller frame.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a tiny seed against a huge effect. In 104 a millet-seed favour counts as large as a palmyra to those who understand it, and in 889 a sesame-seed enmity holds complete ruin. Small things turn out to be large in the Kural in both moral directions.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:891","b":"k:781","type":"qualifies","note":"781 says no guard is as hard to get past as friendship. 891 says not slighting the able outranks every safeguard. Parimelazhagar lists friendship among those it outranks, so a long section that praised friendship and forts closes by ranking something above them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:894","b":"k:765","type":"same_image","note":"Death personified, twice. In 765 an army stands united when Death comes raging. In 894 the fool waves Death over with his own hand. The difference between courage and folly is not whether one faces Death, but whether one sent the invitation.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:896","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both rank fire below something else: in 129 the burn from fire heals and the burn of the tongue doesn't, and in 896 one may survive the fire but not offence given to the great. Fire is the Kural's standard for what is bad but survivable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:898","b":"k:29","type":"develops","note":"29 calls the great those who have climbed a hill of virtue, whose anger can't be withstood even for a moment. 898 calls them hill-like, and 896-897 in Parimelazhagar's reading have the same instant destruction. Chapter 90 develops what chapter 3 states about the renunciant, in the political book.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:899","b":"k:25","type":"develops","note":"25 calls Indra sufficient witness to the power of the one who controls the senses. Parimelazhagar makes 899's வேந்தன் Indra as well. The Kural itself only names Indra in 25, so the connection depends on his gloss.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:899","type":"tension","note":"864 says one who doesn't let go of anger is easy prey for anyone. 899 makes the anger of the exalted able to bring down a king. Anger is weakness or supreme power depending on who has it, and 306 warns that it burns the one who has it.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:906","b":"k:1103","type":"tension","note":"Both set a wife's or beloved's shoulders against heaven. 906 says that fearing those shoulders leaves no worth even in a godlike life, and 1103 asks whether the lotus-eyed god's world is sweeter than sleep on them. Fear and love differ, but the books value the same intimacy in opposite ways.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"Both redefine manliness away from dominance over others. 148 gives பேராண்மை to the man who doesn't look at another's wife, and 907 finds the opposite failure in a man who takes a woman's orders: in each case ஆண்மை is measured by self-command.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:905","b":"k:428","type":"qualifies","note":"428 calls fearing what should be feared the act of the wise. 905 shows the matching error: the fear here lands on doing good, which ought not be feared.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:1107","type":"qualifies","note":"909 says the man who serves a woman's will loses pleasure with everything else. 1107 compares a beloved's embrace to eating one's own share in one's own home, which suggests that the pleasure 909 says is lost belongs to someone still his own master.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:911","b":"k:91","type":"qualifies","note":"91 defines இன்சொல் as speech soaked in love and free of guile. 911 shows what is left when love is removed: the same sweetness becomes the instrument of harm. So the Kural's praise of sweet speech was never about sweetness alone.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:914","b":"k:241","type":"develops","note":"241 ranks the wealth of grace above material wealth, which even the பூரியார் have. 914 applies the same contrast to choice of company, and 919's பூரியர் are the ones who sink, so these verses share a vocabulary of grace against base wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:916","b":"k:149","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie a man's நலம் to whose shoulders he doesn't touch, using the same verb தோய். 149 says it of another's wife and 916 of women who sell themselves, so the two forbidden categories are treated in one idiom of self-possession.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"Fortune as a goddess who leaves or stays: 617 places her at the feet of the unidle man, and 920 says the ones she has dropped can be known by their company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:926","b":"k:1201","type":"tension","note":"926 says that drinkers are eating poison. 1201 says love is sweeter than liquor because it delights even in memory, taking liquor's delight as the benchmark. The book of love borrows, without judging, the pleasure that this chapter condemns.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:926","b":"k:339","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare sleep to death. 339 makes this a serious claim about mortality, with waking as birth, and 926 only uses it as a pointer to lost awareness. The commonplace serves metaphysics in one and a warning in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:923","b":"k:69","type":"same_image","note":"Both place a mother and the worthy in a single scene. In 69 her joy peaks when her son is called சான்றோன்; in 923 his drunkenness pains her and is unthinkable before those same people. Read together, 923 is 69 undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:929","b":"k:930","type":"qualifies","note":"Other people's reasons can't reach a drunk (929), but his own sober reflection might (930). The chapter separates argument from self-recognition as means of change.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:940","b":"k:345","type":"develops","note":"Under Parimelazhagar's reading of 940 the two are one argument: the soul clings to the body through suffering as a gambler clings through loss, and 345 says that for those ending birth even the body is one attachment too many. 940 is the disease and 345 the cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:936","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"Both use முகடி, the goddess of misfortune. 617 lodges her in idleness and 936 equates her with gambling. Parimelazhagar's remark on 936 that her name is too ill-omened to say makes gambling her acceptable alias.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:931","b":"k:932","type":"develops","note":"The hook's bait in 931 becomes the mechanism in 932: the one small win pulls the hundred losses after it. Parimelazhagar explicitly ties them together with அத்தூண்டிற் பொன்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:968","type":"tension","note":"943 teaches how to carry the body far. 968 asks whether keeping the flesh alive is 'medicine' when dignity has been destroyed. The health chapter treats long life as the aim, and the honour chapter, two away, makes it conditional.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:345","type":"tension","note":"The verses value the same body differently. 943's man has received a body and should carry it far, and 345's renouncer finds it one attachment too many.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:949","b":"k:471","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A physician and a ruler think in the same form: 949 weighs patient, illness and time before acting, and 471 weighs the deed, oneself, the opponent and allies. Both end in the same verb of action (செயல்), and both reject a universal prescription.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:227","type":"same_image","note":"Both use fire for the belly. 947's fire is a measure to observe, and 227's is the disease of hunger that won't touch one who shares food. One is the eater's problem and the other's cure is ethical, which widens what 'not falling ill' means.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:950","b":"k:1102","type":"same_image","note":"950 divides medicine into four roles. 1102 says that other illnesses have other remedies, but the bejewelled woman is herself the remedy for the illness she causes, which collapses the roles of 950 into one.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:951","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"951 says that integrity and shame come naturally only to the well-born. 972 says birth is alike for all beings and distinction arises from deeds. This is the Kural's sharpest internal disagreement on lineage, and Kalaignar's reversal of 951 can be read as an attempt to harmonize it with 972.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:957","b":"k:1117","type":"same_image","note":"957 makes the moon's spot the emblem of a visible fault. 1117 asks whether the woman's face, like the moon, carries a spot, and implies it doesn't. The same mark serves moral exposure in one book and the lover's denial of imperfection in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:959","b":"k:91","type":"develops","note":"In 91 sweet speech comes from a love-soaked and guileless mind. 959 uses speech as a sign of what lies underneath, as the sprout shows the soil. Together they make speech a surface that discloses the source.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:955","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"223 says that giving before a poor man can utter 'I have nothing' is found only in the well-born. 955 takes the giver's own means shrinking, and holds his nature constant. Giving is the chapter's real measure of lineage in both.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:958","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"133 says conduct itself is lineage and lapse is low birth. 958 is less radical, since lapse leads people to doubt a man's birth, which keeps birth as the underlying fact while 133 dissolves it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:969","b":"k:131","type":"develops","note":"131 says that conduct must be guarded above life. 969 and 970 turn that ranking into an actual death, and show what the abstract comparison costs.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:961","b":"k:1028","type":"tension","note":"961 gives up even what is indispensable if it would lower one. 1028 warns that a man who is idle and stands on honour will destroy the family he should be building. Honour is the highest thing in one chapter and a hazard in another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:969","type":"same_image","note":"Hair appears at both ends of a value scale in the same chapter. Fallen hair is the worthless thing that a degraded person becomes, and the yak's one lost hair is what can't be survived. The first image is about how others see you, the second about how you see yourself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:780","type":"agrees","note":"Both look at a death through those left behind. 780's soldier dies so that his patron's eyes fill and such death is worth begging for, and 970's man of honour is worshipped by the world. Honour and the warrior ethic share one structure.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"125 says bowing is good for all and an added wealth for the rich. 963 keeps the second half and reverses the first for the poor: they are told to stand tall, not to bow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:56","type":"develops","note":"56 gives the wife's self-guarding, தற்காத்து, as what makes a woman. 974 makes the same self-holding the condition of greatness in anyone. A gendered virtue is universalized, and the phrasing reaches back across the book of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:973","b":"k:409","type":"agrees","note":"409 uses மேல் and கீழ் of birth, and ranks the unlearned high-born below the learned low-born. 973 uses the same words of position and wealth. Between them, the Kural detaches worth from both axes of hierarchy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:975","b":"k:26","type":"agrees","note":"26, in the chapter on renunciants, defines the great as those who do what is hard. 975 repeats that in the book of wealth, with no renunciation. Parimelazhagar himself cites 26 at 971, so this standard crosses from ascetic to householder.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:976","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"443 calls cherishing the great and making them one's own the rarest of rare things, using the same பேணி. 976 explains why it is rare: the small don't even conceive of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:971","b":"k:595","type":"agrees","note":"595 says a person's height is as great as their will, as a lotus stem rises with the water. 971 calls that will one's wealth and radiance. Both make aspiration the measure of a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:984","type":"agrees","note":"Consecutive chapters end up in the same place: 980 ends the chapter on greatness with covering faults, and 984 makes not telling others' faults the defining quality of the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:986","b":"k:967","type":"tension","note":"Chapters 97 and 99 give different answers to humiliation. 967 prefers being called dead to living behind those who scorn you, and 986 makes accepting defeat even from lesser people the touchstone of worth. The two make most sense as distinct ideals, honour and fullness of character, and not as one code.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:988","b":"k:1044","type":"tension","note":"988 says poverty brings no disgrace to one with worth. 1044 says poverty produces degraded speech even in the well-born. Whether character can hold against poverty is answered one way here and the other in the chapter on poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:990","b":"k:151","type":"develops","note":"151 holds up the earth, bearing those who dig it, as the model of forbearance for people. 990 reverses the dependence: the earth's own bearing relies on the worthy. The model has become the dependent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"Two images of bearing a load form a stack: five qualities are pillars carrying worth, and worth carries what the earth bears. The chapter's architecture rests the world on small daily dispositions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:985","b":"k:225","type":"same_image","note":"The formula ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் recurs. In 225 this strength is enduring hunger, ranked below relieving it, and in 985 it is bowing. The Kural keeps redefining strength as some form of restraint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:987","b":"k:314","type":"agrees","note":"314 says one punishes those who harm by doing them good until they are ashamed, and 987 makes that same return a test of whether worth has any use. 314 gives it a purpose, and 987 makes it a measure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:984","b":"k:261","type":"qualifies","note":"261 defines austerity as enduring pain and not hurting living things. 984 picks out not killing as its core, in order to place not speaking of others' faults at the same rank for worth.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:871","type":"tension","note":"The same three words meet. 871 calls enmity the thing that has no பண்பு, not to be wanted even in jest. 995 says that those who know others' feelings have பண்பு even within enmity. Enmity is by definition devoid of courtesy, and yet courtesy can survive inside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:993","b":"k:1071","type":"develops","note":"993 says that likeness of limbs isn't human likeness, and 1071 gives the unsettling result: the base look exactly like people. The chapter on baseness starts where this verse ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:997","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Both call a person a tree for a moral lack: 997 for lacking human courtesy, 576 for having eyes without kindly regard. Each states a difference between being human and being merely alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:996","b":"k:990","type":"agrees","note":"Consecutive chapters end by making the world depend on a moral elite: if the worthy decline the earth can't carry its load (990), and without the courteous the world sinks into it (996). Two images of ground failing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1000","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both judge wealth by where it lands. In 216 wealth in the hands of the kind is a fruit tree in the village, and in 1000 wealth in the discourteous spoils in its vessel. The next chapter's 1008 completes the set with a poisonous tree.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:992","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"972 denies that birth settles distinction, and 992, two chapters later, places birth among courtesy's two sources. Parimelazhagar's gloss, which turns birth into conduct, can be seen as a way of closing the gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:991","b":"k:386","type":"parallel_domain","note":"386 says that the land extols a king easy to see and not harsh in speech. 991 gives ordinary people the same recipe. Accessibility is a virtue at every scale in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:999","b":"k:786","type":"develops","note":"786 distinguishes the laugh of the face from the laugh of the heart in friendship. Parimelazhagar takes 999's laughter as inner joy in others' company, so a failure of that capacity darkens the world and not merely a friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1008","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"An exact counter-image. 216: wealth in the hands of the kind is a useful tree fruiting in the village. 1008: wealth in the hands of the unwanted is a poison tree fruiting mid-town. Same setting, same act of fruiting, and the holder's character decides whether it feeds or kills.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1001","b":"k:214","type":"agrees","note":"214 places one who doesn't do what's fitting 'among the dead', and 1001 calls one who won't use his wealth 'dead'. Both use death as a civil status, assigned for failure to circulate wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1010","b":"k:211","type":"same_image","note":"211 says that duty seeks no return, as with rain. 1010 extends the identification of generous wealth with rain: even their failures are like clouds running dry. The comparison is a consistent one in the Kural.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1005","b":"k:954","type":"same_image","note":"அடுக்கிய கோடி recurs. For the well-born in 954, stacked crores cannot buy a lowering act. For the miser in 1005, they amount to nothing. The same sum measures character in one and its absence in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1003","b":"k:570","type":"qualifies","note":"570 says that a harsh sceptre over the unlearned is the only burden on the earth. 1003 uses the same phrase for hoarders. The same claim to being earth's one burden is made against a tyrant and against a hoarder.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1004","b":"k:233","type":"agrees","note":"233 says nothing is imperishable but high fame, and 1004 asks what the unwanted hoarder imagines will remain. Parimelazhagar's note supplies the link: fame rests only on the giver.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1017","b":"k:1251","type":"tension","note":"1017 says the shame-bearing would give up life before shame. 1251 says that love's axe breaks the door barred by shame. The same supreme barrier falls in the book of love, and the whole book of love depends on that collapse.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1017","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"A nearly identical ranking is made for two virtues. 969 gives up life for மானம் and 1017 for நாண். Kalaignar's gloss of 1017 even uses மானம். The verses suggest honour and shame are the outward and inward faces of a single conviction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1014","b":"k:1089","type":"same_image","note":"Shame is ornament in both. 1014 calls it the ornament of the worthy, and 1089 asks what other ornaments a woman with shame could need. The same figure crosses gender and books.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"315 asks what good knowledge is if one doesn't treat another's pain as one's own. 1015 makes the same move for disgrace: treating another's blame as one's own. Both make moral identification the test of the higher quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1012","b":"k:972","type":"develops","note":"Both first state what is common to all life, and then find சிறப்பு. For 972 that is deeds, and for 1012 it is shame. Read together, the Kural's account of human distinction becomes specific.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1013","b":"k:73","type":"same_image","note":"The soul's hold on the body is the simile for virtue's bond in both: in 73 love's tie is like that of life to bone, and in 1013 worth holds on to shame in the same way.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1020","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Wood is used twice for those without inner quality. The courtesy chapter's man is a tree in 997, and the shameless man here a wooden puppet. The puppet is worse, since it has been shaped to look alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:924","type":"same_image","note":"Personified moral forces flee from the faulty. In 924, Shame the good woman turns her back on the drunk, and in 1018 Virtue herself blushes and departs from one who doesn't blush. Shame is first the one who flees and then the condition for virtue's staying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:380","type":"tension","note":"The same words appear with opposite force. In 380 fate comes first (தான்முந் துறும்), defeating whatever one devises (சூழினும்). In 1023 the divine comes first (தான்முந் துறும்) for the one who resolves, and 1024 adds that the end comes without deliberating (சூழாமல்). Priority over human planning works against effort in one and for it in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:619","type":"develops","note":"619 says that effort pays even when the divine doesn't help. 1023 takes up the other case: effort draws the divine to help. Together they cover both options and in each effort wins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1024","b":"k:467","type":"tension","note":"467 says deliberate and then resolve, and that deliberating afterwards is a fault. 1024 says that for those who work without delay for their people, the outcome comes without deliberating. The two can be reconciled only by treating family work as a special case.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:1025","b":"k:525","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the same play on சுற்றம் and சுற்றுதல். In 525 giving and sweet speech surround one with layers of kin, and 1025 extends this until the whole world gathers around as kin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1026","b":"k:148","type":"develops","note":"148's பேராண்மை is not looking at another's wife, and 1026's நல்லாண்மை is ruling one's house. The Kural keeps coining compound forms of manliness that exclude combat, as Parimelazhagar notes here.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1028","b":"k:481","type":"qualifies","note":"481 says that a king needs the right time, like a crow beating an owl by day. Parimelazhagar explicitly cites 481 at 1028 to mark the exception: one who raises his family needs no season. Timing is wisdom for conquest but an excuse for duty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:980","type":"agrees","note":"Under Manakkudavar's reading of 1029 the two use the same verb, மறைத்தல்: greatness hides others' weakness, and so does the family's support. He bears the cost in his body.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:627","type":"same_image","note":"Both see the body as a place for suffering to land: a target in 627 and a vessel in 1029. For 627 this insight is what frees one from distress, and for 1029 the suffering is chosen on others' behalf.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:151","type":"develops","note":"The same earth responds in two ways. In 151 it supports even those who dig into it, the model of patience. In 1040 it laughs at those who won't dig. Being dug is what it tolerates and even wants, so the virtue chapter's image becomes a joke on the idle.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:924","type":"same_image","note":"'X என்னும் நல்லாள்' is a shared formula. Shame the good woman turns her back on the drunk, and Land the good woman laughs at the idle. Both personified women judge men.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1330","type":"same_image","note":"ஊடல் is love's sweet sulk in 1330, delightful when embrace follows. 1039 borrows the word to warn an absent owner: the land, like a spouse, wants presence, and here the sulk costs him the harvest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1036","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 makes the householder the support of renouncers, and 1036 sets the farmer a level below, as the support of renouncing itself. The chain of dependence runs lower than the book of virtue showed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1033","b":"k:1065","type":"agrees","note":"1065 says even thin gruel earned by one's own effort is the sweetest thing. 1033 makes that a social claim: only those who eat from their own ploughing truly live. The chapters on begging supply this chapter's value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1035","b":"k:1051","type":"qualifies","note":"1051 tells one to ask of those worth asking and makes refusal their fault, not yours. 1035 presents a person who never needs the permission, and who is on the giving end of that same act.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1046","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"423 says the mark of understanding is seeing the truth whoever says it. 1046 reports that a poor man's meaning falls flat however true it is. One gives the ideal, and the other the way the world listens.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1044","b":"k:959","type":"tension","note":"959 says that speech reveals lineage as a sprout reveals soil. 1044 shows the signal failing: poverty brings degraded speech out of the well-born. Speech is a sign of poverty as well as lineage, so the test is less reliable than chapter 96 claims.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1043","b":"k:368","type":"agrees","note":"368 says there is no suffering for those without desire. 1043 makes poverty itself into desire, which Parimelazhagar glosses as: without craving there's no poverty. An ascetic insight enters the chapter on economic misery, while the rest of the chapter denies it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1050","b":"k:378","type":"develops","note":"378 says that the destitute would renounce if what they must suffer would pass. 1050, with the same துப்புரவு இல்லார், tells them to do it. The verse of fate explains why it doesn't happen, while this verse only mocks them for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1042","b":"k:247","type":"qualifies","note":"247 keeps two forms of lack apart: no grace loses the next world, no wealth loses this one. 1042 collapses them: poverty takes both worlds. Grace alone turns out not to secure the next world.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1042","b":"k:168","type":"same_image","note":"'X என ஒரு பாவி' appears in both. Envy is the sinner in 168 and poverty in 1042, with both sending one down. Parimelazhagar links them himself. The state of being poor gets the same grammar as a vice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1047","b":"k:923","type":"same_image","note":"The mother marks a moral limit twice. Her pain at drunkenness (923) still has love in it, because she's the most forgiving audience, and poverty does what drink couldn't by making her look at her son as a stranger.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1051","b":"k:1061","type":"tension","note":"Consecutive chapters open in direct opposition. 1051: ask if you find those worth asking. 1061: not asking, even of those who give gladly and are as dear as eyes, is ten million times better. Parimelazhagar's solution makes 1051 apply only at the edge of death; the verses themselves don't state this.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1058","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"The wooden puppet (மரப்பாவை) marks a lack of real life in both. In 1020 it's a person without shame, and in 1058 a world without askers. Each chapter finds what makes movement into life, one in an inner quality and one in a social transaction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1059","b":"k:232","type":"agrees","note":"232 says that all that speakers praise rests on those who give to askers. 1059 makes the dependence explicit: the fame needs the asker as well. The asker turns out to be necessary for a fame that seemed the giver's alone.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1054","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"Asking from the right person is like giving (1054), yet asking even water for a cow is the most degrading thing the tongue can do (1066). The chapters give opposite values to one act, and the difference of giver doesn't fully explain it, since 1061 refuses even the best givers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:963","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar himself cites 963 here: the poor must stand tall, and asking without speaking lets them do so. The honour chapter's demand shapes how begging can be done at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1062","b":"k:377","type":"tension","note":"377 says that even those with crores enjoy only as the apportioner apportioned. 1062 curses that very apportioner, if the portion includes begging. The chapter on fate accepts the apportioning, and this chapter refuses it, and Parimelazhagar's gloss makes the two nearly the same figure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1065","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Gruel is sweeter than ambrosia twice. 64: when one's child's little hand has stirred it. 1065, in Parimelazhagar's reading: when one's own effort earned it. The two supply the Kural's two sources of value in a poor meal, love and independence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"A flimsy vessel breaks up in both. 1134's raft of shame and good manliness is swept away by the flood of love. 1068's boat of asking is split by the rock of concealment. Each shows a failed crossing, but the vessel in 1134 is what one is proud of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1064","b":"k:971","type":"agrees","note":"971 calls one's radiance the wealth of aspiration, and 1064 gives its most extreme form. Without means to live, the refusal itself is too large for the world.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1080","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"Two responses to the same crisis mark the moral range of the Book of Wealth. When honour or distress comes, those like the yak give up life (969), and the base sell theirs for a price, in haste (1080). The book ends on the second.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:1051","type":"qualifies","note":"A scale of giving. In Parimelazhagar's reading of 1051 the worthiest give before anything is said, in 1078 the worthy give when told, and the base give when crushed. 1077 adds that the base give nothing to any who can't hurt them.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1076","b":"k:587","type":"parallel_domain","note":"A spy in 587 hears the hidden and knows it without doubt, and in 1076 the base also hear it, but proclaim it. Trading in secrets is a skill or a vice according to whether one can hold them.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:368","type":"qualifies","note":"368 says there's no suffering for those without desire, the ascetic's freedom from anguish. 1072's base have that freedom, by not knowing what is good. The verse exposes a counterfeit of equanimity: peace of mind can't itself be the measure.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1018","type":"tension","note":"1018 sees shame as the condition of virtue, with virtue itself ashamed to approach the shameless. 1075 is a kind of counter-case: the base still have conduct, with fear and desire in place of shame. Their conduct looks alike and differs in its source.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1071","b":"k:1073","type":"develops","note":"The same satirical move rises in rank: they're like men, and then they're like gods. What makes them like the gods, acting as they please, is what makes them not like men.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:774","type":"tension","note":"The warrior of 774 pulls a spear from his own body and laughs. The warrior of 1088 has that same reputation and is broken by a forehead. The Kural's courage has no defence against this enemy.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1089","b":"k:1014","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1014 calls modesty the ornament of the great, and without it their proud bearing is a disease. 1089 uses the same idea for a girl's beauty, so the ethical chapter's metaphor returns as a lover's literal claim that jewels are redundant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1090","b":"k:1281","type":"same_image","note":"Both set love above liquor for delighting through sight. 1090 says it at the first encounter and 1281 says it deep in the relationship, so the comparison holds from the start of love through its maturity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1097","b":"k:709","type":"qualifies","note":"Both chapters are named குறிப்பறிதல. In 709, a skilled reader of eyes can tell enmity from friendship in a court. 1097 warns that a lover's eyes can put on enmity while friendly, so the statecraft skill has to work harder here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1091","b":"k:1102","type":"same_image","note":"In 1091 one look wounds and another heals. In 1102 the woman herself is both the disease and its medicine. The paradox grows from a glance to the whole person once they have united.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1101","b":"k:343","type":"tension","note":"343 tells the renunciant to destroy the pull of the five senses and give up all desire at once. 1101 celebrates the same five senses meeting their fill in a single person. The Kural gives each its place in different books without reconciling them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1104","b":"k:1159","type":"same_image","note":"In 1104 he marvels at a fire that burns at a distance and cools up close. In 1159, a voice in separation says ordinary fire can't burn at a distance at all. The same paradox goes from a delighted question to a complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1107","b":"k:1065","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1065 says even thin gruel earned by your own labour is the sweetest food, for the sake of self-respect. 1107 borrows that dignity of one's own table to describe an embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"1109 lists the quarrel as love's reward, and the Kural's last verse, 1330, ends the whole work on the same idea. Parimelazhagar's scornful reading of 1109 is hard to square with the fact that 1330 and the final chapters celebrate the sequence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:396","type":"parallel_domain","note":"396 says knowledge wells up in proportion to study, like water in a sand well. 1110 takes the same unlimited knowledge as the measure of desire, except that in love more of it reveals a lack and not a supply.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1117","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"957 uses the moon's spot to say the flaws of the high-born show as conspicuously as a mark on the moon. 1117 uses the same spot in praise of an unblemished face. In one, a mark reveals a failing; in the other, its absence is the point.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1114","b":"k:1094","type":"same_image","note":"The lily in 1114 would bow and look at the ground, as the woman herself does in 1094. The lily is imagined acting out the shame of being outdone, in a gesture she makes for love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1119","b":"k:1112","type":"develops","note":"Both disqualify a comparison because it is பலர் காணும், seen by many. What starts as a heart scolded about flowers becomes an instruction to the moon, showing how his praise rests on exclusivity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1122","b":"k:338","type":"tension","note":"Both use the same phrase for the friendship of body and life. In 338 it is like a bird flying from an abandoned shell, an image of how easily it ends. In 1122 it is the lover's image for an unbreakable bond. The thing he swears by is what the book on renunciation calls fragile, and in 1124 he seems to know it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1125","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"He says 'I don't know forgetting' with a clever smile. In 1207 the woman uses the same words, மறப்பறியேன், and adds that even remembering burns her. The same claim of constant memory is banter for one and torment for the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1147","type":"develops","note":"Here the town's talk is a misunderstanding she can rebut. In 1147, the talk has become manure for the love itself. Her stance towards gossip shifts from defence to using it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1132","b":"k:1017","type":"tension","note":"1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and never give up shame for life. In 1132, the lover sets shame aside to save his body and life. It reverses the ethic exactly, in a book of its own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"For the man, love's flood carries off the raft he had. For the woman in 1164, there is a sea of love and no raft at all. Her situation is worse than his: she has neither the raft nor his palmyra horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1137","b":"k:54","type":"qualifies","note":"54 asks what has greater worth than a woman, provided she has the firmness of chastity, using the same words, பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்க. 1137 gives the worth a specific content: enduring a sea of desire without a public remedy. The praise comes from a man who is announcing that he won't do the same.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:183","type":"tension","note":"183 says that it is better to die than to live by backbiting, because it is a false kind of life. In 1141 backbiting is what keeps someone else alive. The same act has a very different moral look from the side of its target.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1145","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"930 asks whether a sober drunkard, seeing another drunk, doesn't recognise his own degradation. 1145 takes the same compulsion, wanting more with each intoxication, and uses it with delight. The book that condemns drink reuses its logic to describe love without condemning it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1147","b":"k:1093","type":"same_image","note":"In 1093, the water poured into love's plot is her lowered glance. In 1147 the water is her mother's harsh words. What nourishes love moves from the most private gesture to the most hostile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1153","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"510 warns that trusting without examination and doubting after trust both bring endless grief, for those choosing men to work with. 1153 is that same predicament without any such procedure: there is no way to test the one she has already trusted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1154","b":"k:1149","type":"same_image","note":"Both quote 'fear not' against him. In 1149 his failure removes her shame before the town. In 1154 it raises the question of fault. One promise is doing a lot of work in her argument.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1152","b":"k:1179","type":"develops","note":"Fear of parting spoils union in 1152. By 1179 it is routine: her eyes can't sleep when he is away or when he is home. Anticipation has become a permanent condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1161","b":"k:396","type":"same_image","note":"Both use water that rises as you draw it. In 396, the well is knowledge, and more study brings more. In 1161, the well is pain, and every attempt to bail it out adds more. The image of an inexhaustible resource is the same, and it is a blessing in one and a curse in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1163","b":"k:1132","type":"tension","note":"The man sets shame aside to mount the palmyra horse (1132). The woman carries shame and love in perfect balance and can drop neither (1163). The book states this asymmetry directly: he can choose and she can only bear the weight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1134","type":"develops","note":"The man's flood simply takes his raft. The same phrase, காமக் கடும்புனல், in the woman's verse has her still swimming at midnight with no shore. The image remains and the endurance lengthens.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1180","b":"k:1076","type":"same_image","note":"1076 calls base men a beaten drum because they carry secrets to others. 1180 uses the same அறைபறை for her own eyes. An image of contempt for informers becomes an image of her helplessness: her body is what informs on her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1175","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"319 says harm done to others in the morning comes back to you by itself in the afternoon. Manakkudavar's gloss on 1175 uses this same moral logic, with the eyes as offenders. A serious doctrine is applied, playfully, to a woman's own eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 wishes the eyes could travel to him as the heart does. Next, 1171 begins the attack on them. Parimelazhagar explicitly calls 1170 a preparation for this chapter, so what looks like a change of subject is a single thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1130","type":"qualifies","note":"1130 insists he lives in her heart, so he has not gone anywhere. In 1184 her mind and words are full of him, but her skin has its own view. Her body undercuts what chapter 113 argues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1183","b":"k:1133","type":"same_image","note":"Both are inventories. The man in 1133 once had shame and manliness, and now has the palmyra horse, an instrument he can use. The woman in 1183 had grace and shame, and has been left sickness and pallor. Only his new possession is a tool.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1149","type":"develops","note":"In 1149 he has left her and the talk is about her; she can't be ashamed. In 1188 she states the injustice directly: the talk notices the symptom and not who caused it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1192","b":"k:11","type":"parallel_domain","note":"11 says that the rain, sustaining the world, is to be known as ambrosia. 1192 uses the whole opening doctrine for one person's survival. The same dependence that the Kural gives to all life at its start reappears as a lover's condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1196","b":"k:1163","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the carrying pole (கா). In 1163, love and shame hang at the ends of her life, and the pole is breaking. In 1196, love should hang on both people. The first is a struggle within one self, and the second is a fault between two.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1198","b":"k:1156","type":"develops","note":"வன்கண் is the same word in both. In 1156 he is hard because he could speak of leaving. In 1198 she calls herself hard for going on living without a word from him. The accusation has moved from him to her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1207","type":"tension","note":"1202 says thinking of the beloved brings no pain, and 1207 says even thinking burns. If Parimelazhagar is right that a man speaks 1202 and a woman 1207, then the chapter makes a pointed contrast between their experience of memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1209","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"1122 is the man's oath: the bond is like body and life. 1209 quotes the same promise, வேறல்லம், as the thing killing her. In 1124 he had said her going was death to him, and now she is the one dying.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1210","b":"k:1119","type":"tension","note":"In 1119, the man tells the moon not to appear, as no one else should have her beauty. In 1210, the woman begs the moon to stay, since it's the only thing she may share with him. His moon is a rival to exclusive possession, and hers is the only link she has.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1090","type":"develops","note":"1090 says love beats liquor because sight alone is enough. 1201 says memory alone is enough. As he moves away, the comparison holds, and needs less and less of her presence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:81","type":"parallel_domain","note":"81 says the whole point of keeping a home is to tend guests. 1211 applies that duty to a dream, which can be neither fed nor refused. A woman with an empty house is still measuring her situation in terms of the householder's virtue.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1212","b":"k:1162","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading of 1162, sending a message shames her because it implies she's still alive. In 1212, telling him she has survived is just what she wants, provided it's in a dream. The dream permits what her waking shame forbids.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:339","type":"parallel_domain","note":"339 says death is like sleep and birth is like waking from it, as an image of impermanence. 1216 wishes waking away altogether, for the sake of staying with her lover. If the two were read together, her wish would come close to a wish not to be reborn, though nothing in the verse says this.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1220","b":"k:1130","type":"agrees","note":"Both end by accusing this town of not understanding. In 1130 he dwells in her heart, while in 1220 he comes in her dreams. The defence moves on as her grounds grow thinner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:759","type":"tension","note":"759 tells the reader bluntly to make wealth, for nothing cuts an enemy's pride so sharply. 1230 gives the cost of that advice from where it falls: a man whose nature has become wealth, and a woman who is dying of it. The separate books don't reconcile the claims.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1227","b":"k:1135","type":"agrees","note":"In 1135 the man complains of evening's anguish, and Parimelazhagar says love flowers at evening. 1227 makes that a full image in the woman's voice. Evening afflicts both, so longing's timetable isn't gendered, though only his has the palmyra horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1225","b":"k:1152","type":"develops","note":"1152 is the moment when even union was spoiled by the fear of parting, and on the commentators' reading of 1225 it was morning that brought this fear. Now that the feared thing has happened, morning is innocent. Fear has its own hours.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1231","b":"k:1114","type":"tension","note":"In 1114, the man says the lily would bow to the ground for shame before her eyes. In 1231, the friend says her eyes are ashamed before flowers. The same flower and the same shame have changed sides, measuring what absence has done.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1239","b":"k:1108","type":"develops","note":"1108 calls sweet the embrace that even a breeze can't split, using the verb போழ. In 1239 a breeze does split it, with the same verb போழ, and the result is the first sign of her pallor. The ideal of union and its failure are one word apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1238","b":"k:1187","type":"same_image","note":"The same incident in two voices: in 1187 she turned slightly in his arms and pallor came, and in 1238 he loosened his arms and her forehead paled. In her telling it happens to her, and in his it is what he did.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1247","b":"k:1163","type":"develops","note":"In 1163, love and shame hang in balance on the pole of her life, and she describes the strain. In 1247 she gives an ultimatum to drop one. Nothing has changed except that she can no longer carry them, and the next chapter tells us which goes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1249","b":"k:1130","type":"tension","note":"In 1130 she uses 'he lives within me' to refute the town's gossip. In 1249 she uses the same fact against her own heart, to stop it from leaving. The argument that once consoled her now traps her.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1244","b":"k:1177","type":"develops","note":"In 1177 she curses her eyes to weep themselves dry. In 1244 they are eating her, and she wants them gone. The eyes haven't stopped, and her conflict with them has moved from revenge to getting rid of them.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1255","b":"k:966","type":"tension","note":"966 asks what could be gained, neither fame nor heaven, by going after those who despise you. 1255 is a direct reply from inside love: people with this sickness can't know that kind of honour. The ethics chapter on honour isn't refuted, but declared unavailable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1253","b":"k:1203","type":"same_image","note":"The sneeze appears in two contrary ways: in 1203 it threatens and doesn't come, which she reads as him not thinking of her. In 1253 it comes uncontrollably, like her desire. So his love fails to arrive and hers can't be stopped.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1254","b":"k:1138","type":"agrees","note":"The same phrase, மறையிறந்து மன்று படும், in both. 1138 presents it as a general truth about women's love, with Parimelazhagar seeing her own case in it. In 1254 she says it in the first person, having once thought herself an exception.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1259","b":"k:1284","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell the same story in the first person: she went to sulk, and her heart went to join him. Whichever is read first, the second confirms that this is a repeating pattern, and that she knows it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1270","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"1151 tells him to bring promises of quick return to those who will still be alive. 1270, in Parimelazhagar's reading, has the man finally arriving at that thought himself, from the other end of the journey and perhaps too late. The warning was given at the start of the separation, and he feels it only at the end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:591","type":"tension","note":"591 says ஊக்கம், inner resolve, is the only true possession. In 1263, the woman names a very similar quality in உள்ளம் as his companion in leaving her. The virtue the Book of Wealth values most is what she has been left for.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1268","b":"k:1224","type":"tension","note":"If Parimelazhagar's speaker is correct, evening is a coming feast for the man in 1268, and for the woman in 1224 it comes like a killer to the execution ground. They are thinking about the same hour, far apart, and with opposite expectations.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1267","b":"k:1259","type":"develops","note":"1267 is a plan: sulk, embrace or both. 1259 records how such a plan works out: she goes to sulk, and her heart gives way. The planner in 1267 hasn't learned anything from the experience, or she has and enjoys planning anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1120","type":"tension","note":"In Parimelazhagar's reading, 1120 is the man refusing to elope because her feet can't bear even swan's down. In 1279 she looks at those same feet, her way of saying she will walk. The compliment had been an excuse, and her glance answers it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1273","b":"k:706","type":"same_image","note":"706 says the face shows what is in the heart, as crystal shows what is next to it, in the chapter on reading signs in court. 1273 uses crystal the same way, with a thread inside it. Courtier and lover both depend on the transparency of the surface.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1276","b":"k:1152","type":"agrees","note":"In 1152 the fear of parting spoils union, and Parimelazhagar cites a poem where unusual fussing is the warning sign. 1276 makes that the very argument: too much tenderness is proof of a departure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1277","b":"k:1157","type":"same_image","note":"Both have bangles announcing the departure of the lord of the shore. In 1157 they announce it to others, and in 1277 they tell her, before she herself knows. The sign has moved inward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:104","type":"same_image","note":"104 says that those who know value a favour the size of a millet seed as if it were a palmyra. 1282 uses the same pair for love and sulking. The scale of ethical gratitude is used for measuring love's excess, and this time the small thing is to be removed altogether.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1283","b":"k:1178","type":"agrees","note":"Both state that he doesn't truly cherish her (பேணாது) and that the eyes can't rest without seeing him (காணாது அமை-). In 1178 this is a complaint on her eyes' folly, and in 1283 it's her reason for not quarrelling. The same fact has moved from grief to an explanation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:1127","type":"same_image","note":"In 1127 she won't line her eyes because he's within them and would be hidden. In 1285 the stick is what the eye can't see. In the absence, kohl was an obstacle. In his presence, he himself is like the stick, too close to be seen.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1289","b":"k:90","type":"parallel_domain","note":"90 says that a guest wilts at a cold look as an anicham flower wilts when smelled. 1289 makes love softer still than a flower. In both, what is finest about a relationship fails at a slight change in the other person's face.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1290","b":"k:1259","type":"same_image","note":"1259 tells it in her voice: I went to sulk, my heart gave way. 1290 tells it in his: she sulked with her eyes and came faster than I did. Each tells the other's defeat as a private pleasure, though for her it's defeat, and for him it's a fond memory.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:796","type":"parallel_domain","note":"796 says ruin has one benefit: it's a measuring rod for friends. 1293 applies this proverb to one's own heart, which she treats as a friend who proves false. The inner dialogue has absorbed a whole social ethic of friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:1244","type":"same_image","note":"In 1244 her eyes eat her alive with longing, and in 1296 her heart does. The same verb is used for what are, in effect, her own organs, and these chapters keep treating her as a person being consumed by her parts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"1247 demands that the heart give up either love or shame. 1297 tells us what happened: shame got forgotten. The chapter between them, on breaking restraint, is how.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:1130","type":"tension","note":"ஏதிலர், the 'stranger', has been her word for him in the town's mouth, in 1129 and 1130. In 1300 the same word is used for a stranger, and now the one who isn't one's own is one's own heart. By the end, the outsider is inside.","grade":"forced","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1302","b":"k:1328","type":"same_image","note":"உப்பு is the word in both. In 1302 the salt is the sulk, measured carefully. In 1328 the salt is the sweetness of the union that follows, as Parimelazhagar glosses it. The cook's image runs through both chapters, with the same word for the seasoning and the taste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1306","b":"k:1191","type":"tension","note":"1191 calls requited love the stoneless fruit, the ideal of enjoyment with nothing in the way. 1306 says love without quarrels is fruit gone bad or not yet ripe. So the obstacle that 1191 wishes away, 1306 says is what makes the fruit ripe.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:366","type":"parallel_domain","note":"366 warns the renunciant to fear craving (அவா), because it deceives. In 1310, the sulking man names the same craving as what drives his heart to her. He uses the moral vocabulary correctly, with no intention of following it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1317","b":"k:1203","type":"tension","note":"The same folk belief at two points in the story. In 1203 she longs for a sneeze as proof he's thinking of her. In 1317 he sneezes in front of her, and the same belief proves someone else is thinking of him. A sign that means what one fears can't be falsified.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1316","b":"k:1125","type":"develops","note":"In 1125, the man is clever at the friend's expense: I would remember her if I forgot. 1316 shows the woman turning this logic on him: you say you remembered, so you forgot. His own wit has become the trap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:1262","type":"parallel_domain","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading of 1262, she keeps him in mind at death so as to reach him in the next birth. In 1315, 'this birth' is what makes her cry. Rebirth, for the renunciant a fate to escape, is in this book something lovers want to share.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1113","type":"tension","note":"In 1113, he went over her body part by part, comparing it to shoots, pearls and spears, and she was pleased to be praised. In 1320 she treats that very habit of comparing part by part as evidence that he has others in mind. The praise of chapter 112 is the root of the suspicion.","grade":"wrong","better":null},{"a":"k:1323","b":"k:1103","type":"develops","note":"1103 asks whether the Lotus-eyed one's world is sweeter than sleeping on the arms of one's beloved. 1323 repeats the challenge to heaven with a stranger choice: it's the quarrel that outdoes heaven now. Between these two verses, love has come to value its own frictions most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1327","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"In 1088, a warrior laments his strength being broken by her forehead, and takes it as defeat. In 1327, the man has worked out that losing is the way to win. The book's military imagery, which begins with injury, ends in a pleasantly ruined scoreboard.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1169","type":"tension","note":"In 1169, the woman in separation says the long nights are crueller than the cruel man himself. In 1329, the man in reunion prays for night to lengthen. The same long night is torment or wish, depending on whether the other person is there.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:942","type":"parallel_domain","note":"942 says the body needs no medicine if one eats only after the last meal has digested. 1326 takes the same physician's advice and makes it a rule of love: the pause between meals is what keeps appetite and health.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:1258","type":"agrees","note":"Both say soft words are the weapon that breaks her defences. In 1258 she says it with bitterness and calls him a thief, and in 1324 she says it with something like eagerness. The weapon hasn't changed, and what she wants from it has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1","b":"k:543","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 1 says the world has ஆதி பகவன் as its first (முதற்றே உலகு), as letters have அ. Verse 543 uses the same word for the king's sceptre: it stands as the ஆதி of the antaṇar's books and of aram (அறத்திற்கும் ஆதியாய் நின்றது மன்னவன் கோல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as their cause (காரணம்). The pairing shows the book giving the world one 'first' in its opening and giving aram itself a second, political one in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 2: what has learning yielded, if one does not bow at the good feet (நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்)? Verse 315: does understanding achieve anything (அறிவினான் ஆகுவதுண்டோ) if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own? Both are rhetorical questions that judge knowledge by a test outside knowledge, worship in one and compassion in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:2","b":"k:356","type":"develops","note":"Verse 2 asks what use learning is (கற்றதனால் ஆய பயன் என்கொல்) if the learned do not worship the feet of the one of pure knowledge. Verse 356 states the fruit positively: those who learn here and see the true thing (கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார்) reach the path of not returning. Parimelazhagar reads verse 2's fruit as ending rebirth too, so 356 spells out what verse 2 leaves as a question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:3","b":"k:6","type":"same_image","note":"Verses 3 and 6 end on the same words, நீடுவாழ்வார், 'will live long'. Verse 3 gives this to those who reach the feet (மாணடி சேர்ந்தார்); verse 6 gives it to those who stand in the path of conduct free of falsehood (பொய்தீர் ஒழுக்க நெறி நின்றார்) of the one who quenched the five. The repeated ending puts devotion and conduct side by side as routes to the same result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:350","type":"develops","note":"Verse 4 describes the one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet have no affliction. Verse 350 turns the same description into an instruction: hold to the hold of the one without attachment (பற்றற்றான் பற்றினை), in order to let attachment go (பற்று விடற்கு). Parimelazhagar notes that பற்றற்றான் is chosen to match the invocation chapter; 350 supplies the mechanism verse 4 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:362","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the word வேண்டாமை but use it differently. In verse 4 it is paired with வேண்டுதல் as something the Lord lacks, which Parimelazhagar glosses as aversion (வெறுத்தல்). In 362 வேண்டாமை is desirelessness, the thing to want (வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்) so that birthlessness comes. A reader should not carry one sense into the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:4","b":"k:628","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 4 says there is no இடும்பை at any time for those who reach the feet of the one free of liking and aversion. Verse 628, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, says one who does not desire pleasure and calls இடும்பை natural (இன்பம் விழையான் இடும்பை இயல்பென்பான்) does not come to pain. The Book 2 verse keeps the link between absent desire and absent suffering but drops the devotional route, and it concedes that the affliction itself still arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:38","type":"tension","note":"Verse 5 says both kinds of deed, which belong to darkness (இருள்சேர் இருவினையும்), do not attach to those who dwell on the Lord's praise; Parimelazhagar explains that good deeds too are a cause of birth. Verse 38 says that doing good (நன்றாற்றின்) with no day lost is the stone that blocks the road of one's days, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read as ending rebirth. On those readings one verse counts good deeds among what binds and the other makes them what frees.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:352","type":"develops","note":"Verse 5 ties the two kinds of deed to இருள், which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar gloss as delusion. Verse 352 says that for those of stainless sight who have left confusion (மருள்நீங்கி மாசறு காட்சியவர்க்கு), darkness goes and joy comes (இருள்நீங்கி இன்பம் பயக்கும்). The later verse gives knowledge as what removes the darkness that verse 5 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:5","b":"k:388","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 5 speaks of the praise of இறைவன், the chapter's word for the Lord. Verse 388 says a king who does justice and protects is set down as இறை for the people (மக்கட்கு இறையென்று வைக்கப்படும்). The shared word shows that the title used for the deity in chapter 1 is one a human ruler earns by conduct in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:6","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"Verse 6 promises long life to those who stand in the path of the one who quenched the five. Verse 126 gives the human practice and its reward: if one can draw in the five like a tortoise in one birth (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்), it is a protection for seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Verse 126 says அடக்கல், holding in, where verse 6 says அவித்தான், quenched, so the follower's task is stated more modestly than the exemplar's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:7","b":"k:622","type":"tension","note":"Verse 7 says it is hard to remove the mind's anxiety (மனக்கவலை மாற்றல் அரிது) except for those who reach the feet of the one without equal. Verse 622 says a flood of affliction perishes when a person of understanding thinks in his own mind (அறிவுடையான் உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்). The conflict holds if மனக்கவலை is ordinary mental distress, as Manakkudavar's gloss allows; Parimelazhagar narrows it to the sufferings of rebirth, which 622 does not address.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:7","b":"k:1072","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 7 makes freedom from mental anxiety rare, reserved for those who reach the Lord's feet. Verse 1072 says, with irony, that the base are better off than those who know the good, because they have no distress in their hearts (நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்). Read beside verse 7 it marks that mere absence of worry is not the state the invocation is praising, since the base have it too.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:30","type":"develops","note":"Verse 8 calls the deity அறவாழி அந்தணன், joining அந்தணன் to aram. Verse 30 defines the word: those called அந்தணர் are the people of aram (அறவோர்), because they act with kindness toward every living being (செந்தண்மை பூண்டொழுகலான்). Verse 30 gives the reason for the pairing of title and virtue that verse 8 assumes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:989","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 8 uses ஆழி for the deity (அறவாழி), read as 'sea of aram' by Parimelazhagar, who also records the reading 'wheel of aram'. Verse 989 calls the worthy சான்றாண்மைக்கு ஆழி and says they do not shift even if the age shifts; there Parimelazhagar reads ஆழி as the shore of the sea of worth. The same word is attached to a virtue in both verses, to a god in one and to people in the other, and its sense is disputed in both.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:8","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 8 says the other seas are hard to swim (பிறவாழி நீந்தல் அரிது) except for those who reach the feet of the antaṇan who is a sea of aram; Parimelazhagar takes the other seas to be wealth and pleasure and the feet to be the raft. In 1164 the woman says the sea of kāmam is there and the safe raft to swim it is not (காமக் கடல் ... அதுநீந்தும் ஏமப் புணை ... இல்). Book 3 shows from inside one of the seas that Parimelazhagar finds in verse 8, with the raft named and missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:6","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 9 treats a sense organ that does not grasp (கோளில் பொறி) as the type of uselessness, while verse 6 praises the one who quenched the five of the sense-gates (பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான்). Parimelazhagar reads what is quenched in verse 6 as the five desires that travel by the senses, not the senses. Taken together the two verses of the chapter support that reading: a dead sense is a defect, and what is praised is something else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:418","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 9 says a head that does not bow at the feet of the one of eight qualities is without worth, like a sense organ that does not grasp (கோளில் பொறியின் குணமிலவே). Verse 418 says ears not pierced by listening are of a deaf kind even though they hear (கேட்பினும் கேளாத் தகையவே). Both measure a part of the body by one proper use and call it dead without that use; the use is worship in one and learning by ear in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:9","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 9 compares the unbowing head to an organ that is present and does not sense. Verse 574 asks what eyes without kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be in the face (உளபோல் முகத்து எவன் செய்யும்). The figure of the organ that is there and useless is applied in Book 2 to a ruler's compassion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:8","type":"same_image","note":"Verses 8 and 10 both speak of swimming a sea with the Lord's feet as the condition. Verse 10 names the sea as birth (பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல்); verse 8 says only 'the other seas' (பிறவாழி), which Parimelazhagar takes as wealth and pleasure. On his reading the chapter has two different seas; without it, verse 8 can look like a first statement of verse 10.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:349","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 10 makes reaching the Lord's feet the condition for crossing birth: those who do not reach them do not swim it (நீந்தார் இறைவனடி சேராதார்). Verse 349 gives a condition with no deity in it: the moment attachment is cut, that cuts birth (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும்). The next verse, 350, joins the two by telling one to hold to the one without attachment in order to drop attachment, so 349 shows what reaching the feet has to accomplish.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:10","b":"k:1167","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 10 says those who reach the Lord's feet swim the great sea of birth and others do not. In 1167 the woman says she swims the fierce flood of kāmam and sees no shore (நீந்திக் கரைகாணேன்), alone even at midnight. The swimmer who cannot cross, stated as doctrine in verse 10, is spoken in the first person in Book 3.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:31","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 31 says aram yields wealth and asks what greater gain a living being has. Verse 169 admits the opposite case occurs: the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are to be thought about (நினைக்கப்படும்). Parimelazhagar says such outcomes cannot come from crookedness or uprightness and sends the wise to look for a cause in past deeds; either way 169 limits how far verse 31 can be read as a promise visible in one life. His gloss on 31 itself takes செல்வம் as heavenly goods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:31","b":"k:754","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 31 says aram yields distinction and yields wealth too (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்). Verse 754 uses the same construction with the terms turned round: wealth that came by known means and without wrong yields aram and yields pleasure too (அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). Book 1 has virtue producing wealth and Book 2 has rightly earned wealth producing virtue, in matching words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:117","type":"develops","note":"Verse 32 says no ruin (கேடு) surpasses forgetting aram. Verse 117 gives the other side: the world will not count as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது) the low state of one who stays fair and rests in the good. Together they define ruin by one's relation to virtue and not by one's circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:32","b":"k:532","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 32 names forgetting (மறத்தல்) of aram as the greatest ruin. In the chapter on heedlessness, 532 says forgetfulness kills renown (பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை) as unrelieved poverty kills understanding. The idea that the damage comes through lapse of attention, without any act of wrongdoing, is carried from ethics into statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"Verse 33 asks for aram everywhere and without a break (ஓவாதே ... எல்லாம் செயல்). Verse 297 says that if one keeps to not lying, then not doing the other virtues is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று). On Parimelazhagar's reading of 297, that it is better to hold one virtue unfailingly than to take on many and fail in some, this is advice to narrow what verse 33 tells one to spread as widely as possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:477","type":"develops","note":"Verse 33 limits its command with ஒல்லும் வகையான், 'in the way that is possible'. Parimelazhagar explains this for the householder as acting in proportion to one's means, and 477 says so directly: give knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து ஈக), for that is the way to guard wealth while giving. Verse 477 gives the practical content of the qualifier in verse 33. This edition prints அறவறிந்து in 477; the commentary reads அளவு அறிந்து.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:33","b":"k:673","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 33 says to do the work of aram by whatever means one can (ஒல்லும் வகையான்), without ceasing, wherever it can go (செல்லும்வாய் எல்லாம் செயல்). Verse 673, on how to carry out a task, uses the same words: where it is possible the deed is good, and where it is not, act looking for a way that goes (ஒல்லும்வாய் ... செல்லும்வாய் நோக்கிச் செயல்). The rule for virtue and the rule for state action are built from the same vocabulary of the possible. This edition prints the first word of 673 as ஙல்லும், a typo for ஒல்லும், as its own commentary shows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:34","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"Verse 34 sets inner cleanness against outward show. Verse 298 makes the same division and adds how the inner kind is known: outer purity is had by water, and inner purity is seen through truthfulness (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). It gives a test for the state that verse 34 only asserts. This edition prints புறள்தூய்மை for புறந்தூய்மை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:35","b":"k:161","type":"develops","note":"Verse 35 lists envy (அழுக்காறு) first among what aram must avoid. Verse 161 turns that item into a positive rule: take as your way of conduct (ஒழுக்காறாக் கொள்க) a nature that has no envy in the heart. The chapter on envy opens by treating as a discipline what verse 35 states as an exclusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:35","b":"k:431","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 35 defines aram by the absence of four faults. Verse 431 defines a sound prosperity the same way for a ruler: the increase of those without arrogance, anger and smallness (செருக்கும் சினமும் சிறுமையும் இல்லார்) has dignity. Anger is on both lists; Parimelazhagar glosses சிறுமை in 431 as lust, which would bring the two lists closer still.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:233","type":"tension","note":"Verse 36 calls aram a companion that does not perish (பொன்றாத் துணை). Verse 233 says that apart from high renown nothing in the world stands unperishing (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்று இல்), using the same word. Read literally the second excludes the first. Parimelazhagar's reading of 36, that the merit goes with the soul into another body, puts it outside 'the world' of 233; Kalaignar reads 36 itself as lasting fame, which removes the conflict another way.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:36","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 36 says aram done now stays as a companion at death. Verse 398 says the learning one has acquired in one birth is a protection through seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Both speak of something gained in this life that is not lost with the body; Book 2 says of learning what Book 1 says of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:270","type":"agrees","note":"On the reading of Parimelazhagar and Pope, verse 37 explains the difference between palanquin rider and bearer by aram done before. Verse 270 reasons the same way about a whole society: the reason the have-nots are many is that those who do tavam are few and those who do not are many. Both read present inequality as the result of past practice; Parimelazhagar adds that wealth and want in 270 also mean understanding and its lack.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:37","b":"k:973","type":"tension","note":"Verse 37 says there is no need to state the way of aram: look between the one who carries the palanquin and the one who rides (பொறுத்தானொடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை). Parimelazhagar and Pope take this to mean that the fruit of virtue can be seen in who rides and who carries. Verse 973 says those who are not high are not high even when seated high (மேலிருந்தும் மேலல்லார் மேலல்லர்), and Parimelazhagar reads the seating there as wealth and poverty. On those readings one verse lets position show merit and the other denies that position shows anything about the person. The Tamil of 37 is elliptical and does not itself say which of the two is the virtuous one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:38","b":"k:326","type":"tension","note":"Verse 38 says daily good is a stone that shuts the road of one's வாழ்நாள்; Parimelazhagar takes வாழ்நாள் as the days of living in a body, so that shutting the road means no more births. Verse 326 says Death does not advance on the வாழ்நாள் of one who keeps to not killing. On Parimelazhagar's reading the same word names something virtue ends in 38 and something virtue protects in 326. On Kalaignar's reading of 38, where the stone smooths life's road, there is no conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:38","b":"k:334","type":"develops","note":"Verse 38 treats each day as something that can fall away unused or be filled with good. Verse 334 says what a day is: it shows itself as one thing called a day, and it is a blade sawing through life (உயிர் ஈரும் வாள்), for those who perceive it. Verse 334 gives the reason for the daily urgency of verse 38.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:39","b":"k:156","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 39 says only what comes by aram is joy (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்) and the rest lies outside and has no renown either. Verse 156 allows that those who retaliate do get joy, for one day (ஒறுத்தார்க்கு ஒருநாளை இன்பம்), while those who forbear have renown for as long as things last (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்; until the world perishes, in Parimelazhagar's gloss). It keeps verse 39's pairing of joy and renown but concedes a short pleasure outside virtue, so the 'only' of 39 reads as a judgment of worth and not a denial that such pleasure is felt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:280","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 40 names blame (பழி) as the thing to escape. Verse 280 says neither shaving nor growing the hair is needed if one gives up what the world has blamed (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்துவிடின்). Both make avoidance of the blameworthy the working test, verse 280 applying it to the ascetic in place of outward marks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 40 gives a two-sided rule: what one should do is aram, and what one should escape is blame (செயற்பாலது ... அறனே, உயற்பாலது ... பழி). Verse 466 gives the same two sides to a ruler as consequences: one is ruined by doing what is not fit to do, and ruined also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமையானும் கெடும்). Book 2 restates the pair as policy and makes the omission as costly as the wrong act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:40","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"Verse 40 says blame is what a person should escape. Verse 656 tests that rule at its hardest: even if one must see the hunger of the mother who bore one, do not do the deed the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை). It shows that the avoidance of blame in verse 40 is meant to hold against a competing duty, and it names whose blame counts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:242","b":"k:132","type":"same_image","note":"242 ends பல்லாற்றால் தேரினும் அஃதே துணை: however many ways you examine, அருள் alone is the companion. 132 ends with the same words, தேரினும் அஃதே துணை, said of ஒழுக்கம் (conduct). The identical closing with its exclusive ஏ is given to two different things; read strictly the two claims compete, read as emphasis each chapter is crowning its own subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:242","b":"k:324","type":"develops","note":"242 tells one to search by a good path and hold to அருள் (நல்லாற்றான் நாடி அருளாள்க). 324 asks what the good path is (நல்லாறு எனப்படுவது யாதெனின்) and answers: the way that keeps in view not killing anything (யாதொன்றும் கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி). 324 gives content to the நல்லாறு that 242 leaves open, and ties அருள் to not killing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:243","b":"k:5","type":"same_image","note":"243 says those whose hearts are joined with அருள் (அருள்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்) do not enter the painful world joined with darkness (இருள்சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம்). 5 says the two kinds of deed joined with darkness (இருள்சேர் இருவினையும்) do not join (சேரா) those devoted to the Lord's praise. Both build on சேர், what one is joined to: in 5 darkness is a quality of deeds (Parimelazhagar: மயக்கம்), in 243 it is a place one enters, and joining something else keeps it away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:243","b":"k:121","type":"parallel_domain","note":"243: no entry into the dark painful world for hearts with அருள். 121: restraint leads among the immortals, lack of restraint drives into thick darkness (அடங்காமை ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும்). Both use darkness as the destination of a failed virtue, and Parimelazhagar glosses both as a hell; 243 states only the exemption, 121 states both directions.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:268","type":"same_image","note":"244 pairs மன்னுயிர் and தன்னுயிர்: care for other lives frees one's own life from fear. 268 uses the same pair the other way round: one who has fully gained his own life (தன்னுயிர் தான்அறப் பெற்றானை) is worshipped by all other lives (ஏனைய மன்னுயிரெல்லாம் தொழும்). Together they show the relation running in both directions, from care of others to one's own safety and from self-mastery to others' reverence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"244: one who tends living beings and holds to அருள் (மன்னுயிர் ஓம்பி அருளாள்வாற்கு) has no deed his own life need fear (தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை இல்). 326: on the days of one who lives by not killing, Death that eats life does not advance (வாழ்நாள்மேல் செல்லாது உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று). Both say that sparing other lives secures one's own, once in terms of deeds and once in terms of Death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:244","b":"k:327","type":"qualifies","note":"244 promises that the one who protects lives has nothing his own life need fear. 327 uses the same word, தன்னுயிர், to command not taking another's life even if one's own life goes (தன்னுயிர் நீப்பினும் செய்யற்க). 327 shows that the safety promised in 244 is not the condition for the rule: the rule holds even where one's own life is lost.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:245","b":"k:25","type":"same_image","note":"245 calls the wide earth where the wind moves a witness (ஞாலம் கரி) that those who hold to அருள் have no distress. 25 calls Indra witness enough (இந்திரனே சாலுங் கரி) to the power of one who quenched the five senses. Both close an unprovable claim about a virtue's power by naming a கரி, a witness; 245 appeals to the whole visible world, 25 to one figure from story.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:245","b":"k:169","type":"tension","note":"245 says flatly that those who hold to அருள் have no distress (அல்லல் அருளாள்வார்க்கு இல்லை), and Parimelazhagar takes this to cover this life. 169 says the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடும்) are to be pondered, which concedes that the upright do meet ruin. The conflict holds if அல்லல் includes worldly ruin and if 169 is read as admitting such cases occur; Parimelazhagar softens 169 with 'if they occur' (உளவாயின்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:246","b":"k:32","type":"agrees","note":"246 says those who leave அருள் and go on doing wrong have, they say, parted from பொருள் and forgotten (பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார்). 32 says there is no ruin greater than forgetting virtue (அதனை மறத்தலின் ஊங்கில்லை கேடு). Both treat wrongdoing as a kind of forgetting; Parimelazhagar reads the பொருள் of 246 as the lasting good, i.e. virtue, which brings the two closer, while Kalaignar reads it as being without wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:246","b":"k:250","type":"develops","note":"246 describes the cruel as people who have forgotten (பொச்சாந்தார்); Parimelazhagar says what they forgot is their own suffering. 250 prescribes an act of memory: when going against the weaker, think of yourself before the stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Within the chapter, 250 supplies the remedy for the forgetting that 246 diagnoses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:247","b":"k:248","type":"qualifies","note":"247 puts wealth and அருள் in parallel (பொருளில்லார்க்கு இவ்வுலகம் இல்லாகி யாங்கு). 248 then breaks the parallel: those without wealth may flower one day (பூப்பர் ஒருகால்), but those without அருள் are finished and can hardly become anything again (அற்றார் மற்றாதல் அரிது). The simile of 247 holds for what each lack costs, not for whether the loss can be repaired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:248","b":"k:134","type":"parallel_domain","note":"248: loss of wealth can be reversed, loss of அருள் cannot (அற்றார் மற்றாதல் அரிது). 134: even if forgotten, the recited text can be learned again (மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும்), but a Brahmin's birth is ruined when conduct declines (பிறப்பொழுக்கங் குன்றக் கெடும்). Both set a recoverable possession against an unrecoverable loss of character, in the domains of wealth and of learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:249","b":"k:355","type":"same_image","note":"249 compares virtue done by one without அருள் to an unclear person seeing the true thing (தெருளாதான் மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டற்று). 355 defines understanding by the same phrase: seeing the true thing in whatever thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு). 355 shows why the comparison in 249 bites: seeing the true thing is what clarity consists in, so without clarity it does not happen, or happens only once by chance as Parimelazhagar reads it (ஒருகால்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:250","b":"k:316","type":"develops","note":"316 gives the general rule: what one has oneself felt to be painful one must not do to another (இன்னா எனத்தான் உணர்ந்தவை ... பிறன்கண் செயல்). 250 applies the same use of one's own experience to a difference in strength: recall your own position before the stronger when you stand over the weaker. 250 is the rule of 316 turned into a specific exercise of memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:321","b":"k:254","type":"develops","note":"321 asks what virtuous action is and answers not killing (அறவினை யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை); killing brings all other deeds. 254 uses the same question form for அருள்: what is அருள் and what is not comes down to not killing and killing (யாதெனின் கொல்லாமை கோறல்). Read together, not killing is the content of both அருள் and virtuous action, which joins the chapter on அருள் to the chapter on not killing. (The first letter of 254 is missing in this edition: ருளல்லது for அருளல்லது.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:321","b":"k:297","type":"tension","note":"321 makes not killing the answer to what virtuous action is. 297 says that if one keeps up not lying, it is good not to do other virtues (பொய்யாமை ஆற்றின் அறம்பிற செய்யாமை நன்று). Each verse lets one virtue stand for the whole, and they pick different ones; the conflict holds if both are read as claims of sufficiency and not as chapter emphasis. 323 settles the order inside this chapter by putting not lying second.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:322","b":"k:225","type":"develops","note":"322 puts feeding and sustaining lives at the head of the virtues. 225 says the strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that comes behind the strength of those who remove hunger (அப்பசியை மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்); Parimelazhagar takes the first group to be ascetics. 225 supplies a ranking that fits 322: not harming is completed by actively keeping others alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:323","b":"k:292","type":"develops","note":"323 ranks not lying behind not killing. 292 says even falsehood stands in the place of truth if it yields good free of fault (புரைதீர்ந்த நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்), and Parimelazhagar's example is a false word that saves a life from death. 292 shows the ranking of 323 at work inside the chapter on truth: where truthful speech and a life pull apart, the life decides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:324","b":"k:291","type":"same_image","note":"324: if asked what is called the good path, it is the way that keeps in view not killing anything (யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும் கொல்லாமை சூழும் நெறி). 291: if asked what is called truth, it is speech with no harm at all in it (யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). The two definitions share their wording, எனப்படுவது யாதெனின் யாதொன்றும், and both define a positive term by the complete absence of harm, in action and in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:325","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"325 says that among all who renounced fearing their condition (நிலைஅஞ்சி நீத்தாருள்), the one who fears killing and keeps not killing in view is chief. 261 defines the form of தவம் as bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை). 261 states the two parts of the ascetic's practice; 325 says which part decides rank among ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:325","b":"k:984","type":"agrees","note":"325 ranks the renouncer who keeps to not killing first. 984 says austerity lies in the goodness of not killing (கொல்லா நலத்தது நோன்மை), and sets it beside worth, which lies in not speaking others' faults. The same claim about renouncers appears in the third part of the book of wealth, used there as the measure for a layman's virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:656","type":"parallel_domain","note":"327: even at the cost of your own life, do not do the deed that takes a life. 656: even if you must see your mother hungry, do not do the deed the worthy condemn (ஈன்றாள் பசிகாண்பான் ஆயினும் செய்யற்க). Both have the form 'even at this extreme cost, செய்யற்க', and both name a வினை as what is forbidden; the first is addressed to the virtuous life, the second to a minister's conduct of affairs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:327","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"327 puts another's life above one's own. 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் உயிர்ப்பொருட்டால் நாண்துறவார்). In both, one's own life is not the highest value; what outranks it is another creature's life in 327 and one's own sense of shame in 1017.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:311","type":"agrees","note":"328 refuses gain that comes by killing, however great. 311 says that even if one could get wealth that brings distinction (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும்), not doing hurt to others is the settled stand of the stainless. The two adjacent chapters make the same refusal for the lesser harm and the greatest one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:328","b":"k:657","type":"parallel_domain","note":"328: for the worthy (சான்றோர்க்கு), gain got by killing is the lowest (கடை). 657: the utter poverty of the worthy is above gain got with blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கத்தின் சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவே தலை). Both judge ஆக்கம் by its source from the standpoint of the சான்றோர், with கடை in one and தலை in the other; 328 belongs to the book of virtue and 657 to the conduct of state business.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:329","b":"k:551","type":"develops","note":"329 treats killers as the settled example of baseness. 551 takes that example as a measure and says a king who takes up harassing his people and does wrong is crueller than those who have taken up killing (கொலைமேற்கொண்டாரிற் கொடிதே). The book of statecraft uses the low point fixed in 329 to rank a bad ruler below it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:37","type":"parallel_domain","note":"330 infers a past deed from a present condition: the wretched are those who took life. 37 says there is no need to say 'this is the way of virtue': look between the one who carries the palanquin and the one who rides (சிவிகை பொறுத்தானோடு ஊர்ந்தான் இடை). On Pope's and Parimelazhagar's reading both argue from visible fortune back to deeds, 37 for good fortune and 330 for bad; Kalaignar reads 37 differently, as about how the virtuous bear life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:270","type":"parallel_domain","note":"330 explains diseased and failing lives by past killing. 270 explains why the have-nots are many: those who practise austerity are few and those who do not are many (நோற்பார் சிலர் பலர் நோலாதவர்). Both give a cause in conduct for an unequal condition seen in the world, 330 for individuals and 270 for the proportion in a population.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:330","b":"k:326","type":"develops","note":"330 says those with afflicted bodies and a failing, evil life (செயிர் உடம்பின் செல்லாத்தீ வாழ்க்கையவர்) are, they say, people who separated life from body. 326 says Death does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. The two verses are the chapter's pair of consequences, both stated in terms of body and lifespan: the non-killer's days are spared and the killer's body and living are spoiled. Parimelazhagar places the killing of 330 in a former birth; the Tamil does not say which birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:59","type":"same_image","note":"381 calls the king who has all six limbs (படை, குடி, கூழ், அமைச்சு, நட்பு, அரண்) 'அரசருள் ஏறு', the male beast among kings; 59 says a man whose home lacks a wife who seeks good name has no 'ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை' before those who scorn him. The same figure of the proud male animal is made to depend on what a man has behind him: a kingdom's six supports in one case, a household's good name in the other. (Parimelazhagar takes ஏறு in 59 as a lion; the word itself only says 'male'.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:740","type":"qualifies","note":"381 measures a king by possession (ஆறும் உடையான்), and குடி, which Parimelazhagar takes as the land, is one of the six. 740 says a land that has every excellence is still without use (பயமின்றே) if it lacks accord with its ruler (வேந்தமைவு இல்லாத நாடு). Read together, having the limb is not enough: the limb and the king must fit each other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:381","b":"k:750","type":"qualifies","note":"381 counts the fort (அரண்) among the six things whose possession makes the leading king. 750 says a fort with every excellence (எனைமாட்சித்து ஆகியக் கண்ணும்) is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). The later verse adds the condition that 381's bare 'உடையான்' leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:382","b":"k:863","type":"agrees","note":"382 lists fearlessness, giving, understanding and drive (அஞ்சாமை ஈகை அறிவு ஊக்கம்) as a king's nature. 863 describes the man who is easy prey for an enemy: he fears, does not know, does not fit with others, does not give (அஞ்சும் அறியான் அமைவிலன் ஈகலான்). Three of the four kingly qualities reappear negated, so the weak enemy of chapter 87 is drawn as the reverse of the king of chapter 39.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:605","type":"agrees","note":"383 gives three things that stay with a ruler, beginning with தூங்காமை. 605 gives four that are the pleasure-boat of those bound for ruin: delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep (நெடுநீர் மறவி மடி துயில்). The two lists are near-opposites, and 605 covers both senses the commentators give தூங்காமை in 383 (not delaying, not being slothful).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:383","b":"k:668","type":"develops","note":"383 names promptness and resolve (தூங்காமை, துணிவுடைமை) as two separate standing qualities, with learning between them. 668 turns them into one instruction for a single task: once a deed has been seen clearly and decided (கலங்காது கண்ட வினை), do it without wavering and with delay driven off (தூக்கம் கடிந்து செயல்). The trait list of the king becomes a sequence: decide, then act at once.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:384","b":"k:766","type":"parallel_domain","note":"384 pairs மறன் and மானம் in the king. 766 opens its list of the four safeguards of an army with the same pair (மறம் மானம்), followed by keeping to the proven path and trustworthiness. The same two qualities are required at the top and in the ranks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:384","b":"k:773","type":"develops","note":"384 asks for honour that does not fall short of valour. 773 says fierceness (தறுகண்) is called great manliness, but helping the foe when something befalls him (ஒன்று உற்றக்கால் ஊராண்மை) is its edge (எஃகு). Parimelazhagar explains 384's phrase with examples of not striking the unequal or the fleeing, which is the restraint 773 states directly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:385","b":"k:377","type":"same_image","note":"385 ends the king's four skills with வகுத்தல், apportioning what has been guarded. 377 uses the same verb for what lies outside human skill: even those who have piled up crores can enjoy them only in the way the apportioner has apportioned (வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகை), which Parimelazhagar takes as fate. The pairing sets the king's apportioning inside a larger one he does not control.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:385","b":"k:756","type":"develops","note":"385 says the king must create ways for wealth to come (இயற்றல்) without naming them. 756 names three that count as the king's wealth: what falls in unowned, customs dues, and what is taken from defeated enemies (உறுபொருள், உல்கு பொருள், ஒன்னார்த் தெறுபொருள்). Parimelazhagar's note on 385 gives a similar list of sources.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:386","b":"k:566","type":"agrees","note":"386 says that if a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech (கடுஞ்சொல்லன் அல்லனேல்), his land is spoken of above others. 566 uses the same word for the reverse case: if he is கடுஞ்சொல்லன் and without kindly regard, his long-standing wealth perishes on the spot. One trait is given both its reward and its penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:92","type":"develops","note":"92 ranks the two: being pleasant-spoken with a glad face is better than giving with a glad heart (ஈதலின் நன்றே). 387 does not rank them but joins them, giving 'with' pleasant words (இன்சொலால் ஈத்து), and adds protection. The king's version needs both, where the householder's verse lets speech stand above the gift.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:525","type":"parallel_domain","note":"387 says that for one able to give with pleasant words and protect (இன்சொலால் ஈத்து அளிக்க), the world answers to his word. 525 says one who can give and speak pleasantly (கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின்) will be surrounded by layers of kin. The same two means work at two scales: they hold a family around a man and the world around a king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:387","b":"k:648","type":"agrees","note":"387's result clause is that the world becomes as the king says and intends (தன் சொலால் தான் கண்டனைத்து); Manakkudavar reads this as the world lying under his command. 648 says the world quickly takes up the work it is told (விரைந்து தொழில் கேட்கும் ஞாலம்) when it finds those who can speak in order and sweetly. Both make pleasant speech the cause of the world's compliance; 387 adds giving and protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:547","type":"develops","note":"388: the king who does முறை and protects is counted as இறை. 547 uses the same two words in a loop: the இறை guards the whole earth, and முறை guards him if he carries it out without fail (அவனை முறை காக்கும்). Justice is what earns the title in 388 and what keeps its holder safe in 547.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:388","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"388 has the just king set apart as இறை; 702 says one who knows without doubt what is in another's mind should be regarded as equal to a god (தெய்வத்தோடு ஒப்பக் கொளல்). Parimelazhagar glosses both with the same phrase, 'though a man by birth'. The book grants this standing for a function performed, justice in one case and insight in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:160","type":"parallel_domain","note":"389 praises a king who endures (பொறுக்கும்) bitter words. 160 says those who fast in penance are great, but rank after those who endure the harsh words others speak (இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்பார்). Bearing hard speech is rated as the higher discipline for the ascetic and as the quality that holds a kingdom for the king.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:447","type":"develops","note":"389 says the world rests under the parasol of a king who can bear words bitter to the ear (செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும்). 447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his support people who rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையாரை ஆள்வாரை). Parimelazhagar reads 389's bitter words as those of exactly such rebuking companions, so 447 gives the institution and 389 the temperament it needs. Manakkudavar reads 389 differently, as hearing of others' offences and bearing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:389","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"389 states the king's side: he should bear speech that is bitter to hear. 638 states the minister's side: even if the king destroys good counsel and knows nothing himself, the one at his side is bound to say what is sound (உறுதி உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்). The duty to speak does not wait on the king having 389's quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:390","b":"k:556","type":"same_image","note":"390 calls the one who has giving, kindness, a straight sceptre and care of the people 'வேந்தர்க்கு ஒளி', a light to kings. 556 uses the same word: kings endure through just rule, and without it 'மன்னர்க்கு ஒளி' does not endure. 390 makes செங்கோல் one of four sources of that light; 556 makes it the one without which the light goes out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:390","b":"k:557","type":"develops","note":"390 lists அளி, kindly care, among the four things a king must have. 557 says what its absence is like: as lack of rain is to the earth, so is the king's lack of அளி to the living (துளியின்மை ... வேந்தன் அளியின்மை). An item in a list becomes, in the chapter on the crooked sceptre, something people cannot live without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:561","type":"develops","note":"541 gives the steps of justice: examine, favour no one, deliberate, act (ஓர்ந்து ... தேர்ந்து செய்வஃதே முறை). 561 defines the king by the same sequence and adds two things: the punishment must match the offence (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது) and aim at the wrong not recurring (தலைச்செல்லா வண்ணத்தால்). Chapter 57 thus supplies the measure and purpose that 541 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:541","b":"k:577","type":"tension","note":"541 makes the refusal of கண்ணோட்டம் part of the definition of முறை. 577 says those without கண்ணோட்டம் have no eyes, and those who have eyes are never without it. If 577 is read without exception, the judge of 541 is 'eyeless' in the moment of judging; the conflict disappears only if 577 is limited to dealings outside judgment, as 578 suggests.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:557","type":"same_image","note":"542: the whole world lives looking to the sky, and the people live looking to the king's sceptre (வானோக்கி ... கோல் நோக்கி). 557 states the same comparison from the side of failure: what lack of rain is to the earth, the king's lack of kindly care is to living beings. The pair shows the rain comparison is about dependence, so that a failed king is a drought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:542","b":"k:1192","type":"parallel_domain","note":"542 compares subjects' dependence on the sceptre to the world's dependence on rain. In 1192 the woman says the tenderness the loved one gives to the one who loves is like the sky yielding to those who live by it (வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்றால்). The same figure of living by the rain is used for a people before their king and a lover before the beloved, and the word அளி serves for both the king's care (557, 390) and the lover's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:543","b":"k:19","type":"parallel_domain","note":"543 makes the sceptre the precondition of scripture and virtue. 19 says that giving and austerity will not last in the wide world if the sky does not give (தானம் தவம் இரண்டும் தங்கா ... வானம் வழங்காதெனின்). Rain in Book 1 and just rule in Book 2 hold the same position: neither is itself virtue, but virtue does not continue without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:544","b":"k:387","type":"agrees","note":"544 says the world holds to the king who embraces his people. Parimelazhagar explains the embrace as speaking pleasantly and giving what is needed in hardship, which is what 387 names: to one able to give with pleasant words and protect (இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க), this world answers. 387 says how the embrace is done; 544 adds that it goes together with the sceptre.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:544","b":"k:1025","type":"parallel_domain","note":"544: the world stands embracing the feet (அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும்) of the king who embraces his people (குடிதழீஇ) while wielding the sceptre. 1025: the world gathers round as kin (சுற்றமாச் சுற்றும் உலகு) the man who raises his family and lives without fault. In both the world's attachment is the return for care of one's own குடி, the word meaning the king's subjects in 544 and a man's family in 1025.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:545","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"545 ties rain to the king's right rule. 55 ties it to a wife's fidelity: she who rises worshipping her husband says 'rain' and it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). In the household and in the kingdom the book makes rain answer to a person keeping to their proper role.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:545","b":"k:239","type":"parallel_domain","note":"545 says harvest (விளையுள்) comes with just rule. 239 says land that bears a body without renown declines in its blameless rich yield (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). Both let the moral state of people show in the fertility of the land; 239 does it for any person, 545 for the king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:546","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"546's condition is that the கோல் does not bend (கோடாது). 118 praises not leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை), like the கோல், here the balance beam, that first levels itself and then weighs. The same word and the same verb serve for the king's sceptre and the scales, so the straightness asked of the ruler is the impartiality asked of the worthy in Book 1.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:547","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"547: the king guards the earth, and justice guards the king (இறை காக்கும் ... முறை காக்கும்). 57: what can the guard of confinement do? The guard that women keep by their own steadfastness is the chief guard (சிறை காக்கும் காப்பு ... நிறை காக்கும் காப்பே தலை). Both verses, in the same rhyming pattern, say that real protection comes from the guarded person's own conduct and not from outside force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:547","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"547 says justice protects the king if he does it without fail. 448 calls the king who has no one to rebuke him 'ஏமரா மன்னன்', a king without a guard, and says he perishes even with no one to ruin him. Together they name what guards a ruler: his own just practice and the people who correct him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:386","type":"agrees","note":"548: a king who is not easy of access (எண்பதத்தான் in the negative, as Parimelazhagar construes it) and does not examine and do justice sinks and perishes. 386: a king easy to see (காட்சிக்கு எளியன்) and not harsh in speech has his land praised. Accessibility is stated as a virtue in chapter 39 and its lack as a cause of ruin in chapter 55.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:448","type":"agrees","note":"548 says the unjust, unapproachable king perishes of himself (தானே கெடும்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even without enemies'. 448 says the king with no one to rebuke him perishes though no one sets out to ruin him (கெடுப்பார் இலானும் கெடும்). Both describe a ruler's fall with no outside agent, caused by shutting out complaint in one case and criticism in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:548","b":"k:553","type":"agrees","note":"548 and 553 share most of a line: a king who does not examine and do justice (ஓரா / நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்). In 548 the king himself perishes; in 553 he loses his land day by day (நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). The pairing shows the same neglect ruining the ruler and the country.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:314","type":"qualifies","note":"314 tells a person how to punish those who hurt him: shame them by doing them good and let it go (அவர் நாண நன்னயம் செய்து விடல்). 549 says that for a king, guarding the people and removing offences (குற்றம் கடிதல்) is no stain but his work (வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்). The words 'no stain' answer the doubt that punishing goes against virtue, which Parimelazhagar spells out; 549 limits 314's rule to one's own injuries and exempts the office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:549","b":"k:436","type":"qualifies","note":"549 makes removing offences the king's work. 436 sets an order for it: if he first removes his own fault and then looks at others' (தன்குற்றம் நீக்கிப் பிறர்குற்றம் காண்கிற்பின்), what fault can attach to a ruler? The right to punish in 549 is, by 436, clear of blame only for a king who has corrected himself first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:550","b":"k:1038","type":"same_image","note":"550 compares the king's execution of the cruel to weeding the green crop (பைங்கூழ் களைகட்டதனொடு நேர்). 1038, on farming itself, says that after weeding (கட்டபின்) guarding the crop is better than watering it. In the farmer's own chapter weeding is one step and guarding counts for more, which fits 549's order of guarding the people first and punishing second.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:631","b":"k:483","type":"develops","note":"483 asks whether anything called அருவினை exists for one who acts with கருவி and knowing காலம். 631 uses the same three words but keeps அருவினை as a separate item the minister must be good at. The pair shows where the minister's list comes from: tools and timing are what make a hard task doable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:631","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"631 defines the minister as one excellent in கருவி, காலம், செய்கை and the அருவினை itself. 675 turns nearly the same list into a rule for anyone acting: பொருள், கருவி, காலம், வினை, இடன், 'ஐந்தும் இருள்தீர எண்ணிச் செயல்'. Read together, the minister's defining competence is the checklist of 675, with resources and place added there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:632","b":"k:382","type":"parallel_domain","note":"632 lists the minister's qualities: வன்கண், குடிகாத்தல், கற்றறிதல், ஆள்வினை. 382 lists the king's: அஞ்சாமை, ஈகை, அறிவு, ஊக்கம் (383 adds கல்வி and துணிவுடைமை). Firmness, knowledge and drive appear on both lists, so the minister is described on the king's pattern; Manakkudavar even glosses வன்கண் as அஞ்சாமை. Giving is on the king's list only.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:187","type":"parallel_domain","note":"187 says those who cannot make friends with pleasant talk 'கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்', split even their own kin, by divisive speech. 633 uses the same verb, பிரித்தல், for a minister's skill, which Parimelazhagar restricts to detaching an enemy's allies. The same act is a fault at home and a craft in statecraft, and what differs is whose bond is being broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:530","type":"develops","note":"633 names rejoining those who parted as a minister's skill. 530 says how the king should handle such a return: one who left (உழைப்பிரிந்து) and came back for a reason is to be taken in after thought, 'எண்ணிக் கொளல்'. The pair shows that பொருத்தல் is not unconditional welcome; 530 supplies the caution.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:633","b":"k:679","type":"develops","note":"633 says a minister must be able to divide (பிரித்தல்), keep his own side (பேணிக் கொளல்) and rejoin those who broke away (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). 679 ranks one such act: 'ஒட்டாரை ஒட்டிக் கொளல்', bringing the unattached over, is more urgent than doing good to friends. 679 gives a priority among the alliance skills that 633 only lists.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:634","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"462 tells the one acting that nothing is out of reach if he examines with a chosen circle and thinks it over himself: 'தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு'. 634 describes the minister with the same two verbs, தெரிதல் and தேர்ந்து செயல். The pairing shows the two sides of one arrangement: 462 is the ruler's need for such company, 634 is what that company must be able to do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:634","b":"k:671","type":"develops","note":"634 asks of a minister தெரிதல், தேர்ந்து செயல் and 'ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல்', speaking so that the matter is settled. 671 says 'சூழ்ச்சி முடிவு துணிவெய்தல்': deliberation ends in reaching a resolve, and letting that resolve linger is a fault. Parimelazhagar glosses ஒருதலையாச் சொல்லல் as speaking so that துணிவு arises, which makes the minister's decisive word the point where 671's deliberation closes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:635","b":"k:441","type":"develops","note":"441 tells the king to choose the friendship of those who are 'அறனறிந்து மூத்த அறிவுடையார்', 'திறனறிந்து தேர்ந்து'. 635 describes the counsel-companion in the same words: 'அறனறிந்து ... திறனறிந்தான் தேர்ச்சித் துணை'. 441 is the instruction to seek such a person and 635 is the portrait of him, with settled speech (ஆன்றமைந்த சொல்) added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:635","b":"k:644","type":"develops","note":"635 asks for one who knows அறன், has full and settled speech, and knows திறன். 644 joins the same three terms in one rule: 'திறனறிந்து சொல்லுக சொல்லை', since no அறன் or பொருள் is beyond that. The next chapter thus takes the speech item of 635 and makes fitted speech itself the place where knowing virtue and knowing method show.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:636","b":"k:503","type":"qualifies","note":"636 implies that nothing withstands the person who has native subtlety and learning. 503 says that even in those who have studied hard texts and are free of fault (அரியகற்று ஆசற்றார்), close examination rarely finds no blankness: 'இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு'. The praise in 636 concerns outwitting opposing schemes; 503 limits it, since the learned still have gaps.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:637","b":"k:426","type":"agrees","note":"426 defines அறிவு as living with the world in the way the world lives: 'உலகத்தோடு அவ்வது உறைவது'. 637 gives the working form of this for one who already knows the method: 'உலகத்து இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்'. Together they treat attention to how the world actually goes as part of knowledge and not a concession against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"638 makes it the duty (கடன்) of the one at the king's side to say what is sound (உறுதி) even if the king rejects knowledge and knows nothing himself. 448 gives the stake: a king without those who rebuke, 'இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்', perishes even with no one to ruin him. 448 shows why 638 does not release the minister when the king will not listen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:696","type":"qualifies","note":"638 obliges the minister to speak sound counsel regardless of the king's reception. 696 governs the manner: knowing the king's mood and considering the time, say what is needed without what is disliked, so that it is wanted ('வெறுப்பில வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்'). The duty of 638 stands, and 696 conditions how and when it is discharged.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:638","b":"k:849","type":"tension","note":"849 says 'காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான்': one who tries to show something to a person who does not see ends up not seeing himself, while the other keeps to what he saw. 638 requires exactly this of a minister when the king 'அறிகொன்று அறியான்'. The conflict holds if 849 is read as general advice not to instruct the conceited; Parimelazhagar reads it as a report that the instructor is reviled, which would leave 638 as a duty carried at that cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:817","type":"same_image","note":"639 and 817 use the same construction and the same kind of figure: 'மந்திரியின் ... எழுபது கோடி உறும்' and 'நட்பின் பகைவரால் பத்தடுத்த கோடி உறும்'. In both an inflated count of open enemies is set against one false intimate, a plotting minister in 639 and a friend good only for laughter in 817. Parimelazhagar reads உறும் in 639 as 'that many enemies will come', and notes others read it as 'that many times better', which is how 817 is read.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:639","b":"k:882","type":"agrees","note":"639 says seventy crore enemies are better than a minister at one's side who plots harm ('பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரி'). 882 gives the general rule: do not fear enemies like swords, fear the bond of enemies who are like kin ('கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு'). Parimelazhagar explains 639 on the same ground, that open enemies can be guarded against and the one inside cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:640","b":"k:664","type":"agrees","note":"640 says those without திறப்பாடு leave things unfinished even after planning them in proper order ('முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர்'). 664 says saying is easy for anyone and doing as said is hard ('சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்'). Both locate failure in the gap between a correct plan and its execution; Parimelazhagar glosses the 'saying' of 664 as stating in order how the task will be done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:640","b":"k:728","type":"parallel_domain","note":"640 says sound planning comes to nothing in those who lack the capacity to carry it through. 728 says those who have learned many things are of no use ('பயமிலரே') if they cannot speak well and tellingly in a good assembly. The same pattern appears in two domains: knowledge or planning is counted worthless without the ability that delivers it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:971","b":"k:600","type":"same_image","note":"971 says 'ஒளி ஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை' and 600 says 'உரம் ஒருவற்கு உள்ள வெறுக்கை': the same phrase, abundance of spirit, is called a person's light in one and a person's strength in the other. Each then states the lack: living without it is disgrace (இளி) in 971, and those without it are trees in 600. The repetition shows the chapter on greatness resting on the earlier chapter on ஊக்கம்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:972","b":"k:133","type":"agrees","note":"133 says having conduct is what good family is ('ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை') and a lapse becomes low birth. 972 says birth is equal and distinction follows from deeds. Both move rank from descent to what a person does, though 133 keeps the vocabulary of birth and Parimelazhagar reads it within varna.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:973","b":"k:751","type":"qualifies","note":"751 says nothing but wealth makes people of no account into people of account ('பொருளல்லவரைப் பொருளாகச் செய்யும்'). 973 says the not-high do not become high by being placed high, which Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read as a seat given by wealth. On that reading 973 limits 751: wealth gets a person regarded, and it does not make them great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"57 says confinement does little and the guard a woman keeps by her own steadfastness is the chief guard ('நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பே தலை'). 974 takes this as its comparison: like single-minded women, greatness exists only 'தன்னைத்தான் கொண்டொழுகின்', if one keeps hold of oneself. 974 carries 57's claim about self-guarding into the Porul chapter on greatness, so that greatness, like கற்பு, cannot be kept for someone from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:974","b":"k:965","type":"develops","note":"974 says greatness exists only as long as one keeps oneself. 965 shows the loss: even those like a hill sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a குன்றி seed. Together they present greatness as a condition that continues only through conduct and can be forfeited by a small act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:975","b":"k:468","type":"develops","note":"975 says the great carry out hard deeds 'ஆற்றின்', in the right way. 468 states the other side with the same word: effort not made in the right way ('ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம்') collapses even if many stand guard over it. The pair shows that ஆற்றின் in 975 carries weight: hard deeds done by the wrong route fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:976","b":"k:451","type":"agrees","note":"451 says greatness fears small company while smallness takes it as kin ('சுற்றமாச் சூழ்ந்து விடும்'). 976 says the small have no thought of attaching themselves to the great. Both describe the same sorting from two ends: the small seek their own kind and do not look upward.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:977","b":"k:838","type":"parallel_domain","note":"977 says even distinction (சிறப்பு), if it falls to the unworthy, results in acts of excess ('இறப்பே புரிந்த தொழிற்று'). 838 says a fool getting something in hand is like a man already deranged getting drunk. Both say a good thing makes its holder worse when the holder is unfit; 838 says it of possessions and a fool, 977 of distinction and the small. Manakkudavar reads the சிறப்பு of 977 as wealth, which brings the two closer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:977","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1000 says great wealth obtained by one without பண்பு is like good milk turned by the fault of its vessel. 977 says distinction landing on the unworthy produces excess. The logic is the same, that the recipient's quality decides what a gift becomes, but 1000 has the wealth spoiled and useless while 977 has the distinction turn into overbearing action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:125","type":"agrees","note":"125 says bowing is good for everyone and for the wealthy it is a further wealth. 978 says greatness bows and smallness decks itself out in self-admiration. Both attach humility to those who have most reason for pride; Parimelazhagar glosses the பணிதல் of 125 as being without பெருமிதம், the word 979 uses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:439","type":"develops","note":"439 commands 'வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை', never admire yourself, among faults a ruler must avoid. 978 uses the same verb to define smallness: 'அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து'. What 439 forbids as a fault, 978 treats as the mark by which the small are known.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:978","b":"k:963","type":"qualifies","note":"978 says greatness bows always ('பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை'). 963 says bowing is needed in abundance and standing tall is needed in reduced circumstances ('சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு'). Taken plainly, 963 limits the என்றும் of 978; Parimelazhagar reads என்றும் in 978 as 'even when distinction has come', which makes the two verses say the same thing about prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:979","b":"k:431","type":"same_image","note":"979 defines greatness as the absence of பெருமிதம் and smallness as riding it to the end. 431 uses the same word with approval: the prosperity of those free of செருக்கு, anger and சிறுமை is 'பெருமித நீர்த்து', which Parimelazhagar glosses as having the quality of eminence. The word means conceit in 979 and dignity in 431, and both verses agree that the person without pride is the one who has real stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:979","b":"k:844","type":"parallel_domain","note":"844 defines வெண்மை, poor understanding, as the pride that says 'we have brightness' ('ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு'). 979 defines smallness as being carried away by பெருமிதம். In both a defect is identified with the claim to its opposite: the foolish are those sure of their intelligence, and the small are those sure of their greatness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:846","type":"same_image","note":"846 and 980 share the words அற்றம், மறை and குற்றம். In 846 covering one's own nakedness is itself poor understanding while one's faults stay uncovered, which Parimelazhagar takes as faults not removed. In 980 greatness covers the அற்றம் of others. The image of covering what is exposed is used for self-deception in one verse and for consideration toward others in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:980","b":"k:1079","type":"agrees","note":"1079 says the base, on merely seeing others clothed and fed, are able to find a blemish in them ('வடுக்காண வற்றாகும் கீழ்'). 980 says smallness tells only faults ('குற்றமே கூறி விடும்'). 1079 adds a cause the Tamil of 980 leaves out: Parimelazhagar reads the fault-finding of the base as coming from being unable to bear others' prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1091","b":"k:15","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1091 says the same eyes hold the look that makes him ill and the look that cures it. 15 says rain is both what ruins ('கெடுப்பதூஉம்') and what, as support to the ruined, lifts them again ('எடுப்பதூஉம்'). In both, one source is credited with the harm and with its remedy, once in love and once in the world's subsistence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1092","b":"k:1090","type":"develops","note":"1090 says brewed liquor gladdens only those who drink it and does not, like love, gladden those who merely see ('கண்டார் மகிழ்செய்தல்'). 1092 puts a measure on that: the small look her eyes steal ('கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்') is not an even half of love but more ('செம்பாகம் அன்று பெரிது'). Together they make sight alone a large share of love's pleasure; Parimelazhagar takes the 'half' in 1092 to be half of physical union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1092","b":"k:1326","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1092 ranks a brief stolen look as more than half of love. 1326 ranks sulking above union ('புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது'), as digesting is sweeter than eating. Both verses place the greater part of love's pleasure in something beside the embrace, one before it and one around it.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1093","b":"k:718","type":"same_image","note":"In 1093 her look followed by a lowered head is 'water she poured' ('அட்டிய நீர்') into the 'யாப்பு', which Parimelazhagar reads as the crop of love binding the two. 718 says speaking before those able to understand is like pouring water into the bed of something already growing ('வளர்வதன் பாத்தியுள் நீர்சொரிந்தற்று'). Both use watering for a sign or word given to someone already receptive: the gesture or speech does not start the growth, it feeds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1096","b":"k:826","type":"same_image","note":"1096: though they speak like those unattached ('உறாஅதவர்போல் சொலினும்'), the words of those without anger are quickly understood ('செறாஅர்சொல் ஒல்லை உணரப்படும்'). 826 has the same frame reversed: though they say good things like friends ('நட்டார்போல் நல்லவை சொல்லினும்'), the words of the unreconciled are quickly understood ('ஒட்டார்சொல் ஒல்லை உணரப்படும்'). The shared wording shows one claim applied in love and in statecraft: a spoken mask, harsh or kind, does not long hide the disposition behind it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1097","b":"k:824","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1097 lists small harsh words spoken without anger and looks like an enemy's ('செற்றார்போல் நோக்கும்') as the signs of those who are close while seeming distant. 824 warns of the opposite pairing: a sweet smile on the face with bitterness inside ('முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா'). In both the surface is the reverse of the heart; the lover reads the mismatch with relief, the Book of Wealth reads it with fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1098","b":"k:1274","type":"agrees","note":"In 1098, when he looks she softens and smiles gently ('பசையினள் பைய நகும்'), and he says there is a certain grace in her then ('உண்டு ஆண்டு ஓர் ஏஎர்'). 1274 says there is something inside the bud of her smile, like scent inside a closed bud ('நகைமொக்குள் உள்ளதொன்று உண்டு'). Both treat her slight smile as holding an unspoken meaning, and both leave that meaning as an unnamed 'something'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1099","b":"k:528","type":"same_image","note":"1099 says looking at each other with the common look, like strangers ('ஏதிலார் போலப் பொதுநோக்கு நோக்குதல்'), is found in lovers. 528 uses the same word for a king: he should not look on all in common ('பொதுநோக்கான்') but by rank, and many will live looking to that. In 528 the undifferentiated look is a failure to distinguish; in 1099 it is put on deliberately by the two people who distinguish each other most.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1100","b":"k:705","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1100: when eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words are of no use at all ('வாய்ச்சொற்கள் என்ன பயனும் இல'). 705, in the Book of Wealth's chapter of the same name, asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read a sign by a sign ('என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்'). Both hold that the eye is the organ for knowing another's mind; 705 says so for court service and 1100 for lovers, where it makes speech redundant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1100","b":"k:1280","type":"develops","note":"1100 says that once the eyes agree, words from the mouth serve no purpose. 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading ('கண்ணினால் காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு') adds womanhood to womanhood. 1280 moves from the fact that eyes can replace speech to praise of the woman who chooses to speak that way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1151","b":"k:1160","type":"develops","note":"1151 tells him to announce his quick return to those who will be alive then ('வாழ்வார்க்கு உரை'), implying she will not be. 1160 says many consent to the hard thing, endure the parting and live on afterwards ('பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்'); Parimelazhagar reads this as her ironic reply to the friend, meaning she is not one of them. The chapter opens and closes on the same word, 'வாழ்வார்', for the survivors she sets herself apart from.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1151","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1151 the speaker (the friend for Parimelazhagar, the woman for Manakkudavar) says a promise of quick return is only for those who will survive. In 1263 the woman, after he has gone, says she is still alive through wanting his return ('வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்'). The later speech shows the claim of 1151 as said in the moment: the hope of return that was dismissed there is what keeps her alive here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1152","b":"k:1295","type":"agrees","note":"1152: once even his look was pleasant, but now union itself holds the pain of fearing separation ('பிரிவஞ்சும் புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு'). 1295: her heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting ('பெறின் பிரிவு அஞ்சும்'), so its misery never ends. Both say that the prospect of parting takes away the pleasure of being together; 1295 states it as the heart's standing condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1153","b":"k:514","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1153: trust is hard ('அரிதரோ தேற்றம்'), because parting occurs at some point even with those who understand ('அறிவுடையார் கண்ணும்'). 514: even when people have been trusted after every kind of test ('எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும்'), many turn different through the nature of the work. Both say that knowing a person's qualities does not secure their future conduct, one about a lover and one about a king's appointee.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1154","b":"k:1209","type":"develops","note":"1154 holds up his words 'fear not' and says the fault, if he leaves, is not the believer's. 1209 holds up another of his sayings, 'we are not separate' ('வேறல்லம் என்பார்'), and says her life is ending from thinking on his lack of grace. 1154 is the argument made before the parting; 1209 is the same contrast of word and conduct felt after it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1155","b":"k:1151","type":"develops","note":"1151 asks him to say he is not going, and leaves talk of return to those who will survive. 1155 turns to the friend: if you would protect, prevent his parting ('பிரிவோம்பல்'), since once he goes union is hard ('நீங்கின் அரிதால் புணர்வு'). Both reject return as a remedy and allow only not-leaving; Parimelazhagar supplies that what is to be protected in 1155 is her life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1156","b":"k:1222","type":"same_image","note":"1156 names him 'வன்கண்ணர்' for announcing his departure. In 1222 she asks the evening, which looks as wretched as she is, whether its partner too is hard like hers ('எம்கேள்போல் வன்கண்ணதோ'). The epithet given conditionally in 1156 has become her settled description of him once he is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1157","b":"k:1235","type":"same_image","note":"In 1157 the slipping bangles broadcast ('தூற்றா') his leaving. In 1235 the shoulders that have lost bangles and their old beauty tell of the cruel one's cruelty ('கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்'). Both make her thinning body a speaker that reports on him without her consent; 1235 adds a judgment of him to the bare news of 1157.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1158","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1158: living in a town without one's own people is painful, and parting from one who is dear is more painful still ('அதனினும் இன்னாது இனியார்ப் பிரிவு'). 839 says, with irony, that the friendship of fools is very sweet because parting brings no pain at all ('பிரிவின்கண் பீழை தருவதொன்று இல்'). Read together, both verses use the pain of parting as the measure of what a bond was worth: none for a fool's friendship, more than exile among strangers for a lover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1160","b":"k:1198","type":"agrees","note":"1160 says many agree to the hard thing, put away their pain, endure the parting and live on ('பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்'); Parimelazhagar reads it as irony against the friend's advice. 1198 says outright that none in the world are harder than those who live on ('வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்') without a kind word from the beloved. Both treat surviving separation as something that needs explaining, not as the expected course.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:11","b":"k:1106","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 11 gives rain the name அமிழ்தம் because the world's life continues through it. In 1106 the lover says her shoulders must be made of amrita (அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன தோள்) because his life puts out new shoots at each touch (உயிர் தளிர்ப்ப). Both verses award the word for the same reason, that the thing keeps life going, once for the whole world and once in a lover's speech about himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:13","b":"k:734","type":"develops","note":"Verse 13 says that if the sky proves false (விண் பொய்ப்பின்), hunger stays and torments the world (உள்நின்று உடற்றும் பசி), sea-girt though it is. Verse 734 defines a land as one that severe hunger (உறுபசி), unending disease and destructive enemies do not reach. Read together, the first mark of a proper country in the Book of Wealth rests on something no ruler supplies, the rain of chapter 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:14","b":"k:1036","type":"develops","note":"Verse 14 says ploughing stops when the rain's bounty declines. Verse 1036 says that if the ploughmen's hands fold (உழவினார் கைம்மடங்கின்), even those who say they have given up desire cannot hold their state. Verse 14 names one cause that would fold those hands, so the failure of rain reaches as far as the renouncer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:15","b":"k:1102","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 15 says it is rain that ruins (கெடுப்பதூஉம்) and rain again that stands by the ruined and lifts them (எடுப்பதூஉம்); Parimelazhagar explains that it ruins by not falling. In 1102 the lover says that for other diseases the medicine is something else, but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து). The pattern of one thing being both the harm and the only remedy appears for rain and the world and, in a lover's speech, for the beloved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:16","b":"k:557","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 16 says that unless drops fall from the sky (விசும்பின் துளிவீழின் அல்லால்) not even the tip of green grass can be seen. Verse 557 says that what lack of drops (துளியின்மை) is to the earth, the king's lack of tender regard (அளியின்மை) is to those who live under him. Verse 16 supplies the picture that 557's comparison assumes: without the king's அளி, the people are the bare ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:17","b":"k:211","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 211 asks what the world does for the rain-cloud in return (மாரிமாட்டு என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ உலகு) and makes it the model of duty that wants no repayment. Verse 17 says even the long sea loses its nature if the cloud, having taken from it (தடிந்து, as Parimelazhagar reads it), does not give back. On that reading the cloud is not a pure giver: it returns what it drew from the sea, and the sea suffers if it withholds. Manakkudavar takes தடிந்து as lightning flashing, and on his reading the qualification falls away.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:17","b":"k:1010","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 17 pictures the cloud not giving (எழிலி தான் நல்காதாகி விடின்) and the sea itself shrinking as a result. Verse 1010 says the brief want of the renowned rich has the nature of the rain-cloud gone dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது). Both use the withholding cloud; 1010 turns it into a figure for a giver's temporary incapacity, with the suggestion that it is as general a loss and as passing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:18","b":"k:560","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 18 says that if the sky dries up (வானம் வறக்குமேல்), festival and worship for the gods will not go on here (சிறப்பொடு பூசனை செல்லாது). Verse 560 says that if the guardian does not guard (காவலன் காவான் எனின்), the cows' yield shrinks and those of the six duties forget their book (நூல் மறப்பர்). Both say religious practice has a material precondition that can fail; chapter 2 names the rain and chapter 56 names the king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:559","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 19 says giving and austerity (தானம் தவம் இரண்டும்) will not stay in the world if the sky does not give. Verse 559 says that if the king acts with justice bent (முறைகோடி), the sky will not pour in season (ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்). Chapter 2 makes virtue depend on rain; 559 makes the rain depend in turn on a human virtue, the ruler's justice, so the dependence runs both ways.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:19","b":"k:1036","type":"develops","note":"Verse 19 says austerity (தவம்) cannot last without rain. Verse 1036 gives the step in between: if the ploughmen's hands fold, those who say they have left desire have no standing (விட்டேம் என்பார்க்கும் நிலை இல்லை). The renouncer's practice rests on the farmer's food, and the farmer's on the sky.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:20","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 20 ends the chapter by saying nothing in the world, for anyone, holds without the sky. Verse 55 says that for the wife who rises worshipping her husband, it rains when she says 'rain' (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). Three chapters after rain is made the condition of everything, the rain is said to answer to one person's steadfastness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:20","b":"k:542","type":"agrees","note":"Verse 20 says no one's world can do without water (நீர்இன்று அமையாது உலகு), and that ஒழுக்கு cannot exist without the sky. Parimelazhagar takes ஒழுக்கு as the water's unbroken flow; Pope's verse rendering and Kalaignar take it as ordered conduct. Verse 542 states the first half as a given (வானோக்கி வாழும் உலகெல்லாம்) and sets the people's dependence on the sceptre beside it. On either reading of ஒழுக்கு, 542 treats the dependence 20 argues for as the standard case of something one cannot live without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:48","type":"tension","note":"Verse 21 says the settled view of the texts (பனுவல் துணிவு) sets the greatness of those who have renounced within their discipline (ஒழுக்கத்து நீத்தார் பெருமை) highest among excellent things. Verse 48 says the household life that keeps others on their path and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). If 48 is read as ranking the householder above the ascetic, as its plain wording allows, the two chapters give the first place to different lives; if 48 is read as praising only the householder's endurance, they do not collide.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:21","b":"k:533","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 21 rests its claim on பனுவல் துணிவு, the settled conclusion of the texts. Verse 533 uses the same appeal: that the heedless have no fame is the conclusion of authors of every school (எப்பால் நூலோர்க்கும் துணிவு). These are two places where the Kural, which usually argues from observation, cites the agreement of the books as its ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:22","b":"k:310","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 22 says that to state the measure of the greatness of renouncers (துறந்தார்) is like counting the dead of the world (இறந்தாரை எண்ணிக்கொண்டற்று). Verse 310 rhymes the same two words: those who exceed in anger (இறந்தார்) are like the dead, and those who have renounced anger are equal to renouncers (துறந்தார் துணை). In 22 the dead supply only a number too large to count; in 310 the same pairing sets renouncers and the dead as opposite ends, and extends the renouncer's standing to anyone who gives up anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:23","b":"k:49","type":"tension","note":"Verse 23 says the greatness of those who have discerned the two (இருமை வகைதெரிந்து) and taken on அறம் here stands highest in the world; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read the two as birth and release and the அறம் as renunciation. Verse 49 says that what is called அறம் is household life (அறன் எனப்பட்டதே இல்வாழ்க்கை). On the commentators' reading of 23 the two verses attach the word அறம், and the highest standing, to different lives. Kalaignar reads 23 as choosing good over bad in general, and on that reading there is no conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:24","b":"k:361","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 24 calls the one who guards the five senses a seed (வித்து) for the ground called வரன், which Parimelazhagar takes as release. Verse 361 calls craving the seed (வித்து) that bears unceasing birth for all beings. The book uses the seed for both outcomes: restraint of the senses is sown toward release, and craving is what sprouts as rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:26","b":"k:664","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 26 divides people by whether they can do what is hard to do. Verse 664 says saying is easy for anyone and doing as one has said is hard (அரியவாம் சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்). The chapter on firmness in action applies the renouncer's standard to statecraft and names what is hard in a deed: carrying it out as announced.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:27","b":"k:354","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 27 says the world lies within the one who discerns the kinds of the five (ஐந்தின் வகைதெரிவான் கட்டே உலகு): taste, light, touch, sound and smell. Verse 354 says that even having gained the fivefold awareness (ஐயுணர்வு எய்தியக் கண்ணும்) is of no benefit to those without perception of the real (மெய்யுணர்வு). Verse 354 sets a limit on 27: command of the five is not the end, and without true perception it yields nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:27","b":"k:1101","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 27 lists the five sense objects (சுவை ஒளி ஊறு ஓசை நாற்றம்) and says the world belongs to the one who discerns them. In 1101 the lover lists the same five as acts, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching (கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும் ஐம்புலனும்), and says they are all found in this one woman (ஒண்தொடி கண்ணே உள). The same inventory serves the renouncer as a field to be understood and the lover as a field of enjoyment; the second is a speech in the moment and not a counter-teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:28","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 28 says the greatness of people whose word is full (நிறைமொழி மாந்தர்) is shown on earth by their மறைமொழி; Parimelazhagar explains நிறைமொழி as speech that produces its result whether spoken in grace or in anger. Verse 55 says that when the wife who worships her husband says 'rain', it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). Both give a person's word power over the world as the proof of their standing, for the renouncer through austerity and for the wife through steadfastness at home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:29","b":"k:310","type":"tension","note":"Verse 29 speaks of the anger (வெகுளி) of those who stand on the hill of good qualities; on Parimelazhagar's reading they are sages whose anger, though it lasts a moment, cannot be warded off by its target. Verse 310 says those who have renounced anger are the equals of renouncers (சினத்தைத் துறந்தார் துறந்தார் துணை), which makes freedom from anger the renouncer's mark. On Parimelazhagar's reading the book's model renouncer still gets angry, and dangerously. Kalaignar reads 29 as saying their anger does not stay even a moment, and on that reading the two verses agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:41","b":"k:263","type":"develops","note":"41 makes the householder the support of the three, read by Parimelazhagar as the other orders, ascetics included. 263 asks, with கொல், whether 'the others' (மற்றையவர்கள்) have forgotten தவம் because they want to provide for the renounced (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி); Parimelazhagar glosses 'the others' as householders. The Thavam chapter thus looks at the same arrangement from the ascetic side: the householder's support of renouncers is given as the reason he does no austerity himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:41","b":"k:1036","type":"parallel_domain","note":"41 calls the householder the துணை who stands on the good path (நல்லாற்றின் நின்ற) for 'the three' (இயல்புடைய மூவர்), whom Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take as the other three orders of life (Kalaignar instead reads parents, spouse and children). 1036 says that if the ploughmen's hands fold (கைம்மடங்கின்), even those who say 'we have let go of desire' (விட்டேம் என்பார்) have no standing (நிலை). On the older commentators' reading both verses make the renouncer's state depend on someone who stays in the world and produces: the householder in Book 1, the farmer in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:42","b":"k:41","type":"develops","note":"41 names the householder the support of 'the three of proper nature' (இயல்புடைய மூவர்); 42 repeats the frame இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை and adds three more: துறந்தார், துவ்வாதவர், இறந்தார். Parimelazhagar takes these as the abandoned, the poor and those who died with no one of their own, so the pair moves from the recognised orders to people with no claim on anyone. Pope and Kalaignar read துறந்தார் as ascetics, which would make 42 overlap 41 rather than extend it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:42","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"42 lists துவ்வாதவர், those who have nothing to eat or enjoy, among the people the householder supports. 226 says that ending the destroying hunger of those without means (அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல்) is the place where a person who has gained wealth stores it (பொருள் வைப்புழி). 226 turns the duty stated in 42 into a claim about what wealth is for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:43","b":"k:81","type":"develops","note":"43 lists five quarters to be tended (ஐம்புலத்தாறு ஓம்பல்): ancestors, gods, guests (விருந்து), kin and oneself, and calls this the chief thing (தலை). 81 says that all of staying, guarding one's goods and living at home (இருந்தோம்பி இல்வாழ்வதெல்லாம்) is for the sake of tending guests and doing them good (விருந்தோம்பி வேளாண்மை செய்தற் பொருட்டு). One of the five items of 43 is taken up, with the same verb ஓம்பு, and made the purpose of the whole household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:43","b":"k:524","type":"parallel_domain","note":"43 puts kin (ஒக்கல்) among the five the householder must tend. 524, in the chapters on kingship, says that living so as to be surrounded by one's kin (சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்பட ஒழுகல்) is the fruit of having gained wealth. The household duty of Book 1 reappears in Book 2 as the point of a ruler's prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:112","type":"parallel_domain","note":"44 promises an unfailing line (வழி) to the life that fears blame (பழியஞ்சி) and shares its food; Parimelazhagar reads the fear of blame as applying to how the wealth is earned. 112 says the prosperity of the person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security to those who come after (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). In both, wealth held without wrong is what lasts into the next generation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"44 says a life that fears blame and has பாத்தூண், food shared out, never sees its line fail (வழியெஞ்சல் எஞ்ஞான்றும் இல்). 227 uses the same compound: the one habituated to shared food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை) is hardly touched by the disease called hunger. Both attach a protective result to the same practice, one for the descendants and one for the sharer himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:44","b":"k:1107","type":"same_image","note":"44 names பாத்தூண், shared food, as the mark of the householder's life. In 1107 the lover says the embrace of the woman is like staying in one's own home and eating one's own after sharing it (தம்மில் இருந்து தமது பாத்து உண்டற்று). The Book of Love takes the householder's sharing meal as a standard of pleasure, where 44 gives it as a duty with a reward; the comparison is the speaker's, not a teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:45","b":"k:75","type":"develops","note":"45 says that if household life has love and virtue (அன்பும் அறனும்), that is its character and its fruit (பண்பும் பயனும்). 75 says the distinction reached by those who have had joy in this world is, 'they say', the result of a way of life settled in love (அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கு); Parimelazhagar takes this as household life lived with love, rewarded with heaven. 75 spells out the பயன் that 45 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:46","b":"k:348","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one who conducts household life in the way of virtue would gain by going to the outside way (புறத்தாற்றில் போஒய்ப் பெறுவது எவன்); Parimelazhagar and Pope take the outside way as renunciation. 348 says those who have renounced completely (தீரத் துறந்தார்) have reached the head, and the rest (மற்றையவர்) are confused and caught in the net. If புறத்தாறு is renunciation and the question in 46 is rhetorical, 46 denies the gain that 348 reserves for renouncers alone; if புறத்தாறு means only ways outside virtue, the conflict disappears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:47","b":"k:266","type":"tension","note":"47 says the one who lives household life according to its nature is first among all who strive (முயல்வாருள் எல்லாம் தலை). 266 says those who do தவம் are doing their own work and the rest (மற்றல்லார்), caught in desire, do what is futile (அவஞ்செய்வார்). On Parimelazhagar's reading of 47, where the strivers are those working to leave the senses, the householder is ranked above ascetics, while 266 counts everyone but the ascetic as wasting effort. Manakkudavar reads முயல்வார் as those who strive for wealth, and on that reading the two do not collide.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:48","b":"k:225","type":"agrees","note":"48 says a household life that keeps others on their path (ஆற்றின் ஒழுக்கி) and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). 225 says the strength of the strong is enduring hunger, and that comes behind the strength of those who remove that hunger (மாற்றுவார் ஆற்றலின் பின்). Both rank the one who provides above the one who abstains, and both do it by granting the ascetic's own virtue, endurance, and awarding more of it to the provider.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:49","b":"k:44","type":"qualifies","note":"44 opens with பழியஞ்சி: the household life that fears blame and shares its food keeps its line. 49 calls household life virtue itself, then adds that it is good if there is nothing others blame (பிறன் பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). On Manakkudavar's reading, where அஃதும் still refers to household life, 49 makes absence of blame the condition on the chapter's whole praise of the householder, which 44 had already built into its description.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:49","b":"k:280","type":"develops","note":"49 says what is called virtue is household life, and 'that too' (அஃதும்) is good if it is not something others blame (பிறன் பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). Parimelazhagar takes அஃதும் as renunciation and the blame as false conduct in ascetics (Manakkudavar keeps it on household life). 280 says neither shaving nor growing the hair is needed if one drops what the world has blamed (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்). On Parimelazhagar's reading the chapter on false ascetics works out the condition 49 states in passing: the renouncer's standing rests on being free of blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:50","b":"k:413","type":"parallel_domain","note":"50 places the person who lives rightly on earth (வையத்துள்) among the gods of the sky. 413 says those who have listening as food for the ear are, though on earth (நிலத்து), equal to the gods who are fed by oblations. Both verses set 'on earth' against the gods and say a human excellence closes the gap, right living in one case and learning by listening in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:52","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"52 says that if excellence of the home (மனைமாட்சி) is not in the wife, the life is nothing, whatever excellence it has (எனைமாட்சித் தாயினும் இல்). 750 says that whatever excellence a fort has (எனைமாட்சித் தாகியக் கண்ணும்), it is as nothing in those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). The two share the phrase and the argument: the outward assets of a home or a fortress count for nothing without the quality of the person inside who runs it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:53","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"53 asks what is lacking if the wife has worth (இல்லதென் இல்லவள் மாண்பானால்) and what is there if she does not (உள்ளதென் ... மாணாக் கடை). 430 says those with understanding have everything, and those without it have nothing whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்). The same two-sided formula, one possession that makes all others count or not count, is applied to a wife's character in the home and to understanding in a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:54","b":"k:988","type":"same_image","note":"54 asks what is greater than a woman, if she has the firmness called கற்பு (கற்பென்னும் திண்மை உண்டாகப் பெறின்). 988 says poverty is no disgrace to a person, if he has the firmness called சால்பு (சால்பென்னும் திண்மை உண்டாகப் பெறின்). The second line is the same but for one word, so the book describes a woman's fidelity and a worthy person's character with the same term, திண்மை, a firmness that outweighs outward circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:55","b":"k:2","type":"tension","note":"55 praises a wife who does not worship a god (தெய்வம் தொழாஅள்) and worships her husband, and credits her with power over the rain. 2 asks what use learning is if the learned do not worship the feet of the one of pure knowledge (நற்றாள் தொழாஅர் எனின்), with the same verb in the same negative form. Read literally, 55 commends for the wife the not-worshipping that the first chapter treats as making a person's attainments useless. Parimelazhagar removes the conflict by reading 'other gods' (பிற தெய்வம்) and making the husband her god; the tension depends on taking தெய்வம் தொழாஅள் at face value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:56","b":"k:57","type":"develops","note":"56 defines the woman as one who guards herself (தற்காத்து), cares for her husband and guards the good name (சொற்காத்து) without flagging. 57 asks what guarding by confinement (சிறைகாக்கும் காப்பு) can do, and says the guard women keep by their own நிறை is the chief one. 57 explains why 56 makes her the subject of the verb 'guard': the guarding that counts cannot be done by anyone else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:56","b":"k:59","type":"develops","note":"56 includes guarding the well-founded word, the couple's good name (தகைசான்ற சொற்காத்து), in its definition of the wife. 59 says those whose home does not seek renown (புகழ்புரிந்த இல் இலோர்க்கு) cannot walk with a proud gait before those who deride them. Parimelazhagar connects them directly, saying 59 states the fault that follows when that good name is not kept: the husband's standing in public depends on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:58","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"58 says women who 'obtain the one who obtained them' (பெற்றாற் பெறின்) gain great distinction in the world where the gods live (புத்தேளிர் வாழும் உலகு); Parimelazhagar supplies 'honour' and reads it as wives who revere their husbands. 50 says the man who lives on earth as he should is placed among the gods of the sky. The two adjacent chapters end on the same reward, one for the householder and one for the wife, each for fulfilling the household role.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:59","b":"k:237","type":"develops","note":"59 says those whose home does not seek renown (புகழ்புரிந்த இல்) have no proud gait before those who deride them (இகழ்வார்முன்). 237 asks why those who cannot live with renown (புகழ்பட வாழாதார்) resent those who deride them (இகழ்வாரை நோவது எவன்) and not themselves. Both set புகழ் against இகழ்வார்: 59 says that without renown one has no answer to scorners, and 237 adds that the scorn is then one's own doing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:59","b":"k:1014","type":"same_image","note":"59 says that without a home that seeks renown there is no walking with a bull's (Parimelazhagar: lion's) proud gait, ஏறுபோல் பீடு நடை. 1014 says shame is an ornament to the worthy, and without it their பீடு நடை is a disease. The proud walk appears twice as something that must be earned by an inner or domestic quality, the household's good name in one verse and a sense of shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:60","b":"k:61","type":"develops","note":"60 says the excellence of the home is called its blessing (மங்கலம்) and the gaining of good children (நன்மக்கட் பேறு) its fine ornament. 61, opening the next chapter, says that of all things to be gained we know none like the gaining of children who know what should be known (அறிவறிந்த மக்கட்பேறு). 61 picks up the word மக்கட்பேறு from 60 and specifies what makes the children 'good': their understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:61","b":"k:162","type":"tension","note":"Verse 61 says that among all things gained (பெறுமவற்றுள்) 'we' know of nothing to set beside knowing children. Verse 162 says that among excellent gains (விழுப்பேற்றின்) nothing equals (அஃதொப்பது இல்லை) being free of envy. The two top rankings conflict only if each superlative is read literally as a ranking across all gains; read as each chapter's idiom of praise they do not.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:61","b":"k:300","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses rank their subject first by the author's own witness in the first person plural: 61 has யாமறிவது இல்லை ('we know of none') for knowing children, 300 has யாமெய்யாக் கண்டவற்றுள் இல்லை ('among what we have seen as true there is none') for truthfulness. The pairing shows the same rare device, the poet's 'we', used to vouch for a household good and for a virtue of speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:44","type":"develops","note":"Verse 44 says that if a household life fears blame (பழியஞ்சி) and shares its food, its line (வழி) never fails. Verse 62 says that if one gets children in whom blame does not grow (பழிபிறங்கா), no evil touches one for seven births. In 44 the parent's freedom from blame secures the descendants; in 62 the descendants' freedom from blame secures the parent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:398","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 62 says evils will not touch a person in all seven births (எழுபிறப்பும் தீயவை தீண்டா) if they get children of character who bring no blame. Verse 398 says the learning gained in one birth is a protection in seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). Both name a good of this life whose protection outlasts it by seven births: in one case one's children, in the other one's own learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:62","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 62 has blameless children keep evil away through seven births (எழுபிறப்பும்). Verse 835 has the fool, in one birth's doing (ஒருமைச் செயல்), make the mire (அளறு) he sinks in for seven (எழுமையும்). The same seven-birth reckoning is applied in the opposite direction, once to a safeguard and once to a ruin made by oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:114","type":"develops","note":"Verse 63 says people call their children their wealth (தம்பொருள் என்ப தம் மக்கள்), and ties that wealth to deeds (வினையான் வரும்). Verse 114 says whether people are worthy or not is seen from their எச்சம், what they leave behind, which Parimelazhagar takes as their offspring and Kalaignar as the name they leave. On Parimelazhagar's reading the pair makes children both a person's property and the evidence by which that person is judged.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:63","b":"k:400","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 63 reports the saying that one's children are one's wealth (தம்பொருள்). Verse 400 says learning is the imperishable wealth (கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்) and that other things are not wealth (மாடல்ல). Both move the word for property from goods to something else, but 400 denies the title to everything other than learning, while 63 only reports what people say.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:64","b":"k:1103","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 64 sets a child's messy gruel above amrita. In 1103 the lover asks whether the world of the lotus-eyed one (தாமரைக் கண்ணான் உலகு) can be sweeter (இனிதுகொல்) than sleeping on the soft arms of the one he loves; Parimelazhagar has him say this to a companion who reproved him. The same move, a heavenly standard of sweetness outdone by an intimate bodily one, appears in the parent's voice in Book 1 and in a lover's speech in Book 3.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:65","b":"k:1101","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 65 names two sense pleasures a child gives: touching the child's body is joy to the body (உடற்கின்பம்) and hearing its words is joy to the ear (இன்பம் செவிக்கு). In 1101 the lover says the five senses, seeing, hearing, tasting, smelling and touching (கண்டுகேட்டு உண்டுயிர்த்து உற்றறியும்), all have their objects in one woman. Both list pleasures sense by sense and locate them in a single loved person, a child in one and a lover in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:66","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 66 says only those who have not heard their own children's prattle call the flute (குழல்) and the lute sweet. In 1228 the woman left alone says the herdsman's flute (ஆயன் குழல்) is now a messenger of the burning evening and a weapon that kills; Parimelazhagar notes that it had been sweet to her before. In both the flute stands for accepted sweetness, and in both that sweetness is made to depend on the hearer's situation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:715","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 67 makes it the father's gift that the son sits ahead in the assembly (அவையத்து முந்தி இருப்ப). Verse 715 says the best of good things is the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). The shared root முந்து shows the limit: standing first in the assembly is the goal, but being first to speak before one's seniors is what the book warns against.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:67","b":"k:722","type":"develops","note":"Verse 67 says a father's good to his son is to make him sit foremost in the assembly; Parimelazhagar takes this as making him learned beyond the learned there. Verse 722 says those called learned among the learned (கற்றாருள் கற்றார்) are those who can make what they learned go home before the learned. 722 supplies what the front seat of 67 requires in practice: learning that can be delivered in front of that assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:68","b":"k:399","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 68 says one's children's understanding (அறிவுடைமை) is sweet to all beings on the great earth (மன்னுயிர்க்கெல்லாம் இனிது); Parimelazhagar reads தம்மின் as 'more than to oneself', while Kalaignar and Subramuniyaswami take it as children who surpass their parents. Verse 399 says the learned love learning more on seeing that what delights them delights the world (தாமின்புறுவது உலகின்புறக் கண்டு). Both treat knowledge as a pleasure that is not private: the parent's pride in 68 and the scholar's delight in 399 are each measured against the world's share in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:69","b":"k:1047","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 69 has the mother's joy grow when she hears others call her son worthy (சான்றோன் எனக் கேட்ட தாய்). Verse 1047 says one in poverty unconnected with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல நோக்கப்படும்). In both the mother's regard follows the son's standing, rising with his name in 69 and withdrawn in 1047.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:70","b":"k:265","type":"develops","note":"Verse 70 has onlookers explain a good son by asking what the father did as penance (என்நோற்றான்). Verse 265 says தவம் is striven for here because whatever is wanted is obtained as wanted (வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால்). The onlookers' question in 70 assumes the doctrine 265 states, and it applies that doctrine to a son.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:71","b":"k:1180","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 71 says tears raise a public noise (பூசல் தரும்) about the love inside. In 1180 the woman says it is not hard for the town to get at the secret (மறை) of those like her whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In both the eyes make public what the heart holds, and both describe this as sound: a clamour in 71, a drum in 1180.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:72","b":"k:220","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 72 says those who have love belong to others even in their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). Verse 220 says that if ruin comes through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth buying even by selling oneself (விற்றுக்கோள் தக்கது). Both carry giving past one's goods to one's own person, 72 as a description of love and 220 as the price of duty to the community.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:72","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"Verse 72 says the loveless belong to themselves in everything (எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்) while the loving belong to others (பிறர்க்கு) even in their bones. Verse 1009 says wealth piled up by putting love aside (அன்பொரீஇ), denying oneself and ignoring virtue will be taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). 1009 gives the outcome of the stance in 72: what the loveless keep for themselves ends with பிறர் anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:80","type":"develops","note":"Verse 73 says the life's connection with the bone (என்போடு) goes with love. Verse 80 says the seat of life (உயிர்நிலை) follows love, and that for those without it the body is bone wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). The chapter's last verse gives the negative of 73: take away the love and what remains of the union is the bone alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:338","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 73 treats the joining of life and body (என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) as something fitted to love. Verse 338 says the friendship between body and life (உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை நட்பு) is like a bird flying off and leaving its குடம்பை alone. The pairing sets a limit on 73: the bond that exists for love's sake is, in the chapter on impermanence, one the life will abandon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:73","b":"k:1122","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 73 says the tie of the life with the body (ஆருயிர்க்கு என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) is, they say, a matter of being joined with love (அன்போடு இயைந்த வழக்கு); Parimelazhagar takes embodiment as the result of a course meant for joining with love. In 1122 the lover says the bond between him and the woman is what the bond between body and life is (உடம்பொடு உயிரிடை என்ன). 73 explains the union of body and life by love, and the lover in 1122 measures his love by that union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:74","b":"k:757","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 74 says love gives birth (ஈனும்) to eagerness, and that gives birth to friendship, an unsought distinction (நாடாச் சிறப்பு). Verse 757 calls அருள் the child born of love (அன்பீன் குழவி), raised by the foster-mother wealth. Both use the figure of birth for what comes out of அன்பு, and together they give love two offspring, friendship in the home and grace toward all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:74","b":"k:785","type":"develops","note":"Verse 74 derives friendship (நண்பு) from love by way of eagerness (ஆர்வம்) and calls it a distinction that is not sought (நாடா). Verse 785 says meeting and long acquaintance are not needed, because feeling itself (உணர்ச்சிதான்) gives the claim of friendship. The friendship chapter of Book 2 keeps the account given in Book 1: friendship comes from an inner disposition and not from arrangement or contact.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:75","b":"k:39","type":"qualifies","note":"Verse 39 says only what comes through virtue is joy (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்) and everything else lies outside it. Verse 75 traces the joy and distinction people reach in the world to a life settled in love (அன்புற்று அமர்ந்த வழக்கு). Set side by side, 75 adds love to the source that 39 confines to அறம், which agrees with verse 45's coupling of the two.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:76","b":"k:862","type":"develops","note":"Verse 76 says the ignorant hold that love supports only virtue, while it is the help (துணை) for மறம் as well; Kalaignar reads மறம் as valour, Parimelazhagar as the hostility that love removes. Verse 862 asks how a man without love (அன்பிலன்), without allies and without strength of his own can wear down an enemy's might. On Kalaignar's reading 862 is the case in point: in Book 2 love is listed first among the things a fighter needs.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:80","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 77 compares the loveless to a thing without bones (என்பிலது) dried by the sun; Kalaignar and Subramuniyaswami take it as a worm. Verse 80 says the body of those without love is bones wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). The chapter uses bone twice for the loveless in opposite ways, once as what they lack and once as all they are.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:77","b":"k:204","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 77 says அறம் scorches what has no love as the sun scorches what has no bones (வெயில்போலக் காயுமே). Verse 204 says that if one plots another's ruin, அறம் plots the plotter's ruin (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). In both அறம் is an agent that acts against a person, and Parimelazhagar glosses it in both places as அறக்கடவுள், the god of virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:78","b":"k:576","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 78 likens the loveless life to a withered tree on hard ground. Verse 576 says those who have eyes and no kindly regard are like trees fixed in the soil (மண்ணோடியைந்த மரத்தனையர்). In both a person without the inward quality, அன்பு in one and கண்ணோட்டம் in the other, is reduced to a tree, alive in form and without response.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:78","b":"k:1106","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 78 says a life with no love within is like a dried tree putting out shoots on hard ground (வற்றல் மரம் தளிர்த்தற்று), which Parimelazhagar takes as something that does not happen. In 1106 the lover says her touch makes his life sprout each time they meet (உறுதோறு உயிர் தளிர்ப்ப). The verb தளிர் carries both: life sprouts where love is, and 78 uses the same sprouting to mark what a loveless life cannot do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:79","b":"k:407","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 79 says the outer limbs are of no use without the inner limb, love. Verse 407 says the fine looks (எழில்நலம்) of one without subtle, penetrating understanding are like those of a well-made clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). Both set a complete exterior against a missing interior, love in Book 1 and understanding in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:79","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 79 asks what all the outer parts can do (புறத்துறுப்பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்யும்) for those who lack love, the inner part (அகத்துறுப்பு). Verse 574 asks what eyes do (எவன் செய்யும்), beyond seeming to be in the face, when they have no கண்ணோட்டம். The same question in the same words is put to the whole body in 79 and to one organ in 574: a part is judged by the disposition it serves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:255","type":"same_image","note":"Verse 80 says the seat of life (உயிர்நிலை) goes by way of love (அன்பின் வழியது). Verse 255 says உயிர்நிலை lies in not eating flesh (உண்ணாமை உள்ளது உயிர்நிலை); Parimelazhagar reads this as life's staying in the body resting on that virtue. The rare word உயிர்நிலை is used in both to make a moral quality, love or not eating flesh, the condition of being alive at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:80","b":"k:1020","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Verse 80 says that for those without love the body is only bones wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). Verse 1020 says the movements of those with no shame within (நாண் அகத்தில்லார்) are like a wooden puppet feigning life by its string (மரப்பாவை ... உயிர்மருட்டியற்று). Both describe a moving body that lacks an inward quality as something without life in it, a frame of bone in one and a puppet in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:82","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"82 says eating alone while the guest is outside (விருந்து புறத்ததாத் தானுண்டல்) is not to be desired even if the food were the medicine against death. 229 says eating by oneself alone (தாமே தமியர் உணல்) to fill up one's store is more bitter than begging. Both judge the solitary meal itself, one by setting it against the best imaginable food and the other against the worst condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:83","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"83 says the life of one who tends the arriving guest every day (வைகலும் ஓம்புவான்) does not suffer and go to ruin (பருவந்து பாழ்படுதல் இன்று). 227 says the disease called hunger rarely touches one accustomed to sharing his food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை). Both promise that habitual sharing of food protects the sharer from want, once for the host of guests and once for the giver to the poor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:83","b":"k:480","type":"qualifies","note":"83 says daily hospitality never brings a household to ruin (பாழ்படுதல் இன்று), which Parimelazhagar takes to mean the host's goods are not used up. 480 says helpfulness that does not weigh the extent of what one has (உளவரை தூக்காத ஒப்புரவாண்மை) quickly ends one's wealth (வல்லைக் கெடும்). The Book of Wealth thus attaches a condition of measure to the kind of promise 83 makes without one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:84","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"84 has the goddess of fortune (செய்யாள்) living in the home of the glad-faced host. 617 says she of the lotus (தாமரையினாள்) lives in the effort of the unslothful (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள்), and her dark sister in sloth. The same figure is given two different dwellings, generosity in the Book of Virtue and exertion in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:85","b":"k:545","type":"parallel_domain","note":"85 implies that the field of the host who feeds guests first yields without sowing. 545 says rain and harvest gather together (பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு) in the land of the king who wields the sceptre as he should. In both, right conduct is said to make the land fruitful, at the scale of one household's field and at the scale of a kingdom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:85","b":"k:1039","type":"tension","note":"85 asks whether one who feeds guests and eats what is left (விருந்தோம்பி மிச்சில் மிசைவான்) need even sow his field (வித்தும் இடல்வேண்டும் கொல்லோ); Parimelazhagar supplies that it will bear by itself. 1039 says that if the owner stays away and does not go to it (செல்லான் கிழவன் இருப்பின்), the land sulks like a wife. The conflict holds only if 85 is taken literally, as Parimelazhagar does, and not as a figure of praise; Kalaignar reads 85 quite differently, as asking whether such a host would hold back even his seed-grain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:86","b":"k:50","type":"develops","note":"86 says one who tends the departing guest and watches for the next (வருவிருந்து பார்த்திருப்பான்) will be a good guest to those in the sky (வானத்தவர்க்கு). 50 says one who lives on earth as one should (வாழ்வாங்கு வாழ்பவன்) will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (வான்உறையும் தெய்வத்துள்). 86 names one specific practice that earns what 50 promises the householder in general, and turns it into a reciprocity: the host becomes the guest. (This edition prints நல்வருந்து in 86 and உநற்யும் in 50; both are typos.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:221","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 87 scales the fruit of hospitality to the guest's worth (விருந்தின் துணைத்துணை). 221 says giving to those who have nothing (வறியார்க்கு ஒன்று ஈவதே ஈகை) is the only real giving, and all the rest is like a loan expecting return. One verse values the gift by the receiver's merit and the other by the receiver's need; the conflict depends on reading துணை in 87 as merit and on treating feeding a guest and giving as the same kind of act.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:87","b":"k:259","type":"same_image","note":"87 calls hospitality a வேள்வி (sacrifice) whose fruit has no fixed measure; Parimelazhagar notes it is one of the five great sacrifices. 259 says not taking a life and eating it is better than pouring oblations in a thousand sacrifices (அவிசொரிந்து ஆயிரம் வேட்டலின்). In both verses the ritual word is used to rank an ethical practice: one is named a sacrifice, the other is set above a thousand of them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:88","b":"k:228","type":"agrees","note":"88 says those who do not take up the sacrifice of tending guests guard their goods painfully (பரிந்தோம்பி) and end by saying 'we have no support' (பற்றற்றேம்). 228 asks whether the hard-eyed who store what they have and lose it (வைத்திழக்கும்) do not know the joy of giving. Both describe the same sequence of hoarding and then loss, and both set it against a use of wealth the hoarder never tried.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:88","b":"k:376","type":"develops","note":"88 pictures people guarding their goods with pain (பரிந்தோம்பி) and still losing them. 376 says what is not allotted to one will not stay even if guarded with pain (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல), and what is one's own will not leave even if poured away (சொரியினும் போகா தம). The fate chapter gives a general ground for 88's observation: anxious guarding does not decide whether wealth stays, so spending it on guests risks nothing that guarding would have saved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:89","b":"k:841","type":"develops","note":"89 says poverty in the midst of wealth is the folly (மடமை) of not caring for guests. 841 says lack of understanding is the poverty of poverties (அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை) and that the world does not count other lack as lack. 841 states as a general principle what 89 shows in one case: the real want is a failure of understanding, whatever one owns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:93","type":"develops","note":"90 gives the negative case: a face that turns (முகந்திரிந்து) and looks makes the guest wilt. 93 gives the positive rule in nearly the same terms: with a glad face, looking sweetly (முகத்தான் அமர்ந்து இனிது நோக்கி), then sweet words from the heart. The last verse of the hospitality chapter and the third of the next share the face-and-look sequence; Parimelazhagar on 90 also lists the glad face, the sweet word and the good deed as successive stages of welcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:90","b":"k:707","type":"develops","note":"90 says a guest wilts when looked at with an altered face (முகந்திரிந்து நோக்க). 707 says nothing is as knowing as the face, since whether one is pleased or angry it gets there first (உவப்பினும் காயினும் தான்முந்துறும்). 707 explains why 90's guest is hurt before a word is spoken: the face has already reported the host's mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:91","b":"k:358","type":"same_image","note":"91 says sweet words are the speech of those who have seen the செம்பொருள் (செம்பொருள் கண்டார் வாய்ச்சொல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses here as those who know virtue. 358 defines understanding as seeing the செம்பொருள் so that the folly of birth departs (செம்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு). The same phrase links ordinary kind speech to the insight the renunciation chapters describe, though the commentator gives the word a more modest sense in 91.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:92","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"92 says being sweet-spoken with a glad face is better than giving with a glad heart (அகன்அமர்ந்து ஈதலின் நன்றே). 295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart (மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின்) is above those who do austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). Twice the book ranks a quality of speech above material giving, once for kindness and once for truthfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"93 says virtue lies in sweet words that come from within (அகத்தானாம் இன்சொலினதே அறம்), after a glad face and a kind look. 34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue and the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற); Parimelazhagar counts speech and outward guise among that rest. 93's word அகத்தானாம் is what keeps sweet speech on the right side of 34's line: it counts as virtue because it issues from the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:786","type":"parallel_domain","note":"93 requires the glad face and the sweet word to come from within (முகத்தான் அமர்ந்து ... அகத்தானாம்). 786 says friendship that makes only the face smile is not friendship; friendship that makes the heart within smile is (முகநக ... நெஞ்சத்து அகநக). The same முகம்/அகம் pairing sets the test of genuineness for kind speech and for friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:93","b":"k:824","type":"qualifies","note":"93 places virtue in a glad face followed by sweet words from within (அகத்தானாம்). 824 warns that deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சரை) are to be feared. 824 describes the counterfeit of 93's first half, so the outward signs 93 begins with cannot by themselves be taken as proof of the inner part.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:94","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"94 asserts that sweet speakers do not fall into want, without saying how. 525 says one who can give and speak sweetly (கொடுத்தலும் இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின்) will be surrounded by layer on layer of kin (அடுக்கிய சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). 525 supplies a worldly mechanism for 94's promise: sweet speech gathers people around one, and a person so surrounded is not left destitute.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:94","b":"k:566","type":"agrees","note":"94 says want that brings suffering (துன்புறூஉம் துவ்வாமை) does not come to those whose sweet speech gives joy to everyone. 566 says that if a ruler is harsh of speech and without kindly regard (கடுஞ்சொல்லன் கண்ணிலன்), his long-standing wealth perishes on the spot. The two verses state the same link between speech and prosperity from opposite sides, for anyone in the Book of Virtue and for a king in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:978","type":"same_image","note":"95 makes humility (பணிவு) the true ornament (அணி). 978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம்) while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). The same two roots are set against each other in 978: the small person's self-adornment is the false ornament, and the bowing 95 praises is what the great actually wear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:95","b":"k:1014","type":"qualifies","note":"95 says humility and sweet speech are a person's ornament and other things are not (அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற). 1014 asks whether shame is not the ornament of the worthy (அணிஅன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு), and 575 names kindly regard as the eye's ornament. The exclusive wording of 95 is therefore the chapter's emphasis: other chapters award the same title to other inner qualities, while agreeing with 95 (as Parimelazhagar reads it) that bodily ornaments are not meant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:96","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as what proceeds without slipping into four things, the last being painful speech (இன்னாச்சொல்). 96 says virtue grows and its opposite wears away when one speaks good things sweetly (அல்லவை தேய அறம்பெருகும்). 35 only requires avoiding harsh words; 96 adds that the positive practice of sweet, well-chosen speech actively increases virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:96","b":"k:128","type":"agrees","note":"96 says that if one seeks out good things and says them sweetly (நல்லவை நாடி இனிய சொலின்), what is not virtue wears away and virtue grows. 128 says that if even one harmful word has its effect (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல் பொருட்பயன் உண்டாயின்), what was good turns out not good. Together they give speech power over one's stock of virtue in both directions: good words build it and a single bad word undoes it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:194","type":"same_image","note":"97 says speech that yields benefit and does not part from pleasantness (பயன்ஈன்று பண்பின் தலைப்பிரியாச் சொல்) produces நயன் and brings forth good (நன்றி). 194 says speech with no benefit and no pleasantness (பயன்சாராப் பண்பில்சொல்) is without நயன் and removes one from good (நன்மையின் நீக்கும்). The four terms பயன், பண்பு, நயன் and நன்மை recur in the negative, so 194 is 97 inverted; Parimelazhagar glosses பண்பு as sweetness and நயன் as justice in both.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:97","b":"k:197","type":"qualifies","note":"97 asks for speech that has both benefit (பயன்) and pleasantness (பண்பு), and credits it with producing நயன். 197 says the worthy may say what lacks நயன் if they must, but should not say what lacks பயன் (நயனில சொல்லினும் சொல்லுக ... பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று). 197 thus ranks the qualities 97 holds together: use is the one that cannot be given up. Parimelazhagar takes நயன் in 197 as justice; Subramuniyaswami takes it as pleasantness, which makes the limit on chapter 10 more direct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:98","b":"k:291","type":"parallel_domain","note":"98 says sweet speech that is clear of smallness (சிறுமையுள் நீங்கிய இன்சொல்) gives joy in the next life and this one; Parimelazhagar explains சிறுமை as causing pain to others by what the words mean. 291 defines truth as speech containing no harm at all (யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). Sweetness and truthfulness are both defined by the same test, the absence of harm to others, and not by sound or by literal accuracy. (This edition prints சிறுமையுவு in 98, a typo for சிறுமையுள்.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:98","b":"k:911","type":"qualifies","note":"98 says sweet speech free of smallness gives joy (இன்சொல் ... இன்பம் தரும்) here and hereafter. 911 says the sweet speech of women who desire wealth and not the man brings harm (இன்சொல் இழுக்குத் தரும்). The two predicates answer each other, and the condition in 98 (சிறுமையுள் நீங்கிய) marks the difference: what 98 promises holds only for sweet words that are not serving a mean purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:318","type":"parallel_domain","note":"99 asks why one who sees how sweetly sweet words yield (இன்சொல் இனிதீன்றல் காண்பான்) would use harsh words (எவன்கொலோ வன்சொல் வழங்குவது); Parimelazhagar takes it as one who has felt the pleasure of others' kind words to himself. 318 asks why one who knows what is painful to his own life does painful things to other lives (என்கொலோ மன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னா செயல்). The two rhetorical questions have the same form and the same argument from one's own experience to the treatment of others, applied to words in 99 and to harm in general in 318. (The first line of 318 is misprinted in this edition, but the verse is recoverable.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:99","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"99 asks what could lead someone who knows the good that sweet words do to use harsh ones (வன்சொல்). 784 says friendship is not for laughing together but for going ahead to rebuke when a friend oversteps (மிகுதிக்கண் மேற்சென்று இடித்தற் பொருட்டு). The friendship chapter supplies a case where sharp words have a purpose, so 99's question is best read as aimed at needless harshness and not at every hard word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:831","type":"parallel_domain","note":"100 pictures someone who has ripe fruit at hand and takes the unripe: unpleasant words chosen while pleasant ones exist (இனிய உளவாக இன்னாத கூறல்). 831 defines folly as taking hold of harm and letting gain go (ஏதங்கொண்டு ஊதியம் போக விடல்). Harsh speech in 100 is an instance of exactly the choice 831 names as folly, which fits the chapter's closing argument that harsh words are a bad bargain for the speaker as well as wrong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:100","b":"k:1306","type":"same_image","note":"100 says speaking unpleasant things when pleasant ones are available is like taking the unripe fruit when the ripe is there (கனிஇருப்பக் காய்கவர்ந்தற்று). 1306, a speech in the Book of Love, says that love without serious and light quarrels is like over-ripe fruit and unripe fruit (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று). The ripe/unripe pair is used differently: in 100 the ripe fruit is simply the right choice, while the lover treats fruit that is too ripe and fruit that is too raw as two ways of missing the right point.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:101","b":"k:211","type":"agrees","note":"101 says that for help given without any prior help (செய்யாமல் செய்த உதவி) even earth and heaven are hardly a match (ஆற்றல் அரிது). 211 says obligation wants no return (கைம்மாறு வேண்டா) and asks what the world does for the rain in exchange (என் ஆற்றுங் கொல்லோ). The same verb ஆற்று marks both: one verse looks from the receiver who cannot repay, the other from the giver who does not ask to be repaid.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:101","b":"k:313","type":"same_image","note":"Both open with செய்யாமல், 'without (the other) having done anything first'. 101 applies it to help: unprompted help is beyond repayment. 313 applies it to hatred: even toward those who hate you though you did nothing (செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும்), doing hurt brings sorrow with no escape. Read together, the unprovoked good creates a boundless debt while the unprovoked wrong still gives no licence to retaliate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:102","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"102 says help done at the time of need (காலத்தினால் செய்த நன்றி), though small, is greater than the earth. 788 makes that timeliness the definition of friendship: it removes distress there and then (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது), like the hand going to a slipping garment. 102 values the help by its moment; 788 says what kind of helper acts in that moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:103","b":"k:813","type":"parallel_domain","note":"103 praises help from those who do not weigh the return (பயன்தூக்கார்), calling its good greater than the sea. 813 uses the same weighing verb for the opposite case: friends who weigh what they will get (உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பு) are put level with thieves. The pair shows the book judging help and friendship by one test, whether the giver was calculating.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:104","b":"k:109","type":"develops","note":"104 says a millet of good is taken as a palmyra by those who know its worth. 109 says even harm like killing (கொன்றன்ன இன்னா) vanishes when one thinks of a single good the person did (ஒன்று நன்று உள்ளக் கெடும்). Parimelazhagar connects them in his note on 109: because the small good is held as large, that one good can cancel the later harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:106","b":"k:800","type":"agrees","note":"Both verses use the phrase மாசற்றார் கேண்மை, the bond of the stainless: 106 says do not forget it (மறவற்க), 800 says keep close to it (மருவுக). Each then adds a second command about a kind of friend, 106 never to abandon those who were a support in distress, 800 to drop the unsuitable (ஒப்பிலார்) even at a cost. The chapter on choosing friends repeats the gratitude chapter's rule and supplies its other half, whom to leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:106","b":"k:806","type":"agrees","note":"106 commands: do not abandon (துறவற்க) the friendship of those who were a support in distress. 806 states it as a description: those who keep within the bounds will not abandon (துறவார்) the tie of long-standing friends even in ruin (தொலைவிடத்தும்). The same verb is used in both, with the direction reversed: 106 is loyalty owed for help received in one's own trouble, 806 loyalty kept when trouble comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:107","b":"k:398","type":"same_image","note":"107 says the friendship of one who removed one's distress is remembered through seven births (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும்). 398 says learning gained in one birth (ஒருமைக்கண்) is a protection through seven (எழுமையும்). The seven-birth span is the book's figure for what outlasts a single life, used here for a debt of gratitude and there for learning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:107","b":"k:799","type":"parallel_domain","note":"107 says people will remember (உள்ளுவர்) through seven births the friendship of those who wiped away their distress. 799 says the bond with those who abandoned one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when thought of (உள்ளினும்) at the hour of death. Both are about how conduct in another's hard time is remembered: the helper for lives to come, the deserter up to the last moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:108","b":"k:113","type":"same_image","note":"108 says a wrong done to you should be forgotten that very day (அன்றே மறப்பது). 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness should be let go that very day (அன்றே ஒழிய விடல்). The shared அன்றே gives both the same urgency: neither a grievance nor an unfair gain is to be kept overnight.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:109","b":"k:807","type":"parallel_domain","note":"109 says that even harm like killing disappears when one recalls a single good the person once did (அவர் செய்த ஒன்று நன்று உள்ள). 807 says those whose bond has come by the way of love do not cut off love even when the friend does ruinous things (அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார்). Both have the same 'even if they do grave harm' (செயினும்) clause; what outweighs the harm is a remembered favour in one and long affection in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:110","b":"k:207","type":"same_image","note":"110 says those who kill any good may have an escape (உய்வுண்டாம்), but there is none for one who kills a good done to him (செய்ந்நன்றி கொன்ற மகற்கு). 207 has the same shape: those who incur any enmity (எனைப்பகை உற்றாரும்) escape (உய்வர்), but the enmity of one's deeds follows and kills. Both use 'everything else can be survived' to single out one thing, ingratitude in 110 and evil deeds in general in 207.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:110","b":"k:896","type":"same_image","note":"110 says there is escape (உய்வுண்டாம்) for other wrongs but none (உய்வில்லை) for killing gratitude. 896 says one burned by fire may survive (உய்வுண்டாம்), but those who go on offending the great will not (உய்யார்). The same உய்வு formula marks what each chapter treats as its unforgivable offence, so a reader should take 'only this has no escape' as the book's way of stressing gravity and not as a strict ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:111","b":"k:528","type":"qualifies","note":"111 asks that fairness be kept alike across the divisions of people, on Parimelazhagar's reading toward enemy, stranger and friend. 528 says a king should not look on all in common (பொதுநோக்கான்) but according to rank or merit (வரிசையா நோக்கின்), and many will then stay by him. Together they show that impartiality in 111 does not mean identical treatment: one may not favour by relationship, but one should distinguish by desert.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:111","b":"k:541","type":"parallel_domain","note":"111 says the virtue called தகுதி is good if one keeps to what is due in each case (பகுதியால் பாற்பட்டு ஒழுக); Parimelazhagar takes பகுதி as enemy, stranger and friend. 541 defines a king's justice (முறை) as inquiring, showing favour to no one (யார்மாட்டும் கண்ணோடாது) and acting after consideration. The householder's impartiality of 111 reappears as the procedure of the ruler's court.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security to his descendants. 169 admits the opposite case occurs: the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடு) are things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்). 169 thus limits 112 to the usual course of things; Parimelazhagar explains the exceptions by deeds of earlier births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:112","b":"k:171","type":"agrees","note":"112 says a fair person's wealth is a support to his posterity (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). 171 states the reverse: if one covets others' goods without fairness (நடுவின்றி), the family perishes (குடிபொன்றி) and guilt comes at once. Both tie நடுவு to what happens to one's line, 112 as promise and 171 as warning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:113","b":"k:328","type":"parallel_domain","note":"113 says to give up at once gain that comes from leaving fairness (நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்), even if it yields only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 328 says that, however great the gain that brings good (நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும்), gain that comes by killing (கொன்றாகும் ஆக்கம்) is lowest to the worthy. The same concession, 'even if it is good and large', is refused in both; only the forbidden means differs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:118","type":"same_image","note":"115 and 118 end with the same line, கோடாமை சான்றோர்க்கு அணி, 'not bending is the ornament of the worthy'. In 115 the bending is of the heart under loss and gain (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை); in 118 it is leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை), like a balance. The repetition joins two sides of நடுவு: steadiness under one's own fortunes and impartiality between others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:115","b":"k:169","type":"develops","note":"115 says loss and increase both occur, and the worthy keep the heart from bending because of them. 169 gives the hard case: the crooked-hearted prosper and the upright are ruined (செவ்வியான் கேடு), which calls for reflection. 169 shows the situation in which 115's advice is tested, when fortune seems to reward bending.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:116","b":"k:204","type":"agrees","note":"116 says that if one's heart leaves fairness and does wrong (நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்), one should know 'I will perish' (கெடுவல்யான்). 204 says do not plot another's ruin even by a lapse, for அறம் will plot the plotter's ruin (சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). Both say that harm intended for others returns as one's own ruin; 116 makes it the wrongdoer's own knowledge, 204 gives the agent as அறம்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:117","b":"k:752","type":"tension","note":"117 says the world (உலகம்) will not count as ruin the low state (தாழ்வு) of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. 752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even if they have every good quality'. They conflict if உலகம் in 117 means people in general; Parimelazhagar avoids this by reading உலகம் there as the high-minded (உயர்ந்தோர்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:117","b":"k:988","type":"agrees","note":"117 says the low state of a person who stands fair is not held to be ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது). 988 says poverty is no disgrace (இன்மை இளிவன்று) for one who has the firmness called சால்பு. Both deny that poverty lowers a person of character; 117 says it for fairness in particular, 988 for worth as a whole.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:118","b":"k:561","type":"parallel_domain","note":"118 describes a balance that first makes itself level (சமன்செய்து) and then weighs (சீர்தூக்கும்). 561 says a king is one who examines a case fittingly (தக்காங்கு நாடி) and punishes in proportion (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது); Parimelazhagar glosses the examining as done 'standing in the middle' (நடுவாக நின்று). The two steps of the scale, being level and then measuring, match the two steps of just punishment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:119","b":"k:279","type":"same_image","note":"119 says rectitude is the absence of crookedness in speech (சொற்கோட்டம்), provided there is none within (உட்கோட்டம்). 279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute (யாழ்கோடு) is good, so judge people by what their deeds do. Both use straight and bent to separate appearance from substance: 119 will not take straight words as proof without a straight mind, and 279 will not take outward shape as proof at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:119","b":"k:295","type":"agrees","note":"119 makes unbent speech count as rectitude only if the mind too is unbent (உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை பெறின்). 295 praises one who speaks truth together with the mind (மனத்தொடு வாய்மை மொழியின்) above those who do austerity and give. Both attach the same condition to good speech, agreement between word and mind, in the fields of fairness and of truthfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:120","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"120 says true trade for traders is to look after others' things as their own (பிறவும் தமபோல்). 315 asks what use understanding is if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own (பிறிதின்நோய் தந்நோய்போல்). The same 'as one's own' (தம்போல்) test is applied to goods in the market and to suffering in the chapter on not hurting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:120","b":"k:1015","type":"parallel_domain","note":"120 asks traders to care for what belongs to others as for what is theirs (பிறவும் தமபோல் பேணி). 1015 says the world calls those who feel shame at others' blame and their own (பிறர்பழியும் தம்பழியும் நாணுவார்) the dwelling of shame; Parimelazhagar reads this as weighing the two alike. Both define a virtue, fairness in one and shame in the other, by removing the difference between oneself and others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:121","b":"k:50","type":"agrees","note":"121 says restraint brings one among the immortals (அமரருள் உய்க்கும்); 50 says one who lives on earth as one should will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப்படும்). Both promise a place among the gods to a person living an ordinary household life well, without renunciation, and 121 names restraint as the quality that does it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:121","b":"k:168","type":"same_image","note":"121 ends அடங்காமை ஆரிருள் உய்த்து விடும், and 168 ends with envy, called a பாவி, which destroys fortune and தீயுழி உய்த்து விடும். The same closing verb phrase, 'drives and leaves one there', is used for lack of restraint and for envy, so the two vices are given the same power to deliver a person to a bad place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:122","b":"k:31","type":"develops","note":"31 asks what gain is greater for a living being than virtue (அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு); 122 says there is no gain for a living being beyond restraint (ஆக்கம் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு) and tells one to guard it as wealth. 122 reuses chapter 4's formula almost word for word and applies it to one particular virtue, so restraint is given the rank the earlier verse gave to virtue as a whole.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:122","b":"k:363","type":"same_image","note":"122 tells one to guard restraint as பொருள், wealth, since nothing is a greater gain; 363 says there is no fine wealth (விழுச்செல்வம்) here like not wanting, and nothing equal to it there either. Both verses count an inner holding-back as the real wealth and both use the 'nothing equals it' form, once for the householder's restraint and once for the renouncer's freedom from craving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:123","b":"k:715","type":"same_image","note":"123 calls restraint செறிவு and says that, recognised, it yields distinction; 715 calls not speaking first among elders a செறிவு and ranks it the best of good things (முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). The shared word shows the general restraint of chapter 13 turning up in the assembly as a concrete act, holding back one's speech before those who know more.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:123","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"123 says that if one knows and then restrains oneself in the proper way (அறிவறிந்து ஆற்றின் அடங்கப் பெறின்), that restraint is recognised and yields distinction; Parimelazhagar takes அறிவறிந்து as knowing that restraint is itself what knowledge is. 834 gives the negative: no fool is greater than one who has studied, understood and taught others but is not himself restrained (தானடங்காப் பேதை). Together they say knowledge is finished only when it turns into restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:29","type":"same_image","note":"29 speaks of those who have climbed and stand on the hill called good qualities (குணமென்னும் குன்றேறி நின்றார்), whom Parimelazhagar takes to be ascetics; 124 says the restrained person's stature is greater than a mountain (மலையினும் மாணப் பெரிது), and Parimelazhagar reads நிலை there as the householder's state. On his readings the renouncer stands on a hill while the householder who stays restrained in his own station is said to overtop one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:965","type":"qualifies","note":"124 says the stature of one who is restrained without shifting from his station (நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான்) is far greater than a mountain's. 965 says even those like a hill will sink if they do a lowering thing as small as a kundri seed (குன்றின் அனையாரும் குன்றுவர்). The mountain stature of 124 holds only while the 'not shifting' holds; 965 states how little it takes to lose it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:124","b":"k:989","type":"agrees","note":"124 praises one who is restrained and does not shift from his station (நிலையின் திரியாது); 989 says those called the ஆழி of worth do not shift even when the age shifts (ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம் பெயரார்). Both measure a person by immovability, 124 against a mountain and 989 against the turning of an age.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:125","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"125 treats bowing (பணிதல்) as an excellence, a second wealth for the rich; 985 says bowing is the strength of those who get things done and the weapon by which the worthy turn opponents around (ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல்). The same act is valued as character in the Book of Virtue and as effective power in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:126","b":"k:398","type":"same_image","note":"126 says that drawing in the five like a tortoise in one birth is a protection through seven (ஒருமையுள் ... எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து); 398 says the learning one gains in one birth is a protection through seven (ஒருமைக்கண் ... எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). The closing formula is identical, so sense-restraint and learning are set side by side as the two acquisitions that carry across births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:126","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"126 says restraint of the five in one birth protects through seven; 835 says a fool can, by what he does in one birth (ஒருமைச் செயல்), make the mire he will sink in through seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக்கு அழுந்தும் அளறு). The one-birth and seven-births arithmetic runs in both directions: one life is enough to secure the next seven or to ruin them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:127","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"127 says whatever else they fail to guard, let them guard the tongue, or they will grieve through a fault of speech (நாகாக்க காவாக்கால் சோகாப்பர்); 305 says if you would guard yourself, guard against anger, or anger will kill you (சினங்காக்க காவாக்கால் தன்னையே கொல்லும்). The sentence frame காக்க / காவாக்கால் is the same, and in both the harm of the unguarded thing falls on the person who failed to guard it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:128","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"128 says that if even one bad word has its effect, the good turns out not good (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல் ... நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்); Parimelazhagar takes this as the person's other merit turning bad. 965 says even hill-like people sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed. Both hold that a single small lapse is enough to cancel a large standing good, in speech in one verse and in honour in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:129","b":"k:159","type":"develops","note":"129 says the scar left by the tongue never heals; 159 says those who bear the harsh words from the mouths of people who exceed all bounds (இறந்தார்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல் நோற்கிற்பவர்) are as pure as renouncers. 129 gives the reason 159's praise is so high: what the forbearing person takes without retaliation is the one kind of wound the book says does not close.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:129","b":"k:1049","type":"same_image","note":"129 sets a fire-burn, which heals, against the tongue's scar, which does not; 1049 says one can even sleep in the middle of fire but cannot close an eye in the middle of poverty (நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும்). The book repeatedly takes fire as the measure of bearable pain and places a non-physical hurt beyond it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:204","type":"same_image","note":"130 says அறம் itself watches for the right moment and enters the path of one who has guarded against anger, learned, and is restrained (அறம் பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து); 204 says that if you plot another's ruin, அறம் plots the ruin of the plotter (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). In both verses virtue is an agent that acts on a person, seeking out the restrained and planning against the malicious.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:130","b":"k:594","type":"parallel_domain","note":"130 has virtue looking for its chance and coming along the path to the restrained person; 594 has prosperity asking the way and going to the one of unwavering energy (ஆக்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும்). The same figure, a good that seeks out the person fit for it instead of being pursued, is used for virtue in the first book and for worldly gain in the second. The edition's text of 594 has lost its first letter (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்), but the reading is not in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:131","b":"k:1017","type":"agrees","note":"131 says conduct is to be guarded more than life because it gives excellence (ஒழுக்கம் உயிரினும் ஓம்பப்படும்); 1017 says those ruled by shame will give up life for shame and will not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). Both place a quality of character above life itself; 1017 states as what such people actually do what 131 states as a rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:951","type":"tension","note":"133 makes conduct the thing that constitutes good family (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை); 951 says integrity and shame are found together by nature only in those born in a good house (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை இயல்பாக). If குடிமை in 133 means that conduct alone makes one well-born, 951 runs the other way and makes birth the source of the conduct. The conflict disappears on Parimelazhagar's reading of 133, where conduct only preserves or forfeits a rank that birth has already given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:133","b":"k:1019","type":"agrees","note":"133 says a lapse from conduct turns into low birth; 1019 says a lapse in கொள்கை burns one's lineage (குலஞ்சுடும் கொள்கை பிழைப்பின்), and Parimelazhagar glosses கொள்கை there as ஒழுக்கம். Both say family standing is lost through the individual's failure in conduct, one calling the result a low birth and the other a burning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:134","b":"k:560","type":"same_image","note":"134 treats forgetting the Veda (மறப்பினும் ஓத்துக் கொளலாகும்) as a loss that can be made good; 560 lists among the effects of a king who does not protect that those of the six occupations forget their books (அறுதொழிலோர் நூல்மறப்பர்). The same forgetting appears as a private, repairable lapse in 134 and as a sign of public ruin in 560, while 134 keeps conduct as the loss that cannot be repaired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"135 takes it as given that the envious have no gain (ஆக்கம்); 169 says the gain of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are things to be pondered (அவ்விய நெஞ்சத்தான் ஆக்கமும் ... நினைக்கப்படும்). 169 concedes that the case 135 calls non-existent can occur and needs explaining, which weakens the simile's premise; Parimelazhagar softens it by adding 'if they occur'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:135","b":"k:170","type":"develops","note":"135 argues by comparison: as there is no gain in the envious, there is no elevation in one without conduct (அழுக்காறுடையான்கண் ஆக்கம் போன்று இல்லை). 170 states the premise on its own: none have grown great through envy (அழுக்கற்று அகன்றாரும் இல்லை). 135 takes chapter 17's claim as already known and builds its point about conduct on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:136","b":"k:164","type":"same_image","note":"136 says the strong do not slacken in conduct, knowing the harm that comes of a lapse (இழுக்கத்தின் ஏதம் படுபாக்கறிந்து); 164 says people do no wrong out of envy, knowing the harm that comes of the wrong path (இழுக்காற்றின் ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து). The second lines are nearly the same words, and both verses ground good behaviour in foreseeing consequences, which 136 counts as strength (உரவோர்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:137","b":"k:117","type":"develops","note":"137 says that from conduct people reach high standing and from a lapse they reach எய்தாப் பழி, which Parimelazhagar takes as blame that is not properly theirs, laid on them because the lapse makes it believable. 117 says the world will not count as ruin the low state of one who stays in the right by being fair (கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம்). In both the world's verdict follows character more than facts: it discounts the upright person's fall and charges the lapsed person with more than he did.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:138","b":"k:361","type":"same_image","note":"138 calls good conduct the seed of good (நன்றிக்கு வித்தாகும் நல்லொழுக்கம்) and says bad conduct always gives suffering; 361 calls craving the seed that produces unending birth for all beings (தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து). The seed figure is used for a cause whose yield comes later and keeps coming, once for a good crop and once for a bad one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:199","type":"parallel_domain","note":"139 says speaking evil things even by a slip is not possible for those who have conduct (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல் ஒல்லாவே); 199 says those of stainless sight do not say meaningless things even in forgetfulness (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லார்). Both claim that a settled quality rules out even the accidental lapse of speech, and they trace that quality to conduct in one case and to clear understanding in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:959","type":"agrees","note":"139 makes the absence of evil speech a test of having conduct; 959 says that as a sprout shows the ground it lay in, the words from the mouth show the family of the one born in it (குலத்தில் பிறந்தார் வாய்ச்சொல்). Both treat what comes out of the mouth as the evidence of what a person is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:139","b":"k:1044","type":"qualifies","note":"139 says a person of conduct cannot speak evil even by a slip; 1044 says that even in the well-born, poverty brings the slackness from which degrading words are born (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும் இன்மை இளிவந்த சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு தரும்). 1044 names a condition, want, under which the slip of speech that 139 calls impossible does happen to good people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:140","b":"k:280","type":"parallel_domain","note":"140 makes accord with the world the test that learning must pass; 280 says neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if one gives up what the world condemns (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்துவிடின்). In both, the world's standard outranks a formal qualification, learning for the scholar and outward marks for the ascetic. Parimelazhagar takes உலகம் in both verses as the worthy and not people at large.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:141","b":"k:832","type":"develops","note":"832 defines the folly of all follies (பேதைமையுள் எல்லாம் பேதைமை) as setting one's love on what is not proper to one (கையல்லதன்கண் காதன்மை). 141 names one case of exactly that: the பேதைமை of desiring (பெட்டு ஒழுகும்) a woman who is another's. Read together, 141 is an instance of the general definition that 832 gives much later.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:141","b":"k:910","type":"parallel_domain","note":"141 says the folly (பேதைமை) of desiring a woman who is another's (பிறன்பொருளாள்) is not found (இல்) in those who have seen aram and porul; 910 says the folly that comes of attachment to a woman (பெண்சேர்ந்தாம் பேதைமை) is never found (இல்) in those with a deliberating mind. Both end on 'பேதைமை ... இல்' and treat the lapse as a failure of understanding that knowledge excludes, once about another's wife in the Book of Virtue and once about subservience to one's own in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:142","b":"k:150","type":"agrees","note":"142 says that among all who stand outside virtue (அறன்கடை நின்றாருள் எல்லாம்) none is a greater fool than the one who stands at another's door (பிறன்கடை); 150 says that even one who does not keep to virtue and does wrong (அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும்) does well not to desire another's woman. Both verses rank within the class of the non-virtuous, and both put this one offence at the bottom, 142 by naming the worst case and 150 by naming the one abstention still worth having.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:143","b":"k:214","type":"same_image","note":"143 counts the betrayer of a trusting household as no different from the dead (விளிந்தாரின் வேறல்லர்); 214 says only the one who knows what is fitting lives (உயிர்வாழ்வான்), and anyone else is placed among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). In both, being alive is measured by conduct and not by breath, once for a wrong committed and once for a duty to others left undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:143","b":"k:926","type":"same_image","note":"143 says those who go on doing evil in the home of people who trust them (தெளிந்தாரில்) are 'no different from the dead' (விளிந்தாரின் வேறல்லர்); 926 uses the same construction for sleepers (துஞ்சினார் செத்தாரின் வேறல்லர்) and extends it to drinkers. Parimelazhagar explains 926 by the absence of awareness and 143 by the loss of aram, porul and inbam, so the same formula of living death is applied to a moral failure in one verse and to loss of consciousness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:144","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the millet's measure (தினைத்துணை). In 433 those who are ashamed of blame (பழிநாணுவார்) take a fault of a millet's size as large as a palmyra; in 144 the man entering another's home does not reflect even a millet's worth (தினைத்துணையும் தேரான்). The pair shows the same measure from opposite sides: the careful person enlarges the smallest fault, and the adulterer does not give the smallest thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:144","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"144 asks what greatness comes to, however great a person is (எனைத்துணையர் ஆயினும் என்னாம்), if he enters another's home without weighing it even a millet's worth (தினைத்துணையும் தேரான்). 965 says even those like a hill (குன்றின் அனையார்) sink if they do a lowering deed as small as a kunri seed. Both hold that standing does not outweigh a degrading act; 965 gives the general rule and 144 one named act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:145","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"233 says that apart from high renown nothing in the world stands without perishing (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்று இல்). 145 says the blame of the man who violates another's home also stands forever without dying (எஞ்ஞான்றும் விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). Taken literally 145 names a second undying thing, so the pair shows the book treating lasting blame as the counterpart of lasting fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:145","b":"k:284","type":"parallel_domain","note":"145 says one who crosses into another's home thinking it easy (எளிதென இல்லிறப்பான்) gets blame that stands forever without dying (விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). 284 says a love grown strong in theft (களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல்) yields at harvest a distress that does not die (வீயா விழுமம்). The same logic, a desire for what is another's that looks light at the start and has a permanent result, is applied to another's wife and to another's goods.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:146","b":"k:171","type":"parallel_domain","note":"146 lists four things that never leave the man who crosses into another's home: enmity, sin, fear and blame (பகை பாவம் அச்சம் பழி). 171 says that one who covets another's good property without fairness finds his family ruined and fault brought on at once (குடிபொன்றிக் குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்). Coveting another's wife and coveting another's goods are both given immediate, compound consequences; Parimelazhagar reads the பழி of 146 as blame on the family (குடிப்பழி), which brings it closer to the குடி of 171.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:147","b":"k:41","type":"develops","note":"Both verses use the formula 'the one called a householder' (இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான்). 41 defines him by what he supports: he is the steady help of the three on their good path. 147 defines him by what he refrains from: he lives the household life with aram (அறனியலான்) who does not desire the womanhood of one who belongs to another (பிறனியலாள் பெண்மை நயவாதவன்). Together they give a positive and a negative test for the same title.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:147","b":"k:49","type":"develops","note":"49 says that what is called aram is household life, and that it is good if there is nothing in it that another blames (பிறன்பழிப்பது இல்லாயின்). 147 says the householder living by aram is the one who does not desire another's woman. 147 can be read as naming a specific condition under which the household life of 49 stays free of others' blame; chapter 15 itself (145, 146) names பழி as the result of the offence.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:148","b":"k:282","type":"parallel_domain","note":"148 praises not even looking at (நோக்காத) another's wife; Parimelazhagar glosses this as not taking her into the mind. 282 says that even thinking in the heart (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும்) of taking another's goods (பிறன்பொருளை) by stealth is wrong. In both chapters the prohibition is moved back from the act to the look or the thought, for another's wife and for another's property.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:150","b":"k:297","type":"parallel_domain","note":"150 says not desiring another's woman is good even in one who practises no aram and does wrong; 297 says that if one can keep to not lying, then not doing the other virtues (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை) is still good. Both isolate a single restraint and say it holds its value without the rest of virtue. Set side by side, the book makes this kind of claim for more than one virtue, so each is better read as emphasis than as a ranking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:151","b":"k:579","type":"agrees","note":"151 says bearing with those who scorn us is the foremost thing (பொறுத்தல் தலை). 579, in the chapter on kindly regard in the Book of Wealth, says that showing regard and bearing (கண்ணோடிப் பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை) even with those whose nature is to hurt us is the foremost quality. The same ranking, with the same words பொறுத்தல் and தலை, is given for the householder and again for the ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:152","b":"k:301","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 152 as telling one to bear a wrong even when one is able to punish it (தாம் ஒறுத்தற்கு இயன்ற காலத்தும்). 301 states that condition outright: only the one who holds back anger where it can take effect (செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான்) truly holds it back, and where it cannot, it makes no difference. 301 supplies the reason the commentator's condition matters: forbearance toward those one cannot hurt is not yet forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:152","b":"k:488","type":"qualifies","note":"152 says to bear transgression always (என்றும்) and better still to forget it. 488, advising a king on timing, says: on seeing enemies, bear them (சுமக்க); when their end is seen to have come, the head goes down (கிழக்காம் தலை). In 488 the bearing is temporary and is kept in mind until the moment to act, where 152 asks for a bearing with no end that passes into forgetting. The Book of Wealth thus limits to a tactic what the Book of Virtue states without condition.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:153","b":"k:444","type":"same_image","note":"153 says the strength within strength (வன்மையுள் வன்மை) is bearing with fools (மடவார்ப் பொறை); 444 says the chief of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை) is to live so that those greater than oneself become one's own. Neither verse puts strength in force. One finds it in enduring those below one in understanding and the other in attaching oneself to those above.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:153","b":"k:841","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses name a 'poverty within poverty' (இன்மையுள் இன்மை) and give different answers: in 153 it is turning away a guest, in 841 it is lack of understanding (அறிவின்மை), with other lack not counted as lack by the world. The same superlative formula serves hospitality in the Book of Virtue and intelligence in the Book of Wealth, which shows the formula working as emphasis within each chapter's topic.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:154","b":"k:864","type":"same_image","note":"154 ties நிறை to forbearance: to keep நிறையுடைமை, live guarding பொறையுடைமை. 864 pairs the same two things in the negative, describing the man who does not leave off anger and has no நிறை (நீங்கான் வெகுளி நிறையிலன்) as easy prey for anyone. The commentators gloss நிறை differently in the two places (Parimelazhagar: சால்பு in 154; Pope, following him, has keeping secrets in 864; Manakkudavar on 154 has a conduct of self-guarding), but in both verses its presence goes with restraint of anger and its absence with unchecked anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:154","b":"k:987","type":"develops","note":"154 says that one who wants நிறையுடைமை never to leave should guard forbearance; Parimelazhagar glosses நிறை here as சால்பு. 987 asks what use சால்பு is if one does not do pleasing things even to those who have done one harm (இன்னாசெய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்). On Parimelazhagar's reading the two say the same thing from both ends: forbearance keeps சால்பு in place, and சால்பு that does not show in the treatment of offenders is worth nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:155","b":"k:314","type":"develops","note":"155 says the wise do not count as anything those who punish in return (ஒறுத்தாரை ஒன்றாக வையார்) and keep those who forbear like stored gold. 314 keeps the word ஒறுத்தல் and gives it a new content: the way to punish those who have done harm is to shame them by doing them good (அவர்நாண நன்னயஞ் செய்து விடல்). 314 thus shows the only form of 'punishing' that 155's judgment leaves open. (The Parimelazhagar text displayed for 314 in this edition belongs to 315.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:155","b":"k:549","type":"qualifies","note":"155 says those who punish (ஒறுத்தார்) are not counted as anything. 549 says that guarding the people and removing wrongdoing (குற்றம் கடிதல்) is no stain on a king but his work (வடுவன்று வேந்தன் தொழில்). Parimelazhagar restricts 155 to a harm done to oneself, and 549 concerns a ruler acting for his subjects, so 549 marks the limit of 155: the low regard for punishing applies to personal retaliation and not to the office of justice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:145","type":"parallel_domain","note":"156 says those who forbear have renown until the end (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்), against one day's pleasure for those who retaliate; 145 says the man who violates another's home thinking it easy gets blame that never dies (விளியாது நிற்கும் பழி). The adjacent chapters use the same accounting, a brief gratification weighed against a permanent reputation, with புகழ் on one side and பழி on the other. Manakkudavar takes பொன்றுந் துணையும் as until the person's own death, Parimelazhagar as until the world ends.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:173","type":"parallel_domain","note":"156 sets the one day's joy of those who retaliate (ஒருநாளை இன்பம்) against the lasting renown of those who forbear. 173 says those who want the other joy (மற்றின்பம்) will not do what is not aram out of desire for a small joy (சிற்றின்பம்). Both argue against a wrong by the shortness of the pleasure it buys, for retaliation in one verse and for coveting in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:156","b":"k:856","type":"agrees","note":"156 grants the retaliator one day's pleasure and gives the forbearing renown to the end. 856 says that the life of one who says it is sweet (இனிது) to prevail in hostility is close to failure and ruin (தவலும் கெடலும் நணித்து). Both admit that getting the better of an opponent tastes sweet and both say the sweetness is brief; the verse in the Book of Wealth names ruin where the verse in the Book of Virtue names only the loss of lasting fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"157 says that though others do what is improper to one (திறனல்ல தற்பிறர் செய்யினும்), it is good not to do what is not aram. 312 says that even when someone has done harm out of hatred, not doing harm in return (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை) is the settled principle of the stainless. The same rule appears in the householder's chapter on forbearance and in the ascetic's chapter on not harming, which makes it common to both ways of life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read நோநொந்து in 157 as grieving for the suffering that will come to the wrongdoers. 319 states the mechanism behind that expectation: if one does harm to others in the forenoon, harm comes to oneself in the afternoon of its own accord (தாமே வரும்). On the commentators' reading 157 presupposes 319: the injured person pities the offender because the offence will return on him, and for the same reason does not add a wrong of his own. Kalaignar instead takes the pain in 157 as the injured person's own.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:157","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"157: though others do one wrong, not doing what is not aram is good (அறனல்ல செய்யாமை நன்று). 852: though someone does unbearable things intending division (பகல்கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும்), not doing harm with hostility in mind is the chief thing (இன்னாசெய்யாமை தலை). The 'even if they do ... not doing' structure and the refusal to answer in kind recur in the Book of Wealth's chapter on hostility, where the reason given is political prudence as well as virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"158 says to overcome those who do excessive wrong out of arrogance (மிகுதியான்) by one's own worthiness (தகுதியான் வென்றுவிடல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as forbearance. 985 says the strength of the able is to bow (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Both describe yielding as the means of prevailing and use the vocabulary of contest, வெல்லல் and படை, for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:986","type":"qualifies","note":"158 calls forbearance toward the arrogant a victory (வென்றுவிடல்). 986 says the touchstone of சால்பு is to accept defeat (தோல்வி ... கொளல்) even from those who are not one's equals. The same conduct toward an overbearing lesser person is named winning in one verse and taking a loss in the other; 986 adds that it has to be felt and accepted as a defeat, which guards against reading 158 as a way of scoring over the offender. Manakkudavar on 158 says explicitly that forbearing is not defeat but is itself the victory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:158","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"158 says one defeats the overbearing by one's own forbearing worth. 1327, spoken by the man in the Book of Love, says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as is seen in the union that follows. The paradox that giving way is the real victory appears in ethics and again, in a dramatised speech, in love; the second is a lover's remark and not a moral rule, but the shape of the thought is the same.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:48","type":"agrees","note":"159 says those who endure (நோற்கிற்பவர்) the harsh words of transgressors are as pure as renouncers. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their path and does not slip from aram has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). Both apply the ascetic's verb நோல் to the householder and set him level with or above the ascetic; Parimelazhagar on 159 adds that these are people who remain in household life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:310","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses play on இறந்தார் and துறந்தார். 310: those who go beyond bounds in anger (இறந்தார்) are like the dead, and those who have renounced anger are equal to renouncers (துறந்தார் துணை). 159: those who endure harsh words from the mouths of those who go beyond bounds (இறந்தார்வாய் இன்னாச்சொல்) have the purity of renouncers (துறந்தாரின் தூய்மை). In both, a householder's control over anger, his own or his response to another's, earns the standing of renunciation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:159","b":"k:389","type":"parallel_domain","note":"159 praises those who endure harsh words (இன்னாச்சொல்) from people who overstep. 389 says the world rests under the parasol of the king who has the character to bear words bitter to the ear (செவிகைப்பச் சொற்பொறுக்கும்). The same capacity is a private virtue in one verse and a quality of rule in the other. Parimelazhagar takes the bitter words of 389 to be the rebukes of advisers, so there the speaker means well, unlike the transgressors of 159.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"261 defines the form of tavam as two things: bearing the pain that comes to one (உற்றநோய் நோன்றல்) and doing no harm to living beings. 160 uses the same verb for both the faster and the one who endures harsh words (நோற்பார்). By 261's definition the person in 160 who bears abuse and does not retaliate already meets both parts of tavam, which explains how 160 can rank him ahead of those who fast.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:160","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"160 ranks those who fast behind those who bear harsh words; 295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart is above those who practise austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). In both a virtue of everyday speech, enduring it in one case and keeping it truthful in the other, is placed above formal religious practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:161","b":"k:135","type":"same_image","note":"135 pairs the same two words: as the envious have no ஆக்கம், so one without ஒழுக்கம் has no உயர்வு. 161 fuses them, asking that freedom from அழுக்காறு be taken as ஒழுக்காறு. The rhyme ஒழுக்கு/அழுக்காறு is used in both to set envy and conduct side by side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:162","b":"k:363","type":"parallel_domain","note":"162 says that among excellent gains (விழுப்பேற்றின்) nothing equals being free of envy toward anyone (அஃதொப்பது இல்லை). 363 says there is no excellent wealth (விழுச்செல்வம்) like வேண்டாமை, here or there (அஃதொப்பது இல்). The same formula ranks an absence, of envy in the householder book and of wanting in the ascetic book, above every positive possession.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:163","b":"k:141","type":"parallel_domain","note":"141 says the folly of desiring a woman who is another's (பிறன்பொருளாள்) is not found in those who have seen அறம் and பொருள். 163 says the envier of பிறனாக்கம் is one who wants neither அறன் nor ஆக்கம். In adjacent chapters a wrong attitude to what is another's is measured against the same pair, virtue and wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:163","b":"k:1079","type":"develops","note":"163 says the one who does not cherish another's prosperity (பிறனாக்கம் பேணாது) and envies it is called one who wants neither அறன் nor ஆக்கம். 1079 shows the same reaction in the கீழ்: on seeing others clothed and fed (உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் காணின்) he is quick to find a fault in them. The later verse gives the behaviour that the earlier one judges.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:304","type":"parallel_domain","note":"304 asks whether there is any other enemy than anger (சினத்தின் பகையும் உளவோ பிற), which kills smile and joy. 165 says the envious need no enemy since envy itself brings ruin. Two of the four things 35 excludes from virtue, anger and envy, are each described as the person's own enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"434 says fault itself is the enemy that brings one's end (குற்றமே அற்றம் தரூஉம் பகை). 165 says the same of one fault: envy suffices to ruin its holder without any ஒன்னார். The king's general rule in Book 2 has a specific case already stated in Book 1.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:165","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"165 says envy is enough for the envious: it brings ruin even when enemies fail (ஒன்னார் வழுக்கியும் கேடீன்பது). 448 says a king without people who rebuke him perishes even with no one to ruin him (கெடுப்பார் இலானும் கெடும்). Both describe ruin that needs no outside enemy, once in a private vice and once in a ruler's unguarded state.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:166","b":"k:527","type":"develops","note":"527 says crows do not hide food but call the others and eat, and that ஆக்கம் belongs only to those of that nature (அன்ன நீரார்க்கே). 166 gives the opposite disposition and result: one who grudges giving leaves his சுற்றம் without food or clothing. Both tie a household's kin and its prosperity to the attitude taken toward sharing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:166","b":"k:1079","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the phrase உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம். In 1079 the base man sees others' clothing and food and finds fault with them; in 166 the kin of one who envies what is given (கொடுப்பது அழுக்கறுப்பான்) perish without clothing or food. The pairing shows what is envied returning as what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:167","b":"k:617","type":"same_image","note":"167 says the fair goddess (செய்யவள்) grudges the envious man and shows him to her elder sister (தவ்வை). 617 names both sisters by residence: மாமுகடி lives in sloth, and தாமரையினாள் in the effort of the unslothful. The same pair of goddesses marks envy in Book 1 and sloth in Book 2 as conditions that hand a person over from fortune to misfortune.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:169","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"117 says the world will not set down as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது) the low state of one who stays in what is right through fairness. 169 lists the ruin of the upright (செவ்வியான் கேடு) as something to be pondered. 117 limits how that கேடு should be counted: the upright person's decline is not regarded as real ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:171","b":"k:604","type":"parallel_domain","note":"171 says that coveting another's goods without fairness makes the family perish and brings faults at once (குடிபொன்றிக் குற்றமும் ஆங்கே தரும்). 604 says of the slothful that the family dies down and faults multiply (குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும்). The same double penalty, on the குடி and in குற்றம், is attached to greed in Book 1 and to sloth in Book 2.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:172","b":"k:113","type":"develops","note":"172 says those ashamed of unfairness (நடுவன்மை நாணுபவர்) will not do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain to be had (படுபயன் வெஃகி). 113 gives the instruction that goes with it: gain that comes from leaving fairness (நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்) is to be dropped that very day, even if it brings only good. 172 describes the character and 113 the rule it follows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:172","b":"k:164","type":"parallel_domain","note":"164 says people do no wrong out of envy because they know the harm that comes of the wrong path (ஏதம் படுபாக்கு அறிந்து). 172 says people do nothing blameworthy out of coveting because they are ashamed of unfairness (நாணுபவர்). The neighbouring chapters use the same sentence shape, ending in செய்யார், with knowledge of consequences as the check in one and shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:173","b":"k:754","type":"develops","note":"754 says wealth that comes without wrong and with knowledge of the means (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்) yields both அறன் and இன்பம். 173 says the small joy got by coveting is refused by those who want the other joy. Together they show the choice in 173 is not between wealth and joy: wealth rightly got delivers the joy that coveting forfeits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:205","type":"agrees","note":"174 says those of faultless sight who have conquered the senses will not covet on the thought 'we have nothing' (இலமென்று வெஃகுதல் செய்யார்). 205 forbids doing evil on the same plea (இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க) and adds that doing so makes one poor again. Both name poverty as the excuse and reject it; 205 gives the consequence that 174 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"656 says that even if one must see the mother who bore one go hungry (ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்), one should not do what the worthy condemn. 174 says the thought of being without does not make the self-controlled covet. 656 pushes the same refusal to its hardest case, where the want is another's and not one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:174","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"1040 says the good woman called Land laughs on seeing those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing' (இலமென்று அசைஇ இருப்பாரை). 174 has the same words, இலமென்று, as the thought that does not move the self-controlled to covet. The plea of having nothing is refused twice: it excuses neither taking from others nor sitting idle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:175","b":"k:315","type":"parallel_domain","note":"175 asks what fine and wide understanding comes to (அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவு என்னாம்) if one covets and acts senselessly toward everyone. 315 asks whether anything comes of understanding (அறிவினான் ஆகுவதுண்டோ) if one does not guard against another's pain as one's own. Both test அறிவு by how its owner treats other people, in coveting and in causing hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:175","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"834 says no one is a greater fool than the person who has studied, understood and taught others and yet does not restrain himself (தானடங்கா). 175 says subtle and wide understanding is worth nothing if its owner covets and does வெறிய things. Both hold that learning which does not govern conduct does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:176","b":"k:204","type":"develops","note":"204 says not to plot another's ruin even by a lapse (சூழற்க), because அறம் will plot the ruin of the plotter (அறஞ்சூழும் சூழ்ந்தவன் கேடு). 176 says that one who plots wrongs for wealth (பொல்லாத சூழ) will perish (கெடும்). The shared verb சூழ் places the ruin at the stage of planning, before any act; 204 names the agent of that ruin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:176","b":"k:285","type":"agrees","note":"285 says a loving nature with அருள் in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's lapse. 176 says one who stands on the path desiring அருள் (அருள்வெஃகி) perishes if he desires பொருள் (பொருள்வெஃகி) and plots wrongs. Both verses set அருள் and பொருள் as rival objects of one desire and say the second drives out the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:113","type":"qualifies","note":"177 argues from the outcome: gain got by coveting does not turn out well when enjoyed. 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness is to be given up that very day even if it brings only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 113 removes the dependence on outcome, so the prohibition would stand even where 177's prediction failed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:284","type":"same_image","note":"177 says not to want gain that comes by coveting, since at its ripening (விளைவயின்) the fruit is hard to make good. 284 says the love grown strong in stealth yields, at its ripening (விளைவின்கண்), a sorrow that does not die. Coveting in the householder book and theft in the ascetic book are both judged by the harvest image, where the gain looks sound until it ripens.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:177","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"931 opens with the same prohibition, வேண்டற்க: do not want gambling even if you win, because the winnings are like the hook a fish has swallowed. 177 says வேண்டற்க of the gain got by coveting, whose fruit turns out badly. Both warn against a gain that is real at the moment of taking and harmful afterwards.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:178","b":"k:283","type":"develops","note":"283 says gain made by stealth perishes beyond measure even as it seems to grow (ஆவது போலக் கெடும்). 178 says the way to keep wealth from shrinking is not to covet another's goods. 283 gives the negative side of the same claim: what is added by taking another's property is not a lasting addition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:179","b":"k:167","type":"develops","note":"167 says the fair goddess grudges the envious man and shows him to her elder sister. 179 says திரு goes to those who know virtue and do not covet. The two neighbouring chapters use the goddess in opposite directions: she leaves the one who resents others' goods and comes to the one who does not want them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:179","b":"k:594","type":"same_image","note":"179 says திரு, knowing the way (திறன் அறிந்து), goes to those who know virtue and do not covet. 594 says ஆக்கம் asks the way (அதர்வினாய்) and goes to the one of unwavering drive. In both, fortune is a traveller that seeks the person out; Book 1 gives non-coveting as what draws it and Book 2 gives energy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:180","b":"k:692","type":"parallel_domain","note":"692 says that not desiring what kings desire (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை) brings lasting prosperity through those kings. 180 says not wanting others' goods yields victory, while coveting without thought yields destruction. The courtier's rule in Book 2 follows the same logic as 180: refraining from what belongs to another is what secures gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:181","b":"k:297","type":"parallel_domain","note":"181 holds that not backbiting is still good in a man who otherwise does wrong (அல்ல செயினும் ... இனிது). 297 says that if one keeps to not lying, not doing the other virtues is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று). Both single out one restraint of speech and weigh it against the whole of the rest of conduct, though 297 goes further and treats it as sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:182","b":"k:820","type":"develops","note":"182 calls it worse than denying virtue to slander someone when absent and smile at him when present. 820 tells one not to come near those who are friendly in the house and blame you in the assembly (மனைக்கெழீஇ மன்றில் பழிப்பார்). 820 turns the moral verdict of 182 into practical advice about which friendships to refuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:183","b":"k:230","type":"parallel_domain","note":"183 ranks death above a life kept up by backbiting and pretence. 230 says nothing is more bitter than death (சாதலின் இன்னாத தில்லை), yet even it is sweet when one cannot give. Each verse measures a fault or lack by setting death beside it and finding death the better; the pairing shows how the book uses that comparison for both a wrong done and a good left undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:183","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"183 says that dying yields more of the gain the books of virtue speak of than living by backbiting and falsehood (பொய்த்துயிர் வாழ்தலின் சாதல்). 967 says that rather than live by following those who scorn you (வாழ்தலின்), it is better to be called ruined as you are. Both use the comparative 'than living thus' to say that a kind of life can cost more than its end, once for the slanderer's two faces and once for lost honour.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:184","b":"k:197","type":"parallel_domain","note":"184: though you speak harshly to his face (சொல்லினும்), do not speak (சொல்லற்க) the heedless word behind his back. 197: let the worthy say what lacks நயன் if they must (சொல்லினுஞ் சொல்லுக), it is good that they not say what lacks use. The two neighbouring chapters use the same concessive move, granting a lesser fault of speech in order to isolate the one the chapter is about.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:184","b":"k:795","type":"develops","note":"184 concedes speaking without kindly regard to a person's face (கண்ணின்று கண்ணறச் சொல்லினும்) and forbids only the word said in his absence that does not look to what follows (முன்னின்று பின்நோக்காச் சொல்). 795 goes further and tells one to seek out friends able to speak so that one weeps and to rebuke (அழச்சொல்லி ... இடித்து). What 184 merely tolerates, hard speech face to face, 795 treats as a mark of the friend worth choosing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:185","b":"k:193","type":"parallel_domain","note":"185 says that a man's heart does not really speak virtue is seen from the meanness of his backbiting (புறஞ்சொல்லும் புன்மையாற் காணப்படும்). 193 says that expansive talk of useless things itself declares the speaker to be without நயன் (நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும்). In both a kind of speech is treated as evidence about the speaker, whatever he professes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:185","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"185 reads a man's inner lack of virtue from his backbiting. 959 says that as the sprout shows what lies in the soil, the words of the mouth show the family one is born in (காட்டும் குலத்தில் பிறந்தார் வாய்ச்சொல்). Both treat speech as the visible sign of something hidden, the heart in one case and lineage in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:186","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"186 says the one who tells another's blame (பிறன்பழி கூறுவான்) will have his own faults sorted through and told (தன்பழியுள்ளும் திறன்தெரிந்து கூறப்படும்). 319 says that hurt done to others in the forenoon comes back on oneself in the afternoon (பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்). The same return of the deed appears for speech and for injury; in 186 the return is carried out by other people's tongues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:186","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"186 warns the teller of others' faults that his own will be told. 980 says greatness covers another's weakness while smallness only tells faults (சிறுமைதான் குற்றமே கூறிவிடும்). 186 gives the consequence of the habit and 980 gives its diagnosis: fault-telling is what smallness does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:187","b":"k:525","type":"agrees","note":"187 says those who do not know how to make friends by words that bring laughter (நகச்சொல்லி நட்பாடல் தேற்றாதவர்) will part even their own kin by words that divide (பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்). 525 says one who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in layers (இன்சொலும் ஆற்றின் ... சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). The two state the same link between speech and keeping one's people, from its negative and positive sides, in the Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:187","b":"k:784","type":"qualifies","note":"187 treats speaking so that others laugh (நகச்சொல்லி) as the way friendship is made. 784 says friendship is not for laughing (நகுதற் பொருட்டன்று நட்டல்) but for going ahead to rebuke when the friend oversteps. 784 limits 187: pleasant talk may begin a friendship, but it is not what the friendship is for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:188","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"188 describes people whose habit is to broadcast (தூற்றும்) the faults of those near them. 1076 likens the base to a beaten drum because they carry any secret they have heard to others (கேட்ட மறை பிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்கலான்). 1076 places the habit of 188 in the Kural's portrait of the base person.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:188","b":"k:1165","type":"parallel_domain","note":"188 asks what those whose way is to spread even the faults of people close to them (துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும்) would do to strangers (என்னைகொல் ஏதிலார் மாட்டு). In 1165 the woman asks what those who can bring grief within friendship (நட்பினுள்) would become in enmity. Both argue from conduct toward intimates to what must follow toward others; in 1165 it is a lover's complaint, not a general teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:189","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"189 speaks of the backbiter as a weight the earth bears (வையம் ... பொறை). 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (நிலக்குப் பொறை). The figure of a person as dead weight on the ground is used in both for someone who fails others in ordinary dealings, by malicious speech in one and by lack of regard in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:189","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"189 wonders that the earth bears the backbiter's weight at all. 990 says that if the worthy decline in worth, the great earth will not bear its load (இருநிலந்தான் தாங்காது மன்னோ பொறை). Together they show the image used at both ends: the earth strains under the bad man, and is able to carry its load only while the worthy stay worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:190","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"190 wishes that people would see their own faults as they see a stranger's. 930 asks whether a drinker, when sober and seeing another man drunk, does not think of his own lapse when he drinks (உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு). 930 is a concrete instance of the failure 190 names: the other man's fault is seen clearly and one's own identical fault is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:193","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"193 says that a man's spreading out useless matter in speech itself announces that he is without நயன் (நயனிலன் என்பது சொல்லும்). 959 says the words of the mouth show the family one comes from, as a sprout shows the soil. In both the speech testifies about the speaker; 193 makes the talk itself the subject of the verb 'says', which Parimelazhagar notes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:195","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"195 says useless talk costs people of fine nature their distinction (சீர்மை சிறப்பொடு நீங்கும்). 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து). 957 supplies a reason for 195: a fault that would pass unnoticed in others is conspicuous in those held high.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:195","b":"k:965","type":"parallel_domain","note":"195 says that if people of fine nature (நீர்மையுடையார்) speak useless things, their distinction and the regard it earned leave them. 965 says even those like a hill will sink if they do a lowering thing the size of a kunri seed. Both hold that standing does not protect the eminent from a small lapse; 195 applies this to idle speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:196","b":"k:406","type":"parallel_domain","note":"196 calls the habitual idle talker chaff, a husk with nothing in it. 406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist and are like barren saline land that yields nothing (பயவாக் களரனையர்). Both take a farming image of something that looks like the useful thing and gives no yield, and both turn on பயன், what a person produces.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:193","type":"qualifies","note":"193 says that useless talk at length shows the speaker to be without நயன், so the two faults go together. 197 separates them and ranks them: speech without நயன் may be let pass in the worthy, speech without use may not. Read together, 197 makes uselessness the graver of the two; Parimelazhagar draws the same ranking from சொல்லினும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:197","b":"k:697","type":"parallel_domain","note":"197 lets the worthy say even what lacks நயன் but holds that they should not say what lacks use (பயனில சொல்லாமை நன்று). 697 tells one who serves a king to say what is wanted and never to say what is without use even if asked (வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும் கேட்பினும் சொல்லாவிடல்); Parimelazhagar glosses வினையில as matters without benefit. The rule against useless speech is kept as firmly at court as in private ethics, and neither verse lets circumstances excuse it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:199","b":"k:258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"199 says what those of stainless sight will not say. 258 says those whose sight has parted from fault (செயிரின் தலைப்பிரிந்த காட்சியார்) will not eat flesh that has parted from life. The book repeatedly states a restraint as what people of clear காட்சி simply do not do; here the same form covers speech and diet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the phrase 'என்னும் செருக்கு'. In 201 it is 'தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு', which Parimelazhagar glosses as a delusion (மயக்கம்) and Manakkudavar as an exhilaration (களிப்பு) that the good fear; in 180 it is 'வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு', the pride of not wanting, which yields victory (விறல்). The same word names a heady state to be dreaded in one verse and a state worth having in the other, depending on what it rests on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:244","type":"qualifies","note":"201 makes fear of evil deeds the mark of the excellent. 244 says that for one who sustains living beings and practises அருள் there are no deeds his own life need fear ('தன்னுயிர் அஞ்சும் வினை இல்'). The fear 201 commends is aimed at one's own possible deeds, and 244 names the condition under which nothing of that kind remains to be feared.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:201","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"201 says those practised in evil do not fear 'தீவினை என்னும் செருக்கு' while the excellent (விழுமியார்) do. 428 gives the general rule in the chapter on understanding: not fearing what is to be feared (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சாமை) is folly, and fearing it is 'அறிவார் தொழில்'. Read together, the fear in 201 is a working of knowledge and not timidity; Parimelazhagar on 428 lists sin, blame and ruin among the things to be feared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:202","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"202 says evil deeds are to be feared more than fire ('தீயினும் அஞ்சப்படும்') because they yield evil. 129 sets fire against the tongue: a wound burned by fire heals, the scar burned by the tongue does not ('ஆறாதே நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு'). In both, fire is the harm that ends, used to measure a harm that lasts; Parimelazhagar on 202 makes this explicit, saying fire cannot follow one into another time, place or body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:202","b":"k:896","type":"parallel_domain","note":"202 ranks evil deeds above fire as a thing to fear. 896, in the Book of Wealth, says one burned by fire may still survive ('எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம்') but those who keep wronging the great will not. The same comparison, fire as the survivable danger, is applied once to one's own deeds and once to the anger of the powerful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"203 calls it the chief of all understanding to refrain from doing evil even to those who hate one ('செறுவார்க்கும் செய்யா விடல்'). 312 says that not hurting in return even one who has hurt you out of hatred ('கறுத்து இன்னா செய்த அக்கண்ணும்') is the settled stand of the stainless. The same rule is called knowledge (அறிவு) in the householder's chapter and purity (மாசற்றார் கோள்) in the ascetic's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:203","b":"k:987","type":"develops","note":"203 asks only for restraint: do no evil even to enemies. 987 asks what worth (சால்பு) is good for if it does not do pleasant things even to those who did one harm ('இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்'). The Book of Wealth's ideal of the சான்றோர் goes one step beyond the householder's rule, from not returning harm to returning good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:204","b":"k:319","type":"develops","note":"204 says that if one plots another's ruin, virtue plots one's own. 319 states the return in terms of time: harm done to others in the forenoon brings harm to oneself in the afternoon, of itself ('பிற்பகல் தாமே வரும்'). 319's 'தாமே' leaves the return impersonal where 204 gives it an agent, and Parimelazhagar on 204 adds the same point about speed: virtue begins plotting at the very moment the plotter does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:656","type":"develops","note":"205 rules out poverty as a ground for evil deeds. 656 takes the hardest case: even if one must see the mother who bore one go hungry ('ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்'), one should not do what the worthy condemn. Parimelazhagar notes that 656 sets aside a general rule of the dharma texts allowing wrong to feed one's dependants, which shows how far 205's principle is taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:205","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"205 quotes the plea 'இலன் என்று' as an excuse for wrongdoing. 1040 quotes the same plea, 'இலமென்று', as an excuse for sitting idle, and says the good woman called Land laughs at those who make it. The book hears 'I have nothing' twice as a pretext, once for evil and once for sloth, and rejects it both times.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:206","b":"k:320","type":"agrees","note":"206: one who does not want afflicting things to overpower him ('நோய்ப்பால தன்னை அடல் வேண்டாதான்') should not do evil to others. 320: all pains rest on those who caused pain, so those who want freedom from pain cause none ('நோய் செய்யார் நோயின்மை வேண்டுபவர்'). The claim and its form (நோய், வேண்டு-) are nearly the same, stated once for the householder in the chapter on fearing evil deeds and once among the ascetic virtues.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:208","type":"develops","note":"207 says the enmity of deeds does not leave and follows behind to kill ('வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்'). 208 gives the image for it: the ruin of evildoers is like a person's shadow that does not leave and stays at the foot ('வீயாது அடி உறைந்தற்று'). The repeated 'வீயாது' ties them, and both Manakkudavar and Parimelazhagar say 208 illustrates what 207 asserted.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:207","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"207 treats one's evil deeds as an enemy ('வினைப்பகை') worse than any outward one. 434, advising the king, says fault itself is the enemy that brings ruin ('குற்றமே அற்றம் தரூஉம் பகை') and so guarding against fault should be one's aim. The two verses, one in the Book of Virtue and one in the Book of Wealth, both move the real enemy from outside to one's own conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:208","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"208: the ruin of those who did evil is like a shadow that never leaves a person. 36: virtue done now is, at the time one perishes, a companion that does not perish ('பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை'). Both say a person's deeds stay with him; the good deed stays as a companion and the evil deed's ruin as a shadow at his heel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:208","b":"k:307","type":"parallel_domain","note":"208 uses a simile of something that cannot fail to happen: ruin stays with the evildoer as his shadow stays at his foot. 307 does the same for anger: the ruin of one who treats anger as a thing of worth is like the hand of one who strikes the ground, which cannot miss ('கை பிழையாதற்று'). Both verses take 'கேடு' as their subject and end on a simile of certainty, one for evil deeds and one for anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:209","b":"k:843","type":"develops","note":"209 assumes that doing evil is acting against oneself: one who loves himself stays away from it. 843 says the suffering that those without understanding inflict on themselves is hard even for their enemies ('செறுவார்க்கும்') to inflict. 843 describes the person who fails 209's test, and Parimelazhagar's comment there names poverty, blame and sin as what such people bring on themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:116","type":"agrees","note":"210: know ('அறிக') that one who does not turn aside to do evil has no ruin. 116: if one's heart leaves what is fair and does wrong, let one know ('அறிக') 'I shall perish' ('கெடுவல் யான்'). They state the same law from its two sides with the same imperative, 210 for the one who keeps straight and 116 for the one who leaves the middle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:210","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"210 promises that the one who does no evil will not come to ruin. 117 says the world will not count as ruin ('கெடுவாக வையாது') the low state (தாழ்வு) of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. 117 allows that the upright may be brought low and changes what counts as 'கேடு', which limits how literally 210's promise can be taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:211","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"211 says duty-giving seeks no return (கைம்மாறு). 221, opening the next chapter, defines giving as giving to those who have nothing, and says everything else has the nature of 'குறியெதிர்ப்பை', a measured loan to be returned. 221 turns 211's point into a test: a gift that looks to a return is not giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:212","b":"k:613","type":"develops","note":"212 says all the wealth produced by exertion ('தாளாற்றித் தந்த பொருள்') is for doing 'வேளாண்மை', benefaction. 613 says the proud distinction called benefaction ('வேளாண்மை என்னும் செருக்கு') rests in the quality called exertion ('தாளாண்மை'). The same two words are paired from opposite ends: in the Book of Virtue helping others is the purpose of earning, and in the Book of Wealth effort is the basis that makes helping possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:212","b":"k:1006","type":"develops","note":"212 says wealth is for benefaction, with 'தக்கார்க்கு' read by Manakkudavar and Kalaignar as the worthy who receive it and by Parimelazhagar as the worthy who own it. 1006 describes the failure of this purpose: one who neither enjoys his great wealth nor gives anything to the worthy ('தக்கார்க்கு ஒன்று ஈதல் இயல்பிலாதான்') is an affliction (ஏதம்) to it. 1006 uses 'தக்கார்க்கு' plainly of recipients, which supports the Manakkudavar reading of 212.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:213","b":"k:222","type":"agrees","note":"213 ranks ஒப்புரவு above anything to be had in the gods' world or this one. 222 says giving is good even if there were no higher world ('மேலுலகம் இல்லெனினும் ஈதலே நன்று'). Both detach the worth of helping others from heaven as a reward: in 213 heaven holds nothing better, and in 222 the good stands even without heaven.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:213","b":"k:363","type":"parallel_domain","note":"213: neither in the world of the gods nor here ('புத்தேள் உலகத்தும் ஈண்டும்') is it easy to find another good like ஒப்புரவு. 363: there is no excellent wealth here like not wanting, and nothing there equals it either ('ஈண்டில்லை ஆண்டும் அஃதொப்பது இல்'). The same two-world formula crowns benevolence in the householder's section and desirelessness in the renouncer's; taken literally the two superlatives compete, and the pairing shows each section naming its own highest good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:214","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 'ஒத்தது' in 214 as the way of the world (உலகநடை): one who knows and follows it lives. 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world ('உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்') lack understanding however much they have studied. On that reading both make accord with the world the test of a person, of being alive in 214 and of having understanding in 140; Manakkudavar instead takes ஒத்தது as ஒப்புரவு itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:217","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"217 makes a tree the figure of a generous man's wealth: a tree that is medicine and does not fail ('மருந்தாகித் தப்பா மரம்'). 576 makes a tree the figure of people who have eyes but no kindly regard: they are like trees joined to the soil ('மண்ணோடு இயைந்த மரத்தனையர்'). The book uses the tree in two ways, for what gives to all who come to it and for what stands unmoved, so the image alone carries no fixed value.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:218","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"218: even at a time without means ('இடனில் பருவத்தும்'), those who see what is due do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு. 955: even when what they have to give has shrunk ('வழங்குவது உள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும்'), an old family does not part from its character. The Book of Virtue credits this constancy to insight into duty ('கடனறி காட்சி'), and the Book of Wealth credits it to lineage ('பழங்குடி').","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:219","b":"k:89","type":"parallel_domain","note":"219 redefines poverty for the benevolent man: it is not lacking goods but being unable to do good with them. 89 redefines it from the other side: poverty in the midst of having ('உடைமையுள் இன்மை') is the folly of not tending guests. In both verses poverty is measured by what one does for others and not by what one holds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:780","type":"parallel_domain","note":"220: if ruin comes through ஒப்புரவு, that ruin is worth getting even by selling oneself ('விற்றுக்கோள் தக்கது உடைத்து'). 780: a death at which the eyes of one's lord fill with tears is worth getting even by begging ('இரந்துகோள் தக்கது உடைத்து'). The same closing formula takes something normally shunned, ruin or death, and declares it worth a degrading price, once for the benefactor and once for the soldier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:220","b":"k:1080","type":"same_image","note":"220 speaks of a man selling himself ('ஒருவன் விற்றுக்கோள்') as a price worth paying for the ruin that benevolence brings. 1080 says the base are fit for one thing: when something befalls them, to sell themselves quickly ('விற்றற்கு உரியர் விரைந்து'). Self-sale is honourable in the first as the cost of giving and contemptible in the second as an escape from one's own trouble.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1054","type":"tension","note":"222 says taking is bad even if it were called a good path (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது). 1054 says that asking from those who do not know concealment even in a dream is itself like giving (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). They conflict if கொளல் is read, with Parimelazhagar, as accepting alms without exception; on Kalaignar's reading of கொளல் as taking goods from others by whatever fair means, the two address different acts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:222","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"222 holds receiving to be bad whatever is said in its favour. 1061 says that not asking is worth a crore even from those dear as one's eyes who give gladly without hiding (கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார் கண்ணும் இரவாமை). Both refuse the best case that could be made for receiving: 222 the claim that it is a good path, 1061 the excellence of the giver.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:223","b":"k:205","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the quoted plea 'I have nothing': 223 has இலன் என்னும் எவ்வம், 205 has இலன் என்று தீயவை செய்யற்க. In 205 the plea is an excuse for doing evil and is answered with the warning that one will have nothing all over again; in 223 it is a plea the well-born do not voice. In both the book refuses to let want serve as a reason.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:223","b":"k:1044","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's first reading, 223 says the well-born do not speak to others the beggar's degrading word 'I am poor'. 1044 says that even in the well-born (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்ணேயும்) poverty produces the lapse from which degrading words are born (இளிவந்த சொற்பிறக்கும் சோர்வு). The conflict depends on that reading of 223; if 'இலன்' is the giver's refusal to an asker, as Pope takes it, the two speak of different utterances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:90","type":"same_image","note":"In 224 the giver watches the asker's face and is uneasy until it turns glad. In 90 the guest watches the host's face and wilts, like the anicham flower when smelled, if that face changes (முகந்திரிந்து நோக்க). The face is the measure of the exchange in both, read in opposite directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:228","type":"develops","note":"224 says the unease of being asked lasts until the asker's face is glad. 228 names what follows as a pleasure in its own right, ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம், which Parimelazhagar explains as the joy the giver has from the receiver's rejoicing. 228 then uses that pleasure as a charge against those who hoard and lose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:224","b":"k:1057","type":"parallel_domain","note":"224 gives the giver's side: being asked is painful (இன்னாது இரக்கப்படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face (இன்முகம்). 1057 gives the asker's side: on seeing those who give without scorn or contempt, the heart rejoices deep within (உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து). The two describe one moment from both ends, and the asker's gladness in 1057 is what ends the giver's discomfort in 224.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:226","b":"k:228","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the act of putting wealth away (வை). In 226 feeding the destitute is the வைப்புழி, the place of deposit; in 228 the hard-eyed 'keep and lose' what they own (வைத்திழக்கும்). The chapter sets a store that holds beside a store that is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:227","b":"k:85","type":"agrees","note":"227 promises that the one who shares his food will not meet hunger. 85 asks whether the field of one who feeds guests and eats what is left even needs sowing (வித்தும் இடல் வேண்டும் கொல்லோ). Both state as a rule that feeding others first secures one's own food.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:227","b":"k:1107","type":"same_image","note":"227 praises habitual shared eating (பாத்தூண்). In 1107 the man compares his beloved's embrace to living in one's own home and eating one's own food after sharing it (தமது பாத்து உண்டற்று). The lover's simile takes the householder's sharing of 227 as its standard of pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:598","type":"parallel_domain","note":"228 speaks of a pleasure the hoarder never learns, ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம். 598, which Parimelazhagar applies to kings, says those without inner drive never reach the proud joy of saying 'we have been generous' (வள்ளியம் என்னும் செருக்கு). The pleasure of giving is missed through hardness in the one verse and through lack of energy in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:228","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"228 says only that the hoarder keeps his goods and loses them. 1009 says how: bright wealth gathered by giving up love, denying oneself and ignoring virtue is taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The later verse fills in the 'losing' of 228.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:229","b":"k:527","type":"parallel_domain","note":"229 condemns those who eat alone to build up their own wealth. 527 says crows do not hide their food but call and eat (காக்கை கரவா கரைந்துண்ணும்), and that gain belongs only to those of that nature. The rule against eating alone appears in the ethics of giving in 229 and as advice on keeping kin and prosperity in 527.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:230","b":"k:968","type":"parallel_domain","note":"230 holds death sweet for one who can no longer give. 968 asks whether a life of tending the flesh is any medicine once greatness has lost its dignity (பீடழிய வந்த இடத்து). In both a life is not worth keeping once the thing that gave it worth is gone: giving in 230, honour in 968.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:230","b":"k:1041","type":"qualifies","note":"1041 says that the only thing as painful as poverty is poverty itself (இன்மையின் இன்மையே இன்னாதது). 230 opens by saying nothing is more painful than death (சாதலின் இன்னாதது இல்லை), then reverses it for the person who cannot give. Taken together, 230's second half brings it into line with 1041: the want that stops giving is placed above death in bitterness.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:31","type":"same_image","note":"231 ends 'ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு' and 31 ends 'ஆக்கம் எவனோ உயிர்க்கு': both use the figure of profit to a living being. 31 gives the place to virtue in general, which yields distinction and wealth; 231 gives it to renown that comes from giving. The shared formula shows 231 naming one particular form of the gain that 31 states broadly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:231","b":"k:122","type":"tension","note":"231 says that apart from living with renown through giving there is no profit for a living being (அது அல்லது ஊதியம் இல்லை உயிர்க்கு). 122 says no gain for a living being is greater than restraint (ஆக்கம் அதனினூஉங்கு இல்லை உயிர்க்கு). Read literally the two exclusive claims cannot both hold; the conflict disappears if each is taken as the emphasis its own chapter gives its subject.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:232","b":"k:296","type":"tension","note":"232 makes giving the subject of all praise. 296 says there is no renown like not lying (பொய்யாமை அன்ன புகழில்லை). They conflict if both are read as naming the single greatest source of renown; Parimelazhagar's gloss on 296, that truthfulness is the cause of renown in this life, keeps the claim just as strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:232","b":"k:387","type":"parallel_domain","note":"232 says the world's speech settles as praise on the giver. 387 says that for one able to give and protect with sweet words (இன்சொலால் ஈத்தளிக்க வல்லார்க்கு), this world is as his own word would have it. In the chapter on kingship the same giving that earns the world's words in 232 earns its compliance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:233","b":"k:156","type":"agrees","note":"233 says only renown stands undying. 156 says those who punish have a day's pleasure, while those who forbear have renown பொன்றுந் துணையும், which Pope and Parimelazhagar take as until the world ends. 156 measures a virtue's reward by the lasting quality that 233 attributes to renown, and gives it a source other than giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:234","b":"k:966","type":"same_image","note":"234 joins earthly renown (புகழ்) to honour in the gods' world (புத்தேள் உலகு). 966 names the same pair as the two things lost by following those who scorn you: it brings no renown and does not carry one to the gods' land (புகழ் இன்றால் புத்தேள் நாட்டு உய்யாதால்). In both verses renown here and the gods' world there are counted together, as gained in 234 and forfeited in 966.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:235","b":"k:117","type":"agrees","note":"235 says a loss can be an increase. 117 says the world will not set down as ruin (கெடுவாக வையாது உலகம்) the low state of one who stays in virtue by standing fair. Both deny that the decline of a good person's fortunes is a real loss, 235 from the side of renown and 117 from the world's judgement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:235","b":"k:780","type":"parallel_domain","note":"235 calls rare a death that is really a coming into existence. 780 says that if one can die so that the eyes of those who sustained one fill with tears, that death (சாக்காடு) is worth getting even by begging. The soldier's death in 780 is an instance, in the domain of war, of the death 235 says only the skilled achieve.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:362","type":"tension","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading of தோன்றுதல் as birth, 236 treats being born with renown as good and not being born as only the better alternative for those without it. 362 says that if one wants anything it should be not to be born (வேண்டுங்கால் வேண்டும் பிறவாமை), with no exception. The conflict depends on the birth reading; on Kalaignar's reading, where 236 is about entering a field of activity, the two do not meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:972","type":"qualifies","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 236 as: if born human, be born with the qualities that lead to renown. 972 says birth is alike for all living beings (பிறப்பொக்கும் எல்லா உயிர்க்கும்) and distinction differs through the difference in what they do. On the birth reading, 972 limits 236: no one is born with renown, so the 'appearing with renown' has to be made by deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:236","b":"k:1003","type":"same_image","note":"236 says: if you appear, appear with renown, and for those without it not appearing is better than appearing (தோன்றலின் தோன்றாமை நன்று). 1003 says the appearance (தோற்றம்) of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown (இசை வேண்டா) is a burden to the land. Both judge a person's coming into the world by renown, and 1003 names the misers as a case of those who 'appear' without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:237","b":"k:1060","type":"parallel_domain","note":"237 tells those without renown to blame themselves and not their scorners. 1060 tells the asker not to be angry at a refusal, since the affliction of his own want is witness enough (நிரப்பிடும்பை தானேயும் சாலும் கரி). In both the grievance against another person is turned back to one's own condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:238","b":"k:1004","type":"develops","note":"238 says it is a disgrace to leave no legacy of renown. 1004 asks what the man whom no one wants (நச்சப்படாதவன்) supposes will remain as his எச்சம்; Parimelazhagar explains that he is unwanted because he has never given. 1004 applies 238's standard to the miser and finds nothing left.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:189","type":"same_image","note":"In 239 the land bears (பொறுத்த) a body without renown and yields less. 189 asks whether it is out of regard for virtue that the earth carries the weight (பொறை) of one who speaks ill of a person behind his back. Both picture the earth as bearing a moral load, and they differ in that 189's earth endures while 239's earth declines.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:560","type":"parallel_domain","note":"239 says the land's rich yield shrinks (வண்பயன் குன்றும்) under a person without renown. 560 says the yield of cows shrinks (ஆபயன் குன்றும்) if the guardian does not guard. The same phrase ties natural yield to human conduct, a private person's in 239 and the king's in 560.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:239","b":"k:1003","type":"agrees","note":"239 says land that has borne a body without renown (இசையிலா யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்) will decline in its yield. 1003 says the appearance of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown is a burden to the land (நிலக்குப் பொறை). Both make the person without renown a weight the earth carries; 239 adds the cost to the earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:970","type":"develops","note":"240 says that to live without renown is not to live. 970 says the world worships and praises the radiance of those with honour who will not live when disgrace comes (இளிவரின் வாழாத மானம் உடையார்). 970 shows a person acting on 240's equation, and the result is the renown 240 demands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:240","b":"k:1033","type":"same_image","note":"240 uses the formula வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார் for those who live without blame. 1033 uses the same formula for those who live by eating what they plough (உழுதுண்டு வாழ்வாரே வாழ்வார்), with all others following and bowing for their food. The phrase 'they alone live' is awarded on two different grounds, a good name in 240 and self-reliance in 1033.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:72","type":"same_image","note":"72 says the loveless keep everything for themselves, while the loving belong to others even in their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). 251 describes the reverse movement: another's body (பிறிது ஊன்) taken to build one's own. Read together, the body is the measure in both: love gives it over, and the eater of flesh takes another's.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:315","type":"develops","note":"315 asks what understanding has achieved if one does not guard another's pain as one's own (பிறிதின்நோய் தந்நோய்போல்). 251 sets the same pair, self and other (தன் ஊன் / பிறிது ஊன்), the wrong way round: the other's flesh serves one's own. 315 gives the general test that 251's rhetorical question about அருள் applies to eating.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:251","b":"k:968","type":"same_image","note":"251 asks how one who eats another's flesh to enlarge his own (தன்னூன் பெருக்கற்கு) can exercise அருள்; 968 asks whether a life that nurses the flesh (ஊன் ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை) is any medicine once dignity is lost. Both use ஊன், one's own flesh, for a body tended at the cost of something higher: அருள் in one verse, honour in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:252","b":"k:247","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses explain அருள் by putting it beside பொருள்: 247 says that world is not for those without அருள் just as this world is not for those without wealth, and 252 says holding அருள் (அருளாட்சி) is not for flesh-eaters just as holding wealth (பொருளாட்சி) is not for those who do not guard it. 252 takes the comparison one step on: அருள், like wealth, is something that can be lost through carelessness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:252","b":"k:376","type":"tension","note":"252 takes it as given that holding wealth is not for those who do not look after it (பொருளாட்சி போற்றாதார்க்கு இல்லை). 376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however anxiously it is guarded, and what is one's own will not leave even if carried out and poured away (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் ... சொரியினும் போகா தம). The conflict holds if ஆட்சி is read as possession, as Manakkudavar and Pope read it; Parimelazhagar reads it as getting the benefit of wealth, which softens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:253","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"253 says the mind of one who has eaten a creature's body with relish does not turn to good (நன்னூக்காது), like the heart of one holding a weapon. 285 says a loving nature with அருள் in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's lapse. In both, what the mind is fixed on, flesh or another's property, leaves no room for அருள்; Parimelazhagar glosses 253 the same way, as a mind that looks only to the meat.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:254","b":"k:246","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use பொருள் in an unusual, moral sense alongside அருள். 246 says those who leave அருள் and do what should not be done have left பொருள் and forgotten (பொருள்நீங்கிப் பொச்சாந்தார்); 254 calls eating flesh பொருளல்லது. Parimelazhagar takes பொருள் in 254 as virtue (so that பொருளல்லது is sin) and in 246 as the lasting good one should have done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:255","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 says Death, the eater of life (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று), does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. 255 gives hell a mouth (அண்ணாத்தல்), which on Parimelazhagar's reading has swallowed the eater of flesh and will not open again. In both the language of eating is turned on the human: the one who spares life is not eaten, and the one who eats is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:255","b":"k:835","type":"same_image","note":"255 says that if one eats flesh, அளறு (the mire, hell) will not open its mouth (அண்ணாத்தல் செய்யாது), which Parimelazhagar explains as not opening to let him out again. 835 says a fool can in one birth make the அளறு he will enter and sink in for seven (எழுமையும் தான் புக்கழுந்தும் அளறு). Both use the same rare word for a hell one makes for oneself and does not get out of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:256","b":"k:1059","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1059 asks what distinction givers could have if there were none who wished to ask and receive (இரந்துகோள் மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை). 256 says none would bring flesh to sell if the world did not kill for eating. Both reason from a counterfactual about one party to the existence of the other: askers make givers possible, and eaters make sellers of flesh possible.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:257","b":"k:575","type":"same_image","note":"575 says that without kindly regard the eye is perceived as a wound (புண்ணென்று உணரப்படும்); 257 says flesh would not be eaten if people perceived it as another creature's wound (புண்ணது உணர்வார்). Both use புண் with உணர் to strip something of its usual standing, and both are about compassion: the eye without it is a sore, and food seen with it is a sore.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:258","b":"k:174","type":"parallel_domain","note":"174 says those of faultless vision who have conquered the senses (புலம்வென்ற புன்மையில் காட்சியவர்) will not covet, even thinking 'we have nothing'. 258 says those whose vision is free of fault do not eat flesh. Both attach an abstention that costs something, from another's goods or from meat, to காட்சி, and 174 adds the mastery of the senses that 258 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:259","b":"k:295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"295 says one who speaks truth in accord with his heart is above those who practise austerity together with giving (தவத்தொடு தானஞ்செய்வாரின் தலை). 259 says not killing one creature and eating it is better than a thousand sacrifices. Both rank a plain restraint, not lying and not killing, above the religious practices that carry the most prestige.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:260","b":"k:326","type":"agrees","note":"326 says Death, the eater of life, does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing; 260 says all living beings fold their hands (கைகூப்பி) and worship the one who does not kill and refuses flesh. Both state what the non-killer receives in return, and both put it as a response of life itself: living beings honour him, and the taker of life leaves him alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:261","b":"k:157","type":"parallel_domain","note":"157 says that though others do you wrong, it is good to feel the pain and not do what is contrary to virtue (நோநொந்து அறனல்ல செய்யாமை). 261 defines தவம் by the same two parts: bearing pain, and causing none to living beings. The chapter on forbearance asks of the householder, in dealings with those who wrong him, what 261 gives as the whole form of austerity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:273","type":"develops","note":"262 says தவம் comes to those who already have தவம், and that for those without it taking it up is futile; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read this as merit from earlier births. 273 pictures a person without strength (வலியில் நிலைமையான்) who takes on the form of the strong: a cow grazing in a tiger's skin. 273 shows what the futile undertaking of 262 looks like in practice. (The edition's text of 262 lacks the word அவம் at the start of the second line.)","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:373","type":"parallel_domain","note":"373 says that however many subtle books one studies, the understanding that is truly one's own still prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்). 262 says austerity too succeeds only for those who already have it. Both hold that a discipline taken up now, study or austerity, yields only according to what the person brings to it; Parimelazhagar refers both to what earlier births have fixed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:262","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"262 says that for those without தவம், undertaking it is futile. 620 says those who toil on without flagging or delay will see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்). If the தவம் one must already have is merit from former births, as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it, then 262 makes present effort useless without a prior endowment, which is what 620 denies. On Kalaignar's reading, where it means present firmness and self-control, the conflict disappears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 names the householder as the support of those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்கும் ... இல்வாழ்வான் என்பான் துணை), along with the destitute and the dead. 263 asks, with a wry கொல், whether the rest have forgotten தவம் out of a wish to provide for renouncers (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). It turns the duty stated in 42 into an explanation, or an excuse, for why householders do no austerity themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:48","type":"qualifies","note":"263 speaks of the others, householders on Parimelazhagar's reading, as having forgotten தவம் while they provide for renouncers. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their way and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar reads 'keeps on their way' as supporting ascetics. On 48's account the provider has not forgotten austerity: his life already contains more of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:263","b":"k:1036","type":"agrees","note":"1036 says that if ploughmen fold their hands, even those who say 'we have given up desire' (விட்டேம் என்பார்) cannot keep their state. 263 assumes the same dependence: renouncers need others to supply their needs (துப்புரவு). Both verses note that renunciation rests on people who have not renounced.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:29","type":"agrees","note":"29 says the anger of those who stand on the hill of good qualities cannot be withstood even for a moment (கணமேயும் காத்தல் அரிது), on Parimelazhagar's and Pope's reading; Kalaignar takes it to mean their anger does not last a moment. On the first reading it agrees with 264 that renouncers hold a power to ruin those who oppose them, though 264 makes it a matter of intention (எண்ணின்) and 29 of anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:261","type":"tension","note":"261 says the form of தவம் is just this: bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை அற்றே). 264 says crushing foes (ஒன்னார்த் தெறல்) and raising friends come through தவம், if one so intends. Read plainly, austerity defined by harmlessness is credited three verses later with the power to destroy. Parimelazhagar eases this by taking the foes as enemies of virtue and by reading எண்ணின் as implying that ascetics do not normally think of such things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:264","b":"k:899","type":"agrees","note":"899 says that if those of exalted resolve flare up (ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின்), even the king breaks midway and loses his rule. Parimelazhagar reads them as people of high vows, and Pope likewise. This is the power of 264, ஒன்னார்த் தெறல் through austerity, seen from the side of the king who must not provoke it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:362","type":"qualifies","note":"265 says austerity is practised because through it whatever is wanted is obtained as wanted. 362 says that if one must want, one should want not to be born, and that this comes by wanting not to want (வேண்டாமை வேண்ட வரும்). 362 narrows what the வேண்டிய of 265 should be; Parimelazhagar reads 265 itself as referring to results in the next life and release, while Manakkudavar takes it to include enjoyments.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:265","b":"k:666","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses share a formula: வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால் (265), what is wanted is obtained as it is wanted, and எண்ணிய எண்ணியாங்கு எய்துப (666), what is thought of is obtained as it was thought. 265 gives this as the reason austerity is striven for; 666 makes it depend on the planner's firmness (திண்ணியர் ஆகப் பெறின்). The promise made for austerity in the book of virtue is made for resolute action in the book of statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:266","b":"k:348","type":"agrees","note":"348 divides people the same way: those who have thoroughly renounced have reached the head, and the rest, confused, are caught in the net (மயங்கி வலைப்பட்டார் மற்றையவர்). 266 says those who do தவம் do their own work and the rest act in vain, caught in desire (ஆசையுட் பட்டு). Both use பட்டு / பட்டார், being caught, for everyone outside the ascetic's path; Parimelazhagar supplies 'the net of desire' in his gloss on 266.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:267","b":"k:308","type":"same_image","note":"308 says that even if harm is done to you like the touch of massed flames (இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன), not being angry is good if one can manage it. 267 says pain that burns (துன்பஞ் சுடச்சுட) makes those who can endure it shine like gold in fire. Both picture suffering as fire applied to a person; 308 asks only that it be borne without anger, and 267 says what the bearing produces.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:267","b":"k:630","type":"parallel_domain","note":"267 says that for those able to endure, pain burning them makes them shine (ஒளிவிடும்), as gold shines the more it is heated. 630 says that if one takes what hurts as pleasure (இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின்), there comes a distinction even one's enemies admire. The ascetic's gain from pain in the first book has a worldly counterpart in the second: in both, pain borne in the right way adds to the person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:269","b":"k:620","type":"parallel_domain","note":"620 says those who toil on without flagging will see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்); 269 says even getting past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தலும்) is within reach of those with the power of austerity. Each uses the particle உம், 'even', to name the one thing thought unbeatable and then says sustained effort beats it: labour in the book of statecraft, austerity in the book of virtue. (The edition's text of 269 ends with a stray letter, தலைப்பட்டவர்க்குல்.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:616","type":"parallel_domain","note":"616 says effort creates prosperity and the absence of effort brings in want (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்). 270 says the have-nots (இலர்) are many because few practise austerity (நோற்பார் சிலர்). Both trace poverty to something people fail to do, but they name different things: present exertion in the chapter on labour, and austerity here, which the commentators take to bear fruit across births.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:270","b":"k:842","type":"agrees","note":"842 says that when a person without understanding gives gladly, the cause is nothing other than the receiver's தவம் (பெறுவான் தவம்). 270 says the have-nots are many because those who practise austerity are few. Both explain who gets and who lacks by austerity, and 842 shows the idea used in passing in the second book, as an accepted explanation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:271","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"271 says the five elements laugh within (பூதங்கள் ஐந்தும் அகத்தே நகும்) at the concealed conduct of a deceitful mind; 293 says that after a lie about what one's own heart knows, that heart itself burns one (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). Both place the witness to a hidden fault inside the person; 293 moves it from the body's elements, which only laugh, to the heart, which punishes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:271","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"In 271 the five elements laugh within at a hypocrite's hidden conduct; in 1040 the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்) at those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing'. In both a part of the material world is the one that laughs at a human pretence, and Parimelazhagar glosses the laugh in 1040 too as inward (தன்னுள்ளே).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:272","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue and the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற); 272 says a sky-high appearance (வானுயர் தோற்றம்) does nothing when the heart is in known fault. 272 applies the general rule of 34 to the specific case of the ascetic's appearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:273","b":"k:24","type":"develops","note":"24 praises the one who guards the five senses with the goad called firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான்); 273 likens one of strengthless nature (வலியில் நிலைமையான்) who takes the form of the strong to a cow grazing in a tiger's skin. Parimelazhagar reads the missing strength in 273 as the power to bring the mind under control, which is what 24 names as the mark of the real renouncer, so 273 shows the outward form of 24 without its substance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:274","b":"k:261","type":"develops","note":"261 gives the form of தவம் as bearing one's own pain and doing no harm to living beings (உயிர்க்குறுகண் செய்யாமை); 274 speaks of doing 'what is not it' (அல்லவை) under cover of தவம் and compares this to a hunter snaring birds. Read together, the hunter simile shows the hypocrite doing the very thing, harm to creatures, whose absence 261 makes the definition of austerity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:274","b":"k:828","type":"parallel_domain","note":"274 has the hunter hidden in the bush as the image of evil done under cover of austerity; 828 says a weapon hides even in an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and likewise in his tears. In both a gesture of piety is the cover for harm, once in ascetic life and once in false friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:275","b":"k:655","type":"same_image","note":"275 says the concealed conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' brings many harms with the cry எற்றெற்று; 655 says do not do what will make you grieve saying எற்று ('what is this I have done'). The same exclamation of regret marks the test in both, in the renouncer's conduct and in the minister's action; Manakkudavar, however, takes the cry in 275 as other people's, not the doer's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:276","b":"k:42","type":"develops","note":"42 makes the householder the support of those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்கும் ... துணை); 276 says none are more hard-eyed than those who, unrenounced at heart, live by deceiving as if they had renounced (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து வாழ்வார்). Parimelazhagar takes the deceived to be the givers, so the pairing shows what the false renouncer exploits: the duty of support that 42 lays on the householder.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:276","b":"k:1198","type":"same_image","note":"276 ends 'வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்': none are harder than those who live by posing as renouncers. 1198, spoken by the woman in separation, ends with the same words: none in the world are harder than those who go on living without a kind word from the one they love. The shared formula uses வன்கண் in two senses, the cruelty of the deceiver in 276 and the grim endurance of the one left behind in 1198.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:706","type":"qualifies","note":"706 says the face shows what is strongest in the heart, as crystal shows what is next to it (நெஞ்சம் கடுத்தது காட்டும் முகம்); 277 says there are people red outside and black inside. 277 limits 706: the face may betray a passing feeling to a skilled reader, but a settled outward appearance can be the opposite of the inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:824","type":"parallel_domain","note":"277 says the world has people who look outwardly like the red kunri seed but are inwardly black like its tip (புறம் ... அகம்); 824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய ... அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சர்). The same outside/inside split is described for the false ascetic and for the false friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:277","b":"k:965","type":"same_image","note":"277 uses the kunri seed for its two colours, red body and black tip; 965 uses it for its size, saying even those like a hill sink if they do a lowering thing as small as a kunri (குன்றி அனைய). The same seed serves both verses as a figure for a small dark flaw in something that otherwise looks fine, by colour in one and by measure in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"279 says the straight arrow is cruel and the bent lute is upright, so take people by the nature of their deeds (வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்); 505 says one's own deeds are the touchstone of greatness and smallness (கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்). The same rule appears as a test for ascetics in the book of virtue and as a test for choosing officers in the book of statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:279","b":"k:667","type":"agrees","note":"279 warns against reading uprightness from shape, with the arrow and lute as examples; 667 says do not scorn on seeing someone's form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), because the great chariot has its small linchpin. One verse guards against being impressed by form and the other against despising it, and both replace form with function.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:281","b":"k:178","type":"parallel_domain","note":"281 tells one who wants not to be scorned to guard his heart from stealing anything at all (கள்ளாமை காக்க தன் நெஞ்சு); 178 says that what keeps one's wealth from shrinking is not coveting the goods in another's hand (வெஃகாமை ... பிறன்கைப் பொருள்). Each gives restraint toward others' property as the means to something one wants for oneself, standing in 281 and one's own wealth in 178; Parimelazhagar reads 281 differently, as about one esteemed for not despising release.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:282","b":"k:317","type":"agrees","note":"282 says even thinking in the mind (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளலும்) of taking another's goods by stealth is wrong; 317 says the highest thing is not to do, with the mind (மனத்தானாம்), anything ill to anyone at any time in any degree. Both place the fault at the level of intention before any act, for theft in one and for harm in general in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:283","b":"k:660","type":"agrees","note":"283 says gain made by stealth perishes beyond measure while seeming to grow (ஆவது போலக் கெடும்); 660 says making wealth by deceit (சலத்தால் பொருள்செய்து) and feeling secure in it is like keeping water in an unfired clay pot. The same claim, that wrongly got wealth looks held and is being lost, is made to the renouncer in one book and to the minister in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:284","b":"k:368","type":"develops","note":"368 says those without craving have no sorrow, and where craving exists sorrow comes on and on without fail (தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்); 284 says desire hardened in stealing (கன்றிய காதல்) yields sorrow that does not die (வீயா விழுமம்). 284 is the general law of 368 applied to one desire, with the same stress on sorrow that does not end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:284","b":"k:1092","type":"same_image","note":"284 speaks of காதல் grown in களவு and means desire for stealing, which ends in undying sorrow; in 1092 the man says the small look her eyes steal (கண் களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்) is more than half of love's pleasure. The same word களவு is condemned as theft of goods in the book of virtue and prized as the stolen glance in the book of love, where it is a lover's speech and not a teaching about theft.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:285","b":"k:251","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 says அருள் with love is not found in one who watches to take another's goods; 251 asks how one who eats another creature's flesh to swell his own can hold to அருள் (எங்ஙனம் ஆளும் அருள்). Both test a claim to அருள் against a practice of taking from another for oneself, property in one case and the body in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:285","b":"k:757","type":"qualifies","note":"285 opposes having அருள் in view to having பொருள் in view; 757 says the child அருள், born of அன்பு, is reared by the wealthy foster-mother பொருள். 757 limits how far 285 can be taken: the opposition holds for goods taken from others by watching for their lapses, not for wealth as such, which 757 makes the support of the same அருள் and அன்பு that 285 names.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:286","b":"k:474","type":"parallel_domain","note":"286 says those with hardened desire for stealing cannot stand and walk within அளவு (அளவின்கண் நின்றொழுகல் ஆற்றார்); 474 says the one who does not live in accord, does not know his measure (அளவறியான்) and admires himself perishes quickly. On Kalaignar's reading of அளவு in 286 as a limit one lives within, the two apply one idea, conduct kept inside a known measure, to the renouncer and to the king; Parimelazhagar instead takes அளவு in 286 as the reasoned measuring of the soul and its ends, on which reading the link is only verbal.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:287","b":"k:175","type":"agrees","note":"287 calls stealing a dark knowledge (களவென்னும் காரறி வாண்மை) that is not found in those set on the strength called அளவு; 175 asks what a fine and wide understanding (அஃகி அகன்ற அறிவு) comes to if, coveting, one acts senselessly toward everyone. Both deny that cleverness in the service of taking others' goods counts as real understanding.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:287","b":"k:422","type":"develops","note":"422 defines understanding as what does not let the mind go wherever it has gone, but turns it from evil toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது அறிவு); 287 grants stealing the name அறிவு only with the qualifier கார், dark. By 422's definition the thief's skill fails as அறிவு, which explains why 287 marks it as darkened.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:288","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 locates virtue in the mind being without stain; 288 says virtue stands in the heart of those who know அளவு just as concealment stands in the heart of those who know stealing (நெஞ்சத்து அறம் ... நெஞ்சில் கரவு). 288 adds to 34 that the heart is occupied either way: what one practises, measure or theft, decides whether virtue or deceit takes up residence there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:289","b":"k:479","type":"parallel_domain","note":"289 says those who know nothing but stealing do what is beyond measure and perish right there (அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர்); 479 says the life of one who does not live knowing the measure seems to exist, then is not, and perishes without trace. Both make ignoring அளவு the cause of ruin, in the renouncer's conduct and in the management of means; Parimelazhagar reads அளவல்ல in 289 as wrong thoughts and not as excess of spending, so the likeness is of pattern, not of subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:290","b":"k:121","type":"parallel_domain","note":"290 gives the two outcomes of stealing and not stealing: loss of உயிர்நிலை, and the world of the gods (புத்தேள் உலகு) not failing; 121 gives the two outcomes of restraint and its lack: a place among the immortals (அமரருள்) and thick darkness. Each closes or opens its chapter with the same two-sided reward and penalty attached to a single virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:290","b":"k:255","type":"same_image","note":"290 says that for those who steal the உயிர்நிலை fails, while for those who do not steal the world of the gods does not fail; 255 says உயிர்நிலை rests in not eating flesh, and that hell will not open its mouth for one who eats it. Both verses hang உயிர்நிலை, the standing of life in the body on Parimelazhagar's gloss, on one abstention and pair it with a fate after death; Manakkudavar reads the word in 290 as release itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:291","b":"k:292","type":"develops","note":"291 defines truth (வாய்மை) not as reporting what happened but as speech with no harm in it at all (யாதொன்றும் தீமை இலாத சொலல்). 292 draws the consequence: even a falsehood stands in truth's place (பொய்மையும் வாய்மை யிடத்த) if it yields faultless good. Read together, the second verse is the first's definition applied to its hardest case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:292","b":"k:113","type":"tension","note":"292 makes good results decisive: a falsehood that yields faultless good (நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்) counts as truth. 113 says gain got by leaving fairness is to be dropped that very day even if it yields nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்). The conflict holds only if both are read as answers to whether a good outcome can license a breach of a norm; 113 is about gain and 292 about speech, and 292's புரைதீர்ந்த (free of fault) may already exclude what 113 forbids.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:293","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"In 129 a burn from fire heals inside but the scar burned by the tongue (நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு) does not; in 293 the liar's own heart burns him (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). Both use burning for the lasting damage of speech: the harsh word burns the hearer, the false word burns the speaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:294","b":"k:119","type":"parallel_domain","note":"294 says one who lives without falsehood in the heart (உள்ளத்தாற் பொய்யாது) is present in the hearts of all the world. 119 says rectitude is speech without bend, provided there is surely no bend within (உட்கோட்டம் இன்மை). In truthfulness and in impartiality alike, straight speech is counted only when the inside matches it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:295","b":"k:48","type":"parallel_domain","note":"295 sets the truthful speaker above those who practise austerity and giving. 48 says a household life that keeps others on their way and does not slip from virtue has more endurance than those who do penance (நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து). In both, the ascetic is the standard of comparison and an ordinary life rightly lived is placed above it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:296","b":"k:297","type":"develops","note":"296 says not lying gives all the virtues (எல்லா அறமுந் தரும்); Parimelazhagar takes எய்யாமை as 'without wearying the body'. 297 then concludes that one who keeps steadily to not lying does well even without doing the other virtues (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை நன்று). The second verse depends on the first: the others can be left undone because this one already supplies them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:299","b":"k:601","type":"same_image","note":"299 names not lying the one true lamp for the worthy (பொய்யா விளக்கே விளக்கு). 601 calls lineage an undimming lamp (குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம்) that nevertheless goes out when the grime of sloth covers it. The lamp figure marks what a person's standing depends on, and 601 adds that such a lamp can be put out by neglect.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:301","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"301 counts restraint of anger only where the anger could have its way. 153 says the strength of strengths is bearing with fools (வன்மையுள் வன்மை மடவார்ப் பொறை). Both locate the real test of forbearance where one has the upper hand and nothing outside compels it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:302","b":"k:861","type":"tension","note":"302 says anger is bad where it cannot take effect, and where it can, nothing is worse (செல்லிடத்தும் இல்அதனின் தீய பிற); Parimelazhagar reads the two places as the stronger and the weaker. 861 advises avoiding opposition to the strong and pressing enmity against the weak (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). They conflict if 302 is taken as a rule for everyone; if the anger chapter is addressed to ascetics, as Parimelazhagar holds, and 861 to a king, they are advice for different people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:302","b":"k:895","type":"develops","note":"302's first half says anger where it cannot take effect is bad, which Parimelazhagar explains as harm coming back from the stronger party. 895 states that harm at its extreme: those a king of fierce might is angry with (வேந்து செறப்பட்டவர்) will not survive wherever they go. The chapter on not offending the great fills in what 302 leaves as a bare 'bad'.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:303","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says always bear with an excess done to you, and that forgetting it is better still (அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 303 uses the same verb for the anger itself: forget anger toward anyone (மறத்தல் வெகுளியை யார்மாட்டும்), since evils are born from it. One forgets the offence and the other the anger at it; 303 adds the reason.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:303","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"303 says evils are born from anger (தீய பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்). 360 names anger (வெகுளி) with desire and confusion as the three whose very names must perish for pain to perish. What 303 says of anger as a source of evils, 360 places in a full account of the roots of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:304","b":"k:999","type":"develops","note":"304 counts the death of laughter and joy (நகையும் உவகையும்) as the harm that makes anger the worst enemy. 999 says that for those not capable of laughing (நகல்வல்லர் அல்லார்க்கு) the great world lies in darkness even by day. 999 describes the state that 304 says anger produces.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:306","b":"k:526","type":"develops","note":"306 says anger, the fire that kills what it adjoins (சேர்ந்தாரைக் கொல்லி), burns even the safe raft of one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணை). 526 says no one on earth has more people around him than one who gives greatly and does not cherish anger (பேணான் வெகுளி). The two give the loss and the gain of the same fact: kin stay with the one who is free of anger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:306","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"306 calls one's people a safe raft (ஏமப் புணை) that anger burns. In 1164 the woman says the sea of love certainly exists and the safe raft to swim it (ஏமப் புணை) does not. The same phrase names what keeps a person afloat; in one verse a passion destroys it and in the other a passion leaves the speaker without one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:312","type":"develops","note":"308 says that even if someone does harm like the touch of massed flames (இணர்எரி தோய்வன்ன இன்னா செயினும்), not being angry is good, if one can manage it (புணரின்). 312 says that even when someone has done hurt in hatred, not doing hurt in return is the settled stand of the stainless (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை). 308 governs the feeling and 312 the act that would follow from it; 308 alone concedes that it may not be possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:308","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"308 asks for no anger even under harm like fire. 852, in the chapter on hostility in the Book of Wealth, says that even if someone does what cannot be borne, meaning division, not doing harm in return is best (இன்னா செய்யாமை தலை). The same advice, with the same 'even if ... செயினும்' form, is given as virtue in one book and as policy in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:487","type":"tension","note":"309 praises one who does not think of anger in the heart at all (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளான் வெகுளி). 487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly on the spot but, watching for the time, burn within (காலம்பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). They conflict if both describe how a wise person treats anger inwardly, one removing it and the other keeping it for later; the conflict lessens if 309 is for the ascetic, as Parimelazhagar reads it, and 487 for a ruler facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:309","b":"k:540","type":"parallel_domain","note":"309 says all one has thought of is gained at once (உள்ளியதெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்) if one never thinks of anger in the heart. 540 says gaining what one has thought of is easy (உள்ளியது எய்தல் எளிது) if one can keep thinking of it. Both promise the same result in nearly the same words and set a condition on the mind: one removes a thought, the other holds one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:311","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"113 tells one to let go, that very day, of gain that comes from leaving fairness even if it brings only good (நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கத்தை). 311 refuses wealth that would require hurting others (பிறர்க்கு இன்னா செய்யாமை). Both grant that the gain is real and still rule out the means, one in the householder's ethics and one in the renouncer's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:311","b":"k:755","type":"parallel_domain","note":"755, in the chapter on making wealth, says wealth that does not come with grace and love (அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாராப் பொருளாக்கம்) should be let go and not embraced. 311 makes not hurting others the settled stand of the stainless even at the cost of wealth. The condition that 311 states for the pure is repeated in the Book of Wealth as a rule for acquisition itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:312","b":"k:852","type":"parallel_domain","note":"852, on hostility, says that even if someone does unbearable things intending division, not doing hurt in return out of hostility is the highest course (இகல்கருதி இன்னாசெய்யாமை தலை). 312 says not hurting in return (மறுத்தின்னா செய்யாமை) is the stand of the stainless. The same rule, in nearly the same words, is carried from personal purity into the politics of faction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:313","b":"k:207","type":"develops","note":"313 says that doing hurt, even to those who hated you though you did nothing to them (செய்யாமல் செற்றார்க்கும்), brings sorrow with no escape (உய்யா விழுமம்). 207 says people escape any enmity (உய்வர்) but the enmity of their own deeds follows and kills (வினைப்பகை வீயாது பின்சென்று அடும்). The shared root உய் shows the reasoning: an enemy can be survived, one's own retaliation cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says bearing an excess done to you is good and forgetting it is better still (அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 314 ends on விடல்: do good and let it go. Parimelazhagar takes this விடல் as forgetting both the hurt and the good done in return, which would make 314 the fuller form of 152's advice; the Tamil itself says only 'do and leave'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:314","b":"k:158","type":"develops","note":"158 says to defeat those who did wrong out of arrogance by one's own worthiness (தம் தகுதியான் வென்று விடல்), which Parimelazhagar reads as forbearance. 314 goes a step further: do them a kindness so that they feel shame. Both keep the vocabulary of contest (winning, punishing) while replacing retaliation, and 314 adds the active good that 158 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:317","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்). 317 makes the highest thing the absence of any mind-born ill toward anyone (மனத்தானாம் மாணா செய்யாமை தலை), in a chapter whose first two verses speak of the மாசற்றார். 317 gives 34's general claim a specific content: the unstained mind is one that harbours no hurt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:318","b":"k:209","type":"parallel_domain","note":"209 says that if a person loves himself (தன்னைத்தான் காதலனாயின்) he should not come near evil deeds, however small. 318 asks why one who knows what hurts his own life does hurt to other lives. Both reason from self-regard, by different routes: 209 from the doer's own interest, 318 from the doer's own knowledge of pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:318","b":"k:316","type":"develops","note":"316 gives the rule: what one has perceived as painful, do not do to another (துன்னாமை வேண்டும் பிறன்கண் செயல்). 318 turns it into a question: one who knows what hurts his own life (தன்னுயிர்க்கு இன்னாமை தானறிவான்), why (என்கொலோ) does he hurt other lives? The second verse treats breaking the rule as something needing explanation; Parimelazhagar suggests delusion as the implied answer. (The edition's text of 318 has typos in its first line.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:319","b":"k:208","type":"same_image","note":"208 says the ruin of those who do evil is like a shadow that never leaves and rests at the foot (நிழல் ... அடி உறைந்தற்று). 319 puts the same inevitability in time: forenoon and afternoon (முற்பகல், பிற்பகல்). One verse uses space and the other the day's two halves to say that consequence is attached to the deed; the day figure also fits a shadow lengthening after noon, though the text does not say so.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:320","b":"k:169","type":"qualifies","note":"320 says all pains fall on those who caused pain (நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம்). 169 admits that the crooked-hearted prosper and the upright meet ruin, and says only that this is to be pondered (நினைக்கப் படும்). 169 concedes cases that 320's rule does not show on its face; Manakkudavar's reading of 320, that present pain comes from hurt done earlier, and Parimelazhagar's of 169, that such cases are to be examined for causes, are the way the commentators reconcile them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:331","b":"k:349","type":"develops","note":"349 says the moment attachment ends births are cut off, and otherwise impermanence is what is seen (மற்று நிலையாமை காணப்படும்). 331 faults the understanding that takes the unlasting for lasting. 349 explains why the error in 331 matters: for one still attached, impermanence is the standing condition, and Parimelazhagar on 331 likewise ties the error to attachment and rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:331","b":"k:351","type":"parallel_domain","note":"331 calls lowest the paltry understanding that takes what does not last for lasting (நில்லாதவற்றை நிலையின என்றுணரும்). 351 says a joyless birth comes from the confusion that takes what is not real for real (பொருளல்லவற்றைப் பொருளென்று உணரும் மருள்). The two verses are built on the same sentence frame; 331 applies the misperception to duration and 351 to reality, and 351 adds the consequence, rebirth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:112","type":"tension","note":"332 says great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) arrives and leaves like a theatre crowd, with no exception stated. 112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude does not decay and is a support to his descendants (சிதைவின்றி எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு உடைத்து). If 332 is read as a claim about all wealth, 112's durable wealth contradicts it; the conflict eases if 332 is taken, with Parimelazhagar, as wealth that stays only while its cause in good deeds lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:376","type":"qualifies","note":"376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however it is guarded, and what is one's own will not go even if thrown out (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல ... சொரியினும் போகா தம). 332 says wealth comes and goes like a crowd at a show. 376 puts a cause behind 332's picture: coming and going follow one's allotted portion; Parimelazhagar reads 332 the same way, the crowd coming for the dance and leaving when it ends.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:332","b":"k:592","type":"agrees","note":"332 likens the coming of great wealth to a crowd gathering at a dance hall and its going to that crowd dispersing. 592 says possession of goods does not stay and slips away (பொருளுடைமை நில்லாது நீங்கிவிடும்), while possession of inner drive is real possession. The impermanence of wealth taught to the renouncer is restated in the Book of Wealth, where it becomes a reason to value energy and not a reason to withdraw.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:333","b":"k:437","type":"agrees","note":"437 says the wealth of the miser who does not do with it what should be done (செயற்பால செய்யாது) perishes without anything surviving. 333 says wealth does not stay and should be turned at once into what does. The Book of Wealth gives the same instruction negatively: unused wealth is simply lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:334","b":"k:326","type":"tension","note":"334 presents each day as a blade cutting life, with no one exempted. 326 says Death, the eater of life, does not advance on the days (வாழ்நாள்மேல் செல்லாது) of one who lives by not killing. Read literally, 326 exempts the non-killer from the daily loss of life that 334 treats as universal; the conflict depends on taking 326 as a promise about lifespan and not as praise of the vow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:335","b":"k:342","type":"parallel_domain","note":"342 says if you want what renunciation brings, renounce while there is still something (உண்டாகத் துறக்க), which Parimelazhagar takes as 'while there is time'. 335 says to hurry to good deeds before the death rattle. On that reading the next chapter applies the same urgency to renouncing that 335 applies to good deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:336","b":"k:233","type":"qualifies","note":"336 says this world has the 'greatness' that one who was here yesterday is not here today (நெருநல் உளனொருவன் இன்றில்லை). 233 says nothing in the world stands undying except high renown (புகழல்லால் பொன்றாது நிற்பதொன்றில்). 233 agrees with 336 about everything else and names one exception; the renouncer's chapter offers none.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:336","b":"k:235","type":"qualifies","note":"336 says a person who was yesterday is not today (இன்றில்லை). 235 speaks of a death that is really existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு), possible only for the skilled, which Parimelazhagar explains as the body of fame surviving. The two verses use the same pair, உள and இல், in opposite directions: 336 for the plain fact of death and 235 for the rare death that leaves something that is.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:338","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"338 pictures the body as a குடம்பை, shell or nest, that the bird leaves empty; 340 pictures it as a lodging (துச்சில்) where life stays as a tenant without a home of its own (புக்கில்). Both make the body a dwelling and life its occupant. 338 shows the departure, and 340 asks why there has to be one; Manakkudavar takes the permanent home to be liberation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:339","b":"k:356","type":"qualifies","note":"339 presents death and birth as alternating like sleep and waking, which both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read as a cycle that keeps turning. 356 says those who have learned here and seen the true thing reach the path of not coming back here (மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி). 356 limits 339: the waking that follows each death is the rule only for those who have not seen the real.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:343","type":"qualifies","note":"341's doubled யாதனின் யாதனின் ... அதனின் அதனின் allows release thing by thing: each thing left stops hurting. 343 demands that everything wanted be let go together (வேண்டிய வெல்லாம் ஒருங்கு). Parimelazhagar notices the difference under 341, calling giving up everything at once the chief way while granting that giving things up one by one still removes the pain each brings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:626","type":"parallel_domain","note":"341 says that whatever one has left (யாதனின் நீங்கியான்), from that one has no pain (நோதல் இலன்). 626, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, asks whether those who never guarded wealth saying 'we have got it' (பெற்றேமென்று ஓம்புதல் தேற்றாதவர்) would grieve saying 'we have lost it' (அற்றேமென்று). The renouncer's rule reappears in the book of wealth as a habit of mind for someone who still owns things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:341","b":"k:629","type":"develops","note":"341 ties freedom from pain to having left the thing itself (நீங்கியான்). 629 says one who does not crave the pleasure in pleasure (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்) does not suffer the pain in pain; Parimelazhagar reads this as someone who goes on experiencing the pleasure without wanting it. The pairing shows the same result claimed for an inward letting go, without the outward leaving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"36 tells one to do virtue without saying 'we will see to it then' (அன்றறிவாம் என்னாது). 342's உண்டாகத் துறக்க is read by Parimelazhagar as 'renounce while there is time', i.e. in youth, so the joys last long; Manakkudavar instead reads it as 'if you want gain for your life'. On Parimelazhagar's reading the chapter on renunciation repeats, for renouncing, the earlier rule against postponing virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what a person who runs household life by the way of virtue would gain by going to the outside way (புறத்தாற்றில் போஒய்ப் பெறுவது எவன்). 342 says that after renouncing there are many things to be had here, and urges renouncing if one wants them (வேண்டின் ... துறக்க). The conflict depends on reading புறத்தாறு as the ascetic path, as Pope does: on that reading 46's rhetorical 'what is gained?' gets the answer 'much' in 342.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:342","b":"k:369","type":"agrees","note":"342 says that after renouncing, many things come about here (ஈண்டு இயற்பால பல), which Parimelazhagar takes as joys in this life. 369 says joy is unbroken even here (இன்பம் இடையறாது ஈண்டும்) once craving, the sorrow of sorrows, is gone. Both place the reward of letting go in this world (ஈண்டு), not only after death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:6","type":"agrees","note":"6 describes God as the one who quenched the five whose gates are the senses (பொறிவாயில் ஐந்தவித்தான்) and promises long life to those who keep to his path. 343 lays the same thing on the renouncer as a duty (அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை). The renouncer is asked to do what the invocation names as the mark of the Lord.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:343","b":"k:126","type":"develops","note":"126, in the householder's chapter on restraint, speaks of drawing in the five like a tortoise (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்), which protects through seven births. 343 uses a harder verb for the renouncer: the field of the five must be killed or crushed (அடல்வேண்டும் ஐந்தன் புலத்தை), and every wanted thing dropped at once. Read together they show two degrees of the same discipline, withdrawal that can be reversed and destruction that cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:263","type":"qualifies","note":"344 makes possessionlessness the nature of austerity. 263 asks whether the others have forgotten austerity out of a wish to provide for those who have renounced (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). The pairing shows that the ascetic's having nothing is sustained by householders who keep possessions in order to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1036","type":"qualifies","note":"344 says having nothing at all is natural to the vow (இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கு ஒன்றின்மை) and that owning anything brings back confusion. 1036 says that if ploughmen fold their hands, even those who say 'we have given up what is desired' have no standing (விட்டேம் என்பார்க்கும் நிலை இல்லை). The renouncer's owning nothing rests on others who produce food.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:344","b":"k:1050","type":"qualifies","note":"344 treats having nothing (ஒன்றின்மை) as the proper state of the vow. 1050 speaks of those who have nothing to enjoy (துப்புரவில்லார்) and yet do not fully renounce (துவரத் துறவாமை), calling them death to salt and gruel. Lacking things is therefore not the same as renouncing: poverty without the inner giving up is counted as a burden, not as austerity.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:345","b":"k:73","type":"qualifies","note":"73 says the bond of the precious life with the body (ஆருயிர்க்கு என்போடு இயைந்த தொடர்பு) is the way that came to be joined with love, so embodiment is given a purpose. 345 says that for those undertaking to cut off births (பிறப்பறுக்கல் உற்றார்க்கு) the body itself is excess and other ties (தொடர்ப்பாடு) pointless. The value 73 gives the body-bond holds within the life of love; 345 marks where, for the renouncer, it stops holding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:345","b":"k:627","type":"parallel_domain","note":"345 says even the body is too much for one seeking to end birth. 627, in the book of wealth, says the great do not take trouble as trouble, knowing the body is a target for suffering (இலக்கம் உடம்பு இடும்பைக்கு). Both start from the body as a liability, but 627 uses that view to keep steady in worldly trouble while 345 uses it to argue for giving up every tie.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:346","b":"k:50","type":"develops","note":"50 says one who lives on earth as life should be lived will be placed among the gods who dwell in the sky (வானுறையும் தெய்வத்துள் வைக்கப்படும்). 346 promises the one who ends 'I' and 'mine' a world higher than the gods' (வானோர்க்கு உயர்ந்த உலகம்). Set side by side the two verses give a scale: the good householder reaches the gods, the renouncer goes beyond them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:346","b":"k:72","type":"parallel_domain","note":"346 says one who cuts off the pride of saying 'I' and 'mine' (யான் எனது என்னும் செருக்கு) enters a world above the gods. 72 says the loveless keep everything for themselves (எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்) while the loving belong to others even to their bones (என்பும் உரியர் பிறர்க்கு). The householder's love already loosens 'mine', and even the body as 'I', by giving it to others; renunciation does it by cutting the notion itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:347","b":"k:438","type":"parallel_domain","note":"347 uses பற்று for attachment in general and says its holders are held by griefs. 438, on a ruler's faults, names miserliness as 'the clinging heart' (பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை) and says it is not to be counted with any other fault. The same word and the same refusal to let go appear in the domain of wealth, where Parimelazhagar glosses it as not releasing goods when they ought to be released.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:347","b":"k:940","type":"develops","note":"347 says griefs hold and do not let go of those who hold attachment and do not let go (பற்றினைப் பற்றி விடாஅதவர்க்கு). 940 says that as a gambler loves the game more with each loss, the life grows more fond with each suffering it undergoes (துன்பம் உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்); Parimelazhagar and Pope take the object of that fondness to be the body. 940 supplies what 347 leaves unexplained: suffering does not loosen the grip but tightens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:276","type":"qualifies","note":"348 sets those who have fully renounced above everyone else. 276 says none are harder-hearted than those who have not renounced in the heart (நெஞ்சின் துறவார்) and live by deceiving as though they had (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து). The praise in 348 therefore applies to renunciation of the heart; the outward form without it is placed among the worst.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:348","b":"k:365","type":"develops","note":"348 gives the prize only to those who renounce thoroughly (தீரத் துறந்தார்). 365 says those called 'freed' are those freed of craving, and the others are not freed in that way (மற்றையார் அற்றாக அற்றது இலர்), whatever else they have given up. 365 specifies what 348's 'thoroughly' requires: the giving up must reach craving, not only things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:349","b":"k:361","type":"develops","note":"361 says craving is the seed that yields unceasing birth for all beings (தவாஅப் பிறப்பீனும் வித்து). 349 says the moment attachment is cut off, that cuts off birth (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே பிறப்பறுக்கும்). One verse names the cause and the other states what follows when the cause is removed; Parimelazhagar makes the same cause-and-effect point under 349.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:350","b":"k:359","type":"develops","note":"350 says to hold to the hold of the one who has none. 359 says that if one knows the support and lives so that supports perish (சார்புணர்ந்து சார்புகெட ஒழுகின்), the pains due to come will not come. The two are built alike on a repeated word, பற்று and சார்பு, and Parimelazhagar reads the சார்பு that is known as the true reality on which all rests, so 359 states the result of the practice 350 prescribes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:351","b":"k:1002","type":"same_image","note":"351 says an inglorious birth comes from the delusion that takes what is not real for real (மருளானாம் மாணாப் பிறப்பு). 1002 ends with the same words: the delusion of thinking everything comes by wealth, and so hoarding without giving, produces an inglorious birth. The book of wealth applies the metaphysical formula to one concrete error, treating money as the thing that matters.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:352","b":"k:243","type":"parallel_domain","note":"352 has darkness leave and joy arise for those who see truly. 243 says those whose hearts are joined with compassion do not enter the painful world joined with darkness (இருள்சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம்). 243's wording supports Parimelazhagar's reading of இருள் in 352 as a dark world of suffering, and shows the book promising escape from it by compassion as well as by knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:50","type":"parallel_domain","note":"50 says one who lives on earth (வையத்துள்) as one should is placed among the gods who dwell in the sky. 353 uses the same pair, earth and sky (வையத்தின் வானம்), and says the sky is the nearer of the two for one who is clear of doubt. The householder gets there by right living and the knower by certainty, and in both the sky is brought within reach of someone still on earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:353","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"353 says that for those who have left doubt and become clear (ஐயத்தின் நீங்கித் தெளிந்தார்க்கு) the sky is nearer than the earth. 702 says one who knows without doubt what is within another (ஐயப்படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானை) should be held equal to a god. In both, knowing free of doubt lifts a person toward the divine, once in the knowledge of reality and once in a courtier's reading of minds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:2","type":"parallel_domain","note":"2 asks what learning has achieved (கற்றதனால் ஆய பயனென்கொல்) if the learned do not bow at the feet of the one of pure knowledge. 354 says command of the five senses has no benefit (பயமின்றே) for those without perception of the real. Both verses grant an attainment and then deny its fruit when the final step is missing, learning without worship in one and discipline without insight in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:354","b":"k:126","type":"qualifies","note":"126 says that drawing in the five like a tortoise in one birth is a protection for seven. 354 says the attained fivefold awareness yields no fruit without true perception; Parimelazhagar reads ஐயுணர்வு as the mind withdrawn from the senses and held under control, and the missing fruit as release, while Manakkudavar reads it as knowing all that the five senses can know. On Parimelazhagar's reading sense-restraint earns protection across births, as 126 says, but not the end of birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:355","b":"k:857","type":"parallel_domain","note":"355 makes seeing the true thing the definition of understanding. 857 says those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the true thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காணார்); Parimelazhagar takes it there as the substance of the texts on right policy that bring success. The same phrase marks, in the book of wealth, what a mind bent on quarrel cannot perceive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:356","b":"k:398","type":"develops","note":"398 says the learning acquired in one birth protects through seven (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து). 356 says those who have learned here and seen the true thing (கற்றீண்டு மெய்ப்பொருள் கண்டார்) reach the path of not coming back here (மற்றீண்டு வாரா நெறி). Learning in the book of wealth is an asset carried across births; learning that ends in seeing the real is said to end the births themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:357","b":"k:356","type":"develops","note":"356 speaks of those who learn and see the true thing; 357 adds the mind's own work: if the mind examines and knows with certainty what is (ஓர்த்து ... உள்ளது உணரின் ஒருதலையா), one need not think of birth again. Parimelazhagar reads the two as successive means, hearing instruction in 356 and reasoned examination in 357. The printed text here has உணரான், but Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's glosses read உணரின், which the sense requires.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:358","b":"k:351","type":"develops","note":"351 says a joyless birth comes from the delusion (மருள்) that takes the unreal for real. 358 goes a step further and calls birth itself a folly (பிறப்பென்னும் பேதைமை); Parimelazhagar explains this as naming the effect in place of its cause. The chapter moves from delusion as the cause of birth to birth and delusion spoken of as one thing, removed together by seeing the real.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:358","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"358 speaks of 'the folly called birth' departing when the real is seen. 835, in the chapter on folly in the book of wealth, says a fool can in one birth's doing make the mire he will sink into for seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு). Both tie பேதைமை to the chain of births, 358 as its root and 835 through the fool's deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:359","b":"k:5","type":"parallel_domain","note":"5 says the two kinds of deed do not attach (சேரா) to those devoted to the Lord's praise. 359 says the pains due to attach will not attach (சார்தரா சார்தரு நோய்) to one who knows the support and lives so that supports perish; Parimelazhagar takes these as the fruits of stored deeds still awaiting experience, and Manakkudavar takes சார்பு itself as the hold of deeds. Devotion in 5 and knowledge with practice in 359 are credited with the same effect on karma.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:359","b":"k:347","type":"develops","note":"347 says griefs hold and do not let go of those who hold on to attachment. 359 gives the other case: for one who knows the support and lives so that supports fall away (சார்புகெட ஒழுகின்), the pains that were due do not come to cling. 347 states the bondage and 359 the way out, in the same language of things that cling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:4","type":"agrees","note":"360 says pain perishes when desire, anger and confusion perish down to their names (காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம் ... நாமம் கெட); Parimelazhagar glosses the three as wanting, aversion and ignorance. 4 describes God as the one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet have no suffering. The state 360 sets as the goal is the nature 4 gives to the Lord, with the same result, the end of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:360","b":"k:431","type":"parallel_domain","note":"360 lists desire, anger and confusion as what must perish for pain to end. 431 lists three faults whose absence gives a ruler's prosperity its dignity: arrogance, anger and smallness (செருக்கும் சினமும் சிறுமையும்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as pride, anger and lust. A similar triad, sharing anger and, on that gloss, desire, is the obstacle to release in one book and to good rule in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:364","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"34 says being spotless in mind is the whole of virtue (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல் அனைத்து அறன்). 364 defines purity as the absence of craving (அவாவின்மை). Taken together, 364 names the stain that 34 leaves unnamed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:365","b":"k:275","type":"develops","note":"275 warns that the hidden conduct of those who say 'we are free of attachment' (பற்றற்றேம் என்பார்) brings many harms. 365 says who may rightly be called freed (அற்றவர் என்பார்): only those freed of craving. 275 shows the claim made falsely; 365 gives the inner criterion that the claim has to meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:35","type":"develops","note":"35 defines virtue as what proceeds without slipping into four things, one of which is craving (அழுக்காறு அவா வெகுளி இன்னாச்சொல்). 366 says craving is what deceives a person (வஞ்சிப்பது அவா), and that fearing it is itself virtue (அஞ்சுவதோரும் அறனே). 366 takes one item from the list in 35 and gives the reason for singling it out: it works by deceit, so it needs constant watch.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says fearing what should be feared is the work of the knowing (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்) and leaves the object open. 366 names an object, craving, and calls the fear of it virtue (அஞ்சுவதோரும் அறனே). The general rule about right fear in the book of statecraft gets a specific content in the renouncer's chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:366","b":"k:1075","type":"same_image","note":"Fear and craving appear together in both verses with opposite roles. In 1075 fear is the whole conduct of the base (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), with a little more if there is something they crave (அவாவுண்டேல்); Parimelazhagar takes the fear as fear of punishment. In 366 fear is directed at craving itself and is called virtue. The pairing separates fear that serves craving from fear that guards against it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:367","b":"k:266","type":"agrees","note":"266 says those who do austerity do their own work, and the others, caught in desire, do what is futile (அவஞ்செய்வார் ஆசையுட் பட்டு). 367 says that if craving is cut off thoroughly, the deed that does not fail (தவாவினை) comes in the way one wishes. They state the same link from opposite sides: desire makes action futile, and its removal makes action unfailing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:367","b":"k:309","type":"parallel_domain","note":"309 says all that one has thought of is reached at once if one does not think of anger in the heart (உள்ளியதெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்). 367 says the unfailing deed comes as one wishes (தான் வேண்டுமாற்றான் வரும்) once craving is cut. Each verse removes one passion, anger or craving, and promises that what one intends then arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:341","type":"develops","note":"341 says a person has no pain from each thing he has left (யாதனின் யாதனின் நீங்கியான் நோதல் அதனின் அதனின் இலன்), taking objects one by one. 368 says those without craving have no sorrow at all (அவாஇல்லார்க்கு இல்லாகுந் துன்பம்). 368 moves from the objects given up to the craving behind them, so the relief is complete instead of item by item.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:347","type":"agrees","note":"347 says sorrows hold and do not let go of those who hold attachment and do not let go (பற்றி விடாஅ இடும்பைகள்). 368 says that where craving exists sorrow keeps coming without fail, one after another (தவாஅது மேன்மேல் வரும்). Both describe suffering as continuing for as long as its cause is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:368","b":"k:628","type":"parallel_domain","note":"628, in the chapter on not being undone by trouble, says one who does not desire pleasure (இன்பம் விழையான்) and calls hardship natural does not come to sorrow (துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்). 368 says those without craving have no sorrow. The renouncer's doctrine reappears as practical advice for a person at work in the world, where Parimelazhagar relates it to the pains of effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:369","b":"k:854","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses share one sentence frame. 369: joy is unbroken if the sorrow of sorrows called craving perishes (அவாவென்னும் துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்). 854: it yields the joy of joys if the sorrow of sorrows called hostility perishes (இகலென்னும் துன்பத்துள் துன்பங் கெடின்). The same title, sorrow of sorrows, is given to craving in the book of virtue and to hostility in the book of statecraft, each as the worst thing in its own field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:349","type":"agrees","note":"370 says that giving up craving gives, in that very state (அந்நிலையே), a nature that does not shift (பேரா இயற்கை). 349 says the moment attachment ends (பற்றற்ற கண்ணே) it cuts off birth. Both make the result immediate on the cause ending, with no further step between.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:370","b":"k:940","type":"develops","note":"370 says craving has a nature that is never filled (ஆரா இயற்கை அவா). 940 gives a picture of that: as the gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும்), a living being's love grows with each suffering (துன்பம் உழத்தொறூஉம் காதற்று உயிர்); Parimelazhagar takes this as love of the body. 940 shows why satisfying craving cannot end it, which is why 370 says it has to be given up.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:617","type":"qualifies","note":"617 says the dark goddess of misfortune lives in sloth (மடியுளாள் மாமுகடி) and the lotus goddess lives in the effort of the one without sloth. 371 says sloth (மடி) and its opposite are themselves produced by the fate of loss and the fate of gain. In 617 fortune depends on whether one is slothful; 371 adds that whether one is slothful depends on fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:371","b":"k:618","type":"tension","note":"371 says tireless effort arises from a fate of gain and sloth arises from a fate of loss (போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 618 says lacking good fortune (பொறியின்மை) is no one's disgrace, but lacking effort after learning what should be known is a disgrace (ஆள்வினை இன்மை பழி). If sloth itself is produced by fate, as 371 says on a plain reading, the line 618 draws between blameless fortune and blameworthy idleness is hard to hold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:372","b":"k:429","type":"qualifies","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) meet no affliction that shakes them. 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish (பேதைப் படுக்கும் இழவூழ்); Parimelazhagar adds that this happens even to one who has understanding. The protection 429 promises rests on understanding, and 372 says understanding itself can be taken away by fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:372","b":"k:859","type":"agrees","note":"372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish and a fate of gain, when it arrives, widens understanding (அறிவகற்றும் ஆகலூழ் உற்றக் கடை). 859 says that when gain is coming a person does not look at hostility (இகல்காணான் ஆக்கம் வருங்கால்), and to bring ruin on himself he looks to excel at it. In both, approaching gain or ruin shows first as a change in the person's own judgement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:373","b":"k:823","type":"parallel_domain","note":"823 says that even after learning many good things it is hard for the unworthy to become good in heart (பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்தும் மனநல்லர் ஆகுதல் மாணார்க்கு அரிது); Parimelazhagar applies this to enemies. 373 says that after much study a person's own understanding still prevails. The same limit on what books can change is stated once for understanding and once for the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:169","type":"parallel_domain","note":"169 says the prosperity of the crooked-hearted and the ruin of the upright are things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்), and gives no answer; Parimelazhagar says the wise look for the earlier deed behind it. 374 notes a like mismatch between wealth and understanding and gives it as the two-fold nature of the world. One verse separates wealth from goodness and the other separates it from wisdom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:374","b":"k:408","type":"develops","note":"374 states as a general fact that wealth and clear understanding are separate lots. 408 judges one result of that: wealth lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு). 408 takes for granted the mismatch that 374 explains.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:375","b":"k:483","type":"tension","note":"483 asks whether anything is hard for one who acts with the right means and knowing the time (கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து செயின்). 375 says that in making wealth all that is good turns bad and bad turns good (நல்லவை எல்லாஅந் தீயவாம்); Parimelazhagar takes the good and bad things to be time, place, means and action, reversed by fate. On that reading the favourable conditions 483 relies on do not secure the result, and the two conflict. Kalaignar reads 375 differently, as good and bad deeds turning out contrary to intent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:376","b":"k:659","type":"qualifies","note":"376 says what is not one's portion will not stay however carefully it is guarded (பரியினும் ஆகாவாம் பாலல்ல), and what is one's own will not leave even if thrown out. 659 says all that is taken with others' tears leaves with tears, and what is of the good kind (நற்பாலவை) pays later even if lost. Both say some goods stay and some go whatever one does; 659 gives a test the person controls, the way the wealth was gained, where 376 gives only fate's allotment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:377","b":"k:1005","type":"qualifies","note":"1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing even with crores piled up (அடுக்கிய கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்), and treats this as the miser's own failing. 377 says even those who have gathered crores can enjoy them only as the apportioner has apportioned (வகுத்தான் வகுத்த வகையல்லால் ... துய்த்தல் அரிது). Both separate owning from enjoying; 377 places part of enjoyment outside the owner's choice, which limits the blame in 1005.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:378","b":"k:344","type":"develops","note":"344 says having nothing is natural to austerity (இயல்பாகும் நோன்பிற்கொன்று இன்மை). 378 looks at those who already have nothing through poverty and says they would renounce, but for what fate makes them undergo. Parimelazhagar makes the connection: they have already lost what a renouncer gives up, and still suffer because their intent differs. Having nothing is the condition of austerity in 344, and 378 shows it is not enough by itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:379","b":"k:626","type":"agrees","note":"379 asks why those who see things as good when good comes should grieve (அல்லற்படுவது எவன்) when it does not. 626 asks whether those who never clutched at wealth saying 'we have got it' would grieve (அல்லற்படுபவோ) saying 'we have lost it'. Both are rhetorical questions with the same verb and the same symmetry between gain and loss. 626 adds the practical point that grief at loss depends on how one held the gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:380","b":"k:619","type":"qualifies","note":"380 says fate is the strongest thing and gets ahead of any plan. 619 grants that a thing may not come about because of fate (தெய்வத்தான் ஆகாதெனினும்), and adds that effort still pays the wage of the body's labour (மெய்வருத்தக் கூலி தரும்). 619 accepts the limit that 380 states and marks out what remains for effort within it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:823","type":"qualifies","note":"391 treats right conduct as what should follow flawless learning. 823, on false friends, says that even after studying many good things (பலநல்ல கற்றக் கடைத்தும்) it is hard for the unworthy (மாணார்) to become good at heart. It limits 391: study does not by itself produce the standing-by-it, which is why 391 has to command it separately.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:391","b":"k:834","type":"develops","note":"391 asks for two things in order: learn without flaw (கசடறக் கற்க), then stand in keeping with it (நிற்க அதற்குத் தக). 834 names the person who does the first and skips the second: one who has studied, understood and even taught others but is not himself restrained (தானடங்கா) is the greatest of fools. The pair shows that the second half of 391 is not an afterthought: learning without it is classed as folly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:392","b":"k:445","type":"same_image","note":"392 makes numbers and letters the eyes of the living. 445 says a king goes about with his counsellors as his eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக) and so must choose them with care. Both treat sight as something acquired from outside oneself, through learning in one case and through chosen advisers in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:393","b":"k:418","type":"parallel_domain","note":"393 denies real eyes to the unlearned. 418 says ears not pierced by listening (கேள்வியால் தோட்கப்படாத செவி) are of a deaf kind even though they hear. The next chapter repeats the pattern for the other sense: an organ counts only when it has been trained by learning or listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:394","b":"k:643","type":"develops","note":"394 describes the learned as people whose company others want again after it ends. 643 defines speech as what binds those who heard it and makes even those who did not hear want it (கேளாரும் வேட்ப). 643 gives the means, speech, for the effect 394 describes.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:394","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"394 says the work of the learned is to meet so that others are glad and to part so that others think of them (உள்ளப் பிரிதல்). 839 says, ironically, that friendship with fools is very sweet because parting from them (பிரிவின்கண்) brings no pain at all. Both judge a person by what the parting leaves behind; the fool is the case where it leaves nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:395","b":"k:724","type":"develops","note":"395 praises learning in the stance of one who lacks before one who has. 724 tells even the learned to state what they have learned before the learned and take more from those who know more (மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்). The asking stance of 395 is thus not only for beginners; it continues among the learned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:395","b":"k:1066","type":"qualifies","note":"395 uses the poor standing before the rich (உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல்) as its figure for the learner, and on Parimelazhagar's reading those who learned standing so are foremost while those who would not are lowest. 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking (இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்), even asking water for a cow. Read together, the posture the book calls degrading when the object is goods is the one it honours when the object is learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:397","b":"k:753","type":"parallel_domain","note":"397 says that for the learned any land is one's land and any town one's town (யாதானும் நாடாம் ஊராம்). 753 says the unfailing lamp called wealth goes to whatever country one has in mind (எண்ணிய தேயத்துச் சென்று) and cuts away the darkness there. Learning and wealth are each credited with making foreign places open to a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:399","b":"k:650","type":"qualifies","note":"399 assumes the learned person's learning gives the world joy. 650 says those who cannot unfold what they have learned so that others grasp it (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like a cluster of flowers in bloom without scent. It adds the condition 399 leaves unstated: the world's delight depends on being able to convey the learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:400","b":"k:241","type":"parallel_domain","note":"400 gives the title of real wealth to learning. 241 gives it to grace (அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம்), with the reason that wealth of goods is found even among the base. The same move appears in the Book of Virtue with a different winner, and 241's reason, who can hold it, differs from 400's, whether it can be lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:400","b":"k:411","type":"same_image","note":"400 says learning is the imperishable, excellent wealth and other things are not wealth (மாடல்ல மற்றையவை). 411 says the wealth of the ear is the wealth among wealths and the head of them all (செல்வத்துளெல்லாம் தலை). Adjacent chapters use the same 'true wealth' figure for study and then for listening, without ranking the two against each other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:401","b":"k:728","type":"parallel_domain","note":"401 says holding forth in an assembly without full book-learning (நிரம்பிய நூலின்றிக் கோட்டி கொளல்) is like playing dice without a marked board. 728 says those who have learned many things are still of no use if they cannot speak well and tellingly in a good assembly. Together they give both halves: speech without learning has nothing to stand on, and learning without speech yields nothing.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:402","b":"k:727","type":"parallel_domain","note":"402 compares the unlearned man's desire to speak (சொற்காமுறுதல், as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read it) to a woman without breasts desiring womanhood. 727 compares the learning of one who fears the assembly to a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி among enemies. Both use a figure of lacking what one's role requires, and they cover the two opposite failures: wanting to speak without learning, and having learning without the nerve to speak. Kalaignar reads 402 differently, as desiring to hear the unlearned man's words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:403","b":"k:715","type":"develops","note":"403 says even the unlearned are very good if they can keep from speaking before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 715 says the best of all good things is the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு). 715 turns the rule 403 gives the unlearned into a general virtue of the assembly that binds capable speakers too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:403","b":"k:729","type":"qualifies","note":"403 counts silence before the learned as the unlearned person's merit. 729 says those who have studied and understood yet fear an assembly of the good are lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை). Silence is therefore not good in itself: it is right for the one with nothing to say and a failing in the one who has something.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:423","type":"tension","note":"404 says that however good an unlearned person's brightness (ஒட்பம்) may be, those with understanding do not accept it. 423 says understanding is seeing the true substance of a thing from whoever's mouth it is heard (யார்யார்வாய்க் கேட்பினும்). If 404 means the wise set aside what the unlearned say even when it is good, it conflicts with 423's rule to judge the matter and not the speaker; on Parimelazhagar's reading, that they only decline to credit him with real brightness because an occasional hit is accidental, the conflict is smaller.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:404","b":"k:636","type":"develops","note":"404 says native brightness without learning is not accepted by the wise. 636 says nothing over-subtle can stand before those who have keen native intelligence together with book-learning (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்). 636 states positively what 404 implies: natural intelligence gains its force when joined to study.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:405","b":"k:717","type":"parallel_domain","note":"405 says an unlearned person's standing collapses once he converses. 717 says the learning of those who have studied and understood shines out (விளங்கும்) among those able to examine words without fault. Conversation with the competent is the test in both, with opposite results for the unlearned and the learned.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:405","b":"k:1046","type":"same_image","note":"405 says the unlearned person's self-regard gives way when he engages and speaks (சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்); Parimelazhagar supplies that the talk is with a learned person. 1046 says that even when the poor speak of good matters well understood, their words go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்). The same closing phrase marks two ways speech fails: in 405 for want of learning in the speaker, in 1046 for want of standing with the hearers, though the content is sound.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:406","b":"k:572","type":"parallel_domain","note":"406 grants the unlearned existence only and compares them to land that yields nothing (பயவாக் களர்). 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). Both reduce a person lacking a quality to mere existence and express it through land, as barren soil in one and as weight on the ground in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:406","b":"k:730","type":"same_image","note":"406 says the unlearned are no more than 'they exist' (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are like barren saline ground that yields nothing. 730 says those who, fearing the arena, cannot convey what they have learned are, though they exist, equal to those who do not (உளரெனினும் இல்லாரொடு ஒப்பர்). The same bare-existence wording puts the learned who cannot speak in nearly the place of the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:407","b":"k:667","type":"agrees","note":"407 says good looks without understanding are worth what a clay doll's are. 667 says not to scorn anyone on seeing their form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), since the great chariot has its small linchpin. Both deny that appearance measures a person, 407 for the handsome and empty, 667 for the slight and capable.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:407","b":"k:1020","type":"same_image","note":"407 compares the good looks of one without fine, penetrating understanding to a well-decorated clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). 1020 compares the movements of those with no shame within to a wooden puppet (மரப்பாவை) made to seem alive by its string. Both use a doll for a human form missing its inner part, understanding in one and shame in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:558","type":"parallel_domain","note":"408 says wealth is worse than poverty when it sits with the unlearned (வறுமையின் இன்னாதே). 558 says having is more painful than not having (இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை) under a ruler who does no justice. Both invert the usual ranking of wealth over poverty under a stated condition: the holder's lack of learning in one, the king's injustice in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:408","b":"k:837","type":"develops","note":"408 says wealth lodged with the unlearned (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு) is more painful than poverty lodged with the good. 837 shows what happens when a fool gets great wealth: strangers feast and his own people go hungry. 837 gives one concrete form of the harm; Manakkudavar likewise reads 408 as harm done to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:409","b":"k:133","type":"parallel_domain","note":"409 says the unlearned, though born high, do not have the worth (பாடு) of the learned born low. 133 says having conduct is good family, and lapse from it becomes low birth (இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). Both make birth give way to something acquired, learning in 409 and conduct in 133.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:420","type":"develops","note":"410 says others stand to those who have studied the shining books as beasts stand to people (விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர்). 420 calls those who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear மாக்கள், and asks what it matters whether they die or live. The next chapter carries the comparison on, from those who have not studied to those who do not even listen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:1012","type":"parallel_domain","note":"410 makes book-learning the difference between people and beasts. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and that the distinction of human beings is having shame (நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு). The two verses give different answers, learning and shame, to what sets humans apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:410","b":"k:1071","type":"parallel_domain","note":"410 says the unlearned differ from the learned as beasts from people, though all are human in form. 1071 says the base look exactly like people (மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர்), a likeness the speaker has seen nowhere else. Both deny full human standing to some who have the human shape, for lack of learning in one and for baseness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:412","b":"k:420","type":"develops","note":"412 treats listening as food for the ear (செவிக்குணவு) and lets the stomach have a little (சிறிது) only when that food is absent. 420 turns the same ear-and-mouth pairing into a judgment: for those who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear (செவியிற் சுவையுணரா வாயுணர்வின் மாக்கள்), living and dying come to the same. The chapter's ranking of the two appetites starts as a rule for mealtimes and ends as a measure of a life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:412","b":"k:946","type":"parallel_domain","note":"412 allows the stomach only a little (சிறிது), and Parimelazhagar explains the word by saying heavy eating multiplies disease and desire. 946, in the chapter on medicine, says well-being stays with the one who eats knowing to stop short (இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்) and disease with the heavy eater (கழிபேர் இரையான்). The same restraint at the table is asked for in the name of listening in one verse and in the name of health in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:413","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"413 makes listeners equal to the oblation-fed (ஆன்றாரோடொப்பர்). 702 says one who knows without doubt what is inside another (அகத்தது உணர்வான்) should be taken as equal to a god (தெய்வத்தோடொப்பக் கொளல்). In both, a human being is ranked with the gods for a faculty of taking in what others carry: their teaching in one, their unspoken thought in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:414","b":"k:403","type":"develops","note":"403 says even the unlearned are very good if they can keep from speaking before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 414 addresses the same person (கற்றிலனாயினும்) and tells him to listen (கேட்க). The silence that 403 only recommends as damage control becomes in 414 the means of gaining what he lacks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:139","type":"develops","note":"415 says the words from the mouth of those with conduct (ஒழுக்கமுடையார் வாய்ச்சொல்) are like a staff where the ground is slippery (இழுக்கல் உடையுழி). 139 says of the same people (ஒழுக்கமுடையவர்க்கு) that speaking evil with the mouth, even by a slip (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்), is not possible. 139 gives the reason 415 needs: those who do not slip in what they say are the ones whose words can steady someone who is slipping.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:415","b":"k:447","type":"parallel_domain","note":"415 presents the words of people of conduct as the staff that keeps a person upright on slippery ground. 447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his own those who will rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையார்). Both verses locate safety in another person's speech; 447 applies it to a king and makes the helpful words explicitly unwelcome ones.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:417","b":"k:139","type":"parallel_domain","note":"417 says well-grounded listeners do not speak folly even when they have misunderstood (பிழைத்துணர்ந்தும்). 139 says that for those who have conduct, speaking evil even by a slip (வழுக்கியும்) is not possible. The same guarantee, speech that holds even under error, is attached to listening in the Book of Wealth and to conduct in the Book of Virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:417","b":"k:199","type":"parallel_domain","note":"417 says those whose wide listening rests on their own close inquiry (இழைத்துணர்ந்து ஈண்டிய கேள்வியவர்) do not speak folly even when they have misunderstood (பிழைத்துணர்ந்தும் பேதைமை சொல்லார்). 199 says those of stainless vision, free of delusion (மருள்தீர்ந்த மாசறு காட்சியவர்), do not say empty things even in forgetfulness (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லார்). The two verses have the same shape, a kind of person whose speech stays sound even when the mind lapses, and they credit it to different sources: listening in one, clear vision in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:418","b":"k:577","type":"parallel_domain","note":"418 denies real hearing to ears that listening has not opened. 577 says those without kindly regard have no eyes (கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர்), and those who have eyes do not lack it. The form of argument is the same, an organ is counted as present only when it is used for its higher purpose, applied to instruction in 418 and to kindness in 577.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:95","type":"qualifies","note":"95 says being humble and sweet-spoken (பணிவுடையன் இன்சொலன் ஆதல்) is a person's ornament, and nothing else is. 419 says a mouth that bows (வணங்கிய வாய்) is hard to come by without fine listening. 95 holds humble speech up for everyone, and 419 adds a condition: it has to be prepared for by listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:419","b":"k:844","type":"develops","note":"419 says humble speech is hardly to be had without fine listening. 844 defines வெண்மை, shallowness of understanding, as the conceit that says 'we have brightness' (ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம் என்னும் செருக்கு). The pair links lack of instruction and self-praise from both ends: 419 says the uninstructed will not speak humbly, and 844 says boasting of one's understanding is itself the mark of lacking it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:214","type":"parallel_domain","note":"420 treats those without the ear's taste as people whose death and life are the same. 214 says only one who knows what is fitting truly lives (உயிர்வாழ்வான்), and anyone else is counted among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). The book repeatedly denies real life to those who lack one defining quality; here the quality is listening, and in 214 it is knowing one's duty to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:406","type":"agrees","note":"406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are otherwise like saline ground that yields nothing (பயவாக் களர்). 420 says of those who have no taste for what the ear takes in that their dying or living makes no difference (அவியினும் வாழினும் என்). The chapters on not learning and on listening reach the same verdict, a life that merely exists; Manakkudavar reads 420, as Parimelazhagar reads 406, as about being of no use to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:420","b":"k:1012","type":"parallel_domain","note":"420 asks what it matters whether they die or live, those மாக்கள் who know taste by the mouth and not by the ear. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for all living beings (ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல), and the distinction of மாந்தர் is having shame. Both verses treat eating as what humans share with every creature and put human worth elsewhere, in the ear's taste for one and in shame for the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:421","b":"k:781","type":"parallel_domain","note":"421 names understanding as the tool that guards against ruin (அற்றங் காக்குங் கருவி). 781 asks what guard is as hard for hostile action to get past as friendship (வினைக்கரிய யாவுள காப்பு). The Book of Wealth offers two protections that are not walls or weapons, one within the person and one between persons, and praises each in the vocabulary of defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:422","b":"k:24","type":"develops","note":"24 says one who guards the five with the goad called firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான் ஓரைந்தும் காப்பான்) is a seed for the highest ground; the goad makes the senses elephants, as Parimelazhagar spells out. 422 gives understanding a similar job, not letting one go where one has gone (சென்ற இடத்தால் செலவிடா), which Parimelazhagar explains with a rider directing a horse. 422 goes a step past 24's holding back: it also sorts good from evil and steers toward the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:422","b":"k:787","type":"parallel_domain","note":"422 says understanding does not let one go wherever the mind has gone, but draws away from evil and directs toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). 787 says friendship removes one from what ruins and sets one on the path (அழிவினவை நீக்கி ஆறுய்த்து). The two verses use the same pair of actions and the same verb of steering (உய்); what understanding does for a person from inside, the friend does from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:424","b":"k:650","type":"develops","note":"424 makes it part of understanding to put things so that hearers take them in easily. 650 says those who cannot lay out what they have learned so that others understand (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like a cluster of flowers in bloom with no scent. 650 gives the failure case of 424's first half: learning that cannot be conveyed is counted as lacking something essential. The first word of 650 is misprinted in this edition, though the image is clear from the commentary.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:424","b":"k:701","type":"develops","note":"424 says understanding sees the fine meaning in what others say (பிறர்வாய் நுண்பொருள்); Manakkudavar takes this as seeing what they mean without their spelling it out. 701 praises one who knows what is intended by looking, without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்). 701 carries the second half of 424 one step further, from reading what lies within words to reading what is never put into words.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:115","type":"agrees","note":"115 says loss and increase are not absent from life, and that not bending in the heart (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை) is the ornament of the worthy. Parimelazhagar records a second reading of 425, in which the opening and closing are expanding in wealth and shrinking in want. On that reading the two verses say the same thing, that a person should stay the same through gain and loss, one under the heading of fairness and the other of understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:544","type":"same_image","note":"425 says embracing the world is brightness (உலகம் தழீஇயது ஒட்பம்). 544 uses the same verb twice: the world stands embracing the feet (அடிதழீஇ நிற்கும் உலகு) of the king who rules embracing his people (குடிதழீஇ). In 544 the embrace is returned, which gives a concrete reason why 425 counts drawing the world close as intelligence. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' in 425 to the high-minded.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:425","b":"k:782","type":"parallel_domain","note":"425 says understanding is what has neither opening nor closing (மலர்தலும் கூம்பலும் இல்லது), which Parimelazhagar reads as steadiness in friendship with the high-minded, unlike a pond flower that opens and shuts. 782 says the friendship of the wise fills like the crescent moon and that of fools shrinks like the moon after full. Both verses judge attachment by a natural cycle; 782 praises the bond that grows, while 425 asks for one that does not swing in either direction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:426","b":"k:850","type":"develops","note":"426 makes accord with the world part of what understanding is. 850 says one who says 'it is not' of what the world says is (உலகத்தார் உண்டென்பது இல்லென்பான்) will be set down as a demon on earth. 850, in the chapter on shallow understanding, extends 426 from conduct to belief and states the cost of breaking with the world's view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:372","type":"qualifies","note":"427 makes knowing what is to come the mark of having understanding, as if it were a trait of the person. 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish (பேதைப்படுக்கும் இழவூழ்) and a fate of gain, when it arrives, widens understanding (அறிவகற்றும் ஆகலூழ்). Read with 372, the division in 427 is not fixed: the same person may see ahead or fail to, depending on what fate is at work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"427 defines those with understanding as those who know what is going to be (ஆவதறிவார்). 429 says those whose understanding guards against what is ahead (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that comes to shake them. 429 gives the use of the foresight that 427 only defines: knowing beforehand matters because it lets one guard beforehand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:427","b":"k:461","type":"develops","note":"427 separates the wise from the unwise by whether they know what will be (ஆவது). 461 tells one to act after weighing what will be lost, what will come of it (ஆவதூஉம்), and the gain that follows later. 461 turns the foresight of 427 into a procedure, a reckoning of outcomes made before acting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:428","b":"k:1075","type":"qualifies","note":"428 makes fear of the right things the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில்). 1075 says fear is the whole basis of proper behaviour among the base (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as fear of harm from the king. Together they show that fear in itself does not separate the wise from the low; what separates them is that the wise fear what deserves it and the base behave only for fear of being punished.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:435","type":"agrees","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is ahead have no affliction that comes to shake them (அதிர வருவதோர் நோய்). 435 says the life of one who does not guard before it comes (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like a straw-heap before fire. They are the positive and negative statements of one rule; 435 stands in the chapter on faults, where Parimelazhagar supplies 'fault' as what comes, while 429 speaks of harm in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:429","b":"k:442","type":"develops","note":"429 credits freedom from shock to an understanding that guards against what is coming. 442 tells a ruler to cherish and keep those who can remove the trouble that has come and guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும் பெற்றியார்). 442 shows that the foresight of 429 need not be the ruler's own: it can be secured by choosing the right people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:591","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 defines having by understanding: the wise have all, and the unwise have nothing whatever they own. 591 uses the same turn for energy of mind: it is what makes someone 'one who has' (உடையர் எனப்படுவது ஊக்கம்), and those without it do not really have what they have (உடையது உடையரோ). The book redefines possession in the same words for two different inner qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:430","b":"k:841","type":"agrees","note":"430 says those with understanding have everything, and those without it have nothing whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்). 841 says lack of understanding is the lack among lacks (அறிவின்மை இன்மையுள் இன்மை), and the world does not count other lack as lack. 841, opening the chapter on shallow understanding, restates 430 from the side of poverty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:431","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"431 lists செருக்கு first among the faults whose absence gives prosperity its dignity. 180 names a செருக்கு it approves of, 'வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு', the pride of not wanting, which yields victory (விறல் ஈனும்); Parimelazhagar there glosses the word as wealth. Read together they separate arrogance from a pride that consists in refusing to covet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:531","type":"develops","note":"432 lists 'மாணா உவகை' among a ruler's faults; Parimelazhagar takes it as joy beyond measure. 531 says why such joy is dangerous: the slackness that comes from the elation of great joy (சிறந்த உவகை மகிழ்ச்சியிற் சோர்வு) is worse than excessive anger. 531 supplies the mechanism for the fault 432 only names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:432","b":"k:1028","type":"agrees","note":"432 calls honour that has passed beyond the fine a fault in a ruler. 1028 says that for one raising his family, idling and minding his மானம் brings ruin (மடிசெய்து மானங் கருதக் கெடும்). Both treat a misplaced sense of honour as a fault, one in the king and one in the head of a house.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:957","type":"develops","note":"433 describes how those ashamed of blame weigh a small fault of their own. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up raised high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல் உயர்ந்து). 957 adds the public side: in a person of standing a small fault is seen large by others too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:433","b":"k:965","type":"agrees","note":"433 says the blame-fearing treat a millet-sized fault as palmyra-sized. 965 says even people like a hill are diminished if they do a lowering thing the size of a குன்றி seed. 965 gives the outward fact, that a tiny act does lower the great, which makes the inward magnifying in 433 an accurate estimate and not an exaggeration.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:434","b":"k:304","type":"agrees","note":"434 says fault itself is the enemy that brings ruin. 304 asks whether there is any other enemy (பகையும் உளவோ பிற) than anger, which kills laughter and joy. 304 applies to one named fault the claim 434 makes of fault in general: the real enemy is inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:435","b":"k:535","type":"agrees","note":"435: the life of one who doesn't guard before it comes (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like straw before fire. 535: one who slipped by not guarding beforehand (முன்னுறக் காவாது இழுக்கியான்) will later grieve over his own error. The same failure is given two outcomes, sudden destruction in 435 and late regret in 535.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:228","type":"agrees","note":"437: the wealth of one who clings to it without doing what should be done with it (செயற்பால செய்யாது இவறியான்) perishes without lasting. 228 asks whether the hard-eyed who keep what they have and lose it (வைத்திழக்கும்) don't know the joy of giving. Both assume hoarded wealth is lost anyway; 228 adds what the hoarder missed in the meantime.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"437 says wealth perishes when its owner does not do the things that ought to be done (செயற்பால செய்யாது ... கெடும்). 466 says one perishes by doing what is unfit and also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்). The miser of 437 is a case of 466's second half: ruin by omission.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:437","b":"k:1006","type":"develops","note":"437 says the miser's wealth perishes because he does not do what should be done with it; Parimelazhagar lists these as virtue, increase of wealth and enjoyment. 1006 says one who neither uses his wealth himself nor gives to the deserving (தான்துவ்வான் ... ஈதல் இயல்பிலாதான்) is an ஏதம், a harm, to great wealth. 1006 spells out the two uses left undone and turns the miser himself into the wealth's affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:438","b":"k:432","type":"qualifies","note":"432 lists இவறல் as one of three faults in a ruler, beside excessive honour and excessive joy. 438, on Parimelazhagar's reading, takes it out of any such list as greater than the rest (எற்றுள்ளும் எண்ணப்படுவதொன்று அன்று). Manakkudavar reads 438 differently, as saying miserliness is not to be valued as anything, and on that reading the two verses simply agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:438","b":"k:1002","type":"develops","note":"438 says the miserliness called the clinging heart (பற்றுள்ளம் என்னும் இவறன்மை) is not to be counted among any others; Parimelazhagar takes this to mean it exceeds all other faults. 1002 names what lies behind it, the delusion (மருள்) that everything comes by wealth, so that one clings without giving (ஈயாது இவறும்), and gives its result as an inglorious birth. 1002 supplies a cause and a consequence for the fault 438 ranks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:439","b":"k:474","type":"develops","note":"439 commands: never admire yourself (வியவற்க எஞ்ஞான்றும் தன்னை). 474 gives the outcome for one who doesn't live in accord with others, doesn't know his measure and admires himself (தன்னை வியந்தான்): he perishes quickly. Parimelazhagar connects them in his note on 439, saying that self-admiration keeps one from knowing place, time and strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:439","b":"k:652","type":"same_image","note":"439 ends 'நயவற்க நன்றி பயவா வினை', do not desire a deed that yields no good. 652 ends with the same phrase and extends it: one must always avoid a deed that yields neither renown nor good (புகழொடு நன்றி பயவா வினை). The rule is given in 439 as part of a ruler's guarding against faults and in 652, on Parimelazhagar's reading, as a rule for ministers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:440","b":"k:866","type":"develops","note":"440: if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without the love being known (காதல் அறியாமை உய்க்கிற்பின்), his enemies' designs come to nothing. 866, from the enemy's side, says one of blinding anger and excessive desire (கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்) is an opponent whose enmity is worth having. Together they show the reasoning: a visible passion is an opening for an enemy, which Parimelazhagar states in his note on 440.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:441","b":"k:795","type":"parallel_domain","note":"441 tells a ruler to choose, after weighing, the friendship of those who know virtue and are mature in understanding (கேண்மை ... தேர்ந்து கொளல்). 795 tells anyone to examine and take the friendship (நட்பு ஆய்ந்து கொளல்) of those able to make one weep, to rebuke, and to teach the right way. The same instruction, to select friends for their capacity to guide, is given for the king's advisers and for ordinary friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:442","b":"k:435","type":"develops","note":"435 says the life of one who does not guard before it comes will burn like straw. 442 tells the ruler to cherish and take as his own those whose nature is to remove the affliction that has come and to guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும் பெற்றியார்). The foresight 435 demands of the ruler is, in 442, something he obtains through the people he keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:442","b":"k:787","type":"parallel_domain","note":"442 describes the people a ruler should keep: they remove the trouble that has come and guard in advance against more. 787 describes friendship in similar terms: it removes what leads to ruin (அழிவினவை நீக்கி), sets one on the path, and shares the suffering when ruin comes. The great adviser and the friend are given the same protective work, though 787 adds a sharing of suffering that 442 does not ask of the adviser.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:443","b":"k:781","type":"parallel_domain","note":"443 calls winning the great as one's own the rarest of rare things (அரியவற்றுள் எல்லாம் அரிது). 781 asks what is as hard to make as friendship (செயற்கரிய யாவுள நட்பின்), and what guard is as hard for an enemy's work to get past. Both rank a bond with others as the hardest thing to obtain, and 781 adds that it is also the strongest protection, which is what chapter 45 goes on to claim for the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:891","type":"parallel_domain","note":"444: to conduct oneself so that those greater than oneself are one's own is the chief of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 891: not slighting the power of the able is the chief of all the safeguards of those who guard themselves (போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை). The two verses use the same ranking formula for the two sides of dealing with the great, winning them as the highest strength and not offending them as the highest protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:444","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"444 places the chief strength in attaching oneself to those greater than oneself; Parimelazhagar adds that this means following their lead. 985 says the strength of those who accomplish things is to bow (ஆற்றுவார் ஆற்றல் பணிதல்). Both locate strength in deference and not in standing alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:462","type":"agrees","note":"445 tells the ruler to choose his counsellors after deliberation (சூழ்வாரைச் சூழ்ந்து கொளல்), since they serve as his eyes. 462 says nothing is out of reach for those who act after considering a matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி). Both put the selection of the people one deliberates with before the deliberation itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:581","type":"same_image","note":"445 says a ruler proceeds with his counsellors as his eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக) and so must choose them with thought. 581 says a ruler should hold spies and the respected treatise as his eyes (மன்னவன் கண்). The king's 'eyes' are advisers in one verse and intelligence plus learning in the other, and in both he sees through something other than himself. This follows Parimelazhagar's reading of 445; Manakkudavar takes its second line differently.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:445","b":"k:639","type":"develops","note":"445 says counsellors must be chosen with care because the ruler sees by them. 639 states the cost of choosing badly: a minister at one's side who plots harm (பழுதெண்ணும் மந்திரி) is worse than seventy crore enemies. 639 shows what the care in 445 protects against.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:446","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"446 says enemies can do nothing against one who keeps the company of the worthy. 460 says there is no support greater than good company (நல்லினத்தின் ஊங்கும் துணையில்லை) and nothing that brings more affliction than bad company. 460 states as a general rule what 446 applies to a ruler facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:446","b":"k:868","type":"agrees","note":"446: against one who can conduct himself as having the worthy for his company (தக்கார் இனத்தனாய்), enemies have nothing left to do. 868 gives the reverse case: one with no good qualities and many faults is without company (இனன் இலன்), and that is a protection to his opponents. Both verses use the word இனம் and both make a ruler's exposure to enemies depend on who stands with him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:447","b":"k:638","type":"develops","note":"447 speaks of the ruler who keeps rebukers. 638 gives the matching duty of the one beside him: even if the king rejects knowledge and knows nothing himself, the one at his side is bound to tell him what is sound (உறுதி உழையிருந்தான் கூறல் கடன்). 638 extends the idea to the case where the king is not the willing listener 447 assumes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:447","b":"k:784","type":"parallel_domain","note":"447 values supporters for their rebuking (இடிக்கும்) and says a ruler who has them cannot be ruined. 784 defines friendship by the same act: it is not for laughing but for going ahead to rebuke when one oversteps (மேற்சென்று இடித்தற் பொருட்டு). Rebuke is treated as the purpose of the bond in both statecraft and friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:449","b":"k:1030","type":"parallel_domain","note":"449 says there is no standing (நிலை) for those without the support of a prop (மதலையாம் சார்பு). 1030 says a family with no good person to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its base. The image of a structure that needs a prop is applied to a king in one verse and to a household in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:800","type":"agrees","note":"450 says abandoning one's tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than the enmity of many. 800 says to hold close the friendship of the stainless (மருவுக மாசற்றார் கேண்மை) and to be rid of the friendship of the unfit even at a price. 800 gives the same instruction for friendship and adds its other half, which bonds to drop.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:816","type":"parallel_domain","note":"450 weighs losing the good against the enmity of many and finds it ten times worse. 816 weighs close friendship with a fool against being a stranger to the wise and finds the second ten million times better (கோடி உறும்). Both rank relationships by a multiplied comparison, and in both the quality of the person outweighs whether the relation is friendly or hostile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:450","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"873 says one who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many (பல்லார் பகை கொள்பவன்) is more wretched than the mad. 450 uses the same phrase as its measure: letting go the tie with the good is ten times worse than that (பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்து). Read together, what 873 treats as an extreme of folly becomes the smaller of two harms in 450.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:451","b":"k:201","type":"parallel_domain","note":"451 says greatness fears small company (சிற்றினம் அஞ்சும் பெருமை) while smallness takes it for kin. 201 says those given to evil deeds do not fear them but the excellent do (தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்). In both, what marks the better person is fear of the thing the worse person is at ease with, once about company and once about deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:452","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"452 says water takes the nature of the land, and understanding the nature of one's company (இனத்தியல்பு). 959 says the sprout shows what lay in the ground (நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால் காட்டும்) and speech shows the family one was born in. Both treat a person as carrying the mark of the ground they stand in, 452 for the company kept and 959 for birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:452","b":"k:1323","type":"same_image","note":"452 uses water changing with the nature of the land it meets (நிலத்தியல்பால் நீர் திரிந்து) for understanding taking on the nature of one's company. In 1323 a lover calls the pair those joined as water with earth (நிலத்தொடு நீர் இயைந்தன்னார்), and Parimelazhagar explains that image by water taking on the land's nature. The same picture of water absorbed into soil is a caution about company in one place and a figure of union in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:453","b":"k:505","type":"tension","note":"453 says perception comes from the mind, but the word that one is 'such a person' comes from one's company (இன்னான் எனப்படும் சொல் இனத்தான் ஆம்). 505 says the touchstone of greatness and smallness is each person's own deeds (தத்தம் கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as that and nothing else. If 453 is read as saying what a person really is, and not only what people call them, the two name different tests of character: company or deeds.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:455","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says outer purity comes by water and inner purity is seen through truthfulness (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). 455 says purity of mind comes resting on purity of company. The book thus gives inner purity two supports, one's own truthfulness and the people one keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:456","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"456 says no deed fails to turn out well for those whose company is pure (இனந்தூயார்க்கு இல்லை நன்றாகா வினை), and Parimelazhagar gives the reason: their deeds are thought through with good company. 462 states that mechanism directly: nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining the matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:457","b":"k:651","type":"qualifies","note":"457 says goodness of mind gives gain (ஆக்கம்) and goodness of company gives all praise as well (இனநலம் எல்லாப் புகழும் தரும்). 651 has the same two-step form but says goodness of companions gives gain and goodness of action gives all that is wanted (துணைநலம் ஆக்கம் தரூஉம் வினைநலம் வேண்டிய எல்லாம் தரும்); Parimelazhagar there reads companions as giving wealth only. Company holds the higher place in 457 and the lower in 651, where one's own action outranks it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:458","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"458 says that even for the worthy who have goodness of mind in full, goodness of company is a safeguard (ஏமாப்பு உடைத்து). 448 says a king without the guard of people who rebuke him (இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத ஏமரா மன்னன்) perishes even with no one to ruin him. Both use the same word for protection (ஏமம்), and 448 shows what the company protects against: one's own unchecked faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:459","b":"k:243","type":"develops","note":"243 says those whose hearts are joined to அருள் do not enter the dark world of pain (அருள் சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சினார்க்கு இல்லை இருள் சேர்ந்த இன்னா உலகம் புகல்). 459 agrees that the next world comes from goodness of mind (மனநலத்தின் ஆகும் மறுமை) and adds that even this is made secure by goodness of company. The afterlife that 243 rests on the heart alone is given a second support in 459.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:460","b":"k:32","type":"parallel_domain","note":"460 is built on the form 'nothing beyond X as support, nothing beyond Y as affliction' (நல்லினத்தின் ஊங்கும் துணையில்லை). 32 uses the same form and the same word ஊங்கு for virtue: no gain beyond virtue, no ruin beyond forgetting it (அறத்தினூஉங்கு ஆக்கமும் இல்லை). The pairing shows good company being given, in the book on statecraft, the kind of unqualified ranking the opening chapters give to virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:460","b":"k:450","type":"develops","note":"460 says there is no support greater than good company and nothing that afflicts more than bad company (தீயினத்தின் அல்லற் படுப்பதூஉம் இல்). 450 says letting go the tie with the good is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many (பல்லார் பகை கொளலிற் பத்தடுத்த தீமைத்து). 460 names keeping bad company as the worst harm and 450 names losing good company; both rank these above open enemies, and Parimelazhagar glosses 460's second half as enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:461","b":"k:471","type":"develops","note":"461 lists the outcomes to be weighed before acting: loss, gain and later yield. 471, opening the next chapter with the same imperative, lists the strengths to be weighed: of the deed, oneself, the opponent and the allies (வினைவலியும் தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும் துணைவலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்). The second supplies what the first's calculation of gain and loss depends on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:461","b":"k:676","type":"develops","note":"461 tells one to weigh what will be lost, what will be gained and the later yield, and then act (அழிவதூஉம் ஆவதூஉம் ... ஊதியமும் சூழ்ந்து செயல்). 676 has the same closing command and also three items, but different ones: what it takes to finish, the obstacles, and the great yield at completion (முடிவும் இடையூறும் ... படுபயனும் பார்த்துச் செயல்). 461 is an account of cost and return; 676 keeps the return and adds the course of the work itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:462","b":"k:568","type":"agrees","note":"462 says nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining a matter with chosen company and thinking it through themselves (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு தேர்ந்தெண்ணி). 568 gives the negative case: the fortune of a king who does not think matters through with his circle (இனத்தாற்றி எண்ணாத வேந்தன்) and then rages will shrink. Both make deliberation with one's இனம் the condition of success, stated once as promise and once as warning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:463","b":"k:932","type":"parallel_domain","note":"463 says the wise do not undertake what loses the principal in hope of gain. 932 asks whether gamblers, who get one and lose a hundred (ஒன்றெய்தி நூறிழக்கும் சூதர்), have any way of living well. The gambler is the plain case of the venture 463 rules out, a small gain pursued at the cost of what one already had.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:464","b":"k:172","type":"parallel_domain","note":"464 says those who fear the harm called disgrace do not begin what is not clear to them (தெளிவிலதனைத் தொடங்கார் ... அஞ்சுபவர்). 172 has the same form: those ashamed of unfairness do not do blameworthy things out of desire for the gain (பழிப்படுவ செய்யார் நடுவன்மை நாணுபவர்). In both a fear or shame is what holds a person back, in 172 from a wrong act and in 464 from an unexamined one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:464","b":"k:428","type":"develops","note":"428 says not fearing what should be feared is folly and fearing it is the work of the knowing (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சல் அறிவார் தொழில்). 464 gives one such fear and what it does: fear of disgrace (இளிவென்னும் ஏதப்பாடு) keeps people from beginning what they have not made clear. The general rule of 428 is applied in 464 to the start of an undertaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:473","type":"develops","note":"465 says rising without full deliberation (வகையறச் சூழாது எழுதல்) benefits the enemy. 473 says many who did not know their own strength pressed on in enthusiasm and broke midway (ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்). 473 names one of the things left unconsidered, one's own strength, and shows the cost to oneself where 465 shows the gain to the enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:718","type":"same_image","note":"465 says setting out without thinking through every aspect is a way of setting one's enemies in a planting bed (பகைவரைப் பாத்திப் படுப்பது); Parimelazhagar reads this as establishing them in ground where they grow. 718 says speaking before those who understand is like pouring water into the bed of a growing plant (வளர்வதன் பாத்தியுள் நீர் சொரிந்தற்று). The same garden bed stands for fostering growth in both, wanted in 718 and brought on oneself in 465.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:465","b":"k:879","type":"same_image","note":"465 pictures a rash attack as planting the enemy in a bed where he will grow. 879, in a chapter on enemies, says to cut a thorn tree while it is young, since when hardened it wounds the hand that cuts it (இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க). Both treat the enemy as a plant whose growth is the danger: 879 says to stop it early, 465 warns against tending it by one's own haste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:466","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"466 says one is ruined by doing what is not fit to do, and also by not doing what is fit (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானும் கெடும்). 510 says trusting someone unexamined and doubting someone already examined and trusted both bring unending trouble (தேரான் தெளிவும் தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்). Each verse sets an error of excess beside an error of defect and gives them the same result, once for action and once for trust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:466","b":"k:672","type":"develops","note":"466 says both doing the unfit and leaving the fit undone bring ruin. 672 gives the same two-sided rule for timing: delay what should be done slowly, and do not delay what must be done without delay (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால தூங்கற்க தூங்காது செய்யும் வினை). 672 carries 466's point, that holding back is no safer than acting, into the question of when to act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:467","b":"k:509","type":"parallel_domain","note":"467 fixes the order for action: think first, then resolve, and no thinking postponed until after. 509 fixes the same order for trusting people: trust no one without examining, and after examining, trust what is to be trusted (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக). In both the examination belongs wholly before the commitment, and the commitment is then not to be reopened.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:468","b":"k:616","type":"qualifies","note":"616 says effort produces fortune and lack of effort brings want (முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும்). 468 says exertion that is not made by the right way (ஆற்றின் வருந்தா வருத்தம்) will give way even if many stand guarding it. 468 puts a condition on 616's praise of effort: it has to follow the proper method, and neither strain nor helpers make up for that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:468","b":"k:640","type":"parallel_domain","note":"468 says effort without the right method fails whatever support it has (பலர் நின்று போற்றினும் பொத்துப்படும்). 640 says those without practical capacity leave things unfinished even after planning them in proper order (முறைப்படச் சூழ்ந்தும் முடிவிலவே செய்வர் திறப்பாடு இலாதவர்). The two are complementary failures: effort without method in 468, and method on paper without the ability to carry it out in 640.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:987","type":"tension","note":"469 says doing good can itself be a mistake if the character of the person is not taken into account. 987 asks what worth has achieved if it does not do what is sweet even to those who have done one harm (இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே செய்யாக்கால்), which makes the recipient's conduct irrelevant. The conflict holds if 469's நன்றாற்றல் is read as doing good in general, as Pope and Kalaignar take it; Parimelazhagar narrows it to a king's use of gifts and soft words toward other kings, and on that reading the two do not meet.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:469","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"469 says there is error even in doing good when it is done without knowing each person's character (அவரவர் பண்பறிந்து ஆற்றாக்கடை). 1078 says the worthy are of use at a word, while the base, like sugar-cane, are of use only when crushed (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர் கரும்புபோல் கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்). 1078 is an instance of the difference in character 469 says must be known first: the approach that works on one kind of person does nothing with another.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"470 says to think and do what will not be scorned, because the world does not accept what does not accord with oneself (கொள்ளாத கொள்ளாது உலகு). 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world lack understanding, however much they have studied (உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்). Both make the world's acceptance a test that sound judgement has to meet, 470 for a ruler's choice of action and 140 for conduct in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:470","b":"k:280","type":"parallel_domain","note":"470 tells the one who acts to avoid what the world will scorn and not accept. 280 tells the ascetic that neither shaving nor matted hair is needed if he gives up what the world has condemned (உலகம் பழித்தது ஒழித்து விடின்); Parimelazhagar takes 'the world' there as the high-minded. The same measure, what the world rejects, governs a king's policy in one verse and a renouncer's conduct in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:250","type":"qualifies","note":"471 makes comparing strengths the test of whether to act. 250, in the chapter on compassion, uses the same comparison as a brake: when going against those weaker than oneself (தன்னின் மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து), one should think of oneself before the stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Being the stronger party permits action in 471, while 250 asks the stronger party to remember what that position feels like from below.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"471 tells one to weigh four strengths before acting: of the deed, oneself, the opponent and the allies (வினைவலி, தன்வலி, மாற்றான் வலி, துணைவலி ... தூக்கிச் செயல்). 675 gives a second checklist with the same closing command, five things to think through until the darkness clears: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள் கருவி காலம் வினை இடன் ... எண்ணிச் செயல்). Read together, 471 measures the parties to a contest and 675 adds the circumstances of the act, and only the deed (வினை) is on both lists.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:471","b":"k:861","type":"develops","note":"471 says to weigh one's own strength against the opponent's before acting (தன்வலியும் மாற்றான் வலியும் தூக்கிச் செயல்). 861 states what follows from that weighing when choosing an enemy: avoid opposing the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and pursue enmity with the weaker (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). 861 is 471's comparison turned into a rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:472","b":"k:462","type":"agrees","note":"472 says nothing fails for those who know what they can do and what must be known and proceed resting in that (ஒல்வது அறிவது அறிந்து ... செல்லாதது இல்). 462 says nothing is out of reach for those who act after examining the matter with chosen company and thinking it over themselves (தேர்ந்தெண்ணிச் செய்வார்க்கு அரும்பொருள் யாதொன்றும் இல்). The promise is the same in both and the condition differs: knowledge of one's own capacity in 472, counsel and deliberation in 462.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:472","b":"k:483","type":"parallel_domain","note":"472 promises that nothing fails for one who knows his capacity (ஒல்வது அறிந்து ... செல்லாதது இல்). 483 asks whether anything is hard for one who acts with the means and knows the time (அருவினை என்ப உளவோ கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து செயின்). The chapters on knowing strength and knowing time each make the same claim for their own subject, which suggests each is meant as one necessary condition, not a sufficient one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:594","type":"qualifies","note":"594 says gain asks the way and goes to one whose ஊக்கம் does not waver (அசைவிலா ஊக்கமுடையான் உழை). 473 says many who did not know their own strength set out on ஊக்கம் and broke midway (வலியறியார் ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார் பலர்). The same word names the quality praised in chapter 60 and the cause of failure here, so 473 limits 594: drive pays only when it is matched to a measured strength.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:612","type":"develops","note":"612 warns against letting a task perish in the middle of doing it (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்), because the world gives up on those who leave work unfinished. 473 describes how that happens: people start on enthusiasm without knowing their strength and break midway (இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 612 demands finishing; 473 puts the remedy before the start, in measuring one's strength.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:473","b":"k:899","type":"same_image","note":"473 says many who did not know their strength broke midway (இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 899 uses the same figure for a king: if those of exalted resolve are angered, even the king breaks midway and loses his kingship (இடைமுரிந்து வேந்தனும் வேந்து கெடும்). Parimelazhagar reads 473 as starting something against those stronger than oneself, and 899 gives one such case, a ruler who misjudges the power of the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:474","b":"k:863","type":"agrees","note":"474 lists three failings that bring quick ruin: not living in accord (அமைந்தாங்கு ஒழுகான்), not knowing one's measure (அளவறியான்) and admiring oneself. 863 lists the traits of one who is easy for an enemy, and two of them match: he does not know (அறியான்) and does not fit in with others (அமைவிலன்). The chapter on what makes a good enemy to have describes, from the other side, the man 474 says will perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:474","b":"k:978","type":"same_image","note":"474 says the one who admires himself (தன்னை வியந்தான்) perishes quickly. 978 uses the same phrase to define smallness: greatness always bows, and smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). In 978 self-admiration is a mark of character, and in 474 it is a miscalculation of strength with a practical result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:667","type":"same_image","note":"475 says even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if too many are piled on (பீலிபெய் சாகாடும் அச்சிறும்). 667 says not to scorn people for their appearance, since there are those who are like the linchpin of a great rolling chariot (உருள்பெருந்தேர்க்கு அச்சாணி அன்னார்). Both use the axle of a vehicle to say that size misleads: the light thing can break it and the small thing holds it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"475 says a light load breaks the axle when there is too much of it (அப்பண்டம் சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்). Parimelazhagar takes the verse as an indirect statement that a strong king who makes enemies of many because each is weak loses his strength when they combine. 873 says this outright: one who, alone, takes on the enmity of many (தமியனாய்ப் பல்லார் பகைகொள்பவன்) is more foolish than the mad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:475","b":"k:889","type":"parallel_domain","note":"475 says that feathers, light as they are, break an axle when piled too high. 889 says that enmity within, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்), has ruin in it. Both warn against judging a danger by the smallness of its unit, 475 because small things add up and 889 because of where the small thing sits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:476","b":"k:596","type":"qualifies","note":"596 says that whatever one thinks of, one should think of rising (உள்ளுவதெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல்), and that even if this is thwarted it is as if it were not. 476 says that those who have climbed to the tip of a branch and push beyond it (அஃதிறந்து ஊக்கின்) end their lives. 596 sets no ceiling on aspiration, while 476 sets one on action: there is a point past which striving higher is fatal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:476","b":"k:858","type":"parallel_domain","note":"476 says pushing on past the tip of the branch (இறந்து ஊக்கின்) ends one's life. 858 uses the same verb of hostility: leaning away from it is gain, and if one pushes on to excel in it (மிகல் ஊக்கின்), ruin pushes on in turn (ஊக்குமாம் கேடு). In both, the drive that the book elsewhere praises becomes the cause of ruin when it is carried past a limit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:477","b":"k:943","type":"parallel_domain","note":"477 says to give knowing the measure, for that is the way of guarding wealth while bestowing it (அளவறிந்து ஈக அது பொருள் போற்றி வழங்கு நெறி). 943 says to eat knowing the measure once the last meal is digested, for that is the way to carry the body far (அளவறிந்து உண்க அஃது உடம்பு பெற்றான் நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு). The two verses have the same build, a command to know the measure followed by 'that is the way', applied to wealth in one and the body in the other. Both verses are printed with அறவறிந்து in this edition; the reading அளவு is from Parimelazhagar's gloss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:478","b":"k:463","type":"agrees","note":"478 says a small income does no harm if spending does not exceed it. 463 says those with understanding do not undertake a deed that loses the capital for the sake of gain (ஆக்கம் கருதி முதலிழக்கும் செய்வினை ஊக்கார்). Both put keeping what one has ahead of getting more: 478 for the regular flow of income and spending, 463 for a single venture.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:478","b":"k:512","type":"qualifies","note":"512 says the work should go to one who widens the sources of income (வாரி பெருக்கி) and increases the yield. 478 says that even if the channel of income is narrow (ஆகாறு அளவிட்டிதாயினும்) there is no ruin, as long as the channel of outgo is not wider (போகாறு அகலாக்கடை). 512 values enlarging income, and 478 adds that safety depends on the relation between the two channels and not on the size of the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:479","b":"k:947","type":"parallel_domain","note":"479 says the life of one who lives without knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து வாழாதான்) perishes. 947 says that one who eats a great deal without regard to the measure of his digestive fire (தீயளவன்றித் தெரியான் பெரிதுண்ணின்) gets disease without measure (நோய் அளவின்றிப் படும்). Ignoring a measure brings ruin of wealth in one verse and of health in the other. 479 is printed with a corrupted character in அளவறிந்து in this edition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:480","b":"k:218","type":"qualifies","note":"218 says those who see what is due do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு even in a time without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும் ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்). 480 says ஒப்புரவு that does not weigh the limit of one's means (உளவரை தூக்காத) quickly destroys those means. The Book of Virtue praises not letting scarcity stop one's helpfulness, and the Book of Wealth, speaking of a ruler's resources, makes the weighing of means a condition of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:481","b":"k:500","type":"parallel_domain","note":"481 says the crow beats the owl by day (பகல்வெல்லும் கூகையைக் காக்கை), and Parimelazhagar notes that the owl is the stronger bird. 500 says a jackal kills a fearless war elephant in mud where its feet sink (காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும்). In both a weaker animal defeats a stronger one through a circumstance, time in 481 and ground in 500, which is how the two adjacent chapters make their cases.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:617","type":"parallel_domain","note":"482 pictures fortune (திரு) as something that leaves unless tied, and names timely action as the rope (தீராமை ஆர்க்கும் கயிறு). 617 pictures her as living in the effort of the one without sloth (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள் தாமரையினாள்), with her dark elder sister living in sloth. Both treat fortune as a visitor kept by conduct, and they name different conduct: timing in 482, steady effort in 617.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:482","b":"k:1028","type":"tension","note":"482 says that acting in accord with the season (பருவத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்) is the rope that keeps fortune from leaving. 1028 says those who raise their family have no season (குடிசெய்வார்க்கு இல்லை பருவம்), and that idling and thinking of one's dignity ruins it. The two conflict if பருவம் in 1028 means the right moment for action; Parimelazhagar instead takes it there as seasons of heat, rain and cold used as an excuse to put work off, which removes the conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:483","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"483 names two conditions that make a hard deed possible, means and time (கருவியான் காலம் அறிந்து). 675 lists five things to think through before acting: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள் கருவி காலம் வினை இடன்). 675 keeps 483's pair and sets it inside a fuller list.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:485","type":"develops","note":"484 says that even if one has the world in mind, it comes to hand when one considers the time and acts by the place (ஞாலம் கருதினும் கைகூடும் காலம் கருதி இடத்தாற் செயின்). 485 reverses the same words: those who have the world in mind wait, unshaken, considering the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது ஞாலம் கருதுபவர்). 484 states the condition for acting and 485 its other side, that until the time comes the ambitious do not move.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:497","type":"develops","note":"484 says the world comes to hand if one considers the time and acts by the place (இடத்தாற் செயின்). 497, in the next chapter, uses the same phrase: one needs no support but fearlessness if one thinks everything through and acts by the place (எண்ணி இடத்தால் செயின்). 484 names place as a second condition beside time, and the following chapter, with 497, takes it up as its own subject.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:484","b":"k:610","type":"parallel_domain","note":"484 says a king who considers time and place can gain even the world (ஞாலம் கருதினும் கைகூடும்). 610 says a king without sloth gains at once all the expanse crossed by the one who measured with his foot (மடியிலா மன்னவன் எய்தும் அடியளந்தான் தாஅயதெல்லாம்). Both promise the whole earth, one to timing and the other to unremitting effort, so the two chapters make the same claim for different qualities.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:671","type":"qualifies","note":"485 praises waiting for the right time. 671 says deliberation ends in reaching a resolve, and it is bad for that resolve to rest in delay (அத்துணிவு தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). 671 marks the limit of 485: waiting that is fixed on the time (காலம் கருதி) is part of the plan, and holding back after the decision, for no such reason, is a fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:485","b":"k:672","type":"agrees","note":"485 says those who aim at the world wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர் கலங்காது). 672 says to take one's time over what should be done slowly (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால) and not to delay what should be done without delay. Both treat waiting as a decision that fits some tasks, and 672 adds the second half that 485 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:486","b":"k:827","type":"parallel_domain","note":"486 says the holding back of a person of energy is like a fighting ram stepping back in order to charge (பொருதகர் தாக்கற்குப் பேருந் தகைத்து). 827 says not to trust humble words from an enemy, because the bending of a bow means harm (வில்வணக்கம் தீங்கு குறித்தமையான்). Both use a drawing back that comes before a blow: 486 recommends it to oneself, and 827 teaches one to see it in an opponent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:152","type":"tension","note":"152 says always to bear with an excess done to one, and that forgetting it is better still (பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும் அதனை மறத்தல் அதனினும் நன்று). 487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly at once but, watching for the time, burn within (காலம் பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). Both hold back the outward response, and they differ on what happens inside: 152 wants the wrong forgotten, 487 wants the anger kept. The conflict holds if both are read as general advice; 152 belongs to the householder's virtues and Parimelazhagar takes 487 as addressed to kings facing enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:487","b":"k:301","type":"tension","note":"301 says that the one who truly guards against anger guards it where it can take effect (செல்லிடத்துக் காப்பான் சினங்காப்பான்); where it cannot, guarding or not makes no difference. 487 has the bright hold back anger now, when showing it would not serve, and keep it inside for the right time. On the reading that 487's anger is to be released when it can succeed, it describes the restraint that 301 says does not count, and withholds the one that does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:151","type":"tension","note":"151 says that bearing with those who scorn one, as the earth holds up those who dig into it (அகழ்வாரைத் தாங்கும் நிலம்போல), is the highest thing. 488 uses a verb of carrying for the same act, bearing with those who would harm one (சுமக்க), and makes it a means: one bears them until their end comes and their head goes down. The two conflict if both are read as answers to how one should treat those who wrong one, since forbearance is the goal in 151 and a way to the enemy's fall in 488. 151 is addressed to the householder and 488 to a king, which is the other way to read them.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:488","b":"k:879","type":"qualifies","note":"488 says to bear with enemies until their end comes (இறுவரை காணின்). 879 says to cut down a thorn tree while it is young, because once it has hardened it destroys the hand that cuts it (இளைதாக முள்மரம் கொல்க ... காழ்த்த இடத்து). 879 limits 488's patience: waiting serves when time works against the enemy, and not when the enemy is growing stronger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:489","b":"k:26","type":"develops","note":"26 says the great are those who do what is hard to do, and the small are those who cannot (செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர்). 489 uses the same phrase and adds a condition of time: hard deeds are to be done at the moment a rare opportunity arrives (செய்தற்கரிய செயல்). 26 places the ability in the person; 489 says it also depends on a moment that is itself hard to come by (எய்தற்கரியது).","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:489","b":"k:333","type":"parallel_domain","note":"489 says that when what is hard to get has come, one should at that very point do what is hard to do (இயைந்தக்கால் அந்நிலையே செய்தற்கரிய செயல்). 333 says wealth has the nature of not staying, and that on getting it one should at once do what stays (அதுபெற்றால் அற்குப ஆங்கே செயல்). Both reason from something that will not last, an opportunity in 489 and wealth in 333, to acting at once, and 333 turns the king's rule of timing toward lasting good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:490","b":"k:126","type":"parallel_domain","note":"490 says to be like the heron in the season for folding up (கூம்பும் பருவத்து), and like its strike when the moment is right. 126 says that one who can draw in the five senses like a tortoise (ஆமைபோல் ஐந்தடக்கல்) in one birth has protection for seven. Both teach restraint through an animal that draws itself in. The tortoise's withdrawal is the discipline itself, and the heron's is half of a pair that ends in a strike (குத்து).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:490","b":"k:274","type":"parallel_domain","note":"490 holds up the heron, which stays still until the moment and then strikes, as a model for acting on enemies. 274 says that doing wrong under cover of austerity is like a hunter hiding in a bush to snare birds (புதல்மறைந்து வேட்டுவன் புள்சிமிழ்த்தற்று). The pattern of stillness that hides a coming strike is the same in both. It is recommended to the king waiting for his time, and it is the figure of fraud in one who wears the ascetic's appearance.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:491","b":"k:464","type":"develops","note":"464 says those who fear disgrace do not begin what is not clear to them (தெளிவு இலதனைத் தொடங்கார்). 491 gives the same ban on beginning (தொடங்கற்க எவ்வினையும்) a concrete test: until the place has been seen (இடங்கண்ட பின் அல்லது). Read together, knowing the ground is one specific form of the clarity 464 demands before any start.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:491","b":"k:747","type":"same_image","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 491's முற்றும் இடம் as a place from which the enemy can be encircled, and tells the king neither to act nor to scorn the foe before finding one. 747 defines a fort from the other side as what cannot be taken even by encircling (முற்றியும் ... பற்றற்கு அரியது). The shared siege verb shows attacker and defender reasoning about the same ground; Manakkudavar instead reads 491 as 'the place where the task can be completed', which would loosen this link.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:492","b":"k:741","type":"agrees","note":"492 says that even for those with fighting strength (முரண்சேர்ந்த மொய்ம்பினவர்க்கும்) the advantage of joining a fort (அரண்சேர்ந்தாம் ஆக்கம்) yields many gains. 741 says a fort is of value to those able to go on the offensive (ஆற்றுபவர்க்கும்) as well as to those who guard themselves in fear. Both deny that a fort is only for the weak: the chapter on place and the chapter on forts make the same point about the strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:861","type":"qualifies","note":"861 advises avoiding opposition to the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and pursuing enmity against the weaker. 493 says the weaker side can still become strong and win when it knows the ground and protects itself. 493 therefore sets a condition under which 861's rule about relative strength does not settle the outcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:493","b":"k:878","type":"develops","note":"493 says even those without strength win (ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப) if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves (போற்றிச் செயின்). 878 says the enemy's pride dies when one knows the way, builds oneself up and guards oneself (வகையறிந்து தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப). Self-guarding plus knowledge is the common recipe; 878 generalises 493's 'place' to knowing the way of acting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:494","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"494 says those who had planned (against you) lose their plan (எண்ணியார் எண்ணம் இழப்பர்) when you advance knowing the place and hold close to it. 440 says the enemies' scheme becomes void (ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்) when a ruler enjoys what he loves without letting the love be known. In both, the opponent's design is defeated before any fight by removing what it relied on: bad ground in one case, a known weakness in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:495","b":"k:500","type":"develops","note":"495 shows a strong animal losing outside its own element: the crocodile out of water is killed by others. 500 gives the sharper case, where a jackal kills a fearless war elephant in mud where its feet sink (காலாழ் களரில் நரியடும்). Together they state both halves: one's own ground makes one win, and the wrong ground lets even a much weaker creature win.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:496","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"496 says chariots with strong wheels do not run on the sea and sea-going ships do not run on land (கடலோடா ... நாவாயும் ஓடா நிலத்து); Manakkudavar takes it to mean that instruments must be made to suit the place. 675 lists means and place together (கருவி ... இடனொடு ஐந்தும்) among the five things to think through before acting. 496 shows why the two items in 675's list cannot be weighed separately: a tool is only a tool in its own medium.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:534","type":"develops","note":"497 says that one who thinks everything through and acts by the place needs no support but fearlessness (அஞ்சாமை அல்லால் துணைவேண்டா). 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை): Parimelazhagar explains that forest or hill forts are of no use to a frightened mind. The pair shows the two sides of one claim: place is enough given courage, and place is worthless without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:497","b":"k:875","type":"qualifies","note":"497 says no ally is needed (துணைவேண்டா) beyond fearlessness when the plan is complete and the ground right. 875 advises one who has no ally and two enemies (தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால்) to make one of the enemies a pleasing ally. 875 shows that the book does not treat being without allies as generally safe; 497's claim holds only under its stated condition of complete planning and the right place.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:498","b":"k:473","type":"parallel_domain","note":"473 says many who did not know their own strength pressed on out of zeal and broke midway (ஊக்கத்தின் ஊக்கி இடைக்கண் முரிந்தார்). 498 says the large army's zeal (ஊக்கம்) is destroyed when it enters the small army's ground. In both, drive that outruns a sober measure, of one's strength in one chapter and of the ground in the other, is what gets broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:498","b":"k:744","type":"develops","note":"498 says the drive of a commander with a large army is destroyed (உறுபடையான் ஊக்கம் அழிந்து விடும்) if he enters ground suited to a small army. 744 defines a fort as having little to guard and much room, and as destroying the drive of the foe who comes against it (உறுபகை ஊக்கம் அழிப்பது அரண்). The shared phrase shows that a fort is the built form of 498's principle: ground chosen so that numbers stop counting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:499","b":"k:495","type":"develops","note":"495 gives the image: the crocodile wins in its own deep water. 499 states the human case without an image: even people with no fortress and no great standing (சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும்) are hard to engage on the land where they live (உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது). 499 applies the crocodile's lesson to an invader's estimate of a weak-looking people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:501","b":"k:513","type":"develops","note":"501 names four tests, virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life (அறம்பொருள் இன்பம் உயிரச்சம்), by which a person is examined before being trusted (தேறப்படும்). 513 names four qualities, love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire, in whoever has which trust lies (நன்குடையான் கட்டே தெளிவு). The first four are trials applied from outside (Parimelazhagar describes staged temptations); the second four are the traits such trials are meant to find.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:501","b":"k:690","type":"develops","note":"501's fourth test is fear for one's life (உயிரச்சம்). 690 defines the envoy as one who, even if it brings his end (இறுதி பயப்பினும்), does not fall short in securing his lord's good. The envoy chapter shows what passing that fourth test looks like in service.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"502 makes birth in a good family (குடிப்பிறந்து) a ground for trust alongside freedom from fault. 133 says having good conduct is itself good birth, and a lapse in conduct becomes low birth (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை இழுக்கம் இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). On 133's definition, 502's first criterion cannot be checked apart from its second: family does not vouch for a person whose conduct fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:794","type":"parallel_domain","note":"502 places trust in one born of good family, free of faults, with a shame that recoils from blemish (குடிப்பிறந்து ... வடுப்பரியும் நாணுடையான்). 794 says the friendship of one born of good family who is ashamed of blame on himself (குடிப்பிறந்து தன்கண் பழிநாணுவானை) should be had even at a price. The same two criteria, birth and shame, are used to choose a king's officer and to choose a friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:502","b":"k:951","type":"develops","note":"502 lists good family first and shame last among the grounds for trust. 951 says integrity and shame are found together by nature (இயல்பாகச் செப்பமும் நாணும் ஒருங்கு) only in those born to a good house. 951 supplies the reasoning 502 leaves unstated: family is asked for because it is taken to be where shame comes naturally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:503","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"503 says hard learning and freedom from fault still leave some ignorance when one looks closely. 373 says that however many subtle books one studies, one's own inherent understanding still prevails (தன் உண்மை அறிவே மிகும்). Both set a limit on what study can change in a person, one in the chapter on choosing officers and one in the chapter on fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:503","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"503 observes that even in those who have learned hard things and are free of fault, it is rare on examination to find no blankness (இன்மை அரிதே வெளிறு). 504 gives the rule that follows: examine the qualities and the faults and judge by what predominates (மிகைநாடி மிக்க கொளல்). Parimelazhagar on 504 makes the bridge explicit, saying no one in the world has only qualities or only faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:793","type":"parallel_domain","note":"504 tells the king to examine a person's qualities and faults (குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி) and take whichever predominates. 793 tells one to know a person's qualities, family, faults and kin (குணமும் குடிமையும் குற்றமும் ... இனனும்) before forming a friendship. The same inventory is used in both domains, but only 504 says how to decide when the inventory is mixed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:504","b":"k:965","type":"qualifies","note":"504 lets the predominant side decide what a person is. 965 says even those like a hill are diminished if they do a lowering thing the size of a kundri seed (குன்றி அனைய செயின்). 965 names a kind of fault, the degrading act, that is not outweighed by the mass of a person's merits, which limits the simple weighing of 504.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:505","b":"k:986","type":"same_image","note":"505 says a person's own deeds are the touchstone (கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்) of greatness and smallness. 986 asks what the touchstone of worth is (சால்பிற்குக் கட்டளை யாதெனின்) and answers: accepting defeat even from those not one's equal. Both use the assayer's stone for testing people; 505 names the general test, deeds, and 986 picks one particular deed as the decisive one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:350","type":"same_image","note":"506 treats having no attachment (பற்றிலர்) as a reason not to trust a person, since such a one does not fear blame. 350 tells the renouncer to hold to the one without attachment (பற்றற்றான்) in order to let attachment go. The same word is a disqualification in the chapter on choosing officers and the goal in the chapter on renunciation; the contexts differ, so this is a contrast of valuation across the books and not a contradiction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:506","b":"k:502","type":"develops","note":"502 puts trust in one who has a shame that recoils from blemish (நாணுடையான்). 506 warns against trusting those who are cut off (அற்றார்), because they have no attachment and are not ashamed of blame (பற்றிலர் நாணார் பழி); Parimelazhagar takes அற்றார் as those without kin, Manakkudavar as those without conduct. The two verses are the positive and negative of one criterion, and 506 adds a cause: shame depends on having ties.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:507","b":"k:515","type":"agrees","note":"507 says trusting the ignorant with affection as the ground (காதன்மை கந்தா அறிவறியார்த் தேறுதல்) yields every kind of folly. 515 says work is not to be assigned to someone because he is dear (சிறந்தானென்று ஏவற்பாற்றன்று), only to one who knows, endures and can do it. Both exclude affection as a ground of appointment, one at the stage of trust and the other at the stage of assigning work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:507","b":"k:836","type":"develops","note":"507 warns that trusting those without understanding out of fondness brings all folly on the one who trusts. 836 describes the result from the other side: when a fool who does not know how takes up a task (கையறியாப் பேதை வினைமேற் கொளின்), it fails and he ends in fetters. 836 shows the outcome that 507's warning anticipates.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:508","b":"k:510","type":"develops","note":"508 and 510 share their closing line (தீரா இடும்பை தரும்) and their first fault, trusting without examining. 510 adds a second fault of equal weight: doubting one who has been trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்). Read after 508, 510 shows that the chapter's warning is not simply 'trust less': misplaced doubt is punished in the same words as misplaced trust.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:508","b":"k:792","type":"parallel_domain","note":"508: one who trusts an outsider without examining (தேரான் பிறனைத் தெளிந்தான்) gets trouble that does not end, extending to his descendants (வழிமுறை தீரா இடும்பை). 792: the friendship of one who does not inquire again and again ends in a grief that kills him (கடைமுறை தான்சாம் துயரம்). The same structure, unexamined acceptance followed by lasting harm, is stated for a king's officer and for a friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:518","type":"develops","note":"509 says trust no one without examining, and after examining, trust what is to be trusted (தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக தேறும் பொருள்). 518 says that after examining a person's fitness for the work (வினைக்குரிமை நாடிய பின்றை), make him fully entitled to it (அதற்குரியனாகச் செயல்). 518 turns 509's second half into an act: the trust that follows examination is shown by handing over real authority.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:588","type":"qualifies","note":"509 says that once a person has been examined, what is to be trusted should be trusted. 588 says even what a spy has found and brought back should be checked through another spy (மற்றுமோர் ஒற்றினால் ஒற்றிக் கொளல்). For intelligence reports the book keeps verifying after the agent is chosen, which bears on 509's phrase தேறும் பொருள்: Manakkudavar reads it as the matters in which the person is reliable, not everything.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:509","b":"k:791","type":"parallel_domain","note":"509 orders examination first and commitment after. 791 says nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry, because for those who keep friendship there is no letting go once it is made (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). Friendship follows the same sequence as appointment, and 791 states the reason the order matters: the commitment is hard to reverse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:519","type":"agrees","note":"510 says doubting one who has been trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. 519 says fortune leaves the one who thinks otherwise of the closeness of a man devoted to his work (கேண்மை வேறாக நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு). Both make the ruler's unfounded suspicion of a proven servant a cause of his own loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:510","b":"k:693","type":"parallel_domain","note":"510 speaks to the ruler: doubt of one already trusted brings trouble that does not end. 693 speaks to the courtier: guard against grave faults, because once suspicion has arisen it is hard for anyone to clear it (கடுத்தபின் தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). The two verses describe the same event, a ruler's suspicion, as lasting damage from each side of the relationship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:511","b":"k:422","type":"parallel_domain","note":"511 says the man to employ is one whose nature, having looked into good and bad (நன்மையும் தீமையும் நாடி), has taken to the good (நலம்புரிந்த தன்மையான்). 422 defines understanding as not letting the mind go wherever it goes but turning it from bad to good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). The test for a king's officer is the book's general definition of அறிவு applied to a person at work.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:511","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"504 tells the king to examine a man's qualities and faults and judge by what predominates (குணம்நாடிக் குற்றமும் நாடி ... மிகைநாடி). 511 uses the same verb நாடி, but now it is the candidate who examines good and bad and settles on the good. The king's weighing of the man in chapter 51 becomes, in chapter 52, a check on how the man himself weighs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:513","b":"k:682","type":"agrees","note":"513 places trust in one who has love, understanding, clarity and freedom from desire (அன்பறிவு தேற்றம் அவாவின்மை). 682 opens with the same pair, அன்பறிவு, and adds considered eloquence as the three things an envoy cannot lack. The two lists share a core of loyalty and knowledge and then differ by office: the officer handling affairs needs steadiness and no greed, the envoy needs speech.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:505","type":"develops","note":"505 makes each person's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness (தத்தம் கருமமே கட்டளைக் கல்). 514 says tests of every kind before the appointment still leave many who change once in the work. Both put the decisive evidence in action, and 514 adds that the action that counts is the work itself, after trust has been given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:510","type":"qualifies","note":"510 says that doubting a man one has examined and trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. 514 says that even when tested in every way (எனைவகையான் தேறியக் கண்ணும்), many people turn different through the nature of the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும்). Read together, 514 limits 510: testing does not settle the matter, so the ban on doubt cannot mean a ban on watching how the man changes in office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:514","b":"k:520","type":"develops","note":"514 observes that many who passed every test turn different through the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்). 520 tells the king to look into the one doing the work every day (நாடோறும் நாடுக மன்னன்). The chapter's closing instruction answers the problem 514 raises: since office changes people, examination has to continue after appointment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:515","b":"k:640","type":"develops","note":"515 requires for a task one who knows and endures and can carry it through (அறிந்தாற்றிச் செய்கிற்பாற்கு). 640 says that those without practical ability (திறப்பாடு இலாதவர்), though they plan in proper order, do things that never reach completion. 640 shows what 515's last requirement guards against: knowing is not enough without the capacity to finish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:516","b":"k:675","type":"develops","note":"516 asks the king to examine the doer, examine the deed, and act seeing how they fit the time (செய்வானை நாடி வினைநாடிக் காலத்தோடு எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்). 675 lists five things to think through before acting: resources, means, time, the deed and the place (பொருள்கருவி காலம் வினையிடனொடு ஐந்தும்). Deed and time are in both lists; 516 adds the one item 675 lacks, the person who will do it, which is the concern of a chapter on employing people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:516","b":"k:949","type":"parallel_domain","note":"516: examine the doer, the deed and the fit with the time, then act (காலத்தோடு எய்த உணர்ந்து செயல்). 949: the trained physician acts after considering the measure of the patient, the measure of the illness and the time (உற்றான் அளவும் பிணியளவும் காலமும் ... கருதிச் செயல்). Both end in செயல் after a three-part assessment of person, matter and time, the same procedure in statecraft and in medicine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:517","b":"k:510","type":"agrees","note":"517 says that once the king has reasoned 'this man will finish this by this means', he should leave it with him (அதனை அவன்கண் விடல்). 510 says that doubting one who has been examined and trusted brings endless trouble. Leaving the task with the man is the practical form of 510's rule: examination comes first and is then followed by a real handing over.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:517","b":"k:520","type":"qualifies","note":"517 tells the king to leave the task to the chosen man (அவன்கண் விடல்). 520, in the same chapter, tells the king to look into the one doing the work daily (நாடோறும் நாடுக), because the world stays straight only if he does. The delegation in 517 is therefore not a withdrawal of attention; Parimelazhagar's note on 520 says the checking should be done privately so that the man's standing is not damaged.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:519","b":"k:700","type":"qualifies","note":"519 warns the king against taking amiss the familiarity of a hard-working officer (வினைக்கண் வினையுடையான் கேண்மை). 700, addressed to the courtier, says that familiarity which does improper things on the thought 'we are old associates' (பழையம் எனக்கருதிப் பண்பல்ல செய்யும் கெழுதகைமை) brings ruin. The king must tolerate the liberty and the servant must not presume on it; each verse limits the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:519","b":"k:808","type":"parallel_domain","note":"519 says fortune leaves the one who thinks otherwise of the closeness of a man devoted to the work (கேண்மை வேறாக நினைப்பானை நீங்கும் திரு). 808 praises those with the strength of intimacy not to listen to talk of a friend's lapse (கேளிழுக்கம் கேளாக் கெழுதகைமை). The duty not to reinterpret a close person's conduct appears in kingship and in friendship; Parimelazhagar reads 519 as the king being turned by the words of those who resent the officer, which is the situation 808 names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:546","type":"same_image","note":"520 says that if the one doing the work does not bend, the world will not bend (கோடாமை கோடாது உலகு). 546 says victory comes from the king's sceptre, provided it does not bend (கோலதூஉங் கோடாதெனின்). கோடாமை, not bending, is the mark of the just sceptre, and 520 applies the same word to the officer, on whose straightness the world's straightness is made to depend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:553","type":"agrees","note":"520: let the king look daily into the one doing his work (நாடோறும் நாடுக மன்னன்). 553: the king who does not look daily and do justice loses his land daily (நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன் நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). The same phrase of daily examination is used for officers and for justice, and both tie the state of the land to that routine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:520","b":"k:584","type":"develops","note":"520 requires the king to examine the worker daily. 584 defines the spy as one who examines everyone, beginning with those who do the king's work (வினைசெய்வார்), then his kin and his enemies. 584 supplies the means by which the daily examination of 520 is carried out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:806","type":"parallel_domain","note":"521: kin keep honouring old ties (பழைமை) even when all is lost. 806: those who keep within the bounds do not give up, even in ruin (தொலைவிடத்தும்), the bond of those who have stood with them from of old (தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார் தொடர்பு). Loyalty to a long tie when fortune is gone is claimed as the mark of kin in one chapter and of true old friends in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:521","b":"k:1047","type":"tension","note":"521 says that honouring the old tie even when a man's means are gone (பற்றற்ற கண்ணும் பழைமை பாராட்டுதல்) is found among kin. 1047 says that one in poverty will be looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல நோக்கப்படும்). The conflict holds if 1047 is read as Pope reads it, of poverty in general; the verse's own qualifier அறஞ்சாரா, poverty unconnected with virtue, which Parimelazhagar and Subramuniyaswami keep, narrows it and reduces the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:522","b":"k:884","type":"qualifies","note":"522 says that if a man gets kin whose love does not lapse (விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம் இயையின்), they give many gains (ஆக்கம் பலவும் தரும்). 884 says that if hidden enmity of unreformed mind appears, it gives many ills that turn his circle bad (இனமாணா ஏதம் பலவும் தரும்). The two verses have the same shape with opposite content, and Parimelazhagar notes that 'whose love does not lapse' in 522 is said to exclude the inner enmity of chapter 89: kin are an asset only on that condition.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:524","b":"k:837","type":"develops","note":"524 says the fruit of having gained wealth is to live surrounded by one's kin (சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்பட ஒழுகல்). 837 says that when a fool gets great wealth, strangers eat their fill and his own people go hungry (ஏதிலார் ஆரத் தமர்பசிப்பர்). 837 shows the failure of exactly the use 524 names: wealth that does not reach one's own people has missed its purpose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:526","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"525 names giving and pleasant speech as what surrounds a man with kin. 526 keeps giving, now great giving (பெருங்கொடை), and replaces pleasant speech with not cherishing anger (பேணான் வெகுளி), and raises the result to a following unmatched on earth. The pair is not a repetition: the second verse moves from a positive manner to the removal of what drives people away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:527","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"527 ties gain (ஆக்கம்) to those who, like the crow, call others before eating. 227 says the disease of hunger does not touch one who is habituated to sharing his food (பாத்தூண் மரீஇயவனை). Both hold that sharing food protects the sharer's own fortune, stated in the Book of Virtue as freedom from hunger and here as prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:527","b":"k:1035","type":"same_image","note":"527 praises the crow for not hiding (கரவா) its food and for calling others to eat. 1035 says those who live by eating what their own hands produce do not beg and give to those who ask without hiding (கரவாது). Not concealing what one has, கரவு being the book's word for the withholder's fault in chapters 106 and 107, is the shared mark of the crow and of the farmer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:528","b":"k:542","type":"same_image","note":"528 ends: many will live looking to that (அதுநோக்கி வாழ்வார் பலர்). 542 says the world lives looking to the sky and the people live looking to the king's sceptre (கோல்நோக்கி வாழுங் குடி). The phrase 'live looking to' makes the king's conduct something people depend on as they depend on rain; 528 uses it for his recognition of each person's worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:528","b":"k:972","type":"develops","note":"528 says that if the king looks at people not in common but according to rank (பொதுநோக்கான் வேந்தன் வரிசையா நோக்கின்), many will live looking to that. 972 says birth is alike for all, but distinction is not alike, because of the difference in what people do (சிறப்பொவ்வா செய்தொழில் வேற்றுமையான்). 972 gives a basis for the differential regard of 528: Parimelazhagar reads வரிசை there as each person's merit, and 972 locates such difference in conduct, not birth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:529","b":"k:530","type":"qualifies","note":"529 says estranged kin return of themselves once the cause of discord is gone. 530 turns to one who left and has come back for a reason (காரணத்தின் வந்தானை) and tells the king to attend to it and accept him only after reflection (இழைத்திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்). The pair separates two kinds of return, one that follows from the king's own correction and one prompted by the returner's interest, and prescribes welcome for the first and caution for the second.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:529","b":"k:633","type":"develops","note":"529 says those who were one's own and left will return to kinship when the cause of the discord is absent (அமராமைக் காரணம் இன்றி வரும்). 633 lists among a minister's abilities reuniting those who have parted (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). 529 says how that reunion comes about: the ground of the estrangement has to be removed, which Parimelazhagar takes as a fault on the king's own side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:530","b":"k:509","type":"develops","note":"509 gives the rule: trust no one without examining, then trust (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது). 530 applies examination to a man who was already one's own, left, and came back for a reason: he is to be received after reflection (எண்ணிக் கொளல்). Leaving resets the earlier trust, so the test of chapter 51 has to be done again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:530","b":"k:830","type":"parallel_domain","note":"530 says the king should attend to the reason for which a man who left has come back, wait, and accept him after thinking it over (இழைத்திருந்து எண்ணிக் கொளல்). 830 says that when an enemy turns friend, one should be friendly in face and withhold the heart (முகநட்டு அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்). Both deal with someone who comes over after having been apart and both withhold immediate trust, but 530 ends in acceptance of the kinsman while 830 ends in letting the former enemy go.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:531","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"531 states the danger: slackness from the elation of great joy is worse than excessive anger. 539 gives the practice against it: when one grows strong in one's own joy (மகிழ்ச்சியின் மைந்துறும் போழ்து), call to mind those ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:601","type":"parallel_domain","note":"532 says heedlessness kills fame (பொச்சாப்புக் கொல்லும் புகழை). 601 says the undimmed lamp called family goes out when the grime called sloth creeps over it (மடியென்னும் மாசூர மாய்ந்து கெடும்). In both, an inner lapse, not an outside enemy, destroys a standing built up over time.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:532","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"532 uses as its comparison that constant poverty kills understanding (அறிவினை நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன்றாங்கு). 1046 describes one form of it: even when the poor speak good matter well understood, their words and meaning go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்); Parimelazhagar says nobody listens, so their learning yields nothing. Read together, the 'killing' of understanding in 532 includes its losing all effect in the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 says those without understanding have nothing, whatever they possess (என்னுடைய ரேனும் இலர்). 534 says the heedless have no good (பொச்சாப் புடையார்க்கு நன்கு இல்லை), which Parimelazhagar reads as: even with every kind of wealth, they get no benefit from it. The same logic of having without having is applied to lack of understanding in one verse and to lack of vigilance in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:741","type":"tension","note":"534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை). 741 says a fort is of value both to those who march out and to those who, in fear, protect themselves (அஞ்சித் தற்போற்றுபவர்க்கும் பொருள்). The conflict holds if 534's first half is taken as a plain claim about forts and the fearful; Parimelazhagar's readings ease it, since he takes 534 as fear in the mind making a fort useless and 741 as the weaker side prudently taking refuge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:534","b":"k:750","type":"agrees","note":"750 says a fort, whatever its excellences, is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்). 534 says the fearful have no fort and the heedless have no good (நன்கு). Both hold that an outward advantage counts for nothing without the inner quality needed to use it, and both use the fort to say so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:535","b":"k:429","type":"agrees","note":"429 gives the positive case: those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) meet no affliction that shakes them. 535 gives the negative: one who did not guard beforehand later grieves over his own fault (தன்பிழை பின்னூறு இரங்கி விடும்). Together they show foresight treated as part of understanding and its absence as heedlessness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:535","b":"k:655","type":"develops","note":"655 tells one not to do deeds over which one will grieve, saying 'what have I done' (எற்றென்று இரங்குவ செய்யற்க). 535 uses the same verb, இரங்கு, for the grief of one who failed to guard in advance. The pairing shows regret following from omission in 535 as it follows from commission in 655.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:536","b":"k:582","type":"develops","note":"536 says nothing equals unfailing vigilance toward everyone at all times (யார்மாட்டும் என்றும்). 582 says it is the king's work to know quickly everything that happens to everyone at all times (எல்லார்க்கும் எல்லாம் நிகழ்பவை எஞ்ஞான்றும்). The same universal scope appears in both, and 582 gives the general vigilance of 536 a concrete form in intelligence gathering.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:537","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"611 says one must not lose heart on the thought that a thing is hard (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை வேண்டும்), since effort gives the greatness needed. 537 says nothing is beyond doing as 'hard' (அரிய என்று ஆகாத இல்லை) when done with unforgetting care. Each denies that difficulty is a reason to give up, and each names its own remedy: effort in 611, sustained attention in 537.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:538","b":"k:466","type":"develops","note":"466 says one is ruined by not doing what is fit to do (செய்தக்க செய்யாமை யானுங் கெடும்). 538 says what has been praised must be carefully done, and those who slight it and do not do it (செய்யாது இகழ்ந்தார்க்கு) have nothing through seven births. 538 takes the omission half of 466 and extends its cost beyond this life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:538","b":"k:835","type":"parallel_domain","note":"835 says a fool can make, in one birth, the mire he will sink into for seven (எழுமையும் தான்புக் கழுந்தும் அளறு). 538 says those who neglect what is praised have no good through seven births (எழுமையும் இல்). Both attach a seven-birth consequence to conduct in this life, folly in one and neglect of duty in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:539","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"250 says: when you move against those weaker than you, think of yourself before those stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). 539 says: when you grow strong in your own joy, think of those ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக). Both prescribe a deliberate act of recollection at the moment of feeling powerful, for the sake of compassion in 250 and of vigilance in 539.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:539","b":"k:930","type":"parallel_domain","note":"930 asks whether a drinker, when sober and seeing another man drunk (களித்தானைக் காணுங்கால்), does not reflect on the slackness of his own drinking (உள்ளான்கொல் உண்டதன் சோர்வு). 539 tells one elated with joy to reflect (உள்ளுக) on those whom negligence ruined. Both correct an intoxicated state by looking at its effect in other people, and 930's word சோர்வு is the one 531 uses for the slackness of joy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:551","b":"k:552","type":"develops","note":"551 compares the oppressive king to murderers and finds him worse. 552 compares the king who demands wealth to a man standing with a spear saying 'hand it over' (வேலொடு நின்றான் இடுவென்றது). Together the two opening verses of the chapter set the unjust ruler beside common criminals, first the killer and then the robber.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:552","b":"k:756","type":"qualifies","note":"756 names what is the king's wealth: what comes unowned, customs duty, and tribute from enemies (உறுபொருளும் உல்கு பொருளும் தன் ஒன்னார்த் தெறுபொருளும்). 552 treats a sceptre-holder's 'asking' (இரவு) of his people as highway robbery; Parimelazhagar notes that the word இரவு marks it as something other than the due tax. 756 shows the lawful sources against which 552's demand is measured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:554","b":"k:381","type":"develops","note":"381 lists the six things a king must have, among them people and resources (படைகுடி கூழ்...). 554 says a king who bends the sceptre and acts without reflection loses resources and people together (கூழுங் குடியும் ஒருங்கிழக்கும்). The two things injustice costs him in 554 are two of the six that make a king in 381.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:554","b":"k:568","type":"parallel_domain","note":"568 says the fortune of a king who does not think things through with his circle and then rages in anger will shrink (எண்ணாத வேந்தன் ... சிறுகும் திரு). 554 says a king who acts without reflection (சூழாது) and bends the sceptre loses wealth and people. In both, the king's loss is traced to acting without deliberation, shown in anger in 568 and in injustice in 554.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:567","type":"same_image","note":"567 says harsh words and punishment beyond bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength (அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்). 555 says the tears of the afflicted are a tool that wears down wealth (செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). Both describe the effect of a king's cruelty as slow abrasion by a tool, using the same verb தேய்க்கும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:555","b":"k:828","type":"same_image","note":"828 says a weapon hides within an enemy's folded hands, and his shed tears are the same (படையொடுங்கும் ... அழுத கண்ணீரும் அனைத்து). 555 calls the tears shed by the afflicted the weapon that wears away wealth (அழுத கண்ணீர் ... படை). Both join the same two words, tears and weapon: in 828 the tears conceal a weapon, and in 555 the tears are the weapon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:556","b":"k:563","type":"agrees","note":"563 says a king of cruel sceptre who goes on doing what terrifies will certainly and quickly perish (ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்). 556 says kings endure through just rule (மன்னர்க்கு மன்னுதல் செங்கோன்மை) and without it their lustre does not endure. Both tie how long a king lasts to how he rules; Parimelazhagar takes what endures in 556 to be the king's fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:557","b":"k:1192","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1192 a woman says that the tender care a lover gives the one who loves him (வீழ்வார் அளிக்கும் அளி) is like the sky giving rain to those who live by it (வாழ்வார்க்கு வானம் பயந்தற்று). 557 says a king's lack of அளி is to living beings what lack of rain is to the earth. The same word அளி and the same rain comparison describe dependence on a ruler in one book and on a beloved in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:55","type":"parallel_domain","note":"55 says that when a wife who worships her husband says 'rain', it rains (பெய்யெனப் பெய்யும் மழை). 559 says that when a king bends justice, the sky will not rain (ஒல்லாது வானம் பெயல்). In both, rain responds to a person's keeping or failing the duty of their station, the wife's in the household and the king's in the realm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:559","b":"k:239","type":"parallel_domain","note":"239 says land that bears a body without renown declines in its blameless rich yield (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). 559 says the sky holds back rain when the king bends justice, and the next verse, 560, uses the same verb for cows' yield (ஆபயன் குன்றும்). In each, nature's yield falls in response to a human moral failing, an individual's in 239 and a ruler's in 559.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:560","b":"k:19","type":"parallel_domain","note":"19 says that if the sky does not give, giving and austerity will not remain in the wide world (தானம் தவம்இரண்டும் தங்கா). 560 says that if the guardian does not guard, cows' yield declines and the six-occupation men forget their book. In both, religious and learned life depends on a provider, the rain in 19 and the king in 560, and fails when the provider fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:561","b":"k:579","type":"qualifies","note":"561 makes the king the one who punishes in proportion (ஒத்தாங்கு ஒறுப்பது வேந்து), while 579 calls it the highest quality to show kindly regard and bear with even those whose way is to hurt (ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும் ... பொறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பே தலை). The same root ஒறு sits on opposite sides. Parimelazhagar separates the cases: 561 concerns wrongs done to weaker subjects, 579 wrongs done to the ruler himself, so forbearance is limited to injuries that are the ruler's own to forgive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:562","b":"k:550","type":"qualifies","note":"562 asks for a light stroke after a stern show (மெல்ல எறிக); Manakkudavar takes this to mean the penalty should fall short of what the offence deserves. 550 says a king's punishing the cruel with death (கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல்) is like weeding the young crop. The pairing marks where gentleness stops: for those 550 calls கொடியார், the book accepts the severest penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:562","b":"k:567","type":"develops","note":"562 tells those who want lasting prosperity to raise the rod sharply but strike gently (கடிதோச்சி மெல்ல எறிக). 567 names what happens when the stroke is not gentle: punishment past the bound (கையிகந்த தண்டம்), with harsh words, is a file that wears down the king's strength. 562 gives the practice and 567 the cost of abandoning it; the 'long prosperity' (நெடிதாக்கம்) of one is the 'conquering strength' (அடுமுரண்) eroded in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:563","b":"k:548","type":"agrees","note":"563 says a cruel-sceptred king who keeps doing what terrifies (வெருவந்த செய்தொழுகும் வெங்கோலன்) will certainly and quickly perish (ஒருவந்தம் ஒல்லைக் கெடும்). 548 says a king who is not easy of access and does not inquire and do justice will sink and perish of himself (தானே கெடும்). Neither verse needs an enemy to bring the king down: in both the misrule is itself the cause of the fall.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:564","b":"k:386","type":"agrees","note":"386 says that if a king is easy to see and not harsh of speech, his land is spoken of above others (மீக்கூறும் மன்னன் நிலம்). 564 gives the reverse case: where people say 'our lord is harsh' (இறைகடியன்), the king quickly perishes. Both verses measure a reign by what is said about it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:564","b":"k:555","type":"develops","note":"564 says the king of whom the painful words 'our lord is harsh' (இறைகடியன் என்றுரைக்கும் இன்னாச்சொல்) are spoken will quickly perish. 555 says the tears of those who cannot bear their affliction are the weapon that wears away wealth (செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). In both, what destroys the ruler comes from the subjects' suffering, as speech in one verse and as tears in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:565","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"565 says the great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) of an inaccessible, grim-faced man is as if seen by a demon; Parimelazhagar explains that no one approaches him, so the wealth is of use neither to him nor to others. 1000 says the great wealth (பெருஞ்செல்வம்) gained by one without courtesy (பண்பிலான்) is like good milk spoiled by its vessel. Outside the court the same judgment holds: the holder's manner ruins wealth that is sound in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:566","b":"k:577","type":"develops","note":"566 says that if the ruler is harsh of speech and 'eyeless' (கண்ணிலன்), his long-standing wealth perishes at once; Parimelazhagar takes கண் here to stand for கண்ணோட்டம். 577, in the next chapter, states the equation directly: those without kindly regard have no eyes (கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லவர் கண்ணிலர்). The single word in 566 is the idea the following chapter spells out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:567","b":"k:888","type":"same_image","note":"567 calls harsh words and excessive punishment a file (அரம்) that wears down (தேய்க்கும்) the king's conquering strength. 888 says a family with enmity inside it wears away (தேயும்) like metal rubbed by a file (அரம்பொருத பொன்போல), its strength ground down. In both the file stands for slow loss of strength from within, by the king's own conduct in one and by a house's own members in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:568","b":"k:306","type":"parallel_domain","note":"306 says anger, the fire that kills what it touches, burns even the safe raft called one's people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்). 568 shows this at court: the king who neglects his circle (இனம்) and then turns his anger on them (சினத்தாற்றிச் சீறின்) loses his fortune; Parimelazhagar adds that the frightened ministers leave him. The general warning in the book of virtue becomes a specific danger for a ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:435","type":"parallel_domain","note":"435 says the life of one who does not guard before a fault arrives (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்) perishes like straw before fire. 569 applies the same logic to war: the king who has not prepared his defence beforehand is overtaken when the battle arrives (செருவந்த போழ்தில்) and perishes. Both place the failure in the time before the danger, not in the moment of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:569","b":"k:534","type":"qualifies","note":"569 says a king who has made no defence (சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்) will be seized by fear when war comes and quickly perish. 534 says that for the fearful there is no fort (அச்ச முடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை); Parimelazhagar explains that defences are no use to a frightened mind. One verse says lack of a fort produces fear, the other that fear cancels a fort, so the fort is necessary but not sufficient.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:570","b":"k:447","type":"develops","note":"447 asks who can ruin a ruler who keeps as his own those who will rebuke him (இடிக்குந் துணையாரை யாள்வாரை). 570 shows the opposite court: the harsh sceptre gathers the unlearned around it (கல்லார்ப் பிணிக்கும்), because, as Parimelazhagar says, such a ruler admits only those who will go along with him. The two verses together make a ruler's choice of companions a sign of the kind of rule he runs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:571","b":"k:557","type":"agrees","note":"557 says that a king's lack of tender care (அளியின்மை) is to those who live under him what lack of rain is to the earth. 571 states the positive side: the world exists because kindly regard exists; Parimelazhagar reads this of kings. In both verses the ruler's kindness is treated as a condition of life for the people, not as an ornament only.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:571","b":"k:996","type":"agrees","note":"571 says this world exists because the great beauty called kindly regard exists (கண்ணோட்டம் ... உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு). 996 says the world exists by resting on people of courtesy, and would otherwise sink into the earth and perish (பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம்). Both use the same construction, உண்டு உலகு, to say that a human quality and not power keeps the world going; 571 says it in a chapter on kings and 996 of people in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:983","type":"develops","note":"983 lists kindly regard (கண்ணோட்டம்) with love, shame, obligingness and truth as the five pillars that hold up worth (சால்பு). 572 gives it a wider load: the conduct of the world rests in it (கண்ணோட்டத் துள்ளது உலகியல்). The quality that is one support of personal character in 983 is the support of social life in 572.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:572","b":"k:1003","type":"same_image","note":"572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a burden to the earth (அஃதிலார் உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). 1003 says the very appearance of men who cling to accumulation and do not want renown is a burden to the earth (தோற்றம் நிலக்குப் பொறை). The phrase is identical, and in both the people called a burden are those who are of no use to others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:573","b":"k:79","type":"parallel_domain","note":"79 asks what all the outer limbs can do for those who lack love, the inner limb (புறத்துறுப் பெல்லாம் எவன்செய்யும் ... அன்பிலவர்க்கு). 573 asks the same kind of question of the eye that lacks kindly regard. In both a visible organ is counted useless without the inner quality it should serve.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:573","b":"k:705","type":"parallel_domain","note":"573 asks what use an eye is without kindly regard (கண்என்னாம் கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்), as a melody is no use that does not fit the song. 705 asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read another's sign (என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்). Both judge the eye by what it does for another person, kindness in one verse and understanding in the other, and not by sight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:574","b":"k:406","type":"parallel_domain","note":"406 says the unlearned can only be said to exist (உளரென்னும் மாத்திரையர்) and are otherwise like barren saline land. 574 says eyes without kindly regard only seem to be there in the face (உளபோல் முகத்து) and do nothing more. Both verses grant bare existence and deny any use, of unlearned persons in one case and of unkind eyes in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:574","b":"k:578","type":"develops","note":"574 says eyes without kindly regard in due measure (அளவினால் கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்) do nothing but seem to be in the face; Parimelazhagar takes அளவினால் as excluding excessive indulgence. 578 says what the measure is: kindly regard shown without ruining the task (கருமம் சிதையாமல் கண்ணோட வல்லார்). The one word of limit in 574 becomes the whole subject of 578.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:575","b":"k:1014","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1014 asks whether shame is not an ornament (அணி) to the worthy, and whether without it their proud walk is not a disease (அஃதின்றேல் பிணி). 575 has the same shape: kindly regard is the eye's ornament (அணிகலம்), and without it (அஃதின்றேல்) the eye is a sore (புண்). In both the missing virtue does not leave the thing plain but turns it into an affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:577","b":"k:393","type":"parallel_domain","note":"393 says those who are called 'people with eyes' are the learned (கண்ணுடையர் என்பவர் கற்றோர்). 577 says those without kindly regard are eyeless and those who have eyes do not lack it (கண்ணுடையார் கண்ணோட்டம் இன்மையும் இல்). The same title, கண்ணுடையார், is awarded on two different tests, learning and kindly regard; the verses do not conflict, but a reader should see that the book defines 'having eyes' twice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:579","b":"k:152","type":"develops","note":"152 says always bear with excess against you (பொறுத்தல் இறப்பினை என்றும்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even when you are able to punish'. 579 is that case in the ruler, who has the power to punish and is asked to bear with those who harm him. 152 goes a step further than 579 by preferring forgetting the wrong to bearing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:580","b":"k:805","type":"develops","note":"805 advises that when friends do something painful, one should take it as either folly or great intimacy (பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க). 580 describes people who drink poison they have seen poured. 805 supplies a way of construing a friend's injury that makes the conduct in 580 intelligible, though 580's own stated motive is the wish for நாகரிகம் and not a charitable reading of the giver.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:582","b":"k:520","type":"agrees","note":"520 tells the king to look daily (நாடோறும் நாடுக) into the one doing his work, since the world stays straight if he does; 582 makes constant, quick knowledge of everyone's affairs the king's own occupation (வேந்தன் தொழில்). Both treat continuous inquiry, not occasional inspection, as the core of rule; 582 widens the object from the king's officers to everyone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:582","b":"k:553","type":"develops","note":"582 makes it the king's work to know quickly, at all times (எஞ்ஞான்றும் வல்லறிதல்), all that happens to everyone; 553 says a ruler who does not inquire daily and do justice (நாடொறும் நாடி முறைசெய்யா) loses his land day by day. 553 gives the purpose and the cost: the daily knowing of 582 exists to feed daily justice, and its absence is paid for in the kingdom itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:583","b":"k:423","type":"parallel_domain","note":"583 denies victory to a ruler who has things spied out but does not discern their meaning (ஒற்றிப் பொருள்தெரியா); 423 defines understanding as seeing the true substance of whatever is heard from anyone (அப்பொருள் மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது). The pairing shows the spy chapter applying the general rule about hearsay to intelligence: collecting reports is not yet knowing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:510","type":"tension","note":"510 says that doubting a person one has examined and trusted (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவும்) brings unending trouble; 584 has spies examine everyone (அனைவரையும் ஆராய்வது), including the king's own officers. The conflict holds if standing surveillance of appointed officers counts as the ஐயுறவு that 510 forbids; if spying is read as routine inquiry and not suspicion of a particular person, the two can stand together.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:514","type":"develops","note":"584 defines the spy as one who examines everyone, beginning with those who do the king's work (வினைசெய்வார்); 514 observes that even people trusted after every test often turn different through the nature of the work (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும்). 514 supplies the reason for watching one's own appointed officers: the testing done before appointment does not settle how they behave afterwards.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:584","b":"k:882","type":"develops","note":"584 puts the king's own kin (தம் சுற்றம்) among those a spy examines, alongside his enemies (வேண்டாதார்); 882 says not to fear enemies who are like a sword but to fear the tie with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர்). The chapter on internal enmity explains why 584 does not exempt relatives from scrutiny.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:585","b":"k:686","type":"same_image","note":"585 requires of a spy an unsuspected appearance, not flinching before eyes (கண்ணஞ்சாது) and never letting anything out; 686 requires of an envoy that he not fear the eye (கண் அஞ்சான்), which Parimelazhagar glosses in both verses as the angry look of the other side. The open agent and the covert one are given the same nerve; what differs is that the envoy must speak so it goes home, and the spy must give nothing away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:585","b":"k:689","type":"parallel_domain","note":"585 ends with the spy's ability never to let anything spill (யாண்டும் உகாஅமை); 689 wants the bearer of a king's message to be a hard man whose mouth does not let slip a damaging word (the edition prints வாய்சேரா; Parimelazhagar reads வாய் சோரா). Both make control of what leaves the mouth under pressure the test of the king's agents abroad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:586","b":"k:276","type":"tension","note":"276 says none are more hard-hearted than those who, unrenounced at heart, live by deceiving in the likeness of renouncers (துறந்தார்போல் வஞ்சித்து வாழ்வார்); 586 has the spy take the guise of renouncers (துறந்தார் படிவத்தராகி) to get into places and investigate. The conflict holds if 276 condemns the false ascetic guise as such; Parimelazhagar narrows 276 to cheating donors, and on that reading the spy's disguise in the king's service falls outside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:587","b":"k:695","type":"qualifies","note":"695 tells those who serve a king not to listen in on or press after anything when a secret (மறை) is in progress, and to hear it only when he releases it; 587 makes the ability to hear concealed things (மறைந்தவை கேட்க வற்றாகி) the definition of a spy. The pairing marks a boundary: prying into secrets is a skill the king directs at others, and a fault when his own attendants turn it on him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:587","b":"k:702","type":"parallel_domain","note":"587 defines the spy as able to learn what was concealed and to hold what he has learned without doubt (ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே); 702 ranks with a god the one who knows without doubt what is within another (ஐயப்படாஅது அகத்தது உணர்வானை). Both prize certain knowledge of what others hide, one gained by inquiry among informants and the other by reading a person's mind from signs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:588","b":"k:587","type":"qualifies","note":"587 requires the spy to be free of doubt about what he has learned (அறிந்தவை ஐயப்பாடு இல்லதே); 588 tells the king to check even such a report (தந்த பொருளையும்) through another spy. The spy's certainty is a quality of the source and not a warrant for the king, who is to keep doubting until reports agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:588","b":"k:589","type":"develops","note":"588 says that even what one spy found and brought should be confirmed through another spy (மற்றுமோர் ஒற்றினால்); 589 adds that spies should be run so that one does not know of another, and that what three say in agreement is to be accepted (மூவர் சொற்றொக்க தேறப்படும்). 589 turns the second opinion of 588 into a procedure: sources kept independent, and a threshold of agreement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:589","b":"k:509","type":"agrees","note":"509 says to trust no one without examining and, after examining, to trust what is to be trusted (தேர்ந்தபின் தேறுக); 589 says to run spies unknown to one another and to accept what three say in agreement (தேறப்படும்). Both give the same two-step rule, test first and then commit, 509 for persons and 589 for reports.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:590","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"440 says that if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without others knowing of his love (காதல் அறியாமை), his enemies' schemes come to nothing; 590 forbids showing favour to a spy where others can know of it. Both treat a ruler's visible attachments and rewards as information that others can use against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:590","b":"k:663","type":"parallel_domain","note":"590 warns that a king who honours a spy so that others know of it (சிறப்பறிய) has himself let the secret out (புறப்படுத்தான் ஆகும் மறை); 663 says a deed should be done so that it shows only at the end, since showing midway brings lasting trouble (இடைக்கொட்கின்). In both the actor's own conduct, not an enemy's skill, is what exposes the undertaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:591","b":"k:1005","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1005 says those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing even with crores piled up (கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்); 591 asks whether those without energy really have what they have. Both separate holding goods from having them, 1005 by the test of use and 591 by the test of the holder's drive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:592","b":"k:333","type":"develops","note":"333 says wealth has the nature of not staying (அற்கா இயல்பிற்று) and concludes that on getting it one should at once do what stays; 592 also says goods do not stay (நில்லாது நீங்கிவிடும்) and concludes that the lasting possession is the inner drive (உள்ளம் உடைமை). Starting from the same fact, the Book of Virtue points to enduring deeds and the Book of Wealth to an inner resource that can make wealth again.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:594","b":"k:617","type":"agrees","note":"617 says the lotus goddess lives in the effort of the unslothful (மடியிலான் தாளுளாள்); 594 says prosperity finds its own way to the person of unwavering energy. Both personify fortune as choosing where it lives by the person's exertion, and neither leaves it to chance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:595","b":"k:396","type":"parallel_domain","note":"396 says a sand-well yields water as far as it is dug, and understanding as far as one has learned (தொட்டனைத்து... கற்றனைத்து); 595 says a water-flower's stem is as long as the water is deep, and a person's height as great as their inner drive (வெள்ளத்தனைய... உள்ளத்தனையது). Both use a water image and the same 'as much as' construction to state a strict proportion, one for learning and knowledge, the other for drive and stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:596","b":"k:619","type":"parallel_domain","note":"619 says that even if a thing cannot come about because of fate (தெய்வத்தான் ஆகாதெனினும்), effort pays the wage of the body's strain; 596 says a high aim keeps its worth even when it is thrust aside, which Parimelazhagar takes as being denied by fate. Both hold that what fate withholds in outcome does not cancel the value of the striving or the aim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:597","b":"k:774","type":"parallel_domain","note":"774 shows a warrior who has thrown his spear at an elephant pulling out the spear lodged in his own body and laughing; 597 shows the elephant itself holding firm when struck by arrows, as the image of those who do not give way in ruin. In both, a wound taken is the occasion that shows firmness, and the Book of Wealth draws the general rule in 597 that the war chapter acts out in 774.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:598","b":"k:614","type":"develops","note":"598 says the person without drive does not attain the pride of generosity; 614 says the beneficence of one without effort comes to nothing, like a sword in the hand of a பேடி. 614 adds what happens when such a person tries anyway: the means are present but the capacity to use them is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:599","b":"k:763","type":"parallel_domain","note":"599 says an elephant, though huge and sharp-tusked, fears when a tiger attacks; 763 says a sea of roaring rats perishes when the cobra merely breathes. Both animal pairs teach that bulk or numbers do not decide a contest against an opponent with spirit, 599 of a person's drive and 763 of an army.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:600","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"997 says those without human courtesy are like trees even if sharp as a file (மரம்போல்வர்); 600 says those without abundance of drive are trees, differing only in having human form (மரம் மக்களாதலே வேறு). The tree marks the person who lacks what makes one human, but the two verses name different lacks: fellow-feeling in 997, inner drive in 600.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:601","b":"k:753","type":"same_image","note":"601 calls the family a lamp that does not dwindle (குடியென்னும் குன்றா விளக்கம்) which dies when the grime of sloth spreads over it (மடியென்னும் மாசூர). 753 calls wealth a lamp that does not fail (பொருளென்னும் பொய்யா விளக்கம்) which goes where its owner intends and cuts the darkness. The same 'X என்னும் ... விளக்கம்' figure is used twice in the Book of Wealth, and Parimelazhagar glosses both as a நந்தா விளக்கு; one lamp is shown dispelling darkness abroad, the other being put out from within.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1022","type":"develops","note":"602 states the negative condition for a family's standing: remove sloth. 1022 states the positive one: the family lengthens (நீளும் குடி) through long work in two things, effort and mature knowledge (ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும்). Read together, absence of sloth is necessary but 1022 adds knowledge beside effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:602","b":"k:1028","type":"develops","note":"602 tells those who want their family to be a family (குடியைக் குடியாக வேண்டுபவர்) to live treating sloth as sloth, or killing it (மடியை மடியா ஒழுகல்). 1028 says those who build the family have no season to wait for, and that if they idle and think of their dignity it is ruined (மடிசெய்து மானங் கருதக் கெடும்). 1028 gives 602's rule its practical form: for the family-builder sloth shows up as waiting for the right time and standing on dignity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:371","type":"qualifies","note":"605 treats sloth and its companions as something the ruin-bound choose and desire (காமக் கலன்). 371 says sloth itself arises from a fate of loss (போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 371 puts a cause behind what 605 describes as a preference, which matches 605's own word கெடுநீரார், people whose nature is already to perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:920","type":"parallel_domain","note":"605 lists delay, forgetting, sloth and sleep as the craft that those of a ruin-bound nature desire (கெடுநீரார் காமக் கலன்). 920 lists double-minded women, liquor and dice as the company of those Fortune has cast off (திருநீக்கப் பட்டார் தொடர்பு). Both verses give a short catalogue and tie it to people already marked for ruin, leaving open whether the listed things cause the ruin or are its sign.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:605","b":"k:1068","type":"same_image","note":"605 makes four habits a boat (கலன்) that the ruin-bound love to board; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read it as a vessel that drops its passengers mid-sea, though Parimelazhagar notes others read கலன் as an ornament. 1068 makes begging an unsafe boat (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits on the rock of refusal. In both a way of living is a vessel trusted by the person in it and bound to fail him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:606","b":"k:591","type":"agrees","note":"606: even when the holdings of those who own the earth come to them (படியுடையார் பற்றமைந்தக் கண்ணும்), the slothful rarely reach an excellent fruit. 591: what makes one 'a possessor' is energy; do those without it possess even what they possess (உடையது உடையரோ)? Both deny that having things amounts to benefiting from them when the inner quality is missing. Parimelazhagar notes another reading of 606 in which the earth-owners' support, not their wealth, is what comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:606","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"606 says the greatest possessions yield the slothful no excellent fruit (மாண்பயன் எய்தல் அரிது). 750 says a fort, whatever excellences it has, is as nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது அரண்). The two share the 'ஆகியக் கண்ணும் / அமைந்தக் கண்ணும்' concession: resources do not work without the action of those who hold them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:607","b":"k:1040","type":"agrees","note":"607: the slothful end up hearing words of scorn (எள்ளுஞ் சொல்). 1040: seeing those who sit idle saying 'we have nothing', the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்). Both give idleness the same penalty, being laughed at, once from people and once from the personified earth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:608","b":"k:616","type":"same_image","note":"608: if sloth lodges in one of family standing it puts him into slavery to his enemies (அடிமை புகுத்தி விடும்). 616: absence of effort puts one into want (இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்). The identical closing phrase makes sloth an agent that ushers a person into a condition; the neighbouring chapters thus name two destinations, poverty and subjection, and Parimelazhagar links them in his note on 616, where want leads to destruction by enemies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:604","type":"qualifies","note":"604 says that for the slothful the family dies and faults multiply (குற்றம் பெருகும்). 609 says the fault is destroyed (குற்றம் ... கெடும்) once a man changes his sloth. 609 makes 604's decline reversible; Parimelazhagar goes further and takes 609's faults to include ones that arose from other causes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:609","b":"k:1026","type":"develops","note":"609: when a man ends the rule of sloth over him (மடியாண்மை மாற்ற), the fault that came into his குடியாண்மை is gone; Parimelazhagar splits the word into family and manhood, Manakkudavar reads it as the management of the family. 1026 says good manhood is making the management of the house one was born in one's own (நல்லாண்மை ... இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்). 1026 supports reading the two terms of 609 together: manhood is measured in the household, and sloth is what unseats it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:610","b":"k:606","type":"qualifies","note":"610 gives the unslothful king the whole measured world. 606, in the same chapter, says that even when the wealth of earth-owners arrives the slothful get no excellent fruit from it. Together they separate two things: sloth does not only block gaining the world, it also empties the world of value when it is handed over.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:611","b":"k:26","type":"develops","note":"611: do not flag saying 'it is hard' (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை), for effort will give the greatness (பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்). 26: those who do what is hard to do are great, those who cannot are small (செயற்கரிய செய்வார் பெரியர்). 26 defines greatness by hard deeds as if it were a fixed difference between people; 611 says the greatness needed is itself produced by trying.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:612","b":"k:670","type":"agrees","note":"612: guard against the work failing in the doing, because the world has left those who left the work's need undone (வினைக்குறை தீர்ந்தாரின் தீர்ந்தன்று உலகு), on Parimelazhagar's reading; Manakkudavar takes the second line the other way, that the world does not leave those who finish. 670: whatever other firmness they have, the world does not want those who do not want firmness in action (வேண்டாரை வேண்டாது உலகு). Both make the world's regard answer in kind to how a person treats the task.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:612","b":"k:674","type":"develops","note":"612 warns against abandoning a task midway (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்). 674 says the remainder of a task, like the remainder of an enmity, burns like the remainder of a fire (தீயெச்சம் போலத் தெறும்). 674 supplies a reason 612 does not give: the unfinished part is not inert but goes on doing damage.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:613","b":"k:1035","type":"parallel_domain","note":"613 says the standing of a benefactor rests on labour. 1035 says those whose way is to eat what their hands produce do not beg and give to those who beg without hiding anything (கைசெய்தூண் மாலையவர்). The farming chapter shows 613's general claim in one occupation: the person who works with his hands is the one in a position to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:614","b":"k:219","type":"qualifies","note":"614 says the generosity of a man without effort comes to nothing. 219 says that for a man of good feeling, being poor just is not being able to do the helpful things he would do (செயும்நீர செய்யாது அமைகலா வாறு). 219 shows the same blocked intention from the inside and without blame, so 614's scorn falls only on the case where the lack of means comes from not working.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:615","b":"k:901","type":"agrees","note":"615 praises the one who wants the work (வினைவிழைவான்) and not pleasure. 901 says those who desire the wife do not reach the excellent fruit, and that this is the very thing those who desire action do not want (வினைவிழையார் வேண்டாப் பொருளும் அது). Both set the desire for action against a desire for enjoyment and use nearly the same word for the man of action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:615","b":"k:1030","type":"develops","note":"615 makes the work-loving man a pillar that props his kin (துடைத்தூன்றும் தூண்). 1030 says a family with no good person to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. 1030 states the cost of the absence of the figure 615 praises, with the same verb ஊன்று.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:616","b":"k:371","type":"qualifies","note":"616: effort makes fortune (முயற்சி திருவினை ஆக்கும்) and lack of effort brings one into want. 371: unflagging effort arises from a fate of gain, and sloth from a fate of loss (ஆகூழால் தோன்றும் அசைவின்மை ... போகூழால் தோன்றும் மடி). 616 makes effort the cause of wealth; 371 makes fate the cause of effort, so on 371's account the causal chain in 616 starts one step earlier and is not in the person's hands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:616","b":"k:594","type":"agrees","note":"616 says effort produces fortune. 594 says increase asks the way and goes to the one of unwavering drive (அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும் அசைவிலா ஊக்கமுடையானுழை). Both place the source of prosperity in the person's exertion, and 594 pictures it as prosperity doing the seeking. The first word of 594 is truncated in this edition (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:618","b":"k:988","type":"parallel_domain","note":"618 separates what is no disgrace (having no luck) from what is (not working). 988 says want is no disgrace to a person who has the firmness called worth (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று). Both refuse to count an outward lack as shame and move the test to something in the person, effort in one and character in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:620","b":"k:1024","type":"same_image","note":"620 ends 'தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்', those who labour without delay, and says they see fate's back. 1024 ends with the same phrase, 'தம்குடியைத் தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்க்கு', and says that for them the outcome arrives by itself without deliberation (சூழாமல் தானே முடிவெய்தும்). The same people are described twice, once as defeating an opposing power and once as finding the result come unsought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:621","b":"k:774","type":"parallel_domain","note":"621 says to laugh when trouble comes (இடுக்கண் வருங்கால் நகுக), since nothing presses it back as well. 774 shows a warrior who, having thrown his spear at an elephant, pulls out the one lodged in his own body and laughs (மெய்வேல் பறியா நகும்). The battlefield verse acts out the advice of 621: the laugh comes at the moment of injury, and the injury is turned to use.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:309","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the same play of உள்ளம் (mind) and உள்ளுதல் (to think): in 622 a flood of suffering perishes when the wise person thinks with the mind (உள்ளத்தின் உள்ளக் கெடும்), and in 309 all one has thought of is gained if one does not think anger in the mind (உள்ளத்தால் உள்ளான் வெகுளி எனின்). Read together they make a single act of thought decide the outcome, once by what is thought and once by what is left unthought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:622","b":"k:429","type":"develops","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that arrives to shake them; 622 says that for the person of understanding (அறிவுடையான்) even a flood of suffering already present is ended by thinking. The pair gives understanding two jobs, forestalling trouble and dissolving the trouble that came anyway, which Parimelazhagar takes in 622 to be trouble from fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:623","b":"k:261","type":"parallel_domain","note":"623 says those who do not suffer at suffering (இடும்பைக்கு இடும்பை படாஅதவர்) make suffering itself suffer; Parimelazhagar places this in the middle of an undertaking. 261 makes bearing the pain that comes (உற்றநோய் நோன்றல்) half of the form of தவம். The same endurance is a means to finishing work in one book and the definition of austerity in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:624","b":"k:597","type":"agrees","note":"624 likens the person who pulls through every obstruction to a draught ox (மடுத்தவாயெல்லாம் பகடன்னான்) and says the trouble he meets is itself troubled. 597 says the strong do not give way where things break down (சிதைவிடத்து ஒல்கார்), like an elephant standing firm under arrows. Both make the same claim through a working or fighting animal; the ox adds forward motion to the elephant's holding of ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:624","b":"k:620","type":"develops","note":"624 says trouble is troubled by one who hauls on like an ox through every blocked place. 620 carries the claim to its limit: those who labour on without flagging (தாழாது உஞற்றுபவர்) see the back even of fate (ஊழையும் உப்பக்கம் காண்பர்). What 624 says of obstacles in the road, 620 says of the power behind them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:625","b":"k:989","type":"agrees","note":"625 says that though troubles come stacked (அடுக்கி வரினும்), those of one who is not undone (அழிவிலான்) are themselves in trouble. 989 says those called the shore of worth do not shift even if the age shifts (ஊழி பெயரினும் தாம் பெயரார்). Both define a person by not moving under pressure, one in the chapter on a king's undertakings and one in the chapter on character.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:625","b":"k:1045","type":"qualifies","note":"1045 states as plain fact that within the one suffering called poverty many sufferings arrive and settle (பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்), and offers no remedy. 625 addresses exactly such piling up (அடுக்கி வரினும்) and says it fails against one who is not undone. Set side by side, 625's confidence is conditional on the person, while 1045 describes what the stacking is like for those in want.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:4","type":"parallel_domain","note":"629 makes freedom from pain depend on not desiring pleasure when it is present (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்). 4 describes the Lord as one without wanting or not-wanting (வேண்டுதல் வேண்டாமை இலான்) and says those who reach his feet never have இடும்பை. The same link between having no pull toward or away from things and having no suffering appears as a divine attribute in Book 1 and as a human discipline here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:629","b":"k:115","type":"agrees","note":"629 says one who does not crave pleasure in the midst of pleasure does not suffer in the midst of pain. 115 says loss and increase are both part of life and that not letting the heart bend (நெஞ்சத்துக் கோடாமை) is the ornament of the worthy. Both ask for the same evenness across good and bad turns; 115 treats it as fairness of mind, 629 as protection against pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:630","b":"k:669","type":"develops","note":"669 says to do with resolve the deed that yields joy even though pain comes thick at first (துன்பம் உறவரினும் ... இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை): pain now, joy later. 630 goes a step further and has the person take the pain itself as joy (இன்னாமை இன்பம் எனக்கொளின்). Parimelazhagar reads the pain in both as the strain of effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:630","b":"k:810","type":"same_image","note":"630 measures the reward by the enemy: a distinction one's foes desire or esteem (ஒன்னார் விழையும் சிறப்பு). 810 uses the same measure with the same verb: those who do not depart from their nature toward old friends are wanted even by those who do not want them (விழையார் விழையப்படுப). Each verse marks an unusual constancy, under pain or toward friends who have erred, by saying that even the hostile regard it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:641","b":"k:411","type":"parallel_domain","note":"641 sets the good of the tongue above every other good. 411 calls the wealth of the ear the wealth among wealths and the head of them all (செல்வத்துள் எல்லாம் தலை). The book gives the top place once to listening and once to speaking, in chapters addressed to the learner and to the minister.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:641","b":"k:982","type":"same_image","note":"The two verses end with the same formula, 'அந்நலம்/பிறநலம் யா/எந்நலத்து உள்ளதூஉம் அன்று', used in opposite directions. In 641 the goodness of the tongue (நாநலம்) is not contained in any other goodness, which Parimelazhagar takes to mean it exceeds them all; in 982 every goodness other than the goodness of character (குணநலம்) is not counted among goodnesses at all. One chapter ranks eloquence highest for a minister, the other ranks character alone for the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:642","b":"k:128","type":"develops","note":"642 says ruin as well as gain comes from speech and asks for vigilance against slips. 128 gives the scale of the risk: if even one harmful word has its effect (ஒன்றானும் தீச்சொல்), the good turns to no good (நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்). 128 supplies the reason why 642 treats a lapse (சோர்வு), and not only deliberate bad speech, as the danger.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:643","b":"k:187","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 643 as speech that binds those who have accepted one as a friend (கேட்டார்ப் பிணிக்கும்) and makes even those who have not want friendship. 187 gives the failure of this skill: those who do not know how to make friends with pleasing talk (நகச்சொல்லி நட்பாடல் தேற்றாதவர்) part even their own kin with divisive talk. On that reading the two verses are the positive and negative of one ability, speech as what holds or breaks alliances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:469","type":"parallel_domain","note":"644 says to speak after knowing the திறன் of those concerned. 469 says that even in doing good there is error when it is done without knowing each person's character (அவரவர் பண்பறிந்து ஆற்றாக்கடை). Both make a good thing, a word or a deed, depend on fitting it to the particular person receiving it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:696","type":"develops","note":"644 asks for speech fitted to its hearers and says no virtue or gain exceeds it. 696 applies this to a single hearer, the king: know his mood, consider the time, and say what is needed in a way he will want (குறிப்பறிந்து காலம் கருதி ... வேட்பச் சொலல்). The general rule for a minister's speech becomes a specific rule for speech to power.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:644","b":"k:711","type":"develops","note":"644 says to speak knowing the திறன், which Parimelazhagar takes as the relative standing of speaker and hearers. 711 gives the method: those who know the stock of words should speak after knowing the assembly and examining (அவையறிந்து ஆராய்ந்து சொல்லுக). Chapter 72 works out as a practice what 644 states as a principle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:645","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"645 says to speak a word only after knowing that no other word can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). 467 says to think and then commit to a task, and that saying 'we will think after committing' is a fault (எண்ணித் துணிக). Both put the testing before the irreversible step; 645 applies to an utterance the rule 467 gives for action.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:646","b":"k:724","type":"develops","note":"646 pairs speaking well with drawing the benefit from others' words. 724 sets that pair in the assembly of the learned: make what you have learned go home to them (கற்ற செலச்சொல்லி) and take what is more from those who know more (மிக்காருள் மிக்க கொளல்). 724 adds what 646 leaves open, that listening is how the speaker's own knowledge grows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:647","b":"k:686","type":"agrees","note":"647 lists three things that make a person hard to beat in a contest: able to speak, free of lapses, unafraid (சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான்). 686 defines the envoy with nearly the same set: learned, not afraid of hostile eyes (கண் அஞ்சான்), speaking so it goes home, and knowing what fits the time. The envoy chapter puts the general portrait of 647 into the one post where it is tested before an enemy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:647","b":"k:872","type":"develops","note":"647 says no one can easily defeat a fearless, unfaltering speaker in a contest (இகல் வெல்லல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). 872 gives the advice that follows for the other side: take on the enmity of those whose plough is the bow if you must, but not of those whose plough is the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர் பகை). Parimelazhagar takes the latter to be ministers, the people 647 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:648","b":"k:1046","type":"qualifies","note":"648 says the world listens readily to skilled, pleasing speakers. 1046 says the words of the poor go slack even when they speak good matter that they have understood well (நற்பொருள் நன்குணர்ந்து சொல்லினும் ... சோர்வு படும்). 1046 limits 648: how the world receives speech depends on the speaker's means as well as his skill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:193","type":"agrees","note":"649 treats wanting to say many things as the sign of someone unable to say a few well. 193 says spread-out talk on useless matters (பயனில பாரித்துரைக்கும் உரை) itself announces that the speaker lacks நயன். In both, length of speech is evidence against the speaker; 193 judges his character, 649 his skill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:650","type":"qualifies","note":"649 faults many words and praises a few faultless ones; the next verse faults those who cannot expound what they have learned so that others understand (உணர விரித்துரையாதார்). Manakkudavar reads the pair together: speak briefly, but also expand where it is needed. 650 keeps 649 from being taken as a rule of brevity alone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:649","b":"k:685","type":"develops","note":"649 says those who cannot say a few faultless words (மாசற்ற சில சொல்லல் தேற்றாதவர்) long to say many. 685 makes brevity the envoy's first mark: he speaks compactly (தொகச் சொல்லி), drops what is harsh, and speaks so as to please. 685 shows the positive skill whose absence 649 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:650","b":"k:1274","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use a flower's scent (நாற்றம்) for what is inside a person. In 650 the flower is fully open and gives no scent, like the learned person who cannot convey what he knows; in 1274 the scent is held inside a closed bud, like the unspoken meaning inside the girl's budding smile. The two uses mirror each other: an open display with nothing reaching others, and a closed one whose content the lover already perceives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:651","b":"k:455","type":"qualifies","note":"651 sets வினைநலம் above துணைநலம்: support gives only ஆக்கம், good action gives all that is wanted. 455 says purity of mind and purity of action (செய்வினை தூய்மை) both come leaning on purity of company (இனந்தூய்மை தூவா வரும்). Read together, the action that 651 ranks above one's support is itself said to depend on the people one keeps.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:653","b":"k:962","type":"develops","note":"653 tells those who say 'ஆஅதும்' (we shall rise) to stop doing what dims their ஒளி. 962 says those who want சீர் together with பேராண்மை will not do 'சீரல்ல' even for the sake of சீர். 962 takes the harder case 653 leaves open: the dimming deed may itself be a route to fame, and it is still refused.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:653","b":"k:971","type":"develops","note":"653 treats ஒளி as something the ambitious ('ஆஅதும் என்னுமவர்') must protect by refraining from certain deeds. 971 defines ஒளி as 'உள்ள வெறுக்கை', abundance of spirit, and இளி as saying one will live without it. Together they give ஒளி two sides: it is lost by what one does (653) and it consists in what one aspires to (971).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:654","b":"k:174","type":"parallel_domain","note":"654 says those of unshaken sight do nothing degrading even in distress (இடுக்கண் படினும்). 174 says those of 'புன்மையில் காட்சி' who have conquered the senses will not covet on the plea 'இலம்' (we have nothing). Both verses name a clear-sighted person (காட்சியவர்) and deny that need is an excuse; 174 applies it to coveting, 654 to action generally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:654","b":"k:218","type":"parallel_domain","note":"654: clear-sighted people do not do what is degrading even when trouble comes. 218: those of 'கடனறி காட்சி' do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும் ... ஒல்கார்). One verse says hardship does not make them do wrong, the other that it does not make them stop doing good; the two are the negative and positive halves of the same steadiness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:655","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"655 asks that one not do what will be regretted afterwards. 467 says to think and then resolve (எண்ணித் துணிக), and calls it a lapse to say 'we will think after resolving'. Both put the reflection before the act: 467 as a rule of planning, 655 as a rule about the purity of the deed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:961","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids the blameworthy deed even when the need is a mother's hunger. 961 says to give up things that would diminish one (குன்ற வருப விடல்) even if they are indispensable (இன்றி அமையாச் சிறப்பின ஆயினும்). Both deny that necessity licenses a degrading act; 961 states the principle abstractly under honour, 656 gives it a concrete and painful instance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:656","b":"k:1047","type":"same_image","note":"656 uses the mother who bore one (ஈன்றாள்) as the strongest pull toward wrongdoing for the sake of relief. 1047 says that one in poverty not joined with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும்). The mother marks the limit case in both: 656 holds conduct firm against her need, while 1047 says poverty without virtue loses even her regard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:408","type":"parallel_domain","note":"657: the extreme poverty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவு) is better than wealth got by taking on blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கம்). 408: wealth lodged with the unlearned (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு) is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (நல்லார்கண் பட்ட வறுமை). The same comparison of good people's poverty with bad people's wealth is made from each side, once about how wealth was got and once about who holds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:657","b":"k:988","type":"agrees","note":"657 ranks the utter poverty of the worthy above blame-laden wealth. 988 says poverty (இன்மை) is no disgrace (இளிவன்று) to one who has the firmness called சால்பு. 988 supplies the ground for 657's ranking: for the சான்றோர் poverty carries no இளிவு, whereas the wealth in 657 carries பழி.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"658: forbidden deeds done without shunning them bring anguish even if they succeed (முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும்). 113: give up at once gain that comes from leaving fairness, even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்). Both concede success in advance and still reject the act, so the objection is not that such means fail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:177","type":"agrees","note":"658 warns that the gain of a forbidden deed turns to பீழை later. 177 says not to want gain that comes by coveting (வெஃகியாம் ஆக்கம்), because at the time of enjoying it the fruit is hard to make good (விளைவயின் மாண்டற்கு அரிதாம் பயன்). Both place the harm at the stage of the result and not at the stage of getting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:658","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"658 says forbidden actions bring anguish even when they are accomplished. 931 says not to want gambling even if one wins (வென்றிடினும்), since the winnings are like the hook a fish has swallowed. The gambling verse gives an image for 658's claim: the success itself is what carries the harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:659","b":"k:112","type":"agrees","note":"The second half of 659 says what comes from good ways (நற்பாலவை) yields later even if it is lost (இழப்பினும் பிற்பயக்கும்). 112 says the wealth of a person of rectitude (செப்பம் உடையவன் ஆக்கம்) does not decay and is a security for those who come after (எச்சத்திற்கு ஏமாப்பு). Both give rightly got wealth a benefit that outlasts the moment, 659 for the person later and 112 for descendants.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:660","b":"k:1000","type":"same_image","note":"660: wealth made by deceit is water kept in an unfired clay vessel. 1000: great wealth obtained by one without character is good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). Both use a faulty கலம் for wealth that cannot be kept, but the fault differs: in 660 it is how the wealth was made, in 1000 it is the holder's character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:34","type":"parallel_domain","note":"661: firmness in action is a person's firmness of mind (மனத்திட்பம்); everything else is something other (மற்றைய எல்லாம் பிற). 34: being spotless in mind (மனத்துக்கண் மாசிலன் ஆதல்) is all of virtue; the rest is noisy show (ஆகுல நீர பிற). The two verses have the same shape, reducing a whole domain to a state of mind and dismissing the remainder as 'பிற', once for virtue and once for effective action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:661","b":"k:600","type":"agrees","note":"661 locates firmness in action in the mind and sets other things aside. 600 says a person's real strength (உரம்) is abundance of spirit (உள்ள வெறுக்கை), and those without it are trees. Both place strength within the person; Parimelazhagar's note on 661 lists the outer things meant by 'மற்றைய' as the firmness of army, fort and allies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:662","b":"k:429","type":"qualifies","note":"429 says those whose understanding guards against what is coming (எதிரதாக் காக்கும் அறிவினார்) have no affliction that comes to shake them. 662 keeps that first rule (ஊறொரால்) but adds a second for when harm arrives anyway (உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை). 662 thus allows that foresight is not always enough; Parimelazhagar reads the second case as a task spoiled by fate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:662","b":"k:442","type":"parallel_domain","note":"662 gives two rules as the way of those who have studied the matter: avoid harm (ஊறொரால்) and do not give way once it has come (உற்றபின் ஒல்காமை). 442 tells a king to cherish those who remove the trouble that has come (உற்றநோய் நீக்கி) and guard beforehand against what has not (உறாஅமை முற்காக்கும்). The same before-and-after pair appears as the doer's own discipline in 662 and as the quality to look for in advisers in 442.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:664","b":"k:819","type":"develops","note":"664: saying is easy for anyone; doing as one has said (சொல்லிய வண்ணம் செயல்) is hard. 819: the bond with those whose deed is one thing and word another (வினைவேறு சொல்வேறு பட்டார்) is bitter even in a dream. 664 states the gap between word and deed as a difficulty of execution; 819 judges the same gap in a friend as a fault of character.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:666","b":"k:309","type":"parallel_domain","note":"666: what one has thought of is obtained as thought, if one is firm. 309: all one has thought of is obtained at once (உள்ளிய தெல்லாம் உடனெய்தும்), if one does not think of anger in the heart. The same promise is attached to two different inner conditions, one positive (firmness) and one negative (absence of anger).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:667","b":"k:1032","type":"same_image","note":"667: do not scorn anyone on seeing their form (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை), for there are people like the linchpin (அச்சாணி) of a great rolling chariot. 1032: those who plough are the ஆணி of the world's people, because they bear all who cannot plough and take up other work. Both use the pin that holds the wheel for someone small in appearance on whom everything turns; 667 leaves the person unnamed and 1032 names the farmer. Parimelazhagar supplies the smallness in 667 from the simile.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:668","b":"k:467","type":"develops","note":"467 says to think and then resolve on a task, and that saying 'we will think after resolving' is a lapse. 668 takes up from that point: once the task has been seen without confusion (கலங்காது கண்ட), act without wavering (துளங்காது). Together they give a sequence in which doubt belongs before the decision and not after it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:668","b":"k:671","type":"agrees","note":"668: in a task one has seen clearly and decided on (கலங்காது கண்ட வினை), act without wavering and without delay (தூக்கங் கடிந்து). 671, opening the next chapter, says deliberation ends in reaching resolve (துணிவு) and it is bad for that resolve to rest in delay (தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). The same rule closes the chapter on firmness and opens the chapter on method: once the thinking is done, delay is the fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"669 says to carry through the worthwhile deed despite the pain it brings at first. 611 says one must not slacken on the thought that a thing is hard (அருமை உடைத்தென்று அசாவாமை), since effort gives the greatness needed. Both refuse the difficulty of a task as a reason to hold back; 611 speaks of effort and 669 of resolve (துணிவு).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:669","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"669: do with resolve the deed that yields joy (இன்பம் பயக்கும் வினை), even though pain comes close at first (துன்பம் உறவரினும்). 615: one who does not want pleasure but wants the work (இன்பம் விழையான் வினை விழைவான்) is a pillar who wipes away his kin's துன்பம். Both set இன்பம், துன்பம் and வினை against each other; 669 justifies bearing pain by the joy the deed yields, while 615 describes a doer who is not after pleasure at all and whose work removes others' pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:670","b":"k:750","type":"parallel_domain","note":"670: whatever other firmness they have attained (எனைத்திட்பம் எய்தியக் கண்ணும்), the world does not want those who do not want firmness in action. 750: whatever excellences it has (எனைமாட்சித்து ஆகியக் கண்ணும்), a fort is nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண்). The two chapter-closing verses are built alike and make the same point, that other strengths count for nothing without the capacity to act, once of a minister and once of a fort's defenders.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:490","type":"agrees","note":"490 says to be like the heron in the season for folding up (கூம்பும் பருவத்து) and like its strike when the moment is right (சீர்த்த இடத்து). 672 gives the same double instruction without the image: take time over what is to be done slowly, and do not delay what must not be delayed. Both hold that waiting and speed are each correct in their place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:672","b":"k:671","type":"qualifies","note":"671 calls it a fault when a resolved deed lingers in delay (தாழ்ச்சியுள் தங்குதல் தீது). 672 immediately limits this: there are deeds to be done slowly, and over those one should take time (தூங்குக தூங்கிச் செயற்பால); only the deed that must be done without delay must not be slept on. Delay is therefore wrong by the nature of the task, not in itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:673","b":"k:485","type":"develops","note":"485 says those who have the world in mind wait, unshaken, with their mind on the time (காலம் கருதி இருப்பர்). Manakkudavar reads the second half of 673 in that sense: when the deed is not possible, keep it in mind and act on seeing the place where it becomes possible. Parimelazhagar reads 673 differently, as open force where one is stronger and one of the other three expedients where one is not, so the link holds on Manakkudavar's reading.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:677","b":"k:462","type":"develops","note":"462 says nothing is hard to attain for those who act after examining a matter with chosen company (தெரிந்த இனத்தொடு) and thinking it over themselves. 677 narrows whose counsel counts for a particular task: the one who knows that deed from inside (அவ்வினை உள்ளறிவான்), whom Parimelazhagar takes to be someone who has done it before. General consultation in 462 becomes, in 677, learning the mind of the person with experience of the task.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:678","b":"k:875","type":"develops","note":"678 says to get one deed done by means of another, as one binds an elephant with a rutting elephant (யானையால் யானையாத் தற்று). Manakkudavar takes this as setting an enemy's own enemy against him. On that reading 875 is the case worked out: one who is alone with two enemies should take one of them as an ally (இன்துணையாக் கொள்க அவற்றின் ஒன்று). Parimelazhagar reads 678 more generally, as making a task in hand the means to another like it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:679","b":"k:875","type":"agrees","note":"875 says that one without allies and facing two enemies should take one of them as a pleasing ally. 679 says that winning over ஒட்டார் is more urgent than favours to friends; Manakkudavar takes ஒட்டார் as one's enemies, Parimelazhagar as those not allied with one's enemy. On either reading both verses treat alliance as something to be made out of the hostile or uncommitted when one is pressed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:488","type":"develops","note":"488 says to bear with enemies on meeting them (செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க), for when their end comes their head will go down; Parimelazhagar glosses சுமக்க as bowing. 680 has the small bow to the great to obtain terms. 488 adds what 680 does not say: that such bowing can be a way of waiting for the stronger side's fall.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"861 says to avoid standing against the strong (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக). 680 describes what that means for a weak party: those of small domain, fearing the trembling of their own side (உள்நடுங்கல் அஞ்சி), bow to the greater and accept terms if they can get them (குறைபெறின் கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து). 861 gives the rule and 680 the settlement that follows from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:680","b":"k:967","type":"tension","note":"967, on honour, says it is better to be called ruined as one stands than to live by going behind those who are hostile to one (ஒட்டார்பின் சென்று வாழ்தலின்). 680 says the small will bow to the great and take what terms they can. The conflict depends on reading 967's ஒட்டார் as covering a stronger enemy; Parimelazhagar limits it to following scorners for a living, but he also remarks on 680 that bowing is not the wish of those with honour, which is why the verse says 'they will accept' as a report of worldly practice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:502","type":"agrees","note":"502 places trust in one born in a good family (குடிப்பிறந்து), free of faults and with a shame that recoils from blemish. 681 makes birth in a fit family (ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல்) one of the envoy's three marks. Family is used in both as evidence about a person before he is given a charge, for trust in general in 502 and for the embassy in 681.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:692","type":"develops","note":"681 asks of the envoy 'qualities that kings desire' (வேந்தவாம் பண்புடைமை) without saying what they are. Manakkudavar glosses the phrase as not desiring what the king desires, which is the content of 692 (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை), and Parimelazhagar says this quality is obtained from the following chapter on conduct around kings. 692 therefore supplies, on the commentators' reading, what 681 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:681","b":"k:992","type":"same_image","note":"The first line of 681 and of 992 is the same: அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல். 992 says these two are what is called courtesy (பண்புடைமை என்னும் வழக்கு); 681 lists them and adds a third, the qualities kings desire (வேந்தவாம் பண்புடைமை), as the nature of one who carries a message. The envoy's qualification is thus the book's general definition of courtesy with a courtly requirement added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:683","b":"k:722","type":"agrees","note":"722 says those who can make what they have learned go home before the learned are called learned among the learned (கற்றாருள் கற்றார்). 683 uses the same construction for the envoy: he must be strong in the treatises among those who know treatises (நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன்). Both measure learning by standing among the learned, not against the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:407","type":"qualifies","note":"684 counts presence (உரு) among the three things an envoy needs. 407 says the fine looks of one without fine, penetrating understanding are those of a well-made clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). 407 supplies the limit on 684's உரு: appearance counts only in the union (செறிவு) with understanding and learning that 684 itself demands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:684","b":"k:636","type":"agrees","note":"636 asks what subtle scheme can stand before those who have keen intelligence together with the treatises (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்க்கு). 684 requires of the envoy understanding and well-examined learning (அறிவு ... ஆராய்ந்த கல்வி) held together. Parimelazhagar glosses the first term in each verse as natural intelligence and the second as acquired learning, so both verses ask for the two in combination, for the minister and for the envoy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:685","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"696 tells one who serves a king to say what is needed and not disliked, in a way that is wanted (வெறுப்பில வேண்டுப வேட்பச் சொலல்). 685 has the envoy remove what offends and speak so as to please (தூவாத நீக்கி நகச்சொல்லி) while securing good for his own side. The same manner of speech is prescribed toward one's own king in 696 and toward a foreign king in 685; in both the pleasing manner serves what has to be said.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:686","b":"k:724","type":"agrees","note":"724 says to make what one has learned go home before the learned (கற்றார்முன் கற்ற செலச்சொல்லி). 686 uses the same two terms for the envoy: having learned (கற்று), he speaks so that it goes home (செலச்சொல்லி). In both, learning is complete only when it can be delivered to a demanding audience, the assembly in 724 and a hostile court in 686.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:675","type":"parallel_domain","note":"675 says to act after thinking through resources, means, time, deed and place (காலம் ... இடனொடு ... எண்ணிச் செயல்). 687 uses the same terms for speech: consider the time, know the place, think it through and then speak (காலங் கருதி இடனறிந்து எண்ணி உரைப்பான்). The envoy's speech is planned by the same checklist the book gives for any deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"696 tells the courtier to read the king's mood and consider the time (குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி) before speaking. 687 has the best envoy know what is due, consider the time (காலங் கருதி) and know the place before he speaks. The phrase காலங் கருதி is common to both, so the discipline for speaking to one's own king is the same as for speaking to another's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:687","b":"k:712","type":"agrees","note":"712 says those who know how words work should discern the opening (இடைதெரிந்து), understand well and then speak; Parimelazhagar takes இடை as the receptive moment of the assembly. 687 asks the envoy to consider the time, which Parimelazhagar likewise glosses as the listener's receptive state (செவ்வி). Both make the listener's readiness a condition of speaking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:298","type":"develops","note":"298 says outer purity comes by water and inner purity is seen through truth (அகந்தூய்மை வாய்மையால் காணப்படும்). 688 names தூய்மை first among the envoy's marks and then requires வாய்மை along with all three. 298 gives the reason the two words belong together: truthfulness is how inner purity shows.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:383","type":"parallel_domain","note":"383 names three things that never leave a ruler: promptness, learning and resolve (தூங்காமை கல்வி துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றும்). 688 has the same frame for the envoy who repeats the king's message: purity, support and resolve, these three (தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை இம்மூன்றின்). துணிவுடைமை is the term common to king and envoy; the other two differ with the office.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:688","b":"k:501","type":"develops","note":"501 says a man is to be trusted after being tested by virtue, wealth, pleasure and fear for life. 688 lists the faithful envoy's marks as purity, support, resolve and truthfulness (தூய்மை துணைமை துணிவுடைமை ... வாய்மை). Parimelazhagar explains தூய்மை as being clean in respect of wealth and pleasure and துணிவு as not holding back for fear of harm, which makes 688's qualities the passes of 501's tests.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:689","b":"k:139","type":"parallel_domain","note":"139 says that for people of good conduct it is not possible to speak evil with the mouth even by a slip (வழுக்கியும் வாயாற் சொலல்). 689 asks the same of the envoy in his own field: a firmness whose mouth does not let slip a word that brings blemish (வடுமாற்றம் வாய்சோரா). What Book 1 treats as a result of character, 689 makes a condition for carrying the king's word. (This edition prints வாய்சேரா; the commentaries read வாய்சோரா.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:689","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"642 says to guard against a lapse in speech (சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு) because both gain and ruin come from it. 689 is that rule at the point where the stakes are the king's: the envoy must not let a word that discredits slip from his mouth (வாய்சோரா); Parimelazhagar adds that the slip in question would come from fear for himself. The verb சோர் is shared, and 689 names the hardness (வன்கண்) that prevents the lapse 642 warns of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:638","type":"agrees","note":"638 says that even if the king destroys good counsel and knows nothing himself, the one at his side owes it to him to say what is good for him (உறுதி ... கூறல் கடன்). 690 says the envoy secures the lord's good (உறுதி) even at the cost of his own life. Both use உறுதி for the king's real interest and hold the servant to it under threat, from his own king in 638 and from a foreign one in 690.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:723","type":"qualifies","note":"723 says those who will die among enemies are easy to find and those who do not fear in an assembly are rare (பகையகத்துச் சாவார் எளியர் அரியர் அவையகத்து அஞ்சாதவர்). 690 asks the envoy to face death (இறுதி) while speaking in a hostile court. 723 separates the two kinds of courage and rates the second as rarer; the envoy of 690 needs both together, which indicates how much the verse asks.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:690","b":"k:778","type":"parallel_domain","note":"778 says warriors who do not fear for their life when battle comes (உறின் உயிர் அஞ்சா மறவர்) do not lose their distinction even if their lord is angry. 690 says the envoy is one who, even if it brings his end (இறுதி பயப்பினும்), does not fall short and secures what is good for his lord (இறைவற்கு உறுதி). The envoy's office is given the soldier's standard, with both verses naming the lord (இறைவன்) as the one served.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:896","type":"develops","note":"691 compares the king to a fire one warms at from a measured distance. 896 says one burned by fire may still survive, but those who go on offending the great will not (எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம் உய்யார் பெரியார்ப் பிழைத்தொழுகுவார்). Read together, the fire in 691 understates the danger: 896 ranks the offended great above fire, though Parimelazhagar takes its பெரியார் as those great in austerity, not kings.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:691","b":"k:1104","type":"same_image","note":"691 tells those who serve quarrelsome kings (இகல்வேந்தர்) to be like people warming at a fire, neither moving off nor closing in (அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க). In 1104 a lover asks where she got a fire that burns when he withdraws and is cool when he comes near (நீங்கின் தெறூஉம் குறுகுங்கால் தண்ணென்னும் தீ). The king's fire burns at close range and the lover's reverses that rule, so the court calls for a kept middle distance and the love speech for none.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:692","b":"k:179","type":"parallel_domain","note":"692 says that not desiring what kings desire (மன்னர் விழைப விழையாமை) brings lasting prosperity through those same kings; Parimelazhagar takes விழைப as the enjoyments, adornment and precedence proper to a king. 179 says Fortune herself comes to the wise who, knowing virtue, do not covet (அறனறிந்து வெஃகா அறிவுடையார்ச் சேரும் ... திரு). Both make not-wanting the route by which wealth arrives, once as court prudence and once as virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:693","b":"k:891","type":"agrees","note":"693 says those who would guard themselves should guard against the gravest things (போற்றின் அரியவை போற்றல்), which Parimelazhagar reads as faults a king cannot forgive. 891 says not slighting the power of those able to act is the chief of all the guarding done by those who guard themselves (போற்றுவார் போற்றலுள் எல்லாம் தலை). Both use போற்றல் for self-protection and locate its first object in one's dealings with the powerful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:694","b":"k:715","type":"parallel_domain","note":"694 says that in the presence of the great one should suppress whispering and shared smiles (செவிச்சொல்லும் சேர்ந்த நகையும் அவித்தொழுகல்). 715 calls the restraint of not speaking first among elders (முதுவருள் முந்து கிளவாச் செறிவு) the best of good things. Court and assembly get the same rule of holding speech back before superiors; 694 extends it to private asides and laughter, which Parimelazhagar says the king would take as aimed at himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:695","b":"k:1076","type":"develops","note":"695 treats the king's secret (மறை) as something to be heard only when he lets it go. 1076 says the base are like a beaten drum because they carry to others the secrets they have heard (தாம் கேட்ட மறை பிறர்க்கு உய்த்துரைக்கலான்). The two give the two ends of handling a secret: restraint in acquiring it in 695, and the failure to hold it in 1076.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:696","b":"k:701","type":"develops","note":"696 opens with குறிப்பறிந்து, knowing the king's mood, as the first condition of speaking to him. 701 praises the one who looks and knows what is meant without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்) as an ornament to the earth. The skill 696 names in passing is taken up as the subject of the next chapter, beginning with 701.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:697","b":"k:200","type":"agrees","note":"697 says to speak what is wanted and never to say what has no use, even if asked (வினையில எஞ்ஞான்றும் கேட்பினும் சொல்லாவிடல்). 200 gives the general rule: say words that have use, do not say words that have none (சொல்லிற் பயனிலாச் சொல்). The court verse applies the household rule and tightens it, since here even the king's own request does not license useless talk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:698","b":"k:667","type":"parallel_domain","note":"698 forbids belittling a king on the grounds of youth or kinship (இளையர் இனமுறையர் என்றிகழார்). 667 says one must not scorn by outward form, since the great rolling chariot depends on its linchpin (உருவுகண்டு எள்ளாமை வேண்டும்). Both reject judging a person by a surface measure, whether age, family relation or size, and point instead to the function he carries.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:801","type":"qualifies","note":"700 says the familiarity (கெழுதகைமை) that does improper things on the thought 'we are old associates' (பழையம் எனக்கருதி) brings ruin. 801 defines old friendship (பழைமை) as friendship that in no way cuts down the liberties of intimacy (கிழமையைக் கீழ்ந்திடா நட்பு). The liberty that defines friendship between equals is thus withdrawn where the other party is a king, as Parimelazhagar reads 700.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:700","b":"k:805","type":"tension","note":"700 says intimacy that does what is improper brings ruin (பண்பல்ல செய்யும் கெழுதகைமை கேடு தரும்). 805 says that when friends do something hurtful one should understand it as folly or as great intimacy (பேதைமை ஒன்றோ பெருங்கிழமை என்றுணர்க நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்). The conflict holds on Manakkudavar's gloss of 700, which speaks of the nature of friendship in general; on Parimelazhagar's, 700 concerns a king and the two verses address different relationships.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"k:701","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"701 praises the one who knows what is meant by looking, without its being said (கூறாமை நோக்கிக் குறிப்பறிவான்). 1100, in the love chapter that has the same title குறிப்பறிதல், says that when eyes agree with eyes in their look, spoken words have no use (வாய்ச்சொற்கள் என்ன பயனும் இல). Reading without speech is a minister's rare skill in 701 and the ordinary condition of two lovers in 1100.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:703","b":"k:445","type":"develops","note":"703 urges the king to pay any price for those who read signs. 445 says that since counsellors serve as the ruler's eyes (சூழ்வார் கண்ணாக ஒழுகலான்), he must choose them with care. 445 gives the reason behind 703: the king sees through his advisers, so an adviser who can read what is unsaid is worth what Parimelazhagar lists as wealth, land, elephants and horses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:703","b":"k:794","type":"same_image","note":"703 says to give whatever it takes from one's resources and secure those who read a sign by a sign (யாது கொடுத்தும் கொளல்). 794 says the friendship of one of good family who is ashamed of blame should be had even by giving (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் நட்பு). The shared formula marks the two kinds of person the book thinks worth paying for, the perceptive adviser and the friend with a sense of shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:410","type":"develops","note":"704 stops at saying that those who cannot grasp the unspoken are 'different' (வேறு) from those who can. 410 says others stand to those who have studied the shining books as beasts stand to people (விலங்கொடு மக்கள் அனையர்). Parimelazhagar glosses the வேறு of 704 with this very comparison, the idea being that they are animals, so 410 states outright what 704 leaves implied.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:993","type":"agrees","note":"704 says those who grasp what is meant without being told and the rest are alike in limbs (உறுப்போ ரனையரால்) and yet different. 993 says likeness of limbs is not likeness among people (உறுப்பொத்தல் மக்களொப்பு அன்றால்), and true likeness is likeness of character. Both deny that the shared body makes people alike, with perception the dividing line in 704 and பண்பு in 993.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:704","b":"k:1071","type":"parallel_domain","note":"704 says readers of the unspoken and others share the same limbs but are different (வேறு). 1071 says the base look just like people (மக்களே போல்வர் கயவர்), a resemblance the speaker has seen nowhere else. The same figure of outward human likeness hiding an inner difference serves a point about intelligence in 704 and about moral baseness in 1071.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:393","type":"parallel_domain","note":"705 says eyes that cannot read another's meaning are of no use. 393 says only the learned have eyes, and the unlearned have two sores in the face (முகத்திரண்டு புண்ணுடையர் கல்லாதவர்). In both the physical eye is treated as worthless without a trained capacity behind it, learning in one and the reading of signs in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:705","b":"k:574","type":"parallel_domain","note":"705 asks what use eyes are among the limbs if they cannot read a sign by a sign (என்ன பயத்தவோ கண்). 574 asks what eyes without measured kindly regard do, beyond seeming to be there in the face (உளபோல் முகத்தெவன் செய்யும் ... கண்ணோட்டம் இல்லாத கண்). Each denies the eye its name unless it does more than see, perceiving another's mind in 705 and looking kindly in 574.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:706","b":"k:959","type":"parallel_domain","note":"706 says the face shows what fills the heart, as crystal shows what is beside it. 959 says the sprout shows what lay in the ground, and the spoken word shows the lineage of the one born in it (நிலத்தில் கிடந்தமை கால்காட்டும்). Both rest on காட்டும்: something visible discloses a hidden source, mood in the face and family in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:830","type":"tension","note":"707 says nothing is as knowing as the face, since whether one is pleased or angry it gets there first (உவப்பினும் காயினும் தான்முந்துறும்). 830 advises that when an enemy turns friend one should befriend with the face and let go in the heart (முகநட்டு அகநட்பு ஒரீஇ விடல்). If the face always runs ahead of the mind, as 707 says, the control 830 recommends should not be possible; the two agree only if 707 describes the unguarded face.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:707","b":"k:1277","type":"parallel_domain","note":"707 says the face is ahead of the person in showing pleasure or anger (தான் முந்துறும்). In 1277 a woman says her bangles knew before she did that her lover had left (நம்மினும் முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). Both give the body a knowledge that arrives before the mind's own, a general observation in 707 and a lover's remark on her thinning arms in 1277.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:708","b":"k:1100","type":"parallel_domain","note":"708 says that if one has people who look within and understand what has happened, it is enough to stand looking them in the face (முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும்). 1100 says that once eyes agree with eyes, spoken words are of no use. Both make speech unnecessary between the right pair; Parimelazhagar adds for 708 that going on to speak would lessen both parties, a concern for dignity the love verse does not have.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:710","b":"k:796","type":"same_image","note":"710 says the measuring rod (அளக்குங்கோல்) of those who call themselves subtle is nothing but the eye. 796 says ruin has one benefit: it is a rod that measures one's people to full length (கிளைஞரை நீட்டி அளப்பதோர் கோல்). The book uses the measuring rod twice for gauging other people, the eye taking a present reading of a mind in 710 and adversity testing loyalty over time in 796.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:711","b":"k:721","type":"develops","note":"Both verses end with the identical line சொல்லின் தொகையறிந்த தூய்மையவர். 711 commands such people to know the assembly before speaking; 721 says that, having known the kinds (வகையறிந்து), they do not let their speech slip before the capable (வல்லவை வாய்சோரார்). The repeated line makes 721 the outcome of 711's instruction: knowing the assembly is what removes the fear that causes slips.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:712","b":"k:696","type":"parallel_domain","note":"712 asks the speaker in an assembly to discern the opening (இடைதெரிந்து), which Parimelazhagar glosses as the hearers' readiness to listen. 696 asks the same of one speaking to a king: know his mood and consider the time (குறிப்பறிந்து காலங் கருதி) and say it so that it is wanted. The timing rule is the same before an assembly and before a single ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:713","b":"k:401","type":"agrees","note":"713 says those who take up speaking without knowing the assembly (அவையறியார்) do not know the kinds of speech and have no ability at all. 401 says holding forth in an assembly (கோட்டி கொளல்) without full learning is like playing dice with no marked board. Each names a different missing precondition for speaking in an assembly, knowledge of the hearers in 713 and knowledge of the books in 401, and each treats the speech that results as empty.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:714","b":"k:425","type":"tension","note":"714 tells the speaker to change with the audience, bright before the bright and blank before the blank. 425 says understanding is what does not open and close (மலர்தலும் கூம்பலும் இல்லது அறிவு). If 425's second half is read generally, as steadiness of bearing, the two disagree about whether the wise adapt their display; on Parimelazhagar's reading, where 425 is about constancy in friendship with the world, the conflict disappears.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:714","b":"k:844","type":"same_image","note":"714 uses a colour pair for intelligence: be bright (ஒள்ளியர்) before the bright, and take the colour of white plaster (வான்சுதை வண்ணம்) before the blank (வெளியார்). 844 uses the same pair: whiteness (வெண்மை) is the conceit that says 'we have brightness' (ஒண்மை உடையம் யாம்). Read together, the learned person of 714 puts on whiteness knowingly before those who, by 844, are white because they think themselves bright.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:715","b":"k:419","type":"agrees","note":"715 praises the restraint (செறிவு) of not speaking first among elders. 419 says a mouth that bows (வணங்கிய வாய்) is hard to have for those without fine listening. Both treat modest speech as a mark of what a person has learned, and 419 supplies the source 715 leaves unstated: the habit of listening.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:716","b":"k:642","type":"develops","note":"642 gives the general rule: since gain and ruin come from speech, guard against a lapse in it (சொல்லின்கட் சோர்வு). 716 applies it to one setting, a slip before the widely learned, and measures the damage by comparing it to falling from the path (ஆற்றின் நிலைதளர்ந்தற்று), which Parimelazhagar reads as a renouncer's lapse from the good path.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:716","b":"k:957","type":"parallel_domain","note":"716 says a slip (இழுக்கு) before those who have taken in wide learning is like faltering from one's footing on the path. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows up high, like the spot on the moon (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல்). In both, the weight of a fault depends on where it is seen, the standing of the witnesses in 716 and of the person in 957, and not only on its size.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:717","b":"k:404","type":"qualifies","note":"717 says the learning of those who have studied and understood shines among those able to examine words without fault (சொல்தெரிதல் வல்லார் அகத்து). 404 says the wise do not accept an unlearned person's brightness (ஒட்பம்), however good it is. The same expert hearers decide both cases: before them real learning becomes visible and untaught cleverness is not accepted.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:719","b":"k:728","type":"qualifies","note":"719 and 728 share the phrase நல்லவையுள் நன்கு செலச் சொல்லு-. 728 says those who cannot speak tellingly in a good assembly are of no use though they have learned much; 719 tells those who can do so not to speak in a mean assembly even by forgetting (பொச்சாந்தும் சொல்லற்க). Together they set the limits: silence in a good assembly is a failure, and silence in a mean one is required.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:719","b":"k:849","type":"agrees","note":"719 forbids speaking good things in a mean assembly. 849 gives a reason in another chapter: one who shows something to a person who does not see becomes himself unseeing (காணாதான் காட்டுவான் தான்காணான்), while that person goes on seeing as he saw. Parimelazhagar makes the same point on 719, that both assemblies would then look down on the speaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:720","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"720 says holding forth before those not of one's kind is like nectar spilled in a dirty yard (அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்து). 1000 says great wealth in the hands of one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). Both describe a fine liquid ruined by where it lands, and so place the loss in the receiver and not in the thing given.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:721","b":"k:647","type":"agrees","note":"721 says those who know the body of words and the kinds of assembly do not let their speech slip before the capable (வாய்சோரார்), which Parimelazhagar reads as not slipping out of fear. 647 lists the same qualities, able to speak, without slips, without fear (சொலல்வல்லன் சோர்விலன் அஞ்சான்), and says such a person is hard for anyone to defeat. 647 states the payoff of the fearlessness that 721 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:721","b":"k:716","type":"develops","note":"716 says a slip (இழுக்கு) before those of wide learning is like falling from the path. 721 answers from the next chapter: those who know words and know the assembly do not slip before the capable (வல்லவை வாய்சோரார்). One verse gives the cost of the slip and the other says what prevents it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:722","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"722 says those who make what they have learned go home before the learned (கற்ற செலச் சொல்லுவார்) are called learned among the learned. 424 defines understanding with the same verb: saying things so that they go in easily (எண்பொருளவாகச் செலச்சொல்லி) and seeing the subtle meaning in what others say. Both measure learning or understanding by whether it reaches the hearer, not by what the speaker holds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:722","b":"k:650","type":"agrees","note":"722 gives the title 'learned among the learned' to those who can convey their learning to the learned. 650 gives the reverse case: those who cannot unfold what they have learned so that others understand (கற்றது உணர விரித்துரையாதார்) are like flowers that bloom in clusters without scent. Together they treat communication as part of learning itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:762","type":"tension","note":"723 calls those who die amid enemies எளியர், easy to find. 762 says the hardness that does not fear wounds when the king falters is rare except in an old army (தொல்படைக்கல்லால் அரிது). If 723's first half is read as saying battle courage is common, the two disagree about how rare it is; if 762 is limited to courage in defeat and with reduced numbers, they do not.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:723","b":"k:872","type":"parallel_domain","note":"723 says those who will die amid enemies are easy to find, and those who do not fear within an assembly are hard to find (அவையகத்து அஞ்சாதவர்). 872 says one may take on the enmity of those who plough with the bow but not of those who plough with the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர்). Both rank the word above the weapon, 723 by how rare the courage is and 872 by how dangerous the opponent is.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:724","b":"k:424","type":"agrees","note":"724 joins two acts: convey your learning (செலச்சொல்லி) and take in what those more learned have. 424 joins the same two as its definition of understanding: say things so that they go in (செலச்சொல்லி) and see the subtle meaning in what others say (பிறர்வாய் நுண்பொருள் காண்பது). What 424 calls understanding, 724 presents as what a fearless speaker gains from an assembly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:725","b":"k:391","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both verses open with the command to learn. 391 says learn without flaw and then stand in keeping with it (கற்க கசடற ... நிற்க அதற்குத் தக); 725 says learn with measure so as to give a fearless reply (மாற்றங் கொடுத்தற் பொருட்டு). 391 gives learning its end in conduct, and 725 gives it a second end in public speech.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:725","b":"k:645","type":"develops","note":"725 says to learn by the proper path and with measure (ஆற்றின் அளவறிந்து கற்க) in order to reply in the assembly without fear; Parimelazhagar takes அளவு as the science of logic. 645 says to speak a word only after knowing no other word can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). 725 names the training that makes 645's test possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:726","b":"k:534","type":"parallel_domain","note":"726 asks what a sword is to those who are not hard-eyed, and what books are to those who fear a subtle assembly (நுண்ணவை அஞ்சுபவர்க்கு). 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை), which Parimelazhagar explains as a fortress being of no use to them. In each, fear cancels an instrument that is sound in itself: sword, books, fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:726","b":"k:647","type":"agrees","note":"726 says books are nothing to one who fears a subtle assembly. 647 says the one who can speak, does not slip and does not fear (அஞ்சான்) is hard for anyone to defeat, and Parimelazhagar takes that fearlessness as fearlessness of the assembly. 647 states positively the condition whose absence 726 says makes learning useless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:728","b":"k:140","type":"parallel_domain","note":"728 and 140 have the same form: 'though they have learned much'. In 140, those who have not learned to live in accord with the world are without understanding though they have learned much (பலகற்றும்). In 728, those who cannot speak well in a good assembly are without use though they have learned many things (பல்லவை கற்றும்). Each names a practical ability without which the amount learned does not count.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:729","b":"k:395","type":"qualifies","note":"395 calls the unlearned the lowest (கடையரே கல்லாதவர்). 729 uses the same word to put someone beneath them: those who have studied and understood and still fear an assembly of the good are lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை). 729 thus limits 395's ranking, since learning that is not voiced does not lift a person above the bottom rank and, as Parimelazhagar explains, leaves them with only the labour of having studied.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:730","b":"k:214","type":"same_image","note":"730 counts those who cannot voice their learning as equal to those who are not, though they exist. 214 says one who knows what is fitting truly lives and anyone else is placed among the dead (செத்தாருள் வைக்கப்படும்). Both apply the figure of a living person counted as dead or absent to someone who gives nothing to others, wealth in 214 and learning in 730; Parimelazhagar reads 730's இல்லார் as the dead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:239","type":"develops","note":"731 sets unfailing yield and worthy people (தக்கார்) side by side as marks of a land. 239 says land that bears a body without renown (இசையிலா யாக்கை பொறுத்த நிலம்) declines in its blameless rich produce (வண்பயன் குன்றும்). 239 makes a causal claim where 731 only lists: the land's yield depends on the quality of the people it carries.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"731 makes unfailing yield (தள்ளா விளையுள்) one of three things that must come together (சேர்வது) for a land. 545 says rain and yield (பெயலும் விளையுளும்) gather (தொக்கு) in the land of the king who wields the sceptre as it should be wielded. Read together, the yield that defines a land in 731 is traced in 545 to just rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:731","b":"k:1032","type":"develops","note":"Parimelazhagar takes விளையுள் in 731 to stand for the cultivators, since the verse lists it beside two kinds of people (தக்கார், செல்வர்). 1032 calls those who plough the linchpin (ஆணி) of the world because they carry all who go off to other work. 1032 gives the reason the cultivators come first in 731's list.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:15","type":"develops","note":"732 says a land yields greatly because ruin is rare there (அருங்கேட்டால் ஆற்ற விளைவது). 15 says rain is what ruins (கெடுப்பதூஉம்) and also what raises up the ruined. Parimelazhagar's list of the கேடு meant in 732 begins with too much rain and no rain, so 15 names the chief source of the ruin that 732 wants to be rare.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:732","b":"k:736","type":"develops","note":"732 asks that ruin be rare (அருங்கேடு) so that the land yields abundantly. 736 goes a step further: the foremost land does not know ruin, and its richness does not shrink even where ruin has come (கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா). 732 states the ordinary standard and 736 the highest one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:733","b":"k:552","type":"qualifies","note":"733 defines a land as one that bears burdens arriving all at once and still agrees to give the king his whole due (இறையொருங்கு நேர்வது). 552 says that asking (இரவு) by one who stands with the sceptre is like a man standing with a spear saying 'hand it over'. The land's willingness to pay in 733 is not a licence for the king to demand: Manakkudavar reads 733's burdens as levies beyond the fixed due, which is the situation 552 condemns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:733","b":"k:990","type":"same_image","note":"In 733 the land bears the load that comes upon it (பொறை ... தாங்கி). In 990 the great earth will not bear its load (தாங்காது ... பொறை) if the worthy decline in worth. The same words describe a land's capacity to carry weight, and 990 makes that capacity depend on the character of its best people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:734","b":"k:735","type":"develops","note":"734 lists what must not reach a land from outside or by nature: hunger, disease and the enemy who destroys (செறுபகை). 735 lists what must not exist inside it: many factions (பல்குழு), the inner enemy that lays waste (உட்பகை) and murderous bands that harass the king. The two verses divide the threats into external and internal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:550","type":"develops","note":"735 says a land has no murderous bands that harass the king (வேந்தலைக்கும் கொல்குறும்பு). 550 says a king's punishing the cruel with death (கொலையிற் கொடியாரை வேந்தொறுத்தல்) is like weeding the young crop. 550 gives the ruler's means of producing the condition 735 describes; Parimelazhagar on 735 likewise says these three are to be removed by the king and the inhabitants.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:735","b":"k:889","type":"develops","note":"735 requires a land to be without the inner enemy that lays waste (பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகை). 889 says inner enmity, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவு), holds ruin within it (உள்ளதாங் கேடு). 889 explains why 735 demands its complete absence and not just its reduction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:736","b":"k:955","type":"parallel_domain","note":"736 calls foremost the land whose richness does not shrink even where ruin has come (கெட்ட இடத்தும் வளங்குன்றா). 955 says an old family does not part from its character even when what it has to give has fallen away (வழங்குவது உள்வீழ்ந்தக் கண்ணும் ... பண்பில் தலைப்பிரிதல் இன்று). Both use the same 'even when' construction to test excellence by what remains after loss, one for a land and one for a family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:737","b":"k:381","type":"develops","note":"737 names a strong fort (வல்லரண்) among the limbs (உறுப்பு) of a land, with its waters and hill. 381 names the fort (அரண்) and the people or land (குடி) as two of the six things a king must have, which Parimelazhagar calls his six limbs (அங்கம்). 737 nests one of the king's limbs inside another, which fits the order of the chapters: land, then fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:738","b":"k:734","type":"agrees","note":"738 lists five ornaments (அணி) of a land: freedom from disease (பிணியின்மை), wealth, yield, happiness and protection (ஏமம்). 734 defines a land by three absences: severe hunger, unceasing disease (ஓவாப் பிணி) and destructive enemies. The two lists match as positive and negative: yield answers hunger, பிணியின்மை answers disease, and ஏமம் answers the enemy, while 738 adds wealth and happiness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:594","type":"parallel_domain","note":"739 says lands are those whose riches come without being sought (நாடா வளத்தன). 594 says prosperity itself asks the way and goes (ஆக்கம் அதர்வினாய்ச் செல்லும்) to the person of unwavering drive. Both picture wealth arriving unsought, but 594 makes it the result of the person's energy, where 739 makes it a property of the land.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:739","b":"k:1040","type":"qualifies","note":"739 praises land whose riches need no seeking. 1040 says the good woman called Land laughs (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள் நகும்) at those who say 'we have nothing' and sit idle (அசைஇ இருப்பாரை). 1040 limits 739: the land has riches to give, but not to those who do nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:558","type":"develops","note":"740 says a land's excellences bring no benefit (பயமின்று) without accord between it and its king. 558 says that under the sceptre of a king who does not do justice, having is more painful than not having (இன்மையின் இன்னாது உடைமை). 558 shows one way the wealth praised in this chapter turns useless or worse under the wrong ruler.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:740","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"740 says that a land with every excellence is of no use without accord with its king. 770 says that though an army has plenty of steadfast men (நிலைமக்கள் சால உடைத்தெனினும்), there is no army where there are no leaders (தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்). In both, a body that is complete in its own parts is counted as nothing without its head.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:741","b":"k:569","type":"develops","note":"741 says a fort matters to those who fear attack and look to their own safety. 569 says a king who has not made his defences (சிறைசெய்யா வேந்தன்) will, when war comes, be struck with fear and quickly perish (வெருவந்து வெய்து கெடும்). 569 states the cost of ignoring 741: without a fort prepared beforehand, fear has nowhere to turn.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:742","b":"k:737","type":"same_image","note":"742 says a fort has clear water (மணிநீர்), open ground, a hill (மலை) and a forest of fine shade. 737 lists as limbs of a land the two waters (இருபுனல்), a well-placed hill (வாய்ந்த மலை), the water that comes from it, and a strong fort. The same features of the terrain, water and hill, are counted first as a land's resources and then as a fort's defences, and 737 already names the fort as part of the land.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:743","b":"k:421","type":"parallel_domain","note":"743 defines the fort as a wall complete in four measures: height, breadth, strength and difficulty of approach (உயர்வு அகலம் திண்மை அருமை). 421 calls understanding the tool that guards against ruin and an inner fort that enemies cannot destroy (செறுவார்க்கும் உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்). 421 moves the fort inside the person: the quality 743 seeks in masonry, that it cannot be broken into, 421 claims for understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:745","b":"k:499","type":"develops","note":"745 says a fort is hard to take, holds stored food (கொண்ட கூழ்த்து) and is easy for those inside to hold their position in (அகத்தார் நிலைக்கு எளிது). 499 says that even people with no good defences or standing (சிறைநலனும் சீரும் இலரெனினும்) are hard to take on in the land where they live (உறைநிலத்தோடு ஒட்டல் அரிது). 499 shows that the insiders' advantage of position, which 745 asks the fort to give, already belongs to home ground without walls.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:746","b":"k:762","type":"parallel_domain","note":"746 wants in a fort good men who help at the place where it would otherwise fall; Parimelazhagar glosses இடத்து as the point of destruction by those outside. 762 says the hardness that does not fear a wound when things give way and the force is reduced (உலைவிடத்து ஊறஞ்சா வன்கண்) is rare except in an ancient army. Both measure fighting men by what they do at the moment of collapse, in the fort and in the field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:746","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"746 says a fort has every needed thing and good men who help at the critical place (இடத்துதவும் நல்லாள்). 1030 says a family with no good man to stand by and prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. The same word நல்லாள் names the person on whom a fort and a family each depend at the moment of danger; goods alone hold up neither.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:747","b":"k:764","type":"parallel_domain","note":"747 says a fort is hard to seize by encircling, by assault without encircling, or by subversion (அறைப்படுத்தும்), which Parimelazhagar explains as undermining those inside through people they trust. 764 defines an army as one that does not break and cannot be subverted (அறைபோகாதாகி). The same word அறை marks treachery from within as a danger that fort and army must both be proof against, beside open force.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:748","b":"k:493","type":"agrees","note":"748 says a fort is where those who hold it keep their hold (பற்றாற்றி) and defeat even those strong enough to besiege them; Parimelazhagar notes that the smaller force does not just endure but wins. 493 says even those without strength become strong and win (ஆற்றாரும் ஆற்றி அடுப) if they know the place and act against foes while guarding themselves. 748 is 493's rule about place applied to the fort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:749","b":"k:750","type":"qualifies","note":"749 says a fort has its distinction from what is done in action (வினைமுகத்து வீறெய்தி) so that enemies fall at the front of battle. 750 says that whatever excellence a fort has, it is nothing with those who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). 750 turns 749's praise into a condition; Parimelazhagar says the two verses together show that a fort cannot do without its defenders.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:749","b":"k:767","type":"parallel_domain","note":"749 places a fort's distinction in the actions by which enemies are brought down as soon as battle is joined (முனைமுகத்து); Parimelazhagar explains these as recognising the attack begun and choosing the acts that defeat it. 767 says an army is one that knows the way to bear the battle coming at it (தலைவந்த போர்தாங்கும் தன்மை அறிந்து) and advances. Both ask for trained response to the enemy's opening move, from the walls and in the field.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:750","b":"k:727","type":"parallel_domain","note":"750 says a fort's excellences do not exist for those who lack excellence in action. 727 says the treatise learned by one who fears the assembly is like a bright sword in the hand of a பேடி amid enemies (பகையகத்துப் பேடிகை ஒள்வாள்). In both a good instrument, a fort or learning, is worth nothing in the hands of someone who cannot act when the test comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:751","b":"k:241","type":"qualifies","note":"751 treats wealth's power to give standing to persons of no account as its distinction. 241 notes the same fact, that material wealth is found even among the base (பொருட்செல்வம் பூரியார் கண்ணும் உள), and uses it to rank wealth below grace, the 'wealth among wealths'. What 751 counts in wealth's favour, 241 counts against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:988","type":"qualifies","note":"752 reports that all scorn the poor and all honour the rich. 988 says lack is no disgrace to one who has the firmness called worth (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று சால்பென்னும் திண்மை). 752 describes how the world behaves; 988 denies that the scorn is deserved where character is present, which Parimelazhagar's gloss on 752 (scorned 'even if they have every good') makes pointed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்). 1046 gives a particular case: even when the poor understand a good matter well and say it, their words go slack (சொற்பொருள் சோர்வு படும்). The general contempt of 752 is shown reaching what a poor person says.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:752","b":"k:1047","type":"develops","note":"752's 'everyone' (எல்லாரும்) scorns the have-nots. 1047 carries that to the limit case: one in poverty not joined to virtue is looked on as a stranger even by the mother who bore him (ஈன்ற தாயானும் பிறன்போல). The pairing shows how far 'everyone' reaches, with 1047 adding the condition அறஞ்சாரா.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:753","b":"k:759","type":"agrees","note":"753 says wealth cuts away darkness (இருளறுக்கும்), which Parimelazhagar takes to be enmity. 759 says no steel is sharper than wealth for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்). Both use the verb அறு for what wealth does, once as a lamp and once as a blade; on Parimelazhagar's reading of 753 they make the same claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:754","b":"k:39","type":"develops","note":"754 says wealth that came by knowing the right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure (அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). 39 says only what comes through virtue is pleasure (அறத்தான் வருவதே இன்பம்). 754 fits wealth into 39's rule: wealth produces real pleasure only when it was itself acquired without wrong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:754","b":"k:659","type":"agrees","note":"754 limits its promise to wealth that came without wrong (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்). 659 says what was taken making others weep leaves with weeping, while what came by good ways pays later even if lost (பிற்பயக்கும் நற்பாலவை). Both make the manner of acquisition decide what wealth yields.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"755 says a gain of wealth that does not come with grace and love should be let go, not embraced (புல்லார் புரள விடல்). 113 says gain that comes from leaving fairness should be let go that very day even if it brings only good (அன்றே யொழிய விடல்). Both end on the same command, விடல், for gain acquired the wrong way, one naming love and grace as the test and the other fairness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:755","b":"k:1009","type":"develops","note":"755 tells one to refuse wealth that comes without grace and love. 1009 says what becomes of such wealth: bright wealth amassed by setting love aside and not looking to virtue (அன்பொரீஇ ... அறநோக்காது ஈட்டிய) is taken by others. 1009 supplies a consequence for the wealth 755 rejects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:247","type":"agrees","note":"757 says the child grace, born of love, is reared by the nurse called wealth (பொருளென்னும் செல்வச் செவிலி). 247 sets the two side by side: that world is not for those without grace, as this world is not for those without wealth. Both pair அருள் with பொருள்; 247 keeps them parallel, while 757 makes grace depend on wealth to grow.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:757","b":"k:755","type":"qualifies","note":"757 says grace is raised by wealth. 755, in the same chapter, says wealth that does not come with grace and love (அருளொடும் அன்பொடும் வாரா) is to be let go. The dependence runs both ways: grace needs wealth, but only wealth that itself came with grace and love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:449","type":"agrees","note":"758 says doing a task with something in one's own hand (தன்கைத்தொன்று உண்டாக) is like watching an elephant fight from a hilltop, which Parimelazhagar explains as acting without fear or strain. 449 says those without capital have no profit (முதலிலார்க்கு ஊதியம் இல்லை). Both make resources in hand the precondition of an undertaking; 758 adds the safety they give.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:758","b":"k:593","type":"same_image","note":"758 praises acting with wealth in hand (கைத்து உண்டாக). 593 says those who hold steady energy as what is in their hand (ஊக்கம் ஒருவந்தம் கைத்துடையார்) do not grieve that they have lost their gains. The same word கைத்து is used for money in 758 and for drive in 593, which treats energy as the holding that matters when the money is gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:546","type":"parallel_domain","note":"759 says wealth is sharper than any steel against foes. 546 says it is not the spear that gives victory but the king's sceptre, if it does not bend (வேலன்று வென்றி தருவது). In both the literal weapon is set below something else, wealth in one and just rule in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:878","type":"develops","note":"759 says to make wealth, since no steel is sharper for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும்). 878 says the pride found in enemies dies (மாயும் பகைவர்கண் பட்ட செருக்கு) when one knows the way, builds oneself up and guards oneself (தற்செய்து தற்காப்ப). Both aim at the enemy's செருக்கு; Parimelazhagar glosses 878's தற்செய்து as enlarging oneself, and 759 names wealth as the means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:759","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"759 calls wealth the sharpest steel (எஃகு) against enemies' pride. 985 says bowing is the weapon (படை) by which the worthy turn their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Each names a non-military thing as the real weapon against foes: resources for the ruler, humility for the person of character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:31","type":"same_image","note":"760 makes wealth the route to the other two aims, virtue and pleasure. 31 makes virtue the source of distinction and wealth (சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்). Each verse, speaking from within its own book, puts its own aim first as the one from which the others follow.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:754","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made bright wealth in full, the other two, virtue and pleasure, are easy to have together (ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). 754 says the wealth that yields virtue and pleasure is wealth that came without wrong (தீதின்றி வந்த பொருள்). 754 supplies the condition; Parimelazhagar reads it into 760's ஒண்பொருள் as wealth that comes by the right way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:760","b":"k:1002","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says abundant wealth makes virtue and pleasure easy to get. 1002 calls it a delusion to hoard without giving on the thought that everything comes from wealth (பொருளானாம் எல்லாமென்று ஈயாது இவறும் மருள்), and says it leads to a low birth. 760's claim holds for wealth that is made and used; held back by a miser, the same belief is called delusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:381","type":"agrees","note":"761 says a conquering army, complete in its parts and unafraid of wounds, is the chief of all a king's wealth (வெறுக்கையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 381 lists the six things a king must have and puts the army first (படைகுடி கூழ் ...). The placing in 381's list and the ranking in 761 agree.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:761","b":"k:759","type":"same_image","note":"761 counts the army as a form of the king's wealth (வெறுக்கை). 759 counts wealth as a weapon, the sharpest steel against foes (எஃகு). The two neighbouring chapters describe each thing in terms of the other: wealth is a weapon and the army is wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:764","type":"develops","note":"762 says steadfastness in defeat is rare except in an old army (தொல்படை). 764 defines an army by courage that has come down to it (வழிவந்த வன்கண்), along with not collapsing and not being turned by subversion. 764 makes a requirement of what 762 observes, and both use வன்கண் for the quality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:806","type":"parallel_domain","note":"762 says the hardness that does not fear harm in a time of decline (தொலைவிடத்து) is found only in an old army (தொல்படை). 806 says those who keep to the bounds do not give up, even in decline (தொலைவிடத்தும்), the bond of those who stood by them of old (தொல்லைக்கண் நின்றார்). Both use the same two words to tie loyalty in bad times to length of standing, in an army and in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:762","b":"k:955","type":"parallel_domain","note":"762 says only a long-established army stays firm when it is reduced and the king is faltering. 955 says an old family (பழங்குடி) does not part from its nature even when its means of giving have shrunk. In both, age of standing is what keeps character intact under loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:763","b":"k:873","type":"qualifies","note":"763 says a crowd of weak enemies is nothing to one strong fighter. 873 says one who, being alone, takes on the enmity of many is more foolish than the mad (தமியனாய்ப் பல்லார் பகைகொள்பவன்). 763 holds only where the one is a cobra and the many are rats; 873 is the warning for a lone person who assumes that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:764","b":"k:766","type":"develops","note":"764 says an army has courage that has come down to it (வழிவந்த வன்கண்). 766 lists the four things that protect an army: valour, honour, walking the excellent path of those before (மாண்ட வழிச்செலவு) and தேற்றம். 766 spells out the inherited quality of 764 as following the path of earlier warriors, which is how Parimelazhagar reads வழிச்செலவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:768","b":"k:750","type":"tension","note":"768 says an army gains stature through its array (படைத்தகையால் பாடு பெறும்) even if it lacks striking power and endurance; Parimelazhagar takes படைத்தகை as splendour of appearance. 750 says a fort, whatever excellences it has, is as nothing with people who lack excellence in action (வினைமாட்சி இல்லார்கண் இல்லது). They conflict if 768's பாடு is read as real military worth; if it means only repute, 768 is a concession and 750 still stands.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:769","b":"k:567","type":"develops","note":"769 names a resentment that will not leave (செல்லாத் துனி) among the things an army must be free of to win; Parimelazhagar says it arises from degrading treatment. 567 says harsh words and punishment beyond bounds are a file that wears down a king's conquering strength (அடுமுரண் தேய்க்கும் அரம்). 567 gives a cause on the king's side for the condition 769 warns of.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:769","b":"k:759","type":"develops","note":"769 says an army wins if it is free of dwindling, of resentment that does not leave, and of poverty (வறுமை). 759 says to make wealth because it is the sharpest weapon against foes. Parimelazhagar links them from both sides: under 769 he says desertion and want arise when the king does not pay, and under 759 that making wealth brings a large army.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:770","b":"k:1030","type":"parallel_domain","note":"770 says an army does not exist without leaders. 1030 says a family with no good person to prop it (அடுத்தூன்றும் நல்லாள் இலாத குடி) falls when trouble cuts at its foot. Both make a group's survival depend on a capable person at its head, in the army and in the household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:763","type":"agrees","note":"771 dismisses the enemy by pointing to the many (பலர்) who faced his lord and became stones. 763 asks what it matters if a host of rats roars like the sea, since they perish when the cobra merely breathes (நாகம் உயிர்ப்பக் கெடும்). Both say numbers on the other side count for nothing against one real fighter; Parimelazhagar reads 763 as the hero not fearing a crowd of non-heroes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:894","type":"agrees","note":"771 has a warrior warn enemies not to stand before his lord (என்ஐமுன் நில்லன்மின்), since many who did now stand as memorial stones (கல்நின்றவர்). 894 states the rule behind the warning: for the unable to do harm to the able is like beckoning Death with the hand (கூற்றத்தைக் கையால் விளித்தற்று). The battlefield taunt and the political maxim give the same counsel, one from the strong side and one addressed to the weak.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:771","b":"k:1088","type":"same_image","note":"771 is a warrior's boast about a might that turns opponents into memorial stones. In 1088 a lover says his பீடு, which even enemies who had not met him feared in battle (ஞாட்பினுள் நண்ணாரும் உட்கும்), has broken before her bright forehead. The Book of Love borrows the same figure of the warrior whose name alone frightens foes, here as a speaker's way of measuring her effect on him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:772","b":"k:861","type":"tension","note":"772 prefers the failed throw at an elephant to the successful shot at a hare, and Manakkudavar takes it with 771 as saying the warrior should send the weak away and fight those who can oppose him. 861 advises the reverse: avoid meeting the strong (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக) and keep up enmity against the weak (மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை). The conflict holds if 772 is read as a rule for choosing opponents and 861 as Parimelazhagar reads it; it is the warrior's pride against the king's policy. Kalaignar's gloss of 861 turns it into fighting the strong, which would remove the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:773","b":"k:987","type":"parallel_domain","note":"773 makes help to a foe in trouble the cutting edge of valour; Parimelazhagar illustrates it with the king of Ayodhya telling his disarmed enemy to go and come back tomorrow. 987 asks what use worth (சால்பு) is if one does not do what is sweet even to those who did one harm (இன்னா செய்தார்க்கும் இனியவே). The ideal of the worthy person in the chapter on சால்பு appears here as the height of a soldier's manhood.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:775","b":"k:686","type":"parallel_domain","note":"775 asks whether it is not a rout (ஒட்டு in this edition, which Parimelazhagar glosses as turning the back) for the fierce if the eye glaring at the enemy closes in a blink when a spear is thrown. 686 requires the envoy to be one who does not fear the eye (கண் அஞ்சான்), which Parimelazhagar explains as the hostile king's angry stare; 585 says the same of the spy. Holding one's gaze under threat is a test in the court as on the field.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:775","b":"k:1129","type":"same_image","note":"775 treats a single blink (இமைப்பின்) before a flung spear as equal to flight. In 1129 the woman says she will not blink because her lover, who lives in her eyes, would be hidden (இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல்). Both make the refusal to blink a measure of devotion, to valour in one and, in the speaker's conceit, to the beloved in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"777 says that for those who want fame that circles the earth and do not want life (வேண்டா உயிரார்), tying on the war-anklet is an adornment. 235 says a ruin that is growth and a death that is existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு) are possible only for the skilled, which Parimelazhagar explains by the body of fame outliving the body of flesh. 235 gives the reasoning for the warrior's choice in 777.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:777","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"777 describes warriors who prefer fame (இசை) to life. 1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame and will not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). Both rank a value above staying alive; in one it is public renown won in battle, in the other an inner restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:779","b":"k:772","type":"develops","note":"779 asks who could fault for failure (பிழைத்தது ஒறுக்கிற்பவர்) those who die so as not to break what they vowed (இழைத்தது இகவாமை); Parimelazhagar explains that dying before the vow is accomplished still counts as keeping it. 772 says the spear that missed (பிழைத்த வேல்) the elephant is better to hold than the arrow that hit a hare. Both verses turn on பிழை, a miss, and deny that it is a disgrace when the attempt was great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:781","b":"k:815","type":"qualifies","note":"781 praises friendship as the best protection. 815 says the poor friendship of small people, which gives no safety even after it is made (செய்து ஏமம் சாரா), is better not had than had. The protection claimed in 781 belongs only to friendship with the right people; made with the wrong ones it guards nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:782","b":"k:811","type":"develops","note":"782 contrasts friendships that grow and friendships that wane according to the kind of person. 811 says that with people without character, however ardent they look, it is better for the friendship to shrink than to grow (பெருகலிற் குன்றல் இனிது). The waning that 782 observes in bad friendships is in 811 what one should want for them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:783","b":"k:811","type":"qualifies","note":"783 says that with people of character (பண்புடையாளர்) the bond grows sweeter with practice. 811 says that with those who lack character (பண்பிலார்), friendship is better shrinking than growing, even if they seem to drink one up with affection. The reward of long practice in 783 depends on பண்பு; without it, more closeness is worse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:784","b":"k:795","type":"develops","note":"784 gives rebuke as friendship's purpose. 795 turns it into a test for choosing friends: examine and take the friendship of those able to speak so that one weeps, to rebuke (இடித்து) and to teach what is proper. What 784 says a friend is for, 795 tells the reader to look for beforehand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:785","b":"k:792","type":"tension","note":"Manakkudavar reads 785 as saying many days of acquaintance are not needed to judge a friendship, because for those of the same understanding (உணர்ச்சி) one meeting gives the claim. 792 says the friendship of one who does not examine again and again (ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான்) ends in grief that kills him. On Manakkudavar's reading the two differ on whether friendship can safely be formed quickly; on Parimelazhagar's, 785 only says that feeling, more than contact, is what makes the bond, and there is no conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:786","b":"k:824","type":"develops","note":"786 says friendship in which only the face smiles is not friendship (முகநக நட்பது நட்பன்று), and that friendship in which the heart within smiles is. 824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா). The face and heart contrast that defines true friendship in 786 becomes in 824 the mark of a false friend to be feared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:787","b":"k:799","type":"develops","note":"787 ends with the friend who suffers along in ruin (அழிவின்கண் அல்லல் உழப்பது). 799 says the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when remembered at the hour of death. 799 shows the cost of the failure of the third duty that 787 lists.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:788","b":"k:1023","type":"same_image","note":"In 788 the hand goes at once to the slipping garment (உடுக்கை). In 1023, for one who says he will raise his family, தெய்வம் tucks up its garment (மடிதற்று) and goes ahead of him. Both use a gesture with one's clothing for help that comes unasked and promptly, from a friend in one and from the divine in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:789","b":"k:449","type":"parallel_domain","note":"789 defines the height of friendship as a firm state of propping the other up (ஊன்றும் நிலை). 449 says those without the support of a prop (மதலையாம் சார்பு) have no standing (நிலை), as those without capital have no profit. 789 describes the supporter and 449 the one who needs supporting, in the chapter on a king's reliance on the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:789","b":"k:818","type":"agrees","note":"789 places friendship's throne in supporting the friend without wavering wherever it is possible (ஒல்லும்வாய் ஊன்றும் நிலை). 818 says to let slip without a word the friendship of those who make difficult a task they could do (ஒல்லும் கருமம் உடற்றுபவர்). The same word for what lies within one's power sets the standard in 789 and identifies the friend who fails it in 818.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:790","b":"k:978","type":"parallel_domain","note":"790 says friendship looks shabby (புல்லென்னும்) even when friends dress it up in words (புனையினும்) such as 'they are this to us and we are that to them'. 978 says greatness always bows while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration (அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). In both, the verbal ornament is the sign of the lesser thing: real friendship, like real greatness, does not advertise itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:467","type":"parallel_domain","note":"791: inquire first, since after befriending there is no leaving (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). 467: think and then resolve on a task, and to say 'we will think after resolving' (துணிந்தபின் எண்ணுவம்) is a lapse. The same order of thought before an irreversible step is applied to friendship and to action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:791","b":"k:800","type":"tension","note":"791 grounds the need for inquiry on the claim that for those who keep friendship there is no leaving once it is formed (நட்டபின் வீடில்லை). 800 tells one to leave the friendship of the ஒப்பிலார், even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக), and Parimelazhagar takes this as the remedy for a friendship taken up unknowingly. If வீடில்லை is read as a flat 'there is no release', the two conflict; if it describes only what a true friend will not do, 800 is the exception the chapter allows.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:793","b":"k:453","type":"develops","note":"793 includes a person's இனன், his circle, among the things to learn before befriending him (Parimelazhagar takes it as kin). 453 says that the word 'he is such a one' comes from a person's company (இனத்தானாம் இன்னான் எனப்படுஞ் சொல்). 453 gives the reason the circle belongs on 793's list: it is where a person's character is read from.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:794","b":"k:951","type":"develops","note":"794 picks out as a friend the well-born person who is ashamed of blame (பழிநாணுவான்). 951 says that integrity and shame together are found by nature only in those born in a good house (இற்பிறந்தார் கண்அல்லது இல்லை ... செப்பமும் நாணும்). 951 gives the book's reason for 794 pairing birth with shame as the test.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:795","b":"k:448","type":"parallel_domain","note":"795 says to choose as friends those who can rebuke (இடித்து) and bring one to tears over a wrong. 448 says a king without those who rebuke him (இடிப்பாரை இல்லாத) perishes even with no one to ruin him. The rebuker counts as a safeguard both for the private person choosing friends and for the king choosing his support.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:450","type":"qualifies","note":"797 counts leaving the friendship of fools as gain (ஊதியம்). 450 says that letting go the tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many. Whether breaking a tie is profit or harm depends on who is at the other end of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"797 says leaving a fool's friendship is gain. 816 says that being a stranger to the wise (அறிவுடையார் ஏதின்மை) is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool (பேதை பெருங்கெழீஇ நட்பு); Pope and Subramuniyaswami render ஏதின்மை as enmity. Both verses put a fool's friendship below having no tie at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:797","b":"k:839","type":"same_image","note":"797 calls it gain to give up and leave the friendship of fools (பேதையார் கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்). 839 uses the same phrase, பேதையார் கேண்மை, and says it is very sweet because there is no pain in parting from it. Read together, 839 is the ironic version of 797: the one good thing about such a friendship is the leaving of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:106","type":"qualifies","note":"798 says not to take the friendship of those who cut away in distress (அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார்). 106 says not to give up the friendship of those who were one's strength in sorrow (துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு துறவற்க). The two prohibitions, கொள்ளற்க and துறவற்க, are complementary: how a friend behaves in trouble decides both whom not to take and whom not to drop.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:596","type":"agrees","note":"798 begins: do not think of what makes the spirit shrink (உள்ளற்க உள்ளம் சிறுகுவ). 596 gives the positive form: in all one thinks, think of rising (உள்ளுவதெல்லாம் உயர்வுள்ளல்). Both treat the direction of one's thoughts as something to be chosen for the sake of the spirit; 798 then sets fair-weather friends beside the shrinking thoughts as things to refuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:798","b":"k:814","type":"same_image","note":"798 uses ஆற்றறுப்பார் for friends who fail one in distress. 814 uses the same expression of the untrained horse that fails its rider in battle (அமரகத்து ஆற்றறுக்கும் கல்லாமா) and says solitude is better than such people as one's own. 814 supplies the picture for 798's word: support that holds until the moment it is needed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:799","b":"k:293","type":"same_image","note":"799 says the thought of false friends burns the heart (உள்ளஞ் சுடும்). 293 says that after a lie one's own heart burns one (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). In 293 the heart burns for one's own fault, in 799 for another's desertion, so the same inner fire is given two different causes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:799","b":"k:1207","type":"same_image","note":"799 ends உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்: even to think of the deserter's friendship burns the heart. 1207 ends with the same words in the mouth of the parted woman, for whom even remembering her lover burns. The phrase is used once for betrayal and once for separation in love; in both the pain comes from memory of a bond, not from a present injury.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:800","b":"k:794","type":"same_image","note":"794 says the friendship of the well-born man who fears blame is worth getting even by giving (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்). 800 says the friendship of the ஒப்பிலார் is worth leaving even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). The chapter uses the figure of payment in both directions, for acquiring a good friend and for getting free of a bad one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:800","b":"k:818","type":"develops","note":"800 says to leave the friendship of the unfit even at a cost. 818 says how to leave one kind of bad friend, those who make hard work of what they could do: without speaking of it, let the friendship slip (சொல்லாடார் சோர விடல்). 818 adds a manner of leaving to 800's instruction to leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:802","b":"k:981","type":"develops","note":"802 calls it the duty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கடன்) to take a friend's liberties well. 981 says that for those who take on worthiness knowing what is due, all good things are கடன். 802 is one instance of 981's general statement, with the same word கடன்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:803","b":"k:790","type":"develops","note":"803 asks what use long friendship is if one does not accept a friend's liberty as though one had done it oneself (செய்தாங்கு அமையாக் கடை). 790 says friendship looks shabby even when friends describe each other as 'they are this to us, we are that to them'. Both deny a separation of 'mine' and 'theirs' between friends: 790 in how they speak of each other, 803 in whose act an act is counted to be.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:804","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"804 says that when friends act without asking, out of intimacy (கெழுதகையாற் கேளாது), it is welcomed. 788 compares friendship to the hand of a man whose garment slips, removing the distress there and then (ஆங்கே). The hand in 788 does not ask leave before acting; 804 states the same thing from the receiving side, that such unasked action is to be wanted and not resented.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:805","b":"k:109","type":"parallel_domain","note":"805 says a hurtful act by a friend is to be understood as folly or as great intimacy (பெருங்கிழமை). 109 says that even an injury like killing is cancelled when one thinks of a single good the person did. Both teach a way of reading an injury so that the bond survives it; 109 does it by remembering a past benefit, 805 by a charitable account of the cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:808","b":"k:187","type":"develops","note":"187 says that those who cannot make friends by pleasant speech will divide even their own people by divisive talk (பகச்சொல்லிக் கேளிர்ப் பிரிப்பர்). 808 describes the friend on whom that does not work, who does not listen to reports of a friend's lapse. 187 gives the talebearer's side and 808 the hearer's refusal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:808","b":"k:510","type":"parallel_domain","note":"808 speaks of those with the strength of intimacy not to listen to talk of a friend's lapse (கேளிழுக்கம் கேளா); Parimelazhagar takes this as not accepting it even when others report it. 510 says that doubting one who has been trusted after examination (தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு) brings endless trouble. Both rule out reopening a settled trust, in friendship and in a king's appointments. The second half of 808 is read in opposite ways by Parimelazhagar (the day of the lapse is a day well spent) and Kalaignar (the days are wasted), so the link rests only on the first half.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:809","b":"k:791","type":"develops","note":"791 says that for those who keep friendship there is no leaving once it is formed (வீடில்லை). 809 says the world wants (விழையும் உலகு) those who do not leave (விடாஅர்) the friendship of friends of unbroken long standing. What 791 gives as a reason for caution before befriending, 809 presents as what earns a person general regard.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:811","b":"k:1266","type":"same_image","note":"In 1266 the waiting wife says that when her husband returns she will drink him in (பருகுவன்) so that her pain ends. 811 uses the same verb for friends who look as though they would drink one up (பருகுவார் போலினும்), which Parimelazhagar glosses as from excess of love. The pair shows 'drinking' as the book's figure for consuming affection, sincere in the wife's mouth and only an appearance in the bad friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:799","type":"qualifies","note":"812 treats the friendship of those who leave when the gain ends (அறின் ஒரூஉம்) as a matter of indifference, gained or lost. 799 says that the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin (கெடுங்காலைக் கைவிடுவார்) burns the heart even when recalled at the hour of death (உள்ளினும் உள்ளஞ் சுடும்). Read together, losing such a friend is no loss, yet having trusted one is not painless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:800","type":"develops","note":"Both verses name the ஒப்பிலார். 812 says it makes no difference whether their friendship is had or lost; 800 goes further and says to leave it even by giving something (ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). Parimelazhagar explains the word differently in each place: here as those who fit with no one because they look only to their own gain, there as those who do not accord with the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:812","b":"k:839","type":"agrees","note":"839 says, with irony, that the friendship of fools is very sweet because parting from them brings no pain (பிரிவின்கண் பீழை தருவதொன்றில்). 812 asks what it matters whether one gains or loses (பெறினும் இழப்பினும் என்) the friendship of those who befriend when there is profit and leave when there is none. Both measure a worthless friendship by the same test: its loss costs nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:813","b":"k:912","type":"develops","note":"813 sets friends who weigh what will come to them (உறுவது சீர்தூக்கும் நட்பு) beside 'those who take what they get' (பெறுவது கொள்வார்), whom Parimelazhagar identifies as courtesans, and thieves. 912 describes those women directly: they weigh the profit and then speak of character (பயன்தூக்கிப் பண்புரைக்கும்). The later chapter spells out the comparison 813 makes in a phrase, with the same verb தூக்கு for the calculating look.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:814","b":"k:788","type":"develops","note":"788 defines friendship by the moment of need: like the hand of a man whose garment slips, it removes distress there and then (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது). 814 pictures the reverse, a horse that gives way in the battle itself (அமரகத்து). Both locate the test of a friend at the instant of crisis, one by the hand that acts unasked and one by the mount that fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:815","b":"k:106","type":"develops","note":"106 says not to give up the friendship of those who were one's strength in sorrow (துன்பத்துள் துப்பாயார் நட்பு). 815 says the friendship of the small, which gives no safety (ஏமம்), is better not obtained at all. The same measure, support in trouble, decides in one verse which friends must be kept and in the other which should never be acquired.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:819","b":"k:799","type":"develops","note":"799 says the friendship of those who abandon one in ruin burns the heart even to think of at the time of death (அடுங்காலை உள்ளினும்). 819 says the bond with those whose words and deeds differ is painful even in a dream (கனவினும் இன்னாது). Each verse extends the harm of a false friend beyond ordinary waking life, to the deathbed in one and to sleep in the other.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:820","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"188 asks what people whose habit is to broadcast even the faults of those close to them (துன்னியார் குற்றமும் தூற்றும்) would do to strangers. 820 warns against the bond with those who are friendly in private and blame one in public. Both take the public exposure of an intimate as the mark of a person no one should be near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:821","b":"k:487","type":"parallel_domain","note":"487 says the bright do not flare up outwardly at once but watch for the time and burn within (காலம்பார்த்து உள்வேர்ப்பர்). 821 warns that those who keep close without inner consent will strike when they see the right place. The patience the book recommends to a ruler in 487 is the same conduct it tells him to expect from a false friend in 821.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:821","b":"k:883","type":"agrees","note":"883 says to fear the enemy within, because at the moment of weakness (உலைவிடத்து) it cuts like the tool that cuts the potter's clay (மட்பகை). 821 says the friendship of those who join without inner accord (நேரா நிரந்தவர்) is an anvil (பட்டடை) for striking once they find the right opening (சீரிடம் காணின்). Both verses use a craftsman's tool for the hidden enemy and both place the blow at the moment that suits him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:882","type":"agrees","note":"882 says not to fear enemies who are like swords but to fear the bond with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு). 822 says the friendship of those who seem one's own and are not (இனம்போன்று இனமல்லார்) will turn. Both identify the enemy in the guise of kin as the dangerous one; 882 gives the instruction and 822 the reason, that such a bond does not stay what it looks like.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:920","type":"same_image","note":"920 lists two-minded women (இருமனப் பெண்டிர்) with liquor and dice as the company of those fortune has left. 822 compares false friendship to women's minds that become other (வேறுபடும்). The divided or shifting female mind serves in both as the book's figure for a tie that cannot be relied on, and 920 suggests which women the figure has in view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:822","b":"k:974","type":"tension","note":"822 uses women's minds as its example of something that changes (மகளிர் மனம்போல வேறுபடும்); Parimelazhagar takes this of women in general and supports it with a line from the Valaiyapathi. 974 takes single-minded women (ஒருமை மகளிர்) as its model of the constancy that greatness needs. On Parimelazhagar's general reading of 822 the two figures conflict; if 822's மகளிர் is limited to the courtesans of chapter 92, they do not.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:825","b":"k:509","type":"develops","note":"509 says to trust no one without examining them and, having examined, to trust what is to be trusted (தேறற்க யாரையும் தேராது). 825 says that those who are not at one with you in mind are not to be judged trustworthy by their words in any matter (சொல்லினால் தேறற்பாற்று அன்று). 825 adds to the rule of examination a statement of what cannot count as evidence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:825","b":"k:890","type":"agrees","note":"890 says that life with those who are not in accord (உடம்பாடு இலாதவர்) is like living with a snake in a hut. 825 says those whose minds are not settled with yours (மனத்தின் அமையாதவர்) are not to be trusted on their words. Both make inner agreement, not outward closeness or speech, the thing that decides whether a person near you is safe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:826","b":"k:709","type":"agrees","note":"709 says the eye itself tells of enmity and friendship (பகைமையும் கேண்மையும் கண்ணுரைக்கும்) to those who know how to read it. 826 says an enemy's words are soon recognised even when they sound like a friend's. Both hold that hostility shows through the surface; 709 makes this depend on a skilled reader, a condition 826 leaves unstated.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:826","b":"k:828","type":"qualifies","note":"826 says the words of the unreconciled are quickly seen through although they say good things as friends do. 828, in the same chapter, says a weapon can lie hidden in hands joined in worship (தொழுதகை யுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்) and in an enemy's tears. 828 limits the confidence of 826: the speech may give itself away, but gestures of submission and grief can still conceal the intent.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:488","type":"develops","note":"488 advises that on meeting an enemy one should bear him, which Parimelazhagar glosses as bowing, until his end is in sight (செறுநரைக் காணின் சுமக்க இறுவரை). 827 says not to accept an enemy's bowing speech (சொல்வணக்கம்), because the bending of a bow signals harm. The humility 488 teaches as a tactic is the humility 827 teaches one to distrust in the other side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:827","b":"k:985","type":"qualifies","note":"985 says that bowing (பணிதல்) is the strength of those who accomplish and the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). 827 says that an enemy's verbal bowing should not be accepted, since it points to harm as a bent bow does. Bowing is a means of working on an adversary in both; 985 praises it in the worthy, and 827 says that from an enemy it must not be taken as goodwill.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:488","type":"agrees","note":"488 says to bear an enemy when you meet him until his final limit comes, and then his head goes down. 829 says to keep the false friend pleased and hold him in the appearance of friendship while it dies inwardly. Both advise outward accommodation of an enemy who cannot yet be opposed openly, in the chapter on timing and in the chapter on false friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:829","b":"k:824","type":"develops","note":"824 says to fear deceivers who smile sweetly with the face while bitter within. 829 says that those who make much of you outwardly and despise you (மிகச்செய்து தம்மெள்ளுவார்) should be made to smile and be embraced within the friendship so that it dies (நகச்செய்து நட்பினுள் சாப்புல்லல்). The response 829 prescribes has the same form as the conduct 824 warns against; Parimelazhagar notes that it is allowed as royal policy although such doubleness is otherwise unfitting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:830","b":"k:488","type":"agrees","note":"488 advises bearing with enemies when one meets them until their end is in view. 830 advises a friendly face toward enemies at a time when, as Parimelazhagar explains, they cannot be openly put aside. Both treat outward civility toward an enemy as a matter of timing and not of feeling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:422","type":"agrees","note":"831 defines folly as taking up what harms and letting gain go (ஏதங்கொண்டு ஊதியம் போக விடல்). 422 defines understanding as the reverse movement: turning away from evil and steering toward good (தீதொரீஇ நன்றின்பால் உய்ப்பது). Read together, the two definitions are mirror images, so folly in 831 is not a lack of information but the wrong choice between two things in view.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:797","type":"same_image","note":"831 says folly is to let gain go (ஊதியம் போக விடல்). 797 uses the same two words to say what gain is: giving up and leaving the friendship of fools (ஊதியம் என்பது ... பேதையார் கேண்மை ஒரீஇ விடல்). The fool lets gain go, and for everyone else letting the fool go is the gain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:831","b":"k:925","type":"parallel_domain","note":"831 says folly is accepting harm and letting gain go. 925 gives a concrete case in drinking: handing over wealth to receive ignorance of one's own body (பொருள்கொடுத்து மெய்யறியாமை கொளல்), which it traces to கையறியாமை, not knowing what to do. The drinker's purchase is 831's exchange carried out literally.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:832","b":"k:439","type":"agrees","note":"832 names as the worst folly a liking (காதன்மை) for what is not fit for one. 439 gives the matching instruction: do not set your desire on a deed that yields no good (நயவற்க நன்றி பயவா வினை). Both locate the error in the wanting, before any deed is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:428","type":"agrees","note":"833 lists four absences as the fool's work: no shame, no inquiry, no tenderness, no care for anything (நாணாமை நாடாமை நாரின்மை யாதொன்றும் பேணாமை பேதை தொழில்). 428 defines folly as not fearing what should be feared and calls fearing it the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில்). Both define folly as a missing response where one is due, and both set it out as a characteristic தொழில்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:865","type":"parallel_domain","note":"833 describes the fool by four negations. 865 describes the ideal enemy to have by a similar string: he does not look to the way, does not do what is fitting, does not look to blame, has no character (வழிநோக்கான் ... பழிநோக்கான் பண்பிலன்), and so is sweet to his foes. The same profile that 833 treats as a moral portrait is, in the chapter on enmity, a strategic weakness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:833","b":"k:1018","type":"develops","note":"833 puts shamelessness (நாணாமை) first among the fool's traits. 1018 says what that trait costs: if one is not ashamed of what makes others ashamed, the fault is one that virtue itself would be ashamed of (அறம் நாணத் தக்கது உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar reads it as virtue leaving him. 1018 supplies the consequence that 833 leaves unstated.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:130","type":"agrees","note":"130 praises the one who guards against anger and, having learned, is restrained (கற்றடங்கல் ஆற்றுவான்): virtue itself seeks him out. 834 names the one who has read, understood and taught but is not restrained (ஓதி உணர்ந்தும் ... தானடங்கா) the greatest of fools. Both treat learning and அடங்கல் as a pair in which the learning is judged by the restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:834","b":"k:373","type":"develops","note":"834 presents a puzzle: a person who has studied, understood and taught, and still does not restrain himself. 373 offers the chapter on fate's account of such a person: however many subtle books one studies, one's own underlying understanding prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்), which Parimelazhagar takes as the folly given by fate. 373 explains how the fool of 834 is possible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:835","b":"k:919","type":"same_image","note":"835 calls the fool's destination an அளறு, a mire or hell, into which he enters and sinks (புக்கழுந்தும்). 919 calls the shoulders of women who draw no line the அளறு in which base men sink (பூரியர்கள் ஆழும் அளறு). 919 uses for one present indulgence the image that 835 uses for the result of a whole life of folly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:836","b":"k:468","type":"agrees","note":"836 says that when a fool who does not know the way of doing (கையறியாப் பேதை) takes up a task, the task fails (பொய்படும்) and he himself ends in fetters. 468 says effort not made by the right means will collapse (பொத்துப் படும்) even with many standing by to protect it. Parimelazhagar glosses both verbs as புரைபடும்; 468 states the rule about method and 836 adds the cost to the doer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:836","b":"k:925","type":"same_image","note":"836 describes the fool as கையறியா, not knowing how to act. 925 uses the same compound, கையறியாமை, as the cause of paying money to lose awareness of oneself in drink. In both verses the word names a practical ignorance that ends in the person binding or losing himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:837","b":"k:1000","type":"agrees","note":"837 speaks of great wealth that has come to a fool (பெருஞ்செல்வம் உற்றக்கடை) and benefits the wrong people. 1000 says great wealth obtained by one without character (பண்பிலான் பெற்ற பெருஞ்செல்வம்) is like good milk spoiled by its vessel. Parimelazhagar notes on both verses that the verb marks wealth received and not made; both hold that the holder's defect ruins what the wealth could do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:837","b":"k:1009","type":"parallel_domain","note":"837 says a fool's great wealth feeds strangers (ஏதிலார்) while his own go hungry. 1009 says wealth amassed by giving up love, denying oneself and ignoring virtue will be taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The miser and the fool arrive at the same result by opposite routes, one by holding wealth back and one by letting it go to the wrong people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:838","b":"k:408","type":"agrees","note":"838 likens a fool's possession to drunkenness added to derangement. 408 says fortune lodged with the unlearned is more painful than poverty lodged with the good (கல்லார்கண் பட்ட திரு). Both deny that wealth is a good independent of its holder; 838 gives the image and 408 the comparative judgment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:840","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"840 treats the fool's mere entry (புகல்) into the company of the worthy as a defilement; Parimelazhagar adds that the assembly itself is lowered by it. 403 allows that even the unlearned are very good if they can stay silent before the learned (கற்றார்முன் சொல்லாதிருக்கப் பெறின்). 403 leaves a condition under which presence is acceptable, though its subject is the unlearned and 840's is the fool.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:840","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"840 likens a fool entering an assembly of the worthy to setting an unwashed foot on a bed (கழாஅக்கால் பள்ளியுள் வைத்தற்று). 720 likens speaking before those not of one's own kind to nectar spilled in an unclean yard (அங்கணத்துள் உக்க அமிழ்து). The two are mirror images of the same mismatch: the unclean thing brought into the clean place, and the precious thing let fall in the unclean place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:842","b":"k:70","type":"parallel_domain","note":"842 says that when a person without understanding gives gladly, the cause is nothing but the receiver's தவம். 70 says a son's service to his father is to make people ask what the father did as penance (என்நோற்றான் கொல்) to have him. Both use the same figure, explaining a good by the stored merit of the one who benefits; 70 uses it as praise of the son and 842 to deny the giver any credit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:842","b":"k:92","type":"qualifies","note":"92 treats giving with a glad heart (அகனமர்ந்து ஈதல்) as a real good, surpassed only by a glad face and sweet speech. 842 takes the same act, giving with a glad heart (நெஞ்சுவந்து ஈதல்), and says that in a person without understanding it reflects only the receiver's merit. 842 limits 92's valuation: the glad gift counts for the giver only when understanding is behind it, which is how Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both read it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:165","type":"parallel_domain","note":"843 says the suffering those without understanding inflict on themselves is hard even for their enemies to inflict (செறுவார்க்கும் செய்தல் அரிது). 165 says that for the envious, envy is enough: it brings ruin even when enemies fail to (ஒன்னார் வழுக்கியும் கேடீன்பது). Both measure an inner defect against an outside enemy and find the defect the more effective.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:434","type":"agrees","note":"843 says the fool harms himself beyond what an enemy could. 434 says one's own fault is the enemy that brings ruin (குற்றமே அற்றந் தரூஉம் பகை) and is therefore the thing to guard against. 434 gives the general principle of which 843 is the case of the person who cannot do the guarding.","grade":"sound","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:843","b":"k:847","type":"develops","note":"843 states generally that those without understanding torment themselves (தாந்தம்மைப் பீழிக்கும் பீழை). 847, in the same chapter, names one way it happens: the person without understanding lets the precious மறை slip and so does great harm to himself by himself (பெருமிறை தானே தனக்கு). 847 is a specific instance of 843's claim, with the same stress on தானே.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:844","b":"k:404","type":"develops","note":"844 says the claim to ஒண்மை is itself what வெண்மை is. 404 says that however good an unlearned person's ஒட்பம் may be, those with understanding do not accept it (கொள்ளார் அறிவுடையார்). 404 shows the other side of 844's self-assessment: the brightness one credits to oneself is not credited by those able to judge.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:844","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"844 locates want of understanding in the pride (செருக்கு) of saying 'we are bright'. 978 says smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து) while greatness bows. Both make self-admiration the identifying mark of the deficient state, one for understanding and one for stature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:128","type":"parallel_domain","note":"845 says one false claim to learning casts doubt on what one has mastered without flaw. 128 says that if even one bad word has its effect, the good comes to nothing (நன்றாகாதாகி விடும்). Both describe a single lapse spreading backward over what was sound, in knowledge and in speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:391","type":"same_image","note":"391 tells one to learn what is to be learned without flaw (கற்க கசடற) and then stand by it. 845 uses the same phrase for what a person has truly mastered (கசடற வல்லதூஉம்) and says that carrying on as if one knew what one has not learned (கல்லாத மேற்கொண்டொழுகல்) brings doubt even on that. Pretence undoes the standing of the very learning 391 asks for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:845","b":"k:405","type":"develops","note":"405 says an unlearned person's standing gives way once he meets others and speaks (சொல்லாடச் சோர்வு படும்). 845 describes a further loss for one who has some real learning: by taking on what he has not learned, he puts even his genuine mastery in doubt (ஐயம் தரும்). 405 is the exposure of a claim; 845 is the damage the exposure does to what was true.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:278","type":"parallel_domain","note":"846 asks whether covering one's nakedness is not itself small-minded when one's faults are left uncovered (தம்வயின் குற்றம் மறையா வழி); Parimelazhagar takes covering faults to mean removing them. 278 speaks of many who, with stain in the mind, bathe and go about concealed (மனத்தது மாசாக ... நீராடி மறைந்தொழுகு). Both set a bodily propriety, clothing or bathing, against an inner fault it does nothing about.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:846","b":"k:1012","type":"develops","note":"846 treats clothing the body as trivial beside dealing with one's faults. 1012 says food, clothing and the rest are no different for any living being, and the distinction of humans is having shame (ஊணுடை எச்சம் உயிர்க்கெல்லாம் வேறல்ல நாணுடைமை மாந்தர் சிறப்பு). 1012 gives the reason behind 846: dress is common to all, and what marks a person is shame at fault.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:847","b":"k:418","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 847 as a person who receives rare instruction and lets it run out without taking it in, harming himself. 418 says ears not pierced by listening are deaf even though they hear (கேட்பினுங் கேளாத் தகையவே). On that reading both describe teaching that reaches a person and does not enter; 847 adds that the loss is self-inflicted.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:847","b":"k:1076","type":"parallel_domain","note":"847 says a person without understanding lets the precious மறை slip (அருமறை சோரும்) and so harms himself. Manakkudavar takes மறை as a secret let out to others, a reading Parimelazhagar reports and rejects. On that reading the verse meets 1076, where the base are like a beaten drum because they carry every மறை they hear to others; 1076 notes the harm to others, 847 the harm to the leaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:448","type":"develops","note":"448 says a king without people who rebuke him will perish even with no one to ruin him (கெடுப்பாரிலானுங் கெடும்). 848 takes the case one step further: a person who has people to direct him and still does not act on it, and cannot work it out alone. 448 makes having correctors the safeguard; 848 shows the safeguard is useless to one who will not be corrected.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"848 calls the life of one who neither heeds nor understands a disease until it leaves (போஒம் அளவுமோர் நோய்); Parimelazhagar takes this as a burden hard for the earth to bear, while Kalaignar takes the disease as the person's own. 572 says the existence of those without kindly regard is a weight on the earth (உண்மை நிலக்குப் பொறை). On Parimelazhagar's reading the two share the figure of a person whose mere being alive is an affliction to the world.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:848","b":"k:638","type":"parallel_domain","note":"848 describes one who does not act when told and does not see for himself (ஏவவும் செய்கலான் தான் தேறான்), and calls that life a disease until it departs. 638 describes a king in the same two terms, one who destroys what the knowing say and does not know himself (அறிகொன்று அறியான்), and says the minister must still tell him what is sound. The same double failing appears in both; 848 gives the verdict on the person and 638 the duty of those beside him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:849","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"849 ends with the unseeing person continuing to see as he saw (தான்கண்டவாறு). 373 says that however many subtle books one studies, one's own underlying understanding prevails (தன் உண்மையறிவே மிகும்). Both hold that what is brought from outside, a teacher in 849 and books in 373, does not displace the understanding a person already has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:849","b":"k:929","type":"parallel_domain","note":"849 says one who tries to show something to a person who does not see ends up the unseeing one, while that person goes on having seen it his own way (கண்டானாம் தான்கண்டவாறு). 929 says giving reasons to a drunk man is like searching with a flame for someone sunk under water (கீழ்நீர்க் குளித்தானைத் தீத்துரீஇ அற்று). Both describe instruction that cannot reach its target, and in both the effort reflects on the one making it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:850","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"850 condemns the one who denies what the world affirms. 140 says those who have not learned to conduct themselves in accord with the world (உலகத்தோடு ஒட்ட ஒழுகல்) lack understanding however much they have studied. Parimelazhagar takes 'the world' in both as the high-minded many, so both make their consensus a test that private cleverness does not override.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:851","b":"k:187","type":"same_image","note":"851 names the fault as பகல், splitting apart, and its chapter closes (860) by setting நகல் against இகல். 187 uses the same pair of roots: those who do not know how to make friends by நகச்சொல்லி will பகச்சொல்லி and separate even their kin. The chapter on backbiting already holds the contrast between dividing and laughing together that the chapter on hostility is built on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:851","b":"k:886","type":"develops","note":"851 defines இகல் as the disease (நோய்) that spreads பகல், the ill nature of division, among all living beings. 886 takes the same division to its end inside one's own circle: if ஒன்றாமை arises among ஒன்றியார், escaping death (பொன்றாமை) is hard at any time. The general diagnosis of 851 becomes a specific warning about a ruler's own people; Parimelazhagar reads 886 of a king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:852","b":"k:203","type":"agrees","note":"852 ranks not harming a provoker as தலை, the best course. 203 uses the same ranking word: the head of all understanding (அறிவினுள் எல்லாம் தலை) is not to do evil even to those who hate you (செறுவார்க்கும்). Together they show the text treating non-retaliation as a matter of good sense as well as of virtue; Parimelazhagar on 852 explains that retaliating feeds the enmity while refraining lets it die out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:853","b":"k:156","type":"agrees","note":"853 says that removing the painful disease of hostility gives a brightness that neither fails nor is flawed (தவலில்லாத் தாவில் விளக்கம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as lasting fame. 156 says those who punish have one day's pleasure while those who forbear have praise until the end (பொன்றுந் துணையும் புகழ்). Both make enduring renown the result of declining the quarrel, one in the householder's ethics and one in public life.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:854","b":"k:629","type":"qualifies","note":"854 offers இன்பத்துள் இன்பம், the joy of joys, as what follows when hostility goes. 629 uses the same two phrases differently: one who does not crave pleasure within pleasure (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்) does not feel pain within pain (துன்பத்துள் துன்பம் உறுதல் இலன்). Read together, the joy 854 promises is a result of removing a cause of suffering, and 629 warns against making such joy an object of desire.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"855 says no one sets out to defeat a person who can lean away from hostility. 874 goes a step further: the world rests in the greatness of the person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship (பகை நட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும்). 855 describes safety gained by not answering hostility; 874 describes actively turning the enemy into a friend and gives it a public value.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:985","type":"agrees","note":"855 asks who would be inclined to overcome those able to lean away when hostility confronts them (இகலெதிர் சாய்ந்தொழுக வல்லாரை). 985 says the strength of the capable is to bow (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Both treat yielding as what disarms an opponent; 985 states it as a trait of the சான்றோர், 855 as a skill (வல்லார்), which Parimelazhagar says is hard for kings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:855","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"855 holds that the one who gives way before hostility cannot be beaten. In 1327 the man says that in a lovers' sulk the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as the reunion shows. The speaker in 1327 is a lover talking about play-quarrels, so this is the same pattern of winning by yielding in a different setting, not a doctrine shared with 855.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:856","b":"k:307","type":"parallel_domain","note":"856 says the life of one who declares it sweet to excel in hostility (இகலின் மிகல் இனிது என்பவன்) is close to failure and ruin. 307 says the ruin of one who takes anger to be a thing of worth (சினத்தைப் பொருளென்று கொண்டவன்) is as sure as the hand of one who strikes the ground. In both the fault is the valuing of the passion, and both attach certain or imminent ruin to it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:857","b":"k:423","type":"develops","note":"423 defines understanding as seeing the true thing (மெய்ப்பொருள் காண்பது அறிவு) in whatever one hears from anyone. 857 says those of harmful understanding who are attached to hostility (இகல்மேவல் இன்னா அறிவினவர்) do not see the மெய்ப்பொருள் that carries victory. So 857 names hostility as one thing that turns அறிவு against its defining task; Parimelazhagar narrows மெய்ப்பொருள் here to the content of the treatises on statecraft and says hostility clouds the understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:180","type":"same_image","note":"860 says all ills come from hostility, and from friendly gladness (நகல்) comes 'the pride called good conduct' (நன்னயம் என்னும் செருக்கு). 180 has the same shape and the same closing phrase: coveting without thought yields destruction, and 'the pride called not-wanting' (வேண்டாமை என்னும் செருக்கு) yields victory. In both the verse sets a ruinous impulse against its absence and calls that absence a செருக்கு, which Parimelazhagar glosses in both places as wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:303","type":"agrees","note":"860 says இகலான் ஆம் இன்னாத எல்லாம்: every painful thing arises from hostility. 303 says to forget anger toward anyone, because the birth of evils comes from it (தீய பிறத்தல் அதனான் வரும்). The Book of Virtue makes anger the source of evils and the Book of Wealth makes hostility the source, with nearly the same construction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:860","b":"k:999","type":"develops","note":"860 credits நகல், glad friendliness, with producing good conduct and its dignity. 999 describes those without the capacity: for people not able to laugh with others (நகல் வல்லர் அல்லார்) the wide world lies in darkness even by day. 860 gives what நகல் yields and 999 what its absence costs; Parimelazhagar reads நகல் in both as rejoicing in company with others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:862","b":"k:449","type":"agrees","note":"862 asks how a man with no love, no worthy allies (ஆன்ற துணையிலன்) and no strength of his own can wear down an enemy's might. 449 states the principle: those without capital have no profit, and those without the prop of supporters (மதலையாஞ் சார்பிலார்) have no standing. 862 applies to a contest with an enemy what 449 says of a ruler's position in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:862","b":"k:873","type":"develops","note":"862 describes the man without love, allies or strength as unable to resist an enemy. 873 judges the man in that position who nevertheless provokes: one who, alone (தமியனாய்), takes on the enmity of many is more foolish than the mad. 862 gives the weak position seen by an attacker; 873 gives the advice that follows for the one who is in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:863","b":"k:534","type":"agrees","note":"863 puts fear first among the traits that make a man easy for his enemy. 534 says the fearful have no fortress (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை); Parimelazhagar explains that defences are of no use to one with fear in his mind. Both say that fear cancels whatever other protection a person has.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:863","b":"k:869","type":"develops","note":"863 lists fear and ignorance among the weaknesses that make a man easy prey (அஞ்சும் அறியான்). 869 keeps just those two and states them from the other side: joy does not go far from those who fight if they get enemies who are without understanding and afraid (அறிவிலா அஞ்சும் பகைவர்ப் பெறின்). The chapter moves from describing the weak enemy to stating what the attacker gains from him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:305","type":"agrees","note":"864 says one who does not leave off anger (நீங்கான் வெகுளி) and has no நிறை is easy for anyone, at any time, anywhere. 305 says that if you would guard yourself, guard against anger, because unguarded anger kills oneself (தன்னையே கொல்லுஞ் சினம்). 305 states the self-destruction as an ethical fact; 864 shows how it works in a contest, by making the angry man open to any opponent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:864","b":"k:526","type":"develops","note":"864 says the man who cannot leave anger is easy for anyone to overcome. 526 gives the opposite case: no one on earth has more people at his side than one who gives greatly and does not cherish anger (பேணான் வெகுளி). Put together, anger decides whether a man stands with many around him or exposed to all.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:866","b":"k:360","type":"parallel_domain","note":"866 says the enmity of a man of blind anger and excessive lust (காணாச் சினத்தான் கழிபெருங் காமத்தான்) is to be welcomed. 360 names the same two, with delusion, as what must perish for suffering to perish (காமம் வெகுளி மயக்கம்). What the chapter on true knowledge treats as obstacles to release, the chapter on enemies treats as what makes an opponent beatable; Parimelazhagar says such men destroy themselves.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:867","b":"k:800","type":"parallel_domain","note":"867 advises paying to have a certain man as an enemy rather than keep him near. 800 advises paying to be rid of a certain friend: give something (ஒன்று ஈத்தும்) and leave the friendship of those who are not a match. Both accept a cost to end a harmful closeness; the commentators differ on who the man in 867 is, Parimelazhagar taking him as one who begins a task and acts unsuitably, Kalaignar as one who stays beside you.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:868","b":"k:504","type":"develops","note":"504 tells the ruler to examine a man's qualities, then his faults, and judge by what predominates (மிகை நாடி மிக்க கொளல்). 868 describes the result when the tally is all on one side: with no qualities and many faults (குணனிலனாய்க் குற்றம் பலவாயின்) he is left without companions, and that is a support to his opponents. The test used for choosing servants in 504 is applied by 868 to the ruler himself, with his enemies as the ones who benefit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:868","b":"k:525","type":"develops","note":"868 says a man without qualities and with many faults comes to have no one with him (இனனிலனாம்). 525 gives the positive case: one who can give and speak pleasantly will be surrounded by kin in rows (அடுக்கிய சுற்றத்தால் சுற்றப்படும்). Both make a person's following a product of his character; 868 adds the military consequence of losing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:869","b":"k:772","type":"tension","note":"869 says joy does not go far from those who fight if they get enemies who are ignorant and afraid. 772 says holding the spear that missed an elephant is sweeter (இனிது) than holding the arrow that hit a forest hare. One locates the pleasure of fighting in the easy opponent and the other in the hard one; the conflict holds if both are read as claims about what a fighter should want, and eases if 869 is a king's reckoning of advantage and 772 an individual warrior's sense of honour.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:871","b":"k:867","type":"tension","note":"871 says enmity, a thing without good in it (பண்பிலதனை), is not to be wanted even in jest (நகையேயும் வேண்டற்பாற்று அன்று). 867 says the enmity of one who stays close and does unfitting things must certainly be got, even by paying for it (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற). The conflict holds if 871 is read as a rule about all enmity; Parimelazhagar narrows it to enmity that brings no benefit, which would leave room for 867.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:872","b":"k:129","type":"parallel_domain","note":"872 says that even if you take on the enmity of those whose plough is the bow (வில்லேர் உழவர்), you should not take on that of those whose plough is the word (சொல்லேர் உழவர்). 129 says a wound burned by fire heals within, but the scar burned by the tongue (நாவினாற் சுட்ட வடு) does not. Both rank harm done by words above harm done by a physical instrument, one in statecraft and one in personal conduct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:873","b":"k:875","type":"develops","note":"873 states the folly of standing alone (தமியனாய்) against many enemies. 875 gives the working rule for the smallest such case: with no ally and two enemies (தன்துணை இன்றால் பகையிரண்டால்), make one of them a pleasing ally (இன்துணையாக் கொள்க). 875 turns 873's condemnation into a remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:985","type":"develops","note":"874 praises the one who takes enmity as friendship (பகைநட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும்) without saying how it is done. 985 names a means: bowing (பணிதல்) is the weapon by which the worthy change their opponents (மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). Parimelazhagar glosses this as ending the opponents' enmity, so 985 reads as the method behind 874.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:874","b":"k:996","type":"agrees","note":"874 says the world rests in the greatness of the person of character who conducts himself taking enmity as friendship (பண்புடையாளன் தகைமைக்கண் தங்கிற்று உலகு). 996 says the world exists by resting on those of character (பண்புடையார்ப் பட்டுண்டு உலகம்), and would otherwise sink into the earth. Both make பண்பு the thing the world stands on; 874, in the chapter on enmity, specifies it as the capacity to turn enemies into friends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:875","b":"k:633","type":"parallel_domain","note":"875 advises a ruler caught alone between two enemies to make one of them an ally. 633 lists among a minister's abilities dividing (பிரித்தல்), which Parimelazhagar explains as separating the enemy's supporters from him, and rejoining those who broke away (பிரிந்தார்ப் பொருத்தல்). The advice to the king in 875 is the skill required of the minister in 633.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:876","b":"k:691","type":"parallel_domain","note":"876 says that whether or not one has trusted the enemy before, in a time of ruin (அழிவின்கண்) one should neither join him nor break from him (தேறான் பகாஅன் விடல்); Parimelazhagar explains that joining lets him destroy from inside and breaking makes him add to the ruin. 691 tells those who serve quarrelsome kings to be like people warming at a fire, neither moving off nor coming close (அகலாது அணுகாது). Both prescribe a deliberate middle distance from a dangerous party, once toward an enemy and once toward one's own king.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:877","b":"k:440","type":"parallel_domain","note":"877 says not to let softness show before enemies (மேவற்க மென்மை பகைவர் அகத்து); Parimelazhagar takes மென்மை as lack of strength, which enemies watch for. 440 says that if a ruler can enjoy what he loves without that love being known (காதல் அறியாமை), his enemies' designs come to nothing (ஏதில ஏதிலார் நூல்). In both, what an enemy can use is what he is allowed to learn about one's inner state, weakness in one verse and desire in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:879","b":"k:550","type":"same_image","note":"879 speaks of those who remove the thorn tree with the word களையுநர், weeders. 550 compares a king's putting the cruel to death to weeding the green crop (பைங்கூழ் களைகட்டதனொடு நேர்). Both picture the removal of dangerous people as a cultivator's weeding; 879 adds that timing matters, since the weed grown hard injures the weeder's hand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:880","b":"k:759","type":"develops","note":"880 states the cost of not breaking the pride of one's enemies (செயிர்ப்பவர் செம்மல் சிதைக்கலாதார்): one cannot be counted as living. 759 names the instrument for that task: wealth, the sharpest steel for cutting the pride of foes (செறுநர் செருக்கறுக்கும் எஃகு). One verse gives the necessity and the other the means.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:881","b":"k:522","type":"qualifies","note":"881 says the nature of one's own people is bad if it does harm (தமர்நீரும் இன்னாவாம் இன்னா செயின்). 522 says kin whose affection does not lapse (விருப்பறாச் சுற்றம்) yield many gains that keep branching; Parimelazhagar notes that this wording is chosen to exclude inner enmity. The condition written into 522 is the case 881 describes, so each verse marks the limit of the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:881","b":"k:1309","type":"same_image","note":"881 says even shade and water are bad when they do harm (நிழல்நீரும் இன்னாத இன்னா), and so is the nature of one's own people when it does harm. In 1309 the man says water is sweet in the shade (நீரும் நிழலது இனிதே), and sulking is sweet only with those who love. Both take shade and water as the standard of what is pleasant and then make the pleasantness depend on a condition, which in both cases is the real disposition of those closest to one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:882","b":"k:824","type":"agrees","note":"882 uses the verb of fear for kin-like enemies (அஞ்சுக கேள்போல் பகைவர் தொடர்பு). 824 uses it for deceivers who smile sweetly with the face and are bitter within (முகத்தின் இனிய நகாஅ அகத்தின்னா வஞ்சரை அஞ்சப்படும்). The chapter on false friendship and the chapter on inner enmity reach the same instruction: what is to be feared is hostility presented as closeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:882","b":"k:828","type":"same_image","note":"882 contrasts the enemy who is like a drawn sword (வாள்போல் பகைவர்) with the one who looks like kin, and fears the second. 828 says a weapon hides even within an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகையுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and his tears are the same. In 882 the visible sword is the safer one; 828 shows where the other sword is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:883","b":"k:305","type":"parallel_domain","note":"883 says to fear the enemy within and guard oneself (உட்பகை அஞ்சித் தற்காக்க), or it will destroy one. 305 says that if you would guard yourself, guard against anger (தன்னைத்தான் காக்கின் சினங்காக்க); unguarded, anger kills oneself. The same formula of self-guarding against something lodged inside is applied in one verse to people within the household or court and in the other to a passion within the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:885","b":"k:735","type":"parallel_domain","note":"885 says an inner enmity that comes in the form of kinship (உறல்முறையான் உட்பகை) brings many ills of a deadly kind (இறல்முறையான் ஏதம்) to the ruler. 735 defines a land as one without many factions, without ruinous inner enmity (பாழ்செய்யும் உட்பகை) and without murderous marauders who harass the king. The danger 885 describes for the king's person and household is, in 735, something whose absence is part of what makes a country a country.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:886","b":"k:764","type":"agrees","note":"886 says that if disunity arises among those who are one with a ruler (ஒன்றாமை ஒன்றியார் கட்படின்), it is always hard for him to escape perishing. 764 defines an army as one that is not destroyed, cannot be undermined from within (அறைபோகாதாகி) and has inherited hardness. Both treat the loyalty of one's own side as a condition of survival, stated once as a warning and once as a requirement.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:887","b":"k:277","type":"parallel_domain","note":"887 uses the casket and its lid for a family that fits together outwardly while divided within. 277 uses the kunri seed for false ascetics, red on the outside (புறங்குன்றி கண்டனையர்) and black within like its tip. Each verse chooses an everyday object to show an outside that does not match the inside, one for kin and one for renouncers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:887","b":"k:786","type":"parallel_domain","note":"887 says a family with inner enmity is not joined, though it joins like a casket and its lid (செப்பின் புணர்ச்சிபோல் கூடினும் கூடாதே). 786 says friendship that makes only the face smile is not friendship; friendship is what makes the heart within smile (நெஞ்சத்து அகநக). Both deny the name of union to a joining that is only on the surface, in the family and in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:433","type":"agrees","note":"889 holds that inner enmity is ruinous even at the size of a split sesame seed. 433 says those who are ashamed of blame take a fault the size of a millet seed (தினைத்துணை) to be as large as a palmyra. 433 describes the attitude that 889 calls for: treating the small thing at its eventual size.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:879","type":"agrees","note":"889 says inner enmity carries ruin even when it is as small as a split sesame seed. 879 says to cut a thorn tree while it is young (இளைதாக), since grown hard it destroys the cutter's hand. The two neighbouring chapters, on open and on inner enmity, make the same point that hostility should be judged by what it will grow into; only 879 goes on to say what to do about it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:889","b":"k:965","type":"parallel_domain","note":"889 says that inner enmity, though as small as a split sesame seed (எட்பகவன்ன சிறுமைத்தே ஆயினும்), has ruin in it. 965 says even those like a hill will be lowered if they do a lowering thing the size of a kunri seed (குன்றி அனைய செயின்). Both use a tiny seed as the measure of something that brings down what is large, a family or kingdom in 889 and a person's standing in 965.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:891","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"891 makes not slighting the able the chief safeguard; 861 tells the ruler to avoid standing against the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக). The chapter on enmity gives as a tactical rule what 891 gives as a rule of self-protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:892","b":"k:450","type":"agrees","note":"892 says disregard of the great brings unending trouble (பேரா இடும்பை); 450 says letting go the tie with the good (நல்லார் தொடர் கைவிடல்) is ten times worse than taking on the enmity of many. Both chapters on the great rank a bad relation to them above ordinary dangers, 450 by a number and 892 by permanence.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:892","b":"k:976","type":"develops","note":"892 warns that going on without cherishing the great (பெரியாரைப் பேணாது ஒழுகின்) brings, through them, ills that do not leave. 976 says the intent to cherish and win the great (பெரியாரைப் பேணிக் கொள்வேம் என்னும் நோக்கு) is simply absent from the minds of the small. The shared verb பேணு ties them: 976 names who it is that fails to cherish, 892 what the failure costs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:893","b":"k:861","type":"agrees","note":"893 states as irony what 861 states as a plain command: offending those with the power to destroy (அடல்வேண்டின் ஆற்றுபவர்) is a way to seek one's own ruin, and 861 says to avoid opposing the stronger (வலியார்க்கு மாறேற்றல் ஓம்புக).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:893","b":"k:922","type":"parallel_domain","note":"893 says: if you want ruin (கெடல்வேண்டின்), go ahead without listening and wrong those able to destroy. 922 says: do not drink, but if anyone will, let it be those who do not want the regard of the worthy (எண்ணப்பட வேண்டாதார்). Both forbid by an ironic permission addressed to whoever wants the bad result, once about offending the powerful and once about liquor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:894","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"894 says the unable doing harm to the able (ஆற்றுவார்க்கு ஆற்றாதார் இன்னா செயல்) is like beckoning Death with the hand. 250 asks one about to go against someone weaker (மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து) to think of oneself before the stronger (வலியார்முன்). Both reason from the weak facing the strong: 894 uses it as prudence in the Book of Wealth, 250 turns the same experience into a ground for compassion in the Book of Virtue.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:895","b":"k:691","type":"develops","note":"895 says those who draw the anger of a king of fierce might (வெந்துப்பின் வேந்து செறப்பட்டவர்) cannot go on existing anywhere they go. 691 gives the working rule for those who serve such kings: like people warming at a fire, neither move off nor come close (அகலாது அணுகாது தீக்காய்வார் போல்க). 895 states the danger, and 691 the conduct that lives beside it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:895","b":"k:899","type":"develops","note":"895 makes the king's anger inescapable for those under him; 899 says that if those of exalted resolve flare up (ஏந்திய கொள்கையார் சீறின்), the king himself (வேந்தனும்) breaks midway and loses his kingship. Within the chapter the pair sets up an order of power: the king who destroys others can himself be destroyed by the anger of the great. Parimelazhagar takes வேந்தன் in 899 as Indra.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:896","b":"k:207","type":"tension","note":"207 says those who incur any enmity whatever survive it (எனைப்பகை உற்றாரும் உய்வர்), and only the enmity of one's own evil deeds follows and kills. 896 says those who keep offending the great do not survive (உய்யார்). If எனைப்பகை is taken literally as every human enmity, the two conflict; the conflict eases if 207's phrase is read as a rhetorical foil for the enmity of deeds, or if the harm in 896 is understood as itself the fruit of a wrong deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:897","b":"k:444","type":"qualifies","note":"444 says living so that those greater than oneself become one's own is the head of all strengths (வன்மையுள் எல்லாம் தலை). 897 gives the other side: a fully appointed life and great wealth count for nothing (என்னாம்) once the worthy great are angered. Together they make one's standing with the great, and not one's possessions, the deciding strength.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:897","b":"k:900","type":"agrees","note":"897 asks what a life excellent in every part and lofty wealth (வகைமாண்ட வாழ்க்கையும் வான்பொருளும்) come to if the worthy of great stature are angered (தக்கார் செறின்). 900 says even those with support beyond measure (இறந்தமைந்த சார்பு) do not survive if those of the highest distinction are angered (சீரார் செறின்). The two close on the same word செறின் and between them cancel a person's own assets and then his outside supports.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:898","b":"k:965","type":"same_image","note":"898 plays on குன்று (hill) and குன்ற (to diminish): if the hill-like intend diminishment, the seemingly permanent vanish. 965 uses the same play: even the hill-like (குன்றின் அனையாரும்) will diminish (குன்றுவர்) if they do a lowering thing as small as a kunri seed. In 898 the hill-like bring others low; in 965 they themselves can be brought low by their own act, so height gives power over others but no protection from one's own lapse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:900","b":"k:29","type":"agrees","note":"900 says that even those with support beyond measure will not survive (உய்யார்) if those of the highest distinction are angered (சிறந்தமைந்த சீரார் செறின்). 29 says the anger (வெகுளி) of those standing on the hill of good qualities is hard to ward off (காத்தல் அரிது) even for a moment. The Book of Wealth applies to kings and their resources what the opening chapters said in praise of renouncers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:900","b":"k:303","type":"tension","note":"303 says to forget anger toward anyone at all (யார்மாட்டும்), because every evil is born from it. 900 treats the anger of the most distinguished (சீரார் செறின்) as a force no support can save one from, and Parimelazhagar and Pope take these as people of great austerity. If they are ascetics, the chapter on not being angry would have them without anger; the conflict depends on that identification and on reading 900 as accepting such anger, where it may only be warning the offender of what follows.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:901","b":"k:45","type":"qualifies","note":"45 says that when household life has love and virtue (அன்பும் அறனும்), these are its character and its fruit (பண்பும் பயனும்); Parimelazhagar takes the love as that owed to one's wife. 901 says those who fix their desire on the wife (மனைவிழைவார்) miss the excellent fruit (மாண்பயன்). The pair marks a line between love within the household, which is its fruit, and விழைவு that lets the wife rule the man, which loses it; Parimelazhagar reads 901 as men who follow the wife's lead for pleasure's sake.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:903","b":"k:55","type":"qualifies","note":"903 calls a man's stooping before his wife (இல்லாள்கண் தாழ்ந்த) இயல்பின்மை, something against the natural order, which shames him among the good. 55 praises the wife who rises worshipping her husband (கொழுநன் தொழுதெழுவாள்). The two show that the book fixes the direction of deference in marriage: it is a merit in the wife and a disgrace in the husband.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:903","b":"k:978","type":"qualifies","note":"978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை), and elsewhere the book calls bowing good for everyone. 903 names one bowing that brings shame: lowering oneself before one's wife (தாழ்ந்த இயல்பின்மை), which Parimelazhagar explains as arising from fear. The pair separates humility that comes from the absence of pride from submission that comes from fear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:904","b":"k:665","type":"same_image","note":"904 denies வீறு (distinction) to the action of the wife-fearing man: வினையாண்மை வீறெய்தல் இன்று. 665 says the firmness in action (வினைத்திட்பம்) of those who have attained distinction (வீறெய்தி மாண்டார்) reaches the king and is held in regard. The same phrase வீறு எய்து names what capable action normally earns and what, in 904, fear of the wife withholds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:904","b":"k:1026","type":"develops","note":"904 says the man who fears his wife gets no distinction from his command of action (வினையாண்மை வீறெய்தல் இன்று). 1026 defines good manliness (நல்லாண்மை) as making the mastery of one's house one's own (இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்), which Parimelazhagar takes as leading one's family. Both build on ஆண்மை as mastery: the man of 904 has given up mastery at home, and his mastery of work earns no honour.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:905","b":"k:908","type":"agrees","note":"905 says the man who fears his wife fears to do good to the good; 908 says those who act as she wishes (பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்) neither meet their friends' needs nor do good (நன்றாற்றார்). Both state the cost to others: the wife-ruled man fails the good in 905 and his friends in 908.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:906","b":"k:1088","type":"parallel_domain","note":"906 says that even if they live like the gods (இமையாரின் வாழினும்), men who fear their wife's bamboo-like shoulders (அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சுபவர்) have no dignity (பாடிலர்). In 1088 a lover exclaims that his might (பீடு), feared by enemies in battle, has broken before her bright forehead alone. Both show male standing giving way before a woman's bodily feature; the Book of Wealth counts it a disgrace, while in the Book of Love it is a lover's speech in the moment and carries no judgment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1011","type":"qualifies","note":"1011 ranks shame at one's deeds (கருமத்தால் நாணுதல்) as true shame and sets aside the shame of fair-browed women (திருநுதல் நல்லவர் நாண்) as another kind. 907 says a woman who has shame (நாணுடைப் பெண்) has more greatness than the manhood of one who lives doing a woman's bidding. So the woman's modesty that 1011 treats as the lesser kind still outranks, in 907, manhood that has given up command.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"907 pairs நாண் and ஆண்மை: manhood that obeys a woman is worth less than womanhood with shame. In 1134 a lover says the fierce flood of desire carries off the raft made of shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). Both treat shame and manliness as the two things a man loses together under a woman's hold; 907 states it as a judgment, 1134 as a lover's own complaint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:907","b":"k:1137","type":"agrees","note":"907 gives greatness to the woman with shame over the submissive man's manhood (பெண்ணே பெருமை உடைத்து). 1137, a speech in the Book of Love, says nothing has more worth than womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the palmyra horse (மடலேறாப் பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்கது இல்). In both, a woman's restraint is measured against a man's lack of it and ranked higher.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:908","b":"k:141","type":"same_image","note":"908 describes men who live as the woman wishes, பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்; 141 speaks of the folly of living in desire for another man's woman, பெட்டொழுகும் பேதைமை. The same pairing of பெட்டு (desire, wish) with ஒழுகு (to conduct oneself) describes a life steered by desire in both, in 141 the man's own desire for a forbidden woman and in 908 the wishes of his own wife.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:908","b":"k:788","type":"qualifies","note":"788 defines friendship as removing a friend's distress on the spot (ஆங்கே இடுக்கண் களைவது), like the hand of a man whose garment slips. 908 says those who live as the woman of the fine brow wishes (நன்னுதலாள் பெட்டாங்கு ஒழுகுபவர்) do not see their friends' needs through (நட்டார் குறைமுடியார்). 908 names a condition under which the duty of 788 goes unmet: the man's actions are no longer his own to direct.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:754","type":"parallel_domain","note":"754 says wealth that comes by right means and without wrong yields virtue and also pleasure (அறன் ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்). 909 lists the same three goods as missing in men who do a woman's bidding (பெண்ஏவல் செய்வார்கண் இல்). Where 754 treats the three as flowing from one sound source, 909 treats them as shut off by one fault.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:909","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made abundant wealth the other two, virtue and pleasure, are easily had together (ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). 909 says that deeds of virtue, ample wealth and the other pursuits (அறவினையும் ஆன்ற பொருளும் பிறவினையும்) are all absent in those who do a woman's bidding; Parimelazhagar and Pope take பிறவினை as pleasure. Both use the three aims as the measure of a life: 760 shows how they are gained together, 909 a way they are lost together.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:910","b":"k:917","type":"agrees","note":"910 says those with deliberation and firmness of heart never fall into the folly of attachment to a woman. 917, in the next chapter, says it is those without a firm heart (நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர்) who touch the shoulders of women who embrace with their minds on other things. Both locate the safeguard in the man's own நெஞ்சம், stated positively in 910 for the wife-ruled man and negatively in 917 for the client of courtesans.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:911","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"911 warns that the இன்சொல் of women who want a man's wealth brings him harm. In 1258 a woman calls the soft speech (பணிமொழி) of her lover, a 'thief of many deceptions' (பன்மாயக் கள்வன்), the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). Both treat gentle words as an instrument that undoes the hearer, though 1258 is a lover's complaint in the Book of Love, not a warning the book gives in its own voice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:912","b":"k:103","type":"same_image","note":"103 says help given by those who do not weigh the return (பயன்தூக்கார்) proves, when its worth is weighed (நயன்தூக்கின்), greater than the sea. 912 uses the same pair of words in the same order: the women weigh the gain (பயன்தூக்கி), and the man is to weigh their conduct (நயன்தூக்கி) and not join them. Weighing the return is what spoils a relationship in both verses, and weighing worth is the observer's proper response.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:913","b":"k:80","type":"develops","note":"80 says the seat of life follows love, and that for those without it the body is bone wrapped in skin (என்புதோல் போர்த்த உடம்பு). 913 likens the false embrace (பொய்ம்மை முயக்கம்) of women who want wealth to embracing an unknown corpse in a dark room (ஏதில் பிணம்). 913 applies 80's premise to a particular case: a body offered without love is, for the one who holds it, a dead one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:914","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 says that a loving nature with grace in view (அருள்கருதி) is not found in those who, with goods in view (பொருள்கருதி), watch for another's unguarded moment. 914 opposes those who examine அருட்பொருள் to women for whom பொருள் is the only thing of value. In the chapter on theft and in this one, the same pair, aiming at grace or aiming at goods, sorts people into two kinds that do not mix.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:915","b":"k:636","type":"agrees","note":"636 asks what subtle scheme can stand before those who have native acuteness together with book-learning (மதிநுட்பம் நூலோடு உடையார்). 915 says those whose knowledge is made excellent by goodness of intellect (மதிநலத்தின் மாண்ட அறிவினவர்) do not touch the favours of women common to all; Parimelazhagar reads this too as natural intellect perfecting acquired knowledge. The pairing shows the same compound of mind being credited in statecraft with seeing through stratagems and here with keeping a man from a private vice.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:915","b":"k:1311","type":"same_image","note":"915 calls the women பொதுநலத்தார், those whose favours are common to all, and says the wise will not touch them. In 1311 a wife tells her husband that all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்), calls him பரத்த, and refuses his chest. The reproach of being 'common' is turned on the man there, and it is a sulking wife's charge in the Book of Love, not a judgement the book makes itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:917","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"57 says confinement does little to guard women, and that the guard kept by their own நிறை, firmness, is the chief one. 917 says it is men without a heart of நிறை (நிறைநெஞ்சம் இல்லவர்) who touch the shoulders of women who embrace while their hearts tend other things. The same inner firmness that 57 names as a wife's real protection is here named as what a man lacks when he goes to such women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:917","b":"k:864","type":"parallel_domain","note":"864 says the man who does not let go of anger and has no நிறை (நிறையிலன்) is easy prey for anyone, at any time and place. 917 says those without நிறைநெஞ்சம் are the ones who go to women whose hearts are set on something else. In both the lack of self-command is what exposes a man, to enemies in 864 and to women who want what he has in 917.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:918","b":"k:910","type":"agrees","note":"910 says the folly that comes of attachment to a woman never exists in those whose hearts hold deliberation (எண்சேர்ந்த நெஞ்சத்து இடனுடையார்). 918 says the embrace of deceiving women is an affliction to those who lack examining understanding (ஆயும் அறிவினர் அல்லார்க்கு). The neighbouring chapters, on subservience to a wife and on courtesans, give the same safeguard: a mind that thinks the matter through.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:918","b":"k:1082","type":"same_image","note":"918 says that for those without discerning understanding the embrace of deceiving women is an அணங்கு, which Parimelazhagar glosses as the touch of a spirit-woman who takes life through desire. In 1082 a lover says that her returning his look is like an attacking அணங்கு (தாக்கணங்கு) arriving with an army. The same figure of the woman as an afflicting spirit is a lover's praise in the Book of Love and a warning in 918.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:936","type":"same_image","note":"920 says double-minded women, liquor and dice are the companions of those whom Fortune has removed from herself (திருநீக்கப்பட்டார்). 936 says those covered by the goddess of misfortune named gambling (சூதென்னும் முகடி) do not eat their fill and suffer. Both verses describe a vice through the two sister goddesses: in 920 the vices attend the man Tiru has already left, in 936 one of them is Mugadi herself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:920","b":"k:974","type":"same_image","note":"920 names women of two minds (இருமனப் பெண்டிர்), which Parimelazhagar explains as a mind that joins and does not join a man at once. 974 says greatness, like women of one mind (ஒருமை மகளிர்), exists only by guarding oneself. The two phrases are opposites, and where single-mindedness is 974's model for keeping greatness, doubleness in 920 goes with dice and drink as the company of the unfortunate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:921","b":"k:653","type":"develops","note":"653 tells those who mean to rise to leave off any deed that dims their lustre (ஒளிமாழ்கும் செய்வினை). 921 says those who live in love of liquor (கட்காதல் கொண்டொழுகுவார்) are never held in awe and lose their lustre (ஒளியிழப்பர்). 921 gives a named instance of the kind of act 653 warns against in general.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:921","b":"k:939","type":"parallel_domain","note":"939 says that clothing, wealth, food, lustre (ஒளி) and learning do not come near a man who takes to gaming. 921 says those devoted to liquor lose their ஒளி and are not feared. The adjoining chapters on drink and dice both count the loss of ஒளி, the standing that makes a man regarded, among the costs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:922","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"922 forbids drinking and adds that if anyone drinks, it should be those who do not want to be counted by the worthy (சான்றோரான் எண்ணப்பட வேண்டாதார்). 656 forbids any act the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை), even to relieve one's own mother's hunger. In both the judgement of the சான்றோர் is the measure of conduct; 922 states it as the price a drinker must be willing to pay.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:924","b":"k:1288","type":"same_image","note":"924 says shame turns away from the drinker. In 1288 the speaker tells her lover that his chest is to her as liquor is to those who have enjoyed it, wanted even though it does harm that brings disgrace (இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும்). The simile in the Book of Love takes for granted what 924 teaches, that drink disgraces, and uses it to describe a desire that persists in spite of shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:925","b":"k:838","type":"same_image","note":"838 says a fool getting something into his hand is like a deranged man getting drunk (மையல் ஒருவன் களித்தற்று). 925 says paying out wealth to buy oblivion shows ignorance. 838 uses drunkenness as the image for what a fool does with property, and 925 describes drunkenness itself as a fool's use of property.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:927","b":"k:271","type":"same_image","note":"271 says the five elements laugh inwardly (அகத்தே நகும்) at the hidden conduct of a man with a deceitful mind. 927 says those who drink and droop are seen through and always laughed at by their own town (உள்ளொற்றி உள்ளூர் நகப்படுவர்); Parimelazhagar takes கள்ளொற்றி as drinking in concealment. In both, concealed conduct has a witness that laughs, the elements within the body in 271 and the neighbours who infer what is going on in 927.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:928","b":"k:293","type":"develops","note":"293 says not to lie about what one's own heart knows, for the heart will burn the liar afterwards. 928 concerns one such lie, 'I have never been drunk' (களித்தறியேன்), about something the speaker's heart knows, and says that drink brings the hidden thing out. 293 gives the inner penalty for this kind of lie and 928 adds an outward one, exposure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:928","b":"k:1161","type":"parallel_domain","note":"928 tells the drinker to stop saying he has never been drunk, because what he hid in his heart shows all the more at that moment (ஒளித்ததூஉம் ஆங்கே மிகும்). In 1161 a woman says she would hide her pain, but like spring water for those who draw it, it increases (ஊற்றுநீர் போல மிகும்). Both end on மிகும்: the attempt to conceal an inner state is answered by its overflowing, in drink and in love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:931","b":"k:113","type":"agrees","note":"113 says to let go, that very day, of gain that comes from leaving fairness even if it yields only good (நன்றே தரினும்). 931 uses the same concession, 'even if you win' (வென்றிடினும்), and still refuses the gain, calling it a swallowed hook. In both the apparent success of a wrong source is conceded and set aside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:933","b":"k:385","type":"qualifies","note":"385 defines a king as one able to create sources of wealth, gather it, guard it and apportion it (இயற்றலும் ஈட்டலும் காத்தலும்). 933, which Parimelazhagar reads of a king, says both the gathered wealth and its sources (பொருள் ஆயம்) leave him if he keeps at the dice. One habit undoes the gathering and the guarding that 385 makes the mark of rule.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:933","b":"k:1009","type":"parallel_domain","note":"933 says that if one keeps calling the rolling dice-throw (உருளாயம்), wealth and its income (பொருளாயம்) go and fall outside (புறமே படும்); Parimelazhagar takes 'outside' as into enemies' hands. 1009 says the wealth a miser gathered without love, self-regard or virtue is taken by others (கொள்வார் பிறர்). The gambler and the hoarder, by opposite habits, both end with their wealth in strangers' hands.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:934","b":"k:1045","type":"develops","note":"934 says nothing gives poverty like gambling, which does many small harms and ruins standing (சிறுமை பலசெய்து சீரழிக்கும்). 1045 says that within the one affliction called poverty many sufferings arrive (பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள் சென்று படும்). Read together they form a chain: gambling brings harms on the way to poverty, and poverty then brings its own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:935","b":"k:437","type":"same_image","note":"935 says those who clung (இவறியார்) in pride to dice, gaming hall and skilled hand became people with nothing (இல்லாகியார்). 437 uses the same word of the miser: the wealth of one who clings (இவறியான்) without doing what should be done perishes. இவறுதல், holding on and not letting go, ends in loss in both verses, whether the thing held is money or the game.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:935","b":"k:539","type":"develops","note":"539 advises thinking, when swollen with one's own joy, of those who were ruined by negligence (இகழ்ச்சியின் கெட்டாரை உள்ளுக). 935 supplies such a memory: those who took pride (தருக்கி) in the dice and came to nothing; Parimelazhagar reads them as kings of former times and names the Pandavas. 935 does for gambling what 539 asks a ruler to do in general.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:936","b":"k:167","type":"same_image","note":"167 says the fair goddess, grudging the envious man, shows him to her elder sister (தவ்வையைக் காட்டி விடும்). 936 names that sister, முகடி, as gambling itself, covering those who play. Envy hands a person over to misfortune; gambling, in 936, is misfortune under another name.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:937","b":"k:1043","type":"parallel_domain","note":"937 says time spent in the gaming hall ruins long-held wealth and character (பழகிய செல்வமும் பண்பும் கெடுக்கும்). 1043 says poverty destroys together one's ancient descent and one's தோல் (தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும்), which Pope takes as dignity of speech. The two sentences have the same shape: an inherited standing and a personal quality lost at one stroke, with gambling as a cause and poverty as a cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:938","b":"k:248","type":"develops","note":"938 lists what gambling does: it destroys wealth (பொருள் கெடுத்து), makes one take up lying, destroys compassion (அருள் கெடுத்து) and drives one through misery. 248 ranks two of these losses: those without wealth may flower again one day (பூப்பர் ஒருகால்), but those without அருள் are finished. By 248's measure the third item in 938's list is graver than the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:938","b":"k:757","type":"develops","note":"757 calls அருள் a child born of love that is raised by the wealthy foster-mother called பொருள். 938 has gambling destroy both பொருள் and அருள். Parimelazhagar assigns the losses in 938 to separate causes (losing, winning, resentment), but 757 suggests a link between them: with the nurse gone, the child is harder to raise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:939","b":"k:166","type":"parallel_domain","note":"166 says the kin of one who envies a gift perish without anything to wear or eat (உடுப்பதூஉம் உண்பதூஉம் இன்றி). 939 begins its list of what gaming keeps away with the same pair, clothing and food (உடை ... ஊண்). Envy and gambling are both measured by the loss of the plainest necessities.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:939","b":"k:400","type":"tension","note":"939 names five things, among them learning (கல்வி), that 'அடையா' the one who takes to gaming. Parimelazhagar glosses this as 'do not come to him', but Pope's verse rendering and Kalaignar take the five as departing from him. On that second reading 939 conflicts with 400, which calls learning the wealth that cannot perish (கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்); on Parimelazhagar's reading there is no conflict, since the gambler never acquires it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:940","b":"k:1145","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1145 a lover says love grows sweeter each time it is exposed, as drinking is wanted more each time one is drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால்). 940 has the same 'each time ... the more' construction with another vice, the dice, as the comparison (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல்). The lover uses the figure to praise his state, while 940 uses it to describe an attachment fed by its own losses.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:941","b":"k:466","type":"parallel_domain","note":"941 says the three humours counted by the learned, beginning with wind, cause disease whether they exceed or fall short (மிகினும் குறையினும்); Parimelazhagar refers the excess and shortfall to food and activity. 466 says one is ruined by doing what is unfit and also by not doing what is fit (செயக் கெடும் ... செய்யாமையானும் கெடும்). Both verses locate harm on two sides of a right amount, in the body and in action.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:943","b":"k:479","type":"parallel_domain","note":"943 says to eat knowing the measure once digestion is complete, as the way one who has a body can carry it long (நெடிதுய்க்கும் ஆறு). 479 says the life of one who does not live knowing the measure (அளவறிந்து வாழாதான்) seems to exist and then perishes; Parimelazhagar refers that measure to one's wealth. The same phrase governs both the stomach and the purse. (This edition prints 'அறவறிந்து' in 943; Parimelazhagar's gloss reads அளவு அறிந்து.)","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:946","b":"k:420","type":"parallel_domain","note":"946 says disease stays with the one of very great feed (கழிபேர் இரையான்), as pleasure stays with the one who eats knowing to stop short (இழிவறிந்து உண்பான்). 420 asks what it matters whether those live or die who know taste only by the mouth and not by the ear, and calls them மாக்கள். Both verses use a word with an animal sense for the person ruled by the mouth: இரை, feed, and மாக்கள்.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:289","type":"parallel_domain","note":"289 says those who know nothing but stealth do what is beyond measure and perish right there (அளவல்ல செய்தாங்கே வீவர்). 947 says the one who eats beyond the measure of his fire gets disease beyond measure. Chapter 29 uses அளவு as a moral measure and chapter 95 as a bodily one, and in each case overstepping it brings its own penalty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:947","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"947 says that if one eats greatly without discerning the measure of the fire within (தீயளவு), disease arises without measure (நோயளவின்றிப் படும்). 475 says even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if they are piled too high (சால மிகுத்துப் பெயின்). In both a fixed capacity, the digestive fire or the axle, is what excess breaks, whatever the quality of the load.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:948","b":"k:360","type":"parallel_domain","note":"948 tells the healer to look for the root of the disease (நோய்முதல்) before the remedy. 360 does this for suffering in general: when desire, anger and confusion perish even in name, the நோய் perishes. The chapter on true perception treats pain as chapter 95 treats illness, by naming causes and removing them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:948","b":"k:504","type":"parallel_domain","note":"948 sets out an order of enquiry: look into the disease, then its root, then the means that subdue it, and act so that it works (நோய்நாடி நோய்முதல் நாடி ... வாய்நாடி). 504 has the same threefold நாடி for judging a person: examine the qualities, examine the faults, examine which predominates, and take that. Diagnosis of a patient and assessment of a candidate are given the same grammar of step-by-step examination before a decision.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:949","b":"k:637","type":"agrees","note":"637 says that even when one knows the method of doing a thing, one should act knowing how the world goes (செயற்கை அறிந்தக் கடைத்தும் உலகத்து இயற்கை அறிந்து செயல்); Parimelazhagar glosses the method as what the treatises teach. 949 addresses the one who has learned (கற்றான்) and still requires him to consider this patient, this illness and this moment. Both hold that book learning has to be fitted to the particular case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:952","b":"k:133","type":"qualifies","note":"952 says the well-born do not slip (இழுக்கார்) in conduct, truthfulness and shame. 133 says having conduct is itself குடிமை and that a lapse (இழுக்கம்) turns into low birth (இழிந்த பிறப்பாய் விடும்). Read together, 952's claim is close to a definition: whoever slips has by 133 already left the class of the well-born, a point Parimelazhagar makes on 133 while keeping it within varna.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:952","b":"k:983","type":"parallel_domain","note":"952 lists three things the well-born keep: ஒழுக்கம், வாய்மை, நாண். 983 lists five pillars that hold up சால்பு: love, shame (நாண்), obligingness, kindly regard and truthfulness (வாய்மை). Two of the three marks of lineage reappear as supports of personal worth, so the same core is described once as inherited and once as a structure a person stands on.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:953","b":"k:92","type":"develops","note":"953 names a smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning (நகை ஈகை இன்சொல் இகழாமை) as the share of a true lineage. 92 takes three of these and ranks them: being sweet-spoken with a glad face (முகனமர்ந்து இன்சொலன்) is better than giving with a glad heart (அகனமர்ந்து ஈதல்). The list in 953 is flat; 92 shows the book elsewhere weighing its items against one another.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:953","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"953 puts giving and not scorning (ஈகை ... இகழாமை) side by side among the marks of a good family; Parimelazhagar takes all four as the way one meets the poor who come. 1057 gives the receiver's side: on seeing those who give without scorn or contempt (இகழ்ந்தெள்ளாது ஈவார்), the asker's heart rejoices within. The pairing shows why 'not scorning' sits next to 'giving' in the list.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:311","type":"parallel_domain","note":"954 says the well-born will not do diminishing things (குன்றுவ) even if they get crores piled up (அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும்). 311 says the stainless hold to not hurting others even if it would bring wealth that gives distinction (சிறப்பீனும் செல்வம் பெறினும்). Both use the same 'even if they gained' (பெறினும்) form to set a fixed refusal against a large reward, once for family standing and once for non-harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:657","type":"agrees","note":"954 says no amount of wealth makes the well-born do what lowers them. 657 says the extreme poverty of the worthy (சான்றோர் கழிநல்குரவு) ranks above gain reached by taking on blame (பழிமலைந்து எய்திய ஆக்கம்). Both rank standing above wealth; 657 states the comparison outright, in the context of a minister's clean action.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:954","b":"k:965","type":"develops","note":"954 says the well-born do not do things that diminish (குன்றுவ செய்தல் இலர்), whatever the gain. 965 uses the same word to say what follows if they do: even those like a hill sink (குன்றுவர்) if they do a diminishing thing (குன்றுவ) the size of a kundri seed. 954 gives the rule and 965 the cost of breaking it, at a scale far below crores.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:955","b":"k:1043","type":"tension","note":"955 says an ancient family (பழங்குடி) does not part from its character when its means shrink. 1043 says poverty destroys together one's ancient descent and one's speech (தொல்வரவும் தோலும் கெடுக்கும்). They conflict if 1043 is read as plain poverty undoing lineage; the conflict eases on Parimelazhagar's reading, which stresses that 1043 calls poverty a craving (நசை), so it is the wanting, not the lack, that ruins.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:956","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"956 says those who mean to keep to a spotless line do not do what lacks worth (சால்பில செய்யார்); Parimelazhagar adds 'even in poverty'. 656 says one should not do what the worthy condemn (சான்றோர் பழிக்கும் வினை) even if one must see one's mother hungry. Both set a standard of worthy conduct above need, 656 naming the hardest case.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:956","b":"k:660","type":"develops","note":"956 says those resolved to live by their spotless lineage will not do unworthy things by taking to deceit (சலம் பற்றி). 660 uses the same word for wealth-making: securing wealth made by deceit (சலத்தால் பொருள் செய்து) is like keeping water in an unfired clay pot. 956 rules deceit out on grounds of family honour; 660 adds that its gains do not hold anyway.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:958","b":"k:992","type":"agrees","note":"958 says that if lack of affection (நாரின்மை) shows in a man, people will doubt his lineage (குலத்தின்கண் ஐயப்படும்). 992 names having love and being born in a seasoned family (அன்புடைமை ஆன்ற குடிப்பிறத்தல்) as the two things that make up courtesy. 992 pairs love and birth as partners; 958 shows that when the first is missing the second is no longer believed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:125","type":"agrees","note":"960 says one who wants lineage must want humility toward everyone (யார்க்கும் பணிவு). 125 says bowing is good for all (எல்லார்க்கும் நன்றாம் பணிதல்) and is a further wealth for the rich. Both make humility general, and each singles out the highly placed, by family or by wealth, as those it most becomes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:963","type":"qualifies","note":"960 asks for humility toward all (யார்க்கும் பணிவு) from anyone who wants to keep a lineage. 963, in the next chapter, limits when bowing is called for: in abundance one must bow (பெருக்கத்து வேண்டும் பணிதல்), but in reduced circumstances one must stand high (சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு). The humility of 960 is thus not meant to cover bending under want.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:960","b":"k:1019","type":"same_image","note":"960 pairs நலம் with shame and குலம் with humility: want நலம், then want நாணுடைமை; want குலம், then want பணிவு toward all. 1019 uses the same pair in negative form: a lapse in principle burns one's குலம், and settled shamelessness burns one's நலம் (நலஞ்சுடும் நாணின்மை). The two verses share the குலம்/நலம் pairing and agree that நலம் depends on shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:961","b":"k:131","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Parimelazhagar reads 961's இன்றி அமையா as things without which one would not survive, so the verse puts standing above life. 131 says conduct is to be guarded more than life (உயிரினும் ஓம்பப்படும்) because it gives excellence. The same ranking, a value kept ahead of survival, is made for honour in one chapter and for conduct in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:962","b":"k:113","type":"parallel_domain","note":"962 refuses unworthy acts even when they would bring renown (சீரினும்). 113 says to let go at once of gain that departs from fairness even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும் நடுவிகந்தாம் ஆக்கம்). Both have the form 'even if it yields the thing you want, not by this means', applied to fame in one and to profit in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:962","b":"k:652","type":"qualifies","note":"652 says to avoid any action that yields neither renown nor good (புகழொடு நன்றி பயவா வினை), which makes renown one of the tests of an act. 962 says those who want renown together with great manliness will not do unworthy things even for renown (சீரினும் சீரல்ல செய்யார்). 962 therefore narrows 652's test: bringing fame does not by itself make an act fit to do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:680","type":"tension","note":"963 says that in small and shrunken circumstances one must hold oneself high (சிறிய சுருக்கத்து வேண்டும் உயர்வு). 680 says those whose domain is small (உறைசிறியார்), fearing their own people's trembling, will bow to the greater and accept terms (கொள்வர் பெரியார்ப் பணிந்து). They conflict if 963's 'reduced state' includes weakness of position before a stronger party; if 963 is only about poverty and personal bearing, 680 is separate advice on statecraft.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:963","b":"k:988","type":"develops","note":"963 asks for an upright bearing (உயர்வு) when one's means have shrunk. 988 says poverty is no disgrace (இன்மை ஒருவற்கு இளிவன்று) for one who has the firmness called worth (சால்பென்னும் திண்மை). 988 supplies the ground for 963's demand: want does not itself lower a person, so there is no reason to stoop in it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:124","type":"develops","note":"124 says the stature of one who is restrained without shifting from his position (நிலையின் திரியாது அடங்கியான்) is far greater than a mountain. 964 uses the same word for the opposite case: those who descend from their position (நிலையின் இழிந்த) are like hair off the head. Together they give the two ends of one scale, both measured by whether a person keeps to their நிலை.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:964","b":"k:973","type":"tension","note":"964 says people who have come down from their position (நிலையின் இழிந்தக் கடை) are like hair fallen from the head. 973 says those who are not low are not low even when placed low (கீழிருந்தும் கீழல்லார் கீழல்லவர்). They conflict if 964's நிலை means outward rank or station; on Parimelazhagar's reading it means the high standard the well-born hold themselves to, so the fall is their own doing and the verses agree.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:966","b":"k:1033","type":"same_image","note":"966 asks what is gained by going and standing behind those who scorn you (இகழ்வார் பின் சென்று நிலை). 1033 says only those who eat by their own ploughing truly live, and all others bow to eat and go behind (தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல்பவர்). Both use 'going behind' another as the picture of a dependent life, and 1033 names the livelihood that avoids it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:966","b":"k:1053","type":"qualifies","note":"966 says standing in the train of those who scorn you brings neither renown nor the gods' land. 1053 says that even asking has a certain beauty (ஏஎர் உடைத்து) when one stands before those of unconcealing heart who know what is due (கரப்பிலா நெஞ்சின் கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று). What 966 rejects is therefore not dependence as such but dependence on people who despise the one who comes to them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:967","b":"k:230","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967 prefers being called ruined to a life got by following those who reject you. 230 says nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even that is sweet when giving is out of reach (ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை). In both, a life that cannot be lived on one's own terms, without honour or without the power to give, is ranked below death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:968","b":"k:82","type":"same_image","note":"968 asks whether a life that tends the flesh is a medicine (மருந்தோ) once greatness has lost its dignity; Parimelazhagar takes this as a medicine against dying. 82 says that eating while the guest waits outside is not to be wanted even if the food were the medicine of deathlessness (சாவா மருந்தெனினும்). Both raise the idea of an immortality-medicine only to rank it below a duty, honour in one and hospitality in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:968","b":"k:1017","type":"agrees","note":"968 questions the worth of a life that merely feeds the body (ஊன் ஓம்பும் வாழ்க்கை) when dignity is gone. 1017 says those governed by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர் ... நாண் துறவார்). The chapters on honour and on shame reach the same ordering of life and self-respect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:969","b":"k:779","type":"parallel_domain","note":"969 says those like the கவரிமா give up life when their honour is at stake (உயிர் நீப்பர் மானம் வரின்). 779 asks who could reproach as failures those who die so that what they vowed is not broken (இழைத்தது இகவாமைச் சாவார்). Dying to avoid going back on what one stands for appears as a matter of civil honour in 969 and as a soldier's vow in 779.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:235","type":"develops","note":"970 says the world worships and praises the radiance of the honourable who do not live on in disgrace. 235 says that a ruin which is growth and a death which is existence (உளதாகும் சாக்காடு) are hard to reach except for the skilled. 235 gives the general idea that 970 applies: a death can leave a person present in fame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:970","b":"k:971","type":"same_image","note":"970 sets disgrace against radiance: the world worships the ஒளி of those who will not live when இளி comes. 971 defines the same two words: one's ஒளி is abundance of aspiration, and one's இளி is saying 'we will live without it' (அஃதிறந்து வாழ்தும் எனல்). In both, disgrace is a way of going on living, and radiance belongs to those who refuse it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:981","b":"k:218","type":"develops","note":"981 says that for those who know what is due (கடன் அறிந்து) and take on worth, every good thing is கடன்; Parimelazhagar glosses this as the good qualities being natural to them. 218 uses the same phrase, கடனறி காட்சியவர், for people who do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும்). 218 shows one concrete case of 981's general claim: a duty that is felt as one's own does not wait on circumstances.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:982","b":"k:79","type":"parallel_domain","note":"982 says the worthy have only the goodness of character (குணநலம்), and that other goodness is not counted among goodnesses; Parimelazhagar takes பிறநலம் to be the goodness of the bodily limbs. 79 asks what all the outer limbs (புறத்துறுப்பு) can do for those who lack love, the inner limb (அகத்துறுப்பு). Both verses rank an inner quality over the body's outward parts, one for worth and one for love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:982","b":"k:407","type":"agrees","note":"982 denies that any goodness other than character counts for the worthy. 407 says the handsome looks (எழில்நலம்) of one without fine, penetrating understanding are those of a decorated clay doll (மண்மாண் புனைபாவை). Both dismiss outward நலம் that has nothing inside it, though 407 measures by understanding and 982 by character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:615","type":"same_image","note":"983 calls its five qualities the pillar planted to bear worth (சால்பு ஊன்றிய தூண்). 615 calls the man who wants the work and not pleasure a pillar that wipes away his kin's troubles and props them (துடைத்து ஊன்றும் தூண்). The same figure, with the same verb ஊன்று, is used once for qualities holding up a character and once for a person holding up a family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:983","b":"k:1013","type":"develops","note":"983 lists five pillars that bear worth: அன்பு, நாண், ஒப்புரவு, கண்ணோட்டம், வாய்மை. 1013 singles out one of them: as all lives are fixed on the body (ஊனைக் குறித்த), சால்பு is fixed on the good called shame (நாண் என்னும் நன்மை குறித்தது). Read together, shame is one support among five in 983 and the seat of worth itself in 1013.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:985","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"985 says the strength of those who accomplish is bowing (பணிதல்), and that this is the weapon by which the worthy turn foes. 978 says greatness always bows (பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை) while smallness adorns itself in self-admiration. Both attach bowing to the strong and not the weak; 985 adds that it has an effect on opponents.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:986","b":"k:1327","type":"parallel_domain","note":"986 says the touchstone of worth is accepting defeat (தோல்வி கொளல்) even from those who are not one's equal. In 1327 the man says that in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்), as the reunion shows. Both treat yielding as the better outcome; 1327 is a lover's remark about a sulk, while 986 states a test of character.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:987","b":"k:998","type":"agrees","note":"987 says worth is useless unless it does good even to those who did harm. 998, in the next chapter, says failing in courtesy even toward those who act without friendship or kindness (நயமில செய்வார்க்கும்) is lowness (கடை). Both verses use the particle 'even to' (-க்கும்) to extend the virtue to hostile people, one for சால்பு and one for பண்பு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:988","b":"k:1064","type":"develops","note":"988 calls சால்பு a firmness (திண்மை) that keeps poverty from being a disgrace. 1064 describes that firmness at work: the worth that will not consent to begging even when no means remain (இடமில்லாக் காலும் இரவொல்லாச் சால்பு) is too large for all space to hold. 1064 shows what the poor but worthy person of 988 does and does not do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:990","b":"k:572","type":"same_image","note":"990 says the earth cannot carry its burden (பொறை) once the worthy lose their worth. 572 says those without kindly regard are, by merely existing, a burden to the earth (நிலக்குப் பொறை). The same figure is used from two sides: the worthy are what lets the earth carry its load, and people without கண்ணோட்டம் are dead weight on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:991","b":"k:548","type":"parallel_domain","note":"991 and 548 share the word எண்பதம், easy access. In 548 a king who is not accessible (எண்பதத்தான் ஓரா) and does not examine and do justice falls and perishes by himself; in 991 being accessible to all makes courtesy easy to attain. The quality whose absence ruins a ruler is the one whose presence makes a person courteous.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:994","b":"k:97","type":"same_image","note":"994 says the world honours the character (பண்பு) of those whose usefulness (பயன்) comes from doing good with fairness (நயனொடு நன்றி புரிந்த). 97 uses the same four words of speech: words that yield benefit (பயன்) and do not part from பண்பு produce நயன் and bring forth நன்றி. Parimelazhagar glosses நயன் as justice in both, so the cluster describes good speech in 97 and a good life in 994.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:994","b":"k:216","type":"develops","note":"994 praises people of நயன் whose doing good makes them useful (பயனுடையார்). 216 says wealth falling to one who has நயன் (நயனுடையான்) is like a fruit-bearing tree (பயன்மரம்) ripening in the middle of the town. 216 supplies the picture for 994: the person of நயன் is of பயன் to everyone within reach.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:874","type":"develops","note":"995 says courtesy is present even in enmity (பகையுள்ளும் பண்புள) in those who understand others. 874 says the world rests in the greatness of the person of courtesy who treats enmity as friendship (பகை நட்பாக் கொண்டொழுகும் பண்புடையாளன்). 874 takes the step beyond 995, from staying courteous toward an enemy to turning him into a friend; Parimelazhagar reads 874 as a matter of policy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:995","b":"k:953","type":"agrees","note":"995 warns that இகழ்ச்சி, scorn, wounds even as a joke. 953 lists not scorning (இகழாமை) with a smile, giving and sweet words as the four marks of a true family. 953 names the trait as a mark of good birth, and 995 gives the reason for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:998","b":"k:579","type":"agrees","note":"998 calls it lowness (கடை) to fail in courtesy even toward those who act without friendship or kindness. 579 calls it the highest thing (தலை) to extend kindly regard and bear with even those whose nature is to hurt (ஒறுத்தாற்றும் பண்பினார் கண்ணும்). The verses mark the two ends of the same scale: good conduct toward the hostile is தலை, and its failure is கடை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:998","b":"k:852","type":"agrees","note":"998 requires courtesy even toward those who do unfriendly, unkind things. 852 says that even if someone does what one cannot bear, intending division (பகல் கருதிப் பற்றா செயினும்), it is best not to do harm in return out of hostility. 852 gives the minimum, no retaliation, and 998 asks for more, that courtesy itself not lapse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:999","b":"k:523","type":"develops","note":"999 says that for those not able to laugh (நகல் வல்லர் அல்லார்) the great world lies in darkness even by day; Parimelazhagar takes நகல் as mixing with others and being glad at heart. 523 says the life of one who does not mingle freely (அளவளாவு இல்லாதான்) is like a tank filling without a bank. On Parimelazhagar's reading both describe the cost of not mixing with people, as lost wealth in 523 and a darkened world in 999.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1000","b":"k:1008","type":"agrees","note":"1000 says great wealth obtained by one without courtesy is like good milk spoiled by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று). 1008 says the wealth of a man nobody seeks out is a poison tree bearing fruit in the middle of the village (நச்சு மரம் பழுத்தற்று). Both say a good thing is made useless or harmful by the character of its holder; Parimelazhagar reads 1000 as wealth that benefits no one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1001","b":"k:229","type":"develops","note":"229 says eating alone in order to fill up one's store (நிரப்பிய தாமே தமியர் உணல்) is more bitter than begging; 1001 takes the case one step further, the man who does not even eat it (அஃதுண்ணான்), and calls him dead. Read together they grade the misuse of wealth: unshared use is wretched, no use at all is not living.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1002","b":"k:751","type":"qualifies","note":"751 says nothing but wealth makes people of no account into people of account (பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்). 1002 names nearly the same thought, பொருளானாம் எல்லாம், as what the miser thinks, and says that when it leads to not giving (ஈயாது இவறும்) it is delusion. The chapter on making wealth is thus limited here: the claim holds only for wealth that is given and used.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1004","b":"k:114","type":"same_image","note":"114 says whether people were worthy or not is seen from their எச்சம்; 1004 asks what எச்சம் the unwanted man imagines for himself. Parimelazhagar takes the word as offspring in 114 and as what remains after death, i.e. renown, in 1004, so the pairing shows the same test of a life by what it leaves, applied with two senses of the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1005","b":"k:430","type":"parallel_domain","note":"430 says those without understanding have nothing, whatever they have (என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்); 1005 says the same of those who neither give nor enjoy, even with crores. The same form of argument, that owning is not having unless some further quality is present, is used for understanding in one place and for use and giving in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1006","b":"k:1000","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1000 says great wealth got by a man without courtesy is like good milk turned by the fault of its vessel (கலந்தீமையால் திரிந்தற்று); 1006 says the man who neither uses nor gives is ஏதம் to பெருஞ்செல்வம். In both the wealth is good in itself and the holder is what spoils it, which is how Parimelazhagar reads 1006 (others take the wealth to be a misery to him).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1007","b":"k:226","type":"develops","note":"226 says ending the ruinous hunger of the destitute (அற்றார் அழிபசி தீர்த்தல்) is the place where one who has got wealth stores it. 1007 says the wealth of one who does nothing for the destitute (அற்றார்க்கொன்று ஆற்றாதான்) is like a very beautiful woman growing old alone. 226 says where wealth is kept safe; 1007 shows what happens to wealth kept anywhere else: it passes its time unused.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1007","b":"k:1039","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1039 says land whose owner stays away sulks like a wife (இல்லாளின் ஊடிவிடும்); 1007 likens ungiven wealth to a beautiful woman who ages alone (தமியள் மூத்தற்று). Both picture a good left unattended as a woman without her partner, for farmland in one verse and for money in the other; Parimelazhagar notes for 1007 that the wealth itself loses its fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1009","b":"k:760","type":"qualifies","note":"760 says that for those who have made bright wealth in plenty (ஒண்பொருள் காழ்ப்ப இயற்றியார்க்கு) the other two, virtue and pleasure, come easily. 1009 uses the same word, ஒண்பொருள், for wealth amassed by dropping love, denying oneself and not looking to virtue, and says others will take it. So the promise of 760 fails when the two ends it names are given up in the earning; Parimelazhagar reads 760 as wealth that comes by the right way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1010","b":"k:219","type":"agrees","note":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, 1010 says the brief want (சிறுதுனி) of the rich who have renown is like the rain cloud running dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது). 219 says the poverty of a man of நயன் is just his not being able to do what he is used to doing. Both treat the giver's poverty as a loss to others, not to himself, and 1010 adds by its image that it passes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1011","b":"k:201","type":"parallel_domain","note":"201 says the excellent fear the intoxication called evil deed (விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்); 1011 defines shame proper as shame on account of deeds (கருமத்தால் நாணுதல்). Fear and shame are given the same object, one's own wrong action, in two chapters, and Parimelazhagar glosses நாணுதல் elsewhere (433, 172) as அஞ்சுதல்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1011","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1011 mentions the other shame, that of women, without describing it. In 1251 a woman in the Book of Love describes it from inside: shame is the bolt (நாணுத்தாழ்) on the door of her restraint, which the axe of desire breaks. This is a speech in character, but it shows what the shame 1011 calls பிற is: a guard on conduct in love, not a recoil from a wrong deed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1012","b":"k:993","type":"agrees","note":"993 says likeness of limbs is not likeness among people; the likeness that counts is of character (பண்பொத்தல்). 1012 says the bodily needs are shared by all and shame is what sets people apart. Both refuse to take what is physically common as the measure of a human being.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1013","b":"k:80","type":"parallel_domain","note":"80 says the seat of life goes by way of love (அன்பின் வழியது உயிர்நிலை) and without it a body is bones wrapped in skin; 1013 says worth depends on shame as life depends on flesh. Both use the life-and-body relation to say a quality is what a thing lives by, love for the person in 80 and shame for சால்பு in 1013. Parimelazhagar notes a variant ஊணை ('food') in 1013, which Manakkudavar follows; the link rests on the reading ஊனை.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1014","b":"k:118","type":"agrees","note":"118 (with 115) calls not leaning to one side the ornament of the worthy (கோடாமை சான்றோர்க்கு அணி); 1014 calls shame the ornament of the worthy (அணியன்றோ நாணுடைமை சான்றோர்க்கு). The same phrase names two inner restraints, and neither is an outward decoration.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:980","type":"develops","note":"980 says greatness covers others' exposure (அற்றம் மறைக்கும்) and smallness only speaks their faults. 1015 gives the feeling behind that conduct: being ashamed of another's disgrace as of one's own. One who feels the other's பழி in this way has no reason to tell it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1015","b":"k:1018","type":"develops","note":"1015 praises one who feels shame even for others' disgrace; 1018 describes one who does not feel shame at what makes others ashamed (பிறர் நாணத்தக்கது தான் நாணான்). The two set out the ends of a scale inside the chapter: shame that extends beyond oneself, and shame that fails even for one's own act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:57","type":"parallel_domain","note":"57 says confinement does little to guard women and the guard kept by their own firmness (நிறைகாக்கும் காப்பு) is best; 1016 says the high have shame for their fence (நாண்வேலி). In both the effective guard is a quality inside the person and not an outer barrier, for a wife's fidelity in one and for the conduct of the high in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:311","type":"agrees","note":"311 says the spotless hold to not hurting others even if it would bring wealth that gives distinction. 1016, on Parimelazhagar's reading, says the high take shame as their fence and do not want the wide world (வியன்ஞாலம் பேணலர்) where the two conflict. Both state a refusal of great gain at the price of a restraint; the other reading he reports, that they will not want the world without first having the fence of shame, gives the same result.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1016","b":"k:421","type":"same_image","note":"421 calls understanding an inner fortress enemies cannot destroy (உள்ளழிக்கலாகா அரண்); 1016 calls shame a fence (வேலி), which Parimelazhagar explains as keeping blame and sin from entering. Both give an inner quality the work of a defensive structure, one against ruin and one against disgrace.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:130","type":"develops","note":"130 says virtue watches for the right moment and comes by the path to the one who guards against anger and has learned restraint (அறம் பார்க்கும் ஆற்றின் நுழைந்து). 1018, as the commentators read it, has virtue ashamed to come near the shameless. Together they give the two directions of the same figure: virtue seeks out the restrained and shrinks from those without shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1018","b":"k:204","type":"parallel_domain","note":"204 says that if one plots another's ruin, அறம் plots the plotter's ruin; 1018 says that if one is not ashamed, அறம் is ashamed. In both, virtue is an agent that answers the person's act in the terms of that act, with a penalty in 204 and with withdrawal in 1018.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1019","b":"k:952","type":"develops","note":"952 says the well-born do not slip in three things: conduct, truthfulness and shame (ஒழுக்கமும் வாய்மையும் நாணும்). 1019 says what happens when two of those three fail: a lapse in conduct burns the family, and shamelessness that stays burns every good. The chapter on good family gives the mark and 1019 gives the cost of losing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1021","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"1021 says no greatness has more dignity (பீடு) than that of the one who says 'I will not stay my hand' (கைதூவேன்) from the work. 611 says not to slacken at a task's difficulty because effort itself gives greatness (பெருமை முயற்சி தரும்). Both locate பெருமை in persistence rather than in rank or result; 1021 applies it to the work of raising one's family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1021","b":"k:612","type":"develops","note":"1021 praises the resolve never to stop work once begun (கைதூவேன்). 612 gives the other side: guard against the work dying in mid-course (வினைக்கண் வினைகெடல் ஓம்பல்), since, in Parimelazhagar's reading, the world gives up on those who give up their unfinished work. The pair shows the same refusal to stop as honour in one verse and as a condition of the world's regard in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1022","b":"k:604","type":"agrees","note":"1022 says the family (குடி) lengthens through long-continued work that has both effort (ஆள்வினை) and mature knowledge. 604 states the reverse case: for those sunk in sloth, without worthy exertion (மாண்ட உஞற்றிலவர்), the family dies down and faults multiply (குடிமடிந்து குற்றம் பெருகும்). Together they make one claim from both sides: the fortunes of a குடி follow the exertion of its members.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1022","b":"k:618","type":"agrees","note":"1022 pairs effort with full knowledge (ஆள்வினையும் ஆன்ற அறிவும்) as the two things that extend a family. 618 uses the same pairing when it assigns blame: lacking fortune is no disgrace, but lacking effort after knowing what should be known (அறிவறிந்து ஆள்வினை இன்மை) is. In both verses knowledge and effort are named together as what a person can be held to, as against fortune.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1023","b":"k:617","type":"agrees","note":"1023 has தெய்வம் tying up its garment (மடிதற்று) to go before the family-builder. 617 says the dark goddess lives in sloth (மடி) and the lotus goddess in the effort of the one without sloth (மடியிலான் தாள்). Both put divine favour on the side of the one who works; 1023's மடி (cloth) also echoes the மடி (sloth) that 617 and the chapter on sloth condemn.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1026","b":"k:773","type":"parallel_domain","note":"773 says fierceness is called great manliness (பேராண்மை), and that helping a foe in distress (ஊராண்மை) is its edge. 1026 says good manliness (நல்லாண்மை) is making oneself the one who manages the house one was born in (இல்லாண்மை). Each verse takes ஆண்மை and coins a second compound that turns it toward care for others; Parimelazhagar notes that 'நல்' in 1026 is there to set it apart from the manliness of war.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1027","b":"k:770","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1027 says that as in battle the load falls on the stout-hearted (வன்கண்ணர்), so among kin the burden (பொறை) falls on those able to carry it. 770 says an army has no existence without leaders, however many steady men it has (தலைமக்கள் இல்வழி இல்). 770 is the military fact that 1027 uses as its simile: numbers do not carry a force or a family, a few capable people do.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1029","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"615 describes one who does not want pleasure but wants the work (இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான்) as the pillar who wipes away his kin's sorrows. 1029 looks at that same person from the inside: his body seems to hold nothing but suffering. 1029 states the cost of the role that 615 praises; Parimelazhagar takes the question as noting that pleasure never reaches him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1032","b":"k:42","type":"parallel_domain","note":"42 says the householder is the support (துணை) of renouncers, the destitute and the dead. 1032 says the ploughmen bear (பொறுத்து) everyone who does not plough. Both verses identify one group whose ordinary work holds up all the others: the householder in the Book of Virtue, the farmer in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1033","b":"k:967","type":"develops","note":"1033 describes everyone but the farmer as those who bow, eat and walk behind others (தொழுதுண்டு பின்செல்பவர்). 967 says it is better to be called ruined than to live by going behind those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார் பின்சென்று வாழ்தல்). Both treat 'going behind' another for a living as a lesser life; 967 judges it by honour, and 1033 names farming as the livelihood that avoids it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1034","b":"k:389","type":"same_image","note":"389 says the world rests under the umbrella (கவிகை) of a king who can bear bitter words. 1034 says those who have the shade of grain will see the shade of many umbrellas come under their own umbrella (தங்குடைக்கீழ்), which Parimelazhagar takes as their own king's. Both use the royal umbrella's shade for dominion, but 389 credits the king's character and 1034 credits the farmers' harvest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1034","b":"k:545","type":"develops","note":"545 says rain and harvest gather in the land of the king who rules justly (பெயலும் விளையுளும் தொக்கு). 1034 says those who hold the grain bring many kings' umbrellas under their own. The dependence runs in both directions: in 545 good rule yields harvest, and in 1034 harvest yields the king's wider rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1035","b":"k:1061","type":"develops","note":"1061 says that not begging (இரவாமை), even from those who give gladly without concealing (கரவாது), is worth ten million times more. 1035 says those whose way is to eat what their own hands produce do not beg (இரவார்) and give to askers without concealing (கரவாது ஈவர்). 1035 names the livelihood that makes the ideal of 1061 possible, and puts the farmer on the giving side of the same exchange.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1037","b":"k:1038","type":"qualifies","note":"1037 says that if ploughed soil is dried until a measure of dust (தொடிப்புழுதி) falls to a quarter (கஃசு), the crop thrives without even a handful of manure. 1038 says manuring is better than ploughing (ஏரினும் நன்றால் எருவிடுதல்). The two set tillage and manure against each other from opposite sides: thorough working of the soil can replace manure, yet as a rule manure counts for more than ploughing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1038","b":"k:1147","type":"same_image","note":"1038 treats manure (எரு) and water (நீர்) as what makes a crop grow. In 1147 the woman says her love-sickness grows with the town's gossip as manure and her mother's words as water (கெளவை எருவாக அன்னைசொல் நீராக). The Book of Love takes the two inputs that 1038 gives as farming advice and uses them as a figure for what feeds her passion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:553","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1039 says that if the owner (கிழவன்) stays away and does not go to it, the land turns against him and sulks like a wife. 553 says the king who does not look into things and do justice every day loses his land day by day (நாடொறும் நாடு கெடும்). In both, land is lost through the owner's or ruler's lack of personal attention; Parimelazhagar reads 1039 too as requiring a daily visit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1040","type":"same_image","note":"1039 compares the land to a wife (இல்லாள்) who sulks when her owner stays away. 1040 calls the land a good woman (நிலமென்னும் நல்லாள்) who laughs at those who sit idle pleading poverty. The chapter closes with two verses that make the land a woman who responds to how a man treats her, by sulking in one and by laughing in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1039","b":"k:1304","type":"same_image","note":"1039 describes neglected land with the Book of Love's words for a lovers' sulk: it resents and sulks like a wife (புலந்து ... ஊடி விடும்). 1304, in the chapter on sulking, says that not appeasing one who has sulked (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root. The figures run in opposite directions: the land is compared to a sulking woman in 1039, and the neglected sulking woman to a plant in 1304.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1040","b":"k:616","type":"agrees","note":"616 says effort creates prosperity and lack of effort brings in want (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்திவிடும்). 1040 pictures the Land laughing at people who claim poverty while sitting idle (அசைஇ இருப்பார்). Both hold that such poverty comes from not working; 1040 states it as mockery from the land that would have paid them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1043","b":"k:171","type":"develops","note":"171 says that coveting another's goods without fairness ruins the family (வெஃகின் குடிபொன்றி). 1043 names poverty itself a craving (நல்குரவு என்னும் நசை) and gives it the same effect on ancient descent. Read together, 1043 treats poverty as harmful to a lineage through the desire it breeds, the mechanism 171 states for covetousness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1045","b":"k:1056","type":"develops","note":"1045 says many sufferings arrive within the one affliction called poverty (நல்குரவு என்னும் இடும்பையுள் பல்குரைத் துன்பங்கள்). 1056 says that on seeing those free of the affliction of concealing, all the afflictions of poverty perish together (நிரப்பிடும்பை எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்). The next chapter answers this verse in its own terms: the sufferings that come as a group also leave as a group.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1047","b":"k:117","type":"qualifies","note":"1047 says the person in poverty unconnected with virtue (அறஞ்சாரா நல்குரவு) is looked on as a stranger even by his own mother. 117 says the world does not count as ruin the low state of one who stays in virtue (நன்றிக்கண் தங்கியான் தாழ்வு). The qualifier அறஞ்சாரா in 1047 marks the boundary 117 draws: estrangement follows poverty without virtue, while poverty with virtue is not held against a person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1048","b":"k:532","type":"same_image","note":"1048 is a poor man's cry: will the want that as good as killed me yesterday come today too (நெருநலும் கொன்றது போலும் நிரப்பு)? 532 uses the same noun and verb in a simile, constant poverty killing understanding (நிச்ச நிரப்புக் கொன்றாங்கு). Both treat நிரப்பு as something that kills and that returns daily; Parimelazhagar glosses both with a man who fills his stomach each day only with great pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1049","b":"k:896","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1049 says one can even sleep in the midst of fire, but in want there is no closing the eyes (நெருப்பினுள் துஞ்சலும் ஆகும்). 896 says one burned by fire may yet survive, but those who go on offending the great will not (எரியால் சுடப்படினும் உய்வுண்டாம்). Both measure a non-physical danger by granting that fire can be endured and denying the same of the thing compared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1049","b":"k:1136","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1049 says that in poverty the eyes cannot close by any means (யாதொன்றும் கண்பாடு அரிது). In 1136 the man says his eyes will not close because of the girl, even at midnight (படல் ஒல்லா ... என் கண்). The same sign, eyes that cannot close, marks want in the Book of Wealth and longing in the Book of Love; 1136 is a lover's speech, so this is a shared figure and not a shared teaching.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1051","b":"k:1067","type":"agrees","note":"1051 says ask when you find those fit to be asked (இரத்தக்கார்க் காணின்), and if they withhold, the blame is theirs. 1067, in the chapter on the dread of begging, says: if you must beg, do not beg from those who conceal (கரப்பார் இரவன்மின்). The two chapters take opposite attitudes to begging but give the same rule about whom to ask.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1052","b":"k:224","type":"parallel_domain","note":"224 speaks from the giver's side: being asked is painful (இன்னாது இரக்கப்படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face. 1052 speaks from the asker's side: asking is a pleasure if what is asked comes without suffering (துன்பம் உறாஅ வரின்). Each verse makes the pain or pleasure of one party depend on how the exchange goes for the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1052","b":"k:1066","type":"tension","note":"1052 says even asking is pleasure to a person (இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல்) when the thing comes without pain. 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking, even asking water for a cow (நாவிற்கு இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்). They conflict if 1052 is read as commending asking under good conditions; 1052 speaks of pleasure and 1066 of disgrace, so the conflict depends on taking a disgraceful act as unable to be a pleasant one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:218","type":"develops","note":"218 says those whose sight knows what is due (கடனறி காட்சியவர்) do not falter in ஒப்புரவு even when they lack means. 1053 uses the same description, கடனறிவார், for the people before whom asking has a beauty; Manakkudavar glosses them as those who know ஒப்புரவு. The pairing identifies the fit giver of the begging chapter with the person 218 describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1053","b":"k:708","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1053 says there is a beauty even in asking while standing before those of unconcealing heart who know what is due (கடனறிவார் முன்நின்று இரப்பும்). 708 says that if one has people who look within and understand, it is enough to stand looking at their face (முகம்நோக்கி நிற்க அமையும்). Parimelazhagar reads 1053 the same way: standing before such people is enough and nothing need be said, which is why the asking keeps some grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1054","b":"k:1061","type":"qualifies","note":"1054 says asking from those who do not know concealing even in a dream is like giving (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). 1061 takes the same givers, who give gladly without concealing and are dear as one's eyes (கரவாது உவந்தீயும் கண்ணன்னார்), and says not asking even of them is worth ten million times more (இரவாமை கோடி உறும்). 1061 limits the praise in 1054: the best case of asking still ranks below not asking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1055","b":"k:571","type":"parallel_domain","note":"571 says the world exists because the beauty called kindly regard exists (கண்ணோட்டம் ... உண்மையான் உண்டிவ் வுலகு). 1055 uses the same word and construction: asking goes on because the unconcealing exist (உண்மையால்). In both, a practice or the world itself is said to rest on the existence of a disposition in some people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1055","b":"k:1059","type":"develops","note":"1055 says askers take up standing before others because people who do not conceal exist in the world (கரப்பிலார் வையகத்து உண்மையால்). 1059 says givers would have no renown if there were none who wished to ask and receive (இரந்துகோள் மேவார் இலாஅக் கடை). The two verses state the dependence in both directions: asking exists because of givers, and givers' standing exists because of askers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1056","b":"k:1063","type":"tension","note":"1056 says that on seeing those free of the affliction of concealing, all the affliction of poverty perishes at once (நிரப்பிடும்பை எல்லாம் ஒருங்கு கெடும்). 1063 says nothing is harder than the hardness of thinking 'we will end the affliction of want by begging' (இன்மை இடும்பை இரந்து தீர்வாம்). They conflict on whether asking can end poverty's distress, if 1056 is read as a general claim; Parimelazhagar removes the conflict by reading 1063 as blaming the choice of begging over effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1056","b":"k:1068","type":"qualifies","note":"1056 gives the good outcome of asking: meeting those without concealment ends the afflictions of want. 1068 gives the other outcome: the unsafe boat called asking splits when it strikes the rock called concealment (கரவென்னும் பார்தாக்க). Both make the result of asking turn on கரவு in the person asked, so 1068 states the risk that 1056 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1057","b":"k:228","type":"parallel_domain","note":"228 speaks of the giver's joy, the pleasure of giving and being glad (ஈத்துவக்கும் இன்பம்), which hoarders do not know. 1057 speaks of the asker's joy, the heart glad within (உள்ளுள் உவப்பது), when the giving is free of scorn. The same verb உவ marks gladness on both sides of one act of giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1058","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"1058 says that without askers the world would be puppets coming and going, which makes askers part of a living world; Manakkudavar draws the conclusion that asking is therefore not to be held low. 1062 says that if one must live even by begging, let the maker of the world wander and perish (பரந்து கெடுக உலகியற்றியான்). They conflict on whether a world that contains begging is as it should be; the conflict depends on reading 1058 as approval and not only as a remark about what givers need.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1060","b":"k:250","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1060 tells the asker not to be angry at a refusal, since the affliction of his own want is witness enough (நிரப்பிடும்பை தானேயும் சாலும் கரி); Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it as witness that means are hard to come by for anyone. 250 says that when going against someone weaker, think of yourself before those stronger (வலியார்முன் தன்னை நினைக்க). Both restrain a person by turning him to his own experience as evidence of the other's position.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1063","b":"k:616","type":"develops","note":"616 says effort makes fortune and lack of effort brings want in (முயற்றின்மை இன்மை புகுத்தி விடும்); Parimelazhagar applies it to kings. 1063 calls it hardness (வன்மை) to think poverty's affliction can be ended by begging, and Parimelazhagar explains that the proper path, effort, is being set aside. 1063 applies 616's rule to the poor man and names begging as the wrong substitute for effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1063","b":"k:1068","type":"develops","note":"1063 calls hard the thought that poverty can be ended by begging (இரந்து தீர்வாம்). 1068 gives a picture of why: begging is a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits when it strikes the rock of concealment (கரவென்னும் பார்). Manakkudavar says 1068 states the cause of poverty not ending, which makes it the reason behind 1063's claim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1064","b":"k:218","type":"parallel_domain","note":"218 says those who see their duty do not slacken in ஒப்புரவு even in a season without means (இடனில் பருவத்தும்). 1064 says worth does not consent to asking even at a time without means (இடமில்லாக் காலும்). The same test, what a person does when resources are gone, is applied to giving in 218 and to asking in 1064: the good neither stop giving nor start begging.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:222","type":"agrees","note":"222 says taking is bad even if it is called a good path (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது). 1066 says that even if one asks for water for a cow (ஆவிற்கு நீரென்று இரப்பினும்), nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both take the cow as a case of asking for the sake of virtue. Both verses deny that a good purpose removes what is wrong with asking or receiving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:656","type":"parallel_domain","note":"656 says that even if one must see one's own mother hungry (ஈன்றாள் பசி காண்பான் ஆயினும்), one should not do what the worthy condemn. 1066 says that even asking water for a cow is degrading to the tongue (நாவிற்கு இளிவந்தது). Each picks the most sympathetic motive available, a hungry mother or a thirsty cow, and holds that it does not excuse the act.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1066","b":"k:1053","type":"tension","note":"1053 says that even asking, standing before those of unconcealing heart who know their duty, has a certain beauty (இரப்பும் ஓர் ஏஎர் உடைத்து). 1066 says nothing is more degrading to the tongue than asking (இரவின் இளிவந்தது இல்), even for a cow's water. They conflict if 1066 is read as covering every act of asking; on a narrower reading 1053 speaks only of asking from the right giver, which 1066 does not mention.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1067","b":"k:1077","type":"develops","note":"1067 asks beggars not to beg of those who conceal (கரப்பார்). 1077 describes such people from the other side: the base will not even shake a wet hand (ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார்) for anyone but those whose fist would break their jaw. 1077 shows what asking a concealer comes to, and why 1067 pleads against it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:306","type":"same_image","note":"306 says anger burns even the safe raft called one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணை). 1068 calls asking a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that breaks on concealment. The same word for safety (ஏமம், ஏமாப்பு) marks the vessel in both: one's people are the safe one in 306, and asking is named the unsafe one in 1068.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1068","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"1068 calls asking a boat without safety (ஏமாப்பில் தோணி) that splits on the rock of concealment; the commentators supply poverty as the sea it was meant to cross. In 1164 the woman says the sea of love exists but the safe raft to swim it (ஏமப் புணை) does not. Both use the figure of a sea that must be crossed and a vessel that fails, 1068 as a teaching about begging and 1164 as a lover's complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1069","b":"k:224","type":"agrees","note":"224 says being asked is unpleasant (இன்னாது இரக்கப் படுதல்) until one sees the asker's glad face. 1069 says the heart melts at the thought of asking. Taken together they say asking is painful on both sides, for the one asked in 224 and for the one who asks or contemplates it in 1069, and that only the gift ends the pain.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1069","b":"k:1057","type":"develops","note":"1057 says that on seeing those who give without scorn, the heart rejoices deep within (உள்ளம் உள்ளுள் உவப்பது உடைத்து). 1069 says that at the thought of asking the heart melts (உள்ளம் உருகும்), and at the thought of concealment even what remains is gone (உள்ளதூஉம் இன்றிக் கெடும்). Both follow the heart (உள்ளம்) through the act of asking; 1057 gives its state before a good giver and 1069 its state before a refusal. Parimelazhagar takes the heart in 1069 as the speaker's, while he reports others take it as the asker's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1070","b":"k:223","type":"agrees","note":"223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing' (இலனென்னும் எவ்வம் உரையாமை), and giving, are found in one of good family; Pope takes the words as the giver's excuse, while Parimelazhagar first takes them as the asker's plea. 1070 says the asker's life leaves when the word is spoken (சொல்லாட); Parimelazhagar takes this as the concealer saying 'no'. On Pope's reading of 223 both verses are about the word of refusal, which the well-born never say and which 1070 says kills the hearer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1070","b":"k:230","type":"develops","note":"230 says nothing is more bitter than dying, yet even dying is sweet when giving is not possible (ஈதல் இயையாக் கடை). 1070 says the asker's life goes at the word of refusal (சொல்லாடப் போஒம் உயிர்) and asks where the concealer's life hides (யாங்கொளிக்கும் கொல்லோ). 230 gives the giver's view that life without giving is not worth keeping; 1070 puts that standard to the one who can give and refuses, and wonders that his life stays.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1071","b":"k:600","type":"parallel_domain","note":"600 says those without inner drive are trees, and their being human in form is the only difference (மரம் மக்களாதலே வேறு). 1071 says the base resemble people exactly (மக்களே போல்வர்). Both treat the human shape as something that can be present without what makes a person, lacking drive in 600 and lacking character in 1071.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:201","type":"develops","note":"201 says those given to evil deeds do not fear, and the excellent do fear, the intoxication called evil deed (தீவினையார் அஞ்சார் விழுமியார் அஞ்சுவர்). 1072 says the base are more fortunate than those who know what is good, because they have no distress in the heart (நெஞ்சத்து அவலம் இலர்). 1072 puts 201's fact in mock praise: Parimelazhagar says the verse scorns by implication, and that the point is their not fearing blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1072","b":"k:839","type":"parallel_domain","note":"839 says the friendship of fools is very sweet (பெரிதினிது), because parting from it gives no pain. 1072 says the base are fortunate (திருவுடையர்), because their hearts have no distress. Both verses praise a bad thing for the absence of a pain that only something of value would cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1073","b":"k:1072","type":"agrees","note":"1072 calls the base fortunate (திருவுடையர்) for having no distress of heart, and 1073 calls them like the gods (தேவர் அனையர்) for doing as they please. Both give praise that is meant as blame; Parimelazhagar says so of each. The two together describe one condition, no inner check in 1072 and no outer one in 1073.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1073","b":"k:1075","type":"qualifies","note":"1073 says the base are like the gods because they too act as they please (மேவன செய்தொழுகலான்). 1075 says that where the base show proper conduct (ஆசாரம்) it is fear, and beyond that a little if there is something they desire. 1075 marks the limit on 1073's freedom: their doing as they please stops only at fear or gain; Parimelazhagar says doing what they like is their nature and conduct is not.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1074","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says greatness always bows and smallness adorns itself in admiration of itself (சிறுமை அணியுமாம் தன்னை வியந்து). 1074 says the base man, on seeing those worse than himself, outdoes them and swaggers (மிகப்பட்டுச் செம்மாக்கும்); this follows Parimelazhagar's reading of அகப்பட்டி as those of looser conduct, while Manakkudavar reads the word quite differently. On Parimelazhagar's reading 1074 shows where the self-admiration of 978 finds its material: in comparison with someone lower.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1016","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1016 says the high do not care for the wide world unless they have shame as their fence (நாண்வேலி). 1075 says the conduct of the base is fear (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம்), which Parimelazhagar takes as fear of harm from the king. Each verse names what keeps a kind of person within bounds: shame from inside for the high, fear from outside for the base.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1075","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"1075 says the base behave properly out of fear. 1078 says the base, like sugar-cane, are of use only when crushed (கொல்லப் பயன்படும் கீழ்). 1078 applies the rule of 1075 to giving: what fear does for their conduct, force does for their generosity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1077","b":"k:221","type":"develops","note":"221 says giving to those who have nothing is what giving is (வறியார்க்கொன்று ஈவதே ஈகை), and all other giving has the nature of a loan to be returned. 1077 says the base will not even shake a wet hand (ஈர்ங்கை விதிரார்) except for those whose bent fist would break their jaw; Parimelazhagar sums up that they give nothing to the weak and everything to those who hurt them. The base man's giving in 1077 is the reverse of 221's definition: it goes only to the strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1077","b":"k:1078","type":"develops","note":"1077 says the base give only to those who would break their jaw. 1078 states the same with a contrast and a figure: the worthy are of use at a word (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர்), and the base, like sugar-cane, when crushed (கொல்லப் பயன்படும்). Parimelazhagar treats the two verses as a pair on how the base give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:216","type":"parallel_domain","note":"216 says wealth with a person of நயன் is like a fruit-bearing tree ripening in the middle of the town (பயன்மரம் உள்ளூர்ப் பழுத்தற்று). 1078 says the base are like sugar-cane (கரும்புபோல்), of use only when crushed. Both use a plant's yield (பயன்) as the figure for how a person's wealth reaches others: the tree offers its fruit in the open, and the cane gives nothing until it is pressed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1078","b":"k:223","type":"develops","note":"1078 says the worthy are of use as soon as they are told (சொல்லப் பயன்படுவர் சான்றோர்). 223 says not uttering the misery of 'I have nothing', and giving, belong to one of good family; Parimelazhagar records a second reading on which the giver gives before the asker has to say it. On that reading 223 goes one step beyond 1078: the worthy of 1078 give at a word, and the well-born of 223 give before the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:918","type":"same_image","note":"In 1081 the man cannot decide whether she is an அணங்கு, a choice peahen or a woman, and his heart reels (மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு). 918 says the embrace of deceiving women (மாய மகளிர் முயக்கு) is called an அணங்கு for those without discerning sense. The same word names a spirit that overpowers a man in both books: here it is a lover's wonder, there a warning, and Parimelazhagar glosses it in 918 as a divine woman who takes life through desire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:1082","type":"develops","note":"1081 only asks whether she is an அணங்கு. In 1082, once she returns his look (நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல்), he says it is like an attacking அணங்கு (தாக்கணங்கு) who has also brought an army. The doubt of the first verse becomes a felt assault in the second, and Parimelazhagar reads 1082 as spoken after he has settled that she is human.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1081","b":"k:1112","type":"develops","note":"In 1081 his heart reels (மாலும் நெஞ்சு) because it cannot place her among goddess, peahen and woman. In 1112 he scolds the same heart for being confused at flowers (மலர்காணின் மையாத்தி நெஞ்சே), as if her eyes were like what many can see. The confusion moves from what she is to what could be compared with her, and by 1112 he treats it as the heart's error.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:775","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 775 a warrior whose glaring eye (விழித்த கண்) blinks when a spear is thrown counts that as running away. In 1082 the man looks, and her look answering his (நோக்கெதிர் நோக்குதல்) is like an army brought against him. Both treat meeting another's gaze as combat, one on the battlefield and one at first sight in love.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"1082 says her returning look is like an attacking spirit come with an army (தாக்கணங்கு தானைக்கொண் டன்னது): the look only wounds. 1091 finds two looks in her eyes (இருநோக்கு), one that gives the sickness and one that is its medicine (நோய்நோக்கு ... அந்நோய் மருந்து). The next chapter adds the healing side that 1082 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1082","b":"k:1113","type":"same_image","note":"1082 makes her answering glance an armed host (தானை). 1113, listing her beauties, calls her kohl-lined eyes spears (வேலுண்கண்). The eye as weapon is a fixed figure of the man's praise, used first as his alarm and later as a plain item of description.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1083","b":"k:765","type":"same_image","note":"765 defines an army as the force that can stand together even if Death comes raging at it (கூற்றுடன்று மேல்வரினும்). In 1083 the man says he now knows what is called Death (கூற்று): it has womanly grace and large warring eyes (பேரமர்க் கட்டு). Both set கூற்று in a battle; the Book of Wealth imagines standing against it, and the lover says he has met it in her eyes.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1083","b":"k:1226","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1083: 'I did not know before (பண்டறியேன்) what Death is; now I know'. 1226: the woman did not know (அறிந்ததிலேன்), while her husband had not left, that evening brings sickness. In both a lover says love has taught what was only a word before, the man at first sight and the woman in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:279","type":"parallel_domain","note":"279: the arrow is straight but cruel, the lute bent but sweet, so judge by what a thing does (வினைபடு பாலால் கொளல்). 1084 sets a girl of womanly gentleness (பெண்டகைப் பேதை) against eyes that look as if they eat lives; Parimelazhagar takes அமர்த்தன as 'were at odds' with her nature. Both verses turn on a form that does not match its effect, one as a rule for judging ascetics and one as a lover's surprise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 says Death that eats life (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று) does not advance on the days of one who lives by not killing. 1084 says the eyes of this womanly, artless girl have a look that eats the life of those who see them (கண்டார் உயிருண்ணும் தோற்றம்). The lover gives her eyes the standing epithet of Death, which the previous verse (1083) has just named.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1084","b":"k:1221","type":"same_image","note":"1084: her eyes have a look that eats the life of those who see them (உயிருண்ணும்). 1221: the woman tells evening it is not evening but a time that eats the life of those once wed (மணந்தார் உயிருண்ணும் வேலை). The same phrase marks what kills each lover: for the man her eyes at first sight, for the woman the evening hour in his absence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1085","b":"k:1089","type":"same_image","note":"In 1085 the doe (பிணை) is one of three things her look might be, beside Death. In 1089 her doe-like artless look (பிணையேர் மடநோக்கு) and her modesty are ornament enough, so added jewels are pointless. The same doe glance is first one part of a threat and then her natural adornment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1085","b":"k:1091","type":"develops","note":"1085 asks whether her look is Death, an eye or a doe, and says it has all three (இம்மூன்றும் உடைத்து); Parimelazhagar comments that it gives pleasure and pain together. 1091 states that plainly as two looks, one the sickness and one its cure. The puzzled question of 1085 is resolved into a doubleness he can name.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1086","b":"k:1087","type":"develops","note":"1086 wishes her brows would screen her eyes (மறைப்பின்), for then the eyes would not cause him trembling pain. 1087 says the cloth over her breasts is like the face-cloth over a rutting elephant's eyes (கட்படாம்). Both are about a covering over something that harms him: one he wishes for and one he sees, and Parimelazhagar says of the second that the cloth keeps them from killing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1086","b":"k:1171","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1086 the man says her eyes cause him trembling pain (நடுங்கஞர்) and wishes them screened. In 1171 the woman asks why her own eyes weep, since they showed him to her and so gave her this unending sickness (தண்டாநோய் தாம்காட்ட). Each lover names eyes as the cause of the pain; for him it is hers, for her it is her own.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1087","b":"k:24","type":"same_image","note":"1087: the cloth over her firm breasts is the face-cloth on a rutting bull elephant (கடாஅக் களிற்றின்மேற் கட்படாம்). 24: the renouncer guards the five senses with the goad of firmness (உரனென்னும் தோட்டியான்), a goad being the elephant-driver's tool. Both draw on the dangerous elephant held in check; the ascetic chapter makes the senses the elephant, and the lover makes her covered breasts the elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:1134","type":"develops","note":"1088 reports the first loss: his battlefield strength breaks at the sight of her forehead. 1134 says the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). The later verse generalises what 1088 exclaims, with manliness now lost along with shame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1088","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1088: his might (பீடு), feared even by enemies in battle, has broken (உடைந்ததே) before her bright forehead alone. 1258: the woman says the soft words of that deceiving thief are the weapon that breaks her womanly firmness (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). Each lover uses the language of war and breaking for the loss of the quality they prized in themselves, his strength and her restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1089","b":"k:95","type":"parallel_domain","note":"95: humility and sweet speech are a person's ornament and nothing else is (அணியல்ல மற்றுப் பிற). 1089: what use are foreign ornaments (ஏதில தந்து அணி) to one who has a doe's artless look and modesty? Both deny that added adornment counts beside an inborn quality, 95 as a rule of conduct and 1089 as a lover's praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1090","b":"k:1288","type":"qualifies","note":"1090 uses liquor as the lesser pleasure that love outdoes. 1288 uses it as an exact likeness: 'your chest is to us as liquor is to those who are drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), though it does shaming harm (இளித்தக்க இன்னா)'. The later verse grants what 1090 leaves unsaid, that the pull likened to drink goes on despite disgrace.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1102","b":"k:948","type":"qualifies","note":"948 tells the healer to examine the disease, then its root (நோய்முதல்), then the means that subdue it (அது தணிக்கும் வாய்), as three separate things. 1102 first grants the ordinary rule, that the medicine is other than the disease, and then says love is the exception, where cause and remedy are the same person. The lover's verse marks love's sickness as a case the method of 948 does not cover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1103","b":"k:213","type":"parallel_domain","note":"213 says that in the world of the gods or here (புத்தேளுலகத்தும் ஈண்டும்) it is hard to find another good like ஒப்புரவு, doing one's part for others. 1103 asks whether the lotus-eyed one's world is as sweet as sleeping on the beloved's shoulders. Both measure an earthly thing against heaven and prefer it; in the Book of Virtue it is a duty, and here it is a lover's own claim about his pleasure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1105","b":"k:265","type":"parallel_domain","note":"265: austerity is practised here because whatever is wanted is obtained as it is wanted (வேண்டிய வேண்டியாங்கு எய்தலால்). 1105: her shoulders are like each desired thing at the moment of desiring it (வேட்ட பொழுதின் அவையவை போலுமே). Both describe desire met exactly as it arises, promised to the ascetic through தவம் and claimed by the lover in her embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1105","b":"k:1101","type":"develops","note":"1101 says the objects of all five senses are found in her alone. 1105 says her shoulders are like whatever one craves, at the time one craves it. The first claim is about range, that she holds every kind of pleasure; the second adds timing, that she answers each wish as it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1106","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"64: gruel stirred by the small hands of one's own children is far sweeter than ambrosia (அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிதே). 1106: her shoulders are made of ambrosia (அமிழ்தின் இயன்றன) because their touch revives his life. Parent and lover both reach for அமிழ்து to measure the nearness of someone of their own; the parent ranks the child's gruel above it and the lover equates her with it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1106","b":"k:1124","type":"agrees","note":"1106: his life sprouts afresh whenever he touches her. 1124: she is to his life what living is when they are together, and what dying is when she leaves (வாழ்தல் உயிர்க்கன்னள் ... சாதல் அதற்கன்னள்). Both tie his உயிர் to her presence; 1124 states the other half, which 1106's 'each time' (உறுதோறு) implies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1107","b":"k:229","type":"parallel_domain","note":"229: eating alone to fill one's own store (தாமே தமியர் உணல்) is more bitter than begging. 1107: eating one's own after sharing it, in one's own house, is the model of sweetness, and the embrace is like it. Read together they give both halves of one judgement on eating: bitter when hoarded, sweet when shared.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1108","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"1108 praises the embrace with no gap. In 1187 the woman says she lay embracing, shifted a little (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன்), and at once pallor was on her thick enough to scoop up. The woman's verse gives a reason from her side why the unbroken embrace is the sweet one: the least separation brings the pallor of parting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1108","b":"k:1196","type":"agrees","note":"1108 specifies 'two who desire' (வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே): the close embrace is sweet to both. 1196: love on one side only is painful, and like a carrying pole it is sweet on both ends (இருதலையானும் இனிது). Both make sweetness depend on the love being mutual, 1108 in fulfilment and 1196 from the woman who lacks it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1109 counts sulking (ஊடல்) among the fruits of love. 1302 says sulking is like salt in the right amount, and letting it run a little long is like a little too much salt (சிறிது மிக்கற்றால் நீள விடல்). The later verse sets a limit on the first item of 1109's list: it is a gain only in measure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1109","b":"k:1326","type":"develops","note":"1109 names quarrel, making up and union together as rewards without ranking them. 1326 ranks them: as digesting is sweeter than eating, sulking is sweeter than union (புணர்தலின் ஊடல் இனிது). The later verse takes one item from 1109's list and gives it first place.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:940","type":"parallel_domain","note":"940: as a gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம்), life loves itself more with each suffering (உழத்தொறூஉம்). 1110: each closeness shows love to be more than he knew, as each learning shows ignorance. Both describe an attachment that grows through its own repetition; the gambling chapter gives it as a warning and the lover as a delight.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1110","b":"k:1145","type":"agrees","note":"1145: as drinking is wanted more each time one gets drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்று), love is sweeter each time it is exposed. 1110: love is like finding one's ignorance each time one learns, each time he is close to her. Both say love grows by repetition and does not wear out; 1110 takes its likeness from knowledge and 1145 from drink, and they differ in what is repeated, union or exposure by gossip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1111","b":"k:1289","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1111 says she is more delicate than the anicham (நின்னினும் மென்னீரள்); 1289 says love itself is more delicate than a flower (மலரினும் மெல்லிது காமம்) and few catch its right moment. The same comparative, softer than a flower, is applied first to the beloved's body and later to the experience of love.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1113","b":"k:906","type":"same_image","note":"1113 praises her as bamboo-shouldered (வேய்த்தோளவட்கு). 906, in the chapter on men ruled by their wives, says those who fear the wife's bamboo-like shoulders (அமையார்தோள் அஞ்சுபவர்) have no standing even if they live like the gods. The same stock epithet of beauty is admired in the Book of Love and named in the Book of Wealth as the thing an unmanned husband fears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1113","b":"k:1084","type":"develops","note":"1113 lists her features as metaphors: body a tender shoot (முறி), teeth pearls, and kohl-lined eyes that are spears (வேலுண்கண்). 1084 had already noticed the mismatch this list contains: her eyes, with a look that eats the life of those who see them, are at war (அமர்த்தன) with her gentle womanly nature. Read together, the spear in the inventory of soft things is the same contradiction 1084 names outright.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1115","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"475 warns that even a cart loaded with peacock feathers breaks its axle if the load is piled too high (அச்சிறும்). 1115 says she has worn anicham flowers without removing the stalks (கால்களையாள்), so no good drums will sound for her waist; Parimelazhagar explains that the waist will break under the weight and get the funeral drum. Both make the lightest possible load the breaking one, as counsel on overreach in 475 and as playful hyperbole about her slenderness in 1115.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1115","b":"k:1111","type":"develops","note":"1111 says she is more delicate than the anicham. 1115 pushes the claim to a comic limit: the unremoved stalks of that same flower (அனிச்சப்பூக் கால்களையாள்) are too heavy for her waist. The flower that was the standard of softness becomes a burden.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1116","b":"k:1081","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1081 the man, at first sight, cannot tell whether she is a goddess, a peahen or a woman, and his heart reels (மாலும் என் நெஞ்சு). In 1116 the confusion is moved to the sky: the stars cannot tell the moon from her face (அறியா) and wander from their places (பதியின் கலங்கிய). The lover's own bewilderment about what she is becomes, in the praise chapter, a bewilderment he attributes to the stars.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1116","b":"k:1117","type":"qualifies","note":"1116 says the stars cannot distinguish the moon from her face. 1117 immediately denies that the two are alike: the moon fills out from loss and has a spot, and her face has none (மறுவுண்டோ). Parimelazhagar reads 1117 as mocking the stars for missing an obvious difference, so the second verse withdraws the equality the first seemed to grant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1117","b":"k:782","type":"same_image","note":"782 uses the moon's phases for friendship: with the wise it fills like the crescent, with fools it shrinks like the moon after full. 1117 mentions the same cycle, a moon that shines only after filling its lost parts (அறுவாய் நிறைந்த), as a defect that her face does not share. The waxing that is a hopeful figure in 782 counts in 1117 as evidence of the moon's inconstancy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1118","b":"k:1210","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses address the moon with வாழி மதி. In 1118 the man tells it that it could have his love if it could shine like her face; Parimelazhagar hears the blessing as mockery. In 1210 the woman, left behind, asks it not to set so that her eyes may find the man who has gone without leaving her heart. The moon is a rival to be dismissed in the time of union and a helper to be begged in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1119","b":"k:1311","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1119 the man tells the moon that to resemble her face it must not appear for many to see (பலர்காணத் தோன்றல்). In 1311 the woman refuses the man's chest because all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்). Each lover applies the same standard from the other side: what is shared with every onlooker is no longer one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1120","b":"k:1111","type":"develops","note":"1111 opens the chapter by ranking her above the anicham in softness. 1120 closes it by making the anicham and swan's down hurt her feet like the nerunji fruit, a thorn (மாதர் அடிக்கு நெருஞ்சிப் பழம்). The flower moves from being less soft than she is to being painful to her. Parimelazhagar sets the verse in a refusal to take her on a desert elopement.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1121","b":"k:64","type":"parallel_domain","note":"64 says the gruel one's children have stirred with their small hands is far sweeter than amrita (அமிழ்தினும் ஆற்ற இனிது). 1121 says the moisture at her white teeth is like milk mixed with honey (பாலொடு தேன்கலந் தற்று). In both, affection makes something bodily and ordinary taste better than the finest food, for a parent in one verse and a lover in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1121","b":"k:1258","type":"same_image","note":"1121 calls her பணிமொழி, the woman of soft, low speech, in a verse about the sweetness of her kiss. In 1258 the woman uses the same word for his speech: the soft words (பணிமொழி) of that thief of many deceits are the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve. The quality he savours in her is the one she names in him as a weapon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1123","b":"k:1126","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1123 the man orders the image in the pupil of his eye (கருமணியிற் பாவாய்) to leave, since otherwise there is no room for the woman he loves. In 1126 the woman says her lover never leaves her eyes (கண்ணுள்ளின் போகார்) and is too subtle to be hurt when she blinks. Both lovers place the other inside the eye; he has to make room for her, and she has him there already.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1124","b":"k:1122","type":"develops","note":"1122 says their bond is like that of body and life. 1124 draws the consequence: together she is to his life what living is (வாழ்தல்), and when she leaves she is what dying is (சாதல்). The comparison in 1122 becomes a statement of what separation will mean.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1124","b":"k:1151","type":"agrees","note":"In 1124 the man says her leaving is death to his life (சாதல் அதற்கன்னள் நீங்கும் இடத்து). In 1151 the reply to his announced journey is that news of his quick return should be told to those who will be alive then (வாழ்வார்க் குரை); Parimelazhagar gives the line to her friend, Manakkudavar to the woman. Each side says that separation is not survivable, he at a brief parting after a meeting and her side when he leaves on a long journey.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1126","b":"k:1129","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1126 she says her lover does not leave her eyes and is not hurt even if she blinks (இமைப்பின் பருவரார்), because he is so subtle. In 1129 she will not blink, knowing he would be hidden (இமைப்பின் கரப்பாக்கு அறிவல்). The same conceit is used both ways within one chapter: blinking is harmless in the first verse and a risk in the second, which shows these are playful defences of him and not one consistent picture.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1126","b":"k:1218","type":"agrees","note":"1126 says he never leaves her eyes though the town thinks him gone. 1218 says that when she sleeps he is on her shoulder and when she wakes he hurries into her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து). Parimelazhagar frames both as her defence of him against the charge of absence: she answers by locating him somewhere inside herself at every moment.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1128","b":"k:1205","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1128 his presence in her heart is something she protects, to the point of avoiding hot food. In 1205 the same presence is a complaint: he keeps her out of his own heart and yet is not ashamed to come into hers without pause (எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்). The later verse adds what 1128 leaves out, the question whether the dwelling is mutual.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1128","b":"k:1249","type":"agrees","note":"1128 says that since her lover is in her heart (நெஞ்சத்தார் காதலவராக) she fears eating anything hot, knowing it would burn him. 1249 uses almost the same opening, உள்ளத்தார் காதலவராக, to ask her heart whom it goes out looking for when he is within it. The playful premise of 1128, said to defend him, is used in 1249 to rebuke her own restless heart.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1130","type":"develops","note":"1129 and 1130 end with the same line, ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர். 1129 gives the town's ground for blame, her sleeplessness, which she explains as refusing to blink. 1130 states her answer directly: he lives gladly in her heart always (உவந்துறைவர் உள்ளத்துள் என்றும்), while the town says he lives apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1129","b":"k:1220","type":"agrees","note":"In 1129 she does not blink for fear of losing sight of him, and for this the town calls him a stranger without love (ஏதிலர் என்னும் இவ் வூர்). In 1220 the people of the town say he has left her in waking life, and she asks whether they do not see him in dreams (கனவினால் காணார்கொல்). In both she sets the town's outward evidence against her inner experience of his presence, first in the eye and then in the dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1130","b":"k:1204","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1130 she is sure that he lives in her heart, and gladly (உவந்துறைவர்), against the town's talk. In 1204 she affirms the same thing, that he is always in her heart (எந்நெஞ்சத்து ஓஒ உளரே அவர்), and then asks whether she too has a place in his (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து). The confidence she shows the town in 1130 does not answer the doubt she admits in 1204: his presence in her heart says nothing about her place in his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1131","b":"k:1137","type":"qualifies","note":"1131 calls the palmyra horse the only safeguard for those who suffer in love. 1137 praises womanhood that suffers a sea of desire (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும்) and still does not mount it (மடலேறா). The same verb உழந்து marks the same suffering, so 1137 limits 1131: the remedy is one a woman forgoes, and forgoing it is counted as worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1131","b":"k:1164","type":"qualifies","note":"1131 has the man say that for those worn down by love there is no strength that is a safeguard (ஏமம்) except the palmyra horse (மடலல்லது இல்லை வலி). In 1164 the woman says the sea of love exists but the safe raft to swim it does not (ஏமப் புணைமன்னும் இல்). The one ஏமம் he names is a man's recourse; her verse shows a sufferer for whom even that is missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1132","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"In 1247 the woman tells her heart to give up either desire or shame, since she cannot bear the two together (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண்விடு). 1132 is that choice made: the man keeps desire and puts shame aside (நாணினை நீக்கி) to ride the palmyra horse. Read together, 1247 states the dilemma and 1132 shows one way out of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1026","type":"same_image","note":"1026 defines நல்லாண்மை as making oneself fit to manage the house one was born into (இல்லாண்மை ஆக்கிக் கொளல்). 1133 uses the same word for what the lover had before (நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன்) and has now exchanged for the palmyra horse. Set beside 1026's definition, the lover's loss is of the capacity that makes a man of use to his family, not only of pride.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1088","type":"develops","note":"In 1088 the man, on first seeing her, says his might (பீடு), which enemies dreaded in battle, has broken before her bright forehead. In 1133 he says the shame and good manliness he once had are replaced by the palmyra horse. The later verse carries the first collapse through to its end: what broke at first sight is now given up publicly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1133","b":"k:1254","type":"agrees","note":"In 1133 the man says he once had shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை பண்டுடையேன்) and today has the palmyra horse. In 1254 the woman says she used to say she had restraint (நிறையுடையேன் என்பேன்மன்), yet her desire passes concealment and comes out in public. Each speaker sets a former self-possession against its loss to love; his loss is நாண் and ஆண்மை, hers is நிறை.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:306","type":"parallel_domain","note":"306 says anger burns the safe raft that is one's own people (இனமென்னும் ஏமப் புணையைச் சுடும்). 1134 says the flood of love sweeps off the raft that is shame and good manliness (என்னும் புணை). In both a passion destroys the thing figured as the raft that keeps a person afloat: anger in the chapter on not being angry, desire here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1134","b":"k:1251","type":"agrees","note":"1134 has the flood of love carry away the raft called shame and good manliness (நாணொடு நல்லாண்மை என்னும் புணை). 1251 has the axe of desire break the door called restraint, bolted with the bar of shame (நிறையென்னும் நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு). Both make shame a built defence that love's force destroys, one in the man's voice and one in the woman's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1135","b":"k:1183","type":"agrees","note":"In 1135 the man says the girl with small bangles gave him (தந்தாள்) the palmyra horse and the grief that evening brings. In 1183 the woman says he took her grace and shame and gave in return (கைம்மாறா ... தந்து) sickness and pallor. Each lover describes the affliction as a gift from the other, and in both verses shame is part of what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1136","b":"k:1252","type":"agrees","note":"In 1136 the man's eyes will not close (படல் ஒல்லா) for the girl, so even at midnight (யாமத்தும்) he thinks of riding the palmyra horse. In 1252 the woman says desire, having no pity, rules her heart at its work even at midnight (யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்). Both use midnight, when all else rests, as the proof that love allows no rest.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1138","b":"k:71","type":"parallel_domain","note":"71 asks whether there is a bolt that can shut love in (அன்பிற்கும் உண்டோ அடைக்குந்தாழ்), since the small tears of the loving make it known. 1138 says காமம் breaks through secrecy and appears in public. The Book of Virtue says of அன்பு what the Book of Love says of காமம்: neither can be kept hidden, and each publishes itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1139","b":"k:1180","type":"develops","note":"In 1139 the woman's love, thinking 'not everyone knows' (அறிகிலார் எல்லாரும் என்றே), goes reeling through the street (மறுகின் மறுகும்). In 1180 she says the town easily learns the secret of those whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In both the secret is given away by the lover herself and not by the town: by her love going about in 1139, by her eyes in 1180.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1140","b":"k:877","type":"agrees","note":"877 advises not telling one's pain to those who do not know it (நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு). 1140 shows the reason from inside: those who have not suffered what the speaker suffered (யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா) laugh at her in plain sight. The statecraft maxim and the lover's complaint agree that pain gets no hearing from those who have not felt it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1140","b":"k:1219","type":"agrees","note":"In 1140 the woman says the unknowing laugh where she can see, because they have not undergone what she has (யாம்பட்ட தாம்படா ஆறு). In 1219 she says those who blame him for not being gracious in waking life are those who have not seen a lover in dreams (காதலர்க் காணாதவர்). Both explain the town's judgement by its lack of the lover's experience.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"1141 says that because gossip has risen the precious life holds on (அலரெழ ஆருயிர் நிற்கும்). 1213 says the woman's life exists because she sees in dreams the one who gives nothing in waking life (காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்). In each a lover's life is kept going by something that only stands in for the beloved: the town's talk in one, a dream in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1141","b":"k:1263","type":"agrees","note":"1141 says life stays because the gossip has risen. 1263 says the woman still exists by longing for his return (வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). Both verses name the one thing that keeps life from leaving; in 1141 it comes from outside, from the town, and in 1263 from her own hope.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1142","b":"k:1150","type":"agrees","note":"In 1142 the man says the town, not knowing how hard the flower-eyed woman is to reach (அருமை அறியாது), has given him gossip (அலரெமக்கு ஈந்தது). In 1150 the speaker says the town is raising the very talk 'we want' (யாம்வேண்டும் கெளவை எடுக்கும் இவ்வூர்). Both treat the town's gossip as an unintended service; Parimelazhagar reads ஈந்தது as help given, and reads 1150 as the friend counting on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1143","b":"k:1213","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1143 the man says the gossip the town knows is like having obtained what he has not obtained (பெறாஅது பெற்றன்ன நீர்த்து). In 1213 the woman sees in dreams the man who gives nothing in waking life, and lives by it. Both take a substitute for union as if it were union: public talk for him, a dream for her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1144","b":"k:1147","type":"agrees","note":"In 1144 the man says his love thrives through gossip (கவ்வையால் கவ்விது காமம்) and without it would lose its nature and go slack. In 1147 the woman says the town's gossip is manure and her mother's words water to 'this sickness' (நீளும் இந்நோய்). Both say gossip feeds love; he calls what grows காமம் and welcomes it, she calls it நோய்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1145","b":"k:1281","type":"qualifies","note":"1145 takes drinking as the model for love: each exposure makes love sweeter as each bout makes drink wanted. 1281 says being intoxicated at a thought and glad at a sight belong to love and not to liquor (கள்ளுக்கில் காமத்திற்கு உண்டு). So the likeness in 1145 has a limit the book itself states: liquor works only when drunk, love without contact.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1146","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"In 1146 the woman saw him one day only, and the gossip has spread like the serpent seizing the moon (திங்களைப் பாம்புகொண்டற்று), which Parimelazhagar glosses as spreading everywhere. 957 says a fault in the well-born shows high, like the spot on the moon in the sky (மதிக்கண் மறுப்போல்). Both use the moon as the thing whose blemish everyone sees: a brief event or a small fault becomes public because of where it sits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1147","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1147 makes the sickness of love a crop, manured by gossip and watered by a mother's words, that grows long (நீளும் இந்நோய்). 1227 makes the same இந்நோய் a flower that buds in the morning and opens in the evening. In both the woman describes her lovesickness as a plant with its own growth, fed in 1147 and timed in 1227.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1148","b":"k:1144","type":"develops","note":"1144 says love thrives by gossip and would droop without it. 1148 puts the same point as a rebuttal of those who expect gossip to end the affair: it is ghee on fire. 1148 gives the image and the opposing view that 1144's plain statement lacks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1148","b":"k:1260","type":"same_image","note":"1148 says that to think of putting out love with gossip is like thinking to put out fire with ghee (நெய்யால் எரி நுதுப்பேம்). 1260 asks whether those whose hearts are like fat put in fire (நிணம் தீயில் இட்டன்ன) can say they will hold out in a sulk. Both use fat meeting fire for love: in 1148 the fat feeds the fire, in 1260 the heart is the fat that melts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1149","b":"k:1297","type":"agrees","note":"In 1149 the woman asks whether she can be ashamed at gossip (அலர்நாண ஒல்வதோ) now that he has left her. In 1297 she says she has forgotten even shame (நாணும் மறந்தேன்), caught in a heart that cannot forget him. In both her shame gives way, in 1149 because his leaving has made it pointless and in 1297 because her own heart overrides it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1150","b":"k:1143","type":"agrees","note":"1150 says the town is raising the gossip 'we want' (யாம்வேண்டும் கெளவை), and the lover will grant what is asked if he wishes. In 1143 the man asks whether the gossip the town knows is not his to have, since it is like gaining what he has not gained. Both sides welcome the talk: for him it stands in for union, and in 1150, on Parimelazhagar's reading, it is the ground for asking him to take her away.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1161","b":"k:1253","type":"agrees","note":"Both open with the same words, மறைப்பேன்மன் ('I would hide it'), and both say the attempt fails: in 1161 the pain rises like spring water under baling, in 1253 desire shows itself without her intending it, like a sneeze (குறிப்பின்றித் தும்மல்போல்). Read together, one figure says concealment makes it grow and the other that it escapes her will altogether.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1162","b":"k:1200","type":"develops","note":"1162: she cannot hide the sickness, and telling it to the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). 1200: she tells her heart that it means to tell its pain to one who is not close (உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய்) and might as well fill up the sea. Parimelazhagar reads both as about sending him word; 1200 adds futility to the shame that 1162 gives as the obstacle.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1163","b":"k:1017","type":"develops","note":"1017: those ruled by shame give up life for shame and do not give up shame for life (நாணால் உயிரைத் துறப்பர்). 1163 shows that ranking from inside: she drops neither love nor shame, and it is her life, the pole (உயிர் காவா), that takes the strain; Parimelazhagar adds that the life will snap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1164","b":"k:10","type":"parallel_domain","note":"10: the great sea of birth is swum by those who reach the Lord's feet and not by others (பிறவிப் பெருங்கடல் நீந்துவர்); Parimelazhagar supplies the feet as the raft. 1164: the sea of love exists and the raft to swim it does not (அது நீந்தும் ஏமப்புணை). The same structure of a sea crossed only with a support appears in the first chapter and in a separated woman's complaint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1165","b":"k:805","type":"qualifies","note":"1165: she asks what those who bring grief in friendship would do as enemies; Parimelazhagar says this is aimed, in a sulk, at the friend who has not sent a message. 805 advises that when friends do something hurtful (நோதக்க நட்டார் செயின்) it should be understood as folly or as great intimacy. 805 offers the charitable reading that her question refuses.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1166","b":"k:629","type":"parallel_domain","note":"629: one who does not crave the pleasure within pleasure does not come to pain within pain (இன்பத்துள் இன்பம் விழையாதான்). 1166: the pleasure of love is a sea and its pain when it afflicts is larger still (துன்பம் அதனிற் பெரிது). The woman's experience fits the link between relishing pleasure and suffering pain that 629 states as a rule in the Book of Wealth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1166","b":"k:1137","type":"same_image","note":"1137 speaks of suffering a love like the sea (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும்) and praises womanhood that still does not mount the palmyra horse. 1166 uses the sea twice over: love's pleasure is a sea, and when it afflicts (அடுங்கால்) the pain is greater than that. The sea is the book's measure for love, and 1166 says the measure is exceeded.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1136","type":"agrees","note":"Both lovers are awake at midnight, with the same word யாமத்தும். The man in 1136 says his eyes will not close and even at midnight he thinks of mounting the palmyra horse; the woman in 1167 says that even at midnight she alone is there (யானே உளேன்), swimming without sight of shore. Sleeplessness in the dead of night is given to both sides.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1167","b":"k:1164","type":"develops","note":"1164 says the sea of love exists and there is no raft to swim it. 1167 continues the figure: she has swum the fierce flood (காமக் கடும்புனல் நீந்தி) and sees no shore (கரை காணேன்), and at midnight there is only herself. The second verse turns the missing raft into an actual, endless swim.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1168","b":"k:1222","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1168: the night has put all living beings to sleep and, she says with pity (அளித்து), has no companion but her. 1222: she tells the evening it looks sad-eyed (புன்கண்ணை) and asks whether its partner is as hard as hers. In both she treats a time of day as a fellow sufferer and puts her own state onto it; Parimelazhagar says as much of 1222.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1169","b":"k:1235","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase கொடியார் கொடுமை, 'the cruelty of the cruel one', for the absent man. In 1235 her shoulders, their old beauty faded, declare it; in 1169 she uses it as the measure the long nights exceed (கொடுமையின் தாம் கொடிய). 1169 moves the charge of cruelty from the man to the time itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1169","b":"k:1269","type":"agrees","note":"1169: the nights that pass so long these days (நெடிய கழியும் இரா) are crueller than the cruel one's cruelty. 1269: for those who pine with a set day of return in mind, one day passes like seven (ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல்). Both say that time stretches for the one who waits.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1170","b":"k:1167","type":"same_image","note":"In 1167 she herself swims the fierce flood of love and sees no shore; in 1170 it is her eyes that swim in flood water (வெள்ளநீர் நீந்தல), which is her tears. The chapter's water figure for love ends as literal water in the eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1170","b":"k:1244","type":"develops","note":"1170: if her eyes could go where he is as her heart does (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்), they would not be swimming in a flood of tears. 1244 turns the wish into a request: heart, take the eyes along when you go (கண்ணும் கொளச்சேறி), for they consume her in wanting to see him. Both rest on the idea that the heart can travel to him and the eyes cannot.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1091","type":"same_image","note":"1091 (the man): her kohl-lined eyes hold two looks, one that gives the sickness and one that is its cure (நோய்நோக்கு ... மருந்து). 1171 (the woman): her own eyes showed him to her and so she came to know an unabating sickness (தண்டாநோய்), and she asks why they weep. Eyes are the source of love's sickness in both, the other's eyes for the man and her own for the woman, and only his verse has a cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1171","b":"k:1244","type":"agrees","note":"1171: she asks why her eyes weep, since they showed him to her (தாம் காட்ட யாம் கண்டது). 1244: the eyes consume her in their wish to see him (அவர்க்காணல் உற்று). In both she speaks of her eyes as agents apart from herself, with wants of their own that she pays for.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1172","b":"k:535","type":"parallel_domain","note":"535: one who slipped by not guarding beforehand will grieve afterwards over his own error (முன்னுறக் காவாது ... பின்னூறு இரங்கிவிடும்). 1172: the eyes that looked without foresight now toil in misery (பைதல் உழப்பது). Parimelazhagar's comment on 1172 uses the same terms, that those who do not guard against what is coming can only bear it when it arrives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1172","b":"k:792","type":"parallel_domain","note":"792: the friendship of one who does not take it after examining again and again ends in grief that kills him (ஆய்ந்தாய்ந்து கொள்ளாதான் கேண்மை). 1172, on Parimelazhagar's reading: the eyes looked that day without examining and understanding what would follow (தெரிந்துணரா நோக்கிய) and now suffer. The rule about forming ties without scrutiny is applied to a look. Manakkudavar reads the first phrase differently, as having looked with knowledge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1173","b":"k:435","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1173: the eyes looked hastily of themselves (கதுமெனத் தாம் நோக்கி) and now weep of themselves, which is laughable. 435: the life of one who does not guard before a fault comes is destroyed like straw before fire (வருமுன்னர்க் காவாதான்). Parimelazhagar explains the laughter in 1173 with that same phrase: it is laughable to those who guard before it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1173","b":"k:1140","type":"same_image","note":"1140: the ignorant laugh where she can see it (நகுப அறிவில்லார்), because they have not suffered what she has. 1173: she finds her own eyes' plight fit to be laughed at (நகத்தக்கது உடைத்து). Laughter at a lover's suffering comes from outsiders in one verse and from the sufferer herself in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1174","b":"k:320","type":"parallel_domain","note":"320: all pain falls on those who caused pain (நோயெல்லாம் நோய்செய்தார் மேலவாம்). 1174: the eyes that fixed an inescapable sickness in her (உய்வில்நோய் என்கண் நிறுத்து) have themselves dried up and cannot weep. Parimelazhagar reads 1174 in these terms: having done her harm first, harm came to them of itself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1175","b":"k:1166","type":"develops","note":"1166 says love's pain, when it afflicts, is greater than the sea (அதனிற் பெரிது). 1175 repeats the measure as an epithet, a love-sickness the sea cannot hold (கடலாற்றாக் காமநோய்), and goes on to assign blame for it to her eyes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1176","b":"k:1173","type":"develops","note":"1173 finds it laughable that the eyes looked of themselves and weep of themselves. 1176 goes a step further: it is sweet (ஓஒ இனிதே) that the eyes which gave her this sickness have fallen into it too (தாஅம் இதற்பட்டது). The sequence moves from mockery of her eyes to satisfaction at their suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1177","b":"k:1174","type":"develops","note":"1174 reports that the eyes have dried up and cannot weep (நீருலந்த). 1177 wishes exactly that on them: may the eyes that longed and melted to see him suffer on until their inner water is gone (உள்நீர் அறுக). What one verse states as their condition the other pronounces as a curse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1179","b":"k:1295","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1179: when he does not come the eyes do not sleep, and when he comes they do not sleep (வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின் துஞ்சா). 1295: the heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting (பெறாஅமை அஞ்சும் பெறின் பிரிவு அஞ்சும்). The same two-sided bind is said of the eyes and of the heart, and Parimelazhagar explains 1179 by the fear of parting that 1295 names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1180","b":"k:1271","type":"agrees","note":"1180 (the woman): the town can easily learn the secret of those whose eyes are beaten drums. 1271 (the man): though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes go beyond her control (கரப்பினும் கையிகந்து) and have something to tell. Both lovers say her eyes disclose what she means to conceal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1181","b":"k:1160","type":"develops","note":"1160 speaks, with irony, of the many who agree to the hard thing, endure the parting and live on afterwards (அரிதாற்றி ... பிரிவாற்றிப் பின்இருந்து வாழ்வார் பலர்); Parimelazhagar glosses அரிது ஆற்றி as consenting to the parting. 1181 is the woman who did consent (நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன்) and now cannot bear the result. Read together, 1181 shows the cost of the consent that 1160 doubted anyone could really give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1181","b":"k:1162","type":"develops","note":"In 1162 she cannot hide the sickness, and telling the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). In 1181 the question widens to யார்க்கு உரைக்கோ, to whom can I tell it at all, because she herself agreed to his going; Parimelazhagar adds that telling a self-made sorrow would itself be blame. The pair traces how the possible hearers for her complaint run out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1182","b":"k:1183","type":"develops","note":"1182 has the pallor ride over her body with the pride of saying 'he gave me' (அவர்தந்தார் என்னும் தகையால்). 1183 uses the same verb of giving for an exchange: he took her grace and modesty and gave sickness and pallor (நோயும் பசலையும் தந்து). The pallor that boasts of being his gift in one verse is entered as his payment in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1183","b":"k:211","type":"same_image","note":"211 says obligation wants no return, கைம்மாறு வேண்டா கடப்பாடு, and points to the rain, which the world cannot repay. 1183 uses the same word bitterly: as கைம்மாறு for the grace and modesty he took, he gave sickness and pallor. The word that names the return a good giver does not ask for is here the name of a bad bargain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1125","type":"same_image","note":"1125 and 1184 open with the same words, உள்ளுவன் மன்யான். The man in 1125 says he would remember her qualities if he ever forgot them, and he does not know forgetting; the woman in 1184 says she thinks of him and yet turns pale. The same claim of constant thought is his proof of love and her complaint that such thought gives no protection.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1184","b":"k:1250","type":"develops","note":"In 1184 she thinks of him and speaks only of his merits (உள்ளுவன் ... உரைப்பது அவர்திறம்), and calls it deceit (கள்ளம்) that the pallor comes all the same. In 1250 she tells her heart that by keeping in it the man who left, she will lose still more beauty (இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்). 1250 states as cause and effect what 1184 finds baffling: the faithful thinking does not hold the pallor off.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1185","b":"k:1277","type":"develops","note":"In 1277 her bangles understood before she did that the lord of the shore had parted from her (நம்மினும் முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). In 1185 the pallor spreads while he is still in sight. Both make the body register the parting faster than the mind or the facts do; 1277 pushes it to before the event.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1185","b":"k:1278","type":"agrees","note":"1185 points there to the lover going and here to the pallor spreading (உவக்காண் ... இவக்காண்); Parimelazhagar takes it as an earlier occasion when he had gone only a short way. 1278 says he left only yesterday and her body has been pale seven days (நெருநற்றுச் சென்றார் ... எழுநாளேம் மேனி பசந்து). One verse measures the pallor's speed in space and the other in time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1186","b":"k:285","type":"parallel_domain","note":"285 describes those who, with goods in view, watch for another's lapse (பொச்சாப்புப் பார்ப்பார்), and denies them a loving nature. 1186 gives pallor the same posture: it watches for the weak moment (அற்றம் பார்க்கும்), which Parimelazhagar notes is a figurative use of 'watch'. The book's picture of the thief waiting for inattention is the picture the woman uses for what takes her colour.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1186","b":"k:1238","type":"agrees","note":"1186 is the woman's account: pallor watches for a gap in her husband's embrace (முயக்கற்றம் பார்க்கும்) as darkness watches for a lamp to fail. 1238 is the man's memory of the same thing: he loosened the arms that held her and her forehead turned pale (முயங்கிய கைகளை ஊக்கப் பசந்தது). The two speakers confirm each other from either side of the embrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1187","b":"k:1239","type":"agrees","note":"In 1187 she lay embracing him, shifted a little (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன்), and at once the pallor was thick enough to scoop up. In 1239 the man recalls that a cool breeze cutting between them in the embrace (முயக்கிடைத் தண்வளி போழ) was enough to pale her eyes. Both verses, from the two partners, set the threshold of separation at the smallest physical gap.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1190","type":"qualifies","note":"1188 complains that the talk fixes on her pallor and not on his leaving. 1190 says it is good to get the name of pallor, provided they do not spread talk of his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்); Parimelazhagar sets it as her reply when the friend has blamed him. The complaint of 1188 should therefore not be read as a wish to have him blamed: in 1190 she accepts the very imbalance she resents.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1188","b":"k:1220","type":"qualifies","note":"1188 says people say only 'she has grown pale' and no one says 'he left her' (துறந்தார் அவர் என்பார் இல்). In 1220 the townspeople do say he has left her in waking life (நனவினால் நம்நீத்தார் என்பர்), and she answers that they must not see him in dreams. These are speeches at different moments, but together they show her objecting both when the town blames only her and when it blames him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1189","b":"k:1181","type":"develops","note":"1181 puts the consent on herself: நல்காமை நேர்ந்தேன், I agreed to his absence. 1189 names him as the one who brought her to agree (நயப்பித்தார்) and says let her body pale as it will if he stands well; Parimelazhagar reads நன்னிலையர் as free of fault, while Manakkudavar reads the condition as his suffering too. On Parimelazhagar's reading the two verses share out the responsibility and she keeps the cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1189","b":"k:1236","type":"agrees","note":"In 1189, on Parimelazhagar's reading, she accepts the pallor so long as the man who persuaded her is held blameless. In 1236 her arms thin and her bangles slip, and what pains her is hearing him called cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). In both she ranks his good name above her own wasting.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1190","b":"k:1232","type":"qualifies","note":"1190 hopes that her pallor can be talked of without his நல்காமை being talked of. In 1232 the friend says her pale, weeping eyes themselves seem to tell of the unkindness of the one she desired (நயந்தவர் நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்). The same word நல்காமை shows the limit of her wish: the pallor is itself the report against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1190","b":"k:1236","type":"agrees","note":"1190 says being named for her pallor is good if people do not broadcast his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்). 1236 says the hurt is in hearing him called cruel (கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து), more than in the slipping bangles. Both verses make the talk against him, and not her own disfigurement, the thing she cannot bear.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1191","b":"k:1108","type":"agrees","note":"1108, spoken in union, says the close embrace is sweet to two who desire each other (வீழும் இருவர்க்கு இனிதே). 1191, spoken in separation, says those loved by the ones they love (தாம்வீழ்வார் தம்வீழப் பெற்றவர்) have the stoneless fruit. Both place love's sweetness in the desire being mutual, using the same verb வீழ்; one says it from inside and the other from outside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1193","b":"k:598","type":"same_image","note":"598 says those without drive never reach the pride of saying 'we are generous' (வள்ளியம் என்னும் செருக்கு). 1193 says the pride of saying 'we shall live' (வாழுநம் என்னும் செருக்கு) suits only those loved by the ones they love. The same construction makes a justified pride depend on a prior condition: inner drive in the one case, being loved in return in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1194","b":"k:1191","type":"develops","note":"1191 says those loved by the ones they love have 'obtained' (பெற்றவர் பெற்றாரே) love's stoneless fruit; Parimelazhagar ties the word to earlier good deeds. 1194 gives the negative: even those who are esteemed are கெழீஇயிலர் if the one they love does not love them, which Parimelazhagar glosses as lacking good deeds and Manakkudavar as being without what they desire. The pair states the same condition from both sides, and shows the commentators reading it as fortune and not merit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1195","b":"k:1245","type":"qualifies","note":"1195 asks what the one she loves can do for her when he has no love for her (தாம்காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை). 1245 asks her heart whether a man who does not respond when she draws near (உற்றால் உறாஅதவர்) can be dropped as one who hates her (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ). The second speech limits the first: finding no benefit in an unreturned love does not make her able to give it up.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1196","b":"k:118","type":"parallel_domain","note":"118 praises being level like the scale-beam and not leaning to one side (ஒருபால் கோடாமை) as the ornament of the worthy. 1196 applies the same test to love: borne on one end (ஒருதலையான்) it hurts, and on both it is sweet. Evenness between two sides is a mark of justice in the Book of Virtue and a condition of happiness here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1197","b":"k:1252","type":"develops","note":"1252 says the thing called desire has no eye, that is no pity (கண்ணின்று), since it works her heart even at midnight. 1197 asks whether Kaman, who stays and acts on one person only, does not see her pain and misery (காணான்கொல்). The complaint moves from the feeling to the god, and both use sight as the test of his mercy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1197","b":"k:1308","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1308 the man asks what use suffering is when there is no lover who knows 'this one is suffering' (அஃதறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). In 1197 the woman asks whether Kaman fails to see her suffering. Both speakers treat pain as needing a witness, and each finds the one who ought to notice absent.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1199","b":"k:1198","type":"develops","note":"1198 calls hard those who live without receiving a sweet word from the beloved (இன்சொல் பெறாஅது). 1199 defines how little would count: though he gives no grace, any report of him is sweet to her ears (அவர்மாட்டு இசையும் இனிய செவிக்கு). Parimelazhagar reads இசை as any word at all, even that he is not coming, and links the two verses on that basis; Kalaignar takes இசை as his praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1199","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"In 1213 her life continues because in dreams she sees the man who gives no grace in waking life (நனவினால் நல்காதவரை). In 1199 the man who gives no grace (நல்கார் எனினும்) still sweetens her ears by any word of him. In both she is sustained by a substitute for his presence, a dream or a report, while saying outright that he withholds himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1200","b":"k:1164","type":"same_image","note":"1164 says the sea of love exists and the raft to cross it does not (காமக் கடல் ... ஏமப் புணை இல்). 1200 tells the heart to fill up the sea (கடலைச் செறாஅஅய்) instead of telling its pain to the unloving; Pope takes this sea as her own sorrow, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the sea that torments her. On Pope's reading the two verses share the sea as the measure of her grief, with crossing it and filling it both out of reach.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1207","type":"qualifies","note":"Both verses turn on the word உள்ளினும். In 1201 the man says remembering gives unending joy (தீராப் பெருமகிழ்); in 1207 the woman says that even while she remembers, her heart burns (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்), which Parimelazhagar explains as the separation burning her despite the memory. These are two speakers in two moods, so it is a limit on the first claim and not a contradiction in the book's teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1201","b":"k:1281","type":"agrees","note":"1201 (the man, for Parimelazhagar) says love is sweeter than toddy because it gives great joy that does not end even when only thought of (உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ்). 1281, spoken by the woman, makes the same comparison: elation at a thought and delight at a sight belong to love and not to toddy (கள்ளுக்கு இல் காமத்திற்கு உண்டு). The pairing shows the same judgment given from both sides of the couple, built on the same verb உள்ளுதல்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1209","type":"qualifies","note":"1202 says love is sweet in every way since no pain comes from thinking of the one you love (நினைப்ப வருவதொன்று இல், as the commentators read this edition's garbled line). In 1209 the woman's dear life is wasting because she thinks hard on his lack of tenderness (அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து). The pairing shows that the sweetness of 1202 depends on what is remembered: the shared days, not the present neglect. Manakkudavar in fact reads 1202 as conditional, 'if no pain comes'.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1202","b":"k:1296","type":"qualifies","note":"1202 claims that thinking of the beloved brings no pain. In 1296 the woman says that when she sat alone and thought (தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால்), her heart stayed only to eat her (என்னைத் தினிய); Parimelazhagar specifies that what she thought of was his cruelties. The two speeches mark out the cases in which remembering comforts and in which it wounds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1204","b":"k:1205","type":"develops","note":"1204 asks whether she too is in his heart, since he is always in hers (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர்நெஞ்சத்து). 1205 gives the answer as an accusation: he has put a guard on his heart against her (தம்நெஞ்சத்து எம்மைக் கடிகொண்டார்) and yet comes unceasingly into hers. Manakkudavar reads 1205 as the woman, having doubted, now deciding that he does not think of her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1205","b":"k:1291","type":"agrees","note":"1205 complains that he keeps her out of his heart while entering hers without pause (எம்நெஞ்சத்து ஓவா வரல்). 1291 states the same imbalance to her own heart: his heart stays his (அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல்), so why is hers not hers? In 1205 she blames him for the unequal exchange, and in 1291 she blames her own heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1206","b":"k:1213","type":"agrees","note":"1206: she exists only by remembering the days she had with him (உற்றநாள் உள்ள உளேன்), and asks what else she could live by. 1213: her life exists because she sees in dreams the man who gives nothing in waking (கனவினால் காண்டலின் உண்டென் உயிர்). Both make survival in separation depend on a mental substitute for his presence, memory by day and dream by night.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1206","b":"k:1263","type":"develops","note":"1206 says she lives by looking back at the days of union (உள்ள உளேன்). 1263 says she still lives by longing for his return (வரல் நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). The same verb உளேன் is supported in one verse by the past and in the other by the expected future; Parimelazhagar on 1206 notes that the other supports, such as a messenger from him, are missing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1207","b":"k:1159","type":"develops","note":"1207 says that even as she remembers, her heart is burned (உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1159 describes the same burning as a property of love's sickness: fire burns only when touched, and cannot burn when one moves away as love does (விடிற்சுடல் ஆற்றுமோ தீ). 1159 gives the general paradox of a fire that burns at a distance, and 1207 shows it at work inside memory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1207","b":"k:1262","type":"agrees","note":"In both verses the woman rejects the idea of forgetting him, which Parimelazhagar says the friend had advised in each case. 1207 asks what she would become if she forgot (மறப்பின் எவனாவன்); 1262 answers concretely that if she forgot today (இன்று மறப்பின்) her beauty would go and the bangles slip from her arms. Remembering hurts, but both verses hold that forgetting would be worse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1208","b":"k:1199","type":"develops","note":"1199 says that though the one she longs for gives nothing (நல்கார் எனினும்), even a word about him is sweet to her ear. 1208 measures what an ungiving lover leaves her more sharply: his whole gift (சிறப்பு) is that he does not object to being thought of. Parimelazhagar takes 1208 as scorn of the friend's promise that he will come and be kind, whereas Manakkudavar takes the not-being-angry as real grace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1208","b":"k:1320","type":"same_image","note":"The verses share the words நினை and காய். In 1208 the woman says that however much she thinks of him he is not angry (எனைத்து நினைப்பினும் காயார்), and that this is all the honour he does her. In 1320 the man reports that when he merely sits thinking and looks at her she is angry (நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும் காயும்) and asks whom he is thinking of. The absent lover tolerates everything because he is not there to care, while the present one is questioned about every thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1210","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 says that if her eyes could go where he is, as her mind does (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்), they would not be swimming in tears. 1210 looks for a way round that limit: on Parimelazhagar's reading, since both of them look at the moon, her eyes meet his there (கண்ணினால் காண). Manakkudavar reads it differently, as a complaint that the moon will not set and let her sleep and see him in a dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:1212","type":"develops","note":"In 1211 the dream arrives as the carrier of his message (காதலர் தூதொடு வந்த கனவு). In 1212 she wants to use the dream in the other direction: if her eyes would sleep, she would herself declare to him that she survives (உயலுண்மை சாற்றுவேன்). Parimelazhagar says this is spoken when she was thinking of sending a messenger, and that she would tell him in full what she would hold back from one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1211","b":"k:1268","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses end on the word விருந்து, a feast. In 1211 the woman (for Parimelazhagar) asks what feast she can make for the dream that came bringing her lover's messenger (யாதுசெய் வேன்கொல் விருந்து). In 1268 the man, away on the king's work, looks forward to the evening feast at home once the king has won (மாலை அயர்கம் விருந்து). The real feast waits on his return, and meanwhile she can offer hospitality only to a dream.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1212","b":"k:1179","type":"agrees","note":"1212 makes everything depend on a condition: if her eyes would sleep when she begs them (யானிரப்பத் துஞ்சின்). 1179 says why the condition fails: her eyes do not sleep when he does not come and do not sleep when he does (வாராக்கால் துஞ்சா வரின்துஞ்சா). Set beside chapter 122, this shows that the dream which consoles her in the following verses is not available to her at will.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1213","b":"k:1198","type":"qualifies","note":"1198 says no one in the world is harder than those who go on living without getting even a kind word from the one they love (வாழ்வாரின் வன்கணார் இல்). 1213 has the woman living on though he gives her nothing in waking life (நனவினால் நல்காதவரை), and explains it: her life exists because she sees him in dreams (கனவினால் காண்டலின்). The second verse answers the first's charge of hardness by naming what keeps her alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1214","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"1170 regrets that her eyes cannot travel to where he is as her mind can. In 1214 the dream does the travelling: it seeks out the man who gives nothing in waking life and brings him (நல்காரை நாடித் தரற்கு), and so there is pleasure in the dream. Parimelazhagar spells it out: the dream goes to the country he went to, finds him and delivers him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1215","b":"k:1201","type":"qualifies","note":"1201 says love gives a joy that does not end even in recollection (உள்ளினும் தீரா). 1215 says that what she saw in waking was sweet then, and the dream too is sweet just while it is seen (கண்ட பொழுதே இனிது). Manakkudavar glosses both pleasures as lasting for that moment; Parimelazhagar takes her point to be that dream and waking are therefore equal for her. On either reading the woman's sweetness is tied to the moment of seeing, where the man's persists in thought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:1169","type":"qualifies","note":"1169 calls the long nights of separation crueller than the cruel man himself (கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய). 1216 wants the night's state never to end, with waking itself abolished (நனவென ஒன்றில்லை ஆயின்). The difference is sleep: the night is an enemy when she lies awake and a refuge when it brings the dream, and Parimelazhagar accordingly has her call waking a villain (பாவி).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1216","b":"k:1329","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1216 the woman says that if there were no such thing as waking, her lover would not leave her in the dream (கனவினால் காதலர் நீங்கலர் மன்). In 1329 the man, in the middle of a lovers' quarrel, wishes the night would lengthen so that he can go on pleading (நீடுக மன்னோ இரா). Both wish away the end of the night and both close on the wistful particle மன்; hers is said of a dream meeting and his of a real one.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1217","b":"k:819","type":"parallel_domain","note":"819, on bad friendship, says the tie with those whose deeds are one thing and words another is bitter even in a dream (கனவினும் இன்னாது). In 1217 the lover who gives nothing in waking life hurts her in her dreams as well (கனவினால் ... பீழிப்பது). Both take the dream as the last place a person's unreliability should reach, and both find that it reaches there: one verse in the book of polity, the other in a lover's complaint.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1217","b":"k:1213","type":"qualifies","note":"The two verses open with nearly the same words, நனவினால் நல்காதவரை and நனவினால் நல்காக் கொடியார், and reach opposite verdicts on the dream. In 1213 seeing him in dreams keeps her alive; in 1217 she asks why the cruel one torments her in dreams (எம்மைப் பீழிப்பது). Parimelazhagar places 1217 after she has woken and found him gone, so the dream comforts while it lasts and wounds once it has ended.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1218","b":"k:1217","type":"qualifies","note":"Both verses describe the same event: he is with her in sleep and gone on waking. In 1217 this is his cruelty; Parimelazhagar explains the torment as his being on her shoulder in the dream and vanishing when she wakes. In 1218 she tells it in his favour: asleep he is on her shoulder (தோள்மேலர்), and awake he hurries into her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர் விரைந்து), so he never leaves her at all. Parimelazhagar says 1218 is her defence of him after the friend had spoken against him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1218","b":"k:1249","type":"agrees","note":"1218 says that on waking he quickly becomes one who is in her heart (நெஞ்சத்தர் ஆவர்). 1249 tells her own heart that the lover is inside it (உள்ளத்தார் காதலவர்), so there is no one to go out looking for. Both use his dwelling in her heart to deny that he is absent; 1218 adds the dream so that both night and day are covered.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1219","b":"k:66","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The two verses have the same construction: an opinion is held only by those who lack an experience. In 66, 'the flute is sweet, the lute is sweet' is said by those who have not heard their own children's prattle (மழலைச்சொல் கேளாதவர்). In 1219 those who blame the man for giving nothing in waking life are those who have not seen a lover in dreams (கனவினால் காதலர்க் காணாதவர்). Parental love in the first book and a lover's dream in the third both discredit a judgment by pointing to what its holders have not known.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1219","b":"k:1236","type":"develops","note":"1236: with bangles and arms slackening, what pains her is hearing him called cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). 1219 goes from that pain to a rebuttal: those who blame him (நோவர்) are people who have not seen a lover in their dreams. Parimelazhagar gives both verses the same occasion, the friend speaking ill of the man, and says she puts her reply in 1219 on strangers out of displeasure with the friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1221","b":"k:326","type":"same_image","note":"326 calls Death 'the life-eating' (உயிருண்ணுங் கூற்று) and says it does not advance on the days of one who does not kill. 1221 gives the same epithet, உயிருண்ணும், to the evening that comes on women parted from their husbands. The pairing shows the woman addressing evening in the book's own language for Death; Parimelazhagar's note on 1229 says she thinks of evening as கூற்றம்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1221","b":"k:334","type":"parallel_domain","note":"334 says what shows itself as a 'day' (நாளென ஒன்றுபோற் காட்டி) is a blade that saws through life (உயிர் ஈரும் வாள்), for those who perceive it. 1221 has the parted woman tell the hour it is not evening (மாலையோ அல்லை) but a thing that eats the life of those once wed (உயிருண்ணும் வேலை; Parimelazhagar takes வேலை as a final hour, Manakkudavar and Kalaignar as a spear). Both unmask a unit of time as something that consumes life, one as a general truth of impermanence, the other as one lover's experience of separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1223","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1223 says the evening that used to come budding with chill and looking wan (பனி அரும்பிப் பைதல்கொள் மாலை) now comes with revulsion budding and sorrow growing (துனி அரும்பித் துன்பம் வளர). 1227 says her sickness buds in the morning (காலை அரும்பி), swells through the day and opens in the evening. Both use அரும்பு, budding, for her pain, and together they give evening as both what brings the bud and when it opens.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1224","b":"k:1169","type":"develops","note":"1169 says the long nights now passing are crueller than the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமையின் தாம்கொடிய). 1224 says that where her lover is absent (காதலர் இல்வழி) evening comes like ஏதிலர், pitiless strangers or foes, on a killing ground (கொலைக்களத்து). Both make a stretch of time into a cruel agent set beside human cruelty, and 1224 names the condition: it is so only in his absence.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"k:1225","b":"k:319","type":"parallel_domain","note":"319 states a rule: hurt done to others in the forenoon (முற்பகல்) comes back to oneself in the afternoon (பிற்பகல்), of itself. In 1225 the woman asks what good she did to morning (காலைக்குச் செய்த நன்று) and what enmity she did to evening (மாலைக்குச் செய்த பகை). Her question assumes the same logic of return, that suffering answers something one did, and finds no deed of hers to explain evening's hostility.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1228","b":"k:1258","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1258 the woman says the soft speech (பணிமொழி) of her deceiving lover is the weapon that breaks her womanly reserve (பெண்மை உடைக்கும் படை). In 1228 the herdsman's flute is a weapon that kills (கொல்லும் படை). Both call a gentle sound a படை, one because it draws her to him and the other because it announces the evening without him.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1229","b":"k:1168","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1168 the night has put all living beings to sleep (மன்னுயிர் எல்லாம் துயிற்றி) and she alone is its companion, so her suffering is solitary. In 1229 she says that when evening spreads, bewildering the mind (மதிமருண்டு; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take this as her own mind), the whole town is bewildered and suffers (பதிமருண்டு பைதல் உழக்கும்). The two speeches picture the world around her in opposite ways, asleep and untouched in one and sharing her distress in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:1209","type":"agrees","note":"In 1209 the woman says her dear life is perishing (விளியும் என் இன்னுயிர்) as she thinks much on the lack of kindness of one who said they were not separate. In 1230 her life, which had not died till now (மாயா உயிர்), is dying in the dim evening as she thinks of the man whose nature is wealth (பொருள்மாலையாளரை உள்ளி). Both say that thinking of him is what wears her life away, and each names him by a reproach: his broken word in one, his preference for wealth in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1230","b":"k:1263","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1263 she says he left desiring victory with his own resolve as companion (உரன் நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்), and that she still lives by desiring his return (வரல் நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்). In 1230 thinking of the man given to wealth makes her life die. Both name him by the aim he left for, but in one speech the thought of him keeps her alive and in the other it kills; these are two moments of the same speaker, not a doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1232","b":"k:71","type":"parallel_domain","note":"71 says love cannot be bolted in, because the small tears of the loving make it public (புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்). 1232 says her eyes, pale and running with tears (பசந்து பனிவாரும் கண்), seem to tell of the unkindness of the one she desired (நல்காமை சொல்லுவ போலும்). The same mechanism, tears that disclose, works in both; in 71 they reveal the weeper's love, in 1232 they also accuse the absent lover.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1232","b":"k:1180","type":"agrees","note":"In 1180 the woman says the town easily learns the secret of those like her whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). In 1232 the friend says her pale, weeping eyes seem to be telling of his unkindness. Both treat the eyes as announcing what she would keep hidden; the woman says it of herself and the friend says it to urge her to bear up, as Parimelazhagar frames it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1233","b":"k:1157","type":"agrees","note":"In 1157 the woman asks whether the bangles slipping past her wrist do not proclaim (தூற்றாகொல்) that the lord of the shore has left her. In 1233 the shoulders that swelled on the day of union (மணந்தநாள் வீங்கிய தோள்) seem to announce fully that he has parted (தணந்தமை சால அறிவிப்ப போலும்). In both her body makes the separation public without her speaking; 1233 adds the contrast with how the same shoulders looked at union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1233","b":"k:1277","type":"same_image","note":"In 1277 her bangles understood before she did that the lord of the cool shore had parted (தணந்தமை ... முன்னம் உணர்ந்த வளை). In 1233 her shoulders announce that he has parted, with the same word தணந்தமை. In 1277 the body is first to know of the parting, and in 1233 it is the one that tells others.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1234","b":"k:1235","type":"develops","note":"1234 and 1235 share the line தொல்கவின் வாடிய தோள், shoulders whose old beauty has faded. 1234 describes the loss: with her partner gone (துணை நீங்கி) they have lost their fullness and the bangles slip. 1235 turns the same shoulders into testimony that declares the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்), so the repeated line moves from description to accusation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1234","b":"k:1262","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1262 the woman tells her friend that if she forgets him today (இன்று மறப்பின்), her beauty will leave her shoulders and her ornaments will slip off (கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து). In 1234 the beauty has faded and the bangles are slipping because her partner is gone (துணை நீங்கி). Read together, the bodily loss follows his absence whether she remembers or forgets, though in 1262 she holds that remembering is what prevents it.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1235","b":"k:1188","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1188 the woman complains that people say only 'she has grown pale' and no one says 'he has left her' (துறந்தார் அவர் என்பார் இல்). In 1235 the wasted shoulders themselves declare his cruelty. One speech says the visible change brings talk about her alone, and the other says the same kind of change speaks against him; the difference is in who is reading the body, the town or the friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1235","b":"k:1236","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1235 the friend, in Parimelazhagar's reading, says the shoulders with bangles lost and old beauty faded declare the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை உரைக்கும்). In 1236 the woman answers that with bangles and shoulders slackening, what pains her is the saying that he is cruel (அவரைக் கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). The second verse limits the first: the body's evidence stands, but she refuses the charge drawn from it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1236","b":"k:1169","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1169 the woman herself speaks of the cruelty of the cruel one (கொடியார் கொடுமை) when she says the long nights are crueller still. In 1236 she is pained when he is called cruel (கொடியர்). The pair shows that she uses the word in her own lament and does not accept it from another's mouth; these are different moments of a dramatised speaker, not a contradiction in teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1237","b":"k:1162","type":"qualifies","note":"In 1162 she says she cannot hide this sickness, and that telling it to the one who caused it brings shame (உரைத்தலும் நாணுத் தரும்). In 1237 she asks her heart whether it can gain honour by telling him of her wasting shoulders. The two give opposite values to the same act of telling him, shame in one and possible honour (பாடு) in the other; in 1237 it is the heart and not she herself who would carry the word.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1240","b":"k:1182","type":"same_image","note":"In 1182 the woman says pallor spreads and rides over her body with the pride (தகையால்) that he was the one who gave it. In 1240 the man says the pallor of her eyes felt distress (பருவரல்) on seeing what her forehead had done. Both give the pallor feelings of its own, pride in one and distress in the other, so that பசப்பு is spoken of as a party to the love and not only its symptom.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1240","b":"k:1238","type":"develops","note":"1238 says that when he loosened the embracing arms her forehead turned pale. 1240 says the pallor of her eyes itself came to distress (கண்ணின் பசப்போ பருவரல் எய்தின்றே) on seeing what the bright forehead had done (ஒண்ணுதல் செய்தது கண்டு). 1240 depends on 1238 for what the forehead did; Parimelazhagar explains that the eyes' pallor was abashed that the forehead had paled sooner than they did, at a smaller cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1241","b":"k:1102","type":"develops","note":"In 1102 the man says that medicines for other ailments are things other than the ailment (பிணிக்கு மருந்து பிற), but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து). In 1241 the woman, apart from him, asks her heart to think of any medicine at all (எனைத்தொன்றும் ... மருந்து) for her sickness. Read together, 1102 explains why her heart has nothing to offer: the only cure is the absent person who caused the illness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1242","b":"k:1195","type":"agrees","note":"1195 asks what those we love can do for us if they do not love in return (தாம் காதல் கொள்ளாக் கடை). 1242 draws the conclusion and turns it on her own heart: since he has no love (காதல் அவரிலர் ஆக), its suffering is folly (நோவது பேதைமை). The first states the uselessness of one-sided love, the second names persisting in it as foolishness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1242","b":"k:1308","type":"agrees","note":"1308, which Parimelazhagar gives to the man during a sulk, asks what use suffering is where there is no lover who recognises that one suffers (நோதல் எவன் ... அஃதறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). 1242 has the woman tell her heart that suffering is folly when he has no love (நோவது பேதைமை). Both speakers, from either side of the relationship, make pain depend for its point on the other's loving awareness of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1243","b":"k:1204","type":"develops","note":"1204 asks whether she too is in his heart, since he is always in hers (யாமும் உளேங்கொல் அவர் நெஞ்சத்து). 1243 gives her own bleak answer: a compassionate thought of her does not exist in the one who caused the sickness (பரிந்துள்ளல் பைதல்நோய் செய்தார்கண் இல்). The question of 1204 has by chapter 125 become a settled premise for scolding her heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1243","b":"k:1296","type":"agrees","note":"1243 asks the heart why it sits and suffers in thought (இருந்துள்ளி என் பரிதல்) when he has no pitying thought. 1296 says that when she sits alone and thinks (தனியே இருந்து நினைத்தக்கால்) her heart is there only to eat her (என்னைத் தினிய). Both describe sitting and remembering as self-inflicted harm, and each blames the heart for it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1244","b":"k:1283","type":"agrees","note":"1283 says that though he disregards her and does only as he pleases, her eyes will not rest without seeing him (கொண்கனைக் காணாது அமையல கண்). 1244 says the eyes, wanting to see him (அவர்க் காணல் உற்று), eat her. Both make the eyes an appetite of their own that her judgment of him does not govern.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1245","b":"k:807","type":"parallel_domain","note":"807, on old friendship, says that those whose bond came by the way of love do not cut off love even when ruinous things are done to them (அழிவந்த செய்யினும் அன்பறார்). 1245 asks whether she can let him go as an enemy merely because he does not return her closeness. The logic of long friendship in the Book of Wealth, that injury does not license abandonment, reappears as a lover's rhetorical question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1245","b":"k:1292","type":"agrees","note":"1245 asks the heart whether one can abandon as an enemy (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ) the man who does not respond when she draws close (யாம் உற்றால் உறாஅதவர்). 1292 complains that the heart, even having seen that he is not close (உறாஅதவர்க் கண்ட கண்ணும்), goes to him on the thought that he will not be hostile (செறாஅர் என). The two share the pair உறாதவர் / செறு, and show the same refusal to treat coldness as enmity, once owned by her and once blamed on the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1246","b":"k:1286","type":"develops","note":"1286 says that when she sees him she sees no faults, and when she does not see him she sees nothing but faults (காணுங்கால் காணேன் தவறாய காணாக்கால் காணேன் தவறல்லவை). 1246 calls the heart's anger in his absence false because it never survives the sight of him (காதலர்க் கண்டால் புலந்துணராய்). 1286 states the pattern plainly; 1246 uses it to discredit the anger felt while apart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1246","b":"k:1294","type":"agrees","note":"1246 tells the heart that when it sees the lover it cannot even sulk and then be reconciled (புலந்துணராய்), so its present anger is a false heat (பொய்க்காய்வு காய்தி). 1294 makes the same charge: the heart will not first raise a quarrel and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), so no one will plan with it. In both the heart is accused of being incapable of the sulk it pretends to.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1247","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1247 leaves open which of desire and shame is to go (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண் விடு). 1251, opening the next chapter, reports the outcome: the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint that shame had bolted (காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் ... நாணுத்தாழ் வீழ்த்த கதவு). The choice she offers her heart in 1247 is settled in 1251 by force, not by decision.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1248","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967, on honour, says that being called ruined is better than living by going behind those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார் பின் சென்று ஒருவன் வாழ்தலின்); Parimelazhagar takes this of following scorners for gain. 1248 calls her heart a fool for going behind the one who left without grace (பிரிந்தவர் பின்செல்வாய் பேதை). The standard of honour from the Book of Wealth is the one she applies to her own heart, though in love she cannot enforce it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1248","b":"k:1293","type":"agrees","note":"1248 calls the heart a fool for going after the man who left (பிரிந்தவர் பின்செல்வாய்) while grieving that he gives no grace. 1293 asks the heart whether it follows him as it likes (பெட்டாங்கு அவர் பின் செலல்) because the ruined have no friends. Both speak of the heart 'going behind him' as a desertion of her that she can see but not stop.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1250","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1207 says she does not know how to forget, and that even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1250 says that holding him in her heart will cost her yet more beauty. Both count remembering him as a harm to herself, while 1207 adds that forgetting is not available to her.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1250","b":"k:1262","type":"tension","note":"In 1250 she says that if she keeps in her heart the man who left without coming close (துறந்தாரை நெஞ்சத்து உடையேமா), she will lose still more beauty (இன்னும் இழத்தும் கவின்); Parimelazhagar frames this as a case for forgetting him. In 1262 she tells her friend that if she forgets him today, beauty will leave her and the bangles slip from her arms (இன்று மறப்பின் ... கலங்கழியும் காரிகை நீத்து). The two speeches give opposite accounts of whether remembering or forgetting costs her beauty; both are a speaker's words in the moment, and the conflict holds on Parimelazhagar's reading of the settings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1252","b":"k:1167","type":"agrees","note":"1167 says she swims the flood of desire and sees no shore, and even at midnight only she is awake (யாமத்தும் யானே உளேன்). 1252 says desire has no pity (கண் இன்று), for it sets her heart to work even at midnight (யாமத்தும் ஆளும் தொழில்). Both mark midnight, when Parimelazhagar notes all others rest, as the hour that shows desire does not release her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1253","b":"k:1318","type":"same_image","note":"1253 likens desire to a sneeze that appears without one's intending it; Parimelazhagar glosses that it cannot be held in. In 1318 the man does stifle a sneeze (தும்முச் செறுப்ப), and she weeps that he is hiding from her that his people are thinking of him. The sneeze is in both an involuntary sign of what is within, and 1318 shows that even suppressing it is read as a disclosure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1254","b":"k:1180","type":"develops","note":"1180 says it is not hard for the town to get the secret of those like her, whose eyes are beaten drums (அறைபறை கண்ணார்). 1254 says her desire goes past concealment into the public place (மன்று படும்). 1180 supplies a means by which the exposure described in 1254 happens: her own eyes announce it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1255","b":"k:1245","type":"develops","note":"1245 asks whether she can abandon as hostile (செற்றார் எனக் கைவிடல் உண்டோ) the man who does not answer her love. 1255 turns that private question into a general statement: not following the hostile (செற்றார் பின் செல்லா) is a dignity unknown to those with love's sickness (காமநோய் உற்றார் அறிவதொன்று அன்று). The word செற்றார் links them, and the second explains why the first could only be a rhetorical question.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1256","b":"k:1168","type":"same_image","note":"In 1168 she calls the night அளித்து, pitiable or kindly, because having put every living thing to sleep it has no companion but her. In 1256 she uses the same word of her grief (அளித்தரோ ... துயர்), which Parimelazhagar takes as scorn. In both she speaks to her tormentor in the language of grace and pity, which is how these monologues voice bitterness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1256","b":"k:1255","type":"develops","note":"1255 states in general terms that the lovesick do not know the dignity of not going after one who is hostile. 1256 applies it to herself with irony: how gracious (அளித்தரோ) is this grief, which wants her to go after the one who is hostile (செற்றவர் பின் சேறல் வேண்டி). The pair moves from a maxim about lovers to her own case, and Parimelazhagar reads அளித்து as mockery.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1257","b":"k:1247","type":"develops","note":"In 1247 she cannot bear desire and shame together and asks her heart to give up one (காமம் விடு ஒன்றோ நாண் விடு). In 1257 the matter is settled in his presence: when he does what she longs for, shame is simply not known to her. The conflict that is unbearable in his absence does not arise once he is there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1257","b":"k:1297","type":"agrees","note":"1297 says that, caught up with a foolish heart that cannot forget him, she has forgotten even shame (நாணும் மறந்தேன்). 1257 says she knows nothing of a thing called shame (நாண் என ஒன்றோ அறியலம்) when the one she cherishes does out of desire what she longs for. The two verses give the same loss of shame from two moments, separation in 1297 and reunion in 1257.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1258","b":"k:555","type":"same_image","note":"555 asks whether the tears of those who suffer under a ruler are not the weapon that wears away his wealth (கண்ணீர் அன்றே செல்வத்தைத் தேய்க்கும் படை). 1258 has the same form of question: are his humble words not the weapon that breaks our womanhood (பணிமொழி அன்றோ ... உடைக்கும் படை). In both a soft thing, tears or gentle speech, is named as the real weapon against something that looks strong.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1258","b":"k:985","type":"parallel_domain","note":"985 says that bowing is the strength of those who accomplish things and the weapon by which the worthy turn their opponents (பணிதல் ... மாற்றாரை மாற்றும் படை). 1258 calls her lover's lowly speech (பணிமொழி) the weapon that breaks her firmness. Humility as a படை is praised in the ethics of the worthy, and in the love poems the woman sees the same device used on her and calls its user a thief.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1260","b":"k:1259","type":"develops","note":"1259 narrates one occasion: she went away to sulk and embraced him when she saw her heart joining him. 1260 turns it into a general question about people whose hearts melt like fat in fire, and whether 'we will stand in a sulk' (ஊடி நிற்பேம் எனல்) is even open to them. The second verse gives the reason for the first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1260","b":"k:1287","type":"agrees","note":"1287 asks what use it is to sulk when one knows the sulk will prove false (பொய்த்தல் அறிந்து என் புலந்து), like those who jump into a stream knowing it will carry them off. 1260 asks whether those with hearts like fat put in fire (நிணம் தீயில் இட்டன்ன நெஞ்சினார்) can ever say they will hold out in a sulk. One image is of being swept away and the other of melting, and both conclude that the sulk cannot be sustained.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1261","b":"k:776","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 776 the warrior takes up his days (தன் நாளை எடுத்து) and files every day without a wound as wasted; in 1261 the wife marks off the days of his absence (நாளொற்றி) until her finger wears down. Both keep a ledger of days, the soldier's reckoned by wounds received and the waiting wife's by absence endured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1261","b":"k:1269","type":"develops","note":"1261 shows the woman's finger worn (தேய்ந்த விரல்) from marking the days since he left (அவர்சென்ற நாளொற்றி); 1269 says that for those pining with the return day in mind (வருநாள்வைத்து ஏங்குபவர்க்கு) one day passes like seven. 1269 gives the felt arithmetic behind 1261's tally: the days she counts are each lived as many.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:615","type":"develops","note":"615 praises the man who wants the work and not pleasure (இன்பம் விழையான் வினைவிழைவான்) as a pillar to his kin; in 1263 the wife describes her husband as gone desiring victory with his own resolve for company (உரன்நசைஇ உள்ளம் துணையாக), which Parimelazhagar glosses as not desiring enjoyment. 1263 shows the same man from inside the house: she stays alive only by desiring his return (வரல்நசைஇ இன்னும் உளேன்), the same verb நசைஇ set against his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1263","b":"k:1268","type":"develops","note":"In 1263 she waits for the man who left wanting victory; in 1268, which Parimelazhagar assigns to the man away on the king's service, he wishes the king would engage and win (வினைகலந்து வென்றீக வேந்தன்) so that he can join his wife for the evening. On that reading the pair gives both ends of one separation: her longing for his return and his impatience for the work to finish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1264","b":"k:476","type":"same_image","note":"476 warns that one who has climbed to the tip of a branch (நுனிக்கொம்பர் ஏறினார்) and pushes further loses his life; in 1264 her heart, thinking of his return, கோடுகொடு ஏறும், which Manakkudavar and Kalaignar read as climbing a tree's branches to look for him (Parimelazhagar reads it as swelling up like a tree putting out branches). On the climbing reading the same image marks reckless overreach in the book on statecraft and eager hope here.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1264","b":"k:1170","type":"develops","note":"In 1170 her heart goes to where he is, and she wishes her eyes could follow (உள்ளம்போன்று உள்வழிச் செல்கிற்பின்); in 1264 her heart rises thinking of his return (வரவுள்ளி). Both make the heart the part of her that moves toward him while she stays put; on Manakkudavar's reading of 1264 it even climbs to do the seeing her eyes cannot.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1265","b":"k:1187","type":"develops","note":"In 1187 pallor seizes her the moment she shifts a little in his embrace (புடைபெயர்ந்தேன் அவ்வளவில்); in 1265 she says the pallor on her shoulders will leave once she has seen him to her eyes' fill (கண்ணாரக் கண்டபின் நீங்கும்). Together they give pallor's rule in the Book of Love: it comes with the slightest distance and goes only with his presence, which Parimelazhagar underlines by noting that merely hearing of his return will not do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1265","b":"k:1283","type":"agrees","note":"1283 says her eyes will not rest without seeing her husband (கொண்கனைக் காணாது அமையல கண்) even though he does as he pleases; 1265 prays to see the same கொண்கன் until her eyes are full. Both make the eyes' need to see him the thing that must be satisfied before anything else can mend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1266","b":"k:1101","type":"develops","note":"1101 says all five senses find their objects in her; in 1266 she will 'drink' him (பருகுவன்), which Parimelazhagar explains as drinking that nectar through all five gateways of sense. On his reading 1266 is the woman's version of 1101, with the five-sense enjoyment compressed into a single verb.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1266","b":"k:1102","type":"agrees","note":"In 1102 the man says that medicine for a disease is normally something else, but she is herself the medicine for the sickness she causes (தன்நோய்க்குத் தானே மருந்து); in 1266 the woman says that if he comes for one day she will drink him in so that all her sickness is destroyed (பைதல்நோய் எல்லாம் கெட). Each lover names the other as both the cause and the only cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1267","b":"k:1109","type":"develops","note":"1109 lists sulking, reconciling and uniting (ஊடல் உணர்தல் புணர்தல்) as the rewards of those joined in love; 1267 shows the woman, before he has even returned, unable to choose between sulking and embracing and wondering whether to combine them. What 1109 states as a sequence of pleasures, 1267 shows as one impatient wish for all of them at once, which is how Parimelazhagar explains its place in this chapter.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1269","b":"k:1278","type":"same_image","note":"1269 says one day passes like seven (ஒருநாள் எழுநாள்போல்) for those awaiting the return day; in 1278 she says he left only yesterday (நெருநற்று) and she has been pale for seven days (எழுநாளேம்). Both use seven days to measure how separation distorts time, stretching a day of waiting in 1269 and running ahead of the departure itself in 1278.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1271","b":"k:71","type":"agrees","note":"71 asks if there is any bolt to shut love in, since the small tears of the loving announce it (புன்கணீர் பூசல் தரும்); 1271 says her eyes tell something despite her concealment. The claim that affection cannot be locked away and that the eyes give it out appears in the book on virtue as a general truth about அன்பு and in the Book of Love as a scene.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"k:1271","b":"k:709","type":"parallel_domain","note":"709 says the eye itself tells enmity and friendship (கண்ணுரைக்கும்) to those who know its kinds; in 1271 the man tells her that though she hides it, her eyes have slipped her control and have something to say (உண்கண் உரைக்கல் உறுவது). The minister's skill of reading the eye in the book on statecraft is the lover's skill here, with the same verb உரை for what the eye does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1272","b":"k:1280","type":"develops","note":"1272 says this girl has a great measure of womanly nature (பெண்நிறைந்த நீர்மை பெரிது), which Parimelazhagar takes as artlessness beyond the usual; 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading is womanhood added to womanhood (பெண்ணினால் பெண்மை உடைத்து). The chapter's first observation about her is given its content at the end: her womanliness is her saying it with the eyes and not in words.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1273","b":"k:1274","type":"develops","note":"1273 compares her unspoken meaning to a thread that shows through the gems (திகழ்தரு); 1274 compares it to the scent inside a closed bud (முகைமொக்குள் உள்ளது நாற்றம்). Parimelazhagar distinguishes them: the first lies within yet is visible outside, the second is not visible outside at all, so the pair moves from a sign that can be seen to one that can only be inferred.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1274","b":"k:1227","type":"same_image","note":"1227 describes love's sickness as budding in the morning (காலை அரும்பி), swelling through the day and flowering at evening; 1274 places an unspoken meaning inside the bud of her smile (நகைமொக்குள்). Both use the bud for a feeling that is already there and has not yet opened, pain in the one and a hidden intention in the other.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1091","type":"agrees","note":"1091 finds two looks in her eyes, one that gives the sickness and one that is its medicine (அந்நோய் மருந்து); 1275 finds in her covert behaviour a medicine that ends great sorrow (உறுதுயர் தீர்க்கும் மருந்து). In both the remedy is not anything she says but something to be read in a sign she gives.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1092","type":"same_image","note":"1092 values the small look her eyes steal (கண்களவு கொள்ளும் சிறுநோக்கம்) at more than half of love; 1275 says her கள்ளம், her stealth, contains a medicine. Theft is the figure for her indirect signalling in both, at first meeting in 1092 and after reunion in 1275.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1275","b":"k:1279","type":"develops","note":"1275 says the stealth with which she acted and withdrew (செய்திறந்த கள்ளம்) holds a medicine for great sorrow; 1279 states what she did (அஃதாண்டவள் செய்தது): she looked at her bangles, her shoulders and her feet. Parimelazhagar ties the two explicitly, taking 1279's அஃது to point back to 1275, so that the silent gesture is the 'stealth' and its meaning, going with him, is the cure.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1276","b":"k:827","type":"parallel_domain","note":"827 warns not to take an enemy's bowing words at face value, because a bow's bending signals harm (வில்வணக்கம் தீங்கு குறித்தமையான்); in 1276 the woman reads his unusually great tenderness (பெரிதாற்றிப் பெட்பக் கலத்தல்) as a sign of coming absence. The same inference, that excess of kindness points to its opposite, is advice about false friends in one book and a wife's reading of her husband in the other, though she alleges no enmity.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1278","b":"k:1277","type":"agrees","note":"1277 says her bangles knew of his parting before she did; 1278 says her body has been pale for seven days though he left only yesterday. The two adjacent speeches make the same point with different signs: her body responds to his intention to leave before the leaving is an acknowledged fact.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1093","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1093 reports a wordless act and reads it: she looked, and having looked bowed her head (நோக்கினாள் நோக்கி இறைஞ்சினாள்), and that (அஃது) was water poured on their bond; 1279 reports that she looked at her bangles, her shoulders and her feet, and that (அஃது) is what she did there. Both verses consist of a sequence of looks followed by அஃது, leaving the meaning to be drawn from the gesture, at courtship in 1093 and at the threat of parting in 1279.","grade":"sound","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1279","b":"k:1234","type":"develops","note":"1234 describes separation's effect: the shoulders lose their fullness and old beauty and the bangles slip (பைந்தொடி சோரும்… தோள்); in 1279 she looks at bangles and soft shoulders (தொடிநோக்கி மென்தோளும் நோக்கி) and then at her feet. Read with 1234, her first two glances name what will happen if she is left, and the third, as Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar take it, names her alternative of walking with him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1280","b":"k:1053","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1053 says that even asking has a beauty of its own (இரப்புமோர் ஏஎர் உடைத்து) when done before those who know their duty and do not withhold; 1280 says her asking (இரவு), done with the eyes, has a womanliness of its own (பெண்மை உடைத்து). The word for begging is the same, and both grant it a grace under conditions, though the chapter on begging means alms and 1280 means a lover's plea.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1280","b":"k:1137","type":"agrees","note":"1137 says nothing has greater worth than womanhood that suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the palmyra horse (மடலேறாப் பெண்ணின் பெருந்தக்கது இல்); 1280 says that telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading (கண்ணினால் காமநோய் சொல்லி இரவு) is womanhood upon womanhood. Both praise as specifically womanly the refusal to declare desire openly, 1137 by what she will not do and 1280 by the silent means she uses instead.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1281","b":"k:1288","type":"same_image","note":"In 1281 love is set above liquor: it delights at a thought and a look, which liquor cannot do. In 1288, in the same chapter, his chest is itself like liquor to those who have got drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), wanted though it does shameful harm (இளித்தக்க இன்னா செயினும்). The chapter uses the one image both ways, first to say love exceeds drink and then to say it compels like drink.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:433","type":"same_image","note":"433 says those ashamed of blame treat a fault as small as a millet as if it were a palmyra (தினைத்துணையாங் குற்றம்... பனைத்துணையாக் கொள்வர்). 1282 uses the same two measures in the other direction: with love as large as a palmyra, even a millet of sulking over his fault is to be avoided. In 433 the small fault is magnified; in 1282 the small grievance is to be dropped.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1282","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1302 says sulking is like salt in the right amount and is spoiled only by being prolonged a little (உப்பமைந் தற்றால் புலவி). 1282 says that when love is full to a palmyra's measure, not even a millet of sulking is wanted (தினைத்துணையும் ஊடாமை வேண்டும்). Read together, 1282 states a condition, காமம் நிறைய வரின், under which the measured sulk that 1302 recommends falls to nothing; Parimelazhagar takes 1282 as the woman voicing her own haste in the form of general advice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1283","b":"k:1257","type":"qualifies","note":"1257 says she knows nothing of shame when the one she cherishes does, in desire, what she wants (பேணியார் பெட்ப செயின்). 1283 reuses the two words with the sense turned round: even if he does only what he himself wants, without cherishing her (பேணாது பெட்பவே செய்யினும்), her eyes will not rest without seeing him. The second verse removes the condition of the first: her yielding does not depend on his doing what pleases her.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1284","b":"k:1246","type":"develops","note":"1246 tells the heart that when it sees the lover it cannot stay in a sulk, so its present anger is false (பொய்க்காய்வு). 1284 is that prediction coming true: she set out to sulk and the heart forgot it and went to unite (அதுமறந்து கூடற்கண் சென்றது).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1284","b":"k:1294","type":"develops","note":"1284 reports to her friend that her heart forgot the sulk and went to unite. 1294 gives the consequence: since the heart will not first sulk and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), who will consult with it about such things again (நின்னொடு சூழ்வார் யார்). The report of the heart's desertion in one chapter becomes the quarrel with the heart in the next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:190","type":"parallel_domain","note":"190 asks what harm could come if people saw their own faults as they see strangers' faults (ஏதிலார் குற்றம்போல் தங்குற்றங் காண்கிற்பின்). 1285 says she cannot see her husband's fault when he is before her, as the eye cannot see the kohl-stick touching it. In both, what is nearest is what goes unseen; 190 treats this as a failing to correct, and 1285 as a fact about love.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1285","b":"k:1286","type":"develops","note":"1285 gives the image: the eye does not see the stick that is lining it, and she does not see his blame when she sees him (பழிகாணேன் கண்ட இடத்து). 1286 states the other half that the image implies: when she does not see him she sees nothing but his faults (காணாக்கால் காணேன் தவறல்லவை). Parimelazhagar's gloss on 1285 already supplies this, saying the eye has seen the stick at every other time.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1286","b":"k:1297","type":"develops","note":"1286: seeing him, she sees no faults; not seeing him, she sees nothing else. In 1297 she calls her heart மடநெஞ்சு, and Parimelazhagar defines that மடமை as exactly this reversal: remembering when he is absent, and forgetting when he is present, faults that ought to be remembered at sight and forgotten in absence. On his reading 1297 names and blames what 1286 only describes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1287","b":"k:1134","type":"same_image","note":"1134: the fierce flood of love carries off the raft of shame and manliness (காமக் கடும்புனல் உய்க்கும்). 1287: why sulk knowing it will prove false, like those who leap into a stream knowing it will carry them off (உய்த்தல் அறிந்து புனல்பாய்பவர்). The same water and the same verb, உய்த்தல், are used by the man of his restraint in 1134 and by the woman of her sulk here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1288","b":"k:940","type":"parallel_domain","note":"940: as the gambler loves the game more with each loss (இழத்தொறூஉம் காதலிக்கும் சூதேபோல்), the living being loves life more with each suffering. 1288: liquor is wanted by the drunk even as it does them shameful harm, and so is his chest. Both explain an attachment that grows through injury by a vice from the same group of chapters (dice in 940, liquor in 1288).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1288","b":"k:1145","type":"same_image","note":"1145: as drinking is wanted more each time one gets drunk (களித்தொறும் கள்ளுண்டல் வேட்டற்றால்), love is sweeter each time it is exposed. 1288: his chest is as liquor is to those who have got drunk on it (களித்தார்க்குக் கள்ளற்றே), though it does shameful harm. Both take the drinker's returning appetite as the likeness for love; 1288 adds the harm and the shame that 1145 leaves out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1289","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1289 says few attain love's செவ்வி, its right moment or condition. 1306 gives a figure for missing it on either side: without துனி and புலவி love is like fruit over-ripe or unripe (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று). Parimelazhagar places 1289 in a sulk that will not yield to appeasement, so the fineness it speaks of is the same fineness of timing that 1306 puts in terms of ripeness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1290","b":"k:1327","type":"develops","note":"1290 remembers her sulk ending because her eagerness to embrace was greater than his. 1327 states the rule the man later draws: in a lovers' quarrel the one who loses has won, as is seen in the union (ஊடலில் தோற்றவர் வென்றார்). Her giving way in 1290 is a case of the defeat that 1327 counts as victory.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1291","b":"k:72","type":"parallel_domain","note":"72: the loveless belong wholly to themselves, and the loving belong to others even in their bones (அன்பிலார் எல்லாம் தமக்குரியர்). 1291: his heart is for him (அவர்நெஞ்சு அவர்க்காதல்), so why, heart, are you not for me (நீ எமக்கு ஆகாதது). The woman's complaint is 72's distinction seen from the inside: the one who loves no longer owns her own heart, and the one who does not love keeps his.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1292","b":"k:1255","type":"agrees","note":"1255 says the dignity of not going after one who has turned away (செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை) is something the lovesick cannot know. 1292 shows it: her heart goes to him (சேறி) though it has seen that he is not attached to her. 1255 gives the general statement and 1292 the instance, addressed to the heart that does the going.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:752","type":"agrees","note":"752 says everyone scorns those who have nothing and everyone honours the rich (இல்லாரை எல்லாரும் எள்ளுவர்). 1293 has the woman quote the same worldly observation as a saying, கெட்டார்க்கு நட்டார் இல், and ask her heart whether that is why it leaves her and follows him. The pairing shows a hard fact from the chapter on wealth being used in the Book of Love as a taunt, with the woman as the ruined one and her heart as the fair-weather friend.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:787","type":"qualifies","note":"787 defines friendship as turning a friend from ruin and, when ruin comes, sharing the suffering (அழிவின்கண் அல்லல் உழப்பதாம் நட்பு). The saying in 1293, that the ruined have no friends, describes how the world behaves and not what friendship is; by the standard of 787 her heart, which goes after him as it pleases (பெட்டாங்கு அவர்பின் செலல்), has failed as a friend. The woman's question, என்பதோ, shows she is quoting the saying to reproach, not endorsing it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1293","b":"k:798","type":"develops","note":"798 warns against taking the friendship of those who cut away in distress (அல்லற்கண் ஆற்றறுப்பார் நட்பு). In 1293 the one who deserts in distress is her own heart, which cannot be declined as a friend. The verse turns the advice of the friendship chapters into a predicament with no remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1294","b":"k:1260","type":"agrees","note":"1260 asks whether those with hearts like fat in fire can say they will hold out in a sulk (புணர்ந்தூடி நிற்பேம் எனல்). 1294 tells the heart that it will not sulk first and enjoy afterwards, so no one will plan such things with it. Both say the intention to sulk cannot survive her own heart; 1294 adds that she will stop consulting it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1294","b":"k:1330","type":"develops","note":"1330 says sulking is the pleasure of love, and the pleasure of sulking is to come together and embrace (ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம்). 1294 has the woman want exactly that order, first make a quarrel and then enjoy (துனிசெய்து துவ்வாய்), and complain that her heart will not do it. The book's closing statement of how a sulk should work is, in 1294, a plan she cannot carry out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:293","type":"parallel_domain","note":"293: do not lie about what your heart knows, for after the lie your own heart will burn you (தன்நெஞ்சே தன்னைச் சுடும்). 1296: thinking alone, she finds her own heart is there to eat her. In both the tormentor is one's own heart and the setting is solitude with what one knows; 293 makes this the working of conscience, and 1296 gives it as the experience of the abandoned lover, with no fault of hers involved.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1296","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1207 says she does not know forgetting, and even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்). 1296 says that when she sits alone and thinks (நினைத்தக்கால்), her heart consumes her. Both make thinking of him in his absence the source of the pain; in 1207 the heart is what is burned, in 1296 it is what does the harm.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1017","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1017 says those ruled by shame give up life for shame's sake and do not give up shame for life's sake (நாண்துறவார்). In 1297 the woman has forgotten shame because her heart cannot forget him, and Parimelazhagar glosses the shame as dearer to her than life, the valuation of 1017. The pairing sets the rule from the chapter on shame beside a speaker in the Book of Love who holds that rule and reports failing it; it is her confession, not a contrary teaching.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1297","b":"k:1132","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1132 the man says his body and life, unable to bear it, will mount the palmyra horse, with shame set aside (நாணினை நீக்கி நிறுத்து). In 1297 the woman says she has forgotten shame through her heart's inability to forget him. Each sex is shown losing நாண் to love; the man sets it aside to act in public, and the woman finds it gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:967","type":"parallel_domain","note":"967 says it is better to be called ruined where one stands than to live by following those who do not accept one (ஒட்டார்பின் சென்றொருவன் வாழ்தலின்). In 1298 the heart, described as loving life (உயிர்க்காதல் நெஞ்சு), counts scorning him as the disgrace and goes on dwelling on him. The chapter on honour ranks honour above life; the heart in 1298 keeps the vocabulary of disgrace (இளிவு) but, out of attachment to life, applies it so as to stay with the one who slighted her. This is the heart's reasoning as she reports it, not a rival doctrine.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:1184","type":"same_image","note":"1184: she thinks of him and what she speaks of is his merit (உரைப்பது அவர்திறம்), and still the pallor comes. 1298: her heart thinks of what pertains to him (அவர்திறம் உள்ளும்), judging that to scorn him would be a disgrace. The phrase அவர் திறம் with உள்ளுதல் appears in both; in 1184 it is her own loyal practice that goes unrewarded, and in 1298 it is something her heart does that she stands apart from.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1298","b":"k:1255","type":"qualifies","note":"1255 says the dignity of not going after one who has turned away is unknown to the lovesick (செற்றார்பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை). In 1298 her heart reasons the opposite way about where disgrace lies: it thinks that to scorn him would be a disgrace (எள்ளின் இளிவாம் என்று எண்ணி) and so keeps thinking of his side. 1298 shows the lovesick heart not merely ignorant of that dignity, as 1255 has it, but supplied with its own argument from honour for doing without it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:36","type":"parallel_domain","note":"36 says virtue, done without delay, is the companion that does not die when one dies (பொன்றுங்கால் பொன்றாத் துணை). 1299 says that in grief there is no companion if one's own heart is not one. Both verses ask what stays with a person when other support is gone; Book One answers virtue, and the lover's verse names the heart and finds it missing.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:1263","type":"same_image","note":"1263 describes the man as having left with his own heart or resolve for companion (உள்ளம் துணையாகச் சென்றார்). 1299 asks who can be a companion in grief when one's own heart is no companion (நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி). The heart as துணை is what he had on setting out and, on Parimelazhagar's assignment of 1299 to the man, what he lacks when she will not be reconciled; Manakkudavar gives 1299 to the woman.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1299","b":"k:1300","type":"develops","note":"1299 asks who will be a companion in grief when one's own heart is not (தாமுடைய நெஞ்சந் துணையல் வழி). 1300 repeats the frame, தாமுடைய நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி, and draws the further step: then it is a small matter that strangers are not one's own. The pair moves from the loss of the nearest help to an inference about everyone else, and Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, where the first eight verses of the chapter are the woman's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:188","type":"parallel_domain","note":"188 asks what those who spread even the faults of their closest (துன்னியார்) will do to strangers (ஏதிலார் மாட்டு). 1300 argues that when one's own heart is not one's own, strangers (ஏதிலார்) not being one's own is a slight thing. Both reason from the nearest relation to ஏதிலார்: if the bond fails at the closest point, nothing can be expected further out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:882","type":"parallel_domain","note":"882 says do not fear enemies who are like swords, but fear the tie with enemies who are like kin (கேள்போல் பகைவர்). 1300 finds the same reversal inside one person: the heart that should be தமர், one's own people, is not, and beside that the distance of outsiders hardly counts. The lesson of the chapter on enmity within, that the danger is in what is closest, is applied in 1300 to the heart.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1300","b":"k:1291","type":"agrees","note":"1291 opens the chapter with the woman asking her heart why it is not hers when his heart is his own (நீ எமக்கு ஆகாதது). 1300 closes it with the general form: when one's own heart is not one's kin (நெஞ்சம் தமரல் வழி), it is nothing that strangers are not. Parimelazhagar gives the first to the woman and the last to the man, so the chapter begins and ends with the same complaint from each side.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1301 has the friend urge the woman to hold back her embrace and sulk (புல்லாது இராப் புலத்தை) so they can watch his pain 'a little' (சிறிது). 1302 states the limit that word implies: sulking is like salt in right measure, and letting it run on (நீள விடல்) is like salt in excess. Parimelazhagar reads the சிறிது of 1301 as already pointing to this rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1303","type":"develops","note":"1301 proposes not embracing (புல்லாது) in order to see his அல்லல்நோய். 1303 uses the same two words from the other side: leaving the one who sulks unembraced (புல்லா விடல்) is like giving அல்லல்நோய் to those already in distress (அலந்தாரை). Read together, the pain that 1301 treats as a brief test is the pain 1303 says must not be left standing.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"k:1301","b":"k:1329","type":"agrees","note":"In 1301 the friend wants the sulk kept up so that his suffering can be seen for a while. In 1329 the man himself wishes she would go on sulking (ஊடுக மன்னோ) and the night grow long so that he may keep pleading (யாம் இரப்ப). Both sides treat the sulk as something to prolong on purpose, hers to watch and his to beg.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1302","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1302 gives the measure: a little sulking seasons, a little too long spoils. 1306 gives the two failures by name: without துனி and புலவி, love is like கனி and கருக்காய், which Manakkudavar reads as overripe fruit and unripe fruit. Together they make sulking a matter of degree, judged by taste, in both verses through a food image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1303","b":"k:1304","type":"agrees","note":"1303 says that leaving unembraced those who have sulked (புலந்தாரைப் புல்லா விடல்) is like giving sharp pain to those already suffering (அலந்தாரை அல்லல்நோய் செய்தற்று). 1304 says that not appeasing those who sulk (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதல் அரிந்தற்று). The pair states one claim twice, once as added hurt and once as a finishing cut. Parimelazhagar takes both as the wife's taunt sending the man back to his other women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1304","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"1304 compares leaving a sulking lover unappeased to cutting an already withered creeper at its root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதல் அரிந்தற்று). 1030 says a family with no good man to prop it falls when trouble cuts it at the foot (இடுக்கண் கால் கொன்றிட வீழும்); Parimelazhagar takes this as an axe at the tree's base. Both picture a weakened plant that needed support and got the cut instead, once in love and once in the household.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1305","b":"k:1321","type":"agrees","note":"1305, on Parimelazhagar's reading, has the man find beauty in her sulk even though he is good and blameless. 1321 gives her side: he has no fault (இல்லை தவறு அவர்க்கு), yet the way he shows tenderness (அவர் அளிக்குமாறு) is what makes sulking possible. Both verses detach the sulk from any real offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1305","b":"k:1325","type":"agrees","note":"1305 says that for men good in their qualities (நலத்தகை நல்லவர்க்கு) the beauty (ஏர்) is the sulking in flower-eyed women; Parimelazhagar reads this as 'even for the faultless, sulking is sweet'. 1325 says the same in plainer terms: though without fault (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்), being kept from the soft shoulders of the one they love has something in it (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). Manakkudavar reads 1305 differently, taking நல்லவர் as the other women.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1306","b":"k:1330","type":"agrees","note":"1306 puts it negatively: without quarrels, serious and light, love is fruit at the wrong stage. 1330 puts it positively: sulking is the pleasure of love (ஊடுதல் காமத்திற்கு இன்பம்), and the pleasure of sulking is the embrace that follows. The chapter 133 verse states as a conclusion what 1306 argues by image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1307","b":"k:1152","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1307 finds a pain inside sulking (ஊடலின் உண்டு ஆங்கு ஓர் துன்பம்): the thought of whether union will be delayed or not. In 1152 the woman says union itself holds the misery of fearing separation (பிரிவு அஞ்சும் புன்கண் உடைத்தால் புணர்வு). In both, a state counted as pleasant carries worry about what comes next.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1307","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"1330 calls sulking the pleasure of love, provided the embrace follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). 1307 fastens on that proviso: because one cannot be sure the union will not be long delayed (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல்), the sulk has its own pain. 1307 is the reservation built into 1330's praise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:877","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1308 asks what use it is to suffer where there is no lover who knows 'this one is hurt' (நொந்தார் என்று அஃது அறியும் காதலர் இல்லா வழி). 877, in the chapter on enemies, advises: do not voice your hurt to those who do not know you are hurt (நோவற்க நொந்தது அறியார்க்கு). The same roots நோ and அறி carry one rule in love and in statecraft: displayed pain is wasted on someone who does not perceive it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:1200","type":"agrees","note":"In 1308 suffering is pointless when the other does not know or care that one suffers. In 1200 the woman tells her heart that reporting its great pain to one who is not close (உறாஅர்க்கு உறுநோய் உரைப்பாய்) is like trying to fill in the sea. Man and woman reach the same judgment from opposite sides of the couple.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1308","b":"k:1309","type":"develops","note":"1308 says the man's hurt is useless without a lover who recognises it. 1309 gives the general form: as water is sweet in shade, sulking is sweet only with those who love (வீழுநர் கண்ணே இனிது). Parimelazhagar assigns both to the man, in a sulk that she is not answering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1309","b":"k:1323","type":"agrees","note":"1309 limits the sweetness of sulking to those who love (வீழுநர் கண்ணே). 1323 asks whether any heaven matches sulking with those joined like water with earth (நிலத்தொடு நீர் இயைந்தன்னார் அகத்து). Both say the value of the sulk depends on the closeness of the two, and both use water to say it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1255","type":"agrees","note":"1310 calls the heart's wish to join with one who neglects it mere craving. In 1255 the woman says the dignity of not going after those who have turned away (செற்றார் பின் செல்லாப் பெருந்தகைமை) is something the love-sick do not know. Both admit that desire overrides self-respect toward an indifferent partner.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1292","type":"agrees","note":"In 1310 the man, on Parimelazhagar's reading, says that his heart's intent to join with one who leaves him to wither in the sulk (ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவாரோடு) is just craving (அவா). In 1292 the woman scolds her heart for going to him even after seeing that he is not close to her (உறாஅதவர்க் கண்ட கண்ணும்). Each blames the heart for running to someone who has shown no care.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1310","b":"k:1304","type":"same_image","note":"1304 compares the unappeased sulker to a withered creeper (வாடிய வள்ளி) cut at the root. 1310 speaks of being left to dry up in the sulk (ஊடல் உணங்க விடுவார்). The wife's warning and the man's complaint use the same picture of a plant left without water; Parimelazhagar places 1310 in a sulk she does not answer.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1312","b":"k:1253","type":"same_image","note":"In 1312 the woman says he sneezed while she was sulking, knowing (அறிந்து) she would have to say 'live long' and so speak to him. In 1253 the sneeze is the example of what happens without intention: desire comes out குறிப்பின்றித் தும்மல்போல். Parimelazhagar notes that 1312 treats a natural sneeze as a deliberate one, which is the subtlety the chapter title names.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1313","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"1311 gives her stated ground: every woman takes him in with her eyes (கண்ணின் பொது உண்பர்). 1313 shows the same worry applied to a trifle: a wreath must have been put on to be shown to another woman (ஒருத்தியைக் காட்டிய சூடினீர்). Her jealousy in both is about who sees him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1313","b":"k:1321","type":"develops","note":"In 1313 the man reports that even wearing a wreath makes her angry (காயும்): 'you wore it to show some woman'. In 1321 the woman says he has no fault (இல்லை தவறு அவர்க்கு), yet his way of showing tenderness can bring on a sulk. 1321 supplies from her mouth what the man's complaints in chapter 132 imply: the grounds are invented.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1314","b":"k:645","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1314 he says 'we love more than anyone' (யாரினும் காதலம்) and she turns it on him: 'than whom, than whom?' (யாரினும் யாரினும்). 645 advises speaking a word only after knowing there is no other word that can defeat it (வெல்லுஞ்சொல் இன்மை அறிந்து). The love scene shows that rule failing in a comic way: his own word, repeated back, is the one that beats him.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:107","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1315 a promise limited to this birth (இம்மை) makes her weep. 107 says the good remember through all seven births (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர்) the friendship of those who removed their distress. Her complaint measures his promise against the span the book itself uses for lasting bonds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1315","b":"k:1314","type":"agrees","note":"In 1314 'more than anyone' is heard as implying other loves; in 1315 'we will not part in this birth' (இம்மைப் பிறப்பில் பிரியலம்) fills her eyes with tears, because she hears a parting in the next. Parimelazhagar describes the move in 1315: she takes a plain statement as a hinting one. Both verses turn a strong assurance into an offence by what it leaves unsaid.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1316","b":"k:1207","type":"agrees","note":"1316 has her treat remembering as proof of forgetting. In 1207 the woman, alone, says she does not know forgetting (மறப்பறியேன்) and that even remembering burns. The standard she holds him to in 1316, never having forgotten at all, is the one she claims for herself in separation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1317","b":"k:1312","type":"develops","note":"In 1312 she suspects his sneeze was a trick to get the customary 'live long' out of her. In 1317 she gives that blessing (வழுத்தினாள்) and at once takes it back (அழித்து அழுதாள்) with a new suspicion. One sneeze is read two ways in the chapter, as a stratagem and as proof of another woman.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1318","b":"k:1317","type":"develops","note":"In 1317 sneezing brings the charge that some woman is thinking of him. In 1318 he holds the sneeze back (தும்முச் செறுப்ப) and she weeps anyway: 'are you hiding from me that your people think of you?' (நுமர் உள்ளல் எம்மை மறைத்திரோ). The two verses close both exits: sneezing and not sneezing are both faults.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1319","b":"k:693","type":"parallel_domain","note":"In 1319 even his effort to appease her makes her angry (உணர்த்தினும் காயும்): 'you must be like this with others too'. 693 warns those who serve kings that once suspicion has arisen, clearing it is hard for anyone (கடுத்தபின் தேற்றுதல் யார்க்கும் அரிது). The court maxim and the bedroom scene describe the same trap: reassurance offered to a suspicious mind is taken as more evidence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1319","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1304 says not appeasing one who sulks (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a withered creeper at the root. In 1319 he does appease her (தன்னை உணர்த்தினும்) and that too is a fault. Taken together, the man is blamed in chapter 131 for not doing what chapter 132 shows cannot be done to her satisfaction.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"In 1311 her objection is to other women's eyes on him (கண்ணின் பொது உண்பர்). In 1320 it is to his eyes on her, which she thinks are really on another woman; Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both explain that she supposes he is matching her limbs against someone else's. The chapter opens and closes on the look as the place where she locates unfaithfulness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1320","b":"k:1313","type":"agrees","note":"1313, 1319 and 1320 share the frame '…even if I do X, she is angry (…இனும் காயும்), saying …'. In 1313 the act is wearing a wreath; in 1320 it is only sitting and looking at her (நினைத்திருந்து நோக்கினும்), which she takes as comparing her with someone (யார் உள்ளி நோக்கினீர்). The series runs from adornment down to a silent look, so that no act of his is left free of suspicion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1321","b":"k:1311","type":"develops","note":"In 1311 the woman refuses his chest because all women 'feast on it in common with their eyes' (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர்), a sulk with a stated ground. In 1321 she grants there is no fault at all and says his tenderness itself brings the sulk on; Parimelazhagar explains this as her thought that others too might receive that same tenderness. On his reading the jealousy of 1311 survives in 1321 with its ground removed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1321","b":"k:1325","type":"agrees","note":"In 1321 the woman says he has no fault (இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு), yet the way he shows tenderness (அவர் அளிக்குமாறு) is able to produce a sulk. In 1325 the man says that even when men are faultless (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்), being kept from the soft arms of the one they love 'has a certain something' (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). The chapter opens each speaker's side with the same concession: the sulk it praises needs no offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1010","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses use the rare word சிறுதுனி for a brief bad spell in something good. In 1010 the short want of the renowned rich is like the rain-cloud going dry (மாரி வறங்கூர்ந்தனையது); in 1322 the small pain that sulking brings may make his good tenderness droop (நல்லளி வாடினும்), yet it gains worth (பாடு பெறும்). In each the dryness is temporary and belongs to a giver whose giving is not in doubt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1322 says tenderness may wilt (வாடினும்) under the small pain of a sulk and still come to greatness. 1304 uses the same verb for the limit: not reconciling one who has sulked is like cutting an already wilted creeper at the root (வாடிய வள்ளி முதலரிந்தற்று). The wilting is harmless only if the sulk is ended; Parimelazhagar reads 1304 as the woman speaking of his other women.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1322","b":"k:1306","type":"develops","note":"1306 says love without துனி and புலவி is like fruit over-ripe or unripe (கனியும் கருக்காயும் அற்று), so some quarrel is needed. 1322 names the amount: only the 'small துனி' that arises in a sulk (ஊடலின் தோன்றும் சிறுதுனி), which leaves his tenderness greater even if it droops for a while. Parimelazhagar on 1306 takes துனி as the full-grown quarrel and புலவி as the young one, so 1322's சிறு marks the tolerable dose.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:828","type":"parallel_domain","note":"828 warns that a weapon lies hidden inside an enemy's hands folded in worship (தொழுதகையுள்ளும் படையொடுங்கும்), and his tears are the same. 1324 finds a weapon (படை) inside the lovers' sulk, which Parimelazhagar explains as the other's bowing and humble words. Submission as a concealed weapon is a danger to be detected in false friendship and, in love, the means by which the sulk is ended.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1324","b":"k:1251","type":"develops","note":"1251 says the axe of desire breaks the door of restraint bolted with shame (காமக் கணிச்சி உடைக்கும் நிறையென்னும் ... கதவு). 1324 uses the same verb for the resolve to keep sulking: a weapon arises that breaks the heart (உள்ளம் உடைக்கும் படை). What was a breach forced during separation in 1251 becomes, inside the sulk, a breaking that leads to the embrace that does not let go (புல்லி விடா).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1103","type":"same_image","note":"Both verses turn on the same phrase, 'the soft arms of the one they love' (தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள்). In 1103 sleeping on them is set against the world of the lotus-eyed one; in 1325 being kept away from them (அகறலின்), though one is faultless, 'has a certain something' (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). The repeated phrase shows the book's last chapter finding a pleasure in the very absence of what the first union praised.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1307","type":"qualifies","note":"1325 says that being kept from her arms by a sulk has a pleasure of its own (ஆங்கொன்று உடைத்து). 1307 says sulking also holds one pain: the thought of whether union will be delayed or not (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல் என்று). Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, 1325 after the making up and 1307 during the sulk, so the pleasure of 1325 is one judged in hindsight.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1325","b":"k:1319","type":"develops","note":"In 1319 the man reports that even when he tries to appease her she is angry (உணர்த்தினும் காயும்), saying he is like this with others too: a complaint that he is blamed without cause. In 1325 he grants the same situation, a man without fault (தவறிலர் ஆயினும்) kept from her arms, and finds something to enjoy in it. The groundless sulks listed in chapter 132 are re-read as a pleasure in chapter 133.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:944","type":"parallel_domain","note":"944 instructs: know that the earlier meal is digested (அற்றது அறிந்து) and eat only when thoroughly hungry (துவரப் பசித்து). 1326 says the digesting of what was eaten is sweeter than eating (உணலினும் உண்டது அறல் இனிது), and so sulking is sweeter than union. The chapter on medicine states as a rule of health what the man uses as his simile; Parimelazhagar on 1326 draws the same line, that one who eats when hungry eats more and with more relish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1326","b":"k:1330","type":"qualifies","note":"1326 ranks sulking above union: ஊடல் is sweeter than புணர்தல் for love. 1330 calls sulking love's pleasure but adds that the pleasure of the sulk itself lies in being able to come together and embrace (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). The closing verse makes the sulk's sweetness depend on the union that 1326 placed below it, as digestion has value for the next meal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1327","b":"k:931","type":"parallel_domain","note":"931 says not to want gambling even if you win, since what is won (வென்றதூஉம்) is like the hook a fish has swallowed. 1327 says that in a sulk the loser is the winner (தோற்றவர் வென்றார்). The two verses reverse the apparent result of a contest in opposite directions: the gambler's win is a loss that shows later, and the lover's loss is a win that shows later in union (கூடலிற் காணப்படும்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"k:1328","b":"k:802","type":"same_image","note":"802 says intimate liberty is a limb of friendship and that 'being உப்பு to it' (அதற்கு உப்பாதல்) is the duty of the worthy; Parimelazhagar reads this as being pleasant toward that liberty. 1328 uses உப்பு for the savour of union after a sulk, which Parimelazhagar again glosses as sweetness. In both, salt stands for the relish that makes something a close companion does, a liberty or a sulk, enjoyable and not an offence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1328","b":"k:1238","type":"same_image","note":"In 1238 the man recalls that when he loosened the arms that embraced her, her forehead turned pale (பசந்தது ... பேதை நுதல்). In 1328 he recalls union so intense that her forehead sweated (நுதல் வெயர்ப்ப). Her forehead is the place where he reads both the first sign of separation and the fullness of union.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1052","type":"parallel_domain","note":"1052 says even begging is a pleasure to a person if what is begged for comes without pain (இன்பம் ஒருவற்கு இரத்தல்). In 1329 the man wants the sulk and the night prolonged so that he may beg (யாம் இரப்ப), treating the pleading itself as something to enjoy. Begging, which chapter 107 calls degrading, is here a pleasure because the giver is not in doubt, which is the condition 1052 sets.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1329","b":"k:1307","type":"qualifies","note":"1307 names the one pain within sulking: the thought of whether union will be delayed or not (புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல்). 1329 wishes for that delay, asking that she keep sulking and the night grow long (ஊடுக ... நீடுக). Parimelazhagar gives both to the man, 1307 during a sulk and 1329 after one has ended well, so the delay feared in the moment is desired in retrospect.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1330","b":"k:1302","type":"qualifies","note":"1302 says a sulk is like salt in the right amount, and letting it run long (நீள விடல்) is like a little too much. 1330 makes the sulk love's pleasure only if embrace follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்). Parimelazhagar reads 1330's condition through this measure: the sulk must be ended at the right point, between a quarrel too ripe and one too young, and that is hard to judge.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"k:1330","b":"k:1304","type":"qualifies","note":"1330 says the sulk's own pleasure comes if the lovers get to unite and embrace (பெறின்). 1304 states the other case: not reconciling one who has sulked (ஊடியவரை உணராமை) is like cutting a wilted creeper at the root. The conditional in 1330 is a real one, and 1304 shows what a sulk is when the condition fails.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:1","b":"k:332","type":"agrees","note":"Both books make wealth's coming and going a spectacle: the Kural's crowd gathering and dispersing at a show, the Naladiyar's feaster turned beggar.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:2","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both draw the same conclusion from wealth's instability: act on it at once (Kural 333) or share it from the day it comes (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:3","b":"n:21","type":"same_image","note":"The king on the elephant's neck under the royal umbrella appears in both. Here he falls when his deeds turn; in 21 he simply dies like everyone else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:4","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge good deeds before death stops them. The Kural pictures the hiccup that silences the tongue; this quatrain the angry approach of Death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:5","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject 'later': the Kural says virtue is the companion that does not perish when you do, and this quatrain says those who gave first escape Death's road.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:6","b":"k:269","type":"tension","note":"Kural 269 says those with the power of austerity can leap past Death (கூற்றம் குதித்தலும்); this quatrain says no one here has leapt past Death and escaped. The conflict holds on Parimelazhagar's literal reading of 269; Kalaignar's figurative 'conquering death' softens it. Mudaliar cites 269 here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:8","b":"k:331","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 331 calls taking the impermanent for permanent the lowest புல்லறிவு; this quatrain shows the புல்லறிவாளர் at it, with wealth that flashes and vanishes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:9","b":"k:1005","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that wealth neither enjoyed nor given is as good as none: in the Kural such a man has nothing, here he is thought to have lost it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:10","b":"k:1009","type":"agrees","note":"Both say wealth amassed by denying oneself (Kural தற்செற்று, here உடம்பு செற்றும்) and neglecting virtue goes to others. The Naladiyar adds the honey bee as witness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:11","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Here the wise renounce the world even in childhood. Kural 46 asks what a person living household life by virtue would gain by going the outer way. The conflict depends on reading புறத்தாறு as the ascetic path, as Parimelazhagar and Pope do, and துறந்தார் here as world-renunciation, as Shanmuganar does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:11","b":"k:342","type":"develops","note":"Kural 342 says renounce while there is time; this quatrain pushes the right time back to infancy, because age is certain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:17","b":"n:44","type":"same_image","note":"Both take the spear-like eye of conventional praise and undo it: 17 by age, which makes it a staff-guided eye, 44 by anatomy, which makes it a scooped-out palm fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:19","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both quote the procrastinator's words, 'we will know it later' (மற்றறிவாம் here, அன்றறிவாம் in the Kural), and reject them. The Naladiyar's reason is that the young die too.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:22","b":"k:38","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the phrase வீழ்நாள் படா- for a day not lost. In the Kural it is the good deed done daily; here it is the sun that never fails to rise and so measures out a life. The same words point to opposite sides of the account.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:22","b":"n:7","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the sun the measuring vessel of one's days; in 7 Death does the measuring and eating, in 22 the measure alone is warning enough to do ஒப்புரவு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:23","b":"n:6","type":"same_image","note":"The funeral drum sounds in both: in 6 it may sound tomorrow for the hoarder, in 23 it replaces the wedding drum on the same day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:26","b":"k:340","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat the life as a temporary occupant of the body, a lodger in the Kural and a dancer in a skin bag here, so the body is not its home.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:27","b":"k:349","type":"agrees","note":"Both join seeing impermanence to ending the round of births; on Mudaliar's reading of தடுமாற்றம், the wise here resolve to end what Kural 349 says ends when attachment ends.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:28","b":"n:34","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the rare body must be used to win its fruit before it goes; 34 names that fruit as virtue and adds the sugar-cane image.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:29","b":"k:336","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 336 says one here yesterday is gone today; this quatrain shrinks the span to a sentence, stood, sat, lay down, gone, and draws the command to act now.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:30","b":"k:338","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the life leaving the body as a bird leaving its nest. The Kural makes it a comment on body and life; the Naladiyar applies it to kinsfolk, who leave the body behind for their family.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:31","b":"k:37","type":"agrees","note":"Both read present fortune as the visible fruit of virtue: the Kural's palanquin rider and bearer, the Naladiyar's insider and the one at the gate. The Naladiyar adds that the insider's place is lost when the merit is not renewed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:32","b":"k:32","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 32 says no ruin exceeds forgetting virtue (மறத்தல்); this quatrain stages the heart that forgets virtue (அறமறந்து) for wealth and asks it what it will do now.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:33","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the arrival of past deeds' fruit an occasion for equanimity; the Naladiyar goes further and makes that acceptance the way out of rebirth's confusion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:34","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"Both use crushed sugar-cane. In the Kural the base yield only when crushed, a reproach; here the body should be crushed for its juice, a counsel.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:35","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both say virtue gathered in time stays when the body perishes; the Kural calls it an undying companion, the Naladiyar the jaggery kept when the cane refuse burns.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:36","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on not knowing when death comes: the Kural mocks those who plan crores of things without knowing they will live another moment, and this quatrain answers with Death standing behind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:37","b":"n:22","type":"qualifies","note":"22 urges ஒப்புரவு before one's days end; 37 warns against an ஒப்புரவு done only for the body. Together they mark the word's proper object as others, not oneself.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:38","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a small good large: the Kural's millet-sized help counted as a palmyra by those who know its worth, the Naladiyar's banyan seed. The Naladiyar places the growth in the worthiness of the recipient.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:39","b":"k:334","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 334 says the day looks like a thing but is a saw cutting life, to those who see it; this quatrain describes those who do not see it, and rejoice at each day.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:40","b":"k:968","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue that keeping the body alive at the cost of dignity buys nothing, since the body does not last: the Kural asks if it is a medicine, the Naladiyar makes the bargain conditional on a body that never dies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:41","b":"k:340","type":"same_image","note":"Both call the body a புக்கில், a lodging; Mudaliar even glosses with துச்சில், the Kural's word. The Kural uses it to show the life has no home of its own; the Naladiyar to show the body is a mean hut not worth desiring.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:42","b":"k:80","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 80 says a body without love is bone wrapped in skin, implying love makes it more. The Naladiyar treats every body as filth wrapped in skin and allows no such exception.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:44","b":"k:1114","type":"same_image","note":"The water-lily as a figure for the eye appears in both. In Kural 1114 the lover says the lily would bow before her eyes; here the renunciant calls those who make that comparison eyeless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:45","b":"k:1113","type":"tension","note":"Kural 1113's lover calls her teeth pearls and her eyes spears; this quatrain calls those who praise teeth as pearls untaught and petty, and answers with bones in the burning ground. The conflict holds if the Kural's Book of Love is read as valuing the lover's praise, not only as dramatised speech.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:48","b":"k:913","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 913 likens the embrace of women who sell themselves to embracing a corpse in the dark; the Naladiyar extends the corpse to every admired body, which the Kural does not.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:49","b":"n:50","type":"same_image","note":"In both the skulls laugh and teach: in 49 they speak to all the living, in 50 they cure the infatuated in particular.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:50","b":"k:345","type":"agrees","note":"Both conclude that the body is not worth valuing: for those ending births even the body is excess in Kural 345, and those cured by the skulls here count themselves as nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:51","b":"n:93","type":"develops","note":"Both make good fortune last only as long as the merit behind it: in 51 evil returns when good deeds are exhausted, and in 93 wealth leaves when the deeds that sent it run out (வினையுலந்தக்கால்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"k:266","type":"agrees","note":"Mudaliar quotes Kural 266 here: those who do austerity do their own work (தங்கருமம்), and the rest waste themselves. 52 names the waste as endless scholarship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"k:397","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 397 asks why anyone stays unlearned until death. 52 calls lifelong study of grammar and astrology madness. They are compatible only if the Kural's learning is not the instrumental lore 52 mocks; read together, 52 limits which study is worth a life.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:52","b":"n:135","type":"develops","note":"Mudaliar points to 135, where learning has no shore and life is short, so one must choose what to learn. 52 draws the harder conclusion that endless study is folly when death is near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:53","b":"k:342","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge renouncing while there is time: 342 says renounce while it is still possible, and 53 has the wise renounce without delay once they see that everything fails to last.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:54","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"Kural 46 asks what one would gain by leaving a household life lived in virtue. 54 says the wise, seeing the pain in household pleasure, keep off the household way. Both answer whether household life is a path the wise should stay on, and they answer opposite ways. The conflict holds unless 54 is read as describing only the fools' pleasure-seeking household, not a virtuous one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:55","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both press for the good before the body fails: 335 says do good before the death-rattle stops the tongue, and 55 urges the heart to set out now that youth is gone and age and disease are near.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:56","b":"k:52","type":"develops","note":"Kural 52 says a household is nothing if the wife lacks household excellence. 56 starts from the same case and adds that she can't be put away, then draws a conclusion the Kural never draws: marriage itself is to be shunned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:57","b":"k:267","type":"agrees","note":"Both make hardship the test of austerity: 267 has the ascetic shine like gold in the fire, and 57 counts as blessed only those who hold their vows when unbearable troubles strike.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:58","b":"k:157","type":"agrees","note":"Parimelazhagar glosses the pity of Kural 157 (நோநொந்து) by quoting this quatrain: grieving that the wrongdoer may fall into the fiery hell.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:59","b":"k:24","type":"agrees","note":"Both make control of the five senses the way to release: 24 calls the one who guards the five with firmness a seed for the highest ground, and 59 says the one who steers their craving reaches வீடு.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:60","b":"n:54","type":"agrees","note":"The two share a line and an argument. 54 ends with leaving household life and 60 with refusing to cling to pleasure as it comes.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:60","b":"k:629","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie freedom from pain to not craving pleasure: 629 says one who does not want the pleasure within pleasure feels no pain within pain, and 60 has the higher ones see the pain in each pleasure and let it go.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:61","b":"k:151","type":"agrees","note":"Both find the model of bearing scorn in something below us: 151 in the earth that holds up those who dig it, 61 in the fly that climbs on one's head.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:62","b":"k:969","type":"tension","note":"Kural 969 praises those who, like the yak, give up life when honour is lost. 62 says those set on finishing their work don't give up life whenever disgrace comes. Both answer whether disgrace is a reason to die. The conflict holds if 969's மானம் covers the humiliation 62 has in mind; 62 limits itself to those with a task to complete.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:63","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both make speech a burn. In 129 the tongue's burn on the hearer never heals; in 63 careless words burn the speaker without end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:64","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"153 calls bearing with the ignorant the strength of strengths; 64 shows the excellent doing it and the base failing at it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:65","b":"k:301","type":"agrees","note":"Both count restraint only where it costs something: 301 says one who checks anger where it could take effect truly checks it, and 65 that true patience belongs to one with the power to punish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:65","b":"n:91","type":"agrees","note":"65 calls the gift of one without wealth the true gift; 91 promises open doors in the next world to those who give gladly even when they have little.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:66","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"Both praise bearing the bitter words of those who overstep. 159 likens such people to renunciants in purity; 66 credits their lineage with the restraint.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:67","b":"k:312","type":"agrees","note":"Both make not returning harm to one who has harmed you the mark of the good. 67 adds that this is not weakness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:68","b":"k:29","type":"qualifies","note":"Both make the anger of the good brief. Kural 29, in Parimelazhagar's reading, adds that even while it lasts it can't be withstood; 68 stresses only that it cools of itself, leaving out its force.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:69","b":"k:987","type":"agrees","note":"987 asks what worth is for if it doesn't do good even to those who did harm; 69 says the well-born do exactly that, even to the ungrateful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:71","b":"k:797","type":"agrees","note":"Both make getting away from fools a gain: 797 calls leaving a fool's friendship profit, and 71 advises slipping away rather than talking with one.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:72","b":"n:64","type":"agrees","note":"Both open with the same situation in nearly the same words (நீரல்ல சொல்லியக்கால்): 64 shows the excellent not growing angry, and 72 adds that the world counts retaliation as disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:72","b":"k:155","type":"agrees","note":"Both make public regard the measure: 155 says people count those who retaliate as nothing and keep those who forbear like gold, and 72 that the world takes retaliation as disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:73","b":"k:784","type":"agrees","note":"784 says friendship exists to rebuke a friend who oversteps, not to laugh together. 73 values those harsh words above an outsider's pleasant ones.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:74","b":"k:428","type":"agrees","note":"Both use the phrase 'fear what should be feared' (அஞ்சுவது அஞ்சு-): 428 makes it the work of the wise, and 74 lists it among the marks of those who live free of suffering.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:75","b":"n:76","type":"tension","note":"Adjacent quatrains on a friend who wrongs you: 75 allows quiet withdrawal once one can bear no more, and 76 forbids abandoning a close friend and says to blame oneself. The conflict holds if both concern the same wrong; it weakens if 75's தேற்றா ஒழுக்கம் is the friend's general misconduct rather than hurt done to oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:75","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"Both forbid broadcasting a close friend's faults with the same verb (தூற்று-): 188 condemns those who spread even their intimates' faults, and 75 says to withdraw without doing so.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:76","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"Both hold that close friends are not cast off for the harm they do: 807 says those bound by love keep it even when friends bring ruin, and 76 says to blame oneself rather than leave.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:78","b":"k:1067","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that if one must ask, ask only the right people: 1067 pleads with beggars not to ask those who conceal, and 78 forbids telling one's want to the graceless.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:78","b":"k:1064","type":"agrees","note":"1064 praises the worth that won't consent to begging even with nothing left; 78 implies the same ranking, since only those who lack the resolve to die tell their need at all.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"n:79","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Both put blamelessness above the strongest pull: 656 forbids a deed the worthy would blame even to feed one's starving mother, and 79 forbids blameworthy pleasure even if it would never end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:80","b":"k:205","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse to let ruin excuse wrongdoing: 205 says not to do evil because one is poor, and 80 says not to plot the ruin of the worthy even when one is ruined oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:82","b":"k:146","type":"develops","note":"146 lists four things that never leave the adulterer: enmity, sin, fear and blame. 82 keeps the same four and pairs them with four goods he loses: virtue, renown, friendship and greatness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:84","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"148 calls not looking at another's wife great manliness (பேராண்மை); 84 calls the act unmanliness (ஆணின்மை).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:86","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"Both turn on the look (நோக்கு): 148 praises the manliness that does not look at another's wife, and 86 asks why a married man would look at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:88","b":"k:1134","type":"tension","note":"In 1134 the flood of desire carries off the raft of shame and manliness. In 88 the strong, fearing shame, keep desire hidden until it subsides. Both make claims about whether shame can hold desire, and they answer opposite ways. The conflict depends on reading 1134 as a general claim; it is a lover's plea in the Book of Love, while 88 states a rule about the strong.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"},{"a":"n:89","b":"k:129","type":"same_image","note":"Both contrast an outer burn with an inner one: in 129 the fire's wound heals but the tongue's scar does not, and in 89 arrow, fire and sun burn the body while desire burns the mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:90","b":"k:1159","type":"same_image","note":"Both say desire burns where fire can't: 1159 says fire burns only when touched while love burns at a distance, and 90 that fire can be escaped in water but desire can't.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:90","b":"k:1049","type":"parallel_domain","note":"The same figure in another domain: 1049 says one can sleep in the midst of fire but not of poverty, and 90 that one can escape fire but not desire. Each makes fire the lesser affliction.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:91","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"Both hold that giving does not wait for wealth: 218 says those who know their duty don't falter in helping even when means are gone, and 91 rewards those who give gladly when they have little.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:92","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue from impermanence: 333 says wealth does not stay, so do what lasts at once, and 92 says death, age and disease are near, so share while wealth is in hand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:93","b":"k:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that guarding keeps wealth: 376 says what is not one's own won't stay however it is guarded, and 93 that wealth leaves when its merit runs out however tightly held. 376 names fate; 93 names past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:94","b":"k:270","type":"qualifies","note":"Both explain the many poor by what was not done: 270 by the few who practise austerity, and 94 in Mudaliar's reading by the failure to give in a former birth. They give different causes for the same fact without denying each other.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:95","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"Both put a number on not begging: 1061 says it is worth ten million times more even when givers are glad, and 95 that it is worth twice as much as giving.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:96","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the giver's wealth in a fruiting tree at the town's centre, where all can reach it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:96","b":"k:1008","type":"same_image","note":"1008 keeps the tree in the town's centre but makes the miser's wealth a poison tree fruiting there; 96 moves the miser's barren palm out to the burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:97","b":"k:211","type":"develops","note":"211 makes the rain the model of help that seeks no return. 97 sets rain and the good's giving side by side as the two things the world can't survive without.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:97","b":"k:19","type":"qualifies","note":"In 19 giving depends on rain: if the sky fails, giving and austerity cease. In 97 the two are parallel supports, each needed on its own.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"n:98","b":"k:221","type":"agrees","note":"Both say giving to those who can repay is not giving but lending: 221 calls it a measured return (குறியெதிர்ப்பை), and 98 a profitable loan (பொலிகடன்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:99","b":"k:475","type":"parallel_domain","note":"Both turn on small things adding up: in 475 peacock feathers, piled high enough, break a cart's axle, and in 99 small gifts fill the bowl. One warns, the other encourages.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:100","b":"k:232","type":"agrees","note":"Both make giving the source of lasting praise: 232 says all that speakers praise rests on those who give to the asker, and 100 that the word 'they gave' is heard through three worlds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:101","b":"k:208","type":"agrees","note":"Both make a deed's consequence inseparable from its doer, with different images: the Kural's ruin stays at the heels like a shadow, and the Naladiyar's deed goes looking like a calf for its mother.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:101","b":"n:120","type":"develops","note":"101 says the deed finds its doer among many; 120 says it is the only thing that goes with him at death. Together they make the deed both pursuer and sole companion.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:102","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"Both draw the same conclusion from impermanent wealth: do the lasting good at once. 102 adds beauty, youth and standing to the list and condemns the life that does nothing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:103","b":"k:377","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that enjoyment is measured out in advance: the Kural even for those who amass millions, the Naladiyar for all who want prosperity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:104","b":"k:269","type":"tension","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 104 says that even sages cannot remove what is bound to befall. 269 says that those who attain the power of austerity can even leap past death. Both concern whether ascetic power can overcome what is coming; the tension holds if உறுவர் are ascetics and 269's கூற்றம் is fated death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:104","b":"k:620","type":"tension","note":"104 says no one can remove what is destined, as no one can make rain fall; 620 says those who strive without flagging see even fate's back. The tension depends on reading 620 as effort overcoming fate, as Pope and Kalaignar do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:105","b":"n:3","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the great brought low and give the same cause: in 3 the elephant-riders fall when their deeds are spent, and in 105 the palm-sized become millet-sized through former deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:106","b":"k:406","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that the unlearned truly live: the Kural says they can only be said to exist, like barren land, and the Naladiyar says Death will not even take them, as husks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:107","b":"k:1062","type":"tension","note":"107 makes begging the outworking of the beggar's own former deeds, so the order that produces it is just. 1062 calls down ruin on whoever ordained that anyone should live by begging, treating it as an unjust order. The tension depends on 107's vinai being the beggar's own deeds.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:108","b":"k:834","type":"qualifies","note":"Both describe one who knows and still does wrong. The Kural calls him the greatest fool; the Naladiyar traces the act to former deeds, which explains it without removing the blame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:109","b":"k:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that a person's will has no say in what comes: in the Kural, care cannot keep what is not one's own; here, wishing cannot keep off what is due.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:110","b":"k:379","type":"agrees","note":"Both end a chapter on fate by asking why anyone grieves when the bad arrives, since it is as fixed as the good.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:111","b":"k:110","type":"develops","note":"The Kural makes killing a kindness received the sin with no escape. On both commentators' reading, 111 names something worse: a promise held out and then broken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:111","b":"k:223","type":"qualifies","note":"223 praises never uttering 'I have nothing' and giving instead. 111 allows that saying 'no' is blameless when one truly cannot give; the fault lies in false promising.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:112","b":"k:514","type":"tension","note":"112 says the worthy and unworthy never fall from their natures at any time. 514 says that many people, though trusted after every test, change through the nature of the work. The tension holds if 514 means people really change, as Parimelazhagar says, rather than that the tests misjudged them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:113","b":"k:796","type":"develops","note":"113 observes that kin vanish in trouble; 796 turns that into a benefit, saying ruin is a rod that measures one's people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:114","b":"k:760","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that whoever secures wealth gets the other two aims of life as well. The Naladiyar adds the cost of failing: suffering like being boiled.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:114","b":"n:1","type":"qualifies","note":"114 makes wealth the key to virtue and pleasure; 1, opening the book, shows the rich reduced to begging for gruel and says wealth should not be counted on. Wealth secures the other two only while it lasts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:115","b":"k:1046","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that the poor are not heard however sound their words. The Naladiyar adds the other half: the rich are heard even when unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:116","b":"k:834","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe study that leaves a person unrestrained. The Kural calls such a person the greatest fool; the Naladiyar says it is their nature, as bitterness is the gourd's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:117","b":"k:151","type":"tension","note":"117 says to scorn those who scorn you; 151 says that bearing with them, as the earth bears those who dig it, is the highest thing. Both concern the same situation. The tension depends on reading 117 as advice, as Mudaliar, Shanmuganar and Pope do, and not as irony.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:117","b":"n:72","type":"tension","note":"Within the Naladiyar: 72 says that when unworthy people speak improperly, bearing it in silence is fitting and the world thinks retaliation shameful. 117 says to scorn the scorners first. The tension holds if 117's scorners and 72's speakers are the same kind of person.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:118","b":"k:34","type":"develops","note":"118 says virtue is one in nature whatever form its ways take. 34 says what that one thing is: a spotless mind, everything else being show.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:120","b":"k:36","type":"qualifies","note":"36 makes virtue the companion that does not die with you. 120 says the only thing that goes along is the deed, which includes bad deeds; so the companion can also be a burden.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:121","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 121 makes household life itself the sorrow in which those who fail to renounce end as corpses. 46 asks what anyone would gain by leaving a household life lived in virtue for the other path. The tension depends on Mudaliar's gloss of துக்கம்; on Shanmuganar's it is weaker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:121","b":"k:251","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn eating flesh. The Kural asks how one who fattens his flesh on others' can have grace; the Naladiyar makes his belly a burning ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:122","b":"k:319","type":"agrees","note":"Both say harm done to others returns to the doer. The Naladiyar makes the return copy the harm: those who caged birds wear irons.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:123","b":"k:330","type":"agrees","note":"Both explain a wretched diseased body as the result of having taken life. The Naladiyar names the creature and the disease.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"k:452","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a person takes on the nature of their company, the Kural with water changing with the soil and the Naladiyar with ghee turning to flame.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"n:112","type":"tension","note":"112 says the worthy never fall from their nature, as jaggery is never bitter. 124 says even the upright turn to cruel deeds when they join the wicked. Both concern whether good character can be changed; the tension holds if 124's கோடார் are truly good, as Mudaliar's நடுவுநிலைமையுடையார் implies.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:124","b":"n:139","type":"develops","note":"124 shows company corrupting the upright; 139 shows it improving the unlearned, like a new pot taking on the scent of flowers. The same mechanism works in both directions.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:125","b":"k:782","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the waxing and waning moon for good and bad friendship. The Kural sets the wise against fools, and the Naladiyar the great against the small.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:126","b":"k:792","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn of friendship formed without examination; the Kural says it ends in mortal grief, the Naladiyar finds a snake where sandal was expected.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:127","b":"k:706","type":"tension","note":"127 says no one can know another's mind, because minds and deeds differ. 706 says the face shows what is uppermost in the heart, as crystal shows what is near it. The tension depends on taking 127 as a general claim rather than a warning about deceivers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:127","b":"k:702","type":"qualifies","note":"702 grants that someone may know another's mind without doubt, but calls such a person equal to a god. 127 says no one has that power, which agrees that it is beyond ordinary people.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:128","b":"k:821","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe friendship without inner consent. The Kural calls it an anvil waiting for the right moment to strike; the Naladiyar says it becomes a stain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:129","b":"k:727","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a bright sword in the wrong hands. In the Kural it is learning held by one who fears the assembly; in the Naladiyar it is one's own strength lost through association with the unlearned.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:129","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"Both prefer distance from fools to closeness: the Kural rates the enmity of the wise far above a fool's intimacy, and the Naladiyar says the good course is to get away from the unlearned.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:130","b":"k:61","type":"tension","note":"61 says no gain is known greater than children who learn what should be known. 130 rebukes the heart for pining for children and says there is no gain for the soul but good done. The tension softens if 61 speaks of worldly gains and 130 only of the soul's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:131","b":"k:407","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that outward beauty is worth anything without learning: the Kural compares it to a well-made clay doll, and the Naladiyar refuses it the name of beauty.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:132","b":"k:400","type":"agrees","note":"Both say learning does not perish when other possessions do. The Naladiyar spells out the ways it differs from wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:132","b":"k:398","type":"qualifies","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, 132 says learning lasts as long as its owner lives; 398 says learning gained in one birth protects through seven. Shanmuganar's reading, that it lasts through fame, is closer to the Kural's.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:133","b":"k:409","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank the low-born learned above the unlearned, the Kural by comparing them to the high-born unlearned and the Naladiyar with salt from brackish soil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:133","b":"n:142","type":"qualifies","note":"133 sets the low-born learned in the highest place; 142 says excellence, gentleness and good conduct belong only to the high-born, whatever wealth others gain. Read together, learning can raise one's rank but, in 142, not those qualities.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:134","b":"k:67","type":"agrees","note":"Both make learning the best thing a father gives his children: the Kural as a place at the front of the assembly, the Naladiyar as an inheritance that cannot be taken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:135","b":"k:391","type":"agrees","note":"Both insist on learning what is to be learned. The Naladiyar adds the reason: learning is endless and life is short.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:136","b":"k:423","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the worth of what is taught does not depend on who teaches it: the Kural says to see the truth in whatever is heard from anyone, and the Naladiyar says to learn from the learned whatever their caste.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:137","b":"k:394","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the meeting of the learned a delight. The Kural describes how they part; the Naladiyar ranks their gathering above heaven.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:138","b":"k:783","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that friendship with people of character grows sweeter with time, the Kural like a book reread and the Naladiyar like cane eaten from the tip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:139","b":"k:452","type":"agrees","note":"Both say understanding takes on the nature of one's company, the Kural with water and soil and the Naladiyar with water and flowers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:140","b":"k:356","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the purpose of learning the perception of what is true, which leads out of rebirth on Mudaliar's reading of 140. The Naladiyar adds that worldly learning cannot do this.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:141","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both use an animal that will not go against its nature in extremity: the yak that dies when it loses a hair, and the lion that will not eat grass. The Kural speaks of honour, the Naladiyar of the principles of the well-born.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:142","b":"k:952","type":"agrees","note":"Both name three qualities the well-born never lose. The Kural's are conduct, truthfulness and shame; the Naladiyar's are fullness of character, gentleness and conduct, and it adds that wealth cannot buy them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:144","b":"k:957","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a fault in the well-born is seen by everyone, the Kural like the spot on the moon and the Naladiyar as blame spread by many.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:145","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Both call people who lack a human moral quality trees or wood: in the Kural those without courtesy, however sharp, and in the Naladiyar the low-born who lack the four fears.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:145","b":"k:951","type":"agrees","note":"Both say a moral fear or shame is found naturally only in the well-born.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:146","b":"k:953","type":"agrees","note":"Both list four qualities of a good lineage, and both include giving and sweet speech.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:146","b":"k:972","type":"tension","note":"146 says that good company, sweet speech, giving and goodness of mind are found only among the well-born. 972 says birth is alike for all and distinction comes from the deeds one does. The two disagree about whether excellence follows birth or deeds. The tension holds if 972's distinction means worth of character, as Pope and Kalaignar take it, and if 146's qualities belong to that worth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:147","b":"k:219","type":"qualifies","note":"219 says that, for a good man, poverty is being unable to do what should be done. 147 says the well-born still do it even in hardship, so for them poverty in that sense never fully arrives.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:148","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"The two share the phrase ஒப்புரவிற்கு ஒல்கார்: those who know their duty, or the well-born, do not slacken in serving others when they lack the means. The Naladiyar adds the half-eclipsed moon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:149","b":"k:1077","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base do not give even when they could: in the Kural they won't shake off their wet hands for anyone but those who would break their jaws.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:150","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that the well-born keep their generous nature when their means have shrunk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:151","b":"k:957","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the spot on the moon. In 957 the fault of the well-born shows as plainly as the moon's spot; in 151 the noble differ from the moon in being unable to live with the spot at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:151","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both make honour a matter of life and death: in 969 the honourable give up life like the yak that loses its hair, and in 151 the noble waste away at a single stain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:152","b":"k:596","type":"agrees","note":"Aim high regardless of outcome: 596 says a lofty aim, even if thwarted, keeps its worth, and 152 says the arrow that misses a lion beats the one that kills a jackal.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:153","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Want does not license wrongdoing. 656 holds even if one's mother starves; 153 holds even when one's own body is reduced to sinew.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:154","b":"k:785","type":"agrees","note":"785 says long acquaintance is not needed for friendship, only shared feeling; 154 shows the worthy treating a single meeting as an old bond.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:154","b":"k:791","type":"tension","note":"791 warns that nothing ruins like befriending without inquiry, since the true friend cannot later withdraw. 154 praises binding someone seen once as if an old friend. The tension holds if 154 means any passer-by; Pope's parenthetical '(worthy) men' softens it, but the Tamil has no such qualifier.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:155","b":"k:153","type":"agrees","note":"153 calls bearing with fools the strength of strengths; 155 shows that strength as patient listening to an ignorant speaker so as not to shame him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:155","b":"k:403","type":"qualifies","note":"403 puts the burden on the unlearned: they are good if they stay silent before the learned. 155 takes the case where they do not, and puts the burden on the learned to endure it kindly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:156","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"Both use crushed sugar-cane, to opposite ends. In 1078 the base are like cane, giving only when crushed; in 156 the well-born are like cane, still sweet however crushed.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:156","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"159 makes bearing harsh words from the insolent as pure as renunciation; 156 makes it the mark of good birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:157","b":"k:139","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the virtuous cannot speak evil even by a slip: வழுக்கியும் in 139, தள்ளியும் in 157.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:158","b":"k:148","type":"agrees","note":"148 calls not looking at another's wife both virtue and complete conduct; 158 makes that blindness one of three abstentions that leave nothing more to teach.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:158","b":"k:181","type":"agrees","note":"Both give one abstention near-sufficient moral weight: 181 says it is good to be called one who does not backbite even if one does wrong, and 158 counts silence in backbiting among the restraints that make virtue-teaching unnecessary.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:159","b":"k:224","type":"qualifies","note":"224 admits that being asked is painful until one sees the asker's glad face; 159 describes the excellent as treating the prospect of being asked as good from the outset.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:159","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"1057 is the asker's side: the heart rejoices at givers who do not scorn. 159 is the giver's side: the excellent honour those who may ask instead of scorning them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:160","b":"k:443","type":"agrees","note":"443 calls winning the great as one's own the rarest of all things; 160 calls meeting the well-born a treasure cave, against the base who chase the rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:161","b":"k:700","type":"parallel_domain","note":"700 warns a minister that doing improper things on the strength of long familiarity with the king brings ruin. 161 makes the same point about presuming on the forbearance of the great.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:161","b":"k:896","type":"agrees","note":"896 says one may survive fire but not a lasting offence against the great; 161 says the damage of offending them cannot be undone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:162","b":"k:443","type":"develops","note":"443 calls winning the great the rarest of things. 162 imagines that rarity handed over free and wasted by people who cannot use it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:163","b":"n:165","type":"develops","note":"Both locate worth in the judgment of the worthy. 163 dismisses the verdict of the base, and 165 dismisses one's own verdict on oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:164","b":"k:895","type":"agrees","note":"895: those who have drawn a fierce king's anger exist nowhere, wherever they go. This matches Mudaliar's reading of 164, where the angered great are kings.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:164","b":"k:900","type":"agrees","note":"900: even those with overwhelming support perish if the eminent are angered. This matches the reading of 164 in which the great are the morally great and the fortress is no defence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:165","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says smallness adorns itself by admiring itself; 165 gives the words of that self-admiration and denies it the name of esteem.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:166","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"The same claim with a different measure of time: 782 has the waxing and waning moon, 166 the shortening and lengthening shadow.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:167","b":"k:691","type":"qualifies","note":"691 advises those who serve kings to stay neither too near nor too far, like people warming at a fire. 167 observes that those who stay close are the ones who enjoy a king's wealth, without the warning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:168","b":"k:839","type":"tension","note":"839 says, with irony, that friendship with fools is sweet because parting from them brings no pain. 168 says parting even from the undiscerning brings great sorrow. The two make opposite claims about the pain of parting from fools, if தெரிவிலார் is read as fools, as Mudaliar's gloss அறிவிலார் has it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:168","b":"k:816","type":"qualifies","note":"Both end with கோடி உறும். 816 says being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool, a ranking of companions. 168 drops the ranking: mixing with no one is ten million times better.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:168","b":"n:174","type":"tension","note":"168 concludes that not mixing with anyone is far better because of the pain of parting. 174 says no one would hate even painful birth if they could keep friendship with the noble. One makes withdrawal the remedy for life's pain, the other makes companionship the remedy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:169","b":"k:38","type":"agrees","note":"Both count days: 38 says letting no day fall idle without good blocks the road of births, and 169 says the good have no day without learning, visiting the great or giving. Both use படாஅ for days that do not occur.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:170","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"Both define greatness as bowing: பணியுமாம் என்றும் பெருமை in 978, பெரியார் பெருமை சிறுதகைமை in 170.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:170","b":"k:226","type":"agrees","note":"226 says removing the hunger of the destitute is where one should store wealth; 170 says only those who relieve their dependants' distress are truly wealthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:171","b":"k:455","type":"agrees","note":"455 says purity of mind and deed both lean on purity of company; 171 shows company acting on past deeds as well, clearing the faults of youth.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:172","b":"k:415","type":"agrees","note":"415 calls the words of the good a staff on slippery ground; 172 closes its six precepts with taking in the words of the great every day.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:172","b":"k:159","type":"agrees","note":"Both commend bearing others' harsh words; 159 ranks those who do it with renunciants, 172 lists it among the basic precepts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:173","b":"k:362","type":"agrees","note":"Both treat birth as the thing to escape: 362 says if one must want, want not to be born, and 173 seeks out those who know birth is bitter.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:174","b":"n:173","type":"qualifies","note":"173 declares birth bitter from the start. 174, placed next to it, grants that and adds that friendship with the good makes birth something no one would resent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:174","b":"k:460","type":"develops","note":"460 says there is no greater support than good company. 174 raises the stakes: good company is what reconciles a person to being born at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:175","b":"k:452","type":"same_image","note":"Both use water changed by what it joins. In 452 water takes on the nature of its soil, as a mind takes on its company; in 175 sewage takes the name of the sacred river, as the lowly take the standing of the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:176","b":"n:151","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the moon's dark spot. In 151 the moon tolerates its flaw and the noble cannot; in 176 the flaw is worshipped because it sits in the moon.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:177","b":"k:1000","type":"same_image","note":"Milk and its mixture, in opposite directions: in 1000 good milk is spoiled by a bad vessel, as wealth by a graceless owner; in 177 water is absorbed into milk, as the small into the great.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:178","b":"k:446","type":"agrees","note":"446: enemies can do nothing to one who walks with the worthy. 178 gives the image of grass the plough cannot uproot because it grows against a stump.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:179","b":"k:458","type":"agrees","note":"458 says that even the worthy, whose minds are good, are protected by good company. 179 gives the converse: their worth is wrecked by bad company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:180","b":"k:453","type":"agrees","note":"453 says what a person is called comes from their company; 180 says the pure in mind are scorned because of theirs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:180","b":"n:177","type":"develops","note":"The same mechanism run both ways: in 177 the mean are hidden by the great, in 180 the pure are blackened by the bad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:181","b":"k:1050","type":"agrees","note":"1050 says the destitute who do not renounce merely consume salt and gruel. 181 has someone who can no longer give conclude that renunciation is the fitting course.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:182","b":"k:331","type":"agrees","note":"331 calls taking the impermanent for permanent the lowest understanding; 182 gives the example of the householder who thinks himself settled for good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:182","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one gains by leaving a virtuous household life for the ascetic way. 182 calls those content in home life fools and praises those who see its supports as passing. The conflict holds on Mudaliar's reading, where the failing supports are household attachments themselves; if 182 targets only heedlessness, the two are compatible.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:183","b":"k:335","type":"agrees","note":"Both urge good deeds now because the body may fail without warning: 335 before the death-hiccup stops the tongue, 183 because the body changes without cause.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:184","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a village water source for the generous: 215 compares a wise man's wealth to a full town tank, 184 compares the great in decline to a spring-fed well that still serves in drought.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:184","b":"k:218","type":"agrees","note":"218 says those who know their duty do not slacken in serving others even when they have no means; 184 says the great keep giving in decline.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"n:184","type":"same_image","note":"Consecutive water images for giving in decline: a spring-fed well in 184, a dry riverbed that yields to digging in 185.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"n:153","type":"develops","note":"Both end with செய்வர் செயற்பாலவை. In 153 the poor worthy do their duty within their means; in 185 the once-rich keep doing it after ruin, for fewer people.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:185","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"955: even when what they can give has shrunk, an old family keeps its nature. 185 says the same of the ruined giver who still gives to a few.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:186","b":"k:957","type":"agrees","note":"957 says a fault in the well-born stands out like the moon's spot; 186 says the same with a brand on a white bull, and adds that the crimes of the small go unseen.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:187","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816: being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than intimacy with a fool. 187 goes further: even enmity with the wise brings dignity, while intimacy with the mean brings grief.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:188","b":"k:987","type":"tension","note":"987 asks what worth is for if it cannot do good even to those who do harm. 188 says to meet foes with terror and the false with falseness. The conflict is direct on Mudaliar's reading of சலவர் as liars; on Pope's reading the third clause is milder, but the clause on foes still conflicts.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:188","b":"k:830","type":"agrees","note":"830 advises friendship of the face, not the heart, with a foe who turns friendly. 188 likewise answers each person in their own coin rather than with uniform goodness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:190","b":"k:360","type":"develops","note":"360 says suffering ends when desire, anger and delusion perish. On Shanmuganar's reading of the three faults, 190 offers a practice that removes them: feeding others before oneself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:190","b":"k:227","type":"agrees","note":"227: hunger does not touch one who habitually shares food. 190 says sharing the first-served food keeps the worthy from sorrow till the end.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:191","b":"k:615","type":"agrees","note":"615 calls the one who prefers work to pleasure a pillar who wipes away his kin's troubles. On Shanmuganar's reading of 191, kin rest easy on what the energetic one provides.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:192","b":"k:611","type":"agrees","note":"611 says effort brings the greatness the task requires; 192 shows it as a twig hardening into a post strong enough for an elephant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:193","b":"k:618","type":"agrees","note":"618 says bad fortune is no disgrace but failing to work is; 193 tells the knowledgeable not to scorn humble work when fortune is thin.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:193","b":"n:198","type":"qualifies","note":"198 says the lion-hearted would die rather than do anything disgraceful; 193 says even the tiger eats a frog when hungry. Read together, humble work is not disgrace, and dishonourable deeds are.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:194","b":"k:619","type":"agrees","note":"619 says effort pays its wage even when fate denies the goal; 194 makes persistence through failure the very definition of manliness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:194","b":"n:152","type":"agrees","note":"Both begin from success or failure (இசையும் எனினும் இசையாதெனினும் in 152, இசையாதெனினும் in 194) and judge a person by how they act regardless of outcome.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"972: birth is alike for all beings, and distinction differs by the work done. 195 says good and bad caste are mere words and lineage is made by austerity, learning and effort.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"k:951","type":"tension","note":"951 says integrity and shame exist together naturally only in the well-born. 195 says good and bad lineage are words without substance. Both concern whether birth carries real moral qualities, and they answer opposite ways unless 951's இற்பிறந்தார் is read as a lineage made by conduct, as 195 defines it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:195","b":"n:179","type":"tension","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading of 179, the worthy are formed by the family they are born into, as paddy by its soil; 195 says good and bad lineage are only words. On Mudaliar's reading of குலம் in 179 as one's company, there is no conflict.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:196","b":"k:663","type":"agrees","note":"663 says true manliness lets a deed be known only when finished, since revealing it midway brings grief; 196 says the perceptive keep their plans to themselves until the work is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:196","b":"k:701","type":"agrees","note":"701 calls one who reads what is unsaid an ornament to the world; 196 says the world is under the sign of those who read others' drive from their features.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:197","b":"k:1030","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a structure eaten at the base and propped up. In 1030 a family whose foot is cut by trouble falls if no good person props it; in 197 the son props the decaying father like banyan roots.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:197","b":"k:70","type":"develops","note":"70 says a son repays his father by making people wonder what penance the father did. 197 gives a more practical repayment: covering the father's weakness when he falters.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:198","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656: even seeing one's mother starve, do not do what the worthy condemn. 198: even dying in want at home, the strong do not do what brings disgrace.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:198","b":"k:969","type":"agrees","note":"Both set death against dishonour: 969's yak dies when it loses its hair, 198's lion-hearted would die in want rather than be disgraced.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:199","b":"k:601","type":"agrees","note":"601 says the lamp of lineage dies when sloth covers it; 199 says high birth is worth nothing without great manliness, like a flower that has lost its scent.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:200","b":"k:1065","type":"agrees","note":"Nearly the same claim: 1065 says even watery gruel got by one's own effort (தாள்தந்தது) is the sweetest food, and 200 says water won by one's own effort (தாளாண்மை) becomes nectar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:200","b":"n:296","type":"develops","note":"In 200 the Mutharaiyar are patrons whose feasts the base enjoy; in 296 the poor who never go begging to the rich are themselves called Mutharaiyar. The patron's name moves from giver to the self-reliant.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:201","b":"k:521","type":"develops","note":"521 says that only kin keep honouring old ties when a man has lost everything; 201 describes what that loyalty does inside the sufferer, likening it to a mother forgetting the pains of birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:202","b":"k:216","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the good man a fruiting tree that the many enjoy. 216 says his wealth is like a tree ripening in the middle of town; 202 adds the shade tree and insists he toils for it himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:203","b":"k:1027","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the burden of those who depend on a household on its capable members: 1027 compares them to the brave in battle, 203 to a branch that never fails to bear its own fruit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:204","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"Both contrast a friendship that holds or grows with one that soon fails; 782 uses the waxing and waning moon, 204 the ascetic's unswerving path.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:205","b":"k:790","type":"agrees","note":"Both distrust speech that labels a relation, and their wording is close (இனையர், இன்னர்/இன்னம்). 790 says friendship looks shabby when friends describe what they are to each other; 205 says the best people never sort others into ours and theirs.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:206","b":"k:64","type":"same_image","note":"Both say that plain gruel (கூழ், புற்கை) shared with one's own is sweeter than the finest food. The Kural says it is sweeter than ambrosia when one's children's little hands have stirred it; 206 says it is sweeter than a golden-dish feast from those who are not one's own.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:206","b":"n:217","type":"same_image","note":"The kin chapter and the chapter on testing friendship use the same contrast of poor food given with love and rich food given without it. 217 ends in the bitter strychnos fruit, 206 and 210 in neem.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:207","b":"k:90","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that hospitality without warmth spoils the meal for the guest. 90 says a guest withers like the anicham flower at an averted face; 207 says the punctual feast in an unloving house tastes of neem.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:208","b":"k:799","type":"agrees","note":"Both single out those who leave when ruin comes: 208 compares them to tongs that let go in the fire, and 799 says the memory of such friends burns the heart at the hour of death.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:209","b":"k:787","type":"agrees","note":"787 defines friendship partly as sharing the suffering in ruin; 209 says a friendship that shares only joys can do nothing even in the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:211","b":"n:138","type":"same_image","note":"A near doublet: both compare friendship with the learned to eating sugarcane from the tip and the other kind to eating it from the root. 138 ends on those without character or kindness, 211 on those without sweetness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:211","b":"k:783","type":"agrees","note":"Both say friendship with the right people grows sweeter with use. 783 compares it to a book whose delight grows with study; 211 to sugarcane eaten from the tip.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:212","b":"n:245","type":"tension","note":"212 relies on family as the only workable guarantee that a friend won't change, since minds can't be known. 245 says people are not like their இனம் but like their minds. The tension holds if 245's இனம் includes family, as Mudaliar's 'இனத்தார்' allows; if it means only the company one keeps, the two are about different things.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:212","b":"k:793","type":"qualifies","note":"793 tells one to know a man's qualities, family, faults and connections before befriending him. 212 keeps family but doubts the rest: the mind can't be known, so family is the one dependable ground.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:213","b":"k:807","type":"agrees","note":"Both prize love that survives injury from the friend: 807 says those bound by love don't stop loving even when the friend ruins them, and 213 pictures the dog wagging its tail with the spear in its body.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:214","b":"k:785","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that a shared heart, not meeting or long acquaintance, makes friendship. 214 adds the reverse case: long absence doesn't break it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:215","b":"k:425","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a flower's opening and closing for inconstant friendship. 425 says understanding is not opening and closing; 215 contrasts tree flowers that stay open with pond flowers that close.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:216","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"104 says those who know its value count a millet-sized help as palmyra-sized. On Mudaliar's reading the palmyra in 216 stands for the best friends, who form a great friendship from one small help.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:219","b":"n:187","type":"agrees","note":"Both prefer hostility from the right people to friendship with the wrong. 187 says the enmity of the wise brings dignity; 219 says enmity is better than the friendship of those without clear understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:219","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816 says being a stranger to the wise is ten million times better than close friendship with a fool. 219 goes further: open enmity is better than a fool's friendship.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:220","b":"k:791","type":"agrees","note":"Both argue for testing before befriending because a formed bond can't be cleanly undone. 791 gives the reason as the friend's own loyalty; 220 gives it as the pain of parting, even from a snake.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:220","b":"n:168","type":"agrees","note":"Both say parting from a bad bond still hurts. 168 concludes that it is best to mix with no one; 220 concludes that one must test long before mixing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:221","b":"n:75","type":"qualifies","note":"75 says to bear a friend's unkind conduct as far as one can and, if one cannot, to withdraw without speaking ill. 221 offers no such exit: keep them and keep their faults inside.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:221","b":"k:805","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask for a charitable account of a friend's wrongs. 805 says to put them down to folly or great intimacy; 221 says every good thing has its husk.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:222","b":"k:151","type":"agrees","note":"Both make forbearance toward those who damage you an image from the land: the earth bears those who dig into it (151), and the farmer re-dams the stream that keeps breaking through (222).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:223","b":"k:803","type":"agrees","note":"803 asks what use an old friendship is if one doesn't accept a friend's liberties as one's own acts. 223 puts the same demand as a maxim: one person's patience keeps two in friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:224","b":"k:799","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture a wrong friendship as burning the heart. In 799 it is the memory of friends who deserted one; in 224 it is a friend one cannot leave who lacks good nature.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:225","b":"n:224","type":"same_image","note":"Adjacent quatrains turn fire both ways: in 224 a bad friend is a fire lit to burn the heart, and in 225 the fire that burned the house is still needed daily, so the friend who hurt one is kept.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:226","b":"n:76","type":"agrees","note":"Both forbid casting off a close friend for the hurt they cause. 76 says to blame oneself, since even beasts find parting hard; 226 says no one cuts off the hand that poked their eye.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:227","b":"k:980","type":"agrees","note":"980 says greatness hides others' faults and smallness only tells them. 227 applies this to friends and ranks the teller below the wrongdoer.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:227","b":"k:188","type":"agrees","note":"188 asks what those who spread the faults of their intimates would do to strangers. 227 makes the same judgement on those who publish a friend's wrongs.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:228","b":"k:805","type":"agrees","note":"Both reinterpret a friend's hurtful act. 805 reads it as folly or intimacy; 228 says it becomes good once held in the heart.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:229","b":"k:830","type":"qualifies","note":"830 says to befriend a former enemy with the face, let go in the heart, and then drop the friendship. 229 also hides an inner judgement behind outward regard, but toward a friend who has proved false, and it never says to drop him.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:230","b":"k:504","type":"qualifies","note":"504 tells one to examine a person's qualities and faults and take what predominates. 230 forbids that examination once the friendship is made, so the two divide the labour: examine before, never after.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:230","b":"n:238","type":"same_image","note":"Both are first-person curses with the same frame: may I go where the worst betrayer of a friend goes, while the world laughs. 230 names the one who did not keep a friend's secret, and 238 the one who violated a friend's wife.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:231","b":"k:812","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe friends who stay while there is something to gain. 812 asks what it matters whether one keeps or loses them; 231 shows them putting up with discomfort until their business is done.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:231","b":"n:284","type":"agrees","note":"284 has crowds gathering like crows while wealth lasts and no one asking after you when it goes; 231 shows the same self-interest from inside the friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:232","b":"k:811","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the friendship of the worthless is barren even at its fullest. 811 says it is better shrinking than growing; 232 says even at its height it is like failed rain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:233","b":"n:137","type":"agrees","note":"Both set the company of the learned against heaven. 137 doubts heaven could be sweeter; 233 makes the comparison explicit and adds that bad company is one of the hells.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:233","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"Both put the company one keeps at the extremes: no greater support than good company and no greater affliction than bad (460), heaven and hell (233).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:234","b":"k:782","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe a friendship that seems to grow and then shrinks: the moon past full (782), fire in straw (234).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:235","b":"k:818","type":"agrees","note":"818 says to let slip the friendship of those who make difficulties of what they could do. 235 names the same delay, with empty promising, and says it brings suffering even to ascetics.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:236","b":"n:177","type":"qualifies","note":"177 says the smallness of the small stops showing when they join the great, as water in milk looks like milk. 236 says their deeds stay different though they grow up beside the great. Read together, association changes how the small look, not what they do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:236","b":"k:823","type":"agrees","note":"Both say an outside good doesn't change an inner nature: 823 says the unworthy rarely become good at heart however many good books they study, and 236 says the same of their friendship with the large-natured.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:237","b":"k:825","type":"agrees","note":"Both make inner accord the condition of friendship. 825 says not to trust the words of those not one with you in mind; 237 says friendship with them is painful.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:238","b":"k:787","type":"agrees","note":"787 says friendship shares the suffering when ruin comes. 238 raises the stake to one's own life and curses the friend who holds back.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:239","b":"k:797","type":"agrees","note":"797 calls leaving the friendship of fools a gain. 239 describes the reverse exchange, leaving the discerning for the petty-minded, as ghee swapped for neem oil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:240","b":"n:244","type":"qualifies","note":"240 says the discerning who take up with bad company are ruined; 244 says the good-natured don't turn bad in mind though their company is bad. On Mudaliar's reading of 240 as ruin, the two fit: such men may keep their minds and still lose everything else, as 180 says of the sandal tree in a forest fire.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:240","b":"n:179","type":"agrees","note":"Both say bad company ruins the good: 179 compares it to a storm wrecking a ship, and 240 to a cobra mating with a viper.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"k:773","type":"agrees","note":"773 calls fierceness valour and kindness to a foe who has fallen (ஊராண்மை) its keen edge. 241 says the worthy feel shame to attack a humbled foe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"k:879","type":"tension","note":"879 says to cut a thorn tree while it is young, before it can wound the cutter; 241 says the worthy don't attack an enemy at his weakest. Both concern whether to strike a foe while he is weak. The tension holds if 879's thorn tree is an enemy, as Parimelazhagar reads it in the chapter on enmity.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:241","b":"n:148","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the eclipse snake and the moon. In 148 the moon still lights the world while the snake holds half of it, an image of the nobly born in poverty; in 241 the snake spares the crescent, an image of the worthy sparing a humbled foe.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:242","b":"k:963","type":"tension","note":"963 says humility suits prosperity and standing tall suits adversity; 242 says restraint is the ornament of the poor and that living without humility shames one's family. Both concern how the poor should bear themselves. The tension depends on taking 963's உயர்வு as not bowing, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, and 242's அடக்கம் as bowing; if 242 condemns only living beyond one's means, they fit.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:242","b":"k:125","type":"qualifies","note":"125 says humility is good for all and an extra wealth for the rich. 242 makes it the ornament of the poor.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:243","b":"k:972","type":"agrees","note":"Both deny that origin decides worth: 972 says all are alike in birth and differ by their deeds, and 243 says the next world comes from oneself, not from one's region.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:243","b":"n:195","type":"agrees","note":"195 calls good and bad caste mere words and puts worth in austerity, learning and effort; 243 makes the same claim about the region of one's birth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:244","b":"n:179","type":"tension","note":"179 says goodness perishes when it comes near bad company, as a storm wrecks a ship; 244 says the good-natured do not turn bad in mind though their company is bad. Both concern whether bad company corrupts the good, and the commentators read 244's இனம் as company, which makes the conflict direct.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:244","b":"k:458","type":"qualifies","note":"458 says that even those with full goodness of mind are strengthened by good company. 244 says the good-natured hold firm without it, even among bad company.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:245","b":"k:452","type":"tension","note":"The same image of water leads to opposite claims. 452 says water changes with the soil, so a person's understanding takes on the nature of their company (454 adds that it only seems to be in the mind); 245 says sweet water rises by the sea, so people are as their minds, not their kind. The tension holds if 245's இனம் means the people around one, as the image of surroundings suggests. Shanmuganar's 'not only by their group' softens it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:246","b":"n:215","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn friendship that comes and goes. 215 contrasts the tree flower with the pond flower; 246 says people of steady mind don't leave and rejoin.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:247","b":"n:168","type":"tension","note":"168 says parting even from those without discernment brings great pain, so it is best to mix with none; 247 says that parting from the undiscerning is how the pain parts. Both concern whether leaving the undiscerning hurts, and they share the phrase தெரியத் தெரியும் தெரிவிலா. The tension rests on reading 247 as both commentators and Pope do.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:247","b":"k:460","type":"agrees","note":"Both tie joy to good company and affliction to bad: no greater support than good company and nothing more afflicting than bad (460).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:248","b":"n:192","type":"agrees","note":"192 says a pliant twig becomes a post for tethering elephants, and so does a life if a man makes himself without slackening; 248 says a person alone raises or lowers himself.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:248","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"505 makes one's own deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; 248 makes the self the agent of rising and falling.","grade":"weak","better":"parallel_domain"},{"a":"n:249","b":"n:193","type":"agrees","note":"Both permit the capable to stoop for a purpose: the tiger eats a frog when there is no other meat (193), and the great walk behind the unlearned for a task (249).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:249","b":"k:967","type":"qualifies","note":"967 says it is better to be ruined than to live by following those who scorn you. 249 allows following the unlearned for the sake of a task, which marks a limit on that pride: following for a purpose is not following for a living.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:250","b":"n:114","type":"develops","note":"114 names the three ends of life and says gaining the middle one, wealth, brings the other two. 250 pictures a life that completes all three without hindrance, as a ship reaching port.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:250","b":"k:754","type":"agrees","note":"754 says rightly gained wealth yields virtue and pleasure. 250 counts the life that achieves work, pleasure and virtuous giving together as a voyage completed.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:251","b":"k:841","type":"agrees","note":"Both call want of understanding the true poverty. Kural 841 adds that the world does not count other lack as lack; 251 adds the image of jewels on one who lacks the essential thing.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:251","b":"k:402","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a person lacking the marks of a sex to picture a lack that ornament cannot cover: the unlearned wanting to speak (Kural), the senseless rich in their jewels (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:252","b":"k:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both separate wealth from wisdom. The Kural states it as two natures of the world under fate; 252 makes a comic myth of the same fact, the two goddesses quarrelling.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:252","b":"n:266","type":"develops","note":"252 explains in jest why Fortune avoids the learned; 266 turns on her and curses her for going to the base and leaving the good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:253","b":"k:405","type":"agrees","note":"Both show the unlearned exposed in company. In the Kural his standing wilts as he speaks; here he is asked to read and answers with a stick.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:254","b":"k:403","type":"tension","note":"Kural 403 says the unlearned are very good if they can stay silent before the learned; 254 says that even sitting quietly among the wise, such a man is a dog sitting there. Same situation, opposite verdict. The tension holds on Parimelazhagar's plain reading of 403 (silence keeps them from exposure and may lead them to learning), not if 403 is taken as irony.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:254","b":"k:840","type":"agrees","note":"Both make the fool's mere presence among the worthy a defilement: unwashed feet on a bed (Kural), a dog sitting among them (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:255","b":"k:719","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell those who speak well not to speak in a mean assembly. 255 adds the reason, that the hearers would not take the meaning in, and pictures the low talking freely there.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:256","b":"k:642","type":"agrees","note":"Both use சோர்வு for the lapse in speech to be guarded against. The Kural says it because gain and ruin come from speech; 256 shows the learned guarding against it by silence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:257","b":"k:720","type":"same_image","note":"Good teaching spilt where it is wasted: nectar spilt in a dirty yard (Kural), mango juice pressed into a pig's trough (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:258","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both say teaching cannot override what a person already is: in the Kural one's own understanding prevails over many books; here understanding cannot be driven into a body without merit. On the commentators' reading both rest on past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:260","b":"n:255","type":"develops","note":"Both use புன்கோட்டி, the petty assembly. 255 shows the low talking in such assemblies; 260 shows how one is founded, by a man who rejects the learned and finds his like.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:261","b":"k:1008","type":"agrees","note":"Both picture the ungiving man's wealth as a fruiting tree no one can use: a poison tree in the middle of town (Kural), a wood-apple near at hand that the bats ignore (here). Parimelazhagar also stresses that the man is near the poor.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:263","b":"k:215","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture wealth as drinking water. The Kural's wise man's wealth is the full village tank; here the rich man is the undrinkable sea and the giver the small sweet well.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:264","b":"k:374","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that being rich and being wise are separate fortunes. 264 makes the sharper point that the senseless live in silk while the wise are passed over.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:265","b":"k:169","type":"develops","note":"Kural 169 says the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright are to be pondered. 265 gives the answer such pondering reaches: the fruit of old deeds, and nothing else.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:267","b":"k:169","type":"agrees","note":"Both invite wonder at poverty staying with the good and wealth with the unworthy. Neither verse explains it; Parimelazhagar and 265 explain it by past deeds.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:266","b":"k:179","type":"tension","note":"Kural 179 says Fortune knows the way and goes to the wise who understand virtue; 266 says she leaves the good and goes to the worthless. Both speak of the goddess choosing whom to join. The tension holds if 179 is a claim about how fortune actually goes, as Parimelazhagar takes it, and not only a promise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:270","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not make one learned without understanding: the Kural names that understanding as living in accord with the world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:263","b":"n:275","type":"same_image","note":"Both set the sea against a small well. In 263 people seek out the sweet well as they seek out givers; in 275 they wait at a nearly dry well, as the poverty of the worthy is better than wealth that forgets the next world.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:271","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"Both condemn eating alone to keep one's store full. The Kural ranks it more bitter than begging; 271 shuts the door of the next world on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:272","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"Both reject putting virtue off: the Kural's அன்று அறிவாம் என்னாது ('without saying we'll see to it later') answers the very words of 272's delayers, பின் அறிதும்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:273","b":"k:1001","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe the man who stores wealth, does not use it and dies: the Kural says he achieved nothing, 273 that his own wealth laughs at him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:274","b":"k:1007","type":"same_image","note":"Both liken the miser's wealth to a beautiful young woman, with opposite outcomes: in the Kural she grows old alone, unused; here she is enjoyed in due season by a stranger. Unused or lost, the miser has none of it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:276","b":"k:1005","type":"agrees","note":"Both say that those who neither give nor enjoy have nothing, whatever they hold. 276 makes it a joke in which the speaker owns the miser's wealth as much as he does.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:277","b":"k:1041","type":"qualifies","note":"Kural 1041 says nothing is more painful than poverty. 277 names troubles poverty spares, but only in comparison with the ungiving rich, so it limits 1041 rather than contradicting it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:278","b":"n:274","type":"develops","note":"274 says the miser's wealth goes to a stranger; 278 follows it to the heirs, who repeat his avarice, so the wealth does no good in either generation.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:279","b":"k:1078","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base yield only when crushed: like sugar-cane (Kural), like a cow milked by force (here). Both use கொல்ல for the crushing. The Kural's worthy give at a word; here the giver yields like a cow to its calf.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:280","b":"k:754","type":"tension","note":"Kural 754 says wealth gained by right means and without wrong yields virtue and pleasure; 280 says wealth at every stage, earning included, is the dwelling of suffering. Both speak of wealth as such and its yield. The tension holds if 280 is about wealth in general, as its wording suggests, and not only the miser's, as the chapter setting might imply.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:277","b":"n:280","type":"agrees","note":"277 lists the miser's troubles of guarding, burying and clutching; 280 generalizes them into a sequence of earning, guarding, dwindling and loss.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:281","b":"k:752","type":"agrees","note":"Both say everyone scorns those who have nothing and honours the rich. 281 adds that noble birth does not help: the penniless are lower than a corpse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:281","b":"k:841","type":"tension","note":"Kural 841 says the world does not count lack of wealth as real lack; 281 says the world counts those who lack it lower than a corpse. Both describe how the world regards the poor. The tension holds on Parimelazhagar's reading of 841's second half as a claim about the world's judgment; Kural 752 sides with 281, so the Kural itself is divided.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:283","b":"k:1047","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say the poor lose their own people. Kural 1047 limits it to poverty without virtue, which even a mother treats as a stranger; 283 makes no such exception.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:284","b":"k:527","type":"same_image","note":"The same bird with opposite morals: the Kural's crows call their kin to share their food, an image of the generous; here crows crowding a carcass are the flatterers round the rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:285","b":"k:1043","type":"agrees","note":"Both say poverty destroys the standing of one's ancient family at a stroke. 285 adds valour and learning to what is lost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:287","b":"k:1046","type":"develops","note":"Kural 1046 says the words of the poor lose force even when they understand well. 287 goes further: the understanding itself loses its sharpness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:286","b":"n:288","type":"agrees","note":"Mudaliar says 288 repeats 286: better to leave the village than live there unable to give. The agreement holds on his reading of 286 as advice to a poor man, not Shanmuganar's reading of it as scorn for a miser.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:288","b":"k:229","type":"agrees","note":"Both rank begging above a life that does not give: the Kural the hoarder eating alone, 288 the poor man who stays home unable to give.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:288","b":"k:1050","type":"tension","note":"For the destitute who cannot give, Kural 1050 says the fitting course is to renounce completely, the body included on Parimelazhagar's reading, rather than live off others' salt and gruel. 288 recommends going away and living by begging. Same person, opposite remedies; here the Jain book is the less renunciatory one. The tension depends on Parimelazhagar's reading of 1050.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:289","b":"n:1","type":"agrees","note":"Both show fallen fortune through food: those who once refused a second helping beg for gruel (1), and hands that wore bracelets cook unsalted greens (289).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:290","b":"n:283","type":"agrees","note":"The same image and conclusion, bees leaving a flowerless plant and the poor left without kin; Mudaliar notes the repeat.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:291","b":"n:298","type":"same_image","note":"Both make the honourable mind a fire lit by the rich: a forest fire at their arrogance (291), a forge fire at their contemptuous pity (298).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:292","b":"k:966","type":"agrees","note":"Both refuse 'going behind' those who scorn (Kural இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று, here இயல்பிலார் பின்சென்று). The Kural says it brings neither praise nor heaven; 292 says the honourable go to those who understand unasked.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:292","b":"k:1053","type":"agrees","note":"Both allow asking before the right people: the Kural finds a beauty in asking those of open heart who know their duty, and 292 sends the honourable to those who grasp the need before it is spoken.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:293","b":"k:1057","type":"agrees","note":"Kural 1057 delights in givers who give without scorn; 293 shows the giver who feeds the poor at the back door with contempt, and draws the matching conclusion: forget such friendship.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:295","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"Both set an absolute limit: the worthy will not do what brings blame, not to save a starving mother (Kural) and not to save their own lives (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:296","b":"n:270","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the rich who do not give are poor and the poor who do not beg are rich. 296 adds a name for the honourable poor, the great Muttaraiyar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:297","b":"k:433","type":"agrees","note":"Both make fear of blame the mark of the highest: the Kural's பழிநாணுவார் see a millet-sized fault as a palmyra; here only blame frightens the best, while lower people fear hunger and pain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:300","b":"k:969","type":"same_image","note":"Each book gives honour an animal that dies rather than compromise: the yak that will not live once its hair is gone (Kural), the tiger that starves rather than eat prey fallen on its left (here).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:300","b":"k:966","type":"develops","note":"Kural 966 says that following those who scorn you does not lead to the gods' land. 300 goes further: even if heaven were in hand, the excellent would refuse it at honour's cost.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:301","b":"k:966","type":"agrees","note":"Both ask why a person of worth should stand behind those who look down on him. 966 counts the loss in renown and in heaven; 301 in clear understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:302","b":"k:656","type":"agrees","note":"656 forbids deeds the wise condemn even to relieve one's mother's hunger. 302 makes the same choice for one's own hunger, and grounds it in the shortness of life rather than in the judgement of the wise.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:302","b":"k:339","type":"same_image","note":"Both make death and rebirth as brief as an ordinary act of the eyes, and Mudaliar cites 339 here. The Kural uses the image to show impermanence; 302 uses it to make blameless hunger the cheaper choice.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:303","b":"k:1053","type":"agrees","note":"1053 finds a beauty even in asking before unconcealing givers who know their duty. 303 narrows this further: the worthy approach only givers who first welcome them in, so asking becomes hospitality.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:304","b":"k:963","type":"agrees","note":"963 asks for dignity when circumstances shrink; 304 shows it, with fortune and the gods both gone and the worthy still refusing to bow to the hoarding rich.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:305","b":"k:1061","type":"agrees","note":"The first half restates 1061: not asking even of givers dear as one's eyes, who give without holding back, is far better. Both use the same phrase, கண்ணன்னார்.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:305","b":"k:1069","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the heart melt (உள்ளம் உருகும்) at the thought of begging. 1069 goes on to the one who refuses; 305 to the beggar at the moment of taking.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:306","b":"k:1063","type":"qualifies","note":"Both reject begging as a cure for poverty. In Parimelazhagar's reading the Kural's alternative is effort; 306's is to want less and consent to hardship. They differ on the remedy, not on the refusal, and the difference shows the Naladiyar's turn toward renunciation.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:307","b":"k:1057","type":"tension","note":"1057 speaks of beggars rejoicing when they meet givers who give without scorn, which assumes such givers exist. 307 says the man who does not scorn beggars has never been born. The conflict holds only if 307 is taken literally; Mudaliar's explanations treat it as overstatement or wordplay.","grade":"forced","better":null},{"a":"n:308","b":"k:1070","type":"agrees","note":"In both, the asker's life goes out at the word of refusal. 1070 turns the point against the refuser; 308 stays with the asker.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:309","b":"k:1066","type":"agrees","note":"1066 finds nothing more degrading to the tongue than asking, even for water for a cow. 309 also places the disgrace in the spoken request rather than in dependence.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:310","b":"k:803","type":"agrees","note":"803 asks what use old friendship is if a friend's liberties aren't accepted as one's own. On Shanmuganar's reading, 310 shows the cost of that failure to the friend who relied on it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:311","b":"k:719","type":"agrees","note":"Both tell those who speak well in a good assembly not to speak in a mean one; 311 describes the mean assembly in detail.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:312","b":"n:315","type":"develops","note":"312 warns that the bad scholar will rise to fight; 315 shows the cost to whoever stays to argue: his teeth in his hand.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:313","b":"k:649","type":"agrees","note":"649 says those who cannot say a few faultless words long to say many; 313 shows the same many-worded speaker in debate.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:314","b":"k:403","type":"agrees","note":"403 says even the unlearned are good if they stay silent before the learned; 314's fool fails at exactly that.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:315","b":"k:857","type":"agrees","note":"857 says those of bitter understanding who hold to hostility do not see the truth that brings victory; 315's disputants want victory and will not accept the truth (மெய் கொள்ளார்).","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:316","b":"k:69","type":"same_image","note":"In 69 a mother rejoices more than at his birth when she hears her son called சான்றோன். In 316 the சான்றோர் themselves grieve for the mother of a fool. The two verses are mirror images.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:317","b":"n:318","type":"develops","note":"317 separates a book's text from its meaning; 318 separates those who keep the text from those who grasp the meaning and teach it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:318","b":"k:650","type":"agrees","note":"650 likens those who cannot explain what they learned to flowers without scent; 318 sets them apart from scholars who make others understand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:319","b":"n:318","type":"develops","note":"318 says true scholars make a book's meaning clear to others; 319 sets out what that explanation must contain.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:320","b":"k:409","type":"tension","note":"409 says the learned of low birth have a worth the unlearned of high birth lack. 320 says that however much they learn, those without good birth cannot do what the well-born do. The conflict depends on reading இற்பிறப்பு as birth into a good family, as both commentators do, and on counting the capacity 320 denies as part of the worth 409 gives to learning.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:320","b":"k:992","type":"agrees","note":"992 makes birth in a good family one of the two parts of courtesy; 320 credits good birth with the restraint to overlook another's petty understanding.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:321","b":"n:322","type":"same_image","note":"Both use milk-rice whose taste goes unknown: the ladle that stirs it and the dog that prefers leather.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:321","b":"k:415","type":"agrees","note":"415 makes the words of the good a staff on slippery ground; in 321 the learned value such words while the fool mocks them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:322","b":"k:418","type":"agrees","note":"Both describe hearing that isn't hearing: 418's ear unpierced by instruction is deaf though it hears, and 322's listeners lend an ear and still do not hear (செவிகொடுத்தும் கேட்கலார்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:323","b":"k:420","type":"same_image","note":"Both end on the same verdict, 'what matter whether they die or live'. The Kural aims it at those who taste with the mouth and not the ear; 323 at those who see death coming and do no good.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:324","b":"k:999","type":"agrees","note":"999 says the world is dark even by day for those who cannot laugh with others; 324 asks why anyone would refuse to smile with those he meets.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:325","b":"k:158","type":"agrees","note":"158 tells one to defeat those who wrong one out of arrogance by one's own forbearance; 325 shows that defeat in public, though it doesn't name forbearance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:326","b":"n:332","type":"develops","note":"326 shows the cost of putting off virtue until old age; 332 names the usual excuse, that the household's needs come first.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:327","b":"k:1006","type":"agrees","note":"1006 names the same two failures, not using one's wealth and not giving to the deserving. 327 adds a third: not taking the good path that protects.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:328","b":"k:335","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture the end, when the tongue no longer works. 335 urges good deeds before that moment; 328 shows the gesture that comes too late.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:328","b":"n:332","type":"agrees","note":"Both mock the words of postponement: பின்னறிவாம் here, மற்றறிவாம் in 332.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:329","b":"k:333","type":"agrees","note":"333 says wealth doesn't stay, so do lasting deeds as soon as you have it. 329 shows people who think of such deeds only when their means are gone.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:330","b":"k:336","type":"agrees","note":"336 states the general fact that one here yesterday is gone today. 330 makes it the death of one's own loved ones and still finds people unmoved.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:331","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"337 mocks those who cannot know they will live another moment yet plan in crores; 331's turtle is a picture of that ignorance.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:332","b":"k:36","type":"agrees","note":"36 says to do virtue without saying 'we'll see to it then' (அன்றறிவாம்); 332 mocks the household version of the same words (மற்றறிவாம்).","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:333","b":"k:140","type":"agrees","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading the two make the same claim: learning without knowing how to live with the world is still ignorance. On Mudaliar's reading, where உலகம் is the worlds after death, the link does not hold.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:334","b":"k:997","type":"same_image","note":"Both compare a person without human quality to an inert thing, a tree in 997 and a stone in 334. 334 goes further and ranks the stone above the person for its usefulness.","grade":"weak","better":"develops"},{"a":"n:335","b":"n:313","type":"same_image","note":"Both put the fool's urge to speak in an itching tongue (சுனை): in 313 the itch to debate, here the itch to abuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:336","b":"k:967","type":"agrees","note":"Both warn against following those who do not accept you. 967 counts the cost in honour; 336 in futility.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:337","b":"n:304","type":"qualifies","note":"304 says the worthy will not stand behind the hoarding rich; 337 says people at large do. 304 describes the exception to 337's rule.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:338","b":"n:327","type":"agrees","note":"Both list the goods a fool's life lacks: 327 enjoyment, giving and the good path; 338 adds love and renown.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:339","b":"k:816","type":"agrees","note":"816 rates being a stranger to the wise far above close friendship with a fool; 339 says friendship with the undiscerning is painful even if it brought the whole earth.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:340","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says smallness adorns itself in self-admiration; 340 shows the self-praiser mocked as mad.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:341","b":"k:373","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not override what a person already is. In Parimelazhagar's reading 373 credits the lasting understanding to fate; 341 credits it to base nature.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:341","b":"n:345","type":"same_image","note":"Both use a domestic animal that keeps its habit despite good feeding: the hen to its rubbish heap, the dog to others' leftovers.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:342","b":"k:976","type":"agrees","note":"976 says the small have no thought of cherishing the great and taking them as guides; 342 shows the base man inventing excuses not to go to them.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:343","b":"k:973","type":"agrees","note":"On Shanmuganar's reading 343 matches 973: being placed high does not make the base high, nor does it change the worthy.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:343","b":"k:977","type":"agrees","note":"On Mudaliar's reading, prosperity turns the base man to a new course, as 977 says distinction falling to the unworthy turns into excess.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:344","b":"k:104","type":"agrees","note":"The first half restates 104 with the same millet and palm, and Mudaliar cites it here.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:344","b":"k:105","type":"agrees","note":"105 says a benefit's measure is the worth of the one who receives it; 344's second half shows that measure at zero, with palm-sized help counting for nothing among the ungrateful.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:346","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"978 says greatness always bows and smallness admires itself; 346 shows both, with an emperor's wealth on one side and a fraction of a coin on the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:346","b":"k:963","type":"agrees","note":"963 asks for humility in abundance; 346's worthy keep it even with an emperor's wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:347","b":"k:505","type":"agrees","note":"505 makes one's deeds the touchstone of greatness and smallness; 347 and 350 apply it to the base who have grown rich, whose wealth cannot hide what their deeds show.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:348","b":"k:1079","type":"develops","note":"1079's base man finds fault on seeing others well fed and clothed; 348's rejoices when others suffer. They are the same envy seen from both sides.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:349","b":"k:810","type":"agrees","note":"810 praises those who do not change toward old friends; 349 gives the worthy that constancy and shows the base without it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:350","b":"n:347","type":"agrees","note":"The two share their closing lines word for word: 347 makes the point with an object, the jewelled sandal, and 350 with an animal, the young bullocks.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:352","b":"k:823","type":"agrees","note":"Both say study does not change what a person is: 823 says the unworthy rarely become good at heart however many good books they learn, and 352 says those without fine discernment never gain it from flawless books.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:353","b":"k:184","type":"qualifies","note":"184 ranks harsh words to a man's face above careless words behind his back. 353 doesn't let face-to-face speech off: the base are shameless precisely because they list a man's faults standing beside him.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:354","b":"k:978","type":"agrees","note":"On Mudaliar's reading of 354 as a figure, both make self-display the mark of the small: 978 says smallness adorns itself in self-admiration, and 354's wantons adorn and vaunt themselves.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:355","b":"k:1077","type":"agrees","note":"Both say the base give only to force: 1077's base won't shake their wet hands except for those who would break their jaws, and 355's chisel only moves when struck.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:355","b":"k:1078","type":"same_image","note":"1078's sugar-cane yields only when crushed, and 355's chisel cuts only when struck: two tools of labour for the same claim about the base.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:356","b":"k:108","type":"agrees","note":"108 tells one to keep a good done in mind and forget the bad that very day. 356 says the worthy do exactly this and the base do the reverse, dwelling on abuse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:357","b":"k:109","type":"agrees","note":"Both make one kindness outweigh great injury: in 109 a wrong as painful as killing vanishes at the thought of one good, and in 357 the worthy bear a hundred faults for one kindness. 357 adds the base, for whom the scale runs the other way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:358","b":"k:955","type":"agrees","note":"955 says an old family doesn't part from its nature even when its means shrink, and 358 says the well-born do in poverty what the base won't do in wealth.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:359","b":"k:337","type":"agrees","note":"Both mock plans made without regard to life's shortness: 337's unwise think crores of thoughts without knowing they'll live a moment more, and 359's dreamers say 'today, now, soon' and perish.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:360","b":"k:576","type":"same_image","note":"Both picture people who have the means of feeling and none of the feeling: 576's men have eyes but no kind regard and are like trees in the soil, and 360's rich live in wealth but stay dry as the netti's pith.","grade":"weak","better":"agrees"},{"a":"n:361","b":"k:53","type":"agrees","note":"53 asks what is lacking if the wife has worth and what is present if she lacks it. 361 gives the second half a picture: every grandeur present, and the house a wilderness.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:362","b":"k:57","type":"qualifies","note":"Both say guards can't keep women chaste. 57 concludes that a woman's own steadfastness is the best guard; 362, on Mudaliar's reading, doubts that most women have it.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:363","b":"k:52","type":"develops","note":"52 says a life has nothing if the wife lacks household excellence. 363 names three such failings and calls each a weapon that kills the husband.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:364","b":"k:46","type":"tension","note":"46 asks what one gains by leaving a virtuous household life for the ascetic's way. 364 calls returning to married life a fault like stoning oneself, and Mudaliar takes it as the wise telling one to give household life up. The tension holds on Mudaliar's reading; if the verse is only about remarriage after a wife's death, as Shanmuganar's gloss allows, it is narrower.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:364","b":"n:56","type":"develops","note":"56 explains why the learned call marriage கடி, a dread; 364 opens by citing that name, and Mudaliar points back to 56.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:365","b":"k:47","type":"tension","note":"47 puts the one who lives household life according to its nature first among all who strive; 365 puts austerity first and household life in the middle. Parimelazhagar limits 47's 'all who strive' to those in the third stage, not full renunciants, which softens the conflict.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:365","b":"n:366","type":"develops","note":"Two ranked lists of lives side by side. Both put enjoyment of what one has in the middle and craving at the bottom, but the top changes from austerity to learning.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:367","b":"k:959","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the sprout to read what lies behind it: in 959 the sprout shows the soil as speech shows the lineage, and in 367 the paddy breeds true as a son's mind follows his father's.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:368","b":"k:169","type":"agrees","note":"169 calls the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright a thing to ponder. 368 describes that world: the worthy fallen and the base risen, the umbrella turned upside down.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:369","b":"k:788","type":"agrees","note":"788 says friendship removes distress at once, like a hand catching a slipping garment. 369 says a heart that doesn't respond so is not worth keeping alive.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:370","b":"n:371","type":"same_image","note":"The same frame, word for word in places, with two images of a love that lasts only while it is fed: the flood that ends with the rain, and the lamp that ends with the oil.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:371","b":"k:911","type":"agrees","note":"911 says the sweet words of women who desire wealth, not the man, bring harm. 371 gives the mechanism: their love burns only while the money feeds it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:372","b":"n:376","type":"agrees","note":"Both are the dupe's own speech recalling a vow of inseparable love, a death pact in 372 and a promise never to part in 376, broken when his money ran out.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:374","b":"k:912","type":"agrees","note":"912's women weigh the profit before they speak of their virtue; 374's women weigh only wealth, so the poor man is poison and the oil-presser with money is sugar.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:375","b":"k:920","type":"agrees","note":"920 names இருமனப் பெண்டிர், double-minded women, among the company of the ruined; 375's eel, showing one face to the snake and another to the fish, pictures the double mind.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:376","b":"n:378","type":"same_image","note":"Both use the fighting ram's horn, which twists away, for the courtesan's love turned to hostility.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:377","b":"k:918","type":"agrees","note":"918 says the embrace of deceiving women is an அணங்கு to those without discernment; 377's wild cow, whose lick pleases and then kills in Mudaliar's gloss, is the same danger, with public ridicule added.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:378","b":"k:914","type":"agrees","note":"914 says those who seek the wealth of grace won't touch the base charms of women who count only wealth; 378 says those who mean to reach the right path won't embrace them, which Mudaliar takes as the path to liberation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:379","b":"k:913","type":"agrees","note":"913 compares the false embrace to holding a stranger's corpse in a dark room; 379 says the women belong to no one but their bodies. Both deny that anyone is present in the embrace.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:380","b":"k:917","type":"agrees","note":"917 says those without firmness of heart embrace women whose hearts are on other things; 380 says those full of sin don't grasp the guile of women whose minds are with another man.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:381","b":"k:57","type":"agrees","note":"On the commentators' reading, both place a wife's chastity in her own keeping: 57 calls the guard of her own steadfastness the best, and 381 praises the woman whose conduct leaves no one pursuing her. On Pope's reading, 381 instead credits the absence of temptation.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:382","b":"k:51","type":"qualifies","note":"51's good wife lives within her husband's means; 382's good wife keeps her duties of hospitality even when the means are one pot of water and the guests could drink the sea.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:383","b":"n:361","type":"develops","note":"The two halves of one claim: 361's mansion without a good wife is a wilderness, and 383's leaking hut with one is the only true home. Mudaliar's gloss on 383, that other houses are forests, makes the pairing explicit.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:384","b":"k:1302","type":"agrees","note":"1302 says sulking is right in measure like salt and spoils if prolonged; 384's good wife sulks at the right moment and relents sweetly.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:385","b":"k:1257","type":"qualifies","note":"385's wife never loses her shyness with her husband however often they meet; 1257's woman knows nothing of shame when her beloved does what she desires. The second describes desire overriding reserve after separation, so the two set limits on each other rather than conflict.","grade":"weak","better":"tension"},{"a":"n:387","b":"k:1311","type":"agrees","note":"Both wives refuse a chest that other women have shared: 1311 because all women feast on him with their eyes, and 387 because he comes from the courtesans unbathed.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:388","b":"n:390","type":"same_image","note":"The same speech to the minstrel in the same frame, with two images of the neglected side: the unstruck face of the drum and the sapless end joints of the sugar-cane.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:389","b":"n:387","type":"agrees","note":"Both wives read the courtesans' embraces on the husband's chest, unwashed in 387 and smeared with sandal in 389.","grade":"weak","better":"same_image"},{"a":"n:391","b":"k:1302","type":"same_image","note":"Both make sulking love's salt: 1302 measures it, with too much spoiling the dish, and 391 says love without it has no salt at all.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:391","b":"k:1306","type":"agrees","note":"1306 says love without quarrels, serious and light, is like over-ripe or unripe fruit; 391 says that union and sulking together are love's way.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:384","b":"n:391","type":"agrees","note":"The good wife of 384 sulks at the right moment and relents sweetly; 391 gives the reason, that without sulking love has no salt.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:392","b":"k:1228","type":"same_image","note":"In both, a sound of the season becomes a killing instrument for the separated woman: the rain's thunder is a funeral drum in 392, and the herdsman's flute a weapon in 1228.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:392","b":"n:364","type":"same_image","note":"The funeral drum appears in both: in 364 it should teach a man to give up married life, and in 392 the thunder sounds like it to a woman whose husband is away. The same sound serves renunciation in one book and love in the other.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:393","b":"k:1229","type":"agrees","note":"Both make evening the bewildering hour of separation: 393's மம்மர்கொள் மாலை, and 1229's evening that confuses the mind of the whole town.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:393","b":"n:397","type":"same_image","note":"Two evening scenes in one frame: the day's work falls silent, and the woman casts off her garland and weeps for the absent man.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:394","b":"k:1261","type":"same_image","note":"Both have the woman count the days of absence on her fingers, and Mudaliar cites 1261 with 394. In 1261 she speaks; in 394 the man imagines it.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:395","b":"k:1086","type":"same_image","note":"Both make her brow a bow that guards her eyes: in 1086 the brows, if they would only screen her eyes, would spare him his pain, and in 395 they frighten off the kingfisher.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:396","b":"n:398","type":"qualifies","note":"The mother of 396 doubts that feet which shrank from soft dye can bear the stony path; the girl of 398 answers that the strength comes with the man, as riding comes with the horse.","grade":"sound","better":null},{"a":"n:396","b":"n:399","type":"develops","note":"Both are the mother's speech after her daughter's flight: 396 fears for the girl's feet on the way, and 399 rereads the farewell embrace she didn't understand.","grade":"weak","better":null},{"a":"n:400","b":"k:1225","type":"develops","note":"1225's woman asks what she did to morning and to evening, looking for the cause of her pain in the hours. 400 runs through such suspects and acquits them, blaming only the road her lover took for wealth.","grade":"weak","better":"qualifies"}],"threads":[{"id":"thread:competing_supremes","title":"Superlatives that compete","claim":"The formula recurs beyond wealth and sometimes collides: nothing surpasses virtue, yet nothing surpasses apt speech; nothing beats truth, yet not killing is ranked above it; poverty is unmatched pain, yet wealth under a tyrant hurts more. Commentators reconcile the first two, and 323 is a deliberate ranking, so these superlatives need case-by-case reading.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:644","k:32"],["k:300","k:323"],["k:841","k:1041"],["k:558","k:1041"]],"limits":"","issue":"Fails on restraint (32-122 is only a shared formula; restraint is a kind of virtue) and 92-225 is forced. The last sentence overreaches: 323 is an explicit ranking and Parimelazhagar treats it as settling the order, so not every superlative is mere praise."},{"id":"thread:ethics_vs_statecraft","title":"Absolute ethics and the needs of the state","claim":"Five rules stated without exception in the Book of Virtue meet specific verses in the Book of Wealth that suspend or limit them: killing, anger, hypocrisy, generosity and purity of mind. The verses do not acknowledge this; Parimelazhagar reconciles some by adding limits such as royal duty and enemies only. The Book of Wealth elsewhere upholds ethics (546, 657).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:327","k:550"],["k:487","k:303"],["k:586","k:274"],["k:480","k:220"],["k:829","k:34"]],"limits":"","issue":"All five named areas have a sound link, though two links are the same pair. Overstated in three ways: 480 limits generosity rather than reversing it; 'often' for commentators' added limits rests on clear cases at 550 and 829; and Parimelazhagar directs 303 at renunciants, so some rules were not universal for him. Counterevidence against a book-wide pattern: 546 and 657 show the Book of Wealth upholding ethics."},{"id":"thread:love_overturns","title":"Echoes of the earlier books in the Book of Love","claim":"A few lover-speakers reuse the earlier books' language against its grain: shame worth dying for is set aside to save life (1132/1017), the body-soul bond called flimsy in 338 becomes the image of union (1122), and honour's ban on following those who slight you is called unavailable to the lovesick (1255/966). These are dramatised speeches and do not overturn the earlier teaching.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1132","k:1017"],["k:1122","k:338"],["k:1255","k:966"]],"limits":"","issue":"Of the four named examples only shame holds. Senses and warrior are contrasts without incompatibility, and backbiting is forced because அலர் is not புறங்கூறல். 'Overturns' is false as a claim about the book: these are dramatised speakers in stated situations, on Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's framing, and do not revise the earlier teaching."},{"id":"thread:gender_asymmetry","title":"Where the man and woman differ","claim":"On Parimelazhagar's speaker assignments, the man and woman differ over shame (1132/1163) and memory (1202/1207), and 1137 states the first contrast outright. Manakkudavar assigns some speakers differently, and most other differences in the Book of Love follow union and separation rather than gender.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1163","k:1132"],["k:1202","k:1207"]],"limits":"","issue":"Only shame and memory hold, and memory's gender contrast fails on Manakkudavar, who gives 1202 to the woman. Evening, night and leaving fail: most contrasts are union against separation, not man against woman, and two links lie outside the Book of Love. Counterevidence: 1251 has the woman's shame break, and the man suffers in 1132 and 1268. 1137, the verse that states the asymmetry outright, is not among the links."},{"id":"thread:truth_by_effect","title":"Truth defined by what it does","claim":"On Parimelazhagar's reading, chapter 30 defines truthfulness by harmlessness of effect and explicitly sets aside mere statement of what happened, though 293 still treats lying as going against what one knows. That definition carries real weight: without it, 297's unbroken not-lying and 292's blameless falsehood would conflict.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:292","k:297"],["k:300","k:323"]],"limits":"","issue":"The second sentence holds (292-297). 'Not agreement with fact' is Parimelazhagar's explicit gloss on 291, not the verse's wording, and 291 appears only in a forced link, so most links do not test the claim. Counterevidence: 293 still treats lying as going against what one's own heart knows, in the same chapter."},{"id":"thread:knowing_and_learning","title":"Where does understanding come from?","claim":"The book names at least three sources of understanding that do not sit easily together: study (396), one's own or fated nature (373) and company (454). Parimelazhagar reconciles study with fate by limiting study's reach to where fate does not oppose it. Separately, it is divided on whether a speaker's standing matters (423, 415, 850).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:373","k:396"],["k:454","k:373"],["k:423","k:415"],["k:850","k:423"]],"limits":"","issue":"Only study against fate or innate nature and against company hold. Hearing rests on the wrong 396-416, and poverty's absence has no link in this thread. The two sound links on 423 concern a speaker's credibility, not sources of understanding. Parimelazhagar explicitly reconciles 396 with 373, which the thread does not mention."},{"id":"thread:justice_of_the_world","title":"Does virtue pay? The promise and its escape clauses","claim":"The Book of Virtue states the reward of virtue as a rule: aram yields wealth (31), the fair person's wealth does not decay (112), the envious have no gain (135, 170), the one who does no evil meets no ruin (210), and all pain returns on those who caused it (320). The same book then admits the counter-case: 169 calls the prosperity of the crooked and the ruin of the upright things to be pondered (நினைக்கப்படும்), and 332 says great wealth comes and goes like a theatre crowd, with no exception for the virtuous. The book keeps the promise alive by two moves. Verse 117 changes what counts as ruin: the world does not set down as கேடு the low state of one who stays in virtue, which Parimelazhagar narrows to the judgement of the high-minded (உயர்ந்தோர்) and Manakkudavar reads as the world counting such loss with his gains. The commentators supply the other move, past deeds: Parimelazhagar sends the wise to look for a cause in earlier births at 169, and Manakkudavar reads 320's returning pain as pain done in a former time.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:31","k:169"],["k:112","k:169"],["k:112","k:117"],["k:135","k:169"],["k:169","k:170"],["k:210","k:169"],["k:210","k:117"],["k:320","k:169"],["k:169","k:117"],["k:332","k:112"]],"limits":"This is a claim about chapters 4 to 34 of the Book of Virtue, and the escape clauses at 117 and in the commentaries are not stated by 169 itself, which leaves the puzzle open. The earlier version of this thread also drew in the apportioner verses (63, 377) and the curse at 1062; those are not in this cluster but belong to the same question.","issue":"The claim says the commentators supply the past-deeds move, which is true inside chapters 4 to 34, but the Book of Virtue itself makes that move in chapter 38 (ஊழ், e.g. 375, 377), right after this range; a reader should not take the text as silent on it. At 117 Parimelazhagar also adds the implied 'செல்வம் என்று கொள்ளுவர்', so he too reads the low state as counted among gains, not only Manakkudavar."},{"id":"thread:poverty","title":"Does character survive poverty?","claim":"Two verses say the poor person of character is not lowered: 117 for the fair-minded (the world does not count his தாழ்வு as ruin) and 988 for the one with the firmness called சால்பு (இன்மை இளிவன்று). Verse 752 reports the opposite as fact: everyone scorns the have-nots and honours the rich, which Parimelazhagar sharpens to 'even if they have every good quality', while 841 says the world does not count lack of wealth as lack at all. The poverty chapter then shows character itself giving way: 1044 says poverty produces degrading speech even in the well-born, though 139 called evil speech impossible for those with conduct and 959 made speech the reliable sign of lineage; on Parimelazhagar's reading of 223, the well-born never utter the beggar's 'I have nothing', which 1044 denies. Verse 1047 draws the line the book seems to want: the poverty that estranges even one's mother is அறஞ்சாரா, poverty not joined to virtue, which matches 117's boundary and runs against 521's claim that kin honour old ties even when all is gone.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:117","k:988"],["k:117","k:752"],["k:752","k:988"],["k:988","k:1044"],["k:841","k:752"],["k:139","k:1044"],["k:1044","k:959"],["k:223","k:1044"],["k:1047","k:117"],["k:752","k:1047"],["k:521","k:1047"]],"limits":"The reconciliation through அறஞ்சாரா at 1047 is the verse's own qualifier, but Pope drops it and reads the verse of poverty in general; and 752, 841 and 1044 are not qualified by virtue, so the disagreement over whether the world and the person hold up under want remains. The earlier version of this thread noted that இன்மை also names the ascetic's chosen possessionlessness (408, 532), a different condition not covered by this cluster.","issue":"841 does not say the world counts lack of wealth as no lack 'at all': its point is ranking lack of wisdom as the worst lack, and Parimelazhagar glosses that the world does not count other lack as lack of that kind. On Parimelazhagar's reading the degrading words of 1044 are begging ('give to us'), not the தீய speech (harmful lies etc.) that 139 rules out, so the 139 and 959 contrasts are weaker than stated. His note on 1047 also says all kin abandon the poor man because he must take and cannot give, which sharpens rather than softens the clash with 521."},{"id":"thread:fate_and_agency","title":"Fate and agency: who moves the mover?","claim":"Chapter 38 places fate one step behind every human cause: effort and sloth arise from a fate of gain and a fate of loss (371), and fate is stronger than any plan (380). Chapters 61 and 62 treat the same மடி as a thing a person chooses (605, காமக் கலன்) and can depose, after which the faults it brought are gone (609 against 604); they make effort the cause of wealth (616) and house the two goddesses in sloth and labour (617), and 618 blames idleness but not misfortune, a line 371 makes hard to draw if sloth is fate's product. Chapter 62 then both concedes fate and defies it: 619 grants that a thing may fail through fate and keeps the wage of the body's strain for effort, while 620 says the tireless see fate's back. Verse 262 extends the regress to austerity itself, which on Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's reading needs prior-birth தவம் to be undertaken at all; Kalaignar reads it as present self-control and removes the conflict. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap at 620 by saying fate blocks only a few attempts, and 468 adds that effort counts only when made by the right method.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:371","k:620"],["k:620","k:380"],["k:609","k:371"],["k:371","k:617"],["k:371","k:618"],["k:616","k:371"],["k:605","k:371"],["k:380","k:619"],["k:609","k:604"],["k:262","k:620"],["k:468","k:616"]],"limits":"The verses do not acknowledge one another; the reconciliations are the commentators'. 371 is a general claim about the mind, whereas 616 to 620 are addressed to the king, so the Book of Wealth may be stating a working rule rather than a doctrine. The related question of whether foresight shields a person from fate (429, 372, 380) is treated in its own thread.","issue":"605 is a weak witness for choice: காமக் கலன் says the four are desired, but கெடுநீரார் ('those whose nature is to perish') points toward a predisposition, closer to 371 than against it. Parimelazhagar also glosses 609's மடியாண்மை as the தாமத குணம் behind sloth, a disposition rather than a single act. Kalaignar reads ஊழ் in 371 and 380 as இயற்கை நிலை (natural condition), which softens the regress."},{"id":"thread:foresight_and_fate","title":"Can foresight guard against what is coming?","claim":"Chapter 43 defines understanding as knowing what will be (427, ஆவது அறிவார்) and promises that those whose understanding guards ahead meet no affliction that shakes them (429). Chapter 38 undercuts both ends of that promise: 380 says fate arrives before any counter-plan one devises, and 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish, which Parimelazhagar reads as happening even to one with every kind of understanding, so the trait 427 treats as fixed is itself at fate's disposal. Chapter 67 quietly adjusts 429: 662 keeps avoiding harm beforehand as the first rule but adds not giving way once it has come, which Parimelazhagar reads as the case of a task spoiled by fate. Manakkudavar meets the conflict at 429 directly, allowing that early guarding softens even what comes from past deeds.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:427","k:429"],["k:372","k:429"],["k:427","k:372"],["k:429","k:380"],["k:662","k:429"]],"limits":"The verses are addressed to different readers (chapter 38 to all, chapters 43 and 67 to the king and his ministers), and 429 says 'no affliction that shakes', not 'no affliction', so a narrow reading leaves room for 662. None of the verses names the other.","issue":"Parimelazhagar sets 372 in the chapter's wealth context: the loss-fate is the fate of losing what one holds (கைப்பொருள்). That narrows the claim that understanding is 'at fate's disposal'. Parimelazhagar reads 662's ஊறொரால் as not undertaking acts that will come to grief, not as foreseeing harm. And his gloss on 430 gives understanding the work of rebuilding once fate has destroyed, which is the book's own reconciliation of chapters 38 and 43."},{"id":"thread:household_or_renunciation","title":"Which life stands highest: the household or renunciation?","claim":"The Book of Virtue awards the first place twice. Chapter 3 says the texts' settled view sets the renouncer's greatness above every other excellence (21) and that those who have discerned the two states and taken on அறம் here stand highest in the world (23), and chapters 27 and 35 divide humanity into those who do தவம் or renounce completely and 'the rest', who are caught in desire or in the net (266, 348). Chapter 5 answers in the same register: the householder who lives by virtue gains nothing by going to the outside way (46), stands first among all who strive (47), has more endurance than those who do penance (48), and his life is the thing the word அறம் names (49). Both Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar read 46's புறத்தாறு as renunciation, so the collision between 46 and 342/348 is in the commentaries, not only in Pope; Parimelazhagar fits the two chapters together as a ladder (fame and heaven for householders in 50, release for full renouncers in 348), and reads 49's 'அஃதும் ... நன்று' as granting renunciation parity with the household.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:21","k:48"],["k:21","k:49"],["k:23","k:49"],["k:23","k:46"],["k:46","k:348"],["k:47","k:266"],["k:266","k:46"],["k:342","k:46"],["k:266","k:348"],["k:348","k:50"]],"limits":"The conflict softens on some readings: Manakkudavar takes 47's முயல்வார் as those who strive for wealth, so 47 does not rank the householder above ascetics, and he reads the 'அஃதும்' of 49 as the household life itself (good if the wife is not blameworthy), not as renunciation. Kalaignar reads 23 as choosing good over bad in general. The thread does not claim the Kural contradicts itself knowingly; it claims each chapter speaks in superlatives for its own subject and leaves the ranking to the commentators.","issue":"The claim misreports Parimelazhagar on 46 and 47. He glosses புறத்தாறு as 'இல்லை விட்டு வனத்துச் செல்லும் நிலை', the forest-dweller stage, and takes 47's முயல்வார் as those in the third stage, explicitly leaving out the full renouncers (முற்றத் துறந்தவர் விட்டமையின்). That is how he avoids the collision with 348, so the conflict between 46 and 342/348 rests on Manakkudavar's தவம் and on Pope, not on both commentators. The 23-46 link note concedes this, and the claim contradicts it."},{"id":"thread:renunciation_rests_on_providers","title":"Renunciation rests on those who have not renounced","claim":"The Kural says the ascetic's state is sustained by people who keep what he gives up. Having nothing is the nature of the vow (344), yet those who say 'we have given up what is desired' have no standing if the ploughmen fold their hands (1036), and 263 assumes that 'the others' hold possessions in order to provide for renouncers (துறந்தார்க்குத் துப்புரவு வேண்டி). On who ranks higher, the book splits by chapter: the chapters on the household and on giving grant the ascetic his own virtue of endurance and award more of it to the provider (48: நோற்பாரின் நோன்மை உடைத்து; 225: enduring hunger comes behind removing it), while 263, on both Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's reading, concludes that தவம் exceeds giving (Manakkudavar: தானத்தினும் தவம் மிகுதியுடைத்து).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:263","k:1036"],["k:344","k:263"],["k:344","k:1036"],["k:263","k:48"],["k:48","k:225"],["k:263","k:225"]],"limits":"263 is phrased as a question (மறந்தார்கொல்) and its ranking of தவம் over giving is the commentators' inference, not the verse's statement. 1036 is in the Book of Wealth and praises farmers; its point about ascetics is incidental. The thread claims a dependence the book notes, not a doctrine it argues.","issue":"Kalaignar reads 263 as a warning that the others should not forget their own austerity while helping renouncers, so the dependence and the ranking both rest on the Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar construal of மற்றையவர்கள் as householders who give in place of doing தவம். 344 states the vow's possessionlessness but says nothing about who sustains it; the dependence is carried by 263 and 1036 alone."},{"id":"thread:true_wealth","title":"Which wealth is the true wealth?","claim":"Four chapters crown a different thing as the wealth among wealths or the excellent wealth: grace (241, அருட்செல்வம் செல்வத்துள் செல்வம்), not wanting (363, விழுச்செல்வம்), learning (400, கேடில் விழுச்செல்வம்) and hearing (411, செவிச்செல்வம் ... செல்வத்துள் எல்லாம் தலை), while 751 says nothing but wealth is wealth (பொருளல்லது இல்லை பொருள்). Each verse argues against material wealth by a different defect: 241 by who can hold it (even the base have it), 400 by whether it can be lost (Parimelazhagar: heirs, thieves, the strong and kings cannot take learning), 411 on Parimelazhagar's reading by its impermanence and painful fruit, and 363 by having nothing above it in either world. The form is the same and the winners and the reasons differ, which suggests a rhetorical figure that each chapter fills for its own subject rather than a single ranking. 751 inverts the move: the fact that wealth is found among the base, which 241 counts against it, is exactly the power 751 counts in its favour.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:363","k:400"],["k:400","k:241"],["k:400","k:411"],["k:411","k:241"],["k:751","k:241"],["k:751","k:400"]],"limits":"The verses do not rank the crowned wealths against one another; 400 and 411 sit in adjacent chapters and neither mentions the other. 751 is in the Book of Wealth and speaks of standing in the world, not of the goods the Book of Virtue values, so its absolute is in a different register. No commentator treats these as a contradiction.","issue":"The figure has further instances the claim does not count, notably 592 (உள்ளம் உடைமை உடைமை, with material wealth நில்லாது நீங்கி விடும, an impermanence argument) and the child as wealth in 63; these strengthen the 'rhetorical figure' reading. The reasons attributed to 400 and 411 are Parimelazhagar's, as the claim says; the verses themselves give no reason beyond கேடில் in 400."},{"id":"thread:wealth_on_conditions","title":"Wealth gives everything, on conditions the chapter does not state","claim":"Chapter 76 makes large claims for wealth: nothing else makes persons of no account into persons of account (751), grace itself is a child nursed by wealth (757), and those who have made bright wealth in full have virtue and pleasure together for the asking (760, ஒண்பொருள் ... ஏனை இரண்டும் ஒருங்கு). The conditions come from inside the same chapter and from later ones. 754 requires wealth that came by right means and without wrong, 755 tells one to let go wealth that does not come with grace and love, 1009 uses 760's own word ஒண்பொருள் for wealth amassed by setting love aside and ignoring virtue and says others will take it, and 1002 names nearly 751's thought, பொருளானாம் எல்லாம், as the miser's delusion when it leads to not giving. 973, on Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's reading of the high and low seats as wealth and poverty, limits 751 further: wealth gets a person regarded without making them great. Read together, chapter 76's promises hold for wealth rightly earned and spent, and the book supplies that limit elsewhere rather than in the promising verses.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:760","k:754"],["k:760","k:1002"],["k:1009","k:760"],["k:757","k:755"],["k:755","k:1009"],["k:1002","k:751"],["k:973","k:751"]],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reads chapter 76 as the king's revenue (754's தீது as tyranny, 755's grace and love as the one-sixth tax), so on his reading 751-760 and the private miser of 1002 and 1009 concern different agents. 757's dependence of grace on wealth is not withdrawn anywhere; 755 only conditions which wealth may nurse it. The thread does not claim the Kural disparages wealth.","issue":"The title says the chapter does not state the conditions, but the claim itself draws them from 754 and 755 inside chapter 76, and on Parimelazhagar (and Pope's 'honestly acquired') 760's ஒண்பொருள் already means rightly earned wealth, so the 'promising verses' are not unconditioned. In 1002 Parimelazhagar takes பொருளானாம் எல்லாம் as correct knowledge (என்றறிந்து) and makes the delusion the later clinging, so 1002 grants 751's premise rather than limiting it."},{"id":"thread:giving_and_asking","title":"Asking: praised in one chapter, refused in the next","claim":"Chapter 106 (இரவு) and chapter 107 (இரவச்சம்) judge the same act from opposite sides, and the difference is not explained by who the giver is. Chapter 106 praises asking only under a condition on the giver: those worth asking (1051), a gift that comes without pain (1052), a heart without concealment that knows what is due (1053), one who would not conceal even in a dream (1054), where asking is itself 'like giving' (இரத்தலும் ஈதலே போலும்). Chapter 107 takes exactly those best givers, who give gladly without concealing and are dear as eyes, and says not asking is worth a crore more (1061); 1066 then says no purpose, not even water for a dying cow, lifts the disgrace from the tongue. 1066 repeats the move of 222 in the Book of Virtue, which calls taking bad 'even if it were called a good path' (நல்லாறு எனினும் கொளல் தீது): both verses refuse the best case that could be made for receiving. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap by reading chapter 106 as addressed to the destitute (நல்கூர்ந்தார்) and by glossing 1054's 'like giving' as merely not losing the honour one already has; on his reading even the best asking is neutral toward honour, never a gain.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:222","k:1051"],["k:222","k:1054"],["k:222","k:1061"],["k:1051","k:1061"],["k:1052","k:1066"],["k:1054","k:1061"],["k:1054","k:1066"],["k:1066","k:222"],["k:1066","k:1053"]],"limits":"The verses themselves state no such restriction; the reconciliation is the commentator's. 1052 speaks of pleasure and 1066 of disgrace, so they conflict only if a disgraceful act cannot also be a pleasant one. Kalaignar reads கொளல் in 222 as taking goods by any fair means, which would make 222 broader than begging. This thread covers asking; the valuation of giving for itself (chapters 22-23) is not argued here beyond 222.","issue":"Chapter 106 already partly frames asking as a survival exception: Parimelazhagar on 1055 says those who ask by standing silently do so to preserve life and would otherwise die rather than lose honour, so the chapter's praise is less unconditional than the claim's opening suggests."},{"id":"thread:standing_before","title":"Standing before another: when is the humble posture honourable?","claim":"The book uses one posture, standing in want before someone (முன் நின்று, பின் சென்று நிலை), and awards it opposite values according to what is sought and before whom one stands. For learning the posture is honoured: on Parimelazhagar's and Kalaignar's reading of 395, those who learned while yearning 'like the poor before the rich' (உடையார்முன் இல்லார்போல் ஏக்கற்றும்) are foremost and those who would not stoop are lowest (கடையர்); 403 makes the unlearned 'very good' if they stay silent before the learned. For goods the same posture is degrading (1066), except that 1053 allows 'a certain beauty' in standing before a giver of unconcealing heart. 966 names the bad case exactly: standing behind those who scorn you (இகழ்வார்பின் சென்று நிலை) wins neither fame nor heaven. 729 turns the ranking word of 395 around: the one who learned yet fears the assembly of the good is lower than the unlearned (கல்லாதவரின் கடை), so the deference praised in 395 and 403 is right only for the one with nothing yet to say. The Book of Love then puts the honour of 966 out of reach: the dignity of not following one who has turned away is 'not something the lovesick know' (1255), and in 1298 the heart argues from honour the other way, that to scorn him would itself be the disgrace (எள்ளின் இளிவாம்).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:395","k:1066"],["k:403","k:729"],["k:729","k:395"],["k:966","k:1053"],["k:1255","k:966"],["k:1298","k:1255"]],"limits":"Pope reads 395 as the unlearned standing before the learned, not the learner before the teacher; the honour-of-learning point depends on the Tamil commentators' construal of ஏக்கற்றும் கற்றார். 1255 and 1298 are dramatised speeches, not teaching, and 1255-966 is also read in thread:love_overturns. The verses never state the rule 'judge the posture by its object'; it is the pattern of the cases.","issue":"The 'one posture' is the writer's construct. 403 and 729 are about speaking or staying silent in an assembly, and 1066 is about asking; none of the three is standing in want before someone. The inference that the deference of 395 is right 'only for the one with nothing yet to say' misreads 395: its learners stand before a teacher in order to learn, not because they are silent. In 1255 Parimelazhagar takes பின் செல்லல் as thinking of him without pause, not physically following."},{"id":"thread:sulk_in_measure","title":"The sulk: love's fruit, but only in measure and only if union follows","claim":"The last three chapters of the book treat the lovers' sulk (ஊடல்) as a good, and then fence it on three sides. 1109 lists sulk, making-up and union together as the fruit of love; 1326 ranks the sulk above union, as digesting is sweeter than eating; the book's final verse, 1330, calls the sulk love's pleasure. But 1330 adds the condition 'if they get to come together and embrace' (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்), so the sulk's sweetness rests on the union 1326 placed beneath it. 1302 gives the measure: salt in proportion, and a little too long is a little too much; 1307 names the pain inside the sulk, the fear that union will be delayed; 1304 shows the failed case, leaving the sulker unreconciled is cutting a withered creeper at the root. 1282 states the limit from the other direction: when love is full to a palmyra's measure, not a millet of sulking is wanted. Parimelazhagar reads the whole sequence through அளவு, measure: already at 1109 he glosses உணர்தல் as leaving the sulk 'knowing its measure', and at 1330 he makes the embrace depend on ending the sulk between one too ripe and one too young. 1329 and 1307, both the man's on his assignment, show the same delay feared in the moment and wished for afterwards.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1109","k:1302"],["k:1109","k:1326"],["k:1109","k:1330"],["k:1282","k:1302"],["k:1301","k:1302"],["k:1307","k:1330"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1329","k:1307"],["k:1330","k:1302"],["k:1330","k:1304"]],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reads 1304 as the wife telling the man to go reconcile his courtesans, a taunt rather than a rule, so its use as the sulk's failed case follows Kalaignar's plain reading. He also reads 1282 as the woman's own haste dressed as general advice, not a doctrine. The chapters do not say which of 1326 and 1330 is the final word; they stand side by side.","issue":"The claim reads 1307 as fear that union will be delayed, but Parimelazhagar glosses புணர்வது நீடுவது அன்றுகொல் as worry about whether the sweet union will last long (நீட்டிக்குமோ நீட்டியாதோ), and Kalaignar agrees. On that reading 1329's wish that the night be long (நீடுக) matches 1307's worry, so the 'feared then desired' contrast depends on a reading the claim does not name. The claim's sentence 'both the man's on his assignment' is also garbled."},{"id":"thread:who_should_fear","title":"Fear: the mark of the excellent and the conduct of the base","claim":"The book awards fear and fearlessness to opposite ranks of people. Fearlessness (அஞ்சாமை) is one of the four things a king must never lack (382), yet not fearing what should be feared is folly and fearing it is the work of the knowing (அறிவார் தொழில், 428), and the excellent fear evil deeds that the practised wrongdoer does not (201). At the other end, fear is the whole of the base person's good conduct (அச்சமே கீழ்களது ஆசாரம், 1075), the only check on a freedom 1073 compares to the gods'. Taken together, the verses make fear itself morally neutral: what ranks a person is its object. Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar both notice the collision at 428 and resolve it the same way, saying that since fearlessness was called a king's excellence, 428 names the things that must be feared (sin, blame, ruin), while Parimelazhagar reads 1075's fear as fear of the king's punishment.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:201","k:428"],["k:201","k:1075"],["k:382","k:428"],["k:428","k:1075"],["k:1073","k:1075"]],"limits":"This does not claim the book has one doctrine of fear; the reconciliation by object is the commentators', and 382 and 1075 do not themselves say what is or is not feared. 201's 'fear' is of a sin-induced intoxication on Parimelazhagar's reading, which is further from 1075 than the shared word suggests.","issue":"1075 does not make fear the whole of the base person's conduct or the only check on 1073's freedom: its second half (எச்சம் அவாவுண்டேல் உண்டாம் சிறிது) names desire for gain as a second motive, and Parimelazhagar glosses it as mostly fear, sometimes gain. Manakkudavar on 428 notices the tension with fearlessness (மேல் அஞ்சாமை வேண்டு மென்றாராயினும்) but does not list sin, blame and ruin; that list is Parimelazhagar's alone."},{"id":"thread:fort_and_fear","title":"Does a fort cure fear, or does fear cancel a fort?","claim":"Three verses in the Book of Wealth tie fortification and fear together in a loop. A fort is of value to those who, afraid, look to their own safety (அஞ்சித் தற்போற்றுபவர்க்கும் பொருள், 741); a king who has made no defence will be seized by fear when war comes and quickly perish (569); yet the fearful have no fort at all (அச்சமுடையார்க்கு அரணில்லை, 534). Read together they make the fort necessary but not sufficient: its absence produces fear, but fear in the mind makes it useless, which is how Parimelazhagar reads 534 (forests and hills give no profit to a frightened mind). This also gives the kingly fearlessness of 382 a material condition, since 569 shows even a king coming to fear without preparation.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:534","k:741"],["k:569","k:534"],["k:741","k:569"],["k:569","k:382"]],"limits":"534's fort is a simile for the heedless losing their wealth, so its first half is not primarily a claim about statecraft; and 741's 'afraid' is, on Parimelazhagar's reading, the weaker party prudently taking refuge, not the cowardice 534 condemns. The loop is a reader's construction across chapters 54, 57 and 75, not something any verse states.","issue":"The extension to 382 is the reader's inference: 569 says a king without சிறை comes to fear, but nothing in 382 or 569 makes fearlessness depend on defences."},{"id":"thread:when_to_bow","title":"Bowing: good for all, yet shameful in some","claim":"The book commends பணிதல் without restriction in three places: bowing is good for everyone and a further wealth for the rich (125), whoever wants a lineage must be humble toward all (யார்க்கும் பணிவு, 960), and greatness always bows while smallness admires itself (978). Other verses then draw lines the general rule does not. 963 makes bowing the duty of abundance and standing tall the duty of want; 903 names a bowing that brings lasting shame, stooping before one's wife; and the honour chapter prefers being called ruined to living by following those who slight you (967), while 680 reports that rulers of small domains do bow to the great and take terms. Read together, the praised humility is the absence of pride in those who could be proud (Parimelazhagar glosses 125 and 978 by பெருமிதம் and தருக்கு), and bowing from fear or for a living is a different act the book does not praise: Parimelazhagar says of 680 that bowing is not the wish of the honourable, so the verse says 'they will accept' as worldly practice.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:963","k:125"],["k:978","k:125"],["k:960","k:963"],["k:978","k:963"],["k:903","k:978"],["k:680","k:967"],["k:963","k:680"]],"limits":"The unifying distinction between humility and submission is the commentators' more than the text's; Parimelazhagar reads 978's என்றும் as 'even when distinction has come', which removes its tension with 963, and Manakkudavar takes 680 as plain prescription for the weak, not a reluctant report. 963's 'reduced circumstances' is poverty on both commentators' readings, so its clash with 680 holds only if personal honour extends to statecraft.","issue":"Parimelazhagar restricts 960's யார்க்கும் to those who deserve reverence (பணியப்படுவார் யாவர் மாட்டும்), so 'without restriction' holds only on the plain text there as well as at 978. The 680 and 967 links bear on the claim only indirectly."},{"id":"thread:birth_across_book","title":"Birth: cause of conduct, or its name?","claim":"The Kural's verses on lineage (குடி, பிறப்பு) pull in two directions. 951 and 952 make birth the source of conduct: integrity and shame are natural (இயல்பாக) only to the well-born, who do not slip in conduct, truth and shame; 502 and 793 treat family as a criterion to check before trusting a minister or making a friend, and 992 names birth as one of courtesy's two sources. 133 reverses the direction: having conduct is itself குடிமை, and a lapse becomes low birth, so that by 133 whoever slips has already left the class 952 describes; 958 sits between, where a lapse in affection makes people doubt a man's birth but leaves birth as the underlying fact; and 972 denies that birth settles distinction at all, leaving only deeds. The commentators divide along the same line: Parimelazhagar keeps 133 within varna (conduct raises or lowers one's standing within the rank one is born to) and reads 951 as saying the well-born need no teaching, while Kalaignar reads 951 and 952 backwards, as tests by which the well-born are recognised, which aligns them with 133 and 972.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:133","k:951"],["k:502","k:133"],["k:502","k:951"],["k:793","k:972"],["k:951","k:972"],["k:952","k:133"],["k:958","k:133"],["k:972","k:133"],["k:992","k:972"]],"limits":"This does not claim the book rejects birth: most verses here (502, 793, 951, 952, 958, 992) treat it as real and informative, and only 972 denies it outright. Nor does 133 abolish the vocabulary of birth; it redefines it, and on Parimelazhagar's reading does so only within a fixed scheme of varna.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 972's எல்லா உயிர்க்கும் as all human lives (மக்களுயிர்) and its சிறப்பு as greatness or smallness from deeds, so 972 levels birth without naming lineage. Kalaignar also reads 502 backwards, as a test by which the well-born are recognised, which the claim does not mention."},{"id":"thread:strong_and_weak","title":"வலியார் and மெலியார்: one pair of words, three kinds of advice","claim":"The book gives opposite advice about the stronger and the weaker in the same words. In the Book of Virtue, 250 tells one who moves against the weaker (மெலியார்மேல் செல்லுமிடத்து) to remember standing before the stronger, and 302 says anger is bad where it cannot take effect and worst where it can, which Parimelazhagar glosses as anger against the stronger and the weaker. In the Book of Wealth, 861 tells a ruler to avoid opposing the stronger and to want enmity with the weaker without holding back (ஓம்பா மெலியார்மேல் மேக பகை), and the warrior's song in 771-772 prefers the spear that missed an elephant to the arrow that hit a hare, which Manakkudavar reads as sending the weak away to fight those who can resist. 493 adds that strength is not fixed: the weaker side wins if it knows the ground and guards itself. Parimelazhagar separates the audiences, taking chapter 31 as addressed to ascetics and 861 to a king; Kalaignar instead rewrites 861 as a wish to fight the strong, which removes the conflict by changing the verse.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:861","k:250"],["k:302","k:861"],["k:772","k:861"],["k:493","k:861"]],"limits":"The conflict depends on reading 250 and 302 as rules for everyone; if chapter 31 addresses ascetics, as Parimelazhagar holds, the Book of Virtue and 861 speak to different people. 772 is a warrior's boast, not a counsel of policy, and 772 and 861 both favour fighting only when read as rules for choosing an opponent. This thread is a sibling of ethics_vs_statecraft, with its own verses.","issue":"The title's 'same words' holds only for 250 and 861. 302's stronger and weaker are Parimelazhagar's gloss on செல்லா இடம் and செல்லிடம், and 772 uses neither word. Parimelazhagar's gloss on 861 counts allies and sound counsel within strength, which makes வலியார் and மெலியார் a reckoning of resources. 773, beside 772 in the same chapter, praises helping an enemy who has fallen, which counts against reading chapter 78 as a mere wish to fight."},{"id":"thread:enmity_chosen_and_refused","title":"Enmity: chosen, bought, and refused","claim":"Chapter 87 (பகைமாட்சி) treats enmity as a thing to be chosen and kept: 861 tells the ruler to want enmity with the weaker, and 867 says the enmity of one who stays close and acts unfittingly must be acquired even at a price (கொடுத்தும் கொளல்வேண்டும் மன்ற). The next chapter opens, in 871, by calling enmity a thing without பண்பு that no one should want even in jest. 995 then says those who know how others feel keep பண்பு even inside enmity, so the quality enmity is said to lack can survive within it. The commentators settle 871 differently: Parimelazhagar narrows it to unworthy enmity (மாணாத பகை), which leaves room for 861 and 867, while Manakkudavar takes it as saying that taking up enmity is bad everywhere (எவ்விடத்தும் பகைகோடல் தீது).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:861","k:871"],["k:871","k:867"],["k:995","k:871"]],"limits":"The verses themselves do not distinguish worthy from unworthy enmity; that distinction is Parimelazhagar's. 995 is about conduct toward an enemy one already has and does not say whether enmity should be sought, so it qualifies 871's wording rather than contradicting 871's advice.","issue":"Only two links carry the claim. The 995 step trades on a shift in பண்பு: in 871 பண்பிலது means 'yielding no good' (Parimelazhagar: தீமை பயப்பது), while 995's பண்பு means courteous qualities. Saying the 'quality enmity is said to lack' survives inside it is wordplay, not the same quality. Also, neither commentator reads 867's அடுத்திருந்து as 'staying close': Parimelazhagar takes it as having begun a work, and Kalaignar reads 861 the opposite way (fight the strong)."},{"id":"thread:one_virtue_for_all","title":"Can one virtue stand for all of them?","claim":"Verse 33 asks for virtue to be done without pause wherever it can reach (ஓவாதே ... எல்லாம் செயல்). Two later verses each let a single virtue stand for the whole, and they pick different ones: 297 says that if one keeps up not lying, leaving the other virtues undone is good (அறம்பிற செய்யாமை செய்யாமை நன்று), and 321 answers the question 'what is virtuous action?' with not killing, since killing brings every other sin; 323 then ranks the two with not killing first. Whether 297 is really a claim of sufficiency is a dispute the commentators settle differently: Parimelazhagar reads it as advice to hold one virtue unfailingly rather than take on many and fail in some, and records that others construe it the other way; Manakkudavar is one of them, reading it as 'only if one keeps not-lying unbroken is doing other virtues good, otherwise harmful', on which 297 states a precondition, not a sufficiency, and the tension with 33 and 321 largely dissolves. Even the one virtue 297 singles out carries an exception, since 292 counts a falsehood yielding faultless good as truth, so its 'unbroken' not-lying is unbroken only under chapter 30's definition of truth by effect.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:33","k:297"],["k:321","k:297"],["k:292","k:297"]],"limits":"This does not claim the book is incoherent: 323 is a deliberate ranking within the chapter, and on Parimelazhagar's reading of 321 அறவினை names the whole class of virtues rather than one deed. The superlative form of 300 and 323 is treated in thread:competing_supremes; this thread concerns sufficiency, not superlatives.","issue":"The commentary already raises this conflict itself: Parimelazhagar on 323 says the ranking was given because 297 and 300 had left in doubt which of the two virtues is higher. He also says of both 297 and 321 that the one virtue yields the fruit of all the others, which strengthens the sufficiency reading the claim treats as disputed."},{"id":"thread:good_results_as_licence","title":"Can a good result excuse the breach?","claim":"The Book of Virtue answers this differently for speech and for gain. For speech, 292 says yes: a falsehood that yields faultless good (புரைதீர்ந்த நன்மை பயக்கும் எனின்) belongs with truth, and this is what keeps 297's unbroken not-lying consistent with it. For gain, 113 says no: gain got by leaving fairness is to be dropped that very day even if it brings nothing but good (நன்றே தரினும்), while 177, three chapters later, refuses covetous gain by arguing from its outcome, that its fruit is not good when enjoyed, an argument 113's concession makes unnecessary. Parimelazhagar narrows the gap from both sides: on 113 he takes நன்றே தரினும் as a concession made for the sake of argument, since such gain in fact yields no good, and on 292 the good that licenses falsehood is itself அறம், such as saving a life from ruin or death; for him the outcome that counts is harm or benefit to others, never advantage to oneself.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:177","k:113"],["k:292","k:113"],["k:292","k:297"]],"limits":"113 concerns wealth and 292 speech, and 292's புரைதீர்ந்த (free of fault) may already exclude the gain 113 forbids, so the verses are not a flat contradiction. The internal logic of chapter 30 is covered by thread:truth_by_effect; this thread adds the comparison with the treatment of gain.","issue":"Chapter 12 itself argues gain from outcome as well (116: கெடுவல்யான் என்பது அறிக ... நடுவொரீஇ அல்ல செயின்), and Parimelazhagar sums up 112 and 113 as fair wealth yielding good and the other yielding evil, so the contrast between outcome-free 113 and outcome-based 177 holds only on 113's literal concession."},{"id":"thread:trust_and_watching","title":"Trust once given, and the watching that goes on","claim":"Chapter 51 ends by forbidding two errors, trusting one not examined and doubting one examined and trusted (510, தெளிந்தான்கண் ஐயுறவு), after 501 has set out the fourfold test, and 517 draws the practical conclusion: having reasoned who will finish what by what means, leave the work with him (அவன்கண் விடல்). The next chapter and chapter 59 pull the other way: 514 says many who passed every test change through the nature of the work itself (வினைவகையான் வேறாகும் மாந்தர் பலர்), 520 tells the king to look into the one doing the work every day (நாடோறும் நாடுக), and 584 makes the king's own officers the first object of the spy's inquiry. Testing before appointment therefore does not end examination, and the book's rule against doubt has to coexist with daily inspection. The commentators reconcile them by distinguishing watching from visible suspicion: Parimelazhagar on 510 says the forbidden doubt is suspicion without a fault found, which the man notices and which slackens his work and invites enemies to detach him; on 520 the daily inquiry is to be made within the king himself so that the man's standing (உரிமை) is not destroyed; on 514 he recommends a middle-sized task to expose those who change after appointment. Manakkudavar reads 514 as a warning not to grow careless because one has already trusted.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:514","k:501"],["k:514","k:510"],["k:514","k:520"],["k:517","k:510"],["k:517","k:520"],["k:520","k:510"],["k:520","k:584"],["k:584","k:510"],["k:584","k:514"]],"limits":"The verses themselves never say the inspection must be private; that is the commentators' addition. 584's spy examines kin and enemies as well as officers, so spying may be routine inquiry rather than the particular suspicion 510 forbids, and 520-510 is a tension only on the stricter reading.","issue":"584 lists வினைசெய்வார் first but gives no priority among the three groups. Manakkudavar takes 517 as aimed mainly at the army commander."},{"id":"thread:memory_sweet_or_burning","title":"Does remembering the beloved comfort or burn?","claim":"Chapter 121, the lament of those who remember, holds two opposite accounts of memory. 1201 and 1202, which Parimelazhagar gives to the man, make recollection a joy that does not fade even in thought (உள்ளினும் தீராப் பெருமகிழ்) and say no pain comes from thinking of the one you love. 1207, 1209 and 1296, spoken by the woman, make the same act the wound: even remembering burns her heart (உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும்), thinking hard on his lack of tenderness (அளியின்மை ஆற்ற நினைந்து) wastes her life, and thinking alone leaves her heart there only to eat her. The verses together locate the difference in what is remembered: Parimelazhagar glosses the object in 1201-1202 as the pleasure of past union and in 1209 and 1296 as his neglect and cruelties, and Manakkudavar reads 1202 as conditional, sweet only if no pain comes from the thinking. 1215 adds that for the woman even the sweetness of seeing him, awake or in a dream, lasts only for the moment of seeing, where the man's persists in thought.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1201","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1209"],["k:1202","k:1296"],["k:1215","k:1201"],["k:1296","k:1207"]],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches in different moods, not a doctrine the book states and then retracts. The gender contrast rests on Parimelazhagar's speaker assignments; Manakkudavar gives 1202 to the woman, which keeps the condition but dissolves the contrast. The 1202/1207 pair is already cited in thread:gender_asymmetry, which this thread does not replace since it treats memory rather than gender.","issue":"1296 is in chapter 130, not 121. Parimelazhagar takes the object remembered in 1207 to be the same past pleasure as in 1201 (அவ்வின்பத்தை மறத்தலறியேன்) and says separation burns despite the memory, so 1207 does not fit the claim's resolution by 'what is remembered'. On 1215 the 'only for the moment' reading is Manakkudavar's (அப்பொழுதைக்கு இன்பமாம்); Parimelazhagar reads the verse as saying waking and dream are equal for her, and the contrast with the man's lasting joy is the writer's construction."},{"id":"thread:body_as_testimony","title":"Who may call him cruel?","claim":"In separation the woman's body is read as public testimony, and the verses dispute what it testifies to and whether she wants it heard. In 1188 she complains that the town says only that she has grown pale and no one says he has left her. Yet in 1190 she calls the name of pallor good provided people do not broadcast his unkindness (நல்காமை தூற்றார் எனின்), and in 1236 what hurts her, more than her slackening bangles and shoulders, is hearing him called cruel (கொடியர் எனக்கூறல் நொந்து). The friend reads the same body the other way: in 1232 the pale weeping eyes seem to tell of the lover's unkindness, and in 1235 the wasted shoulders declare the cruelty of the cruel one; both commentators frame 1232, 1235 as the friend's blaming of the man to console her (இயற்பழித்தல்), and 1190, 1236 as her refusals. The asymmetry is exact: she herself uses கொடியார் கொடுமை of him in 1169, where the long nights outdo his cruelty, but will not accept the word from another mouth.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1188","k:1190"],["k:1190","k:1232"],["k:1190","k:1236"],["k:1235","k:1188"],["k:1235","k:1236"],["k:1236","k:1169"],["k:1169","k:1235"]],"limits":"This is a consistency in one dramatised speaker across chapters 117, 119 and 124, not a teaching stated by the book. 1188 has no aligned commentary in the corpus, so its speaker is inferred from context; and 1169 is tied to 1235 by shared phrasing alone, so its weight in the thread is the word she uses, not a link of argument.","issue":"The claim that 'both commentators frame 1232, 1235 as the friend's blaming of the man (இயற்பழித்தல்)' is wrong for 1232. Manakkudavar has the friend warn that the tears are becoming gossip so that she will bear up, and Parimelazhagar has the friend console her (இனி நீ ஆற்றல் வேண்டும்). Only Manakkudavar on 1235 says இயற்பழித்து outright; Parimelazhagar's 1235 is 'how shall we hide it'. Manakkudavar takes தூற்றார் in 1190 as the townspeople, not the friend."},{"id":"thread:fortune_goddess","title":"The goddess of fortune and her elder sister: one figure, many doorsteps","claim":"The Kural personifies fortune as a goddess (செய்யாள் in 84, செய்யவள் in 167, திரு in 179, 482 and 920, தாமரையினாள் in 617) and misfortune as her elder sister (தவ்வை in 167, மாமுகடி in 617, முகடி in 936), and the figure recurs across both books with a different moral trigger each time. She settles in the home of the glad-faced host (84), leaves the envious man and hands him to her sister (167), seeks out those who know virtue and do not covet (179), lives in the labour of the unslothful while her sister lives in sloth (617), is tied down by timely action (482), has already abandoned those who keep two-minded women, drink and dice (920), and in 936 gambling is her sister under another name, which Parimelazhagar explains by saying the sister's real name is too ill-omened to speak. 594 gives the same personification without the goddess: ஆக்கம் asks the way to the man of unwavering energy. Taken together, the verses show the goddess is a portable image that each chapter lends to its own virtue, generosity, non-envy, non-coveting, exertion and timing, not a doctrine about what in fact draws wealth; and 371 undercuts the residence rule of 617 by making sloth and effort themselves products of fate.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:84","k:167"],["k:84","k:617"],["k:167","k:617"],["k:179","k:167"],["k:179","k:594"],["k:482","k:617"],["k:594","k:617"],["k:920","k:617"],["k:920","k:936"],["k:936","k:167"],["k:936","k:617"],["k:371","k:617"]],"limits":"The thread claims a shared figure, not a theology: Kalaignar reads the two goddesses of 617 as mere names for the industrious and the idle. The verses never rank the triggers against each other, and the fate question raised by 371 belongs to thread:fate_and_agency. The first word of 594 is truncated in this edition (க்கம் for ஆக்கம்).","issue":"The recurring figure is well documented, but the conclusion that it is 'a portable image ... not a doctrine about what in fact draws wealth' goes beyond the verses. Having many triggers does not make them non-causal, and Parimelazhagar treats 617 as stating the cause (ஏது) of fortune and misfortune. In 482 he glosses திரு simply as செல்வம் with no goddess, so counting it among the goddess's names is a reading."},{"id":"thread:ill_gotten_gain","title":"Wrongly got gain: refused because it fails, and refused even if it succeeds","claim":"The Kural refuses wealth got by wrong means on two different grounds that sit side by side. One set argues from outcome: gain by coveting turns out badly when enjoyed (177), stolen gain perishes while seeming to grow (283), what is taken with others' tears leaves with tears (659), wealth made by deceit is water in an unfired pot (660), and wrongdoing to escape poverty only renews it (205). The other set concedes that the gain is real and good and still rules it out, using a fixed concessive form: நன்றே தரினும் (113), நன்றாகும் ஆக்கம் பெரிதெனினும் (328), செல்வம் பெறினும் (311), அடுக்கிய கோடி பெறினும் (954), வென்றிடினும் (931), and 657 ranks the utter poverty of the worthy above blame-laden wealth. The concessive verses make the prohibition independent of the prediction, so it stands where 177 or 205 might fail. The Book of Wealth carries the rule into acquisition itself: the promise that wealth yields virtue and pleasure (754, 760) is restricted to wealth that came திறனறிந்து தீதின்றி, 755 orders wealth without grace and love to be let go with the same verb விடல் as 113, and 1009 says such wealth is taken by others.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:177","k:113"],["k:205","k:657"],["k:113","k:328"],["k:311","k:113"],["k:328","k:311"],["k:931","k:113"],["k:113","k:657"],["k:328","k:657"],["k:954","k:311"],["k:755","k:113"],["k:283","k:659"],["k:283","k:660"],["k:754","k:659"],["k:755","k:660"],["k:760","k:754"],["k:1009","k:760"],["k:755","k:1009"]],"limits":"The two grounds do not contradict each other and the book never says which is primary. On Parimelazhagar, 311 concerns yogic powers and 328 sacrificial killing by renunciants, so reading them as general rules about wealth follows Pope and Kalaignar rather than his gloss. 754 and 760 are addressed to the king.","issue":"931 is misfiled in the concessive set: it grants வென்றிடினும் but then argues from the outcome (வென்றதூஉம் தூண்டிற்பொன் மீன்விழுங்கி அற்று), so it does not show the prohibition holding apart from a prediction. 1009 sits in the chapter on wealth that does no good (நன்றியில்செல்வம்), and Parimelazhagar reads it as the miser's unspent hoard, not wrongly acquired wealth; only Manakkudavar and Kalaignar put its அன்பொரீஇ ... அறநோக்காது at the stage of acquisition. 954's குன்றுவ is any degrading act, not a way of getting wealth."},{"id":"thread:wealth_and_holder","title":"Owning is not having: wealth measured by its holder","claim":"In both books the Kural denies that holding goods amounts to having them unless the holder has some further quality, and it states this in one recurring form, 'whatever they have, they have nothing': a wife without worth (53, உள்ளதென் இல்லவள் மாணாக்கடை), a person without understanding (430, என்னுடையரேனும் இலர்; 841 calls want of understanding இன்மையுள் இன்மை), one without energy (591, உடையது உடையரோ), one who neither gives nor enjoys (1005, கோடியுண்டாயினும் இல்). The same judgment is pushed further into an inversion of wealth over poverty when the holder is wrong: wealth with the unlearned hurts more than poverty with the good (408), having hurts more than not having under an unjust king (558), great wealth with the discourteous is milk spoiled by its vessel (1000), with the grim-faced it is as if seen by a ghost (565), with a fool it feeds strangers while kin starve (837). The paired images fix where the fault lies: 660 blames the making (unfired pot) and 1000 the holder (bad vessel, good milk); 216 and 1008 put the same fruiting tree in the same village and let the holder's character decide whether it feeds or poisons.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:53","k:430"],["k:430","k:591"],["k:430","k:841"],["k:1005","k:430"],["k:591","k:1005"],["k:408","k:558"],["k:408","k:1000"],["k:408","k:837"],["k:837","k:1000"],["k:1000","k:1008"],["k:1008","k:216"],["k:660","k:1000"],["k:565","k:1000"]],"limits":"This is a claim about the condition under which பொருள் counts as உடைமை, not about which thing deserves the name செல்வம் (thread:true_wealth). The chapter on making wealth pulls the other way, 751 and 752 saying wealth alone gives standing and everyone honours the rich, though 973 and 1002 qualify those in turn. Parimelazhagar grounds 430 and 591 alike in the holder's inability to keep or remake wealth, which is narrower than the verses' wording.","issue":"558 does not fit 'when the holder is wrong': its condition is the unjust king (முறைசெய்யா மன்னவன்), not any defect in the one who has the wealth. 53 likewise puts the deciding quality in the wife, not the holder. 216 and 1008 are a counter-image of two different trees (பயன்மரம், நச்சு மரம்), not 'the same fruiting tree'."},{"id":"thread:foresight_as_understanding","title":"Understanding as foresight, and its limits","claim":"In the Book of Wealth அறிவு is repeatedly defined as knowing and guarding against what is coming: 427 makes knowing what will be the mark of the wise, 429 promises them no shaking affliction, and 435 and 535 give the failure two outcomes, sudden ruin and late regret. The book then qualifies this in three directions: the foresight can be obtained through others the ruler keeps (442), understanding also dissolves trouble that has already come (622), and 372 says a fate of loss makes a person foolish, which Parimelazhagar extends to one who has every understanding. Read together, foresight is the working core of understanding in statecraft, but neither wholly one's own nor proof against fate.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:427","k:429"],["k:429","k:435"],["k:435","k:535"],["k:429","k:442"],["k:622","k:429"],["k:372","k:429"],["k:427","k:372"]],"limits":"The verses do not say foresight is the whole of அறிவு; elsewhere the term is tested morally (203, 315) and by seeing the real (355). The pull between 372 and 429 belongs with the fate-and-agency conflict and is not resolved in the verses.","issue":"Only 427 and 429 tie the foresight to அறிவு; 435 and 535 speak of guarding before (வருமுன்னர், முன்னுற) without the word, and Parimelazhagar reads them as guarding against faults and against enemies exploiting a lapse. Parimelazhagar also records an alternative reading of 427's ஆவதறிவார் as 'know what is good for them'."},{"id":"thread:world_as_measure","title":"Does understanding follow the world or test it?","claim":"Several verses make accord with the world part of what understanding is: the much-studied who cannot move with the world lack understanding (140), to live as the world lives is அறிவு (426), and even after knowing a method one should act knowing how the world goes (637). 850 extends this from conduct to belief: one who denies what the world affirms is set down as a demon. Yet 423, in the same chapter as 426, defines understanding as finding the true substance of a thing whoever says it. Parimelazhagar narrows 'the world' in 140 and 850 to the high-minded many and reads 426 as advice to a king, and Kalaignar's 'shown by evidence' moves 850 toward 423.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:140","k:426"],["k:637","k:140"],["k:637","k:426"],["k:426","k:850"],["k:850","k:140"],["k:850","k:423"],["k:214","k:140"]],"limits":"On Parimelazhagar's reading 214 also makes knowing the way of the world the test of being alive, but Manakkudavar takes ஒத்தது there as ஒப்புரவு, so it is weak support. The 850-423 conflict is already noted under knowing_and_learning; this thread concerns the world as a standard, not the speaker's standing.","issue":"The narrowing of 'world' to the high-minded is not Parimelazhagar's alone: Kalaignar also glosses 140 and 426 by உயர்ந்தோர். Parimelazhagar on 637 grounds the rule in avoiding blame (செய்யின் அது பழிக்கும்), which is prudential rather than epistemic."},{"id":"thread:measure_of_the_sulk","title":"The sulk: love's best part, in measure","claim":"The Book of Love keeps returning to ஊடல், and its verses raise and then bound it. 1109 lists sulking with reconciliation and union as love's rewards; 1326 ranks sulking above union, as digestion above eating; 1330, the book's last verse, calls it love's pleasure but places its own pleasure in the embrace that follows. 1302 and 1306 make it a matter of degree, salt that seasons and spoils if prolonged, fruit unripe or overripe without it, and 1282 says that when love is full not a millet-seed of sulking is wanted. Together they treat the sulk as valuable only as a measured step toward union, which Parimelazhagar makes explicit in glossing 1109 and 1330 as ending it at the right point.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1109","k:1302"],["k:1109","k:1326"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1330","k:1302"],["k:1302","k:1306"],["k:1306","k:1330"],["k:1282","k:1302"]],"limits":"The verses come from different speakers and situations (1282 is the woman's impatience voiced as general advice, per Parimelazhagar), so this is a pattern across voices, not one doctrine. Pope's translation of 1109 as lust is a translator's reading.","issue":"'1330, the book's last verse' holds only in Parimelazhagar's order; Manakkudavar closes the Book of Love at 1323. The commentators assign கனி and கருக்காய் in 1306 to துனி and புலவி differently: Parimelazhagar takes கனி as rich ripeness, not spoilage."},{"id":"thread:love_and_liquor","title":"Love measured against liquor","claim":"The Book of Love uses liquor, which chapter 93 condemns, as its standard comparison for love, and uses it both ways. In 1090, 1201 and 1281 love surpasses toddy: liquor delights only when drunk, love at a look or a thought, from first sight (1090) through separation (1201) to the woman's longing (1281). In 1145 and 1288 love instead works like drink: wanted more with each exposure, and in 1288 craved though it does shameful harm, which Parimelazhagar spells out as loss of shame, restraint, conduct and awareness, the very charges laid against drink in chapter 93.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1090","k:1281"],["k:1201","k:1090"],["k:1201","k:1281"],["k:1145","k:1281"],["k:1281","k:1288"],["k:1288","k:1145"]],"limits":"The chapter 93 verses are not in this packet; the link to them rests on Parimelazhagar's gloss of 1288. Speakers differ by commentator for 1090, 1145 and 1288, so the thread does not claim a single voice holds both views.","issue":"In 1145 the thing that grows sweeter is love's becoming rumour (Parimelazhagar: காமம் அலராந்தோறும் இனிது), not love at each meeting, so 'wanted more with each exposure' means public exposure."},{"id":"thread:deeds_come_back","title":"How does a harmful deed return to the doer?","claim":"Across the chapters on fearing evil deeds, not harming, not coveting, not stealing and clean action, the return of harm is described in several distinct ways rather than one. Its inescapability is spatial in 207 and 208 (the enmity of deeds follows and kills, ruin stays at the foot like a shadow, both with வீயாது), and 313 sharpens it with உய்யா: an enemy can be survived, one's own hurtful act cannot. It is temporal in 319 (forenoon harm comes back in the afternoon, தாமே, of itself), an agent's act in 204 (அறம் plots the plotter's ruin), and a near-tautology of desire in 206 and 320 (whoever wants no pain causes none). A separate figure, the ripening, places the harm at the harvest and not at the getting: coveted gain in 177, theft in 284, and forbidden deeds that succeed in 658 all go bad at விளைவு, and 659 extends the return to wealth taken with tears, while adding that rightly got wealth pays later even if lost.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:207","k:208"],["k:313","k:207"],["k:319","k:207"],["k:204","k:319"],["k:206","k:320"],["k:177","k:284"],["k:658","k:177"],["k:659","k:319"]],"limits":"The thread describes how the return is pictured, not whether the book thinks it always happens; 169 concedes that the crooked prosper and the upright fall, which thread:justice_of_the_world takes up. Who or what returns the harm (அறம் in 204, nobody in 319, Parimelazhagar's அறக்கடவுள் and pāvam in his glosses) is left unsettled by the verses.","issue":"658 does not use விளைவு; its timing comes only from முடிந்தாலும் பீழை தரும் (even when the deed succeeds), so it belongs to the ripening figure by sense rather than by wording. 284's harvest is of love of stealing (களவின்கண் கன்றிய காதல்), not of stolen gain as such."},{"id":"thread:checklist_for_action","title":"One checklist for action, from the king's deed to the minister's definition","claim":"A short list of conditions for successful action recurs across five Porul chapters and changes role as it moves. In 483 means and time (கருவி, காலம்) make any 'hard deed' (அருவினை) possible; 631 reuses the same three words but turns them into the definition of a minister, adding method (செய்கை); 675 expands them into a five-item rule for anyone acting (resources, means, time, deed, place); and 516, in the chapter on employing people, keeps deed and time and adds the one item the others lack, the doer. A parallel pair, 461 and 676, shares the closing command (சூழ்ந்து / பார்த்துச் செயல்) and the yield as its last term, but 461 counts loss against gain while 676 weighs the course of the work, its completion and obstacles. Read together, the minister's defining skill is the general checklist for action, and the book adjusts the list to the chapter it sits in.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:483","k:675"],["k:631","k:483"],["k:631","k:675"],["k:516","k:675"],["k:461","k:676"]],"limits":"This is a claim about recurring vocabulary and list structure, not that the lists are meant as one doctrine; Parimelazhagar glosses the items differently in each place (in 675 each term covers both one's own side and the enemy's). It does not cover the separate theme of execution failing after sound planning (640, 664).","issue":"The 461 and 676 pair shares none of the கருவி/காலம் vocabulary, so it is a second, parallel list rather than part of the same checklist, and its closing verbs differ (சூழ்ந்து, பார்த்து). Parimelazhagar reads 483's actor as the king (அரசரால்)."},{"id":"thread:one_virtue_alone","title":"Can a single virtue stand without the rest?","claim":"Several verses isolate one restraint and say it keeps its value even when the rest of virtue is absent, using a shared 'even if he does wrong' frame: not desiring another's wife in 150 (அறன்வரையான் அல்ல செயினும்) and not backbiting in 181 (அறங்கூறான் அல்ல செயினும்), with 142 ranking the adulterer lowest among those outside virtue. 297 goes further for not lying, saying the other virtues may be left undone, and 296 gives the reason: not lying yields all the virtues by itself. Yet 321 answers 'what is virtuous action?' with not killing, and 33 tells one to do virtue everywhere and without break. Because the book makes this move for different virtues, the verses read best as emphasis on each chapter's virtue, and Parimelazhagar on 150 and 181 says only that the one quality 'stands out' despite the faults, not that it suffices.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:150","k:181"],["k:142","k:150"],["k:150","k:297"],["k:181","k:297"],["k:296","k:297"],["k:321","k:297"],["k:33","k:297"]],"limits":"Only 296-297 claim sufficiency; 150 and 181 claim only that the one abstention is still good. Parimelazhagar reconciles 297 with 33 by reading it as advice to keep one virtue unfailingly rather than fail at many, and 323 itself ranks not killing above not lying (see thread:competing_supremes and thread:truth_by_effect).","issue":"Parimelazhagar narrows 142's அறன்கடை நின்றார் to those in sin through desire, so it ranks adulterers among lovers gone wrong rather than among all who stand outside virtue. Kalaignar reads 150 as a comparison (desiring another's wife is worse than straying from virtue), not as the 'even if' frame."},{"id":"thread:virtue_and_wealth_reversed","title":"Does virtue yield wealth, or wealth yield virtue?","claim":"Book 1 says virtue yields distinction and wealth (31, சிறப்பு ஈனும் செல்வமும் ஈனும்), and Book 2 turns the same construction round: wealth that came by knowing the means and without wrong yields virtue and pleasure (754, அறன்ஈனும் இன்பமும் ஈனும்), and for those who amass bright wealth the other two come easily together (760). The reversal holds only with a condition on how the wealth was got: 754's தீதின்றி fits wealth into 39's rule that only pleasure through virtue is pleasure, 659 says what is taken with tears leaves with tears, and 1009 uses 760's own word ஒண்பொருள் for wealth amassed by ignoring virtue, which others carry off. So the two books do not rank the ends differently; they make the causal arrow run both ways and let the manner of acquisition decide which way it runs.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:31","k:754"],["k:754","k:39"],["k:760","k:754"],["k:1009","k:760"],["k:754","k:659"]],"limits":"The condition is explicit in 754 but only read into 760 by Parimelazhagar, who glosses ஒண்பொருள் there as wealth that comes by the right way; on its face 760 promises virtue to abundance as such. Parimelazhagar also reads 754 as a king's revenue raised without tyranny, so its scope may be royal wealth only.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 31's சிறப்பு as liberation and its செல்வம் as heaven and similar rewards (துறக்கம் முதலிய செல்வம்), so on his reading 31 is not about worldly wealth and the 'reversal' with 754 is only verbal. 1009's fault is not how the wealth was got but its hoarding: Parimelazhagar glosses அறநோக்காது as not giving to the poor and அன்பொரீஇ as dropping love so as not to give. 1009 therefore shows use, not manner of acquisition, deciding the outcome, and its own ஒண்பொருள் cuts against reading 760's ஒண்பொருள் as 'rightly got'."},{"id":"thread:sulk_on_conditions","title":"Is the lovers' sulk a pleasure, and on what terms?","claim":"The Book of Love praises the sulk in rising terms: one of love's rewards (1109), sweeter than union (1326), beyond heaven (1323), love's very pleasure (1330). Almost every praise is hedged by a condition stated nearby. It must be measured like salt and spoils if prolonged (1302), only a small துனி is wanted (1322), yet love without any quarrel is fruit at the wrong stage (1306). It is sweet only if the embrace follows (1330, கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின்), the doubt about that sequel is its own pain (1307), and it is sweet only with one who loves (1309). 1326 puts the sulk above union, and 1330 makes its sweetness depend on union.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1109","k:1302"],["k:1282","k:1302"],["k:1302","k:1306"],["k:1322","k:1306"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1307","k:1330"],["k:1330","k:1302"],["k:1309","k:1323"]],"limits":"These are speeches by characters, not a treatise; 1282, which says no sulk at all is wanted when love is full, is on Parimelazhagar's reading the woman's impatience dressed as advice. The conditions limit the praise but are never offered as a retraction of it.","issue":"1322 does not say that 'only a small துனி is wanted'. It says the small துனி that a sulk brings leads to greatness even if his grace droops; 'only' comes from pairing it with Parimelazhagar's துனி/புலவி distinction on 1306. Manakkudavar reads 1322 as the sulk being pleasant even without union, which cuts against the claim that union is the condition."},{"id":"thread:sulk_without_fault","title":"A sulk that needs no offence","claim":"In chapter 132 the man lists sulks with thinner and thinner grounds: a wreath (1313), his attempt to appease her (1319), a silent look (1320), each drawing anger (காயும்). Only 1311 states a ground, other women's eyes on him. Chapter 133 then has both speakers concede there is no fault: she says he has none, yet his tenderness can bring on a sulk (1321, இல்லை தவறவர்க்கு), and he says that though men are faultless, being kept from her arms has its own savour (1325, தவறிலர் ஆயினும்); on Parimelazhagar's reading 1305 says the same. Read together, the book detaches the sulk from wrongdoing and treats the groundless sulk of chapter 132 as a pleasure in chapter 133.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1305","k:1321"],["k:1321","k:1325"],["k:1305","k:1325"],["k:1313","k:1321"],["k:1325","k:1319"],["k:1321","k:1311"]],"limits":"Manakkudavar reads 1305's நல்லவர் as the other women, and Parimelazhagar explains 1321 by her thought that others too may receive his tenderness, so jealousy survives with its ground removed. Real offence is not absent from these chapters: 1311 names him பரத்த.","issue":"'Only 1311 states a ground' undersells chapter 132: in 1313, 1319 and 1320 she also names a ground, always another woman (ஒருத்தியைக் காட்டிய, பிறர்க்கும், யாருள்ளி). The difference is that the man calls those grounds baseless, not that she gives none."},{"id":"thread:living_on_in_separation","title":"Can one live through separation, and why does she?","claim":"Several speeches say separation cannot be survived: parting is death to his life (1124), news of his quick return is for those still alive then (1151), and on Parimelazhagar's reading 1160's 'many endure the parting and live on' is irony. 1198 turns survival into a charge: none are harder (வன்கண்) than those who live on without a kind word, the word 1156 had applied to him for announcing the parting. Yet the same speaker is later alive and gives reasons: she lives by seeing him in dreams (1213) and by longing for his return (1263), the very return 1151 dismissed. Taken together, the verses deny that she can survive, then accuse her of surviving, then name what keeps her alive.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1124","k:1151"],["k:1151","k:1160"],["k:1160","k:1198"],["k:1213","k:1198"],["k:1151","k:1263"],["k:1198","k:1156"]],"limits":"This is a dramatic sequence, not a teaching about grief. Speakers vary by commentator (Manakkudavar gives 1151 to the woman), and Parimelazhagar takes 1213 as said to reassure the friend, so its explanation may be partly for show.","issue":"1124 is a simile (சாதல் அதற்கன்னள்) spoken by the man at a brief daytime parting on Parimelazhagar's reading, so it says separation feels like death more than that it cannot be survived. On Parimelazhagar, 1198 is the woman's self-reproach (யான் வன்கண்ணேனாகலின்), which is what lets it count as the charge against her."},{"id":"thread:body_as_witness","title":"The body testifies; who decides what it proves?","claim":"In the separation chapters her body speaks before she does: slipping bangles broadcast his leaving (1157, தூற்றா), shoulders announce it (1233), the bangles knew it before she did (1277), and she has been pale seven days though he left yesterday (1278; also 1185). All parties accept this evidence, but they dispute its verdict. The town reads the pallor as news about her and never says he left her (1188); the friend reads the wasted shoulders as declaring the cruel one's cruelty (1235); and she refuses that inference, pained to hear him called cruel (1236).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1157","k:1235"],["k:1233","k:1157"],["k:1277","k:1157"],["k:1278","k:1277"],["k:1185","k:1277"],["k:1235","k:1188"],["k:1235","k:1236"]],"limits":"Speaker assignments differ (Parimelazhagar gives 1233 and 1235 to the friend, Manakkudavar to the woman), and Parimelazhagar's 'before' in 1277 depends on reading the parting as one of mind while they are still together. She herself uses கொடியார் கொடுமை of him in 1169, so her refusal in 1236 is of the charge from another's mouth.","issue":"Parimelazhagar takes 1157's bangles as telling the friend (உனக்கு அறிவியாவோ), not broadcasting to the town, and he reads 1185 as recalling an earlier departure, so the public and immediate force the claim gives these two rests on the plain wording."},{"id":"thread:limits_of_friendship","title":"What can friendship survive?","claim":"The book says those devoted to friendship do not leave a friend once made (791, நட்டபின் வீடில்லை) and that the world loves those who do not give up old friends (809), yet it calls leaving a fool's friendship gain (797) and orders the unfit dropped even at a price (800, ஒன்றீத்தும் ஒருவுக). It also says the bond grows with practice (783) yet does not need it (785), and on Manakkudavar's reading 785's quick bond sits against 792's demand to examine again and again. The growth question is settled by character, not time: the bond with பண்புடையார் deepens with practice (783), while with பண்பிலார் shrinking is better than growing (811), the waning that 782 observes in fools' friendship. Parimelazhagar supplies the limit on leaving: exit is for a friendship taken up without knowing (800).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:791","k:797"],["k:791","k:800"],["k:809","k:791"],["k:783","k:785"],["k:785","k:792"],["k:783","k:811"],["k:782","k:811"]],"limits":"On Parimelazhagar's reading 785 only says feeling, more than contact, makes the bond, and there is no conflict with 783 or 792. Old friends who harm you are kept (807), which he treats as a separate case.","issue":"The claim says the growth question is 'settled by character, not time', but 783 against 785 is about whether contact is needed to make the bond (புணர்ச்சி பழகுதல் வேண்டா), which 811's பண்பிலார் does not address; character settles only whether growth is desirable. 783 itself speaks of growing delight (நயம்), not of a bond that needs practice."},{"id":"thread:friend_in_ruin","title":"The friend is judged at the hour of ruin","claim":"In all three books one moment decides a friendship: what the friend does when the other falls. The Book of Virtue says never abandon those who were a support in sorrow (106) and that their help is remembered through seven births (107); chapter 79 defines friendship as sharing the suffering in ruin (787) and acting there and then like a hand catching a slipping garment (788). The same measure condemns: the deserter's bond burns the heart even at death (799), those who fail at the crisis are an untrained horse worse than solitude (814, 798's ஆற்றறுப்பார்), and friends who give no safety are better never acquired (815). In the Book of Love, 1293 turns the saying that the ruined have no friends against the speaker's own heart, a deserter she cannot decline.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:106","k:806"],["k:107","k:799"],["k:787","k:799"],["k:814","k:788"],["k:798","k:814"],["k:815","k:106"],["k:1293","k:787"],["k:1293","k:798"]],"limits":"This is one standard among several: other verses make rebuke (784, 795) or inner feeling (785, 786) the mark of friendship. 1293 is a lover's dramatised reproach quoting a saying, not a teaching about friends.","issue":""},{"id":"thread:learning_judged_by_conduct","title":"Learning that conduct cancels","claim":"391 asks for two things in order: learn without flaw, then stand in keeping with it. Several verses across the Books of Virtue and Wealth enforce the second half by withdrawing the status of the learned from those who skip it: whoever has learned much but cannot move in accord with the world is unlearned and without understanding (140); whoever has read, understood and taught but is not restrained is the greatest fool (834); wide understanding is worth nothing if one covets (175); and restraint after learning is what virtue itself seeks out (130). In statecraft and medicine the same rule becomes professional: knowing the method from the treatises, act knowing the world (637), and the learned physician must weigh this patient, this illness, this time (949).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:391","k:140"],["k:391","k:834"],["k:175","k:834"],["k:834","k:130"],["k:637","k:140"],["k:949","k:637"]],"limits":"The verses do not claim that study produces good conduct; 823 says study rarely makes the unworthy good at heart, which is why 391 has to command conduct separately. The thread does not cover the question of where understanding comes from, treated in thread:knowing_and_learning.","issue":"637 and 949 are about fitting treatise knowledge to circumstances, not about conduct cancelling learning, so 'the same rule becomes professional' stretches the thread's rule. Parimelazhagar reads மாணார் in 823 as enemies who do not turn friend through study, not as the unworthy in general, so the limits lean on a broader reading than the chapter gives."},{"id":"thread:learning_must_be_voiced","title":"Learning that is not spoken","claim":"The book ranks learned people by whether they can deliver their learning before an audience, and is harsh to those who cannot. Those who cannot unfold what they learned are scentless flowers (650); those who cannot speak tellingly in a good assembly are useless however much they learned (728); and those who learned yet fear the assembly are lower than the unlearned (729), using the very word கடை that 395 gives the unlearned. Silence is therefore graded by who keeps it: it is the unlearned person's merit before the learned (403) and the learned person's disgrace. The positive title follows the same measure: 'learned among the learned' is whoever makes learning go home before the learned (722), and the envoy is judged the same way among treatise-holders and in a hostile court (683, 686, 724).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:728","k:650"],["k:722","k:650"],["k:729","k:395"],["k:403","k:729"],["k:683","k:722"],["k:686","k:724"]],"limits":"These verses concern speech before assemblies and courts, mostly in the Book of Wealth's chapters on speech and assemblies; they do not say learning has no private worth (398 makes it a protection across births). 720 adds that an unfit audience wastes even good speech.","issue":"683 concerns the envoy's mastery of statecraft treatises among ministers (நூலாருள் நூல்வல்லன்), not delivering speech, so it adds little to the claim about voicing. Parimelazhagar puts the failure in 728 and 729 down to fear (அச்சத்தான்), which separates the chapter on the assembly from 650's plain inability to expound."},{"id":"thread:hoard_and_store","title":"Where is wealth kept safe?","claim":"Across the Book of Virtue and the Book of Wealth, a run of verses turns the hoarder's own logic of keeping against him. Feeding the destitute is the place to deposit wealth (226, வைப்புழி; Parimelazhagar: what is so given comes back to help the giver), while those who keep what they own lose it (228, வைத்திழக்கும்), eating alone to fill one's store is worse than begging (229), the man who does not even use his heap is as dead (1001), and wealth withheld from the destitute ages unused like a beauty growing old alone (1007). The positive side recurs in three chapters: shared food keeps hunger from the sharer (227), keeps his line from failing (44), and gain belongs only to those who, like the crow, call others before eating (527).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:226","k:228"],["k:1007","k:226"],["k:228","k:1001"],["k:1001","k:229"],["k:229","k:527"],["k:527","k:227"],["k:44","k:227"]],"limits":"The book does not rest giving on return alone: 228 names giving's own joy and 222 calls giving good even if there were no higher world. 82 and 229 condemn the solitary meal by comparison rather than by consequence, and the protective results in 44, 227 and 527 are asserted, not argued.","issue":"Only 226 and 228 turn the hoarder's logic of keeping (வை-) against him in their own words; 1001 and 1007 condemn wealth left unused rather than wealth lost by being kept. 82, cited only in the limits, concerns hospitality to a guest and does not bear on storing wealth."},{"id":"thread:manner_of_giving","title":"Giving and the manner that goes with it","claim":"The book rarely praises giving bare: it pairs it with a glad face, sweet words, no scorn or no anger, and once ranks the manner above the gift, since 92 says a glad face and sweet speech are better than giving with a glad heart. 953 lists smile, giving, sweet words and not scorning flatly as marks of a good lineage (Parimelazhagar: the way one meets the poor who come), and 1057 shows why scorn matters, since the asker rejoices at giving without contempt, while 224 has the giver's own unease end only at the asker's glad face. In the Book of Wealth the same pair becomes an instrument: giving with sweet words wins the world for a king (387) and surrounds a man with kin (525), and 526 swaps sweet speech for not cherishing anger; Parimelazhagar on 525 matches the pair to the northern texts' dana and sama.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:387","k:92"],["k:953","k:92"],["k:953","k:1057"],["k:224","k:1057"],["k:387","k:525"],["k:526","k:525"]],"limits":"92 sits in the chapter praising sweet speech and may be that chapter's emphasis rather than a fixed ranking. Other verses define real giving by its recipient, not its manner (221, 223), and 387 and 525 treat giving as a means to loyalty, which is not the same claim as 92's ethical ranking.","issue":"'The book rarely praises giving bare' is overstated: most of chapter 23 (ஈகை) and 231 to 232 praise giving with no word on manner, which is more than the limits' recipient verses 221 and 223. 224 is about the giver's discomfort ending when the asker is satisfied, not about the manner of giving."},{"id":"thread:two_shames","title":"One word for two shames","claim":"1011 splits நாண் in two: real shame is shame at one's deeds, and the rest is the shame of women of fair brow (Parimelazhagar: restraint of mind, speech and body proper to women of family). The Book of Love's shame is the second kind: in 1163 and 1247 it is, on Parimelazhagar's reading, what stops the woman telling him or going to him, and in 1251 it is the bolt on the door of restraint. Yet the Book of Love prizes that shame in the first kind's terms: it is the ornament that makes jewels needless (1089, as moral shame is the ornament of the worthy in 1014), and it is held dearer than life (Parimelazhagar on 1297; 1163's life strained on the pole), which is the valuation 1017 gives moral shame.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1011","k:1251"],["k:1089","k:1014"],["k:1297","k:1017"],["k:1163","k:1017"]],"limits":"The love verses are dramatised speeches, and the bridge to 1014 and 1017 is partly the commentator's: 1297 itself does not say shame is dearer than life. Kalaignar reads 1014 as remorse for a fault and Pope translates நாண் in 1017 as modesty, so the line between the two shames is drawn differently by different readers.","issue":"1163 does not rank shame above life: on Parimelazhagar love and shame hang in equal balance (ஒன்றினொன்று மிகாது) on the life-pole, so it supports 1017's valuation only loosely."},{"id":"thread:shame_gives_way","title":"In the Book of Love shame never holds","claim":"Several lover-speakers set shame against desire, and in none of them does shame survive; what varies is how it goes. 1247 puts the choice to the heart (give up desire or shame, I cannot bear both); 1132 makes it by decision, setting shame aside to ride the palmyra horse; in 1134 the flood of love carries shame off and in 1251 the axe of desire breaks the door it bolted; in 1297 it is forgotten in his absence, and in 1257 it is simply not felt in his presence (Parimelazhagar: on his return from another woman). Read together, the dilemma 1247 states is settled by force, forgetting or reunion as often as by choice.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1132","k:1247"],["k:1134","k:1251"],["k:1247","k:1251"],["k:1257","k:1247"],["k:1297","k:1247"],["k:1257","k:1297"]],"limits":"These are separate dramatised moments, and reading them as one sequence follows the chapter order, not any statement in the text. 1163 holds love and shame in balance without a loss, and 1149 is about shame at gossip rather than at desire. The man also loses shame by force in 1134, which qualifies the existing gender_asymmetry contrast between his choosing and her bearing.","issue":"The title 'never holds' overreaches the Book of Love: 1137 praises the woman who suffers a sea of desire and still does not mount the மடல் (கடலன்ன காமம் உழந்தும் மடலேறாப் பெண்), so her restraint holds there. The claim is accurate only for the verses it cites. The 1297-1247 note's 'the chapter between them' is wrong, since 1247 is in chapter 125 and 1297 in chapter 130."},{"id":"thread:inside_decides","title":"The inside decides what counts","claim":"Several verses in different domains apply one test: an outward act counts only if the mind behind it matches. Virtue is wholly a spotless mind and the rest is noisy show (34), and firmness in action is wholly firmness of mind with all else 'பிற' (661), in the same shape. Sweet speech is virtue because it comes from within (93), friendship is the smile of the heart, not the face (786), straight speech is rectitude only if there is no bend within (119), and truth spoken with the mind outranks austerity and giving (295, 294). Ritual bathing is the case in point: water reaches only the outside (298), and many bathe with impure minds (278).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:93","k:34"],["k:661","k:34"],["k:93","k:786"],["k:294","k:119"],["k:119","k:295"],["k:278","k:34"],["k:298","k:278"],["k:825","k:890"]],"limits":"The book does not make the mind self-sufficient: 455 and 459 say purity and goodness of mind rest on purity of company. It also asks elsewhere whether the inside can be read from outside at all (thread:signs_and_faces), and 829 permits a show of friendship toward enemies (thread:ethics_vs_statecraft).","issue":"661 is about firmness, not about outward acts being genuine; it shares only the shape of 34. 298 does not say water reaches 'only' the outside; it pairs outer purity from water with inner purity seen through truth."},{"id":"thread:what_one_thinks_one_gets","title":"One promise, several conditions: you obtain what you think of","claim":"The book makes nearly the same promise, that one obtains what one has thought of (உள்ளியது / எண்ணிய ... எய்தும்), three times and attaches a different inner condition each time: never harbouring anger (309), keeping one's mind on the goal without forgetting (540), and firmness (666). Parimelazhagar assigns these to different people, 309 to the ascetic and 540 to the king, so one formula serves both Aram and Porul. The power of thought reaches suffering too: a flood of trouble perishes when the wise person merely thinks it through (622), yet 7 says the mind's anxiety is hard to remove except for those who reach the feet of the incomparable one.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:309","k:540"],["k:666","k:309"],["k:666","k:540"],["k:622","k:309"],["k:7","k:622"]],"limits":"The three conditions are not rivals; commentators treat each as sufficient within its own chapter's concern. The 7-622 conflict holds only on Manakkudavar's wider reading of மனக்கவலை; Parimelazhagar narrows 7 to the sorrows of rebirth, which 622 does not address.","issue":""},{"id":"thread:lover_as_cure","title":"Is the beloved the cure for love's sickness?","claim":"Several verses make the one who causes the sickness its only medicine: one look in her eyes wounds and the other heals (1091), she is herself the medicine for the disease she gives (1102), and the woman says one day of his return would destroy all her sickness (1266). This explains why her heart can name no medicine at all in 1241, and 1275 finds the remedy only in a sign. Yet other verses deny that presence cures: union itself now holds the pain of fearing separation (1152), her eyes do not sleep whether he comes or not (1179), and her heart fears not having him and, having him, fears parting (1295).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1091","k:1102"],["k:1266","k:1102"],["k:1241","k:1102"],["k:1275","k:1091"],["k:1152","k:1295"],["k:1179","k:1295"],["k:1152","k:1179"]],"limits":"The cure verses 1091 and 1102 belong to the man in the first meetings, while the denials are the woman's in a settled relationship facing separation, so the difference may track the stage of love rather than a contradiction. The speaker of 1275 is the man for Parimelazhagar and the friend for Manakkudavar.","issue":"1241 only asks the heart for a medicine (நினைத்தொன்று சொல்லாயோ); it does not say the heart can name none, and the claim's 'this explains why' reads 1102 into it. 1275 does not place the cure only in a sign of the beloved: on Parimelazhagar the medicine is the friend's assurance that the beloved will not part, and on Manakkudavar the friend is telling the man his suit will succeed."},{"id":"thread:blaming_own_organs","title":"The woman turns on her own eyes and heart","claim":"Where the man finds the source of love's sickness in her eyes (1091), the woman in separation finds it in her own: her eyes showed him to her (1171), and she mocks them as laughable (1173), rejoices that they now suffer the sickness they gave (1176), and curses them to weep until their water is gone, which 1174 reports has happened (1177). Her heart is treated the same way across later chapters: thinking of him alone, her heart is there only to eat her (1296), remembering burns it (1207), and she rebukes it for sitting and suffering over one who has no pity (1243).","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1171","k:1091"],["k:1176","k:1173"],["k:1177","k:1174"],["k:1243","k:1296"],["k:1296","k:1207"]],"limits":"Chapters 118, 125 and 130 are built around addressing the eyes or the heart, so the pattern partly follows the book's chapter scheme; the claim is that the same self-division runs across them, not that it is a hidden theme. On Parimelazhagar's reading of 1296 the heart's harm comes from dwelling on his cruelties, not from memory as such.","issue":"1207 does not turn on the heart: in உள்ளினும் உள்ளம் சுடும் the heart is what is burned, the victim of memory and separation, so it fits the self-division theme less than 1243 and 1296, where the heart is blamed."},{"id":"thread:remembering_the_absent","title":"Does remembering the absent lover comfort or wound?","claim":"Chapter 121 opens by calling remembrance an unfailing joy: love is sweeter than toddy because even thinking of it gives delight that does not end (1201), and thinking of the beloved brings no pain (1202); the woman makes the same toddy comparison in 1281. The same chapter and later ones then make thinking the source of pain: even remembering burns (1207), her life wastes as she dwells on his want of grace (1209), and when she sits alone and thinks, her heart eats her (1296). Parimelazhagar separates the cases by what is remembered: past union in 1201, his neglect in 1209, his cruelties in 1296. Yet forgetting is refused as worse (1207, 1262), while 1250 argues that keeping him in her heart costs her beauty, the very loss 1262 attributes to forgetting.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1201","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1207"],["k:1202","k:1209"],["k:1202","k:1296"],["k:1296","k:1207"],["k:1201","k:1281"],["k:1207","k:1262"],["k:1250","k:1262"]],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches in different moods, not a doctrine of memory, and the speaker assignments (man in 1201 and 1202) are Parimelazhagar's; Manakkudavar gives 1202 to the woman and reads it as conditional. The gender side of 1202/1207 is already in thread:gender_asymmetry.","issue":"On Parimelazhagar, what the woman remembers in 1207 is the past union (அவ்வின்பத்தை), the same object as 1201, and it is the separation that burns, so 'what is remembered' does not separate every case. He also reads 1250's கவின் as the inner beauty left after the outer is lost, so it is not quite 'the very loss' of 1262's காரிகை and bangles."},{"id":"thread:lover_in_her_heart","title":"He lives in her heart: a defence that becomes a doubt","claim":"In chapters 113 and 122 the woman answers the charge that her lover is absent and loveless by locating him inside herself: in her eyes (1126), in her heart gladly and always while the town says he lives apart (1129, 1130), on her shoulder in sleep and in her heart on waking (1218), so that her heart has no one to go looking for (1249). Parimelazhagar frames several of these as replies to the friend's blame of him. In chapter 121 the same image turns: he is always in her heart, but is she in his (1204)? He bars her from his heart and is not ashamed to keep entering hers (1205), and by 1243 his lack of any pitying thought is a settled premise. Her body also contradicts the claim of presence in 1184, where constant thought does not stop the pallor.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1129","k:1130"],["k:1126","k:1218"],["k:1218","k:1249"],["k:1130","k:1204"],["k:1204","k:1205"],["k:1128","k:1205"],["k:1243","k:1204"],["k:1184","k:1130"]],"limits":"The sequence is the reader's arrangement across chapters, not a stated argument; 1205's guarded heart is read as accusation by Manakkudavar. The thread does not claim the man's verses share this doubt.","issue":"1249 is in chapter 125, not 113 or 122. Parimelazhagar ties 1184 to her recalling his parting promises (தெளிவித்த சொற்கள்) and treats the pallor as the body betraying mind and speech; reading it as undercutting 'presence in her heart' is the writer's framing."},{"id":"thread:conduct_makes_family","title":"Is good family the source of conduct or its name?","claim":"133 defines good family as conduct (ஒழுக்கம் உடைமை குடிமை) and says a lapse becomes low birth, and 1019 agrees that a lapse in principle burns one's lineage. Chapter 96 runs the other way: integrity and shame are natural only to the well-born (951), the well-born do not slip in conduct, truth and shame (952), and a lapse only makes people doubt a man's lineage (958), keeping birth as the underlying fact. The selection verses rely on chapter 96's direction, asking for good family alongside shame when choosing an officer (502) or a friend (794); on 133's definition that first test cannot be checked apart from the second.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:133","k:951"],["k:952","k:133"],["k:958","k:133"],["k:502","k:133"],["k:133","k:1019"],["k:502","k:951"],["k:794","k:951"]],"limits":"The conflict depends on reading 133 as dissolving birth into conduct; Parimelazhagar reads it within varna, as conduct keeping or losing a rank birth already gave, which removes it. This complements thread:birth_across_book (972 and the screening verses) rather than replacing it, since its 972 links are not in this packet.","issue":"Kalaignar reads 951, 952 and 502 in 133's direction (those who keep these are to be counted well-born), so chapter 96's 'birth first' direction is Parimelazhagar's and Manakkudavar's construal more than the bare Tamil. 958's failing is specifically நாரின்மை, lack of kindness (Parimelazhagar: not giving, harsh speech), not any lapse in conduct."},{"id":"thread:family_built_by_effort","title":"The family as something built, not inherited","claim":"Where chapter 96 treats குடி as inherited character, chapters 61 and 103 treat it as something kept up or raised by work: those who want their family to be a family must drive out sloth (602), the family lengthens through long effort and mature knowledge (1022), and for the slothful it dies down while faults multiply (604). The same double penalty on the family and in fault is attached to covetousness in 171, and 609 makes the decline reversible once a man ends sloth's rule over him. 1028 turns this against honour: the family-builder who idles and stands on dignity (மானம்) ruins the family, whereas 961, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, gives up even indispensable things that would lower one's birth.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:602","k:1022"],["k:1022","k:604"],["k:171","k:604"],["k:609","k:604"],["k:602","k:1028"],["k:961","k:1028"]],"limits":"The two views are not set against each other by the text; one chapter says what a family is, the other what its members owe it, and 1022/1028 can be read as about a clan's prosperity rather than its rank. The 961/1028 tension depends on reading both as concerning the family's standing.","issue":"The opening contrast with chapter 96, where குடி is inherited, has no link of its own and is asserted rather than shown. Kalaignar reads 1028's மானம் as being shaken by insults rather than standing on dignity, which would weaken the 961 tension."},{"id":"thread:greatness_forfeited","title":"Can greatness survive one small lapse?","claim":"Several verses in different books grant a person great stature and then make it hang on a single act. 124 sets the restrained householder's stature above a mountain, and 974 says greatness exists only while one keeps hold of oneself; 965 states the loss as a rule, that even the hill-like sink (குன்றுவர்) for a lowering act the size of a kunri seed, while 144 (entering another's home) and 195 (idle speech) name particular acts that undo a person's worth however great. 898 uses the same hill pun for the opposite direction: the hill-like can bring others to nothing, so in the book's imagery height gives power over others but no protection against one's own lapse.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:124","k:965"],["k:974","k:965"],["k:144","k:965"],["k:195","k:965"],["k:898","k:965"]],"limits":"The verses do not say greatness can never be regained; they only make its loss quick and cheap. Parimelazhagar reads the hill-like of 965 as those great by birth and those of 898 as ascetics, so the 'height' is not one kind of standing throughout.","issue":"On Manakkudavar, 898's குன்ற மதிப்பின் means others slighting the hill-like (அவமதித்தல்), so the hill-like are the ones diminished there too and the 'opposite direction' contrast holds only on Parimelazhagar's reading of them as ascetics who wish ruin."},{"id":"thread:greatness_by_deed","title":"Greatness measured by hard deeds, from renouncer to householder","claim":"26, in the chapter on renouncers, defines the great as those who do what is hard to do and the small as those who cannot; 975, in the Book of Wealth, says the same of those who have greatness, with no renunciation in view (Parimelazhagar adds: even when they are poor). 611 turns the definition into a process, saying effort itself produces the greatness a hard task needs, and 1021 puts the highest dignity in the resolve not to stop work. 505 and 972 generalise the test: deeds are the touchstone of greatness and smallness, and distinction differs by the work done though birth is alike.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:975","k:26"],["k:611","k:26"],["k:1021","k:611"],["k:505","k:972"]],"limits":"Parimelazhagar narrows 26's 'hard things' to yogic disciplines and reads 26 as a ranking among those of equal birth, and he takes 965's hill-like as great by birth; the book elsewhere treats birth as a real factor (see thread:birth_across_book). 26 can also be read as a fixed difference between people, which 611 does not say.","issue":"Parimelazhagar's gloss on 505 counts birth, quality and knowledge (பிறப்பு குணம் அறிவு) as sources of greatness and makes deeds only the test of them, so 505 does not by itself say birth is irrelevant."},{"id":"thread:effort_never_wasted","title":"Effort that pays whatever fate does","claim":"Against 380, where fate outruns every plan, several verses give effort a return in each case fate can take. If fate denies the outcome, effort still pays the wage of the body's strain (619), and a high aim thrust aside keeps the quality of not having been thrust aside (596, which Parimelazhagar reads as thwarted by fate); if the divine helps, it girds itself and goes ahead of the one resolved to raise his family (1023), and for those who toil without delay the end comes of itself (1024). Persistence is said to trouble trouble itself (624) and to see even fate's back (620). Parimelazhagar draws the conclusion at 619: since effort is not wasted either way, one should strive without waiting on fate.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:380","k:619"],["k:596","k:619"],["k:1023","k:619"],["k:620","k:1024"],["k:624","k:620"]],"limits":"This thread is about how the book secures effort's value, not whether effort is free; 371 makes effort itself a product of fate, a conflict taken up in thread:fate_and_agency. Parimelazhagar narrows 620 to fate blocking only one or two attempts.","issue":""},{"id":"thread:conditions_on_effort","title":"Effort praised without condition, then bounded","claim":"616 says flatly that effort makes fortune and its absence brings want, and 596 sets no ceiling on aspiration, but verses elsewhere fix effort's conditions. 468 says exertion not made by the right method collapses however many guard it; 476 says that pushing beyond the tip of the branch ends one's life; 14 makes the plough, which 1031 says the whole world follows, itself depend on the rain. 739 even defines the true land as one whose wealth comes without seeking, though 1040 has the land mock those who sit idle claiming poverty.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:468","k:616"],["k:476","k:596"],["k:14","k:1031"],["k:739","k:1040"]],"limits":"None of these verses denies that effort is necessary; they add method, measure of strength and natural supply as further conditions. The fate-based limit (371) is treated separately in thread:fate_and_agency.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 596 as addressed to kings and takes its தள்ளினும் as thwarting by fate (பால்வகை), so 596 itself concedes a limit. 739 concerns a country's natural endowment for its people, not the sufficiency of individual effort, so its pairing with 1040 is loose."},{"id":"thread:sulk_versus_union","title":"Which is sweeter, union or the sulk?","claim":"The first union chapter sets sleeping on the beloved's soft arms above the lotus-eyed god's world (1103) and lists sulking, making up and uniting as love's rewards without ranking them (1109). The closing chapter re-ranks: sulking with one joined as water to earth outdoes heaven (1323), being kept from those same soft arms (தாம்வீழ்வார் மென்றோள், the phrase of 1103) 'has a certain something' (1325), and sulking is sweeter than union as digesting is sweeter than eating (1326). Yet the last verse makes the sulk's pleasure depend on the embrace that follows (கூடி முயங்கப் பெறின், 1330), and 1307 finds pain inside the sulk in the fear that union will be delayed, so the sulk outranks union only as a way back to it. Parimelazhagar already reads 1109's ஊடல் as needed so that union becomes sweet.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1323","k:1103"],["k:1325","k:1103"],["k:1109","k:1326"],["k:1326","k:1330"],["k:1307","k:1330"]],"limits":"These are lovers' speeches, mostly the man's in the joy-after-sulking chapter, not the author ranking pleasures in his own voice; and the ranking is of moments within one marriage, not of union against sulking as rival goods.","issue":"'The last verse' (1330) is Parimelazhagar's order; Manakkudavar ends the book at 1323. Manakkudavar takes the world in 1103 as Indra's heaven, while Parimelazhagar takes it as the world ascetics attain."},{"id":"thread:smallest_gap","title":"How small a gap counts as separation?","claim":"1108 calls sweet the embrace that not even a breeze can split (வளியிடை போழப் படாஅ). Later verses test that ideal and find the threshold of separation at exactly that size: a cool breeze splitting the embrace, with the same verb போழ, pales her eyes (1239); his loosening his arms pales her forehead (1238); she shifted slightly and pallor came thick enough to scoop up (1187); pallor watches for any lapse in the embrace as darkness watches for a lamp to fail (1186). The woman and the man report it from either side. On Parimelazhagar's reading the man recalls 1238 and 1239 on his way home from a campaign, so the tiny gap is the measure against which the present separation by hills and forests is felt.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1108","k:1187"],["k:1187","k:1239"],["k:1239","k:1108"],["k:1186","k:1238"]],"limits":"1108 itself says nothing of pallor, and Parimelazhagar frames it as a plea for marriage (union without interruption); the pallor verses are hyperbole from the separation chapters, not a separate doctrine.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 1238 and 1239 as one remembered incident (the breeze entered because he loosened his arms), so they are one threshold seen twice, not two. He reads 1186-1187 and 1238-1239 alike as a fortiori arguments: if this for a slight gap, what now in separation? That reading strengthens the claim."},{"id":"thread:eyes_tell_secrets","title":"Eyes that say what the mouth withholds","claim":"Across the Book of Love, man, woman and friend all treat the eyes as a channel that speaks where the mouth does not, and that cannot be kept quiet. When eyes agree, words are useless (1100); telling love's sickness with the eyes and pleading adds womanhood to womanhood (1280, which Parimelazhagar glosses as not saying it by mouth even to her friends); eyes like beaten drums let the town learn her secret (1180); though she hides it, her kohl-lined eyes go beyond her control and have something to say (1271); her pale, weeping eyes seem to tell others of his unkindness (1232). In love, concealment fails at the eyes.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1100","k:1280"],["k:1180","k:1271"],["k:1232","k:1180"]],"limits":"This holds for lovers' eyes; the Book of Wealth says enemies' smiles can hide hostility even from the face (824, see thread:signs_and_faces), so it is not a general claim that faces are honest. 1232 says only that the eyes seem to tell, and the speakers of 1100 and 1280 differ between Parimelazhagar and Manakkudavar.","issue":"1100 and 1280 are about eyes chosen as the channel, not eyes that betray; the claim's 'cannot be kept quiet' rests on 1180, 1271 and 1232 alone."},{"id":"thread:seen_by_many","title":"What many eyes see is no longer one's own","claim":"The man disqualifies flowers as likenesses of her eyes because many people see them (பலர்காணும் பூ, 1112), and tells the moon it may resemble her face only if it stops appearing for many to see (பலர்காணத் தோன்றல், 1119); Parimelazhagar glosses both as his having her beauty for himself alone. The woman applies the same standard to him: she refuses his chest because all women feast on him in common with their eyes (கண்ணின் பொதுஉண்பர், 1311), which Parimelazhagar takes as making it others' leftovers, and she suspects even his gaze at her of being on another woman (1320). Looking is treated as a kind of possession, by both lovers.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:1119","k:1112"],["k:1119","k:1311"],["k:1320","k:1311"]],"limits":"1311 and 1320 come from the chapter on subtle sulking, where Parimelazhagar presents the woman's charges as pretexts for a lover's sulk, and 1112 and 1119 are praise hyperbole; neither side states a rule about fidelity.","issue":"Only two links carry the 'seen by many' standard; 1320 is about whom he is thinking of as he looks (யாருள்ளி நோக்கினீர்), not about being seen by many, so its fit with 'looking as possession' is loose."},{"id":"thread:one_birth_seven_births","title":"One birth's leverage on seven","claim":"Several verses across Aram and Porul share a fixed arithmetic: what is done in one birth (ஒருமை) settles the next seven (எழுமை). Restraint of the five senses (126) and learning (398) end in the identical formula எழுமையும் ஏமாப்புடைத்து, and blameless children keep evil off for seven births (62); the same reckoning runs the other way when the fool makes in one birth the mire he sinks in for seven (835) and neglect of what is praised leaves nothing through seven (538). Taken together they make a single life the lever on all later ones, for good or ill, and place sense-restraint, learning and children side by side as goods that outlast the body.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:126","k:398"],["k:835","k:398"],["k:126","k:835"],["k:62","k:398"],["k:62","k:835"],["k:538","k:835"]],"limits":"This is a recurring formula in five verses, not a doctrine the book argues for. Parimelazhagar reads எழுமை as seven future births, while Kalaignar renders it as seven generations or simply all time, which removes rebirth from these verses.","issue":"Only 126, 398 and 835 pair ஒருமை with எழுமை; 62 (எழுபிறப்பும்) and 538 (எழுமையும்) give the seven-birth consequence without the one-birth term. 107 (எழுமை எழுபிறப்பும் உள்ளுவர்) is another எழுமை verse the thread does not mention."},{"id":"thread:births_to_secure_or_end","title":"Births: a span to secure or a cycle to end?","claim":"The chapters on renunciation and true knowledge treat birth itself as the evil: craving is its seed (361) and cutting attachment cuts it off (349); delusion produces a joyless birth (351) and birth is then called a folly (358); learning that sees the real leads to the path of not returning (356), and certain knowledge makes further birth unthinkable (357), against 339's endless round of sleep and waking. Elsewhere births are a span to protect, and the two attitudes meet over learning: in 398 it protects through seven births, in 356 it ends them. Likewise 73 gives the body-bond a purpose in love, while for one set on cutting births even the body is excess (345).","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:356","k:398"],["k:339","k:356"],["k:349","k:361"],["k:358","k:351"],["k:357","k:356"],["k:345","k:73"]],"limits":"The verses never set the two attitudes against each other; on Parimelazhagar's reading they belong to different stages, the householder and the renouncer, so this is a shift of aim rather than a contradiction. Kalaignar reads 356 as not returning to household life, which drops rebirth from it.","issue":"The second attitude is thin: 398 values learning carried across births (எழுமையும் ஏமாப்பு), not births as a span to secure, and Kalaignar reads எழுமை as generations. 73 values the life-body bond as the fruit of love, which is the only real counterweight. The 'birth as evil' half is well supported; the contrast the title draws rests on two weak verses."},{"id":"thread:king_falls_by_himself","title":"Who guards the king?","claim":"Several verses across chapters 39, 45, 55 and 56 place a ruler's safety and ruin inside his own conduct rather than with enemies. Justice, which earns him the name இறை in 388, is what guards him in 547 if he practises it without fail. A king without rebukers perishes with no one to destroy him (448), and the unapproachable king who does not examine and do justice perishes by himself (548), while neglect of daily justice ruins his land day by day (553). The temperament that prevents this is the ability to bear bitter words (389), which Parimelazhagar reads as the speech of the rebuking companions of 447 and 448.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:388","k:547"],["k:547","k:448"],["k:548","k:448"],["k:548","k:553"],["k:389","k:448"],["k:389","k:447"]],"limits":"The claim is about how these verses frame the king's fall; the book elsewhere treats foreign enemies and fortifications at length. Manakkudavar reads 389 differently, so the 389 links depend on Parimelazhagar.","issue":"553 has the land ruined day by day, not the king perishing by himself, so it widens the claim from the ruler's safety to the realm's."},{"id":"thread:sceptre_as_rain","title":"The sceptre as rain","claim":"Chapters 55 and 56 build the king's rule onto the image of rain. People live looking to the sceptre as the world lives looking to the sky (542); the just sceptre brings rain and harvest together (545), and the bent one stops the rain (559); the king's lack of kindness is to his people what drought is to the earth (557), and when the guardian does not guard, cows yield less and the learned forget their books (560), the books whose origin 543 makes the sceptre. Read together, the verses present just rule not only as like rain but, on Parimelazhagar's reading of 545 and 559, as a cause of the actual weather.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:559","k:545"],["k:542","k:557"],["k:560","k:543"],["k:390","k:557"],["k:528","k:542"]],"limits":"Whether the weather verses are literal is a commentators' dispute: Kalaignar turns 545 into a comparison and 559 into neglected reservoirs. 542 and 557 are explicit comparisons and do not by themselves claim moral weather.","issue":"The links from 390 and 528 add little: 390 only lists அளி, and 528's 'live looking to' concerns kin and the king's recognition of rank, not rain."},{"id":"thread:enmity_shunned_and_sought","title":"Is enmity to be shunned or sought?","claim":"Chapters 86 to 88 give both answers. 871 says enmity, having no good in it, should not be wanted even in jest, and 852 and 855 praise not returning harm and leaning away from hostility. Yet 861 says to keep enmity with the weak, 867 says a certain man's enmity must be got even by paying for it, 879 says to fell the thorn tree while young, and 880 says those who do not break a foe's pride are not even alive, with 759 naming wealth as the blade for it. The same section prizes turning enmity into friendship (874), while 830 says an enemy who turns friend gets only one's face.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:861","k:871"],["k:871","k:867"],["k:879","k:852"],["k:880","k:759"],["k:855","k:874"],["k:874","k:830"]],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reconciles 871 with 861 and 867 by limiting it to enmity that brings no gain (மாணாத பகை); the verses do not draw that line. Kalaignar reads 861 the other way, as fighting the strong rather than the weak, which removes one side of the conflict on his reading.","issue":"874 and 830 are less opposed than the claim suggests: Parimelazhagar reads 874's turning enmity to friendship as a king's policy used when needed (வேண்டியவழி), and Manakkudavar reads it as treating enemies like friends, which is close to 830's face-only friendship. Manakkudavar narrows 867 too, saying such a foe cannot do real harm, so the conflict with 871 is eased by both commentators, not by Parimelazhagar alone."},{"id":"thread:enemy_within","title":"The enemy inside is worse than the enemy outside","claim":"Across the chapters on ministers, the land, bad friendship and inner enmity, the book ranks the hidden or intimate enemy below the open one. 639 and 817 share one construction, setting crores of open enemies against a single plotting minister or a laughing friend; 882 gives the rule (fear not the sword-like foe but the kin-like one), 828 shows the weapon hidden in folded hands, and 735 adds inner enmity (உட்பகை) to 734's outside threats to a land.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:639","k:817"],["k:639","k:882"],["k:882","k:828"],["k:734","k:735"]],"limits":"Parimelazhagar reads உறும் in 639 as 'that many enemies will arise' rather than 'is better', which weakens its parallel with 817. 734-735 only lists the inner enemy beside the outer one and does not rank them.","issue":"'Ranks the hidden enemy below the open one' means 'as worse than', which the wording leaves ambiguous. Chapter 89 (883, 884, 889) adds direct support the thread does not use."},{"id":"thread:reading_signs_court_and_love","title":"One art of reading signs, in court and in love","claim":"Chapters 71 and 110 share the title குறிப்பறிதல், and the verses apply one theory of the speaking eye and face in both books. The minister who knows the king's mind without being told (701), the eye that reads a sign by a sign (705), the face one need only stand before (708) and the eye that tells enmity and friendship (709) return in love as eyes that agree and make words useless (1100), eyes that speak against the beloved's will (1271, using the same verb உரை as 709), and the crystal image of 706 in 1273. In court the reading is a rare skill a subordinate exercises on a ruler; in love it is mutual and replaces speech.","grade":"partly_supported","links":[["k:701","k:1100"],["k:708","k:1100"],["k:709","k:1100"],["k:1100","k:705"],["k:1271","k:709"],["k:1273","k:706"]],"limits":"The book does not cross-refer between the chapters; the parallel rests on shared title, vocabulary and images. Parimelazhagar adds for 708 that speaking would lessen both parties, a concern for dignity the love verses do not share.","issue":"The love-side verses 1271 and 1273 are not mutual: the man reads a sign she hides, and in 1273 Parimelazhagar has him admit he cannot read it and ask the friend. In court the reading is not only of a ruler: in 709 Parimelazhagar's ministers read foreign kings' eyes, and in 708 the one read is a petitioner. Parimelazhagar takes 1100's வாய்ச்சொற்கள் as words not meant in the heart, so the eyes expose speech rather than simply replacing it."},{"id":"thread:signs_and_faces","title":"Can signs be trusted?","claim":"The face shows what is strongest in the heart (706) and gets there before the mind (707), and the love books push this further: bangles know of the lover's parting before the woman does (1277), and desire breaks out like a sneeze without intent (1253). Yet signs can be worn against the heart: deceivers smile sweetly while bitter within (824, against 706), lovers put on hostile looks while close (1097), which complicates 709's reading of enmity in the eye, and in 1312 the woman takes his sneeze, the type of the involuntary in 1253, as calculated. The book holds both that the body betrays the heart and that it can be made to lie, and leaves the reader's skill to decide.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:824","k:706"],["k:1097","k:824"],["k:1097","k:709"],["k:707","k:1277"],["k:1312","k:1253"]],"limits":"The feigned signs differ in kind: 824 is an enemy's deception, 1097 a lover's play, read with relief rather than fear. This extends the existing thread; its link 824-826 (enemies' words soon seen through) lies outside this packet and should be kept when merging, as should the note that 71 and 828 on tears do not conflict.","issue":"Parimelazhagar reads 1277's தணந்தமை as his mental separation while still physically with her, so the bangles detect an inner change, which strengthens the 'signs before mind' point."},{"id":"thread:sulk_defeated_by_heart","title":"Why the planned sulk always fails","claim":"Across four chapters of the Book of Love the woman sets out to sulk and her own heart defeats the plan. 1246 predicts it (the heart's anger is false heat, since it cannot hold out once it sees him), 1259 and 1284 report it as something that happened (she went to sulk; the heart went to unite), 1260 gives the reason as a general nature (hearts like fat in fire cannot say 'we will hold out in a sulk'), and 1294 draws the consequence that she will no longer plan with her heart. Together they treat ஊடல், the sulk that a lover's art requires, as a plan the speaker's own heart reliably breaks.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1259","k:1284"],["k:1260","k:1259"],["k:1284","k:1246"],["k:1284","k:1294"],["k:1246","k:1294"],["k:1294","k:1260"]],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches of one speaker and do not claim the sulk is never kept; the man's 1310 calls his own heart's wish to join mere craving, so the pattern is not confined to the woman, but this thread rests on her verses.","issue":"In 1246 it is the heart's own anger, refusing to go to him over his cruelty in Parimelazhagar's frame, that she calls false. So 1246 is not her plan to sulk but the heart's pretended sulk, which still fits the pattern."},{"id":"thread:whose_heart_is_whose","title":"Whose heart belongs to whom?","claim":"A complaint about the ownership of hearts runs from chapter 121 to the close of chapter 130. 1204 asks whether she has a place in his heart since he is always in hers; 1205 answers that he bars her from his heart yet keeps entering hers, and 1243 takes his lack of pitying thought as settled. 1291 turns the same imbalance inward: his heart stays his, so why is hers not hers? 1299 and 1300 generalise it (when one's own heart is no companion or kin, no stranger can be), and Parimelazhagar gives these two to the man, so the chapter ends with the complaint voiced from the other side.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1204","k:1205"],["k:1243","k:1204"],["k:1205","k:1291"],["k:1300","k:1291"],["k:1299","k:1300"]],"limits":"Pope reads 1205 the other way (he has imprisoned her in his heart), and Manakkudavar gives 1299 to the woman, which removes the two-sided ending. Elsewhere his dwelling in her heart is something she cherishes, not a grievance (1128, 1218, 1249).","issue":""},{"id":"thread:renown_as_remainder","title":"A life judged by what remains of it","claim":"Several verses measure a life by what is left after it (எச்சம்) and make renown that remainder: 238 calls it a disgrace to leave no legacy of renown, 1004 asks what the unwanted hoarder imagines will remain of him, and 233 says nothing but renown stands undying. The same logic reaches death in war: 777's warrior wants fame more than life, and 235, as Parimelazhagar glosses it, explains a death that is existence through the body of fame outliving the body of flesh; 970 shows the world praising those who choose death over disgrace, as 240 says a life without renown is no life.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:238","k:114"],["k:238","k:1004"],["k:1004","k:114"],["k:1004","k:233"],["k:777","k:235"],["k:970","k:235"],["k:240","k:970"]],"limits":"The word எச்சம் is read differently: Parimelazhagar takes it as offspring in 114 but as surviving renown in 1004, so 114 belongs to the thread only on the Kalaignar-style reading. The link from 235 to the warrior depends on Parimelazhagar's gloss of a compressed verse.","issue":"970 is in the chapter on honour (மானம்): what it describes is refusing to live under disgrace and being praised for it, so it says nothing directly about fame outliving the body. The war-death strand depends on Parimelazhagar's gloss of 235 and also on reading 970 through 235."},{"id":"thread:sources_of_renown","title":"Many roads to renown, and one it may not take","claim":"The book names renown as the reward of different virtues: giving (231, 232), not lying (296, 'no renown like it'), forbearance (156) and dropping hostility (853, renown on Parimelazhagar's reading), and dying in battle (777, set beside giving in 231). 232 and 296 both speak as if naming the chief source, though Parimelazhagar narrows 296 to the cause of renown in this life. Then the Book of Wealth limits renown as a motive: 652 makes it one test of an act, but 962 says those who want honour will not do the unworthy even for renown, taking up the case 653 leaves open.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:232","k:296"],["k:777","k:231"],["k:233","k:156"],["k:853","k:156"],["k:962","k:652"],["k:653","k:962"]],"limits":"The superlatives may be rhetorical rather than a ranking (compare thread:competing_supremes). 652 pairs renown with good (or, for Parimelazhagar, with virtue hereafter), so it never made renown alone sufficient; 962 sharpens rather than reverses it.","issue":"Parimelazhagar sends several of these roads back through giving: he grounds 233's unmatched renown in giving life, limbs and goods, and 853's renown in friends, wealth and then கொடை. On his reading the sources converge more than the claim suggests."},{"id":"thread:maanam_virtue_and_fault","title":"Honour as virtue and as fault","claim":"The same word மானம் is required of the king, paired with valour (384), and of the army (766), and chapter 97 ranks it above even what is indispensable (961, which Parimelazhagar reads as life itself). Yet 432 lists மாண்பிறந்த மானம், honour that has passed beyond the fine, among a ruler's faults, and 1028 says the one raising his family ruins it if he idles and minds his மானம். The book marks the line between the virtue and the fault by a qualifier or by context, not by a separate word, so the term cannot be read as uniformly praised.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:384","k:766"],["k:432","k:384"],["k:432","k:1028"],["k:961","k:1028"]],"limits":"Commentators fill the boundary differently: Parimelazhagar makes 432's fault refusing to bow to elders and forcing through what was resolved, and 1028's a resentment at suffering for the family's sake, while Kalaignar reads 1028 as being shaken by insult. On those readings 961 and 1028 do not strictly conflict.","issue":"961 does not use the word மானம்; its place in the honour chapter and Parimelazhagar's gloss (lowering of one's birth) supply the link. 384's மானம் is also qualified (மறனிழுக்கா), so qualifiers mark the virtue as well as the fault."},{"id":"thread:life_ranked_below","title":"What is worth more than staying alive?","claim":"Four chapters rank continued life below another value in the same form: one gives up life rather than honour (969, 968, 970), rather than shame (1017), or to keep a vow in battle (779). 971 turns the ranking into a definition: disgrace is saying 'we will live without it'. Parimelazhagar gives the shared reason at 969: the body passes and honour lasts. Read together, these verses make 'choosing to live on' itself the mark of the lesser person across civil, military and inner virtue.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:969","k:779"],["k:968","k:1017"],["k:1017","k:969"],["k:970","k:971"]],"limits":"971's 'it' is aspiration (Parimelazhagar's ஊக்கம்), not honour, and 779 concerns a soldier's vow. In the Book of Love 1132 sets shame aside to save life (see thread:love_overturns), and 963 and 988 answer shrunken circumstances with bearing, not death.","issue":"Parimelazhagar ties 968's பெருந்தகைமை to the honour that comes with high birth (உயர்குடிப் பிறப்பு), so on his reading part of this ranking belongs to the well-born rather than to everyone."},{"id":"thread:body_betrays_secret","title":"Who gives the secret away?","claim":"Although chapter 115 is about the town's gossip, verses across chapters 114, 116, 118, 124 and 126 locate the leak in the lover herself: her love reels through the street (1139), her eyes are beaten drums that make her secret easy to learn (1180), slipping bangles and wasted shoulders announce his leaving (1157, 1233, 1235), and the same line, மறையிறந்து மன்று படும், is said of love in general (1138) and then of herself after she thought herself firm (1254). Together they make the town's talk downstream of what her own body publishes.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1139","k:1180"],["k:1254","k:1180"],["k:1254","k:1138"],["k:1232","k:1180"],["k:1157","k:1235"],["k:1233","k:1157"]],"limits":"Most of these are framed as 'seems to' (போலும்) or rhetorical questions, and Parimelazhagar gives 1233 and 1235 to the friend (Manakkudavar gives 1233 to the woman). The thread does not claim she wishes the secret known.","issue":"Both commentators read 1157's bangles as telling the woman or her friend of his leaving (Parimelazhagar: உனக்கு அறிவியாவோ; Manakkudavar: எமக்கு அறிவியாவோ), not as broadcasting it to the town. 1139 has her love, not her body, reeling through the street."},{"id":"thread:blame_for_the_absent_lover","title":"Should the absent lover be blamed?","claim":"Several speeches have the woman rebut the town's verdict that he is loveless or has left her, by appeal to a presence it cannot see: in her unblinking eye (1129), her heart (1130) and her dreams (1219, 1220); Parimelazhagar frames 1129 and 1219 as replies to the friend's blaming him. But 1188 complains the opposite, that people talk only of her pallor and no one says he left her, and 1190 then accepts that name if only his unkindness is not spread. The friend's voice in 1232 and 1235 (on Parimelazhagar's assignment) says her body itself reports his unkindness. The book keeps her divided between wanting his fault named and shielding him from it.","grade":"supported","links":[["k:1129","k:1130"],["k:1129","k:1220"],["k:1220","k:1130"],["k:1140","k:1219"],["k:1188","k:1190"],["k:1190","k:1232"],["k:1235","k:1188"]],"limits":"These are dramatised speeches in different situations, and 1188's 'no one' and 1220's townspeople need not be the same audience. On Parimelazhagar's reading the laughers of 1140 are the friend's teasing, not the town.","issue":"The 'wanting his fault named' side rests on 1188 alone, and this edition has no Parimelazhagar or Manakkudavar gloss for that verse, so its reading follows Pope and Kalaignar. In 1190 Parimelazhagar's தூற்றார் are நட்டோர, close friends, not the town."}]};
